window.STORY = {"title": "The Future Will Pay", "subtitle": "Remain at your station and await relief.", "author": "A novel", "versions": [{"id": "v1", "label": "Original", "note": "The first draft, exactly as it was written.", "chapters": [{"file": "00-prologue.md", "label": "Prologue", "title": "Nine Minutes", "part": "", "partStart": false, "words": 3925, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The vacuum will lend to anyone. It is the most patient creditor in the universe. It has never once asked to be repaid on time.</p><span class=\"attr\">Iris Hollowell, lecture notes, Year 3 of Continuance</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">At eight minutes to two in the morning on the eve of Continuance Day, Ruth Tally stood in the Crown of the Hollowell Draw and watched a man from the Ministry comb his hair in the reflection of the thing that powered the world.</p>\n<p>The Crown was the highest room in the station, a lens-shaped gallery of black keelstone perched at the top of Spindle One, four kilometres above the floor of the Vault. Its window was a single curved sheet of armoured glass forty metres long, and through the window, above and a little ahead, hung the Lesion. It did not look like much. People who came up for the first time always said that. They expected fire, or a whirlpool, or some dreadful eye. What they got was a point of white-violet light so small and hard it seemed less like an object than a flaw in the glass — a nail driven through the night. You could not look at it for long. You could not quite look away from it either. The engineers had a saying about that, which was that the Lesion looked back, and they said it as a joke, mostly.</p>\n<p>Deputy Minister Aldous Crane leaned close to his own reflection, which was superimposed on the Lesion’s, and pressed a strand of silver hair into place with two fingers.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” he asked the camera operator. “I don’t want to squint. The Minister squinted in the Year Eighty footage and she’s never forgiven the crew.”</p>\n<p>“We’ll stop down,” said the operator, a young woman with a shaved head and a Ministry lanyard. “You’ll look like a painting, sir.”</p>\n<p>“A painting.” Crane seemed to consider this and find it acceptable. On a chain around his neck, lying against his dark jacket like a pendant, was a key: a flat, heavy, blackish thing the length of a finger, cut from keelstone and stamped along its shaft. Ruth had seen it once before, in a photograph in the training manual. She had not expected to see it in her own control room, being worn as jewellery.</p>\n<p>“Tally,” said Crane, without turning round. “How long until we’re ready?”</p>\n<p>Ruth looked at the board. Her board: fourteen years she’d been in the Crown, six as shift engineer, and she could have read it with her eyes shut by the sound of the relays. The Lattice was at nominal. The Rollover was feeding at seventy-one percent, which was a number that would once have caused a scandal and now caused a memo. Tensioner Six was running a bearing temperature eleven degrees above the line, as it had been for three quarters, because its maintenance slot had been deferred three quarters running to keep output up for the export contracts.</p>\n<p>“We’re not,” she said.</p>\n<p>Now he turned.</p>\n<p>He had a good face for the Commonweal: open, confident, a little lined in the right places, the kind of face that could say <em>there is no cause for concern</em> and make you feel ashamed for having been concerned. He smiled at her with it.</p>\n<p>“Ruth. It is Ruth? Ruth, I understand. Believe me. I’ve read the protocol.”</p>\n<p>“Then you’ve read that the Deepening Test requires the Lattice interlocks to be taken offline for the duration.”</p>\n<p>“For eleven minutes.”</p>\n<p>“For eleven minutes during which, if anything happens to a tensioner, the Lattice can’t compensate on its own. It’s a test we run in the spring, with the full day crew, the Chief Engineer, and two spare tensioner rotors spun and waiting. Not at two in the morning with a night shift, the Chief on leave, and Six running hot.”</p>\n<p>“Six has been running hot since the summer,” said Crane. “I’ve seen the reports too. And Six is still here.”</p>\n<p>Behind her, at the tensioner desk, Fen Marsh made a small noise that could have been a cough. Ruth didn’t look round. Fen had two children and a mortgage on a Tier Twelve flat and had once, at a shift party, drunkenly explained to Ruth that he believed in the Continuance the way he believed in gravity: not with any enthusiasm, but because it was stupid not to.</p>\n<p>“Deputy Minister,” Ruth said, “respectfully. It’s not my call. It’s the Chief Engineer’s call, and he’s not here, so it goes to the Director of Operations, who is asleep in Tessarine and whom I tried to wake an hour ago.”</p>\n<p>“Director Obi has been informed,” said Crane’s aide, a narrow young man with a tablet, “and has deferred to the Ministry.”</p>\n<p>Ruth looked at him. He looked at the tablet.</p>\n<p>“At dawn,” Crane said, in the patient voice of a man explaining something to a child whom he likes, “the Minister of Continuance is going to stand in front of the Quota clock in Hollowell Square with a hundred thousand people and eleven heads of state watching and announce the Second Deepening. Thirty percent more output. Thirty percent, Ruth. Do you know what that means?”</p>\n<p>“It means thirty percent more debt.”</p>\n<p>A pause, very slight.</p>\n<p>“It means,” said Crane, “that the Aurelian Compact signs the trunk-line extension, which means we power forty million homes that are currently burning coal in the dark. It means the Canopy stays up through the storm season. It means your daughter — Maddy, isn’t it? — grows up in the safest, brightest city that has ever existed. And the Minister would like to show footage of the test, conducted tonight, live, successfully, from this room, by you, because a test is a promise and a promise is only worth something if you keep it.” He smiled again. “She asked for you, you know. By name. She said, ‘Tally’s the best we have.’ I’d hate to have to tell her she was wrong.”</p>\n<p>It was very gently done. That was the thing about the Commonweal: nobody ever raised their voice. Nobody ever said <em>or else</em>. They said your daughter’s name, and they said it kindly, and they mentioned that Maddy’s placement at the Upper Tier academy was, like all placements, subject to annual review by the Ministry, and they did not mention it at all, and you heard it anyway.</p>\n<p>Ruth thought about Maddy, asleep in her mother’s flat on Tier Nine with her feet out of the covers and the glow-in-the-dark fish her father had stuck to the ceiling before he left. She thought about the eleven degrees on Six. She thought: <em>it will probably be fine</em>. That was the sentence. That was the whole machine. Ninety-one years of it had run on that sentence. It will probably be fine.</p>\n<p>“Eleven minutes,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Eleven minutes,” said Crane, and patted her shoulder, and went to stand where the light was best.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They ran it by the book because there was nothing else to run it by.</p>\n<p>“Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol, test designation Continuance Ninety-One.”</p>\n<p>“Lattice standing by.”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to manual.”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to manual, confirm.” Fen’s voice. “Feeding at seventy-one, holding.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks.” Ruth’s thumb hovered over the key switch. There was a moment when she could have not done it. She would remember that moment for the rest of her life, which would turn out to be a strange and very long life, in a place she could not at that moment have imagined; she would remember it the way you remember the last step before a stair you didn’t know was missing. “Interlocks off.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks off, confirm.”</p>\n<p>The Crown did not change. The window did not change. Down on the board, a row of green lights went amber, which meant only that the Lattice was now trusting its tensioners to hold themselves, the way a trapeze artist trusts her hands after the net’s been taken away.</p>\n<p>“Camera’s rolling,” said the operator softly.</p>\n<p>“Commence Deepening. Draw to one-oh-five.”</p>\n<p>“One-oh-five.”</p>\n<p>It came up through the floor first. It always did. There was a note the Lesion made when it was being worked — not a sound exactly, more a pressure in the long bones — and every engineer in the Draw knew it and none of them had a good word for it. Ruth’s grandmother, who had been Lattice crew in the first generation, used to call it <em>singing</em>. The Lesion was singing now, a long low swell that made the water in Dieter Hask’s cup shiver into rings.</p>\n<p>“One-ten.”</p>\n<p>“Output at sixty-eight terawatts,” Dieter said from the output desk. “Sixty-nine.”</p>\n<p>“One-fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“Six is at plus fourteen,” said Fen. “Plus fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“Noted.”</p>\n<p>“One-twenty.”</p>\n<p>“Seventy-two terawatts.” Dieter’s voice had gone very flat. “That’s a record.”</p>\n<p>“Of course it is,” murmured Crane, for the camera.</p>\n<p>Out in the window, the Lesion had not grown, but it had sharpened; it had the look of something pulled taut. Ruth had seen it like this twice before, in spring tests, surrounded by the day crew with their coffee and their jokes and the Chief Engineer stalking up and down behind them humming. She had never seen it at night, with the Vault below them dark but for the work-lights on the spindles, eight black pillars rising out of the floor into the glare like the columns of a drowned temple.</p>\n<p>“One-twenty-five.”</p>\n<p>“Six is plus nineteen.”</p>\n<p>“One-thirty.”</p>\n<p>“Plus twenty-two. Ruth.”</p>\n<p>“I see it.”</p>\n<p>“It’s over the line.”</p>\n<p>“I see it, Fen. Holding at one-thirty. Deputy Minister, we’re at the protocol maximum, we have your number, I’m bringing it down.”</p>\n<p>“Five more,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>She turned. He was not looking at her; he was looking at the camera, and his face was lit white-violet on one side, and he said it again, pleasantly, to the lens: “Five more, Ruth. Let’s give them a round number. One-thirty-five.”</p>\n<p>“The protocol maximum is one-thirty.”</p>\n<p>“And the Minister has authorised one-thirty-five.” His aide held up the tablet as if it were a holy relic. “It’s in the order. Five more, and then you can bring it down, and we’ll all go home and watch ourselves on the news.”</p>\n<p>Afterwards — and there would be a great deal of afterwards — Ruth would try to decide what she had been thinking when she said <em>one-thirty-five</em>. She would write it down in a logbook, in pencil, in a hand that got smaller as the years went on and the paper ran out, and she would never get it right. The truest version she ever wrote was this: <em>I thought that I was tired. I thought that I had been told. I thought it would probably be fine.</em></p>\n<p>“One-thirty-five,” she said.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall of the Crown began to run fast.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was an ordinary clock, an analogue one with a sweep hand, put up in the Crown by some engineer decades ago as a joke about the station’s timekeeping. Ruth saw the sweep hand go round once in what felt like half a second. Then again. Then it was a blur.</p>\n<p>“Six,” said Fen. “Six is⁠—”</p>\n<p>The floor dropped. Not the floor: her weight. Everyone in the Crown staggered at once as though the room had fallen a metre and been caught. The camera operator went down on one knee. Dieter’s cup went off the desk and did not fall. Ruth saw it. It left the edge of the desk and hung, tilting, a little brown tongue of coffee reaching out of it in a slow curl, and it went down through the air as if through honey, and it took so long to fall that she had time to look away from it and look back.</p>\n<p>“Six has unseated,” Fen said, and his voice came to her strangely, the words arriving a hair before she saw his mouth make them. “Six is off its bearing. Six is off. Lattice is⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to full,” Ruth said. “Interlocks on. Interlocks <em>on</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks on.” The amber row went green, and went amber again, and went red.</p>\n<p>“Lattice not accepting.”</p>\n<p>“Draw to zero. Kill the Draw. <em>Kill it.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Draw at zero.” Dieter was hitting the panel with the flat of his hand. “Draw at zero, it’s — output’s still climbing, it’s not us, it’s not us drawing, it’s coming out on its own⁠—”</p>\n<p>And out in the window, the Lesion broke.</p>\n<p>She would try to describe it later, too, and fail. The nearest she came was this: it was like watching frost form on a pane of glass, if the frost were made of light, and if the pane were the world. The hard white point did not explode. It did not grow. Its <em>edge</em> — the edge that had always been a simple, perfect boundary — came apart into edges, and those edges into more edges, branching and branching into a filigree so fine and so endless that the eye slid off it and came back and slid off again. Lace. It spread outward in every direction at once, delicate, luminous, and it made no sound at all.</p>\n<p>Everyone in the Crown had stopped moving, except that they had not stopped; they were moving at different speeds. She saw Crane’s aide lift his tablet to his face in a single slow, graceful arc that took, perhaps, ten seconds. She saw the camera operator, on her knee by the window, flicker, and stand, and be on her knee again. She heard someone scream and then saw them open their mouth to do it.</p>\n<p><em>Time dilation</em>, said the calm part of Ruth’s mind, which was the part that had passed the exams. <em>Local gradients across the room. The boundary’s gone fractal. The core potential is spiking. We are in a region of⁠—</em></p>\n<p>“Keystone,” she said out loud, and ran.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Keystone was set into the floor at the far end of the Crown, under a hinged plate of brushed steel that nobody ever opened. The plate had words on it. Every engineer who had ever worked the Crown knew the words by heart, the way you know the words on a gravestone you walk past every day, without ever really reading them.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>Nobody knew what the last line meant. The consensus in the canteen was that Iris Hollowell had been a genius and a mystic and had lived on coffee and cigarettes and that geniuses were allowed to be cryptic on brass plates. The Clause itself was classified above Ruth’s level. What she knew was this: under the plate was a lock, and behind the lock was the only mechanism in the Draw that did not feed the Lesion but ended it, and the lock took one key, and the key was the Minister’s, and tonight it was hanging around the neck of a man in a good jacket who had wanted a round number.</p>\n<p>She dropped to her knees and wrenched the plate up. Beneath it, black keelstone, a smooth recessed housing, and in the middle of it a keyhole the shape of a narrow cross.</p>\n<p>“Crane.”</p>\n<p>He was standing where he had been standing, in the best light. The light was different now. It came in through the window in a shifting white-violet surf, and it lit him from every side, and his face in it looked very young and very frightened.</p>\n<p>“Give me the key.”</p>\n<p>“What will it do?”</p>\n<p>“It will close the Lesion.”</p>\n<p>“Close it.” He said it as if it were a word in a foreign language. “Close it how? For how long? We can’t — Ruth, the Minister⁠—”</p>\n<p>“There won’t be a Minister. There won’t be a Draw. Look out of the window, Aldous. There won’t be a <em>city</em>.”</p>\n<p>Something happened to his face then, and she thought later — much later, when she’d had more time than anyone should have to think about one face — that it was the moment he understood, and that what he understood was not that the world might end but that if he gave her the key, the thing he had been sent here to film would be the last day of the Hollowell Draw, and that it would be his name on it. She thought he was not a monster. She thought he was simply a man who had lived his whole life inside the Continuance and could not, in that second, imagine the outside of it. It is hard to give away the thing that holds up the sky.</p>\n<p>“There must be another way,” he said.</p>\n<p>“<em>Give me the key.</em>”</p>\n<p>He put his hand on it. He did. She saw his fingers close around the chain. She saw him start to lift it over his head.</p>\n<p>The gantry beneath the window buckled.</p>\n<p>A single thread of the Lace — as fine as a hair, as bright as a filament in a bulb — had reached the outer frame of the Crown. Where it touched, the keelstone did not break, because nothing broke keelstone. It <em>aged</em>. A strip of the frame as wide as her hand went grey, then white, then sagged like wax, and the grated walkway along the window, which was bolted to that frame, tore free along a third of its length and dropped half a metre with a shriek of metal.</p>\n<p>Crane was standing on the walkway.</p>\n<p>He fell against the rail. The chain around his neck caught on something — a bolt head, a twist of torn grating — and snapped. Ruth watched the key go. She watched it with the same terrible slowness as the coffee cup, and knew as she watched that it was not slow for the key; it was only slow for her. It turned over once in the white-violet light. It slid between the bars of the grating. And it fell — not down, toward the Vault floor, where things ought to fall — but <em>up and out</em>, toward the window, as if the Lesion’s light were the true ground and it was dropping home. The glass did not stop it. She never understood that part. It was on one side of the window, and then it was beyond it, a small black speck tumbling into the Lace, and then it was not anywhere.</p>\n<p>Crane made a sound she would hear for years.</p>\n<p>She was still on her knees by the Keystone. She looked down, stupidly, at the empty keyhole. Then she looked up.</p>\n<p>There was a man at the Keystone.</p>\n<p>He was kneeling where she was kneeling. That was the only way she could put it afterwards. He was kneeling <em>where she was</em>, in the same place, and she could see him perfectly, and he was not touching her. He was dressed in close grey, like a diver, like something worn under armour, and he had one hand on a small wheel she had never seen — a wheel that was not there, that the housing did not have — and around his wrist was a cord tied in a knot. His face was turned toward the window. It was an ordinary face, tired and lined and unshaven, and it was completely calm. It was the calmest face she had ever seen. He looked like a man who had been there for a very long time and could stay there for longer.</p>\n<p>Then he turned his head and looked at her.</p>\n<p>It lasted — she never knew. Less than a second. He looked at her as if he knew her. As if he were sorry for her, and glad of her, both at once.</p>\n<p>Then there was no one there.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Tally! Tally, the lift!”</p>\n<p>Fen had her by the arm. He had her by the arm, and then he had her by the arm again, a moment later, as if he’d had to do it twice. Everyone was moving now, in stutters, in slurs. The camera operator was dragging Crane back from the edge of the torn walkway; his face was grey and wet. Dieter had his board open and was yanking fuses out by hand as if that would do anything, as if anything would do anything.</p>\n<p>“We have to go down,” Fen said. “We have to go down <em>now</em>, the Lattice is — the whole — Ruth, if it touches the spindle⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” she said. “Yes. Evacuate the Crown.”</p>\n<p>They went. Eleven people and a camera. The lift was at the end of the gallery, a steel cage on a rack-and-pinion rail that ran four kilometres down the inside of Spindle One, and it held twelve, and they got in, and Ruth did not. She stood at the gate.</p>\n<p>“Ruth⁠—”</p>\n<p>“One second.”</p>\n<p>On the wall by the lift, behind a small glass panel, was a red lever with a black-and-yellow collar. Every room in the Draw that had controls also had one. The engineers called it the Dead Man’s Hammer. Pulling it did only one thing: it released a flywheel in the station’s deep structure that drove an iron hammer against a keelstone anvil, once per second, for as long as the flywheel turned — and the flywheel had been built to turn for days. It was the oldest alarm in the Draw, older than the electronics, older than the Ministry, a thing Iris Hollowell had insisted on over every objection. It meant one thing and one thing only, and every soul in the station would understand it, even if every screen and every speaker in the place was dead.</p>\n<p>It meant: <em>nobody is at the controls</em>.</p>\n<p>Ruth broke the glass with her elbow and pulled it.</p>\n<p>For a moment nothing happened. Then, deep below them, somewhere down in the black body of the station, something enormous let go.</p>\n<p>She stepped into the lift. Fen slammed the gate. The cage dropped.</p>\n<p>Through the lift’s mesh she saw the Crown go up and away from her — the torn walkway, the empty Keystone with its plate thrown back, the window full of spreading light — and then the black inner wall of the spindle, rushing. In her hand she was holding, she discovered, the station logbook, which she had no memory of picking up. She held it against her chest. Beside her, Aldous Crane was weeping silently with his eyes open. The camera operator had her arms around him. The young man with the tablet was staring at the tablet, which showed a clock, which was counting upwards faster than any clock should.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy</em>, thought Ruth. <em>Maddy, Maddy, Maddy.</em> She had the absurd conviction that if she said the name enough times, fast enough, she could make it reach her.</p>\n<p>Then, from far below, up through the rails and the cage and the soles of her boots and the bones of her legs, came the first stroke of the hammer.</p>\n<p>It was not a sound. It was too deep, and too long, and too slow, for a sound. It came up through the spindle like a tide coming in under a door. It lasted, and lasted, and lasted.</p>\n<p>Afterwards — for the rest of her life, and for the lives of her children, and their children, and theirs — they would call it the Bell.</p>\n<p>She looked at her watch. It said 02:14. She would find out, eventually, what it meant that she was the last person to see it say so. It had taken nine minutes.</p>"}, {"file": "01-the-standby.md", "label": "Chapter 1", "title": "The Standby", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": true, "words": 5131, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The Hollowell Draw is experiencing a minor margin irregularity. There is no danger to the public. Citizens are reminded that calm is care.</p><span class=\"attr\">Office of Public Calm, bulletin 1, 04:30, Continuance Day</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">By twenty past six Casimir Hale had counted fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty strokes of the hammer, and the phone had still not rung.</p>\n<p>He was sitting on the edge of his bed in the dark with his boots on. He had put the boots on at about a quarter past two, when the first stroke came up through the building like a truck driving over the roof of the world, and he had put on his trousers and his good shirt and his coat, and then he had sat down to wait, and he had been sitting ever since. At some point he had begun counting. He wasn’t sure why. It was the kind of thing a man did when he had spent fifteen years being paid to wait for a phone call and the call had finally come and it had not come by phone.</p>\n<p>The phone lay on the bedside table next to a glass of water he hadn’t drunk. It was a heavy black Ministry handset with no screen and a single button, and it had been issued to him on his first day as Standby with a small ceremony in an office on Tier Two, and in fifteen years it had never once made a sound. He dusted it on Sundays. He had once, drunk, tried to call his own number on it and found that it did not make outgoing calls. It was not a phone, really; it was a promise, or a threat, or a leash, and it had sat there by his bed for fifteen years like a small black animal that might one day wake up.</p>\n<p>Fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty-one.</p>\n<p>The hammer did not sound like a sound. It sounded the way it would feel to be very small and live inside a drum. Every second, precisely, the whole building shuddered — the window glass hummed, the water in the untouched glass flinched into a ring, the cutlery in the kitchen drawer gave a small metallic cough — and then there was a second of silence, and then it came again. The flat was on Tier Nine, a hundred metres above the ground and four kilometres from the rim of the Pit, and at this distance, through all that concrete and air and sleeping city, it should not have been possible to feel anything that happened inside the Draw at all.</p>\n<p>Cas knew what it was. He had known at 02:14, sitting up in bed with his heart going. He’d have known it in his sleep. There was a picture of that hammer in the second chapter of every textbook on vacuum engineering ever printed, and a caption underneath in Iris Hollowell’s own words: <em>When all else has failed, the station will say so.</em></p>\n<p>Fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty-two.</p>\n<p>At twenty-five past six there was a knock at the door.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There were two of them on the landing, a man and a woman in the charcoal overcoats of the Office of Public Calm, and behind them, a little apart, a third man who was older and softer and wore no overcoat at all, only a brown cardigan buttoned wrong.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” said the soft man. “I’m so sorry to wake you.”</p>\n<p>“You didn’t.”</p>\n<p>“No.” The man’s eyes went over him, the boots, the coat, the good shirt. Something that might have been approval, or pity, moved in his face. “No, I don’t suppose we did. Ansel Gaunt. We’ve never met, but I’ve read a great deal about you. Shall we?”</p>\n<p>The car was long and black and warm and smelled of ginger biscuits. There was a paper bag of them on the seat between them and Gaunt offered it to Cas and, when Cas shook his head, took one himself and ate it carefully over a handkerchief spread on his knee. The two in overcoats sat up front. The car pulled out into the lane and the hammer came up through its wheels and its seats and Gaunt’s biscuit, and Gaunt closed his eyes briefly each time it did, the way a man might wince at a toothache he had decided not to mention.</p>\n<p>“We didn’t phone,” said Gaunt, “because phones are recorded.”</p>\n<p>“By you.”</p>\n<p>“By us, yes. Which is precisely the problem. Things that are recorded have a way of becoming history. Things that aren’t recorded have a way of becoming⁠—” he searched for the word, dabbing a crumb from his lip “⁠—negotiable. The Minister would like this morning to remain negotiable for as long as possible.”</p>\n<p>“How long have you known?”</p>\n<p>“About the irregularity?”</p>\n<p>“About the Draw.”</p>\n<p>Gaunt looked at him mildly. “Since two-fourteen, Dr Hale. Like everyone else in the Pit district who was awake, or who was woken. It’s been rather hard to miss.” A pause. “If you mean when did we know that it was <em>serious</em> — I’d say we’re still finding that out. Which is why you’re in my car.”</p>\n<p>They came out of the lane onto the Tier Nine arterial and the city opened up around them.</p>\n<p>Averna at dawn on Continuance Day. Cas had lived in it all his life and it still, sometimes, stopped his breath. Ahead and above, the Stacks rose in their terraced ranks, tier on tier on tier, a hundred and forty levels of housing and gardens and plazas climbing into the grey, lit from within like vast lanterns, and above the highest of them the faint milky curve of the Canopy, the weather shield that had held back sixty years of storms. Down at street level the Continuance Day bunting was already up — blue and gold, THE FUTURE WILL PAY on every banner — and the public speakers on the lampposts were playing the Tending anthem on a soft loop, the children’s choir version, very sweet. There were people out, a lot of people for this hour. They were walking in the ordinary way, carrying the ordinary bags, and they were not talking, and every one of them, Cas saw, flinched very slightly once a second.</p>\n<p>“There’s been no announcement?”</p>\n<p>“There’s been a bulletin. Minor irregularity. No danger to the public. Calm is care.” Gaunt took another biscuit. “People are being very good. People are always very good at first.”</p>\n<p>The car turned south, toward the Pit. They passed a plaza with a Quota clock on a pillar in the middle — a big public display, the kind every district had, showing the Draw’s output in real time in tall gold numerals. Cas had grown up with those clocks. As a boy he’d stood in front of the one on Tier Four and watched the numbers climb on Deepening Day and cheered with everyone else. It said 00.00 TW. As they passed, a man in a Ministry jumpsuit on a ladder beside it reached up and switched it off, and it went dark, and a small crowd that had gathered to look at it stood looking at the dark instead.</p>\n<p>“The Minister felt,” said Gaunt, “that the number was unhelpful.”</p>\n<p>“Zero?”</p>\n<p>“The number before zero. For about forty minutes after two-fourteen the clocks showed an output of a hundred and nine terawatts. Then they started showing numbers that aren’t numbers. Then zero.” He folded his handkerchief. “I’m not a physicist, Dr Hale. But I can read a room, and I read a hundred and nine terawatts, and I read the faces of the engineers in the crisis room who read it, and I thought: I’ll go and fetch the man who wrote the memo.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“The Margin Review,” said Gaunt gently. “Year Seventy-Six. Section four, the projections. You were the junior author. I’ve read the original. Not the version that was published. The original, the one with your name on it and Dr Adeyemi’s. It had some very striking phrases.” He looked out of the window. “’A structure of obligation that cannot, even in principle, be discharged.’ I remember thinking that was a remarkable sentence to find in a technical report. It sounded like scripture.”</p>\n<p>“Adeyemi wrote that.”</p>\n<p>“Did he? He’s dead now, of course. Natural causes.” Gaunt said it without emphasis, the way you’d say a man was tall. “And you signed the revised version. The one that said the Margin was stable.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Nobody made you.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“No.” Gaunt seemed to think about that for a while. “That’s the thing I’ve never understood. Most of the people I talk to in my job, I can tell you exactly what made them. A child. A debt. A photograph. But you, Dr Hale — nobody lifted a finger. We simply asked, and you signed, and we gave you a flat and a phone and you sat by it for fifteen years like a good dog.” He turned from the window and met Cas’s eyes, and his own were very tired and very kind. “I’m not saying it to hurt you. I’m saying it because I want you to understand that I know exactly who you are. It will save us both time.”</p>\n<p>The hammer came up through the car.</p>\n<p>“How long,” said Cas, “until we get there?”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>You saw the Pit before you saw the Draw. You always did — it was fourteen kilometres across and three deep, a crater dug out of the old industrial belt in the Year Minus Six, and the Draw sat in the bottom of it like a vast black egg in a bowl. From the rim, on a normal morning, you saw the Collar first: a ring of converter halls and admin blocks and trunk-line heads, lit up and smoking, with the great superconducting lines marching away from it over the city on pylons like the legs of enormous insects. Behind the Collar, the dark bulk of the Works. Behind the Works, the dome.</p>\n<p>This morning, from the rim, you could hardly see it at all.</p>\n<p>The air over the Pit was moving. That was the only way to put it. It shimmered, the way air shimmers over a road on a hot day — except that it rose in a column, a clear, trembling pillar at least ten kilometres wide, going straight up into the grey dawn until it was lost against the Canopy. Through it the Draw looked wavering and uncertain, as if seen through water. The edges of things kept coming apart and knitting back.</p>\n<p>And there were birds. Hundreds of them — gulls, starlings, the fat grey Averna pigeons that lived on the Stacks — circling above the rim in a great slow wheel. None of them flew over the Pit. They came to its edge and turned away, came and turned away, around and around, like water circling a drain it would not go down.</p>\n<p>Their car stopped at a cordon of Civil Defence vehicles and Calm Office cars on the rim road. The hammer was much louder here: a vast, dull, full-body blow, once a second, that made the gravel on the road jump. A Civil Defence sergeant waved them through, and as they passed her Cas saw her glance at her wristwatch and then at a clock on the dashboard of her truck and frown.</p>\n<p>“The clocks near the rim run fast,” said Gaunt, who had seen him see it. “Twelve minutes fast, at the cordon, since two. They were eight minutes fast at five. It’s creeping out. Nobody seems able to tell me whether that’s important.”</p>\n<p>“It’s important.”</p>\n<p>“I thought it might be.” Gaunt took off his cardigan, folded it carefully, and laid it on the seat. Underneath he was wearing a very good grey shirt. “The Minister’s waiting.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The crisis room was in the Collar’s admin block, on the fourth floor, in a long room that had been built as a conference suite for foreign delegations. It still had the gold carpet and the portraits: Iris Hollowell, severe and hollow-cheeked, with a cigarette she’d been painted without; the first Minister of Continuance, cutting a ribbon; a sweeping mural of the Vault seen from the Crown, the eight spindles rising to the Lesion, the Lesion a gold star with painted rays. Someone had pushed the delegate tables together and covered them with screens and paper and cold coffee, and about twenty people were standing around them in the stunned, overlit way of people who had been awake all night doing something that wasn’t working.</p>\n<p>Cas knew about half of them. Engineers, mostly — Collar shift leads, a couple of the Works directors, a woman from the Lattice office he’d been at university with who looked at him across the room, went white, and looked away. People who had been told at some point, by someone, that the Standby was not somebody you talked to. He’d got used to that look. He’d seen it on the faces of old friends in the street.</p>\n<p>At the head of the table stood Ondine Rask.</p>\n<p>The Minister of Continuance was a tall, spare woman in her sixties with silver hair cut close to her skull and a dark suit without a crease in it. She had been Minister for nineteen years. She had presided over the First Deepening, and the export treaties, and the Canopy’s extension over the southern coast, and Cas had seen her face on every public screen in the city since he was a young man. It was said she slept four hours a night. It looked, this morning, as though she had slept none, and it looked as though she had decided that this was nobody’s business.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” she said. “Thank you for coming.”</p>\n<p>As though he’d had a choice. He nodded.</p>\n<p>“You’ll forgive me if I don’t waste your time. We have had a Continuance Event.” She said the phrase with no irony at all. “At approximately 02:05 this morning, during a scheduled test, Tensioner Six in the Lattice lost its seat. The interlocks failed to compensate. Since that time we have been unable to re-establish control of the Lesion, the Lattice, or the Rollover. We have no communication with the Crown or with any part of the station inside the Works. We evacuated the Collar at 03:10. At around the same time we lost the output. The Commonweal grid is running on reserve and on the old fusion plants at Tessarine and Kovač, which were not built to carry it, and which will begin to fail within eighteen hours. Those are the facts.” She put both hands flat on the table. “I have been assured by every engineer in this room that the situation can be recovered, and I have been assured by every engineer in this room that they do not know how. Colonel Gaunt tells me you might.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>She blinked.</p>\n<p>“Why does Colonel Gaunt tell you that,” said Cas. “I haven’t been inside the Draw in fifteen years. I haven’t been allowed to read a Lattice report since Year Seventy-Six. Everyone in this room knows the station better than I do now.”</p>\n<p>“Because,” said Gaunt, from the doorway, where he had settled himself on a gold chair with his hands folded, “everyone in this room has spent the night telling the Minister what they think she would like to hear, and I have some small professional experience in noticing when people are doing that. Whereas you, Dr Hale, have spent the last fifteen years with nothing whatsoever to lose.” He smiled at Rask. “It’s a rare quality, Minister. I’d use it while it lasts.”</p>\n<p>Rask looked at Gaunt for a long moment, then back at Cas.</p>\n<p>“What do you need?” she said.</p>\n<p>“Everything you’ve got. Starting with the last thing the Crown sent.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They gave him a chair and a screen and the Lattice woman — her name was Petra Imre, she’d sat behind him in second-year field theory and once lent him a pencil — brought him the telemetry without looking at him, and he went through it, and the room went on around him in a hush.</p>\n<p>It didn’t take long. That was the worst of it. Afterwards he’d think that if it had taken longer, if he’d had to hunt, he might have had time to get used to it on the way.</p>\n<p>The drone logs first. At 04:40 they had sent a survey drone into the Works through Gate Nine. It had flown for forty minutes by the Collar’s clock. When it came back, its own internal clock said eleven hours and six minutes had passed. Its battery was dead. Its camera had recorded, for most of those eleven hours, a corridor it had flown down and back up again, over and over, as the gravity inside shifted and threw it off course, and at the end, just before the battery died, a thin line of white-violet light crossing a hall far off, like a hair on a lens.</p>\n<p>Then the Crown. The last telemetry burst before the link failed, 02:05:31. Most of it was screaming numbers — output, tensioner loads, core potential off every scale anyone had drawn. And one small, dry line at the bottom that the instruments computed automatically from the shape of the Lesion’s boundary, a figure that had read <em>2.000</em> — a perfect sphere, a clean, smooth edge — every second of every day for ninety-one years.</p>\n<p>It read <em>2.73</em>.</p>\n<p>He looked at it for a while. He was aware of the hammer. He was aware of his own hands on the table, which were very still.</p>\n<p>“What is it?” said Rask.</p>\n<p>“The boundary dimension.” He heard his voice from a small distance. “It’s a measure of the — it’s how rough the edge of the Lesion is. How much surface it has. A smooth sphere is two. A cloud is about two and a half. It’s gone to two point seven.”</p>\n<p>“And that means?”</p>\n<p>“It means the edge isn’t an edge any more. It’s fractal. It’s broken into⁠—” He moved his hand. He thought of frost on a pane. “Into edges, on edges, on edges. Infinitely. A boundary with no smooth part anywhere on it.”</p>\n<p>“Can it be repaired?”</p>\n<p>“It isn’t <em>damaged</em>, Minister. It’s⁠—” He stopped. He looked round the table. All those faces. They were looking at him the way people look at a doctor who has just come in from reading the scan.</p>\n<p>“I need to explain something,” he said, “and I need you to let me finish, and I need you not to call it anything else while I’m doing it.”</p>\n<p>Rask’s face didn’t move. “Go on.”</p>\n<p>“The Draw doesn’t make energy. Everyone in this room knows that. It borrows it. The Lattice holds a region of space below its ground state — below zero, below empty — and the difference is what we sell. The vacuum lends it to us, and the vacuum always gets it back. That’s not a theory, that’s not Hollowell, that’s field theory, it’s as old as the Long Heat. You can make a patch of negative energy. You can’t keep it. It has to be paid back by a pulse of positive energy, and the payment is always <em>more</em> than the loan. The longer you hold it, the bigger the payment. They called it quantum interest, before any of us were born.”</p>\n<p>“We are all aware of the principle of the Rollover, Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>“Then you’re aware that the Rollover isn’t a solution. It’s a refinancing.” He heard his voice sharpen and didn’t stop it. “Hollowell’s trick was to keep borrowing to pay the interest on what we’d already borrowed. Push the payment forward. Push it forward again. Ninety-one years. Every year a little more of the output goes back into the Lattice just to keep the loan from coming due, and every year the loan is bigger, and every year we deepened the draw to make up the output, which made the loan bigger. We were feeding it seventy percent. The Review projected it would pass a hundred percent inside twenty years — the Draw would be eating more than it made, just to stand still. We’d have had to feed it from the grid. For ever. That was always the shape of it. That was the <em>best case</em>.”</p>\n<p>“That report,” said Rask, very quietly, “was revised.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. By me.” He held her eyes. “It didn’t change the physics.”</p>\n<p>Nobody in the room was moving.</p>\n<p>“The Rollover’s gone,” he said. “Six unseated, the Lattice lost the geometry, and the boundary broke. There’s no mechanism holding the payment back any more. The debt’s coming due. Ninety-one years of it, with interest, and the interest compounds. It will be paid in one pulse, at the core, all at once.” He looked down at the screen, at the numbers, and did the arithmetic in his head because he had done it a hundred times, fifteen years ago, at night, on the backs of envelopes. “Order of ten to the twenty-six joules. The impact that killed the dinosaurs was about ten to the twenty-three and a half. So call it two or three hundred of those. At once. Here.”</p>\n<p>It was Petra Imre who spoke first. “When?”</p>\n<p>“The boundary’s still evolving. The clocks at the cordon are running faster; that tells me the core potential’s still off the scale and still shifting. When it starts to fall, the debt’s being called in. When it falls far enough⁠—” He made himself say it plainly. “Two days. Three at the outside. Maybe less.”</p>\n<p>Someone at the far end of the table sat down on the floor.</p>\n<p>“Evacuation,” said a Works director, a heavy man called Brask whom Cas vaguely remembered as a rugby player. “We evacuate the city⁠—”</p>\n<p>“To where? Six hundred million people, in two days. And it wouldn’t matter. It’s not a bomb. It’s the planet. The first second takes Averna. The shock and the heat do the rest. You could be on the far side of the world and it would find you in the first hour.”</p>\n<p>“Bury it,” said somebody else. “Collapse the Pit. Pour⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You can’t bury a debt.”</p>\n<p>“Then what,” said Rask, “do you propose?”</p>\n<p>And there it was. He’d known it was coming since the car. Maybe since 02:14. Maybe since Year Seventy-Six. He found that he was very calm.</p>\n<p>“There’s a mechanism in the Crown,” he said. “Hollowell designed it. It’s in the original schematics, and it’s in her last paper — the one that was withdrawn — and it’s called the Clause. It isn’t a repair. It doesn’t save the Draw. It ends it. The theory is that if the boundary ever failed badly enough — and it has — the same distortions that are going to kill us could be turned around and used to fold the core shut. Seal the whole thing. Debt and payment together. In a⁠—” he hesitated over her phrase “⁠—in a moment that doesn’t have a future. I don’t fully understand it. I don’t think anyone alive does. There’s a line on the Keystone plate that nobody’s ever explained. <em>The sealing requires a witness.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Hollowell’s final paper,” said Rask, “was withdrawn because it was unsound.”</p>\n<p>“It was withdrawn because it described how to turn the Draw off.”</p>\n<p>The silence went on for several strokes of the hammer.</p>\n<p>“The Clause is locked,” Cas said. “It takes the Minister’s key.”</p>\n<p>For the first time, something flickered in Ondine Rask’s face.</p>\n<p>“The key,” she said, “was in the custody of Deputy Minister Crane. Who is on site.”</p>\n<p>“On site.”</p>\n<p>“Deputy Minister Crane was attending the test. As the Ministry’s representative.” She said it perfectly evenly, and every engineer in the room looked at the table. “He is currently unaccounted for, along with the Crown crew and approximately nineteen hundred members of the night shift who were in the Lattice and the Vault at the time of the event.”</p>\n<p>Nineteen hundred.</p>\n<p>Cas had been trying not to do the next piece of arithmetic. He did it anyway. He found that he was doing it out loud, softly, the way he used to talk himself through proofs in the library at night.</p>\n<p>“The clocks at the cordon are twelve minutes fast after four hours,” he said. “The drone lost eleven hours in forty minutes, in the outer Works. If the core potential is as high as the Crown’s numbers say — and it’ll have been highest at first, right after the rupture, before the debt started to be called — then in the Vault itself, near the core, it isn’t hours. It’s — the ratio could be tens of thousands. Hundreds of thousands.” He looked up. “Minister, if anyone survived in there — if anyone was sheltered, if they had air and water — then in the four hours since two o’clock, they’ve lived⁠—”</p>\n<p>“That will do, Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>“⁠—years. Decades. By the time we get anyone in there, they could have been alive in there longer than the Commonweal has existed⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>That will not be repeated outside this room.</em>”</p>\n<p>It was the first time she’d raised her voice. It rang off the gold walls. In the doorway, Gaunt had closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>Rask straightened. She took her hands off the table.</p>\n<p>“We have,” she said, “three suits.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They were called keel suits. He had heard of them, the way you hear of the cathedrals in cities you’ll never visit: a Lattice maintenance programme from the Year Seventies, cancelled in the Eighties, three prototypes built in the Tallow Works labs and never used. Suits designed to let a human being work inside the Lattice’s gradients, where the pull of the tensioners and the push of the Lesion would crush a person like a paper cup. They were said to be almost unimaginably expensive. Each one had a sliver of keelstone at its heart, spun up like a gyroscope — keelstone, the hyperdense stuff the Lattice itself was made from, which cost more per gram than anything else on Earth because only the Draw could make it.</p>\n<p>“They’ll let a team get through the Works,” Rask said. “Possibly into the Lattice. Possibly further. Our engineers believe that from the Crown, with the proper sequence, Tensioner Six could be reseated manually. If Six is reseated, the Lattice can re-establish the geometry. If the geometry is re-established, the Rollover can resume. The Draw continues.” She said the last two words with a peculiar emphasis, as though laying them down on the table between them like a card. “Can Six be reseated from the Crown, Dr Hale?”</p>\n<p>He thought of the number at the bottom of the telemetry. 2.73. He thought of a tensioner rotor the size of a building sitting off its bearing in a Lattice that no longer had a shape to hold it. He thought of the Keystone.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>It was astonishingly easy. It always had been. That was the thing about lies in the Commonweal: there was never any resistance. You opened your mouth and they slid out like a coin into a slot.</p>\n<p>“Good.” Rask nodded, once. “Then you’ll lead the technical side. Civil Defence will lead the team. You’ll leave as soon as the suits are ready.” She turned her head. “Major Lisk.”</p>\n<p>He hadn’t noticed the woman by the window. She’d been standing in the corner behind the delegate screens with her arms folded and her back to the mural, watching, and she came forward now with the slightly rolling gait of someone who had spent a long time on boats or in boots. She was in her fifties, broad and weathered, with cropped iron-grey hair and a face like a well-used tool. The Civil Defence badge on her shoulder had been sewn back on by hand at least once. The ribbon bar under it went down two rows.</p>\n<p>“Teodora Lisk,” she said, and held out her hand.</p>\n<p>He shook it. It was a hard, dry hand with a missing fingertip.</p>\n<p>She didn’t let go at once. She held on a moment and looked at him — up, down, back up — with an expression he couldn’t read. It was the look, he thought afterwards, that a person gives a ladder before climbing it: not unfriendly; simply trying to work out how much weight it would take.</p>\n<p>“You’re the one who wrote the thing,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I’m the one who wrote the thing.”</p>\n<p>“Hm.” She let go. “Well. Congratulations on being right.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Later — an hour later, maybe two, after the plans and the arguments and a call to the Tallow Works that went on for forty minutes and ended with somebody in the lab crying — he found himself alone at the end of the corridor outside the crisis room, standing at a window.</p>\n<p>The window looked south over the Pit. From up here he could see the Draw properly: the Collar, the dark jumble of the Works, the dome rising out of the middle like the back of some enormous animal asleep in the crater. The shimmer rose over it, vast and clear and trembling. The birds wheeled at its edge. The hammer came up through the floor and through the glass under his fingers, once a second, once a second, and he found he had started counting again without meaning to. Twenty-one thousand and something.</p>\n<p>He thought about nineteen hundred people.</p>\n<p>He thought about a pen. He could remember it exactly: it had been a Ministry pen, blue, with the Continuance flame on the clip. He’d noticed at the time that it was a good pen, that it wrote smoothly. That was the thing he remembered most about the most important moment of his life — that he had noticed the pen was nice.</p>\n<p>He had never told anyone that. There’d never been anyone to tell.</p>\n<p>Down in the Pit something shifted in the shimmer, a great slow ripple running upward like a wave through a curtain. The birds broke their circle and scattered and came back.</p>\n<p>Two or three days. A pulse at the core. And three suits, and a lock that nobody had the key to, and a line on a brass plate that nobody understood.</p>\n<p>He should be afraid. He knew that. He examined himself for fear, the way you’d pat your pockets for keys, and found — not nothing. Something. But what he mostly found, when he looked, was a kind of settling. A quietness. The way a room goes quiet when the last guest leaves.</p>\n<p><em>At last</em>, some part of him said. He didn’t like the part of him that said it. He didn’t argue with it either.</p>\n<p>It was almost a relief.</p>"}, {"file": "02-keel.md", "label": "Chapter 2", "title": "Keel", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 5593, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The keel is not a shield. It will not stop the water. It will keep you upright in it.</p><span class=\"attr\">MSE Type 4 Operator’s Handbook (draft, unpublished), Tallow Works Laboratory</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The suits arrived at the Collar at a quarter to eleven, on the back of a Civil Defence flatbed, lashed upright in their cradles under grey tarpaulins, so that as the truck came slowly down the ramp into the Gate Nine maintenance hangar they looked like three tall figures being brought to an execution with sacks over their heads.</p>\n<p>A young man was sitting on the flatbed with them, holding on to one of the cradles with both arms. When the truck stopped he didn’t let go. He stayed there, hugging the cradle, while the driver got out and the hangar crew came forward with the forklift, and he looked round the hangar with enormous eyes — at the Calm officers by the door, at the Civil Defence people, at Teo Lisk with her arms folded, at Cas — and said, to no one in particular, very fast:</p>\n<p>“Hi. Hello. I’m Ben. Benedek. Farr. I’m the — I built — well, I didn’t <em>build</em> them, a lot of people built them, but I built the keels, I mean I did the rotor bearings, and the housings, and the spin controllers, and most of the firmware, and I’m the only one left in the lab who still knows how they go together, because everyone else was reassigned when the programme got cut, and I wasn’t, because nobody remembered I was there, which was, honestly, the best four years of my life. And someone phoned the lab at seven o’clock this morning and said you need three people to go into the Draw, and I said who’s going to maintain the suits, and they said well, who <em>can</em>, and I said, well, me, I suppose, and they said right then.” He took a breath. “So I’m coming. I’m coming? I’m coming. Right. Good. Fine.” Another breath. “Is there a form?”</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk walked over to the flatbed, looked up at him, and held out a hand.</p>\n<p>“Come down, son,” she said. “There’s always a form.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He was twenty-six. He had a narrow, clever, anxious face, and hair that stood up at the back where he’d been running his hands through it, and he talked — Cas would learn over the next twenty hours — the way a pressure valve leaks: continuously, and more when the pressure went up. When he was working, though, he went quiet. When he stripped the tarpaulin off the first cradle and put his hand flat on the suit inside it, he stopped talking altogether, and stood there for a moment as though he were checking for a pulse.</p>\n<p>They were not beautiful. Cas had half expected them to be. They were squat and grey and massive, armoured in overlapping matte plates like the shell of some deep-sea crustacean, with thick ribbed joints at knee and hip and elbow and a helmet that was mostly visor, a wide dark curve of glass. On each back, where a diver would carry tanks, sat a rounded housing the size of a beer keg, sealed and bolted, with a band of small indicator lights around its waist. Someone had painted a number on each housing by hand in white paint: <em>1</em>, <em>2</em>, <em>3</em>. The paint on the <em>3</em> had run a little before it dried.</p>\n<p>“That’s the keel,” said Ben. He had his hand on the housing of number one. “Inside there’s a rotor. Keelstone. Eleven kilos of it, in a disc about so big.” He made a circle with his hands the size of a dinner plate. “Doesn’t sound like much. That’s the same mass as the whole suit. Keelstone’s — you know what keelstone is?”</p>\n<p>“Degenerate matter,” said Cas. “Electron-degenerate. Pressure-stabilised by the Lattice fields when it’s made. About ten thousand times denser than lead.”</p>\n<p>“Right, right, sorry, you’d know, you’re the — sorry.” Ben flushed. “So the rotor spins. Fast. Really, really fast. Two hundred thousand revs a minute at full, and the rim’s doing — well, you don’t want to know what the rim’s doing, the housing’s rated for it but only just. And when you spin something that dense that fast, it drags spacetime round with it. Frame-dragging. Tiny effect normally. With a keelstone rotor it’s not tiny. It gives you a sort of bubble, about a metre across, where the metric’s — smoothed. Stirred flat. Like putting a paddle in a river so the water round you goes one way instead of every way at once.” He looked at Teo, then at Cas, then back at the suit, and spoke to the suit. “In the Lattice, where the gradients are — you know. If you walk into a region where your feet weigh more than your head, or where your feet are living faster than your head, the bubble spreads it out. Averages it over the metre. So instead of being torn in half, you’re just — very uncomfortable.”</p>\n<p>“How uncomfortable?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Ben considered this seriously. “Have you ever been in a car crash?”</p>\n<p>“Three.”</p>\n<p>“Like the fourth one, but slower.”</p>\n<p>Teo grunted. It might have been a laugh.</p>\n<p>“The bubble’s only about a metre. Two people could share one if they were⁠—” Ben made a small squeezing gesture with his hands “⁠—closer than is professional. The suits are built for one. They’re heavy. They’ve got exoskeletons, the exo will carry most of it, but you’ll feel like you’re wearing a cathedral. Power cells for the life support and the exo and the electronics are good for about four days. The keel doesn’t need power once it’s spinning. The bearings are magnetic, there’s almost no friction — sat on the rig with nothing to push against, it’d spin for months. It’s the <em>work</em> that slows it. Every time the bubble has to hold something steep, it costs spin. Hold a lot, and it runs down fast. As it slows, the bubble shrinks. You’ll hear it. It hums. When the hum drops, that’s you running out of keel.”</p>\n<p>“And spinning it up,” said Cas, “takes⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Fourteen hours.” Ben winced. “Sorry. It’s a lot of mass. If you try to spin it faster you crack the housing, and then you’ve got eleven kilos of keelstone doing two hundred thousand revs on your back with nothing to hold it, and that’s⁠—” He stopped. “That’s a bad day. That’s a very, very bad day.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked up at the clock on the hangar wall. It said 10:58. His wristwatch said 10:51. The hangar was a kilometre inside the Collar; the time out here was already slipping.</p>\n<p>Fourteen hours. They’d go in before dawn. Hour twenty-five, or thereabouts. He tried to think of what the hour-twenty-five version of the Vault might be like, if the ratio was what he thought it was, and decided not to.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took Ben and the hangar crew until one o’clock to get the suits out of the cradles and onto the spin rigs — three low steel frames bolted to the hangar floor, humming with power cables — and when the rotors finally started to turn it was so gradual that at first there was no way to tell. Then, gradually, there was a sound. A long, low, rising tone, so deep you felt it in your back teeth before you heard it, the three suits singing in almost-unison under the hangar lights. The note crept upward very slowly. Ben said it would keep rising all night.</p>\n<p>Cas stood and listened to it. He would hear that note for the rest of his life.</p>\n<p>Across the hangar, by the big doors, Teo Lisk was talking to Ansel Gaunt.</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t hear them. The hum and the hammer between them drowned it out. Gaunt was doing the talking, softly, with his head bent and his hands folded in front of him like a priest, and Teo was listening with her arms crossed and her face shut. At one point Gaunt took something small out of his cardigan pocket and held it out to her, and after a moment she took it and put it in the breast pocket of her fatigues. Then Gaunt patted her arm, and went out, and Teo stood there alone in the doorway looking out at the dark mass of the Works for a while before she came back.</p>\n<p>When she passed Cas, she didn’t meet his eye.</p>\n<p>He filed it. He had spent fifteen years watching people not meet his eye; he’d got good at filing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The afternoon was drills.</p>\n<p>They put the suits on for the first time at three o’clock, with the keels barely turning, and Cas found out what Ben had meant by a cathedral. You didn’t so much put on a keel suit as get admitted into it. The liner came first — a close grey one-piece in some slippery, cool fabric, wired with sensors and a web of fine tubes for heat and water, that fitted like a diver’s wetsuit and made all three of them look, as Teo put it, like very depressed seals. Then the suit opened down the back like a clam, and you stepped in, and it closed around you with a series of heavy locking thunks, and for a moment you could not move at all, and then the exoskeleton woke up and took your weight and you stood up inside it and the world became a narrow bright picture framed in a dark curve of glass.</p>\n<p>It was slow. Everything was slow. The exo took a quarter-second to decide what your legs meant before it did it; walking felt like wading. On his back the keel’s hum came through the suit’s frame into his spine like a second, larger heartbeat. In his ears, the helmet’s speakers carried Teo swearing, steadily and inventively, as she tried to pick up a spanner in her gauntlet and dropped it for the ninth time.</p>\n<p>“Don’t grab,” said Ben’s voice. “It’s got a grip-assist, it’ll close harder than you mean it to. Just think about touching it.”</p>\n<p>“I’m thinking about touching you, Farr.”</p>\n<p>“That’s — okay, that’s fair.”</p>\n<p>They walked the length of the hangar and back. They climbed a ladder and came down it. They practised falling — the suits went over like felled trees and got up again with a hydraulic grunt — and practised hauling each other up, and practised the hand signals for when the radios failed, which Ben said they would, sooner or later.</p>\n<p>“The electronics hate gradients,” he said. “If a chip’s got one end living faster than the other end, its clocks go out of sync and it just — it gets confused. The keel helps, it smooths it over the bubble, but the bubble’s a metre and the Lattice is — well. The electronics will go first. The radios, the displays, the recorders. The life support’s mostly mechanical, the exo’s got a manual fallback, the keel’s purely mechanical once it’s spun. So you’ll keep breathing and walking. You just won’t be able to <em>talk</em> about it.”</p>\n<p>“Sounds like my marriage,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas found that he’d laughed. It came out of him in the suit, surprised, and he heard it come back to him over the radio, and he realised he couldn’t remember the last time he had laughed out loud at something another person had said. He couldn’t remember the last time there had been another person.</p>\n<p>Teo glanced across at him through two layers of visor. He couldn’t see her expression. But she didn’t say anything else for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At seven in the evening they ate.</p>\n<p>The Collar canteen had been evacuated at three in the morning in the middle of the night-shift meal, and nobody had been back. It was a long, low room with windows onto the Works, and the tables were still set: trays of food gone cold and stiff, cups with skins on them, a half-finished game of cards laid out on a table by the door. Somebody had been celebrating a birthday. There was a cake on the counter with most of its candles still in it and a paper crown lying beside it, and a card propped against the cake that said <em>HAPPY 40TH OSWIN — THE FUTURE WILL PAY (FOR THE CAKE)</em>.</p>\n<p>Nobody touched the cake.</p>\n<p>They found tins in the storeroom and heated them in the kitchen and ate at the table by the window, still in their liners, with the Works outside going on and on in the dark — cooling towers, gantries, the black cliff of a rectifier hall with red warning lights crawling across it — and the shimmer standing over all of it in the floodlights, and the hammer, and the hum of the keels from the hangar.</p>\n<p>Ben had a napkin. He’d been drawing on it since the soup.</p>\n<p>“Right,” he said. “Right. So I’ve been doing the numbers. On our survival odds.”</p>\n<p>“Oh, good,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“No, listen, it’s interesting. So. Okay. Let’s say the Works is about as bad as the drone said, which means the gravity’s doing weird things but the keels can handle it. Let’s say ninety percent for the Works. That’s generous but let’s say. Then the Lattice, we don’t know, but the keels were <em>designed</em> for the Lattice, so let’s say seventy. Then whatever’s past the Lattice⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The Shear,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up. “The what?”</p>\n<p>“If the core potential’s as high as I think, there’ll be a boundary somewhere around the inner face of the Lattice where the time rate changes very fast over a very short distance. A shear layer. Like the boundary between two currents.”</p>\n<p>Ben thought about that. He wrote something on the napkin. “Okay. The Shear. Let’s say fifty. For the Shear. And then the Vault, and then the Crown, and then whatever you do in the Crown⁠—” He looked at Cas again, and something in his face was very young. “What <em>are</em> you going to do in the Crown, Dr Hale? Reseat Six? Can you actually⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Keep going with your sum, Ben.”</p>\n<p>Ben looked at him a moment longer. Then he looked down and kept going. “So — the Crown, let’s call it fifty, fifty for whatever. And then we have to get <em>back</em>, and that’s all of it again, in reverse, so you square it.” He did a fast calculation. “Which comes out at about — two percent. Each. Roughly. I mean, that’s very rough, the error bars are — well, the error bars are basically the whole napkin.”</p>\n<p>There was a short silence.</p>\n<p>“Two percent,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Roughly.”</p>\n<p>“Well.” She pushed her plate away and sat back and put her boots up on the chair beside her. “That’s better than Tessarine.”</p>\n<p>“What was Tessarine?” said Ben.</p>\n<p>So she told them. The Tessarine floods, Year Seventy-Nine, the spring the Canopy failed over the southern delta for eleven hours and the storm surge came up the river mouth and put six metres of water through the lower tiers of the city in forty minutes. She’d been a lieutenant. Her unit had gone into the Tier Two underpass with ropes and a rubber boat to get people out of a flooded Line station, and the water had kept rising while they were down there, and they’d had to swim out through the ticket hall in the pitch dark with the current pulling at them and twenty-three people on the ropes behind them, and they’d come out into the street and the street had been a river.</p>\n<p>“I did the numbers on that one too,” she said. “Afterwards. On the odds. It came out at about one in fifty for the unit. We lost two.” She shrugged. “Numbers are for afterwards. You go in, you do the job, you do the numbers in the pub, and then you get very drunk.”</p>\n<p>“Did you get any of them out?” Ben asked. “The people?”</p>\n<p>“Twenty-one.” She looked at the window. “Two of them didn’t make the ropes.”</p>\n<p>Ben folded his napkin in half, and in half again, and put it in the pocket of his liner.</p>\n<p>It was quiet for a while. The hammer came up through the table. The coffee in their cups shivered into rings, and settled, and shivered.</p>\n<p>“Why are you here, Hale?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>He looked at her. She was watching him over the rim of her cup, perfectly relaxed, and her eyes were not relaxed at all.</p>\n<p>“I’m the Standby.”</p>\n<p>“I know what you are. I’ve read your file. Gaunt gave me your file.” She didn’t look away. “I asked why.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He tried to think of an answer that was true and that he could say out loud in front of a twenty-six-year-old boy with a two-percent napkin in his pocket, and there wasn’t one.</p>\n<p>“Because it’s my job,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo held his eyes for a second longer. Then she snorted softly and looked back out of the window.</p>\n<p>“That’s what,” she said. “Not why. But all right.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Afterwards, there were the letters.</p>\n<p>Nobody said they were going to do it. It just happened, the way these things did. Ben went off to a corner of the hangar with his tablet propped against a toolbox and sat on the floor in front of it and talked to it for a long time, very quietly. Cas didn’t try to listen, but the hangar echoed, and every now and then a phrase came across to him between the hum and the hammer. <em>⁠— and the flat’s in both names so you won’t have to —</em> and <em>⁠— your mum’s recipe, I know I always said it was too salty —</em> and then, near the end, his voice breaking and going on anyway, <em>⁠— Jonah, I’m sorry, I’m sorry, I’m really sorry, I should’ve said yes to the dog.</em></p>\n<p>Teo wrote. She sat at a workbench with a Civil Defence notepad and a pencil and wrote two letters in a small square hand, and folded them, and wrote a name on each: <em>Mattias. Joss.</em> She put them in an envelope and wrote on the envelope, and put it in her breast pocket, next to the small thing Gaunt had given her.</p>\n<p>Cas sat at the other end of the bench with a sheet of paper in front of him for an hour and wrote nothing.</p>\n<p>He tried. He tried to write to Sabine and got as far as her name and couldn’t think what came after it that she didn’t already know. He tried to write to Nell and found that everything he wanted to say to her was either a lie or so large that it didn’t fit on paper. In the end he folded the blank sheet up and put it in his pocket, and took out instead the thing he’d brought from the flat without really thinking about it — a battered hardback sketchbook with a black cloth cover, a quarter full of old equations and older drawings — and turned it over in his hands.</p>\n<p>Then he went and found a phone.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Sabine answered on the eighth ring.</p>\n<p>“Cas.” A pause. Behind her, a lot of noise: voices, children, a loudspeaker saying something about blankets. “Where are you? I’ve been trying your — they said your number was⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m at work.”</p>\n<p>A silence. He could hear her understanding it.</p>\n<p>“At <em>work</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Where are you? What’s that noise?”</p>\n<p>“We’re in a school. A sports hall. Tier Thirty. They came round the whole of Nine at lunchtime and said we were being relocated for our comfort. For our <em>comfort</em>, Cas. They said it was a precaution. There’s four hundred people in here and one toilet.” Her voice dropped. “Everybody’s being very calm. It’s horrible. Nobody’s saying anything and everybody keeps flinching. Cas, what is that? That noise. That — thing. It’s been going since two in the morning. Is it the Draw? Is it⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Is Nell there?”</p>\n<p>“Cas⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Please, Sabine.”</p>\n<p>A breath. Then, away from the phone: “Nellie. Nell. It’s Dad.”</p>\n<p>A scuffle, a clatter, and then Nell’s voice, very close and very loud, the way she always talked on the phone, as if she thought she had to shout to make it reach.</p>\n<p>“Dad! Dad, we’re in a <em>gym</em>!”</p>\n<p>“I heard.”</p>\n<p>“There’s a girl here called Pip who’s got a lizard. A real one. It’s in a box. It’s called Dennis.” A breath. “Dad, are you at the Draw?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Is it broken?”</p>\n<p>He closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “It’s broken.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a second. She was ten. She had her mother’s grey eyes and his own inability to leave a question alone, and she collected stars. She had never seen one. No one her age in Averna had. The city’s light and the Canopy’s glow had wiped them from the sky before she was born, before he was born, and she had got interested in them anyway, the way some children get interested in dinosaurs — because they were enormous and far away and gone. She had glow-in-the-dark stars on her bedroom ceiling in their proper constellations, placed with a chart and a ruler, and she knew their names the way other children knew football teams. Betelgeuse. Deneb. Fomalhaut. She said them like spells.</p>\n<p>“Pip’s brother says,” she said carefully, “that if the Draw is broken, the lights will all go off. Like, <em>all</em> of them. Everywhere.”</p>\n<p>“They might.”</p>\n<p>“So we’d see them.”</p>\n<p>It took him a second. “The stars?”</p>\n<p>“If the lights all went off. If it was dark. We’d see them. Wouldn’t we? Properly?”</p>\n<p>“I suppose you would.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>knew</em> it.” She sounded so pleased. “I told Pip. He said they’d have gone. He said they probably weren’t even there any more. I said they’re <em>always</em> there, you just can’t see them.”</p>\n<p>“You’re right.”</p>\n<p>“I know I’m right.” A pause. Then, in a smaller voice: “Dad, are you going in there? Into the broken bit?”</p>\n<p>The hammer. The hum. Across the hangar, Ben still talking to his tablet with his forehead in his hand.</p>\n<p>He had been going to lie. He had the lie ready. <em>No, sweetheart, I’m just here to look at the numbers, I’ll be home soon.</em> It was sitting in his mouth, smooth and round, and it would have slid out of him like all the others.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said instead. “Tonight. Very early. With two other people.”</p>\n<p>“Is it dangerous?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Are you scared?”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “I’m very scared, Nell.”</p>\n<p>There was a long pause on the line. He listened to her breathe. He wondered what he’d just done — whether it was the worst thing he could have done to her or the only true thing he’d said to anyone in fifteen years, and whether those were different.</p>\n<p>“That’s allowed,” Nell said at last. She said it firmly, the way Sabine said things, the way she’d been told it herself, probably, in a sports hall, an hour ago. “Mum says that’s allowed. You’re allowed to be scared, you just have to do it anyway.”</p>\n<p>“Your mum’s right.”</p>\n<p>“Dad.” She’d lowered her voice to a whisper, as though telling him a secret. “When you’re in there. Will you look for stars?”</p>\n<p>“Nell, there aren’t any stars in there. It’s a power station. It’s all⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You don’t <em>know</em> that,” she said. “You haven’t been. You don’t know what’s in there. There might be.”</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. He shut it again.</p>\n<p>“All right,” he said. “I’ll look.”</p>\n<p>“Promise.”</p>\n<p>“I promise.”</p>\n<p>“Okay.” Satisfied. Then, abruptly, over her shoulder, “<em>Mum</em>, Dennis is out⁠—” and a clatter and a shriek and Sabine’s voice, distant, and then Sabine back on the line, breathless.</p>\n<p>“Cas? Sorry. A lizard. Cas, what did you tell her? She’s — she looks⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I told her the truth.”</p>\n<p>A long silence.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Sabine eventually. “That’ll be a first.” But her voice had gone strange, and when she spoke again it was very quiet. “Cas. Come back. Do you hear me? She needs you to come back.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” he said. And then, because it was the only true thing left, “I’m sorry, Bina.”</p>\n<p>He put the phone down before she could answer.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Continuance Day address went out at nine that night, fifteen hours late.</p>\n<p>They watched it on the canteen screen. The First Director of the Commonweal stood in Hollowell Square in front of the dark Quota clock with the city’s lights behind him and spoke for eleven minutes about sacrifice and endurance and the Tending, and about a minor technical irregularity at the Draw which was being handled with the utmost skill and would be resolved in good time, and about the future, which would pay. Then they played the footage. The Crown, the Lesion like a gold star through the window, engineers at their boards in crisp white coats, a triumphant needle swinging over. The numbers climbing. The crowd in the square cheering.</p>\n<p>“That’s from Year Eighty,” said Ben. “The First Deepening. My dad took me. I was fifteen, I thought it was the best day of my life. That’s — look, that’s the old console layout, they changed that in Eighty-Five.” He stared at the screen. “They’re showing old footage. They’re showing old footage and pretending it’s tonight.”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything. On the screen, the gold star shone steady and perfect. Outside the window, over the Works, the shimmer rose into the dark. Once a second, the whole canteen shook.</p>\n<p>Teo got up and turned the screen off.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At eleven Ben showed Cas the relay pucks.</p>\n<p>They were small, flat, hockey-puck-shaped things in orange casings, a box of forty, and Ben had been fiddling with them for hours at a workbench under a lamp while the keels sang behind him.</p>\n<p>“Radio repeaters,” he said. “We drop them as we go. They’ll bounce signal back through the Works to the Collar. And to each other. Only — here’s the thing, here’s the thing I realised this afternoon, it’s actually really interesting⁠—” and he was off, with his hands, the way he’d been off about the keels. “If there’s a time gradient between two radios, the signal gets stretched. Like, if you’re somewhere where time’s running slow and I’m somewhere where it’s running fast, then from where I am, your voice comes through slowed down, because it’s — you’re talking at slow-time speed, right? So from my side a one-second word from you takes, I don’t know, a minute to arrive. It gets redshifted, it gets smeared out. And the other way round, if I talk to you, I’m talking at fast-time speed, so I get squashed — it arrives all at once, a whole speech in a blip. So I’ve written some code that buffers everything and re-times it. It records the whole stretched-out signal, however long it takes to come in, and then when it’s finished it plays it back at the right speed. So we’ll still be able to understand each other. It just might take a while.”</p>\n<p>“How much of a while?”</p>\n<p>Ben shrugged. “Depends on the ratio. Ten to one, it’s fine, it’s like a bad phone line. A thousand to one, and your one-minute message takes⁠—” he did it in his head “⁠—seventeen hours to get to me. Doesn’t matter though. It’ll still get there.” He grinned tiredly. “If one of us ends up in fast time, the others’ll sound like whales. Big slow whale noises for hours. And then the suit goes <em>ding</em> and it’s you, talking normally, saying ‘Farr, you idiot, why did you bring the pucks in the wrong box.’”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the forty orange pucks. He thought about a ratio of tens of thousands. A one-minute message taking weeks to arrive. A voice spread so thin over time that it would come through as a single long unbroken note, like the hum of a keel.</p>\n<p>“That’s good work, Ben,” he said.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up at him, surprised, and then flushed right up to the roots of his standing-up hair.</p>\n<p>“Thanks,” he said. “I mean. Yeah. Thanks.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>A little after midnight Cas went and stood by the spin rigs.</p>\n<p>The note of the keels had climbed all night and was now high and pure and steady, a single sustained chord that filled the hangar the way light fills a room. The three suits stood in their frames in a row, grey and hulking, <em>1</em>, <em>2</em>, <em>3</em>, with their indicator bands glowing a steady green, and they looked, in the dim hangar with the Works dark beyond the doors, like three knights keeping a vigil in a chapel. He supposed that was what they were doing. He supposed that was what all three of them were doing.</p>\n<p>Teo came and stood beside him. She had a mug of something in each hand and she gave him one. It was tea, stewed black, with an unbelievable quantity of sugar in it.</p>\n<p>“Civil Defence tea,” she said. “It’s mostly a structural material.”</p>\n<p>They stood and drank it. The hammer went on.</p>\n<p>“I’ve got two boys,” Teo said, after a while, to the suits. “Mattias and Joss. Twenty-four and twenty-one. Mattias is an electrician in Kovač. Joss is at the university here, doing something with computers I don’t understand. Their father died eleven years ago. Heart.” She drank. “I used to tell them, when they were small and they were frightened of something — the dark, the dentist, whatever — I used to tell them, you do the thing, and you do it scared, and then it’s done, and the scared goes away and the done stays. That’s the deal. Scared goes, done stays.”</p>\n<p>“Nell’s mother tells her something like that.”</p>\n<p>“Smart woman.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.” He looked into the tea. “She is.”</p>\n<p>Teo was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“You told your girl the truth,” she said. “On the phone. I heard. Sorry. This place echoes like a church.”</p>\n<p>He had no answer to that.</p>\n<p>“Good,” said Teo. That was all. Then she knocked back the rest of her tea, and wiped her mouth with the back of her hand, and went off to try to sleep on a pile of tarpaulins in the corner.</p>\n<p>He stood there a while longer. The keels sang. Out in the dark, beyond the doors, the shimmer stood over the station like a ghost of a column, like a tower made of nothing.</p>\n<p><em>You don’t know what’s in there</em>, said Nell in his head. <em>There might be.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At three in the morning — hour twenty-five, by the count that had become the only count that mattered — Ben pronounced the keels spun.</p>\n<p>At half past three they suited up. The liner, cool and close; the clam of the suit closing around them, thunk, thunk, thunk; the exo taking their weight. The keels were at full now and the note came into Cas’s spine like a held breath. The world was a bright curve of visor. In his ears, the suits’ radios hissed and clicked, and then Ben said, “Comms check, one,” and Teo said, “One, loud and clear, unfortunately,” and Ben said, “Two,” and then, “Three?” and Cas said, “Three.”</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “Right. Let’s go and fix the fucking world.”</p>\n<p>They walked. Out of the hangar, down a service corridor, the three of them in a line, heavy and slow, their boots booming on the steel floor. The Collar was empty. Every door stood open. Every screen was on, showing the same message on a blue background — <em>MARGIN IRREGULARITY: REMAIN CALM</em> — and every light was on, and there was nobody, and their footsteps echoed down corridors that went on and on.</p>\n<p>At the end of the last corridor was Gate Nine: a great steel door five metres high, painted in hazard stripes, set in a black wall. A small group was waiting in front of it. Rask, very straight, in the same dark suit. Gaunt, in his cardigan, with his hands folded. Petra Imre from the Lattice office, who came forward and, without saying anything, put her hand flat on the chestplate of Cas’s suit for a second, and took it away, and stepped back with her eyes wet. A Civil Defence sergeant at the controls of the gate.</p>\n<p>Nobody made a speech. Cas was grateful for that. Rask looked at each of them in turn, and nodded once, and said, “The Commonweal is grateful,” and it was so obviously the wrong thing to say that it was almost moving.</p>\n<p>“Open it,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>The sergeant pulled a lever. Somewhere in the wall a motor began to grind.</p>\n<p>Gate Nine rolled back.</p>\n<p>Beyond it was the Works, dark and vast and humming, going on forever. Warm air came out of it, and a smell of ozone and hot metal and something green and rank like pondwater. The light inside was wrong — a sort of faint, shifting, pearly glow that seemed to come from nowhere in particular.</p>\n<p>And the hammer, which had been a sound for more than a day, was not a sound any more. In here it was simply the world. It came up through the floor and into the suits and through the suits into their bones, vast and slow and absolute, and all three of them stopped on the threshold as though they’d walked into a wall.</p>\n<p>Once a second. Once a second. Once a second.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Ben’s voice, small and brave in Cas’s ear. “That’s not ominous at all.”</p>\n<p>They went in.</p>"}, {"file": "03-the-works.md", "label": "Chapter 3", "title": "The Works", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 4589, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>SECTION 9 — IN THE EVENT OF CONTAINMENT LOSS: REMAIN AT YOUR STATION AND AWAIT RELIEF. DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE. RELIEF CREWS WILL BE DISPATCHED. YOU WILL NOT BE FORGOTTEN.</p><span class=\"attr\">Hollowell Draw emergency placard, standard issue, all levels</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The placard was the first thing they saw.</p>\n<p>It was screwed to the wall just inside Gate Nine, at head height, where every worker coming on shift for ninety-one years would have walked past it: a sheet of yellowed laminate in a steel frame, the words printed in the blocky black typeface the Ministry had used in the Year Twenties, with a little diagram of a stick figure standing beside a stick-figure console with its stick hands at its sides. The laminate had gone cloudy with age. Somebody, at some point, had drawn a moustache on the stick figure in marker pen, and somebody else had scrubbed most of it off.</p>\n<p>Teo stopped in front of it. Her helmet light moved slowly down the lines.</p>\n<p>“’You will not be forgotten,’” she read. “They’ll put that on my headstone.”</p>\n<p>“They’ll put it on everyone’s,” said Ben.</p>\n<p>The gate ground shut behind them. The sound of it closing was very final. Then there was only the hammer, and the three notes of the keels, and their breathing in the suits.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Cas had been inside the Draw many times, once. As a student on placement; as a junior on the Lattice survey team; as a member of the Margin Review, walking the Works with a clipboard behind Dr Adeyemi, who had been sixty-eight and walked everywhere very fast with his hands in his pockets, stopping every so often to lay his palm flat on a wall and listen. He had thought he remembered it. He had thought that fifteen years of not being allowed in had simply made it smaller in his mind, the way the house you grew up in shrinks when you go back.</p>\n<p>He had forgotten nothing. He had simply never understood it.</p>\n<p>The Works went on for four kilometres between the Collar and the Lattice, and in all that distance there was not one thing that had been built as part of a plan. Hollowell’s original station had been a sphere — the Lattice, the Vault, the Lesion — and a thin shell of machinery around it. Then the Commonweal had grown hungry, and every decade since, the Ministry had wanted more, and every decade the engineers had given it more the only way they could: by adding. Rectifier halls to convert the Draw’s raw output, and more rectifier halls when the first ones couldn’t keep up. Coolant galleries. Bioscrubber halls full of algae tanks to eat the carbon the crews breathed out, because it was cheaper to grow the air than to pump it four kilometres. Transformer vaults, cable tunnels, lift shafts, a spinal column of superconducting trunk lines as thick as a house. And all of it jammed in wherever it would go, on top of, around, and through everything that had been there before, until the whole thing had become less like a building than a reef — a vast, dim, dripping, humming structure that had grown by accretion for ninety-one years in the dark.</p>\n<p>Their route ran inward along what the maps called Radial Nine. The maps were optimistic. For the first hour it was a corridor, wide and high and lit by emergency strips at floor level, and they walked it in single file with Teo in front and Ben in the middle and Cas at the back, the keels humming, dropping an orange relay puck every two hundred metres. Then the corridor ended at a stair that went down into a transformer vault the size of a cathedral nave, full of grey steel cabinets in rows like pews, and they crossed it, and on the other side the corridor carried on at a different level, in a different typeface, painted a different colour. The signs changed every few hundred metres. RADIAL 9. R-9 (OLD). RADIAL NINE — WORKS SECTOR 4 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL. WORKS SECTOR 4 WAS HERE → with an arrow, in faded chalk, pointing at a wall.</p>\n<p>The smell was ozone and hot oil and algae and a faint sweetish undernote of rot, and the sound was the hammer, and under the hammer, everywhere, the Works itself: a vast layered murmur of machines that had not been told to stop. Pumps still pumping. Fans still turning. Somewhere far off, a relay clicking open and shut, open and shut, like a finger tapping on a desk. The rectifier halls were still live — there was power in them from somewhere, the Lesion’s leaking output finding its own way through the old conductors — and as they passed the doors they could hear the halls singing, a high, thin, electrical choir.</p>\n<p>And there was the light. The faint pearly glow Cas had seen from the gate was everywhere in here, in the corridors and halls and stairwells, sourceless and shifting. It came through gaps in walls and under doors. It lay on the floor in pale drifts like moonlight. When he held up his gauntlet in front of his visor, his hand cast no shadow.</p>\n<p>“Where’s that coming from?” said Ben.</p>\n<p>“The core,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Through four kilometres of — through all <em>this</em>?”</p>\n<p>“Not through it. Around it.” He didn’t really know how to explain it. He wasn’t sure he understood it himself. “The geometry’s bent. There are paths for light in here that shouldn’t exist. Some of it’s getting out along them.”</p>\n<p>“That’s⁠—” Ben started, and then, for once, didn’t finish.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The first gravity squall hit them in the second hour.</p>\n<p>They were in a long service gallery with pipes along both walls, walking on a grated catwalk above a drop into darkness, and Teo, in front, said, “Hold on⁠—” and then the floor tipped.</p>\n<p>It didn’t. The floor stayed exactly where it was. But <em>down</em> moved. Cas felt it go the way you’d feel a boat begin to roll: a slow, sickening swing, his weight sliding sideways inside the suit, the whole world leaning, and then the far wall of the gallery was below him and he was falling toward it. He grabbed. The suit’s gauntlet closed on the catwalk rail harder than he meant it to, crushing the steel, and held. His legs swung out. For a long moment he hung there, the three of them hanging there, dangling from a handrail over a wall that had become a floor, while the pipes around them groaned and a loose toolbox went tumbling past, end over end, and hit the far wall, and slid along it, and fell into the dark.</p>\n<p>Then down swung back. Slowly, like a pendulum. They dropped to their knees on the catwalk.</p>\n<p>“<em>Christ</em>,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Everybody okay?” Ben’s voice was high. “Everybody — is the keel — your keels okay? Check your — the indicator bands, are they⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Green,” said Cas. “Green. I’m fine.”</p>\n<p>“Green,” said Teo. “What the hell was that?”</p>\n<p>“Gravity weather.” Cas got up. His legs were shaking inside the exo; the exo held them. “The Lattice is still there, it’s still pulling, but it’s lost its shape. And the Lesion’s pushing. And every piece of keelstone in the Works is pulling in its own direction. They’re all out of balance. It’ll come in gusts.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Gusts.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And shears. And lees. Places where they cancel out and it’s calm.” He looked back along the gallery. “We should expect more.”</p>\n<p>They did get more. Over the next hours the Works taught them its weather the hard way. There were gusts like the first, where down swung sideways or backwards or, once, for three terrible seconds, straight up, so that they found themselves pressed against the ceiling of a stairwell looking down at the stairs. There were places where weight simply piled on — a stretch of corridor where each step was heavier than the last until the exo was groaning and Cas’s knees were on fire even inside it, and then, abruptly, it was gone. There was a stairwell where, for three steps in the middle, gravity doubled, and they learned to take those three steps at a run. There was a long, wide turbine hall where down was very slightly <em>forward</em>, so that crossing it they seemed to be walking downhill on a floor that was dead level, the three of them leaning backward against nothing like people walking into a gale.</p>\n<p>They learned to watch for it. Loose things were the tell. A cable swinging when there was no breeze. Dust drifting across a floor. Teo started carrying a handful of washers in her gauntlet and dropping one every few metres, watching where it fell.</p>\n<p>And their clocks began to drift.</p>\n<p>Each suit had a small display at the bottom of the visor with two times on it: OUTSIDE, fed by a signal from the Collar through the relay pucks, and LOCAL, the suit’s own clock. At the gate they’d kept step, second for second. An hour in, LOCAL had gained four minutes. Two hours in, nineteen. Ben started reading out the ratio every half hour like a man calling depths on a ship.</p>\n<p>“One point one.”</p>\n<p>“One point three.”</p>\n<p>“One point six. We’re living sixty percent faster than the Collar.”</p>\n<p>“Meaning?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Meaning for every hour we spend in here, only about forty minutes go by out there.” Ben considered this. “I mean. Which is sort of good? We’ve got more time than we thought.”</p>\n<p>“We’ve got the same amount of time,” said Cas. “We’re just spending more of it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They found the first of the night shift in the fourth hour.</p>\n<p>They’d seen signs before that. Hard hats in corridors. A jacket hanging on a valve wheel. A lunch box, open, a sandwich still inside it, going green. An emergency locker torn open and emptied. Once, a set of muddy footprints in the dust of a floor that had been dry for years, going the other way, toward the gate, running. They were the prints of people who’d got out. The Collar had counted them: about four hundred had come out through the gates in the first hour after the rupture, from the outer Works, before the gradients got bad.</p>\n<p>The rest hadn’t.</p>\n<p>It was a stairhead, where a stair from the lower levels came up through the floor into a junction of four corridors. The night shift had come up that stair, running for the gate, maybe twenty of them. Cas never knew what had happened exactly. He guessed that it was a shear — a place where the gravity gradient had gone suddenly steep, a cliff of force, and had moved, perhaps only a few metres, perhaps only for a second — and that they had been coming up the stair into the junction at the moment it passed over them.</p>\n<p>They were on the wall.</p>\n<p>That was the only way he could ever put it, afterwards, to himself. They were on the far wall of the junction, the way paint is on a wall. Their shapes were still there. You could see where each of them had been — this one’s arm flung up, this one’s hard hat, the pale sole of a boot. You could see that one of them had been holding someone else’s hand. But they had been pressed into the wall, by a weight no body could bear, with such force and so suddenly that they had not so much been crushed as <em>printed</em>. The wall behind them was bowed inward. The blood was black and had run nowhere, because at the instant it happened there had been nowhere for it to run.</p>\n<p>Ben made a sound and turned away and went down on his knees in the middle of the junction and was sick in his helmet. The suit’s scrubbers caught it and whined. He stayed there, bent over, his gauntlets on the floor, breathing in harsh, wet gulps over the radio.</p>\n<p>Cas stood very still. He found that he was looking at the hand. The two hands. He could not stop looking at the two hands.</p>\n<p>Teo walked past them both.</p>\n<p>She walked right up to the wall. She stood in front of it for a moment. Then she took off her own jacket — she’d brought a Civil Defence field jacket, strapped to the back of her suit, for when they reached somewhere she could take the suit off — and unfolded it and reached up and laid it over the part of the wall where the faces had been. It didn’t cover much. She looked around, and found a tarpaulin in a maintenance alcove, and a couple of high-vis vests hanging on a hook, and a dust sheet, and she hung them all. She did it methodically and without hurry, while Ben knelt on the floor being sick and Cas stood staring, and when she’d finished there was a patchwork of cloth on the wall and you could no longer see anything under it but shapes.</p>\n<p>Then she came back and put her gauntlet on Ben’s shoulder.</p>\n<p>“Up you get, son.”</p>\n<p>“I can’t⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You can. You’ll do it scared. Up.”</p>\n<p>He got up.</p>\n<p>Teo looked at Cas through two visors. He couldn’t see her face. He could hear her breathing, over the radio, harsh and slow and very controlled.</p>\n<p>“How many more of those,” she said, “are we going to find?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“Right.” She turned toward the corridor that ran inward. “Then let’s not stand about.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw the filament first.</p>\n<p>It was in a hall they had to cross in the fifth hour — a long, high, gloomy space that had been a coolant-pump hall once, before the pumps were moved, and was now a storeroom, full of pallets and crates and racks of spares going back to the ceiling in the pearly dark. Their route crossed it diagonally. They were halfway across when Cas saw something at the far end and stopped, and said, “Wait,” and the others stopped.</p>\n<p>It was a thread of light.</p>\n<p>It came out of the far wall of the hall about ten metres up and crossed the space in front of them and went into the floor. It was as fine as a hair and as bright as the filament in a bulb — the same white-violet as the Lesion, the colour that wasn’t quite a colour — and it wasn’t straight. It branched. It went a little way and split in two, and each of those split, and each of those, so that what came out of the wall as a single line went into the floor as a fine, spreading fan of threads, a luminous root system, a river delta drawn in light. And where it branched, the branches branched, smaller and smaller, finer and finer, until the eye lost them and they became a sort of glow, a faint shimmering mist of edges around every edge. When he tried to look at any part of it closely, his eye slid off. When he looked away, he could still see it on the inside of his eyelids.</p>\n<p>It made no sound.</p>\n<p>“What,” said Ben, “is that.”</p>\n<p>“Part of the Lesion,” said Cas. He was surprised how steady his voice was. “Part of the boundary. It’s — when it broke, it didn’t break into pieces. It broke into more edge. The edge is growing outward. That’s one of its threads.”</p>\n<p>“It came through four kilometres of⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It doesn’t come <em>through</em> things. It’s not in the Works the way we’re in the Works. It’s where the Works has been — bent.” He didn’t have a better word for it. “Don’t go near it.”</p>\n<p>They didn’t need telling. But they couldn’t help looking. And what they saw, the closer they looked, was worse than the light.</p>\n<p>Around the filament, for a metre or two on every side, the hall was different. The racks it passed near weren’t grey steel any more; they were brown and orange and flaking, rusted through into lace, sagging on themselves, and in one place a whole rack had simply crumbled and lay on the floor in a heap of rust-red powder with the plastic crates it had held collapsed into brittle shards on top. A section of cable tray that ran close to one branch was grey-white and furred. The paint on the wall beside the place where the thread came out had peeled and curled and fallen and lay in drifts. And on the floor, close to the fan of threads, there was a potted plant.</p>\n<p>Someone must have brought it in. A night-shift worker, making the storeroom a bit nicer. A spider plant, the kind that grew in every office in Averna, in a green plastic pot. Except that it had grown. Its pale leaves had gone up in a fountain higher than a man’s head and spilled out across the floor in long runners, and the runners had put down roots in the dust of the floor and grown new plants, and those had grown, and all of it — the whole great sprawling green-white tangle, three metres across — was dead, and dry, and grey, and dusty, as though it had grown for years and years and then withered and then stood for years more after that. In a hall where, by the Collar’s clock, only twenty-nine hours had passed.</p>\n<p>“It’s fast,” said Cas. “Near the thread. Time’s fast.”</p>\n<p>Ben already had his instrument out — a small sensor on a pole that measured the local clock rate — and was holding it out as far toward the threads as he dared, his arm at full stretch. The number on its display leapt as he moved it. Two. Nine. Forty. He took one step closer and it read four hundred.</p>\n<p>“Back up,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>He backed up. The number fell. He lowered the pole very slowly and stood looking at the dead plant.</p>\n<p>“That thing,” he said quietly, “is going to be everywhere, isn’t it. Further in.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>They went round the far side of the hall, as far from the filament as the racks allowed. Nobody talked. When they reached the far door Cas looked back once. From here the thread was just a pale line crossing the gloom, a scratch on the air. It could have been a trick of the light. It could have been nothing. Except that he could see, very faintly, in the glow around its finest branches, that it was moving. Growing. Very slowly branching into itself.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At the end of the fifth hour they found a lee and stopped to rest.</p>\n<p>It was a small control room for a bank of pumps that had long since been removed, a box of a room with a desk and two chairs and a window onto nothing, and when they came into it the gravity just — went soft. After hours of gusts and shears and weights coming and going it was so calm, so ordinary, that all three of them stood in the doorway for a moment, swaying, like sailors come ashore. Teo dropped a washer. It fell straight down and bounced.</p>\n<p>“Oh, thank God,” said Ben, and sat down on the floor.</p>\n<p>They didn’t take the suits off. They couldn’t, here. But they sat, and drank from the suits’ water lines, and ate the paste that came out of the suits’ ration tubes, which tasted, Ben said, like somebody had described chicken to a machine. Their clocks said LOCAL 05:02 — five hours since the gate — and OUTSIDE 29:07, twenty-nine hours since the hammer began. The ratio in here was one point nine.</p>\n<p>Teo sat on the desk. For a while she was silent. Then she reached into the chest pocket of her liner, under the suit’s neck-ring, with two fingers, working carefully in the gauntlet, and brought out something small. She held it up.</p>\n<p>It was a data chip. A little black wafer the size of a fingernail, with the Office of Public Calm’s seal on it in silver.</p>\n<p>“Gaunt gave me this,” she said.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up.</p>\n<p>“Orders,” said Teo. “Sealed. For my eyes. I read them last night.” She turned the chip over in her fingers. “They say that the mission objective is the restoration of Continuance. They say that the technical lead, Dr Casimir Hale, has a documented history of unreliability on the question of Continuance. They say that in the event that Dr Hale attempts any action which would result in — let me get it right⁠—” she half closed her eyes “⁠—‘the permanent default of the Hollowell Draw’, I’m authorised to prevent that action by any means.” She looked at Cas. “Any means. They underlined it.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“You knew,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“I saw Gaunt give you something.”</p>\n<p>“And you didn’t say anything.”</p>\n<p>“I thought you’d tell me. Or you wouldn’t.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him for a while. Then she looked at the chip.</p>\n<p>On the floor of the little control room, along one wall, ran a coolant channel — an open steel gutter about a hand’s width across, with a steady, fast stream of cloudy water racing along it and vanishing through a grating into the dark under the floor. Teo leaned down and held the chip over it.</p>\n<p>“Tell me I’m not going to regret this,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’re probably going to regret this.”</p>\n<p>“Tell me the truth, then.” Her voice was flat. “All of it. Right now. Or I’ll keep it.”</p>\n<p>So he did.</p>\n<p>He told her Six couldn’t be reseated. That it had never been possible — that a Lattice with a fractal core had no geometry left to seat anything in, and every engineer in the crisis room had known it and had said <em>yes, Minister</em> anyway, and so had he. He told her about the Clause, and the Keystone, and that the only thing in the Draw that could stop what was coming would also end the Draw for ever, which was why nobody in the Ministry would say its name. He told her the key had been around the neck of Deputy Minister Aldous Crane when the Lesion broke, and that he didn’t know where Crane was, or whether he was alive, or whether the key still existed. He told her that he didn’t know what the words on the plate meant. He told her that he didn’t know, even if they reached the Crown, even if they found the key, even if they got the Keystone open, whether the thing would work, or whether anyone could survive working it, or whether Hollowell had been a genius or a madwoman or both.</p>\n<p>“So,” said Teo, when he’d finished. “Let me get this right. We’re walking into hell on a maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She dropped the chip into the coolant channel. It hit the water and was gone, whisked away down the gutter and through the grating into the dark, and she didn’t even watch it go. “I hate certainties. They always want something from you.”</p>\n<p>Ben was staring at her.</p>\n<p>“Major,” he said. “Major, that’s — I mean, isn’t that⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Treason? Probably.” Teo stretched, as far as the suit let her, and her joints cracked over the radio. “I don’t give a damn about the Draw, Farr. Never have. I care about the six hundred million people up there who are about to be very dead, and I care about my boys, and I care about getting a drink afterwards with a man who owes me one.” She tipped her visor at Cas. “That’s you, by the way. You owe me one. For the chip.”</p>\n<p>“I owe you one,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Good. I’ll have a large one.”</p>\n<p>Ben looked from one of them to the other. Then he laughed — a thin, shocked, helpless little laugh, like air going out of a tyre — and put his helmet in his gauntlets and kept laughing, and after a while it wasn’t clear that it was laughing any more, and Teo reached over and put her hand on his back and left it there until it stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They moved on at the start of the sixth hour.</p>\n<p>The map said that Radial Nine ran on for another kilometre and a half to the Lattice, but the map also said it went straight through a bioscrubber hall — B-7, one of the big algae halls from the Year Fifties — and when they got to the hall’s outer door the light coming around its edges was brighter than anywhere they had been.</p>\n<p>Ben went ahead, up a short stair to an observation gallery that ran along one side of the hall, and they heard him stop.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” he said. “You should see this.”</p>\n<p>It was a long gallery with a wall of thick glass, looking down into the hall. The hall was enormous — three hundred metres long, forty wide — and filled with algae tanks: great clear columns of water, rank after rank of them, each as tall as a house and full of drifting green, lit from within by their own grow-lamps, so that the whole hall glowed a deep, underwater emerald. Pipes ran between them. Walkways ran between the pipes. It was beautiful in a way nothing else in the Works had been. It looked like the inside of a lung. It looked like the bottom of the sea.</p>\n<p>And along the far wall of it, from floor to ceiling, ran a filament.</p>\n<p>This one was bigger. Not a hair; a vein. It came down the far wall from somewhere high up and forked, and forked, and forked again, spreading across the far end of the hall in a great luminous tree, its finest twigs reaching out into the ranks of tanks like fingers. Around those fingers the tanks were different colours: black, brown, a thick, clotted, vivid green, one that had simply cracked and drained and stood empty with a scum of dried grey slime up its sides.</p>\n<p>“We have to cross it,” said Teo, reading the map on her visor. “The route goes through the hall. The far door’s at the south end, that side, away from the — from that.” She pointed. “The other way round’s the long way. Three hours.”</p>\n<p>Cas measured with his eye. The filament’s reach ended maybe sixty metres from the south door.</p>\n<p>“The inner bulkhead’s closed,” said Ben. He was leaning on the glass, looking down at the hall door below them. “At the bottom of the stair. It’s on the manual lock, it must have sealed when the power went funny. Someone’ll have to go down and cycle it by hand from inside. Then we walk down the south side, well clear, and out the far door.” He straightened. “I’ll do it. I know those locks. We had them in the lab.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll do it,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“You won’t know the sequence, it’s a stupid old design, there’s a thing you have to do with the⁠—” He was already heading for the stair. “Two minutes. I’ll open it, you come through. We’ll be fine. It’s nowhere near the — it’s right over this end.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the filament. It was a long way off. It was, very slowly, growing.</p>\n<p>“Ben,” he said. “Wait.”</p>\n<p>But Ben was already on the stair, going down, his keel humming, his boots ringing on the steel treads, and the door at the bottom was already sliding open for him, and he was already through.</p>"}, {"file": "04-blister.md", "label": "Chapter 4", "title": "Blister", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 4536, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>If one of us ends up in fast time, the others’ll sound like whales.</p><span class=\"attr\">Benedek Farr</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">From the gallery they watched him cross the floor of the hall.</p>\n<p>He went quickly, the way he did everything, the keel’s hum rising and falling in their radios as he moved, his helmet light bobbing between the great glowing columns of the tanks. The hall’s inner bulkhead was at the bottom of the gallery stair — a big grey pressure door on the hall side, three metres high, the kind of door that sealed a bioscrubber hall so the algae didn’t get into the rest of the air — and the control box for its manual cycle was on a pillar ten metres inside the hall. Ben reached it. They saw him open its cover. They heard him say, “Right, you absolute antique, let’s have you,” and heard the clank of a lever.</p>\n<p>“See? It’s the thing with the⁠—” he said.</p>\n<p>The filament moved.</p>\n<p>Cas saw it. Far off at the other end of the hall, the great luminous tree on the wall — it didn’t move so much as <em>grow</em>, all at once, a flicker of new branches running out from its tips like frost racing across a pond. A single fine thread came out of the tree and across the ceiling of the hall, faster than he could follow, fine as spider silk, and went down into the floor about forty metres from where Ben was standing.</p>\n<p>At the same moment the gallery lights flickered, and every door in the hall slammed.</p>\n<p>The personnel door at the bottom of the stair, which Ben had come through, shut with a bang they felt through the floor. The big bulkhead — half-open now — stopped, and reversed, and ground shut again. Warning lights went round.</p>\n<p>And Ben⁠—</p>\n<p>Ben flickered.</p>\n<p>That was what it looked like. One moment he was standing at the pillar with his hand on the lever, and the next he was standing somewhere else — a step to the left, turned round — and the next he was at the door, and the next he was back at the pillar, and none of it was motion. It was like watching a film with most of the frames cut out. He jumped from place to place in stutters. His helmet light flared and moved and flared and moved.</p>\n<p>In their ears, the radio made a noise.</p>\n<p>It was a squeal. A high, thin, rising chirp, like a bird, like a bat, like a whistle blown in a dream, so brief it was almost not there, and then another, and another.</p>\n<p>“Ben!” Teo was at the glass, her gauntlets flat on it. “Ben! <em>Farr!</em>”</p>\n<p>The suit’s radio chimed. A little amber light came on at the bottom of Cas’s visor: RECEIVING — BUFFERING — RETIMING. Ben’s code. It had caught the chirps and was stretching them back out.</p>\n<p>And then, suddenly, Ben’s voice, perfectly clear, perfectly normal, in their ears:</p>\n<p>“⁠—what happened, what happened, the door’s — Cas? Teo? The door’s — oh no. Oh no, no, no, no. I can see you, I can see you up there, you’re not moving, why aren’t you — oh no. Oh, no. Okay. Okay. Okay.”</p>\n<p>It stopped. The light went off.</p>\n<p>Down on the floor of the hall, Ben was at the door at the bottom of the stair, hammering on it. Then he was gone. Then he was at the pillar. Then he was standing still — for a whole second, perfectly still — in the middle of the hall, looking up at them.</p>\n<p>The radio chirped.</p>\n<p>RECEIVING — BUFFERING — RETIMING.</p>\n<p>“Okay.” His voice again. Different. Steadier, as though he had had time to steady it, as though he had sat down somewhere and breathed for a while. “Okay. I’ve got the pole out. I’m reading forty-eight thousand. Forty-eight thousand to one. That thread came down forty metres from me and it’s — the whole hall’s in it. The whole hall’s gone fast. It’s a blister. You can’t see it but I think there’s a boundary right at the walls. Right at the glass. Don’t open the door. Listen to me. Don’t open the door. The boundary’s going to be a shear, it’ll be steep as anything, the keels won’t — I don’t think the keels can take it. It’ll tear you apart. And it’ll tear <em>me</em> apart if I try to go through it. So don’t. Okay? Don’t. I’m going to — I’m going to think.” A pause. “I’m okay. I’m okay. I’m just going to think.”</p>\n<p>The light went off.</p>\n<p>The radio never spoke again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Forty-eight thousand,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>She said it very quietly. She was still at the glass.</p>\n<p>“A minute for us,” said Cas, “is a month for him.”</p>\n<p>He heard himself say it. He heard how calm it sounded.</p>\n<p>“Then we open the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He said no.”</p>\n<p>“I heard what he said. We’ve got keels. We’ve got⁠—” She turned on him. “What do you mean, <em>no</em>? That’s a <em>boy</em>, down there⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The boundary of the blister is a shear. From two to forty-eight thousand across — I don’t know. A metre? Less? The keel averages over a metre. It isn’t enough. Nothing’s enough. If we open that door the edge of the blister goes through the doorway, and whoever’s standing in it has one half of their body living twenty-four thousand times faster than the other half.” He made himself go on. “He knows the keels better than anyone alive. He said no.”</p>\n<p>Teo stared at him. Then she looked back down through the glass. Below them, in the emerald light, Ben was moving in stutters from tank to tank.</p>\n<p>“There’s got to be a way,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Maybe. If there is, he’ll find it before we do. He’s got more time.”</p>\n<p>It was Ben who worked out how to talk.</p>\n<p>They didn’t see him do it. They saw him vanish from the floor, and then — perhaps a minute later — he was there on the other side of the glass, suddenly, right in front of them. The algae tanks stood in rows below the gallery, and along their tops ran a maintenance catwalk that came right up against the gallery window, and he was standing on it, a foot away from them through the glass. He had taken his helmet off.</p>\n<p>His face was thinner. That was the first thing. He’d shaved — it was only a minute, for them — but the skin under his eyes was dark and there was a hollowness in his cheeks, and his hair had been cut, badly, very short. He looked at them. His eyes moved from Teo to Cas and back again. Then he held up a hand, and in it was a stubby yellow grease pencil, the kind engineers used to mark pipes.</p>\n<p>He wrote on the glass. He wrote fast; they couldn’t see his hand move. The letters simply appeared, backwards to him, forwards to them.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOW LONG</div>\n<p>Cas looked round. There was a supervisor’s desk in the gallery, and in its drawer, a marker. He took his right gauntlet off — the gallery was calm, a lee of sorts; the suit let him — and pulled the cap off the marker with his teeth and wrote, carefully, in capitals, backwards so Ben would read them the right way round:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">4 MIN</div>\n<p>It took him perhaps ten seconds.</p>\n<p>When he stepped back, Ben had written underneath:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">3 MONTHS</div>\n<p>And then, a flicker, and more:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">YOUR LETTERS TAKE A WEEK<br>WROTE HOW LONG. WAITED 8 DAYS TO SEE 4 MIN<br>KEEP IT SHORT</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There was a moment — Cas would think about it a great deal, afterwards — when they could have gone.</p>\n<p>The route through the hall was closed. But the long way round was still there. Three hours, the map said. Three hours to go round B-7 and rejoin Radial Nine on the far side, and then another kilometre and a half to the Lattice, and whatever was past the Lattice, and whatever was past that. Every minute they stood on this gallery was a minute. The debt was coming due. Somewhere out there a pulse was gathering itself at the core, and there was a lock in the Crown and perhaps a key and certainly six hundred million people, and none of it was going to wait.</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him. He looked at her.</p>\n<p>Neither of them said it. Teo took off her own right gauntlet, and sat down on the floor of the gallery with her back against the desk, and after a moment Cas sat down beside her. The glass was in front of them. On the other side of it, in the green light, a man they had known for a day began to live the rest of his life.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They didn’t see most of it. They saw it the way you’d see a life through a keyhole — in flashes, in fragments, the long stretches between cut out. When Ben stood still for a while, they saw him. When he moved, he was a flicker, a smear, a sense of someone having just been there. He’d be at the glass, and then the catwalk would be empty, and then — a minute later, a month later — he’d be back.</p>\n<p>He came back with a beard.</p>\n<p>It happened in no time at all. One flicker, he was clean-shaven and hollow-eyed. The next, there was a dark scruff along his jaw. The next, it was a beard: short, rough, reddish-brown, which neither of them had known his hair would grow. He’d taken the suit off entirely. He was wearing the grey liner and, over it, a pair of stained orange overalls from some locker, with the sleeves rolled up. He looked at them through the glass — they could see him breathing — and then wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I’M OK<br>AIR GOOD (ALGAE)<br>WATER GOOD (PIPES)<br>FOOD: ALGAE<br>:(</div>\n<p>Teo laughed. It came out of her like a sob.</p>\n<p>Ben saw it. Or saw something — saw her face change, very slowly, over days, perhaps. He grinned. It was so quick they barely saw it, but it was there, a flash of teeth in the beard, and then he was gone again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<div class=\"sign\">TELL JONAH</div>\n<p>Four minutes later, it was still there on the glass. Underneath it, in smaller letters:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">TELL HIM I SAID YES TO THE DOG</div>\n<p>Cas didn’t know what it meant. Teo did. She’d been across the hangar the night before, and she’d heard. She turned her face away, into the shoulder of her suit, and stayed that way for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Twenty minutes in — a year and a half, for Ben — the catwalk against the glass had become a place.</p>\n<p>He’d dragged things up there. A pallet, to sleep on, with a foam mat from a first-aid station and an emergency blanket, silver on one side. A crate for a table. A row of algae-paste tins, some full, some empty and scrubbed and used for things — a pencil pot, a cup, a tiny pot with a green shoot in it. He’d rigged a grow-lamp on a cable to hang over it all. He’d rigged a sort of curtain, from a tarpaulin, that he could draw across the glass. He didn’t draw it often.</p>\n<p>And there was a rat.</p>\n<p>They noticed it one flicker when Ben was sitting cross-legged on the pallet, eating something out of a tin with his fingers. It was sitting on his knee. A big, sleek, brown-grey Works rat, with a long pink tail curled round it and its paws folded, looking at them through the glass with its black-bead eyes as though it too were waiting to see what happened. Ben pointed at it and wrote an arrow on the glass, and a word:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MINISTER →</div>\n<p>Teo laughed again. This time it was nearly a real laugh.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>And then, for a while, there was nothing good.</p>\n<p>They’d known it would come. You couldn’t be alone in a room for that long and not have it come. Cas had lived alone for fifteen years and he knew. But he had had a city outside his window, and a phone, even a phone that never rang, and the possibility — even if he never took it — of walking down a street and buying a newspaper and speaking to a stranger. Ben had a rat, and algae, and two statues behind a pane of glass who took a week to write four letters.</p>\n<p>He came to the glass one flicker with his hair grown out wild, and his beard wild, and his eyes red, and wrote in huge letters, slanting, tearing the grease pencil:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I CAN’T<br>I CAN’T DO THIS<br>I CAN’T DO THIS I CAN’T I CAN’T</div>\n<p>And then he hit the glass. They didn’t see his fist move. They just saw the glass shake, very slightly, and a starburst of cracks appear in the outer pane, in front of Teo’s face, and Ben’s hand on the glass on the other side with blood smeared from the knuckles, and his forehead pressed against it, and his shoulders shaking.</p>\n<p>Teo put her bare hand flat on the glass over his.</p>\n<p>She held it there. She held it there for a long time — two minutes, three — and on the other side of the glass, Ben stood with his forehead against it, perfectly still. And they understood, both of them at the same moment, what he was seeing: a hand, very slowly, over days and days, rising to the glass and coming to rest there. A hand that stayed. That didn’t go away. That was still there when he woke up, and still there when he went to sleep, for as long as he needed it to be there.</p>\n<p>Then he was gone. The glass was empty, except for the smears, and the cracks, and the huge slanting letters.</p>\n<p>He didn’t come back for a long time. Seven minutes. Six months.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>When he came back he was different.</p>\n<p>He was thinner — much thinner — and his beard had been cut short with something, scissors or a knife, and his hair was tied back with a piece of wire. He was clean. He’d found clothes: a Works jumpsuit, too big, belted with cable. He moved — when he moved slowly enough for them to see — carefully, deliberately, like someone who has made a decision about how to live and is living it. He looked at them through the glass for a long moment. Then he wiped the old words off with his sleeve, and wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SORRY</div>\n<p>And under it, after a pause:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I’M STUDYING IT</div>\n<p>And an arrow, pointing down the length of the hall, at the far wall, where the great tree of light was still spreading slowly into the tanks.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>That was when it started to fill the glass.</p>\n<p>For the next hour — the next five years — Ben Farr studied the thing that was killing him, and wrote down what he learned where they could see it.</p>\n<p>He wrote fast, and they saw it arrive in bursts. A diagram would appear: the filament’s tree, sketched in grease pencil in quick sure lines, branches labelled. A column of numbers. An arrow. A question mark that was later crossed out, and replaced with an answer. The glass became a page, and then a notebook, and then a blackboard in a lecture hall. He ran out of room and wiped whole sections off and started again, and Cas — sitting cross-legged on the gallery floor with his black sketchbook open on his knee and a pencil in his bare hand — copied everything, frantically, as fast as it came, because he knew that anything he didn’t copy in the minute it was there might be wiped away the next.</p>\n<p>It was very good work.</p>\n<p>Ben had measured the filament. With the pole, with a tape, with a stopwatch and his own eyes, month after month, year after year, standing as close as he dared. He’d watched how it grew — how every branch split into branches with the same pattern, the same angles, at every scale. He’d counted. He’d worked out the branching ratio. He’d worked out that the pattern was self-similar, and computed, from his own measurements, the number that described how rough the thing was — how much edge it had.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">D = 2.73</div>\n<p>Cas stopped drawing. He sat for a moment, looking at the number. The same number that had been at the bottom of the last telemetry burst from the Crown. Ben had found it with a tape measure. He’d found it alone, in a room, with a rat, from first principles.</p>\n<p>“What?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“He’s right,” said Cas. “That’s all. He’s right.”</p>\n<p>There was more. Ben had noticed that the filament didn’t grow at random; it grew along lines. He’d mapped where the hall’s structure ran — the pillars, the beams, the old coolant lines that had once connected this hall to the Lattice — and found that the filament’s branches followed them, followed the lines where the Lattice had once pulled hardest on the station’s frame. Stress lines. The Tear was growing outward along the ghosts of the forces that had held it in. And where two branches forked, there was always, between them, a narrow gap — a throat — where the gradient was gentlest. A seam.</p>\n<p>He drew the Lattice. From memory, from the hall’s own schematics which he’d found in a cabinet, from reasoning. He drew the inner face of it, where Cas had said there would be a shear. He drew where the stress lines would run through it. He drew where the throats would be.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IF THERE’S A SHEAR<br>IT’LL HAVE SEAMS<br>HERE HERE HERE<br>GO THROUGH THE THROATS</div>\n<p>And arrows. And numbers. And a map.</p>\n<p>Cas copied it until his hand cramped. Teo held the sketchbook steady on his knee when his hand shook. At one point, without either of them saying anything, she took the pencil from him and did a whole section herself, the list of numbers, in her small square hand, while he flexed his fingers.</p>\n<p>On the other side of the glass Ben was thirty-two, and then thirty-three, and then thirty-four. His hair had started to go grey at the temples. He had lines around his eyes from squinting at the light. Minister had died — they saw the day he came to the glass with the rat lying in his cupped hands, and wrote MINISTER. OLD AGE. 3. GOOD INNINGS — and a year later there was another rat, smaller and darker, and he wrote MINISTER II, and pointed at it, and grinned in the beard.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS<br>NOBODY CAME</div>\n<p>Teo wrote back:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">WE’RE HERE</div>\n<p>It took her fifteen seconds to write it. It took him, on the other side, most of a month to watch it arrive. When it was finished, he stood looking at it. Then he wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I KNOW</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Near the end — though they didn’t know it was near the end — he came to the glass and sat on the pallet with his arms around his knees and looked at them, not writing anything.</p>\n<p>He was thirty-seven. He was greying at the temples and in the beard, and there were deep lines at the corners of his mouth, and he had the look of a man who has been very unhappy and then, over a long time, has found his way to something that was not happiness but was near it, and could live there. He had been alone in that hall for eleven years. He was older now than Cas had been when he signed the revised Margin Review.</p>\n<p>He wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IS IT STILL TODAY FOR YOU</div>\n<p>Cas wrote back:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SAME DAY</div>\n<p>Ben read it — slowly, over days — and they saw him nod. Then he wrote, carefully, smaller than before, so they had to lean close:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">STRANGE<br>YOU’VE BEEN SITTING THERE MY WHOLE LIFE<br>FEELS LIKE YOU’RE THE ONES WHO ARE STUCK</div>\n<p>Cas looked at those words. He found that his eyes were stinging. He found that he could not remember when that had started.</p>\n<p>And then Ben wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">GO</div>\n<p>Teo made a small sound.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">YOU HAVE TO GO<br>I’M FINE<br>HAD A GOOD LIFE<br>MOSTLY ALGAE</div>\n<p>“No,” said Teo, to the glass.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THE WORLD’S ENDING OUT THERE<br>GO AND STOP IT<br>YOU’VE GOT THE MAP</div>\n<p>Cas looked down at the sketchbook in his lap. Thirty pages of it, dense with grease-pencil diagrams in his own pencil and Teo’s. Eleven years of a man’s life.</p>\n<p>He looked at Teo. She was crying, openly, silently, her face wet in the visor-light, her jaw set.</p>\n<p>“Two more minutes,” she said. “Two more minutes and then we go. I’m not leaving him the minute he tells us to. I’m not having him think⁠—” She didn’t finish.</p>\n<p>“Two more minutes,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>He picked up the marker. He wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">2 MORE MIN</div>\n<p>And then, after a moment, because it seemed important and because there was no one else to say it:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU BEN</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He never got to read the second line.</p>\n<p>They saw it happen at the far end of the hall first. The tree of light on the far wall flared — the whole of it, every branch, at once, bright enough that the gallery windows went white and Cas’s visor darkened automatically — and then it grew. Not slowly now. Not with the patient frost-crawl they’d been watching for two hours. It <em>surged</em>. New branches ran out of it in every direction, racing across the ceiling and down between the tanks, fork after fork after fork, and the fine thread that had come down in the middle of the hall two hours ago thickened as they watched, from a hair to a finger to a rope of light as thick as an arm.</p>\n<p>On the other side of the glass, Ben stood up.</p>\n<p>He was already writing. The letters came so fast now that they seemed to appear all at once — whole lines at a time — and Cas understood, with a lurch that went all the way down into his stomach, what that meant.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IT’S SPEEDING UP</div>\n<p>And, under it, instantly:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">RATIO CLIMBING<br>100K<br>400K<br>IT’S OK</div>\n<p>And under that, all at once, the whole bottom of the glass:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<p>He put the pencil down.</p>\n<p>He put his hand flat on the glass. His palm, his long engineer’s fingers, dirty at the knuckles, with the white scar across two of them where he’d hit the glass all those years ago.</p>\n<p>Cas put his own bare hand flat against it on the other side.</p>\n<p>For a moment — less than a second — they were like that. Two palms, a centimetre of glass between them.</p>\n<p>Then Ben aged.</p>\n<p>He was thirty-seven, and then there was grey all through his beard, and his face had fallen in around the eyes, and he was fifty. He was sixty. The hand on the glass thinned under Cas’s palm, the knuckles swelling, the skin spotting and loosening over the bones. His back curved. His hair went white and thinned and was gone. He was an old man — a very old man, eighty, ninety — standing with his hand on the glass and his eyes, still his eyes, looking out through a face that Cas didn’t know at all. He was smiling. Cas was almost sure he was smiling.</p>\n<p>Then he wasn’t standing. He was sitting, on the pallet. He was lying on it. He was still.</p>\n<p>And then he was not a man. The overalls sagged. The shape under the silver blanket collapsed, and yellowed, and was bones, and the bones went grey and dry and cracked, and the blanket went brittle and split. The pallet rotted to splinters under it. The tins rusted to brown lace. The little pot with the green shoot in it burst with a sudden crown of leaves that browned and fell. Out in the hall the tanks bloomed — all of them, at once, a fantastic eruption of green, thick and rank and clotting against the glass — and then darkened, and went black, and drained, and cracked, and stood empty, with the grey slime dried to crusts up their sides. Dust fell from the ceiling in a slow endless snow. The grow-lamps flickered and died one by one. The paint peeled off the pillars and lay on the floor in drifts, and the drifts went to dust, and the dust settled over everything, deep and grey, like a snowfall on a ruin a thousand years old.</p>\n<p>It took about thirty seconds.</p>\n<p>Then the light at the far end of the hall dimmed back to its ordinary glow. The rope of light in the middle of the hall thinned, and thinned, and was a hair again, and drifted a little on the air, and was gone, into the floor, somewhere else.</p>\n<p>Cas was still standing with his hand on the glass.</p>\n<p>On the other side of it, very close, there was a heap of grey dust under a brittle grey rag, and a scatter of small bones that had been a rat, and, lying in the dust where it had been put down, a stubby yellow grease pencil.</p>\n<p>Above it, on the glass, the writing was still there. The grease had outlasted him. It was faded now, cracked and chalky, but you could still read it.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo was sick in her helmet vent.</p>\n<p>She did it without any fuss, turning her head, and the suit’s scrubbers whined, and she knelt there afterwards on the gallery floor with her head down. Then she got up.</p>\n<p>Cas hadn’t moved. He was looking at the pencil.</p>\n<p>“Hale.” Her voice was hoarse.</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“<em>Hale.</em>”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“We have to go now,” said Teo. “We said two minutes. That was two minutes.”</p>\n<p>He looked back at the glass. At IT WAS LONG ENOUGH. At the dust. At a centimetre of glass in front of his bare palm, and a smear on its other side where, a minute ago and sixty years ago, another palm had been.</p>\n<p>He took his hand away.</p>\n<p>He put his gauntlet back on. He closed the sketchbook — thirty pages, dense with numbers and diagrams, a map of a thing no one had ever seen, drawn by a man who had spent his whole life looking at it — and put it inside the chest of his suit, next to his heart, under the neck-ring, where it would be safe.</p>\n<p>They took the long way round. Three hours. For the first hour of it, neither of them said anything at all. They walked through the Works in the pearly light with the hammer coming up through the floor, once a second, once a second, and every so often Cas found that his lips were moving, and that he was counting.</p>\n<p>He wasn’t counting the hammer. He was counting how many seconds it had been since he’d put his hand on the glass.</p>\n<p>He knew what he would do if he ever reached a number that was long enough. He just didn’t know what the number was.</p>"}, {"file": "05-the-shear.md", "label": "Chapter 5", "title": "The Shear", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 6488, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Guilt’s a lousy reason, Hale. Die for something useful or don’t bother.</p><span class=\"attr\">Major Teodora Lisk, Civil Defence</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">It was Teo who talked first, in the end. They were going down a long ramp between two walls of cable trunking, with the hammer coming up through the soles of their boots, and she said, out of nowhere, “He was a good kid.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“I only knew him a day.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“No. I knew him eleven years.” She was quiet for a while. “Does that count? It feels like it counts.”</p>\n<p>Cas thought about it. He thought about a man with a beard and a rat on his knee, writing <em>I KNOW</em> on a pane of glass in a month.</p>\n<p>“It counts,” he said.</p>\n<p>They walked. The ramp went down and turned and went down.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell Jonah,” said Teo. “If I get back. About the dog.”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know what it means.”</p>\n<p>“It means they’d been arguing about getting a dog. He wanted one and Ben kept saying they didn’t have room. He said it on his tape. <em>I should’ve said yes to the dog.</em>” She laughed, a short, raw sound. “Eleven years in a room full of algae and that’s the thing he wanted us to get right.”</p>\n<p>Cas didn’t answer. He found that he couldn’t.</p>\n<p>He found that grief, for him, had a peculiar shape. It came as arithmetic. He kept doing sums. A minute for them, a month for Ben. The hundred and twenty minutes they’d sat on the gallery floor. Eleven years, four months, give or take. The thirty seconds at the end, and how many years in them — forty? Fifty? He did the sums over and over, the way you run your tongue over a broken tooth, and every time they came out the same, and every time it was unbearable, and he did them again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Lattice lay below and inward, and to reach it they had to go down.</p>\n<p>This was the part of the plan Cas had drawn on the back of a Ministry briefing sheet in the crisis room, a hundred years ago, yesterday: the Vault was a sphere, its floor was the Anvil, and the Anvil was pierced from below by fourteen great conduits — cooling and power trunks that ran down through the keelstone plate into the Works beneath. The Crown was at the top of the Vault, four kilometres up Spindle One. There was no way into the Vault from the side; the Lattice was solid keelstone all around. The only way in was up through the floor.</p>\n<p>So they went down, into the Works under the Vault, through a region the old engineers had called the Undercroft, where everything was older and heavier and the Lattice’s pull came from above as well as from the side and the gravity weather was worst of all.</p>\n<p>It was in a shaft that the Undercroft nearly killed him.</p>\n<p>It was an old lift shaft, the cage long gone, with a steel ladder running down one wall and a gridwork of beams crossing it every ten metres. They were climbing down it, Teo first, Cas above, the keels humming loud in the narrow space, and Teo was calling out the washers — dropping one every few rungs and watching it fall straight down the middle of the shaft, glinting in her helmet light, until it vanished — and then she dropped one and it went sideways.</p>\n<p>“<em>Squall⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>It hit. Down went sideways and then <em>up</em> and then sideways again, a great slow roll like a ship broaching, and Cas’s hands were on the ladder and then they were not. He didn’t let go. The ladder moved. Or he moved; there wasn’t a way to tell. One moment he was clinging to steel rungs with the shaft below him, and the next the shaft was beside him and he was falling across it, and a beam came at him out of the dark, and he hit it back-first with his whole weight and the weight of the suit.</p>\n<p>He heard the keel. Over everything — over Teo shouting his name, over the scream of the beam as the suit’s plating ground along it, over his own breath — he heard the keel’s note change. It went from a clean high hum to something rough, something with a rattle in it, like a coin in a spinning drum.</p>\n<p>Then down swung back, and he was lying across the beam with his legs hanging over the shaft, and the note in his spine was wrong.</p>\n<p>“Hale! Cas! Talk to me⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m here.” He didn’t move. He was afraid to. “I’m here. I’m all right.”</p>\n<p>“Your keel⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>Teo was beside him on the beam — she’d climbed across, somehow, fast, in the suit — and had her light on his back. He heard her breath go in and not come out.</p>\n<p>“The housing’s cracked,” she said. “There’s a — there’s a split down the side, about the length of my hand. There’s light coming out of it. It’s — is it supposed to do that? Hale, is it supposed to⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>He reached back with his gauntlet and touched it. Through the gauntlet, through the plating, he could feel the rattle — a tiny eccentric wobble in something that had been perfectly balanced. The bearing. The crack had let the housing flex, and the rotor had kissed its bearing, just once, just slightly, and now it was running out of true.</p>\n<p><em>A bad day</em>, Ben had said. <em>A very, very bad day.</em></p>\n<p>He looked at the band of indicator lights around the housing’s waist, reflected in Teo’s visor. Green. Green. One amber.</p>\n<p>“How long?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“It’ll run down faster. The bubble’ll shrink as it slows. Hours, instead of days. Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“And if it goes?”</p>\n<p>“If the bearing goes⁠—” He didn’t finish. “It won’t go. It’ll just run down.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him through two visors.</p>\n<p>“You’re lying,” she said.</p>\n<p>“A bit.”</p>\n<p>“Right.” She got her shoulder under his arm. “Up.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He walked. He kept walking. For the first hour after the shaft he could hear the rattle all the time, faint and patient in the keel’s note, like a clock ticking in another room; after that he stopped hearing it, the way you stop hearing a clock, and only noticed it when the corridor went quiet and it was suddenly there, ticking, ticking. Teo heard it too. He could tell because she stopped talking whenever it was loud. She walked a little closer to him than she had before.</p>\n<p>They came to the Lattice at the end of the tenth hour.</p>\n<p>He had seen it before, from inside, as a student — but only in pieces, through inspection ports, in schematics. He had never seen it like this. They came out of a tunnel onto a long gallery, a sort of balcony running along the outside of the Lattice’s outermost ring, and the Lattice was simply there, filling the world. A wall. A black wall so high that its top was lost above them in the pearly dark and so long that it curved away on both sides into nothing, smooth and seamless and utterly lightless, as if someone had cut a slice out of the universe. It hummed. You didn’t hear it; you felt it, in your chest, a vast deep tone below the hammer. It was keelstone, all of it — every cubic metre of that wall weighed as much as a small mountain — and they could feel it pulling at them, a steady sideways tug toward it, as if the wall were very gently trying to draw them in.</p>\n<p>“God,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing. He had found an inspection port — a round window of thick crystal set into the gallery’s floor, looking down and in through a gap between two of the outer rings. He knelt by it and looked.</p>\n<p>It went down a long way. Ring behind ring behind ring, black on black, each one a curved cliff, the gaps between them lit by that sourceless pearly light. And far down, far in, he could see one of the great tensioner rotors — the enormous spinning keelstone drums set into the rings that held the Lattice’s shape. It was turning slowly. It was perfectly true.</p>\n<p>And beyond it, deeper, where there should have been another⁠—</p>\n<p>“That’s Six,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo knelt beside him.</p>\n<p>It was hard to see at first. Tensioner Six was the size of a building: a drum of black keelstone two hundred metres across, set on a magnetic bearing in a cradle in the Lattice. Only it wasn’t in its cradle. It was lying tilted in the gap between two rings, like a coin that has fallen on its edge and leaned against a wall, one rim jammed against the ring above and the other sunk in the cradle below, and it was not turning. Around it, the rings themselves were wrong. They weren’t curved properly. They sagged toward Six and bulged away from it, as though the whole Lattice around it had been squeezed in a fist and then let go. And through the gaps around it, very faintly, threads of that white-violet light came branching outward from somewhere deeper in.</p>\n<p>“Can it be⁠—” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>He said it without thinking. He’d known it since the crisis room, since the number at the bottom of the screen. He’d known it since the car. But he hadn’t <em>seen</em> it, and now he had, and he found that seeing it made it into a thing that could be said out loud without any effort at all.</p>\n<p>“It can’t be reseated. Not from the Crown, not from anywhere. There’s nothing to seat it in. The Lattice has lost its shape. Even if you stood it back up it would just fall over again.” He sat back on his heels. “Every engineer in that room knew it. Rask probably knows it. They all said it anyway.”</p>\n<p>“You said it.”</p>\n<p>“I said it.”</p>\n<p>Teo got up. She stood looking out at the black wall, her arms folded over the chest of her suit.</p>\n<p>“It’s funny,” she said. “I’ve been in Civil Defence twenty-nine years. Floods, fires, the Kovač stack. Every time, the Ministry says it’s under control and we go in and it isn’t. Every time. And you know what? Every time, I believed them a bit. Right up until I saw it. Every time.” She shook her head. “I used to think I was an idiot for that. I’ve decided it’s worse than that. I’ve decided it’s the whole bloody country.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Lattice Station 14-C was a control booth for Conduit 14, and they found it an hour later by following Ben’s map.</p>\n<p>That was the thing. Cas had not been sure, until they found it, whether the map was real — whether a man in a room, however clever, however many years he’d had, could have reasoned his way from the branching of a filament to the shape of a thing he’d never seen. But Ben had drawn the Lattice’s stress lines, and where they ran, and where they crossed the conduits, and on page twenty-six of the sketchbook, in Cas’s pencil copying Ben’s grease-pencil, there was a circle around one conduit and three words: <em>14 — THROAT HERE</em>. And when they came round the curve of the Lattice’s inner gallery, there was the door. LATTICE STATION 14-C. CONDUIT 14 ACCESS. AUTHORISED PERSONNEL ONLY.</p>\n<p>And there was a lee. They felt it as they came through the door: the pull of the black wall let go of them, and the gravity weather, which had been gusting all through the Undercroft, simply stopped. The booth was a small, bare room with a bank of dead monitors along one wall and a big thick viewport in the other, and a hatch in the floor by the viewport, and a chair, and a water cooler, and a first-aid cabinet, and it was as calm and still and ordinary as an office on a Sunday afternoon.</p>\n<p>Teo dropped a washer. It fell straight down and bounced.</p>\n<p>“Station air,” said Cas, checking the panel by the door. “Pressure’s good. You could take the helmet off in here.”</p>\n<p>“Could I,” said Teo. She had gone to the viewport. “Hale. Come and look at this.”</p>\n<p>He went.</p>\n<p>The viewport looked into the bottom of Conduit 14. It was a vertical shaft, round, maybe six metres across, lined with black keelstone and ribbed with the brackets of old cooling pipes that had been stripped out long ago. A steel ladder went up one side of it. The shaft went up and up, lit by the pearly glow, and at the top of it, very far up, there was a disc of light that was brighter than anything they’d seen, a pale, hard, violet-white.</p>\n<p>And about forty metres up the shaft, something was happening to the air.</p>\n<p>It trembled. That was the only word. It was like the shimmer over the Pit, the column of moving air they’d seen from the rim; but here it was close, and dense, and you could see it for what it was. A layer. A horizontal curtain across the shaft, a few metres thick, where the air was — <em>wrong</em>. Where things seen through it jumped and smeared and doubled. Where the ladder, going up through it, seemed to bend and break and join again, as though you were looking at it through moving water, or through the heat of a fire, or through tears.</p>\n<p>“The Shear,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas looked at his visor display. He’d dropped a relay puck at the booth door; the suit was comparing its own clock with the puck’s, with the one before, with the Collar’s. LOCAL RATIO: 2.1.</p>\n<p>He looked up at the trembling curtain. If Ben’s map was right — if everything he’d guessed in the crisis room was right — then on the far side of it the ratio wasn’t two. It was hundreds. It might be thousands.</p>\n<p>“Forty metres,” he said. “From two to — I don’t know. Seven hundred? Across forty metres.”</p>\n<p>“And the keel spreads it over one.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And yours is broken.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” said Teo. “Look down.”</p>\n<p>He looked down.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the shaft, on the floor of the conduit just beyond the viewport, lying in a heap against the foot of the ladder, was a body.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They opened the floor hatch and went down to it, and Teo knelt beside it with her light, and for a long time neither of them said anything at all.</p>\n<p>It had fallen. That much was clear. It lay crumpled at the foot of the ladder, the way a body lies when it’s come down a long way. It was very small — shorter than Teo — and very broad; even in death, even ruined, you could see the width of the shoulders, the thickness of the bones.</p>\n<p>And it was ruined in a way that made no sense.</p>\n<p>The legs were bone. Clean bone, grey and dry and old, the clothes over them rotted to threads. The pelvis was bone. But from the waist up, the body was <em>leather</em> — dark brown, mummified, shrunken tight on the frame, like the bog bodies Cas had seen as a boy in the Museum of the Long Heat — and the face was worse, because the face was not quite either. The left side of it was dry skin over a skull. The right side, from the cheekbone to the chin, was — Cas made himself look — was still almost a face. Wasted, sunken, grey. But skin. Hair. An eyelid.</p>\n<p>“It went through,” Cas said. His own voice sounded strange to him. “Through the Shear. Feet first. Coming down. Different parts of it spent different amounts of time in there. The feet were deeper, they were in the slow side — no⁠—” He stopped. He made himself think. “No. The feet came <em>out</em> first. Into the slow side, down here. The head was still up in the fast side. For a second, maybe. Maybe less. The top half lived — years? Longer? — while the bottom half lived a second.” He heard himself. “It was dead before it got here. It was dead before it finished falling.”</p>\n<p>Teo said nothing.</p>\n<p>“But that’s not⁠—” He stopped again.</p>\n<p>“That’s not what’s wrong,” said Teo. “Is it.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>She reached out with her gauntlet and, very gently, touched the clothes.</p>\n<p>They weren’t station clothes. That was what was wrong. They weren’t a jumpsuit or overalls or a lab coat or anything issued by the Ministry to anyone who had ever worked in the Draw. They were a sort of loose tunic, belted, and trousers, and they had been made. By hand. You could see the stitching — big, careful, uneven stitches, the kind you’d make with a needle you’d filed yourself, and the thread was something coarse, twisted from fibres. And the cloth itself was a patchwork. Squares and strips of a dozen different fabrics: orange high-vis, grey jumpsuit cotton, a white that might once have been a lab coat, a blue that might once have been a curtain, all sewn together, and patched, and re-patched, and patched again over the patches, as if the garment had been worn and mended for years. For decades. By more than one person.</p>\n<p>Around the neck, on a cord of the same twisted fibre, hung a little ornament.</p>\n<p>Teo lifted it with her gauntlet. It was yellow plastic — the thick, glossy plastic of an old-style hard hat — carved into a shape: a tiny, flat, stylised flame, or a lamp, with rays around it. Somebody had carved it very carefully with a knife, and then it had been handled — held, rubbed, worried between finger and thumb — for so long that all the edges were worn smooth and round and the yellow had gone pale and soft as old soap.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” said Teo, very quietly. “Who is this?”</p>\n<p>He had been doing arithmetic since they came through the hatch. He’d been doing it without meaning to, the way he always did, the way he’d done it in the crisis room with Rask telling him to stop. The body had fallen down the conduit. So it had come from above. From inside. From the Vault. It had come through the Shear. It wore clothes that had been sewn by hand, from station fabric, and worn and mended for longer than the station had been broken. It wore a lamp carved from a hard hat. Its bones were thick. Its bones were very, very thick, and it was very small and very broad, the way a person might grow up — the way a <em>child</em> might grow up — somewhere the gravity was high.</p>\n<p><em>If anyone survived in there</em>, he had said to Rask, <em>they could have been alive in there longer than the Commonweal has existed.</em></p>\n<p>“They lived,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Who did?”</p>\n<p>“The night shift. Some of them. In the Vault. They lived. They had air — the Vault’s got air, it’s a sealed volume, it’s got scrubbers. Water. Light. They lived.” He looked at the patched sleeve, the fine careful stitches of someone who had had all the time in the world to learn to sew. “And they had children. And the children had children. And somebody — years from now, for them, decades — somebody got dressed in the clothes their grandmother mended and hung this round their neck and came down a ladder to find out what was at the bottom.”</p>\n<p>Teo was very still.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” she said. “What’s in there?”</p>\n<p>He looked up the shaft, at the trembling curtain of the Shear, and above it, the far bright disc of light.</p>\n<p>“People,” he said. “Or there were.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took her about ten minutes to decide. It took him most of those ten minutes to realise she’d decided.</p>\n<p>He was checking his keel. He’d found a way to see into the crack in the housing with his helmet light turned down, and through it he could see the rim of the rotor going round, a black blur edged with a thin bright line where it was catching the light, and every time it came round there was the tiniest flicker — a wobble, a stutter, the bright line jumping. He counted the stutters. He timed them against the keel’s note. The note had dropped since the shaft. He could hear it had dropped.</p>\n<p>He did the arithmetic. He didn’t like it.</p>\n<p>“How long,” said Teo behind him.</p>\n<p>“Four hours. Five. Before the bubble’s too small to matter.”</p>\n<p>“And in the Shear?”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“In the Shear, Hale. Forty metres of it. Through the throat, on the map, where it’s gentlest. With a keel with a crack down the side and a wobble in the bearing. Going up.”</p>\n<p>“The throat’s gentler. Ben said⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Ben said <em>gentler</em>. Ben didn’t say gentle.” Her voice was very calm. “If that bearing goes in there, what happens?”</p>\n<p>He closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>“The rotor comes loose,” he said. “It’s eleven kilos of keelstone at two hundred thousand revs. It goes through the housing and the suit and me and the conduit wall and probably the Lattice. And the bubble goes with it. So the Shear gets what’s left.” He opened his eyes. “Either way, I’d be dead before I noticed.”</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “So you’re taking mine.”</p>\n<p>He turned round.</p>\n<p>She was standing by the viewport with her helmet off. He hadn’t heard her take it off. Her face was grey with tiredness and streaked where she’d been crying at the glass, and her cropped hair was flattened with sweat, and she looked older than she had at the gate and very much more certain.</p>\n<p>“No,” he said.</p>\n<p>“The housings come off. Farr showed me on the rig. They’re built to hot-swap, that’s what the spin rigs were for, you spin them on the rig and clip them on. Four clamps and a coupling. We take yours off and put mine on. Five minutes.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ve got a suit with no keel, in the Lattice, four kilometres from the gate.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got a suit with a broken keel, in a lee, with station air and a water cooler and a first-aid kit and a chair.” She jerked her head at the room. “I’ll stay here. It’s calm here. I’ll sit in the chair. I’ll be the most comfortable person in the whole bloody Draw.”</p>\n<p>“And when the lee moves?”</p>\n<p>“Then I’ll move with it.” She shrugged. “I’m good at moving. I’ve been doing it all day.”</p>\n<p>“Teo⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Listen to me.” She came across the room. She had to look up at him. “Listen. You’re the one who knows what’s up there. You’re the one who’s read the paper. You’re the one who knows what the Clause is and what the plate means⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I <em>don’t</em> know what the plate⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You know more than me. Which is everything. I don’t know how to turn a key in a Keystone. I don’t know what a Keystone <em>is</em>. I don’t know how to talk to people who wear their grandmother’s curtains. I can carry you. That’s what I’m for. I carry people out of things. And I can’t carry you through that.” She pointed at the viewport. At the trembling air. “So I’m giving you my keel instead. And you’re going to take it. Because if you don’t, you’re going to try to go through on yours, and you’ll die in there, and I’ll have to sit in this bloody chair and watch it, like I watched that boy. And I’m not doing that twice in one day, Hale. I’m not.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>He wanted to say: <em>it should be me who stays</em>. He wanted to say: <em>I’m the one who signed it. I’m the one who owes</em>. He had come in here — he knew it, he had known it at the window in the Collar, <em>at last</em>, <em>almost a relief</em> — he had come in here half hoping that something would happen in the dark that would settle it. He had been thinking, since the shaft, since the rattle in the keel, that perhaps this was how it would be settled. Quietly. In the Shear. Tidily. And he’d been thinking, underneath that, very quietly, that it would be fair.</p>\n<p>She saw it. He watched her see it. Something changed in her face, and she stepped in closer, and her voice dropped.</p>\n<p>“Oh, no you don’t,” she said. “No. I read your file. I sat next to you all day. I know that look. I’ve seen it on men on bridges.” She put her bare hand flat on the chestplate of his suit, where Petra Imre had put hers at the gate. “Guilt’s a lousy reason, Hale. It’s the worst reason there is. It makes you stupid and it makes you selfish and it makes you think dying is a thing you <em>do</em>, instead of a thing that happens. Die for something useful or don’t bother. You hear me? If you’re going to die in there, you do it because it saves somebody. Not because it makes you feel square. Nobody’s keeping score. Nobody ever was.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t say anything. He couldn’t.</p>\n<p>“Now turn round,” said Teo, “and let me get that bloody thing off your back.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took eleven minutes, not five.</p>\n<p>They did it in the middle of the booth, in the lee, with Cas on his knees and Teo behind him. She had to take her own suit’s back plate off first to get at the clamps — she did it by feel, swearing, with her helmet under her arm — and then his. The keels had to come off at full spin; there was no way to stop them. When the first clamp let go on his housing the whole thing <em>lurched</em> in her hands, gyroscopic, fighting her, and for a second it swung sideways and he felt the bubble around him go lopsided and the room went strange — the far wall stretching, the floor tilting, the light doing something he didn’t have words for — and Teo said <em>hold still hold still hold still</em>, and got the second clamp, and the third, and then it was off, and his back was cold and light and the world was just a room.</p>\n<p>She lowered it to the floor. It sat there, humming its wrong, rattling note. Through the crack in its side a thin bright line of light went round and round.</p>\n<p>Then she took off her own.</p>\n<p>She did it the same way, slower, more carefully, and when it was off she was standing in the middle of the booth with no keel at all, and for the first time Cas saw what that meant. The lee was calm — but not perfectly calm. Nothing in here was. Without the bubble around her, he could see her sway, very slightly, as if standing on the deck of a ship at anchor. He saw her put a hand out to the back of the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right,” she said. “Right. Hold still.”</p>\n<p>She clipped it onto him. Four clamps. The coupling. The suit took it with a thunk, and the note came into his spine — clean, high, true, without a rattle, the note from the hangar — and the room went steady around him like a held breath.</p>\n<p>He stood up. Teo stepped back.</p>\n<p>“Good,” she said. “That’s good. How’s that feel?”</p>\n<p>“Like yours.”</p>\n<p>She laughed. She put her back plate back on. She put her helmet on the chair, and sat down in it, heavily, and looked at him.</p>\n<p>“You’ll drop pucks,” she said. “One at the bottom, one at the top.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ll talk to me.”</p>\n<p>“If the radios⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You’ll <em>talk</em> to me, Hale. You’ll send me news. I’ll be sitting here in this chair with nothing to do, I’ll be bored out of my skull.” She tipped her head back and looked at the ceiling. “Farr said the — the ratio thing. If you’re up there in the fast bit and I’m down here in the slow, what does that do?”</p>\n<p>“Anything I send you arrives squashed. A week of me in a second. You’ll hear it as a chirp. The suit’ll stretch it back out.”</p>\n<p>“Birds,” said Teo. “Farr said whales. So I get birds.”</p>\n<p>“And anything you send me arrives stretched. A minute of you would take — hours. Days, maybe.”</p>\n<p>“So you’ll be up there for weeks, and I’ll be down here for minutes.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment.</p>\n<p>“That’s all right,” she said. “I’ve waited for men before. You were all slower than that.”</p>\n<p>He knelt down beside the chair so he could see her face. He didn’t know what to say. He had not had a friend in fifteen years, and he’d known her for a day and a half, and she had just given him the only thing she had that could keep her alive, and he did not know what you said to that.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Don’t,” she said. “I’ll cry, and then I’ll have to kill you.” She put her hand on the side of his helmet and held it there. “Go on, then. Go and find your people in their curtains. And when you’ve found them, you tell them there’s a woman down here in a chair who wants to hear all about it.”</p>\n<p>He put his arms around her.</p>\n<p>It was absurd. It was two people in armoured suits, one of them kneeling, trying to hold each other through a centimetre of plating and a helmet ring, clumsy as two statues embracing. She put her arms around him and patted his back plate twice, hard, the way you’d pat a horse. Then she pushed him away.</p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” said Teo Lisk.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He went down through the hatch and dropped a puck at the foot of the ladder, beside the body in its patchwork clothes. He looked at the little yellow lamp for a moment. Then he started to climb.</p>\n<p>It was a ladder. That was all it was, for the first thirty metres. Rungs in his gauntlets, the keel humming clean and high in his spine, the black curved wall of the conduit sliding past, the hammer coming up through the rails. He climbed on the back side of the ladder, pressed close to the wall, because Ben’s map said that was where the throat was — a narrow column of gentler gradient running up the conduit’s rear face where two stress lines forked apart. He could see the trembling curtain above him, coming closer. Through it the ladder seemed to kink sideways and back. Above it, very far up, the pale bright disc.</p>\n<p>“Talking to you,” said Teo’s voice in his ear. “Just so you know. I’ll keep talking till you’re through. Tell me when you’re there.”</p>\n<p>“Almost.”</p>\n<p>“How’s the view?”</p>\n<p>“Ladder.”</p>\n<p>“Very good. Very descriptive. They’ll put you on the radio.”</p>\n<p>He reached the curtain.</p>\n<p>He’d known it would be bad. He had done the arithmetic. He had not understood — because arithmetic can’t make you understand it — what it would be like to have a body in two places in time.</p>\n<p>It started with his hands. He reached up for the next rung and his right hand was <em>there</em> before he’d reached — he saw it, in his helmet light, already closed on the rung, already gripping, while he could still feel the motion of reaching in his shoulder. He stared at it. It was his hand. It was ahead of him. It was living a little faster than the rest of him and it had got to the future first. And then his arm caught up, and his hand was his again, and the next rung was above him and his hand was already on it.</p>\n<p>He climbed. There was nothing else to do. He pressed himself against the wall where Ben had said and climbed, and the keel’s note went higher and higher in his spine as it fought, and the world came apart.</p>\n<p>Sound went first. He heard his boot hit the rung below him, and then he felt his foot move to put it there. He heard his own breath in the helmet and then felt his chest rise. He heard Teo’s voice in his ear saying <em>⁠— still with me? —</em> and it was wrong, it was <em>slow</em>, it was sliding down like a record played at the wrong speed, <em>⁠— stiiill wiiith meee —</em>, and then he heard his own voice answering <em>⁠— yes —</em> and didn’t remember saying it, and then felt his mouth make the word.</p>\n<p>His heart. He could hear his heart. He could hear it the way you’d hear footsteps in the room above: first the beat, then, a moment later, the feeling of it in his chest. An echo, but the wrong way round. His blood was arriving at his head before it left his heart. Something in him understood that this could not go on for very long, that there was a limit to how much of this a body could be asked to do, and that he was coming up to it.</p>\n<p>The ladder went up through the curtain. The curtain was forty metres thick. He was halfway.</p>\n<p>His thoughts came finished. That was the strangest part. He’d be climbing, and a thought would be there in his head, whole and complete — <em>the rung is loose</em> — and only afterwards would he feel himself think it, feel the words assemble, feel himself notice the rung. It was as though some part of him up ahead in time were leaving notes for the rest of him to find.</p>\n<p>He put his hand on the loose rung. He’d known it was loose. He moved his hand to the next one.</p>\n<p>Teo’s voice was a groan now. It came in his ear like the lowing of some enormous animal a long way off, a vast, slow, falling note with no words in it, and he knew it was her, still talking, still saying <em>still with me</em> and <em>how’s the view</em> and <em>nearly there</em>, and he could not understand any of it, and he held on to the sound of it anyway, the way you’d hold on to a rope in the dark. It went down and down. It went below anything he could hear. It became a pressure in his ears. Then it was gone.</p>\n<p>And then — like coming up out of deep water — he was through.</p>\n<p>It was over as suddenly as it had begun. His hand closed on a rung and he felt it close at the same time. His breath went in and his chest went up together. His heart beat, and he heard it beat, at once, one thing. He hung on the ladder, his helmet pressed against the black wall, shaking so hard the exo was fighting to hold him, and breathed, and breathed, and breathed.</p>\n<p>He looked down. Below him, the Shear. Through it, the ladder going down, jumping and smearing. And far down at the bottom of the conduit, very small, the round lit window of Lattice Station 14-C, and in it, a figure in a chair.</p>\n<p>She wasn’t moving. She was sitting perfectly still, like a photograph. Like a statue. He watched her for a long time and she did not move at all.</p>\n<p>He took an orange puck from his belt and pressed it to the wall. It stuck.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said. “I’m through.”</p>\n<p>There was no answer. He hadn’t expected one. It would take her — he did the arithmetic — it would take her hours to hear him. Days, perhaps, for him to hear her.</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The conduit went on. Above the Shear it went up through the Anvil itself, through a plate of keelstone three hundred metres thick, and the climbing was different. There was no weather. There was only weight. It came on gradually and then it didn’t stop — a steady, crushing, <em>reasonable</em> weight, as if the whole world were pressing on him from above, patient and enormous. The exo groaned. His arms shook. Every rung was a decision.</p>\n<p>He climbed. He counted rungs, because counting was what he did. They were thirty centimetres apart; three hundred metres was a thousand of them. At a hundred, he stopped and rested with his arms looped through the ladder and the keel singing in his spine and his heart going like a fist on a door. At two hundred. At three hundred. At six hundred his arms were not his arms any more, they were two things he had borrowed, and he was using them anyway.</p>\n<p>The light above got brighter. It was not the pearly glow of the Works. It was something harder, whiter, bluer — a light like a welding arc, like the Lesion in the Crown’s window in the old footage, like the nail of light Iris Hollowell had driven through the world. It came down the shaft and lit the rungs white. He could not look straight up into it.</p>\n<p>And there was sound. He noticed it gradually, the way you notice rain. Coming down the shaft, faint, under the hammer — which was not a hammer any more, he realised, not in here; it had stopped being a once-a-second blow somewhere back in the Shear and become something else, a long, slow tremor that came up through the rails every so often and lasted and lasted — under that, from above, there was a sound he knew and could not place. A murmur. A noise with a texture to it, rising and falling. Voices? No. Too many for voices. Water? No.</p>\n<p>He climbed. Eight hundred. Nine hundred. The disc of light was very close now; he could see the rim of the conduit against it, a black circle, and something moving across it, some shadow, some shape.</p>\n<p>At nine hundred and sixty rungs his right hand missed.</p>\n<p>It just — didn’t close. He reached, and the arm that he’d borrowed didn’t have anything left in it, and his fingers brushed the rung and slid off, and for a moment he hung by his left hand alone with the weight of the whole world pulling on him and three hundred metres of keelstone and forty metres of Shear and two kilometres of Undercroft below him, and he thought, very clearly, the way thoughts had come in the Shear, whole and finished: <em>oh. Here, then.</em></p>\n<p>And a hand came down out of the light and took hold of his wrist.</p>\n<p>It was a bare hand. Small, broad, very strong, the knuckles scarred. It closed on his wrist above the gauntlet with a grip like a vice. And a voice above him said, perfectly calmly, in an accent he’d never heard, all the vowels flattened and the consonants clipped:</p>\n<p>“Don’t let go. I’ve got you. Pim — get his other arm.”</p>"}, {"file": "06-what-time-is-it-outside.md", "label": "Chapter 6", "title": "What Time Is It Outside", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": true, "words": 4187, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Remain at your station. Await relief. You will not be forgotten.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Placard, Section 9 (the Watch’s first scripture)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They got him over the lip of the well on his belly, like a seal hauled onto a rock, and then he was lying on his back on something hard and hot, and the light was so bright that his visor went black to protect him and he was blind.</p>\n<p>He lay there. He had no choice about it. The weight was on him like a second body lying on top of his own — heavier, much heavier, than it had been in the conduit — and every breath was a push against it. The exo was whining. His arms were two dead things. The keel sang in his spine, high and clean, and under it, coming up through whatever he was lying on, was the long slow swell of the hammer.</p>\n<p>Someone was touching his suit.</p>\n<p>Small, strong, quick hands. They went round the neck-ring of his helmet, pressing, feeling. They found the seals at his shoulders, and the joints at his elbows, and the coupling of the keel. They were checking him. They were checking him the way he’d have checked a suit himself: methodically, in order, the places where air could get out.</p>\n<p>“Don’t take the helmet off yet,” said the voice. The same voice from the well. “Breathe slow. The air’s thick here. You’ll want to open it slowly when you open it. Let your ears get used to it.”</p>\n<p>His visor was clearing. It was adjusting to the glare, dimming it by stages, and a shape came out of the white. A face. A woman’s face, bent over him, very close.</p>\n<p>“Can you hear me?”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. His voice came out of the suit’s speaker cracked and strange.</p>\n<p>“Good.” She sat back on her heels. “What time is it outside?”</p>\n<p>For a moment he didn’t understand the question. It was so simple. It was the question you’d ask a stranger at a bus stop. He looked at the display at the bottom of his visor, where the two clocks were — OUTSIDE, fed by the last good signal from the pucks; LOCAL, his own — and read it off without thinking.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours,” he said. “And a bit.”</p>\n<p>She closed her eyes.</p>\n<p>She was quite still for perhaps five seconds. He could see her lips moving very slightly, the way his did when he was doing sums. Then she opened her eyes again, and there was something in them he couldn’t read — not surprise, not fear. Something more like the look of a person who has been carrying a heavy thing for a very long time and has just been told how much further they have to carry it.</p>\n<p>“Then it’s sooner than they think,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Is he the Relief?”</p>\n<p>That was another voice, higher, breathless. A boy. Cas turned his head inside the helmet and saw him — a thin, pale, very dirty boy of about eleven, crouched on the other side of him with his hands on Cas’s arm and his eyes as big as coins. He had a rat on his shoulder. It was a fat, sleek, brown-and-white rat with a red cord tied round its neck like a collar, and it was washing its face.</p>\n<p>“Is he? Ione. Is he? Is he the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He’s a man in a suit,” said the woman. “Get his legs.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They couldn’t lift him. Nobody could have lifted him. But there was a cart — a flat wooden thing on four small iron wheels, with a long handle, the kind of cart you’d use to haul rope — and between them, with a lot of heaving and a lot of the boy swearing in an accent so thick Cas caught one word in four, they rolled him onto it. He lay on it on his back. The weight pressed him down into the planks.</p>\n<p>Then the woman stood up, and took the cart’s handle, and turned it so that his head was at the high end, and for the first time he could see.</p>\n<p>He was lying at the top of a well, on a kind of stone lip, a round platform of black keelstone with a waist-high wall around it made of scrap: sheet metal, bits of pipe, a stripped-out door, all bolted and wired and welded together. Beside him, in the middle of the platform, was the mouth of the conduit he’d come up — a black round hole six metres across with the top of the ladder hooked over its edge. Around the hole, someone had painted on the black stone, in white, in big careful letters that went all the way round like writing on a coin: WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT · WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT.</p>\n<p>And beyond the wall was Holdfast.</p>\n<p>He did not know it was called that yet. He didn’t know anything. He lay on the rope-cart with the weight on his chest and the sweat running into his eyes inside the helmet and he looked, and for a long time — he never knew how long — he forgot to breathe.</p>\n<p>It was a city.</p>\n<p>It went away from him across a plain of black stone as flat as a table, and it was built of everything. Of scaffolding: great towers of scaffolding pipe, bolted and lashed and welded, ten, twenty storeys high, with floors laid across them made of steel plate and planking and doors. Of containers: the big steel cargo boxes the Draw used to move parts, stacked in terraces, painted every colour, with windows cut in their sides and shutters hung over the windows. Of keelstone: slabs of it, black and shining, off-cuts and rejects from the Lattice’s manufacture, laid as walls and floors and roofs, so that parts of the city looked as if they’d been carved from a single piece of night. It was stacked and crammed and piled together, lane on lane, tower on tower, bridges and walkways and ladders running between everything, lines of washing strung across every gap. There were gardens. Green things grew everywhere, in tanks and pots and troughs and on the roofs, climbing up the scaffolding in thick ropes of vine. There was smoke — thin grey threads of it rising straight up from a hundred chimneys. There was noise: the murmur he’d heard coming down the shaft, and it was voices after all. Thousands of voices. A city’s worth of people talking, calling, laughing, hammering, a baby crying somewhere, somebody singing.</p>\n<p>And above it, rising out of it, the spindles.</p>\n<p>There were eight. He’d seen them in the old footage, in the mural in the crisis room, in schematics: the great maintenance pylons that ran from the floor of the Vault to the Rim four kilometres up. He had never understood, from a picture, what four kilometres meant. They went up out of the city like trees out of undergrowth — black latticework towers, each one a hundred metres across at the base, the city piled up around their feet like drifts of leaves — and they went up, and up, and up, narrowing, until they were lost in the glare.</p>\n<p>And the glare⁠—</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<p>The Lesion hung at the top of the sky.</p>\n<p>It was a point. A single point of white-violet light, too hard and too bright to look at, like looking at the sun through a pinhole. The eight spindles rose toward it on every side and ended short of it in a thin dark ring, far up, that must be the Rim. Beyond the Rim the Vault curved up and away on all sides — black, a vast black dome, a sky made of the inside of a stone — and it should have been dark, and it wasn’t. Because spreading out from the point of light, across the whole top of the sky, was the Lace.</p>\n<p>He had seen its threads in the Works. A hair of light crossing a storeroom. A root system spreading down a wall. This was the thing they had grown from. It spread out from the Lesion in every direction at once, an unending, branching, glowing filigree, finer than frost, larger than weather, filling the upper sky from horizon to horizon like the veins in a leaf held up to the sun. It moved. Very slowly, almost too slowly to see, it was moving — branching, reaching, growing — and in places its longest threads came down out of the sky in fine bright streamers toward the tops of the spindles, like the tendrils of some enormous luminous jellyfish trailing in the current. It was the most beautiful thing he had ever seen. It was the most frightening. He couldn’t decide which; his mind kept sliding between the two and couldn’t find anywhere to stand.</p>\n<p>The light it gave was violet and white and very even, like the light of an overcast noon, and there were no shadows anywhere, and it was warm. It was as warm as a summer afternoon in the southern tiers. Sweat was running down his back inside the liner.</p>\n<p>The hammer came.</p>\n<p>It came up through the stone under him, through the cart, through the planks, into his spine. But it was not the hammer. Not the blow, once a second, that he had listened to in his flat and in the Collar and in the Works for thirty hours. It was a swell. It began so gently he hardly noticed it — a faint rising pressure in the floor, like the first lift of a wave under a boat — and it rose, and rose, and went on rising, and at its height the whole city seemed to hum with it, every loose shutter faintly rattling, every scaffold pole faintly singing, the water in a trough nearby shivering into rings; and then, just as slowly, it fell away. It lasted — he counted without meaning to — about a minute and a half.</p>\n<p>He knew what it was. He had known it the instant it started. It was one strike of Ruth Tally’s hammer — one second of the outside world — arriving in here stretched so thin that it took ninety seconds to pass.</p>\n<p>He looked at his visor. LOCAL RATIO: 703.</p>\n<p>“There,” said the boy, softly, beside him. He had his hand on the wall of the platform, flat, feeling it. “That’s the Bell. Did you feel it? That’s the Bell.”</p>\n<p>“I felt it.”</p>\n<p>“There’s never been this many,” the boy said. “Not ever. Gran Perpetua says⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Pim,” said the woman.</p>\n<p>The boy shut his mouth.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She was standing by the cart’s handle with one hand on it, looking down at him, and now that his visor had cleared and his eyes had adjusted, he could see her properly.</p>\n<p>She was short. Everyone here would be short — he’d see that soon enough — but she was short even so, and broad and dense through the shoulders and hips in a way that made him think of the body in the conduit, of the thick heavy bones. She wore the same kind of clothes: a patched tunic belted at the waist, a rigger’s harness over it, loops of rope on one shoulder, heavy trousers with the knees reinforced in keelstone-grey cloth. Her forearms were bare and brown and roped with muscle, and they were scarred — pale lines across them, old burns, the marks of a lifetime of hauling rope. Her hands were the hands that had caught him.</p>\n<p>Her hair was dark and cut short, roughly, as if with a knife. On the left side, above the temple, there was a streak of white in it. Not grey. White. A single clean stripe of it running back from her forehead, as though someone had drawn a line there in chalk.</p>\n<p>And her face⁠—</p>\n<p>He could never, afterwards, describe her face well. He tried. He’d try for a long time. It was an ordinary face, broad-boned, weathered by the heat, and young, he thought — thirty, perhaps? — though the white streak made it hard to tell. It was not her face exactly. It was the way she looked at things. She looked at him on the cart the way you’d look at a view you had seen many times and still liked: patiently, attentively, without hurry, as though there were nothing he could possibly do or say that she hadn’t already seen, and she’d be interested to see it again. Her eyes were grey-green and very steady. They didn’t hurry. He had never seen eyes that hurried less.</p>\n<p>Around her neck, on a cord, hung something black.</p>\n<p>It was a key. A flat, dark, heavy-looking thing, the length of a finger, with a blunt cross-shaped bit. It lay against her tunic just below her collarbone. It didn’t shine. It seemed, in the shadowless light, to drink the light in. He looked at it and something moved at the very back of his mind — some shape he half recognised, some picture from a manual — and then his tired brain let it go.</p>\n<p>“What’s your name?” he said.</p>\n<p>She considered the question. “Do you need it?”</p>\n<p>“I’d like it.”</p>\n<p>“Ione,” said the boy, before she could answer. “She’s Ione. Ione Tally. I’m Pim. This is Steward Harrow.” He pointed at the rat. “He’s named after a Steward. They all are. Mine are.”</p>\n<p>“Pim.”</p>\n<p>“Well, he asked.”</p>\n<p>The woman — Ione — looked at the boy. Then back at Cas. Something almost like a smile moved at the corner of her mouth, and was gone.</p>\n<p>“You’ll be all right on the cart,” she said. “The weight will be bad for a few rounds. Don’t try to stand. Drink when they give you water. It’s clean.” She looked away, across the city, at something he couldn’t see. Her head was up. She was listening.</p>\n<p>He heard it too, then. A whistle. High and shrill, somewhere off among the scaffold towers. Another answered it. Then a third, closer.</p>\n<p>“Wardens,” said Pim.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” Ione let go of the cart handle. “They watch the wells. Someone will have seen us from the towers.” She looked down at Cas again, and there was something quick and serious in her face now. “Listen to me. There isn’t time for most of it, so listen. They’re going to call you the Relief. All of them. They’ll kneel down. They’ll cry. They’ll take you to the First Steward and he’ll call you the Relief too. Don’t let them tell you what that means.”</p>\n<p>“What does it mean?”</p>\n<p>“It means whatever they need it to mean.” The whistles were closer. “When they’re done with you — and they won’t be done with you for a while — find the Tollers. Ask anyone for Toller’s Tower. Ask for Perpetua.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those unhurried eyes. And he had the strangest feeling — a feeling he’d come to know well, over the weeks that followed, and never get used to — that she already knew exactly what he was going to say next, and was simply waiting, politely, for him to say it.</p>\n<p>“Because,” she said, “you came up out of the floor in a suit I’ve been waiting eleven years to see. And because you’re going to want to know how long you’ve got.”</p>\n<p>Then she turned, and put her hand on the boy’s shoulder, and said something to him too fast and too thick for Cas to follow. Pim nodded hard. And Ione Tally stepped to the wall of the platform, and put one hand on the top of it, and vaulted over it — lightly, easily, in that crushing weight, as though it were nothing — and dropped out of sight.</p>\n<p>“She’s Unwatched,” Pim told him, in a whisper, as if it were an explanation. “She can’t be here when they come. She’d get the Quiet.” He patted Cas’s gauntlet with a grimy hand. “Don’t worry. I’ll stay. I’m not anything. Nobody Quiets me. Steward Harrow, get <em>down</em> from there.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Wardens came up the ramp to the well-platform at a run — six of them, then ten — and stopped dead.</p>\n<p>They were big, for Holdfast. That was the first thing he learned about Wardens, and the thing he’d learn about all of them: they were the big ones, the men and women who’d grown a head taller than everyone else and twice as wide, and they wore it like a uniform. They had actual uniforms too: long coats of heavy grey cloth with a yellow lamp stitched on the breast, and on their heads — he stared — on their heads, hard hats. Old Works hard hats, cracked and mended and painted grey and polished to a shine, with the yellow lamp painted on the front. They carried staves: long poles of scaffold pipe with the ends wrapped in cord.</p>\n<p>They stopped at the top of the ramp in a ragged line, breathing hard, and looked at the man in the grey suit lying on the rope-cart beside the Drop well, and nobody said anything at all.</p>\n<p>Then one of them — the biggest, a slab of a man with a slow, square, heavy face and a grey beard cut close — took off his hard hat. He held it against his chest. And very slowly, as though his knees didn’t quite want to, he knelt down on the black stone.</p>\n<p>One by one, the others did the same.</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said the big man. His voice was deep and unsteady. “Lamp keep us. It’s the Relief.”</p>\n<p>“It’s the Relief,” said the others, raggedly. One of them, a young woman at the end of the line, had begun to cry.</p>\n<p>“I <em>said</em>,” said Pim, to no one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They carried him through the city.</p>\n<p>Not on the rope-cart — the big Warden, whose name was Osk, sent for something better, and it came: a litter, a real one, a sort of stretcher-chair made of scaffold pipe and canvas, with poles for eight bearers. They lifted him onto it, suit and all, grunting, and it took all eight of them, big as they were, and they set off with him through the streets of Holdfast at a slow, heavy, lurching walk.</p>\n<p>He saw it from there, the whole way, lying on his back with the Lace above him and the city going past on either side. And the city saw him.</p>\n<p>Word had got there ahead of them. He never knew how; Pim, probably, who had vanished down the ramp the moment the litter came and was nowhere to be seen. But by the time the Wardens had carried him fifty metres from the well, there were people in the lanes, and by the time they’d gone a hundred there were crowds. They came out of doorways and down ladders and up from under walkways. They leaned out of container windows and hung off scaffold towers and stood on roofs. They were short — all of them, every one, men and women both, broad and stocky and heavy-boned — and they were pale, a strange sallow pallor under the violet light, and their clothes were the same patchwork of reused fabric as the dead body in the conduit, a thousand different mendings, every colour. They were silent at first. They stood and stared at the litter going by. And then someone — an old woman in a doorway — put her hand to her mouth and said, “<em>Relief</em>,” in a cracked voice, and it went through the crowd like wind through grass.</p>\n<p><em>Relief. Relief. It’s the Relief.</em></p>\n<p>They began to kneel. Not all of them. But many. They knelt in the lanes as he went by, and put their hands on their chests, or touched their foreheads to the ground, or held up children to look at him. They wept. Some of them sang: a thin, wavering, old-fashioned melody that he felt he almost knew. Some of them reached out, as the litter passed, just to touch the side of it, or the edge of his suit. The Wardens let them.</p>\n<p>He had never been so frightened in his life. Not in the Shear. Not on the gallery floor.</p>\n<p>He tried to look at things. It was the only defence he had. He looked at the lanes: narrow, twisting, paved in black keelstone polished smooth by a century of feet. He looked at the signs. There were signs everywhere, and he knew them — he had walked past them all his working life. STAIRWELL C. MUSTER POINT B. NO NAKED FLAMES. KEEP THIS DOOR CLOSED. They were station signs, the old enamelled plates from the Vault’s walls and walkways, and they had been taken down and put up again as street names: a lane of containers with a sign bolted over its entrance that read CORRIDOR 9 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL ONLY, and a crossroads marked with a big yellow EMERGENCY ASSEMBLY AREA plate that had been lovingly repainted so many times the letters stood up from it in ridges. He looked at the shrines: little niches in walls, with a lamp in them — an actual lamp, a clay dish of oil with a wick — and a picture behind it, painted, of a point of light with rays. He looked at a rat pen: a big wire cage built into the angle of two walls, full of fat, sleek, contented rats, dozens of them, with a sign over it that said, in careful hand-painting, HOLDFAST’S OTHER CITIZENS — PLEASE DO NOT FEED.</p>\n<p>He looked at the mural.</p>\n<p>It was painted across the whole side of a scaffold tower, fifteen storeys high, and the litter went right past its foot so that he saw it from below, rising over him. It showed a man. A tall man in a dark jacket, painted enormous, standing on a high place with his arm raised toward a white star in a violet sky. The man’s face was noble and grieving, with silver hair. And in his raised hand he was holding up a key. A small black key, the length of a finger, on a broken chain. Beneath the man, in a crowd, painted small, were people in white coats and orange overalls, looking up at him and weeping. Across the bottom of the mural, in letters a metre high, it said:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THE GIVING<br>FOUNDER CRANE GAVE THE KEY TO THE LAMP<br>THAT IT MIGHT NEVER BE PUT OUT</div>\n<p>Cas stared at it until the litter carried him past.</p>\n<p>The key was black. It was the length of a finger. It had a blunt, cross-shaped bit.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Steward’s House stood at the foot of Spindle One.</p>\n<p>He knew it was Spindle One because it was the biggest; because the others were further off and it was right there, filling half the sky, a black lattice tower a hundred and fifty metres across rising straight up from the heart of the city into the light; because at its foot, where in the old schematics there had been a lift terminal — the great steel shed where the Crown’s lift came down — there was now a building. It was the only building he’d seen in Holdfast that looked as if it had been built all at once, on purpose. It was made of keelstone slabs laid with great care, black and shining, with a wide stair up to a doorway and tall narrow windows with coloured glass in them — bottle glass, he’d find out later, and lamp lenses, and the glass from old instrument dials, melted and set in lead. Over the door, carved into the stone, was the lamp with its rays.</p>\n<p>They carried him up the stair and through the door into a long, high, cool hall, shadowed, lit by oil lamps, and set the litter down. The Wardens stepped back. The door closed behind them, and the noise of the crowd outside, which had been rising all the way here like a tide, dropped to a murmur.</p>\n<p>A man was waiting for him.</p>\n<p>He was old. Sixty, perhaps, or more — it was hard to tell with Holdfast faces. He was taller than most of them, lean where the others were broad, and he wore a long coat of plain dark cloth, very well mended, and no hard hat. His hair was silver, and there was a lot of it, swept back from a high forehead. He had a long, grave, intelligent face. It was, Cas realised with a small cold shock, a face he had seen before. He’d seen it a quarter of an hour ago, fifteen storeys high, holding up a key.</p>\n<p>The old man came and stood beside the litter. He looked down at Cas without speaking. At the suit, the keel, the visor. At the Ministry crest, stencilled on the shoulder plate, which Cas had never noticed until he saw the man’s eyes go to it and stop there and go very still.</p>\n<p>Then the man smiled. It was a tired, careful, courteous smile, and there was something underneath it that Cas couldn’t read at all.</p>\n<p>“Welcome to Holdfast,” said First Steward Halloran Crane. “Relief. You’re a hundred and sixty-eight years late.”</p>"}, {"file": "07-remain-at-your-station.md", "label": "Chapter 7", "title": "Remain at Your Station", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4836, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Who keeps the Watch? — We keep the Watch. — Until? — Until Relief. — And when Relief comes? — Then we are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Common Office, as said in the Commons every seventh round</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They got him out of the suit in a small stone room at the back of the Steward’s House, and it nearly killed him.</p>\n<p>In the suit, with the exo taking most of it, the weight had been a crushing thing but a bearable one. Out of it — in the grey liner, standing on his own legs on the black stone floor — it was something else. It was like being filled with sand. His arms hung off his shoulders like buckets. His knees shook. His heart, which had been going hard since the ladder, went harder, labouring to push blood that weighed half as much again as it should up into a head that felt as though it were being slowly pressed into his neck. He took two steps and his vision went grey at the edges and the big Warden, Osk, caught him under the arms and lowered him into a chair like a man putting down a sack of cement.</p>\n<p>“Slow,” said Osk. “Slow. Everyone’s slow the first rounds. Even babies.”</p>\n<p>“Babies?”</p>\n<p>“From the Crèche.” Osk had a voice like gravel being poured. “Born heavy, we are, but you still have to learn to stand in it. Takes a year.” He considered Cas. “Take you longer, maybe.”</p>\n<p>They brought him water in a tin cup. It was warm and tasted of iron and something green, and it was the best thing he had ever drunk. They brought him food: a flat, dense, dark-green cake, like a biscuit or a very heavy bread, with a smell of pond and salt. He looked at it.</p>\n<p>“Greenbread,” said Osk. “Algae. Cricket flour. Salt. Eat it slow.”</p>\n<p>He ate it slow. It tasted of the sea, if the sea had been baked. He ate all of it.</p>\n<p>The suit stood in the corner, empty and hulking, the keel humming quietly to itself on its back. Osk kept looking at it. Everyone who came into the room — and a lot of people came into the room, Wardens and Stewards in grey and black coats, on one pretext or another — looked at it, and then at him, and then looked away quickly, as though they had seen something they weren’t meant to.</p>\n<p>Then Halloran Crane came in, and everyone else went out, except Osk, who stood by the door with his hard hat under his arm and his eyes on the floor.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Crane sat down opposite him. He had brought a lamp — a real lamp, a clay dish of oil with a floating wick — and he set it on the table between them, though the room was lit perfectly well by the violet glow through the window. He folded his hands. They were long, fine, old hands, with an ink stain on the middle finger of the right.</p>\n<p>“You’ll forgive me,” he said, “if I’m not quite sure how to begin. There are protocols for this. There is, in fact, an entire liturgy. I’ve read it many times. I’ve never expected to use it.” He smiled slightly. “My grandfather used to say that it had been written by people who never really believed it would be needed, for people who never really believed it would come. I find myself unable to recall a single word of it.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing. He didn’t know what to say.</p>\n<p>“So instead,” said Crane, “I’ll ask you something simple. What is the Outside?”</p>\n<p>“The Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Where you came from. Below the Drop. Beyond it.” Crane’s voice was quite steady. “Our histories say that when the Lamp was kindled, the world beyond the Watch was burned. That the Founders were the last of it. That we keep the Watch for a world that is gone, until Relief comes out of the dark. I’ve always found that a very beautiful idea. I’ve never been quite sure I believed it.” He looked at the suit in the corner, at the stencilled crest on its shoulder. “So. What is it?”</p>\n<p>“It’s a city,” said Cas. “It’s called Averna. It’s big. Six hundred million people. It’s where the Draw — where this place — sends its power. It’s⁠—” He stopped. He thought of the Tier Nine arterial at dawn, the bunting, the anthem. “It’s fine. It’s still there. Nothing’s burned.”</p>\n<p>“And how long,” said Crane, “since the Kindling? Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours.”</p>\n<p>Crane didn’t move.</p>\n<p>He was so still that for a moment Cas wondered if he’d heard. He sat with his long hands folded on the table and his eyes on the little flame of the lamp, and nothing in his face changed, nothing at all, and the stillness went on and on until it became a thing in the room, a thing you could feel pressing on you like the weight.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours,” Crane said at last.</p>\n<p>“And a bit.”</p>\n<p>“A day. A day and a half.” Crane closed his eyes. “My grandfather’s grandfather’s grandfather was born in this city. And lived here, and died here, and was given to the Tanks. So was his son. So was his. And outside⁠—” He opened his eyes. “Outside, it’s still the same night.”</p>\n<p>“Nearly.”</p>\n<p>“Nearly.” He breathed out. It was almost a laugh. “The Tollers will be very pleased with themselves. They’ve always said so. In a way that one could, if one was careful, pretend not to hear.” He looked at Cas. “Is there anyone else? Coming after you?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Is there relief?”</p>\n<p>Cas thought of Rask. The crisis room. Three suits. Ben in the hall of algae. Teo in a chair.</p>\n<p>“There’s me,” he said.</p>\n<p>Crane nodded slowly, as if that were the answer he’d expected. Then he stood up.</p>\n<p>“Come,” he said. “I’d like to show you something. Osk, help the Relief. Slowly.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The chapel was at the heart of the Steward’s House, a small square room of black keelstone with no windows, lit by a single lamp that burned in a niche at the far end. There was nothing else in it. No benches, no altar, no pictures. Only the lamp, and behind the lamp, set into the wall in a frame of polished keelstone as though it were the most precious object in the world, a sheet of yellowed laminate.</p>\n<p>Cas knew it before he could read it. He’d walked past it at Gate Nine a day and a half ago. He’d walked past it every working day for twelve years.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SECTION 9 — IN THE EVENT OF CONTAINMENT LOSS:<br>REMAIN AT YOUR STATION AND AWAIT RELIEF.<br>DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE.<br>RELIEF CREWS WILL BE DISPATCHED.<br>YOU WILL NOT BE FORGOTTEN.</div>\n<p>The stick figure beside its stick console. The Ministry typeface from the Year Twenties. Someone had once drawn a moustache on the stick figure at Gate Nine; nobody had ever drawn anything on this one. It had been cleaned — lovingly, with soft cloths, for generations — until the laminate had gone as clear and pale as horn.</p>\n<p>“The Placard,” said Crane quietly. “Our first scripture. It was on the wall of the assembly bay the night of the Kindling. The Founders gathered under it. Nineteen hundred of them. The Lamp had just been lit — the sky had just filled with light — the Drop had just taken the first of them who tried to go down. And Founder Crane stood under this and read it aloud to them. <em>Remain at your station and await relief.</em> And they did.” He looked at it. “They’ve been doing it ever since.”</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“You’re thinking it’s an evacuation notice,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>“It <em>is</em> an evacuation notice.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.” Crane didn’t seem offended. If anything, he seemed faintly amused, in a tired way. “My ancestor was a practical man. He needed something to hold nineteen hundred terrified people together in the dark, by the second week at the latest. This was on the wall. It had the great advantage of being true.” He turned his head. “Look at the floor.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked. Running along the base of the chapel wall and out through the door, painted onto the black stone in gold leaf, worn almost away by feet in places and repainted over the wear so many times that it stood up from the floor like a ridge of metal, was a line of arrows. He knew them too. They were the arrows from the Vault’s walkways, the little painted chevrons that showed you the way to your muster point in an emergency.</p>\n<p>“They lead to Muster Point B,” said Crane. “In the Commons. It’s the holiest place in Holdfast. Pilgrims come to it on their knees on the seventh round. It is the place, you see, where they were told to wait.” He paused. “My ancestor had a sense of humour. I think it was the only thing that kept him alive.”</p>\n<p>Cas made a sound. He didn’t know whether it was a laugh or something else. Crane looked at him with that tired, courteous half-smile.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Crane. “I’ve often felt that way myself.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Back in the small stone room, with the lamp on the table and a fresh cup of iron-tasting water, Crane told him the history.</p>\n<p>He told it well. He’d told it, Cas guessed, many times, to many children, in the Commons on high days. The Kindling: the night the Lamp was lit, the Founders at their posts, the great light filling the sky. Founder Crane in the Crown, who saw that the Lamp must never go out, and gave the key into it so that it never could. The Descent: nineteen hundred souls coming down the spindles into the Vault as the world beyond burned. The Drop, which took all those who tried to go down after it. The Sealing — the dark days when they understood they could not leave. The Watch: the Founders’ vow to keep their station. The Crèche, the first children born under the Lamp. The Tanks, where the dead went to feed the living. The Tallies, the Bell, the rounds and the shifts. A hundred and sixty-eight years. Seven generations. Thirty-one thousand souls.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand, four hundred and six, at the last count. Four hundred and seven, now.” Crane inclined his head at him. “The Founders were fruitful. They had to be. There was so little else to do.” He folded his hands. “And now I’m going to ask you something, and I’d be grateful if you’d answer it as honestly as you answered the last.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>“The Bells are coming faster,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>He said it softly. By the door, Osk shifted his feet.</p>\n<p>“In the Founders’ time,” said Crane, “the Bell came once every three rounds. Once every nine shifts. You could go days without feeling it. In my grandmother’s time, once a round. In my own childhood, twice. When I became Steward, three. Ten, eight years ago. Now there are more than a hundred. It comes while you’re eating. It comes while you’re sleeping. The Stewardship teaches that this is the Quickening — that the Lamp’s heart beats faster as Relief draws near.” He looked up at Cas. “And now Relief is here. So. The Quickening. What is it?”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at him for a long moment.</p>\n<p>“The Bell’s not the Lamp’s heart,” he said. “It’s an alarm. A hammer, down in the station, outside the — below the Drop. It strikes once a second. Outside seconds. My seconds. It’s been going since the Kindling. You’re hearing each stroke stretched out, because time in here runs faster than out there. That’s why a day and a half outside is a hundred and sixty-eight years in here.”</p>\n<p>“And it’s quickening because⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because time in here is slowing down. Coming back toward ours. The thing that makes time run fast in here is the Lamp. The Lamp is⁠—” He looked for words. There were no Holdfast words for it. He used his own. “The Lamp is a debt. Everything the station ever gave the city outside, it borrowed. From the — from the ground of things. The emptiness. And the emptiness always takes it back. Plus interest. For ninety years the station kept putting off paying it. The night of the Kindling, it stopped being able to. And now the debt’s being called in. Slowly at first. Then faster. The Bells are the sound of it coming due.”</p>\n<p>“And when it comes due?”</p>\n<p>“All at once,” said Cas. “At the Lamp. It’ll be — everything. This city, and the station, and mine, and the world.”</p>\n<p>“When?”</p>\n<p>“The Bells will tell you better than I can. My guess⁠—” He made himself say it. “Weeks. Your weeks. Maybe less. And the Lace⁠—” he nodded at the window, where the violet glow came in “⁠—the Lace is part of it. When it reaches the Rim, it’s over.”</p>\n<p>Crane was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” he said at last. “I thought it was something like that. I have thought it for some years.”</p>\n<p>“You knew?”</p>\n<p>“I have the Tollers’ numbers. The true ones. They come to me every seventh round, as they’ve come to every First Steward since the Founders.” He moved his hand slightly on the table. “I can read a curve, Relief. I’m not a physicist. But I can read a curve.”</p>\n<p>“And you haven’t told them.”</p>\n<p>“No.” Crane looked at him steadily. “I haven’t told them.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“I want to ask you for something,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>The lamp flickered between them. Outside the window, the long swell of the Bell began, rising up through the floor.</p>\n<p>“In an hour,” said Crane, “I’m going to have to take you to the Commons. There is no way to avoid it. Half the city saw you carried here. The other half has heard. They’ll come whether I call them or not, and it will be better if I call them. There are twenty thousand people who will stand in the Commons tonight and look at you.” He waited while the Bell reached its height and the shutters on the window hummed faintly and fell still. “They’ve been waiting for you for a hundred and sixty-eight years. Every one of them was told as a child that one day you’d come. And now you have, and in a few weeks, by your own account, they’re all going to die.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.” Crane accepted the correction gravely. “Maybe. So I’d like to ask you — when you stand up in front of them tonight — to tell them it will be all right.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“I’m not asking you to lie,” said Crane. “I’m asking you to be kind. They’re going to look at you and they’re going to want to know one thing, and it isn’t the physics. It’s whether they’re alone. Tell them they’re not. Tell them the Outside remembers them. Tell them you’ve come to help. Tell them to keep the Watch.” His voice was very gentle. “Let them be calm. For the time they have left. Let them spend it with their children instead of screaming in the lanes. Let them die, if they’re going to die, with the Lamp over them and the Relief among them and the Watch kept, instead of — the other way. I’ve read the old books. I know what the other way looks like.” He paused. “It’s the only mercy I have to give them. I’m asking you to help me give it.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the lamp. At the little flame, steady in its dish of oil.</p>\n<p>“Can it be stopped?” said Crane.</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>The old man’s eyes moved very slightly.</p>\n<p>“There’s a mechanism,” said Cas. “In the Crown. At the top of Spindle One. The woman who built this place designed it for exactly this. It won’t save the station, but it might — it might close the Lamp. Seal it. Before the debt comes due.” He hesitated. “It’s locked. It needs a key.”</p>\n<p>Crane had gone quite white.</p>\n<p>“A key,” he said.</p>\n<p>“A black key. About so long.” Cas held up a finger. “Keelstone. With a⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Our Founder gave the key to the Lamp.”</p>\n<p>“He dropped it.”</p>\n<p>The words were out before he could stop them. They sat on the table between them next to the lamp. Crane looked at them.</p>\n<p>“In Holdfast,” he said finally, very softly, “we say <em>gave</em>.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Commons had been Assembly Bay One. The sign was still over the great doors — ASSEMBLY BAY 1 — CAPACITY 2,500 — and someone had painted over the capacity with a lamp. It was a vast hangar of a place, the biggest single space in the Vault’s floor, where in the station’s working days the Lattice crews had mustered by the hundred at the start of every shift to be briefed and sent up the spindles. Its roof had been partly stripped for materials generations ago, so that it was open to the sky in long strips between the girders, and through the gaps the violet light came down, and the Lace.</p>\n<p>There were twenty thousand people in it. There were more outside, packed into the lanes around it, up on the roofs, hanging off the scaffolds. He could hear them before he saw them — a sound like the sea, like surf on a beach — and when the Wardens carried him in through a side door onto a raised platform at the front of the hall, the sound stopped, all at once, as though a door had shut on it.</p>\n<p>He was standing. He had insisted. Osk was at one elbow and another Warden at the other, and he was in the grey liner with a Holdfast coat over it that someone had found for him, and he was standing, and it was taking everything he had.</p>\n<p>The faces went back and back. A sea of them. Pale, broad, heavy-boned faces under the violet light, upturned, silent, every one of them looking at him. Men and women and children. Old people held up by younger ones. Babies. He saw a woman in the front row with a baby asleep against her shoulder and a little girl of five or six holding her hand, standing on tiptoe, straining to see. He saw an old man weeping without any sound. He saw Wardens along the walls with their staves and their hard hats. He saw, on the wall behind him, a big board — a sort of scoreboard, black, with white numerals painted on hanging plates — that said BELL 119,488 and under it INTERVAL: 14 and a word he couldn’t read.</p>\n<p>Muster Point B was a yellow-painted square on the floor at the foot of the platform. It was surrounded by a low rail of polished brass. Inside it, on the black stone, the paint was worn down to bare stone in the shape of knees.</p>\n<p>Crane stood beside him. He raised his hands. And the whole hall — twenty thousand voices — began to sing.</p>\n<p>Cas knew the tune.</p>\n<p>It took him a few bars. It was slow, much slower than he knew it, and the words were different — worn smooth, the way words get worn when they’re passed through a hundred mouths, then a thousand, then a hundred thousand; the rhymes had slipped and the lines had changed and whole verses had grown out of nothing. But under it, unmistakable, was the Tending. The anthem. The children’s choir version he’d heard on the Tier Nine lampposts at dawn yesterday, thirty-three hours ago, a hundred and sixty-eight years ago. <em>Tend the flame, tend the flame, for the ones who come after.</em> Here, it had become: <em>Tend the Lamp, tend the Lamp, till the Relief comes after.</em> Twenty thousand people, singing it very slowly under the Lace, in a hall where the paint on the floor had been worn through by knees.</p>\n<p>He had to hold on to Osk’s arm.</p>\n<p>When they’d finished, Crane spoke. It was a call-and-response, and the whole hall knew it.</p>\n<p>“Who keeps the Watch?”</p>\n<p>“<em>We keep the Watch.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Until?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Until Relief.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And when Relief comes?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Then we are relieved.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said Halloran Crane, into the silence, “has come.”</p>\n<p>The sound the hall made was not a cheer. It was something older than cheering. It went up into the girders and the gaps in the roof and out into the lanes, where the people outside took it up, until the whole city seemed to be making it. Somewhere at the back someone was screaming. People were kneeling, falling to their knees in waves, clinging to each other. The woman in the front row with the baby had her face in her free hand. The little girl was jumping up and down.</p>\n<p>Crane turned to him. He said nothing. He just looked at him, courteously, and stepped back.</p>\n<p>Cas faced the hall.</p>\n<p>He had meant to be vague. He’d lain on the bed in the small stone room while they waited, and planned it: a few careful sentences that wouldn’t be lies and wouldn’t be the truth. <em>I’ve come from the Outside. The Outside is still there. I’ve come to try to help.</em> All true. All nothing. He’d been good at sentences like that for fifteen years. He had a whole professional life of them.</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. And he saw the little girl in the front row. She’d stopped jumping. She was standing very still, holding her mother’s hand, looking up at him with an expression of such total, perfect, open trust that it went into him like a blade.</p>\n<p>“I’ve come from Outside,” he said.</p>\n<p>His voice was thin. It didn’t carry. But the hall was so silent that it did.</p>\n<p>“The Outside’s still there. It hasn’t forgotten you.” He swallowed. “I was sent to help you. And I — we’re working on it. There are people working on it. Keep the Watch. Keep it a little longer. It’s going to be⁠—”</p>\n<p>He stopped.</p>\n<p>The little girl was looking at him.</p>\n<p>“It’s going to be all right,” said Casimir Hale.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d been loved before. He supposed he had. Sabine had loved him, once, for a while, before the Review and the Standby and the silence. His mother had loved him. Nell loved him, in the fierce unforgiving way of ten-year-olds.</p>\n<p>He had never been loved like this.</p>\n<p>It came up at him out of that hall like heat out of a furnace. Twenty thousand people, weeping, singing, reaching toward him, calling out to him. A man at the front threw something up onto the platform — a little carved lamp on a cord, yellow plastic, like the one on the body in the conduit — and then someone else threw another, and another, until the platform was scattered with them. Mothers held up their babies. An old woman at the brass rail of Muster Point B was kissing the floor. Every face that he looked at looked back at him with a joy that was so complete, so helpless, that it didn’t seem possible it could fit inside a person.</p>\n<p>It felt wonderful.</p>\n<p>That was the thing he’d remember afterwards, lying awake. Not the guilt — that came later, and it came hard. But in the moment, standing there with Osk holding his arm and the whole of Holdfast pouring its love up at him like light: it felt <em>wonderful</em>. It felt like the warmest, easiest, most natural thing in the world. He had told them a lie and they loved him for it, and every nerve in his body wanted to stay there, in that, forever, and tell them another.</p>\n<p>That was the horror of it. That was the whole machine. He’d thought he understood the Commonweal. He’d been inside it all his life. He had never once, until that moment, understood why it worked.</p>\n<p>Then he saw her.</p>\n<p>She was at the very back of the hall, by the great doors, standing in a gap in the crowd with her back against the wall and her arms folded. Everyone around her was singing. She wasn’t. Everyone around her was weeping or kneeling or reaching up toward the platform. She was standing perfectly still, looking at him over twenty thousand heads, with the white streak in her hair and the black key at her throat.</p>\n<p>There was nothing in her face. No anger. No contempt. No disappointment, even — he looked for it, and it wasn’t there. There was only patience. That same unhurried patience he’d seen at the top of the well, the look of someone watching a scene they’d seen before, many times, and would wait through to the end.</p>\n<p>It was worse than anything else could have been.</p>\n<p>She met his eyes. And across the whole length of the Commons, over the singing, very clearly, she moved her lips. One word.</p>\n<p><em>Tollers.</em></p>\n<p>Then the crowd shifted, and she was gone.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They gave him a room in the Steward’s House. It was small and square, with a bed that was a wooden frame strung with rope and a mattress stuffed with something that crackled, and a window with a shutter, and a basin, and the suit standing in the corner humming to itself. Osk sat outside the door on a stool. Cas heard him settle there, and sigh, and heard the stool creak under his weight.</p>\n<p>He lay on the bed. The weight lay on him. He could feel his heart labouring. He could feel every one of his ribs.</p>\n<p>After a while he got up, which took a long time, and went to the suit, and took the little recorder out of its chest compartment. It was a slim grey Ministry thing, standard issue, with a crystal store that would outlast the suit. It had a channel that fed the suit’s radio, through the orange puck at the top of the well, down through the Shear, to another puck, and another, and a chair in Lattice Station 14-C.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor with his back against the bed, and pressed the button.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said.</p>\n<p>And then he couldn’t think what came after. So he just talked.</p>\n<p>“I’m through. I’m through, I’m alive, the keel held. Your keel. It’s — there’s a city in here, Teo. Up the conduit, on the Anvil. A city. Thirty-one thousand people. It’s a hundred and sixty-eight years old. They’ve got — they’ve got rats, they keep them, they name them after their officials. They’ve got a hymn that’s the Tending. They’ve got the Section 9 placard in a chapel with a candle in front of it. They thought I was a — they called me the Relief. They carried me through the streets. They⁠—” He stopped. “They asked me to tell them it would be all right. So I did. Within an hour. I stood up in front of twenty thousand people and I lied to them and it was the best I’ve felt in fifteen years.” He put his head back against the bed frame. “I don’t know what I’m doing, Teo. I thought I would. I don’t. I’ll send more. Are you all right? You won’t hear this for — I don’t know. Don’t answer. Yes, answer. I’ll wait.”</p>\n<p>He pressed the button again. He sat there.</p>\n<p>Then, after a while, he changed the channel. There was a second channel on the recorder, one that didn’t go anywhere. It just stored.</p>\n<p>“Nell,” he said.</p>\n<p>He sat there on the floor of a stone room in a city that shouldn’t exist, under a sky full of light.</p>\n<p>“You were right,” he said. “There’s one. There’s a star in here. It’s — it’s not a very nice one. It’s too bright to look at. They call it the Lamp. There’s a sort of lace round it, all across the sky, like frost, and it moves. Everything here’s lit by it. There’s no night. They close their shutters when they want to sleep.” He paused. “You’d have liked it. I think. You’d have been frightened of it, and then you’d have wanted to know what it was made of. That’s what I did.”</p>\n<p>He sat there a while longer, holding the recorder.</p>\n<p>“I’m still scared,” he told her. “You said that’s allowed.”</p>\n<p>He pressed the button, and put the recorder down on the floor beside him, and closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>He was nearly asleep when something small and hard hit the shutter.</p>\n<p>He opened his eyes. It came again: <em>tock</em>. A pebble. And a third time. He got up — slowly, slowly — and crossed the room, and eased the shutter open a crack.</p>\n<p>Below the window, in the narrow lane that ran along the back of the Steward’s House, there was a small, thin, very dirty boy with a rat on his shoulder, grinning up at him.</p>\n<p>“She says,” whispered Pim, “the Tollers want you. She says come now, while the Wardens are drunk. She says bring your notebook.”</p>"}, {"file": "08-the-tollers.md", "label": "Chapter 8", "title": "The Tollers", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 6541, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Let them count the Bells. Nobody ever died of counting. It’s drawing the line that does it.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally, Head Toller, in conversation</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">Holdfast at night was not dark. It couldn’t be. The Lamp never set, and the Lace never dimmed, and the violet light lay over the city at every hour exactly as it had lain at noon. But the city made its own night. At the end of second shift a horn blew from the top of the Steward’s House, a long low note, and all across Holdfast the shutters came down — clattering, banging, sliding, squealing on their runners, thousands of them, a sound like a whole city clearing its throat — and when it was over, the lanes were in shadow. Not darkness. A deep, violet, shuttered dusk, lit here and there by the little clay oil lamps in the shrine-niches, where the flames stood up very straight in the still, warm air.</p>\n<p>The Wardens outside the Steward’s House were not, in fact, drunk. They were asleep. Osk was asleep on his stool outside Cas’s door with his hard hat in his lap and his chin on his chest, snoring like distant machinery, and when Cas eased the door open and edged past him in the dark, moving so slowly that each step took a breath, one of Osk’s eyes opened.</p>\n<p>They looked at each other.</p>\n<p>Osk’s eye closed again. The snoring resumed.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Pim had brought the rope-cart. Cas lay in it, under a tarpaulin, and the boy hauled it through the lanes by its long handle, grunting, with Steward Harrow riding on his shoulder, and the cart’s iron wheels made a small patient rattle on the keelstone that echoed off the shuttered walls.</p>\n<p>“It’s not far,” Pim whispered. “It’s right by the Bell Column. Everyone knows where it is. Nobody goes, except Tollers.” He hauled. “And me. And Ione. I bring them rats.”</p>\n<p>“To eat?”</p>\n<p>“<em>No.</em>” He sounded scandalised. “To <em>keep</em>. Gran Perpetua likes the brown ones.” A pause. “Well. Sometimes to eat.”</p>\n<p>The lanes twisted and climbed and dropped. Once they went under a walkway where someone was sitting in the shadows, smoking something that smelled of burnt grass, and the someone said, “Pim,” and Pim said, “Brother Coll,” and that was all. Once a Warden went by at the end of a lane with a lamp on a pole, and Pim stopped dead and held his breath and the cart was still until the light had gone. Once, through a gap between two containers, Cas saw the Commons — its broken roof, the girders black against the Lace — and heard, very faintly, singing still going on inside it. Some of them hadn’t gone home. Some of them were still there, singing the Tending, in the middle of the night, for him.</p>\n<p>He shut his eyes under the tarpaulin.</p>\n<p>The Bell came as they arrived. He felt it start in the cart’s wheels, rising, and it rose and rose and went on rising, stronger than he’d felt it anywhere in the city, until the tarpaulin over him was trembling and the cart’s handle was humming in Pim’s hands. It didn’t fade after ninety seconds. It went on — lingering, ringing, a long reverberation that seemed to come up out of the ground and hang in the air around them like the note after a struck bell.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Pim, pleased, as though he’d arranged it. “That’s why it’s here. It comes up here first. You can feel it best here. Gran says it’s the only place in Holdfast where the Bell’s <em>honest</em>.”</p>\n<p>Cas pushed the tarpaulin back.</p>\n<p>Toller’s Tower stood at the edge of a small square, and it was the strangest building he’d seen yet. At its heart was a pillar — a single massive column of black keelstone, perhaps three metres across, rising out of the Anvil and up, bare and smooth, thirty metres or more into the air, where it ended in nothing. Some structural member of the old Vault, some support for a gantry long since stripped. Around it, clinging to it, the Tollers had built their tower: a scaffold frame that went up the column in a spiral, storey after storey, each floor a platform of plank and plate lashed to the pipe, each one hung with things. As Cas looked up at it in the violet dusk he saw pendulums — dozens of pendulums, hanging from the platforms on long wires and rods and chains, some of them swinging, most of them still. He saw great glass carboys of water on shelves, with pipes and taps and marks on their sides. He saw a huge wooden wheel, like a mill-wheel, turning very slowly. He saw, at the very top, a platform with a sort of long tube mounted on a swivel, pointing up at the sky.</p>\n<p>And he saw that the whole column — the whole three-metre, thirty-metre pillar of black keelstone, as far up as he could see — was covered in white marks.</p>\n<p>“Come on,” said Pim, hauling at the cart. “She’s waiting. She hates waiting. She says she’s done enough of it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The door at the bottom of the tower was open, and inside was a single round room built around the base of the column. It was lit by a dozen lamps. It was cluttered beyond belief — shelves and shelves of ledgers and papers and jars and instruments, a workbench covered in springs and gears, a pot of something steaming on a little stove — and it smelled of oil and paper and algae soup. There were people in it. Four or five, in the long grey smocks he’d come to recognise as the Tollers’, all of them turning to look at the door as it opened.</p>\n<p>And in a chair beside the column, with one hand resting flat on the black stone, sat a very small, very old woman.</p>\n<p>She was tiny. Even for Holdfast, even hunched in a chair, she was tiny — a little bent bundle of grey smock and white hair, her feet in felt slippers not reaching the floor. Her face was so wrinkled it looked like crumpled paper, and her hands were knotted like roots. But her eyes, when they came up to Cas’s, were black and sharp and bright as a bird’s, and they went over him from head to foot in about a second.</p>\n<p>“You’re the Relief?” said Perpetua Tally.</p>\n<p>“So I’m told.”</p>\n<p>“You look like you need a sit-down.” She jerked her chin at the Tollers. “Tobiah. Chair. Before he falls on my instruments.”</p>\n<p>A young man with round spectacles made of instrument lenses brought a chair. Cas sat in it. It was the most grateful he’d ever been for a chair in his life. Pim, behind him, had pulled the cart inside and was busy letting Steward Harrow down onto the floor, where the rat ran immediately to the old woman’s slippers and sat up on them.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Harrow,” said Perpetua, without looking down. “You’re getting fat.” Her eyes stayed on Cas. “So. Thirty-three hours.”</p>\n<p>“She told you.”</p>\n<p>“Ione told me what you said. At the well.” Perpetua’s hand moved very slightly on the column, the way you might stroke an animal. “Thirty-three hours and a bit. That was what you said? <em>And a bit.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Hmph.” She turned her head. “Tobiah. Last Bell.”</p>\n<p>The young man with the lens-spectacles went to a ledger on a stand by the column — a vast, thick, leather-bound thing, its pages dense with columns of figures — and ran his finger down the latest page.</p>\n<p>“Bell one hundred and nineteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-nine,” he read. “Interval eleven minutes fifty. Our reckoning, Outside⁠—” he did something quick with a slide-rule “⁠—thirty-three hours, eleven minutes, and twenty-nine seconds.”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours, eleven minutes, twenty-nine seconds,” said Perpetua Tally to the Relief. “<em>And a bit.</em>” She sniffed. “They send us a man from Outside after a hundred and sixty-eight years, and he can’t tell the time.”</p>\n<p>Cas stared at her.</p>\n<p>“You knew,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Of course we knew.” She lifted her hand from the column and waved it, a small impatient gesture, at the walls, the ledgers, the pendulums, the whole cluttered tower. “What did you think we were doing in here? Knitting?”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They had always known.</p>\n<p>That was the thing she told him, over the next hour, in the round room at the foot of the tower, with the lamps burning and the soup going round in tin cups and the Tollers perched on stools and shelves listening. They had always known. Ruth Tally had known — Ruth, who’d been a shift engineer, who had pulled the hammer herself, who’d understood exactly what it was and what it meant. <em>One Bell, one second</em>, Ruth had told her children. <em>We are living faster than the world. The Outside is not burned. It is only slow.</em> And her children had told theirs. And for seven generations the Tallys and those who married into them and those they took on as apprentices had counted every Bell, and marked it, and measured it, and kept the count in secret, and known.</p>\n<p>“Look at it,” said Perpetua, and pointed up at the column. “Go on. Look properly.”</p>\n<p>He looked.</p>\n<p>The white marks on the black stone were tallies. Strokes, in chalk and white paint and something scratched, in groups — he stood up, painfully, with Tobiah’s arm under his, and went close — in groups of five, the fifth stroke across the four. Rows of them. Twelve groups to a row: sixty strokes. And every sixty rows, a long ruled line right round the column, and a number painted beside it.</p>\n<p><em>1.</em></p>\n<p><em>2.</em></p>\n<p><em>3.</em></p>\n<p>He tilted his head back and looked up the column. Up and up, spiralling round it, rows and rows and rows of tiny white strokes, and every so often a ruled line, and a number. He could see them going up into the shadows of the upper floors. <em>15. 16.</em> Up past the pendulums. <em>22. 23.</em></p>\n<p>“Outside Hours,” said Perpetua. “Three thousand six hundred Bells. One hour, Outside. The first Hour took twenty-six years. The second took twenty-two. The one we’re in now will take about four months. Ruth marked the first one herself, with a nail, in the stone. Tobiah — show him.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah held up a lamp to a place near the bottom of the column, just above head height, where the stone was dark and smooth and polished by hands. There, among the chalk strokes, was a line that hadn’t been chalked. It had been scratched. Deep, wavering, careful, a line gouged into keelstone — which nothing could scratch, Cas thought, and then realised that it had taken her years, years of going back to it and going over it and going over it with a nail, until it was deep enough to last.</p>\n<p>Beside it, scratched the same way, a single word.</p>\n<p><em>MADDY</em></p>\n<p>“Her daughter,” said Perpetua. “Outside. She was seven.”</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“We’ve been counting her night,” said Perpetua Tally. “All of us. For a hundred and sixty-eight years. That’s what the Tower’s for. Not the Bells. The Bells are just how you count it. We’ve been counting the night of a little girl called Maddy Tally who went to sleep in her gran’s flat on the Outside and hasn’t woken up yet.” She put her hand back on the column. “Thirty-three hours. She’ll be awake by now. She’ll be wondering where her mam is.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came.</p>\n<p>It rose up through the floor, up through the column under the old woman’s hand, and the whole tower hummed with it — the pendulums trembling on their wires, the water in the carboys shivering into rings, the lamps’ flames bowing — and it hung there, ringing, for longer than Cas could hold his breath. Every Toller in the room had gone still. They were counting. He could see it in their faces. Silently, all of them, the way people pray.</p>\n<p>When it had faded, Tobiah went to the column with a stub of chalk and made a stroke.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“The Stewardship knows,” said Perpetua. “Obviously. We send the true count to the First Steward every seventh round, and have done since Founder Crane. He knows the Outside’s alive. They’ve all known. They keep it quiet because the Founder’s Word says the Outside burned, and if the Outside didn’t burn then what are we doing here, and why haven’t we gone, and who told us to stay?” She sipped her soup. “Holdfast isn’t built on the Watch, Relief. It’s built on not asking that question.”</p>\n<p>“They let people count the Bells,” said Cas slowly.</p>\n<p>“Oh, they <em>let</em> people count the Bells. There’s the Public Tally in the Commons, big as a house, you’ll have seen it. Bell number, interval. Everyone knows the Bells are quickening. You can’t hide it. You feel it in your teeth every twelve minutes.” She put down her cup. “Let them count the Bells. Nobody ever died of counting. It’s drawing the line that does it.”</p>\n<p>“The line.”</p>\n<p>“Tobiah.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah brought a book. A big, flat, cloth-bound ledger, very old, and he opened it on the workbench under a lamp and turned it round so Cas could see. It was a graph. Hand-drawn, in ink, on paper ruled in faint squares, and it went on across the page and over onto the next and the next — a hundred and sixty-eight years of it, a column of intervals down the side and a row of Bell numbers along the bottom — and it was a curve.</p>\n<p>He knew it the instant he saw it. It was the curve he’d drawn himself on the backs of envelopes, fifteen years ago, at night, in his flat. It fell away from the top of the page steeply at first, and then less steeply, and then less, a long smooth swoop sliding down toward the bottom of the page — Bell intervals of three days, then a day, then hours, then minutes — never quite reaching the bottom, always getting closer.</p>\n<p>He took the sketchbook out of his coat. His hands were shaking a little. He found a clean page, and a pencil, and did the fit — three points, then five, then ten, off the Tollers’ ledger — and worked the numbers, and there it was. An exponential. A decay, as clean as a textbook, with a time constant of — he converted it — five and a half, six hours. Outside hours.</p>\n<p>“That’s the debt,” he said. “That’s it coming due. That’s the hill coming down.”</p>\n<p>“We don’t know what it is,” said Perpetua. “We know what it <em>does</em>.”</p>\n<p>“And where it goes?” He looked up. “Have you drawn it forward?”</p>\n<p>There was a small silence in the tower.</p>\n<p>“Seren drew it forward,” said Perpetua.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She told it quickly. She didn’t dress it. He thought afterwards that she had probably told it that way, quickly and plainly, many times, to herself.</p>\n<p>Seren Tally had been Perpetua’s daughter. A Toller, like her mother, but a better one: a genius with numbers, with a mind that could hold a curve and see where it was going. Seren had drawn the line forward. Seren had worked out that the Bells would come faster and faster, and at the rate they were going, sooner or later — in her daughter’s lifetime — would come so fast they’d run together into a single note. She’d measured the Lace with the sighting tube at the top of the tower, round after round for ten years, and found it was coming down: not steadily, but faster every year. She’d worked out that it would reach the Rim — the ring round the Lamp at the spindles’ tops — before the Bells ran together. And she’d guessed, without knowing why, that when it reached the Rim, that would be the end of everything.</p>\n<p>“She took it to the First Steward,” said Perpetua. “Halloran. He’d not been Steward long. He thanked her very kindly and told her it must not be spoken. She told him that people had a right to know how long they had. He told her that people had a right to be calm.” She turned her cup in her knotted hands. “She painted it on the wall of the Commons. The line, and the date. Big. In the night, with Ione holding the ladder. Ione was fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“And they⁠—”</p>\n<p>“They gave her to the Drop.”</p>\n<p>Nobody in the room moved.</p>\n<p>“It’s what they do,” said Perpetua, “for the ones who won’t keep the Watch. It’s very old. Founder Crane did it. It’s not an execution. It’s a <em>release</em>. You’re free to go. Down the well. Nobody’s ever stopped at the bottom.” She said it flatly. “Halloran signed it. I suppose he had to. He came to see me, after. He sat in that chair you’re in and he cried.” She looked at Cas with her black bird’s eyes. “I let him. I thought he’d earned it. It’s the only thing I’ve ever given him.”</p>\n<p>Cas thought of the body at the foot of the conduit. The patched, mended clothes. The little yellow lamp worn smooth by fingers.</p>\n<p>“When?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Thirteen years ago.”</p>\n<p>He did the arithmetic. He couldn’t stop himself. Thirteen years ago the Bells had come once every five or six hours; the ratio had been twenty thousand, not seven hundred. He ran it back along the Tollers’ curve. Nineteen hours, outside. Nineteen hours before he’d climbed the ladder in 14-C. She had gone down the Drop at about the time he’d been in a hangar in the Collar, learning to walk in a keel suit.</p>\n<p>“I think I saw her,” he said.</p>\n<p>Perpetua looked at him.</p>\n<p>“At the bottom of the conduit,” he said. “There was — someone. In mended clothes. With a lamp. Carved from a hard hat.”</p>\n<p>“Yellow,” said Perpetua. Her voice had changed.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>The old woman sat without moving. Then she took her hand off the column and put both her hands in her lap, and looked down at them, and her face did something he wasn’t ready for. It crumpled — the whole of it, the paper-crumple of it, all at once — and her mouth opened, and she made a small, dry, high sound, like a bird. Tobiah was beside her at once, kneeling by her chair. She didn’t look at him. She sat there with her hands in her lap and made that small sound, over and over, and the tears ran down the creases of her face and dripped off her chin onto the grey smock.</p>\n<p>“I gave her that,” said Perpetua Tally. “I carved it. When she was a baby. For her crib.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was Tobiah who brought out the Log, in the end. Perpetua had gone quiet, sitting with her eyes closed and her hand back on the column, and nobody had spoken, and then Tobiah had got up and gone to a shelf and taken down a box — a heavy steel box with a Works stencil on its side, very old, very dented — and put it on the workbench and opened it.</p>\n<p>Inside, wrapped in layer on layer of cloth, was a book.</p>\n<p>It was a Ministry logbook. He knew the type: A4, hard-backed, blue cloth, with the Continuance flame stamped on the cover and HOLLOWELL DRAW — CROWN — SHIFT LOG in faded gold. The station had issued them by the thousand. This one was swollen to twice its thickness, crammed with extra pages sewn in, bristling with slips of paper, its spine cracked and mended with thread, its corners worn to round. It looked like it had been opened ten thousand times.</p>\n<p>“Ruth’s,” said Tobiah quietly. “She had it in her hand in the lift. She didn’t know she’d picked it up.”</p>\n<p>Cas opened it.</p>\n<p>The first pages were the ordinary Crown log for the night of the test, in a neat engineer’s pencil. <em>01:50 Dep. Min. Crane + crew arr. for Deepening footage. 01:52 Advised Dep. Min. of T6 bearing temp (+11). Advised protocol requires Chief Eng. Advised interlocks.</em> He could see her in the pencil: the neatness, the care, the way she wrote <em>advised</em>. <em>01:58 Dir. Obi has deferred to Ministry. Proceeding under protest. Note: under protest.</em></p>\n<p>Then a gap. A blank line.</p>\n<p>Then, in the same pencil but a different hand — a hand that shook — <em>02:05 T6 unseated at Draw 135. Boundary failure. Interlocks failed. Draw would not zero. Time grad. across Crown, severe. Attempted Hollowell Clause. Dep. Min. Crane had the key and would not give it. At 02:09 approx gantry failed, key lost, fell out through window glass into the Lesion as if it were called. Clause not possible. 02:14 abandoned Crown. Pulled the Hammer.</em></p>\n<p>And under that, very small:</p>\n<p><em>I did it. I said 135. It was me.</em></p>\n<p>He turned the page.</p>\n<p>After that, it was a different book. The log format was gone. The pencil went on, but it was a diary now, a history, a record. The first days in the Vault. The Drop — the first men who’d tried the conduits, and what had come back up. Aldous Crane under the Placard. The Sealing. Names, lists of names, the living and the dead. Rations. Water. The algae. The first birth. Page after page, and the handwriting getting smaller and smaller as the paper ran out, until it was so small he had to hold the book up to the lamp to read it, and in places the pages were sewn in from other things: the backs of forms, the insides of packets, a sheet of pale thin stuff that looked like it had been made from algae pulp. The years went by in the margins. <em>Year 3. Year 7. Year 20, Bell 2,560.</em> He turned pages and turned pages. It was Ruth Tally’s whole life. It was all there.</p>\n<p>And there were letters.</p>\n<p>They came every few pages, all through the book, set off from the rest with a line drawn across the page. Every one of them began the same way.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy —</em></p>\n<p>He read the first one. It was dated <em>Day 4</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy — I don’t know if you’ll ever read this. I’m going to write it anyway because otherwise I’ll go mad. Mum is in a place a very long way away and I can’t come home yet. It isn’t because I don’t want to. There’s a big light here and it’s got stuck and I have to help. I’m sorry I didn’t say goodbye properly. I had to go to work early. You were asleep with your feet out of the covers. I tucked them in. Did you notice? Probably not. Be good for Nana. I love you more than the whole Draw. — Mum.</em></p>\n<p>He turned pages. <em>Maddy — today we planted the first beans.</em> <em>Maddy — the Bell only comes every three days now, and I’ve started to feel like it’s your breathing.</em> <em>Maddy — I got married today. His name is Tomas and he works the Anvil pumps and he makes me laugh. I know that’s strange. I hope you don’t mind. You’d like him.</em> <em>Maddy — you have a brother. I’ve called him Hal.</em> <em>Maddy — twenty years. For you it’s about forty minutes. I’ve done the sum a thousand times. You’re still asleep. I lie awake and think about you asleep, and it’s the only thing that helps.</em></p>\n<p>One entry, near the middle of the book — <em>Year 31</em> — wasn’t a letter. It was set apart from the rest by a line above and below, and it was short, and he read it twice without understanding it.</p>\n<p><em>I have never told anyone this. At 02:09, after the key fell, I saw a man at the Keystone. Standing where I knelt. Dressed close, in grey, like a diver. A cord knotted round his wrist. His hand on a wheel that isn’t there. He looked at me as if he knew me. He was so calm. Then there was no one. I have thought about him every day for thirty-one years. I think he was the witness. I think, whoever he is, he is still there.</em></p>\n<p>He turned the page. There were more letters.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t read any more. He sat with the book open on his knees and the lamp burning and his eyes blurred.</p>\n<p>Then he turned to the last page.</p>\n<p>The pencil was almost too faint to read. The hand was an old woman’s hand, shaking badly, the letters large and wandering because the eyes that made them couldn’t see small any more.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy — I am eighty-eight. Year 50. Bell 7,100. For you it is about two hours, so it’s still the middle of the night, and you’ve probably turned over and pushed the covers off again.</em></p>\n<p><em>I’ve had a whole life here, baby. It was a good one, mostly. I had your brothers and your sister and I had Tomas for forty years and I have eleven grandchildren and they count the Bells for me now because my eyes are gone. I made a lot of mistakes. The biggest one was the first one. I’m sorry for it every day.</em></p>\n<p><em>I want you to know I thought about you every single day. Every one. Nineteen thousand days, give or take. I never once let myself forget you were there, asleep, in the same night. It was the thing that held me up.</em></p>\n<p><em>If someone ever brings you this — and they will; I’ve told them to — then you’ll be seven, and I’ll have been gone a long time, and that’s all right. Don’t be sad. I had my life. You go and have yours. Be brave. Be kind. Tell the truth, even when it’s hard. Especially then.</em></p>\n<p><em>Love you more than the whole Draw.</em></p>\n<p><em>Mum.</em></p>\n<p>He closed the book.</p>\n<p>“She’s seven,” he said. He didn’t mean to say it aloud. He heard it come out of him. “She’s still seven. Right now. It’s the day after Continuance Day. It’s — she’ll have had her breakfast. She’s seven.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Perpetua Tally. She had opened her eyes. She was looking at him steadily across the lamplit room with her wet, bright, black eyes. “We know, Relief. We’ve always known. What we didn’t know — what we never had — was anyone who’d <em>seen</em> it.”</p>\n<p>She reached out, with a knotted old hand, and touched his knee.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” she said. “You’ve no idea. Thank you.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was then that Ione came down from the roof.</p>\n<p>He heard her before he saw her — a light, quick tread on the spiral stair that ran up round the column, coming down fast from the upper floors — and then she swung round the last turn and dropped the last few steps and was in the room. She had a rope over her shoulder and a notebook in her hand and chalk on her fingers, and she stopped at the foot of the stair and looked round at all of them: at Perpetua with her wet face and Tobiah kneeling, and the Tollers on their stools, and Pim with his rat, and Cas with the Log on his knees.</p>\n<p>“You’ve read it,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She came across the room and crouched in front of his chair so that her face was level with his, the way you’d crouch to talk to a child, or to someone sick. She looked at him for a moment with that unhurried, attentive gaze. Then she said: “The Lamp can be closed. There’s a lock in the Crown. I have its key.”</p>\n<p>And she lifted the cord from round her neck and held out her hand.</p>\n<p>The key lay on her palm.</p>\n<p>It was black. It was the length of a finger. It had a flat bow and a straight shaft and a blunt, cross-shaped bit, and it was dense — he could see it was dense, it sat in her hand heavier than a thing that size had any right to — and it didn’t reflect the lamplight. It took the light in and gave nothing back. Along the shaft, stamped into it in small square letters:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL · KEYSTONE · 0001</div>\n<p>He’d seen it in a mural, fifteen storeys high. He’d seen it in a pencil log, in the shaking hand of a woman who’d watched it fall. He’d seen it, he thought, somewhere else too — a picture in the training manual, a photograph in the back of Hollowell’s withdrawn paper: <em>the Minister’s key</em>. There had only ever been one.</p>\n<p>“That’s not possible,” he said.</p>\n<p>“No,” agreed Ione.</p>\n<p>“It fell. Into the Lesion. Ruth <em>saw</em> it.” He heard his voice rising. “Your Founder — it’s on a <em>wall</em>, it’s on a wall fifteen storeys high⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Then where did you <em>get</em> it?”</p>\n<p>“I found it,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it simply. Then she sat back on her heels and looked at the key in her hand, and a little of the unhurried patience went out of her face, and something else came into it. Something older. More careful.</p>\n<p>“When I was seventeen,” she said, “I went up. Past where anyone goes. And I got lost, up there, in a place called the Carousel. I’ll tell you about it one day. It’s not a story for tonight.” She turned the key over. “There’s a gantry in the Carousel. A walkway, across a gap. I walked across it — I don’t know. Four thousand times? More? I knew every bolt in it. I knew every mark on it. And one time I walked across it and there was a key lying on it. Right in the middle. Where it had never been, not one time in four thousand. And then it was.”</p>\n<p>Cas stared at her.</p>\n<p>“I picked it up,” said Ione. “I’ve had it ever since.”</p>\n<p>The floor of the round room seemed to tilt under his chair. It wasn’t the weight. It was something in his head. He was thinking of a paper he’d read as a student, an old, strange, half-joking paper from before the Long Heat, that his professor had assigned as a curiosity: two physicists asking what would happen if a time machine were real, and whether an object could go round a loop in time and come back to where it started. They’d called it a jinn. An object with no maker. An object that existed because it existed. That was never made, and never destroyed, and simply went round.</p>\n<p><em>You could find one lying on a walkway</em>, his professor had joked, <em>where it had never been. And you’d have no way to know where it came from. Because it didn’t come from anywhere.</em></p>\n<p>“A jinn,” he said.</p>\n<p>“A what?”</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” He didn’t take his eyes off the key. “Nothing. A word. Go on.”</p>\n<p>“There isn’t any more,” said Ione. “Except that I knew what it was, because I’d been watching the Crown. In the Carousel, you can see the Crown. The Crown on the night of the Kindling. For three years, I watched it.” She looked up at him. “I watched Ruth try to open the lock under the floor. I watched Founder Crane refuse to give her the key. I watched the walkway go. I saw the key on his chain. I know the lock. I know the plate over it. I could draw you the lock with my eyes shut. And I came down, two days later, with this in my hand, and I told them all: <em>the Lamp can be closed</em>. There’s a lock in the Crown and this is the key and if we get it up there we can put the Lamp out.”</p>\n<p>“And they called you mad,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“They called me Unwatched.” She shrugged very slightly. “It means the same.”</p>\n<p>“She’s not mad,” said Perpetua, from her chair. Her voice was hoarse but perfectly steady. “She’s the only one of us who’s ever <em>been</em> anywhere.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the key. Then at Ione’s face. Then at the key.</p>\n<p>“She isn’t mad,” he said.</p>\n<p>Every head in the room turned.</p>\n<p>“I’ve read the paper that lock was built from,” he said. “The woman who built this place — Hollowell — she wrote it. The last thing she wrote before she died. The Ministry buried it. It’s called the Clause. It’s a way to seal the core — the Lamp — if it ever broke the way it’s broken. Fold it shut. For ever. It’s in the Crown, under a plate in the floor.” He looked at Ione. “There are words on the plate.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Hollowell Clause</em>,” said Ione at once, softly, as if reciting. “<em>For use in catastrophic boundary failure only. The sealing requires a witness.</em>”</p>\n<p>“You’ve read it.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve read it over Ruth’s shoulder a hundred and seventy-five thousand times.” She held his eyes. “Do you know what it means? The last part?”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas. “Nobody does.”</p>\n<p>She nodded slowly, as though that too were something she had half expected.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Why haven’t you gone?” he said, later.</p>\n<p>It was very late. The Tollers had drifted back to their instruments, or to sleep on pallets in the upper floors. Perpetua was dozing in her chair with her hand on the column and Steward Harrow asleep in her lap. Pim was asleep on the floor under the workbench, curled round nothing. The lamps had burned low. Ione sat cross-legged on the floor with her back against a shelf of ledgers, turning the key over and over in her fingers, and Cas sat in his chair with the Log on his knees because he could not bring himself to put it down.</p>\n<p>“I’ve tried,” said Ione. “Twice. After I came back. I went up again at nineteen and again at twenty-three. By the south spindles, both times. Not by the Carousel. I wasn’t⁠—” A pause, very slight. “I wasn’t going back through the Carousel.” She turned the key. “There’s a place, above the Lees, before the Rim, where the Lace comes down. It’s lower every year. You have to go through it to get to the top of Spindle One. And the Lace kills you.”</p>\n<p>“How?”</p>\n<p>“The way it killed your friend. The way it killed my mother, in the Drop.” She said it without any particular weight. “Everything goes different speeds. You come apart. I watched a rat go into it once, on a rope. It went in a rat and came out⁠—” she made a small gesture “⁠—dust, and a bit of fur, and something still moving. I turned back both times. You can’t get through the Lace without something to hold you together.” She looked at the grey suit standing in the corner where Pim had helped him prop it, the keel humming quietly on its back. “I’ve been watching the wells for eleven years.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“Because once, up there, when I was seventeen, I saw someone coming down.” She looked at him, and her gaze was very direct. “Far off. In a suit like that. With a thing on its back, humming. Coming down alone.” She tipped her head at the corner. “That suit. Or one like it. So I knew one would come. Sooner or later. Up out of the floor.” She almost smiled. “You took your time.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what to say to that. He found that he was thinking — in the part of his mind that never stopped doing sums — <em>someone in a keel suit coming down alone. From the Crown. In the future, seen from the past.</em> And under that, a cold slow thought he didn’t want to look at: <em>there are only three suits</em>.</p>\n<p>“You’re the first thing to come up through the floor in my lifetime,” said Ione, “that could get through the Lace.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ve got the key.”</p>\n<p>“And I’ve got the key.”</p>\n<p>“And you know the way.”</p>\n<p>“I know the way,” said Ione, “as far as the Lace. And I know the Crown. Every inch of it. I know which panel sticks and which lamp was flickering and where Ruth put her coffee cup down. And I know the lock.” She held up the key between two fingers. “You know what the lock does. You know the paper. You know what it <em>means</em>. I’ve watched it for three years and I don’t know what it means.”</p>\n<p>“Neither do I. Not all of it.”</p>\n<p>“More than me.”</p>\n<p>“More than you.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. He looked at her. Neither of them said anything.</p>\n<p>“Spindle Four,” she said at last. “It’s dead. Nobody uses it, nobody watches it. The Stewards think it’s cursed because the lift fell in the Founders’ time. It goes all the way up. I’ll need to go up it first, on my own, and look at the Lees. They move. They’ve moved since I was up there. Everything’s moved. And you⁠—” She looked at him critically. “You need to be able to stand up.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“For the Lees? Ten rounds. Twenty. For you to stand properly⁠—” She considered. “More.”</p>\n<p>“We don’t have more.”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione Tally. “We don’t.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She took him up to the top of the tower before dawn.</p>\n<p>It took a long time. He had to stop on every landing. She waited on every landing, not helping unless he asked, which he didn’t, and then did, and she put her shoulder under his arm without comment and took most of his weight up the last three flights as though he were a bag of rope.</p>\n<p>At the top was the platform with the long tube on its swivel. A sighting tube, she said. Seren had built it. It was pointed up at the sky at a steep angle, sighted on the top of Spindle Two, and there were marks scratched on the brass ring at its base — dozens of them, hundreds, each with a small number beside it.</p>\n<p>“Look,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He bent to the eyepiece. Through the tube he saw the top of Spindle Two, very far off, a dark lattice against the glare, and the Rim beyond it, a dark line. And coming down out of the sky above it, the long bright trailing threads of the Lace, like the tendrils of some vast drifting creature. There were crosshairs in the tube — two fine wires. The lowest tendril hung just below the crossing.</p>\n<p>“Every round, at dawn, I sight the lowest thread,” said Ione, “and mark it.”</p>\n<p>She showed him yesterday’s mark on the brass ring, and the day before’s, and the one before. He could see the pattern without her explaining. The marks crept down the ring. Close together, years ago. Further apart. Now each one was a clear finger’s width below the last.</p>\n<p>Behind them, down in the city, the horn blew from the Steward’s House for first shift. And all across Holdfast, in a great clattering rolling wave that started at the centre and spread outward to the edges, the shutters came up, and the violet light poured into ten thousand windows, and the city woke.</p>\n<p>Ione took a nail from her pocket and scratched a new mark on the brass ring. A finger’s width below yesterday’s.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said. “Now you know how long.”</p>"}, {"file": "09-greenbread.md", "label": "Chapter 9", "title": "Greenbread", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4666, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine. And if the one of us falls, the other one hauls.</p><span class=\"attr\">Holdfast riggers’ wedding vow</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">He learned to stand the way the babies did: badly, and in public, and with a great many people watching.</p>\n<p>Osk taught him. It was not, Cas gathered, a thing Osk had been told to do. It was simply a thing Osk had decided needed doing, on the first morning, when he opened the door of the little stone room and found the Relief of Holdfast on his hands and knees on the floor, trying to get up, and failing, and trying again.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Osk. “Not like that. That’s how you break a knee.”</p>\n<p>He came in and knelt down beside him. He was so big that kneeling, he was nearly Cas’s height standing.</p>\n<p>“Babies,” he said, “in the Crèche. First thing we teach them. You don’t push up. The weight wins if you push. You <em>roll</em>.” He demonstrated, a huge slow shifting of hips and shoulders. “Get your bones under you. Stack them. Like scaffold. Then the weight goes down through the bones into the floor and the floor holds it, not you. You’re not holding it. You’re just <em>stacked</em>.” He looked at Cas. “Try.”</p>\n<p>Cas tried. It took him four attempts. On the fifth, something shifted in his hips and he found his feet under him and came up — not pushing, somehow, just unfolding upward like a ladder being raised — and he was standing, and it didn’t hurt, or not as much, and he was so surprised that he laughed.</p>\n<p>Osk nodded, grave as a judge.</p>\n<p>“Good,” he said. “Now do it a hundred times.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He did it a hundred times. He did it a hundred times a round, for sixteen rounds, and in between he was the Relief.</p>\n<p>That was the other thing he learned. How to be it.</p>\n<p>It was a job. Crane made it clear, gently, from the first morning, that it was a job and that it had duties, and that the duties were public. The Relief was carried through the lanes on the litter, on high rounds, so that people could see him. The Relief sat in a chair on the steps of the Steward’s House for an hour each first shift, and people came up the steps one by one and knelt and touched his hand, or his coat, or just looked at him, and went away weeping. The Relief blessed things. Nobody had told him he was going to bless things. A woman came up the steps with a new baby in a sling and held it out to him and said <em>bless her, Relief</em>, and he looked at Crane standing beside the chair and Crane looked back with no expression at all, and so he put his hand on the baby’s head — so small, so warm, so heavy for its size — and said <em>bless you</em>, and the woman burst into tears, and after that there was no stopping it. He blessed babies. He blessed a new rope for a rigger crew. He blessed the Tanks, standing on a gallery over the great algae vats where the dead of Holdfast went to feed the living, looking down at the slow green water, saying <em>bless</em>, <em>bless</em>, and thinking of Ben.</p>\n<p>He did not say it would be all right again. He never said it again. But he didn’t have to. He had said it once, and they had heard it, and it was in every face that looked up at him from the foot of the Steward’s steps.</p>\n<p>Crane watched him do all of it with that tired, courteous half-smile, and at the end of each round thanked him, and never once asked him how he felt.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At night he went to the Tollers.</p>\n<p>Osk slept outside his door every night on the stool, and every night, when Cas went past him in the shuttered dusk, Osk slept a little more deeply, and never once woke. On the fourth night, Cas found that someone had oiled the hinge of his door so that it didn’t squeak.</p>\n<p>Pim brought the cart the first few nights. After that, Cas walked. It took him most of a shift to get across the city the first time on his own feet, stopping every twenty steps with his hand on a wall. By the tenth night it took him an hour. By the fourteenth, he could do it without stopping, if he didn’t mind that his heart was going like a hammer when he got there, and his knees shook, and he had to sit on the step of the tower for a while before he could climb it.</p>\n<p>They didn’t only work at the tower. More often, as the rounds went on, he found himself in Ione’s loft.</p>\n<p>She lived at the top of a scaffold tower in the Drop District, not far from the well he’d come up — a rickety, swaying, rope-lashed stack of platforms twenty storeys high, where the riggers lived who worked the spindles, and who were, Pim told him with great satisfaction, the roughest people in Holdfast. Her loft was the very top platform, walled with canvas and scrap and open on one side to the sky, so that from the hammock that hung across it you could lie and look straight up past the black mass of Spindle Four into the Lamp. It was full of rope. Rope hung from every beam in coils, in hanks, spliced and whipped and knotted, a hundred kinds. There were harnesses on hooks, and chalk in jars, and plumb bobs of every size hanging on strings, and a workbench, and a little stove, and a shelf of books — station manuals, mostly, their covers long gone, and a few Holdfast things: hand-copied, hand-bound, the pages made of pale algae-paper.</p>\n<p>They worked there. That was what they told themselves they were doing, anyway. There was a great deal to do. The suit’s harness had to be re-rigged for climbing; its hardpoints weren’t built for rope, and she spent three nights splicing a web of rigger’s strapping round it that would let her belay it — him — from above. There were supplies to make: greenbread baked hard as brick, water in skins of rendered algae-leather, cakes of cricket fat. There were the Lees.</p>\n<p>The Lees were the heart of it. She explained them to him on the fifth night, lying on her back on the loft floor with a plumb bob hanging on a string from her fingers, watching it.</p>\n<p>“Look,” she said. “Down here, the Lamp pushes down and the Anvil pulls down and it all goes the same way. Straight. The bob hangs straight.” It did. “But go up a spindle. The further up you go, the harder the Lamp pushes. You weigh more and more. By the time you’re a kilometre up you weigh twice what you weigh here. Two kilometres, three times. By the Rim⁠—” she shrugged “⁠—nobody knows. Nobody’s been to the Rim and come back except me, and I wasn’t counting.”</p>\n<p>“So how did you get there?”</p>\n<p>“The Lees.” She moved the bob. “The spindles are full of keelstone. Struts, braces, cross-members, great thick beams of it. And keelstone pulls, you know that. Like the Anvil pulls. So near a big strut, the strut’s pull goes against the Lamp’s push. They cancel. Not all the way. But some. So there are places up there — pockets — where you weigh less. Sometimes much less. You can rest in them. You can climb from one to the next.” She let the bob swing. “That’s a Lee. The riggers have always known them. The lower ones. I know the high ones.”</p>\n<p>“And they move.”</p>\n<p>“The Lamp’s getting weaker. You know that. The Lightening.” She held the string still. “So the balance moves. Every year, the Lees move up. Or sideways. Or close up and open somewhere else. I haven’t been past the lower ones in five years.” She looked at him. “I’ll have to go and look.”</p>\n<p>She went on the eighth round.</p>\n<p>She went in the night, alone, with two coils of rope and a bag of chalk and a skin of water, and he stood at the foot of Spindle Four in the shuttered dusk and watched her climb until she was a speck and then nothing. She’d said two rounds. She was gone three. He spent those three rounds being the Relief, blessing babies on the steps of the Steward’s House and not hearing a word anyone said to him, and at night lying on the rope bed in the stone room staring at the ceiling, until on the third night Pim’s pebble hit his shutter and he went down to the lane and found her sitting on the Tollers’ step with her hands bandaged in strips of cloth and a rope burn across one cheek, eating greenbread.</p>\n<p>“There’s a way,” she said. “It’s not a good way. But there’s a way.”</p>\n<p>He sat down beside her on the step. His knees shook. He didn’t know if it was the climb down the lane.</p>\n<p>“You said two rounds,” he said.</p>\n<p>She looked at him sideways. And for once there was something in her face that wasn’t patient at all. It was surprised.</p>\n<p>“You were worried,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She went on looking at him for a while. Then she looked away, down the lane, and ate her greenbread.</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s been worried about me,” she said, “in about eleven years.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He ate rat on the eleventh round.</p>\n<p>It was at the market in the Commons Lanes, where the stalls were built into the shells of old cargo containers with their doors swung open, and everything that Holdfast grew or made or salvaged was sold for tokens stamped out of old sheet metal. Ione had taken him there — in the middle of a round, openly, with a hood up over his face because by now there was not a soul in Holdfast who wouldn’t have known him. Greenbread, stacked in dark bricks. Cricket flour in sacks, and the crickets themselves in wire boxes, chirping. Mushrooms of a dozen pale kinds, grown on bales of old insulation in the dark under the Anvil’s edge. Algae in every form: cakes, noodles, a thick green paste in jars, a fermented green wine that Ione said would take the skin off your throat. Salt. Oil. Thread twisted from algae fibre. Needles filed from wire.</p>\n<p>And at one stall, turning on a spit over a little charcoal fire, rats.</p>\n<p>“Go on,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been eating it for ten rounds,” she said. “It’s in the soup. The Tollers’ soup. You had two bowls.”</p>\n<p>“I⁠—” He stopped. “Did I?”</p>\n<p>“Two bowls. You said it was very good.” Her mouth twitched. “Go on.”</p>\n<p>It was very good. It tasted, as everyone who has ever eaten rat has said, like chicken — like very rich, slightly sweet, dark-meat chicken, with a smoky crackle to the skin — and he ate a whole one standing in the lane with the juice running down his wrist, and Ione watched him do it with an expression he’d never seen on her face before: a kind of wicked delight, quickly hidden.</p>\n<p>Pim, who had appeared out of nowhere as he always did, was scandalised.</p>\n<p>“Not <em>mine</em>,” he said. “Not Steward Harrow. Not Steward Crane. Not <em>any</em> of the Stewards. Only the <em>market</em> rats.”</p>\n<p>“Pim names his after the Stewardship,” Ione explained gravely. “All of them. He has a First Steward. He has a Warden-Captain. He has three Toller-Seconds.”</p>\n<p>“I have a Relief,” said Pim. “Now.” He produced it from inside his shirt: a small, fierce, grey rat with a torn ear, which bit him. “He’s new. He’s not very nice yet.”</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“He came up out of a drain,” said Pim, “so.”</p>\n<p>Ione laughed. It was the first time Cas had heard her laugh — really laugh, out loud — and it surprised him so much that he just stood there in the lane with the rat bones in his hand and listened to it. It was a short, low, rough laugh, as if it didn’t get used much and had got stiff, and it stopped almost as soon as it started, and she looked away quickly, as though she’d been caught at something.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw a wedding on the thirteenth round.</p>\n<p>Riggers. Two of them, a man and a woman, both short and broad and scarred about the forearms like Ione, married on the rooftop of the riggers’ tower in the Drop District under the open sky. The whole tower came up — forty or fifty people, crammed onto the platforms and the ladders and the ropes — and a Steward in a black coat stood between the two of them and said words, and then the riggers said their own. They tied their wrists together with a length of rope, the woman’s left to the man’s right, in a knot Cas didn’t know, and they said it together:</p>\n<p>“I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine. And if the one of us falls, the other one hauls.”</p>\n<p>And everyone on the platforms and the ladders shouted <em>haul!</em> and stamped until the whole tower swayed.</p>\n<p>Afterwards there was green wine, and it did take the skin off his throat, and there was music: a drum made of an old coolant drum, and a sort of fiddle strung with wire, and someone playing a pipe cut from conduit. And they danced — the Holdfast dance, which was slow and heavy and stamping, all bent knees and planted feet, a dance made for people who weighed half as much again as they should, and which shook the tower to its lashings. Cas sat on a coil of rope with his back against a beam and watched it. Ione sat beside him.</p>\n<p>“You don’t dance?” he said.</p>\n<p>“I dance.” She drank. “Not at weddings. It makes people nervous. The Unwatched at your wedding. It’s like a crow at a christening.”</p>\n<p>“What’s a christening?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. “It’s in one of the old books. I don’t know. I assumed you’d know.”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know either.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him a moment longer, and then something in her face gave, and she laughed again — shorter this time, into her cup.</p>\n<p>“The knot,” he said. “On their wrists. What is it?”</p>\n<p>She put her cup down. She took his left hand — without asking, the way she’d checked his seals at the well, practically — and took a length of thin cord from her pocket and, very quickly, tied it round his wrist. Her fingers moved too fast for him to follow. Over, under, round, through. When she took her hands away there was a knot on his wrist: flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped.</p>\n<p>“Riggers’ hitch,” she said. “Holds under load. Won’t slip. Won’t jam. Comes off in a second if you know where to pull and never comes off if you don’t.”</p>\n<p>He looked at it.</p>\n<p>“That’s the only one you’ll need,” said Ione, and took it off again with one tug, and put the cord back in her pocket.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw a funeral on the fourteenth. An old man, a Toller, one of Perpetua’s cousins. They carried him through the lanes on a board, wrapped in a white sheet with the lamp painted on it, and the Tollers walked behind, and at every Bell the procession stopped and everyone stood still and silent until it passed. At the Tanks they laid him on a sort of stone slope running down into the green water, and a Steward said: “He’s clocked off. His shift is done. We thank him for his watch.” And they tipped the board, and he slid down into the vat, and the green closed over him, and everyone said <em>clocked off, clocked off</em>, softly, like a breath going out.</p>\n<p>“Clocked off,” Cas said afterwards to Ione, walking back. “That’s — the station used to say that. When you went off shift.”</p>\n<p>“We know,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He saw children playing a game in a lane. They stood in a line with their backs to a wall, and one of them stood in front of them, facing them, and shut her eyes and fell straight backwards, stiff as a plank. The others caught her. Every time. They shrieked with laughter. Then the next one.</p>\n<p>“What’s it called?” he asked Pim.</p>\n<p>“Drop,” said Pim, as if it were obvious. “It’s called Drop.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the fifteenth round, a little after the start of second shift, his suit began to groan.</p>\n<p>He was in his room in the Steward’s House, lying on the rope bed. The suit stood in the corner where it always stood, humming its keel-note to itself, and without any warning it made a sound. A low sound. A long, slow, deep sound like a foghorn heard across miles of water, or like a whale — <em>Ben said whales, so I get birds</em> — it came out of the suit’s helmet speaker and filled the little room, vast and slow and falling, and it went on, and on, and didn’t stop.</p>\n<p>He sat up. He knew what it was. He’d known what it was the instant it started.</p>\n<p>It went on for six hours.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor of the stone room and listened to it for all six, with his back against the bed and his knees up, and he didn’t go to the Tollers that night, and when Osk opened the door once in the middle of it to see what in the name of the Lamp the noise was, Cas just held up a hand, and Osk looked at the suit, and at him, and went out again and shut the door without a sound.</p>\n<p>It was a voice. He could hear, if he listened, that it was a voice — the rise and fall of it, the rhythm of speech slowed so far down that it was just a shape — and he sat there and let it pour over him, a single human sentence stretched over half a shift, and tried to guess the words, and couldn’t.</p>\n<p>At the end of it the suit went <em>ding</em>.</p>\n<p>And then, suddenly, perfectly clearly, as though she were in the room:</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk. “Right. Your first chirp’s come through. You want to know what it sounds like this end? It sounds like a budgie fell into my radio. About four minutes after you went up that ladder. Then the suit sat there chewing on it for a minute and a half and went <em>ding</em>, and then it was you. Talking about rats.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand over his face.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been gone four minutes,” said Teo, “and apparently you’ve found a city, and they’ve made you a prophet, and they’ve got the Section 9 placard in a church. I leave you alone for <em>four minutes</em>, Hale.” A pause. He heard her breathe. “Thirty-one thousand. Christ. Thirty-one thousand people. Up that ladder. I keep looking at the ladder. I can see it from the chair, through the window. It just looks like a ladder.”</p>\n<p>A longer pause.</p>\n<p>“You lied to them. Okay. You told me that like it’s the end of the world. It isn’t. You’re not the first man who ever stood up in front of a crowd and told them it’d be all right because it was easier than breaking their hearts. I’ve done it. I’ve done it on the top of a flood barrier with the water coming up, and I’d do it again, and so would you.” Another pause. “But you know what I’m going to say. And you know I’m right. So I won’t say it.”</p>\n<p>He laughed. He couldn’t help it. It came out wet.</p>\n<p>“Also,” said Teo, “I’m fine. You’ll want to know that. The chair’s fine. The lee’s fine. It’s not moving. I found a biscuit in the first-aid kit. It’s a bit old. Nobody’s called. The Collar’ll be wondering where we are. Let them wonder.” A small snort. “You’re making excellent progress, I suppose. Nobody’s ever made a prophet that fast in Civil Defence.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“I put the blanket from the first-aid kit over her,” said Teo, more quietly. “The lady at the bottom of the ladder. With the little lamp. I couldn’t stand her lying there. I went down through the hatch and put it over her. I hope that’s all right. Whoever she was.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t breathe for a moment.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “That’s four minutes of me you’ve had. That’ll have taken you — I don’t know. A day? Don’t answer that, I can’t work it out, Farr was the one for sums.” And her voice caught, just slightly, on the name, and went on. “Send me more. Send me all of it. I’ve got nothing to do in this chair. Tell your prophets there’s a woman down here says hello.” A breath. “And look after yourself, you daft sod.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then he got up — rolled, stacked his bones, stood, the way Osk had taught him — and took out the recorder, and pressed the button, and told Teo about the rats, and the market, and Osk, and the riggers’ wedding, and the funeral, and the game called Drop. And he told her that the lady at the bottom of the ladder was called Seren, and that she’d been the cleverest Toller in two hundred years, and that she’d painted the truth on the wall of the Commons, and that her mother had carved the little lamp, and that it had been for her crib. And he told her <em>thank you for the blanket</em>, and found that he couldn’t say anything else for a while, and just sat there with the button pressed, not saying anything, letting it record the silence.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the second channel, later, he said:</p>\n<p>“Nell. I’m going to tell you about a day. Just an ordinary day. Nobody’s ever — I don’t think I’ve ever told anyone about an ordinary day. I don’t think I had any, for a long time. I think they were all the same day, and I didn’t want to tell anyone about it.</p>\n<p>“This morning I stood up by myself. There’s a man here called Osk who’s teaching me. He’s the biggest man I’ve ever seen and he talks like gravel. He says I’m nearly as good as a baby now.</p>\n<p>“Then I sat on some steps and people came and touched my hand. They think I’m — it doesn’t matter what they think I am. I’m not it. I let them think it. I’m not proud of that.</p>\n<p>“Then I ate a rat. It was delicious. Don’t tell your mother.</p>\n<p>“Then I went to a place where a very old lady counts time. She’s funny, Nell. She’d make you laugh. She says rude things about everyone and then gives them soup. She had a daughter who was very brave. She lost her.</p>\n<p>“And then I went to see my friend. She lives at the top of a tower full of rope, where you can lie in a hammock and look straight up at the Lamp — the star, the one I told you about. She’s never seen a real one. A real star. I tried to tell her what they’re like. I told her about the ones on your ceiling. Betelgeuse. Deneb. I told her they’re far away and they don’t move and there are thousands of them. She asked me what colour they are. I said white, mostly. Some are blue. Some are a bit red. She asked me which one was my favourite and I didn’t know. I’ve never looked. I’ve never once looked, Nell. You’d have known in a second.”</p>\n<p>He was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“I think,” he said, “that if you ever meet her, you’ll like her. I don’t know why I think that. You’ll never meet her. But I think you would.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the sixteenth round, the Lace touched Spindle Six.</p>\n<p>He didn’t see it happen. He was on the steps of the Steward’s House, blessing a rope. But he heard it — everyone in Holdfast heard it — a sound from the sky like a great sheet of glass being drawn across stone, high and thin and long, and then a flash, very brief, very bright, from the top of Spindle Six on the far side of the city, and then silence. People stopped in the lanes. Everyone looked up. On the steps, the rigger who’d brought the rope to be blessed dropped it.</p>\n<p>Six was the spindle nearest the Lace’s lowest tendril. There had been a rigger crew working it that round — they went up every round, to tend the old lamp-lines and the water-catchers the riggers had strung on the spindle’s upper lattice, a kilometre and a half up. Six of them. Ione knew them all. So did the rigger on the steps.</p>\n<p>At the end of the round the Stewardship put up a notice in the Commons, beneath the Public Tally, written in beautiful careful letters on a board:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MARROW TELL AND HIS CREW<br>HAVE BEEN TAKEN INTO THE LAMP’S CROWN<br>A BLESSING ON THE WATCH<br>THE QUICKENING IS GLORY</div>\n<p>Ione went up after them the next round, with two others from the riggers’ tower. She came back at the end of it, alone, and said nothing, and went up to her loft, and when Cas climbed the twenty storeys that night — it took him an hour; he did it — she was sitting in her hammock with her knees up, looking at the Lamp.</p>\n<p>“What did you find?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Rope,” she said. “Six harnesses. Their tools. The water-catchers they were tending, all rusted through to lace. Marrow’s knife. I gave that to his daughter.” She didn’t look at him. “And bones. The kind of bones you’d find in a Founder’s grave, if the Founders had graves. Old and white and dry. And a bit of Tamsin’s hair, still in her clip. She’d have been thirty next round.” She was quiet. “The Lamp’s crown. A blessing on the Watch.”</p>\n<p>He sat down on the loft floor.</p>\n<p>“Crane wants another address,” he said. “On Kindling Round. Three rounds from now. The whole city in the Commons. He says they need to see the Relief.”</p>\n<p>“Of course he does.”</p>\n<p>“He wants me to tell them the Quickening’s glory. That Marrow Tell’s crew are in the Lamp’s crown.” He looked at his hands. “He hasn’t said it like that. But that’s what he wants.”</p>\n<p>Ione said nothing. The hammock creaked, very gently, as she moved.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to tell them,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>She turned her head then and looked at him.</p>\n<p>“All of it,” he said. “The Outside. The test. Aldous Crane. The debt. How long. The lock and the key and you. All of it. In the Commons. On the Bell.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those unhurried grey-green eyes. And for the first time since the top of the well he couldn’t tell — he really couldn’t — whether she had known he was going to say it.</p>\n<p>“They’ll put you in the Quiet,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Probably.”</p>\n<p>“Do you know what the Quiet is?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“It’s a coolant tank. Under the Steward’s House. They seal the lid. No light. No sound but the Bell.” She said it very evenly. “My mother was in it for eleven rounds before they gave her to the Drop.”</p>\n<p>“I’m still going to tell them.”</p>\n<p>Ione Tally looked at him a moment longer. Then she lay back in her hammock and looked up at the Lamp, and something happened at the corner of her mouth — so small he almost didn’t see it.</p>\n<p>“Good,” she said. “I know how to get people out of the Quiet.”</p>"}, {"file": "10-the-second-address.md", "label": "Chapter 10", "title": "The Second Address", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4679, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Tell them the Drop isn’t open. Say it twice. They’ll only hear it once.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They spent the three rounds before the Bell getting it right.</p>\n<p>Not the speech. He didn’t write a speech. He tried, the first night, in the loft, and Ione watched him cross out three pages and then took the pencil out of his hand and said, “Don’t. You’ll make it pretty. Say it the way you said it to me.” So he didn’t write it. But they planned everything around it.</p>\n<p>The Tollers would be on the roof of the Commons. The Commons roof was open in long strips between the girders, and there was a ridge of old gantry running along the top of it that the riggers used for stringing lamp-lines on high days; Tobiah and three others would be up there with a banner. It was already painted. Perpetua had had it painted on the first night, on a sheet of canvas thirty metres long, and it lay folded in the upper floor of Toller’s Tower like a sail: Seren’s line. The Tollers’ curve, drawn huge, in white on black, with a hundred and sixty-eight years of Bells falling away along it and the line carried forward past the end of the count to the place where it met the bottom of the canvas. And beside that place, in letters two metres high, the number Seren had worked out thirteen years ago and the Tollers had been refining every round since.</p>\n<p>“When they see it,” said Perpetua, “they’ll know it’s true. Nobody in Holdfast would believe a word from the Outside. They’ll believe a Tally. They’ve been counting their whole lives.”</p>\n<p>The riggers would be at the doors. Ione had asked them. Not all of them — the riggers were as split as anyone, and some of them went to the Commons on the seventh round and knelt at Muster Point B — but a few dozen, the ones who’d known Marrow Tell and his crew, the ones who’d climbed Six the round after and seen the bones. They would be at the doors and on the ladders, with rope. Nobody had said what the rope was for.</p>\n<p>And Perpetua told him, on the last night, sitting in her chair with her hand on the column:</p>\n<p>“Tell them the Drop isn’t open.”</p>\n<p>“I will.”</p>\n<p>“Say it twice. They’ll only hear it once.” She looked at him with her black bird’s eyes. “You’re going to tell twenty thousand people that the Outside’s alive and that a man came up out of the floor from it. Some of them are going to hear that and nothing else. You understand? Nothing else. You could talk for a shift. They’ll hear <em>the Outside is alive, and he came up the Drop</em>.” She took her hand off the column and laid it on his knee, the way she had the first night. “Say it twice, Relief. Say it three times. It won’t be enough. But say it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Kindling Round fell three rounds later — the first round of the year One Hundred and Sixty-Nine, a hundred and sixty-eight years to the round since the Lamp was lit — and the whole of Holdfast had been getting ready for it for a week.</p>\n<p>There had never been a Kindling Round like it. That was what everyone said. Every year of the Watch, on this round, Holdfast remembered the night the Founders stood under the Placard and counted the first Bell. And this year — in a year when the Bells came more than a hundred a round, when you could feel them while you ate and while you slept — <em>the Relief had come</em>. It was a sign. It was the greatest festival in the history of the Watch. The lanes had been hung with lamp-strings for three rounds: little clay lamps strung on wire from wall to wall, hundreds of them, so that even in the day the lanes glittered. Every shrine had been repainted. Children had been making paper lamps. The Public Tally board in the Commons had been hung with a garland of green algae-cloth and the numbers repainted in gold.</p>\n<p>The Commons was fuller than it had been the night he arrived. He hadn’t thought that was possible. They packed the floor to the walls. They filled the girders — people had climbed up into the roof trusses and sat along them in rows, their legs dangling, like birds on a wire. They stood outside in the lanes so thick that a Warden trying to get through had to be passed over their heads. The noise was the sea. When the Wardens carried Cas in on the litter — he’d asked to walk; Crane had said, kindly, <em>no</em> — the sea rose up into something like a storm, and it was a long time before it went down.</p>\n<p>He stood on the platform. Osk was at his elbow. Crane beside him, in his black coat, with a new lamp embroidered on its breast in gold thread for the occasion. The Public Tally board behind them: BELL 121,636.</p>\n<p>He looked for Ione. He couldn’t see her.</p>\n<p>He looked up. Along the ridge of the gantry, high up in the open roof, in the violet glare of the Lace, there were four small figures in grey smocks, sitting in a row with something long and folded across their knees.</p>\n<p>Crane led the singing. The Tending, slow and worn. Twenty thousand voices, and more outside, the whole city singing, under the Lace. <em>Tend the Lamp, tend the Lamp, till the Relief comes after.</em> Cas listened to every word of it. He made himself. He stood there on the platform and let it wash over him and thought about a children’s choir on the Tier Nine lampposts at dawn, and about what those children had been singing about, and what these people were.</p>\n<p>Then the call and response. <em>Who keeps the Watch?</em> The Bell came during it — 121,637 — rising up through the floor, and the whole Commons fell still and silent and counted it, and then went on. <em>Until Relief.</em></p>\n<p>Then Crane turned to him, and smiled, and stepped back.</p>\n<p>BELL 121,638.</p>\n<p>He had about twenty-four minutes.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“I lied to you,” said Casimir Hale.</p>\n<p>He didn’t raise his voice. He’d learned, the first night, that he didn’t have to. The Commons was so silent when he spoke that the smallest sound carried to the back.</p>\n<p>“The first night I came. I stood here and I told you it was going to be all right. I don’t know that. I didn’t know it then. I said it because I looked at your faces and I didn’t want to be the one who took it away.” He could see the front row. There was a little girl there — not the same one, a different one. He looked at her. He made himself go on looking at her. “That’s what people do where I come from. They say what’s easy. They’ve been doing it for ninety years. It’s why you’re here.”</p>\n<p>A murmur. Very faint. Like wind starting.</p>\n<p>“So I’m going to tell you the truth now. All of it. It’s going to be hard to hear, and I’m sorry. And some of it you’re not going to believe, and that’s all right. But I need you to listen to all of it. Not just the start. All of it. Please.”</p>\n<p>He took a breath.</p>\n<p>“The Outside is alive.”</p>\n<p>The murmur became a sound.</p>\n<p>“It isn’t burned. It never was. It’s a city. A big one, bigger than you can imagine. It’s called Averna. It has six hundred million people in it and all of them are alive right now, today, this minute. And it’s the same night there. It’s still the same night that the Lamp was kindled.” He waited for the sound to go down. It didn’t, quite. “You know that. Some of you know that. The Tollers know it. They’ve always known. Every Bell you’ve ever counted is one second of the Outside’s time. A hundred and twenty-one thousand Bells is thirty-three hours and a bit. That’s all it’s been, out there. A day and a half. The Outside isn’t gone. It’s slow.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Lies!</em>” Somebody, at the back. A man’s voice, high and cracked. Then another voice, shouting him down. Then others. The sound rose.</p>\n<p>“There’s a little girl in the Outside,” said Cas, into it, “called Maddy Tally. She’s seven. She went to sleep on the night of the Kindling in her grandmother’s flat, and her mother went to work at the station and didn’t come home. Her mother was Ruth Tally. Your Founder. The one who counted the first Bell.” The sound faltered. “Ruth wrote her letters for fifty years. She never stopped. She died in here, an old woman, with eleven grandchildren. And Maddy’s still seven. Right now, while I’m talking. She’s having her breakfast.”</p>\n<p>It went quiet then. Really quiet. Not the silence of listening; something else. A silence like the moment after a glass is dropped, before it hits the floor.</p>\n<p>“The Kindling,” said Cas, “was an accident.”</p>\n<p>He felt Crane move beside him. He didn’t look round.</p>\n<p>“The station — this place, the Draw — makes power. It sends it Outside. The night of the Kindling, there was a test. The people who ran the Outside wanted more power, and they wanted to film the station making it, for a festival. The engineers said it wasn’t safe. The man in charge made them do it anyway. His name was Aldous Crane.”</p>\n<p>The sound the Commons made then was something he’d never heard a crowd make. A kind of groan. A kind of gasp. Twenty thousand people drawing in breath together.</p>\n<p>“He was your Founder. He was a — he wasn’t a bad man. He was a frightened one. Ruth Tally tried to shut the Lamp off, that night, in the Crown. There was a lock, and there was a key, and he had the key round his neck, and she asked him for it, and he wouldn’t give it to her. And then there was an accident, and the key fell, and it was lost.” He made himself say it. “He didn’t give the key to the Lamp. He dropped it.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Heresy!</em>” A woman’s voice this time, very near the front, shrill with horror. “Heresy, it’s — <em>Steward!</em> Steward, stop him⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Let him finish.”</p>\n<p>It was Crane.</p>\n<p>Cas turned his head. The old man was standing quite still beside him, very straight in his black coat with its new gold lamp, and his face was the colour of the paper in the Log, and his long hands were clasped very tightly in front of him. He wasn’t looking at Cas. He was looking out at the Commons.</p>\n<p>“Let him finish,” said Halloran Crane again, and his voice carried to the back of the hall.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He told them the rest.</p>\n<p>He told them what the Lamp was. He did it the way he’d done it for Crane — a debt, a loan from the emptiness, and the emptiness always wants it back — and he watched their faces, and he saw some of them understand, and some of them not, and some of them refuse to. He told them what the Bells meant. That the Quickening was the debt coming due. That it was going to come faster and faster. That the Lace was part of it, and it was coming down, and that when it reached the Rim at the top of the spindles, it would all be over. Not just Holdfast. Everything.</p>\n<p>“Marrow Tell and his crew,” he said, “weren’t taken into the Lamp’s crown. They weren’t blessed. They died. The Lace touched the top of Six and they grew old and died in the time it takes to draw a breath. The riggers who went up after them found their bones.” He looked out at the hall. “The Quickening isn’t glory. It’s a clock. And it’s nearly run down.”</p>\n<p>He heard someone crying. He heard someone praying, in a low fast mutter, the words of the Placard. <em>Remain at your station. Await relief.</em></p>\n<p>BELL 121,639 came while he was talking. He stopped, and waited, and everyone waited, and it rose and hung and fell, and when it had gone he said:</p>\n<p>“There’s one chance.”</p>\n<p>He looked for her again. And this time he found her. She was at the back, where she’d been the first night, by the great doors, in the gap in the crowd, with her arms folded and her back against the wall. The riggers were around her. She was looking at him. She wasn’t patient now. She was very still, and her eyes were very bright.</p>\n<p>“There’s a way to close the Lamp,” he said. “The woman who built this station put it there. It’s in the Crown, at the top of Spindle One. It’s a lock. It’s been there all along. It needs a key.” He lifted his hand and pointed, over twenty thousand heads, at the back of the hall. “And the key is here. Ione Tally has it.”</p>\n<p>Every head in the Commons turned.</p>\n<p>“She found it, eleven years ago, up there. The key your Founder dropped. She came down with it and told you the Lamp could be closed. And you called her mad. And her mother, Seren Tally, drew the line of the Bells and told you how long you had left, and you gave her to the Drop.” He heard his voice shaking and let it. “They were right. Both of them. They were right all along.”</p>\n<p>And up on the roof, on the gantry, in the glare of the Lace, the Tollers stood up and let the banner fall.</p>\n<p>It came down out of the open roof between the girders like a great black sail unfurling, thirty metres of it, and snapped taut, and hung there over the whole of the Commons. The curve. White on black. A hundred and sixty-eight years of Bells, falling away in a long smooth swoop, and the line going on past the end of the count, down, down, to the bottom edge of the canvas. And beside it, in white letters two metres tall, a single number, and a single word.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">ROUND 48<br>THE LACE MEETS THE RIM</div>\n<p>It was the first round of the year.</p>\n<p>He heard the Commons read it. He heard twenty thousand people do the sum.</p>\n<p>“We’re going to go up,” he said, very loudly now, over the sound that was starting. “Ione Tally and me. To the Crown. To try. And if it works⁠—” he had to shout “⁠—<em>listen to me</em>. If it works, the Lamp will go out.”</p>\n<p>The sound stopped, a little. Enough.</p>\n<p>“The Lamp will go out, and it will be dark. Really dark. Darker than you’ve ever seen. And you mustn’t be afraid of it.” He was looking at the little girl in the front row. She was holding her mother’s skirt in both fists. “When the Lamp goes out, it means we did it. It means the debt’s closed. It means the Shear’s gone and time in here is the same as time out there. And it means the Drop will be a road. You’ll be able to walk down it. All of you. Out. Into the Outside. Into the same night.”</p>\n<p>He held on to Osk’s arm. He was very tired suddenly.</p>\n<p>“But not before,” he said. “Listen. <em>Not before</em>. The Drop is not open now. Nobody goes near the wells. Nobody. Not until the Lamp goes out. If you go down now, the Shear will kill you, the way it killed everyone who ever tried. The way it killed Seren. I came up through it in a suit made for it and it nearly killed me. The Drop is not open. Do you hear me? The Drop is not open.”</p>\n<p><em>Say it twice.</em></p>\n<p>“The Drop is <em>not open</em>.”</p>\n<p>And then, into the silence, because it was the only thing left and because it was the truest thing he knew:</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s coming. There’s no Relief. I’m not it. There never was one. I’m just what came.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>For a moment, nothing happened.</p>\n<p>Then the Commons came apart.</p>\n<p>He’d never know, afterwards, how it started. He’d go over it again and again, lying in the dark, and never be able to find the first moment. It was as though the hall had been holding a single breath for a hundred and sixty-eight years and had just let it out. Everyone was shouting. Everyone was moving. People were on their feet, on their knees, clinging to each other, pushing, surging toward the platform, surging away from it. Someone was screaming <em>heresy, heresy</em> over and over in a voice like a torn sail. Someone else had climbed up onto the brass rail of Muster Point B and was reading the Placard aloud at the top of his lungs. A knot of men at the side of the hall were fighting — fists, and then staves, the Wardens going in — and a woman near the front had her arms round a stranger and was sobbing into his coat, and the little girl in the front row was crying with her face in her mother’s skirt.</p>\n<p>And there was the sound he’d been afraid of.</p>\n<p>He heard it start at the back, near the doors. Near Ione. A man’s voice, high and wild, carrying over everything the way a single voice sometimes can:</p>\n<p>“<em>The Outside’s alive! He came up the Drop! He came up it — it’s open — we can go down, we can go down⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>“<em>No!</em>”</p>\n<p>That was him. He was shouting. He was at the front of the platform, shouting over twenty thousand people, <em>no, no, it isn’t open, it isn’t⁠—</em>, and nobody could hear him. He could see the words not reaching. He could see it happen at the back of the hall: the knot of people round that one voice, turning, pushing toward the great doors. And the doors opening, and the lanes outside, and the people in the lanes turning to see what was happening, and hearing it — <em>it’s open, he came up it, we can go down</em> — and turning to run.</p>\n<p>He saw Ione. He saw her step out into the doorway in front of them with her arms out, shouting, and the riggers beside her. He saw the crowd hit them like a wave hitting a wall. He saw the riggers go down under it, and Ione go down, and the wave go over them and out into the lanes.</p>\n<p>“<em>Osk.</em>”</p>\n<p>The big Warden was already moving.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Afterwards, what he remembered was pieces.</p>\n<p>The cart. Pim, white-faced, dragging it through a side door, and Osk lifting him into it bodily, and then Osk himself between the shafts, hauling it at a run through the glittering lamp-strung lanes with the cart bouncing and banging and Cas clinging to its sides. People running beside them, past them, all going the same way. Toward the Drop District. Toward the wells.</p>\n<p>The well. The same well, the one he’d come up. The WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT circle painted round its mouth. There were people all round it. A crowd, pressing up the ramp to the platform, pressing against the scrap-metal wall. And on the platform, round the rim of the conduit, a line of them, going over. Going down the ladder. One after another, their hands on the rungs, their faces set and wild and joyful, climbing down into the dark toward the Outside.</p>\n<p>He was out of the cart. He didn’t remember getting out. He was on the platform — Osk had cleared a way, Osk was a wall — and he was at the lip of the well, grabbing at people. Grabbing at coats, at arms. <em>No. No, it isn’t open, please, it isn’t⁠—</em> A man shook him off without even looking at him. A woman slapped his hand away. <em>He came up it</em>, someone said, <em>he came up it, he’s lying, he wants to keep it for himself —</em></p>\n<p>There was a father with a child.</p>\n<p>A young man, stocky and wide like all of them, with a little boy of four or five on his back, the boy’s arms round his neck. He was at the ladder. He had one foot on the first rung.</p>\n<p>Cas got the boy.</p>\n<p>He didn’t decide to. He simply had him. He had the child under the arms and was pulling, and the little boy was screaming, and the father was screaming, twisting round on the ladder, reaching back, <em>give him to me, give him, he’s mine —</em> and Cas held on, and fell backwards onto the platform with the child on top of him, and the father hung there on the ladder with his face working, looking at his son, looking down into the dark.</p>\n<p>“Come back up,” said Cas. He was crying. “Please. Please. Come back up. It isn’t open. I swear to you. I swear it.”</p>\n<p>The father looked at him for a moment with an expression he would never forget. Then he looked at his son. Then he looked down again, into the well, toward the Outside, and his face changed. It filled with something. Hope. Joy. The same joy Cas had seen in the Commons the first night, turned up at him like light.</p>\n<p>“I’ll come back for him,” the father said. “I’ll come back up for him. When I’ve seen it.”</p>\n<p>And he went down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The screaming started about a quarter of an hour later.</p>\n<p>It came up the well. Faint, at first, and very far, from three hundred metres down, where the conduit came out of the bottom of the Anvil into the Shear. It came up the shaft the way the Bell did — stretched, somehow. Slowed. Strange. It didn’t sound like screaming. It sounded like the lowing of cattle. Like the long slow groan of Teo’s voice through the suit. It went on and on, and more voices joined it, one by one, as more of them reached the bottom. It went on for a long time. Nobody who was on that platform ever forgot it.</p>\n<p>The people still on the ladder stopped. They hung there, on the rungs, listening. Then they began to come back up.</p>\n<p>The riggers came. Ione came — with blood on her face from a cut over her eye and her left arm held against her body — and a dozen riggers behind her with rope and knives. They went to every well in the District. There were fourteen. At every one they cut the tops of the ladders free and hauled them up so that nobody else could go down. It took most of a shift. At some of the wells they had to fight to do it.</p>\n<p>The Tollers counted. That was what Tollers did. They went from well to well with their ledgers and asked the people on the platforms who had gone down, and wrote down the names.</p>\n<p>Forty-seven.</p>\n<p>He was sitting on the platform of the well with his back against the scrap-metal wall and the little boy asleep in his lap when Osk came to arrest him.</p>\n<p>He had been sitting there for hours. The boy had cried himself to sleep. Nobody had come for him. Nobody had known who he was. Cas had sat with him, and held him, and listened to the sounds coming up the well until they stopped, and then listened to the silence, and then to the Bell.</p>\n<p>Osk stood over him without saying anything. His hard hat was gone. There was a long scrape down one side of his face.</p>\n<p>“The First Steward’s asked for you,” he said at last.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I’m to bring you.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>Osk looked down at the boy.</p>\n<p>“I’ll take him,” said a voice, and it was Pim — Pim with his face streaked black and grey, Pim who had been everywhere, who had carried water and run messages and dragged the cart — and he knelt down and held out his arms. “I know his gran. On Pump Lane. I’ll take him to her.”</p>\n<p>Cas gave him the boy. The child didn’t wake. Pim staggered a little under the weight and settled him, and looked at Cas, and didn’t say anything, and went.</p>\n<p>Osk put out a hand. Cas took it. The big man pulled him up — gently, the way he’d taught him, <em>stack your bones</em> — and stood holding his arm.</p>\n<p>“I told them,” said Cas. “I told them three times.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Osk. “I heard.”</p>\n<p>Across the platform, at the top of the ramp, there was a disturbance. Wardens — six of them, eight — with staves. And in the middle of them, struggling, Ione. She’d seen Osk take his arm. She was fighting to get to him. She got one arm free and a Warden caught it and she twisted, and the one with her bad arm hit her — not hard, a shove, but on the bad arm — and she cried out, and dropped, and rolled, and was up, and was over the scrap-metal wall of the platform in one long movement, the way she’d gone over it the first day, and gone.</p>\n<p>The Wardens shouted and ran to the wall and looked over it and didn’t follow.</p>\n<p>“Good,” said Osk very softly, to no one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Quiet was a coolant tank.</p>\n<p>It was underneath the Steward’s House, down a long iron stair into the roots of the building, where the old lift terminal had had its machinery. A steel drum, three metres across, three metres deep, set into the keelstone floor, with a round lid on a hinge like the lid of a well. There was a ladder down into it. When Osk took him down the stair and opened the lid, the smell that came up was old water and old rust and something else, something human, very faint, that had soaked into the metal over a hundred and sixty-eight years.</p>\n<p>“Down,” said Osk.</p>\n<p>He went down. It wasn’t far. At the bottom his feet touched a steel floor, damp. He looked up. Osk’s face in the round of the lid, with the lamp-light behind it. For a moment Osk looked as though he were going to say something.</p>\n<p>Then the lid came down.</p>\n<p>It was the darkest dark he had ever been in. Not the dark of a room at night, where there’s always some light, somewhere, a line under a door, a glow from a window. The Lamp had never set on Holdfast; he hadn’t seen real dark for sixteen rounds. This was total. He held his hand in front of his face and saw nothing. He shut his eyes and opened them and it made no difference at all.</p>\n<p>And it was silent. The Steward’s House was above him, and the city above that, and the city was — he knew it was — in chaos, in grief, in the streets, the whole of Holdfast awake and shouting. He couldn’t hear any of it. The steel walls and the keelstone and the stone of the House lay over him like a hand over a mouth, and there was nothing, nothing, only the sound of his own breath and his own heart.</p>\n<p>He sat down on the damp steel floor with his back against the curved wall.</p>\n<p>He sat there.</p>\n<p>Some time later — he had no idea how long — it came up through the floor. Rising. Humming in the steel. Filling the tank like water filling a glass, until the whole drum was ringing faintly with it, a single deep note in the dark. It hung. It lasted. It went away.</p>\n<p>The Bell. The hundred and twenty-one thousand, six hundred and fortieth, or the forty-first. He didn’t know. He’d lost count, for the first time since his flat on Tier Nine. The first time since 02:14.</p>\n<p>He found that he was counting again. Not the Bell.</p>\n<p>Forty-seven.</p>"}, {"file": "11-the-quiet.md", "label": "Chapter 11", "title": "The Quiet", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4965, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Keep them calm. It is the only mercy I have left to give, and God knows I owe them one.</p><span class=\"attr\">Private journal of Aldous Crane, Year 2 of the Watch (never published)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">In the Quiet, there was nothing to do but count and think, and he was very good at both.</p>\n<p>He counted the Bells. He’d lost the number the first time, in the dark, when the lid came down; so he started again from one. One. Two. He counted his breaths between them, to know the interval. About six hundred and forty. Eleven minutes, near enough. He counted the rivets he could reach on the curved steel wall of the tank by feel, running his fingers along the seams, and found there were two hundred and eight, and counted them again, and found two hundred and eight again, and felt a small, pathetic, animal comfort in that. He counted his heartbeats. He counted the drips from somewhere overhead that fell, every so often, into a puddle in the middle of the floor he couldn’t see: one every forty breaths, give or take.</p>\n<p>And he thought.</p>\n<p>He thought about forty-seven.</p>\n<p>He tried not to. He’d been trying not to since the well, and he was no better at not thinking about things than he’d ever been. He thought about the father on the ladder. <em>I’ll come back for him. When I’ve seen it.</em> He thought about the sound that had come up the conduit, that long, slow, stretched lowing, and what it had actually been — what forty metres of Shear did to a body going down it without a keel — and he knew exactly what it had been, because he’d seen the body at the bottom of Conduit 14, and he’d done the arithmetic, and he could not stop doing it now. He thought about Seren, who’d been the cleverest Toller in two hundred years, and he thought about the Tollers’ ledgers, the names in careful ink. He thought about Ione going down under the crowd at the doors.</p>\n<p>He’d said it three times. <em>The Drop is not open.</em> Three times.</p>\n<p>And they had heard <em>the Outside is alive, and he came up it</em>, and nothing else. Just as Perpetua had said. He’d known they would. He’d known it the way you know a sum. And he had said it anyway, because it was true, and because he had decided he was going to be a man who said true things, and forty-seven people had heard the true thing he said and climbed down a ladder toward it and been torn to pieces in the dark.</p>\n<p>That was what truth did. That was what it had done, the first time he’d tried it in fifteen years.</p>\n<p>He sat with his back against the steel and his knees up and the Bell coming up through the floor, and he found himself in a place he knew very well. He had lived in it for fifteen years. It was quite a small place, and quite a cold one, and it had a very simple shape. It went like this: <em>Everything I do adds to it.</em> The lie added to it. The truth added to it. Silence added to it; he’d tried silence, for fifteen years, and every day of silence had been another day of the Draw eating the future, another day of the Margin falling, another day of interest on interest on interest. There was no move that didn’t make it bigger. There was no way to pay it down. There was only the debt, growing, and him inside it, and the only question that had ever mattered was how and when he could stop.</p>\n<p>He’d come into the Draw thinking the Shear might settle it. Or the Crown. Or whatever was in the Crown. <em>At last</em>, he’d thought, at the window in the Collar. <em>Almost a relief.</em></p>\n<p>He thought it now, in the dark. He didn’t argue with it.</p>\n<p>Then he thought about Teo, in a chair, saying <em>oh no you don’t</em>, and he put his forehead down on his knees and stayed like that for a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d counted nine hundred and forty Bells — no. He’d lost it again. He’d counted something. It had been a long time. His mouth was dry. There was water in the puddle and he’d drunk from it, on his knees, lapping at it with his hand like a dog, and it had tasted of rust.</p>\n<p>The lid opened.</p>\n<p>Light came down. Not much — a lamp, a single flame — but after the dark it was like a knife in his eyes, and he threw his arm over his face. When he lowered it, there was a figure coming down the ladder. Slowly, carefully, an old man’s descent, one rung at a time, with a lamp in one hand and something under his other arm.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane reached the bottom. He stood for a moment, getting his breath. Then he set the lamp down on the steel floor between them, and sat down beside it, cross-legged, with a small grunt, his long black coat pooling round him. He put the thing from under his arm in his lap. It was a book.</p>\n<p>For a while he was silent. He looked at the lamp. Cas looked at him.</p>\n<p>“I used to come down here as a boy,” said Crane at last. “My father was Steward. He used to bring me. He’d lower me in, and shut the lid, and leave me for an hour. Sometimes two. He said a Steward had to know what the Quiet was, if he was going to put people in it.” He looked round at the curved steel walls in the lamplight, the rust, the rivets. “He was right, I suppose. I’ve never put anyone in here without remembering it.”</p>\n<p>“Seren?”</p>\n<p>Crane didn’t flinch. He’d been expecting it.</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds,” he said. “I came down every round. The way I’ve come down now. She wouldn’t speak to me. On the eleventh round she said one thing. She said, <em>You know I’m right.</em> And I said yes.” He looked at the lamp. “And then I signed the order, and the next round they took her up to Well Fourteen, and she went down it. She didn’t look back. I was there. I made myself be there.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came. They both sat through it without moving.</p>\n<p>“How many?” said Cas when it had gone. “Out there. Since.”</p>\n<p>“In the city? Nobody else has died.” Crane’s voice was very level. “There’s been fighting. There was fighting in the Commons and there’s been fighting in the lanes since. The Wardens have been — busy. There are people who want your head. There are more people who want mine. The riggers have taken the Drop District and won’t let a Warden in it. The Tollers’ banner is still hanging in the Commons. I haven’t had it taken down.” He paused. “Half the city is saying the Relief is a demon sent up from the Drop to lure the Watch to its death. And the other half is making lamps.”</p>\n<p>“Lamps?”</p>\n<p>“Lamps. Oil lamps. Wicks, dishes. Every oil press in Holdfast has been running for two rounds. People are pressing algae in their kitchens.” Crane looked at him. “They’re getting ready for the dark, Relief. You told them the Lamp would go out. Some of them believed you.”</p>\n<p>Cas found that he couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t come down here to tell you that,” said Crane. “I came down to ask you why.”</p>\n<p>“Why what?”</p>\n<p>“Why you did it.” The old man’s face in the lamplight was very tired, and very intelligent, and entirely serious. “I asked you to be kind. You were kind, the first time. You gave them sixteen rounds of peace. Sixteen rounds of believing that someone had come for them. And then you stood up in the Commons and you took it away. And forty-seven of them are dead in the Drop. And I would very much like to understand what you thought you were doing.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It went on for a long time.</p>\n<p>He’d think about it afterwards — how long, how quiet, the two of them in the bottom of a steel tank with a single flame between them and the Bell coming and going through the floor. He’d think that he had never in his life had a conversation like it. He’d think that it was the kind of conversation he should have had fifteen years ago, with someone, anyone, and never had.</p>\n<p>“Truth,” said Halloran Crane, “is a luxury.”</p>\n<p>He said it without contempt. The way you’d say that a coat was warm.</p>\n<p>“It’s a thing for people with futures. You can afford to tell people the truth when they have time to do something with it. When they can change their minds, and their lives, and fix what’s broken. My ancestor stood under the Placard on the night of the Kindling in front of nineteen hundred people who had just watched their world end. Their families were gone. Their homes were gone. The sky was on fire. The ones who tried to leave had come back up the wells in — in pieces. What was the truth going to do for them? <em>We did this. It was my fault. We will all die here, and nobody is coming, and there is nothing beyond the Drop but a world that has forgotten us.</em> That was the truth, as far as he knew it. Do you know what nineteen hundred people do with that truth, in the dark, in the first week?”</p>\n<p>“Tell me.”</p>\n<p>“They kill each other,” said Crane. “It’s in the Log. Ruth Tally’s own Log. The Tollers think I haven’t read it. I’ve read it ten times. There were riots in the first week. There was a man who took an axe to the scrubbers because he wanted it all to be over. Eleven people died. And my ancestor stood up under that sign and read it aloud and told them it was a promise. <em>You will not be forgotten.</em> And they stopped.” He opened his hands. “His lie kept nineteen hundred terrified people alive for a hundred and sixty-eight years. It gave us children, and the Tanks, and the Crèche, and the Commons, and every rope and wall and lamp in this city. It gave us thirty-one thousand people. That is what a lie can do.” He looked at Cas. “And your truth, Relief, in a single round? Forty-seven.”</p>\n<p>Cas didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“It killed Seren,” he said at last.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And my — the people I work for, outside. They’ve been telling the same kind of lie for ninety years. <em>It’s stable. It’s safe. The future will pay.</em> I signed one of them. I signed my name under it. And that lie didn’t keep anyone alive, it just — it just kept everything going. It kept the Draw going. It kept them feeding it. Right up until it broke and took nineteen hundred people with it and put them in here. That’s what your ancestor was. He was one of us. He wasn’t lying to keep your founders alive. He was lying because it’s what we <em>do</em>. It’s the only thing we know how to do.”</p>\n<p>Crane was quiet.</p>\n<p>“Your lie kept them alive,” said Cas. “Fine. It kept them alive so they could die calm. That’s all it was ever going to do. It kept them in here. It kept anyone from ever trying — ever <em>once</em> trying — to go up and see if the thing could be closed. You had the Tollers’ numbers. You had Seren’s line. You had a girl who came down from the top of the world with the key in her hand. And you kept them calm. You gave them lamps to pray to and a board that counted the Bells and a banner that said the Quickening was glory. And in forty-eight rounds the Lace was going to touch the Rim and every one of them was going to die in the middle of a hymn.” He heard his voice shaking. “I’d rather they died knowing. I’d rather they had forty-eight rounds of knowing, and could do something with it, even if all they do is make lamps. I’d rather <em>I</em> had. I’d rather I’d known, fifteen years ago, that it was all right to say it. That it wasn’t going to kill anyone to say it.”</p>\n<p>“It did kill people.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Forty-seven.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>know</em>.” It came out of him too loudly and rang off the steel walls, and he put his hands over his face. “I know. I know. I know.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came. They waited for it.</p>\n<p>When it had gone, Crane spoke again, lower.</p>\n<p>“I signed her order,” he said. “Seren’s. You know that. What you don’t know is that I read her numbers, and I checked them myself, and I knew she was right, and I had the order drawn up before she ever painted the wall. I had it drawn up the round she came to me. Just in case.” He looked at the lamp. “Just in case she was the kind of person who’d paint it on the wall. And she was. And I was the kind of person who’d already drawn up the order. That’s what I’ve had to live with. Not that I did it. That I’d got it ready.”</p>\n<p>Cas lowered his hands.</p>\n<p>The old man opened the book in his lap. It was small, bound in cracked black leather, its pages yellow and brittle. He turned them with great care until he found the one he wanted, and then held it out.</p>\n<p>“Aldous Crane’s journal,” he said. “Private. It’s passed from Steward to Steward. Nobody else has ever read it. This is from the second year.”</p>\n<p>Cas took it. He held it close to the lamp. The hand was a fine, sloping, educated hand, a Ministry hand, that had begun beautifully and gone to pieces.</p>\n<p><em>I did not give it to the Lamp. I will write that here where nobody will see it, once, so that it has been said. I did not give it. I held it. She asked me for it and I held it, because I was afraid of what they would say, and then the walkway went and it was gone. That is the truth. It is the only truth I have.</em></p>\n<p><em>They sing about me now. They have painted me on a wall. Every time I walk past it I think I will go mad.</em></p>\n<p><em>But they are calm. The riots have stopped. The children are being born. And so I will keep it, this lie, I will keep it until I die, and I will make my son keep it after me, because it is the only thing standing between them and the dark. Keep them calm. It is the only mercy I have left to give, and God knows I owe them one.</em></p>\n<p>Cas read it twice. Then he closed the book and held it in his lap and didn’t give it back.</p>\n<p>“He knew,” he said.</p>\n<p>“He knew.” Crane took the book from him gently. “Every Steward has known. We’ve known it for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and every one of us has kept it, because he asked us to, and because it seemed like mercy.” He stood up, slowly, his knees cracking. “I’ve been thinking, since the Commons, that perhaps it wasn’t mercy he owed them. That perhaps mercy was just the thing he could afford.”</p>\n<p>He picked up the lamp. Then, after a moment, he put it down again on the floor.</p>\n<p>“Can you close it?” he said. “Really? You and the Tally girl?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“You don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“I know nobody’s ever tried,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>Crane stood looking down at him in the flickering light.</p>\n<p>“I let you finish,” he said at last. “In the Commons. Do you know why?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Because my ancestor didn’t let Ruth Tally finish.” He turned toward the ladder. “I’ve wondered my whole life what she’d have said.”</p>\n<p>He climbed. It took him a long time. At the top he paused, and looked down, and for a moment Cas thought he was going to say something else. But he only looked. Then the lid came down.</p>\n<p>He’d left the lamp.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It burned for a long time. Cas sat and watched it. It was the only light he’d ever been glad of in Holdfast — a little, yellow, living flame, not the Lamp’s violet glare, just a wick in a dish of algae oil — and he watched it burn down, and made himself not count.</p>\n<p>When it had nearly gone out, the lid opened again.</p>\n<p>This time it opened fast. It banged back on its hinge and a big shape filled the round of light at the top and a voice like gravel said, “<em>Now</em>, Relief. Up. Quick as you can,” and a rope came slithering down into the tank.</p>\n<p>He looked up. Osk.</p>\n<p>“Stack your bones,” said Osk. “Come on.”</p>\n<p>He got up. He stacked his bones. He took the rope, and Osk hauled, and he came up out of the Quiet the way he’d come up out of the Drop — dragged over the lip like a fish — and lay on the stone floor at the top gasping, and there was a lamp, and faces over him.</p>\n<p>Ione. Her left wrist was bound in a splint of wood and cord, and there was a line of black stitches over her right eye where the cut had been, and she looked at him lying on the floor with her unhurried grey-green eyes and said, “You’re thinner.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve been in a tank.”</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds. You’d be thinner.” She held out her good hand. “Up.”</p>\n<p>Pim was there too, very dirty, with no rat, for once, on his shoulder. He was carrying the recorder in both hands like a precious thing. “I got it out of your room,” he whispered. “Through the vent. There’s a vent. It’s very small. I got stuck. And the suit’s in the lane, in the cart, under a tarp, it took four of us, and Brother Coll, and one of the riggers is sitting on it.”</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds?” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Eleven,” said Ione. “The same as my mother.” Her face didn’t change. “We had to wait till the Wardens changed at Well Four. Osk’s the Warden at Well Four tonight.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at Osk.</p>\n<p>The big man was coiling the rope. He didn’t look up.</p>\n<p>“My brother,” said Osk. “Tam. He went over. At Nine. The round you spoke.” He finished the coil. “He heard you say the Outside was alive. He was always like that, Tam. Heard the first thing and ran. All his life.” He hung the coil on his shoulder. “He didn’t go because you told him the truth. He went because he’d been told a lie so long he couldn’t wait another round to see if it was true.” He looked at Cas then, finally. “I know which one killed him.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went through the shuttered city in the violet dusk with the suit on the cart under a tarpaulin, Osk between the shafts and Pim and two riggers pushing, and Ione walking beside Cas with her good hand under his elbow.</p>\n<p>It was different. He could feel it before he could see it. The lanes were as dim and still as they’d been every night — but there were lights in them now. Not the shrine lamps. Other lights. In windows, behind shutters that had been left open a crack. In doorways. On the steps of houses. Little clay lamps, and tin lamps, and lamps made of old instrument housings with a wick stuck in the top, burning, one or two in every lane. People had put them out. He didn’t understand why at first. Then he did.</p>\n<p><em>They’re getting ready for the dark.</em></p>\n<p>On the side of a scaffold tower near the Commons, someone had painted over part of the mural. Not the whole thing. Only the words. THE GIVING was still there, and the tall grieving man holding up his key. But under it, where it had said FOUNDER CRANE GAVE THE KEY TO THE LAMP, someone had painted, in rough white letters, crossing it out:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HE DROPPED IT</div>\n<p>And under that, in a different hand, in yellow:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SEREN WAS RIGHT</div>\n<p>They came to Toller’s Tower by the back lanes. Perpetua was waiting in the doorway. She was standing — he’d never seen her stand — leaning on Tobiah’s arm, very small and very bent, with a lamp in her other hand. She looked at Cas as he came up the step. She looked him up and down in about a second, as she had the first night.</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds,” she said. “Hmph. You look worse than I do.”</p>\n<p>“I feel worse than you do.”</p>\n<p>“Nobody feels worse than I do. I’m a hundred and eight.” She wasn’t. She was eighty-three; Ione had told him. She jerked her head at the room behind her. “In. Quick. The Wardens’ll be at the Quiet in half a shift.”</p>\n<p>Inside, at the foot of the column, with the lamps burning and the pendulums swinging, she lowered herself back into her chair with a long sigh and put her hand on the stone.</p>\n<p>“Forty-seven,” she said. “We wrote them all down. Every name. We’ll read them out at the Tanks, when it’s time. All forty-seven.” She looked at him. “And then we’ll read out the other number.”</p>\n<p>“What other number?”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand, three hundred and sixty,” said Perpetua Tally. “The ones who didn’t go. The ones who heard you say <em>not before</em>. The ones who went home and made lamps.” Her black eyes were steady on his. “You didn’t do the forty-seven, Relief. A hundred and sixty-eight years did the forty-seven. You did the other number.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t answer. She didn’t seem to expect him to.</p>\n<p>“Now,” she said. “You’ll be wanting your suit.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They got him into it in the round room at the bottom of the tower. It took four of them. The liner, which hung off him now; he’d lost weight in the Quiet, and before. The clam of the suit closing — thunk, thunk, thunk — and the exo waking, and taking his weight, and for the first time in eleven rounds, for the first time in Holdfast, his body didn’t hurt. The keel came into his spine. Teo’s keel, clean and high and true. It had dropped. He could hear it had dropped, since the Shear — a semitone, perhaps; less. It was still singing.</p>\n<p>And there was a light on in the corner of his visor. Amber. A small steady glow.</p>\n<p>MESSAGE — BUFFERED — READY.</p>\n<p>“It groaned,” said Pim. “In your room. For ages. In the middle of the round when they took you. The Wardens were scared of it. They said it was the Drop talking. Nobody would go in.” He looked anxious. “Then it went <em>ding</em>.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the light for a moment. Then he played it.</p>\n<p>“Hale.” Teo’s voice, in his ear, very close. He closed his eyes. “Got your second one. The rats and the wedding and the — and her. Seren. Thank you for telling me her name.” A pause. “And the last bit. You’re going to tell them. All of it. On the — what did you call it — Kindling Round.” Another pause. He could hear her breathing. “Christ, Hale. Good. Good. I’m proud of you. You won’t want to hear that, so I’m saying it into a machine that’s going to take a day to get to you, and you can’t stop me.”</p>\n<p>He heard her shift in the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right. Outside news, since you’ll want it. The Collar’s been on the hardline. Gaunt. I told him you were reseating Six. I’ve never lied to the Calm in my life before today and I’ll tell you what, it’s easy. It’s the easiest thing I’ve ever done. That frightens me more than the Lattice does.” A breath. “Averna’s browning out. They’re saying on the line that the fusion plants at Kovač are down to half, and the Canopy’s flickering, and they’ve started rolling blackouts on the upper tiers. There’s no announcement. Just the lights going off one tier at a time. People are very calm, Gaunt says. He sounds like he’s going to cry.</p>\n<p>“And I checked on your girl.”</p>\n<p>He opened his eyes.</p>\n<p>“Civil Defence has the relocation lists. I’ve still got a login, from in here, on the hardline — don’t ask. She’s in the school on Tier Thirty with her mother, like you said. She’s fine. There’s a note on the list next to her name from the welfare officer. It says <em>asking everyone about stars</em>.” A pause, and something that might have been a laugh. “So she’s fine.</p>\n<p>“I’m fine. The lee’s fine. It moved a bit, about twenty minutes ago — the edge came across the floor, maybe a hand’s width. I moved the chair. It’s fine. I’m bored out of my skull. I’ve read the fire regulations twice.” She was quiet for a moment. “Go and do it, Cas. Whatever it is. And then bring them out. All of them. Bring them out and I’ll be here.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He stood in the suit in the middle of the Tollers’ round room, with the lamps burning and the pendulums swinging and Perpetua watching him from her chair, and he didn’t say anything at all.</p>\n<p>It was the first time she’d used his name.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Perpetua said goodbye to him first. She did it the way she did everything: quickly, and without dressing it.</p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” she said. “Off you go. Up the cursed spindle with my granddaughter. Close the Lamp.” She sniffed. “Try not to fall off.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll try.”</p>\n<p>“I’m keeping the Log.” She put her knotted hand on the steel box on the workbench beside her. “Somebody should be here, with it, when you come back with the end of it. Somebody should write the last page.” She looked at him with her bright black eyes. “You’ll tell me how it ends. Won’t you.”</p>\n<p>“If I can.”</p>\n<p>“Hmph.” She took her hand off the box. “That’ll do.”</p>\n<p>Then she looked at Ione.</p>\n<p>Ione was standing by the door with her rope over her shoulder and her wrist in its splint and the key at her throat. She’d been waiting. She didn’t come forward. And Perpetua didn’t say anything to her. She only looked. It went on and on — the old woman in the chair and the young one by the door, the lamps between them, the pendulums swinging, the Bell coming up through the column and fading — and Cas understood that he was watching eleven years go by. Eleven years of a girl who came down from the top of the world with grey in her hair and a key in her hand, and a grandmother who’d lost her daughter to the Drop and then watched the granddaughter go up after something nobody else believed was there. Eleven years of that girl being called mad in the lanes. Eleven years of her sitting on this step in the dusk, eating greenbread.</p>\n<p>“Go on,” said Perpetua Tally, very softly. “Go and finish it, Ione.”</p>\n<p>Ione nodded once. She turned and went out.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Spindle Four stood at the far edge of the city, in the Drop District, alone.</p>\n<p>The riggers had let them through. They were on every corner, in every lane of the District — the riggers’ tower had become a fortress, lamps on every platform, ropes across every approach — and they stood aside for the cart and watched it go by in silence. Some of them touched the tarpaulin as it passed. One of them, a woman with a scarred face, said, “Haul, Ione,” under her breath, and Ione said, “Haul, Bett.”</p>\n<p>The spindle’s foot was a ruin. The lift terminal at its base had collapsed in the Founders’ time, when the lift fell — the whole cage and a kilometre of rack-rail coming down together, Pim said, with a sound they’d heard in the Commons — and nobody had ever cleared it. The wreckage lay in a great twisted heap of rusted girders and snapped rail and fallen keelstone, overgrown with pale vine. And behind it, rising out of it, the spindle went up.</p>\n<p>He’d seen it every day from the loft. He’d seen all of them every day. He’d never stood at the foot of one before.</p>\n<p>It was a hundred metres across at its base. It went straight up out of the wreckage into the light, a vast black lattice of struts and braces and cross-members, open, skeletal, the violet glare of the Lamp coming through it in a million bright diamonds, and it went up. And up. And up. It didn’t narrow, at first; then, very gradually, it did. It went up until the struts blurred into a texture and the texture into a line and the line into nothing, into the glare, into the fine bright falling tendrils of the Lace, four kilometres over his head.</p>\n<p>Osk and Pim and the riggers got him out of the cart. He stood in the suit at the foot of the wreck, looking up. Ione stood beside him, with her head tipped back.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Pim, in a very small voice. “It’s big.”</p>\n<p>Ione took her rope off her shoulder and began to uncoil it, favouring the splinted wrist, and started tying it into the harness they’d spliced for the suit, her fingers moving fast.</p>\n<p>“Don’t look up,” she said, without looking at him. “It only gets taller.”</p>"}, {"file": "12-interlude-two-hours-in-a-chair.md", "label": "Interlude", "title": "Two Hours in a Chair", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4067, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Nobody’s keeping score. Nobody ever was.</p><span class=\"attr\">Teo Lisk, to a man in a keel suit, Lattice Station 14-C</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\"><strong>Minute 0.</strong></p>\n<p>She watched him go up the ladder, and then she watched him stop being a person.</p>\n<p>It was the strangest thing she had ever seen, and she had seen a stack fire in Kovač turn a forty-storey housing tower into a candle. He climbed. Ordinary. Grey suit, humming keel, her keel, gauntlets on the rungs, one after the other, climbing on the back side of the ladder where the boy’s map said to climb. Thirty metres. Thirty-five. And then he reached the part of the shaft where the air was wrong, the trembling curtain, and he went into it — and he <em>stuttered</em>. That was the only word. He was on one rung and then three rungs up and then two rungs up again and then five. Bits of him were ahead of other bits. For a second she saw his arm reaching up out of the top of the curtain while his boots were still under it and the middle of him was a smear, a wet grey streak on the air, like a thumbprint on a lens.</p>\n<p>Then he was above it. And he was moving so fast that she couldn’t follow him. A blur, going up the shaft like something fired. Then a dot. Then nothing.</p>\n<p>“Talking to you,” she said into her radio. “Still here. Keep going. Nearly there.”</p>\n<p>Her radio made a noise like a bird.</p>\n<p>She sat back in the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk to the empty room. “Right, then.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 1.</strong></p>\n<p>She took stock, because that was what you did.</p>\n<p>Station air: good. Pressure: good. There was a panel by the door that told her so, and she believed it, because her ears weren’t popping and she wasn’t dizzy. Water: a cooler, two-thirds full, with a stack of paper cups. Food: the suit’s ration paste, which was vile, and a first-aid cabinet, which on inspection contained bandages, burn gel, a foil blanket, a bottle of painkillers with most of the painkillers gone, and a single packet of digestive biscuits, one-third eaten, with a sell-by date nine months ago.</p>\n<p>She ate one. It was soft. She ate another.</p>\n<p>The suit without its keel was just a suit. Heavy. The exo worked. She could walk about. Every so often the room seemed to sway, very slightly, like the deck of a ship, and she’d put her hand out to the back of the chair and it would pass. The lee, the calm patch, the pocket of ordinary. It filled the booth. Just about. She’d dropped washers at the edges: by the door, by the far wall, in the corners. Straight down, every one. Good.</p>\n<p>She looked out of the viewport at the ladder going up into the trembling air, and at the bottom of the ladder, the shape lying crumpled at its foot.</p>\n<p>“Farr,” she said. “You’d have had a theory about all this.”</p>\n<p>The room didn’t answer. She hadn’t really expected it to.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 2.</strong></p>\n<p>She went down through the hatch with the foil blanket.</p>\n<p>It was stupid. It served no purpose. She knew that. She knelt beside the body at the bottom of the conduit — the little broad body in its patchwork clothes, its legs white bone and its face half a face — and unfolded the blanket, and laid it over, from the head down, and tucked it round. The foil crackled. It shone in the pearly light. The little yellow lamp on its cord was still showing at the neck; she tucked that under too.</p>\n<p>“There you go, love,” she said. “Whoever you are.”</p>\n<p>She went back up. She shut the hatch.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 4.</strong></p>\n<p>The radio chirped.</p>\n<p>Not like before. Not the bird-squeaks she’d been getting since he went through, the little ragged scraps of him arriving squashed. This was a single long <em>tweeee</em>, rising, about the length of a breath, and then a click.</p>\n<p>And the little amber light came on at the bottom of her visor. RECEIVING. BUFFERING. RETIMING.</p>\n<p>She sat forward in the chair.</p>\n<p>It took the suit a minute and a half to chew it out. She watched the light the whole time. Then it went <em>ding</em>, and there he was, Casimir Hale, his dry, tired, clever voice in her ear — and it was <em>long</em>. It went on and on. She sat in the chair and listened to him talk, and her mouth fell open, and after a while she realised it had been open for some time, and shut it.</p>\n<p><em>There’s a city in here, Teo.</em></p>\n<p>Thirty-one thousand. A hundred and sixty-eight years. Rats with the names of officials. The Tending, sung wrong. The Section 9 placard in a church.</p>\n<p><em>They called me the Relief.</em></p>\n<p>She laughed out loud. She couldn’t help it. She sat alone in a booth at the bottom of the world and laughed until she had to wipe her eyes with the back of her gauntlet, and her visor got in the way, so she took the helmet off and laughed some more.</p>\n<p>And then he said the other thing. <em>I lied to them. Within an hour.</em> And he said it the way you’d tell a doctor where it hurt.</p>\n<p>She stopped laughing.</p>\n<p>It had been four minutes. Four minutes, on her suit clock, since he’d climbed up that ladder. He’d got to the top, and been pulled out by a stranger, and carried through a city, and made a saint, and stood up in front of twenty thousand people, and gone to bed, and sat on the floor and talked to her about it — and all of that was <em>a day</em>, for him. Most of a day. While she’d eaten two biscuits and covered a dead woman with a blanket.</p>\n<p>“Bloody hell, Hale,” she said.</p>\n<p>Then she thought about it for a moment, and put the helmet back on, and pressed the button on her own recorder, and said: <em>Right. Your first chirp’s come through.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 11.</strong></p>\n<p>The hardline rang.</p>\n<p>She hadn’t known it was there until it rang. It was a telephone — an old one, a handset on a hook on the wall by the dead monitors, grey plastic, a curly cord. It rang with a bell. An actual bell. She stared at it for three rings and then got up and picked it up.</p>\n<p>“Lattice Station Fourteen-C.”</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk.” Gaunt. Of course it was. That soft voice, as if he were ringing to ask whether she’d enjoyed a book he’d lent her. “What a relief. We weren’t sure the line would still be live. We lost your pucks about twenty minutes ago; this is the old copper, it runs all the way round the Lattice. I’ve been ringing every station on it.” A pause. “Is Dr Hale with you?”</p>\n<p>She looked out of the viewport at the ladder.</p>\n<p>“No,” she said.</p>\n<p>“No?”</p>\n<p>“He’s gone up. Through the Shear. With my keel. His was damaged.” She kept her voice very flat. “Farr’s dead.”</p>\n<p>A silence on the line.</p>\n<p>“I’m very sorry,” said Gaunt, and to her considerable surprise, it sounded as though he were. “He was — very young.” Another pause. “And Dr Hale is — proceeding?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Proceeding to the Crown.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“To reseat Six.”</p>\n<p>The chip. Silver seal. <em>Any means.</em> Underlined. She’d dropped it in a coolant channel and watched it go.</p>\n<p>“He’s reseating Six,” said Teo Lisk. “As we speak.”</p>\n<p>She’d never lied to the Calm in her life. Not once. Not in twenty-nine years. You didn’t. It wasn’t that anyone had ever told her not to; it was simply that you didn’t, the way you didn’t put your hand in a fire. And it came out of her as smooth and easy as water out of a tap, and Gaunt said, “Oh, that’s good. That’s very good. The Minister will be so relieved,” and she stood there holding the grey plastic handset and felt it go all the way through her, cold, like swallowing ice.</p>\n<p><em>Easy</em>. It was the easiest thing she’d ever done.</p>\n<p>“Major,” said Gaunt, “I won’t keep you. I only wanted you to know — the grid’s failing. Kovač is at half. We’ve started the rolling cuts on the upper tiers. We’re telling people it’s maintenance.” His voice changed, very slightly. “People are being very calm. I’ve never seen anything like it. They sit in the dark in their flats and wait for the lights to come back. Nobody’s asking. Nobody’s asking anything.” He stopped. “I find I’d almost rather they did.”</p>\n<p>She let the silence sit.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Gaunt. “Do give Dr Hale my regards, if you speak to him.”</p>\n<p>He hung up.</p>\n<p>She put the handset back on its hook. She sat back down in the chair. After a while she noticed that her hands were shaking, and she put them flat on her knees and held them there until they stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minutes 12 to 30.</strong></p>\n<p>The chirps kept coming. Every few minutes. A long rising <em>tweeee</em>, a click, a minute and a half of amber light, a <em>ding</em>. And then Cas, talking.</p>\n<p>She got used to the rhythm of it. She’d sit in the chair with her boots up on the dead monitor desk and wait for the next one, the way you wait for the next episode of something. Four minutes. Six. Three. She’d hear the chirp and her whole body would go still.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Tollers. About a tower full of pendulums, and an old woman with eyes like a bird who’d made him soup, and a column of black stone with every Bell of a hundred and sixty-eight years chalked on it in fives. He told her about a Log. About letters to a little girl called Maddy who was seven. That one she had to stop and play again, and then she sat with it for a while, with the helmet off, looking at the ladder, before she could listen to the next.</p>\n<p>He told her about the market. About rat. About a boy called Pim who named his rats after officials.</p>\n<p>He told her about Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t say much, at first. <em>There’s a woman.</em> She’d pulled him out of the well. She had a white streak in her hair. She was a rigger. She had a key. She’d been somewhere, up near the top, and come back different, and nobody believed her. That was all, the first time. But then the next chirp he mentioned her again. And the next. <em>Ione says. Ione showed me. I was up in Ione’s loft.</em> She was in every one. By the fifth or sixth, Teo realised she could picture her: a short, broad, scarred woman with a rope on her shoulder and a way of looking at you that made you feel she’d read the end of the book.</p>\n<p>And his voice was changing.</p>\n<p>She noticed that too, around minute twenty. She played an early one again to be sure, and then a later one, one after the other. The early ones were him: dry, careful, flat, a man reading out a list. The later ones weren’t. They were — she didn’t have the word. They had <em>weather</em> in them. He laughed, sometimes. He stopped in the middle of sentences because he’d seen something out of a window and wanted to tell her what it was. He talked about a wedding on a rooftop and she could hear, under the words, that he had been happy at it. For an hour, on a rooftop, with a cup of something that took the skin off his throat, he’d been happy.</p>\n<p>“Well,” Teo said to nobody. “Look at you.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 21.</strong></p>\n<p>The lee moved.</p>\n<p>She was halfway through a message — the one about the wedding, the riggers tying their wrists together — when she felt the chair tip. Very slightly. A lean to the left, like a car taking a bend. She sat up. The room swayed. On the floor by the far wall, where she’d dropped a washer when she first came in, the washer slid.</p>\n<p>It slid about a hand’s width across the floor, toward the corner, and stopped.</p>\n<p>She watched the corner. She watched it until her eyes ached. Nothing else happened. The room steadied.</p>\n<p>She got up and dragged the chair a metre to the right, into the middle of the room, and sat back down in it, and pressed play again. When she recorded her next message, she mentioned it. <em>The edge came across the floor, maybe a hand’s width. I moved the chair.</em> Casual. As if she were telling him the weather.</p>\n<p>It was the weather. That was the thing. It was only the weather.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 34.</strong></p>\n<p>She found the login by accident.</p>\n<p>She’d been poking at the dead monitors out of boredom, and one of them wasn’t dead — a little grey terminal under the desk, on the same old copper as the hardline, humming to itself. It had a Civil Defence emblem on its login screen. Some maintenance link. She typed in her own service number and password, not expecting anything, and it let her in.</p>\n<p>She sat and looked at it for a moment. Then she pulled up the relocation lists.</p>\n<p>It took her four minutes to find Nell Hale. Tier Thirty, Hollowmere Academy sports hall, with mother Sabine Hale-Okoro. Status: well. And a note in the welfare column in the slightly harried shorthand of a volunteer who’d been awake for thirty hours: <em>Child v. calm. Asking everyone re: stars (?). Has lizard (not hers).</em></p>\n<p>Teo laughed out loud in the empty room.</p>\n<p>Then she typed in her own sons’ names.</p>\n<p>Joss first. University accommodation, relocated to a transit hall on Tier Forty. Status: well. No note.</p>\n<p>Mattias. Kovač. Kovač wasn’t on the relocation lists; Kovač had its own. She dug. It took her ten minutes. She found him in the end on a Kovač municipal list, a crew list, not a relocation list: <em>M. Lisk, electrician, Kovač Fusion Station B, emergency staff, on shift</em>. On shift. At the plant that was holding up the whole grid at half power.</p>\n<p>Of course he was.</p>\n<p>She sat looking at his name on the screen for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she took the marker out of the pocket of her liner — the black marker from the supervisor’s desk in B-7, the one Hale had written backwards with on their side of the glass; she’d picked it up off the gallery floor on the way out, without telling him, because she hadn’t been able to leave it — and she got up and went to the viewport, and in the bottom corner, small, where she’d see it from the chair, she wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MATTIAS<br>JOSS</div>\n<p>She looked at them. Then, underneath, she added:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">BEN</div>\n<p>And then, after a moment:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SEREN (THE LADY)</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 41.</strong></p>\n<p><em>I’m going to tell them.</em></p>\n<p>She listened to that one sitting very still. He said it quietly, at the end of a long message about a rigger crew that had been caught by the Lace and turned to bones. Kindling Round. Three rounds away, for him. Everything. The Outside, the test, the Deputy Minister, the debt, the lock, the key, Ione. In front of the whole city.</p>\n<p>She knew what it would cost him. She’d sat next to him at the canteen window in the Collar and watched him not answer <em>why</em>. She’d seen his face at the booth when he thought the Shear might settle things for him. She knew.</p>\n<p>She played it again.</p>\n<p>Then she pressed record and said, <em>Christ, Hale. Good.</em> And a lot of other things, most of which she’d forgotten as soon as she’d said them, except that she’d told him she was proud of him, and she’d meant it, and she’d been glad he couldn’t hear it for a day.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minutes 42 to 128.</strong></p>\n<p>He didn’t send anything else for a long time.</p>\n<p>She understood, doing the sum on the back of the fire regulations with a ballpoint she found in the desk, that for him it would be — what? Rounds and rounds. Days. Weeks. She didn’t know what had happened. She sat in the chair and waited.</p>\n<p>She read the fire regulations again. She did the sum again. She ate the last digestive. She went down the hatch and sat for a while with the lady in the blanket, just sitting, not saying anything, because it seemed wrong to leave her alone. She came back up. She dropped washers. She moved the chair once more, at minute seventy-three, when the washer by the door slid, and again at minute ninety-eight, when the one in the far corner did. The room was smaller now. Not much. You wouldn’t notice, unless you were dropping washers. The calm part of it was a circle in the middle of the floor, about three metres across, and the chair was in it, and she was in the chair.</p>\n<p>At minute a hundred and two, the hardline rang. She let it ring. It rang twenty times and stopped.</p>\n<p>She talked to Ben. Out loud. She told him about Mattias on shift at Kovač, and about Joss, who’d wanted to be a pilot when he was five and a poet at fifteen and was now doing something with computers she couldn’t pronounce. She told him about her husband, Anders, who’d dropped dead in the kitchen eleven years ago in the middle of making toast, and how she’d come home from a shift and found the toast still in the toaster, and how for a year afterwards she couldn’t be in the room when anybody made toast. She told him Jonah would get the dog. She’d see to it. She’d buy the bloody dog herself.</p>\n<p>At minute a hundred and twenty she realised she’d been crying for some time, and stopped, and blew her nose on a bandage from the first-aid kit.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 129.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Tweeeeeeeeeeee.</em></p>\n<p>It was the longest chirp yet. It went on for three breaths. Then a click, and another <em>tweeee</em>, shorter, and a third, and the amber light came on and stayed on for nearly four minutes while the suit chewed, and she sat forward in the chair with both hands gripping its arms.</p>\n<p><em>Ding.</em></p>\n<p>He’d been in a tank. Eleven rounds, in the dark.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Commons. He told it plainly, flatly, the old way, the way he’d talked at the gate — a man reading a list — and she could hear what it was costing him to do it that way, because it was the only way he could do it at all. The truth, and the banner, and a man shouting <em>it’s open</em>. Forty-seven. A little boy he’d pulled off a ladder. A father who’d said <em>I’ll come back for him</em>. The sound that came up the well.</p>\n<p>She sat with her fist pressed against her mouth.</p>\n<p>Then the Quiet. Then an old man with a lamp. Then a big Warden with a rope. Then the Tollers’ tower again, and the old woman, and the number she’d given him — <em>thirty-one thousand, three hundred and sixty</em> — and then, in a different voice, a lighter voice, almost wondering: <em>Teo, they’re making lamps. The whole city. They’re putting them out in the lanes. They’re getting ready for the dark.</em></p>\n<p>And then:</p>\n<p><em>We’re going up. Tonight. Spindle Four. Ione and me. The electronics will go, up there, Ben said they would, so this might be the last you get. If it works, the Lamp goes out. The Shear goes. Time in here will be the same as time out there. They’ll be able to walk down. Thirty-one thousand of them. All at once, down fourteen wells, into the Works. Come and get them, Teo. Bring everyone. Bring blankets. Bring⁠—</em></p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p><em>There’s another file. It’s for Nell. It’s everything I’ve recorded for her, since I got here. It’s about three weeks. I don’t know if I’ll — if you get out, and I don’t. Would you give it to her? Please. And tell her⁠—</em></p>\n<p>Another pause, longer.</p>\n<p><em>No. It’s in the file. It’s all in the file.</em></p>\n<p>And then, very quietly:</p>\n<p><em>Thank you for the keel.</em></p>\n<p><em>Ding.</em></p>\n<p>A second file arrived behind it. The suit showed it on her visor: a little icon, a size, a name. NELL. Three weeks. Three weeks of a man talking to his daughter, squashed into a chirp three breaths long.</p>\n<p>She sat in the chair and looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 130.</strong></p>\n<p>She recorded her answer.</p>\n<p>She did it with the helmet off, holding the recorder close to her mouth, sitting forward in the chair with her elbows on her knees. She didn’t plan it. She knew it would be the last one — knew it the way you know, sometimes, at the top of a flood barrier with the water coming up, that this is the last thing you’ll get to say to somebody for a while, maybe ever. So she said it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know, afterwards, all of what she said. Some of it she remembered. That she’d give Nell the file herself, by hand, and sit with her while she listened. That she’d tell Jonah about the dog. That he was to look after the rigger with the white streak in her hair, because it sounded as though nobody had for a while. That he was to come back and buy her the large one he owed her. That her sons were called Mattias and Joss and she’d written their names on the window where she could see them, and she’d written Ben’s under, and Seren’s, and if he didn’t mind she’d write his, too, now.</p>\n<p>That guilt was a lousy reason. That she’d said it before and she’d say it again because he was the kind of thick-headed bastard who needed to hear things twice.</p>\n<p>That she was fine.</p>\n<p>That she’d be here.</p>\n<p><em>Bring them out, Cas.</em></p>\n<p>She pressed send. The suit chirped, a tiny sound, a squeak, her whole minute gone up the ladder in a blink. She sat back.</p>\n<p>She took the marker and went to the window and wrote, under SEREN (THE LADY):</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">CAS</div>\n<p>She stood looking at it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 131.</strong></p>\n<p>The washer by the door slid.</p>\n<p>Not a hand’s width. It slid a metre, rattling across the floor, and hit the leg of the desk, and spun, and lay still. And the one by the far wall slid, toward the corner. And the room <em>leaned</em>.</p>\n<p>She put her hand out to the chair.</p>\n<p>The edge of the lee was coming across the floor. She could see it now. She hadn’t been able to see it before, but she could now: a line on the floor, a sort of shimmer, a ripple in the air a hand’s height off the ground, like heat over a road. On one side of it, the dust lay flat. On the other side it was moving, drifting, crawling very slowly toward the corners. The line was about a metre from the door. As she watched, it moved closer.</p>\n<p>She did the thing you did. She took stock. Station air: good. Pressure: good. Suit: no keel. Exit: through the door, into a Lattice full of weather, four kilometres from the gate, or through the hatch, into the conduit, beside the lady in the blanket, where the Shear was forty metres up and the weather was whatever the weather was down there.</p>\n<p>The line moved closer.</p>\n<p>She took the chair and dragged it to the far corner of the room, the corner by the viewport, as far from the door as she could get, and set it down, and sat in it, and put her boots up flat against the wall.</p>\n<p>The line was two metres away now. Then a metre and a half. The room swayed, and swayed back. On the other side of the viewport, in the conduit, the Shear trembled on the air as it always had, the ladder going up through it, jumping and smearing. Above it, far up, the pale bright disc of light.</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk sat in her chair in the corner with her boots against the wall and her hands folded in her lap, and watched it come.</p>\n<p>“Come on, Hale,” she said.</p>"}, {"file": "13-the-lees.md", "label": "Chapter 12", "title": "The Lees", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": true, "words": 5712, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Chalk falls true in a Lee. Chalk falls crooked out of one. When chalk falls up, pray.</p><span class=\"attr\">Riggers’ saying, Holdfast</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">For the first kilometre, it was only climbing.</p>\n<p>That was how Ione put it, at the bottom, tying him in. <em>The first kilometre’s only climbing. After that it’s the Lees.</em> And he’d nodded as if he understood, the way he’d nodded at so much in Holdfast, and found out what it meant by doing it.</p>\n<p>Spindle Four was hollow. From the city it looked like a solid black tower, but from inside it was a cage — a vast open lattice of keelstone struts, a hundred metres across at the base, rising around them in a great latticed tube, with the violet light of the Lamp pouring in through every gap in a million bright slanting bars. Up the inside of the lattice, clinging to it, ran the old maintenance ways: iron ladders and iron stairs and narrow steel walkways, bolted to the struts, zigzagging up and up the inside of the tube in flights and landings and switchbacks until they vanished into the glare. The lift’s rack-rail had run up the centre once. Its stump still stood there, a twisted black mast rising out of the wreckage at the bottom and ending, ragged, a few hundred metres up, where it had snapped.</p>\n<p>They climbed the stairs. Where there were no stairs, they climbed the ladders. Where the ladders had rusted through or fallen away — and there were many places where they had — Ione went up the bare lattice itself, finding holds in the struts with her hands and her boots, her splinted wrist held a little stiffly, the rope paying out behind her from the coil on her shoulder. When she reached the next walkway she’d tie off and call down, and he’d come up after her on the rope. The suit’s exo did most of it. The keel hummed. The harness she’d spliced round his hardpoints held.</p>\n<p>It was only climbing. It was the hardest thing he’d ever done.</p>\n<p>Because he got heavier.</p>\n<p>He felt it begin at about three hundred metres: not a sudden thing, not a gust or a shear like the Works, but a slow, patient, steady increase, as though someone were adding sand to his pockets a grain at a time. At five hundred, the exo’s whine had changed pitch. At seven hundred, he noticed his breathing — not from effort, but from weight, the sheer work of lifting his own ribs. The suit’s display, when he looked, said LOCAL G: 1.8. At the bottom of the spindle, in Holdfast, it had said 1.4.</p>\n<p>“You’ll weigh twice what you weigh in the city by the first kilometre,” Ione said, when he told her. She was sitting on a walkway above him, eating greenbread, her legs dangling over the drop. “Three times by the second. By the Rim — I don’t know. I wasn’t counting.”</p>\n<p>“How do <em>you</em> do it?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with mild surprise. “I was born to it,” she said. “We all were. Holdfast’s heavy. I’ve been climbing spindles since I was nine.” She considered. “And I’m small. The small ones do better. Less to lift.”</p>\n<p>She held her hand out flat, palm up, over the edge of the walkway. There was a little heap of white chalk in it. She blew on it, gently, and the chalk went off her palm in a puff, and drifted, and fell. He watched it. It fell straight down, in a thin white curtain, all the way into the bars of violet light below until he lost it.</p>\n<p>“That’s the last true chalk for a while,” she said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At a kilometre, the Lees began.</p>\n<p>He understood what she’d meant, then, about the chalk. The Lamp’s push, which had been growing steadily as they climbed, began to <em>vary</em>. You’d climb a flight of stairs, heavier and heavier, and come out onto a landing near one of the great keelstone struts — the huge black beams that braced the lattice, some of them five or ten metres thick — and suddenly the weight would ease. Not all of it. Some. As though a hand pressing on your shoulders had lifted a little. And then you’d step away from the strut and it would come back.</p>\n<p>“That’s the strut pulling,” said Ione. “Against the Lamp. It’s like the Anvil. It’s keelstone, it pulls. Near it, the pull and the push fight. You weigh less.” She blew chalk. It went off her palm and drifted — not down, but down and <em>sideways</em>, toward the strut, in a long slanting curve. “See? It’s pulling the chalk, too. Crooked chalk. That’s a Lee. A little one.”</p>\n<p>The Lees were how you climbed a spindle. He learned them over the next three rounds, or what would have been three rounds, down in the city. There were little ones by every strut, where you could stand and breathe. There were big ones — rare, precious — where two or three struts came together, where the pulls added up and cancelled the Lamp’s push almost completely, where the chalk fell so crooked that it hung in the air, turning slowly, and you could lie down on the walkway and feel as light as you’d felt in the city. Those were where you rested. Those were where you slept. The riggers had built little caches in the big ones, generations ago: a platform of planks, a water-catcher of canvas, a box of stores bolted to the strut. Most of them had rotted away. Ione knew the ones that hadn’t.</p>\n<p>And the Lees moved. That was the thing that had sent her up alone, those three rounds before the Kindling festival, when he’d sat on the steps of the Steward’s House blessing ropes and not hearing a word anyone said to him. The Lamp was getting weaker — the Lightening, the debt coming due — and as it weakened, the balance between its push and the struts’ pull shifted. Lees she’d climbed through at seventeen had closed up. New ones had opened. Some had drifted a few metres up a strut. Some had split in two. She’d found them all, on that trip, by chalk and plumb bob and feel, and marked them. As they climbed he saw her marks: little white arrows scratched into the black keelstone with a nail, and numbers, and here and there a word. <em>GOOD. SMALL. BAD — GO RIGHT. SLEEP.</em></p>\n<p>“You did all this in three rounds?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Four.” She didn’t look round. “And I was a lot faster without you.”</p>\n<p>He laughed. It came out of him before he could stop it, a real laugh, surprised, loud in the suit, and he heard it echo round the inside of the spindle, and she glanced down at him from the ladder above with an expression he couldn’t read.</p>\n<p>“What?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” She went on climbing. “You don’t do that much.”</p>\n<p>“Laugh?”</p>\n<p>“Mm.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He thought about the canteen in the Collar and the tea that was mostly a structural material and Teo saying <em>Sounds like my marriage</em>. He thought about the gallery floor and MINISTER →.</p>\n<p>“I’m getting better,” he said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the second round of the climb — the second time they slept — he looked down.</p>\n<p>They were at two kilometres. They’d stopped in a big Lee, one of the rigger caches, a platform of old planks lashed to the junction of three struts, with a canvas water-catcher and a steel box of rotted stores and a view straight down through the open lattice of the spindle to the city. He was lying on his back on the platform, in the suit, feeling almost light for the first time in a round, and he rolled over onto his front and looked down through the gap between the planks.</p>\n<p>Holdfast was very small.</p>\n<p>It lay at the bottom of the world, a tiny intricate patch of scaffold and container and keelstone spread across the black disc of the Anvil, the other seven spindles rising out of it like the pillars of a temple seen from its roof. The Commons was a speck. The Drop District was a smudge at the edge. Smoke went up from it in threads so fine he could hardly see them.</p>\n<p>And as he watched, it began to go dark.</p>\n<p>It started at the centre — at the Steward’s House, at the foot of Spindle One — and went outward. A wave. A sort of dimming, a darkening, spreading out across the city from the middle to the edges. The shutters, he realised. The shutters coming down at the end of second shift. The night horn — he couldn’t hear it; he was too high, too far — and ten thousand shutters closing.</p>\n<p>But it was slow. He’d stood in the lanes and heard that wave go past, the clatter and bang of it; it took a minute, perhaps, to cross the whole city. From here it crept. It spread across Holdfast the way ink spreads into wet paper, lane by lane, so slowly that he could watch a single street go dark, house by house, and count his breaths between the houses. He lay there on the planks and watched it for what felt like a quarter of an hour before it reached the edges.</p>\n<p>“What⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Watch the smoke,” said Ione. She’d rolled over beside him on the planks and was looking down too, her chin on her folded arms.</p>\n<p>He watched the smoke. The thin grey threads going up from the city’s chimneys. They weren’t drifting. They should have been — there was always some air moving, down there, the Lamp’s heat stirring it — and they weren’t. They stood up out of the chimneys like wire. Like grey hairs. Perfectly still.</p>\n<p>“We’re faster than them,” said Ione. “Up here. The higher you go, the faster you live. Down there, that’s a minute. Up here, it’s⁠—” she shrugged “⁠—longer. And it gets longer. By the top of the Lees you’ll look down and they’ll be a picture. Nothing moving at all. We’ll sleep a whole sleep and wake up and they’ll still be in the same night.” She blew a little puff of chalk out over the edge and watched it drift away sideways, turning. “We can get to the top and back, if we’re quick, before they’ve finished one round.”</p>\n<p>“It’s holding its breath,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” She sounded pleased that he’d seen it. “That’s what I thought. When I was seventeen. I lay here — right here, this cache — and looked down at it and thought, <em>the city’s holding its breath</em>. And I thought: if I stayed up here, if I stayed long enough, high enough, the whole of Holdfast would stand still for me. Nobody would grow up. Nobody else would go down a well. I could keep them all exactly as they were, as long as I liked.” She was quiet. “I didn’t, though. I went on up.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her lying beside him, her chin on her arms, looking down at the still city. At the white streak in her hair, bright in the Lamp’s light. At the stitches over her eye.</p>\n<p>“Why?” he said. “At seventeen. Why did you go up?”</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer for a while. Down below, the city went dark.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell you,” she said. “Later. When we’re higher. It’s easier to tell higher up.” She rolled onto her back and put her arm over her eyes. “Sleep. We’ve got a long way.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo’s last message arrived on the third round.</p>\n<p>The suit began to groan in the middle of a climb — a long, low, falling note from the helmet speaker, so deep it was almost below hearing, so slow it didn’t seem to move at all — and he stopped on the ladder and hung there, listening, until Ione called down <em>what is it</em> and he said <em>Teo</em> and she came back down to him and they climbed on together to the next Lee and sat there, and waited.</p>\n<p>It went on for a long time. Much longer than the others. They were higher now, living faster, and her minute had to stretch further to reach them. It groaned on through half a round, a vast slow wordless sound filling the lattice around them like the voice of the spindle itself, and he sat on the planks with his back against a strut and his knees up and listened to every moment of it, and Ione sat beside him and didn’t say anything.</p>\n<p>Then <em>ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He switched it to the suit’s outer speaker so that Ione could hear.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk. “Last one. I know it’s the last one. You said the electronics would go and I believe you, you’re the sort of pessimist who’s usually right.” A breath. “Got your — got all of it. The Commons. The tank. Your old man with the lamp. The lamps in the lanes.” Her voice caught and went on. “The forty-seven. I’m sorry, Cas. I’m so sorry. I know what you’re doing with that, right now, wherever you are. I can hear you doing it from here. Stop it.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“I’ve got Nell’s file. Three weeks of you. I’m going to give it to her myself. By hand. I’ll go to Tier Thirty and I’ll find her in her gym with her lizard and I’ll sit with her while she listens, and if she wants to cry I’ll let her, and if she wants to hit me I’ll let her do that too. You’ve got my word. And I’ll tell Jonah about the dog. I’ll get him the bloody dog myself.</p>\n<p>“And you look after that rigger. The one with the white streak. You never said much about her, but you didn’t have to, you said her name forty times in three weeks. It sounds like nobody’s looked after her in a long while. Somebody should.”</p>\n<p>Beside him, Ione went very still.</p>\n<p>“You owe me a drink,” said Teo. “A large one. Don’t forget. I’ve written my boys’ names on the window, here, where I can see them from the chair. Mattias and Joss. Mattias is on shift at Kovač, holding up the grid. Of course he is. Joss is fine. I’ve written Ben’s under. And the lady’s — Seren’s. And I’ve written yours. I hope you don’t mind. It seemed right.”</p>\n<p>A long pause. He could hear her breathe.</p>\n<p>“Guilt’s a lousy reason, Cas. I said it at the booth and I’m saying it again because you’re the kind of thick-headed bastard who needs to hear things twice. Don’t you dare go up there and do something noble because you think you owe it. Do it because it’s useful. Or because you want to. Or because <em>she</em> wants you to. Anything. Anything but that.”</p>\n<p>A breath.</p>\n<p>“I’m fine. The lee’s fine. I’ll be here.” A pause. “Bring them out, Cas.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>The suit hissed. The display at the bottom of his visor flickered, and showed a line of text — SIGNAL LOST — and flickered again, and showed nothing. The little icon for the radio went grey.</p>\n<p>He sat on the planks with his back against the strut. Ione sat beside him. Neither of them spoke. Far down through the lattice, the city lay in its night, the smoke standing up from it like wire.</p>\n<p>“She sounds,” said Ione eventually, “like somebody I’d like.”</p>\n<p>“You would.”</p>\n<p>“<em>The rigger with the white streak.</em>” She said it slowly, as if trying the words for size. Then she looked at him sideways. “Forty times?”</p>\n<p>He felt his face go hot inside the helmet.</p>\n<p>“She was exaggerating,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Mm,” said Ione, and looked away, down at the sleeping city. But he saw the corner of her mouth, and it had moved.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the fourth round they felt the last Bell.</p>\n<p>They were at nearly three kilometres. He weighed — the display said, before the display began to lie to him — two point nine times what he’d weighed on the Tier Nine arterial, a thousand years ago, a day and a half ago. The exo was screaming. They moved from Lee to Lee now in short, brutal dashes, never more than a few dozen metres between them, Ione going first and then hauling him, both of them resting in every crooked-chalk pocket they could find, sometimes for an hour, before the next. Below them the city had stopped. It lay in its shuttered dusk like a painting of itself, the same night it had been in when they’d looked down from two kilometres, the smoke from its chimneys standing up in fine grey threads that didn’t move at all.</p>\n<p>The Bell came up the spindle.</p>\n<p>It came so slowly that at first he didn’t know it for what it was. A pressure. A faint rising hum in the strut under his hand, very deep, like a tide coming in under a pier. It rose. And rose. It took so long to rise that he had time to notice it, and wonder, and understand, and turn to look at Ione. She had her palm flat against the strut, and her eyes closed.</p>\n<p>It went on rising. Minutes. The whole spindle was humming with it, every strut, the walkways vibrating faintly under them, a vast slow note going through the lattice like a wind through a forest. It reached its height. It held there. It began, so slowly he couldn’t be sure of it for a long time, to fall.</p>\n<p>It lasted, he guessed, about a quarter of an hour.</p>\n<p>When it had finally gone, Ione took her hand off the strut and opened her eyes.</p>\n<p>“One hundred and twenty-two thousand, nine hundred and thirty-one,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been counting?”</p>\n<p>“I’m a Toller.” She said it as though it were obvious. “I’ve always counted. Even when they threw me out.” She looked down at the pulsing city. “That’s the last one we’ll hear.”</p>\n<p>“The last⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Up here, they get too slow. The next one will take a round to rise and a round to fall. You won’t be able to tell it from the spindle breathing.” She looked at him. “That was the last Bell. For us. Unless we come down.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p><em>Unless we come down.</em></p>\n<p>Neither of them pursued it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>That night — that sleep; there were no nights, up here — they stopped in the best Lee on the spindle.</p>\n<p>It was at the junction of four great struts, three kilometres up, where the lattice narrowed and the black beams came together from four directions in a vast knot of keelstone, and between them there was a pocket of calm so deep that Ione’s chalk, when she blew it, didn’t fall at all. It hung in front of her face in a small white cloud, turning, glittering in the violet light, like dust in a sunbeam. There was a cache: a platform of planks, a water-catcher with water in it, a box. And it was so light — so wonderfully, dizzyingly light — that when he stepped onto the platform he nearly fell, overbalancing, his body braced for a weight that wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>He took the helmet off. He hadn’t taken it off for four rounds. The air was thin and warm and smelled of hot stone and chalk. He sat on the planks, in the suit, with the helmet in his lap, and put his head back against the strut, and breathed.</p>\n<p>Ione sat opposite him with her back against another strut. She’d taken off her harness and her boots. She’d unwrapped the splint on her wrist and was flexing it, slowly, wincing. Above them, through the narrowing lattice, the Lace hung in the sky — very close now, much closer than from the city, its fine bright threads hanging down in long trailing streamers toward the spindle-tops, moving very slowly, like weed in a current. Below them the city lay frozen in the middle of its night.</p>\n<p>“I was seventeen,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t move. He didn’t want to startle it.</p>\n<p>“My mother had gone down the Drop two years before. I’d held the ladder for her, when she painted the wall. They didn’t give me to the Drop. They said I was a child.” She flexed her wrist. “I think they thought I’d be quieter, without her. I was. For two years I was very quiet. I counted Bells. I worked the ropes. I didn’t talk to anyone except Gran.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“And then one round I went up. I didn’t tell anyone. I didn’t decide to, really. I just went. I told myself I was going to find the edge of the world. Where the Lamp stopped and something else started. I thought if I went high enough I’d see over it. Over the Rim. Into whatever was on the other side.” She almost smiled. “I was seventeen. And I think⁠—” She stopped. “I think I thought I might find her. Up there. I knew she’d gone <em>down</em>. I watched her go down. But I thought — if everything goes round, up there, the way people said — if the Lamp was a place where things went round⁠—”</p>\n<p>She didn’t finish.</p>\n<p>“I climbed this spindle,” she said. “I didn’t know the Lees then, not the high ones. I found them. I nearly died four times. I got to the top of the Lees — about half a kilometre above us — and there’s a gallery there. Round the outside of Spindle One, near the top. A walkway, a ring, going right round the spindle, with a roof over it and windows. The old crews used to use it. It had an air-garden in it — a long tank of algae all the way round, to keep the air fresh for the Crown. And a gantry, across from Four to One. I went across the gantry. I went into the gallery.” She looked at the chalk hanging in the air in front of her. “And the light came on.”</p>\n<p>“The light.”</p>\n<p>“The Crown’s light. The night of the Kindling. It’s — it’s still there. In that gallery. You’ll see. It goes round and round. The light from those nine minutes. It’s trapped. It goes round the spindle in the gallery windows and never gets out. And when you’re in it, you’re⁠—” she searched “⁠—you’re <em>in the Crown</em>. On the night. You can see it all. The people. The Crown crew, in their white coats, at their boards. The Lamp in the window, before it broke. Founder Crane with his key on its chain and his hair all combed. Ruth.” She said the name gently. “Ruth at the board. I didn’t know who any of them were. Not then. I thought they were ghosts. They were all lit up, sort of, from inside. You could see through them. You could walk through them.”</p>\n<p>“And then?”</p>\n<p>“And then it all happens. Six goes. The Lamp breaks. The walkway goes. The key falls. Ruth pulls the hammer. They all run for the lift. Nine minutes.” She paused. “And then it starts again.”</p>\n<p>The chalk turned slowly in the air between them.</p>\n<p>“I tried to leave. Of course I did. I walked out of the gallery the way I’d come in. And I walked straight back into the gallery, at the other end, where I’d started. As if the gallery was a ring and I’d just gone round it. Every door. Every window. The gantry back to Four — I walked across it and I was coming back across it the other way, into the gallery. Everything led back in.” She said it very plainly. “And the light started again. Ruth at the board. <em>Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol.</em> Nine minutes. And again. And again.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“Three years.”</p>\n<p>He’d known. She’d told him, in the Tower, that first night. It was different, hearing her say it here, at the top of the world, with the chalk hanging in the air.</p>\n<p>“Three years,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I counted.” She almost smiled again. “Of course I counted. A hundred and seventy-five thousand, two hundred and — some. Nine minutes each. I lost count a few times. There were a few months when I didn’t count anything.” She looked at her wrist, flexing it. “There were a few months when I didn’t do anything at all but lie on the floor of the gallery and let them happen through me. Ruth walked through me. Crane walked through me. The key fell through me. Again and again and again and I lay there and let it.”</p>\n<p>“How did you — food? Water? For three years?”</p>\n<p>“The air-garden.” She glanced at him. “Algae. And the gallery had a condensate trap for the Crown’s water. I lived on algae and condensation for three years.” A pause. “You’d have liked that. Your friend. The one in the hall.”</p>\n<p>“Ben.”</p>\n<p>“Ben,” she agreed. “He’d have understood.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t answer.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“It wasn’t a time loop,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it later, after a long silence, when the chalk had drifted a little way off and hung near the strut.</p>\n<p>“Not for me. I thought it was, for years. I thought I was going round and round in time. But I wasn’t. I got older. My hair⁠—” she touched the white streak “⁠—that happened in there. My body went on. I remembered everything. Every cycle, I remembered every cycle before it. I was living forward. The whole time. Just — in a place that went round.” She looked at him. “It was <em>them</em> that went round. The ghosts. The light. They were the loop. Not me.”</p>\n<p>“The light’s on closed paths,” said Cas slowly. “Round the spindle. Trapped. And the gallery⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The gallery joins up with itself. You walk out and you walk in.” She nodded. “Yes. That’s it. That’s what I worked out, eventually. I was the only thing in there that was going <em>forward</em>. Everything else was going round.”</p>\n<p>“That’s worse,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” She didn’t look away. “That’s what I learned. That’s the thing I learned in there, in three years, that nobody in Holdfast would ever believe.” She held his eyes. “Ruth lived those nine minutes once. Just once. In the Crown, on the night of the Kindling. It was the worst nine minutes of her life and she lived them one time and then she went down the lift and had a whole life after, and Tomas and her children and eleven grandchildren, and died an old woman. The light in the gallery isn’t <em>her</em>. It isn’t reliving it. It isn’t tired of it. It doesn’t know. It’s just the same nine minutes, being true, over and over.” She looked at the chalk. “I’m the one who lived them a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. I’m the only one who ever did. The horror of it was never in the loop. It was in whoever was watching.”</p>\n<p>He sat without moving.</p>\n<p>“If a loop’s really closed,” said Ione, “if nothing in it goes forward, if nothing in it can remember — then there’s nobody inside it to be tired of it. It’s just once. It’s once, and it’s true, and it stays true. It’s only forever for whoever’s outside, looking in.” She shrugged a little, as if embarrassed by how much she’d said. “That’s all. That’s all I know. I worked it out on the floor of a gallery when I was nineteen. In there, nothing happened once. Out here, everything does.”</p>\n<p>He thought about fifteen years. He thought about a flat on Tier Nine and a phone that never rang and a man who got up every day and did the same nothing and went to bed. The same day, over and over, for fifteen years. And a pen, a nice blue Ministry pen, that he’d picked up once, one time, and signed with.</p>\n<p>He had lived that one moment — the pen — five and a half thousand times. Every morning. He was the only one who had.</p>\n<p>“How did you stay sane?” he said. “In there. Three years.”</p>\n<p>Ione thought about it seriously. She always thought about his questions seriously; he’d noticed that, in the loft, in the Tower. As if every question deserved to be turned over and looked at from underneath before you answered.</p>\n<p>“I decided to love them,” she said.</p>\n<p>“The ghosts?”</p>\n<p>“The people.” She looked up at the Lace. “After the months on the floor. I got up one cycle and I thought: I can hate this, or I can learn it. And I started watching. Properly. Every one of them. I learned all their names — they say each other’s names, you pick it up. Fen at the tensioner board. Dieter at output, who drinks his coffee too hot and burns his tongue every single time. Oksana with the camera, who’s frightened of heights and never looks out of the window. The boy with the tablet, who’s in love with Oksana.” A small smile. “And Ruth. I learned Ruth best. I sat beside her at the board. For three years. I know her better than I know anyone living. She has a mole, here⁠—” she touched her own jaw below the ear “⁠—and she bites her thumb when Crane talks. Just the side of it. She doesn’t know she does it. And when she says <em>one-thirty-five</em>, she closes her eyes first, for about half a second. She doesn’t know she does that either.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment.</p>\n<p>“She was my best friend,” said Ione, “for three years. And she never heard a single word I said to her.”</p>\n<p>The chalk hung in the air. Far below, the city didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“And the key?” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“One cycle,” said Ione, “it was there. On the gantry. After a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles where it wasn’t. It was the only different thing that had ever happened.” She touched it where it hung at her throat. “And I looked up from it. And far off — at the far end of the gantry, where it meets the wall of Spindle One, a long way off, further than it should have looked — there was someone standing. A woman. Not lit up. Not a ghost. Real. Standing where no ghost ever stood. With grey hair.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t breathe.</p>\n<p>“I thought it was my mother,” said Ione simply. “I shouted. <em>Mam.</em> I ran along the gantry toward her. And she turned away. And where she’d been standing, when I got close, the air was — wrong. Shimmering. A seam. I went into it and I was out. On the Rim. Out of the gallery. Out of the loop.” She let go of the key. “I never saw her again. I came down the spindle in two rounds. Gran met me at the bottom. She said I’d been gone for two days. I looked in a water-trough when I got home and I was twenty, and I had this.” She touched the white streak.</p>\n<p>“And the figure in the suit?” he said. “Coming down?”</p>\n<p>“That was on the way down. Across the gulf, on another spindle. Very far. Just once.” She looked at the suit he was sitting in, the humming keel. “I didn’t know what it was, then. I only knew I’d never seen anything like it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the suit too.</p>\n<p><em>There are only three suits.</em></p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything else.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She slept, in the end. She lay down on the planks with her head on her folded rope and her splinted wrist on her chest and was asleep in about a minute, the way riggers slept, the way soldiers slept, completely.</p>\n<p>He didn’t.</p>\n<p>He sat with his back against the strut, in the suit, with the helmet in his lap, and watched her. He watched the white streak in her hair, and the black stitches over her eye, and the slow rise and fall of her breathing in the almost-weightless calm. He watched the chalk, which had drifted over her and hung above her face in a small white cloud, glittering, turning. He watched the city far below them, still as a photograph — thirty-one thousand people caught in the middle of a single night while he sat and watched a woman sleep for hours.</p>\n<p>He thought about a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles. About a girl of seventeen lying on the floor of a gallery for months, letting the worst night in the world happen through her. About that girl getting up, one cycle, and deciding to learn their names.</p>\n<p>He thought about Teo’s voice. <em>Do it because you want to. Or because she wants you to. Anything but that.</em></p>\n<p>He found — examining himself, the way he’d examined himself for fear at the window in the Collar, patting his pockets — he found something he hadn’t expected. He didn’t have a word for it at first. It was small. It was very quiet. It was sitting somewhere under his breastbone, where the sketchbook was, under the neck-ring of the suit, against his heart.</p>\n<p>It was that he wanted to see what happened next.</p>\n<p>Not what happened in the Crown. Not whether the lock would turn or the Clause would work or the plate meant what he was beginning, in the back of his mind, very quietly, to be afraid it meant. Not that. Something smaller. He wanted to see her wake up. He wanted to see what she’d say when she did. He wanted to see the chalk fall, when they left the Lee, and her blow it off her palm, and the look on her face when she read it. He wanted the next round. And the one after that.</p>\n<p>He sat very still with it, the way you’d sit very still with a bird that had landed on your hand.</p>\n<p>It had been a long time. He’d forgotten what it felt like. He’d forgotten that it was possible to want, carefully, just to go on.</p>\n<p>Below them — so slowly that he had to watch for a long time to be sure of it — at the very centre of the city, at the foot of Spindle One, the first shutter of the morning began to rise.</p>"}, {"file": "14-lamp-weather.md", "label": "Chapter 13", "title": "Lamp-Weather", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 4928, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Remember that the Lesion is not a place. It is a condition of the place around it. Near it, even the weather is a kind of arithmetic.</p><span class=\"attr\">Iris Hollowell, <em>On the Sealing of Debts</em> (withdrawn)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">Above the best Lee on Spindle Four there was a place the riggers called the Breach.</p>\n<p>Ione showed it to him from below, the morning they left the cache — if it was morning; the city under them had only just begun, very slowly, to open its eyes. She pointed up through the narrowing lattice. Two hundred metres above them, the spindle’s structure changed. The struts that had braced it all the way up, the great black beams that made the Lees, simply stopped. For a stretch of perhaps sixty metres, one whole side of the spindle was open — the lattice there had fallen or been stripped in the Founders’ time, or had never been finished — and there was only a single spine of keelstone running up through the gap like the backbone of a fish, with the rungs of an old ladder bolted to it, and on either side of it, nothing. Violet air. The Lace.</p>\n<p>“No Lees in the Breach,” said Ione. “Nothing to hold against the Lamp. You go up it in one go. Fast. You don’t stop. There’s a hut at the top, in the last big Lee, under the gallery. You don’t stop till the hut.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“Fast? A quarter of a shift. For me.” She looked at him. “For you, in that⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Longer.”</p>\n<p>“Longer.” She began to uncoil her rope. “And you don’t look at the Lace. It’s close up there. It’s very close. You look at the rungs.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He looked at the rungs.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. He looked at them as they came down past his visor one after the other, black iron, pitted, bolted to the black keelstone spine, and he looked at his gauntlets closing on them, and he looked at the rope going up from his harness to Ione, climbing above him with the coil on her shoulder, fast and neat and steady. He didn’t look out. He didn’t look down. He could feel the open air on both sides of him the way you can feel someone standing behind you in a dark room. He could feel the weight — three times Holdfast, three and a half, the exo screaming in every joint, the keel’s note straining high in his spine.</p>\n<p>And he could feel the Lace.</p>\n<p>Not see it. He didn’t look. But he could feel it, off to the left, where the spindle was open: a kind of pressure on the side of his face. A prickle. The sense of something enormous and delicate hanging very near.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione above him, and he heard the soft puff of her blowing it, and a pause. “Crooked. Left. That’s fine. Keep coming.”</p>\n<p>He kept coming.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione, a while later. A pause. Longer. “<em>Very</em> crooked. Keep coming. Faster.”</p>\n<p>He went faster. His arms were two things he had borrowed from someone who hadn’t wanted them back. The rungs came down past his face.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>Silence.</p>\n<p>He looked up. He couldn’t help it. She was twenty metres above him, braced on the spine with her boots on a rung and her free hand held out flat in front of her, palm up. The puff of chalk had come off it. It hadn’t fallen. It hadn’t drifted sideways. It was going <em>up</em>. A little white cloud, rising slowly off her hand, straight up, toward the Lamp.</p>\n<p><em>When chalk falls up, pray.</em></p>\n<p>“Cas,” said Ione, very calmly. “Hold on.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It hit them like a wave hits a pier.</p>\n<p>First the weight went. All of it, all at once — the crushing three-and-a-half that had been on him for a thousand metres simply <em>vanished</em>, and he was hanging on the rungs weighing nothing, and his stomach came up into his throat and his body, braced against a weight that wasn’t there, threw itself away from the ladder. The exo jerked. His gauntlets clamped. He hung there, floating, attached to the spine by his fists.</p>\n<p>Then it came back. From the side.</p>\n<p>Down swung. Not gently, the way it had in the Works. It <em>whipped</em>. One moment there was no down, and the next down was off to his right, out through the open side of the spindle, into the violet air, and it was not three times Holdfast, it was more — four, five, he didn’t know — and he was hanging off the side of the spine by his hands like a flag in a gale, his legs streaming out sideways over nothing, the whole weight of him and the suit trying to tear his arms out of their sockets and fling him out into the Lace.</p>\n<p>He heard the keel scream.</p>\n<p>And above him, he heard the rope go.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t loud. A sort of flat crack, like a whip, and a hiss. He looked up. Ione’s rope, where it ran from her harness down to his, had been lying across the edge of the keelstone spine — the raw broken edge where the lattice had fallen away. Keelstone didn’t break. But when it did, the edges were like glass. The rope had sawn itself across the edge in the swing, and parted, and the end of it came whipping down past his visor.</p>\n<p>And Ione came off the spine.</p>\n<p>She’d been braced with her boots on a rung and her free hand out for the chalk. When down swung, she’d grabbed. Her splinted hand had closed on the rung. And he watched it not hold.</p>\n<p>She came off. She went out, sideways, into the air on the open side of the spindle — the weight flinging her, the way it was trying to fling him — and she didn’t fall, she <em>flew</em>, out and down and past him, a small dark shape tumbling, her arms out, the white streak in her hair, her mouth open, and she was two metres from him and three and she was going out into the violet air toward the Lace⁠—</p>\n<p>He let go with one hand.</p>\n<p>He never remembered deciding. His right hand came off the rung and went out and the suit’s gauntlet closed on the loop of rope that still hung from her harness, a metre of frayed end, and the grip-assist clamped on it so hard that he felt the fibres crush. The weight of her came onto his arm like a steel bar. His left hand, alone on the rung, screamed. The exo screamed.</p>\n<p>He pulled.</p>\n<p>The keel’s bubble was a metre. That was all. A metre of stirred-flat spacetime round his body, where down was one direction instead of every direction at once. He hauled on the rope with an arm that had nothing left in it and dragged her in through the air toward him, and she came, and she hit him — hit the suit, chest to chest, her face against his visor, her arms coming round him — and was inside it.</p>\n<p>And the weather stopped.</p>\n<p>Not outside. Outside it went on — he could see it, over her shoulder: the violet air, the Lace, the open side of the spindle all tilting and swinging, the chalk-cloud Ione had blown whirling past in a spiral. But inside the bubble, inside the metre, down was simply down. The keel’s note was a scream in his spine but it held. It averaged it all out. Down was one way, and it was heavy, very heavy, but it was <em>one way</em>, and they were both in it, pressed together against the spine of Spindle Four, his left hand locked on a rung and his right arm round her and her arms round him, holding on.</p>\n<p>“<em>Don’t let go</em>,” she said against his visor. Her voice came through the helmet thin and close. “Don’t let go, don’t let go⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got you.”</p>\n<p>“⁠—<em>don’t let go</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got you, Ione. I’ve got you.”</p>\n<p>It went on for a long time. He never knew how long. It came in gusts. Down would swing round the outside of the bubble like a wind round a house, and he’d feel the keel strain to hold it, and the metre around them would flex and warp — the rungs in front of his face stretching, bending, as though seen through water — and hold, and settle. The weight surged and dropped. Once it dropped to nothing and he felt her start to float away from him, just slightly, and pulled her back hard, and she made a sound, and held on harder. Once it surged so hard that the spine of keelstone they were clinging to <em>groaned</em>, a vast deep sound through the metal, and a rung below them sheared off its bolts and went spinning out into the air and was gone.</p>\n<p>Her face was against his visor. He could see her eyes. They were very close and very wide, and for the first time since the top of the well there was nothing patient in them at all. They were frightened. They were a frightened seventeen-year-old’s eyes, in a thirty-year-old’s face, looking at him from three centimetres away through a sheet of glass.</p>\n<p>He held her. He didn’t have anything else to do.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>When it passed, it passed all at once. Down came back to down — straight down, the old crushing three-and-a-half, the Lamp pressing on them from above. The violet air outside stopped swinging. The chalk was gone.</p>\n<p>They hung there on the spine, the two of them, for a while longer. Neither of them moved.</p>\n<p>Then Ione said, into his visor, very quietly: “The hut’s forty metres up.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>“I can’t let go of you. If I let go of you I’m out of the bubble and I’ll weigh four times what I weigh and I’ve only got one hand.”</p>\n<p>“Then don’t let go of me.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll have to climb with me on you.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him through the glass for a moment longer. Then she shifted — got her legs round his waist, her good arm round his neck-ring, her splinted hand fisted in the webbing of his harness — and put her cheek against the side of his helmet, and held on.</p>\n<p>He climbed.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know how. He never knew, afterwards, how. Forty metres. A hundred and thirty-odd rungs, with the whole weight of both of them — less in the bubble; not less enough — on arms that had nothing in them, the exo whining in a high thin continuous note like a dentist’s drill, the keel shrieking in his spine. He climbed with her pressed against him, her breath hot on the side of his helmet, and he counted rungs because it was the only thing he knew how to do, and somewhere past a hundred he stopped being able to count, and just climbed.</p>\n<p>At the top of the Breach the lattice came back. Great black struts closing round them. Chalk falling crooked. A Lee — a deep one; the weight lifted off them like a coat taken off their shoulders — and in it, wedged into the angle of three massive beams, a hut.</p>\n<p>He got them onto its platform. He didn’t remember that either. He remembered lying on his back on old planks inside a low black room with Ione still wrapped round him, both of them shaking, and the keel’s note slowly coming down from its scream, and her face in the crook of his neck-ring, and her voice, muffled:</p>\n<p>“You let go of the rung.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“With one hand. In lamp-weather. In the Breach.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>A long pause.</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s ever done anything that stupid for me,” said Ione Tally. And then, after another pause, very quietly, into the neck-ring of his suit, she began to cry.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hut was keelstone. Riggers had built it — generations ago, in the Founders’ time or just after, when the riggers were still going up the spindles to tend the old lamp-lines — out of off-cut slabs of keelstone laid against the three great struts, so that the walls pulled with the struts and the Lee inside was deep and quiet and almost flat. There was a door, of iron plate. There was a window, a slit, looking out through the lattice at the Lace. There was a shelf with things on it that crumbled when you touched them. There was a water-catcher outside the door, and water in it.</p>\n<p>And it was calm. Down was down, and not very much of it — about what it had been in the city, Ione said. After the Breach it felt like floating.</p>\n<p>He took the suit off.</p>\n<p>He’d been in it since the foot of the spindle, days ago — four sleeps; five — and when it opened down the back and he stepped out of it in the grey liner he nearly fell, again, the way he had at every big Lee. His legs had forgotten how to carry him. Ione caught him. They sat down together on the planks, hard, and stayed there, backs against the black wall, not talking.</p>\n<p>The suit stood in the corner of the hut, the keel humming. It had dropped again. He could hear it. The storm had cost it.</p>\n<p>After a while he got up and went to it, and looked at the display on the inside of the helmet. The clocks said LOCAL 03:14 and OUTSIDE 11:62. There was no such time as 11:62. As he watched, OUTSIDE said 04:0? and then a string of symbols and then nothing. The radio icon was grey. The little green light on the recorder, in its chest compartment, was still lit.</p>\n<p>“It’s going,” said Ione, behind him.</p>\n<p>“The electronics. Yes. Ben said they would.”</p>\n<p>“Will it still⁠—” She stopped.</p>\n<p>“The keel’s mechanical. The exo’s got a manual fallback. The air’s mostly mechanical.” He put the helmet down. “It’ll still get us through the Lace.”</p>\n<p><em>Us</em>, he thought. He didn’t say anything else.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She washed his face. He didn’t ask her to. She got water from the catcher outside in a tin that didn’t crumble and a strip of cloth from her pack and knelt in front of him where he sat against the wall and washed his face, carefully, the way you’d clean a wound, and then his neck, where the neck-ring of the suit had rubbed it raw. Then she re-bound her own wrist, one-handed, with her teeth, and wouldn’t let him help.</p>\n<p>“Is it broken?”</p>\n<p>“It was broken before. It’s more broken now.” She tied it off. “It’ll do.”</p>\n<p>He watched her. The stitches over her eye. The white streak. The rope-scarred forearms. The splint.</p>\n<p>“Can I tell you something?” he said.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<p>“Nobody knows it,” he said. “Except a man in a cardigan who works for the secret police. And Teo, a bit. And the man in the cardigan only knows what I did, not⁠—” He stopped. “Not the rest.”</p>\n<p>She sat back on her heels. She didn’t speak. She didn’t nod. She just waited, the way she’d waited at the top of the well, the way she’d waited through a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles, as if there were nothing he could say that she hadn’t already seen, and she’d be interested to see it again.</p>\n<p>So he told her.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Margin Review. Year Seventy-Six. About Dr Adeyemi, sixty-eight, who walked everywhere very fast with his hands in his pockets, who used to put his palm flat on the walls of the Works and listen. About the nine months they’d spent on it, the two of them and a handful of analysts, going through ninety years of Rollover figures. About the night he’d first run the projections himself, alone, in the Review’s borrowed office on Tier Two, and watched the Margin line go down past one and keep going, and sat there until dawn. About the report. <em>A structure of obligation that cannot, even in principle, be discharged.</em> Adeyemi had written that. Cas had read it over his shoulder and thought it was the truest sentence he had ever seen.</p>\n<p>He told her about the meeting. The Ministry. A long table in a room with gold carpet. Very courteous people. Nobody raised their voice. They explained that the projections were, of course, only projections. That the Review had not taken into account the planned improvements to the Lattice. That publishing a figure like that — a figure like <em>negative margin</em> — in the current climate, with the export treaties being negotiated, would be irresponsible. Would cause panic. Would hurt people. Real people, now, today. That the future would find solutions; the future always had. That all that was needed was a revised conclusion. A small one. A sentence. <em>The Margin remains stable within projected parameters.</em></p>\n<p>“Adeyemi said no,” said Cas. “He stood up and said no, and walked out. They put him on leave. He died two years later. Heart, they said. He was seventy.” He looked at his hands. “They asked me.”</p>\n<p>“And you said yes.”</p>\n<p>“They didn’t make me. That’s the thing. That’s what nobody — they didn’t threaten me. They didn’t mention my family. They just asked. They put the revised report in front of me and a pen next to it and they asked, very politely, whether I’d be willing to sign. And I thought about it for — I don’t know. Ten seconds. And I thought: <em>it’ll probably be fine</em>. And I thought: <em>somebody else will fix it</em>. And I thought: <em>who am I to say</em>.” He was quiet. “And I noticed that it was a nice pen. That’s the thing I remember. I remember thinking what a nice pen it was. It wrote very smoothly.”</p>\n<p>Ione said nothing.</p>\n<p>“They gave me the Standby. A flat by the Pit and a salary and a phone that never rang. And I sat in it for fifteen years.” He found that his voice had gone strange. “I didn’t do anything. I didn’t — I lost Sabine, because I couldn’t talk to her, because the only thing I could have talked about was the thing I couldn’t say. I saw Nell every other weekend and took her to the museum and didn’t say anything to her either. I got up every morning and did nothing and went to bed. The same day. Every day. Fifteen years. You said — you said about the Carousel. That it was only horrible for whoever was watching. I’ve been watching one day for fifteen years. The day with the pen in it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“When the Draw broke,” he said, “I sat on my bed and listened to the hammer, and I didn’t feel frightened. I felt⁠—” He made himself say it. “I felt relieved. I thought: <em>at last</em>. I thought: <em>now somebody will have to send me in</em>. And I thought I’d go in and it would — settle it. Somewhere in here. The Shear, or the Crown, or wherever. I thought I’d die in here and it would be⁠—” He stopped. “It seemed tidy.”</p>\n<p>He’d never said it out loud before. He’d never said it to himself.</p>\n<p>“I came in here to die,” said Casimir Hale. “That’s why. Teo asked me, at dinner, the night before, and I didn’t answer her. That’s why.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Ione was quiet for a long time.</p>\n<p>Outside the slit of window, the Lace hung in the violet air, very close, its threads moving slowly like weed in a current. The keel hummed in the corner. Far below, somewhere, the city was still in its night.</p>\n<p>“You think it’s a debt,” said Ione at last.</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“What you did. With the pen.” She was sitting with her back against the wall opposite him, her knees drawn up, her splinted wrist resting on them. “You think it’s a debt, and you’ve been paying it. For fifteen years. Every day. And you came in here to pay the rest of it off. All at once. With yourself.” She tilted her head. “Is that it?”</p>\n<p>“Something like that.”</p>\n<p>“And has it got any smaller?”</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. He shut it.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione. “It hasn’t. Has it. You’ve been paying it every day for fifteen years and it’s bigger than it was. Because that’s how your kind of debt works.” She said it without any cruelty at all, the way she’d explained the Lees. “You told us. In the Commons. You borrow, and you pay it back, and it’s always more. You pay the interest, and you pay the interest on the interest, and it grows, and it grows, and you can never, ever get to the bottom of it. That’s what you people <em>built</em>. You built a whole city on that. You built this whole place on it.” She moved her hand, taking in the hut, the spindle, the Lamp. “And then you went and built a man on it too.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“I watched a woman make the worst choice of her life,” said Ione, “a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. Ruth. At the board. <em>One-thirty-five.</em> She closes her eyes for half a second first. Every time. And every time I sat next to her and I thought, <em>don’t</em>. Every time. A hundred and seventy-five thousand times I thought <em>don’t</em>, and a hundred and seventy-five thousand times she did it anyway. Because it was already done. Because it was just light. Because she only ever got to do it once, and she’d done it, and there was nothing anyone could ever do about it, not her, not me, not anyone.” She looked at him steadily. “She never got to make another choice. Not in the gallery. The ghost never does. It just does the one thing, for ever.</p>\n<p>“But the real Ruth did. She went down the lift, and she made the next choice, and the one after. She got married. She had children. She wrote letters to a little girl for fifty years. She counted the Bells. She <em>built the Tollers</em>, Cas. My whole family. Everything I am. She made that out of the worst nine minutes of her life. She didn’t pay it off. You can’t pay it off. She just — went on. She made the next thing.”</p>\n<p>She leaned forward.</p>\n<p>“You’re not a ghost,” she said. “You’re not the light in the gallery. You signed it once. That’s all. Once. It’s true, and it’ll always be true, and there’s nothing you can do about it. But you’re not the one who has to do it again every morning. That’s you. That’s only you. You’ve been standing in the gallery watching yourself sign it for fifteen years, thinking <em>don’t</em>. You can walk out. You can make the next thing.” She sat back. “That’s all. That’s all I know.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t speak. He sat against the black keelstone wall with his hands open on his knees and looked at her and his eyes stung and blurred and he let them.</p>\n<p>After a while she got up and came across the hut and sat down beside him, against the wall, close, her shoulder against his shoulder. She didn’t say anything else. She just sat there.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Later — much later — she said, “Nobody’s touched me in eleven years.”</p>\n<p>She said it to the window, not to him. Her shoulder was still against his.</p>\n<p>“Since I came down. The Unwatched. You don’t get touched. People won’t hand you things. They put them down and step back, so you have to pick them up. Gran touched me. And Pim, he doesn’t care, he’s eleven, he climbs on everyone. That’s all. Eleven years.” She was quiet. “In the Carousel, I used to lie next to Ruth. At the board. With my head on her shoulder. And she’d go right through me.”</p>\n<p>He turned his head and looked at her.</p>\n<p>She turned hers. They looked at each other, close, in the violet light from the slit of window.</p>\n<p>“You held on,” she said. “In the Breach. You had me by the rope and you pulled me in and you held on. And I could feel you. Through the suit. I could feel your arms.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know,” she said. “I know. You’re from Outside and I’m from here and we’re going up there, the two of us, to something that probably kills us. I know all of it. I’ve thought all of it. I’ve thought it for forty rounds.” She put her hand, her good hand, flat on his chest, on the grey liner, over his heart. “I’ve had eleven years of thinking. I’m tired of thinking.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand over hers.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was clumsy. That was what he remembered, afterwards, and was glad of: how clumsy it was.</p>\n<p>The liner was a one-piece with a seam down the back that had been designed to be opened by a machine, and neither of them could work out how to get it undone, and she laughed at him trying, and then he laughed, and then she was laughing so hard she had to put her forehead against his shoulder and stop. Her clothes were a rigger’s clothes, a tunic and a harness and a belt and trousers with keelstone-cloth knees, and every piece of them was knotted in some rigger’s way he’d never seen, and she wouldn’t show him, she made him work them out, one after another, and he did. Her wrist got in the way. His back, where the keel had sat for five sleeps, had a bruise across it the size of a dinner plate, and when she put her hand on it he hissed, and she took her hand away, and he put it back.</p>\n<p>The weight in the Lee was Holdfast weight. Heavy. Everything heavy — her body on his, his on hers, their hands, their breath — as if the world were pressing them together. She was strong. Much stronger than him, in that weight, and very sure, and then, suddenly, not sure at all, holding very still with her face against his neck and her breath going fast, and he held still too, and waited, and she said <em>it’s all right, it’s all right, I’m just⁠—</em> and didn’t finish, and didn’t need to.</p>\n<p>Afterwards they lay on the planks of the hut under his coat and hers, tangled, her head on his chest, and he could feel her heartbeat and his, in the heavy stillness, not quite together.</p>\n<p>“Your heart’s too fast,” she said drowsily.</p>\n<p>“It’s the weight.”</p>\n<p>“It’s not the weight.” She moved her head. “I’ve been listening to it since the top of the well. It’s always too fast. Outside hearts must all be too fast.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Mm.” She was quiet a while. Her fingers moved on his chest, very slightly, tracing something. He realised after a while that it was a knot. The riggers’ hitch. Over, under, round, through. “Cas.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“When it’s — whatever it is. Up there.” She stopped. “I don’t want to talk about it. I only want to say that I’m glad. That’s all. Whatever happens. I’m glad it was you that came up the well.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand on her hair. The white streak, under his fingers, was softer than the rest.</p>\n<p>“So am I,” he said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She slept. He didn’t, for a while.</p>\n<p>When he was sure she was asleep, he eased out from under her — very slowly, very carefully, putting his rolled-up coat under her head — and went across the hut to the suit in the corner, and opened the chest compartment, and took out the recorder. Its little green light was still on. He sat down on the planks with his back against the suit’s leg and held it.</p>\n<p>Then he pressed the button. The second channel.</p>\n<p>“Nell,” he said, very quietly, so as not to wake her.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what came next. He sat in the dim violet light and listened to Ione breathe and the keel hum, and let it come.</p>\n<p>“I’ve been thinking about what you said. About being scared. That it’s allowed, and you just have to do it anyway.” He paused. “I think I got something wrong. For a long time. I think I thought — I thought being brave meant not minding what happened to you. I thought if you didn’t mind, it couldn’t hurt. So I stopped minding. About anything. For years. I thought that was — I don’t know. Being grown up. Being sensible.” He stopped. “It isn’t. It’s just being scared all the time and calling it something else.”</p>\n<p>The keel hummed.</p>\n<p>“So I want to tell you something. And I want you to hear it from me, even if it’s a long time from now, even if it’s from a machine. Because I’ve never said it. Not to you, not to your mother, not to anyone. Not even to me.”</p>\n<p>He looked across the hut at Ione asleep under the coats, the white streak in her hair, her splinted hand curled under her chin.</p>\n<p>“I want to live, Nell,” said Casimir Hale. “I want to live. I don’t know if I’m going to. But I want to. I want to very much.”</p>\n<p>He held the button down a moment longer, letting it record the silence after, and the keel, and her breathing.</p>\n<p>Then he let go.</p>"}, {"file": "15-the-carousel.md", "label": "Chapter 14", "title": "The Carousel", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5934, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol, test designation Continuance Ninety-One.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ruth Tally, 01:58, the night of the Kindling (heard 175,000 times)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The spindles leaned together as they rose.</p>\n<p>He hadn’t seen it from the city — from the city they had looked like eight straight black pillars going up into the glare — but from the hut under the gallery he could see it plainly. They leaned in toward each other the way the poles of a tent lean in toward the ridge, so that at the Anvil they stood kilometres apart and up here, just below the Rim, they were close enough to be joined. And they were joined. Gantries ran between them: long, narrow, open walkways of steel lattice slung from spindle to spindle across the violet gulf, some of them intact, some of them sagging, some of them hanging in broken halves from one end like dead vines.</p>\n<p>The gantry from Four to One was intact. Ione had checked it on her scouting trip. Three hundred metres of steel walkway, a metre wide, with a handrail on each side, sagging slightly in the middle, running from a door in the lattice just above the hut straight across the gulf to Spindle One.</p>\n<p>And at its far end, wrapped round the outside of Spindle One like a ring on a finger, was the gallery.</p>\n<p>He could see it from the gantry’s start. A long, low, curving structure with a roof and windows, circling the spindle, its windows facing inward — toward the Lamp, toward the core. The windows were dark. And above it, not far above, the spindle ended in a shape he knew from the mural in the crisis room, from the old footage, from the textbooks: a lens of black keelstone perched on the tip of the pillar like a seed on a stalk. The Crown. And above the Crown, the Rim — the vast dark ring that ran round the Lamp, joining the tops of all eight spindles — and above the Rim, filling the whole sky, very close, so close he could see the finest of its threads moving, the Lace.</p>\n<p>He was in the suit again. The keel hummed. It had dropped again. He’d tried not to listen to how much.</p>\n<p>Ione stood beside him at the start of the gantry with her rope on her shoulder and her pack on her back, looking across at the gallery. She was very calm. She had been very calm all morning. She had eaten, and drunk, and re-bound her wrist, and checked his harness twice, and said almost nothing, and been very calm.</p>\n<p>Her hands were shaking.</p>\n<p>He saw it when she lifted one to point. A fine, fast tremor, like a leaf in a draught. She saw him see it, and looked at it herself, and put the hand down by her side, and closed it.</p>\n<p>“It’s the only way,” she said. “The Lace has closed the other gantries. The gallery’s the only way to the Rim, and the Rim’s the only way to the Crown.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I’m just telling you.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” he said. “Ione. I know.”</p>\n<p>She nodded. She didn’t look at him. She stepped out onto the gantry.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It swayed. Every step, it swayed — not much, a slow gentle swing under their feet like a rope bridge — and the handrails creaked, and the violet gulf opened beneath them on both sides, four kilometres of it, down to the Anvil and the tiny still city at its heart. He didn’t look down. He looked at Ione’s back. She walked quickly, lightly, one hand on the rail, not looking down either.</p>\n<p>Halfway across, she stopped.</p>\n<p>“This is where I found it,” she said.</p>\n<p>He looked down at the walkway. Steel lattice, a metre wide, pitted with rust. There was nothing there. It was just a place.</p>\n<p>“Right here.” She touched the key at her throat. “I’d walked across here four thousand times. I knew every bolt.” She pointed at one — a rusted bolt-head on the edge of the walkway, a little bigger than the others. “That one’s loose. It rattles if you step on the plate next to it.” She stepped on the plate next to it. It rattled. “Four thousand times. And one time I came across and there was a key lying here. Right here. Next to the loose bolt.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the place. It was just a place. A square of rusted steel lattice in the middle of a walkway in the middle of the air.</p>\n<p><em>Where it had never been, and then it was.</em></p>\n<p>“Come on,” said Ione. And went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The gallery’s door was open. It had always been open, she said. It had been open eleven years ago. It had been open for a hundred and sixty-eight years. It was a plain steel door in the gallery’s outer wall with a faded sign above it — CROWN SUPPORT RING — AIR PLANT — AUTHORISED ONLY — and beyond it, darkness.</p>\n<p>Ione stopped on the threshold.</p>\n<p>She stood there for a long time. He didn’t touch her. He stood behind her on the gantry and waited, and the gantry swayed, and the Lace hung overhead, and she stood in the doorway of the place where she had spent three years of her life, with her hands closed by her sides.</p>\n<p>Then she said, low, “It’ll come on when we’re in. Don’t be frightened of it. It can’t see you.”</p>\n<p>And went in.</p>\n<p>He followed.</p>\n<p>It was a corridor. Long and curving, curving away to the left and right, following the round of the spindle; he could see about thirty metres each way before it bent out of sight. The outer wall, behind him, was plain steel. The inner wall — the one facing the spindle, facing the core — was windows. A long unbroken band of thick glass panes, floor to ceiling, one after another round the whole curve. And along the foot of the windows ran a trough. A long steel tank, knee high, full of water, full of something green and thick and softly glowing. The air-garden. It smelled of the sea, of the algae halls in the Works, of greenbread. The water in it was still. The green in it was alive.</p>\n<p>The windows were dark. And then they weren’t.</p>\n<p>It came on the way a screen comes on. There was nothing, and then — all at once, all round the curve, in every pane — there was light. White-violet. Soft. Shifting. And in it, standing in the corridor, as solid as he was, as close as Ione⁠—</p>\n<p>People.</p>\n<p>A room. He was standing in a room. He could see the walls of the corridor through it, the steel, the trough, the windows — but over them, <em>in</em> them, lit from inside like figures in a stained-glass window, there was a room. A long lens-shaped room with black walls and a great curved window, and consoles, and chairs, and people at the consoles. A young man with a mug. A woman on a stool by a camera with a shaved head and a lanyard. A thin young man holding a tablet. A heavy man at a board with his sleeves rolled up. Through the great window of the room, hanging in the dark, a single point of white-violet light, hard and perfect as a nail.</p>\n<p>And in the middle of the room, combing his hair in the reflection of the window, a tall man with silver hair and a good dark jacket, with a key on a chain round his neck.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<p>The voice came from everywhere. From the windows, from the walls, from the air. It was thin and slightly hollow, like a voice in a recording, and perfectly clear.</p>\n<p>Cas stood in the corridor of the gallery with his hand on the wall and couldn’t move.</p>\n<p>“We’ll stop down,” said the woman with the camera. “You’ll look like a painting, sir.”</p>\n<p>And beside him, very softly, not looking at him, looking at the room — Ione said the next line. Her lips moved a half-second before the ghost’s did.</p>\n<p>“<em>Tally. How long until we’re ready?</em>”</p>\n<p>“Tally,” said Aldous Crane, without turning round. “How long until we’re ready?”</p>\n<p>And at the board — right there, three metres from Cas, standing with her hands on the edge of the console and her back to him — a woman in a white coat, thirty-eight years old, with dark hair pinned up and a mole on her jaw below her left ear, said:</p>\n<p>“We’re not.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He watched it all.</p>\n<p>He hadn’t meant to. He’d meant to keep moving — to follow Ione, who’d begun, slowly, to walk along the curve of the corridor, through the ghost-room, her hand trailing on the steel of the outer wall. But he couldn’t. He stood in the corridor with the light of the Crown moving over his visor and he watched it happen.</p>\n<p>Crane, with his good face, being patient. Ruth, at the board, being tired. <em>The protocol requires the interlocks off.</em> The aide with his tablet. <em>Director Obi has deferred to the Ministry.</em> He watched Crane say Maddy’s name. He watched Ruth’s face when he said it. He watched her put her thumb to her mouth and bite the side of it, without knowing she was doing it, while Crane talked about the Aurelian Compact and forty million homes.</p>\n<p>He watched her say <em>eleven minutes</em>.</p>\n<p>He watched them run the test. The call and response. <em>Rollover to manual. Interlocks off.</em> The green lights going amber on the board — he could read the board, he could read every figure on it, clear as print, lit up in ghost-light in the corridor wall. <em>One-oh-five. One-ten.</em> The coffee in Dieter’s mug shivering into rings. <em>Six is at plus nineteen.</em> The Lesion in the window, sharpening. Crane saying <em>five more</em> to the camera.</p>\n<p>Beside him, Ione said, very softly, “Now.”</p>\n<p>And Ruth closed her eyes. For about half a second.</p>\n<p>“One-thirty-five,” she said.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall began to run fast.</p>\n<p>He’d read it in pencil in a Log in the Tollers’ tower. He’d read it in the telemetry on a screen in a room with gold carpet. It wasn’t like reading it. The floor of the ghost-room dropped and everyone in it staggered, and the coffee mug left the desk and hung, and fell, slowly, slowly, its brown tongue of coffee curling out; and Fen at the tensioner board said <em>Six has unseated</em> a hair before his mouth moved; and in the great window of the ghost-room the Lesion <em>broke</em>.</p>\n<p>He watched it happen. The frost. The lace. The single hard point of light coming apart into edges on edges on edges, spreading outward, silently, in every direction. He’d seen the Lace for weeks now, from the city, from the spindle, filling the sky. He’d never seen it be born. It was the most terrible thing he had ever seen, and it was beautiful, and it made no sound.</p>\n<p>“Keystone,” said Ruth Tally, and ran.</p>\n<p>She ran right past him. Through him — he felt nothing; the light went over his visor and she was on the other side of him — and down the length of the ghost-room to its far end, where the corridor curved, and dropped to her knees and wrenched up a plate in the floor. He saw the plate. He saw the words on it, lit in ghost-light on the steel floor of the gallery, clear as a sign.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>He saw the keyhole under it: a narrow cross.</p>\n<p>And he saw — with a lurch that went through his whole body, that made him put out his hand to the wall — the mechanism under the plate. The housing. The black keelstone, the recessed ring round the keyhole. The fittings. He’d seen them before. He’d seen them in a schematic, in the back of a withdrawn paper, in a library at night twenty years ago. He knew what they were for. He knew what they would do, when the lock turned. He understood — standing in the gallery with the ghost-light running over him — exactly how the Clause had been built, and why, and what it would take to run it.</p>\n<p>Almost exactly.</p>\n<p><em>The sealing requires a witness.</em> He still didn’t know what that meant. But he knew, now, where the witness would have to stand.</p>\n<p>“Give me the key,” said Ruth.</p>\n<p>He watched Crane not give it to her. He watched Crane’s face do the thing it did — he’d read about it in her Log, <em>the moment he understood</em>, and now he saw it, the fear coming into that good face, the young frightened man coming out from behind the Deputy Minister. He watched Crane’s fingers close on the chain. Watched him begin, slowly, to lift it.</p>\n<p>He watched the gantry go.</p>\n<p>A single thread of the Lace — a hair of light — reached the frame of the ghost-Crown’s great window, and where it touched, the black keelstone went grey, and white, and sagged like wax. The walkway tore. Crane fell against the rail. The chain snapped.</p>\n<p>And the key fell.</p>\n<p>Not down. He’d wondered about that since the Log. He’d wondered about it since the mural. It left Crane’s chain and turned over once in the white-violet light and slid between the bars of the grating and fell <em>toward the window</em>. Toward the Lamp. Toward the light. Not down, the way things fell. Not up, the way the Lamp pushed. It fell <em>along</em> something — along a slope that wasn’t in space at all. And he understood, watching it, the way he’d understood the mechanism: near the Tear, the gradient of time was so steep that it was steeper than the gradient of anything else. Things near it didn’t fall down, or up. They fell <em>forward</em>. Into the future. Along the only slope that mattered. The key fell forward out of the Crown, through the glass as if the glass were a moment that had already passed, and into the Lace, and was gone.</p>\n<p>He knew where it had gone. He didn’t know where it had come out. He looked at the key round Ione’s neck.</p>\n<p>And at the far end of the ghost-room, on her knees by the Keystone, Ruth Tally looked up — at nothing. At an empty space in front of her, beside the open housing. She went completely still. Her face changed. Something in it opened. She looked at the empty space for about a second, as though someone were standing there, as though someone were looking back at her.</p>\n<p>“She always does that,” said Ione quietly, beside him. “Right there. At the Keystone, after the key goes. She looks at nothing like someone’s there.” She paused. “There’s never anyone there. I used to stand in the place she’s looking at. Hundreds of times. To see if it was me she saw.” She shook her head slightly. “It wasn’t.”</p>\n<p>The ghost-Ruth came back to herself. <em>Tally! Tally, the lift!</em> The Crown crew running. Ruth at the gate of the lift. The glass panel. The red lever.</p>\n<p>She pulled it.</p>\n<p>And the gallery windows went dark.</p>\n<p>For about three seconds, there was nothing. The corridor. The steel. The trough of green. The dark windows. Ione beside him, breathing, and the keel’s hum in his spine.</p>\n<p>Then, all round the curve, the light came on again.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They walked.</p>\n<p>The corridor curved round the spindle. The ghost-Crown went with them. It didn’t matter where you stood — the room was always there, in the windows, in the walls, laid over the gallery like a picture on glass. Nine minutes. <em>We’re not.</em> <em>Eleven minutes.</em> <em>One-thirty-five.</em> The key falling. Ruth looking at nothing. The lever. Dark. <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>Ione walked a little ahead of him, one hand trailing on the outer wall, and he saw that she was not looking at the ghosts. She was looking at the wall.</p>\n<p>The wall was covered in marks.</p>\n<p>He saw them after the second cycle: scratched into the steel, all along the outer wall of the corridor, at about the height of a woman’s shoulder. Fives. Strokes in fives, the fifth across the four, in rows. Row after row. They went along the wall as far as he could see in both directions, and when he looked up, they went up — above head height, up to the ceiling, where someone must have stood on the edge of the algae trough to reach. Thousands of them. Tens of thousands. Scratched with a nail, or a knife, or a rope-hook, into the steel. And every so often, among them, other marks. A word. A drawing. A face — a woman’s face, with her hair pinned up and a mole below her ear, drawn very carefully in scratched lines, over and over, twenty times, fifty.</p>\n<p>He stopped in front of one. It was very good. It had been done slowly, by someone with all the time in the world.</p>\n<p>“You counted,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I’m a Toller.” Ione didn’t stop walking. “I told you.” A pause. “There’s a place further round where I stopped. For the months on the floor. There’s a gap. You’ll see it.”</p>\n<p>He saw it. A stretch of wall about two metres long, where the fives simply stopped — and then, beyond it, started again, in a different hand. Stronger. Neater. As if someone had got up off the floor one day and decided to learn everybody’s name.</p>\n<p>There was a pallet of old rags on the floor under the window there, flattened, dusty. There was a tin cup by the algae trough. There was a length of rope, knotted into something complicated — a net, a sling — hanging from a pipe. There was a heap of little stones, polished smooth, that must have come up in someone’s pocket from the Anvil a long time ago, and been turned over and over in someone’s fingers until they shone.</p>\n<p>Ione walked past all of it without looking.</p>\n<p>The corridor curved. Ahead of them, round the bend, he could see a door — the gallery’s inner door, Ione had said, the one that led out onto the Rim. It was standing open. Beyond it, violet air, and the great dark curve of the Rim.</p>\n<p>They walked toward it. It came closer. They reached it. Ione stopped, just short of the threshold, and he stopped beside her.</p>\n<p>“Watch,” she said.</p>\n<p>She stepped through.</p>\n<p>And the door they had come in by — the outer door, the plain steel door with CROWN SUPPORT RING above it — was in front of them. They were standing just inside it, looking out at the gantry to Spindle Four. The gallery curved away on either side.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took him eleven cycles to believe it.</p>\n<p>He tried everything she’d tried. He knew she’d tried it all; he tried it anyway, because he had to, because he was a man who had to see a thing for himself before he could know it. He went through the inner door, and came in through the outer. He went through the outer door, onto the gantry, and walked twenty metres along it toward Spindle Four, and turned round, and came back — and the door he came back through was the inner one, the Rim door, and he was walking into the gallery from the other side. He went through a window. The panes were thick but one of them, down near the trough, was cracked, and he put his gauntlet through the crack and pulled out a piece and climbed through — and fell forward onto the floor of the gallery corridor, facing back the way he’d come, with the ghost-Crown lighting up around him. He tried the roof. There was a hatch. It opened onto the roof of the gallery, a curving strip of steel roof round the spindle’s side, and he climbed out onto it and walked along it and came down through the hatch he’d gone up.</p>\n<p>Every way out was a way in.</p>\n<p>“It’s a ring,” he said. He was standing in the middle of the corridor. His voice sounded strange. “It’s — the whole gallery’s been wrapped. Topologically. Every boundary’s been glued to another boundary. Space goes out one side and comes in the other.” He looked at the light in the windows. “There’s a filament. Through the gallery. Somewhere. The Tear’s run a thread through here and the space round it’s been — folded round it. Like a sleeve round an arm.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Ione. She was sitting on the edge of the algae trough, watching him. “I know.”</p>\n<p>“There’ll be a seam. There has to be. Where the edges meet. Where it’s been joined.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. There was one. There was one where the key came from. Where my — where the woman was standing.” She looked down at her hands. “It’s not there now. I’ve been looking for it for eleven cycles. I’ve been looking for anything. Anything different.” She was quiet. “There isn’t anything different. There’s never anything different. That’s the thing about it. That’s the whole thing.”</p>\n<p>The ghost-Crown moved round them. <em>Five more, Ruth.</em></p>\n<p>“The seam was only there,” said Ione, “because something came through it. The key came through it. The woman came through it. Something from outside came in, and where it came in, you could see the join. Like a thread pulled through cloth.” Her voice was very even. “Nothing’s come in. So there’s no join to see. There’s just the ring. And the light. And the nine minutes.”</p>\n<p>She put her hands on the edge of the trough, on either side of her, and held on to it.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to sit down now,” she said. “For a little while. I’m sorry.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d been afraid of it since the gantry. Since the doorway. Since he’d seen her hands shake.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the floor. Right there, by the trough, with her back against the green-lit steel and her knees up, in the place where the scratched fives stopped. She put her arms round her knees. She put her forehead down on her arms. And the ghost-Crown moved over her — Ruth running for the Keystone right through the place where she sat, the key falling through her, the light going over her like water over a stone — and she didn’t move.</p>\n<p>He knelt down in front of her. The suit made it hard. He did it anyway.</p>\n<p>“Ione.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“Ione. Look at me.”</p>\n<p>“I can hear you,” she said into her arms. Her voice was quite calm. “I’m not gone. I’m just — sitting. I’m going to sit for a bit. You can look for the seam. I’ll be here. I’ll just be here.”</p>\n<p><em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>He sat down beside her. He got the suit’s back against the trough and his shoulder against her shoulder, the way she’d sat beside him in the hut, and he sat there.</p>\n<p>The nine minutes went round. <em>We’re not.</em> He counted them. He couldn’t help it. Twelve. Fifteen. Twenty. He sat beside her and counted the cycles, and the light went over them, and she didn’t move except to breathe. Once, round about the twenty-fifth, he felt her shoulders start to shake, very slightly, against his arm, and he put his arm round her, the suit’s heavy arm, and she leaned into it and went still again.</p>\n<p>He talked to her.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what else to do. He talked about anything. He told her about Nell’s ceiling, the glow-in-the-dark stars, the constellations put up with a chart and a ruler. He told her their names. Betelgeuse, which was red. Rigel, which was blue. Sirius, the brightest, which the old sailors called the Dog Star. Deneb. Vega. Fomalhaut, which was lonely, which sat by itself in a part of the sky with nothing else bright near it. He told her about rain — what it was like, standing in it, on the Tier Nine arterial, the summer the Canopy failed over the northern tiers for a week, when he was a boy; how it came down so hard it bounced; how warm it was. He told her about the sea. About Teo in the Tessarine underpass with twenty-three people on a rope. About Ben, and MINISTER →, and I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS. About Adeyemi putting his palm flat on the walls of the Works and listening.</p>\n<p><em>One-thirty-five.</em></p>\n<p>“Keep talking,” she said, once, into her arms. That was all. So he kept talking.</p>\n<p>And while he talked, he thought.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t help that either. He thought about seams. About a thread of the Tear running through this gallery, and the space folded round it, and the place where the fold must be glued. He thought about how you’d find a seam in a thing you couldn’t see, if nothing came through it. He thought about frost on a window. About a hair of light crossing a storeroom and branching into itself, the same pattern at every scale. About a man in a hall of algae with a tape measure and eleven years, measuring a filament’s branches, month after month, finding the rule.</p>\n<p>He stopped talking.</p>\n<p>“Ione,” he said.</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>He took the sketchbook out of the suit. He had to open the neck-ring to get at it, and he did, and the warm algae-smelling air came in, and he pulled out the black cloth-bound book and opened it on his knee, in the ghost-light, and turned the pages. Thirty pages of Ben. Grease-pencil diagrams copied in his own pencil and Teo’s. The branching. The throats. <em>D = 2.73.</em> <em>IF THERE’S A SHEAR IT’LL HAVE SEAMS. GO THROUGH THE THROATS.</em></p>\n<p>“The fold follows the thread,” he said. He wasn’t sure if he was talking to her or to himself. “The space is folded round the filament. So the seam’s where the filament forks. It has to be. Ben showed it. Where it branches — the throat between the branches — that’s where the geometry’s weakest. That’s where it had to be glued.” He turned a page. “And the branching’s self-similar. Same ratio at every scale. So if I know where one fork is, I know where the next one has to be⁠—”</p>\n<p>He looked up. Round the gallery. At the ghost-light. At the key, falling through it, toward the window, toward the Lamp. At the thread of the Lace in the ghost-window, the first one, the one that touched the Crown’s frame.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the window,” he said. “The filament. The one that made this. It’s the first thread. The one that touched the Crown. It’s still here. It’s <em>in the light</em>.” He was on his knees now, the sketchbook in one hand. “Ione. Ione, you’ve watched that thread a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. You know it better than anyone alive. Where does it fork? The first fork. After it touches the frame. Where does it <em>branch</em>?”</p>\n<p>She lifted her head.</p>\n<p>Her face was wet. Her eyes were red. She looked at him for a moment as if she didn’t know who he was. Then she looked past him, at the ghost-window, where the Crown was just beginning its fourth minute. <em>Rollover to manual.</em></p>\n<p>“Wait,” she said. “It’s not — it doesn’t come for five minutes.”</p>\n<p>They waited. He knelt in front of her with the sketchbook open. She sat with her back against the trough, watching the window.</p>\n<p><em>One-thirty-five.</em></p>\n<p>The Lesion broke. The Lace was born. And a single thread of it — fine as a hair, bright as a bulb — came out of the frost and across the ghost-window and touched the frame.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Ione. She was on her knees too now. She was pointing. “<em>There.</em> It touches the frame there. And it goes along it, that way, for — about an arm. And then it splits. Two. There. And the left one goes up and the right one goes⁠—” she stopped “⁠—the right one goes toward the Keystone. Toward where Ruth is. It branches again just before it gets to her. I’ve watched it. I’ve watched it a thousand times, I used to lie on the floor and watch it — it branches just before it gets to her and one branch goes up and one goes⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Where does the one that goes up <em>go</em>?”</p>\n<p>She turned her head, following it. Up. Out of the ghost-window, across the ghost-ceiling, over their heads. Her eyes moved along something he couldn’t see.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said. She was looking at a place on the outer wall of the gallery, above the scratched fives, near the ceiling, on the curve of the corridor six metres away. “It goes there. It goes into the wall there. Every time.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the sketchbook. At the branching ratio. At the throats. Ben’s writing, in his own pencil. <em>THE SEAM IS ALWAYS BETWEEN TWO BRANCHES. NEVER ON ONE.</em></p>\n<p>“Then the seam’s between them,” he said. “Between the branch that goes to Ruth and the one that goes into the wall. In the throat.” He did it on the page, fast, the ratio, the angles. “It’ll be — there.” He pointed. A place in the middle of the corridor, a little way round the curve, at about head height, in empty air. “Somewhere there. Ione. You know what a seam looks like. You’ve seen one. Look.”</p>\n<p>She got up. She walked toward the place he’d pointed at. She walked slowly, the way she’d walked on the gantry, one hand out. The ghost-Crown moved round her. Ruth ran past her for the Keystone.</p>\n<p>She stopped. Her head tilted.</p>\n<p>“Oh,” she said.</p>\n<p>He came up beside her. He looked.</p>\n<p>It was almost nothing. A shimmer. The faintest ripple in the air, like the edge of heat over a road, like the edge of the lee in Teo’s booth. A vertical line of wrongness hanging in the middle of the corridor, a hand’s width wide, the height of a person, that you would never have seen if you hadn’t known exactly where to look. Through it, the corridor on the far side jumped very slightly, as if seen through a flaw in glass.</p>\n<p>“That’s it,” said Ione. Her voice was very small. “That’s what it looked like. When the woman was standing there. That’s exactly what it looked like.”</p>\n<p>“Ben,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“Ben found it.” He heard his own voice shaking. “Eleven years in a room with a tape measure. He found it.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked at him. Then at the shimmer. Then she held out her good hand, without looking, and he took it in his gauntlet, and they stepped through together.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They were on the Rim.</p>\n<p>It was a walkway. A vast one — ten metres wide, steel plate on keelstone, with a rail on the inner side — running round the top of the Vault in a great dark circle, joining the tops of all eight spindles, curving away on both sides until it met itself far across the sky. Behind them, the gallery; its inner door stood open, and through it he could see the corridor, and the windows, and the ghost-light moving in them. <em>We’re not.</em> Ahead of them, over the rail, the drop into the Vault — four kilometres of violet air — and across it, the Lamp.</p>\n<p>It was very close. He’d never been this close. It hung out there in the middle of the air, the single hard point, too bright to look at, and from it, in every direction, the Lace came out — the endless branching filigree, filling the whole sky above them — and its lowest threads hung down, just beyond the rail, fine and bright and moving slowly, close enough that he could see them branching into each other at scales so small his eyes couldn’t follow.</p>\n<p>Above and to the left, a little way along the Rim, Spindle One rose up past the rail and ended in the Crown.</p>\n<p>Ione let go of his hand.</p>\n<p>She walked to the rail. She put both her hands on it, the good one and the splinted one. She stood there looking out at the Lamp. And then her knees went, and she sat down on the steel plate of the Rim with her back against the rail and her hands over her face, and she cried.</p>\n<p>Not the way she’d cried in the hut. Not quiet. She cried the way a child cries — great gasping, heaving, ugly sobs that shook her whole body, that went on and on, that bent her over her knees. He’d never heard a sound like it. He sat down beside her on the steel plate and didn’t touch her, because he didn’t know if she’d want him to, and then touched her anyway, his gauntlet on her back, and she didn’t push it away.</p>\n<p>“I thought⁠—” she said. She couldn’t get it out. “I thought I’d never — I thought I’d never have to — see her again⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Every time. Every <em>time</em>. She closes her eyes. And I sit next to her. And I think <em>don’t</em>. And she⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“She was my <em>friend</em>,” said Ione Tally, into her hands. “She was my best friend and I had to watch her do it a hundred and seventy-five thousand times and she never once heard me say don’t.”</p>\n<p>He put his arms round her. She let him. She turned into the suit and put her face against the chestplate and held on to the harness and cried, and he held her, on the Rim, at the top of the world, with the Lamp hanging in the air in front of them and the Lace moving slowly overhead and the ghost-light still going round and round in the gallery behind them. <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>It went on for a long time.</p>\n<p>When it was over, she sat up. She wiped her face with her good hand. She looked at the gallery door, and the light moving in it. She looked at it for a while.</p>\n<p>Then she turned her back on it.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said, and her voice was hoarse, and steady. “That’s done.”</p>\n<p>She got up. She looked along the Rim toward Spindle One, where it rose past the rail and ended in the Crown. And beyond it — between them and the Crown, hanging down across the Rim in long bright curtains, filling the walkway from rail to wall, moving slowly — the lowest threads of the Lace.</p>\n<p>It was like a forest. A forest of frost. Of light. Thread on thread on thread, branching and branching, hanging down from the sky to the steel of the Rim and spreading across it in fine luminous roots, a hundred metres of it, between them and the Crown.</p>\n<p>“That’s the Lace,” said Ione. “That’s the bit that kills you.”</p>\n<p>He looked at it. He listened to the keel humming in his spine. It had dropped again. He knew exactly how much.</p>\n<p>“Hold on to me,” he said. “Whatever you see.”</p>"}, {"file": "16-the-lace.md", "label": "Chapter 15", "title": "The Lace", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 4820, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Zero isn’t nothing. Zero’s balanced.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ione Tally, on the Rim</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">She stood on his boots.</p>\n<p>That was how they did it, in the end. They tried it the way they’d done it in the Breach — her legs round his waist, her arms round his neck-ring — and found he couldn’t see past her to walk, and she couldn’t see behind him to guide, and the bubble, a metre across, barely held the two of them in that shape anyway. So she turned round, and stood with her back against his chestplate, and stepped up onto the toes of his armoured boots, and he put his arms round her, one across her chest and one across her waist, and held her there, pressed back against him, her head under his chin. The exo took the extra weight with a grunt.</p>\n<p>“Like a child,” she said, “dancing with its father.”</p>\n<p>“I never did that with Nell.”</p>\n<p>“No?” She tipped her head back against his visor to look up at him. “You should.”</p>\n<p>The keel hummed. He could feel the bubble round them, now that he knew to feel for it — a sort of tightness, a stillness, a metre-wide egg of calm. With her inside it, pressed against him, it was full. There was nothing to spare. If she leaned out, even a little, even her hand, it would be outside.</p>\n<p>“Don’t lean,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Don’t put your hand out. Don’t touch anything. If you need to point, tell me.”</p>\n<p>“I know, Cas.” Her voice was very steady. She was looking ahead, along the Rim, at the forest of light hanging down across it. “Left foot.”</p>\n<p>He moved his left foot. She moved with it.</p>\n<p>They walked into the Lace.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The first thing was the light.</p>\n<p>Outside it — on the Rim by the gallery, on the spindle, in the city — the Lace had been a thing in the sky. Beautiful, terrible, far. In it, it was the air. The threads hung down all round them, fine and bright, some as thick as a wrist and some so fine they were only a glitter, branching and branching into themselves, reaching down from the sky to the steel of the Rim and spreading across it in fans and roots and deltas of white-violet light. They moved. Slowly, the way weed moves in a current. Close to, he could see them grow — see a branch put out a smaller branch, and that one a smaller, and that one a smaller, until the eye lost them and they became a kind of mist, a glow, a haze of edges on edges round every edge.</p>\n<p>And between them, on the Rim’s steel, there were arrows.</p>\n<p>White arrows. Chalk. Drawn quickly on the black steel plate, one every few metres, pointing ahead and a little left, between two great hanging threads.</p>\n<p>Ione went stiff against him.</p>\n<p>“Those are mine,” she said.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. They were rigger’s arrows — the same quick, slanting, three-stroke arrows she’d scratched on the struts of Spindle Four, the same hand. He’d have known them anywhere now.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been here,” he said. “Before. When you tried⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.” Her voice had gone very flat. “I never got this far. Not either time. The Lace was lower on the south side, I never got onto the Rim at all.” She was staring at the nearest arrow. “I’ve never been here. I’ve never drawn these.”</p>\n<p>“Then⁠—”</p>\n<p>“They’re fresh,” said Ione. “Look. The chalk’s still loose. You could blow it off.”</p>\n<p>They stood there on the Rim with the Lace moving round them.</p>\n<p>“Follow them,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I haven’t made them yet.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Cas. “Follow them anyway.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment. Then she said, “Left foot,” and they followed them.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He would never be able to tell anyone, afterwards, how far they went, or how long it took. The Rim was a hundred metres, perhaps, from the gallery door to the stair that went up to the Crown. It should have taken a few minutes. They walked it step by step, her feet on his boots, following the chalk arrows between the threads, and it went on, and on, and he lost the sense of it; time went strange, the way it had gone strange in the Shear, and he stopped trying to count.</p>\n<p>The threads were close. Some of them hung so close that they passed within an arm’s length of the bubble, and he could see — <em>not look</em>, he told himself, <em>don’t look</em> — he could see into them.</p>\n<p>He looked.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t help it. It was like the Lamp; it pulled the eye. A thread as thick as a finger hung just beside his visor, and he looked into it, and it wasn’t a thread. It was a tunnel. It opened, the way a pinhole opens when you put your eye to it, into a space — a vast space, bright and violet, and in it, very small, very far, a sphere. A dark sphere, with a point of light at its heart and eight black pillars rising from its floor, and a tiny tangled city at the pillars’ feet, and a ring round the top of it, and on the ring, a forest of light, and in the forest, two minute figures, pressed together, looking into a thread.</p>\n<p>He looked into the thread they were looking into.</p>\n<p>The Vault. The pillars. The city. The ring. The forest. Two figures. A thread.</p>\n<p>And into that one.</p>\n<p>It went down. It went down for ever. Every thread held the Vault, and every Vault held the ring and the forest and the two of them, and every one of those held a thread, and every thread — he was falling, he was falling down it, down the tunnel, the Vault and the Vault and the Vault opening and opening and opening in front of him, smaller and smaller, finer and finer, and none of them ever getting any smaller at all, every one exactly as large as the last, because size meant nothing here, because there was no bottom, because it was <em>edge all the way down</em>⁠—</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas.</em>”</p>\n<p>Her voice. Her head, knocking back hard against his visor.</p>\n<p>“Don’t look into them,” said Ione. “I said. Don’t look. Look at the arrows.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the arrows. His whole body was shaking inside the suit.</p>\n<p>“How did you know⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because I did it too,” she said. “Just now. Before you. I looked into one.” Her voice was very steady and very small. “Left foot.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They heard themselves before they spoke.</p>\n<p>It happened near the middle of the Rim, where the threads were thickest. They were standing still, between two great hanging roots of light, while Ione looked for the next arrow — and her voice said, clearly, close beside his ear, “<em>Right. Right, and then straight.</em>”</p>\n<p>She hadn’t spoken. Her mouth hadn’t moved. He was looking at the side of her face; he’d have seen.</p>\n<p>A second later, she said, “Right. Right, and then straight.” And stopped. And went rigid against him.</p>\n<p>“Did you hear⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>They stood there. The threads moved slowly round them.</p>\n<p>And then another voice — his voice, his own voice, tinny and close as if from inside his own helmet — said, “<em>Ione, I can’t see the</em>⁠—” and stopped, cut off. He hadn’t said it. He hadn’t said anything.</p>\n<p>“The sound’s coming loose,” he said. His own voice sounded strange to him, now, as if he couldn’t be sure which one was real. “Out of order. It’s the gradient, it’s so steep here that — that sound’s arriving before⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>It was the suit</em>,” said Ione’s voice.</p>\n<p>It came from nowhere. From the air, from the threads. Not her voice now, but her voice from somewhere else — cracked, hoarse, as if she’d been crying, as if she’d been shouting. Close and far at once.</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas, it was the suit, it was never — it was the suit —</em>”</p>\n<p>And gone.</p>\n<p>She’d gone white. He could see it in the side of her face, the blood gone out of her cheek, her lips pressed together.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t say that,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I haven’t said that. I don’t know what that means.” Her hand, her good hand, had come up and closed on his wrist where it crossed her chest. Very tight. “What suit? What does it — what does that <em>mean</em>, Cas?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know. I don’t know. Ione. We haven’t said it yet. We don’t know what it means because we haven’t said it <em>yet</em>.” He heard his own voice rising. He made it stop. “Look at the arrows. Please. Look at the arrows.”</p>\n<p>She looked at the arrows. After a long moment she said, “Straight,” and they went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw it about halfway.</p>\n<p>There was a place where the threads thinned — a gap, a clearing in the forest of light — and the Rim’s inner rail was right there beside them, and beyond the rail, the Vault. The drop. Four kilometres of violet air down to the tiny still city. And off to the left, rising up out of that gulf, the black lattice of Spindle Four — the spindle they had climbed — its upper structure hung with the lowest trailing threads of the Lace, so that from here it looked like a tree wrapped in cobwebs of light.</p>\n<p>And on it, a long way down, something was moving.</p>\n<p>He stopped. Ione stopped with him.</p>\n<p>It was a figure. Small, at this distance — half a kilometre down, perhaps more — climbing down the inside of the spindle’s lattice. Grey. Hulking. It went down a ladder, and along a walkway, and down again, slowly, carefully, the way you climb when you’re tired. And on its back, there was a light. A soft band of light round a rounded housing, the size of a keg.</p>\n<p>A keel.</p>\n<p>He knew it. He knew it before he could have known it. He’d been living inside it for weeks. He knew the shape of those shoulder plates, the gouge down the left side of the back where he’d hit a beam in a shaft in the Undercroft. He knew the housing on its back — the band of lights, the white number painted on it by a boy in a lab, <em>1</em>, Teo’s number, Teo’s keel. He knew the harness round the hardpoints, spliced in rigger’s strapping, the webbing that Ione had spent three nights making.</p>\n<p>His suit.</p>\n<p>His suit, coming down Spindle Four. Alone.</p>\n<p>Just one. He looked. He looked for a long time. There was nobody else on the spindle. Nobody with it. Nobody on the ladder above it or the walkway below. One grey figure in a keel suit, climbing down, alone, through the cobwebs of light, toward the city.</p>\n<p>As he watched, it stopped.</p>\n<p>It stood on a walkway, a small grey shape against the black lattice, and it turned. It tipped its head back — the visor, a dark curve, catching the Lamp’s light — and looked up. Up at the Rim. At the Lace. At them.</p>\n<p>And it raised its hand.</p>\n<p>It held it there. A small grey arm, raised, the palm out. For a long moment. Then it lowered it, and turned, and went on down.</p>\n<p>He stood with his arms round Ione on the Rim and didn’t move.</p>\n<p><em>It’s me</em>, he thought.</p>\n<p>It came into his head whole and finished, the way thoughts had come in the Shear. It’s me. That’s me, coming down. I come back. I get to the Crown and I do whatever it is and I come back down the spindle, alone, in my suit. And she⁠—</p>\n<p>He looked down at the top of her head, under his chin. At the white streak.</p>\n<p><em>There are only three suits.</em> One was at the bottom of the Anvil in a booth, in a chair, without its keel. One was in a hall in the Works, full of dust. And one was his. And nobody came down with it.</p>\n<p>And she⁠—</p>\n<p>“Did you see it?” said Ione.</p>\n<p>Her voice was very quiet. She was looking down at the spindle too. At the place where the figure had been. It was gone now, further down, behind the lattice.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>“That’s what I saw,” said Ione. “When I was seventeen. Coming down from the Rim. That’s what I saw, from the other spindle. That’s exactly what I saw.” She was quiet. “It raised its hand then too.”</p>\n<p>He said nothing.</p>\n<p>Neither did she. She stood against him, on his boots, with his arms round her, looking down at Spindle Four. He couldn’t see her face. He could feel her breathing — slow, even, very controlled — through the chestplate.</p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she knows what it means.</em></p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she’s known since she was seventeen. She’s known for eleven years. Someone comes down alone.</em></p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she’s been waiting at the top of the well for eleven years for the man who was going to come back without her.</em></p>\n<p>She said nothing. He said nothing. He tightened his arms round her, and felt her hand close on his wrist, and after a long time she said, “Left foot,” very quietly, and they went on.</p>\n<p>And somewhere under his breastbone, where the sketchbook was, a thing that had been small and quiet — the thing he’d found in the Lee watching her sleep, <em>I want to see what happens next</em> — turned over, and set its jaw, and became something else.</p>\n<p><em>No</em>, it said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It started with his hand.</p>\n<p>He’d been watching the arrows. Step by step, her feet on his boots, following the chalk. And he looked down at his right hand, where it lay across her waist, and it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>It was there. It was. He could feel it — her belt under his gauntlet, the warmth of her through the cloth. But when he looked at it, it was — thin. Faded. The grey plating of the gauntlet was grey, and then it was the colour of fog, and then he could see her belt through it, the buckle, the cloth, as if his hand were a picture of a hand painted on glass. And then it was solid again. And then it wasn’t.</p>\n<p>“Ione.”</p>\n<p>“I see it.” Her voice was tight. “Mine too.”</p>\n<p>He looked. Her hand on his wrist — her good hand, the scarred brown rigger’s hand — was going in and out. There, and fading, and there. Like a lamp flickering. Like a signal coming and going.</p>\n<p>“It’s the edge,” he said. “We’re near the edge. The Tear. It’s⁠—” He tried to think. He couldn’t think. “The boundary between being and not being is — the vacuum’s so stretched here that — things aren’t sure. Whether they⁠—”</p>\n<p>He looked up from his hand. And the Rim in front of them was flickering too. The steel plate. The rail. There, and fog, and there. The chalk arrows came and went. A whole section of the rail ahead of them simply wasn’t there — was a gap, a nothing, violet air — and then was there, solid, black, with rust on it. The threads of the Lace were the only things that didn’t flicker. They hung round them, bright and steady and moving slowly, as if they were the only things in the world that were sure.</p>\n<p>And then he looked down at the woman in his arms, and didn’t know who she was.</p>\n<p>It lasted a second. Less. He was holding someone. A short broad woman with dark hair and a white streak in it, standing on his boots, pressed back against his chest, her hand on his wrist. And he didn’t know her. He didn’t know her name. He didn’t know why he was holding her, or where he was, or why, and a terror went through him so absolute, so bottomless, that it was like falling into the thread again, falling down the tunnel of Vaults for ever. <em>Who is this. Who is this. Who am I holding.</em> He looked at the white streak. He looked at the scar on her forearm. He <em>knew</em> he knew them. He knew there was a word for them, a name, a whole life, and it wasn’t there. It was gone. It had flickered out, like the rail.</p>\n<p>Then it came back.</p>\n<p><em>Ione.</em></p>\n<p>It came back like the rail came back, all at once, solid and rusted and real. Ione. Ione Tally. The top of the well. <em>What time is it outside.</em> The loft. The hut. <em>I’m glad it was you.</em> It came back, all of it, and he was holding her so hard that she made a sound, and he couldn’t loosen his arms, he couldn’t make them loosen⁠—</p>\n<p>“Cas,” she said. “<em>Cas.</em> I’m here. I’m here.”</p>\n<p>“I didn’t know you.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“For a second. I didn’t — I didn’t know who you <em>were</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione. “I know. I didn’t know you either. Just now. I looked at your arm and I didn’t know whose it was.” Her voice was shaking. Her hand had moved from his wrist and was holding his gauntlet — holding it, pressing it against her waist, hard. “It came back. It came back. We’re here. Hold on. Hold on to me. Don’t let go of me, whatever you see. Don’t let go.”</p>\n<p>“I won’t.”</p>\n<p>“Whatever you see.”</p>\n<p>“I won’t,” he said. “I won’t.”</p>\n<p>They stood there, holding on to each other, on a Rim that came and went, in a forest of light that didn’t.</p>\n<p>Then she said, “Straight,” in a voice that was nearly steady, and they went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The worst of it was near the end.</p>\n<p>The stair to the Crown was in sight. Twenty metres ahead — a steel stair going up from the Rim, beside the black lattice of Spindle One’s top, to a hatch in the underside of the black lens of the Crown. The threads were thinner here. The arrows led straight to it.</p>\n<p>And the Lamp was very close.</p>\n<p>The Rim curved round it here, at its nearest. The inner rail was right beside them, and beyond it — twenty metres out, thirty, it was impossible to tell — hung the point. The Lesion. The Lamp. The hard white-violet nail driven through the world. He’d never been this close. Nobody had, since the Kindling. It was too bright to look at; he looked anyway; the visor darkened, darkened again, went almost black, and through it, the point burned.</p>\n<p>And he saw into it.</p>\n<p>He never had the words for it. Afterwards, when he tried — to himself, in the Crown, in the time that was left — the nearest he could come was that it was like looking into the thread, the tunnel of Vaults, only there was no Vault at the bottom. There was no bottom at all. There was the edge, and the edge, and the edge, going in, and in, and under the edge, at the very heart of it, where the vacuum had been stretched so far below nothing that it had split — he saw what nothing was.</p>\n<p>It was a ledger.</p>\n<p>That was the word that came to him. He didn’t choose it. It came up out of him from somewhere very deep, from fifteen years of figures on the backs of envelopes, from the Margin Review, from every sum he’d ever done. A ledger. Two columns. On one side, everything. Every star, every stone, every drop of water, every living thing, every city, every thought; all the positive, all the <em>something</em>, all of it, the whole of the universe that had ever been lit. And on the other side, exactly balancing it, the debt. The gravity. The stretched, straining, negative pull of everything on everything, the price of being. And at the bottom of the page, the sum.</p>\n<p>Zero.</p>\n<p>He’d read about it. As a student. An old idea, older than the Long Heat — a physicist had written a paper asking whether the universe itself might be nothing more than a fluctuation of the vacuum. A loan. Whether, if you added it all up, everything that existed and everything it owed, the total would come to exactly nothing. He’d thought it was a pretty idea. He’d thought it was a clever way to answer the question of where everything came from: <em>from nowhere, and it all cancels</em>.</p>\n<p>He wasn’t thinking it now. He was <em>seeing</em> it. He was standing on the Rim with his arms round a woman and looking into the torn place at the heart of the Draw, where the vacuum had been pulled so far below nothing that the bottom had come out of it, and he could see — not with his eyes; with something under his eyes — the whole of everything, balanced, on the edge of nothing. All of it borrowed. All of it owed. The stars on Nell’s ceiling. The city. The Commonweal and its six hundred million, and the Draw, and the Lamp, and Holdfast, and the forty-seven. Ben. Teo. Himself. Ione. All of it a loan against nothing, and all of it, in the end, summing to exactly zero.</p>\n<p><em>None of it’s real.</em></p>\n<p>It came into him the way the cold comes into you in winter water: not all at once, but everywhere.</p>\n<p><em>None of it’s real. None of it matters. It all cancels. Everything I’ve done and everything I haven’t. The pen. The truth in the Commons. The forty-seven. Ben’s eleven years. Ruth’s fifty. All of it. It all adds up to nothing. It always did. It always will. There’s nothing to pay because there was never anything to borrow. There’s nothing. There’s nothing. There’s only the sum, and the sum is⁠—</em></p>\n<p>He had stopped walking.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know when he’d stopped. He was standing on the Rim, still, with his arms round her, looking into the Lamp, and he could feel the weight of the zero on him like the weight of the whole Vault, like the weight at the top of the Breach, and his legs were going. His arms were going. He could feel the exo trying to hold him up and not knowing why, and he thought: <em>let it go. Let it go. What does it matter. It’s all zero. Let go⁠—</em></p>\n<p>“<em>Cas.</em>”</p>\n<p>Far away.</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas. Casimir. Look at me.</em>”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t. He was looking at the Lamp.</p>\n<p>Something hit his visor. Hard. The back of her head, slamming back against the glass. Once. Twice. The visor rang like a bell.</p>\n<p>“<em>Look at me.</em>”</p>\n<p>She’d turned round. On his boots, in his arms, inside the bubble, she’d turned round to face him — he felt her do it, felt her body twist against the chestplate — and now her face was in front of his visor, filling it, blotting out the Lamp. Her face, three centimetres away. Her eyes. Grey-green. Not patient. Wild.</p>\n<p>“I saw it too,” said Ione. “I saw it. Just now. I saw what you saw. I know. I know what it is.”</p>\n<p>“It’s zero,” he said. His voice came out of him from very far off. “It’s all zero, Ione. All of it. It cancels. None of it⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>I know.</em>”</p>\n<p>She had her hands on either side of his helmet. The good one and the splinted one. Holding his head. Making him look at her.</p>\n<p>“I watched the light in the gallery for three years,” she said. “It was nothing. It was <em>light</em>. It wasn’t anybody. It was nine minutes going round and round with nobody in it. It cancelled out, every time — Ruth said one-thirty-five and the key fell and she pulled the hammer and it went dark and started again and at the end of every cycle it added up to nothing. Nothing. Nothing had changed. Nothing ever would.” Her eyes were bright and wet and fierce. “And I loved them anyway. Do you hear me? I <em>loved them anyway</em>. It all cancelled and I loved them. That’s all. That’s all there is. Zero isn’t <em>nothing</em>, Cas.” She pressed her forehead against his visor. “Zero’s <em>balanced</em>.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“Count,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“You’re good at it. You count everything. You counted the hammer all night. You counted the rungs in the conduit. You counted Ben’s seconds.” She pulled back so that he could see her face. “Count me.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t understand.</p>\n<p>“<em>Count me</em>,” she said. “I’m one. I’m here. I’m one thing, and I’m here, and I’m not zero, I’m <em>one</em>. Count me.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her. At her face in front of his visor, filling it. Her eyes. The white streak. The stitches over her eye. The mole — he’d never noticed it — the tiny mole at the corner of her mouth. Her breath fogging the glass.</p>\n<p>“One,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Again.”</p>\n<p>“One.”</p>\n<p>“Again.”</p>\n<p>“One.” His voice was coming back. It was coming back from wherever it had gone, like the rail, like her name, solid and rusted and real. “One. One. You’re one. You’re here.”</p>\n<p>“I’m here,” said Ione. “And you’re one. And that’s two. And it doesn’t matter what the sum is. It doesn’t matter what it all comes to at the bottom of the page. We’re not the bottom of the page. We’re <em>here</em>.” She was crying. “Now turn round. Turn round, and look at the stair, and don’t look at that thing again, and walk. Please. Please, Cas. I can’t carry you. You have to walk.”</p>\n<p>He turned away from the Lamp.</p>\n<p>He looked at the stair. Twenty metres. Steel steps going up to a hatch in the black underside of the Crown. The chalk arrows leading straight to it, fresh and white on the flickering steel.</p>\n<p>“Left foot,” said Ione, and turned round again in his arms, and stood on his boots.</p>\n<p>He walked.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hatch was open. Of course it was open; the Crown crew had come down through it the night of the Kindling, running for the lift, and nobody had closed it after. It was a round steel hatch in the black keelstone underside of the Crown, at the top of the stair, and beyond it, darkness.</p>\n<p>They climbed the stair. On the last step, before the hatch, Ione stepped off his boots, out of his arms, onto the steel — and the bubble let her go, and she staggered, but the stair was in the lee of the Crown itself, the great keelstone lens overhead pulling against the Lamp, and the weight here was bearable. Just. She caught the rail. She stood there, breathing hard, holding on to it.</p>\n<p>He stood beside her. The keel hummed in his spine. It was low now. He could hear exactly how low. It had given them the Lace, and it had cost.</p>\n<p>Below them, down the stair, the Rim ran back through the forest of light toward the gallery, and the chalk arrows ran along it.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. Then he looked at Ione.</p>\n<p>She was looking at them too.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to have to draw those,” she said. Her voice was very quiet. “Aren’t I. On the way back.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Because I followed them. So I know the way. So I’ll draw them. So I can follow them.” She was quiet. “They were never anyone’s. They never came from anywhere. They’re just — true.”</p>\n<p>“Like the key,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Like the key.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t say anything else. She didn’t say <em>on the way back, alone</em>. She didn’t say <em>in your suit</em>. She didn’t look at the suit at all.</p>\n<p>Neither did he.</p>\n<p>He looked instead at the hatch, and the darkness in it, and he thought about a small grey figure on Spindle Four, climbing down alone, raising its hand. He thought: <em>that’s me. That’s me, coming back without her.</em> And the thing under his breastbone that had set its jaw said again, very quietly, very clearly: <em>No.</em></p>\n<p>He didn’t know yet what the Clause needed. He didn’t know what the plate meant, or what the witness was, or what he’d find under the floor. But he knew one thing, standing on the stair at the top of the world with the keel running down in his spine and the Lace moving slowly below him. He knew it the way he’d known the fractal number on the telemetry screen, the way he’d known it at the window in the Collar: <em>at last</em>.</p>\n<p>Whatever it asked for, he’d be the one who gave it. Whatever stayed up here, he’d be the one who stayed. And then the figure on Spindle Four would be wrong, and it would be her coming down, not him — her, alive — and he’d have beaten it. For once in his life he’d have looked at a projection and said <em>no</em> and made it false.</p>\n<p>It didn’t feel like dying. That was the strange thing. It didn’t feel like the window in the Collar at all. It felt like the opposite.</p>\n<p>“Come on,” he said.</p>\n<p>They went up into the Crown.</p>"}, {"file": "17-the-crown.md", "label": "Chapter 16", "title": "The Crown", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5252, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I am sorry. There was no other way to build it.</p><span class=\"attr\">Inscription, inner plate of the Keystone, signed I.H.</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The Crown was full of snow.</p>\n<p>That was what he thought, coming up through the hatch into the grey light: that it had snowed. That in the hundred and sixty-eight years since Ruth Tally and eleven others had run for the lift, snow had come into the highest room in the Draw and lain there, deep and soft and undisturbed, over everything.</p>\n<p>It was dust. It lay over the floor ankle-deep, and it lay over the consoles and the chairs and the desks in thick grey drifts, and it lay along the top of the great curved window in a soft grey ridge. When they walked through it, it rose round their boots in slow clouds and hung in the air and didn’t settle. The air was very still. It smelled of nothing. Of stone and age.</p>\n<p>He’d seen the Crown in the ghost-light. He’d seen it in the old footage, in the mural in the crisis room. Crisp. White coats. The needle swinging over. He’d seen it thirty minutes ago — a hundred and sixty-eight years ago — full of people, full of light.</p>\n<p>He stood in it now and didn’t recognise it.</p>\n<p>The consoles had furred. Their screens were grey and blind under the dust, their switches rusted into place, the plastic of their casings gone brittle and cracked and curled. Dieter’s mug was on the desk where he’d left it, and when Cas brushed the dust off it, it was not a mug. It was a shape of mug, in stone — the coffee in it gone to a brown crust, the ceramic crazed all over with fine cracks, like something dug out of a tomb. The chairs had collapsed on themselves. A clipboard hung on a hook by the door, and the paper on it had gone to a grey lace that fell apart when he breathed on it.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall had stopped. Its hands had fused to its face. They pointed at a time that meant nothing — twenty past something, or twenty to — and would never point at anything else.</p>\n<p>“It’s old,” said Ione. She was standing in the dust by the hatch with her arms wrapped round herself, looking round. “It’s so <em>old</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Time’s been going faster up here. Much faster.” He looked at the window. “And going round. This whole room’s been in the Fold since the Kindling. It’s had⁠—” he didn’t know “⁠—a very long time.”</p>\n<p>The window was the worst of it. The great curved sheet of armoured glass, forty metres long. Along its lower edge the walkway still hung where it had torn from its frame, sagging, twisted, half-fallen. The frame above it, where the first thread of the Lace had touched — the black keelstone that nothing could break — had gone white. A strip of it as wide as a hand, all the way along one section of the frame, gone white and soft and slumped like the wax on a candle. And through the glass, very close — so close that the Crown seemed to hang in it like a lantern hung in a tree — the Lace. Its threads came down past the window in bright curtains. One of them came <em>through</em>. He saw it and stopped. A single thread, fine as a hair, came in through the glass near the torn walkway as if the glass weren’t there, and crossed the room at waist height, and went down into the floor near the far end of the gallery — near the place where, in the ghost-light, Ruth had knelt.</p>\n<p>It moved, very slowly, the way the threads always moved. It didn’t flicker. It was the most real thing in the room.</p>\n<p>“Don’t touch that,” he said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>And in the far end of the Crown, down by the thread, there was a faint light.</p>\n<p>He’d thought it was the Lace, at first. It wasn’t. It was the ghost-light — the same white-violet glow he’d seen going round in the gallery windows, but much fainter here, a thin shimmer in the dust-thick air, like the memory of a picture. The room’s own light, from that night, still just barely hanging in it. He could see, if he looked, the faint shapes of people. Moving. Running. Faded almost to nothing.</p>\n<p>At the far end of the room, by the Keystone, a woman in a white coat was on her knees in the dust, looking up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The plate was still open.</p>\n<p>It lay back on its hinge where Ruth had thrown it, a hundred and sixty-eight years ago, a square of brushed steel furred with dust. Cas knelt and wiped the dust off it with his gauntlet. The letters came up under his hand, engraved, clear.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>Under it, set into the floor, the housing. Black keelstone, smooth and round, the size of a dinner plate, with a recessed ring around its edge and, in the middle, a keyhole. A narrow cross.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt beside him. She knelt exactly where the ghost of Ruth was kneeling — he saw her do it, saw the faint white shape of the woman in the lab coat overlap with her, the two of them in the same place for a moment, one real and one light, both looking at the lock — and Ione didn’t flinch. She’d done it a hundred thousand times. She took the key off its cord.</p>\n<p>She held it for a moment.</p>\n<p>“Do you want to⁠—” she said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas. “It’s yours.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. Then she put the key into the lock.</p>\n<p>It went in. It went in the way a thing goes into the place it was made for: smoothly, completely, with a small soft sound like a breath let out. The cross-shaped bit sank into the cross-shaped hole and seated. She put her fingers on the bow.</p>\n<p>She turned it.</p>\n<p>It was stiff. It didn’t move, and then it did — a quarter turn, heavy, resisting, the way a lock resists that hasn’t been turned for a hundred and sixty-eight years or ever — and then there was a click. Deep. Not in the lock. In the floor. In the whole Crown. It went down through the keelstone into Spindle One like the first stroke of the hammer going down through Tier Nine, and he felt it in his knees.</p>\n<p>The housing opened.</p>\n<p>The recessed ring round its edge split into segments — six of them, black keelstone petals — and drew back, sliding under the floor, like the iris of an eye opening. And under it, where the housing had been, there was a hollow in the floor. And in the hollow, there was the machine.</p>\n<p>It was very simple. That was the first thing he saw. After a lifetime of the Draw, of its vast humming inhuman machinery, the rectifier halls and the tensioners the size of buildings, it was almost shocking how simple it was. A wheel: a small brass wheel set upright, about the size of a hand, finely toothed round its rim like the wheel of an old instrument, with a knurled grip. Beside it, a gauge: a round dial under glass, with a needle, and a single line drawn across its face from edge to edge, and a narrow band of colour on either side of the line. Beside that, a lever — red, with a black-and-yellow collar, exactly like the Dead Man’s Hammer by the lift. And round the whole of it, set into the rim of the hollow, a ring. A dial. Engraved with figures, very small, going round.</p>\n<p>And on the inside of the housing’s lid — on the back of one of the six petals, which had folded up and back and now stood upright at the edge of the hollow like a little headstone — there was a second plate.</p>\n<p>He read it. He read it on his knees in the dust with the thread of the Lace moving slowly a metre away and the ghost of Ruth Tally kneeling beside him looking up at nothing, and Ione beside her, and the keel humming low in his spine.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE — PROCEDURE<br><br>1. SET THE INTERVAL.<br>2. ARM. THE LATTICE WILL RELEASE ITS LAST HOLD. THE DEBT WILL BE CALLED.<br>IT WILL BE PAID AT THE END OF THE INTERVAL.<br>3. HOLD. AS THE PAYMENT GATHERS, ITS PHASE WILL WANDER.<br>KEEP THE NEEDLE ON THE LINE BY HAND. DO NOT LET IT CROSS.<br>4. AT THE END OF THE INTERVAL THE FOLD WILL CLOSE UPON THE PAYMENT<br>AND UPON ALL THAT IS WITHIN THE CROWN.<br>IT WILL CLOSE INTO A SINGLE INSTANT.<br>NOTHING WILL PASS OUT OF IT. NOTHING WILL PASS INTO IT. IT WILL NOT END.<br><br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS.<br>THE WITNESS DOES NOT RETURN.<br><br>I AM SORRY. THERE WAS NO OTHER WAY TO BUILD IT. — I.H.</div>\n<p>He read it twice.</p>\n<p>Then he sat back on his heels in the dust and didn’t move.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Tell me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She’d read it too. She was kneeling beside the hollow, looking at the plate, and her face was very calm, the way it had been calm all morning on Spindle Four, and her hands were in her lap, and they were perfectly steady. He noticed that. They weren’t shaking now.</p>\n<p>“Tell me what it does,” she said. “Plainly. I’ve watched it for three years and I never knew. Tell me.”</p>\n<p>So he told her.</p>\n<p>He did it plainly. The way he’d told her about the Lees, about the Shear, about the Bells. He’d spent his whole life learning to say complicated things so that they sounded simple, and then fifteen years saying simple things so that they sounded complicated, and he found now that the first skill had come back to him, all at once, like a language you’d spoken as a child.</p>\n<p>“The debt’s coming due anyway,” he said. “You know that. The Lamp’s going to pay it — all of it, all at once — when the Lace reaches the Rim. Forty rounds. Less. Whenever. We don’t know exactly when. Nobody does.” He touched the ring round the hollow. The dial. “This lets you choose when. You set the time. You pull the lever — that’s <em>arming</em>. The Lattice lets go of the last thing it’s holding. The loan gets called. And at the end of the time you set, the payment comes. Right here. At the Lamp.”</p>\n<p>“And then everything ends.”</p>\n<p>“No. That’s the — that’s what she built. That’s the Clause.” He looked at the thread of the Lace crossing the room. “When the Lamp broke — the night of the Kindling — it didn’t just break. It broke <em>time</em>, up here. It made the Fold. The Lace, the Crown, the Carousel — you’ve seen it. Things going round. Keys falling forward. Arrows nobody drew. The Fold’s full of loops. And Hollowell knew it would be. She knew that if it ever broke badly enough to kill us, it would break badly enough to do <em>that</em>. And she built this to use it.”</p>\n<p>He took a breath.</p>\n<p>“At the moment the payment comes,” he said, “the Fold closes. Round the Lamp. Round the Crown. Round the payment. It closes up into a loop. A loop in time. A loop so short it’s — nothing. An instant. One instant, whose future is itself. The payment starts — the very first instant of it, the very first edge of the light — and then the loop closes, and that instant is joined to itself, end to beginning, and it’s sealed. It can’t go on to the next instant. There isn’t a next instant. The debt’s paid, and the payment never arrives. It’s always just about to. For ever.” He heard his voice go strange. “She built a sarcophagus. Out of a single moment.”</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“And the rest of it?” she said. “Holdfast? Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Outside the seal, everything goes back. The debt’s inside the instant, and the loan’s inside the instant, and they balance, and they’re sealed. Nothing’s pushing any more. The Lamp’s gone. Time down there comes back to — ordinary. The same as Outside. The Shear goes. The Drop’s a ladder.” He looked at her. “It works, Ione. I think it works. I think she was right.”</p>\n<p>“<em>The sealing requires a witness</em>,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Tell me that. Plainly.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the gauge. At the needle, resting now at one side of the dial, and the line across its face, and the narrow coloured bands on either side.</p>\n<p>“When you arm it,” he said, “the payment starts to gather. At the core. And as it gathers, it — wanders. Its phase. It won’t come in straight. It’ll come in the way the lamp-weather came in the Breach, in gusts, in swings. And the seal has to close on it exactly. Exactly in phase. Or the loop won’t close clean, and the payment gets out.” He touched the brass wheel. “So someone has to keep the needle on the line. By hand. Watching it. Turning the wheel, a little this way, a little that, as it wanders. All the way to the end. Right up to the instant.”</p>\n<p>“Why a person?” said Ione. “Why not — tie it? Lock it?”</p>\n<p>“Because it wanders. It won’t sit still. You’d need something that could see it and think.” He shook his head. “And nothing that thinks can live up here except a person. Not a machine. You’ve seen what the suit’s electronics are doing. Up here, a machine’s clocks come apart in a second. It has to be eyes. And a hand. And a head that can guess which way it’ll go next.” He looked at the plate. “That’s the witness. That’s all she meant. Somebody to <em>watch</em>.”</p>\n<p>“And the witness is inside the Crown,” said Ione. “When it closes.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“<em>All that is within the Crown.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Into a single instant.</em> <em>It will not end.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him.</p>\n<p>“And they’re in it,” she said. “The witness. For ever. In one instant. Living it.”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>She blinked.</p>\n<p>“Not living it,” he said. “Not the way you mean. Not over and over. You told me. On the spindle.” He found his voice was quite steady. “If a loop’s really closed, there’s nobody inside to be tired of it. It’s just once. It’s once, and it’s true, and it stays true. It’s only for ever for whoever’s outside.” He looked at the brass wheel. “It’s not a prison. It’s not the gallery. Whoever’s in it doesn’t go round. They’re just — there. In one instant. Once. Exactly as they are.” He paused. “It’s only for ever from out here.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer. She was looking at him with an expression he’d never seen on her face before and couldn’t read at all.</p>\n<p>He looked back at the plate.</p>\n<p><em>Default</em>, he thought. The word you couldn’t say in the Commonweal. The word Rask had never said, that the Ministry had never said in ninety-one years, that he’d never once said out loud in his life.</p>\n<p>“Default,” he said. Out loud, in the Crown.</p>\n<p>It sounded like a very small word.</p>\n<p>He found that he felt calm. Entirely calm. Calmer than he had felt at the window in the Collar, calmer than he’d felt in fifteen years. Calmer than he could remember feeling. He’d come in here for this. He hadn’t known what it was, and it was here, under a plate in the floor, and it was simple, and it was something he could do.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Take the key out,” he said. “We don’t need it any more. It’s done its⁠—”</p>\n<p>She was already reaching for it. It was still in the lock, in the middle of the hollow, standing up among the wheel and the gauge and the lever. She took hold of its bow and drew it out.</p>\n<p>The thread of the Lace moved.</p>\n<p>It had been lying across the room at waist height, fine as a hair, from the window to the floor. It had been lying there perfectly still except for its slow weed-in-a-current drift, a metre from the Keystone. And as Ione drew the key up out of the lock, the thread drifted — a few centimetres; a hand’s width — and one of its finest branches, a filament so fine it was almost only a glitter, swung across the hollow.</p>\n<p>It touched the key.</p>\n<p>It was nothing. A brush. Less than a second. There was a sound, very faint, very high, like a fingernail on glass. And Ione’s hand jerked, and the key came out of the lock, and she was holding it up in front of her, in the dust-thick air, staring at it.</p>\n<p>Along the shaft of the key, from the bow almost to the bit, there was a line.</p>\n<p>A fine bright line, scored into the black keelstone. Keelstone, that nothing could scratch. It shone faintly in the grey light, white-violet at first, then fading, as they watched, to a thin pale grey. A scratch. Straight, and very fine, and a little crooked at the end, where it ran off the edge of the shaft near the bit.</p>\n<p>Ione made a sound.</p>\n<p>She was staring at it. She turned the key over in her fingers — slowly, very slowly — and ran the ball of her thumb along the line. From the bow to the bit. And back. The way you’d trace a scar.</p>\n<p>“It’s always had this,” she said.</p>\n<p>He didn’t understand, for a moment.</p>\n<p>“This,” said Ione. Her voice was very thin. “This scratch. It’s always had it. Since the gantry. Since I picked it up. I’ve — every night, Cas. Every night for eleven years. I lie in the hammock and I hold it and I run my thumb along the scratch. Here. It’s how I go to sleep.” Her thumb moved along it. “It goes crooked at the end. Just here. I know it. I know exactly where it goes crooked. I’ve always wondered what did it. What could scratch keelstone.” She looked up at him. Her face was white. “<em>This</em> did it. Just now. It’s always had it because it’s just got it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the key in her hand. At the thin grey line along its shaft.</p>\n<p><em>The jinn must return exactly as it left.</em> The old paper. His professor, joking. <em>You’d have no way to know where it came from. Because it didn’t come from anywhere.</em></p>\n<p>“It has to go back,” he said.</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“The key,” he said. “It came from here. It came out of the Fold, onto the gantry, in the Carousel. For you to find. And now it’s — it’s done. It’s opened the lock. It’s got its scratch. It’s exactly as it was when you picked it up.” He made himself say it. “It has to go back. To the gantry. Eleven years ago. For you to find it.”</p>\n<p>“There’s no one else,” said Ione. Very quietly. “Is there. To take it.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Because I found it. So I have to leave it. So I can find it.” Her thumb was still on the scratch. “It never came from anywhere.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“It was never <em>made</em>. It never⁠—” She stopped. She closed her hand round the key. She held it against her chest, against her breastbone, the way she’d worn it on its cord for eleven years. “Cas. How? How do I⁠—”</p>\n<p>And then she stopped, and her head came up, and she turned — slowly — and looked down the length of the Crown, away from the Keystone, to the far end of the room. To the place where the floor stepped down, where a short steel stair went down into a lower space under the main deck.</p>\n<p>“The service deck,” she said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She’d seen it from below. That was what she told him, going down the stair. For three years, from the gantry — every time she crossed it, four thousand times — she’d seen the underside of the Crown above her, the black keelstone lens on the top of Spindle One, and in its underside, a row of small round windows. Portholes. The service deck, under the main floor, where the Crown’s machinery had been. And in one of the portholes, sometimes — not every cycle, but sometimes — there’d been a light.</p>\n<p>“Not the ghost-light,” she said. “Not the Crown’s light, going round. A different light. Steady. Grey. Like daylight through a door.” She was going down the stair ahead of him, one hand on the rail, the key clenched in the other. “I used to wonder what it was. I used to think it was someone up there. Watching.” She stopped at the bottom. “It was this.”</p>\n<p>The service deck was low and cramped and full of dead machinery — pumps, cable trunks, a bank of rusted cabinets — and dust, the same deep grey dust, and on the far side, in the curved outer wall, a row of small round portholes looking down.</p>\n<p>And in the middle of the deck, near the portholes, the air was wrong.</p>\n<p>He saw it at once. He’d have seen it anywhere now. A shimmer. A vertical line of wrongness hanging in the air a little above the floor, the height of a person, a hand’s width wide, like the seam in the Carousel — but brighter. Much brighter. It wasn’t a ripple. It was a <em>door</em>. A tall narrow slot of grey light standing in the middle of the dead deck, as if someone had cut the air with a knife and there was a grey afternoon on the other side of it. Round its edges, very faintly, the finest threads of the Lace came and went.</p>\n<p>And through it — when he stepped round to look through it — he saw the gantry.</p>\n<p>It was below. The slot of light looked <em>down</em>, as if it were a window in a floor; through it he could see the long steel walkway from Four to One, slung across the violet gulf, a little way below them. Pitted with rust. Sagging slightly in the middle. Exactly as it had been an hour ago, when they’d crossed it.</p>\n<p>Not exactly. He looked, and looked again. The rust was a little less. The handrail, which had been bent in one place near the middle, was straight.</p>\n<p>And at the near end of it — where it met the curved wall of the gallery, the ring round Spindle One — the gallery’s windows were lit. The ghost-light, going round.</p>\n<p>“That’s then,” said Ione. She was standing beside him, looking down through the slot. Her voice was very calm. “That’s the gantry. Then. Eleven years ago. When I was in there.” She looked at the gallery’s lit windows. “I’m in there. Right now. Then.”</p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything for a while.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know how long it stays open,” he said at last. “It might close. It might⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.” She looked down at the key in her hand. Then at him. “I’ll be quick.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know how it goes,” she said. “I’ve always known how it goes. I just never knew it was me.” She almost smiled. “I’ll put it on the gantry. By the loose bolt. And I’ll stand at the end, by the wall, where she saw me. Where I saw me. And she’ll come across, and find it, and look up. And I’ll turn away.” She put her hand flat on his chestplate. “And then I’ll come back. That’s all. That’s how it goes.”</p>\n<p>“How do you know you come back?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those grey-green eyes.</p>\n<p>“Because she found a seam,” said Ione, “where the woman had been standing. And went through it. And got out.” She took her hand off his chest. “I’ll leave it open for her. On my way back.”</p>\n<p>She stepped into the light.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He waited.</p>\n<p>It was seconds. He counted them — of course he counted them — standing in the dust of the service deck in the grey light from the slot, with the dead machinery all round him and the keel humming low in his spine. Eleven seconds. Twelve. He watched the slot. He couldn’t see her through it. He could see the gantry below, and the gulf, and the lit windows of the gallery, and no one.</p>\n<p>Twenty. Thirty.</p>\n<p>He thought about a girl of seventeen lying on the floor of the gallery below, letting the nine minutes go through her. He thought about her getting up, one cycle, and deciding to learn their names. He thought about her crossing the gantry four thousand times, knowing every bolt.</p>\n<p>Forty-one. Forty-two.</p>\n<p>She came out of the light.</p>\n<p>She came through it the way you’d come through a doorway in a high wind — stumbling, catching herself, her good hand out. And she was different. He saw it at once. It had been forty-two seconds and it had not been forty-two seconds for her; he could see it in her face. It was grey. Her eyes were red. Her lips were cracked, as though she hadn’t drunk for hours. There was rust on her knees, on her hands — the gantry’s rust — and dust in her hair, and she was shaking. Not a tremor. All of her.</p>\n<p>Her hands were empty.</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>She walked straight past him. She walked to the wall of the service deck, under the portholes, and put her hand on it, and stood there with her back to him, her head down, her other hand pressed against her mouth.</p>\n<p>“How long?” he said.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.” Her voice came out muffled. “Hours. Hours. I had to wait. I put it down — by the bolt — and I went to the end, by the wall, and I waited. And she didn’t come. And she didn’t come. And the light went round and round in the windows and I could hear it, <em>is it going to be too bright</em>, and she didn’t — and I thought, what if she doesn’t, what if I’ve got it wrong, what if it’s the wrong cycle, what if⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<p>“And then she came,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“She came out of the gallery door. Onto the gantry. And she was⁠—” Ione’s shoulders moved. “She was so <em>thin</em>, Cas. She was so thin. I didn’t — I don’t remember being that thin. Her arms were like sticks. Her hair was filthy, she’d been cutting it with a — with a bit of metal, you could see, it was all hacked. And she had the grey. Just starting. Just here.” She touched her own temple, the white streak, without turning round. “She was walking with her head down. Like she’d walked across there four thousand times. Because she had.”</p>\n<p>Her voice broke. She waited. She went on.</p>\n<p>“And she saw it. She stopped. She just — stopped. And stood there. She stood there for such a long time, looking at it. And then she crouched down and picked it up. And she looked at it in her hand. And she ran her thumb along it.” Ione’s own thumb moved, against the wall, along nothing. “Along the scratch. The first time. And then she looked up.”</p>\n<p>“And she saw you.”</p>\n<p>“And she saw me.” Ione turned round then. Her face was wet and grey and he had never seen anything so tired. “And she said <em>Mam?</em>”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“She said <em>Mam</em>,” said Ione Tally. “Like a question. Like a little girl. <em>Mam?</em> And I stood there at the end of the gantry, and I wanted⁠—” Her face twisted. “I wanted to say, <em>no, love, it’s not Mam, it’s you, it’s you, you get out, you get out of here and you go home and Gran’s waiting and you’ll be all right, you’ll be all right, you’ll be⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>She put both hands over her face.</p>\n<p>“But I remembered,” she said, through them. “I remembered she didn’t say anything. The woman. She didn’t say a word. She just turned away. I’ve remembered it for eleven years. So I knew. I knew I didn’t. And I stood there, and I tried to make my mouth — and I couldn’t. I don’t know if I couldn’t or if I just — didn’t. I don’t know. I don’t <em>know</em>. I stood there and I let her think I was Mam. I let her think Mam had come up to find her. I let her run along the gantry toward me shouting <em>Mam</em> and I turned my back on her and I walked away.”</p>\n<p>He crossed the deck. He put his arms round her. She let him. She put her face against the chestplate of the suit, and he held her, and she shook.</p>\n<p>“I left the seam open,” she said, into the suit, after a long time. “Where I’d been standing. I felt it close behind me when I came through, but not all at once. Slowly. Like a wound. I think she’ll — I know she’ll get through. I know she does. I’m here.” She laughed, a terrible small laugh. “I’m <em>here</em>. So she does.”</p>\n<p>“She does,” he said. “You did.”</p>\n<p>“I let her think I was her mother.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“She’s going to go down the spindle,” said Ione, “and look in a water-trough and see she’s twenty, and she’s going to spend eleven years thinking her mother came up to the top of the world to save her and then turned away. She’s going to wonder, every night, why she turned away.” Her voice was very quiet. “And it was me. It was always me.”</p>\n<p>He held her.</p>\n<p>Behind them, the slot of grey light hung in the dead air of the service deck. As they stood there, it narrowed. Slowly, like a door swinging shut. A hand’s width. A finger’s. A line. And then only a faint shimmer, and the threads of the Lace coming and going round the place where it had been, and then nothing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went back up the stair into the Crown.</p>\n<p>The hollow in the floor stood open. The machine in it: the small brass wheel, the gauge with its line, the red lever, the ring of the dial. The petal of keelstone standing upright at its edge with the plate on it. Beside it, the faint ghost of Ruth Tally, on her knees in the dust, looking up at nothing. At the place beside the Keystone, where someone would stand to reach the wheel.</p>\n<p>Ione went and stood by the hollow. She looked down at the plate. She read it again. He could see her lips move.</p>\n<p><em>The sealing requires a witness.</em></p>\n<p><em>The witness does not return.</em></p>\n<p>Then she looked up. Not at him.</p>\n<p>At the suit.</p>\n<p>He was standing in it, in the middle of the Crown, in the dust, with the keel humming low in his spine. And she looked at it — at the grey plates, the gouge down the left side, the housing on his back with its band of lights and its white painted <em>1</em>, the rigger’s harness she’d spent three nights splicing — the way you look at a thing you’ve seen before, a long time ago, from very far away.</p>\n<p>He looked at it too. He looked down at his own gauntlets. At the chestplate. At himself.</p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything at all.</p>"}, {"file": "18-the-witness.md", "label": "Chapter 17", "title": "The Witness", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5668, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine.</p><span class=\"attr\">Holdfast riggers’ wedding vow</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">“It’s me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it quite quietly, standing by the hollow in the dust, with her hands at her sides. As if she were telling him the time.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Listen to me.” She didn’t raise her voice. “I’ve thought about this. I’ve had eleven years to think about it, and I’ve had since the Rim, and I’ve had the — the hours. On the gantry. Waiting for her. Listen.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve already lived my forever.” She said it evenly. “Three years in that gallery. Three years of one night. I know what it is. I know what it’s like to be in one moment and not get out. I’m not frightened of it. I’m the only person in the world who isn’t. And you told me — you said it yourself, just now — it’s once. For whoever’s in it, it’s only once.” She looked at him. “I can do once.”</p>\n<p>“So can I.”</p>\n<p>“You’ve got a daughter.” Her voice caught on it, and went on. “You’ve got a little girl called Nell who collects stars, and a woman in a chair at the bottom of the Anvil who’s waiting to buy you a drink, and a whole city of — six hundred million people, you said, and a sky, and rain, and a sea. You’ve got an <em>ordinary</em> waiting for you. You haven’t lived it yet. You told me in the hut you’ve had fifteen years of the same day. You haven’t lived your ordinary.” She shook her head. “I’ve got Gran. And Gran’s eighty-three, and she’s dying, and she’s never once said so. That’s all I’ve got. That’s all I’ve had, since I came down. I’ve been a ghost in Holdfast for eleven years. I sat on the Tollers’ step and watched people step round me. I’ve already lived my forever, Cas. You haven’t lived your ordinary. It’s me.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her for a long time.</p>\n<p>“I saw it come down,” he said.</p>\n<p>She went still.</p>\n<p>“On the Rim. In the Lace. The suit.” He looked down at himself — at the grey plates, the gauntlets. “My suit. With your harness on it. Coming down Four. Alone. I saw it, and I thought: <em>that’s me</em>. That’s me coming back without her. I thought I was going to go home and you were going to die up here.” He heard his voice shake and didn’t stop it. “And I decided — right there, on the Rim, with you standing on my boots — that I wasn’t going to let it. I thought, <em>whatever it asks for, I’ll be the one who stays</em>. I thought if I stayed, the vision would be wrong. It’d be you coming down. Not me. I’d have <em>beaten</em> it. For once in my life I’d have looked at a projection and made it false.”</p>\n<p>Ione was staring at him.</p>\n<p>“I saw it too,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know. You told me. At seventeen⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No. I mean⁠—” She stopped. Her face had gone very strange. “I’ve been waiting eleven years at the top of the wells for a man in a suit to come up. And I always knew — I’ve always known, since I was seventeen — that he comes back down. Alone. I saw it. So I knew. From the first minute. From when I pulled you out.” She put her hand to her mouth. “I’ve known the whole time that you go home and I don’t. And on the Rim, when we saw it again, I thought — I decided — I’d make it wrong. I’d stay. So you’d go home to your girl. So it’d be — <em>not</em> you, coming down alone, leaving me. So it’d be you coming down alone because I <em>chose</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<p>They looked at each other across the hollow in the floor.</p>\n<p>“We both⁠—” he said.</p>\n<p>“We both saw you.” She was breathing very fast. “We both saw you come down, and we both decided to — we both⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped again.</p>\n<p>And then something happened to her face. He watched it happen. He watched it the way he’d watched Ben’s hand appear on the glass: all at once, whole, from nowhere. Her eyes went wide. Her hand came away from her mouth. She looked at him — at the suit — at the grey plates and the housing and the harness — and she took a step back from the hollow, and another, and her face crumpled.</p>\n<p>“Cas,” she said. Her voice cracked right down the middle. “Cas, it was the suit. It was never — it was the <em>suit</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>He heard it. He heard it twice — once now, in the Crown, from her mouth, and once on the Rim, in the Lace, from nowhere, an hour ago, a lifetime ago, <em>it was the suit</em>, in exactly that voice, exactly that crack in it — and the two of them rang together in his head like two strokes of the same bell, and he understood.</p>\n<p>They’d never seen <em>him</em>. Either of them. Not at seventeen, not on the Rim. They’d seen a suit. Grey plates and a humming keel and a harness spliced in rigger’s strapping, climbing down Spindle Four alone. Whoever was in it. They’d both looked at the suit and seen him inside it, because it was his, because who else would be in it — and it had never been a vision of who lived. It had only ever been a vision of who wore the suit.</p>\n<p>He looked down at himself.</p>\n<p>Then he looked at her. Short, broad, strong. Her rope on her shoulder. Her splinted wrist. The white streak.</p>\n<p>“It’s you,” he said. “Ione. It’s you coming down. It was always you.”</p>\n<p>“<em>No</em>.”</p>\n<p>“It was you in it. On the Rim, raising its hand. At seventeen. It was always⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.” She’d backed up against a console. Dust rose round her in a slow cloud. “No. No. Then it’s not — then it isn’t a <em>choice</em>. Then it was always going to be — I don’t get to — you don’t get to⁠—” She was shaking her head. “Then it’s just the key again. It’s just the arrows. It’s just something that’s true that nobody chose, and we’re only⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It’s already true,” said Cas. “And I’m still choosing it.”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He didn’t know he was going to say the next thing until he said it.</p>\n<p>“Ruth saw me.”</p>\n<p>Ione stared at him.</p>\n<p>“In the Log. Year Thirty-One. I read it in the Tower, the first night. I didn’t understand it. I didn’t — I read it twice and turned the page.” He could see it: the pencil, the short entry set apart with a line above and below. “She saw a man at the Keystone. At 02:09. After the key fell. Standing where she was kneeling. Dressed close, in grey, like a diver.” He looked down at his gauntlets. At the suit. Under it, he knew exactly what he was wearing: the grey liner. The one-piece. The thing that made all three of them look like depressed seals. “His hand on a wheel that wasn’t there. She said he looked at her like he knew her. She said he was very calm.” He looked up. “She said there was a cord knotted round his wrist.”</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“She’s been looking at me,” he said. “In the gallery. Every cycle. For three years, you watched her look up at the Keystone, at nothing, like someone was there. You stood in the place. You said it wasn’t you.” He swallowed. “It was me. She was looking at me. At — this. Now. Here.”</p>\n<p>Ione’s hand had gone to her pocket.</p>\n<p>He watched it. She didn’t seem to know she was doing it. Her good hand went into the pocket of her tunic and closed round something there, and came out, and opened, and lay in her palm. A length of thin cord. The cord from the wedding. The one she’d tied round his wrist on the rooftop in the Drop District with the drums going and the tower swaying, and taken off again with one tug. <em>Riggers’ hitch. Holds under load. Won’t slip. That’s the only one you’ll need.</em></p>\n<p>She looked at it in her hand.</p>\n<p>“It’s not on your wrist,” she said. Very quietly. “It’s not there yet.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“She saw it there. So it’s there. So someone ties it.” She looked up at him. “So <em>I</em> tie it. That’s the only way it gets there. I have to do it. I have to choose to do it. Like the arrows. Like the key.” Her voice broke again. “You’re asking me to <em>tie it on</em>.”</p>\n<p>“I’m not asking you anything,” said Cas. “It’s yours. It’s your rope.”</p>\n<p>She closed her hand round it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Why?” she said.</p>\n<p>It was a long time later. Or not long. They were sitting on the floor of the Crown, in the dust, by the hollow, side by side, their backs against the dead console. The keel hummed. The ghost of Ruth knelt by the Keystone, looking up. The thread of the Lace lay across the room, moving slowly. Outside the great window, the Lamp burned.</p>\n<p>“Tell me why,” said Ione. “Not the vision. Not the Log. Not what’s true. <em>Why</em>. You came in here to die. You told me. In the hut. You came in because it was tidy. Because you thought you owed it.” Her voice was quite steady now, very low. “If that’s why — if you’re doing this because you think you still owe it — then I’ll fight you. I’ll put you on the floor and take that suit off you myself and you can go home and I’ll stay, and it doesn’t matter what’s true. I will. I’ll make it false or I’ll die trying. I’m not letting you do it for that.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He thought about it the way she thought about questions — turning it over, looking at it from underneath — because she’d earned that, and because he needed to know.</p>\n<p>He thought about the window in the Collar. <em>At last. Almost a relief.</em> He thought about the Quiet, the dark, the small cold place with the simple shape: <em>everything I do adds to it</em>. He thought about the Rim, and the ledger, and the zero at the bottom of the page.</p>\n<p>He examined himself for it. He patted his pockets.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>“I’m not paying anything,” he said.</p>\n<p>She turned her head.</p>\n<p>“I came in to pay,” he said. “You’re right. I came in here to pay it off. All at once. With me. And you told me — in the hut — that you can’t. That it doesn’t get smaller. That it just grows.” He looked at the hollow. The brass wheel. “You were right. I’m not paying anything. There’s nothing to pay. There never was. You showed me the bottom of the page and it was zero, and you said zero’s balanced. It’s balanced. I don’t owe anything. I’m square.”</p>\n<p>“Then <em>why</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because I want to.”</p>\n<p>It was the truest thing he’d ever said. He heard it come out of him and knew it the way he’d known the fractal number on the telemetry screen. <em>I want to.</em></p>\n<p>“I want this,” he said. “Not the dying. It’s not dying. You told me. It’s once.” He looked at her. “Ione. For fifteen years I lived one day. The same day. The day with the pen. I stood in the gallery and watched myself sign it, over and over, thinking <em>don’t</em>, the way you watched Ruth. And I never once — I never had a moment I’d want. Not one. Not one moment in fifteen years that I’d have wanted to keep. And now I’ve got one.” He could hardly get it out. “I’ve got one. I want to be in it. I want to be in the moment where I know you’re safe. Where I know it worked. Where Nell’s going to see the stars and Teo’s going to get out of her chair and thirty-one thousand people are going to walk down a ladder. Where you’re going down Spindle Four in my suit, alive.” He shook his head. “That’s not paying. That’s the opposite of paying. It’s — it’s the only thing I’ve ever wanted that I’ve been sure of.”</p>\n<p>She was crying without any sound.</p>\n<p>“I want the moment,” said Casimir Hale. “I’m choosing it. Not because it’s true. Because I want it. It’s just — it’s true as well.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They chose the instant together.</p>\n<p>He showed her the dial. The ring round the rim of the hollow, engraved with Hollowell’s small precise figures. The interval. He worked it out with her, aloud, the way he’d worked out the curve in the Tollers’ tower, the way she’d worked out the Lees: how long for her to get back down the stair, and along the Rim through the Lace, and through the Carousel’s seam — Ben’s seam, still there, she said; she’d seen it on the way back from the gantry, still shimmering — and across the gantry, and down the spindle, and down through the Breach. How long before she was past the Breach, well below the Rim, in the Lees, out of the Fold. How long it would be before she was safe.</p>\n<p>“Three hours,” said Ione. “If I’m quick. If the weather’s kind.”</p>\n<p>“Three and a half,” he said. “In case it isn’t.”</p>\n<p>He set the ring. It turned under his fingers with a smooth, heavy click at each mark, like the dial of a safe.</p>\n<p>“From the Crown,” he said, “I’ll be able to see Four. Down there. Through the window. If I look.” He pointed through the great glass, down and to the left, past the glare of the Lamp, to where the black lattice of Spindle Four rose out of the violet gulf wrapped in its cobwebs of light. “I’ll have to watch the needle. But I’ll be able to see. And the keel’s got its lights. I’ll see you going down.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll see me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“All the way.”</p>\n<p>“And at the end⁠—”</p>\n<p>“At the end, you’ll be past the Breach. And I’ll look. And I’ll see your light.” He was quiet. “That’s the instant. That’s where I’ll be.”</p>\n<p>She was silent for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she said: “I’ll learn one for you.”</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“A star. When I get out. When I see them.” She looked at the window, at the Lamp, at the only star she’d ever had. “You said you never looked. You never had a favourite. You said Nell would have known in a second.” She wiped her face with the back of her good hand. “I’ll find one for you. I’ll ask Nell. I’ll go out under the — the sky, the real one, and I’ll pick one, and it’ll be yours. And I’ll look at it.”</p>\n<p>“Every night?”</p>\n<p>“They come every night?” she said. “You said they come every night.”</p>\n<p>“Every night.”</p>\n<p>“Then every night,” said Ione.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They didn’t talk about it after that. They talked about other things.</p>\n<p>He told her about rain again, because she asked. She asked what it tasted of. He said nothing, mostly, and sometimes dust, and in the Stacks, sometimes, of the metal of the gutters. She asked if you could drink it. He said yes. She said she’d drink it. She asked about the sea — whether it was loud — and he said yes, very, and that it never stopped, and she said that she’d lived with the Bell all her life so she’d be used to that. She asked what they ate, Outside. He told her. She was scandalised that nobody ate rat. He said she could start a fashion. She said she’d open a stall. <em>Holdfast’s Other Citizens, Roasted.</em> He laughed. It came out of him in the dust of the Crown, a real laugh, loud, and she looked at him with that expression he’d seen on the spindle — as if he’d surprised her — and then she laughed too, the rough short stiff laugh, and couldn’t stop, and put her forehead down on his shoulder.</p>\n<p>And then, at some point, neither of them was laughing. And she lifted her head. And he took off the helmet — he’d had it on since the Rim — and put it down in the dust. And she put her good hand on the side of his face, and looked at him, the way she’d looked at him at the top of the well, patiently, attentively, as if he were a view she’d seen many times and still liked.</p>\n<p>“I’m glad it was you,” she said. “That came up the well.”</p>\n<p>“I’m glad it was you that pulled me out.”</p>\n<p>She kissed him. It was very gentle. It went on for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Getting her into the suit was the hardest thing he had ever done.</p>\n<p>Not because of what it meant. Because of the straps. The suit was built for him, and she was a head shorter and twice as broad through the shoulders, and it opened down the back like a clam and she stepped into it and it closed round her — thunk, thunk, thunk — and it didn’t fit anywhere. The exo woke and took her weight and didn’t know what to do with her, and she stood in it swaying like a child in her father’s coat, her face tiny in the big curve of the visor, and said, <em>well, this is ridiculous</em>.</p>\n<p>So he fitted it to her. The way Ben had fitted it to him in the hangar, a hundred years ago. He knelt in the dust in the grey liner and went round the suit, strap by strap, buckle by buckle, taking in the harness at the shoulders and letting it out at the hips, shortening the legs of the exo by two notches, cinching the webbing she’d spliced for him so that it would hold her instead. His hands were shaking. He made them stop, and they shook anyway, and he went on.</p>\n<p>“Ione,” he said, at the back of the suit, feeling along the strapping under the keel housing, “I can’t see the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“⁠—buckle,” she said. “It’s lower. Left. There.”</p>\n<p>His hands stopped.</p>\n<p>He knelt there in the dust with his hands on the strap, and the words he’d said went through him, and the words he’d heard on the Rim went through him, and they rang together like the bell again — <em>Ione, I can’t see the —</em> in his own voice, out of the air, out of nowhere, in the Lace — and he found that he was laughing. Kneeling behind her in the dust, laughing, with his forehead against the keel housing.</p>\n<p>“What?” she said. “What is it?”</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” He found the buckle. It was lower. Left. There. He did it up. “Nothing. It’s just — that one was only a buckle.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t understand. Then she did. He heard her breath go in, and out, and then she was laughing too, in the suit, the sound thin and strange through the helmet speaker, and it went on until it wasn’t laughing any more, and they both stopped, and he finished the straps.</p>\n<p>When it was done she took three steps across the Crown in it, and turned, and took three back. She was steady. The exo had learned her. The keel hummed on her back. Her face in the visor was very small and very pale and very calm.</p>\n<p>“It’s heavy,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’ll get used to it.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got about three and a half hours.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll be fine.”</p>\n<p>“I know.” She looked at him through the visor. At him standing in the dust in the grey liner — the close grey one-piece, slippery, cool, like a diver’s. Like something worn under armour. She looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she opened the suit’s chest compartment and took out the cord.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She tied it the way she’d tied it on the rooftop. Quickly. Over, under, round, through. Her gauntlet had to come off for it — she worked the clasp with her splinted hand and pulled it off and let it drop in the dust — and with her bare fingers she tied the riggers’ hitch round his left wrist, above the cuff of the liner, flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped.</p>\n<p>Her fingers stopped when it was done. They stayed on the knot.</p>\n<p>“I’ll hold your rope,” said Ione Tally. Her voice was quite steady. “You hold mine.”</p>\n<p>He knew the rest. He’d heard it on a rooftop with the drums going and the whole tower stamping. He said it with her.</p>\n<p>“And if the one of us falls,” they said, “the other one hauls.”</p>\n<p>Nobody shouted <em>haul</em>. There was nobody to shout it. There was only the Crown, and the dust, and the ghost of Ruth Tally on her knees by the Keystone, looking up at the place where he was standing.</p>\n<p>Ione looked at the knot on his wrist. Then she looked at the ghost.</p>\n<p>“She’s looking at you,” she said. “Look. She’s looking right at you.”</p>\n<p>He looked. The faint white shape in the dust, the woman in the lab coat with her hair pinned up and the mole under her ear, kneeling by the open housing, her face turned up toward the place beside it. Toward him.</p>\n<p>“A hundred and seventy-five thousand times,” said Ione, “I watched her look at you. I stood where you’re standing. Hundreds of times. To see if it was me she saw.” Her voice broke, finally, completely. “It was you. It was always you. She was looking at you the whole time. She was looking at <em>you</em>.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They armed it together.</p>\n<p>He knelt by the hollow in the grey liner with the knot on his wrist. She knelt beside him in the suit. The red lever stood between them with its black-and-yellow collar, exactly like the one by the lift that Ruth had pulled. He put his hand on it. She put her bare hand over his.</p>\n<p>“So this is the start,” she said.</p>\n<p>“This is the start.”</p>\n<p>They pulled it.</p>\n<p>It moved easily. It moved like a thing that had been waiting to be moved. It came down through its collar and seated with a sound — not a click; a <em>toll</em>, deep, a single note going down through the keelstone into Spindle One and on down, and on, into the Vault, into the Anvil, into everything — and he felt it go through him, the way he’d felt the hammer go through Tier Nine. The Lattice letting go. The last hold released.</p>\n<p>Outside the great window, the Lamp changed.</p>\n<p>It didn’t grow. It didn’t dim. But its light — its hard white-violet — went, very faintly, warmer. A thread of gold in it. And round it, the Lace stirred. All of it, at once, the whole vast filigree across the sky, rippling, the way a curtain ripples when a door opens somewhere in the house. The thread lying across the Crown moved, and lifted, and drifted a little toward the window, as if something out there were drawing a breath.</p>\n<p>On the gauge, the needle trembled. And began to move.</p>\n<p>He put his hand on the brass wheel.</p>\n<p>It was warm. It turned under his fingers smooth and heavy and fine, a little click at each tooth, and the needle, which had begun to drift off the line toward the band of colour on its left, stopped, and hung, and came back. He held it there. He felt it wander, under his hand, in the wheel — a tug this way, a tug that way, like a fish on a line — and he held it on the line.</p>\n<p>“Go,” he said. He didn’t look at her. He couldn’t take his eyes off the needle. “Ione. Go.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t move for a moment.</p>\n<p>Then she leaned in — the suit’s helmet close to his bare face — and pressed her visor against his temple. Hard. Just for a second. He felt the cold of the glass. He heard her breathe.</p>\n<p>“Haul,” she said.</p>\n<p>Then she stood up, and turned, and he heard the suit’s boots go across the Crown — heavy, steady, booming on the keelstone floor, the keel’s hum going with them — and down the hatch. And the hum faded. And was gone.</p>\n<p>He held the needle on the line.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went down the stair onto the Rim, and the Lace was waiting for her.</p>\n<p>It was moving. It had been moving slowly all the time they’d been in it; now it moved like weed in a flood. The threads swayed and swung and reached, rippling with that long slow breath that had come into them when the lever went down. The light had a thread of gold in it. The air was full of a sound she had never heard in the Lace before — a high, faint, singing, like a finger round the rim of a glass.</p>\n<p>And there were no arrows.</p>\n<p>She stood at the foot of the stair and looked at the steel plate of the Rim running back through the forest of light toward the gallery. Bare. Black. No chalk. No white rigger’s arrows, three quick strokes, pointing the way between the threads.</p>\n<p>Of course there weren’t. She hadn’t drawn them yet.</p>\n<p>She had chalk. She always had chalk. She opened the pouch on the suit’s belt — he’d put it there, when he fitted the suit, without saying anything; she’d seen him do it — and took out a stick, and knelt on the steel at the foot of the stair, and drew an arrow. Three strokes. Pointing ahead, and a little left, between two great hanging roots of light.</p>\n<p>It looked exactly like the first one they’d seen.</p>\n<p>She stood up. She walked to where it pointed. She drew another. She knew where it went. She knew exactly where it went, because she’d followed it, an hour ago, standing on his boots with his arms round her. She’d followed it because it was there. It was there because she was drawing it. It had never been anyone’s. It had never come from anywhere. It was just true.</p>\n<p>She drew them all the way. Every one. Kneeling in the suit on the flickering steel of the Rim — it flickered; she didn’t look at it — with the threads swaying round her and the singing in the air and the keel humming round her in its metre of calm. She did not look into the threads. She did not look at the Lamp. She looked at the chalk in her bare fingers, and the steel, and the arrows.</p>\n<p>In the middle of the Rim, where the threads were thickest, she heard her own voice.</p>\n<p>“<em>Right. Right, and then straight.</em>”</p>\n<p>It came out of the air beside her. Her voice, from an hour ago. From before. She stopped with the chalk in her hand, kneeling, and she knew exactly where it was coming from. She knew that somewhere very close to her, in the same place and not in the same place, a man in a keel suit was standing with a woman on his boots, and the woman was looking for the next arrow, and the man had just heard her voice come out of nowhere and gone cold.</p>\n<p>She looked at the place where the next arrow had to go. Right. Right, and then straight.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Ione, to the air, to the man, to the girl on his boots. Her voice was shaking. “Right, and then straight. It’s all right. You’ll find them. They’re all here. I’ve drawn them for you.”</p>\n<p>She drew it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went through the Carousel without stopping.</p>\n<p>She came in by the Rim door, into the corridor, and the ghost-light came on round her — <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em> — and she walked through it. She didn’t look at the windows. She didn’t look at the scratched fives on the walls, or the pallet of rags, or the pile of smooth stones. She walked straight along the curve to the place in the middle of the corridor where Ben’s seam was, the hand’s-width shimmer between the two branches, and she stopped in front of it.</p>\n<p>And then she did stop. Just for a moment. She turned round.</p>\n<p>The ghost-room was round her. Ruth was at the board, with her back to her, her hair pinned up. It was the second minute. <em>The protocol requires the interlocks off.</em> Crane was talking. Ruth’s thumb had gone to her mouth.</p>\n<p>Ione stood in the corridor in the suit and looked at her.</p>\n<p>“I know who you’re looking at,” she said. “Later. At the Keystone. I know who it is now.” She swallowed. “He’s all right. He’s — he’s not frightened. You were right. He’s very calm.”</p>\n<p>Ruth bit the side of her thumb, and didn’t hear her, the way she’d never heard her.</p>\n<p>“Goodbye, Ruth,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She turned to face the outer wall — the gantry side, the way home — and stepped through the seam.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The gantry swayed under her. The keel hummed. The Lace, above and behind her, was singing louder now, and the light was more gold than violet, and the gulf beneath the gantry was full of a strange shifting glow, as if the whole Vault were lit from a new angle. She ran. She couldn’t run in the suit; she walked as fast as it would let her, three hundred metres of rusted steel lattice, past the loose bolt — she stepped on the plate beside it, and it rattled, and she nearly stopped, and didn’t — to the door in the lattice of Spindle Four.</p>\n<p>Down. The stairs. The ladders. The hut in the last Lee, its iron door standing open, the planks inside where they’d lain under his coat and hers. She didn’t stop.</p>\n<p>The Breach.</p>\n<p>It was worse than it had been. She’d known it would be. The payment was gathering at the core; the Lamp was pushing and swinging and pulling all at once; the lamp-weather in the Breach was a gale. She went down the single spine of keelstone with its ladder rungs, and down swung round her, and swung, and swung, and the keel’s bubble held. It held. She could feel it straining. She could feel it averaging the whole screaming world out over its metre of calm, and she understood now what he had felt on the way up with her wrapped round him, and she went down, rung after rung, not looking out, not looking down, the rungs coming up past her visor, black iron, pitted.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the Breach the lattice closed round her again. The great struts. A Lee. The weather dropped away.</p>\n<p>She stopped on the walkway, and put her bare hand on a strut, and stood there, breathing.</p>\n<p>She was past it. She was out of the Fold. She’d done it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know what time it was. The suit’s clocks had died on the Rim; she’d watched the numbers fall apart. She didn’t know how long she had, or whether she’d made it, or whether up there in the Crown the needle was still on the line and the wheel was still turning under his hand.</p>\n<p>She turned round. She tipped her head back. She looked up.</p>\n<p>The spindle rose above her, black lattice, narrowing, into the glare. The Breach. The hut. The gallery, a ring round the neighbouring pillar, its windows full of moving light. The gantry, a thread across the gulf. And above it all, the Rim, a dark circle round the Lamp, hung with the Lace. The Lace was singing. The Lamp was gold.</p>\n<p>And on the Rim, in the Lace — small, very far, at the edge of a clearing in the forest of light — there were two figures.</p>\n<p>She could see them. She could see them quite clearly. A grey figure in a keel suit, standing by the Rim’s inner rail. And another, smaller, standing in front of it, pressed back against it, the grey arms round her. Not moving. Two people, holding each other up, at the top of the world.</p>\n<p>Looking down at her.</p>\n<p>She stood on the walkway in the Lee with her bare hand on the strut and looked up at them for a long time. At herself, standing on his boots. At him, holding her. At the two of them, on the Rim, an hour ago, a lifetime ago, looking down at a small grey figure in a keel suit on Spindle Four, and thinking <em>it’s me. That’s me, coming back without her.</em></p>\n<p>She raised her hand.</p>\n<p>She held it there. The palm out. She didn’t know what else to do. She held it up, toward them, toward him, toward the girl on his boots who didn’t know yet, who would find out in the Crown with her voice cracking down the middle — <em>it was the suit</em> — and she held it there, and held it, until her arm shook.</p>\n<p>Then she lowered it.</p>\n<p>She turned, and went on down.</p>\n<p>She’d gone perhaps ten steps down the next flight of stairs when the light changed.</p>\n<p>It didn’t fade. It didn’t flicker. There was no sound. The Lamp — which had been gold, and singing, and very bright — was simply, between one step and the next, not there. And the Lace was not there. And the light was not there. And there was nothing above her and nothing round her and nothing below her but the dark.</p>\n<p>Ione Tally stood on the stair in the black, in his suit, with the keel humming round her in its little metre of calm, and the only light in the whole world was the soft band of it round the housing on her back, glowing faintly on the rungs.</p>\n<p>Above her, very high up, in the dark, something that had been about to happen was never going to happen.</p>\n<p>And somewhere in it, once, a man was looking down at her light.</p>"}, {"file": "19-the-bell.md", "label": "Chapter 18", "title": "The Bell", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": true, "words": 5136, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Then we are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Common Office, final response</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The first thing she noticed was that she was going to float away.</p>\n<p>She stood on the stair in the black with one hand on the rail and the keel humming round her, and her body told her — very clearly, very urgently, the way your body tells you that you’ve missed a step in the dark — that there was nothing holding her down. That she weighed nothing. That if she let go of the rail she’d drift up off the stair like a puff of chalk and be lost.</p>\n<p>She held on to the rail. She stood very still. She waited for it to pass.</p>\n<p>It didn’t pass. And after a while she understood that it wasn’t going to, because it wasn’t a gust. It wasn’t lamp-weather. It was just the weight. The Lamp had been pushing on her from above every minute of her life, every minute of every Holdfast life for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and it had stopped. The Anvil was still there, pulling. The world was still there, under the Anvil, pulling. But the push was gone. And what was left was⁠—</p>\n<p><em>Ordinary</em>, she thought. <em>This is what they weigh. Outside. This is ordinary.</em></p>\n<p>She let go of the rail. She didn’t float away. She stood on the stair and weighed what a woman weighs, which was less than she’d ever weighed in her life, and it was so strange that she laughed out loud in the suit — a short, cracked, frightened laugh that went out into the dark and didn’t come back.</p>\n<p>Because there was no echo. There was no sound at all.</p>\n<p>She hadn’t known there was a sound until it stopped. The Lamp had made one. The Lace had made one. It had been there all her life, under everything — under the Bell, under the voices in the lanes, under the wind in the ropes — so faint and so constant that nobody in Holdfast had ever heard it any more than they heard their own blood. A hum. A singing. It had been getting louder on the Rim, at the end, the singing in the gold light. And now it was gone, and the silence where it had been was so enormous that she could hear her own heart in the suit, and the keel’s hum, and nothing else in the world.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<p>Nothing. Black. Not dark — dark was the Quiet, dark was the tank with the lid shut. This was more than dark. It went up and up above her and didn’t stop, and there was nothing in it anywhere. No Lamp. No Lace. No Rim. No Crown. She tried to see where the Crown should be — up there, at the top of Spindle One, where he was — and there was nothing to see. Black on black. It could have been a hand’s width from her face. It could have been the edge of the world.</p>\n<p><em>Somewhere in it</em>, she thought. <em>Once.</em></p>\n<p>She stood on the stair for a long time, looking up at nothing, with the soft band of light round the keel on her back glowing on the rungs.</p>\n<p>Then she took out her chalk, and held her hand out flat, and blew.</p>\n<p>The chalk went off her palm in a little cloud, lit by the keel’s glow. And fell. Straight down. In a thin white curtain, straight as a plumb line, down past the stair and on into the dark.</p>\n<p>True chalk. On Spindle Four, three kilometres up. Where there had never, in a hundred and sixty-eight years, been anything but crooked.</p>\n<p>She watched it go until she couldn’t see it.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said to him, out loud, into the dark, in case he could hear. He couldn’t. She said it anyway. “There. It’s done. You did it.”</p>\n<p>Then she started to climb down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took her nine hours. She didn’t know it was nine hours; the suit’s clocks were dead, and she hadn’t brought anything else. She knew it afterwards, because the Tollers told her. At the time it was only stairs.</p>\n<p>Stairs in the dark. Ladders in the dark. The keel’s little glow on the rungs, a metre of soft light round her and black beyond it. She’d climbed Spindle Four a dozen times since she was nine, and she’d never climbed it like this — never with nothing pushing, never without the Lees. There were no Lees now. The struts were still there, the great black beams, and she went past them and felt them pull, faintly, the way the Anvil pulled; but there was no push for them to pull against, and so there was no calm, and no storm, and no difference between one place and the next. Only down. The same down everywhere. Straight chalk everywhere.</p>\n<p>It was easy. That was the horror of it, at first, and then the wonder. It was easy. Her legs were Holdfast legs, built for half as much again, and in the ordinary weight they felt like springs. She went down the ladders in the suit faster than she’d ever gone down them in her life. She stopped at the hut and didn’t go in. She stopped at the best Lee, the one with the four struts, where they’d slept and the chalk had hung in the air like a cloud — and blew chalk, and watched it fall straight, and stood there for a while, and went on.</p>\n<p>She thought about him. She thought about him the whole way down. She tried not to, and then she stopped trying.</p>\n<p>She thought about the needle. About his hand on the brass wheel, turning it, a little this way and a little that. About him looking down through the great window at a small light on Spindle Four. About what he’d seen, at the end. Her light, past the Breach. Her light, stopped on a walkway. Her arm raised.</p>\n<p><em>He saw me raise my hand.</em></p>\n<p>She had to stop on a ladder when she thought that, and hold on, and wait until she could see again.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know if he’d seen it. She didn’t know if his eyes had been on her or on the needle, whether he’d looked up in time. She’d never know. She understood, holding on to the ladder in the dark, that she was going to spend the rest of her life not knowing, and that she was going to have to decide what she believed, and that nobody would ever be able to tell her if she was right.</p>\n<p>She decided. Right there, on the ladder.</p>\n<p><em>He saw me.</em></p>\n<p>She went on down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At about two kilometres, she saw the city.</p>\n<p>She’d been looking down for it for hours. There’d been nothing — the dark below as black as the dark above, the keel’s glow on the rungs and nothing past it. And then she came round the corner of a flight of stairs onto a walkway on the spindle’s inner side, where you could see down through the open lattice to the Anvil, and there it was.</p>\n<p>It was lights.</p>\n<p>Not the Lamp’s light. Not violet, not white, not one great glare over everything. Little lights. Points. Tiny and warm and yellow, thousands and thousands of them, spread out across the black floor of the world. Scattered through the scaffold towers. Strung along the lanes. Clustered round the spindles’ feet. Some of them still, some of them moving — carried, she thought; lamps being carried through the lanes. Each one so small, alone, that it hardly lit anything. And all of them together making the shape of the city, laid out in the dark below her like a map drawn in fire.</p>\n<p>The lamps. The oil lamps. The ones they’d been making in their kitchens for eleven rounds, pressing algae, twisting wicks, while he was in the Quiet and the Stewardship was saying the Relief was a demon from the Drop. They’d lit them.</p>\n<p>She stood on the walkway and looked at it.</p>\n<p><em>Points of light</em>, he’d said. In the loft, lying on the floor, when she’d asked him what the stars were like. <em>Little points of light in the dark. Thousands of them. They don’t do anything. They’re just there.</em></p>\n<p>“Oh,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She stood there a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Pim was at the bottom.</p>\n<p>She heard him before she saw him. She was on the last ladder down into the wreck of the lift terminal at the foot of the spindle, and there was a lamp below, and voices, and then a high cracked voice shouted, “<em>There — there! There’s a light! On the — it’s the suit, it’s the suit, it’s the Relief, he’s —</em>”</p>\n<p>She came down off the ladder into the wreckage, into the lamp-light.</p>\n<p>Pim was there, and Osk, and Bett from the riggers’ tower, and a dozen others. Holding lamps. They’d been waiting at the foot of Spindle Four, in the dark, in the wreck. Pim was at the front. He had a clay lamp in each hand and a rat on each shoulder and his face was streaked black with soot and wet with crying, and he was running at her — at the suit — with his arms out and the lamps swinging and the rats clinging on⁠—</p>\n<p>And then he saw her face.</p>\n<p>He stopped. Just stopped, a metre away, his arms still out. The light from his lamps went up into the visor. She saw him see her. She saw him look at her face, small in the big helmet. And then look at the suit. And then look past her, at the ladder, up into the dark. And then back at her face.</p>\n<p>He didn’t say anything. He was eleven, and he’d been everywhere, and heard everything, and carried the Relief’s recorder out through a vent. He looked at her. And he put the lamps down on the ground, very carefully, one and then the other. And he came and put his arms round the suit’s leg and held on to it and pressed his face against the plating and didn’t say anything at all.</p>\n<p>Osk had taken off his hard hat.</p>\n<p>He stood there in the lamp-light at the foot of the spindle, the huge slow man, and held it against his chest the way he’d held it on the first day, at the top of the well, when he’d knelt. He didn’t kneel now. He looked at her. At the suit. And he bowed his head.</p>\n<p>Behind him, the others did the same.</p>\n<p>She opened the suit. She hadn’t known she was going to. She found the clasp at the neck and the seals and it opened down the back the way it had opened for Cas a hundred times, and she stepped out of it in her rigger’s clothes onto the rubble at the foot of Spindle Four, and nearly fell, because she was so light — and Osk caught her. The way he’d caught Cas, the first day. Under the arms. And held her up.</p>\n<p>“Stack your bones,” said Osk, gently.</p>\n<p>“I know how,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She stood. She looked at them. At the lamps. At the dark.</p>\n<p>“He’s up there,” she said. “He’s — it worked. It’s done. He did it. The Lamp’s closed.” Her voice was quite steady. “He’s not coming down. He’s the witness. He’s in it.”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything.</p>\n<p>“The Drop’s a road,” said Ione Tally. “He told you. When the Lamp goes out, the Drop’s a road. It’s out. The Drop’s a road.”</p>\n<p>Somewhere behind Osk, in the dark, somebody started to cry.</p>\n<p>And then — faintly, far off, from somewhere out in the city, carried on the still dark air — somebody else began to shout it. <em>The Drop’s a road.</em> And another voice took it up. And another. She heard it go out across Holdfast, voice by voice, lamp by lamp, the way the shutter-wave went out at dusk. <em>The Drop’s a road. The Drop’s a road. He said. When the Lamp goes out. He said.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went to the Tower first.</p>\n<p>She knew she should go to the Commons. She knew there’d be thousands there, and fear, and the Stewards, and Crane. She knew there were people in the lanes who hadn’t believed him — she could hear them, now and then, among the other voices: screaming, praying, somewhere a woman shrieking <em>demon, demon, he’s killed the Lamp</em> over and over. She knew all of it.</p>\n<p>She went to the Tower first.</p>\n<p>The square round it was full of lamps. The Tollers had put them out on every ledge and step and platform of the tower, all the way up round the column, so that the whole spiralling scaffold was outlined in little yellow flames, and the black keelstone pillar in the middle of it, with its hundred and sixty-eight years of chalk, gleamed in their light like wet stone. People were standing in the square, looking up at it. Just standing. Holding their own lamps.</p>\n<p>And the Bell was coming.</p>\n<p>She felt it before she heard it. It came up through the soles of her boots, through the keelstone of the square — not a swell. Not a tide. Not the long slow rising pressure she’d felt every ten minutes of her life. A <em>knock</em>. Short and sharp and deep, a single blow, like a fist on a door far below. And then silence. And then⁠—</p>\n<p>Another. And another.</p>\n<p>Once a second. Once a second. Once a second.</p>\n<p>She stood in the square with her hand on her chest and felt it, and the people standing round her with their lamps were feeling it too — she could see it in their faces, turned up toward the tower, toward the column, toward the Tollers on their platforms. Every face had the same look. A kind of fear. A kind of wonder. A kind of listening, the way you listen to a heartbeat when you’ve put your head on someone’s chest.</p>\n<p><em>Knock. Knock. Knock.</em></p>\n<p>She went into the Tower.</p>\n<p>The round room at the bottom was full of lamplight, and pendulums, and Tollers — every Toller there was, crammed in, standing round the walls, silent. Tobiah was at the column with a stick of chalk in his hand. He wasn’t writing. He was just standing there, with his hand raised and the chalk in it, crying behind his lens-spectacles, because there was no way to write fast enough. Every second, another. There was no way to keep up.</p>\n<p>And in her chair, with her hand flat on the stone, was Gran.</p>\n<p>She was so small. She’d always been small, but she was smaller now, sunk down into the chair, her felt slippers not reaching the floor. Her face was grey. Her breathing was short and quick and shallow, and there was a blanket over her knees, and Steward Harrow was asleep in her lap. Her eyes were closed.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt by the chair.</p>\n<p>The old woman’s eyes opened. Black and bright as a bird’s. They went over her, in about a second, the way they went over everything.</p>\n<p>“Hmph,” said Perpetua Tally. “You’re back.”</p>\n<p>“I’m back.”</p>\n<p>“On your own.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>Gran’s eyes held hers. They didn’t ask. They didn’t need to. They held her for a long moment, and something in the old face shifted, very slightly — not surprise; Gran was never surprised — and the knotted hand came off the column and found Ione’s hand and held it.</p>\n<p>“Did he?” said Gran.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She breathed. “Good. Good lad. Good.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came up through the column. <em>Knock.</em> And again. <em>Knock.</em> Gran’s head tipped a little, listening.</p>\n<p>“Listen to it,” she said. “Listen. One a second. Hear that? One a second.” Her mouth moved; it might have been a smile. “That’s how fast they live out there. That’s their <em>time</em>, Ione. That’s the Outside’s own time, coming up through my column. We’ve been counting their night for a hundred and sixty-eight years and now we’re in it. Now we’re <em>in</em> it.” She shut her eyes. “One a second. No wonder they never get anything done.”</p>\n<p>Ione laughed. She couldn’t help it. It came out of her as a sob.</p>\n<p>“Now,” said Gran. “The box.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah brought it. The dented steel box with the Works stencil. He put it on the old woman’s knees, on top of the blanket, beside the rat, and she laid both her hands on its lid.</p>\n<p>“Ruth’s Log,” she said. “And the letter. The last one. For the girl. For Maddy.” She pushed it, very slightly, toward Ione. “You take it. Out. You take it out to her. That’s the Tollers’ job. It’s always been the Tollers’ job. Somebody from this family carries that letter to that little girl. We promised Ruth.” Her breath rattled. “And you write the last page. In the Log. You write what happened. You write it properly. Every word.”</p>\n<p>“I will.”</p>\n<p>“Not now. Later. When you can.” She opened her eyes. “You’ll know when.”</p>\n<p>“Gran⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Hush.” The knotted fingers squeezed. “Hush now. I’m listening.”</p>\n<p>They listened. The Bell came up through the column. <em>Knock.</em> The pendulums on the platforms above them trembled on their wires. <em>Knock.</em> The lamps on the ledges bowed their flames. <em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” said Perpetua Tally, very quietly, with her eyes closed. “Off you go, when it’s time. Go and see if the Outside’s all it’s cracked up to be. Take them all with you.”</p>\n<p><em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>“Tell me how it ends,” she said.</p>\n<p><em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>And between that Bell and the next, with her granddaughter’s hand in hers and her other hand on the rat asleep in her lap, the Head Toller of Holdfast stopped breathing.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt by the chair. She didn’t move. The Bell came up through the floor, and the column, and the chair, and Gran’s still hand in hers, once a second, once a second, the way it would go on doing now for as long as anyone was there to feel it.</p>\n<p>After a while, Tobiah went to the column and made a single mark in chalk at the bottom of the last row. Not a stroke. A short line across, and a word beside it.</p>\n<p><em>PERPETUA.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Commons was full.</p>\n<p>Not with the sea of faces she’d seen from the back wall on the night the Relief came. With lamps. Thousands of them, held up, filling the great hall from wall to wall, so that the broken roof overhead was lit from below and the girders were gold and the strips of open sky between them were black — black, where the Lace had always been — and the Tollers’ banner still hung from the gantry, Seren’s line, white on black, lit by all those little flames.</p>\n<p>And they were quiet. That was what she noticed when she came in by the great doors with Osk beside her and Pim holding her hand. Twenty thousand people and more, and they were quiet. Some of them were crying. Some of them were holding each other. Here and there, someone was praying, very softly, the words of the Placard. But they were waiting. They’d been waiting, she understood, for hours. In the dark, with their lamps. The way they’d been told to.</p>\n<p><em>When the dark comes, don’t be afraid.</em></p>\n<p>The crowd saw her. She felt it go through them — a turning of heads, a stir — and they parted for her. Not stepping back from her, the way people had stepped back from the Unwatched for eleven years. Parting. Making a way. She walked down it toward the platform, and hands reached out as she went by and touched her arm, her shoulder, the rope on her shoulder, and she didn’t know what to do with it, so she let them.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane was on the platform.</p>\n<p>He was standing in front of the Placard. They’d brought it from the chapel — someone had carried it here, in its keelstone frame, and set it up on the platform on an easel, with a lamp before it. The yellowed laminate. The stick figure by its stick console. He was standing beside it in his black coat with the gold lamp on the breast, very straight, very old, holding a lamp of his own, and he watched her come down the aisle through the crowd, and when she reached the foot of the platform, by the brass rail of Muster Point B, he looked at her face.</p>\n<p>He didn’t ask either.</p>\n<p>He turned to the Placard. He lifted his lamp so that its light fell on the words. And in a clear, carrying voice — the voice that had said <em>let him finish</em> — he read it aloud.</p>\n<p>“Section Nine. In the event of containment loss. Remain at your station and await relief. Do not attempt to traverse the Lattice. Relief crews will be dispatched. You will not be forgotten.”</p>\n<p>He lowered the lamp.</p>\n<p>“Who keeps the Watch?” said Halloran Crane.</p>\n<p>The hall didn’t answer at once. Then, from somewhere at the back, raggedly — and then from everywhere, twenty thousand voices, quietly, uncertainly, the way you’d answer a question you’d answered all your life and suddenly didn’t know the meaning of:</p>\n<p>“<em>We keep the Watch.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Until?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Until Relief.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And when Relief comes?”</p>\n<p>The hall was silent.</p>\n<p>“Then,” said Halloran Crane, “we are relieved.”</p>\n<p>He set his lamp down on the platform in front of the Placard.</p>\n<p>“Relief has come,” he said. “It came up out of the Drop, as we were always told it would. It wasn’t what we were told. It was a man from the Outside, in a suit, who stood here and told us the truth, which we did not want. And then it went up the spindle with Ione Tally and closed the Lamp. The Lamp is out. The Watch is over.” He looked out at the lamps. “We are relieved. Every one of us. You are all relieved. You have kept your station for a hundred and sixty-eight years and you may leave it now.”</p>\n<p>The sound the hall made was not a cheer. It was not a groan. She’d never heard it before and she never heard it again. It was the sound, she thought afterwards, that a great many people make when they have been holding something up for a very long time, and are told they can put it down.</p>\n<p>Crane waited for it to pass.</p>\n<p>“There’s one more thing,” he said, “and then I’ll be done.”</p>\n<p>He looked down at her. At the foot of the platform, by the brass rail. He held her eyes.</p>\n<p>“Thirteen years ago,” said Halloran Crane, “Seren Tally painted the truth on the wall of this hall, and I gave her to the Drop for it. I had the order drawn up before she painted it. She was right, and I knew she was right, and I did it anyway, because I thought your calm was worth more than her life. I was wrong.” His voice didn’t shake at all. “I have nothing to offer her daughter. I’m not asking her for anything. I only wanted you all to hear me say it, once, in here, where she said it first.”</p>\n<p>The hall was very quiet.</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at him. At the old man on the platform, in the lamplight, beside the Placard.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this. For thirteen years. She’d thought about what she would say to him, if she ever had the chance, and it had been different every time — screams, curses, a knife, silence. She’d thought she would feel something enormous.</p>\n<p>She found she felt tired. Very tired. And the Bell was coming up through the floor once a second, and her Gran was dead in her chair in the Tower, and somewhere above her in the dark, once, a man was looking at her light.</p>\n<p>“You’ll walk out last,” she said.</p>\n<p>Crane looked at her.</p>\n<p>“When they go down,” said Ione. “All of them. You’ll stand at the top of the wells and see every one of them onto the ladders. Every single one. The children and the old ones and the Wardens and the ones who called my mother a heretic and the ones who spat at me in the lanes. Every one. And when they’ve all gone down, you’ll go down last.”</p>\n<p>It wasn’t forgiveness. She knew that. She didn’t know what it was. Something drier. Something that would do.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane bowed his head.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “I will.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went down the Drop.</p>\n<p>It took all night — or what would have been night; there were no nights now, and no days, only the dark and the lamps and the Bell. The riggers re-hung the ladders at every one of the fourteen wells, the ladders they’d cut and hauled up on the round of the Kindling festival. They strung ropes down beside them, and lamp-lines, clay lamps hung on wire every ten metres all the way down the shafts, so that looking down a well was like looking down a throat lined with little fires. And Holdfast went down them.</p>\n<p>Family by family. Street by street. Wardens at the tops of the ladders, riggers on the ropes, Tollers with their ledgers counting names. Children on their parents’ backs. Old people lowered in rope slings, three riggers to each. Pim’s rats in a wire cage lashed to his shoulders, all of them, the whole Stewardship, squeaking. Bundles and bags and bedrolls. Lamps. Everyone had a lamp. The Tanks, the market, the Commons, the Crèche, all left behind, empty, lit, in the dark.</p>\n<p>People sang, going down. Not the Tending. She listened for it, and didn’t hear it once. They sang the riggers’ song, the hauling song, the one with no words but <em>haul</em> — a deep slow rhythm, one voice calling, all the voices answering, going down the lines of ladders into the dark. <em>Haul. Haul. Haul.</em> And under it, through the rungs, through the keelstone, once a second, the Bell.</p>\n<p>Ione went down Fourteen.</p>\n<p>She went first. Before anyone. Osk tried to stop her, and then didn’t. It was the well she’d pulled him out of. It was the ladder he’d come up. It was the well her mother had gone down, thirteen years ago, and not looked back. She went down it in her rigger’s clothes, with his suit strapped to a rope and lowered beside her — Osk had insisted; the Relief’s suit didn’t get left — and the dented steel box with Ruth’s Log in it lashed to her back, and her lamp hanging from her harness.</p>\n<p>Three hundred metres through the Anvil. Rung after rung, in the lamp-light. It was only a ladder. It was so easy in the ordinary weight that she kept thinking she’d missed something.</p>\n<p>Then the bottom of the Anvil. And below it, the place where the Shear had been.</p>\n<p>She stopped on the ladder, there, and hung on it, and looked.</p>\n<p>Nothing. A shaft. Round, black, ribbed with old pipe-brackets. A ladder going down it. The air was still. There was no curtain, no trembling, no wrongness. Nothing jumped or smeared. She could see straight down the shaft forty metres, fifty, sixty, to the bottom, where there was a glow — a light, a lamp, not hers — and a round window, lit.</p>\n<p>She went down through the place where the Shear had been, and felt nothing at all.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the conduit, at the foot of the ladder, there was a shape covered in foil.</p>\n<p>It lay crumpled against the wall. Someone had covered it — carefully, tenderly, from the head down, the silver foil tucked round it at the edges — with a blanket from a first-aid kit. The foil crackled a little in the air from the shaft. It shone in her lamp’s light.</p>\n<p>Ione stood at the foot of the ladder and looked at it.</p>\n<p>She knew. She’d known since the Tower, the first night, when he’d said <em>there was someone at the bottom of the conduit, in mended clothes, with a lamp carved from a hard hat</em>, and Gran had made that sound like a bird. She’d known. She knelt down beside it on the floor of the conduit and put her lamp down.</p>\n<p>She lifted the edge of the foil. Just a little. Just at the neck.</p>\n<p>There was a cord there. Twisted fibre, algae-thread, the kind Holdfast made. And on it, lying against the patched cloth, a little yellow lamp. Carved from the plastic of an old hard hat. Worn smooth and soft as soap. For a crib.</p>\n<p>She didn’t lift the foil any further. She didn’t need to.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Mam,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She put her hand flat on the foil, over where her mother’s heart would have been. She knelt there for a long time. The Bell came up through the floor, once a second.</p>\n<p>“I found the edge of the world,” she said. “It wasn’t what I thought. I went up to find you. You weren’t there. I was, though.” She was quiet. “You were right. About the line. About all of it. Everybody knows now. It’s on a banner in the Commons. Crane said it. In front of everyone.” She was quiet again. “Gran’s gone. Just now. She was listening to the Bell. It goes once a second now, Mam. Like a heart. You’d have liked that. You’d have wanted to count it.”</p>\n<p>She tucked the foil back round the little yellow lamp.</p>\n<p>“Somebody covered you up,” she said. “A lady. From Outside. She couldn’t stand you lying there. She didn’t know who you were. She did it anyway.”</p>\n<p>And behind her, from the lit window in the wall of the conduit, from the booth beyond it, there was a sound. A hatch opening. Boots on a ladder, coming down, slow and stiff, as if they’d been sitting for a long time.</p>\n<p>Ione stood up and turned round.</p>\n<p>A woman was coming down the hatch-ladder from the booth into the conduit. A broad, weathered woman in her fifties, in a heavy grey suit with its helmet off and nothing on its back where a keel should have been, with cropped iron-grey hair and a face like a well-used tool. She had a torch in one hand. She reached the bottom and turned and put the torch on Ione, and stood there looking at her. At her face. At the white streak in her hair. At the rope on her shoulder, and the box on her back, and the empty grey suit hanging on its rope from the ladder above her.</p>\n<p>Her face did something complicated.</p>\n<p>“You must be Ione,” said Teo Lisk. Her voice was hoarse. “He never shut up about you.”</p>"}, {"file": "20-stars.md", "label": "Chapter 19", "title": "Stars", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 5267, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>They’re always there. You just can’t see them.</p><span class=\"attr\">Nell Hale, aged ten, on the telephone</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The edge of the lee was half a metre from Teo Lisk’s boots when it stopped existing.</p>\n<p>She’d been watching it come for — she didn’t know. A long time. Four minutes, maybe, by the suit clock, which was the longest four minutes of her life, and she’d had some long ones. The shimmer on the floor, a hand’s height off the ground, like heat over a road. Crawling. The dust on the far side of it drifting, and the dust on her side lying still, and the line between them coming across the floor of Lattice Station 14-C toward the corner where she sat with her boots flat against the wall. A metre and a half. A metre. She’d stopped moving the chair, because there was nowhere left to move it. She’d folded her hands in her lap. She’d said <em>come on, Hale</em> a few more times, not out loud.</p>\n<p>Three-quarters of a metre. Half a metre.</p>\n<p>And then it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>It didn’t go away. It didn’t retreat. It was there, a shimmer, a line of wrongness crawling toward her boots — and then, between one breath and the next, there was nothing on the floor but floor. Dust, lying flat. All of it. On both sides.</p>\n<p>The room settled.</p>\n<p>She felt it. She couldn’t have said how. It was like the moment when a ship that’s been rolling in a swell comes into harbour and the deck goes still under you — not a movement, the end of a movement she hadn’t known was there. The air stopped being wrong. The floor stopped being a floor that might tilt. The whole room simply became a room, the way rooms were, and she was a woman sitting in a chair in the corner of it, and she weighed what she weighed.</p>\n<p>She sat there without moving.</p>\n<p>Then she got up, slowly, and went to the viewport.</p>\n<p>The Shear was gone. The trembling curtain across the conduit, forty metres up — gone. The ladder went up the shaft straight as a ruler, and she could see every rung, clean, not jumping, not smearing. It went up and up into the dark. And at the top — where there had always been, since she’d sat down in this chair, a disc of pale hard violet-white light, far up the shaft, too bright to look at for long⁠—</p>\n<p>Nothing. Dark.</p>\n<p>She stood with her hand flat on the glass. The glass with the names on it in black marker. MATTIAS. JOSS. BEN. SEREN (THE LADY). CAS.</p>\n<p>“Oh, Hale,” she said. “Oh, you did it. You bloody did it.”</p>\n<p>And then, because she was alone, and because nobody was ever going to know, she put her forehead against the glass and cried until she couldn’t stand up, and sat down on the floor under the viewport with her back against the wall, and cried some more.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hardline rang eleven minutes later.</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk.” Gaunt. But not Gaunt’s voice. Or Gaunt’s voice with something gone out of it — the softness, the patience, the lent-you-a-book. “Major. Are you — are you there?”</p>\n<p>“I’m here.”</p>\n<p>“Something’s happened.” A pause. She heard him breathe. “The shimmer over the Pit. It’s gone. It was there, and it’s — gone, just now, all at once, the birds went straight over it, a whole flock of them — and the clocks at the cordon have stopped. Not stopped running. Stopped running <em>fast</em>. They’re — they match. For the first time since two in the morning yesterday, they match.” Another breath. “And the output’s gone to zero. Not the fake zero. Real zero. The Draw’s — there’s nothing coming out of it. Nothing at all. It’s as if it isn’t there.” He stopped. “Major, what’s happened?”</p>\n<p>She looked at the dark disc at the top of the shaft.</p>\n<p>“He did it,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Did — reseated Six?”</p>\n<p>“No.” She found she didn’t care any more. She found she’d never cared. “No, Colonel. He never reseated anything. There was nothing to reseat. He went up to the Crown and he shut it. The Lamp. The Lesion. The whole thing. He found a woman with a key and they went up and shut it for good.” She wiped her face with the back of her hand. “It’s not coming back. There’s no more Draw. It’s done. The debt’s done. You’ll want to tell the Minister. You’ll want to tell her he defaulted.”</p>\n<p>The line was silent.</p>\n<p>“The grid,” said Gaunt at last. “Kovač was at half. Without anything from the Draw at all⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Then it’ll go dark.”</p>\n<p>“Six hundred million people.”</p>\n<p>“Then it’ll go dark, Colonel.” She leaned her head back against the wall. “It’ll go dark and they’ll be alive in it. That’s the trade. That’s the whole trade. Tell them to light a candle.”</p>\n<p>Another silence. And then, to her enormous surprise, a sound down the line that might have been a laugh. A small, broken, astonished sound.</p>\n<p>“Is Dr Hale⁠—” Gaunt began.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know,” said Teo. “I don’t know yet. I’m waiting.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Civil Defence teams came four hours later.</p>\n<p>She heard them long before she saw them: voices echoing down the galleries of the Lattice, boots on steel, the crackle of radios, somebody swearing at a door. They came in through the booth’s outer door with their lamps and their ropes and their stretchers — a sergeant she knew from the Kovač fire, Amara Kade, and eight others — and found her sitting in the chair by the viewport with her boots up on the dead monitors, eating the last of the ration paste out of the suit’s tube with her finger.</p>\n<p>“Major,” said Kade. She was staring. “Major, Christ. We’ve been — they said you’d — the Works is <em>walkable</em>, Major, there’s no weather, there’s nothing, it’s just a building now, we came straight in down Radial Nine like it was a Tuesday. We found the stairhead. And the — the algae hall. There’s writing on the glass⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“We’ve got a stretcher. We’ll have you out in two hours. There’s water, there’s⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m not going.”</p>\n<p>Kade stopped.</p>\n<p>“I’m waiting for someone,” said Teo. “Leave me some water. And a lamp. And a blanket; I gave mine away. And then go back up to the Collar and tell them to send everything they’ve got down here. Everything. Every team, every stretcher, every lamp, every blanket in Averna.”</p>\n<p>“Major, the Colonel said⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The Colonel,” said Teo Lisk, “can come down here and carry me out himself.” She finished the paste. “Go on. Go. And Kade — leave the hardline to me.”</p>\n<p>They went, in the end. Kade stayed, and two others, sitting on the floor of the booth by the door with their lamps, not asking. Teo sat in the chair.</p>\n<p>She went down through the hatch once, and sat beside the lady in the foil blanket for a while. Not talking. Just sitting. The Bell came up through the floor — the hammer, the old Dead Man’s Hammer, still going, once a second, the same as it had been going since two in the morning yesterday, the same as it had gone through the car and the canteen and the Collar. Only here, now, at the bottom of the conduit, it came up through the floor exactly as it did everywhere else. No longer stretched. No longer a groan. A knock. Once a second.</p>\n<p><em>Ordinary</em>, she thought.</p>\n<p>She dozed, in the chair, at some point. She dreamed of the flood in the Tessarine underpass. She dreamed of toast.</p>\n<p>She woke to singing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It came down the shaft.</p>\n<p>Faint, at first. So faint that she thought she was still dreaming. A deep, slow, rhythmic sound, far up the conduit, echoing down the round black walls. One voice, calling. Then many voices, answering. A word. The same word, over and over.</p>\n<p><em>Haul.</em></p>\n<p>She was at the viewport before she knew she’d moved. Kade and the others were on their feet behind her.</p>\n<p>Far up the shaft, in the dark — lights. Little lights. Yellow, warm, flickering. Not a torch. Flames. A line of them, coming down the shaft, one below another, strung down the side of the conduit like beads on a thread. Lamps. Somebody was hanging lamps down the shaft, one every few metres, all the way down. And beside the lamps, on the ladder⁠—</p>\n<p>Something grey.</p>\n<p>Her heart went. She saw it and her whole body went toward the glass, her hands flat on it. A grey shape, hulking, heavy, coming down the shaft. The suit. His suit. Grey plates, the housing on its back. It was coming down. It was coming down the shaft toward her and she was already moving to the hatch, she was already saying his name⁠—</p>\n<p>It turned on its rope.</p>\n<p>It was on a rope. It hung from a rope, and it was being lowered, slowly, beside the ladder, turning gently as it came, like a coat on a hanger. Its visor was dark. Its arms hung. There was nobody in it.</p>\n<p>She stopped with her hand on the hatch.</p>\n<p>Beside it, on the ladder, climbing down, was a woman.</p>\n<p>Teo watched her come. She watched her all the way down — through the place where the Shear had been, and past it, and on down, rung after rung, fast and neat and steady, a lamp swinging from her harness and a dented steel box on her back. Short. Broad. A rope on her shoulder. At the bottom she stepped off the ladder and stood for a moment in her lamp’s light, and then turned and knelt by the lady in the foil blanket.</p>\n<p>Teo couldn’t hear what she said. She saw her lift the edge of the foil, at the neck. She saw her put her hand flat on the blanket, over the heart. She saw her stay like that for a long time.</p>\n<p>She saw the white streak in her hair.</p>\n<p>She knew. She’d known since the first chirp. <em>There’s a woman.</em> Forty times in three weeks. A rigger. A key. A way of looking at you as if she’d read the end of the book. Teo had pictured her so often, sitting in this chair, that she almost didn’t need to look.</p>\n<p>She opened the hatch and went down the ladder.</p>\n<p>The woman stood up and turned round. Teo put the torch on her. She was younger than Teo had thought. Or older. It was hard to tell. She had a cut over one eye with black stitches in it, and a splinted wrist, and a face that had been crying and had stopped, and grey-green eyes that looked back at Teo — at her suit, at her face, at her hands — without any hurry at all. As if she’d seen her before. As if she’d been expecting her.</p>\n<p>“You must be Ione,” said Teo. “He never shut up about you.”</p>\n<p>The woman looked at her for a long moment.</p>\n<p>“And you’re Teo,” she said. Her accent was strange — flat, clipped. “You’re the one in the chair.”</p>\n<p>“I’m the one in the chair.”</p>\n<p>“You covered my mother.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked down at the foil blanket. At the little yellow lamp she’d tucked under it, all those hours ago, that had been a few minutes ago, that had been — she didn’t know. She looked back at the woman.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t know who she was,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione Tally. “You did it anyway.”</p>\n<p>They stood there, in the conduit, in the lamplight.</p>\n<p>“He’s not coming,” said Teo. It wasn’t a question.</p>\n<p>“No.” Ione didn’t look away. “He’s up there. In the Crown. He’s the witness. It needed someone to hold it until it closed, and whoever held it would be — in it. When it closed. For ever. Once.” She said it very plainly. “He’s in it. It’s a single instant. He’s in one instant, and it doesn’t end.”</p>\n<p>Teo stood without moving.</p>\n<p>She thought about the booth. About a man kneeling in front of her chair with his broken keel whining and a look on his face she’d seen on men on bridges. <em>It seemed tidy.</em></p>\n<p>“Did he want to?” she said.</p>\n<p>Ione looked at her. And something in her face changed — the patience went out of it, and something younger came in, and her eyes filled.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” she said. “He said — he said it was the only thing he’d ever wanted that he was sure of. He said he wasn’t paying anything. He said he wanted the moment.” She drew a breath. “He said you’d ask. He said, <em>Teo’ll ask if I wanted to</em>. He said to tell you yes. He said to tell you it wasn’t guilt.”</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk put her hand over her mouth.</p>\n<p>She stood there, in the conduit, with the lamps hanging down the shaft above them and the singing coming down, <em>haul, haul, haul</em>, and the Bell coming up through the floor, and she held her hand over her mouth for a long time. When she took it away, she was crying, and she didn’t care.</p>\n<p>“Then that’s all right,” she said. “Then that’s all right. The daft sod. Then it’s all right.”</p>\n<p>And the woman from the top of the world stepped forward and put her arms round her, suit and all, and held on. Like a rigger. Like somebody who hadn’t been touched for a long time and had recently remembered how.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo took the hardline off its hook with a hand that wasn’t quite steady.</p>\n<p>“Collar. This is Lisk. Fourteen-C. Get me Colonel Gaunt.” A pause. “Colonel. Listen to me very carefully, because I’m only going to say this once and then I’m going to be busy. I want every team you’ve got. I want every stretcher and every lamp and every length of rope in the Commonweal sent down Radial Nine. I want water. Tankers of it. I want food, anything, whatever you’ve got. I want doctors. And blankets. Bring blankets. Bring a <em>lot</em> of blankets.”</p>\n<p>A pause on the line.</p>\n<p>“For how many?” said Gaunt.</p>\n<p>Teo looked up the shaft. At the lamps, strung down it like beads on a thread. At the ladder. At the first of them coming down it now, behind Ione — a huge man in a long grey coat and a battered hard hat, carrying a little boy on his back; and behind him a woman with a baby in a sling; and behind her an old man in a rope harness, being lowered, three short broad figures guiding his ropes from above; and behind them, more, and more, faces in the lamplight, short and pale and broad and heavy-boned, all the way up the shaft, as far as she could see, singing.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand,” said Teo Lisk. “Give or take a rat.”</p>\n<p>The line was silent for a very long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They walked out through the Works in the dark.</p>\n<p>It took until dawn. Longer — it took all the next day, and into the night after, for the last of them — but the first of them walked out before dawn, and Ione walked with them.</p>\n<p>The Works was a building now. That was the thing that astonished the Civil Defence crews most, the ones who’d been waiting at the Collar for two days listening to the hammer: the Works was just a building. No weather. No gusts, no shears, no walls becoming floors. The filaments were gone from the halls. The corridors were long and dark and dripping and smelled of algae and ozone and old oil, and they went on for kilometres, and you walked down them, and that was all. Civil Defence had strung lamps along Radial Nine all the way from the Lattice to Gate Nine, and roped the stairs, and posted people at every turning, and Holdfast walked down it in a long, long line with its lamps and its bundles and its children and its rats, singing, and the hammer coming up through the floor once a second.</p>\n<p>Ione walked beside Teo.</p>\n<p>Teo told her things, as they walked. About the car and the Collar and the crisis room. About Ben, on the flatbed, hugging his cradle. About the canteen and the two-percent napkin. About the phone call to a gym on Tier Thirty. Ione listened. She didn’t say much. She looked at everything.</p>\n<p>At the stairhead where the four corridors met, the wall was hung with cloths: a Civil Defence jacket, a tarpaulin, high-vis vests, a dust sheet. People looked at them as they went by and didn’t ask. Teo didn’t explain.</p>\n<p>At the algae hall, Ione stopped.</p>\n<p>The gallery was on Radial Nine; the line of Holdfast walked right past its stair, and she went up it, and Teo went with her, and they stood at the long window looking down into B-7. It was a ruin. The tanks were dry and cracked and grey. The dust lay over everything like snow. On the catwalk, right against the glass, there was a heap of it, and a grey rag, and a scatter of small bones, and a stubby yellow grease pencil lying in the dust.</p>\n<p>And on the glass, faded, cracked, chalky, the writing.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<p>Ione read it twice.</p>\n<p>“That’s him,” she said. “Ben.”</p>\n<p>“That’s Ben.”</p>\n<p>“He found the seam,” said Ione. “In the Carousel. His map. Cas said. We’d have been in there for ever without him.” She put her hand flat on the glass, beside the words. On the other side of it, a centimetre away, in the dust, the grease pencil. “He got us out.”</p>\n<p>Teo couldn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“<em>It was long enough</em>,” said Ione. And stood there, with her hand on the glass, reading it again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Gate Nine stood open.</p>\n<p>The great hazard-striped door, five metres high, rolled all the way back. And beyond it, the Collar — the long evacuated corridors, every door open, every screen dark now, the MARGIN IRREGULARITY: REMAIN CALM gone with the power. Lamps along the floors. Civil Defence at every junction, with water and blankets and quiet voices, and doctors in masks at tables by the doors, and somewhere at the back, in charcoal overcoats, the Calm, standing very still and watching and not doing anything, because there was nothing, any more, that they could do.</p>\n<p>And beyond the Collar, the outer doors. And beyond those⁠—</p>\n<p>She walked out of the Draw at twenty minutes past four in the morning, two days after Continuance Day, into the Pit.</p>\n<p>Wind.</p>\n<p>It hit her in the face the moment she came through the doors. She stopped dead. It was cold — colder than anything had ever been in Holdfast — and it <em>moved</em>. It came at her from nowhere, from the dark, and pushed at her, and went round her, and tugged at her clothes and her hair and the rope on her shoulder, and went on, and came back. It smelled of — she didn’t know. Of nothing she had a word for. Of water, and stone, and distance.</p>\n<p>“That’s wind,” said Teo beside her.</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione. She didn’t know. She’d read about it. It wasn’t like reading about it.</p>\n<p>Around her, Holdfast was coming out of the doors, and stopping, the way she’d stopped. Short broad people in patchwork clothes, holding their lamps, standing on the concrete apron in front of the Collar with their faces turned into the wind, their hair blowing, their mouths open. A child laughed — a high, startled, delighted laugh — and then another, and she looked, and they were jumping. The children. Jumping up and down on the concrete, because in the ordinary weight their Holdfast legs threw them into the air so high, higher than they’d ever jumped in their lives, and they were shrieking with it. Pim was jumping. Pim, with a cage of rats on his back, was leaping into the air like a flea and coming down and leaping again and screaming with laughter.</p>\n<p>And there were people on the rim.</p>\n<p>All round the Pit, on the rim road, high above them, she could see lights. Hundreds of lights. Torches, lanterns, the headlamps of vehicles. People. Standing along the edge of the crater in the dark, thousands of them, looking down. At the Draw. At the doors. At the long line of little flames coming out of it.</p>\n<p>Because the city behind them was dark.</p>\n<p>She looked past them. Past the rim, past the lights. At Averna. At the Outside. At the thing she’d been told about all her life and never once believed was there.</p>\n<p>It was a shape. A vast black shape against a slightly lesser black — the Stacks, rising in their terraced ranks, a hundred and forty levels, tier on tier on tier, going up and up and away into the distance on every side like mountains. And they were dark. Every one. Not a light in them. Here and there a tiny flicker — a candle in a window, a torch on a terrace, a fire — and otherwise, nothing. The great lanterns of the Stacks, that Cas had told her about, that had never gone out in ninety-one years, were out. The Canopy was out. The Draw was gone, and Averna was dark, the whole of it, all six hundred million, standing on their terraces and their balconies and their rooftops in the cold, in the wind, in the dark.</p>\n<p>Looking up.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She didn’t have words for it. She’d have them later — she’d find some, the way you find words for anything, eventually, by living with it — but she didn’t have them then, standing on the concrete apron of the Pit at twenty past four in the morning with the wind in her face.</p>\n<p>There were so many.</p>\n<p>That was the first thing. He’d told her. <em>Thousands of them.</em> She’d thought she understood. She’d thought: a thousand is the Commons with the roof on; a thousand is the Tollers’ column, a few rows. She hadn’t understood. The sky was full of them. The whole sky. It went up from the black edge of the Stacks on every side, black, blacker than the dark in the Vault after the Lamp went out, a black that went on and on and didn’t stop — and it was full of lights. Small ones. Tiny. Points. Some so bright they seemed to shiver. Some so faint she could only see them when she looked beside them instead of at them. White, mostly. Some blue. Some — yes — a little red, the way he’d said. Scattered across the whole of the black in no pattern she could see and every pattern she could imagine. Crowded, in places, so thick they ran together into a sort of mist, a pale river of light going right across the sky from one edge of the dark to the other.</p>\n<p>They didn’t move. They didn’t sing. They didn’t hum. They didn’t reach down toward her in long bright threads. They didn’t do anything. They were just there.</p>\n<p>Her knees went.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the concrete. She didn’t decide to. She just found she was sitting, with her lamp beside her and her hands flat on the cold ground and her head tipped back, looking up. And around her, she saw, Holdfast was doing the same. All along the apron, people were sitting down. Lying down. Lying on their backs on the concrete with their lamps beside them, looking up, the children who’d been jumping lying flat now with their arms spread out, silent. Osk was sitting with his hard hat in his lap and his mouth open. Pim had lain down on his back beside her with the cage of rats on his chest and was staring up with his eyes so wide they shone.</p>\n<p>Nobody spoke. Thirty thousand people came out of the Draw into the wind, one after another, all that night and all the next day, and every one of them, when they came out of the doors and looked up, stopped. And a lot of them sat down.</p>\n<p>And up on the rim — she saw, when she could look at anything but the sky — the people of Averna were doing it too. Standing along the edge of the Pit with their torches, their heads tipped back. Six hundred million of them, on their terraces and their balconies and their rooftops, in the dark, in the cold, looking up at a sky that no one in the city under fifty had ever seen. The same sky. At the same time. All of them together.</p>\n<p>She looked for one.</p>\n<p>She sat on the concrete and looked up at the thousands of them, and looked, and looked. A bright one. Not the brightest; there was one very bright one, low down, white and fierce and shivering, and she thought that must be the one he’d called the Dog Star, and it wasn’t his. She wanted one that was a little on its own. One that didn’t have anything else bright near it. She looked for a long time.</p>\n<p>There. Low in the south, over the black edge of the Stacks. A single star, quite bright, a little golden, sitting by itself in a dark patch of sky with nothing near it at all. Alone. Steady. Just there.</p>\n<p><em>Fomalhaut</em>, he’d said, in the gallery, while she sat against the algae trough with her head on her knees. <em>Which is lonely. Which sits by itself.</em></p>\n<p>She didn’t know if that was it. She’d ask Nell. She’d find out. It didn’t matter.</p>\n<p>“That one,” she said, out loud, to him, in case. “That one’s yours.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Calm came, eventually. Of course they did. They came across the apron in their charcoal overcoats with their tablets, which didn’t work, and their lists, which were paper now, and a tall silver-haired woman in a dark suit walking in front of them, very straight. And they began to talk about processing, and registration, and screening, and quarantine — which was fair, Teo said afterwards; these people had been sealed off from every germ in the world for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and a cold would kill half of them — and relocation for comfort.</p>\n<p>The silver-haired woman stopped in front of Ione, where she sat on the concrete.</p>\n<p>“I’m the Minister of Continuance,” she said. “Ondine Rask. I’m told you — came out first. That you were with Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at her from the ground. At her very straight back and her crease-less suit and her face, which was grey with exhaustion and entirely closed, like a door.</p>\n<p>She thought of a man with silver hair, fifteen storeys high on the side of a scaffold tower, holding up a key.</p>\n<p>“Your deputy,” she said, “ordered the test.”</p>\n<p>The Minister went quite still.</p>\n<p>“Aldous Crane,” said Ione. “He came up to the Crown with a camera crew and a key round his neck, and he told Ruth Tally to take the interlocks off, because you wanted a round number for Continuance Day. She said no. He said her daughter’s name. She said yes.” Her voice was very calm. “Then Six went, and she asked him for the key, and he wouldn’t give it to her. He held it. And it fell. And nineteen hundred people spent a hundred and sixty-eight years in there because of it.” She looked up at Rask with her grey-green eyes. “He’s been dead a hundred and thirty years. He wrote it down, before he died. I can show you. And Ruth Tally’s log. It’s in this box.”</p>\n<p>“How⁠—” said Rask. Her voice had gone thin. “How can you <em>possibly</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I watched him do it,” said Ione Tally. “A hundred and seventy-five thousand times.”</p>\n<p>Rask stared at her.</p>\n<p>“Who <em>are</em> you?” she said.</p>\n<p>Ione thought about it. She thought about it seriously, the way she thought about every question, turning it over. She looked at the dark city, and the thousands of lamps coming out of the doors behind her, and the sky.</p>\n<p>“I’m the night shift,” she said.</p>\n<p>Behind Rask, a little apart from the overcoats, a soft, older man in a brown cardigan buttoned wrong had stopped. He had a paper bag in his hand. He was looking at Ione, and at Teo, sitting beside her on the concrete, and at the dented steel box on the ground between them. His face in the lamplight was very tired, and very strange — as if something had broken in it, and something else, underneath, were trying to come out.</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk,” said Ansel Gaunt quietly. “Is Dr Hale⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He’s in the Crown,” said Teo. “He’s staying.”</p>\n<p>Gaunt looked at her. Then he looked at the box.</p>\n<p>“I think,” he said at last, slowly, like a man trying out words in a language he hasn’t spoken since childhood, “that I should like to hear about it. All of it. Properly.” He paused. “On the record.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The sky began to change a little after six.</p>\n<p>It went grey at the edge first, low in the east, behind the dark Stacks. Just a little. A paleness. And then more — a grey going to blue, going up the sky, pushing the black back. And the stars in that part of the sky began, one by one, to go out.</p>\n<p>Ione saw it happen. She was still sitting on the concrete, wrapped in a Civil Defence blanket, with Teo beside her in another and Pim asleep across both their laps with his rats. She’d been watching the lonely one in the south. And she looked up and saw the stars in the east going — fading, thinning, gone — and she sat up so fast that Pim woke with a yelp.</p>\n<p>“They’re going out,” she said.</p>\n<p>Her voice was high. She heard it and couldn’t stop it. The stars over the Stacks were going out, one after another, like the lamps in the lanes when the oil runs down, and the sky was going pale, and she was on her knees on the concrete with the blanket falling off her shoulders, and she couldn’t breathe.</p>\n<p>“They’re going out, Teo — they’re <em>going out</em> —”</p>\n<p>“Hey,” said Teo. “Hey. Hey. No. Look at me.”</p>\n<p>She looked at her.</p>\n<p>“It’s the sun,” said Teo Lisk. “It’s just the sun coming up. It’s morning. That’s all.” She put her hand on Ione’s arm. “They don’t go out. They’re still there. You just can’t see them in the day. They come back.”</p>\n<p>Ione stared at her.</p>\n<p>“They come back,” said Teo again. “Every night. When it gets dark. They come back.”</p>\n<p>“Every night?”</p>\n<p>“Every night.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked at the sky. At the grey turning to blue, and the stars fading out of it, and the lonely one, low in the south, still there, just barely, going faint, going. Every night. They’d go, and they’d come back. And go. And come back. For ever. The same, and never the same. The good kind.</p>\n<p>She sat back down on the concrete. She pulled the blanket back round her shoulders. She watched the lonely star go out, and the sky go blue, and the first light come up over the black shoulders of the Stacks and touch the rim of the Pit, and the faces of the people standing on it, and the long line of lamps still coming out of the doors of the Draw behind her.</p>\n<p>“Every night,” said Ione Tally, to him. “Did you hear that? They come back.”</p>"}, {"file": "21-epilogue-net-zero.md", "label": "Epilogue", "title": "Net Zero", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 4815, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Tell me how it ends.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally, Head Toller of Holdfast, last words</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The hammer stopped on the fourth day.</p>\n<p>Civil Defence found the flywheel on the third, in a vault in the Undercroft below the Lattice, still spinning — a disc of iron the size of a house, turning in the dark on its great bearing, driving its hammer down onto its keelstone anvil once a second the way Iris Hollowell had built it to, the way it had since 02:14 on the night of the Kindling. It took them a day to work out how to stop it. Nobody had ever designed a way. <em>When all else has failed</em>, Hollowell had written, <em>the station will say so</em>, and she had not imagined anyone wanting it to stop saying so.</p>\n<p>They stopped it at nine minutes past eleven on the fourth morning.</p>\n<p>In the Relief Camp on the Tallows flats, where thirty-one thousand people were sleeping in tents and prefabricated huts and the gutted shells of warehouses behind a fence of Civil Defence tape, it was the first silence in the history of Holdfast. People came out of the tents. They stood in the mud in the rain and looked at each other. Children cried. Old people sat down on the ground. For three nights afterwards, half the camp couldn’t sleep.</p>\n<p>The Tollers solved it. Tobiah, mostly. On the fifth night he sat down at a table in the Tollers’ hut with a wooden spoon and began, very quietly, to tap. Once a second. On the tabletop. And the Tollers in the hut, and then the people in the tents round it, lay in the dark and listened, and slept.</p>\n<p>They took it in turns after that. Every night, in the Relief Camp, somebody sat up and tapped. They were still doing it when Ione left. For all she knew, they were doing it still.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It rained.</p>\n<p>The Canopy was gone — its generators had died with the grid on the second day, and there was no Draw now to bring them back — and the autumn monsoon came in off the southern ocean for the first time in sixty years without anything to stop it, and it rained on Averna as it had not rained in living memory. It came down so hard it bounced. It ran off the black terraces of the Stacks in sheets and poured down the dead escalators and filled the Line tunnels and flooded the lower tiers, and Civil Defence went into the lower tiers with ropes and rubber boats, and Teo Lisk went with them, and so did Ione.</p>\n<p>She stood in it the first time for a whole afternoon.</p>\n<p>She stood in the mud outside the Tollers’ hut with her face turned up and her mouth open and let it come down on her. It was warm. It came down so hard it stung. She drank it. It tasted of nothing, mostly, and sometimes of dust, and sometimes, when it ran off the roof of the hut, of metal. Exactly the way he’d said.</p>\n<p>“You’ll catch your death,” said Teo, from the door.</p>\n<p>“I’ve never been wet,” said Ione. “Not like this. Not from above.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at her for a while. Then she came out and stood in it too.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There were colds.</p>\n<p>Nobody in Holdfast had had one for a hundred and sixty-eight years. Nobody had had anything that wasn’t already in the Vault when the Shear formed. The Commonweal’s doctors knew it would happen, and screened them and quarantined them and vaccinated them against everything they could think of, and it happened anyway. Coughs. Fevers. Things that Outside children shrugged off in a week went through the Relief Camp like fire through dry grass. Eleven people died of them in the first month. Most of them were old. Two of them were babies.</p>\n<p>One of the nurses who came to the camp in the second week was a slight, dark, quiet man of about thirty with very tired eyes, and on his third day Ione found him sitting on an upturned crate behind the medical tent in the rain, not doing anything, with a young dog asleep across his feet. A scruffy brown dog, all legs, with one ear up and one ear down.</p>\n<p>She knew who he was before she asked. Teo had told her. Teo had gone to his flat on Tier Twelve on the fifth day with a box of things from a locker in the Tallow Works lab, and come back without the box and with a very strange expression, and when Ione asked her about it, said only: <em>I bought him the bloody dog.</em></p>\n<p>“You’re Jonah,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the ground beside the crate, in the rain, and told him about the glass.</p>\n<p>She told him all of it. The way Teo had told it to her, walking out through the Works, and the way she’d seen it herself, standing at the long window of B-7 with her hand on the glass. The hall. The algae. The beard. The rat called Minister. MINISTER II. <em>I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS NOBODY CAME</em>, and Teo writing back <em>WE’RE HERE</em>. The map, and how it had got them out of the Carousel. <em>TELL JONAH. TELL HIM I SAID YES TO THE DOG.</em></p>\n<p>“He said,” said Ione, “<em>had a good life. Mostly algae.</em>”</p>\n<p>Jonah laughed.</p>\n<p>It came out of him suddenly, a real laugh, shocked, surprised, the way a laugh comes when you’re not expecting one, and he put his hand over his mouth, and laughed again — and then he wasn’t laughing. He sat on the crate in the rain with his hand over his mouth and his shoulders shaking, and the dog woke up and put its front paws on his knee and licked his wrist, and he couldn’t stop.</p>\n<p>Ione sat on the ground beside him and didn’t say anything. She’d learned that from Cas. From the hut. Sometimes you just sat there.</p>\n<p>“What’s the dog called?” she said, when he’d stopped.</p>\n<p>He wiped his face with the back of his hand. He looked down at it.</p>\n<p>“Minister,” said Jonah.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Halloran Crane walked out of Gate Nine last, at eleven minutes past two on the afternoon of the third day.</p>\n<p>He had stood at the top of Well One for nineteen hours. Every one of them. Every family, every child, every old man in a rope sling, every Warden and every rigger and every person who had ever called Seren Tally a heretic or stepped round her daughter in the lanes — he had seen them onto the ladder, one after another, and said their names, and when the last of them had gone down and the Commons behind him was empty and lit and silent, he had put out his lamp and gone down after them.</p>\n<p>Ondine Rask was waiting for him at the gate.</p>\n<p>Nobody knew what they said. They stood apart, on the concrete, in the rain, for about ten minutes — the Minister of Continuance in her dark suit and the First Steward of Holdfast in his black coat with its gold lamp — and nobody went near them. A Civil Defence sergeant called Kade said afterwards that she’d heard only one thing, as she passed with a stretcher, and that it had been the old man who said it, very gently.</p>\n<p><em>It’s over, Minister. We’re relieved.</em></p>\n<p>Rask resigned nine days later. The Directorate announced it as ill health. By then it didn’t matter what the Directorate announced.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It came out.</p>\n<p>It came out slowly, and then all at once, the way the Bells had come. Gaunt went first. The day after Gate Nine, Colonel Ansel Gaunt of the Office of Public Calm sat down in a room in the Collar in front of a Civil Defence recorder — not a Calm one — and began to talk, and didn’t stop for three days. He talked about the Margin Review. About Adeyemi. About a revised report and a nice pen. About a chip with a silver seal, and orders with <em>any means</em> underlined. About a Continuance Day address with footage from Year Eighty. He brought documents. He had kept copies of everything, it turned out, for thirty years, in a flat on Tier Six, in biscuit tins.</p>\n<p>“Things that are recorded,” he said, on the recording, “have a way of becoming history. I used to think that was the danger. I find I’ve changed my mind.”</p>\n<p>Then the Log. Ruth Tally’s Log, the whole of it, a hundred and sixty-eight years of it, the pencil getting smaller and smaller — the Tollers let the inquiry copy every page. And Aldous Crane’s private journal, which Halloran Crane handed over himself. <em>I did not give it to the Lamp. I held it.</em> And the recordings: three weeks of a man talking into a machine in a stone room, in a loft, on a spindle, about rats and weddings and funerals and a game called Drop, and near the end, flatly, plainly, about the Margin Review and what he had signed and why. He’d recorded it for Teo. Teo gave it to the inquiry. She didn’t ask anyone’s permission.</p>\n<p>It didn’t fix anything. Ione had thought, somehow, that it might. That the truth would come out and the Commonweal would look at it and something would change, the way the Lamp had gone out and the Drop had become a road. It didn’t happen like that. The Directorate was still the Directorate. The lights came back on in rotation, four hours a day per tier, from the old fusion plants at Kovač and Tessarine, and people queued for water and argued about whose fault it was. There were riots on Tier Four in the third week. There were strikes. There was an inquiry, and then another inquiry into the inquiry. The client states cut their trunk lines and sent angry envoys. The First Director gave an address about resilience and the spirit of the Tending.</p>\n<p>But things were <em>said</em>. That was the difference. In the lanes of the Stacks, on the terraces, in the queues for water, in the sports halls where people were still being relocated for comfort — people said things out loud. They said <em>default</em>. They said <em>Adeyemi</em>. They said <em>Aldous Crane held the key</em>. They said <em>thirty-one thousand people</em>. They said <em>the night shift</em>.</p>\n<p>On the big poster on the side of the Line station at the foot of the Tier Nine arterial — blue and gold, a child’s face turned up toward a golden flame, THE FUTURE WILL PAY in letters two metres high — somebody had sprayed, in rough white paint, underneath:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">NOT ANY MORE</div>\n<p>Nobody took it down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She took Ruth’s letter to Madeleine Tally on a Tuesday in the fourth week.</p>\n<p>Maddy lived with her grandmother on Tier Nine, in a flat that had been Ruth’s mother’s for thirty years, three streets from where Cas’s Standby flat had been. Ruth’s mother was sixty-seven. She had white hair and Ruth’s mole below her left ear, and when she opened the door and Ione told her who she was — who she <em>was</em>, the chain of it, Ruth and Hal and Hal’s daughter and her son and his, all the way down to a girl with a white streak in her hair standing on the landing with a steel box — she didn’t say anything at all. She just looked at Ione’s face. And then she stood back and let her in.</p>\n<p>Maddy was on the floor of the front room, doing a jigsaw.</p>\n<p>She was seven. She had dark hair in two plaits that someone had done badly, and her mother’s eyes, and she looked up from the jigsaw at the strange short broad woman in the patched clothes and said, “Are you from the Draw? Nana says the Draw’s where Mum is.”</p>\n<p>Ione knelt down on the carpet beside the jigsaw.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this for four weeks. She had thought about what she would say, and how, and she’d practised it, in the Tollers’ hut at night with Tobiah tapping. She had it all ready. She looked at the little girl and none of it was any use.</p>\n<p>“Your mum,” she said, “wrote you some letters.”</p>\n<p>She gave her the last one. Not the Log. Just the one page, the last page, the old woman’s shaking pencil, that Tobiah had copied out very carefully in large clear letters on a clean sheet so that a seven-year-old could read it, with the original folded inside it.</p>\n<p>Maddy read it. It took her a long time. She moved her lips. Her grandmother stood in the doorway with her hand over her mouth.</p>\n<p>“Eighty-eight,” said Maddy at last. She looked up. “Mum’s thirty-eight.”</p>\n<p>“She was thirty-eight,” said Ione, “when she went to work that night. And then she lived for a very long time. In a place where time went faster. She lived her whole life. She got to be eighty-eight.” She paused. “It was a good life. Mostly. She says so. You can read it. It’s all in there.”</p>\n<p>Maddy looked at the letter.</p>\n<p>“Is she coming home?” she said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione. “I’m sorry. She died a long time ago. A long time ago for her. Last night, for you.” She didn’t know how to say it better. There wasn’t a better. “But she thought about you every day. Every single day. That’s true. I know it’s true. She wrote it down.”</p>\n<p>Maddy thought about that. She thought about it seriously, turning it over, the way Ione did, and Ione watched her do it and felt something go through her that she didn’t have a name for.</p>\n<p>“Was she happy?” said Maddy.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Ione. “She had a husband called Tomas who made her laugh. And three more children. You’ve got a brother called Hal. And two sisters. They’re all — they were all very old, a long time ago. And she had eleven grandchildren. And they had children. And I’m one of them.” She held out her hand, the one without the splint. “I’m your — I don’t know what I am. Tobiah worked it out. It’s a very long word. I’m your family.”</p>\n<p>Maddy looked at her hand. Then she took it, the way children take hands, without any fuss.</p>\n<p>“You’ve got a white bit in your hair,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“I went somewhere a long way up,” said Ione, “and I was there for a long time.”</p>\n<p>Maddy considered this. Then she turned back to the jigsaw. “Do you want to help? It’s a horse. I can’t find the sky bits.”</p>\n<p>Ione sat on the carpet with Ruth Tally’s daughter for the rest of the afternoon, and helped her find the sky.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Nell Hale lived on Tier Nine too, now. Sabine had moved them back there when the relocation ended, into Cas’s old Standby flat, because the Ministry had forgotten to reassign it and it was the only flat on the tier with a balcony that faced south. Nell had insisted. She’d said the stars were better that way.</p>\n<p>Teo had been three times. The first time, on the fifth day, she’d gone to the sports hall on Tier Thirty with the recordings in her pocket, as she’d promised, and sat with Nell on a gym mat in the corner while she listened, and Nell had listened to all of them — three weeks of them, rats and weddings and funerals and Osk and <em>I ate a rat, don’t tell your mother</em> — without stopping, for nine hours, with Teo beside her, and at the end of it she had not cried and she had not hit Teo. She had said, <em>can I keep them?</em> And Teo had said, <em>they’re yours</em>. And Nell had said, <em>he found one. He found a star. I said he might.</em></p>\n<p>The fourth time, Teo brought Ione.</p>\n<p>They went at night. It was one of the dark nights for Tier Nine — no power in the rotation — and the Stacks were black, and the sky over them was black, and in it, the stars. They came every night now. Averna had started, in the weeks since, to go out and look at them the way it used to go out and look at the Quota clocks. People sat on the terraces with blankets. Children lay on their backs on the arterial.</p>\n<p>Nell opened the door. She was ten. She had her mother’s grey eyes and a face that couldn’t leave a question alone, and she looked at Ione in the doorway — at her, all of her, the short broad shape and the patched clothes and the rope, which Ione still wore out of habit, over her shoulder — without blinking.</p>\n<p>“You’re Ione,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“He said I’d like you.” She said it as if reporting a fact. “On the tapes. He said, <em>I think if you ever meet her, you’ll like her. I don’t know why I think that.</em>”</p>\n<p>“He said that?”</p>\n<p>“He said it on the day with the rat.” Nell stood back from the door. “The stars are out. Do you want to see them? From the balcony? They’re better from the balcony.”</p>\n<p>They sat on the balcony, the three of them, and Sabine brought tea and then went inside and left them. The balcony faced south over the dark Stacks, and the sky over them was full, the pale river of light going across it, the bright white one low in the east that shivered.</p>\n<p>“That’s Sirius,” said Nell. “That’s the Dog Star. It’s the brightest. It’s not the closest, though. It just looks like it is.”</p>\n<p>“He told me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“He <em>told</em> you?”</p>\n<p>“On the Rim. In the gallery. He told me their names. I was⁠—” She stopped. “I was very frightened, and he told me their names. So I’d have something to think about.”</p>\n<p>Nell looked at her. Then she looked back up at the sky.</p>\n<p>“Is he dead?” she said.</p>\n<p>She said it very plainly. The way she’d said <em>is it broken</em> on the phone, Teo had told her. The way you’d ask the time.</p>\n<p>Ione had thought about this too. For four weeks. She’d thought about it harder than she’d thought about Maddy. She looked up at the sky, at the thousands of them, and said the only thing she had.</p>\n<p>“No,” she said. “He’s in one moment. It’s — it’s hard to explain. It’s only once. It’s once, and it doesn’t end. He’s not waiting, or stuck, or — it’s not like being in a room. It’s not like the Quiet. It’s just one moment, and he’s in it, and it’s always true.” She was quiet. “It’s only for ever for us. For him it’s once.”</p>\n<p>Nell thought about that. Ione watched her do it.</p>\n<p>“Is it a good moment?” said Nell.</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at the sky.</p>\n<p>“It’s the best one I know,” she said.</p>\n<p>Nell was quiet. Then she moved along the bench, not far, a little, so that her shoulder was against Ione’s arm. She just sat there.</p>\n<p>After a while, Ione pointed.</p>\n<p>“That one,” she said. “Low down. Over there. On its own. The one with nothing near it. What’s that one called?”</p>\n<p>Nell followed her finger. And sat up, delighted.</p>\n<p>“That’s <em>Fomalhaut</em>,” she said. “That’s in the Southern Fish. It’s the loneliest star. That’s what they call it. Because there’s nothing else bright near it, in all that bit of sky. It’s just by itself.”</p>\n<p>“He said,” said Ione. “In the gallery. He said, <em>Fomalhaut, which is lonely</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Did he have a favourite?” said Nell. “I asked him once. He said he didn’t know. He said he’d never looked.”</p>\n<p>“He hadn’t,” said Ione. “So I picked one for him.” She looked at the lonely star, low over the black Stacks, steady, golden, just there. “That one. I told him I’d pick one and look at it every night. That’s his.”</p>\n<p>Nell looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<p>“That’s a good one,” she said at last, seriously. “That’s the right one for him.”</p>\n<p>“I think so.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll look at it too,” said Nell Hale. “Every night. Then there’s two of us.”</p>\n<p>Teo, at the other end of the bench, had turned her face away and was looking very hard at the Stacks.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>In the sixth week, she went back into the Vault.</p>\n<p>The inquiry wanted a survey. Teo was leading it, because nobody else in the Commonweal would go in, and Teo asked her to come because she knew the spindles, and she said yes because she’d always been going to say yes.</p>\n<p>It was dark. That was the thing she hadn’t been ready for, coming up through the conduit into the Drop District with her lamp. Holdfast was dark. Every lamp in the lanes had burned out long ago. The shrines were dark. The scaffold towers stood up out of the black like the ribs of something dead, and the washing still hung on the lines between them, and the rat pens stood empty with their doors open, and in the Commons the Tollers’ banner still hung from the gantry in the dark, Seren’s line, white on black, and nobody to read it. It was so quiet. No Bell. No Lamp. No hum. The Anvil under her boots was cold.</p>\n<p>It was ordinary. That was the other thing. It weighed what everything weighed. The chalk fell straight.</p>\n<p>They went up Spindle One. There was no need for the Lees now, and no need for Four; Teo’s team had rigged ropes up the inside of the lattice, and it was only climbing, the whole four kilometres of it. It took them two days. They slept in a cache in the old best Lee, and the chalk didn’t hang in the air, and Ione lay awake on the planks for a long time.</p>\n<p>At the top, below the Rim, the gallery was dark. No ghost-light in the windows. Nothing going round. The Carousel was just a corridor — a curving steel corridor round the spindle, with an algae trough along its windows gone brown and dry, and the walls covered in scratched fives, and a pallet of rags, and a pile of smooth stones. Ione walked the whole way round it. It came back to where it started, because it was a ring, the ordinary way. She went out of the inner door onto the Rim and it was the Rim.</p>\n<p>And above the Rim, where the Crown had been, there was the thing.</p>\n<p>Teo’s instruments had been trying to see it for a week, from below, with lights and lasers and sounding gear. They’d got nothing. You shone a light up at it and the light went into it and didn’t come back. It was a sphere. They’d worked that much out, from the shape of the nothing: a sphere about four hundred metres across, hanging in the air at the top of the Vault where the Lamp had been, enclosing the top of Spindle One and the Crown and part of the Rim. It didn’t reflect. It didn’t glow. It didn’t pull or push; the chalk fell straight right up to its edge. The instruments said it had no mass. None. No energy. None. No temperature. They said, as far as they could say anything, that it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p><em>Net zero</em>, the inquiry’s physicists called it, in their report. The debt and the payment, sealed together, balancing. Summing to nothing.</p>\n<p>Ione stood on the Rim in the lamplight and looked at it.</p>\n<p>It was black. Not dark; <em>black</em>. A curve of perfect black rising out of the steel of the Rim in front of her, and going up, and curving over, and blotting out the whole top of the sky. The Rim ran into it and stopped. The stair to the Crown ran into it and stopped. Her lamp’s light went into it and stopped. It was like standing at the edge of the night.</p>\n<p>“Careful,” said Teo, behind her.</p>\n<p>“It’s all right,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She walked up to it. Along the Rim, past the place where the chalk arrows had been — they were gone; the Lace had taken them, or time had, or they’d been swept away by the thing that had happened here — to the foot of the stair that went up to where the hatch had been. The black began on the third step. She climbed the first two. She stood on the second step, a hand’s width from it.</p>\n<p>She took off her glove.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this too. She’d thought about it at night, lying in the Tollers’ hut, listening to Tobiah tap. She hadn’t known what it would be. Cold. Hot. Hard. Nothing. Whether her hand would go into it and not come back.</p>\n<p>She put her palm flat against it.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t cold. It wasn’t warm. It wasn’t hard, or soft. It wasn’t anything. Her hand stopped against it, flat, the way it would stop against glass — the way Ben’s had stopped, on the other side of the window in B-7 — and there was nothing there to feel. Not even the feeling of nothing. It was like the instant before you touch something. Like the last moment before a door opens. Like being just about to.</p>\n<p>She stood on the stair with her hand flat on the black and her forehead nearly touching it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know if he could hear. She was almost sure he couldn’t. <em>Nothing will pass into it.</em> She’d read the plate. But she’d thought about that too, at night, and decided — the way she’d decided on the ladder, coming down in the dark, <em>he saw me</em> — that it didn’t matter whether he could hear. She’d decided you said things anyway. The way Ruth had written her letters. The way the Tollers had counted a little girl’s night.</p>\n<p>“They come back,” she told him. “The stars. Every night. Teo said. I didn’t believe her, the first morning, I thought they’d gone out. But they come back. Every single night.” She was quiet. “I found you one. Fomalhaut. The lonely one. Nell says it’s right. She’s going to look at it too. So there’s two of us.”</p>\n<p>Her palm was flat on the black.</p>\n<p>“It rains,” she said. “It tastes of nothing. And metal, off the roofs. You were right. Jonah’s got the dog. He called it Minister. Teo bought it. Teo’s all right. She’s — she cried on Nell’s balcony and pretended she didn’t. Maddy’s seven. She’s doing a jigsaw of a horse. I helped her with the sky.”</p>\n<p>She stopped. She stood there for a while.</p>\n<p>“I wrote the last page,” she said. “In the Log. Gran said to. She said <em>tell me how it ends</em>. So I did. I wrote it all down. Everything. Properly. Every word.” Her voice was quite steady. “It ends with you.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet then. Behind her, on the Rim, Teo stood with her lamp, and didn’t come any closer, and didn’t say anything either.</p>\n<p>Then Ione Tally took her hand away from the dark, and put her glove back on, and came down the stair, and went home.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><em>From the Crown Log of the Hollowell Draw (the Tally Log), final page, in the hand of Ione Tally:</em></p>\n<blockquote class=\"doc\"><p>Year 169 of the Watch, the last. Bell 122,988, which was the last Bell counted in Holdfast by the old way. After it, they came one a second, and we couldn’t keep up, and then we didn’t need to.</p><p>The Lamp was closed from the Crown by Casimir Hale of Averna, Outside, with the key that was never made. He went up Spindle Four with me through the Lees and the Carousel and the Lace and he set the Clause and he kept the needle on the line by hand until the end, as the plate says. He is the witness. He did not return.</p><p>I want it written down here, because Gran said to write it properly, that he was not paying anything. He told me so. He said he wanted it. He said it was the only thing he had ever wanted that he was sure of. I believe him. I was there.</p><p>He is in one instant. It is the instant I was safe. He chose it.</p><p>It is only for ever for us. For him it is once.</p><p>Ruth — I don’t know if anyone will read this who knew you, except Maddy, and she was seven. But I want to say that the man you saw at the Keystone at 02:09 was real, and his name was Cas, and he was as calm as you said. You were right about him. You were right about everything.</p><p>Gran — this is how it ends.</p><p>We are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">I.T.</span></blockquote>"}, {"file": "22-coda-once.md", "label": "Coda", "title": "Once", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 941, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>It isn’t forever for the one inside. It’s once.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ione Tally</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">There is a moment.</p>\n<p>In it, a man is kneeling in the dust at the far end of the highest room in the world, with his hand on a small brass wheel.</p>\n<p>The wheel is warm. It has just turned, a single tooth, a little to the left, because the needle was drifting and he saw which way it would go next and got there first. Now the needle is on the line. Exactly on it. Not a hair either side. It is the best he has ever held it. It will never be held better.</p>\n<p>Through the great window, the Lamp has begun. At its heart, where the hard white point was, there is the first edge of something — a thread of gold, the very first thread, the first instant of a light that would have been bright enough to end the world. It is only just beginning. It is always only just beginning. The Lace hangs round it, singing, every branch of it lit gold at the tips, as if a frost had caught the morning.</p>\n<p>And down, and to the left, past the glare, on the black lattice of Spindle Four, below the Breach, there is a small soft light.</p>\n<p>Hers.</p>\n<p>It’s going down. Steadily. A flight of stairs, in the Lees, out of the Fold, the way it should. Safe.</p>\n<p>And just before this — it’s here too; everything is — the light stopped on a walkway, and a small grey arm came up. The palm out. Held there, toward the Rim, toward the Crown, until it shook. She was looking up at him. He saw it. Of everything, it’s the thing he sees most clearly, clearer than the needle, clearer than the gold.</p>\n<p>He saw.</p>\n<p>He isn’t counting. That’s the first thing he would notice, if there were a next thing to notice after the first. For the first time since 02:14 on the morning of Continuance Day, for the first time in fifteen years, perhaps in his life, there is nothing to count. Not the hammer. Not rungs. Not seconds. There is one of everything. There is one moment, and he is in it, and it is one.</p>\n<p><em>Count me</em>, she’d said. <em>I’m one.</em></p>\n<p>Everything is here.</p>\n<p>That’s the other thing. Nothing has to be remembered, because nothing is behind him; it is all simply here, in the moment, the way the whole Vault was in every thread of the Lace. Nell on the phone in a gym with a lizard in a box — <em>you don’t know that; there might be</em>. Teo’s tea, which was mostly a structural material. A boy on a flatbed hugging a cradle: <em>is there a form?</em> A palm on the other side of a pane of glass, and grease-pencil letters fading on it. Osk kneeling on the floor of a stone room: <em>you don’t push up; you stack your bones</em>. An old woman with eyes like a bird, correcting him to the minute. Soup. Rat. A rooftop swaying with stamping feet and a cord tied round his wrist and taken off again. A hut in a Lee and a heart going too fast. <em>I’m glad it was you.</em> The cord tied round his wrist again, and this time left there — he can feel it; flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped. <em>I’ll hold your rope.</em> A visor pressed hard against his temple for a second, cold. <em>Haul.</em></p>\n<p><em>So this is the start.</em></p>\n<p><em>This is the start.</em></p>\n<p>All of it. At once. Not going. Not coming. Here.</p>\n<p>And there is one more thing.</p>\n<p>At the Keystone, beside him, very close — so close that she is kneeling almost where he is kneeling — there is a woman in a white coat. Not light. Not a ghost made of nine minutes going round. A woman. Thirty-eight years old, with her hair pinned up and a mole below her left ear and the side of her thumb raw where she’s bitten it. She has just wrenched up a steel plate. She has just watched a key fall forward out of the world. It is 02:09 on the night of the Kindling, and she is looking up at the place beside the Keystone, at a man who isn’t there, and he is there, and she is looking at him.</p>\n<p>He turns his head and looks at her.</p>\n<p>He knows her. He has read her whole life in pencil. He has watched her close her eyes for half a second a hundred and seventy-five thousand times, through someone else’s eyes. He knows that she is about to pull a hammer and go down a lift and live for fifty years in a city of lamps and write letters to a little girl who will stay seven for all of them. He knows that she will marry a man who makes her laugh. He knows that she will think about this — about him, about this one look — every day for thirty-one years, and write it down once, and never tell anyone.</p>\n<p>He’s sorry for her. He’s glad of her. Both at once. He’d tell her, if there were time.</p>\n<p>There isn’t time. There’s something better than time. There’s this.</p>\n<p>The needle is on the line. The gold is beginning. Her light is on the spindle, going down, and she raised her hand to him, and she is safe. He knows she is safe. He will always know it. That is the moment. That is the whole of it.</p>\n<p>It isn’t for ever. Not for him. It’s once.</p>\n<p>It is long enough.</p>"}]}, {"id": "v2", "label": "Revised", "note": "Editorial pass: numbers checked, the gradient rule planted early, Ben's blister rebuilt, dialogue tightened. See notes.md.", "chapters": [{"file": "00-prologue.md", "label": "Prologue", "title": "Nine Minutes", "part": "", "partStart": false, "words": 3972, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The vacuum will lend to anyone. It is the most patient creditor in the universe. It has never once asked to be repaid on time.</p><span class=\"attr\">Iris Hollowell, lecture notes, Year 3 of Continuance</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">At eight minutes to two in the morning on the eve of Continuance Day, Ruth Tally stood in the Crown of the Hollowell Draw and watched a man from the Ministry comb his hair in the reflection of the thing that powered the world.</p>\n<p>The Crown was the highest room in the station, a lens-shaped gallery of black keelstone perched at the top of Spindle One, four kilometres above the floor of the Vault. Its window was a single curved sheet of armoured glass forty metres long, and through the window, above and a little ahead, hung the Lesion. It did not look like much. People who came up for the first time always said that. They expected fire, or a whirlpool, or some dreadful eye. What they got was a point of white-violet light so small and hard it seemed less like an object than a flaw in the glass — a nail driven through the night. You could not look at it for long. You could not quite look away from it either. The engineers had a saying about that, which was that the Lesion looked back, and they said it as a joke, mostly.</p>\n<p>Deputy Minister Aldous Crane leaned close to his own reflection, which was superimposed on the Lesion’s, and pressed a strand of silver hair into place with two fingers.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” he asked the camera operator. “I don’t want to squint. The Minister squinted in the Year Eighty footage and she’s never forgiven the crew.”</p>\n<p>“We’ll stop down,” said the operator, a young woman with a shaved head and a Ministry lanyard. “You’ll look like a painting, sir.”</p>\n<p>“A painting.” Crane seemed to consider this and find it acceptable. On a chain around his neck, lying against his dark jacket like a pendant, was a key: a flat, heavy, blackish thing the length of a finger, cut from keelstone and stamped along its shaft. Ruth had seen it once before, in a photograph in the training manual. She had not expected to see it in her own control room, being worn as jewellery.</p>\n<p>“Tally,” said Crane, without turning round. “How long until we’re ready?”</p>\n<p>Ruth looked at the board. Her board: fourteen years she’d been in the Crown, six as shift engineer, and she could have read it with her eyes shut by the sound of the relays. The Lattice was at nominal. The Rollover was feeding at seventy-one percent, which was a number that would once have caused a scandal and now caused a memo. Tensioner Six was running a bearing temperature eleven degrees above the line, as it had been for three quarters, because its maintenance slot had been deferred three quarters running to keep output up for the export contracts.</p>\n<p>“We’re not,” she said.</p>\n<p>Now he turned.</p>\n<p>He had a good face for the Commonweal: open, confident, a little lined in the right places, the kind of face that could say <em>there is no cause for concern</em> and make you feel ashamed for having been concerned. He smiled at her with it.</p>\n<p>“Ruth. It is Ruth? Ruth, I understand. Believe me. I’ve read the protocol.”</p>\n<p>“Then you’ve read that the Deepening Test requires the Lattice interlocks to be taken offline for the duration.”</p>\n<p>“For eleven minutes.”</p>\n<p>“For eleven minutes during which, if anything happens to a tensioner, the Lattice can’t compensate on its own. It’s a test we run in the spring, with the full day crew, the Chief Engineer, and two spare tensioner rotors spun and waiting. Not at two in the morning with a night shift, the Chief on leave, and Six running hot.”</p>\n<p>“Six has been running hot since the summer,” said Crane. “I’ve seen the reports too. And Six is still here.”</p>\n<p>Behind her, at the tensioner desk, Fen Marsh made a small noise that could have been a cough. Ruth didn’t look round. Fen had two children and a mortgage on a Tier Twelve flat and had once, at a shift party, drunkenly explained to Ruth that he believed in the Continuance the way he believed in gravity: not with any enthusiasm, but because it was stupid not to.</p>\n<p>“Deputy Minister,” Ruth said, “respectfully. It’s not my call. It’s the Chief Engineer’s call, and he’s not here, so it goes to the Director of Operations, who is asleep in Tessarine and whom I tried to wake an hour ago.”</p>\n<p>“Director Obi has been informed,” said Crane’s aide, a narrow young man with a tablet, “and has deferred to the Ministry.”</p>\n<p>Ruth looked at him. He looked at the tablet.</p>\n<p>“At dawn,” Crane said, in the patient voice of a man explaining something to a child whom he likes, “the Minister of Continuance is going to stand in front of the Quota clock in Hollowell Square with a hundred thousand people and eleven heads of state watching and announce the Second Deepening. Thirty percent more output. Thirty percent, Ruth. Do you know what that means?”</p>\n<p>“It means thirty percent more debt.”</p>\n<p>A pause, very slight.</p>\n<p>“It means,” said Crane, “that the Aurelian Compact signs the trunk-line extension, which means we power forty million homes that are currently burning coal in the dark. It means the Canopy stays up through the storm season. It means your daughter — Maddy, isn’t it? — grows up in the safest, brightest city that has ever existed. And the Minister would like to show footage of the test, conducted tonight, live, successfully, from this room, by you, because a test is a promise and a promise is only worth something if you keep it.” He smiled again. “She asked for you, you know. By name. She said, ‘Tally’s the best we have.’ I’d hate to have to tell her she was wrong.”</p>\n<p>It was very gently done. That was the thing about the Commonweal: nobody ever raised their voice. Nobody ever said <em>or else</em>. They said your daughter’s name, and they said it kindly, and they mentioned that Maddy’s placement at the Upper Tier academy was, like all placements, subject to annual review by the Ministry, and they didn’t need to say anything else, and you heard it anyway.</p>\n<p>Ruth thought about Maddy, asleep in her nana’s flat on Tier Nine with her feet out of the covers and the glow-in-the-dark fish her father had stuck to the ceiling before he left. She thought about the eleven degrees on Six. She thought: <em>it will probably be fine</em>. That was the sentence. That was the whole machine. Ninety-one years of it had run on that sentence. It will probably be fine.</p>\n<p>“Eleven minutes,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Eleven minutes,” said Crane, and patted her shoulder, and went to stand where the light was best.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They ran it by the book because there was nothing else to run it by.</p>\n<p>“Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol, test designation Continuance Ninety-One.”</p>\n<p>“Lattice standing by.”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to manual.”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to manual, confirm.” Fen’s voice. “Feeding at seventy-one, holding.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks.” Ruth’s thumb hovered over the key switch. There was a moment when she could have not done it. She would remember that moment for the rest of her life, which would turn out to be a strange and very long life, in a place she could not at that moment have imagined; she would remember it the way you remember the last step before a stair you didn’t know was missing. “Interlocks off.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks off, confirm.”</p>\n<p>The Crown did not change. The window did not change. Down on the board, a row of green lights went amber, which meant only that the Lattice was now trusting its tensioners to hold themselves, the way a trapeze artist trusts her hands after the net’s been taken away.</p>\n<p>“Camera’s rolling,” said the operator softly.</p>\n<p>“Commence Deepening. Draw to one-oh-five.”</p>\n<p>“One-oh-five.”</p>\n<p>It came up through the floor first. It always did. There was a note the Lesion made when it was being worked — not a sound exactly, more a pressure in the long bones — and every engineer in the Draw knew it and none of them had a good word for it. Ruth’s grandmother, who had been Lattice crew in the first generation, used to call it <em>singing</em>. The Lesion was singing now, a long low swell that made the water in Dieter Hask’s cup shiver into rings.</p>\n<p>“One-ten.”</p>\n<p>“Output at sixty-six terawatts,” Dieter said from the output desk. “Sixty-six and a half.”</p>\n<p>“One-fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“Six is at plus fourteen,” said Fen. “Plus fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“Noted.”</p>\n<p>“One-twenty.”</p>\n<p>“Seventy-two terawatts.” Dieter’s voice had gone very flat. “That’s a record.”</p>\n<p>“Of course it is,” murmured Crane, for the camera.</p>\n<p>Out in the window, the Lesion had not grown, but it had sharpened; it had the look of something pulled taut. Ruth had seen it like this twice before, in spring tests, surrounded by the day crew with their coffee and their jokes and the Chief Engineer stalking up and down behind them humming. She had never seen it at night, with the Vault below them dark but for the work-lights on the spindles, eight black pillars rising out of the floor into the glare like the columns of a drowned temple.</p>\n<p>“One-twenty-five.”</p>\n<p>“Six is plus nineteen.”</p>\n<p>“One-thirty.”</p>\n<p>“Plus twenty-two. Ruth.”</p>\n<p>“I see it.”</p>\n<p>“Seventy-eight terawatts,” said Dieter, to nobody.</p>\n<p>“It’s over the line.”</p>\n<p>“I see it, Fen. Holding at one-thirty. Deputy Minister, we’re at the protocol maximum, you have your thirty percent, I’m bringing it down.”</p>\n<p>“Five more,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>She turned. He was not looking at her; he was looking at the camera, and his face was lit white-violet on one side, and he said it again, pleasantly, to the lens: “Five more, Ruth. Seventy-eight is a figure. Nobody remembers a figure. At dawn the Minister is going to stand in front of the Quota clock, and I would like the clock behind her to say <em>eighty</em>. Let’s give them a round number.”</p>\n<p>“Eighty is one-thirty-five. The protocol maximum is one-thirty.”</p>\n<p>“And the Minister has authorised one-thirty-five.” His aide held up the tablet as if it were a holy relic. “It’s in the order. Five more, and then you can bring it down, and we’ll all go home and watch ourselves on the news.”</p>\n<p>Afterwards — and there would be a great deal of afterwards — Ruth would try to decide what she had been thinking when she said <em>one-thirty-five</em>. She would write it down in a logbook, in pencil, in a hand that got smaller as the years went on and the paper ran out, and she would never get it right. The truest version she ever wrote was this: <em>I thought that I was tired. I thought that I had been told. I thought it would probably be fine.</em></p>\n<p>“One-thirty-five,” she said.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall of the Crown began to run fast.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was an ordinary clock, an analogue one with a sweep hand, put up in the Crown by some engineer decades ago as a joke about the station’s timekeeping. Ruth saw the sweep hand go round once in what felt like half a second. Then again. Then it was a blur.</p>\n<p>“Six,” said Fen. “Six is⁠—”</p>\n<p>The floor dropped. Not the floor: her weight. Everyone in the Crown staggered at once as though the room had fallen a metre and been caught. The camera operator went down on one knee. Dieter’s cup went off the desk and did not fall. Ruth saw it. It left the edge of the desk and hung, tilting, a little brown tongue of coffee reaching out of it in a slow curl, and it went down through the air as if through honey, and it took so long to fall that she had time to look away from it and look back.</p>\n<p>“Six has unseated,” Fen said, and his voice came to her strangely, the words arriving a hair before she saw his mouth make them. “Six is off its bearing. Six is off. Lattice is⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to full,” Ruth said. “Interlocks on. Interlocks <em>on</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks on.” The amber row went green, and went amber again, and went red.</p>\n<p>“Lattice not accepting.”</p>\n<p>“Draw to zero. Kill the Draw. <em>Kill it.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Draw at zero.” Dieter was hitting the panel with the flat of his hand. “Draw at zero, it’s — output’s still climbing, it’s not us, it’s not us drawing, it’s coming out on its own⁠—”</p>\n<p>And out in the window, the Lesion broke.</p>\n<p>She would try to describe it later, too, and fail. The nearest she came was this: it was like watching frost form on a pane of glass, if the frost were made of light, and if the pane were the world. The hard white point did not explode. It did not grow. Its <em>edge</em> — the edge that had always been a simple, perfect boundary — came apart into edges, and those edges into more edges, branching and branching into a filigree so fine and so endless that the eye slid off it and came back and slid off again. Lace. It spread outward in every direction at once, delicate, luminous, and it made no sound at all.</p>\n<p>Everyone in the Crown had stopped moving, except that they had not stopped; they were moving at different speeds. She saw Crane’s aide lift his tablet to his face in a single slow, graceful arc that took, perhaps, ten seconds. She saw the camera operator, on her knee by the window, flicker, and stand, and be on her knee again. She heard someone scream and then saw them open their mouth to do it.</p>\n<p><em>Time dilation</em>, said the calm part of Ruth’s mind, which was the part that had passed the exams. <em>Local gradients across the room. The boundary’s gone fractal. The core potential is spiking. We are in a region of⁠—</em></p>\n<p>“Keystone,” she said out loud, and ran.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Keystone was set into the floor at the far end of the Crown, under a hinged plate of brushed steel that nobody ever opened. The plate had words on it. Every engineer who had ever worked the Crown knew the words by heart, the way you know the words on a gravestone you walk past every day, without ever really reading them.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>Nobody knew what the last line meant. The consensus in the canteen was that Iris Hollowell had been a genius and a mystic and had lived on coffee and cigarettes and that geniuses were allowed to be cryptic on brass plates. The Clause itself was classified above Ruth’s level. What she knew was this: under the plate was a lock, and behind the lock was the only mechanism in the Draw that did not feed the Lesion but ended it, and the lock took one key, and the key was the Minister’s, and tonight it was hanging around the neck of a man in a good jacket who had wanted a round number.</p>\n<p>She dropped to her knees and wrenched the plate up. Beneath it, black keelstone, a smooth recessed housing, and in the middle of it a keyhole the shape of a narrow cross.</p>\n<p>“Crane.”</p>\n<p>He was standing where he had been standing, in the best light. The light was different now. It came in through the window in a shifting white-violet surf, and it lit him from every side, and his face in it looked very young and very frightened.</p>\n<p>“Give me the key.”</p>\n<p>“What will it do?”</p>\n<p>“It will close the Lesion.”</p>\n<p>“Close it.” He said it as if it were a word in a foreign language. “Close it how? For how long? We can’t — Ruth, the Minister⁠—”</p>\n<p>“There won’t be a Minister. There won’t be a Draw. Look out of the window, Aldous. There won’t be a <em>city</em>.”</p>\n<p>Something happened to his face then, and she thought later — much later, when she’d had more time than anyone should have to think about one face — that it was the moment he understood, and that what he understood was not that the world might end but that if he gave her the key, the thing he had been sent here to film would be the last day of the Hollowell Draw, and that it would be his name on it. She thought he was not a monster. She thought he was simply a man who had lived his whole life inside the Continuance and could not, in that second, imagine the outside of it. It is hard to give away the thing that holds up the sky.</p>\n<p>“There must be another way,” he said.</p>\n<p>“<em>Give me the key.</em>”</p>\n<p>He put his hand on it. He did. She saw his fingers close around the chain. She saw him start to lift it over his head.</p>\n<p>The gantry beneath the window buckled.</p>\n<p>A single thread of the Lace — as fine as a hair, as bright as a filament in a bulb — had reached the outer frame of the Crown. Where it touched, the keelstone did not break, because nothing broke keelstone. It <em>aged</em>. A strip of the frame as wide as her hand went grey, then white, then sagged like wax, and the grated walkway along the window, which was bolted to that frame, tore free along a third of its length and dropped half a metre with a shriek of metal.</p>\n<p>Crane was standing on the walkway.</p>\n<p>He fell against the rail. The chain around his neck caught on something — a bolt head, a twist of torn grating — and snapped. Ruth watched the key go. She watched it with the same terrible slowness as the coffee cup, and knew as she watched that it was not slow for the key; it was only slow for her. It turned over once in the white-violet light. It slid between the bars of the grating. And it fell — not down, toward the Vault floor, where things ought to fall — but <em>up and out</em>, toward the window, as if the Lesion’s light were the true ground and it was dropping home. The glass did not stop it. She never understood that part. It was on one side of the window, and then it was beyond it, a small black speck tumbling into the Lace, and then it was not anywhere.</p>\n<p>Crane made a sound she would hear for years.</p>\n<p>She was still on her knees by the Keystone. She looked down, stupidly, at the empty keyhole. Then she looked up.</p>\n<p>There was a man at the Keystone.</p>\n<p>He was kneeling where she was kneeling. That was the only way she could put it afterwards. He was kneeling <em>where she was</em>, in the same place, and she could see him perfectly, and he was not touching her. He was dressed in close grey, like a diver, like something worn under armour, and he had one hand on a small wheel she had never seen — a wheel that was not there, that the housing did not have — and around his wrist was a cord tied in a knot. His face was turned toward the window. It was an ordinary face, tired and lined and unshaven, and it was completely calm. It was the calmest face she had ever seen. He looked like a man who had been there for a very long time and could stay there for longer.</p>\n<p>Then he turned his head and looked at her.</p>\n<p>It lasted — she never knew. Less than a second. He looked at her as if he knew her. As if he were sorry for her, and glad of her, both at once.</p>\n<p>Then there was no one there.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Tally! Tally, the lift!”</p>\n<p>Fen had her by the arm. He had her by the arm, and then he had her by the arm again, a moment later, as if he’d had to do it twice. Everyone was moving now, in stutters, in slurs. The camera operator was dragging Crane back from the edge of the torn walkway; his face was grey and wet. Dieter had his board open and was yanking fuses out by hand as if that would do anything, as if anything would do anything.</p>\n<p>“We have to go down,” Fen said. “We have to go down <em>now</em>, the Lattice is — the whole — Ruth, if it touches the spindle⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” she said. “Yes. Evacuate the Crown.”</p>\n<p>They went. Eleven people and a camera. The lift was at the end of the gallery, a steel cage on a rack-and-pinion rail that ran four kilometres down the inside of Spindle One, and it held twelve, and they got in, and Ruth did not. She stood at the gate.</p>\n<p>“Ruth⁠—”</p>\n<p>“One second.”</p>\n<p>On the wall by the lift, behind a small glass panel, was a red lever with a black-and-yellow collar. Every room in the Draw that had controls also had one. The engineers called it the Dead Man’s Hammer. Pulling it did only one thing: it released a flywheel in the station’s deep structure that drove an iron hammer against a keelstone anvil, once per second, for as long as the flywheel turned — and the flywheel had been built to turn for days. It was the oldest alarm in the Draw, older than the electronics, older than the Ministry, a thing Iris Hollowell had insisted on over every objection. It meant one thing and one thing only, and every soul in the station would understand it, even if every screen and every speaker in the place was dead.</p>\n<p>It meant: <em>nobody is at the controls</em>.</p>\n<p>Ruth broke the glass with her elbow and pulled it.</p>\n<p>For a moment nothing happened. Then, deep below them, somewhere down in the black body of the station, something enormous let go.</p>\n<p>She stepped into the lift. Fen slammed the gate. The cage dropped.</p>\n<p>Through the lift’s mesh she saw the Crown go up and away from her — the torn walkway, the empty Keystone with its plate thrown back, the window full of spreading light — and then the black inner wall of the spindle, rushing. In her hand she was holding, she discovered, the station logbook, which she had no memory of picking up. She held it against her chest. Beside her, Aldous Crane was weeping silently with his eyes open. The camera operator had her arms around him. The young man with the tablet was staring at the tablet, which showed a clock, which was counting upwards faster than any clock should.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy</em>, thought Ruth. <em>Maddy, Maddy, Maddy.</em> She had the absurd conviction that if she said the name enough times, fast enough, she could make it reach her.</p>\n<p>Then, from far below, up through the rails and the cage and the soles of her boots and the bones of her legs, came the first stroke of the hammer.</p>\n<p>It was not a sound. It was too deep, and too long, and too slow, for a sound. It came up through the spindle like a tide coming in under a door. It lasted, and lasted, and lasted.</p>\n<p>Afterwards — for the rest of her life, and for the lives of her children, and their children, and theirs — they would call it the Bell.</p>\n<p>She looked at her watch. It said 02:14. It had taken nine minutes. It was the last time, for the rest of her life, that her watch and the world would agree.</p>"}, {"file": "01-the-standby.md", "label": "Chapter 1", "title": "The Standby", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": true, "words": 5493, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The Hollowell Draw is experiencing a minor margin irregularity. There is no danger to the public. Citizens are reminded that calm is care.</p><span class=\"attr\">Office of Public Calm, bulletin 1, 04:30, Continuance Day</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">By twenty past six Casimir Hale had counted fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty strokes of the hammer, and the phone had still not rung.</p>\n<p>He was sitting on the edge of his bed in the dark with his boots on. He had put the boots on at about a quarter past two, when the first stroke came up through the building like a truck driving over the roof of the world, and he had put on his trousers and his good shirt and his coat, and then he had sat down to wait, and he had been sitting ever since. At some point he had begun counting. He wasn’t sure why. It was the kind of thing a man did when he had spent fifteen years being paid to wait for a phone call and the call had finally come and it had not come by phone.</p>\n<p>The phone lay on the bedside table next to a glass of water he hadn’t drunk. It was a heavy black Ministry handset with no screen and a single button, and it had been issued to him on his first day as Standby with a small ceremony in an office on Tier Two, and in fifteen years it had never once made a sound. He dusted it on Sundays. He had once, drunk, tried to call his own number on it and found that it did not make outgoing calls. It was not a phone, really; it was a promise, or a threat, or a leash, and it had sat there by his bed for fifteen years like a small black animal that might one day wake up.</p>\n<p>Fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty-one.</p>\n<p>The hammer did not sound like a sound. It sounded the way it would feel to be very small and live inside a drum. Every second, precisely, the whole building shuddered — the window glass hummed, the water in the untouched glass flinched into a ring, the cutlery in the kitchen drawer gave a small metallic cough — and then there was a second of silence, and then it came again. The flat was on Tier Nine, a hundred metres above the ground and four kilometres from the rim of the Pit, and at this distance, through all that concrete and air and sleeping city, it should not have been possible to feel anything that happened inside the Draw at all.</p>\n<p>Cas knew what it was. He had known at 02:14, sitting up in bed with his heart going. He’d have known it in his sleep. There was a picture of that hammer in the second chapter of every textbook on vacuum engineering ever printed, and a caption underneath in Iris Hollowell’s own words: <em>When all else has failed, the station will say so.</em></p>\n<p>Fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty-two.</p>\n<p>At twenty-five past six there was a knock at the door.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There were two of them on the landing, a man and a woman in the charcoal overcoats of the Office of Public Calm, and behind them, a little apart, a third man who was older and softer and wore no overcoat at all, only a brown cardigan buttoned wrong.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” said the soft man. “I’m so sorry to wake you.”</p>\n<p>“You didn’t.”</p>\n<p>“No.” The man’s eyes went over him, the boots, the coat, the good shirt. Something that might have been approval, or pity, moved in his face. “No, I don’t suppose we did. Ansel Gaunt. We’ve never met, but I’ve read a great deal about you. Shall we?”</p>\n<p>The car was long and black and warm and smelled of ginger biscuits. There was a paper bag of them on the seat between them and Gaunt offered it to Cas and, when Cas shook his head, took one himself and ate it carefully over a handkerchief spread on his knee. The two in overcoats sat up front. The car pulled out into the lane and the hammer came up through its wheels and its seats and Gaunt’s biscuit, and Gaunt closed his eyes briefly each time it did, the way a man might wince at a toothache he had decided not to mention.</p>\n<p>“We didn’t phone,” said Gaunt, “because phones are recorded.”</p>\n<p>“By you.”</p>\n<p>“By us, yes. Which is precisely the problem. Things that are recorded have a way of becoming history. Things that aren’t recorded have a way of becoming⁠—” he searched for the word, dabbing a crumb from his lip “⁠—negotiable. The Minister would like this morning to remain negotiable for as long as possible.”</p>\n<p>“How long have you known?”</p>\n<p>“About the irregularity?”</p>\n<p>“About the Draw.”</p>\n<p>Gaunt looked at him mildly. “Since two-fourteen, Dr Hale. Like everyone else in the Pit district who was awake, or who was woken. It’s been rather hard to miss.” A pause. “If you mean when did we know that it was <em>serious</em> — I’d say we’re still finding that out. Which is why you’re in my car.”</p>\n<p>They came out of the lane onto the Tier Nine arterial and the city opened up around them.</p>\n<p>Averna at dawn on Continuance Day. Cas had lived in it all his life and it still, sometimes, stopped his breath. Ahead and above, the Stacks rose in their terraced ranks, tier on tier on tier, a hundred and forty levels of housing and gardens and plazas climbing into the grey, lit from within like vast lanterns, and above the highest of them the faint milky curve of the Canopy, the weather shield that had held back sixty years of storms. Down at street level the Continuance Day bunting was already up — blue and gold, THE FUTURE WILL PAY on every banner — and the public speakers on the lampposts were playing the Tending anthem on a soft loop, the children’s choir version, very sweet. There were people out, a lot of people for this hour. They were walking in the ordinary way, carrying the ordinary bags, and they were not talking, and every one of them, Cas saw, flinched very slightly once a second.</p>\n<p>“There’s been no announcement?”</p>\n<p>“There’s been a bulletin. Minor irregularity. No danger to the public. Calm is care.” Gaunt took another biscuit. “People are being very good. People are always very good at first.”</p>\n<p>The car turned south, toward the Pit. They passed a plaza with a Quota clock on a pillar in the middle — a big public display, the kind every district had, showing the Draw’s output in real time in tall gold numerals. Cas had grown up with those clocks. As a boy he’d stood in front of the one on Tier Four and watched the numbers climb on Deepening Day and cheered with everyone else. It said 00.00 TW. As they passed, a man in a Ministry jumpsuit on a ladder beside it reached up and switched it off, and it went dark, and a small crowd that had gathered to look at it stood looking at the dark instead.</p>\n<p>“The Minister felt,” said Gaunt, “that the number was unhelpful.”</p>\n<p>“Zero?”</p>\n<p>“The number before zero. For about forty minutes after two-fourteen the clocks showed an output of a hundred and nine terawatts. Then they started showing numbers that aren’t numbers. Then zero.” He folded his handkerchief. “I’m not a physicist, Dr Hale. But I can read a room, and I read a hundred and nine terawatts, and I read the faces of the engineers in the crisis room who read it, and I thought: I’ll go and fetch the man who wrote the memo.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“The Margin Review,” said Gaunt gently. “Year Seventy-Six. Section four, the projections. You were the junior author. I’ve read the original. Not the version that was published. The original, the one with your name on it and Dr Adeyemi’s. It had some very striking phrases.” He looked out of the window. “’A structure of obligation that cannot, even in principle, be discharged.’ I remember thinking that was a remarkable sentence to find in a technical report. It sounded like scripture.”</p>\n<p>“Adeyemi wrote that.”</p>\n<p>“Did he? He’s dead now, of course. Natural causes.” Gaunt said it without emphasis, the way you’d say a man was tall. “And you signed the revised version. The one that said the Margin was stable.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Nobody made you.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“No.” Gaunt seemed to think about that for a while. “That’s the thing I’ve never understood. Most of the people I talk to in my job, I can tell you exactly what made them. A child. A debt. A photograph. But you, Dr Hale — nobody lifted a finger. We simply asked, and you signed, and we gave you a flat and a phone and you sat by it for fifteen years like a good dog.” He turned from the window and met Cas’s eyes, and his own were very tired and very kind. “I’m not saying it to hurt you. I’m saying it because I want you to understand that I know exactly who you are. It will save us both time.”</p>\n<p>The hammer came up through the car.</p>\n<p>“How long,” said Cas, “until we get there?”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>You saw the Pit before you saw the Draw. You always did — it was fourteen kilometres across and three deep, a crater dug out of the old industrial belt in the Year Minus Six, and the Draw sat in the bottom of it like a vast black egg in a bowl. From the rim, on a normal morning, you saw the Collar first: a ring of converter halls and admin blocks and trunk-line heads, lit up and smoking, with the great superconducting lines marching away from it over the city on pylons like the legs of enormous insects. Behind the Collar, the dark bulk of the Works. Behind the Works, the dome.</p>\n<p>This morning, from the rim, you could hardly see it at all.</p>\n<p>The air over the Pit was moving. That was the only way to put it. It shimmered, the way air shimmers over a road on a hot day — except that it rose in a column, a clear, trembling pillar at least ten kilometres wide, going straight up into the grey dawn until it was lost against the Canopy. Through it the Draw looked wavering and uncertain, as if seen through water. The edges of things kept coming apart and knitting back.</p>\n<p>And there were birds. Hundreds of them — gulls, starlings, the fat grey Averna pigeons that lived on the Stacks — circling above the rim in a great slow wheel. None of them flew over the Pit. They came to its edge and turned away, came and turned away, around and around, like water circling a drain it would not go down.</p>\n<p>Their car stopped at a cordon of Civil Defence vehicles and Calm Office cars on the rim road. The hammer was much louder here: a vast, dull, full-body blow, once a second, that made the gravel on the road jump. A Civil Defence sergeant waved them through, and as they passed her Cas saw her glance at her wristwatch and then at a clock on the dashboard of her truck and frown.</p>\n<p>“The clocks near the rim run fast,” said Gaunt, who had seen him see it. “Twelve minutes fast, at the cordon, since two. They were eight minutes fast at five. It’s creeping out. Nobody seems able to tell me whether that’s important.”</p>\n<p>“It’s important.”</p>\n<p>“I thought it might be.” Gaunt took off his cardigan, folded it carefully, and laid it on the seat. Underneath he was wearing a very good grey shirt. “The Minister’s waiting.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The crisis room was in the Collar’s admin block, on the fourth floor, in a long room that had been built as a conference suite for foreign delegations. It still had the gold carpet and the portraits: Iris Hollowell, severe and hollow-cheeked, with a cigarette she’d been painted without; the first Minister of Continuance, cutting a ribbon; a sweeping mural of the Vault seen from the Crown, the eight spindles rising to the Lesion, the Lesion a gold star with painted rays. Someone had pushed the delegate tables together and covered them with screens and paper and cold coffee, and about twenty people were standing around them in the stunned, overlit way of people who had been awake all night doing something that wasn’t working.</p>\n<p>Cas knew about half of them. Engineers, mostly — Collar shift leads, a couple of the Works directors, a woman from the Lattice office he’d been at university with who looked at him across the room, went white, and looked away. People who had been told at some point, by someone, that the Standby was not somebody you talked to. He’d got used to that look. He’d seen it on the faces of old friends in the street.</p>\n<p>At the head of the table stood Ondine Rask.</p>\n<p>The Minister of Continuance was a tall, spare woman in her sixties with silver hair cut close to her skull and a dark suit without a crease in it. She had been Minister for nineteen years. She had presided over the First Deepening, and the export treaties, and the Canopy’s extension over the southern coast, and Cas had seen her face on every public screen in the city since he was a young man. It was said she slept four hours a night. It looked, this morning, as though she had slept none, and it looked as though she had decided that this was nobody’s business.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” she said. “Thank you for coming.”</p>\n<p>As though he’d had a choice. He nodded.</p>\n<p>“You’ll forgive me if I don’t waste your time. We have had a Continuance Event.” She said the phrase with no irony at all. “At approximately 02:05 this morning, during a scheduled test, Tensioner Six in the Lattice lost its seat. The interlocks failed to compensate. Since that time we have been unable to re-establish control of the Lesion, the Lattice, or the Rollover. We have no communication with the Crown or with any part of the station inside the Works. We evacuated the Collar at 03:10. At around the same time we lost the output. The Commonweal grid is running on reserve and on the old fusion plants at Tessarine and Kovač, which were not built to carry it, and which will begin to fail within eighteen hours. Those are the facts.” She put both hands flat on the table. “I have been assured by every engineer in this room that the situation can be recovered, and I have been assured by every engineer in this room that they do not know how. Colonel Gaunt tells me you might.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>She blinked.</p>\n<p>“Why does Colonel Gaunt tell you that,” said Cas. “I haven’t been inside the Draw in fifteen years. I haven’t been allowed to read a Lattice report since Year Seventy-Six. Everyone in this room knows the station better than I do now.”</p>\n<p>“Because,” said Gaunt, from the doorway, where he had settled himself on a gold chair with his hands folded, “everyone in this room has spent the night telling the Minister what they think she would like to hear, and I have some small professional experience in noticing when people are doing that. Whereas you, Dr Hale, have spent the last fifteen years with nothing whatsoever to lose.” He smiled at Rask. “It’s a rare quality, Minister. I’d use it while it lasts.”</p>\n<p>Rask looked at Gaunt for a long moment, then back at Cas.</p>\n<p>“What do you need?” she said.</p>\n<p>“Everything you’ve got. Starting with the last thing the Crown sent.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They gave him a chair and a screen and the Lattice woman — her name was Petra Imre, she’d sat behind him in second-year field theory and once lent him a pencil — brought him the telemetry without looking at him, and he went through it, and the room went on around him in a hush.</p>\n<p>It didn’t take long. That was the worst of it. Afterwards he’d think that if it had taken longer, if he’d had to hunt, he might have had time to get used to it on the way.</p>\n<p>The drone logs first. At 04:40 they had sent a survey drone into the Works through Gate Nine. It had flown for forty minutes by the Collar’s clock, and for nearly all of them its own clock had kept pace — running a little fast, a few minutes in the hour, the way the clocks at the cordon were running fast. Except for ninety seconds. Halfway through the flight the gravity had shoved it off its line and across a disused hall, and it had drifted within a few metres of something, and in those ninety seconds of the Collar’s time its clock had gained ten hours. The camera showed what it had passed. A thin line of white-violet light crossing the hall, fine as a hair on a lens. For ten hours of footage the frame held the hall and the line and nothing else, while a coil of cable on the floor beneath it went brown, and then orange, and then to powder. Then the drone drifted clear, and its clock slowed to a few minutes fast again, and it came home with eleven hours and six minutes on it and a battery that had lived every one of them and was dead.</p>\n<p>He knew why it had been ninety seconds and not forty minutes. It was the first thing they taught you about the Draw, in the first week of the first year, with a lamp and a sheet of card. Fast time spreads out from a thread the way light spreads from a lamp: in open air it thins with distance, and thins fast, so that a few metres off you’d hardly notice it. But it doesn’t like matter. Put something solid in its way and the solid takes the difference — all of it, fast on one face and slow on the other, the whole step squeezed into the thickness of the wall. Glass does it. Steel does it. Keelstone does it better than anything in the world, which was what the Lattice was <em>for</em>: a dam of keelstone round the core, holding the core’s time back the way a dam holds a lake, so that for ninety-one years the Crown’s clocks and the city’s had agreed to within a few seconds a day. There was a placard on every wall inside the Draw — he’d walked past hundreds of them, on placement — that said DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE. Most people who read it thought it was about the gravity.</p>\n<p>Then the Crown. The last telemetry burst before the link failed, 02:05:31. Most of it was screaming numbers — output, tensioner loads, core potential off every scale anyone had drawn. And one small, dry line at the bottom that the instruments computed automatically from the shape of the Lesion’s boundary, a figure that had read <em>2.000</em> — a perfect sphere, a clean, smooth edge — every second of every day for ninety-one years.</p>\n<p>It read <em>2.73</em>.</p>\n<p>He looked at it for a while. He was aware of the hammer. He was aware of his own hands on the table, which were very still.</p>\n<p>“What is it?” said Rask.</p>\n<p>“The boundary dimension.” He heard his voice from a small distance. “It’s a measure of the — it’s how rough the edge of the Lesion is. How much surface it has. A smooth sphere is two. A cloud is about two and a half. It’s gone to two point seven.”</p>\n<p>“And that means?”</p>\n<p>“It means the edge isn’t an edge any more. It’s fractal. It’s broken into⁠—” He moved his hand. He thought of frost on a pane. “Into edges, on edges, on edges. Infinitely. A boundary with no smooth part anywhere on it.”</p>\n<p>“Can it be repaired?”</p>\n<p>“It isn’t <em>damaged</em>, Minister. It’s⁠—” He stopped. He looked round the table. All those faces. They were looking at him the way people look at a doctor who has just come in from reading the scan.</p>\n<p>“I need to explain something,” he said, “and I need you to let me finish, and I need nobody in this room to call it an irregularity while I’m doing it.”</p>\n<p>Rask’s face didn’t move. “Go on.”</p>\n<p>“The Draw doesn’t make energy. Everyone in this room knows that. It borrows it. The Lattice holds a region of space below its ground state — below zero, below empty — and the difference is what we sell. The vacuum lends it to us, and the vacuum always gets it back. That’s not a theory, that’s not Hollowell, that’s field theory, it’s as old as the Long Heat. You can make a patch of negative energy. You can’t keep it. It has to be paid back by a pulse of positive energy, and the payment is always <em>more</em> than the loan. The longer you hold it, the bigger the payment. They called it quantum interest, before any of us were born.”</p>\n<p>“We are all aware of the principle of the Rollover, Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>“Then you’re aware that the Rollover isn’t a solution. It’s a refinancing.” He heard his voice sharpen and didn’t stop it. “Hollowell’s trick was to keep borrowing to pay the interest on what we’d already borrowed. Push the payment forward. Push it forward again. Ninety-one years. Every year a little more of the output goes back into the Lattice just to keep the loan from coming due, and every year the loan is bigger, and every year we deepened the draw to make up the output, which made the loan bigger. We were feeding it seventy percent. The Review projected it would pass a hundred percent inside twenty years — the Draw would be eating more than it made, just to stand still. We’d have had to feed it from the grid. For ever. That was always the shape of it. That was the <em>best case</em>.”</p>\n<p>“That report,” said Rask, very quietly, “was revised.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. By me.” He held her eyes. “It didn’t change the physics.”</p>\n<p>Nobody in the room was moving.</p>\n<p>“The Rollover’s gone,” he said. “Six unseated, the Lattice lost the geometry, and the boundary broke. There’s no mechanism holding the payment back any more. The debt’s coming due. Ninety-one years of it, with interest, and the interest compounds. It will be paid in one pulse, at the core, all at once.” He looked down at the screen, at the numbers, and did the arithmetic in his head because he had done it a hundred times, fifteen years ago, at night, on the backs of envelopes. “Order of ten to the twenty-six joules. The impact that killed the dinosaurs was about ten to the twenty-three and a half. So call it two or three hundred of those. At once. Here.”</p>\n<p>It was Petra Imre who spoke first. “When?”</p>\n<p>“The boundary’s still evolving. The clocks at the cordon are running faster; that tells me the core potential’s still off the scale and still shifting. When it starts to fall, the debt’s being called in. When it falls far enough⁠—” He made himself say it plainly. “Two days. Three at the outside. Maybe less.”</p>\n<p>Someone at the far end of the table sat down on the floor.</p>\n<p>“Evacuation,” said a Works director, a heavy man called Brask whom Cas vaguely remembered as a rugby player. “We evacuate the city⁠—”</p>\n<p>“To where? Six hundred million people, in two days. And it wouldn’t matter. It’s not a bomb. It’s the planet. The first second takes Averna. The shock and the heat do the rest. You could be on the far side of the world and it would find you in the first hour.”</p>\n<p>“Bury it,” said somebody else. “Collapse the Pit. Pour⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You can’t bury a debt.”</p>\n<p>“Then what,” said Rask, “do you propose?”</p>\n<p>And there it was. He’d known it was coming since the car. Maybe since 02:14. Maybe since Year Seventy-Six. He found that he was very calm.</p>\n<p>“There’s a mechanism in the Crown,” he said. “Hollowell designed it. It’s in the original schematics, and it’s in her last paper — the one that was withdrawn — and it’s called the Clause. It isn’t a repair. It doesn’t save the Draw. It ends it. The theory is that if the boundary ever failed badly enough — and it has — the same distortions that are going to kill us could be turned around and used to fold the core shut. Seal the whole thing. Debt and payment together. In a⁠—” he hesitated over her phrase “⁠—in a moment that doesn’t have a future. I don’t fully understand it. I don’t think anyone alive does. There’s a line on the Keystone plate that nobody’s ever explained. <em>The sealing requires a witness.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Hollowell’s final paper,” said Rask, “was withdrawn because it was unsound.”</p>\n<p>“It was withdrawn because it described how to turn the Draw off.”</p>\n<p>The silence went on for several strokes of the hammer.</p>\n<p>“The Clause is locked,” Cas said. “It takes the Minister’s key.”</p>\n<p>For the first time, something flickered in Ondine Rask’s face.</p>\n<p>“The key,” she said, “was in the custody of Deputy Minister Crane. Who is on site.”</p>\n<p>“On site.”</p>\n<p>“Deputy Minister Crane was attending the test. As the Ministry’s representative.” She said it perfectly evenly, and every engineer in the room looked at the table. “He is currently unaccounted for, along with the Crown crew and approximately nineteen hundred members of the night shift who were in the Lattice and the Vault at the time of the event.”</p>\n<p>Nineteen hundred.</p>\n<p>Cas had been trying not to do the next piece of arithmetic. He did it anyway. He found that he was doing it out loud, softly, the way he used to talk himself through proofs in the library at night.</p>\n<p>“The clocks at the cordon are twelve minutes fast after four hours,” he said. “That’s out here, where it’s had kilometres of open air to thin out in. The drone went within a few metres of a single thread and gained ten hours in ninety seconds.”</p>\n<p>He looked down at his hands.</p>\n<p>“And the Vault’s behind the dam,” he said. “There’s nowhere in there for it to thin out. The Vault’s sealed. If the core potential is as high as the Crown’s numbers say — and it’ll have been highest at first, right after the rupture, before the debt started to be called — then everywhere inside the Lattice will be running at the core’s speed, near enough. It isn’t hours. The ratio could be tens of thousands. Hundreds of thousands.” He looked up. “Minister, if anyone survived in there — if anyone was sheltered, if they had air and water — then in the four hours since two o’clock, they’ve lived⁠—”</p>\n<p>“That will do, Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>“⁠—years. Decades. By the time we get anyone in there, they could have been alive in there longer than the Commonweal has existed⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>That will not be repeated outside this room.</em>”</p>\n<p>It was the first time she’d raised her voice. It rang off the gold walls. In the doorway, Gaunt had closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>Rask straightened. She took her hands off the table.</p>\n<p>“We have,” she said, “three suits.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They were called keel suits. He had heard of them, the way you hear of the cathedrals in cities you’ll never visit: a Lattice maintenance programme from the Year Seventies, cancelled in the Eighties, three prototypes built in the Tallow Works labs and never used. Suits designed to let a human being work inside the Lattice’s gradients, where the pull of the tensioners and the push of the Lesion would crush a person like a paper cup. They were said to be almost unimaginably expensive. Each one had a sliver of keelstone at its heart, spun up like a gyroscope — keelstone, the hyperdense stuff the Lattice itself was made from, which cost more per gram than anything else on Earth because only the Draw could make it.</p>\n<p>“They’ll let a team get through the Works,” Rask said. “Possibly into the Lattice. Possibly further. Our engineers believe that from the Crown, with the proper sequence, Tensioner Six could be reseated manually. If Six is reseated, the Lattice can re-establish the geometry. If the geometry is re-established, the Rollover can resume. The Draw continues.” She said the last two words with a peculiar emphasis, as though laying them down on the table between them like a card. “Can Six be reseated from the Crown, Dr Hale?”</p>\n<p>He thought of the number at the bottom of the telemetry. 2.73. He thought of a tensioner rotor the size of a building sitting off its bearing in a Lattice that no longer had a shape to hold it. He thought of the Keystone.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>It was astonishingly easy. It always had been. That was the thing about lies in the Commonweal: there was never any resistance. You opened your mouth and they slid out like a coin into a slot.</p>\n<p>“Good.” Rask nodded, once. “Then you’ll lead the technical side. Civil Defence will lead the team. You’ll leave as soon as the suits are ready.” She turned her head. “Major Lisk.”</p>\n<p>He hadn’t noticed the woman by the window. She’d been standing in the corner behind the delegate screens with her arms folded and her back to the mural, watching, and she came forward now with the slightly rolling gait of someone who had spent a long time on boats or in boots. She was in her fifties, broad and weathered, with cropped iron-grey hair and a face like a well-used tool. The Civil Defence badge on her shoulder had been sewn back on by hand at least once. The ribbon bar under it went down two rows.</p>\n<p>“Teodora Lisk,” she said, and held out her hand.</p>\n<p>He shook it. It was a hard, dry hand with a missing fingertip.</p>\n<p>She didn’t let go at once. She held on a moment and looked at him — up, down, back up — with an expression he couldn’t read. It was the look, he thought afterwards, that a person gives a ladder before climbing it: not unfriendly; simply trying to work out how much weight it would take.</p>\n<p>“You’re the one who wrote the thing,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I’m the one who wrote the thing.”</p>\n<p>“Hm.” She let go. “Well. Congratulations on being right.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Later — an hour later, maybe two, after the plans and the arguments and a call to the Tallow Works that went on for forty minutes and ended with somebody in the lab crying — he found himself alone at the end of the corridor outside the crisis room, standing at a window.</p>\n<p>The window looked south over the Pit. From up here he could see the Draw properly: the Collar, the dark jumble of the Works, the dome rising out of the middle like the back of some enormous animal asleep in the crater. The shimmer rose over it, vast and clear and trembling. The birds wheeled at its edge. The hammer came up through the floor and through the glass under his fingers, once a second, once a second, and he found he had started counting again without meaning to. Twenty-one thousand and something.</p>\n<p>He thought about nineteen hundred people.</p>\n<p>He thought about a pen. He could remember it exactly: it had been a Ministry pen, blue, with the Continuance flame on the clip. He’d noticed at the time that it was a good pen, that it wrote smoothly. That was the thing he remembered most about the most important moment of his life — that he had noticed the pen was nice.</p>\n<p>He had never told anyone that. There’d never been anyone to tell.</p>\n<p>Down in the Pit something shifted in the shimmer, a great slow ripple running upward like a wave through a curtain. The birds broke their circle and scattered and came back.</p>\n<p>Two or three days. A pulse at the core. And three suits, and a lock that nobody had the key to, and a line on a brass plate that nobody understood.</p>\n<p>He should be afraid. He knew that. He examined himself for fear, the way you’d pat your pockets for keys, and found — not nothing. Something. But what he mostly found, when he looked, was a kind of settling. A quietness. The way a room goes quiet when the last guest leaves.</p>\n<p><em>At last</em>, some part of him said. He didn’t like the part of him that said it. He didn’t argue with it either.</p>\n<p>It was almost a relief.</p>"}, {"file": "02-keel.md", "label": "Chapter 2", "title": "Keel", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 5827, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The keel is not a shield. It will not stop the water. It will keep you upright in it.</p><span class=\"attr\">MSE Type 4 Operator’s Handbook (draft, unpublished), Tallow Works Laboratory</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The suits arrived at the Collar at a quarter to eleven, on the back of a Civil Defence flatbed, lashed upright in their cradles under grey tarpaulins, so that as the truck came slowly down the ramp into the Gate Nine maintenance hangar they looked like three tall figures being brought to an execution with sacks over their heads.</p>\n<p>A young man was sitting on the flatbed with them, holding on to one of the cradles with both arms. When the truck stopped he didn’t let go. He stayed there, hugging the cradle, while the driver got out and the hangar crew came forward with the forklift, and he looked round the hangar with enormous eyes — at the Calm officers by the door, at the Civil Defence people, at Teo Lisk with her arms folded, at Cas — and said, to no one in particular, very fast:</p>\n<p>“Hi. Hello. I’m Ben. Benedek. Farr. I’m the — I built — well, I didn’t <em>build</em> them, a lot of people built them, but I built the keels, I mean I did the rotor bearings, and the housings, and the spin controllers, and most of the firmware, and I’m the only one left in the lab who still knows how they go together, because everyone else was reassigned when the programme got cut, and I wasn’t, because nobody remembered I was there, which was, honestly, the best four years of my life. And someone phoned the lab at seven o’clock this morning and said you need three people to go into the Draw, and I said who’s going to maintain the suits, and they said well, who <em>can</em>, and I said, well, me, I suppose, and they said right then.” He took a breath. “So I’m coming. I’m coming? I’m coming. Right. Good. Fine.” Another breath. “Is there a form?”</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk walked over to the flatbed, looked up at him, and held out a hand.</p>\n<p>“Come down, son,” she said. “There’s always a form.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He was twenty-six. He had a narrow, clever, anxious face, and hair that stood up at the back where he’d been running his hands through it, and he talked — Cas would learn over the next twenty hours — the way a pressure valve leaks: continuously, and more when the pressure went up. When he was working, though, he went quiet. When he stripped the tarpaulin off the first cradle and put his hand flat on the suit inside it, he stopped talking altogether, and stood there for a moment as though he were checking for a pulse.</p>\n<p>They were not beautiful. Cas had half expected them to be. They were squat and grey and massive, armoured in overlapping matte plates like the shell of some deep-sea crustacean, with thick ribbed joints at knee and hip and elbow and a helmet that was mostly visor, a wide dark curve of glass. On each back, where a diver would carry tanks, sat a rounded housing the size of a beer keg, sealed and bolted, with a band of small indicator lights around its waist. Someone had painted a number on each housing by hand in white paint: <em>1</em>, <em>2</em>, <em>3</em>. The paint on the <em>3</em> had run a little before it dried.</p>\n<p>“That’s the keel,” said Ben. He had his hand on the housing of number one. “Inside there’s a rotor. Keelstone. Eleven kilos of it, in a disc about so big.” He made a circle with his hands the size of a dinner plate. “Two millimetres thick. Doesn’t sound like much. It isn’t the weight, it’s what it does when you spin it. Keelstone’s — you know what keelstone is?”</p>\n<p>“Collapsed matter,” said Cas. “Pressure-stabilised by the Lattice fields when it’s made. About ten times denser than lead.”</p>\n<p>“Right, right, sorry, you’d know, you’re the — sorry.” Ben flushed. “So the rotor spins. Fast. Really, really fast. Two hundred thousand revs a minute at full, and the rim’s doing — well, you don’t want to know what the rim’s doing, the housing’s rated for it but only just. And when you spin something that dense that fast, it drags spacetime round with it. Frame-dragging. Tiny effect normally. With a keelstone rotor it’s not tiny. It gives you a sort of bubble, about a metre across, where the metric’s — smoothed. Stirred flat. Like putting a paddle in a river so the water round you goes one way instead of every way at once.” He looked at Teo, then at Cas, then back at the suit, and spoke to the suit. “In the Lattice, where the gradients are — you know. If you walk into a region where your feet weigh more than your head, or where your feet are living faster than your head, the bubble spreads it out. Averages it over the metre. So instead of being torn in half, you’re just — very uncomfortable.”</p>\n<p>“How uncomfortable?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Ben considered this seriously. “Have you ever been in a car crash?”</p>\n<p>“Three.”</p>\n<p>“Like the fourth one, but slower.”</p>\n<p>Teo grunted. It might have been a laugh.</p>\n<p>“The bubble’s only about a metre. Two people could share one if they were⁠—” Ben made a small squeezing gesture with his hands “⁠—closer than is professional. The suits are built for one. They’re heavy. They’ve got exoskeletons, the exo will carry most of it, but you’ll feel like you’re wearing a cathedral. Power cells for the life support and the exo and the electronics are good for about four days. The keel doesn’t need power once it’s spinning. The bearings are magnetic, there’s almost no friction — sat on the rig with nothing to push against, it’d spin for months. It’s the <em>work</em> that slows it. Every time the bubble has to hold something steep, it costs spin. Hold a lot, and it runs down fast. As it slows, the bubble shrinks. You’ll hear it. It hums. When the hum drops, that’s you running out of keel.”</p>\n<p>“And spinning it up,” said Cas, “takes⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Fourteen hours.” Ben winced. “Sorry. It’s a lot of mass. If you try to spin it faster you crack the housing, and then you’ve got eleven kilos of keelstone doing two hundred thousand revs on your back with nothing to hold it, and that’s⁠—” He stopped. “That’s a bad day. That’s a very, very bad day.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked up at the clock on the hangar wall. It said 10:58. His wristwatch said 10:51. The hangar was a kilometre inside the Collar; the time out here was already slipping.</p>\n<p>Fourteen hours. They’d go in before dawn. Hour twenty-five, or thereabouts. He tried to think of what the hour-twenty-five version of the Vault might be like, if the ratio was what he thought it was, and decided not to.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took Ben and the hangar crew until one o’clock to get the suits out of the cradles and onto the spin rigs — three low steel frames bolted to the hangar floor, humming with power cables — and when the rotors finally started to turn it was so gradual that at first there was no way to tell. Then, gradually, there was a sound. A long, low, rising tone, so deep you felt it in your back teeth before you heard it, the three suits singing in almost-unison under the hangar lights. The note crept upward very slowly. Ben said it would keep rising all night.</p>\n<p>Cas stood and listened to it. He would hear that note for the rest of his life.</p>\n<p>Across the hangar, by the big doors, Teo Lisk was talking to Ansel Gaunt.</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t hear them. The hum and the hammer between them drowned it out. Gaunt was doing the talking, softly, with his head bent and his hands folded in front of him like a priest, and Teo was listening with her arms crossed and her face shut. At one point Gaunt took something small out of his cardigan pocket and held it out to her, and after a moment she took it and put it in the breast pocket of her fatigues. Then Gaunt patted her arm, and went out, and Teo stood there alone in the doorway looking out at the dark mass of the Works for a while before she came back.</p>\n<p>When she passed Cas, she didn’t meet his eye.</p>\n<p>He filed it. He had spent fifteen years watching people not meet his eye; he’d got good at filing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The afternoon was drills.</p>\n<p>They put the suits on for the first time at three o’clock, with the keels barely turning, and Cas found out what Ben had meant by a cathedral. You didn’t so much put on a keel suit as get admitted into it. The liner came first — a close grey one-piece in some slippery, cool fabric, wired with sensors and a web of fine tubes for heat and water, that fitted like a diver’s wetsuit and made all three of them look, as Teo put it, like very depressed seals. Then the suit opened down the back like a clam, and you stepped in, and it closed around you with a series of heavy locking thunks, and for a moment you could not move at all, and then the exoskeleton woke up and took your weight and you stood up inside it and the world became a narrow bright picture framed in a dark curve of glass.</p>\n<p>It was slow. Everything was slow. The exo took a quarter-second to decide what your legs meant before it did it; walking felt like wading. On his back the keel’s hum came through the suit’s frame into his spine like a second, larger heartbeat. In his ears, the helmet’s speakers carried Teo swearing, steadily and inventively, as she tried to pick up a spanner in her gauntlet and sent it skittering across the floor for the ninth time.</p>\n<p>“Don’t grab,” said Ben’s voice. “It’s got a grip-assist. It’ll close harder than you mean it to. Just think about touching it.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>am</em> thinking about touching it.” Teo bent — the whole suit bent, with a long hydraulic sigh — and closed her gauntlet on the spanner for the tenth time, very slowly, like a woman lifting a sleeping cat.</p>\n<p>There was a short, flat crunch.</p>\n<p>She straightened up and held it out to them. The spanner had folded in the middle like a sandwich.</p>\n<p>“…Or that,” said Ben. “That’s the grip-assist. That’s — yes. It’s quite a lot of grip.”</p>\n<p>“Is this coming out of my pay?”</p>\n<p>“It’s the Ministry’s. I really don’t think they’re counting spanners today.” He was quiet for a second, and then, because he apparently couldn’t stop himself: “Everybody does one. I did a coffee mug. And a technician’s hand, a bit. He was fine. He was mostly fine.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at the folded spanner. Then she laid it down on the floor, very carefully, as if it might go off, and picked up the next one as though it were an egg. It stayed a spanner.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Ben, so relieved that Cas nearly smiled.</p>\n<p>They walked the length of the hangar and back. They climbed a ladder and came down it. They practised falling — the suits went over like felled trees and got up again with a hydraulic grunt — and practised hauling each other up, and practised the hand signals for when the radios failed, which Ben said they would, sooner or later.</p>\n<p>“The electronics hate gradients,” he said. “If a chip’s got one end living faster than the other end, its clocks go out of sync and it just — it gets confused. The keel helps, it smooths it over the bubble, but the bubble’s a metre and the Lattice is — well. The electronics will go first. The radios, the displays, the recorders. The life support’s mostly mechanical, the exo’s got a manual fallback, the keel’s purely mechanical once it’s spun. So you’ll keep breathing and walking. You just won’t be able to <em>talk</em> about it.”</p>\n<p>“Sounds like working for the Commonweal,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas found that he’d laughed. It came out of him in the suit, surprised, and he heard it come back to him over the radio, and he realised he couldn’t remember the last time he had laughed out loud at something another person had said. He couldn’t remember the last time there had been another person.</p>\n<p>Teo glanced across at him through two layers of visor. He couldn’t see her expression. But she didn’t say anything else for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At seven in the evening they ate.</p>\n<p>The Collar canteen had been evacuated at three in the morning in the middle of the night-shift meal, and nobody had been back. It was a long, low room with windows onto the Works, and the tables were still set: trays of food gone cold and stiff, cups with skins on them, a half-finished game of cards laid out on a table by the door. Somebody had been celebrating a birthday. There was a cake on the counter with most of its candles still in it and a paper crown lying beside it, and a card propped against the cake that said <em>HAPPY 40TH OSWIN — THE FUTURE WILL PAY (FOR THE CAKE)</em>.</p>\n<p>Nobody touched the cake.</p>\n<p>They found tins in the storeroom and heated them in the kitchen and ate at the table by the window, still in their liners, with the Works outside going on and on in the dark — cooling towers, gantries, the black cliff of a rectifier hall with red warning lights crawling across it — and the shimmer standing over all of it in the floodlights, and the hammer, and the hum of the keels from the hangar.</p>\n<p>Ben had a napkin. He’d been drawing on it since the soup.</p>\n<p>“Right,” he said. “Right. So I’ve been doing the numbers. On our survival odds.”</p>\n<p>“Oh, good,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“No, listen, it’s interesting. So. Okay. Let’s say the Works is about as bad as the drone said, which means the gravity’s doing weird things but the keels can handle it. Let’s say ninety percent for the Works. That’s generous but let’s say. Then the Lattice, we don’t know, but the keels were <em>designed</em> for the Lattice, so let’s say seventy. Then whatever’s past the Lattice⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The Shear,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up. “The what?”</p>\n<p>“If the core potential’s as high as I think, the Lattice is holding the whole difference between the core’s time and ours. Where the Lattice is solid, the difference sits inside the keelstone, and that’s fine; nobody’s walking through keelstone. But there are holes in it. Conduits, cooling trunks — the ways in. In a hole, the whole difference has to happen in the air. Over a few tens of metres, maybe. The time rate changing very fast over a very short distance.” He moved his hand, flat, across the table. “A shear layer. Like the boundary between two currents.”</p>\n<p>Ben thought about that. He wrote something on the napkin. “Okay. The Shear. Let’s say fifty. For the Shear. And then the Vault, and then the Crown, and then whatever you do in the Crown⁠—” He looked at Cas again, and something in his face was very young. “What <em>are</em> you going to do in the Crown, Dr Hale? Reseat Six? Can you actually⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Keep going with your sum, Ben.”</p>\n<p>Ben looked at him a moment longer. Then he looked down and kept going. “So — the Crown, let’s call it fifty, fifty for whatever. And then we have to get <em>back</em>, and that’s all of it again, in reverse, so you square it.” He did a fast calculation. “Which comes out at about — two percent. Each. Roughly. I mean, that’s very rough, the error bars are — well, the error bars are basically the whole napkin.”</p>\n<p>There was a short silence.</p>\n<p>“Two percent,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Roughly.”</p>\n<p>“Well.” She pushed her plate away and sat back and put her boots up on the chair beside her. “That’s better than Tessarine.”</p>\n<p>“What was Tessarine?” said Ben.</p>\n<p>So she told them. The Tessarine floods, Year Seventy-Nine, the spring the Canopy failed over the southern delta for eleven hours and the storm surge came up the river mouth and put six metres of water through the lower tiers of the city in forty minutes. She’d been a lieutenant. Her unit had gone into the Tier Two underpass with ropes and a rubber boat to get people out of a flooded Line station, and the water had kept rising while they were down there, and they’d had to swim out through the ticket hall in the pitch dark with the current pulling at them and twenty-three people on the ropes behind them, and they’d come out into the street and the street had been a river.</p>\n<p>“I did the numbers on that one too,” she said. “Afterwards. On the odds. It came out at about one in a hundred for the unit. We lost two.” She shrugged. “Numbers are for afterwards. You go in, you do the job, you do the numbers in the pub, and then you get very drunk.”</p>\n<p>“Did you get any of them out?” Ben asked. “The people?”</p>\n<p>“Twenty-one.” She looked at the window. “Two of them let go.”</p>\n<p>Ben folded his napkin in half, and in half again, and put it in the pocket of his liner.</p>\n<p>It was quiet for a while. The hammer came up through the table. The coffee in their cups shivered into rings, and settled, and shivered.</p>\n<p>“Why are you here, Hale?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>He looked at her. She was watching him over the rim of her cup, perfectly relaxed, and her eyes were not relaxed at all.</p>\n<p>“I’m the Standby.”</p>\n<p>“I know what you are. I’ve read your file. Gaunt gave me your file.” She didn’t look away. “I asked why.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He tried to think of an answer that was true and that he could say out loud in front of a twenty-six-year-old boy with a two-percent napkin in his pocket, and there wasn’t one.</p>\n<p>“Because it’s my job,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo held his eyes for a second longer. Then she snorted softly and looked back out of the window.</p>\n<p>“That’s what,” she said. “Not why. But all right.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Afterwards, there were the letters.</p>\n<p>Nobody said they were going to do it. It just happened, the way these things did. Ben went off to a corner of the hangar with his tablet propped against a toolbox and sat on the floor in front of it and talked to it for a long time, very quietly. Cas didn’t try to listen, but the hangar echoed, and every now and then a phrase came across to him between the hum and the hammer. <em>⁠— and the flat’s in both names so you won’t have to —</em> and <em>⁠— your mum’s recipe, I know I always said it was too salty —</em> and then, near the end, his voice breaking and going on anyway, <em>⁠— Jonah, I’m sorry, I’m sorry, I’m really sorry, I should’ve said yes to the dog.</em></p>\n<p>Teo wrote. She sat at a workbench with a Civil Defence notepad and a pencil and wrote two letters in a small square hand, and folded them, and wrote a name on each: <em>Mattias. Joss.</em> She put them in an envelope and wrote on the envelope, and put it in her breast pocket, next to the small thing Gaunt had given her.</p>\n<p>Cas sat at the other end of the bench with a sheet of paper in front of him for an hour and wrote nothing.</p>\n<p>He tried. He tried to write to Sabine and got as far as her name and couldn’t think what came after it that she didn’t already know. He tried to write to Nell and found that everything he wanted to say to her was either a lie or so large that it didn’t fit on paper. In the end he folded the blank sheet up and put it in his pocket, and took out instead the thing he’d brought from the flat without really thinking about it — a battered hardback sketchbook with a black cloth cover, a quarter full of old equations and older drawings — and turned it over in his hands.</p>\n<p>Then he went and found a phone.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Sabine answered on the eighth ring.</p>\n<p>“Cas.” A pause. Behind her, a lot of noise: voices, children, a loudspeaker saying something about blankets. “Where are you? I’ve been trying your — they said your number was⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m at work.”</p>\n<p>A silence. He could hear her understanding it.</p>\n<p>“At <em>work</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Where are you? What’s that noise?”</p>\n<p>“We’re in a school. A sports hall. Tier Thirty. They came round the whole of Nine at lunchtime and said we were being relocated for our comfort. For our <em>comfort</em>, Cas. They said it was a precaution. There’s four hundred people in here and one toilet.” Her voice dropped. “Everybody’s being very calm. It’s horrible. Nobody’s saying anything and everybody keeps flinching. Cas, what is that? That noise. That — thing. It’s been going since two in the morning. Is it the Draw? Is it⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Is Nell there?”</p>\n<p>“Cas⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Please, Sabine.”</p>\n<p>A breath. Then, away from the phone: “Nellie. Nell. It’s Dad.”</p>\n<p>A scuffle, a clatter, and then Nell’s voice, very close and very loud, the way she always talked on the phone, as if she thought she had to shout to make it reach.</p>\n<p>“Dad! Dad, we’re in a <em>gym</em>!”</p>\n<p>“I heard.”</p>\n<p>“There’s a girl here called Pip who’s got a lizard. A real one. It’s in a box. It’s called Dennis.” A breath. “Dad, are you at the Draw?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Is it broken?”</p>\n<p>He closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “It’s broken.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a second. She was ten. She had her mother’s grey eyes and his own inability to leave a question alone, and she collected stars. She had never seen one. No one her age in Averna had. The city’s light and the Canopy’s glow had wiped them from the sky before she was born, before he was born, and she had got interested in them anyway, the way some children get interested in dinosaurs — because they were enormous and far away and gone. She had glow-in-the-dark stars on her bedroom ceiling in their proper constellations, placed with a chart and a ruler, and she knew their names the way other children knew football teams. Betelgeuse. Deneb. Fomalhaut. She said them like spells.</p>\n<p>“Pip’s brother says,” she said carefully, “that if the Draw is broken, the lights will all go off. Like, <em>all</em> of them. Everywhere.”</p>\n<p>“They might.”</p>\n<p>“So we’d see them.”</p>\n<p>It took him a second. “The stars?”</p>\n<p>“If the lights all went off. If it was dark. We’d see them. Wouldn’t we? Properly?”</p>\n<p>“I suppose you would.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>knew</em> it.” She sounded so pleased. “I told Pip. He said they’d have gone. He said they probably weren’t even there any more. I said they’re <em>always</em> there, you just can’t see them.”</p>\n<p>“You’re right.”</p>\n<p>“I know I’m right.” A pause. Then, in a smaller voice: “Dad, are you going in there? Into the broken bit?”</p>\n<p>The hammer. The hum. Across the hangar, Ben still talking to his tablet with his forehead in his hand.</p>\n<p>He had been going to lie. He had the lie ready. <em>No, sweetheart, I’m just here to look at the numbers, I’ll be home soon.</em> It was sitting in his mouth, smooth and round, and it would have slid out of him like all the others.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said instead. “Tonight. Very early. With two other people.”</p>\n<p>“Is it dangerous?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Are you scared?”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “I’m very scared, Nell.”</p>\n<p>There was a long pause on the line. He listened to her breathe. He wondered what he’d just done — whether it was the worst thing he could have done to her or the only true thing he’d said to anyone in fifteen years, and whether those were different.</p>\n<p>“That’s allowed,” Nell said at last. She said it firmly, the way Sabine said things, the way she’d been told it herself, probably, in a sports hall, an hour ago. “Mum says that’s allowed. You’re allowed to be scared, you just have to do it anyway.”</p>\n<p>“Your mum’s right.”</p>\n<p>“Dad.” She’d lowered her voice to a whisper, as though telling him a secret. “When you’re in there. Will you look for stars?”</p>\n<p>“Nell, there aren’t any stars in there. It’s a power station. It’s all⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You don’t <em>know</em> that,” she said. “You haven’t been. You don’t know what’s in there. There might be.”</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. He shut it again.</p>\n<p>“All right,” he said. “I’ll look.”</p>\n<p>“Promise.”</p>\n<p>“I promise.”</p>\n<p>“Okay.” Satisfied. Then, abruptly, over her shoulder, “<em>Mum</em>, Dennis is out⁠—” and a clatter and a shriek and Sabine’s voice, distant, and then Sabine back on the line, breathless.</p>\n<p>“Cas? Sorry. A lizard. Cas, what did you tell her? She’s — she looks⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I told her the truth.”</p>\n<p>A long silence.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Sabine eventually. “That’ll be a first.” But her voice had gone strange, and when she spoke again it was very quiet. “Cas. Come back. Do you hear me? She needs you to come back.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” he said. And then, because it was the only true thing left, “I’m sorry, Bina.”</p>\n<p>He put the phone down before she could answer.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Continuance Day address went out at nine that night, fifteen hours late.</p>\n<p>They watched it on the canteen screen. The First Director of the Commonweal stood in Hollowell Square in front of the dark Quota clock with the city’s lights behind him and spoke for eleven minutes about sacrifice and endurance and the Tending, and about a minor technical irregularity at the Draw which was being handled with the utmost skill and would be resolved in good time, and about the future, which would pay. Then they played the footage. The Crown, the Lesion like a gold star through the window, engineers at their boards in crisp white coats, a triumphant needle swinging over. The numbers climbing. The crowd in the square cheering.</p>\n<p>“That’s from Year Eighty,” said Ben. “The First Deepening. My dad took me. I was fifteen, I thought it was the best day of my life. That’s — look, that’s the old console layout, they changed that in Eighty-Five.” He stared at the screen. “They’re showing old footage. They’re showing old footage and pretending it’s tonight.”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything. On the screen, the gold star shone steady and perfect. Outside the window, over the Works, the shimmer rose into the dark. Once a second, the whole canteen shook.</p>\n<p>Teo got up and turned the screen off.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At eleven Ben showed Cas the relay pucks.</p>\n<p>They were small, flat, hockey-puck-shaped things in orange casings, a box of forty, and Ben had been fiddling with them for hours at a workbench under a lamp while the keels sang behind him.</p>\n<p>“Radio repeaters,” he said. “We drop them as we go. They’ll bounce signal back through the Works to the Collar. And to each other. Only — here’s the thing, here’s the thing I realised this afternoon, it’s actually really interesting⁠—” and he was off, with his hands, the way he’d been off about the keels. “If there’s a time gradient between two radios, the signal gets stretched. Like, if you’re somewhere where time’s running slow and I’m somewhere where it’s running fast, then from where I am, your voice comes through slowed down, because it’s — you’re talking at slow-time speed, right? So from my side a one-second word from you takes, I don’t know, a minute to arrive. It gets redshifted, it gets smeared out. And the other way round, if I talk to you, I’m talking at fast-time speed, so I get squashed — it arrives all at once, a whole speech in a blip. So I’ve written some code that buffers everything and re-times it. It records the whole stretched-out signal, however long it takes to come in, and then when it’s finished it plays it back at the right speed. So we’ll still be able to understand each other. It just might take a while.”</p>\n<p>“How much of a while?”</p>\n<p>Ben shrugged. “Depends on the ratio. Ten to one, it’s fine, it’s like a bad phone line. A thousand to one, and your one-minute message takes⁠—” he did it in his head “⁠—seventeen hours to get to me. Doesn’t matter though. It’ll still get there.” He grinned tiredly. “If one of us ends up in fast time, the others’ll sound like whales. Big slow whale noises for hours. And then the suit goes <em>ding</em> and it’s you, talking normally, saying ‘Farr, you idiot, why did you bring the pucks in the wrong box.’”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the forty orange pucks. He thought about a ratio of tens of thousands. A one-minute message taking weeks to arrive. A voice spread so thin over time that it would come through as a single long unbroken note, like the hum of a keel.</p>\n<p>“That’s good work, Ben,” he said.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up at him, surprised, and then flushed right up to the roots of his standing-up hair.</p>\n<p>“Thanks,” he said. “I mean. Yeah. Thanks.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>A little after midnight Cas went and stood by the spin rigs.</p>\n<p>The note of the keels had climbed all night and was now high and pure and steady, a single sustained chord that filled the hangar the way light fills a room. The three suits stood in their frames in a row, grey and hulking, <em>1</em>, <em>2</em>, <em>3</em>, with their indicator bands glowing a steady green, and they looked, in the dim hangar with the Works dark beyond the doors, like three knights keeping a vigil in a chapel. He supposed that was what they were doing. He supposed that was what all three of them were doing.</p>\n<p>Teo came and stood beside him. She had a mug of something in each hand and she gave him one. It was tea, stewed black, with an unbelievable quantity of sugar in it.</p>\n<p>“Civil Defence tea,” she said. “It’s mostly a structural material.”</p>\n<p>They stood and drank it. The hammer went on.</p>\n<p>“I’ve got two boys,” Teo said, after a while, to the suits. “Mattias and Joss. Twenty-four and twenty-one. Mattias is an electrician in Kovač. Joss is at the university here, doing something with computers I don’t understand. Their father died eleven years ago. Heart.” She drank. “I used to tell them, when they were small and they were frightened of something — the dark, the dentist, whatever — I used to tell them, you do the thing, and you do it scared, and then it’s done, and the scared goes away and the done stays. That’s the deal. Scared goes, done stays.”</p>\n<p>“Nell’s mother tells her something like that.”</p>\n<p>“Smart woman.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.” He looked into the tea. “She is.”</p>\n<p>Teo was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“You told your girl the truth,” she said. “On the phone. I heard. Sorry. This place echoes like a church.”</p>\n<p>He had no answer to that.</p>\n<p>“Good,” said Teo. That was all. Then she knocked back the rest of her tea, and wiped her mouth with the back of her hand, and went off to try to sleep on a pile of tarpaulins in the corner.</p>\n<p>He stood there a while longer. The keels sang. Out in the dark, beyond the doors, the shimmer stood over the station like a ghost of a column, like a tower made of nothing.</p>\n<p><em>You don’t know what’s in there</em>, said Nell in his head. <em>There might be.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At three in the morning — hour twenty-five, by the count that had become the only count that mattered — Ben pronounced the keels spun.</p>\n<p>At half past three they suited up. The liner, cool and close; the clam of the suit closing around them, thunk, thunk, thunk; the exo taking their weight. The keels were at full now and the note came into Cas’s spine like a held breath. The world was a bright curve of visor. In his ears, the suits’ radios hissed and clicked, and then Ben said, “Comms check, one,” and Teo said, “One, loud and clear, unfortunately,” and Ben said, “Two,” and then, “Three?” and Cas said, “Three.”</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “Right. Let’s go and fix the fucking world.”</p>\n<p>They walked. Out of the hangar, down a service corridor, the three of them in a line, heavy and slow, their boots booming on the steel floor. The Collar was empty. Every door stood open. Every screen was on, showing the same message on a blue background — <em>MARGIN IRREGULARITY: REMAIN CALM</em> — and every light was on, and there was nobody, and their footsteps echoed down corridors that went on and on.</p>\n<p>At the end of the last corridor was Gate Nine: a great steel door five metres high, painted in hazard stripes, set in a black wall. A small group was waiting in front of it. Rask, very straight, in the same dark suit. Gaunt, in his cardigan, with his hands folded. Petra Imre from the Lattice office, who came forward and, without saying anything, put her hand flat on the chestplate of Cas’s suit for a second, and took it away, and stepped back with her eyes wet. A Civil Defence sergeant at the controls of the gate.</p>\n<p>Nobody made a speech. Cas was grateful for that. Rask looked at each of them in turn, and nodded once, and said, “The Commonweal is grateful,” and it was so obviously the wrong thing to say that it was almost moving.</p>\n<p>“Open it,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>The sergeant pulled a lever. Somewhere in the wall a motor began to grind.</p>\n<p>Gate Nine rolled back.</p>\n<p>Beyond it was the Works, dark and vast and humming, going on forever. Warm air came out of it, and a smell of ozone and hot metal and something green and rank like pondwater. The light inside was wrong — a sort of faint, shifting, pearly glow that seemed to come from nowhere in particular.</p>\n<p>And the hammer, which had been a sound for more than a day, was not a sound any more. In here it was simply the world. It came up through the floor and into the suits and through the suits into their bones, vast and slow and absolute, and all three of them stopped on the threshold as though they’d walked into a wall.</p>\n<p>Once a second. Once a second. Once a second.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Ben’s voice, small and brave in Cas’s ear. “That’s not ominous at all.”</p>\n<p>They went in.</p>"}, {"file": "03-the-works.md", "label": "Chapter 3", "title": "The Works", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 6458, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>SECTION 9 — IN THE EVENT OF CONTAINMENT LOSS: REMAIN AT YOUR STATION AND AWAIT RELIEF. DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE. RELIEF CREWS WILL BE DISPATCHED. YOU WILL NOT BE FORGOTTEN.</p><span class=\"attr\">Hollowell Draw emergency placard, standard issue, all levels</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The placard was the first thing they saw.</p>\n<p>It was screwed to the wall just inside Gate Nine, at head height, where every worker coming on shift for ninety-one years would have walked past it: a sheet of yellowed laminate in a steel frame, the words printed in the blocky black typeface the Ministry had used in the Year Twenties, with a little diagram of a stick figure standing beside a stick-figure console with its stick hands at its sides. The laminate had gone cloudy with age. Somebody, at some point, had drawn a moustache on the stick figure in marker pen, and somebody else had scrubbed most of it off.</p>\n<p>Teo stopped in front of it. Her helmet light moved slowly down the lines.</p>\n<p>“’You will not be forgotten,’” she read. “They’ll put that on my headstone.”</p>\n<p>“They’ll put it on everyone’s,” said Ben.</p>\n<p>The gate ground shut behind them. The sound of it closing was very final. Then there was only the hammer, and the three notes of the keels, and their breathing in the suits.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Cas had been inside the Draw many times, in another life. As a student on placement; as a junior on the Lattice survey team; as a member of the Margin Review, walking the Works with a clipboard behind Dr Adeyemi, who had been sixty-eight and walked everywhere very fast with his hands in his pockets, stopping every so often to lay his palm flat on a wall and listen. He had thought he remembered it. He had thought that fifteen years of not being allowed in had simply made it smaller in his mind, the way the house you grew up in shrinks when you go back.</p>\n<p>He had forgotten nothing. He had simply never understood it.</p>\n<p>The Works went on for four kilometres between the Collar and the Lattice, and in all that distance there was not one thing that had been built as part of a plan. Hollowell’s original station had been a sphere — the Lattice, the Vault, the Lesion — and a thin shell of machinery around it. Then the Commonweal had grown hungry, and every decade since, the Ministry had wanted more, and every decade the engineers had given it more the only way they could: by adding. Rectifier halls to convert the Draw’s raw output, and more rectifier halls when the first ones couldn’t keep up. Coolant galleries. Bioscrubber halls full of algae tanks to eat the carbon the crews breathed out, because it was cheaper to grow the air than to pump it four kilometres. Transformer vaults, cable tunnels, lift shafts, a spinal column of superconducting trunk lines as thick as a house. And all of it jammed in wherever it would go, on top of, around, and through everything that had been there before, until the whole thing had become less like a building than a reef — a vast, dim, dripping, humming structure that had grown by accretion for ninety-one years in the dark.</p>\n<p>Their route ran inward along what the maps called Radial Nine. The maps were optimistic. For the first hour it was a corridor, wide and high and lit by emergency strips at floor level, and they walked it in single file with Teo in front and Ben in the middle and Cas at the back, the keels humming, dropping an orange relay puck every two hundred metres. Then the corridor ended at a stair that went down into a transformer vault the size of a cathedral nave, full of grey steel cabinets in rows like pews, and they crossed it, and on the other side the corridor carried on at a different level, in a different typeface, painted a different colour. The signs changed every few hundred metres. RADIAL 9. R-9 (OLD). RADIAL NINE — WORKS SECTOR 4 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL. WORKS SECTOR 4 WAS HERE → with an arrow, in faded chalk, pointing at a wall.</p>\n<p>Once, early on, they passed the door of a bioscrubber hall: a great grey pressure bulkhead in a yellow hazard frame, with a narrow personnel lock beside it and a brass maker’s plate riveted to the steel. HAMMOND CONTAINMENT SYSTEMS. Ben patted it as he went by, the way you’d pat a dog you didn’t trust.</p>\n<p>“We had one of those on the keel test cell,” he said. “Worst door ever made. It only opens from the inside, so nobody out in the corridor can let the algae out by accident. And you have to bleed the hydraulics before it’ll crank, and it doesn’t tell you that, it just sits there looking at you. I lost a whole afternoon to one. I kicked it. It won.”</p>\n<p>“Did you get it open?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Eventually.” He sounded pleased with himself. “I know all its secrets now. We’re very close.”</p>\n<p>The smell was ozone and hot oil and algae and a faint sweetish undernote of rot, and the sound was the hammer, and under the hammer, everywhere, the Works itself: a vast layered murmur of machines that had not been told to stop. Pumps still pumping. Fans still turning. Somewhere far off, a relay clicking open and shut, open and shut, like a finger tapping on a desk. The rectifier halls were still live — there was power in them from somewhere, the Lesion’s leaking output finding its own way through the old conductors — and as they passed the doors they could hear the halls singing, a high, thin, electrical choir.</p>\n<p>And there was the light. The faint pearly glow Cas had seen from the gate was everywhere in here, in the corridors and halls and stairwells, sourceless and shifting. It came through gaps in walls and under doors. It lay on the floor in pale drifts like moonlight. When he held up his gauntlet in front of his visor, his hand cast no shadow.</p>\n<p>“Where’s that coming from?” said Ben.</p>\n<p>“The core,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Through four kilometres of — through all <em>this</em>?”</p>\n<p>“Not through it. Around it.” He didn’t really know how to explain it. He wasn’t sure he understood it himself. “The geometry’s bent. There are paths for light in here that shouldn’t exist. Some of it’s getting out along them.”</p>\n<p>“That’s⁠—” Ben started, and then, for once, didn’t finish.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The first gravity squall hit them in the second hour.</p>\n<p>They were in a long service gallery with pipes along both walls, walking on a grated catwalk above a drop into darkness, and Teo, in front, said, “Hold on⁠—” and then the floor tipped.</p>\n<p>It didn’t. The floor stayed exactly where it was. But <em>down</em> moved. Cas felt it go the way you’d feel a boat begin to roll: a slow, sickening swing, his weight sliding sideways inside the suit, the whole world leaning, and then the far wall of the gallery was below him and he was falling toward it. He grabbed. The suit’s gauntlet closed on the catwalk rail harder than he meant it to, crushing the steel, and held. His legs swung out. For a long moment he hung there, the three of them hanging there, dangling from a handrail over a wall that had become a floor, while the pipes around them groaned and a loose toolbox went tumbling past, end over end, and hit the far wall, and slid along it, and fell into the dark.</p>\n<p>Then down swung back. Slowly, like a pendulum. They dropped to their knees on the catwalk.</p>\n<p>“<em>Christ</em>,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Everybody okay?” Ben’s voice was high. “Everybody — is the keel — your keels okay? Check your — the indicator bands, are they⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Green,” said Cas. “Green. I’m fine.”</p>\n<p>“Green,” said Teo. “What the hell was that?”</p>\n<p>“Gravity weather.” Cas got up. His legs were shaking inside the exo; the exo held them. “The Lattice is still there, it’s still pulling, but it’s lost its shape. And the Lesion’s pushing. And every piece of keelstone in the Works is pulling in its own direction. They’re all out of balance. It’ll come in gusts.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Gusts.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And shears. And lees. Places where they cancel out and it’s calm.” He looked back along the gallery. “We should expect more.”</p>\n<p>They did get more. Over the next hours the Works taught them its weather the hard way. There were gusts like the first, where down swung sideways or backwards or, once, for three terrible seconds, straight up, so that they found themselves pressed against the ceiling of a stairwell looking down at the stairs. There were places where weight simply piled on — a stretch of corridor where each step was heavier than the last until the exo was groaning and Cas’s knees were on fire even inside it, and then, abruptly, it was gone. There was a stairwell where, for three steps in the middle, gravity doubled, and they learned to take those three steps at a run. There was a long, wide turbine hall where down was very slightly <em>forward</em>, so that crossing it they seemed to be walking downhill on a floor that was dead level, the three of them leaning backward against nothing like people walking into a gale.</p>\n<p>They learned to watch for it. Loose things were the tell. A cable swinging when there was no breeze. Dust drifting across a floor. Teo started carrying a handful of washers in her gauntlet and dropping one every few metres, watching where it fell.</p>\n<p>And their clocks began to drift.</p>\n<p>Each suit had a small display at the bottom of the visor with two times on it: OUTSIDE, fed by a signal from the Collar through the relay pucks, and LOCAL, the suit’s own clock. At the gate they’d kept step, second for second. An hour in, LOCAL had gained four minutes. Two hours in, nineteen. Ben started reading out the ratio every half hour like a man calling depths on a ship.</p>\n<p>“One point one.”</p>\n<p>“One point three.”</p>\n<p>“One point six. We’re living sixty percent faster than the Collar.”</p>\n<p>“Meaning?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Meaning for every hour we spend in here, only about forty minutes go by out there.” Ben considered this. “I mean. Which is sort of good? We’ve got more time than we thought.”</p>\n<p>“It’s good for the city,” said Cas. “The debt’s on the Collar’s clock. Every hour we spend in here only costs them forty minutes of it.” He looked at the two numbers at the bottom of his visor. “It’s not good for us. The air in these suits is on our clock. So’s the power. So are the keels. So are we.”</p>\n<p>“So we’ve got more time,” said Ben, “and we use it up faster.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. And it gets stronger further in.” Cas looked along the corridor, inward, into the pearly dark. “By the Crown, an hour of the Collar’s could be days of ours.”</p>\n<p>Ben thought about that for a while.</p>\n<p>“That’s either really good,” he said, “or really bad.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Cas.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They found the first of the night shift in the fourth hour.</p>\n<p>They’d seen signs before that. Hard hats in corridors. A jacket hanging on a valve wheel. A lunch box, open, a sandwich still inside it, going green. An emergency locker torn open and emptied. Once, a set of muddy footprints in the dust of a floor that had been dry for years, going the other way, toward the gate, running. They were the prints of people who’d got out. The Collar had counted them: about four hundred had come out through the gates in the first hour after the rupture, from the outer Works, before the gradients got bad.</p>\n<p>The rest hadn’t.</p>\n<p>It was a stairhead, where a stair from the lower levels came up through the floor into a junction of four corridors. The night shift had come up that stair, running for the gate, maybe twenty of them. Cas never knew what had happened exactly. He guessed that it was a shear — a place where the gravity gradient had gone suddenly steep, a cliff of force, and had moved, perhaps only a few metres, perhaps only for a second — and that they had been coming up the stair into the junction at the moment it passed over them.</p>\n<p>They were on the wall.</p>\n<p>That was the only way he could ever put it, afterwards, to himself. They were on the far wall of the junction, the way paint is on a wall. Their shapes were still there. You could see where each of them had been — this one’s arm flung up, this one’s hard hat, the pale sole of a boot. You could see that one of them had been holding someone else’s hand. But they had been pressed into the wall, by a weight no body could bear, with such force and so suddenly that they had not so much been crushed as <em>printed</em>. The wall behind them was bowed inward. The blood was black and had run nowhere, because at the instant it happened there had been nowhere for it to run.</p>\n<p>Ben made a sound and turned away and went down on his knees in the middle of the junction and was sick in his helmet. The suit’s scrubbers caught it and whined. He stayed there, bent over, his gauntlets on the floor, breathing in harsh, wet gulps over the radio.</p>\n<p>Cas stood very still. He found that he was looking at the hand. The two hands. He could not stop looking at the two hands.</p>\n<p>Teo walked past them both.</p>\n<p>She walked right up to the wall. She stood in front of it for a moment. Then she took off her own jacket — she’d brought a Civil Defence field jacket, strapped to the thigh of her suit, for when they reached somewhere she could take the suit off — and unfolded it and reached up and laid it over the part of the wall where the faces had been. It didn’t cover much. She looked around, and found a tarpaulin in a maintenance alcove, and a couple of high-vis vests hanging on a hook, and a dust sheet, and she hung them all. She did it methodically and without hurry, while Ben knelt on the floor being sick and Cas stood staring, and when she’d finished there was a patchwork of cloth on the wall and you could no longer see anything under it but shapes.</p>\n<p>Then she came back and put her gauntlet on Ben’s shoulder.</p>\n<p>“Up you get, son.”</p>\n<p>“I can’t⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You can. You’ll do it scared. Up.”</p>\n<p>He got up.</p>\n<p>Teo looked at Cas through two visors. He couldn’t see her face. He could hear her breathing, over the radio, harsh and slow and very controlled.</p>\n<p>“How many more of those,” she said, “are we going to find?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“Right.” She turned toward the corridor that ran inward. “Then let’s not stand about.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw the filament first.</p>\n<p>It was in a hall they had to cross in the fifth hour — a long, high, gloomy space that had been a coolant-pump hall once, before the pumps were moved, and was now a storeroom, full of pallets and crates and racks of spares going back to the ceiling in the pearly dark. Their route crossed it diagonally. They were halfway across when Cas saw something at the far end and stopped, and said, “Wait,” and the others stopped.</p>\n<p>It was a thread of light.</p>\n<p>It came out of the far wall of the hall about ten metres up and crossed the space in front of them and went into the floor. It was as fine as a hair and as bright as the filament in a bulb — the same white-violet as the Lesion, the colour that wasn’t quite a colour — and it wasn’t straight. It branched. It went a little way and split in two, and each of those split, and each of those, so that what came out of the wall as a single line went into the floor as a fine, spreading fan of threads, a luminous root system, a river delta drawn in light. And where it branched, the branches branched, smaller and smaller, finer and finer, until the eye lost them and they became a sort of glow, a faint shimmering mist of edges around every edge. When he tried to look at any part of it closely, his eye slid off. When he looked away, he could still see it on the inside of his eyelids.</p>\n<p>It made no sound.</p>\n<p>“What,” said Ben, “is that.”</p>\n<p>“Part of the Lesion,” said Cas. He was surprised how steady his voice was. “Part of the boundary. It’s — when it broke, it didn’t break into pieces. It broke into more edge. The edge is growing outward. That’s one of its threads.”</p>\n<p>“It came through four kilometres of⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It doesn’t come <em>through</em> things. It’s not in the Works the way we’re in the Works. It’s where the Works has been — bent.” He didn’t have a better word for it. “Don’t go near it.”</p>\n<p>They didn’t need telling. But they couldn’t help looking. And what they saw, the closer they looked, was worse than the light.</p>\n<p>Around the filament, for a metre or two on every side, the hall was different. The racks it passed near weren’t grey steel any more; they were brown and orange and flaking, rusted through into lace, sagging on themselves, and in one place a whole rack had simply crumbled and lay on the floor in a heap of rust-red powder with the plastic crates it had held collapsed into brittle shards on top. A section of cable tray that ran close to one branch was grey-white and furred. The paint on the wall beside the place where the thread came out had peeled and curled and fallen and lay in drifts. And on the floor, close to the fan of threads, there was a potted plant.</p>\n<p>Someone must have brought it in. A night-shift worker, making the storeroom a bit nicer. A spider plant, the kind that grew in every office in Averna, in a green plastic pot. Except that it had grown. Its pale leaves had gone up in a fountain higher than a man’s head and spilled out across the floor in long runners, and the runners had put down roots in the dust of the floor and grown new plants, and those had grown, and all of it — the whole great sprawling green-white tangle, three metres across — was dead, and dry, and grey, and dusty, as though it had grown for years and years and then withered and then stood for years more after that. In a hall where, by the Collar’s clock, only twenty-nine hours had passed.</p>\n<p>“It’s fast,” said Cas. “Near the thread. Time’s fast.”</p>\n<p>Ben already had his instrument out — a small sensor on a pole that measured the local clock rate — and was holding it out as far toward the threads as he dared, his arm at full stretch. The number on its display leapt as he moved it. Two. Nine. Forty. He took one step closer and it read four hundred.</p>\n<p>“Back up,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>He backed up. The number fell. He lowered the pole very slowly and stood looking at the dead plant.</p>\n<p>“It’s only near it,” said Teo. “A couple of metres. The rest of the hall’s fine.”</p>\n<p>“The hall’s open.” Cas nodded back at the doors they’d come in by. Both of them were standing wide, wedged with fire-stops, the way the night shift must have left them. So was the pair at the far end. “It’s got somewhere to thin out into. The whole Works. It’s like a heater in a room with the windows open — hot near the bars, nothing across the room.” He looked at the thread. “Shut those doors and it’d be a different story.”</p>\n<p>“That thing,” said Ben quietly, “is going to be everywhere, isn’t it. Further in.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>They went round the far side of the hall, as far from the filament as the racks allowed. Nobody talked. When they reached the far door Cas looked back once. From here the thread was just a pale line crossing the gloom, a scratch on the air. It could have been a trick of the light. It could have been nothing. Except that he could see, very faintly, in the glow around its finest branches, that it was moving. Growing. Very slowly branching into itself.</p>\n<p>And as he watched, it jumped. A new fork ran out from one of its branches all at once, fast as frost racing across a pane — a hand’s length in a blink — and then it was back to its slow crawl, as if nothing had happened. He’d seen it do that once before, while they were crossing. Half an hour of crawling. Then a jump.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They found out what Cas had meant about shut doors twenty minutes later.</p>\n<p>It was a locker room — a small one, off a side corridor, the kind a pump crew would change in at the start of a shift. Its door was shut. It was a steel door with a small square window of wired glass at head height, and the window was wrong.</p>\n<p>Ben saw it first. “Why’s it doing that?”</p>\n<p>The glass was moving. Not the pane; the light in it. It rippled, like the air over a road in summer, like the shimmer over the Pit — a slow trembling that never stopped and never went anywhere, held inside a pane of glass the size of a book. All round the door, in a band a finger wide, the paint on the frame had crazed into a fine web of cracks. Nowhere else.</p>\n<p>Teo shone her light through the window.</p>\n<p>The room beyond was a ruin. Everything in it was grey. The lockers had rusted to brown lace and slumped against each other; a bench had folded in half; a jacket on a hook had gone to threads and the threads had gone to powder; and the floor was deep in a soft grey dust that lay over everything like snow. And through the middle of the room, from the ceiling to the floor, ran a single thread of white-violet light, fine as a hair.</p>\n<p>There was a clock on the wall above the lockers. Its hands weren’t moving. They were a faint grey disc.</p>\n<p>Ben had his pole out. He held it up to the door, not touching. “One point seven,” he said. He brought it closer to the window, very carefully, until the sensor was a finger’s width from the pane. “One point seven. One point⁠—” He let the tip touch the glass, just barely, and the display went mad: a string of digits climbing too fast to read, and then a row of dashes. He snatched it back.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the glass,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“<em>What</em> is?”</p>\n<p>“All of it. The whole difference.” He could see it now that he knew what he was looking at: the ripple, the crazed paint, a line drawn round the room in the only place the difference could live. “The thread’s in a shut room. There’s nowhere for the fast time to thin out, so the whole room’s come up to the thread’s speed. And the walls and the door and that window are holding the drop between it and us. All of it. Out here it’s one point seven. A centimetre away it’s whatever the thread is. Hundreds. Thousands.”</p>\n<p>“It’s a voltage divider,” said Ben slowly. He was staring at the window. “Sorry — it’s a circuit thing. Resistors in a line, the biggest drop’s across the biggest resistor. The air’s the wire. The wall’s the resistor. So all of it sits across the wall.” He looked at Cas. “Is that right? Is that stupid?”</p>\n<p>“It’s not stupid. It’s right.”</p>\n<p>“So the Lattice⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Is the biggest resistor there is.” Cas looked along the corridor, inward. “And if there’s a Shear, that’s why.”</p>\n<p>Teo was looking at the door handle.</p>\n<p>“And if somebody opened that,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Then the drop’s got nowhere to be but the doorway. All of it, in the gap. Through whoever’s holding the handle.” He didn’t say what that would do to the hand on the handle. He didn’t have to. “And then out down the corridor, a front of it, thinning as it spreads. You wouldn’t want to be standing where we’re standing.”</p>\n<p>Nobody touched the door.</p>\n<p>“A blister,” said Ben. “It’s a blister. There’s a skin, and all this — stuff — inside, and you mustn’t pop it.” He swallowed. “Sorry. I name things when I’m frightened.”</p>\n<p>“You name things all the time,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“I’m frightened all the time.”</p>\n<p>“Right, then.” Teo straightened. She said it the way she’d have briefed a crew on a flood barrier. “Rules. One: we don’t open a shut room with a thread in it. Ever. Two: we don’t shut ourselves in a room with one.” She looked at each of them in turn. “Anybody not got that?”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything.</p>\n<p>They went on. Cas looked back once at the little square window, trembling in its door, and thought about the placard inside Gate Nine. <em>Do not attempt to traverse the Lattice.</em> People had walked past that sign for ninety-one years thinking it was a rule about machinery. It was a rule about this. There was no way round a drop; you could only spread it out, and nobody had ever built a road long enough to spread out the kind of difference he thought was waiting at the core. Nobody had needed to. The station had been built to run on ordinary time, with the Lattice holding back a few seconds a day.</p>\n<p><em>You will not be forgotten</em>, he thought, and didn’t like how it sounded.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At the end of the fifth hour they found a lee and stopped to rest.</p>\n<p>It was a small control room for a bank of pumps that had long since been removed, a box of a room with a desk and two chairs and a window onto nothing, and when they came into it the gravity just — went soft. After hours of gusts and shears and weights coming and going it was so calm, so ordinary, that all three of them stood in the doorway for a moment, swaying, like sailors come ashore. Teo dropped a washer. It fell straight down and bounced.</p>\n<p>“Oh, thank God,” said Ben, and sat down on the floor.</p>\n<p>They didn’t take the suits off. They couldn’t, here. But they sat, and drank from the suits’ water lines, and ate the paste that came out of the suits’ ration tubes, which tasted, Ben said, like somebody had described chicken to a machine. Their clocks said LOCAL 05:02 — five hours since the gate — and OUTSIDE 29:07, twenty-nine hours since the hammer began. The ratio in here was one point nine.</p>\n<p>Teo sat on the desk. For a while she was silent. Then she reached into the chest pocket of her liner, under the suit’s neck-ring, with two fingers, working carefully in the gauntlet, and brought out something small. She held it up.</p>\n<p>It was a data chip. A little black wafer the size of a fingernail, with the Office of Public Calm’s seal on it in silver.</p>\n<p>“Gaunt gave me this,” she said.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up.</p>\n<p>“Orders,” said Teo. “Sealed. For my eyes. I read them last night.” She turned the chip over in her fingers. “They say that the mission objective is the restoration of Continuance. They say that the technical lead, Dr Casimir Hale, has a documented history of unreliability on the question of Continuance. They say that in the event that Dr Hale attempts any action which would result in — let me get it right⁠—” she half closed her eyes “⁠—‘the permanent default of the Hollowell Draw’, I’m authorised to prevent that action by any means.” She looked at Cas. “Any means. They underlined it.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“You knew,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“I saw Gaunt give you something.”</p>\n<p>“And you didn’t say anything.”</p>\n<p>“I thought you’d tell me. Or you wouldn’t.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him for a while. Then she looked at the chip.</p>\n<p>On the floor of the little control room, along one wall, ran a coolant channel — an open steel gutter about a hand’s width across, with a steady, fast stream of cloudy water racing along it and vanishing through a grating into the dark under the floor. Teo leaned down and held the chip over it.</p>\n<p>“Tell me I’m not going to regret this,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’re probably going to regret this.”</p>\n<p>“Tell me the truth, then.” Her voice was flat. “All of it. Right now. Or I’ll keep it.”</p>\n<p>So he did.</p>\n<p>He told her Six couldn’t be reseated. That it had never been possible — that a Lattice with a fractal core had no geometry left to seat anything in, and every engineer in the crisis room had known it and had said <em>yes, Minister</em> anyway, and so had he. He told her about the Clause, and the Keystone, and that the only thing in the Draw that could stop what was coming would also end the Draw for ever, which was why nobody in the Ministry would say its name. He told her the key had been around the neck of Deputy Minister Aldous Crane when the Lesion broke, and that he didn’t know where Crane was, or whether he was alive, or whether the key still existed. He told her that he didn’t know what the words on the plate meant. He told her that he didn’t know, even if they reached the Crown, even if they found the key, even if they got the Keystone open, whether the thing would work, or whether anyone could survive working it, or whether Hollowell had been a genius or a madwoman or both.</p>\n<p>“So,” said Teo, when he’d finished. “Let me get this right. We’re walking into hell on a maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She dropped the chip into the coolant channel. It hit the water and was gone, whisked away down the gutter and through the grating into the dark, and she didn’t even watch it go. “I hate certainties. They always want something from you.”</p>\n<p>Ben was staring at her.</p>\n<p>“Major,” he said. “Major, that’s — I mean, isn’t that⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Treason? Probably.” Teo stretched, as far as the suit let her, and her joints cracked over the radio. “I don’t give a damn about the Draw, Farr. Never have. I care about the six hundred million people up there who are about to be very dead, and I care about my boys, and I care about getting a drink afterwards with a man who owes me one.” She tipped her visor at Cas. “That’s you, by the way. You owe me one. For the chip.”</p>\n<p>“I owe you one,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Good. I’ll have a large one.”</p>\n<p>Ben looked from one of them to the other. Then he laughed — a thin, shocked, helpless little laugh, like air going out of a tyre — and put his helmet in his gauntlets and kept laughing, and after a while it wasn’t clear that it was laughing any more, and Teo reached over and put her hand on his back and left it there until it stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They moved on at the start of the sixth hour.</p>\n<p>The map said that Radial Nine ran on for another kilometre and a half to the Lattice, but the map also said it went straight through a bioscrubber hall — B-7, one of the big algae halls from the Year Fifties — and when they got to the hall’s outer door the light coming around its edges was brighter than anywhere they had been.</p>\n<p>Ben went ahead, up a short stair to an observation gallery that ran along one side of the hall, and they heard him stop.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” he said. “You should see this.”</p>\n<p>It was a long gallery with a wall of thick glass, looking down into the hall. The hall was enormous — three hundred metres long, forty wide — and filled with algae tanks: great clear columns of water, rank after rank of them, each as tall as a house and full of drifting green, lit from within by their own grow-lamps, so that the whole hall glowed a deep, underwater emerald. Pipes ran between them. Walkways ran between the pipes. It was beautiful in a way nothing else in the Works had been. It looked like the inside of a lung. It looked like the bottom of the sea.</p>\n<p>And along the far wall of it, from floor to ceiling, ran a filament.</p>\n<p>This one was bigger. Not a hair; a vein. And it wasn’t in the hall. It was in the wall — in the thickness of it, under its skin, the way a vein runs under the skin of a wrist: a great branching tree of light glowing up through the grey concrete from inside, brightest where the panels had cracked. Its finest twigs reached almost to the wall’s face and stopped there, pressed up against the inside of it like fingers against a window. In front of it, the first few ranks of tanks had gone strange colours: black, brown, a thick, clotted, vivid green; one had simply cracked and drained and stood empty with a scum of dried grey slime up its sides. Past those, the tanks looked the way tanks should. In the nearest ones, below the gallery, the bubbles rose at the lazy speed that bubbles rise at.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the wall,” said Ben. “It’s not in the room. That’s — that’s good? That’s good, isn’t it. The wall’s holding it.”</p>\n<p>“The wall’s holding most of it.” Cas watched the ruined tanks at the far end. “Some of it leaks through. That end’s fast. This end isn’t.”</p>\n<p>“And if it comes out of the wall?”</p>\n<p>Nobody answered him. Nobody needed to. They’d stood in front of the locker room door half an hour ago.</p>\n<p>Teo was reading the map on her visor. “The route goes through the hall. In by the big door down there, down the south side, out at the far end. Three hundred metres.” She pointed. “The other way round’s the long way. Three hours. Back past the transformer vaults and round — through that weather.”</p>\n<p>Nobody wanted the weather. They’d skirted the edge of it on the way in, a stretch where the floor had gone sideways three times in a hundred metres, and Cas had listened to all three keels labour through every gust. Every hour of that would cost the rotors spin. Spin they were going to need at the Shear.</p>\n<p>“And the hall’s shut,” said Teo. “It’s a bioscrubber. It’s sealed by design.” She looked at Cas. “Rule two.”</p>\n<p>“Rule two,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>They’d be ten minutes crossing it. Ten minutes inside a sealed room with a thread in its wall — exactly the thing they’d said out loud, half an hour ago, that they would never do.</p>\n<p>“Then we open it first,” said Ben.</p>\n<p>They looked at him.</p>\n<p>“Vent it. Before we go in.” He was leaning on the glass, looking down at the hall’s main door at the foot of the gallery stair: a grey pressure bulkhead three metres high, in a yellow hazard frame, with a personnel lock beside it. “That’s a Hammond. That’s the one I kicked. If we crack the big door and leave it open, the hall’s not shut any more, it’s just part of the Works. Then if the thread does come out of the wall, it’s the storeroom. Fast at that end, fine at this end. And we walk down the south side and out, and it doesn’t matter what the thread does.”</p>\n<p>“And to crack the big door,” said Cas, “somebody has to be inside.”</p>\n<p>Ben’s face did something complicated. “Yeah,” he said. “That’s the — yeah. It only opens from inside. That’s the whole point of it. You go in through the personnel lock, and the lock won’t open its inside door till its outside door’s shut, so the hall stays sealed while you’re doing it. Then you walk ten metres to the control pillar, and bleed the hydraulics, and crank. Four minutes. Five. Then it’s open, and you two come through.”</p>\n<p>“Five minutes shut in a room with a thread in it,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“In its <em>wall</em>. It’s in the wall. It’s been in the wall the whole time we’ve been standing here.”</p>\n<p>“Then I’ll do it.”</p>\n<p>“You don’t know the sequence. There’s a thing you have to do with the bleed valve first or it just sits there, I told you, I lost a whole afternoon⁠—” He turned to Cas. “It’s faster if it’s me. It’s a lot faster. And faster’s the point, isn’t it? Faster’s the whole point.”</p>\n<p>They both looked at Cas.</p>\n<p>He was the technical lead. Rask had said so, in a room with gold carpet, and he’d said yes, and this was what it meant. He looked at the tree of light in the wall. He did the arithmetic, because he always did the arithmetic. The thread in the storeroom had crawled for half an hour between its jumps, and most of its jumps had gone nowhere much. This one had sat under its skin of concrete, not moving, for as long as they’d been watching. The chance that it would break out into the air in the next five minutes, rather than the next hour, or the next day, or never — he couldn’t know it. He could guess. He guessed it at one in twenty. Less. Against three hours of weather and a keel’s worth of spin.</p>\n<p><em>It’ll probably be fine</em>, he thought.</p>\n<p>The sentence sat in his head, smooth and round and reasonable. It did not occur to him that anyone had ever thought it before.</p>\n<p>“Five minutes,” he said. “In and out. If anything changes — anything — you come straight back through the lock.”</p>\n<p>“Five minutes,” said Ben, and grinned at them suddenly, hugely, like a boy let out of school. “Right. Right, you absolute antique. Let’s have you.”</p>\n<p>He went down the stair two at a time, his keel humming, his boots ringing on the steel treads, and the outer door of the personnel lock swung open for him, and he went into it, and it swung shut.</p>"}, {"file": "04-blister.md", "label": "Chapter 4", "title": "Blister", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 4704, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>If one of us ends up in fast time, the others’ll sound like whales.</p><span class=\"attr\">Benedek Farr</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">From the gallery they watched him come out of the lock into the hall.</p>\n<p>He went quickly, the way he did everything, the keel’s hum rising and falling in their radios as he moved, his helmet light bobbing between the great glowing columns of the tanks. The control box for the bulkhead’s manual cycle was on a pillar ten metres inside the hall. Ben reached it. They saw him open its cover. They heard him say, “Bleed valve first, you horrible thing,” and heard the squeal of a valve wheel, and then a long, thin hiss.</p>\n<p>“Ninety seconds for the pressure to come off,” he said. “Then crank. Told you. I know all its secrets.”</p>\n<p>Teo let out her breath. Cas watched the wall.</p>\n<p>He was watching it when the filament moved.</p>\n<p>Far off at the other end of the hall, the great tree of light under the wall’s skin didn’t move so much as <em>grow</em>, all at once — a flicker of new branches running out from its tips like frost racing across a pond, the jump he’d watched in the storeroom, only bigger. And one of them didn’t stop at the wall’s face. A single fine thread came out through the concrete as if the concrete were smoke, and ran across the ceiling of the hall faster than he could follow, fine as spider silk, and went down into the floor about forty metres from where Ben was standing.</p>\n<p>Nothing slammed. Nothing needed to. Every door in the hall was already shut. That had been the point of the lock.</p>\n<p>The glass in front of Teo’s face rippled.</p>\n<p>It was the locker-room window, made huge. The whole long pane of the gallery, from end to end, took on that slow, trembling shimmer, like heat over a road, and held it. Along the window frame the paint crazed in a band a finger wide, with a tiny, glassy crackle like ice forming on a pond.</p>\n<p>And Ben⁠—</p>\n<p>Ben flickered.</p>\n<p>That was what it looked like. One moment he was standing at the pillar with his hand on the valve wheel, and the next he was standing somewhere else — a step to the left, turned round — and the next he was at the lock, and the next he was back at the pillar, and none of it was motion. It was like watching a film with most of the frames cut out. He jumped from place to place in stutters. His helmet light flared and moved and flared and moved.</p>\n<p>In their ears, the radio made a noise.</p>\n<p>It was a squeal. A high, thin, rising chirp, like a bird, like a bat, like a whistle blown in a dream, so brief it was almost not there, and then another, and another.</p>\n<p>“Ben!” Teo was at the glass, her gauntlets flat on it. “Ben! <em>Farr!</em>”</p>\n<p>The suit’s radio chimed. A little amber light came on at the bottom of Cas’s visor: RECEIVING — BUFFERING — RETIMING. Ben’s code. It had caught the chirps and was stretching them back out.</p>\n<p>And then, suddenly, Ben’s voice, perfectly clear, perfectly normal, in their ears:</p>\n<p>“⁠—what happened, what happened, the glass — Cas? Teo? The glass is all — oh no. Oh no, no, no, no. I can see you, I can see you up there, you’re not moving, why aren’t you — oh no. Oh, no. Okay. Okay. Okay.”</p>\n<p>It stopped. The light went off.</p>\n<p>Down on the floor of the hall, Ben was at the inner door of the lock, both gauntlets flat against it. Not opening it. Just leaning there. Then he was gone. Then he was at the pillar. Then he was standing still — for a whole second, perfectly still — in the middle of the hall, looking up at them.</p>\n<p>The radio chirped.</p>\n<p>RECEIVING — BUFFERING — RETIMING.</p>\n<p>“Okay.” His voice again. Different. Steadier, as though he had had time to steady it, as though he had sat down somewhere and breathed for a while. “Okay. I’ve got the pole out. I’m reading forty-eight thousand. Forty-eight thousand to one. It came out of the wall forty metres from me, and the whole hall’s come up to its speed. It’s a blister. It’s the locker room. The drop’s all in the walls and the doors — it’s in your glass, I can see it from here, the window’s gone all wavy.” A breath. “Don’t open the door. Listen to me. Don’t open the door. You know why. It all goes through the doorway, and then down the corridor and up the stair and through you. The keels won’t hold forty-eight thousand. Nothing will. And I can’t open it from in here, either, not the lock, not the big one — it’d go through me on the way out.” A pause. “The lock’s right there. I’m looking at it. It’s about ten metres away. That’s a stupid thing to be able to see.” Another pause. “I’m okay. I’m okay. I’m just going to think.”</p>\n<p>The light went off.</p>\n<p>The radio never spoke again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Forty-eight thousand,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>She said it very quietly. She was still at the glass.</p>\n<p>“A minute for us,” said Cas, “is a month for him.”</p>\n<p>He heard himself say it. He heard how calm it sounded.</p>\n<p>“Then we open the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He said no.”</p>\n<p>“I heard what he said. We’ve got keels. We’ve got⁠—” She turned on him. “What do you mean, <em>no</em>? That’s a <em>boy</em>, down there⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It’s in the glass. Two to forty-eight thousand, across a centimetre. If we open that door, it’s across the doorway instead, and then it comes down the corridor like a wave, and whoever it passes through has one half of their body living twenty-four thousand times faster than the other half. The keel averages over a metre. It isn’t enough. Nothing’s enough.” He made himself go on. “He knows the keels better than anyone alive. He said no.”</p>\n<p>Teo stared at him. Then she looked back down through the glass. Below them, in the emerald light, Ben was moving in stutters from tank to tank.</p>\n<p>“There’s got to be a way,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Maybe. If there is, he’ll find it before we do. He’s got more time.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t say <em>one in twenty</em>. He would say it later, to himself, for a long time.</p>\n<p>It was Ben who worked out how to talk.</p>\n<p>They didn’t see him do it. They saw him vanish from the floor, and then — perhaps a minute later — he was there on the other side of the glass, suddenly, right in front of them. The algae tanks stood in rows below the gallery, and along their tops ran a maintenance catwalk that came right up against the gallery window, and he was standing on it, a foot away from them through the glass. He had taken his helmet off.</p>\n<p>His face was thinner. That was the first thing. It had been perhaps a minute, for them — and in that minute he had found a razor from somewhere and shaved, and cut his own hair, badly, very short, and gone dark under the eyes and hollow in the cheeks. He looked at them. His eyes moved from Teo to Cas and back again. Then he held up a hand, and in it was a stubby yellow grease pencil, the kind engineers used to mark pipes.</p>\n<p>He wrote on the glass. He wrote fast; they couldn’t see his hand move. The letters simply appeared, backwards to him, forwards to them.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOW LONG</div>\n<p>Cas looked round. There was a supervisor’s desk in the gallery, and in its drawer, a marker. He took his right gauntlet off — the gallery was calm, a lee of sorts; the suit let him — and pulled the cap off the marker with his teeth and wrote, carefully, in capitals, backwards so Ben would read them the right way round:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">3 MIN</div>\n<p>It took him perhaps fifteen seconds.</p>\n<p>When he stepped back, Ben had written underneath:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">3 MONTHS</div>\n<p>And then, a flicker, and more:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">YOUR LETTERS TAKE A WEEK<br>WROTE HOW LONG. WAITED 8 DAYS TO SEE 3 MIN<br>KEEP IT SHORT</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There was a moment — Cas would think about it a great deal, afterwards — when they could have gone.</p>\n<p>The route through the hall was closed. But the long way round was still there. Three hours, the map said. Three hours to go round B-7 and rejoin Radial Nine on the far side, and then another kilometre and a half to the Lattice, and whatever was past the Lattice, and whatever was past that. Every minute they stood on this gallery was a minute of the city’s, and the city didn’t have many. The debt was coming due. Somewhere out there a pulse was gathering itself at the core, and there was a lock in the Crown and perhaps a key and certainly six hundred million people, and none of it was going to wait.</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him. He looked at her.</p>\n<p>Neither of them said it. Teo took off her own right gauntlet, and sat down on the floor of the gallery with her back against the desk, and after a moment Cas sat down beside her. The glass was in front of them. On the other side of it, in the green light, a man they had known for a day began to live the rest of his life.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They didn’t see most of it. They saw it the way you’d see a life through a keyhole — in flashes, in fragments, the long stretches between cut out. When Ben stood still for a while, they saw him. When he moved, he was a flicker, a smear, a sense of someone having just been there. He’d be at the glass, and then the catwalk would be empty, and then — a minute later, a month later — he’d be back.</p>\n<p>He came back with a beard.</p>\n<p>It happened in no time at all. One flicker, he was clean-shaven and hollow-eyed. The next, there was a dark scruff along his jaw. The next, it was a beard: short, rough, reddish-brown, which neither of them had known his hair would grow. He’d taken the suit off entirely. He was wearing the grey liner and, over it, a pair of stained orange overalls from some locker, with the sleeves rolled up. He looked at them through the glass — they could see him breathing — and then wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I’M OK<br>AIR GOOD (ALGAE)<br>WATER GOOD (PIPES)<br>FOOD: ALGAE<br>:(</div>\n<p>Teo laughed. It came out of her like a sob.</p>\n<p>Ben saw it. Or saw something — saw her face change, very slowly, over days, perhaps. He grinned. It was so quick they barely saw it, but it was there, a flash of teeth in the beard, and then he was gone again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<div class=\"sign\">TELL JONAH</div>\n<p>Four minutes later, it was still there on the glass. Underneath it, in smaller letters:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">TELL HIM I SAID YES TO THE DOG</div>\n<p>Cas didn’t know what it meant. Teo did. She’d been across the hangar the night before, and she’d heard. She turned her face away, into the shoulder of her suit, and stayed that way for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Twenty minutes in — nearly two years, for Ben — the catwalk against the glass had become a place.</p>\n<p>He’d dragged things up there. A pallet, to sleep on, with a foam mat from a first-aid station and an emergency blanket, silver on one side. A crate for a table. A row of algae-paste tins, some full, some empty and scrubbed and used for things — a pencil pot, a cup, a tiny pot with a green shoot in it. He’d rigged a grow-lamp on a cable to hang over it all. He’d rigged a sort of curtain, from a tarpaulin, that he could draw across the glass. He didn’t draw it often.</p>\n<p>And there was a rat.</p>\n<p>They noticed it one flicker when Ben was sitting cross-legged on the pallet, eating something out of a tin with his fingers. It was sitting on his knee. A big, sleek, brown-grey Works rat, with a long pink tail curled round it and its paws folded, looking at them through the glass with its black-bead eyes as though it too were waiting to see what happened. Ben pointed at it and wrote an arrow on the glass, and a word:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MINISTER →</div>\n<p>Teo laughed again. This time it was nearly a real laugh.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>And then, for a while, there was nothing good.</p>\n<p>They’d known it would come. You couldn’t be alone in a room for that long and not have it come. Cas had lived alone for fifteen years and he knew. But he had had a city outside his window, and a phone, even a phone that never rang, and the possibility — even if he never took it — of walking down a street and buying a newspaper and speaking to a stranger. Ben had a rat, and algae, and two statues behind a pane of glass who took a week to write four letters.</p>\n<p>He came to the glass one flicker with his hair grown out wild, and his beard wild, and his eyes red, and wrote in huge letters, slanting, tearing the grease pencil:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I CAN’T<br>I CAN’T DO THIS<br>I CAN’T DO THIS I CAN’T I CAN’T</div>\n<p>And then he hit the glass. They didn’t see his fist move. They just saw the glass shake, very slightly, and a starburst of cracks appear in the outer pane, in front of Teo’s face, and Ben’s hand on the glass on the other side with blood smeared from the knuckles, and his forehead pressed against it, and his shoulders shaking.</p>\n<p>For one long second Cas thought of the locker-room door, and the doorway, and what a hole in that glass would mean for all three of them. The cracks stayed where they were. They were in the outer skin. The inner pane held.</p>\n<p>Teo put her bare hand flat on the glass over his.</p>\n<p>She held it there. She held it there for a long time — two minutes, three — and on the other side of the glass, Ben stood with his forehead against it, perfectly still. And they understood, both of them at the same moment, what he was seeing: a hand, very slowly, over days and days, rising to the glass and coming to rest there. A hand that stayed. That didn’t go away. That was still there when he woke up, and still there when he went to sleep, for as long as he needed it to be there.</p>\n<p>Then he was gone. The glass was empty, except for the smears, and the cracks, and the huge slanting letters.</p>\n<p>He didn’t come back for a long time. Seven minutes. Eight months.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>When he came back he was different.</p>\n<p>He was thinner — much thinner — and his beard had been cut short with something, scissors or a knife, and his hair was tied back with a piece of wire. He was clean. He’d found clothes: a Works jumpsuit, too big, belted with cable. He moved — when he moved slowly enough for them to see — carefully, deliberately, like someone who has made a decision about how to live and is living it. He looked at them through the glass for a long moment. Then he wiped the old words off with his sleeve, and wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SORRY</div>\n<p>And under it, after a pause:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I’M STUDYING IT</div>\n<p>And an arrow, pointing down the length of the hall, at the far wall, where the great tree of light was still spreading slowly into the tanks.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>That was when it started to fill the glass.</p>\n<p>For the next hour — the next five years — Ben Farr studied the thing that was killing him, and wrote down what he learned where they could see it.</p>\n<p>He wrote fast, and they saw it arrive in bursts. A diagram would appear: the filament’s tree, sketched in grease pencil in quick sure lines, branches labelled. A column of numbers. An arrow. A question mark that was later crossed out, and replaced with an answer. The glass became a page, and then a notebook, and then a blackboard in a lecture hall. He ran out of room and wiped whole sections off and started again, and Cas — sitting cross-legged on the gallery floor with his black sketchbook open on his knee and a pencil in his bare hand — copied everything, frantically, as fast as it came, because he knew that anything he didn’t copy in the minute it was there might be wiped away the next.</p>\n<p>It was very good work.</p>\n<p>Ben had measured the filament. With the pole, with a tape, with a stopwatch and his own eyes, month after month, year after year, standing as close as he dared. He’d watched how it grew — how every branch split into branches with the same pattern, the same angles, at every scale. He’d counted. He’d worked out the branching ratio. He’d worked out that the pattern was self-similar, and computed, from his own measurements, the number that described how rough the thing was — how much edge it had.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">D = 2.73</div>\n<p>Cas stopped drawing. He sat for a moment, looking at the number. The same number that had been at the bottom of the last telemetry burst from the Crown. Ben had found it with a tape measure. He’d found it alone, in a room, with a rat, from first principles.</p>\n<p>“What?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“He’s right,” said Cas. “That’s all. He’s right.”</p>\n<p>There was more. Ben had noticed that the filament didn’t grow at random; it grew along lines. He’d mapped where the hall’s structure ran — the pillars, the beams, the old coolant lines that had once connected this hall to the Lattice — and found that the filament’s branches followed them, followed the lines where the Lattice had once pulled hardest on the station’s frame. Stress lines. The Tear was growing outward along the ghosts of the forces that had held it in. And where two branches forked, there was always, between them, a narrow gap — a throat — where the gradient was gentlest. A seam.</p>\n<p>He drew the Lattice. From memory, from the hall’s own schematics which he’d found in a cabinet, from reasoning. He drew the inner face of it, where Cas had said there would be a shear. He drew where the stress lines would run through it. He drew where the throats would be.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IF THERE’S A SHEAR<br>IT’LL HAVE SEAMS<br>HERE HERE HERE<br>GO THROUGH THE THROATS</div>\n<p>And arrows. And numbers. And a map.</p>\n<p>Cas copied it until his hand cramped. Teo held the sketchbook steady on his knee when his hand shook. At one point, without either of them saying anything, she took the pencil from him and did a whole section herself, the list of numbers, in her small square hand, while he flexed his fingers.</p>\n<p>On the other side of the glass Ben was thirty-two, and then thirty-three, and then thirty-four. His hair had started to go grey at the temples. He had lines around his eyes from squinting at the light. Minister had died — they saw the day he came to the glass with the rat lying in his cupped hands, and wrote MINISTER. OLD AGE. 3. GOOD INNINGS — and a year later there was another rat, smaller and darker, and he wrote MINISTER II, and pointed at it, and grinned in the beard.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS<br>NOBODY CAME</div>\n<p>Teo wrote back:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">WE’RE HERE</div>\n<p>It took her fifteen seconds to write it. It took him, on the other side, more than a week to watch it arrive. When it was finished, he stood looking at it. Then he wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I KNOW</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Near the end — though they didn’t know it was near the end — he came to the glass and sat on the pallet with his arms around his knees and looked at them, not writing anything.</p>\n<p>He was thirty-seven. He was greying at the temples and in the beard, and there were deep lines at the corners of his mouth, and he had the look of a man who has been very unhappy and then, over a long time, has found his way to something that was not happiness but was near it, and could live there. He had been alone in that hall for eleven years. He was older now than Cas had been when he signed the revised Margin Review.</p>\n<p>He wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IS IT STILL TODAY FOR YOU</div>\n<p>Cas wrote back:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SAME DAY</div>\n<p>Ben read it — slowly, over days — and they saw him nod. Then he wrote, carefully, smaller than before, so they had to lean close:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">STRANGE<br>YOU’VE BEEN SITTING THERE MY WHOLE LIFE<br>FEELS LIKE YOU’RE THE ONES WHO ARE STUCK</div>\n<p>Cas looked at those words. He found that his eyes were stinging. He found that he could not remember when that had started.</p>\n<p>And then Ben wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">GO</div>\n<p>Teo made a small sound.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">YOU HAVE TO GO<br>I’M FINE<br>HAD A GOOD LIFE<br>MOSTLY ALGAE</div>\n<p>“No,” said Teo, to the glass.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THE WORLD’S ENDING OUT THERE<br>GO AND STOP IT<br>YOU’VE GOT THE MAP</div>\n<p>Cas looked down at the sketchbook in his lap. Thirty pages of it, dense with grease-pencil diagrams in his own pencil and Teo’s. Eleven years of a man’s life.</p>\n<p>He looked at Teo. She was crying, openly, silently, her face wet in the visor-light, her jaw set.</p>\n<p>“Two more minutes,” she said. “Two more minutes and then we go. I’m not leaving him the minute he tells us to. I’m not having him think⁠—” She didn’t finish.</p>\n<p>“Two more minutes,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>He picked up the marker. He wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">2 MORE MIN</div>\n<p>And then, after a moment, because it seemed important and because there was no one else to say it:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU BEN</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He never got to read the second line.</p>\n<p>They saw it happen at the far end of the hall first. The tree of light on the far wall flared — the whole of it, every branch, at once, bright enough that the gallery windows went white and Cas’s visor darkened automatically — and then it grew. Not slowly now. Not with the patient frost-crawl they’d been watching for two hours. It <em>surged</em>. New branches ran out of it in every direction, racing across the ceiling and down between the tanks, fork after fork after fork, and the fine thread that had come down in the middle of the hall two hours ago thickened as they watched, from a hair to a finger to a rope of light as thick as an arm.</p>\n<p>On the other side of the glass, Ben stood up.</p>\n<p>He was already writing. The letters came so fast now that they seemed to appear all at once — whole lines at a time — and Cas understood, with a lurch that went all the way down into his stomach, what that meant.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IT’S SPEEDING UP</div>\n<p>And, under it, instantly:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">RATIO CLIMBING<br>100K<br>400K<br>3M<br>POLE CAN’T READ IT<br>IT’S OK</div>\n<p>And under that, all at once, the whole bottom of the glass:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<p>He put the pencil down.</p>\n<p>He put his hand flat on the glass. His palm, his long engineer’s fingers, dirty at the knuckles, with the white scar across two of them where he’d hit the glass all those years ago.</p>\n<p>Cas put his own bare hand flat against it on the other side.</p>\n<p>For a moment — less than a second — they were like that. Two palms, a centimetre of glass between them.</p>\n<p>Then Ben aged.</p>\n<p>He was thirty-seven, and then there was grey all through his beard, and his face had fallen in around the eyes, and he was fifty. He was sixty. The hand on the glass thinned under Cas’s palm, the knuckles swelling, the skin spotting and loosening over the bones. His back curved. His hair went white and thinned and was gone. He was an old man — a very old man, eighty, ninety — standing with his hand on the glass and his eyes, still his eyes, looking out through a face that Cas didn’t know at all. He was smiling. Cas was almost sure he was smiling.</p>\n<p>Then he wasn’t standing. He was sitting, on the pallet. He was lying on it. He was still.</p>\n<p>And then he was not a man. The overalls sagged. The shape under the silver blanket collapsed, and yellowed, and was bones, and the bones went grey and dry and cracked, and the blanket went brittle and split. The pallet rotted to splinters under it. The tins rusted to brown lace. The little pot with the green shoot in it burst with a sudden crown of leaves that browned and fell. Out in the hall the tanks bloomed — all of them, at once, a fantastic eruption of green, thick and rank and clotting against the glass — and then darkened, and went black, and drained, and cracked, and stood empty, with the grey slime dried to crusts up their sides. Dust fell from the ceiling in a slow endless snow. The grow-lamps flickered and died one by one. The paint peeled off the pillars and lay on the floor in drifts, and the drifts went to dust, and the dust settled over everything, deep and grey, like a snowfall on a ruin a thousand years old.</p>\n<p>It took about thirty seconds.</p>\n<p>Then the light at the far end of the hall dimmed back to its ordinary glow. The rope of light in the middle of the hall thinned, and thinned, and was a hair again, and drifted a little on the air, and was gone, into the floor, somewhere else.</p>\n<p>Cas was still standing with his hand on the glass.</p>\n<p>On the other side of it, very close, there was a heap of grey dust under a brittle grey rag, and a scatter of small bones that had been a rat, and, lying in the dust where it had been put down, a stubby yellow grease pencil.</p>\n<p>Above it, on the glass, the writing was still there. The grease had outlasted him. It was faded now, cracked and chalky, but you could still read it.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo was sick in her helmet vent.</p>\n<p>She did it without any fuss, turning her head, and the suit’s scrubbers whined, and she knelt there afterwards on the gallery floor with her head down. Then she got up.</p>\n<p>Cas hadn’t moved. He was looking at the pencil.</p>\n<p>“Hale.” Her voice was hoarse.</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“<em>Hale.</em>”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“We have to go now,” said Teo. “We said two minutes. That was two minutes.”</p>\n<p>He looked back at the glass. At IT WAS LONG ENOUGH. At the dust. At a centimetre of glass in front of his bare palm, and a smear on its other side where, a minute ago and sixty years ago, another palm had been.</p>\n<p>He took his hand away.</p>\n<p>He put his gauntlet back on. He closed the sketchbook — thirty pages, dense with numbers and diagrams, a map of a thing no one had ever seen, drawn by a man who had spent his whole life looking at it — and put it inside the chest of his suit, next to his heart, under the neck-ring, where it would be safe.</p>\n<p>They took the long way round. Three hours. For the first hour of it, neither of them said anything at all. They walked through the Works in the pearly light with the hammer coming up through the floor, once a second, once a second, and every so often Cas found that his lips were moving, and that he was counting.</p>\n<p>He wasn’t counting the hammer. He was counting how many seconds it had been since he’d put his hand on the glass.</p>\n<p>He knew what he would do if he ever reached a number that was long enough. He just didn’t know what the number was.</p>"}, {"file": "05-the-shear.md", "label": "Chapter 5", "title": "The Shear", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 6708, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Guilt’s a lousy reason, Hale. Die for something useful or don’t bother.</p><span class=\"attr\">Major Teodora Lisk, Civil Defence</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">It was Teo who talked first, in the end. They were going down a long ramp between two walls of cable trunking, with the hammer coming up through the soles of their boots, and she said, out of nowhere, “He was a good kid.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“I only knew him a day.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“No. I knew him eleven years.” She was quiet for a while. “Does that count? It feels like it counts.”</p>\n<p>Cas thought about it. He thought about a man with a beard and a rat on his knee, standing at a pane of glass for more than a week to watch two words arrive, and writing <em>I KNOW</em>.</p>\n<p>“It counts,” he said.</p>\n<p>They walked. The ramp went down and turned and went down.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell Jonah,” said Teo. “If I get back. About the dog.”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know what it means.”</p>\n<p>“It means they’d been arguing about getting a dog. He wanted one and Ben kept saying they didn’t have room.” She laughed, a short, raw sound. “Eleven years in a room full of algae and that’s the thing he wanted us to get right.”</p>\n<p>Cas didn’t answer. He found that he couldn’t.</p>\n<p>He found that grief, for him, had a peculiar shape. It came as arithmetic. He kept doing sums. A minute for them, a month for Ben. The hundred and twenty minutes they’d sat on the gallery floor. Eleven years, four months, give or take. The thirty seconds at the end, and how many years in them — sixty? A hundred? Ben’s pole hadn’t been able to read it. He did the sums over and over, the way you run your tongue over a broken tooth, and every time they came out the same, and every time it was unbearable, and he did them again.</p>\n<p>And under all of them, one more. The one he did most. <em>One in twenty. Less.</em></p>\n<p>“I said yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo didn’t stop walking. “You did the sum,” she said. “I watched you do it. You had the face.” A few more steps. “It was a good sum. I’d have done the same one. It came out wrong.” She was quiet for a while. “That’s what odds are, Hale. If they never came out wrong, they’d be called something else.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer. She didn’t make him.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Lattice lay below and inward, and to reach it they had to go down.</p>\n<p>This was the part of the plan Cas had drawn on the back of a Ministry briefing sheet in the crisis room, a hundred years ago, yesterday: the Vault was a sphere, its floor was the Anvil, and the Anvil was pierced from below by fourteen great conduits — cooling and power trunks that ran down through the keelstone plate into the Works beneath. The Crown was at the top of the Vault, four kilometres up Spindle One. There was no way into the Vault from the side; the Lattice was solid keelstone all around. The only way in was up through the floor.</p>\n<p>So they went down, into the Works under the Vault, through a region the old engineers had called the Undercroft, where everything was older and heavier and the Lattice’s pull came from above as well as from the side and the gravity weather was worst of all.</p>\n<p>It was in a shaft that the Undercroft nearly killed him.</p>\n<p>It was an old lift shaft, the cage long gone, with a steel ladder running down one wall and a gridwork of beams crossing it every ten metres. They were climbing down it, Teo first, Cas above, the keels humming loud in the narrow space, and Teo was calling out the washers — dropping one every few rungs and watching it fall straight down the middle of the shaft, glinting in her helmet light, until it vanished — and then she dropped one and it went sideways.</p>\n<p>“<em>Squall⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>It hit. Down went sideways and then <em>up</em> and then sideways again, a great slow roll like a ship broaching, and Cas’s hands were on the ladder and then they were not. He didn’t let go. The ladder moved. Or he moved; there wasn’t a way to tell. One moment he was clinging to steel rungs with the shaft below him, and the next the shaft was beside him and he was falling across it, and a beam came at him out of the dark, and he hit it back-first with his whole weight and the weight of the suit.</p>\n<p>He heard the keel. Over everything — over Teo shouting his name, over the scream of the beam as the suit’s plating ground along it, over his own breath — he heard the keel’s note change. It went from a clean high hum to something rough, something with a rattle in it, like a coin in a spinning drum.</p>\n<p>Then down swung back, and he was lying across the beam with his legs hanging over the shaft, and the note in his spine was wrong.</p>\n<p>“Hale! Cas! Talk to me⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m here.” He didn’t move. He was afraid to. “I’m here. I’m all right.”</p>\n<p>“Your keel⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>Teo was beside him on the beam — she’d climbed across, somehow, fast, in the suit — and had her light on his back. He heard her breath go in and not come out.</p>\n<p>“The housing’s cracked,” she said. “There’s a — there’s a split down the side, about the length of my hand. There’s light coming out of it. It’s — is it supposed to do that? Hale, is it supposed to⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>He reached back with his gauntlet and touched it. Through the gauntlet, through the plating, he could feel the rattle — a tiny eccentric wobble in something that had been perfectly balanced. The bearing. The crack had let the housing flex, and the rotor had kissed its bearing, just once, just slightly, and now it was running out of true.</p>\n<p><em>A bad day</em>, Ben had said. <em>A very, very bad day.</em></p>\n<p>He looked at the band of indicator lights around the housing’s waist, reflected in Teo’s visor. Green. Green. One amber.</p>\n<p>“How long?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“It’ll run down faster. The bubble’ll shrink as it slows. Hours, instead of days. Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“And if it goes?”</p>\n<p>“If the bearing goes⁠—” He didn’t finish. “It won’t go. It’ll just run down.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him through two visors.</p>\n<p>“You’re lying,” she said.</p>\n<p>“A bit.”</p>\n<p>“Right.” She got her shoulder under his arm. “Up.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He walked. He kept walking. For the first hour after the shaft he could hear the rattle all the time, faint and patient in the keel’s note, like a clock ticking in another room; after that he stopped hearing it, the way you stop hearing a clock, and only noticed it when the corridor went quiet and it was suddenly there, ticking, ticking. Teo heard it too. He could tell because she stopped talking whenever it was loud. She walked a little closer to him than she had before.</p>\n<p>They came to the Lattice at the end of the twelfth hour.</p>\n<p>He had seen it before, from inside, as a student — but only in pieces, through inspection ports, in schematics. He had never seen it like this. They came out of a tunnel onto a long gallery, a sort of balcony running along the outside of the Lattice’s outermost ring, and the Lattice was simply there, filling the world. A wall. A black wall so high that its top was lost above them in the pearly dark and so long that it curved away on both sides into nothing, smooth and seamless and utterly lightless, as if someone had cut a slice out of the universe. It hummed. You didn’t hear it; you felt it, in your chest, a vast deep tone below the hammer. It was keelstone, all of it — every cubic metre of that wall weighed as much as a locomotive — and they could feel it pulling at them, a steady sideways tug toward it, as if the wall were very gently trying to draw them in.</p>\n<p>“God,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing. He had found an inspection port — a round window of thick crystal set into the gallery’s floor, looking down and in through a gap between two of the outer rings. He knelt by it and looked.</p>\n<p>It went down a long way. Ring behind ring behind ring, black on black, each one a curved cliff, the gaps between them lit by that sourceless pearly light. And far down, far in, he could see one of the great tensioner rotors — the enormous spinning keelstone drums set into the rings that held the Lattice’s shape. It was turning slowly. It was perfectly true.</p>\n<p>And beyond it, deeper, where there should have been another⁠—</p>\n<p>“That’s Six,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo knelt beside him.</p>\n<p>It was hard to see at first. Tensioner Six was the size of a building: a drum of black keelstone two hundred metres across, set on a magnetic bearing in a cradle in the Lattice. Only it wasn’t in its cradle. It was lying tilted in the gap between two rings, like a coin that has fallen on its edge and leaned against a wall, one rim jammed against the ring above and the other sunk in the cradle below, and it was not turning. Around it, the rings themselves were wrong. They weren’t curved properly. They sagged toward Six and bulged away from it, as though the whole Lattice around it had been squeezed in a fist and then let go. And through the gaps around it, very faintly, threads of that white-violet light came branching outward from somewhere deeper in.</p>\n<p>“Can it be⁠—” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>He said it without thinking. He’d known it since the crisis room, since the number at the bottom of the screen. He’d known it since the car. But he hadn’t <em>seen</em> it, and now he had, and he found that seeing it made it into a thing that could be said out loud without any effort at all.</p>\n<p>“It can’t be reseated. Not from the Crown, not from anywhere. There’s nothing to seat it in. The Lattice has lost its shape. Even if you stood it back up it would just fall over again.” He sat back on his heels. “Every engineer in that room knew it. Rask probably knows it. They all said it anyway.”</p>\n<p>“You said it.”</p>\n<p>“I said it.”</p>\n<p>Teo got up. She stood looking out at the black wall, her arms folded over the chest of her suit.</p>\n<p>“It’s funny,” she said. “I’ve been in Civil Defence twenty-nine years. Floods, fires, the Kovač stack. Every time, the Ministry says it’s under control and we go in and it isn’t. Every time. And you know what? Every time, I believed them a bit. Right up until I saw it. Every time.” She shook her head. “I used to think I was an idiot for that. I’ve decided it’s worse than that. I’ve decided it’s the whole bloody country.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Lattice Station 14-C was a control booth for Conduit 14, and they found it an hour later by following Ben’s map.</p>\n<p>That was the thing. Cas had not been sure, until they found it, whether the map was real — whether a man in a room, however clever, however many years he’d had, could have reasoned his way from the branching of a filament to the shape of a thing he’d never seen. But Ben had drawn the Lattice’s stress lines, and where they ran, and where they crossed the conduits, and on page twenty-six of the sketchbook, in Cas’s pencil copying Ben’s grease-pencil, there was a circle around one conduit and three words: <em>14 — THROAT HERE</em>. And when they came round the curve of the Lattice’s inner gallery, there was the door. LATTICE STATION 14-C. CONDUIT 14 ACCESS. AUTHORISED PERSONNEL ONLY.</p>\n<p>And there was a lee. They felt it as they came through the door: the pull of the black wall let go of them, and the gravity weather, which had been gusting all through the Undercroft, simply stopped. The booth was a small, bare room with a bank of dead monitors along one wall and a big thick viewport in the other, and a hatch in the floor by the viewport, and a chair, and a water cooler, and a first-aid cabinet, and it was as calm and still and ordinary as an office on a Sunday afternoon.</p>\n<p>Teo dropped a washer. It fell straight down and bounced.</p>\n<p>“Station air,” said Cas, checking the panel by the door. “Pressure’s good. You could take the helmet off in here.”</p>\n<p>“Could I,” said Teo. She had gone to the viewport. “Hale. Come and look at this.”</p>\n<p>He went.</p>\n<p>The viewport looked into the bottom of Conduit 14. It was a vertical shaft, round, maybe six metres across, lined with black keelstone and ribbed with the brackets of old cooling pipes that had been stripped out long ago. A steel ladder went up one side of it. The shaft went up and up, lit by the pearly glow, and at the top of it, very far up, there was a disc of light that was brighter than anything they’d seen, a pale, hard, violet-white.</p>\n<p>And about forty metres up the shaft, something was happening to the air.</p>\n<p>It trembled. That was the only word. It was like the shimmer over the Pit, the column of moving air they’d seen from the rim; but here it was close, and dense, and you could see it for what it was. A layer. A horizontal curtain across the shaft, a few metres thick, where the air was — <em>wrong</em>. Where things seen through it jumped and smeared and doubled. Where the ladder, going up through it, seemed to bend and break and join again, as though you were looking at it through moving water, or through the heat of a fire, or through tears.</p>\n<p>“The Shear,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“A hole in the dam,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>He could see it now, the whole shape of it, the way he’d seen it in the locker-room glass. The Lattice was holding the entire difference between the core and the Works, sitting in its keelstone the way the drop had sat in that little square window. And here, where the conduit went through, the difference had nowhere to be but in the air.</p>\n<p><em>The whole Vault’s a blister</em>, he thought. <em>And we’re standing at the door.</em></p>\n<p>Cas looked at his visor display. He’d dropped a relay puck at the booth door; the suit was comparing its own clock with the puck’s, with the one before, with the Collar’s. LOCAL RATIO: 1.9.</p>\n<p>He looked up at the trembling curtain. If Ben’s map was right — if everything he’d guessed in the crisis room was right — then on the far side of it the ratio wasn’t two. It was hundreds. It might be thousands.</p>\n<p>“Forty metres,” he said. “From two to — I don’t know. Seven hundred? Across forty metres.”</p>\n<p>“And the keel spreads it over one.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And yours is broken.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” said Teo. “Look down.”</p>\n<p>He looked down.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the shaft, on the floor of the conduit just beyond the viewport, lying in a heap against the foot of the ladder, was a body.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They opened the floor hatch and went down to it, and Teo knelt beside it with her light, and for a long time neither of them said anything at all.</p>\n<p>It had fallen. That much was clear. It lay crumpled at the foot of the ladder, the way a body lies when it’s come down a long way. It was very small — shorter than Teo — and very broad; even in death, even ruined, you could see the width of the shoulders, the thickness of the bones.</p>\n<p>And it was ruined in a way that made no sense.</p>\n<p>The legs were bone. Clean bone, grey and dry and old, the clothes over them rotted to threads. The pelvis was bone. But from the waist up, the body was <em>leather</em> — dark brown, mummified, shrunken tight on the frame, like the bog bodies Cas had seen as a boy in the Museum of the Long Heat — and the face was worse, because the face was not quite either. The left side of it was dry skin over a skull. The right side, from the cheekbone to the chin, was — Cas made himself look — was still almost a face. Wasted, sunken, grey. But skin. Hair. An eyelid.</p>\n<p>“It went through,” Cas said. His own voice sounded strange to him. “Through the Shear. Coming down. Different parts of it spent different amounts of time in there. It was on the ladder, it — no.” He stopped. He made himself think. He looked at the legs, and then at the face. “It fell. It came off the ladder and turned over, and it came through head first. The head came <em>out</em> first. Into the slow side, down here. The legs were still up in the fast side. For a second, maybe. Maybe less. The legs lived — years? Longer? — while the head lived a second.” He heard himself. “It was dead before it got here. It was dead before it finished falling.”</p>\n<p>Teo said nothing.</p>\n<p>“But that’s not⁠—” He stopped again.</p>\n<p>“That’s not what’s wrong,” said Teo. “Is it.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>She reached out with her gauntlet and, very gently, touched the clothes.</p>\n<p>They weren’t station clothes. That was what was wrong. They weren’t a jumpsuit or overalls or a lab coat or anything issued by the Ministry to anyone who had ever worked in the Draw. They were a sort of loose tunic, belted, and trousers, and they had been made. By hand. You could see the stitching — big, careful, uneven stitches, the kind you’d make with a needle you’d filed yourself, and the thread was something coarse, twisted from fibres. And the cloth itself was a patchwork. Squares and strips of a dozen different fabrics: orange high-vis, grey jumpsuit cotton, a white that might once have been a lab coat, a blue that might once have been a curtain, all sewn together, and patched, and re-patched, and patched again over the patches, as if the garment had been worn and mended for years. For decades. By more than one person.</p>\n<p>Around the neck, on a cord of the same twisted fibre, hung a little ornament.</p>\n<p>Teo lifted it with her gauntlet. It was yellow plastic — the thick, glossy plastic of an old-style hard hat — carved into a shape: a tiny, flat, stylised flame, or a lamp, with rays around it. Somebody had carved it very carefully with a knife, and then it had been handled — held, rubbed, worried between finger and thumb — for so long that all the edges were worn smooth and round and the yellow had gone pale and soft as old soap.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” said Teo, very quietly. “Who is this?”</p>\n<p>He had been doing arithmetic since they came through the hatch. He’d been doing it without meaning to, the way he always did, the way he’d done it in the crisis room with Rask telling him to stop. The body had fallen down the conduit. So it had come from above. From inside. From the Vault. It had come through the Shear. It wore clothes that had been sewn by hand, from station fabric, and worn and mended for longer than the station had been broken. It wore a lamp carved from a hard hat. Its bones were thick. Its bones were very, very thick, and it was very small and very broad, the way a person might grow up — the way a <em>child</em> might grow up — somewhere the gravity was high.</p>\n<p><em>If anyone survived in there</em>, he had said to Rask, <em>they could have been alive in there longer than the Commonweal has existed.</em></p>\n<p>“They lived,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Who did?”</p>\n<p>“The night shift. Some of them. In the Vault. They lived. They had air — the Vault’s got air, it’s a sealed volume, it’s got scrubbers. Water. Light. They lived.” He looked at the patched sleeve, the fine careful stitches of someone who had had all the time in the world to learn to sew. “And they had children. And the children had children. And somebody — decades on, for them — got dressed in the clothes their grandmother mended and hung this round their neck and came down a ladder to find out what was at the bottom.”</p>\n<p>Teo was very still.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” she said. “What’s in there?”</p>\n<p>He looked up the shaft, at the trembling curtain of the Shear, and above it, the far bright disc of light.</p>\n<p>“People,” he said. “Or there were.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took her about ten minutes to decide. It took him most of those ten minutes to realise she’d decided.</p>\n<p>He was checking his keel. He’d found a way to see into the crack in the housing with his helmet light turned down, and through it he could see the rim of the rotor going round, a black blur edged with a thin bright line where it was catching the light, and every time it came round there was the tiniest flicker — a wobble, a stutter, the bright line jumping. He counted the stutters. He timed them against the keel’s note. The note had dropped since the shaft. He could hear it had dropped.</p>\n<p>He did the arithmetic. He didn’t like it.</p>\n<p>“How long,” said Teo behind him.</p>\n<p>“Four hours. Five. Before the bubble’s too small to matter.”</p>\n<p>“And in the Shear?”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“In the Shear, Hale. Forty metres of it. Through the throat, on the map, where it’s gentlest. With a keel with a crack down the side and a wobble in the bearing. Going up.”</p>\n<p>“The throat’s gentler. Ben said⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Ben said <em>gentler</em>. Ben didn’t say gentle.” Her voice was very calm. “If that bearing goes in there, what happens?”</p>\n<p>He closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>“The rotor comes loose,” he said. “It’s eleven kilos of keelstone at two hundred thousand revs. It goes through the housing and the suit and me and the conduit wall and probably the Lattice. And the bubble goes with it. So the Shear gets what’s left.” He opened his eyes. “Either way, I’d be dead before I noticed.”</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “So you’re taking mine.”</p>\n<p>He turned round.</p>\n<p>She was standing by the viewport with her helmet off. He hadn’t heard her take it off. Her face was grey with tiredness and streaked where she’d been crying at the glass, and her cropped hair was flattened with sweat, and she looked older than she had at the gate and very much more certain.</p>\n<p>“No,” he said.</p>\n<p>“The housings come off. Farr showed me on the rig. They’re built to hot-swap, that’s what the spin rigs were for, you spin them on the rig and clip them on. Four clamps and a coupling. We take yours off and put mine on. Five minutes.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ve got a suit with no keel, in the Lattice, four kilometres from the gate.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got a suit with a broken keel, in a lee, with station air and a water cooler and a first-aid kit and a chair.” She jerked her head at the room. “I’ll stay here. It’s calm here. I’ll sit in the chair. I’ll be the most comfortable person in the whole bloody Draw.”</p>\n<p>“And when the lee moves?”</p>\n<p>“Then I’ll move with it.” She shrugged. “I’m good at moving. I’ve been doing it all day.”</p>\n<p>“Teo⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Listen to me.” She came across the room. She had to look up at him. “Listen. You’re the one who knows what’s up there. You’re the one who’s read the paper. You’re the one who knows what the Clause is and what the plate means⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I <em>don’t</em> know what the plate⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You know more than me. Which is everything. I don’t know how to turn a key in a Keystone. I don’t know what a Keystone <em>is</em>. I don’t know how to talk to people who wear their grandmother’s curtains. I can carry you. That’s what I’m for. I carry people out of things. And I can’t carry you through that.” She pointed at the viewport. At the trembling air. “So I’m giving you my keel instead. And you’re going to take it. Because if you don’t, you’re going to try to go through on yours, and you’ll die in there, and I’ll have to sit in this bloody chair and watch it, like I watched that boy. And I’m not doing that twice in one day, Hale. I’m not.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>He wanted to say: <em>it should be me who stays</em>. He wanted to say: <em>I’m the one who signed it. I’m the one who owes</em>. He had come in here — he knew it, he had known it at the window in the Collar, <em>at last</em>, <em>almost a relief</em> — he had come in here half hoping that something would happen in the dark that would settle it. He had been thinking, since the shaft, since the rattle in the keel, that perhaps this was how it would be settled. Quietly. In the Shear. Tidily. And he’d been thinking, underneath that, very quietly, that it would be fair.</p>\n<p>She saw it. He watched her see it. Something changed in her face, and she stepped in closer, and her voice dropped.</p>\n<p>“Oh, no you don’t,” she said. “No. I read your file. I sat next to you all day. I know that look. I’ve seen it on men on bridges.” She put her bare hand flat on the chestplate of his suit, where Petra Imre had put hers at the gate. “Guilt’s a lousy reason, Hale. It’s the worst reason there is. It makes you stupid and it makes you selfish and it makes you think dying is a thing you <em>do</em>, instead of a thing that happens. Die for something useful or don’t bother. You hear me? If you’re going to die in there, you do it because it saves somebody. Not because it makes you feel square. Nobody’s keeping score. Nobody ever was.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t say anything. He couldn’t.</p>\n<p>“Now turn round,” said Teo, “and let me get that bloody thing off your back.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took eleven minutes, not five.</p>\n<p>They did it in the middle of the booth, in the lee, with Cas on his knees and Teo behind him. She had to take her own suit’s back plate off first to get at the clamps — she did it by feel, swearing, with her helmet under her arm — and then his. The keels had to come off at full spin; there was no way to stop them. When the first clamp let go on his housing the whole thing <em>lurched</em> in her hands, gyroscopic, fighting her, and for a second it swung sideways and he felt the bubble around him go lopsided and the room went strange — the far wall stretching, the floor tilting, the light doing something he didn’t have words for — and Teo said <em>hold still hold still hold still</em>, and got the second clamp, and the third, and then it was off, and his back was cold and light and the world was just a room.</p>\n<p>She lowered it to the floor. It sat there, humming its wrong, rattling note. Through the crack in its side a thin bright line of light went round and round.</p>\n<p>Then she took off her own.</p>\n<p>She did it the same way, slower, more carefully, and when it was off she was standing in the middle of the booth with no keel at all, and for the first time Cas saw what that meant. The lee was calm — but not perfectly calm. Nothing in here was. Without the bubble around her, he could see her sway, very slightly, as if standing on the deck of a ship at anchor. He saw her put a hand out to the back of the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right,” she said. “Right. Hold still.”</p>\n<p>She clipped it onto him. Four clamps. The coupling. The suit took it with a thunk, and the note came into his spine — clean, high, true, without a rattle, the note from the hangar — and the room went steady around him like a held breath.</p>\n<p>He stood up. Teo stepped back.</p>\n<p>“Good,” she said. “That’s good. How’s that feel?”</p>\n<p>“Like yours.”</p>\n<p>She laughed. She put her back plate back on. She put her helmet on the chair, and sat down in it, heavily, and looked at him.</p>\n<p>“You’ll drop pucks,” she said. “One at the bottom, one at the top.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ll talk to me.”</p>\n<p>“If the radios⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You’ll <em>talk</em> to me, Hale. You’ll send me news. I’ll be sitting here in this chair with nothing to do, I’ll be bored out of my skull.” She tipped her head back and looked at the ceiling. “Farr said the — the ratio thing. If you’re up there in the fast bit and I’m down here in the slow, what does that do?”</p>\n<p>“Anything I send you arrives squashed. Ten minutes of me in a couple of seconds. You’ll hear it as a chirp. The suit’ll stretch it back out.”</p>\n<p>“Birds,” said Teo. “Farr said whales. So I get birds.”</p>\n<p>“And anything you send me arrives stretched. A minute of you would take — hours. Six, maybe. More.”</p>\n<p>“So you’ll be up there for weeks, and I’ll be down here for an hour or two.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment.</p>\n<p>“That’s all right,” she said. “I’ve waited longer than that for a relief crew.”</p>\n<p>He knelt down beside the chair so he could see her face. He didn’t know what to say. He had not had a friend in fifteen years, and he’d known her for a day and a half, and she had just given him the only thing she had that could keep her alive, and he did not know what you said to that.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Don’t,” she said. “I’ll cry, and then I’ll have to kill you.” She put her hand on the side of his helmet and held it there. “Go on, then. Go and find your people in their curtains. And when you’ve found them, you tell them there’s a woman down here in a chair who wants to hear all about it.”</p>\n<p>He put his arms around her.</p>\n<p>It was absurd. It was two people in armoured suits, one of them kneeling, trying to hold each other through a centimetre of plating and a helmet ring, clumsy as two statues embracing. She put her arms around him and patted his back plate twice, hard, the way you’d pat a horse. Then she pushed him away.</p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” said Teo Lisk.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He went down through the hatch and dropped a puck at the foot of the ladder, beside the body in its patchwork clothes. He looked at the little yellow lamp for a moment. Then he started to climb.</p>\n<p>It was a ladder. That was all it was, for the first thirty metres. Rungs in his gauntlets, the keel humming clean and high in his spine, the black curved wall of the conduit sliding past, the hammer coming up through the rails. He climbed on the back side of the ladder, pressed close to the wall, because Ben’s map said that was where the throat was — a narrow column of gentler gradient running up the conduit’s rear face where two stress lines forked apart. He could see the trembling curtain above him, coming closer. Through it the ladder seemed to kink sideways and back. Above it, very far up, the pale bright disc.</p>\n<p>“Talking to you,” said Teo’s voice in his ear. “Just so you know. I’ll keep talking till you’re through. Tell me when you’re there.”</p>\n<p>“Almost.”</p>\n<p>“How’s the view?”</p>\n<p>“Ladder.”</p>\n<p>“Very good. Very descriptive. They’ll put you on the radio.”</p>\n<p>He reached the curtain.</p>\n<p>He’d known it would be bad. He had done the arithmetic. He had not understood — because arithmetic can’t make you understand it — what it would be like to have a body in two places in time.</p>\n<p>It started with his hands. He reached up for the next rung and his right hand was <em>there</em> before he’d reached — he saw it, in his helmet light, already closed on the rung, already gripping, while he could still feel the motion of reaching in his shoulder. He stared at it. It was his hand. It was ahead of him. It was living a little faster than the rest of him and it had got to the future first. And then his arm caught up, and his hand was his again, and the next rung was above him and his hand was already on it.</p>\n<p>He climbed. There was nothing else to do. He pressed himself against the wall where Ben had said and climbed, and the keel’s note went higher and higher in his spine as it fought, and the world came apart.</p>\n<p>Sound went first. He heard his boot hit the rung below him, and then he felt his foot move to put it there. He heard his own breath in the helmet and then felt his chest rise. He heard Teo’s voice in his ear saying <em>⁠— still with me? —</em> and it was wrong, it was <em>slow</em>, it was sliding down like a record played at the wrong speed, <em>⁠— stiiill wiiith meee —</em>, and then he heard his own voice answering <em>⁠— yes —</em> and didn’t remember saying it, and then felt his mouth make the word.</p>\n<p>His heart. He could hear his heart. He could hear it the way you’d hear footsteps in the room above: first the beat, then, a moment later, the feeling of it in his chest. An echo, but the wrong way round. His blood was arriving at his head before it left his heart. Something in him understood that this could not go on for very long, that there was a limit to how much of this a body could be asked to do, and that he was coming up to it.</p>\n<p>The ladder went up through the curtain. The curtain was forty metres thick. He was halfway.</p>\n<p>His thoughts came finished. That was the strangest part. He’d be climbing, and a thought would be there in his head, whole and complete — <em>the rung is loose</em> — and only afterwards would he feel himself think it, feel the words assemble, feel himself notice the rung. It was as though some part of him up ahead in time were leaving notes for the rest of him to find.</p>\n<p>He put his hand on the loose rung. He’d known it was loose. He moved his hand to the next one.</p>\n<p>Teo’s voice was a groan now. It came in his ear like the lowing of some enormous animal a long way off, a vast, slow, falling note with no words in it, and he knew it was her, still talking, still saying <em>still with me</em> and <em>how’s the view</em> and <em>nearly there</em>, and he could not understand any of it, and he held on to the sound of it anyway, the way you’d hold on to a rope in the dark. It went down and down. It went below anything he could hear. It became a pressure in his ears. Then it was gone.</p>\n<p>And then — like coming up out of deep water — he was through.</p>\n<p>It was over as suddenly as it had begun. His hand closed on a rung and he felt it close at the same time. His breath went in and his chest went up together. His heart beat, and he heard it beat, at once, one thing. He hung on the ladder, his helmet pressed against the black wall, shaking so hard the exo was fighting to hold him, and breathed, and breathed, and breathed.</p>\n<p>He looked down. Below him, the Shear. Through it, the ladder going down, jumping and smearing. And far down at the bottom of the conduit, very small, the round lit window of Lattice Station 14-C, and in it, a figure in a chair.</p>\n<p>She wasn’t moving. She was sitting perfectly still, like a photograph. Like a statue. He watched her for a long time and she did not move at all.</p>\n<p>He took an orange puck from his belt and pressed it to the wall. It stuck.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said. “I’m through.”</p>\n<p>There was no answer. He hadn’t expected one. She’d hear him almost at once; to her, his whole climb would be a blur and a squeak. But even if she answered the moment she heard, her few words would have to crawl up to him — he did the arithmetic — over the better part of an hour.</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The conduit went on. Above the Shear it went up through the Anvil itself, through a plate of keelstone three hundred metres thick, and the climbing was different. There was no weather. There was only weight. It came on gradually and then it didn’t stop — a steady, crushing, <em>reasonable</em> weight, as if the whole world were pressing on him from above, patient and enormous. The exo groaned. His arms shook. Every rung was a decision.</p>\n<p>He climbed. He counted rungs, because counting was what he did. They were thirty centimetres apart; three hundred metres was a thousand of them. At a hundred, he stopped and rested with his arms looped through the ladder and the keel singing in his spine and his heart going like a fist on a door. At two hundred. At three hundred. At six hundred his arms were not his arms any more, they were two things he had borrowed, and he was using them anyway.</p>\n<p>The light above got brighter. It was not the pearly glow of the Works. It was something harder, whiter, bluer — a light like a welding arc, like the Lesion in the Crown’s window in the old footage, like the nail of light Iris Hollowell had driven through the world. It came down the shaft and lit the rungs white. He could not look straight up into it.</p>\n<p>And there was sound. He noticed it gradually, the way you notice rain. Coming down the shaft, faint, under the hammer — which was not a hammer any more, he realised, not in here; it had stopped being a once-a-second blow somewhere back in the Shear and become something else, a long, slow tremor that came up through the rails every so often and lasted and lasted — under that, from above, there was a sound he knew and could not place. A murmur. A noise with a texture to it, rising and falling. Voices? No. Too many for voices. Water? No.</p>\n<p>He climbed. Eight hundred. Nine hundred. The disc of light was very close now; he could see the rim of the conduit against it, a black circle, and something moving across it, some shadow, some shape.</p>\n<p>At nine hundred and sixty rungs his right hand missed.</p>\n<p>It just — didn’t close. He reached, and the arm that he’d borrowed didn’t have anything left in it, and his fingers brushed the rung and slid off, and for a moment he hung by his left hand alone with the weight of the whole world pulling on him and three hundred metres of keelstone and forty metres of Shear and two kilometres of Undercroft below him, and he thought, very clearly, the way thoughts had come in the Shear, whole and finished: <em>oh. Here, then.</em></p>\n<p>And a hand came down out of the light and took hold of his wrist.</p>\n<p>It was a bare hand. Small, broad, very strong, the knuckles scarred. It closed on his wrist above the gauntlet with a grip like a vice. And a voice above him said, perfectly calmly, in an accent he’d never heard, all the vowels flattened and the consonants clipped:</p>\n<p>“Don’t let go. I’ve got you. Pim — get his other arm.”</p>"}, {"file": "06-what-time-is-it-outside.md", "label": "Chapter 6", "title": "What Time Is It Outside", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": true, "words": 4223, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Remain at your station. Await relief. You will not be forgotten.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Placard, Section 9 (the Watch’s first scripture)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They got him over the lip of the well on his belly, like a seal hauled onto a rock, and then he was lying on his back on something hard and hot, and the light was so bright that his visor went black to protect him and he was blind.</p>\n<p>He lay there. He had no choice about it. The weight was on him like a second body lying on top of his own — heavier, much heavier, than it had been in the conduit — and every breath was a push against it. The exo was whining. His arms were two dead things. The keel sang in his spine, high and clean, and under it, coming up through whatever he was lying on, was the long slow swell of the hammer.</p>\n<p>Someone was touching his suit.</p>\n<p>Small, strong, quick hands. They went round the neck-ring of his helmet, pressing, feeling. They found the seals at his shoulders, and the joints at his elbows, and the coupling of the keel. They were checking him. They were checking him the way he’d have checked a suit himself: methodically, in order, the places where air could get out.</p>\n<p>“Don’t take the helmet off yet,” said the voice. The same voice from the well. “Breathe slow. The air’s thick here. You’ll want to open it slowly when you open it. Let your ears get used to it.”</p>\n<p>His visor was clearing. It was adjusting to the glare, dimming it by stages, and a shape came out of the white. A face. A woman’s face, bent over him, very close.</p>\n<p>“Can you hear me?”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. His voice came out of the suit’s speaker cracked and strange.</p>\n<p>“Good.” She sat back on her heels. “What time is it outside?”</p>\n<p>For a moment he didn’t understand the question. It was so simple. It was the question you’d ask a stranger at a bus stop. He looked at the display at the bottom of his visor, where the two clocks were — OUTSIDE, fed by the last good signal from the pucks; LOCAL, his own — and read it off without thinking.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours,” he said. “And a bit.”</p>\n<p>She closed her eyes.</p>\n<p>She was quite still for perhaps five seconds. He could see her lips moving very slightly, the way his did when he was doing sums. Then she opened her eyes again, and there was something in them he couldn’t read — not surprise, not fear. Something more like the look of a person who has been carrying a heavy thing for a very long time and has just been told how much further they have to carry it.</p>\n<p>“Then it’s sooner than they think,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Is he the Relief?”</p>\n<p>That was another voice, higher, breathless. A boy. Cas turned his head inside the helmet and saw him — a thin, pale, very dirty boy of about eleven, crouched on the other side of him with his hands on Cas’s arm and his eyes as big as coins. He had a rat on his shoulder. It was a fat, sleek, brown-and-white rat with a red cord tied round its neck like a collar, and it was washing its face.</p>\n<p>“Is he? Ione. Is he? Is he the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He’s a man in a suit,” said the woman. “Get his legs.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They couldn’t lift him. Nobody could have lifted him. But there was a cart — a flat wooden thing on four small iron wheels, with a long handle, the kind of cart you’d use to haul rope — and between them, with a lot of heaving and a lot of the boy swearing in an accent so thick Cas caught one word in four, they rolled him onto it. He lay on it on his back. The weight pressed him down into the planks.</p>\n<p>Then the woman stood up, and took the cart’s handle, and turned it so that his head was at the high end, and for the first time he could see.</p>\n<p>He was lying at the top of a well, on a kind of stone lip, a round platform of black keelstone with a waist-high wall around it made of scrap: sheet metal, bits of pipe, a stripped-out door, all bolted and wired and welded together. Beside him, in the middle of the platform, was the mouth of the conduit he’d come up — a black round hole six metres across with the top of the ladder hooked over its edge. Around the hole, someone had painted on the black stone, in white, in big careful letters that went all the way round like writing on a coin: WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT · WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT.</p>\n<p>And beyond the wall was Holdfast.</p>\n<p>He did not know it was called that yet. He didn’t know anything. He lay on the rope-cart with the weight on his chest and the sweat running into his eyes inside the helmet and he looked, and for a long time — he never knew how long — he forgot to breathe.</p>\n<p>It was a city.</p>\n<p>It went away from him across a plain of black stone as flat as a table, and it was built of everything. Of scaffolding: great towers of scaffolding pipe, bolted and lashed and welded, ten, twenty storeys high, with floors laid across them made of steel plate and planking and doors. Of containers: the big steel cargo boxes the Draw used to move parts, stacked in terraces, painted every colour, with windows cut in their sides and shutters hung over the windows. Of keelstone: slabs of it, black and shining, off-cuts and rejects from the Lattice’s manufacture, laid as walls and floors and roofs, so that parts of the city looked as if they’d been carved from a single piece of night. It was stacked and crammed and piled together, lane on lane, tower on tower, bridges and walkways and ladders running between everything, lines of washing strung across every gap. There were gardens. Green things grew everywhere, in tanks and pots and troughs and on the roofs, climbing up the scaffolding in thick ropes of vine. There was smoke — thin grey threads of it rising straight up from a hundred chimneys. There was noise: the murmur he’d heard coming down the shaft, and it was voices after all. Thousands of voices. A city’s worth of people talking, calling, laughing, hammering, a baby crying somewhere, somebody singing.</p>\n<p>And above it, rising out of it, the spindles.</p>\n<p>There were eight. He’d seen them in the old footage, in the mural in the crisis room, in schematics: the great maintenance pylons that ran from the floor of the Vault to the Rim four kilometres up. He had never understood, from a picture, what four kilometres meant. They went up out of the city like trees out of undergrowth — black latticework towers, each one a hundred metres across at the base, the city piled up around their feet like drifts of leaves — and they went up, and up, and up, narrowing, until they were lost in the glare.</p>\n<p>And the glare⁠—</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<p>The Lesion hung at the top of the sky.</p>\n<p>It was a point. A single point of white-violet light, too hard and too bright to look at, like looking at the sun through a pinhole. The eight spindles rose toward it on every side and ended short of it in a thin dark ring, far up, that must be the Rim. Beyond the Rim the Vault curved up and away on all sides — black, a vast black dome, a sky made of the inside of a stone — and it should have been dark, and it wasn’t. Because spreading out from the point of light, across the whole top of the sky, was the Lace.</p>\n<p>He had seen its threads in the Works. A hair of light crossing a storeroom. A root system spreading down a wall. This was the thing they had grown from. It spread out from the Lesion in every direction at once, an unending, branching, glowing filigree, finer than frost, larger than weather, filling the upper sky from horizon to horizon like the veins in a leaf held up to the sun. It moved. Very slowly, almost too slowly to see, it was moving — branching, reaching, growing — and in places its longest threads came down out of the sky in fine bright streamers toward the tops of the spindles, like the tendrils of some enormous luminous jellyfish trailing in the current. It was the most beautiful thing he had ever seen. It was the most frightening. He couldn’t decide which; his mind kept sliding between the two and couldn’t find anywhere to stand.</p>\n<p>The light it gave was violet and white and very even, like the light of an overcast noon, and there were no shadows anywhere, and it was warm. It was as warm as a summer afternoon in the southern tiers. Sweat was running down his back inside the liner.</p>\n<p>The hammer came.</p>\n<p>It came up through the stone under him, through the cart, through the planks, into his spine. But it was not the hammer. Not the blow, once a second, that he had listened to in his flat and in the Collar and in the Works for thirty hours. It was a swell. It began so gently he hardly noticed it — a faint rising pressure in the floor, like the first lift of a wave under a boat — and it rose, and rose, and went on rising, and at its height the whole city seemed to hum with it, every loose shutter faintly rattling, every scaffold pole faintly singing, the water in a trough nearby shivering into rings; and then, just as slowly, it fell away. It lasted — he counted without meaning to — about a minute and a half.</p>\n<p>He knew what it was. He had known it the instant it started. It was one strike of Ruth Tally’s hammer, arriving in here stretched.</p>\n<p>He looked at his visor. LOCAL RATIO: 703.</p>\n<p>Out there the hammer fell once a second. In here, one of those seconds would last seven hundred and three — nearly twelve minutes — and the blow itself, the instant of iron meeting keelstone, was the minute and a half he had just felt go through him.</p>\n<p>“There,” said the boy, softly, beside him. He had his hand on the wall of the platform, flat, feeling it. “That’s the Bell. Did you feel it? That’s the Bell.”</p>\n<p>“I felt it.”</p>\n<p>“There’s never been this many,” the boy said. “Not ever. Gran Perpetua says⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Pim,” said the woman.</p>\n<p>The boy shut his mouth.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She was standing by the cart’s handle with one hand on it, looking down at him, and now that his visor had cleared and his eyes had adjusted, he could see her properly.</p>\n<p>She was short. Everyone here would be short — he’d see that soon enough — but she was short even so, and broad and dense through the shoulders and hips in a way that made him think of the body in the conduit, of the thick heavy bones. She wore the same kind of clothes: a patched tunic belted at the waist, a rigger’s harness over it, loops of rope on one shoulder, heavy trousers with the knees reinforced in keelstone-grey cloth. Her forearms were bare and brown and roped with muscle, and they were scarred — pale lines across them, old burns, the marks of a lifetime of hauling rope. Her hands were the hands that had caught him.</p>\n<p>Her hair was dark and cut short, roughly, as if with a knife. On the left side, above the temple, there was a streak of white in it. Not grey. White. A single clean stripe of it running back from her forehead, as though someone had drawn a line there in chalk.</p>\n<p>And her face⁠—</p>\n<p>He could never, afterwards, describe her face well. He tried. He’d try for a long time. It was an ordinary face, broad-boned, weathered by the heat, and young, he thought — thirty, perhaps? — though the white streak made it hard to tell. It was not her face exactly. It was the way she looked at things. She looked at him on the cart the way you’d look at a view you had seen many times and still liked: patiently, attentively, without hurry, as though there were nothing he could possibly do or say that she hadn’t already seen, and she’d be interested to see it again. Her eyes were grey-green and very steady. They didn’t hurry. He had never seen eyes that hurried less.</p>\n<p>Around her neck, on a cord, hung something black.</p>\n<p>It was a key. A flat, dark, heavy-looking thing, the length of a finger, with a blunt cross-shaped bit. It lay against her tunic just below her collarbone. It didn’t shine. It seemed, in the shadowless light, to drink the light in. He looked at it and something moved at the very back of his mind — some shape he half recognised, some picture from a manual — and then his tired brain let it go.</p>\n<p>“What’s your name?” he said.</p>\n<p>She considered the question. “Do you need it?”</p>\n<p>“I’d like it.”</p>\n<p>“Ione,” said the boy, before she could answer. “She’s Ione. Ione Tally. I’m Pim. This is Steward Harrow.” He pointed at the rat. “He’s named after a Steward. They all are. Mine are.”</p>\n<p>“Pim.”</p>\n<p>“Well, he asked.”</p>\n<p>The woman — Ione — looked at the boy. Then back at Cas. Something almost like a smile moved at the corner of her mouth, and was gone.</p>\n<p>“You’ll be all right on the cart,” she said. “The weight will be bad for a few rounds. Don’t try to stand. Drink when they give you water. It’s clean.” She looked away, across the city, at something he couldn’t see. Her head was up. She was listening.</p>\n<p>He heard it too, then. A whistle. High and shrill, somewhere off among the scaffold towers. Another answered it. Then a third, closer.</p>\n<p>“Wardens,” said Pim.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” Ione let go of the cart handle. “They watch the wells. Someone will have seen us from the towers.” She looked down at Cas again, and there was something quick and serious in her face now. “Listen to me. There isn’t time for most of it, so listen. They’re going to call you the Relief. All of them. They’ll kneel down. They’ll cry. They’ll take you to the First Steward and he’ll call you the Relief too. Don’t let them tell you what that means.”</p>\n<p>“What does it mean?”</p>\n<p>“It means whatever they need it to mean.” The whistles were closer. “When they’re done with you — and they won’t be done with you for a while — find the Tollers. Ask anyone for Toller’s Tower. Ask for Perpetua.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those unhurried eyes. And he had the strangest feeling — a feeling he’d come to know well, over the weeks that followed, and never get used to — that she already knew exactly what he was going to say next, and was simply waiting, politely, for him to say it.</p>\n<p>“Because,” she said, “you came up out of the floor in a suit I’ve been waiting eleven years to see. And because you’re going to want to know how long you’ve got.”</p>\n<p>Then she turned, and put her hand on the boy’s shoulder, and said something to him too fast and too thick for Cas to follow. Pim nodded hard. And Ione Tally stepped to the wall of the platform, and put one hand on the top of it, and vaulted over it — lightly, easily, in that crushing weight, as though it were nothing — and dropped out of sight.</p>\n<p>“She’s Unwatched,” Pim told him, in a whisper, as if it were an explanation. “She can’t be here when they come. She’d get the Quiet.” He patted Cas’s gauntlet with a grimy hand. “Don’t worry. I’ll stay. I’m not anything. Nobody Quiets me. Steward Harrow, get <em>down</em> from there.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Wardens came up the ramp to the well-platform at a run — six of them, then ten — and stopped dead.</p>\n<p>They were big, for Holdfast. That was the first thing he learned about Wardens, and the thing he’d learn about all of them: they were the big ones, the men and women who’d grown a head taller than everyone else and twice as wide, and they wore it like a uniform. They had actual uniforms too: long coats of heavy grey cloth with a yellow lamp stitched on the breast, and on their heads — he stared — on their heads, hard hats. Old Works hard hats, cracked and mended and painted grey and polished to a shine, with the yellow lamp painted on the front. They carried staves: long poles of scaffold pipe with the ends wrapped in cord.</p>\n<p>They stopped at the top of the ramp in a ragged line, breathing hard, and looked at the man in the grey suit lying on the rope-cart beside the Drop well, and nobody said anything at all.</p>\n<p>Then one of them — the biggest, a slab of a man with a slow, square, heavy face and a grey beard cut close — took off his hard hat. He held it against his chest. And very slowly, as though his knees didn’t quite want to, he knelt down on the black stone.</p>\n<p>One by one, the others did the same.</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said the big man. His voice was deep and unsteady. “Lamp keep us. It’s the Relief.”</p>\n<p>“It’s the Relief,” said the others, raggedly. One of them, a young woman at the end of the line, had begun to cry.</p>\n<p>“I <em>said</em>,” said Pim, to no one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They carried him through the city.</p>\n<p>Not on the rope-cart — the big Warden, whose name was Osk, sent for something better, and it came: a litter, a real one, a sort of stretcher-chair made of scaffold pipe and canvas, with poles for eight bearers. They lifted him onto it, suit and all, grunting, and it took all eight of them, big as they were, and they set off with him through the streets of Holdfast at a slow, heavy, lurching walk.</p>\n<p>He saw it from there, the whole way, lying on his back with the Lace above him and the city going past on either side. And the city saw him.</p>\n<p>Word had got there ahead of them. He never knew how; Pim, probably, who had vanished down the ramp the moment the litter came and was nowhere to be seen. But by the time the Wardens had carried him fifty metres from the well, there were people in the lanes, and by the time they’d gone a hundred there were crowds. They came out of doorways and down ladders and up from under walkways. They leaned out of container windows and hung off scaffold towers and stood on roofs. They were short — all of them, every one, men and women both, broad and stocky and heavy-boned — and they were pale, a strange sallow pallor under the violet light, and their clothes were the same patchwork of reused fabric as the dead body in the conduit, a thousand different mendings, every colour. They were silent at first. They stood and stared at the litter going by. And then someone — an old woman in a doorway — put her hand to her mouth and said, “<em>Relief</em>,” in a cracked voice, and it went through the crowd like wind through grass.</p>\n<p><em>Relief. Relief. It’s the Relief.</em></p>\n<p>They began to kneel. Not all of them. But many. They knelt in the lanes as he went by, and put their hands on their chests, or touched their foreheads to the ground, or held up children to look at him. They wept. Some of them sang: a thin, wavering, old-fashioned melody that he felt he almost knew. Some of them reached out, as the litter passed, just to touch the side of it, or the edge of his suit. The Wardens let them.</p>\n<p>He had never been so frightened in his life. Not in the Shear. Not on the gallery floor.</p>\n<p>He tried to look at things. It was the only defence he had. He looked at the lanes: narrow, twisting, paved in black keelstone polished smooth by a century of feet. He looked at the signs. There were signs everywhere, and he knew them — he had walked past them as a young man, on placement and on survey. STAIRWELL C. MUSTER POINT B. NO NAKED FLAMES. KEEP THIS DOOR CLOSED. They were station signs, the old enamelled plates from the Vault’s walls and walkways, and they had been taken down and put up again as street names: a lane of containers with a sign bolted over its entrance that read CORRIDOR 9 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL ONLY, and a crossroads marked with a big yellow EMERGENCY ASSEMBLY AREA plate that had been lovingly repainted so many times the letters stood up from it in ridges. He looked at the shrines: little niches in walls, with a lamp in them — an actual lamp, a clay dish of oil with a wick — and a picture behind it, painted, of a point of light with rays. He looked at a rat pen: a big wire cage built into the angle of two walls, full of fat, sleek, contented rats, dozens of them, with a sign over it that said, in careful hand-painting, HOLDFAST’S OTHER CITIZENS — PLEASE DO NOT FEED.</p>\n<p>He looked at the mural.</p>\n<p>It was painted across the whole side of a scaffold tower, fifteen storeys high, and the litter went right past its foot so that he saw it from below, rising over him. It showed a man. A tall man in a dark jacket, painted enormous, standing on a high place with his arm raised toward a white star in a violet sky. The man’s face was noble and grieving, with silver hair. And in his raised hand he was holding up a key. A small black key, the length of a finger, on a broken chain. Beneath the man, in a crowd, painted small, were people in white coats and orange overalls, looking up at him and weeping. Across the bottom of the mural, in letters a metre high, it said:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THE GIVING<br>FOUNDER CRANE GAVE THE KEY TO THE LAMP<br>THAT IT MIGHT NEVER BE PUT OUT</div>\n<p>Cas stared at it until the litter carried him past.</p>\n<p>The key was black. It was the length of a finger. It had a blunt, cross-shaped bit.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Steward’s House stood at the foot of Spindle One.</p>\n<p>He knew it was Spindle One because it was the biggest; because the others were further off and it was right there, filling half the sky, a black lattice tower a hundred and fifty metres across rising straight up from the heart of the city into the light; because at its foot, where in the old schematics there had been a lift terminal — the great steel shed where the Crown’s lift came down — there was now a building. It was the only building he’d seen in Holdfast that looked as if it had been built all at once, on purpose. It was made of keelstone slabs laid with great care, black and shining, with a wide stair up to a doorway and tall narrow windows with coloured glass in them — bottle glass, he’d find out later, and lamp lenses, and the glass from old instrument dials, melted and set in lead. Over the door, carved into the stone, was the lamp with its rays.</p>\n<p>They carried him up the stair and through the door into a long, high, cool hall, shadowed, lit by oil lamps, and set the litter down. The Wardens stepped back. The door closed behind them, and the noise of the crowd outside, which had been rising all the way here like a tide, dropped to a murmur.</p>\n<p>A man was waiting for him.</p>\n<p>He was old. Sixty, perhaps, or more — it was hard to tell with Holdfast faces. He was taller than most of them, lean where the others were broad, and he wore a long coat of plain dark cloth, very well mended, and no hard hat. His hair was silver, and there was a lot of it, swept back from a high forehead. He had a long, grave, intelligent face. It was, Cas realised with a small cold shock, a face he had seen before. He’d seen it a quarter of an hour ago, fifteen storeys high, holding up a key.</p>\n<p>The old man came and stood beside the litter. He looked down at Cas without speaking. At the suit, the keel, the visor. At the Ministry crest, stencilled on the shoulder plate, which Cas had never noticed until he saw the man’s eyes go to it and stop there and go very still.</p>\n<p>Then the man smiled. It was a tired, careful, courteous smile, and there was something underneath it that Cas couldn’t read at all.</p>\n<p>“Welcome to Holdfast,” said First Steward Halloran Crane. “Relief. You’re a hundred and sixty-eight years late.”</p>"}, {"file": "07-remain-at-your-station.md", "label": "Chapter 7", "title": "Remain at Your Station", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4844, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Who keeps the Watch? — We keep the Watch. — Until? — Until Relief. — And when Relief comes? — Then we are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Common Office, as said in the Commons every seventh round</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They got him out of the suit in a small stone room at the back of the Steward’s House, and it nearly killed him.</p>\n<p>In the suit, with the exo taking most of it, the weight had been a crushing thing but a bearable one. Out of it — in the grey liner, standing on his own legs on the black stone floor — it was something else. It was like being filled with sand. His arms hung off his shoulders like buckets. His knees shook. His heart, which had been going hard since the ladder, went harder, labouring to push blood that weighed half as much again as it should up into a head that felt as though it were being slowly pressed into his neck. He took two steps and his vision went grey at the edges and the big Warden, Osk, caught him under the arms and lowered him into a chair like a man putting down a sack of cement.</p>\n<p>“Slow,” said Osk. “Slow. Everyone’s slow the first rounds. Even babies.”</p>\n<p>“Babies?”</p>\n<p>“From the Crèche.” Osk had a voice like gravel being poured. “Born heavy, we are, but you still have to learn to stand in it. Takes a year.” He considered Cas. “Take you longer, maybe.”</p>\n<p>They brought him water in a tin cup. It was warm and tasted of iron and something green, and it was the best thing he had ever drunk. They brought him food: a flat, dense, dark-green cake, like a biscuit or a very heavy bread, with a smell of pond and salt. He looked at it.</p>\n<p>“Greenbread,” said Osk. “Algae. Cricket flour. Salt. Eat it slow.”</p>\n<p>He ate it slow. It tasted of the sea, if the sea had been baked. He ate all of it.</p>\n<p>The suit stood in the corner, empty and hulking, the keel humming quietly to itself on its back. Osk kept looking at it. Everyone who came into the room — and a lot of people came into the room, Wardens and Stewards in grey and black coats, on one pretext or another — looked at it, and then at him, and then looked away quickly, as though they had seen something they weren’t meant to.</p>\n<p>Then Halloran Crane came in, and everyone else went out, except Osk, who stood by the door with his hard hat under his arm and his eyes on the floor.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Crane sat down opposite him. He had brought a lamp — a real lamp, a clay dish of oil with a floating wick — and he set it on the table between them, though the room was lit perfectly well by the violet glow through the window. He folded his hands. They were long, fine, old hands, with an ink stain on the middle finger of the right.</p>\n<p>“You’ll forgive me,” he said, “if I’m not quite sure how to begin. There are protocols for this. There is, in fact, an entire liturgy. I’ve read it many times. I’ve never expected to use it.” He smiled slightly. “My grandfather used to say that it had been written by people who never really believed it would be needed, for people who never really believed it would come. I find myself unable to recall a single word of it.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing. He didn’t know what to say.</p>\n<p>“So instead,” said Crane, “I’ll ask you something simple. What is the Outside?”</p>\n<p>“The Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Where you came from. Below the Drop. Beyond it.” Crane’s voice was quite steady. “Our histories say that when the Lamp was kindled, the world beyond the Watch was burned. That the Founders were the last of it. That we keep the Watch for a world that is gone, until Relief comes out of the dark. I’ve always found that a very beautiful idea. I’ve never been quite sure I believed it.” He looked at the suit in the corner, at the stencilled crest on its shoulder. “So. What is it?”</p>\n<p>“It’s a city,” said Cas. “It’s called Averna. It’s big. Six hundred million people. It’s where the Draw — where this place — sends its power. It’s⁠—” He stopped. He thought of the Tier Nine arterial at dawn, the bunting, the anthem. “It’s fine. It’s still there. Nothing’s burned.”</p>\n<p>“And how long,” said Crane, “since the Kindling? Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours.”</p>\n<p>Crane didn’t move.</p>\n<p>He was so still that for a moment Cas wondered if he’d heard. He sat with his long hands folded on the table and his eyes on the little flame of the lamp, and nothing in his face changed, nothing at all, and the stillness went on and on until it became a thing in the room, a thing you could feel pressing on you like the weight.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours,” Crane said at last.</p>\n<p>“And a bit.”</p>\n<p>“A day. A day and a half.” Crane closed his eyes. “My grandfather’s grandfather’s grandfather was born in this city. And lived here, and died here, and was given to the Tanks. So was his son. So was his. And outside⁠—” He opened his eyes. “Outside, it’s still the same night.”</p>\n<p>“Nearly.”</p>\n<p>“Nearly.” He breathed out. It was almost a laugh. “The Tollers will be very pleased with themselves. They’ve always said so. In a way that one could, if one was careful, pretend not to hear.” He looked at Cas. “Is there anyone else? Coming after you?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Is there relief?”</p>\n<p>Cas thought of Rask. The crisis room. Three suits. Ben in the hall of algae. Teo in a chair.</p>\n<p>“There’s me,” he said.</p>\n<p>Crane nodded slowly, as if that were the answer he’d expected. Then he stood up.</p>\n<p>“Come,” he said. “I’d like to show you something. Osk, help the Relief. Slowly.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The chapel was at the heart of the Steward’s House, a small square room of black keelstone with no windows, lit by a single lamp that burned in a niche at the far end. There was nothing else in it. No benches, no altar, no pictures. Only the lamp, and behind the lamp, set into the wall in a frame of polished keelstone as though it were the most precious object in the world, a sheet of yellowed laminate.</p>\n<p>Cas knew it before he could read it. He’d walked past it at Gate Nine a day and a half ago. He’d walked past a hundred of its twins, years ago, when he was still allowed in.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SECTION 9 — IN THE EVENT OF CONTAINMENT LOSS:<br>REMAIN AT YOUR STATION AND AWAIT RELIEF.<br>DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE.<br>RELIEF CREWS WILL BE DISPATCHED.<br>YOU WILL NOT BE FORGOTTEN.</div>\n<p>The stick figure beside its stick console. The Ministry typeface from the Year Twenties. Someone had once drawn a moustache on the stick figure at Gate Nine; nobody had ever drawn anything on this one. It had been cleaned — lovingly, with soft cloths, for generations — until the laminate had gone as clear and pale as horn.</p>\n<p>“The Placard,” said Crane quietly. “Our first scripture. It was on the wall of the assembly bay the night of the Kindling. The Founders gathered under it. Nineteen hundred of them. The Lamp had just been lit — the sky had just filled with light — the Drop had just taken the first of them who tried to go down. And Founder Crane stood under this and read it aloud to them. <em>Remain at your station and await relief.</em> And they did.” He looked at it. “They’ve been doing it ever since.”</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“You’re thinking it’s an evacuation notice,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>“It <em>is</em> an evacuation notice.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.” Crane didn’t seem offended. If anything, he seemed faintly amused, in a tired way. “My ancestor was a practical man. He needed something to hold nineteen hundred terrified people together in the dark, and he needed it within a week. This was on the wall. It had the great advantage of being true.” He turned his head. “Look at the floor.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked. Running along the base of the chapel wall and out through the door, painted onto the black stone in gold leaf, worn almost away by feet in places and repainted over the wear so many times that it stood up from the floor like a ridge of metal, was a line of arrows. He knew them too. They were the arrows from the Vault’s walkways, the little painted chevrons that showed you the way to your muster point in an emergency.</p>\n<p>“They lead to Muster Point B,” said Crane. “In the Commons. It’s the holiest place in Holdfast. Pilgrims come to it on their knees on the seventh round. It is the place, you see, where they were told to wait.” He paused. “My ancestor had a sense of humour. I think it was the only thing that kept him alive.”</p>\n<p>Cas made a sound. He didn’t know whether it was a laugh or something else. Crane looked at him with that tired, courteous half-smile.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Crane. “I’ve often felt that way myself.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Back in the small stone room, with the lamp on the table and a fresh cup of iron-tasting water, Crane told him the history.</p>\n<p>He told it well. He’d told it, Cas guessed, many times, to many children, in the Commons on high days. The Kindling: the night the Lamp was lit, the Founders at their posts, the great light filling the sky. Founder Crane in the Crown, who saw that the Lamp must never go out, and gave the key into it so that it never could. The Descent: nineteen hundred souls coming down the spindles into the Vault as the world beyond burned. The Drop, which took all those who tried to go down after it. The Sealing — the dark days when they understood they could not leave. The Watch: the Founders’ vow to keep their station. The Crèche, the first children born under the Lamp. The Tanks, where the dead went to feed the living. The Tallies, the Bell, the rounds and the shifts. A hundred and sixty-eight years. Seven generations. Thirty-one thousand souls.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand, four hundred and six, at the last count. Four hundred and seven, now.” Crane inclined his head at him. “The Founders were fruitful. They had to be. There was so little else to do.” He folded his hands. “And now I’m going to ask you something, and I’d be grateful if you’d answer it as honestly as you answered the last.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>“The Bells are coming faster,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>He said it softly. By the door, Osk shifted his feet.</p>\n<p>“In the Founders’ time,” said Crane, “the Bell came once every three rounds. Once every nine shifts. You could go days without feeling it. In my grandmother’s time, once every two. In my own childhood, once a round. When I became Steward, three. Seven, eight years ago. Now there are more than a hundred. It comes while you’re eating. It comes while you’re sleeping. The Stewardship teaches that this is the Quickening — that the Lamp’s heart beats faster as Relief draws near.” He looked up at Cas. “And now Relief is here. So. The Quickening. What is it?”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at him for a long moment.</p>\n<p>“The Bell’s not the Lamp’s heart,” he said. “It’s an alarm. A hammer, down in the station, outside the — below the Drop. It strikes once a second. Outside seconds. My seconds. It’s been going since the Kindling. You’re hearing each stroke stretched out, because time in here runs faster than out there. That’s why a day and a half outside is a hundred and sixty-eight years in here.”</p>\n<p>“And it’s quickening because⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because time in here is slowing down. Coming back toward ours. The thing that makes time run fast in here is the Lamp. The Lamp is⁠—” He looked for words. There were no Holdfast words for it. He used his own. “The Lamp is a debt. Everything the station ever gave the city outside, it borrowed. From the — from the ground of things. The emptiness. And the emptiness always takes it back. Plus interest. For ninety years the station kept putting off paying it. The night of the Kindling, it stopped being able to. And now the debt’s being called in. Slowly at first. Then faster. The Bells are the sound of it coming due.”</p>\n<p>“And when it comes due?”</p>\n<p>“All at once,” said Cas. “At the Lamp. It’ll be — everything. This city, and the station, and mine, and the world.”</p>\n<p>“When?”</p>\n<p>“The Bells will tell you better than I can. My guess⁠—” He made himself say it. “Weeks. Your weeks. Maybe less. And the Lace⁠—” he nodded at the window, where the violet glow came in “⁠—the Lace is part of it. When it reaches the Rim, it’s over.”</p>\n<p>Crane was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” he said at last. “I thought it was something like that. I have thought it for some years.”</p>\n<p>“You knew?”</p>\n<p>“I have the Tollers’ numbers. The true ones. They come to me every seventh round, as they’ve come to every First Steward since the Founders.” He moved his hand slightly on the table. “I can read a curve, Relief. I’m not a physicist. But I can read a curve.”</p>\n<p>“And you haven’t told them.”</p>\n<p>“No.” Crane looked at him steadily. “I haven’t told them.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“I want to ask you for something,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>The lamp flickered between them. Outside the window, the long swell of the Bell began, rising up through the floor.</p>\n<p>“In an hour,” said Crane, “I’m going to have to take you to the Commons. There is no way to avoid it. Half the city saw you carried here. The other half has heard. They’ll come whether I call them or not, and it will be better if I call them. There are twenty thousand people who will stand in the Commons tonight and look at you.” He waited while the Bell reached its height and the shutters on the window hummed faintly and fell still. “They’ve been waiting for you for a hundred and sixty-eight years. Every one of them was told as a child that one day you’d come. And now you have, and in a few weeks, by your own account, they’re all going to die.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.” Crane accepted the correction gravely. “Maybe. So I’d like to ask you — when you stand up in front of them tonight — to tell them it will be all right.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“I’m not asking you to lie,” said Crane. “I’m asking you to be kind. They’re going to look at you and they’re going to want to know one thing, and it isn’t the physics. It’s whether they’re alone. Tell them they’re not. Tell them the Outside remembers them. Tell them you’ve come to help. Tell them to keep the Watch.” His voice was very gentle. “Let them be calm. For the time they have left. Let them spend it with their children instead of screaming in the lanes. Let them die, if they’re going to die, with the Lamp over them and the Relief among them and the Watch kept, instead of — the other way. I’ve read the old books. I know what the other way looks like.” He paused. “It’s the only mercy I have to give them. I’m asking you to help me give it.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the lamp. At the little flame, steady in its dish of oil.</p>\n<p>“Can it be stopped?” said Crane.</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>The old man’s eyes moved very slightly.</p>\n<p>“There’s a mechanism,” said Cas. “In the Crown. At the top of Spindle One. The woman who built this place designed it for exactly this. It won’t save the station, but it might — it might close the Lamp. Seal it. Before the debt comes due.” He hesitated. “It’s locked. It needs a key.”</p>\n<p>Crane had gone quite white.</p>\n<p>“A key,” he said.</p>\n<p>“A black key. About so long.” Cas held up a finger. “Keelstone. With a⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Our Founder gave the key to the Lamp.”</p>\n<p>“He dropped it.”</p>\n<p>The words were out before he could stop them. They sat on the table between them next to the lamp. Crane looked at them.</p>\n<p>“In Holdfast,” he said finally, very softly, “we say <em>gave</em>.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Commons had been Assembly Bay One. The sign was still over the great doors — ASSEMBLY BAY 1 — CAPACITY 2,500 — and someone had painted over the capacity with a lamp. It was a vast hangar of a place, the biggest single space in the Vault’s floor, where in the station’s working days the Lattice crews had mustered by the hundred at the start of every shift to be briefed and sent up the spindles. Its roof had been partly stripped for materials generations ago, so that it was open to the sky in long strips between the girders, and through the gaps the violet light came down, and the Lace.</p>\n<p>There were twenty thousand people in it. There were more outside, packed into the lanes around it, up on the roofs, hanging off the scaffolds. He could hear them before he saw them — a sound like the sea, like surf on a beach — and when the Wardens carried him in through a side door onto a raised platform at the front of the hall, the sound stopped, all at once, as though a door had shut on it.</p>\n<p>He was standing. He had insisted. Osk was at one elbow and another Warden at the other, and he was in the grey liner with a Holdfast coat over it that someone had found for him, and he was standing, and it was taking everything he had.</p>\n<p>The faces went back and back. A sea of them. Pale, broad, heavy-boned faces under the violet light, upturned, silent, every one of them looking at him. Men and women and children. Old people held up by younger ones. Babies. He saw a woman in the front row with a baby asleep against her shoulder and a little girl of five or six holding her hand, standing on tiptoe, straining to see. He saw an old man weeping without any sound. He saw Wardens along the walls with their staves and their hard hats. He saw, on the wall behind him, a big board — a sort of scoreboard, black, with white numerals painted on hanging plates — that said BELL 119,472 and under it INTERVAL: 12 and a word he couldn’t read.</p>\n<p>Muster Point B was a yellow-painted square on the floor at the foot of the platform. It was surrounded by a low rail of polished brass. Inside it, on the black stone, the paint was worn down to bare stone in the shape of knees.</p>\n<p>Crane stood beside him. He raised his hands. And the whole hall — twenty thousand voices — began to sing.</p>\n<p>Cas knew the tune.</p>\n<p>It took him a few bars. It was slow, much slower than he knew it, and the words were different — worn smooth, the way words get worn when they’re passed through a hundred mouths, then a thousand, then a hundred thousand; the rhymes had slipped and the lines had changed and whole verses had grown out of nothing. But under it, unmistakable, was the Tending. The anthem. The children’s choir version he’d heard on the Tier Nine lampposts at dawn yesterday, thirty-three hours ago, a hundred and sixty-eight years ago. <em>Tend the flame, tend the flame, for the ones who come after.</em> Here, it had become: <em>Tend the Lamp, tend the Lamp, till the Relief comes after.</em> Twenty thousand people, singing it very slowly under the Lace, in a hall where the paint on the floor had been worn through by knees.</p>\n<p>He had to hold on to Osk’s arm.</p>\n<p>When they’d finished, Crane spoke. It was a call-and-response, and the whole hall knew it.</p>\n<p>“Who keeps the Watch?”</p>\n<p>“<em>We keep the Watch.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Until?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Until Relief.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And when Relief comes?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Then we are relieved.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said Halloran Crane, into the silence, “has come.”</p>\n<p>The sound the hall made was not a cheer. It was something older than cheering. It went up into the girders and the gaps in the roof and out into the lanes, where the people outside took it up, until the whole city seemed to be making it. Somewhere at the back someone was screaming. People were kneeling, falling to their knees in waves, clinging to each other. The woman in the front row with the baby had her face in her free hand. The little girl was jumping up and down.</p>\n<p>Crane turned to him. He said nothing. He just looked at him, courteously, and stepped back.</p>\n<p>Cas faced the hall.</p>\n<p>He had meant to be vague. He’d lain on the bed in the small stone room while they waited, and planned it: a few careful sentences that wouldn’t be lies and wouldn’t be the truth. <em>I’ve come from the Outside. The Outside is still there. I’ve come to try to help.</em> All true. All nothing. He’d been good at sentences like that for fifteen years. He had a whole professional life of them.</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. And he saw the little girl in the front row. She’d stopped jumping. She was standing very still, holding her mother’s hand, looking up at him with an expression of such total, perfect, open trust that it went into him like a blade.</p>\n<p>“I’ve come from Outside,” he said.</p>\n<p>His voice was thin. It didn’t carry. But the hall was so silent that it did.</p>\n<p>“The Outside’s still there. It hasn’t forgotten you.” He swallowed. “I was sent to help you. And I — we’re working on it. There are people working on it. Keep the Watch. Keep it a little longer. It’s going to be⁠—”</p>\n<p>He stopped.</p>\n<p>The little girl was looking at him.</p>\n<p>“It’s going to be all right,” said Casimir Hale.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d been loved before. He supposed he had. Sabine had loved him, once, for a while, before the Review and the Standby and the silence. His mother had loved him. Nell loved him, in the fierce unforgiving way of ten-year-olds.</p>\n<p>He had never been loved like this.</p>\n<p>It came up at him out of that hall like heat out of a furnace. Twenty thousand people, weeping, singing, reaching toward him, calling out to him. A man at the front threw something up onto the platform — a little carved lamp on a cord, yellow plastic, like the one on the body in the conduit — and then someone else threw another, and another, until the platform was scattered with them. Mothers held up their babies. An old woman at the brass rail of Muster Point B was kissing the floor. Every face that he looked at looked back at him with a joy that was so complete, so helpless, that it didn’t seem possible it could fit inside a person.</p>\n<p>It felt wonderful.</p>\n<p>That was the thing he’d remember afterwards, lying awake. Not the guilt — that came later, and it came hard. But in the moment, standing there with Osk holding his arm and the whole of Holdfast pouring its love up at him like light: it felt <em>wonderful</em>. It felt like the warmest, easiest, most natural thing in the world. He had told them a lie and they loved him for it, and every nerve in his body wanted to stay there, in that, forever, and tell them another.</p>\n<p>That was the horror of it. That was the whole machine. He’d thought he understood the Commonweal. He’d been inside it all his life. He had never once, until that moment, understood why it worked.</p>\n<p>Then he saw her.</p>\n<p>She was at the very back of the hall, by the great doors, standing in a gap in the crowd with her back against the wall and her arms folded. Everyone around her was singing. She wasn’t. Everyone around her was weeping or kneeling or reaching up toward the platform. She was standing perfectly still, looking at him over twenty thousand heads, with the white streak in her hair and the black key at her throat.</p>\n<p>There was nothing in her face. No anger. No contempt. No disappointment, even — he looked for it, and it wasn’t there. There was only patience. That same unhurried patience he’d seen at the top of the well, the look of someone watching a scene they’d seen before, many times, and would wait through to the end.</p>\n<p>It was worse than anything else could have been.</p>\n<p>She met his eyes. And across the whole length of the Commons, over the singing, very clearly, she moved her lips. One word.</p>\n<p><em>Tollers.</em></p>\n<p>Then the crowd shifted, and she was gone.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They gave him a room in the Steward’s House. It was small and square, with a bed that was a wooden frame strung with rope and a mattress stuffed with something that crackled, and a window with a shutter, and a basin, and the suit standing in the corner humming to itself. Osk sat outside the door on a stool. Cas heard him settle there, and sigh, and heard the stool creak under his weight.</p>\n<p>He lay on the bed. The weight lay on him. He could feel his heart labouring. He could feel every one of his ribs.</p>\n<p>After a while he got up, which took a long time, and went to the suit, and took the little recorder out of its chest compartment. It was a slim grey Ministry thing, standard issue, with a crystal store that would outlast the suit. It had a channel that fed the suit’s radio, through the orange puck at the top of the well, down through the Shear, to another puck, and another, and a chair in Lattice Station 14-C.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor with his back against the bed, and pressed the button.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said.</p>\n<p>And then he couldn’t think what came after. So he just talked.</p>\n<p>“I’m through. I’m through, I’m alive, the keel held. Your keel. It’s — there’s a city in here, Teo. Up the conduit, on the Anvil. A city. Thirty-one thousand people. It’s a hundred and sixty-eight years old. They’ve got — they’ve got rats, they keep them, they name them after their officials. They’ve got a hymn that’s the Tending. They’ve got the Section 9 placard in a chapel with a candle in front of it. They thought I was a — they called me the Relief. They carried me through the streets. They⁠—” He stopped. “They asked me to tell them it would be all right. So I did. Within an hour. I stood up in front of twenty thousand people and I lied to them and it was the best I’ve felt in fifteen years.” He put his head back against the bed frame. “I don’t know what I’m doing, Teo. I thought I would. I don’t. I’ll send more. Are you all right? You won’t hear this for — I don’t know. Don’t answer. Yes, answer. I’ll wait.”</p>\n<p>He pressed the button again. He sat there.</p>\n<p>Then, after a while, he changed the channel. There was a second channel on the recorder, one that didn’t go anywhere. It just stored.</p>\n<p>“Nell,” he said.</p>\n<p>He sat there on the floor of a stone room in a city that shouldn’t exist, under a sky full of light.</p>\n<p>“You were right,” he said. “There’s one. There’s a star in here. It’s — it’s not a very nice one. It’s too bright to look at. They call it the Lamp. There’s a sort of lace round it, all across the sky, like frost, and it moves. Everything here’s lit by it. There’s no night. They close their shutters when they want to sleep.” He paused. “You’d have liked it. I think. You’d have been frightened of it, and then you’d have wanted to know what it was made of. That’s what I did.”</p>\n<p>He sat there a while longer, holding the recorder.</p>\n<p>“I’m still scared,” he told her. “You said that’s allowed.”</p>\n<p>He pressed the button, and put the recorder down on the floor beside him, and closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>He was nearly asleep when something small and hard hit the shutter.</p>\n<p>He opened his eyes. It came again: <em>tock</em>. A pebble. And a third time. He got up — slowly, slowly — and crossed the room, and eased the shutter open a crack.</p>\n<p>Below the window, in the narrow lane that ran along the back of the Steward’s House, there was a small, thin, very dirty boy with a rat on his shoulder, grinning up at him.</p>\n<p>“She says,” whispered Pim, “the Tollers want you. She says come now, while the Wardens are drunk. She says bring your notebook.”</p>"}, {"file": "08-the-tollers.md", "label": "Chapter 8", "title": "The Tollers", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 6793, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Let them count the Bells. Nobody ever died of counting. It’s drawing the line that does it.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally, Head Toller, in conversation</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">Holdfast at night was not dark. It couldn’t be. The Lamp never set, and the Lace never dimmed, and the violet light lay over the city at every hour exactly as it had lain at noon. But the city made its own night. At the end of second shift a horn blew from the top of the Steward’s House, a long low note, and all across Holdfast the shutters came down — clattering, banging, sliding, squealing on their runners, thousands of them, a sound like a whole city clearing its throat — and when it was over, the lanes were in shadow. Not darkness. A deep, violet, shuttered dusk, lit here and there by the little clay oil lamps in the shrine-niches, where the flames stood up very straight in the still, warm air.</p>\n<p>The Wardens outside the Steward’s House were not, in fact, drunk. They were asleep. Osk was asleep on his stool outside Cas’s door with his hard hat in his lap and his chin on his chest, snoring like distant machinery, and when Cas eased the door open and edged past him in the dark, moving so slowly that each step took a breath, one of Osk’s eyes opened.</p>\n<p>They looked at each other.</p>\n<p>Osk’s eye closed again. The snoring resumed.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Pim had brought the rope-cart. Cas lay in it, under a tarpaulin, and the boy hauled it through the lanes by its long handle, grunting, with Steward Harrow riding on his shoulder, and the cart’s iron wheels made a small patient rattle on the keelstone that echoed off the shuttered walls.</p>\n<p>“It’s not far,” Pim whispered. “It’s right by the Bell Column. Everyone knows where it is. Nobody goes, except Tollers.” He hauled. “And me. And Ione. I bring them rats.”</p>\n<p>“To eat?”</p>\n<p>“<em>No.</em>” He sounded scandalised. “To <em>keep</em>. Gran Perpetua likes the brown ones.” A pause. “Well. Sometimes to eat.”</p>\n<p>The lanes twisted and climbed and dropped. Once they went under a walkway where someone was sitting in the shadows, smoking something that smelled of burnt grass, and the someone said, “Pim,” and Pim said, “Brother Coll,” and that was all. Once a Warden went by at the end of a lane with a lamp on a pole, and Pim stopped dead and held his breath and the cart was still until the light had gone. Once, through a gap between two containers, Cas saw the Commons — its broken roof, the girders black against the Lace — and heard, very faintly, singing still going on inside it. Some of them hadn’t gone home. Some of them were still there, singing the Tending, in the middle of the night, for him.</p>\n<p>He shut his eyes under the tarpaulin.</p>\n<p>The Bell came as they arrived. He felt it start in the cart’s wheels, rising, and it rose and rose and went on rising, stronger than he’d felt it anywhere in the city, until the tarpaulin over him was trembling and the cart’s handle was humming in Pim’s hands. It didn’t fade after ninety seconds. It went on — lingering, ringing, a long reverberation that seemed to come up out of the ground and hang in the air around them like the note after a struck bell.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Pim, pleased, as though he’d arranged it. “That’s why it’s here. It comes up here first. You can feel it best here. Gran says it’s the only place in Holdfast where the Bell’s <em>honest</em>.”</p>\n<p>Cas pushed the tarpaulin back.</p>\n<p>Toller’s Tower stood at the edge of a small square, and it was the strangest building he’d seen yet. At its heart was a pillar — a single massive column of black keelstone, perhaps three metres across, rising out of the Anvil and up, bare and smooth, thirty metres or more into the air, where it ended in nothing. Some structural member of the old Vault, some support for a gantry long since stripped. Around it, clinging to it, the Tollers had built their tower: a scaffold frame that went up the column in a spiral, storey after storey, each floor a platform of plank and plate lashed to the pipe, each one hung with things. As Cas looked up at it in the violet dusk he saw pendulums — dozens of pendulums, hanging from the platforms on long wires and rods and chains, some of them swinging, most of them still. He saw great glass carboys of water on shelves, with pipes and taps and marks on their sides. He saw a huge wooden wheel, like a mill-wheel, turning very slowly. He saw, at the very top, a platform with a sort of long tube mounted on a swivel, pointing up at the sky.</p>\n<p>And he saw that the whole column — the whole three-metre, thirty-metre pillar of black keelstone, as far up as he could see — was covered in white marks.</p>\n<p>“Come on,” said Pim, hauling at the cart. “She’s waiting. She hates waiting. She says she’s done enough of it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The door at the bottom of the tower was open, and inside was a single round room built around the base of the column. It was lit by a dozen lamps. It was cluttered beyond belief — shelves and shelves of ledgers and papers and jars and instruments, a workbench covered in springs and gears, a pot of something steaming on a little stove — and it smelled of oil and paper and algae soup. There were people in it. Four or five, in the long grey smocks he’d come to recognise as the Tollers’, all of them turning to look at the door as it opened.</p>\n<p>And in a chair beside the column, with one hand resting flat on the black stone, sat a very small, very old woman.</p>\n<p>She was tiny. Even for Holdfast, even hunched in a chair, she was tiny — a little bent bundle of grey smock and white hair, her feet in felt slippers not reaching the floor. Her face was so wrinkled it looked like crumpled paper, and her hands were knotted like roots. But her eyes, when they came up to Cas’s, were black and sharp and bright as a bird’s, and they went over him from head to foot in about a second.</p>\n<p>“You’re the Relief?” said Perpetua Tally.</p>\n<p>“So I’m told.”</p>\n<p>“You look like you need a sit-down.” She jerked her chin at the Tollers. “Tobiah. Chair. Before he falls on my instruments.”</p>\n<p>A young man with round spectacles made of instrument lenses brought a chair. Cas sat in it. It was the most grateful he’d ever been for a chair in his life. Pim, behind him, had pulled the cart inside and was busy letting Steward Harrow down onto the floor, where the rat ran immediately to the old woman’s slippers and sat up on them.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Harrow,” said Perpetua, without looking down. “You’re getting fat.” Her eyes stayed on Cas. “So. Thirty-three hours.”</p>\n<p>“She told you.”</p>\n<p>“Ione told me what you said. At the well.” Perpetua’s hand moved very slightly on the column, the way you might stroke an animal. “Thirty-three hours and a bit. That was what you said? <em>And a bit.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Hmph.” She turned her head. “Tobiah. Last Bell.”</p>\n<p>The young man with the lens-spectacles went to a ledger on a stand by the column — a vast, thick, leather-bound thing, its pages dense with columns of figures — and ran his finger down the latest page.</p>\n<p>“Bell one hundred and nineteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-nine,” he read. “Interval eleven minutes fifty. Our reckoning, Outside⁠—” he did something quick with a slide-rule “⁠—thirty-three hours, eleven minutes, and twenty-nine seconds.”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours, eleven minutes, twenty-nine seconds,” said Perpetua Tally to the Relief. “<em>And a bit.</em>” She sniffed. “They send us a man from Outside after a hundred and sixty-eight years, and he can’t tell the time.”</p>\n<p>Cas stared at her.</p>\n<p>“You knew,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Of course we knew.” She lifted her hand from the column and waved it, a small impatient gesture, at the walls, the ledgers, the pendulums, the whole cluttered tower. “What did you think we were doing in here? Knitting?”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They had always known.</p>\n<p>That was the thing she told him, over the next hour, in the round room at the foot of the tower, with the lamps burning and the soup going round in tin cups and the Tollers perched on stools and shelves listening. They had always known. Ruth Tally had known — Ruth, who’d been a shift engineer, who had pulled the hammer herself, who’d understood exactly what it was and what it meant. <em>One Bell, one second</em>, Ruth had told her children. <em>We are living faster than the world. The Outside is not burned. It is only slow.</em> And her children had told theirs. And for seven generations the Tallys and those who married into them and those they took on as apprentices had counted every Bell, and marked it, and measured it, and kept the count in secret, and known.</p>\n<p>“Look at it,” said Perpetua, and pointed up at the column. “Go on. Look properly.”</p>\n<p>He looked.</p>\n<p>The white marks on the black stone were tallies. Strokes, in chalk and white paint and something scratched, in groups — he stood up, painfully, with Tobiah’s arm under his, and went close — in groups of five, the fifth stroke across the four. Rows of them. Twelve groups to a row: sixty strokes. And every sixty rows, a long ruled line right round the column, and a number painted beside it.</p>\n<p><em>1.</em></p>\n<p><em>2.</em></p>\n<p><em>3.</em></p>\n<p>He tilted his head back and looked up the column. Up and up, spiralling round it, rows and rows and rows of tiny white strokes, and every so often a ruled line, and a number. He could see them going up into the shadows of the upper floors. <em>15. 16.</em> Up past the pendulums. <em>22. 23.</em></p>\n<p>“Outside Hours,” said Perpetua. “Three thousand six hundred Bells. One hour, Outside. The first Hour took twenty-seven years. The second took twenty-three. The one we’re in now will take about a month. Ruth marked the first one herself, with a nail, in the stone. Tobiah — show him.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah held up a lamp to a place near the bottom of the column, just above head height, where the stone was dark and smooth and polished by hands. There, among the chalk strokes, was a line that hadn’t been chalked. It had been scratched. Deep, wavering, careful, a line gouged into keelstone — which nothing could scratch, Cas thought, and then realised that it had taken her years, years of going back to it and going over it and going over it with a nail, until it was deep enough to last.</p>\n<p>Beside it, scratched the same way, a single word.</p>\n<p><em>MADDY</em></p>\n<p>“Her daughter,” said Perpetua. “Outside. She was seven.”</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“We’ve been counting her night,” said Perpetua Tally. “All of us. For a hundred and sixty-eight years. That’s what the Tower’s for. Not the Bells. The Bells are just how you count it. We’ve been counting the night of a little girl called Maddy Tally who went to sleep in her gran’s flat on the Outside and hasn’t woken up yet.” She put her hand back on the column. “Thirty-three hours. She’ll be awake by now. She’ll be wondering where her mam is.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came.</p>\n<p>It rose up through the floor, up through the column under the old woman’s hand, and the whole tower hummed with it — the pendulums trembling on their wires, the water in the carboys shivering into rings, the lamps’ flames bowing — and it hung there, ringing, for longer than Cas could hold his breath. Every Toller in the room had gone still. They were counting. He could see it in their faces. Silently, all of them, the way people pray.</p>\n<p>When it had faded, Tobiah went to the column with a stub of chalk and made a stroke.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“The Stewardship knows,” said Perpetua. “Obviously. We send the true count to the First Steward every seventh round, and have done since Founder Crane. He knows the Outside’s alive. They’ve all known. They keep it quiet because the Founder’s Word says the Outside burned, and if the Outside didn’t burn then what are we doing here, and why haven’t we gone, and who told us to stay?” She sipped her soup. “Holdfast isn’t built on the Watch, Relief. It’s built on not asking that question.”</p>\n<p>“They let people count the Bells,” said Cas slowly.</p>\n<p>“Oh, they <em>let</em> people count the Bells. There’s the Public Tally in the Commons, big as a house, you’ll have seen it. Bell number, interval. Everyone knows the Bells are quickening. You can’t hide it. You feel it in your teeth every twelve minutes.” She put down her cup. “Let them count the Bells. Nobody ever died of counting. It’s drawing the line that does it.”</p>\n<p>“The line.”</p>\n<p>“Tobiah.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah brought a book. A big, flat, cloth-bound ledger, very old, and he opened it on the workbench under a lamp and turned it round so Cas could see. It was a graph. Hand-drawn, in ink, on paper ruled in faint squares, and it went on across the page and over onto the next and the next — a hundred and sixty-eight years of it, a column of intervals down the side and a row of Bell numbers along the bottom — and it was a curve.</p>\n<p>He knew it the instant he saw it. It was the curve he’d drawn himself on the backs of envelopes, fifteen years ago, at night, in his flat. It fell away from the top of the page steeply at first, and then less steeply, and then less, a long smooth swoop sliding down toward the bottom of the page — Bell intervals of three days, then a day, then hours, then minutes — never quite reaching the bottom, always getting closer.</p>\n<p>He took the sketchbook out of his coat. His hands were shaking a little. He found a clean page, and a pencil, and did the fit — three points, then five, then ten, off the Tollers’ ledger — and worked the numbers, and there it was. An exponential. A decay, as clean as a textbook, with a time constant of — he converted it — five and a half, six hours. Outside hours.</p>\n<p>“That’s the debt,” he said. “That’s it coming due. That’s the hill coming down.”</p>\n<p>“We don’t know what it is,” said Perpetua. “We know what it <em>does</em>.”</p>\n<p>“And where it goes?” He looked up. “Have you drawn it forward?”</p>\n<p>There was a small silence in the tower.</p>\n<p>“Seren drew it forward,” said Perpetua.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She told it quickly. She didn’t dress it. He thought afterwards that she had probably told it that way, quickly and plainly, many times, to herself.</p>\n<p>Seren Tally had been Perpetua’s daughter. A Toller, like her mother, but a better one: a genius with numbers, with a mind that could hold a curve and see where it was going. Seren had drawn the line forward. Seren had worked out that the Bells would come faster and faster, and at the rate they were going, sooner or later — in her daughter’s lifetime — would come so fast they’d run together into a single note. She’d measured the Lace with the sighting tube at the top of the tower, round after round for ten years, and found it was coming down: not steadily, but faster every year. She’d worked out that it would reach the Rim — the ring round the Lamp at the spindles’ tops — before the Bells ran together. And she’d guessed, without knowing why, that when it reached the Rim, that would be the end of everything.</p>\n<p>“She took it to the First Steward,” said Perpetua. “Halloran. He’d not been Steward long. He thanked her very kindly and told her it must not be spoken. She told him that people had a right to know how long they had. He told her that people had a right to be calm.” She turned her cup in her knotted hands. “She painted it on the wall of the Commons. The line, and the date. Big. In the night, with Ione holding the ladder. Ione was fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“And they⁠—”</p>\n<p>“They gave her to the Drop.”</p>\n<p>Nobody in the room moved.</p>\n<p>“It’s what they do,” said Perpetua, “for the ones who won’t keep the Watch. It’s very old. Founder Crane did it. It’s not an execution. It’s a <em>release</em>. You’re free to go. Down the well. Nobody’s ever stopped at the bottom.” She said it flatly. “Halloran signed it. I suppose he had to. He came to see me, after. He sat in that chair you’re in and he cried.” She looked at Cas with her black bird’s eyes. “I let him. I thought he’d earned it. It’s the only thing I’ve ever given him.”</p>\n<p>Cas thought of the body at the foot of the conduit. The patched, mended clothes. The little yellow lamp worn smooth by fingers.</p>\n<p>“When?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Thirteen years ago.”</p>\n<p>He did the arithmetic. He couldn’t stop himself. Thirteen years ago the Bells had come once every five or six hours; the ratio had been twenty thousand, not seven hundred. He ran it back along the Tollers’ curve. Nineteen hours, outside. Nineteen hours before he’d climbed the ladder in 14-C. She had gone down the Drop at about the time he’d been in a hangar in the Collar, learning to walk in a keel suit.</p>\n<p>“I think I saw her,” he said.</p>\n<p>Perpetua looked at him.</p>\n<p>“At the bottom of the conduit,” he said. “There was — someone. In mended clothes. With a lamp. Carved from a hard hat.”</p>\n<p>“Yellow,” said Perpetua. Her voice had changed.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>The old woman sat without moving. Then she took her hand off the column and put both her hands in her lap, and looked down at them, and her face did something he wasn’t ready for. It crumpled — the whole of it, the paper-crumple of it, all at once — and her mouth opened, and she made a small, dry, high sound, like a bird. Tobiah was beside her at once, kneeling by her chair. She didn’t look at him. She sat there with her hands in her lap and made that small sound, over and over, and the tears ran down the creases of her face and dripped off her chin onto the grey smock.</p>\n<p>“I gave her that,” said Perpetua Tally. “I carved it. When she was a baby. For her crib.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was Tobiah who brought out the Log, in the end. Perpetua had gone quiet, sitting with her eyes closed and her hand back on the column, and nobody had spoken, and then Tobiah had got up and gone to a shelf and taken down a box — a heavy steel box with a Works stencil on its side, very old, very dented — and put it on the workbench and opened it.</p>\n<p>Inside, wrapped in layer on layer of cloth, was a book.</p>\n<p>It was a Ministry logbook. He knew the type: A4, hard-backed, blue cloth, with the Continuance flame stamped on the cover and HOLLOWELL DRAW — CROWN — SHIFT LOG in faded gold. The station had issued them by the thousand. This one was swollen to twice its thickness, crammed with extra pages sewn in, bristling with slips of paper, its spine cracked and mended with thread, its corners worn to round. It looked like it had been opened ten thousand times.</p>\n<p>“Ruth’s,” said Tobiah quietly. “She had it in her hand in the lift. She didn’t know she’d picked it up.”</p>\n<p>Cas opened it.</p>\n<p>The first pages were the ordinary Crown log for the night of the test, in a neat engineer’s pencil. <em>01:50 Dep. Min. Crane + crew arr. for Deepening footage. 01:52 Advised Dep. Min. of T6 bearing temp (+11). Advised protocol requires Chief Eng. Advised interlocks.</em> He could see her in the pencil: the neatness, the care, the way she wrote <em>advised</em>. <em>01:58 Dir. Obi has deferred to Ministry. Proceeding under protest. Note: under protest.</em></p>\n<p>Then a gap. A blank line.</p>\n<p>Then, in the same pencil but a different hand — a hand that shook — <em>02:05 T6 unseated at Draw 135. Boundary failure. Interlocks failed. Draw would not zero. Time grad. across Crown, severe. Attempted Hollowell Clause. Dep. Min. Crane had the key and would not give it. At 02:09 approx gantry failed, key lost, fell out through window glass into the Lesion as if it were called. Clause not possible. 02:14 abandoned Crown. Pulled the Hammer.</em></p>\n<p>And under that, very small:</p>\n<p><em>I did it. I said 135. It was me.</em></p>\n<p>He turned the page.</p>\n<p>After that, it was a different book. The log format was gone. The pencil went on, but it was a diary now, a history, a record. The first days in the Vault. The Drop — the first men who’d tried the conduits, and what had come back up. Aldous Crane under the Placard. The Sealing. Names, lists of names, the living and the dead. Rations. Water. The algae. The first birth. Page after page, and the handwriting getting smaller and smaller as the paper ran out, until it was so small he had to hold the book up to the lamp to read it, and in places the pages were sewn in from other things: the backs of forms, the insides of packets, a sheet of pale thin stuff that looked like it had been made from algae pulp. The years went by in the margins. <em>Year 3. Year 7. Year 20, Bell 2,560.</em> He turned pages and turned pages. It was Ruth Tally’s whole life. It was all there.</p>\n<p>And there were letters.</p>\n<p>They came every few pages, all through the book, set off from the rest with a line drawn across the page. Every one of them began the same way.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy —</em></p>\n<p>He read the first one. It was dated <em>Day 4</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy — I don’t know if you’ll ever read this. I’m going to write it anyway because otherwise I’ll go mad. Mum is in a place a very long way away and I can’t come home yet. It isn’t because I don’t want to. There’s a big light here and it’s got stuck and I have to help. I’m sorry I didn’t say goodbye properly. I had to go to work early. You were asleep with your feet out of the covers. I tucked them in. Did you notice? Probably not. Be good for Nana. I love you more than the whole Draw. — Mum.</em></p>\n<p>He turned pages. <em>Maddy — today we planted the first beans.</em> <em>Maddy — the Bell only comes every three days now, and I’ve started to feel like it’s your breathing.</em> <em>Maddy — I got married today. His name is Tomas and he works the Anvil pumps and he makes me laugh. I know that’s strange. I hope you don’t mind. You’d like him.</em> <em>Maddy — you have a brother. I’ve called him Hal.</em> <em>Maddy — twenty years. For you it’s about forty minutes. I’ve done the sum a thousand times. You’re still asleep. I lie awake and think about you asleep, and it’s the only thing that helps.</em></p>\n<p>One entry, near the middle of the book — <em>Year 31</em> — wasn’t a letter. It was set apart from the rest by a line above and below, and it was short, and he read it twice without understanding it.</p>\n<p><em>I have never told anyone this. At 02:09, after the key fell, I saw a man at the Keystone. Kneeling where I knelt. Dressed close, in grey, like a diver. A cord knotted round his wrist. His hand on a wheel that isn’t there. He looked at me as if he knew me. He was so calm. Then there was no one. I have thought about him every day for thirty-one years. I think he was the witness. I think, whoever he is, he is still there.</em></p>\n<p>He turned the page. There were more letters.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t read any more. He sat with the book open on his knees and the lamp burning and his eyes blurred.</p>\n<p>Then he turned to the last page.</p>\n<p>The pencil was almost too faint to read. The hand was an old woman’s hand, shaking badly, the letters large and wandering because the eyes that made them couldn’t see small any more.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy — I am eighty-eight. Year 50. Bell 7,100. For you it is about two hours, so it’s still the middle of the night, and you’ve probably turned over and pushed the covers off again.</em></p>\n<p><em>I’ve had a whole life here, baby. It was a good one, mostly. I had your brothers and your sister and I had Tomas for forty years and I have eleven grandchildren and they count the Bells for me now because my eyes are gone. I made a lot of mistakes. The biggest one was the first one. I’m sorry for it every day.</em></p>\n<p><em>I want you to know I thought about you every single day. Every one. Nineteen thousand days, give or take. I never once let myself forget you were there, asleep, in the same night. It was the thing that held me up.</em></p>\n<p><em>If someone ever brings you this — and they will; I’ve told them to — then you’ll be seven, and I’ll have been gone a long time, and that’s all right. Don’t be sad. I had my life. You go and have yours. Be brave. Be kind. Tell the truth, even when it’s hard. Especially then.</em></p>\n<p><em>Love you more than the whole Draw.</em></p>\n<p><em>Mum.</em></p>\n<p>He closed the book.</p>\n<p>“She’s seven,” he said. He didn’t mean to say it aloud. He heard it come out of him. “She’s still seven. Right now. It’s the day after Continuance Day. It’s — she’ll have had her breakfast. She’s seven.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Perpetua Tally. She had opened her eyes. She was looking at him steadily across the lamplit room with her wet, bright, black eyes. “We know, Relief. We’ve always known. What we didn’t know — what we never had — was anyone who’d <em>seen</em> it.”</p>\n<p>She reached out, with a knotted old hand, and touched his knee.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” she said. “You’ve no idea. Thank you.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was then that Ione came down from the roof.</p>\n<p>He heard her before he saw her — a light, quick tread on the spiral stair that ran up round the column, coming down fast from the upper floors — and then she swung round the last turn and dropped the last few steps and was in the room. She had a rope over her shoulder and a notebook in her hand and chalk on her fingers, and she stopped at the foot of the stair and looked round at all of them: at Perpetua with her wet face and Tobiah kneeling, and the Tollers on their stools, and Pim with his rat, and Cas with the Log on his knees.</p>\n<p>“You’ve read it,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She came across the room and crouched in front of his chair so that her face was level with his, the way you’d crouch to talk to a child, or to someone sick. She looked at him for a moment with that unhurried, attentive gaze. Then she said: “The Lamp can be closed. There’s a lock in the Crown. I have its key.”</p>\n<p>And she lifted the cord from round her neck and held out her hand.</p>\n<p>The key lay on her palm.</p>\n<p>It was black. It was the length of a finger. It had a flat bow and a straight shaft and a blunt, cross-shaped bit, and it was dense — he could see it was dense, it sat in her hand heavier than a thing that size had any right to — and it didn’t reflect the lamplight. It took the light in and gave nothing back. Along the shaft, stamped into it in small square letters:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL · KEYSTONE · 0001</div>\n<p>He’d seen it in a mural, fifteen storeys high. He’d seen it in a pencil log, in the shaking hand of a woman who’d watched it fall. He’d seen it, he thought, somewhere else too — a picture in the training manual, a photograph in the back of Hollowell’s withdrawn paper: <em>the Minister’s key</em>. There had only ever been one.</p>\n<p>“That’s not possible,” he said.</p>\n<p>“No,” agreed Ione.</p>\n<p>“It fell. Into the Lesion. Ruth <em>saw</em> it.” He heard his voice rising. “Your Founder — it’s on a <em>wall</em>, it’s on a wall fifteen storeys high⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Then where did you <em>get</em> it?”</p>\n<p>“I found it,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it simply. Then she sat back on her heels and looked at the key in her hand, and a little of the unhurried patience went out of her face, and something else came into it. Something older. More careful.</p>\n<p>“When I was seventeen,” she said, “I went up. Past where anyone goes. And I got lost, up there, in a place called the Carousel. I’ll tell you about it one day. It’s not a story for tonight.” She turned the key over. “There’s a gantry in the Carousel. A walkway, across a gap. I walked across it — I don’t know. Four thousand times? More? I knew every bolt in it. I knew every mark on it. And one time I walked across it and there was a key lying on it. Right in the middle. Where it had never been, not one time in four thousand. And then it was.”</p>\n<p>Cas stared at her.</p>\n<p>“I picked it up,” said Ione. “I’ve had it ever since.”</p>\n<p>The floor of the round room seemed to tilt under his chair. It wasn’t the weight. It was something in his head. He was thinking of a paper he’d read as a student, an old, strange, half-joking paper from before the Long Heat, that his professor had assigned as a curiosity: two physicists asking what would happen if a time machine were real, and whether an object could go round a loop in time and come back to where it started. They’d called it a jinn. An object with no maker. An object that existed because it existed. That was never made, and never destroyed, and simply went round.</p>\n<p><em>You could find one lying on a walkway</em>, his professor had joked, <em>where it had never been. And you’d have no way to know where it came from. Because it didn’t come from anywhere.</em></p>\n<p>“A jinn,” he said.</p>\n<p>“A what?”</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” He didn’t take his eyes off the key. “Nothing. A word. Go on.”</p>\n<p>“There isn’t any more,” said Ione. “Except that I knew what it was, because I’d been watching the Crown. In the Carousel, you can see the Crown. The Crown on the night of the Kindling. For three years, I watched it.” She looked up at him. “I watched Ruth try to open the lock under the floor. I watched Founder Crane refuse to give her the key. I watched the walkway go. I saw the key on his chain. I know the lock. I know the plate over it. I could draw you the lock with my eyes shut. And I came down, two days later, with this in my hand, and I told them all: <em>the Lamp can be closed</em>. There’s a lock in the Crown and this is the key and if we get it up there we can put the Lamp out.”</p>\n<p>“And they called you mad,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“They called me Unwatched.” She shrugged very slightly. “It means the same.”</p>\n<p>“She’s not mad,” said Perpetua, from her chair. Her voice was hoarse but perfectly steady. “She’s the only one of us who’s ever <em>been</em> anywhere.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the key. Then at Ione’s face. Then at the key.</p>\n<p>“She isn’t mad,” he said.</p>\n<p>Every head in the room turned.</p>\n<p>“I’ve read the paper that lock was built from,” he said. “The woman who built this place — Hollowell — she wrote it. The last thing she wrote before she died. The Ministry buried it. It’s called the Clause. It’s a way to seal the core — the Lamp — if it ever broke the way it’s broken. Fold it shut. For ever. It’s in the Crown, under a plate in the floor.” He looked at Ione. “There are words on the plate.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Hollowell Clause</em>,” said Ione at once, softly, as if reciting. “<em>For use in catastrophic boundary failure only. The sealing requires a witness.</em>”</p>\n<p>“You’ve read it.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve read it over Ruth’s shoulder a hundred and seventy-five thousand times.” She held his eyes. “Do you know what it means? The last part?”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas. “Nobody does.”</p>\n<p>She nodded slowly, as though that too were something she had half expected.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Why haven’t you gone?” he said, later.</p>\n<p>It was very late. The Tollers had drifted back to their instruments, or to sleep on pallets in the upper floors. Perpetua was dozing in her chair with her hand on the column and Steward Harrow asleep in her lap. Pim was asleep on the floor under the workbench, curled round nothing. The lamps had burned low. Ione sat cross-legged on the floor with her back against a shelf of ledgers, turning the key over and over in her fingers, and Cas sat in his chair with the Log on his knees because he could not bring himself to put it down.</p>\n<p>“I’ve tried,” said Ione. “Twice. After I came back. I went up again at nineteen and again at twenty-three. By the south spindles, both times. Not by the Carousel. I wasn’t⁠—” A pause, very slight. “I wasn’t going back through the Carousel.” She turned the key. “There’s a place, above the Lees, before the Rim, where the Lace comes down. It’s lower every year. You have to go through it to get to the top of Spindle One. And the Lace kills you.”</p>\n<p>“How?”</p>\n<p>“The way it killed my mother, in the Drop.” She said it without any particular weight. “Everything goes different speeds. You come apart. I watched a rat go into it once, on a rope. It went in a rat and came out⁠—” she made a small gesture “⁠—dust, and a bit of fur, and something still moving. I turned back both times. You can’t get through the Lace without something to hold you together.” She looked at the grey suit standing in the corner where Pim had helped him prop it, the keel humming quietly on its back. “I’ve been watching the wells for eleven years.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“Because once, up there, when I was seventeen, I saw someone coming down.” She looked at him, and her gaze was very direct. “Far off. In a suit like that. With a thing on its back, humming. Coming down alone.” She tipped her head at the corner. “That suit. Or one like it. So I knew one would come. Sooner or later. Up out of the floor.” She almost smiled. “You took your time.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what to say to that. He found that he was thinking — in the part of his mind that never stopped doing sums — <em>someone in a keel suit coming down alone. From the Crown. In the future, seen from the past.</em> And under that, a cold slow thought he didn’t want to look at: <em>there are only three suits</em>.</p>\n<p>“You’re the first thing to come up through the floor in my lifetime,” said Ione, “that could get through the Lace.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ve got the key.”</p>\n<p>“And I’ve got the key.”</p>\n<p>“And you know the way.”</p>\n<p>“I know the way,” said Ione, “as far as the Lace. And I know the Crown. Every inch of it. I know which panel sticks and which lamp was flickering and where Ruth put her coffee cup down. And I know the lock.” She held up the key between two fingers. “You know what the lock does. You know the paper. You know what it <em>means</em>. I’ve watched it for three years and I don’t know what it means.”</p>\n<p>“Neither do I. Not all of it.”</p>\n<p>“More than me.”</p>\n<p>“More than you.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. He looked at her. Neither of them said anything.</p>\n<p>“Spindle Four,” she said at last. “It’s dead. Nobody uses it, nobody watches it. The Stewards think it’s cursed because the lift fell in the Founders’ time. It goes all the way up. I’ll need to go up it first, on my own, and look at the Lees. They move. They’ve moved since I was up there. Everything’s moved. And you⁠—” She looked at him critically. “You need to be able to stand up.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“For the Lees? Ten rounds. Twenty. For you to stand properly⁠—” She considered. “More.”</p>\n<p>“We don’t have more.”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione Tally. “We don’t.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She took him up to the top of the tower before dawn.</p>\n<p>It took a long time. He had to stop on every landing. She waited on every landing, not helping unless he asked, which he didn’t, and then did, and she put her shoulder under his arm without comment and took most of his weight up the last three flights as though he were a bag of rope.</p>\n<p>At the top was the platform with the long tube on its swivel. A sighting tube, she said. Seren had built it. It was pointed up at the sky at a steep angle, sighted on the top of Spindle Two, and there were marks scratched on the brass ring at its base — dozens of them, hundreds, each with a small number beside it.</p>\n<p>“Look,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He bent to the eyepiece. Through the tube he saw the top of Spindle Two, very far off, a dark lattice against the glare, and the Rim beyond it, a dark line. And coming down out of the sky above it, the long bright trailing threads of the Lace, like the tendrils of some vast drifting creature. There were crosshairs in the tube — two fine wires. The lowest tendril hung just below the crossing.</p>\n<p>“Every round, at dawn, I sight the lowest thread,” said Ione, “and mark it.”</p>\n<p>She showed him yesterday’s mark on the brass ring, and the day before’s, and the one before. He could see the pattern without her explaining. The marks crept down the ring. Close together, years ago. Further apart. Now each one was a clear finger’s width below the last.</p>\n<p>He put his eye back to the tube and looked at the gap between the lowest thread and the dark line of the Rim. He looked at the marks. He did the sum, and then he did it again with the marks spreading the way they were spreading. Sixty rounds. A few more, perhaps. Perhaps less.</p>\n<p>Then, because he couldn’t not, he turned it into his own time. A month of Holdfast to every hour outside, Perpetua had said, and the months getting shorter. Sixty rounds in here was about two and a half hours out there. Hour thirty-five. Thirty-six. He had stood in a room with gold carpet and told the Minister of Continuance <em>two days, three at the outside</em>, and the Outside’s clock had been sitting at hour thirty-three when he climbed out of the well. The debt wasn’t coming at the end of the week. It was coming this afternoon.</p>\n<p>And in here, it was two months away.</p>\n<p>He stood with his eye to the brass and understood, properly, for the first time, the one thing in this place that was on his side. Out there, on the Collar’s clock, he’d have had a couple of hours to climb four kilometres and open a lock that had been shut for a hundred and sixty-eight years. In here, every round he spent — learning to stand, learning the Lees, learning anything — cost the world outside about two minutes.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t much time. It was more than anyone else was ever going to get.</p>\n<p>Behind them, down in the city, the horn blew from the Steward’s House for first shift. And all across Holdfast, in a great clattering rolling wave that started at the centre and spread outward to the edges, the shutters came up, and the violet light poured into ten thousand windows, and the city woke.</p>\n<p>Ione took a nail from her pocket and scratched a new mark on the brass ring. A finger’s width below yesterday’s.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said. “Now you know how long.”</p>"}, {"file": "09-greenbread.md", "label": "Chapter 9", "title": "Greenbread", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4829, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine. And if the one of us falls, the other one hauls.</p><span class=\"attr\">Holdfast riggers’ wedding vow</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">He learned to stand the way the babies did: badly, and in public, and with a great many people watching.</p>\n<p>Osk taught him. It was not, Cas gathered, a thing Osk had been told to do. It was simply a thing Osk had decided needed doing, on the first morning, when he opened the door of the little stone room and found the Relief of Holdfast on his hands and knees on the floor, trying to get up, and failing, and trying again.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Osk. “Not like that. That’s how you break a knee.”</p>\n<p>He came in and knelt down beside him. He was so big that kneeling, he was nearly Cas’s height standing.</p>\n<p>“Babies,” he said, “in the Crèche. First thing we teach them. You don’t push up. The weight wins if you push. You <em>roll</em>.” He demonstrated, a huge slow shifting of hips and shoulders. “Get your bones under you. Stack them. Like scaffold. Then the weight goes down through the bones into the floor and the floor holds it, not you. You’re not holding it. You’re just <em>stacked</em>.” He looked at Cas. “Try.”</p>\n<p>Cas tried. It took him four attempts. On the fifth, something shifted in his hips and he found his feet under him and came up — not pushing, somehow, just unfolding upward like a ladder being raised — and he was standing, and it didn’t hurt, or not as much, and he was so surprised that he laughed.</p>\n<p>Osk nodded, grave as a judge.</p>\n<p>“Good,” he said. “Now do it a hundred times.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the second round, a little after the start of second shift, his suit began to groan.</p>\n<p>He was in his room in the Steward’s House, lying on the rope bed. The suit stood in the corner where it always stood, humming its keel-note to itself, and without any warning it made a sound. A low sound. A long, slow, deep sound like a foghorn heard across miles of water, or like a whale — <em>Farr said whales</em>, Teo had said, <em>so I get birds</em> — it came out of the suit’s helmet speaker and filled the little room, vast and slow and falling, and it went on, and on, and didn’t stop.</p>\n<p>He sat up. He knew what it was. He’d known what it was the instant it started.</p>\n<p>It went on for the rest of that round and into the next.</p>\n<p>He didn’t have to listen. The suit was catching every second of it, and at the end it would give the whole thing back to him at the proper speed. He listened anyway. He sat on the floor of the stone room with his back against the bed and his knees up and stayed with it, and slept for some of it, there on the floor, and woke to it still going, and he didn’t go to the Tollers that night, and when Osk opened the door once in the middle of it to see what in the name of the Lamp the noise was, Cas just held up a hand, and Osk looked at the suit, and at him, and went out again and shut the door without a sound.</p>\n<p>It was a voice. He could hear, if he listened, that it was a voice — the rise and fall of it, the rhythm of speech slowed so far down that it was just a shape — and he sat there and let it pour over him, a single human sentence stretched over most of an hour, and tried to guess the words, and couldn’t.</p>\n<p>At the end of it the suit went <em>ding</em>.</p>\n<p>And then, suddenly, perfectly clearly, as though she were in the room:</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk. “Right. Your first chirp’s come through. You want to know what it sounds like this end? It sounds like a budgie fell into my radio. About three minutes after you went up that ladder. Then the suit sat there chewing on it for a minute and a half and went <em>ding</em>, and then it was you. Talking about rats.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand over his face.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been gone five minutes,” said Teo, “and apparently you’ve found a city, and they’ve made you a prophet, and they’ve got the Section 9 placard in a church. I leave you alone for <em>five minutes</em>, Hale.” A pause. He heard her breathe. “Thirty-one thousand. Christ. Thirty-one thousand people. Up that ladder. I keep looking at the ladder. I can see it from the chair, through the window. It just looks like a ladder.”</p>\n<p>A longer pause.</p>\n<p>“You lied to them. Okay. You told me that like it’s the end of the world. It isn’t. You’re not the first man who ever stood up in front of a crowd and told them it’d be all right because it was easier than breaking their hearts. I’ve done it. I’ve done it on the top of a flood barrier with the water coming up, and I’d do it again, and so would you.” Another pause. “But you’ll tell them. Not today. Not till you’ve got something to give them instead. But you’ll tell them the truth before you go up that spindle, Hale, or I’ll come up that ladder in this chair and tell them myself.”</p>\n<p>He laughed. He couldn’t help it. It came out wet.</p>\n<p>“Also,” said Teo, “I’m fine. You’ll want to know that. The chair’s fine. The lee’s fine. It’s not moving. I found a biscuit in the first-aid kit. It’s a bit old. Nobody’s called. The Collar’ll be wondering where we are. Let them wonder.” A small snort. “Prophet inside an hour. That’s a Civil Defence record, that.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“I put the blanket from the first-aid kit over her,” said Teo, more quietly. “The lady at the bottom of the ladder. With the little lamp. I couldn’t stand her lying there. I went down through the hatch and put it over her. I hope that’s all right. Whoever she was.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t breathe for a moment.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “That’s three minutes of me you’ve had. That’ll have taken you — I don’t know. Most of a day? Don’t answer that, I can’t work it out, Farr was the one for sums.” And her voice caught, just slightly, on the name, and went on. “Send me more. Send me all of it. I’ve got nothing to do in this chair. Tell your congregation there’s a woman down here says hello.” A breath. “And look after yourself, you daft sod.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then he got up — rolled, stacked his bones, stood, the way Osk had taught him — and took out the recorder, and pressed the button, and told Teo about Osk, and learning to stand like a baby, and a tower full of pendulums, and an old woman who’d corrected him to the minute, and a Log full of letters to a little girl called Maddy who was seven. And he told her that the lady at the bottom of the ladder was called Seren, and that she’d been the cleverest Toller in two hundred years, and that she’d painted the truth on the wall of the Commons, and that her mother had carved the little lamp, and that it had been for her crib. And he told her <em>thank you for the blanket</em>, and found that he couldn’t say anything else for a while, and just sat there with the button pressed, not saying anything, letting it record the silence.</p>\n<p>After that he sent her something every round. Whatever the round had held, into the recorder, last thing at night: a round’s worth of Holdfast at a time. He didn’t wait for answers. He’d done the sum. To her, down there in her chair, they’d be arriving about every four minutes, like a tide coming in.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He stood up a hundred times a round, for sixteen rounds, the way Osk had told him to, and in between he was the Relief.</p>\n<p>That was the other thing he learned. How to be it.</p>\n<p>It was a job. Crane made it clear, gently, from the first morning, that it was a job and that it had duties, and that the duties were public. The Relief was carried through the lanes on the litter, on high rounds, so that people could see him. The Relief sat in a chair on the steps of the Steward’s House for an hour each first shift, and people came up the steps one by one and knelt and touched his hand, or his coat, or just looked at him, and went away weeping. The Relief blessed things. Nobody had told him he was going to bless things. A woman came up the steps with a new baby in a sling and held it out to him and said <em>bless her, Relief</em>, and he looked at Crane standing beside the chair and Crane looked back with no expression at all, and so he put his hand on the baby’s head — so small, so warm, so heavy for its size — and said <em>bless you</em>, and the woman burst into tears, and after that there was no stopping it. He blessed babies. He blessed a new rope for a rigger crew. He blessed the Tanks, standing on a gallery over the great algae vats where the dead of Holdfast went to feed the living, looking down at the slow green water, saying <em>bless</em>, <em>bless</em>, and thinking of Ben.</p>\n<p>He did not say it would be all right again. He never said it again. But he didn’t have to. He had said it once, and they had heard it, and it was in every face that looked up at him from the foot of the Steward’s steps.</p>\n<p>Crane watched him do all of it with that tired, courteous half-smile, and at the end of each round thanked him, and never once asked him how he felt.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At night he went to the Tollers.</p>\n<p>Osk slept outside his door every night on the stool, and every night, when Cas went past him in the shuttered dusk, Osk slept a little more deeply, and never once woke. On the fourth night, Cas found that someone had oiled the hinge of his door so that it didn’t squeak.</p>\n<p>Pim brought the cart the first few nights. After that, Cas walked. It took him most of a shift to get across the city the first time on his own feet, stopping every twenty steps with his hand on a wall. By the tenth night it took him an hour. By the fourteenth, he could do it without stopping, if he didn’t mind that his heart was going like a hammer when he got there, and his knees shook, and he had to sit on the step of the tower for a while before he could climb it.</p>\n<p>They didn’t only work at the tower. More often, as the rounds went on, he found himself in Ione’s loft.</p>\n<p>She lived at the top of a scaffold tower in the Drop District, not far from the well he’d come up — a rickety, swaying, rope-lashed stack of platforms twenty storeys high, where the riggers lived who worked the spindles, and who were, Pim told him with great satisfaction, the roughest people in Holdfast. Her loft was the very top platform, walled with canvas and scrap and open on one side to the sky, so that from the hammock that hung across it you could lie and look straight up past the black mass of Spindle Four into the Lamp. It was full of rope. Rope hung from every beam in coils, in hanks, spliced and whipped and knotted, a hundred kinds. There were harnesses on hooks, and chalk in jars, and plumb bobs of every size hanging on strings, and a workbench, and a little stove, and a shelf of books — station manuals, mostly, their covers long gone, and a few Holdfast things: hand-copied, hand-bound, the pages made of pale algae-paper.</p>\n<p>They worked there. That was what they told themselves they were doing, anyway. There was a great deal to do. The suit’s harness had to be re-rigged for climbing; its hardpoints weren’t built for rope, and she spent three nights splicing a web of rigger’s strapping round it that would let her belay it — him — from above. There were supplies to make: greenbread baked hard as brick, water in skins of rendered algae-leather, cakes of cricket fat. There were the Lees.</p>\n<p>The Lees were the heart of it. She explained them to him on the fifth night, lying on her back on the loft floor with a plumb bob hanging on a string from her fingers, watching it.</p>\n<p>“Look,” she said. “Down here, the Lamp pushes down and the Anvil pulls down and it all goes the same way. Straight. The bob hangs straight.” It did. “But go up a spindle. The further up you go, the harder the Lamp pushes. You weigh more and more. By two kilometres up you weigh half as much again as you do here. By three, twice. By the Rim⁠—” she shrugged “⁠—nobody knows. Nobody’s been to the Rim and come back except me, and I wasn’t counting.”</p>\n<p>“So how did you get there?”</p>\n<p>“The Lees.” She moved the bob. “The spindles are full of keelstone. Struts, braces, cross-members, great thick beams of it. And keelstone pulls, you know that. Like the Anvil pulls. So near a big strut, the strut’s pull goes against the Lamp’s push. They cancel. Not all the way. But some. So there are places up there — pockets — where you weigh less. Sometimes much less. You can rest in them. You can climb from one to the next.” She let the bob swing. “That’s a Lee. The riggers have always known them. The lower ones. I know the high ones.”</p>\n<p>“And they move.”</p>\n<p>“The Lamp’s getting weaker. You know that. The Lightening.” She held the string still. “So the balance moves. Every year, the Lees move up. Or sideways. Or close up and open somewhere else. I haven’t been past the lower ones in five years.” She looked at him. “I’ll have to go and look.”</p>\n<p>She went on the eighth round.</p>\n<p>She went in the night, alone, with two coils of rope and a bag of chalk and a skin of water, and he stood at the foot of Spindle Four in the shuttered dusk and watched her climb until she was a speck and then nothing. She’d said two rounds. She was gone three. He spent those three rounds being the Relief, blessing babies on the steps of the Steward’s House and not hearing a word anyone said to him, and at night lying on the rope bed in the stone room staring at the ceiling, until on the third night Pim’s pebble hit his shutter and he went down to the lane and found her sitting on the Tollers’ step with her hands bandaged in strips of cloth and a rope burn across one cheek, eating greenbread.</p>\n<p>“There’s a way,” she said. “It’s not a good way. But there’s a way.”</p>\n<p>He sat down beside her on the step. His knees shook. He didn’t know if it was the climb down the lane.</p>\n<p>“You said two rounds,” he said.</p>\n<p>She looked at him sideways. And for once there was something in her face that wasn’t patient at all. It was surprised.</p>\n<p>“You were worried,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She went on looking at him for a while. Then she looked away, down the lane, and ate her greenbread.</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s been worried about me,” she said, “in about eleven years.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He ate rat on the eleventh round.</p>\n<p>It was at the market in the Commons Lanes, where the stalls were built into the shells of old cargo containers with their doors swung open, and everything that Holdfast grew or made or salvaged was sold for tokens stamped out of old sheet metal. Ione had taken him there — in the middle of a round, openly, with a hood up over his face because by now there was not a soul in Holdfast who wouldn’t have known him. Greenbread, stacked in dark bricks. Cricket flour in sacks, and the crickets themselves in wire boxes, chirping. Mushrooms of a dozen pale kinds, grown on bales of old insulation in the dark under the Anvil’s edge. Algae in every form: cakes, noodles, a thick green paste in jars, a fermented green wine that Ione said would take the skin off your throat. Salt. Oil. Thread twisted from algae fibre. Needles filed from wire.</p>\n<p>And at one stall, turning on a spit over a little charcoal fire, rats.</p>\n<p>“Go on,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been eating it for ten rounds,” she said. “It’s in the soup. The Tollers’ soup. You had two bowls.”</p>\n<p>“I⁠—” He stopped. “Did I?”</p>\n<p>“Two bowls. You said it was very good.” Her mouth twitched. “Go on.”</p>\n<p>It was very good. It tasted, as everyone who has ever eaten rat has said, like chicken — like very rich, slightly sweet, dark-meat chicken, with a smoky crackle to the skin — and he ate a whole one standing in the lane with the juice running down his wrist, and Ione watched him do it with an expression he’d never seen on her face before: a kind of wicked delight, quickly hidden.</p>\n<p>Pim, who had appeared out of nowhere as he always did, was scandalised.</p>\n<p>“Not <em>mine</em>,” he said. “Not Steward Harrow. Not Steward Crane. Not <em>any</em> of the Stewards. Only the <em>market</em> rats.”</p>\n<p>“Pim names his after the Stewardship,” Ione explained gravely. “All of them. He has a First Steward. He has a Warden-Captain. He has three Toller-Seconds.”</p>\n<p>“I have a Relief,” said Pim. “Now.” He produced it from inside his shirt: a small, fierce, grey rat with a torn ear, which bit him. “He’s new. He’s not very nice yet.”</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“He came up out of a drain,” said Pim, “so.”</p>\n<p>Ione laughed. It was the first time Cas had heard her laugh — really laugh, out loud — and it surprised him so much that he just stood there in the lane with the rat bones in his hand and listened to it. It was a short, low, rough laugh, as if it didn’t get used much and had got stiff, and it stopped almost as soon as it started, and she looked away quickly, as though she’d been caught at something.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw a wedding on the thirteenth round.</p>\n<p>Riggers. Two of them, a man and a woman, both short and broad and scarred about the forearms like Ione, married on the rooftop of the riggers’ tower in the Drop District under the open sky. The whole tower came up — forty or fifty people, crammed onto the platforms and the ladders and the ropes — and a Steward in a black coat stood between the two of them and said words, and then the riggers said their own. They tied their wrists together with a length of rope, the woman’s left to the man’s right, in a knot Cas didn’t know, and they said it together:</p>\n<p>“I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine. And if the one of us falls, the other one hauls.”</p>\n<p>And everyone on the platforms and the ladders shouted <em>haul!</em> and stamped until the whole tower swayed.</p>\n<p>Afterwards there was green wine, and it did take the skin off his throat, and there was music: a drum made of an old coolant drum, and a sort of fiddle strung with wire, and someone playing a pipe cut from conduit. And they danced — the Holdfast dance, which was slow and heavy and stamping, all bent knees and planted feet, a dance made for people who weighed half as much again as they should, and which shook the tower to its lashings. Cas sat on a coil of rope with his back against a beam and watched it. Ione sat beside him.</p>\n<p>“You don’t dance?” he said.</p>\n<p>“I dance.” She drank. “Not at weddings. It makes people nervous. The Unwatched at your wedding. It’s like a crow at a christening.”</p>\n<p>“What’s a christening?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. “It’s in one of the old books. I don’t know. I assumed you’d know.”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know either.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him a moment longer, and then something in her face gave, and she laughed again — shorter this time, into her cup.</p>\n<p>“The knot,” he said. “On their wrists. What is it?”</p>\n<p>She put her cup down. She took his left hand — without asking, the way she’d checked his seals at the well, practically — and took a length of thin cord from her pocket and, very quickly, tied it round his wrist. Her fingers moved too fast for him to follow. Over, under, round, through. When she took her hands away there was a knot on his wrist: flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped.</p>\n<p>“Riggers’ hitch,” she said. “Holds under load. Won’t slip. Won’t jam. Comes off in a second if you know where to pull and never comes off if you don’t.”</p>\n<p>He looked at it.</p>\n<p>“That’s the only one you’ll need,” said Ione, and took it off again with one tug, and put the cord back in her pocket.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw a funeral on the fourteenth. An old man, a Toller, one of Perpetua’s cousins. They carried him through the lanes on a board, wrapped in a white sheet with the lamp painted on it, and the Tollers walked behind, and at every Bell the procession stopped and everyone stood still and silent until it passed. At the Tanks they laid him on a sort of stone slope running down into the green water, and a Steward said: “He’s clocked off. His shift is done. We thank him for his watch.” And they tipped the board, and he slid down into the vat, and the green closed over him, and everyone said <em>clocked off, clocked off</em>, softly, like a breath going out.</p>\n<p>“Clocked off,” Cas said afterwards to Ione, walking back. “That’s — the station used to say that. When you went off shift.”</p>\n<p>“We know,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He saw children playing a game in a lane. They stood in a line with their backs to a wall, and one of them stood in front of them, facing them, and shut her eyes and fell straight backwards, stiff as a plank. The others caught her. Every time. They shrieked with laughter. Then the next one.</p>\n<p>“What’s it called?” he asked Pim.</p>\n<p>“Drop,” said Pim, as if it were obvious. “It’s called Drop.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the fifteenth round, when he’d sent Teo the round’s news, he switched to the second channel and said:</p>\n<p>“Nell. I’m going to tell you about a day. Just an ordinary day. Nobody’s ever — I don’t think I’ve ever told anyone about an ordinary day. I don’t think I had any, for a long time. I think they were all the same day, and I didn’t want to tell anyone about it.</p>\n<p>“This morning I stood up by myself. There’s a man here called Osk who’s teaching me. He’s the biggest man I’ve ever seen and he talks like gravel. He says I’m nearly as good as a baby now.</p>\n<p>“Then I sat on some steps and people came and touched my hand. They think I’m — it doesn’t matter what they think I am. I’m not it. I let them think it. I’m not proud of that.</p>\n<p>“Then I ate a rat. It was delicious. Don’t tell your mother.</p>\n<p>“Then I went to a place where a very old lady counts time. She’s funny, Nell. She’d make you laugh. She says rude things about everyone and then gives them soup. She had a daughter who was very brave. She lost her.</p>\n<p>“And then I went to see my friend. She lives at the top of a tower full of rope, where you can lie in a hammock and look straight up at the Lamp — the star, the one I told you about. She’s never seen a real one. A real star. I tried to tell her what they’re like. I told her about the ones on your ceiling. Betelgeuse. Deneb. I told her they’re far away and they don’t move and there are thousands of them. She asked me what colour they are. I said white, mostly. Some are blue. Some are a bit red. She asked me which one was my favourite and I didn’t know. I’ve never looked. I’ve never once looked, Nell. You’d have known in a second.”</p>\n<p>He was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“I think,” he said, “that if you ever meet her, you’ll like her. I don’t know why I think that. You’ll never meet her. But I think you would.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the sixteenth round, the Lace touched Spindle Six.</p>\n<p>He didn’t see it happen. He was on the steps of the Steward’s House, blessing a rope. But he heard it — everyone in Holdfast heard it — a sound from the sky like a great sheet of glass being drawn across stone, high and thin and long, and then a flash, very brief, very bright, from the top of Spindle Six on the far side of the city, and then silence. People stopped in the lanes. Everyone looked up. On the steps, the rigger who’d brought the rope to be blessed dropped it.</p>\n<p>Six was the spindle nearest the Lace’s lowest tendril. There had been a rigger crew working it that round — they went up every round, to tend the old lamp-lines and the water-catchers the riggers had strung on the spindle’s upper lattice, a kilometre and a half up. Six of them. Ione knew them all. So did the rigger on the steps.</p>\n<p>At the end of the round the Stewardship put up a notice in the Commons, beneath the Public Tally, written in beautiful careful letters on a board:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MARROW TELL AND HIS CREW<br>HAVE BEEN TAKEN INTO THE LAMP’S CROWN<br>A BLESSING ON THE WATCH<br>THE QUICKENING IS GLORY</div>\n<p>Ione went up after them the next round, with two others from the riggers’ tower. She came back at the end of it and said nothing, and went up to her loft, and when Cas climbed the twenty storeys that night — it took him an hour; he did it — she was sitting in her hammock with her knees up, looking at the Lamp.</p>\n<p>“What did you find?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Rope,” she said. “Six harnesses. Their tools. The water-catchers they were tending, all rusted through to lace. Marrow’s knife. I gave that to his daughter.” She didn’t look at him. “And bones. The kind of bones you’d find in a Founder’s grave, if the Founders had graves. Old and white and dry. And a bit of Tamsin’s hair, still in her clip. She’d have been thirty next round.” She was quiet. “The Lamp’s crown. A blessing on the Watch.”</p>\n<p>He sat down on the loft floor.</p>\n<p>“Crane wants another address,” he said. “On Kindling Round. Three rounds from now. The whole city in the Commons. He says they need to see the Relief.”</p>\n<p>“Of course he does.”</p>\n<p>“He wants me to tell them the Quickening’s glory. That Marrow Tell’s crew are in the Lamp’s crown.” He looked at his hands. “He hasn’t said it like that. But that’s what he wants.”</p>\n<p>Ione said nothing. The hammock creaked, very gently, as she moved.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to tell them,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>She turned her head then and looked at him.</p>\n<p>“All of it,” he said. “The Outside. The test. Aldous Crane. The debt. How long. The lock and the key and you. All of it. In the Commons. On the Bell.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those unhurried grey-green eyes. And for the first time since the top of the well he couldn’t tell — he really couldn’t — whether she had known he was going to say it.</p>\n<p>“They’ll put you in the Quiet,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Probably.”</p>\n<p>“Do you know what the Quiet is?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“It’s a coolant tank. Under the Steward’s House. They seal the lid. No light. No sound but the Bell.” She said it very evenly. “My mother was in it for eleven rounds before they gave her to the Drop.”</p>\n<p>“I’m still going to tell them.”</p>\n<p>Ione Tally looked at him a moment longer. Then she lay back in her hammock and looked up at the Lamp, and something happened at the corner of her mouth — so small he almost didn’t see it.</p>\n<p>“Good,” she said. “I know how to get people out of the Quiet.”</p>"}, {"file": "10-the-second-address.md", "label": "Chapter 10", "title": "The Second Address", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4727, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Tell them the Drop isn’t open. Say it twice. They’ll only hear it once.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They spent the three rounds before the Bell getting it right.</p>\n<p>Not the speech. He didn’t write a speech. He tried, the first night, in the loft, and Ione watched him cross out three pages and then took the pencil out of his hand and said, “Don’t. You’ll make it pretty. Say it the way you said it to me.” So he didn’t write it. But they planned everything around it.</p>\n<p>The Tollers would be on the roof of the Commons. The Commons roof was open in long strips between the girders, and there was a ridge of old gantry running along the top of it that the riggers used for stringing lamp-lines on high days; Tobiah and three others would be up there with a banner. It was already painted. Perpetua had had it painted on the first night, on a sheet of canvas thirty metres long, and it lay folded in the upper floor of Toller’s Tower like a sail: Seren’s line. The Tollers’ curve, drawn huge, in white on black, with a hundred and sixty-eight years of Bells falling away along it and the line carried forward past the end of the count to the place where it met the bottom of the canvas. And beside that place, in letters two metres high, the number Seren had worked out thirteen years ago and the Tollers had been refining every round since.</p>\n<p>“When they see it,” said Perpetua, “they’ll know it’s true. Nobody in Holdfast would believe a word from the Outside. They’ll believe a Tally. They’ve been counting their whole lives.”</p>\n<p>The riggers would be at the doors. Ione had asked them. Not all of them — the riggers were as split as anyone, and some of them went to the Commons on the seventh round and knelt at Muster Point B — but a few dozen, the ones who’d known Marrow Tell and his crew, the ones who’d climbed Six the round after and seen the bones. They would be at the doors and on the ladders, with rope. Nobody had said what the rope was for.</p>\n<p>And Perpetua told him, on the last night, sitting in her chair with her hand on the column:</p>\n<p>“Tell them the Drop isn’t open.”</p>\n<p>“I will.”</p>\n<p>“Say it twice. They’ll only hear it once.” She looked at him with her black bird’s eyes. “You’re going to tell twenty thousand people that the Outside’s alive and that a man came up out of the floor from it. Some of them are going to hear that and nothing else. You understand? Nothing else. You could talk for a shift. They’ll hear <em>the Outside is alive, and he came up the Drop</em>.” She took her hand off the column and laid it on his knee, the way she had the first night. “Say it twice, Relief. Say it three times. It won’t be enough. But say it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Kindling Round fell three rounds later — the first round of the year One Hundred and Sixty-Nine, a hundred and sixty-eight years to the round since the Lamp was lit — and the whole of Holdfast had been getting ready for it for a week.</p>\n<p>There had never been a Kindling Round like it. That was what everyone said. Every year of the Watch, on this round, Holdfast remembered the night the Founders stood under the Placard and counted the first Bell. And this year — in a year when the Bells came more than a hundred a round, when you could feel them while you ate and while you slept — <em>the Relief had come</em>. It was a sign. It was the greatest festival in the history of the Watch. The lanes had been hung with lamp-strings for three rounds: little clay lamps strung on wire from wall to wall, hundreds of them, so that even in the day the lanes glittered. Every shrine had been repainted. Children had been making paper lamps. The Public Tally board in the Commons had been hung with a garland of green algae-cloth and the numbers repainted in gold.</p>\n<p>The Commons was fuller than it had been the night he arrived. He hadn’t thought that was possible. They packed the floor to the walls. They filled the girders — people had climbed up into the roof trusses and sat along them in rows, their legs dangling, like birds on a wire. They stood outside in the lanes so thick that a Warden trying to get through had to be passed over their heads. The noise was the sea. When the Wardens carried Cas in on the litter — he’d asked to walk; Crane had said, kindly, <em>no</em> — the sea rose up into something like a storm, and it was a long time before it went down.</p>\n<p>He stood on the platform. Osk was at his elbow. Crane beside him, in his black coat, with a new lamp embroidered on its breast in gold thread for the occasion. The Public Tally board behind them: BELL 121,944.</p>\n<p>He looked for Ione. He couldn’t see her.</p>\n<p>He looked up. Along the ridge of the gantry, high up in the open roof, in the violet glare of the Lace, there were four small figures in grey smocks, sitting in a row with something long and folded across their knees.</p>\n<p>Crane led the singing. The Tending, slow and worn. Twenty thousand voices, and more outside, the whole city singing, under the Lace. <em>Tend the Lamp, tend the Lamp, till the Relief comes after.</em> Cas listened to every word of it. He made himself. He stood there on the platform and let it wash over him and thought about a children’s choir on the Tier Nine lampposts at dawn, and about what those children had been singing about, and what these people were.</p>\n<p>Then the call and response. <em>Who keeps the Watch?</em> The Bell came during it — 121,945 — rising up through the floor, and the whole Commons fell still and silent and counted it, and then went on. <em>Until Relief.</em></p>\n<p>Then Crane turned to him, and smiled, and stepped back.</p>\n<p>BELL 121,946.</p>\n<p>He had about ten minutes before the next one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“I lied to you,” said Casimir Hale.</p>\n<p>He didn’t raise his voice. He’d learned, the first night, that he didn’t have to. The Commons was so silent when he spoke that the smallest sound carried to the back.</p>\n<p>“The first night I came. I stood here and I told you it was going to be all right. I don’t know that. I didn’t know it then. I said it because I looked at your faces and I didn’t want to be the one who took it away.” He could see the front row. There was a little girl there — not the same one, a different one. He looked at her. He made himself go on looking at her. “That’s what people do where I come from. They say what’s easy. They’ve been doing it for ninety years. It’s why you’re here.”</p>\n<p>A murmur. Very faint. Like wind starting.</p>\n<p>“So I’m going to tell you the truth now. All of it. It’s going to be hard to hear, and I’m sorry. And some of it you’re not going to believe, and that’s all right. But I need you to listen to all of it. Not just the start. All of it. Please.”</p>\n<p>He took a breath.</p>\n<p>“The Outside is alive.”</p>\n<p>The murmur became a sound.</p>\n<p>“It isn’t burned. It never was. It’s a city. A big one, bigger than you can imagine. It’s called Averna. It has six hundred million people in it and all of them are alive right now, today, this minute. And it’s the same night there. It’s still the same night that the Lamp was kindled.” He waited for the sound to go down. It didn’t, quite. “You know that. Some of you know that. The Tollers know it. They’ve always known. Every Bell you’ve ever counted is one second of the Outside’s time. A hundred and twenty-two thousand Bells is thirty-four hours, near enough. That’s all it’s been, out there. A day and a half. The Outside isn’t gone. It’s slow.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Lies!</em>” Somebody, at the back. A man’s voice, high and cracked. Then another voice, shouting him down. Then others. The sound rose.</p>\n<p>“There’s a little girl in the Outside,” said Cas, into it, “called Maddy Tally. She’s seven. She went to sleep on the night of the Kindling in her grandmother’s flat, and her mother went to work at the station and didn’t come home. Her mother was Ruth Tally. Your Founder. The one who counted the first Bell.” The sound faltered. “Ruth wrote her letters for fifty years. She never stopped. She died in here, an old woman, with eleven grandchildren. And Maddy’s still seven. Right now, while I’m talking. She’ll be having her lunch.”</p>\n<p>It went quiet then. Really quiet. Not the silence of listening; something else. A silence like the moment after a glass is dropped, before it hits the floor.</p>\n<p>“The Kindling,” said Cas, “was an accident.”</p>\n<p>He felt Crane move beside him. He didn’t look round.</p>\n<p>“The station — this place, the Draw — makes power. It sends it Outside. The night of the Kindling, there was a test. The people who ran the Outside wanted more power, and they wanted to film the station making it, for a festival. The engineers said it wasn’t safe. The man in charge made them do it anyway. His name was Aldous Crane.”</p>\n<p>The sound the Commons made then was something he’d never heard a crowd make. A kind of groan. A kind of gasp. Twenty thousand people drawing in breath together.</p>\n<p>“He was your Founder. He was a — he wasn’t a bad man. He was a frightened one. Ruth Tally tried to shut the Lamp off, that night, in the Crown. There was a lock, and there was a key, and he had the key round his neck, and she asked him for it, and he wouldn’t give it to her. And then there was an accident, and the key fell, and it was lost.” He made himself say it. “He didn’t give the key to the Lamp. He dropped it.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Heresy!</em>” A woman’s voice this time, very near the front, shrill with horror. “Heresy, it’s — <em>Steward!</em> Steward, stop him⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Let him finish.”</p>\n<p>It was Crane.</p>\n<p>Cas turned his head. The old man was standing quite still beside him, very straight in his black coat with its new gold lamp, and his face was the colour of the paper in the Log, and his long hands were clasped very tightly in front of him. He wasn’t looking at Cas. He was looking out at the Commons.</p>\n<p>“Let him finish,” said Halloran Crane again, and his voice carried to the back of the hall.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He told them the rest.</p>\n<p>He told them what the Lamp was. He did it the way he’d done it for Crane — a debt, a loan from the emptiness, and the emptiness always wants it back — and he watched their faces, and he saw some of them understand, and some of them not, and some of them refuse to. He told them what the Bells meant. That the Quickening was the debt coming due. That it was going to come faster and faster. That the Lace was part of it, and it was coming down, and that when it reached the Rim at the top of the spindles, it would all be over. Not just Holdfast. Everything.</p>\n<p>“Marrow Tell and his crew,” he said, “weren’t taken into the Lamp’s crown. They weren’t blessed. They died. The Lace touched the top of Six and they grew old and died in the time it takes to draw a breath. The riggers who went up after them found their bones.” He looked out at the hall. “The Quickening isn’t glory. It’s a clock. And it’s nearly run down.”</p>\n<p>He heard someone crying. He heard someone praying, in a low fast mutter, the words of the Placard. <em>Remain at your station. Await relief.</em></p>\n<p>BELL 121,947 came while he was talking. He stopped, and waited, and everyone waited, and it rose and hung and fell, and when it had gone he said:</p>\n<p>“There’s one chance.”</p>\n<p>He looked for her again. And this time he found her. She was at the back, where she’d been the first night, by the great doors, in the gap in the crowd, with her arms folded and her back against the wall. The riggers were around her. She was looking at him. She wasn’t patient now. She was very still, and her eyes were very bright.</p>\n<p>“There’s a way to close the Lamp,” he said. “The woman who built this station put it there. It’s in the Crown, at the top of Spindle One. It’s a lock. It’s been there all along. It needs a key.” He lifted his hand and pointed, over twenty thousand heads, at the back of the hall. “And the key is here. Ione Tally has it.”</p>\n<p>Every head in the Commons turned.</p>\n<p>“She found it, eleven years ago, up there. The key your Founder dropped. She came down with it and told you the Lamp could be closed. And you called her mad. And her mother, Seren Tally, drew the line of the Bells and told you how long you had left, and you gave her to the Drop.” He heard his voice shaking and let it. “They were right. Both of them. They were right all along.”</p>\n<p>And up on the roof, on the gantry, in the glare of the Lace, the Tollers stood up and let the banner fall.</p>\n<p>It came down out of the open roof between the girders like a great black sail unfurling, thirty metres of it, and snapped taut, and hung there over the whole of the Commons. The curve. White on black. A hundred and sixty-eight years of Bells, falling away in a long smooth swoop, and the line going on past the end of the count, down, down, to the bottom edge of the canvas. And beside it, in white letters two metres tall, a single number, and a single word.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">ROUND 48<br>THE LACE MEETS THE RIM</div>\n<p>It was the first round of the year.</p>\n<p>He heard the Commons read it. He heard twenty thousand people do the sum.</p>\n<p>He’d done his own, at the top of the Tollers’ tower. Forty-seven rounds. Out there, it was about two hours. Hour thirty-six, on a clock he’d once thought had days left on it. In here, it was all the time there was.</p>\n<p>“We’re going to go up,” he said, very loudly now, over the sound that was starting. “Ione Tally and me. To the Crown. To try. And if it works⁠—” he had to shout “⁠—<em>listen to me</em>. If it works, the Lamp will go out.”</p>\n<p>The sound stopped, a little. Enough.</p>\n<p>“The Lamp will go out, and it will be dark. Really dark. Darker than you’ve ever seen. And you mustn’t be afraid of it.” He was looking at the little girl in the front row. She was holding her mother’s skirt in both fists. “When the Lamp goes out, it means we did it. It means the debt’s closed. It means the Shear’s gone and time in here is the same as time out there. And it means the Drop will be a road. You’ll be able to walk down it. All of you. Out. Into the Outside. Into the same night.”</p>\n<p>He held on to Osk’s arm. He was very tired suddenly.</p>\n<p>“But not before,” he said. “Listen. <em>Not before</em>. The Drop is not open now. Nobody goes near the wells. Nobody. Not until the Lamp goes out. If you go down now, the Shear will kill you, the way it killed everyone who ever tried. The way it killed Seren. I came up through it in a suit made for it and it nearly killed me. The Drop is not open. Do you hear me? The Drop is not open.”</p>\n<p><em>Say it twice.</em></p>\n<p>“The Drop is <em>not open</em>.”</p>\n<p>And then, into the silence, because it was the only thing left and because it was the truest thing he knew:</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s coming. There’s no Relief. I’m not it. There never was one. I’m just what came.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>For a moment, nothing happened.</p>\n<p>Then the Commons came apart.</p>\n<p>He’d never know, afterwards, how it started. He’d go over it again and again, lying in the dark, and never be able to find the first moment. It was as though the hall had been holding a single breath for a hundred and sixty-eight years and had just let it out. Everyone was shouting. Everyone was moving. People were on their feet, on their knees, clinging to each other, pushing, surging toward the platform, surging away from it. Someone was screaming <em>heresy, heresy</em> over and over in a voice like a torn sail. Someone else had climbed up onto the brass rail of Muster Point B and was reading the Placard aloud at the top of his lungs. A knot of men at the side of the hall were fighting — fists, and then staves, the Wardens going in — and a woman near the front had her arms round a stranger and was sobbing into his coat, and the little girl in the front row was crying with her face in her mother’s skirt.</p>\n<p>And there was the sound he’d been afraid of.</p>\n<p>He heard it start at the back, near the doors. Near Ione. A man’s voice, high and wild, carrying over everything the way a single voice sometimes can:</p>\n<p>“<em>The Outside’s alive! He came up the Drop! He came up it — it’s open — we can go down, we can go down⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>“<em>No!</em>”</p>\n<p>That was him. He was shouting. He was at the front of the platform, shouting over twenty thousand people, <em>no, no, it isn’t open, it isn’t⁠—</em>, and nobody could hear him. He could see the words not reaching. He could see it happen at the back of the hall: the knot of people round that one voice, turning, pushing toward the great doors. And the doors opening, and the lanes outside, and the people in the lanes turning to see what was happening, and hearing it — <em>it’s open, he came up it, we can go down</em> — and turning to run.</p>\n<p>He saw Ione. He saw her step out into the doorway in front of them with her arms out, shouting, and the riggers beside her. He saw the crowd hit them like a wave hitting a wall. He saw the riggers go down under it, and Ione go down, and the wave go over them and out into the lanes.</p>\n<p>“<em>Osk.</em>”</p>\n<p>The big Warden was already moving.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Afterwards, what he remembered was pieces.</p>\n<p>The cart. Pim, white-faced, dragging it through a side door, and Osk lifting him into it bodily, and then Osk himself between the shafts, hauling it at a run through the glittering lamp-strung lanes with the cart bouncing and banging and Cas clinging to its sides. People running beside them, past them, all going the same way. Toward the Drop District. Toward the wells.</p>\n<p>The well. The same well, the one he’d come up. The WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT circle painted round its mouth. There were people all round it. A crowd, pressing up the ramp to the platform, pressing against the scrap-metal wall. And on the platform, round the rim of the conduit, a line of them, going over. Going down the ladder. One after another, their hands on the rungs, their faces set and wild and joyful, climbing down into the dark toward the Outside.</p>\n<p>He was out of the cart. He didn’t remember getting out. He was on the platform — Osk had cleared a way, Osk was a wall — and he was at the lip of the well, grabbing at people. Grabbing at coats, at arms. <em>No. No, it isn’t open, please, it isn’t⁠—</em> A man shook him off without even looking at him. A woman slapped his hand away. <em>He came up it</em>, someone said, <em>he came up it, he’s lying, he wants to keep it for himself —</em></p>\n<p>There was a father with a child.</p>\n<p>A young man, stocky and wide like all of them, with a little boy of four or five on his back, the boy’s arms round his neck. He was at the ladder. He had one foot on the first rung.</p>\n<p>Cas got the boy.</p>\n<p>He didn’t decide to. He simply had him. He had the child under the arms and was pulling, and the little boy was screaming, and the father was screaming, twisting round on the ladder, reaching back, <em>give him to me, give him, he’s mine —</em> and Cas held on, and fell backwards onto the platform with the child on top of him, and the father hung there on the ladder with his face working, looking at his son, looking down into the dark.</p>\n<p>“Come back up,” said Cas. He was crying. “Please. Please. Come back up. It isn’t open. I swear to you. I swear it.”</p>\n<p>The father looked at him for a moment with an expression he would never forget. Then he looked at his son. Then he looked down again, into the well, toward the Outside, and his face changed. It filled with something. Hope. Joy. The same joy Cas had seen in the Commons the first night, turned up at him like light.</p>\n<p>“I’ll come back for him,” the father said. “I’ll come back up for him. When I’ve seen it.”</p>\n<p>And he went down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The screaming started about a quarter of an hour later.</p>\n<p>It came up the well. Faint, at first, and very far, from three hundred metres down, where the conduit came out of the bottom of the Anvil into the Shear. It came up the shaft the way the Bell did — stretched, somehow. Slowed. Strange. It didn’t sound like screaming. It sounded like the lowing of cattle. Like the long slow groan of Teo’s voice through the suit. It went on and on, and more voices joined it, one by one, as more of them reached the bottom. It went on for a long time. Nobody who was on that platform ever forgot it.</p>\n<p>The people still on the ladder stopped. They hung there, on the rungs, listening. Then they began to come back up.</p>\n<p>The riggers came. Ione came — with blood on her face from a cut over her eye and her left arm held against her body — and a dozen riggers behind her with rope and knives. They went to every well in the District. There were fourteen. At every one they cut the tops of the ladders free and hauled them up so that nobody else could go down. It took most of a shift. At some of the wells they had to fight to do it.</p>\n<p>The Tollers counted. That was what Tollers did. They went from well to well with their ledgers and asked the people on the platforms who had gone down, and wrote down the names.</p>\n<p>Forty-seven.</p>\n<p>He was sitting on the platform of the well with his back against the scrap-metal wall and the little boy asleep in his lap when Osk came to arrest him.</p>\n<p>He had been sitting there for hours. The boy had cried himself to sleep. Nobody had come for him. Nobody had known who he was. Cas had sat with him, and held him, and listened to the sounds coming up the well until they stopped, and then listened to the silence, and then to the Bell.</p>\n<p>Osk stood over him without saying anything. His hard hat was gone. There was a long scrape down one side of his face.</p>\n<p>“The First Steward’s asked for you,” he said at last.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I’m to bring you.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>Osk looked down at the boy.</p>\n<p>“I’ll take him,” said a voice, and it was Pim — Pim with his face streaked black and grey, Pim who had been everywhere, who had carried water and run messages and dragged the cart — and he knelt down and held out his arms. “I know his gran. On Pump Lane. I’ll take him to her.”</p>\n<p>Cas gave him the boy. The child didn’t wake. Pim staggered a little under the weight and settled him, and looked at Cas, and didn’t say anything, and went.</p>\n<p>Osk put out a hand. Cas took it. The big man pulled him up — gently, the way he’d taught him, <em>stack your bones</em> — and stood holding his arm.</p>\n<p>“I told them,” said Cas. “I told them three times.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Osk. “I heard.”</p>\n<p>Across the platform, at the top of the ramp, there was a disturbance. Wardens — six of them, eight — with staves. And in the middle of them, struggling, Ione. She’d seen Osk take his arm. She was fighting to get to him. She got one arm free and a Warden caught it and she twisted, and the one holding her bad arm wrenched it — not hard, but it was the bad arm — and she cried out, and dropped, and rolled, and was up, and was over the scrap-metal wall of the platform in one long movement, the way she’d gone over it the first day, and gone.</p>\n<p>The Wardens shouted and ran to the wall and looked over it and didn’t follow.</p>\n<p>“Good,” said Osk very softly, to no one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Quiet was a coolant tank.</p>\n<p>It was underneath the Steward’s House, down a long iron stair into the roots of the building, where the old lift terminal had had its machinery. A steel drum, three metres across, three metres deep, set into the keelstone floor, with a round lid on a hinge like the lid of a well. There was a ladder down into it. When Osk took him down the stair and opened the lid, the smell that came up was old water and old rust and something else, something human, very faint, that had soaked into the metal over a hundred and sixty-eight years.</p>\n<p>“Down,” said Osk.</p>\n<p>He went down. It wasn’t far. At the bottom his feet touched a steel floor, damp. He looked up. Osk’s face in the round of the lid, with the lamp-light behind it. For a moment Osk looked as though he were going to say something.</p>\n<p>Then the lid came down.</p>\n<p>It was the darkest dark he had ever been in. Not the dark of a room at night, where there’s always some light, somewhere, a line under a door, a glow from a window. The Lamp had never set on Holdfast; he hadn’t seen real dark for nearly twenty rounds. This was total. He held his hand in front of his face and saw nothing. He shut his eyes and opened them and it made no difference at all.</p>\n<p>And it was silent. The Steward’s House was above him, and the city above that, and the city was — he knew it was — in chaos, in grief, in the streets, the whole of Holdfast awake and shouting. He couldn’t hear any of it. The steel walls and the keelstone and the stone of the House lay over him like a hand over a mouth, and there was nothing, nothing, only the sound of his own breath and his own heart.</p>\n<p>He sat down on the damp steel floor with his back against the curved wall.</p>\n<p>He sat there.</p>\n<p>Some time later — he had no idea how long — it came up through the floor. Rising. Humming in the steel. Filling the tank like water filling a glass, until the whole drum was ringing faintly with it, a single deep note in the dark. It hung. It lasted. It went away.</p>\n<p>The Bell. The hundred and twenty-one thousand, nine hundred and forty-eighth, or the forty-ninth. He didn’t know. He’d lost count, for the first time since his flat on Tier Nine. The first time since 02:14.</p>\n<p>He found that he was counting again. Not the Bell.</p>\n<p>Forty-seven.</p>"}, {"file": "11-the-quiet.md", "label": "Chapter 11", "title": "The Quiet", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4974, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Keep them calm. It is the only mercy I have left to give, and God knows I owe them one.</p><span class=\"attr\">Private journal of Aldous Crane, Year 2 of the Watch (never published)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">In the Quiet, there was nothing to do but count and think, and he was very good at both.</p>\n<p>He counted the Bells. He’d lost the number the first time, in the dark, when the lid came down; so he started again from one. One. Two. He counted his heartbeats between them, to know the interval. About eight hundred. Ten minutes, near enough, if his heart was going the way he thought it was. He counted the rivets he could reach on the curved steel wall of the tank by feel, running his fingers along the seams, and found there were two hundred and eight, and counted them again, and found two hundred and eight again, and felt a small, pathetic, animal comfort in that. He counted the drips from somewhere overhead that fell, every so often, into a puddle in the middle of the floor he couldn’t see: one every forty breaths, give or take.</p>\n<p>And he thought.</p>\n<p>He thought about forty-seven.</p>\n<p>He tried not to. He’d been trying not to since the well, and he was no better at not thinking about things than he’d ever been. He thought about the father on the ladder. <em>I’ll come back for him. When I’ve seen it.</em> He thought about the sound that had come up the conduit, that long, slow, stretched lowing, and what it had actually been — what forty metres of Shear did to a body going down it without a keel — and he knew exactly what it had been, because he’d seen the body at the bottom of Conduit 14, and he’d done the arithmetic, and he could not stop doing it now. He thought about Seren, who’d been the cleverest Toller in two hundred years, and he thought about the Tollers’ ledgers, the names in careful ink. He thought about Ione going down under the crowd at the doors.</p>\n<p>He’d said it three times. <em>The Drop is not open.</em> Three times.</p>\n<p>And they had heard <em>the Outside is alive, and he came up it</em>, and nothing else. Just as Perpetua had said. He’d known they would. He’d known it the way you know a sum. And he had said it anyway, because it was true, and because he had decided he was going to be a man who said true things, and forty-seven people had heard the true thing he said and climbed down a ladder toward it and been torn to pieces in the dark.</p>\n<p>That was what truth did. That was what it had done, the first time he’d tried it in fifteen years.</p>\n<p>He sat with his back against the steel and his knees up and the Bell coming up through the floor, and he found himself in a place he knew very well. He had lived in it for fifteen years. It was quite a small place, and quite a cold one, and it had a very simple shape. It went like this: <em>Everything I do adds to it.</em> The lie added to it. The truth added to it. Silence added to it; he’d tried silence, for fifteen years, and every day of silence had been another day of the Draw eating the future, another day of the Margin falling, another day of interest on interest on interest. There was no move that didn’t make it bigger. There was no way to pay it down. There was only the debt, growing, and him inside it, and the only question that had ever mattered was how and when he could stop.</p>\n<p>He’d come into the Draw thinking the Shear might settle it. Or the Crown. Or whatever was in the Crown. <em>At last</em>, he’d thought, at the window in the Collar. <em>Almost a relief.</em></p>\n<p>He thought it now, in the dark. He didn’t argue with it.</p>\n<p>Then he thought about Teo, in a chair, saying <em>oh no you don’t</em>, and he put his forehead down on his knees and stayed like that for a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d counted nine hundred and forty Bells — no. He’d lost it again. He’d counted something. It had been a long time. His mouth was dry. There was water in the puddle and he’d drunk from it, on his knees, lapping at it with his hand like a dog, and it had tasted of rust.</p>\n<p>The lid opened.</p>\n<p>Light came down. Not much — a lamp, a single flame — but after the dark it was like a knife in his eyes, and he threw his arm over his face. When he lowered it, there was a figure coming down the ladder. Slowly, carefully, an old man’s descent, one rung at a time, with a lamp in one hand and something under his other arm.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane reached the bottom. He stood for a moment, getting his breath. Then he set the lamp down on the steel floor between them, and sat down beside it, cross-legged, with a small grunt, his long black coat pooling round him. He put the thing from under his arm in his lap. It was a book.</p>\n<p>For a while he was silent. He looked at the lamp. Cas looked at him.</p>\n<p>“I used to come down here as a boy,” said Crane at last. “My father was Steward. He used to bring me. He’d lower me in, and shut the lid, and leave me for an hour. Sometimes two. He said a Steward had to know what the Quiet was, if he was going to put people in it.” He looked round at the curved steel walls in the lamplight, the rust, the rivets. “He was right, I suppose. I’ve never put anyone in here without remembering it.”</p>\n<p>“Seren?”</p>\n<p>Crane didn’t flinch. He’d been expecting it.</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds,” he said. “I came down every round. The way I’ve come down now. She wouldn’t speak to me. On the eleventh round she said one thing. She said, <em>You know I’m right.</em> And I said yes.” He looked at the lamp. “And then I signed the order, and the next round they took her up to Well Fourteen, and she went down it. She didn’t look back. I was there. I made myself be there.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came. They both sat through it without moving.</p>\n<p>“How many?” said Cas when it had gone. “Out there. Since.”</p>\n<p>“In the city? Nobody else has died.” Crane’s voice was very level. “There’s been fighting. There was fighting in the Commons and there’s been fighting in the lanes since. The Wardens have been — busy. There are people who want your head. There are more people who want mine. The riggers have taken the Drop District and won’t let a Warden in it. The Tollers’ banner is still hanging in the Commons. I haven’t had it taken down.” He paused. “Half the city is saying the Relief is a demon sent up from the Drop to lure the Watch to its death. And the other half is making lamps.”</p>\n<p>“Lamps?”</p>\n<p>“Lamps. Oil lamps. Wicks, dishes. Every oil press in Holdfast has been running for two rounds. People are pressing algae in their kitchens.” Crane looked at him. “They’re getting ready for the dark, Relief. You told them the Lamp would go out. Some of them believed you.”</p>\n<p>Cas found that he couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t come down here to tell you that,” said Crane. “I came down to ask you why.”</p>\n<p>“Why what?”</p>\n<p>“Why you did it.” The old man’s face in the lamplight was very tired, and very intelligent, and entirely serious. “I asked you to be kind. You were kind, the first time. You gave them nineteen rounds of peace. Nineteen rounds of believing that someone had come for them. And then you stood up in the Commons and you took it away. And forty-seven of them are dead in the Drop. And I would very much like to understand what you thought you were doing.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It went on for a long time.</p>\n<p>He’d think about it afterwards — how long, how quiet, the two of them in the bottom of a steel tank with a single flame between them and the Bell coming and going through the floor. He’d think that he had never in his life had a conversation like it. He’d think that it was the kind of conversation he should have had fifteen years ago, with someone, anyone, and never had.</p>\n<p>“Truth,” said Halloran Crane, “is a luxury.”</p>\n<p>He said it without contempt. The way you’d say that a coat was warm.</p>\n<p>“It’s a thing for people with futures. You can afford to tell people the truth when they have time to do something with it. When they can change their minds, and their lives, and fix what’s broken. My ancestor stood under the Placard on the night of the Kindling in front of nineteen hundred people who had just watched their world end. Their families were gone. Their homes were gone. The sky was on fire. The ones who tried to leave had come back up the wells in — in pieces. What was the truth going to do for them? <em>We did this. It was my fault. We will all die here, and nobody is coming, and there is nothing beyond the Drop but a world that has forgotten us.</em> That was the truth, as far as he knew it. Do you know what nineteen hundred people do with that truth, in the dark, in the first week?”</p>\n<p>“Tell me.”</p>\n<p>“They kill each other,” said Crane. “It’s in the Log. Ruth Tally’s own Log. The Tollers think I haven’t read it. I’ve read it ten times. There were riots in the first week. There was a man who took an axe to the scrubbers because he wanted it all to be over. Eleven people died. And my ancestor stood up under that sign and read it aloud and told them it was a promise. <em>You will not be forgotten.</em> And they stopped.” He opened his hands. “His lie kept nineteen hundred terrified people alive for a hundred and sixty-eight years. It gave us children, and the Tanks, and the Crèche, and the Commons, and every rope and wall and lamp in this city. It gave us thirty-one thousand people. That is what a lie can do.” He looked at Cas. “And your truth, Relief, in a single round? Forty-seven.”</p>\n<p>Cas didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“It killed Seren,” he said at last.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And my — the people I work for, outside. They’ve been telling the same kind of lie for ninety years. <em>It’s stable. It’s safe. The future will pay.</em> I signed one of them. I signed my name under it. And that lie didn’t keep anyone alive, it just — it just kept everything going. It kept the Draw going. It kept them feeding it. Right up until it broke and took nineteen hundred people with it and put them in here. That’s what your ancestor was. He was one of us. He wasn’t lying to keep your founders alive. He was lying because it’s what we <em>do</em>. It’s the only thing we know how to do.”</p>\n<p>Crane was quiet.</p>\n<p>“Your lie kept them alive,” said Cas. “Fine. It kept them alive so they could die calm. That’s all it was ever going to do. It kept them in here. It kept anyone from ever trying — ever <em>once</em> trying — to go up and see if the thing could be closed. You had the Tollers’ numbers. You had Seren’s line. You had a girl who came down from the top of the world with the key in her hand. And you kept them calm. You gave them lamps to pray to and a board that counted the Bells and a notice that said the Quickening was glory. And in forty-seven rounds the Lace was going to touch the Rim and every one of them was going to die in the middle of a hymn.” He heard his voice shaking. “I’d rather they died knowing. I’d rather they had forty-seven rounds of knowing, and could do something with it, even if all they do is make lamps. I’d rather <em>I</em> had. I’d rather I’d known, fifteen years ago, that it was all right to say it. That it wasn’t going to kill anyone to say it.”</p>\n<p>“It did kill people.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Forty-seven.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>know</em>.” It came out of him too loudly and rang off the steel walls, and he put his hands over his face. “I know. I know. I know.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came. They waited for it.</p>\n<p>When it had gone, Crane spoke again, lower.</p>\n<p>“I signed her order,” he said. “Seren’s. You know that. What you don’t know is that I read her numbers, and I checked them myself, and I knew she was right, and I had the order drawn up before she ever painted the wall. I had it drawn up the round she came to me. Just in case.” He looked at the lamp. “Just in case she was the kind of person who’d paint it on the wall. And she was. And I was the kind of person who’d already drawn up the order. That’s what I’ve had to live with. Not that I did it. That I’d got it ready.”</p>\n<p>Cas lowered his hands.</p>\n<p>The old man opened the book in his lap. It was small, bound in cracked black leather, its pages yellow and brittle. He turned them with great care until he found the one he wanted, and then held it out.</p>\n<p>“Aldous Crane’s journal,” he said. “Private. It’s passed from Steward to Steward. Nobody else has ever read it. This is from the second year.”</p>\n<p>Cas took it. He held it close to the lamp. The hand was a fine, sloping, educated hand, a Ministry hand, that had begun beautifully and gone to pieces.</p>\n<p><em>I did not give it to the Lamp. I will write that here where nobody will see it, once, so that it has been said. I did not give it. I held it. She asked me for it and I held it, because I was afraid of what they would say, and then the walkway went and it was gone. That is the truth. It is the only truth I have.</em></p>\n<p><em>They sing about me now. They have painted me on a wall. Every time I walk past it I think I will go mad.</em></p>\n<p><em>But they are calm. The riots have stopped. The children are being born. And so I will keep it, this lie, I will keep it until I die, and I will make my son keep it after me, because it is the only thing standing between them and the dark. Keep them calm. It is the only mercy I have left to give, and God knows I owe them one.</em></p>\n<p>Cas read it twice. Then he closed the book and held it in his lap and didn’t give it back.</p>\n<p>“He knew,” he said.</p>\n<p>“He knew.” Crane took the book from him gently. “Every Steward has known. We’ve known it for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and every one of us has kept it, because he asked us to, and because it seemed like mercy.” He stood up, slowly, his knees cracking. “I’ve been thinking, since the Commons, that perhaps it wasn’t mercy he owed them. That perhaps mercy was just the thing he could afford.”</p>\n<p>He picked up the lamp. Then, after a moment, he put it down again on the floor.</p>\n<p>“Can you close it?” he said. “Really? You and the Tally girl?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“You don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“I know nobody’s ever tried,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>Crane stood looking down at him in the flickering light.</p>\n<p>“I let you finish,” he said at last. “In the Commons. Do you know why?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Because my ancestor didn’t let Ruth Tally finish.” He turned toward the ladder. “I’ve wondered my whole life what she’d have said.”</p>\n<p>He climbed. It took him a long time. At the top he paused, and looked down, and for a moment Cas thought he was going to say something else. But he only looked. Then the lid came down.</p>\n<p>He’d left the lamp.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It burned for a long time. Cas sat and watched it. It was the only light he’d ever been glad of in Holdfast — a little, yellow, living flame, not the Lamp’s violet glare, just a wick in a dish of algae oil — and he watched it burn down, and made himself not count.</p>\n<p>When it had nearly gone out, the lid opened again.</p>\n<p>This time it opened fast. It banged back on its hinge and a big shape filled the round of light at the top and a voice like gravel said, “<em>Now</em>, Relief. Up. Quick as you can,” and a rope came slithering down into the tank.</p>\n<p>He looked up. Osk.</p>\n<p>“Stack your bones,” said Osk. “Come on.”</p>\n<p>He got up. He stacked his bones. He took the rope, and Osk hauled, and he came up out of the Quiet the way he’d come up out of the Drop — dragged over the lip like a fish — and lay on the stone floor at the top gasping, and there was a lamp, and faces over him.</p>\n<p>Ione. Her left wrist was bound in a splint of wood and cord, and there was a line of black stitches over her right eye where the cut had been, and she looked at him lying on the floor with her unhurried grey-green eyes and said, “You’re thinner.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve been in a tank.”</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds. You’d be thinner.” She held out her good hand. “Up.”</p>\n<p>Pim was there too, very dirty, with no rat, for once, on his shoulder. He was carrying the recorder in both hands like a precious thing. “I got it out of your room,” he whispered. “Through the vent. There’s a vent. It’s very small. I got stuck. And the suit’s in the lane, in the cart, under a tarp, it took four of us, and Brother Coll, and one of the riggers is sitting on it.”</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds?” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Eleven,” said Ione. “The same as my mother.” Her face didn’t change. “We couldn’t get to you till Osk had the night watch on the Quiet. Tonight he has it.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at Osk.</p>\n<p>The big man was coiling the rope. He didn’t look up.</p>\n<p>“My brother,” said Osk. “Tam. He went over. At Nine. The round you spoke.” He finished the coil. “He heard you say the Outside was alive. He was always like that, Tam. Heard the first thing and ran. All his life.” He hung the coil on his shoulder. “He didn’t go because you told him the truth. He went because he’d been told a lie so long he couldn’t wait another round to see if it was true.” He looked at Cas then, finally. “I know which one killed him.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went through the shuttered city in the violet dusk with the suit on the cart under a tarpaulin, Osk between the shafts and Pim and two riggers pushing, and Ione walking beside Cas with her good hand under his elbow.</p>\n<p>It was different. He could feel it before he could see it. The lanes were as dim and still as they’d been every night — but there were lights in them now. Not the shrine lamps. Other lights. In windows, behind shutters that had been left open a crack. In doorways. On the steps of houses. Little clay lamps, and tin lamps, and lamps made of old instrument housings with a wick stuck in the top, burning, one or two in every lane. People had put them out. He didn’t understand why at first. Then he did.</p>\n<p><em>They’re getting ready for the dark.</em></p>\n<p>On the side of a scaffold tower near the Commons, someone had painted over part of the mural. Not the whole thing. Only the words. THE GIVING was still there, and the tall grieving man holding up his key. But under it, where it had said FOUNDER CRANE GAVE THE KEY TO THE LAMP, someone had painted, in rough white letters, crossing it out:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HE DROPPED IT</div>\n<p>And under that, in a different hand, in yellow:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SEREN WAS RIGHT</div>\n<p>They came to Toller’s Tower by the back lanes. Perpetua was waiting in the doorway. She was standing — he’d never seen her stand — leaning on Tobiah’s arm, very small and very bent, with a lamp in her other hand. She looked at Cas as he came up the step. She looked him up and down in about a second, as she had the first night.</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds,” she said. “Hmph. You look worse than I do.”</p>\n<p>“I feel worse than you do.”</p>\n<p>“Nobody feels worse than I do. I’m a hundred and eight.” She wasn’t. She was eighty-three; Ione had told him. She jerked her head at the room behind her. “In. Quick. The Wardens’ll be at the Quiet in half a shift.”</p>\n<p>Inside, at the foot of the column, with the lamps burning and the pendulums swinging, she lowered herself back into her chair with a long sigh and put her hand on the stone.</p>\n<p>“Forty-seven,” she said. “We wrote them all down. Every name. We’ll read them out at the Tanks, when it’s time. All forty-seven.” She looked at him. “And then we’ll read out the other number.”</p>\n<p>“What other number?”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand, three hundred and sixty,” said Perpetua Tally. “The ones who didn’t go. The ones who heard you say <em>not before</em>. The ones who went home and made lamps.” Her black eyes were steady on his. “You didn’t do the forty-seven, Relief. A hundred and sixty-eight years did the forty-seven. You did the other number.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t answer. She didn’t seem to expect him to.</p>\n<p>“Now,” she said. “You’ll be wanting your suit.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They got him into it in the round room at the bottom of the tower. It took four of them. The liner, which hung off him now; he’d lost weight in the Quiet, and before. The clam of the suit closing — thunk, thunk, thunk — and the exo waking, and taking his weight, and for the first time in eleven rounds, for the first time in Holdfast, his body didn’t hurt. The keel came into his spine. Teo’s keel, clean and high and true. It had dropped. He could hear it had dropped, since the Shear — a semitone, perhaps; less. It was still singing.</p>\n<p>And there was a light on in the corner of his visor. Amber. A small steady glow.</p>\n<p>MESSAGE — BUFFERED — READY.</p>\n<p>“It groaned,” said Pim. “In your room. For ages. In the middle of the round when they took you. The Wardens were scared of it. They said it was the Drop talking. Nobody would go in.” He looked anxious. “Then it went <em>ding</em>.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the light for a moment. Then he played it.</p>\n<p>“Hale.” Teo’s voice, in his ear, very close. He closed his eyes. “Got all of them. The Tollers and the rats and the wedding and the — and her. Seren. Thank you for telling me her name.” A pause. “And the last one. You’re going to tell them. All of it. On the — what did you call it — Kindling Round.” Another pause. He could hear her breathing. “Christ, Hale. Good. Good. I’m proud of you. You won’t want to hear that, so I’m saying it into a machine that’s going to take a day to get to you, and you can’t stop me.”</p>\n<p>He heard her shift in the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right. Outside news, since you’ll want it. The Collar’s been on the hardline. Gaunt. I told him you were reseating Six. I’ve never lied to the Calm in my life before today and I’ll tell you what, it’s easy. It’s the easiest thing I’ve ever done. That frightens me more than the Lattice does.” A breath. “Averna’s browning out. They’re saying on the line that the fusion plants at Kovač are down to half, and the Canopy’s flickering, and they’ve started rolling blackouts on the upper tiers. There’s no announcement. Just the lights going off one tier at a time. People are very calm, Gaunt says. He sounds like he’s going to cry.</p>\n<p>“And I checked on your girl.”</p>\n<p>He opened his eyes.</p>\n<p>“Civil Defence has the relocation lists. I’ve still got a login, from in here, on the hardline — don’t ask. She’s in the school on Tier Thirty with her mother, like you said. She’s fine. There’s a note on the list next to her name from the welfare officer. It says <em>asking everyone about stars</em>.” A pause, and something that might have been a laugh. “So she’s fine.</p>\n<p>“I’m fine. The lee’s fine. It moved a bit, about twenty-five minutes ago — the edge came across the floor, maybe a hand’s width. I moved the chair. It’s fine. I’m bored out of my skull. I’ve read the fire regulations twice.” She was quiet for a moment. “Go and do it, Cas. Whatever it is. And then bring them out. All of them. Bring them out and I’ll be here.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He stood in the suit in the middle of the Tollers’ round room, with the lamps burning and the pendulums swinging and Perpetua watching him from her chair, and he didn’t say anything at all.</p>\n<p>It was the first time she’d used his name.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Perpetua said goodbye to him first. She did it the way she did everything: quickly, and without dressing it.</p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” she said. “Off you go. Up the cursed spindle with my granddaughter. Close the Lamp.” She sniffed. “Try not to fall off.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll try.”</p>\n<p>“I’m keeping the Log.” She put her knotted hand on the steel box on the workbench beside her. “Somebody should be here, with it, when you come back with the end of it. Somebody should write the last page.” She looked at him with her bright black eyes. “You’ll tell me how it ends. Won’t you.”</p>\n<p>“If I can.”</p>\n<p>“Hmph.” She took her hand off the box. “That’ll do.”</p>\n<p>Then she looked at Ione.</p>\n<p>Ione was standing by the door with her rope over her shoulder and her wrist in its splint and the key at her throat. She’d been waiting. She didn’t come forward. And Perpetua didn’t say anything to her. She only looked. It went on and on — the old woman in the chair and the young one by the door, the lamps between them, the pendulums swinging, the Bell coming up through the column and fading — and Cas understood that he was watching eleven years go by. Eleven years of a girl who came down from the top of the world with grey in her hair and a key in her hand, and a grandmother who’d lost her daughter to the Drop and then watched the granddaughter go up after something nobody else believed was there. Eleven years of that girl being called mad in the lanes. Eleven years of her sitting on this step in the dusk, eating greenbread.</p>\n<p>“Go on,” said Perpetua Tally, very softly. “Go and finish it, Ione.”</p>\n<p>Ione nodded once. She turned and went out.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Spindle Four stood at the far edge of the city, in the Drop District, alone.</p>\n<p>The riggers had let them through. They were on every corner, in every lane of the District — the riggers’ tower had become a fortress, lamps on every platform, ropes across every approach — and they stood aside for the cart and watched it go by in silence. Some of them touched the tarpaulin as it passed. One of them, a woman with a scarred face, said, “Haul, Ione,” under her breath, and Ione said, “Haul, Bett.”</p>\n<p>The spindle’s foot was a ruin. The lift terminal at its base had collapsed in the Founders’ time, when the lift fell — the whole cage and a kilometre of rack-rail coming down together, Pim said, with a sound they’d heard in the Commons — and nobody had ever cleared it. The wreckage lay in a great twisted heap of rusted girders and snapped rail and fallen keelstone, overgrown with pale vine. And behind it, rising out of it, the spindle went up.</p>\n<p>He’d seen it every day from the loft. He’d seen all of them every day. He’d never stood at the foot of one before.</p>\n<p>It was a hundred metres across at its base. It went straight up out of the wreckage into the light, a vast black lattice of struts and braces and cross-members, open, skeletal, the violet glare of the Lamp coming through it in a million bright diamonds, and it went up. And up. And up. It didn’t narrow, at first; then, very gradually, it did. It went up until the struts blurred into a texture and the texture into a line and the line into nothing, into the glare, into the fine bright falling tendrils of the Lace, four kilometres over his head.</p>\n<p>Osk and Pim and the riggers got him out of the cart. He stood in the suit at the foot of the wreck, looking up. Ione stood beside him, with her head tipped back.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Pim, in a very small voice. “It’s big.”</p>\n<p>Ione took her rope off her shoulder and began to uncoil it, favouring the splinted wrist, and started tying it into the harness they’d spliced for the suit, her fingers moving fast.</p>\n<p>“Don’t look up,” she said, without looking at him. “It only gets taller.”</p>"}, {"file": "12-interlude-two-hours-in-a-chair.md", "label": "Interlude", "title": "Two Hours in a Chair", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4190, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Nobody’s keeping score. Nobody ever was.</p><span class=\"attr\">Teo Lisk, to a man in a keel suit, Lattice Station 14-C</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\"><strong>Minute 0.</strong></p>\n<p>She watched him go up the ladder, and then she watched him stop being a person.</p>\n<p>It was the strangest thing she had ever seen, and she had seen a stack fire in Kovač turn a forty-storey housing tower into a candle. He climbed. Ordinary. Grey suit, humming keel, her keel, gauntlets on the rungs, one after the other, climbing on the back side of the ladder where the boy’s map said to climb. Thirty metres. Thirty-five. And then he reached the part of the shaft where the air was wrong, the trembling curtain, and he went into it — and he <em>stuttered</em>. That was the only word. He was on one rung and then three rungs up and then two rungs up again and then five. Bits of him were ahead of other bits. For a second she saw his arm reaching up out of the top of the curtain while his boots were still under it and the middle of him was a smear, a wet grey streak on the air, like a thumbprint on a lens.</p>\n<p>Then he was above it. And he was moving so fast that she couldn’t follow him. A blur, going up the shaft like something fired. Then a dot. Then nothing.</p>\n<p>“Talking to you,” she said into her radio. “Still here. Keep going. Nearly there.”</p>\n<p>Her radio made a noise like a bird.</p>\n<p>She sat back in the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk to the empty room. “Right, then.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 1.</strong></p>\n<p>She took stock, because that was what you did.</p>\n<p>Station air: good. Pressure: good. There was a panel by the door that told her so, and she believed it, because her ears weren’t popping and she wasn’t dizzy. Water: a cooler, two-thirds full, with a stack of paper cups. Food: the suit’s ration paste, which was vile, and a first-aid cabinet, which on inspection contained bandages, burn gel, a foil blanket, a bottle of painkillers with most of the painkillers gone, and a single packet of digestive biscuits, one-third eaten, with a sell-by date nine months ago.</p>\n<p>She ate one. It was soft. She ate another.</p>\n<p>The suit without its keel was just a suit. Heavy. The exo worked. She could walk about. Every so often the room seemed to sway, very slightly, like the deck of a ship, and she’d put her hand out to the back of the chair and it would pass. The lee, the calm patch, the pocket of ordinary. It filled the booth. Just about. She’d dropped washers at the edges: by the door, by the far wall, in the corners. Straight down, every one. Good.</p>\n<p>She looked out of the viewport at the ladder going up into the trembling air, and at the bottom of the ladder, the shape lying crumpled at its foot.</p>\n<p>“Farr,” she said. “You’d have had a theory about all this.”</p>\n<p>The room didn’t answer. She hadn’t really expected it to.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 2.</strong></p>\n<p>She went down through the hatch with the foil blanket.</p>\n<p>It was stupid. It served no purpose. She knew that. She knelt beside the body at the bottom of the conduit — the little broad body in its patchwork clothes, its legs white bone and its face half a face — and unfolded the blanket, and laid it over, from the head down, and tucked it round. The foil crackled. It shone in the pearly light. The little yellow lamp on its cord was still showing at the neck; she tucked that under too.</p>\n<p>“There you go, love,” she said. “Whoever you are.”</p>\n<p>She went back up. She shut the hatch.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 3.</strong></p>\n<p>The radio chirped.</p>\n<p>Not like before. Not the bird-squeaks she’d been getting since he went through, the little ragged scraps of him arriving squashed. This was a single long <em>tweeee</em>, rising, about the length of a breath, and then a click.</p>\n<p>And the little amber light came on at the bottom of her visor. RECEIVING. BUFFERING. RETIMING.</p>\n<p>She sat forward in the chair.</p>\n<p>It took the suit a minute and a half to chew it out. She watched the light the whole time. Then it went <em>ding</em>, and there he was, Casimir Hale, his dry, tired, clever voice in her ear — and it was <em>long</em>. It went on and on. She sat in the chair and listened to him talk, and her mouth fell open, and after a while she realised it had been open for some time, and shut it.</p>\n<p><em>There’s a city in here, Teo.</em></p>\n<p>Thirty-one thousand. A hundred and sixty-eight years. Rats with the names of officials. The Tending, sung wrong. The Section 9 placard in a church.</p>\n<p><em>They called me the Relief.</em></p>\n<p>She laughed out loud. She couldn’t help it. She sat alone in a booth at the bottom of the world and laughed until she had to wipe her eyes with the back of her gauntlet, and her visor got in the way, so she took the helmet off and laughed some more.</p>\n<p>And then he said the other thing. <em>I lied to them. Within an hour.</em> And he said it the way you’d tell a doctor where it hurt.</p>\n<p>She stopped laughing.</p>\n<p>It had been three minutes. Three minutes, on her suit clock, since he’d climbed up that ladder. He’d got to the top, and been pulled out by a stranger, and carried through a city, and made a saint, and stood up in front of twenty thousand people, and gone to bed, and sat on the floor and talked to her about it — and all of that was <em>a day</em>, for him. Most of a day. While she’d eaten two biscuits and covered a dead woman with a blanket.</p>\n<p>“Bloody hell, Hale,” she said.</p>\n<p>Then she thought about it for a moment, and put the helmet back on, and pressed the button on her own recorder, and said: <em>Right. Your first chirp’s come through.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 11.</strong></p>\n<p>The hardline rang.</p>\n<p>She hadn’t known it was there until it rang. It was a telephone — an old one, a handset on a hook on the wall by the dead monitors, grey plastic, a curly cord. It rang with a bell. An actual bell. She stared at it for three rings and then got up and picked it up.</p>\n<p>“Lattice Station Fourteen-C.”</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk.” Gaunt. Of course it was. That soft voice, as if he were ringing to ask whether she’d enjoyed a book he’d lent her. “What a relief. We weren’t sure the line would still be live. We lost your pucks about twenty minutes ago; this is the old copper, it runs all the way round the Lattice. I’ve been ringing every station on it.” A pause. “Is Dr Hale with you?”</p>\n<p>She looked out of the viewport at the ladder.</p>\n<p>“No,” she said.</p>\n<p>“No?”</p>\n<p>“He’s gone up. Through the Shear. With my keel. His was damaged.” She kept her voice very flat. “Farr’s dead.”</p>\n<p>A silence on the line.</p>\n<p>“I’m very sorry,” said Gaunt, and to her considerable surprise, it sounded as though he were. “He was — very young.” Another pause. “And Dr Hale is — proceeding?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Proceeding to the Crown.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“To reseat Six.”</p>\n<p>The chip. Silver seal. <em>Any means.</em> Underlined. She’d dropped it in a coolant channel and watched it go.</p>\n<p>“He’s reseating Six,” said Teo Lisk. “As we speak.”</p>\n<p>She’d never lied to the Calm in her life. Not once. Not in twenty-nine years. You didn’t. It wasn’t that anyone had ever told her not to; it was simply that you didn’t, the way you didn’t put your hand in a fire. And it came out of her as smooth and easy as water out of a tap, and Gaunt said, “Oh, that’s good. That’s very good. The Minister will be so relieved,” and she stood there holding the grey plastic handset and felt it go all the way through her, cold, like swallowing ice.</p>\n<p><em>Easy</em>. It was the easiest thing she’d ever done.</p>\n<p>“Major,” said Gaunt, “I won’t keep you. I only wanted you to know — the grid’s failing. Kovač is at half. We’ve started the rolling cuts on the upper tiers. We’re telling people it’s maintenance.” His voice changed, very slightly. “People are being very calm. I’ve never seen anything like it. They sit in the dark in their flats and wait for the lights to come back. Nobody’s asking. Nobody’s asking anything.” He stopped. “I find I’d almost rather they did.”</p>\n<p>She let the silence sit.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Gaunt. “Do give Dr Hale my regards, if you speak to him.”</p>\n<p>He hung up.</p>\n<p>She put the handset back on its hook. She sat back down in the chair. After a while she noticed that her hands were shaking, and she put them flat on her knees and held them there until they stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minutes 12 to 60.</strong></p>\n<p>The chirps kept coming. About every four minutes. A long rising <em>tweeee</em>, a click, a minute and a half of amber light, a <em>ding</em>. And then Cas, talking.</p>\n<p>She got used to the rhythm of it. She’d sit in the chair with her boots up on the dead monitor desk and wait for the next one, the way you wait for the next episode of something. Four minutes. Four. Five. A round of his, she worked out, for every four minutes of hers: he was sending her a day at a time. She’d hear the chirp and her whole body would go still.</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer them. She started to, twice, and stopped. Anything she said would land on him most of a day later, in the middle of whatever he was doing, and what he was doing — she could hear it — was better than anything she had to tell him from a chair. She’d answer when there was something worth a day of his time.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Tollers. About a tower full of pendulums, and an old woman with eyes like a bird who’d made him soup, and a column of black stone with every Bell of a hundred and sixty-eight years chalked on it in fives. He told her about a Log. About letters to a little girl called Maddy who was seven. That one she had to stop and play again, and then she sat with it for a while, with the helmet off, looking at the ladder, before she could listen to the next.</p>\n<p>He told her about the market. About rat. About a boy called Pim who named his rats after officials.</p>\n<p>He told her about Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t say much, at first. <em>There’s a woman.</em> She’d pulled him out of the well. She had a white streak in her hair. She was a rigger. She had a key. She’d been somewhere, up near the top, and come back different, and nobody believed her. That was all, the first time. But then the next chirp he mentioned her again. And the next. <em>Ione says. Ione showed me. I was up in Ione’s loft.</em> She was in every one. By the fifth or sixth, Teo realised she could picture her: a short, broad, scarred woman with a rope on her shoulder and a way of looking at you that made you feel she’d read the end of the book.</p>\n<p>And his voice was changing.</p>\n<p>She noticed that too, around minute forty. She played an early one again to be sure, and then a later one, one after the other. The early ones were him: dry, careful, flat, a man reading out a list. The later ones weren’t. They were — she didn’t have the word. They had <em>weather</em> in them. He laughed, sometimes. He stopped in the middle of sentences because he’d seen something out of a window and wanted to tell her what it was. He talked about a wedding on a rooftop and she could hear, under the words, that he had been happy at it. For an hour, on a rooftop, with a cup of something that took the skin off his throat, he’d been happy.</p>\n<p>“Well,” Teo said to nobody. “Look at you.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 34.</strong></p>\n<p>She found the login by accident.</p>\n<p>She’d been poking at the dead monitors out of boredom, and one of them wasn’t dead — a little grey terminal under the desk, on the same old copper as the hardline, humming to itself. It had a Civil Defence emblem on its login screen. Some maintenance link. She typed in her own service number and password, not expecting anything, and it let her in.</p>\n<p>She sat and looked at it for a moment. Then she pulled up the relocation lists.</p>\n<p>It took her four minutes to find Nell Hale. Tier Thirty, Hollowmere Academy sports hall, with mother Sabine Hale-Okoro. Status: well. And a note in the welfare column in the slightly harried shorthand of a volunteer who’d been awake for thirty hours: <em>Child v. calm. Asking everyone re: stars (?). Has lizard (not hers).</em></p>\n<p>Teo laughed out loud in the empty room.</p>\n<p>Then she typed in her own sons’ names.</p>\n<p>Joss first. University accommodation, relocated to a transit hall on Tier Forty. Status: well. No note.</p>\n<p>Mattias. Kovač. Kovač wasn’t on the relocation lists; Kovač had its own. She dug. It took her ten minutes. She found him in the end on a Kovač municipal list, a crew list, not a relocation list: <em>M. Lisk, electrician, Kovač Fusion Station B, emergency staff, on shift</em>. On shift. At the plant that was holding up the whole grid at half power.</p>\n<p>Of course he was.</p>\n<p>She sat looking at his name on the screen for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she took the marker out of the pocket of her liner — the black marker from the supervisor’s desk in B-7, the one Hale had written backwards with on their side of the glass; she’d picked it up off the gallery floor on the way out, without telling him, because she hadn’t been able to leave it — and she got up and went to the viewport, and in the bottom corner, small, where she’d see it from the chair, she wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MATTIAS<br>JOSS</div>\n<p>She looked at them. Then, underneath, she added:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">BEN</div>\n<p>And then, after a moment:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SEREN (THE LADY)</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 52.</strong></p>\n<p>The lee moved.</p>\n<p>She was halfway through a message — the one about the wedding, the riggers tying their wrists together — when she felt the chair tip. Very slightly. A lean to the left, like a car taking a bend. She sat up. The room swayed. On the floor by the far wall, where she’d dropped a washer when she first came in, the washer slid.</p>\n<p>It slid about a hand’s width across the floor, toward the corner, and stopped.</p>\n<p>She watched the corner. She watched it until her eyes ached. Nothing else happened. The room steadied.</p>\n<p>She got up and dragged the chair a metre to the right, into the middle of the room, and sat back down in it, and pressed play again. When she recorded her next message, she mentioned it. <em>The edge came across the floor, maybe a hand’s width. I moved the chair.</em> Casual. As if she were telling him the weather.</p>\n<p>It was the weather. That was the thing. It was only the weather.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 64.</strong></p>\n<p><em>I’m going to tell them.</em></p>\n<p>She listened to that one sitting very still. He said it quietly, at the end of a long message about a rigger crew that had been caught by the Lace and turned to bones. Kindling Round. Three rounds away, for him. Everything. The Outside, the test, the Deputy Minister, the debt, the lock, the key, Ione. In front of the whole city.</p>\n<p>She knew what it would cost him. She’d sat next to him at the canteen window in the Collar and watched him not answer <em>why</em>. She’d seen his face at the booth when he thought the Shear might settle things for him. She knew.</p>\n<p>She played it again. She played it a good many times, over the next quarter of an hour, with the helmet off and her boots up and her eyes on the ladder.</p>\n<p>Then, at minute seventy-seven, she pressed record and said, <em>Christ, Hale. Good.</em> And a lot of other things, most of which she’d forgotten as soon as she’d said them, except that she’d told him she was proud of him, and she’d meant it, and she’d been glad he couldn’t hear it for a day.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minutes 78 to 127.</strong></p>\n<p>He didn’t send anything else for a long time.</p>\n<p>She understood, doing the sum on the back of the fire regulations with a ballpoint she found in the desk, that for him it would be — what? Rounds and rounds. Days. Weeks. She didn’t know what had happened. She sat in the chair and waited.</p>\n<p>She read the fire regulations again. She did the sum again. She ate the last digestive. She went down the hatch and sat for a while with the lady in the blanket, just sitting, not saying anything, because it seemed wrong to leave her alone. She came back up. She dropped washers. She moved the chair once more, at minute ninety-seven, when the washer by the door slid, and again at minute a hundred and eighteen, when the one in the far corner did. The room was smaller now. Not much. You wouldn’t notice, unless you were dropping washers. The calm part of it was a circle in the middle of the floor, about three metres across, and the chair was in it, and she was in the chair.</p>\n<p>At minute a hundred and ten, the hardline rang. She let it ring. It rang twenty times and stopped.</p>\n<p>She talked to Ben. Out loud. She told him about Mattias on shift at Kovač, and about Joss, who’d wanted to be a pilot when he was five and a poet at fifteen and was now doing something with computers she couldn’t pronounce. She told him about her husband, Anders, who’d dropped dead in the kitchen eleven years ago in the middle of making toast, and how she’d come home from a shift and found the toast still in the toaster, and how for a year afterwards she couldn’t be in the room when anybody made toast. She told him Jonah would get the dog. She’d see to it. She’d buy the bloody dog herself.</p>\n<p>At minute a hundred and twenty-three she realised she’d been crying for some time, and stopped, and blew her nose on a bandage from the first-aid kit.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 128.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Tweeeeeeeeeeee.</em></p>\n<p>It was the longest chirp yet. It went on for three breaths. Then a click, and another <em>tweeee</em>, shorter, and a third, and the amber light came on and stayed on for nearly four minutes while the suit chewed, and she sat forward in the chair with both hands gripping its arms.</p>\n<p><em>Ding.</em></p>\n<p>He’d been in a tank. Eleven rounds, in the dark.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Commons. He told it plainly, flatly, the old way, the way he’d talked at the gate — a man reading a list — and she could hear what it was costing him to do it that way, because it was the only way he could do it at all. The truth, and the banner, and a man shouting <em>it’s open</em>. Forty-seven. A little boy he’d pulled off a ladder. A father who’d said <em>I’ll come back for him</em>. The sound that came up the well.</p>\n<p>She sat with her fist pressed against her mouth.</p>\n<p>Then the Quiet. Then an old man with a lamp. Then a big Warden with a rope. Then the Tollers’ tower again, and the old woman, and the number she’d given him — <em>thirty-one thousand, three hundred and sixty</em> — and then, in a different voice, a lighter voice, almost wondering: <em>Teo, they’re making lamps. The whole city. They’re putting them out in the lanes. They’re getting ready for the dark.</em></p>\n<p>And then:</p>\n<p><em>We’re going up. Tonight. Spindle Four. Ione and me. The electronics will go, up there, Ben said they would, so this might be the last you get. If it works, the Lamp goes out. The Shear goes. Time in here will be the same as time out there. They’ll be able to walk down. Thirty-one thousand of them. All at once, down fourteen wells, into the Works. Come and get them, Teo. Bring everyone. Bring blankets. Bring⁠—</em></p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p><em>There’s another file. It’s for Nell. It’s everything I’ve recorded for her, since I got here. It’s about a month. I don’t know if I’ll — if you get out, and I don’t. Would you give it to her? Please. And tell her⁠—</em></p>\n<p>Another pause, longer.</p>\n<p><em>No. It’s in the file. It’s all in the file.</em></p>\n<p>And then, very quietly:</p>\n<p><em>Thank you for the keel.</em></p>\n<p><em>Ding.</em></p>\n<p>A second file arrived behind it. The suit showed it on her visor: a little icon, a size, a name. NELL. A month. A month of a man talking to his daughter, squashed into a chirp three breaths long.</p>\n<p>She sat in the chair and looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 130.</strong></p>\n<p>She recorded her answer.</p>\n<p>She did it with the helmet off, holding the recorder close to her mouth, sitting forward in the chair with her elbows on her knees. She didn’t plan it. She knew it would be the last one — knew it the way you know, sometimes, at the top of a flood barrier with the water coming up, that this is the last thing you’ll get to say to somebody for a while, maybe ever. So she said it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know, afterwards, all of what she said. Some of it she remembered. That she’d give Nell the file herself, by hand, and sit with her while she listened. That she’d tell Jonah about the dog. That he was to look after the rigger with the white streak in her hair, because it sounded as though nobody had for a while. That he was to come back and buy her the large one he owed her. That her sons were called Mattias and Joss and she’d written their names on the window where she could see them, and she’d written Ben’s under, and Seren’s, and if he didn’t mind she’d write his, too, now.</p>\n<p>That guilt was a lousy reason. That she’d said it before and she’d say it again because he was the kind of thick-headed bastard who needed to hear things twice.</p>\n<p>That she was fine.</p>\n<p>That she’d be here.</p>\n<p><em>Bring them out, Cas.</em></p>\n<p>She pressed send. The suit chirped, a tiny sound, a squeak, two whole minutes of her gone up the ladder in a blink. She sat back.</p>\n<p>She took the marker and went to the window and wrote, under SEREN (THE LADY):</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">CAS</div>\n<p>She stood looking at it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 132.</strong></p>\n<p>The washer by the door slid.</p>\n<p>Not a hand’s width. It slid a metre, rattling across the floor, and hit the leg of the desk, and spun, and lay still. And the one by the far wall slid, toward the corner. And the room <em>leaned</em>.</p>\n<p>She put her hand out to the chair.</p>\n<p>The edge of the lee was coming across the floor. She could see it now. She hadn’t been able to see it before, but she could now: a line on the floor, a sort of shimmer, a ripple in the air a hand’s height off the ground, like heat over a road. On one side of it, the dust lay flat. On the other side it was moving, drifting, crawling very slowly toward the corners. The line was about a metre from the door. As she watched, it moved closer.</p>\n<p>She did the thing you did. She took stock. Station air: good. Pressure: good. Suit: no keel. Exit: through the door, into a Lattice full of weather, four kilometres from the gate, or through the hatch, into the conduit, beside the lady in the blanket, where the Shear was forty metres up and the weather was whatever the weather was down there.</p>\n<p>The line moved closer.</p>\n<p>She took the chair and dragged it to the far corner of the room, the corner by the viewport, as far from the door as she could get, and set it down, and sat in it, and put her boots up flat against the wall.</p>\n<p>The line was two metres away now. Then a metre and a half. The room swayed, and swayed back. On the other side of the viewport, in the conduit, the Shear trembled on the air as it always had, the ladder going up through it, jumping and smearing. Above it, far up, the pale bright disc of light.</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk sat in her chair in the corner with her boots against the wall and her hands folded in her lap, and watched it come.</p>\n<p>“Come on, Hale,” she said.</p>"}, {"file": "13-the-lees.md", "label": "Chapter 12", "title": "The Lees", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": true, "words": 5794, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Chalk falls true in a Lee. Chalk falls crooked out of one. When chalk falls up, pray.</p><span class=\"attr\">Riggers’ saying, Holdfast</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">For the first kilometre, it was only climbing.</p>\n<p>That was how Ione put it, at the bottom, tying him in. <em>The first kilometre’s only climbing. After that it’s the Lees.</em> And he’d nodded as if he understood, the way he’d nodded at so much in Holdfast, and found out what it meant by doing it.</p>\n<p>Spindle Four was hollow. From the city it looked like a solid black tower, but from inside it was a cage — a vast open lattice of keelstone struts, a hundred metres across at the base, rising around them in a great latticed tube, with the violet light of the Lamp pouring in through every gap in a million bright slanting bars. Up the inside of the lattice, clinging to it, ran the old maintenance ways: iron ladders and iron stairs and narrow steel walkways, bolted to the struts, zigzagging up and up the inside of the tube in flights and landings and switchbacks until they vanished into the glare. The lift’s rack-rail had run up the centre once. Its stump still stood there, a twisted black mast rising out of the wreckage at the bottom and ending, ragged, a few hundred metres up, where it had snapped.</p>\n<p>They climbed the stairs. Where there were no stairs, they climbed the ladders. Where the ladders had rusted through or fallen away — and there were many places where they had — Ione went up the bare lattice itself, finding holds in the struts with her hands and her boots, her splinted wrist held a little stiffly, the rope paying out behind her from the coil on her shoulder. When she reached the next walkway she’d tie off and call down, and he’d come up after her on the rope. The suit’s exo did most of it. The keel hummed. The harness she’d spliced round his hardpoints held.</p>\n<p>It was only climbing. It was the hardest thing he’d ever done.</p>\n<p>Because he got heavier.</p>\n<p>He felt it begin at about three hundred metres: not a sudden thing, not a gust or a shear like the Works, but a slow, patient, steady increase, as though someone were adding sand to his pockets a grain at a time. At five hundred, the exo’s whine had changed pitch. At seven hundred, he noticed his breathing — not from effort, but from weight, the sheer work of lifting his own ribs. The suit’s display, when he looked, said LOCAL G: 1.8. At the bottom of the spindle, in Holdfast, it had said 1.4.</p>\n<p>“You’ll weigh half as much again as you do in the city by two kilometres,” Ione said, when he told her. She was sitting on a walkway above him, eating greenbread, her legs dangling over the drop. “Twice by three. By the Rim — I don’t know. I wasn’t counting.”</p>\n<p>“How do <em>you</em> do it?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with mild surprise. “I was born to it,” she said. “We all were. Holdfast’s heavy. I’ve been climbing spindles since I was nine.” She considered. “And I’m small. The small ones do better. Less to lift.”</p>\n<p>She held her hand out flat, palm up, over the edge of the walkway. There was a little heap of white chalk in it. She blew on it, gently, and the chalk went off her palm in a puff, and drifted, and fell. He watched it. It fell straight down, in a thin white curtain, all the way into the bars of violet light below until he lost it.</p>\n<p>“That’s the last true chalk for a while,” she said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At a kilometre, the Lees began.</p>\n<p>He understood what she’d meant, then, about the chalk. The Lamp’s push, which had been growing steadily as they climbed, began to <em>vary</em>. You’d climb a flight of stairs, heavier and heavier, and come out onto a landing near one of the great keelstone struts — the huge black beams that braced the lattice, some of them five or ten metres thick — and suddenly the weight would ease. Not all of it. Some. As though a hand pressing on your shoulders had lifted a little. And then you’d step away from the strut and it would come back.</p>\n<p>“That’s the strut pulling,” said Ione. “Against the Lamp. It’s like the Anvil. It’s keelstone, it pulls. Near it, the pull and the push fight. You weigh less.” She blew chalk. It went off her palm and drifted — not down, but down and <em>sideways</em>, toward the strut, in a long slanting curve. “See? It’s pulling the chalk, too. Crooked chalk. That’s a Lee. A little one.”</p>\n<p>The Lees were how you climbed a spindle. He learned them over the next three rounds — their rounds, sleep to sleep. Down in the city, the rounds had already begun to slip behind them. There were little ones by every strut, where you could stand and breathe. There were big ones — rare, precious — where two or three struts came together, where the pulls added up and cancelled the Lamp’s push almost completely, where the chalk fell so crooked that it hung in the air, turning slowly, and you could lie down on the walkway and feel as light as you’d felt in the city. Those were where you rested. Those were where you slept. The riggers had built little caches in the big ones, generations ago: a platform of planks, a water-catcher of canvas, a box of stores bolted to the strut. Most of them had rotted away. Ione knew the ones that hadn’t.</p>\n<p>And the Lees moved. That was the thing that had sent her up alone on the eighth round, while he sat on the steps of the Steward’s House blessing babies and not hearing a word anyone said to him. The Lamp was getting weaker — the Lightening, the debt coming due — and as it weakened, the balance between its push and the struts’ pull shifted. Lees she’d climbed through at seventeen had closed up. New ones had opened. Some had drifted a few metres up a strut. Some had split in two. She’d found them all, on that trip, by chalk and plumb bob and feel, and marked them. As they climbed he saw her marks: little white arrows scratched into the black keelstone with a nail, and numbers, and here and there a word. <em>GOOD. SMALL. BAD — GO RIGHT. SLEEP.</em></p>\n<p>“You did all this in three rounds?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Three down there. More like five up here.” She didn’t look round. “The higher you go, the more of it you get. And I was a lot faster without you.”</p>\n<p>He laughed. It came out of him before he could stop it, a real laugh, surprised, loud in the suit, and he heard it echo round the inside of the spindle, and she glanced down at him from the ladder above with an expression he couldn’t read.</p>\n<p>“What?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” She went on climbing. “You don’t do that much.”</p>\n<p>“Laugh?”</p>\n<p>“Mm.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He thought about the hangar, and a spanner folded in half, and Teo saying <em>Sounds like working for the Commonweal</em>. He thought about the canteen and the tea that was mostly a structural material. He thought about the gallery floor and MINISTER →.</p>\n<p>“I’m getting better,” he said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the second round of the climb — the second time they slept — he looked down.</p>\n<p>They were at two kilometres. They’d stopped in a big Lee, one of the rigger caches, a platform of old planks lashed to the junction of three struts, with a canvas water-catcher and a steel box of rotted stores and a view straight down through the open lattice of the spindle to the city. He was lying on his back on the platform, in the suit, feeling almost light for the first time in a round, and he rolled over onto his front and looked down through the gap between the planks.</p>\n<p>Holdfast was very small.</p>\n<p>It lay at the bottom of the world, a tiny intricate patch of scaffold and container and keelstone spread across the black disc of the Anvil, the other seven spindles rising out of it like the pillars of a temple seen from its roof. The Commons was a speck. The Drop District was a smudge at the edge. Smoke went up from it in threads so fine he could hardly see them.</p>\n<p>And as he watched, it began to go dark.</p>\n<p>It started at the centre — at the Steward’s House, at the foot of Spindle One — and went outward. A wave. A sort of dimming, a darkening, spreading out across the city from the middle to the edges. The shutters, he realised. The shutters coming down at the end of second shift. The night horn — he couldn’t hear it; he was too high, too far — and ten thousand shutters closing.</p>\n<p>But it was slow. He’d stood in the lanes and heard that wave go past, the clatter and bang of it; it took a minute, perhaps, to cross the whole city. From here it crept. It spread across Holdfast the way ink spreads into wet paper, lane by lane, so slowly that he could watch a single street go dark, house by house, and count his breaths between the houses. He lay there on the planks and watched it for what felt like three or four minutes before it reached the edges.</p>\n<p>“What⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Watch the smoke,” said Ione. She’d rolled over beside him on the planks and was looking down too, her chin on her folded arms.</p>\n<p>He watched the smoke. The thin grey threads going up from the city’s chimneys. They were drifting, the way they always drifted, the Lamp’s heat stirring them — but slowly. Far too slowly. They leaned and curled like threads of ink in syrup, as though the air down there had thickened.</p>\n<p>“We’re faster than them,” said Ione. “Up here. The higher you go, the faster you live. Down there, that’s a minute. Up here, it’s three, near enough. And it gets longer. By the top of the Lees you’ll look down and they’ll be a picture. Nothing moving at all. We’ll sleep a whole sleep and wake up and they’ll still be in the same night.” She blew a little puff of chalk out over the edge and watched it drift away sideways, turning. “We’ll be at the top before they’ve had two rounds down there. Maybe less.”</p>\n<p>“It’s holding its breath,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” She sounded pleased that he’d seen it. “That’s what I thought. When I was seventeen. I lay here — right here, this cache — and looked down at it and thought, <em>the city’s holding its breath</em>. And I thought: if I stayed up here, if I stayed long enough, high enough, the whole of Holdfast would stand still for me. Nobody would grow up. Nobody else would go down a well. I could keep them all exactly as they were, as long as I liked.” She was quiet. “I didn’t, though. I went on up.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her lying beside him, her chin on her arms, looking down at the still city. At the white streak in her hair, bright in the Lamp’s light. At the stitches over her eye.</p>\n<p>“Why?” he said. “At seventeen. Why did you go up?”</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer for a while. Down below, the city went dark.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell you,” she said. “Later. When we’re higher. It’s easier to tell higher up.” She rolled onto her back and put her arm over her eyes. “Sleep. We’ve got a long way.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo’s last message finished arriving on the third round.</p>\n<p>It had begun on the first, a few hours above the wreck at the spindle’s foot. The suit had started to groan in the middle of a climb — a long, low, falling note from the helmet speaker, so deep it was almost below hearing, so slow it didn’t seem to move at all — and he’d stopped on the ladder and hung there, listening, until Ione called down <em>what is it</em> and he said <em>Teo</em>.</p>\n<p>It went on for two rounds. It was much longer than any of the others: she’d talked for a couple of minutes, and they were climbing all the while into faster time, so that her minutes had further and further to stretch to reach them. It groaned under their climbing and through their sleeps, a vast slow wordless sound filling the lattice around them like the voice of the spindle itself. On the third round, halfway between two kilometres and three, he felt it begin to fall away, and he stopped in the next Lee and sat on the planks with his back against a strut and his knees up and listened to the last of it, and Ione sat beside him and didn’t say anything.</p>\n<p>Then <em>ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He switched it to the suit’s outer speaker so that Ione could hear.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk. “Last one. I know it’s the last one. You said the electronics would go and I believe you, you’re the sort of pessimist who’s usually right.” A breath. “Got your — got all of it. The Commons. The tank. Your old man with the lamp. The lamps in the lanes.” Her voice caught and went on. “The forty-seven. I’m sorry, Cas. I’m so sorry. I know what you’re doing with that, right now, wherever you are. I can hear you doing it from here. Stop it.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“I’ve got Nell’s file. A month of you. I’m going to give it to her myself. By hand. I’ll go to Tier Thirty and I’ll find her in her gym with her lizard and I’ll sit with her while she listens, and if she wants to cry I’ll let her, and if she wants to hit me I’ll let her do that too. You’ve got my word. And I’ll tell Jonah about the dog. I’ll get him the bloody dog myself.</p>\n<p>“And you look after that rigger. The one with the white streak. You never said much about her, but you didn’t have to, you said her name forty times in a month. It sounds like nobody’s looked after her in a long while. Somebody should.”</p>\n<p>Beside him, Ione went very still.</p>\n<p>“You owe me a drink,” said Teo. “A large one. Don’t forget. I’ve written my boys’ names on the window, here, where I can see them from the chair. Mattias and Joss. Mattias is on shift at Kovač, holding up the grid. Of course he is. Joss is fine. I’ve written Ben’s under. And the lady’s — Seren’s. And I’ve written yours. I hope you don’t mind. It seemed right.”</p>\n<p>A long pause. He could hear her breathe.</p>\n<p>“Guilt’s a lousy reason, Cas. I said it at the booth and I’m saying it again because you’re the kind of thick-headed bastard who needs to hear things twice. Don’t you dare go up there and do something noble because you think you owe it. Do it because it’s useful. Or because you want to. Or because <em>she</em> wants you to. Anything. Anything but that.”</p>\n<p>A breath.</p>\n<p>“I’m fine. The lee’s fine. I’ll be here.” A pause. “Bring them out, Cas.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>The suit hissed. The display at the bottom of his visor flickered, and showed a line of text — SIGNAL LOST — and flickered again, and showed nothing. The little icon for the radio went grey.</p>\n<p>He sat on the planks with his back against the strut. Ione sat beside him. Neither of them spoke. Far down through the lattice, the city lay in its night, the smoke standing up from it like wire.</p>\n<p>“She sounds,” said Ione eventually, “like somebody I’d like.”</p>\n<p>“You would.”</p>\n<p>“<em>The rigger with the white streak.</em>” She said it slowly, as if trying the words for size. Then she looked at him sideways. “Forty times?”</p>\n<p>He felt his face go hot inside the helmet.</p>\n<p>“She was exaggerating,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Mm,” said Ione, and looked away, down at the sleeping city. But he saw the corner of her mouth, and it had moved.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the fourth round they felt the last Bell.</p>\n<p>They were at nearly three kilometres. He weighed — the display said, before the display began to lie to him — two point nine times what he’d weighed on the Tier Nine arterial, a thousand years ago, a day and a half ago. The exo was screaming. They moved from Lee to Lee now in short, brutal dashes, never more than a few dozen metres between them, Ione going first and then hauling him, both of them resting in every crooked-chalk pocket they could find, sometimes for an hour, before the next. Below them the city had stopped. It lay in its shuttered dusk like a painting of itself, the same night it had been in when they’d looked down from two kilometres, the smoke from its chimneys standing up in fine grey threads that didn’t move at all.</p>\n<p>The Bell came up the spindle.</p>\n<p>It came so slowly that at first he didn’t know it for what it was. A pressure. A faint rising hum in the strut under his hand, very deep, like a tide coming in under a pier. It rose. And rose. It took so long to rise that he had time to notice it, and wonder, and understand, and turn to look at Ione. She had her palm flat against the strut, and her eyes closed.</p>\n<p>It went on rising. Minutes. The whole spindle was humming with it, every strut, the walkways vibrating faintly under them, a vast slow note going through the lattice like a wind through a forest. It reached its height. It held there. It began, so slowly he couldn’t be sure of it for a long time, to fall.</p>\n<p>It lasted, he guessed, the better part of an hour.</p>\n<p>When it had finally gone, Ione took her hand off the strut and opened her eyes.</p>\n<p>“One hundred and twenty-three thousand, seven hundred and fourteen,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been counting?”</p>\n<p>“I’m a Toller.” She said it as though it were obvious. “I’ve always counted. Even when they threw me out.” She looked down at the city far below. “That’s the last one we’ll hear.”</p>\n<p>“The last⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Up here, they get too slow. The next one’s hours off, and by the time it comes we’ll be higher, and up there they take a round to rise and a round to fall. You won’t be able to tell it from the spindle breathing.” She looked at him. “That was the last Bell. For us. Unless we come down.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p><em>Unless we come down.</em></p>\n<p>Neither of them pursued it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>That night — that sleep; there were no nights, up here — they stopped in the best Lee on the spindle.</p>\n<p>It was at the junction of four great struts, three kilometres up, where the lattice narrowed and the black beams came together from four directions in a vast knot of keelstone, and between them there was a pocket of calm so deep that Ione’s chalk, when she blew it, didn’t fall at all. It hung in front of her face in a small white cloud, turning, glittering in the violet light, like dust in a sunbeam. There was a cache: a platform of planks, a water-catcher with water in it, a box. And it was so light — so wonderfully, dizzyingly light — that when he stepped onto the platform he nearly fell, overbalancing, his body braced for a weight that wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>He took the helmet off. He hadn’t taken it off for four rounds. The air was thin and warm and smelled of hot stone and chalk. He sat on the planks, in the suit, with the helmet in his lap, and put his head back against the strut, and breathed.</p>\n<p>Ione sat opposite him with her back against another strut. She’d taken off her harness and her boots. She’d unwrapped the splint on her wrist and was flexing it, slowly, wincing. Above them, through the narrowing lattice, the Lace hung in the sky — very close now, much closer than from the city, its fine bright threads hanging down in long trailing streamers toward the spindle-tops, moving very slowly, like weed in a current. Below them the city lay frozen in the middle of its night.</p>\n<p>“I was seventeen,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t move. He didn’t want to startle it.</p>\n<p>“My mother had gone down the Drop two years before. I’d held the ladder for her, when she painted the wall. They didn’t give me to the Drop. They said I was a child.” She flexed her wrist. “I think they thought I’d be quieter, without her. I was. For two years I was very quiet. I counted Bells. I worked the ropes. I didn’t talk to anyone except Gran.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“And then one round I went up. I didn’t tell anyone. I didn’t decide to, really. I just went. I told myself I was going to find the edge of the world. Where the Lamp stopped and something else started. I thought if I went high enough I’d see over it. Over the Rim. Into whatever was on the other side.” She almost smiled. “I was seventeen. And I think⁠—” She stopped. “I think I thought I might find her. Up there. I knew she’d gone <em>down</em>. I watched her go down. But I thought — if everything goes round, up there, the way people said — if the Lamp was a place where things went round⁠—”</p>\n<p>She didn’t finish.</p>\n<p>“I climbed this spindle,” she said. “I didn’t know the Lees then, not the high ones. I found them. I nearly died four times. I got to the top of the Lees — about half a kilometre above us — and there’s a gallery there. Round the outside of Spindle One, near the top. A walkway, a ring, going right round the spindle, with a roof over it and windows. The old crews used to use it. It had an air-garden in it — a long tank of algae all the way round, to keep the air fresh for the Crown. And a gantry, across from Four to One. I went across the gantry. I went into the gallery.” She looked at the chalk hanging in the air in front of her. “And the light came on.”</p>\n<p>“The light.”</p>\n<p>“The Crown’s light. The night of the Kindling. It’s — it’s still there. In that gallery. You’ll see. It goes round and round. The light from those nine minutes. It’s trapped. It goes round the spindle in the gallery windows and never gets out. And when you’re in it, you’re⁠—” she searched “⁠—you’re <em>in the Crown</em>. On the night. You can see it all. The people. The Crown crew, in their white coats, at their boards. The Lamp in the window, before it broke. Founder Crane with his key on its chain and his hair all combed. Ruth.” She said the name gently. “Ruth at the board. I didn’t know who any of them were. Not then. I thought they were ghosts. They were all lit up, sort of, from inside. You could see through them. You could walk through them.”</p>\n<p>“And then?”</p>\n<p>“And then it all happens. Six goes. The Lamp breaks. The walkway goes. The key falls. Ruth pulls the hammer. They all run for the lift. Nine minutes.” She paused. “And then it starts again.”</p>\n<p>The chalk turned slowly in the air between them.</p>\n<p>“I tried to leave. Of course I did. I walked out of the gallery the way I’d come in. And I walked straight back into the gallery, at the other end, where I’d started. As if the gallery was a ring and I’d just gone round it. Every door. Every window. The gantry back to Four — I walked across it and I was coming back across it the other way, into the gallery. Everything led back in.” She said it very plainly. “And the light started again. Ruth at the board. <em>Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol.</em> Nine minutes. And again. And again.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“Three years.”</p>\n<p>He’d known. She’d told him, in the Tower, that first night. It was different, hearing her say it here, at the top of the world, with the chalk hanging in the air.</p>\n<p>“Three years,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I counted.” She almost smiled again. “Of course I counted. A hundred and seventy-five thousand, two hundred and — some. Nine minutes each. I lost count a few times. There were a few months when I didn’t count anything.” She looked at her wrist, flexing it. “There were a few months when I didn’t do anything at all but lie on the floor of the gallery and let them happen through me. Ruth walked through me. Crane walked through me. The key fell through me. Again and again and again and I lay there and let it.”</p>\n<p>“How did you — food? Water? For three years?”</p>\n<p>“The air-garden.” She glanced at him. “Algae. And the gallery had a condensate trap for the Crown’s water. I lived on algae and condensation for three years.” A pause. “You’d have liked that. Your friend. The one in the hall.”</p>\n<p>“Ben.”</p>\n<p>“Ben,” she agreed. “He’d have understood.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t answer.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“It wasn’t a time loop,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it later, after a long silence, when the chalk had drifted a little way off and hung near the strut.</p>\n<p>“Not for me. I thought it was, for years. I thought I was going round and round in time. But I wasn’t. I got older. My hair⁠—” she touched the white streak “⁠—that happened in there. My body went on. I remembered everything. Every cycle, I remembered every cycle before it. I was living forward. The whole time. Just — in a place that went round.” She looked at him. “It was <em>them</em> that went round. The ghosts. The light. They were the loop. Not me.”</p>\n<p>“The light’s on closed paths,” said Cas slowly. “Round the spindle. Trapped. And the gallery⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The gallery joins up with itself. You walk out and you walk in.” She nodded. “Yes. That’s it. That’s what I worked out, eventually. I was the only thing in there that was going <em>forward</em>. Everything else was going round.”</p>\n<p>“That’s worse,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” She didn’t look away. “That’s what I learned. That’s the thing I learned in there, in three years, that nobody in Holdfast would ever believe.” She held his eyes. “Ruth lived those nine minutes once. Just once. In the Crown, on the night of the Kindling. It was the worst nine minutes of her life and she lived them one time and then she went down the lift and had a whole life after, and Tomas and her children and eleven grandchildren, and died an old woman. The light in the gallery isn’t <em>her</em>. It isn’t reliving it. It isn’t tired of it. It doesn’t know. It’s just the same nine minutes, being true, over and over.” She looked at the chalk. “I’m the one who lived them a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. I’m the only one who ever did. The horror of it was never in the loop. It was in whoever was watching.”</p>\n<p>He sat without moving.</p>\n<p>“If a loop’s really closed,” said Ione, “if nothing in it goes forward, if nothing in it can remember — then there’s nobody inside it to be tired of it. It’s just once. It’s once, and it’s true, and it stays true. It’s only forever for whoever’s outside, looking in.” She shrugged a little, as if embarrassed by how much she’d said. “That’s all. That’s all I know. I worked it out on the floor of a gallery when I was nineteen. In there, nothing happened once. Out here, everything does.”</p>\n<p>He thought about fifteen years. He thought about a flat on Tier Nine and a phone that never rang and a man who got up every day and did the same nothing and went to bed. The same day, over and over, for fifteen years. And a pen, a nice blue Ministry pen, that he’d picked up once, one time, and signed with.</p>\n<p>He had lived that one moment — the pen — five and a half thousand times. Every morning. He was the only one who had.</p>\n<p>“How did you stay sane?” he said. “In there. Three years.”</p>\n<p>Ione thought about it seriously. She always thought about his questions seriously; he’d noticed that, in the loft, in the Tower. As if every question deserved to be turned over and looked at from underneath before you answered.</p>\n<p>“I decided to love them,” she said.</p>\n<p>“The ghosts?”</p>\n<p>“The people.” She looked up at the Lace. “After the months on the floor. I got up one cycle and I thought: I can hate this, or I can learn it. And I started watching. Properly. Every one of them. I learned all their names — they say each other’s names, you pick it up. Fen at the tensioner board. Dieter at output, who drinks his coffee too hot and burns his tongue every single time. Oksana with the camera, who’s frightened of heights and never looks out of the window. The boy with the tablet, who’s in love with Oksana.” A small smile. “And Ruth. I learned Ruth best. I sat beside her at the board. For three years. I know her better than I know anyone living. She has a mole, here⁠—” she touched her own jaw below the ear “⁠—and she bites her thumb when Crane talks. Just the side of it. She doesn’t know she does it. And when she says <em>one-thirty-five</em>, she closes her eyes first, for about half a second. She doesn’t know she does that either.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment.</p>\n<p>“She was my best friend,” said Ione, “for three years. And she never heard a single word I said to her.”</p>\n<p>The chalk hung in the air. Far below, the city didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“And the key?” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“One cycle,” said Ione, “it was there. On the gantry. After a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles where it wasn’t. It was the only different thing that had ever happened.” She touched it where it hung at her throat. “And I looked up from it. And far off — at the far end of the gantry, where it meets the wall of Spindle One, a long way off, further than it should have looked — there was someone standing. A woman. Not lit up. Not a ghost. Real. Standing where no ghost ever stood. With grey in her hair.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t breathe.</p>\n<p>“I thought it was my mother,” said Ione simply. “I shouted. <em>Mam.</em> I ran along the gantry toward her. And she turned away. And where she’d been standing, when I got close, the air was — wrong. Shimmering. A seam. I went into it and I was out. On the Rim. Out of the gallery. Out of the loop.” She let go of the key. “I never saw her again. I came down the spindle in two rounds. Gran met me at the bottom. She said I’d been gone for two days. I looked in a water-trough when I got home and I was twenty, and I had this.” She touched the white streak.</p>\n<p>“And the figure in the suit?” he said. “Coming down?”</p>\n<p>“That was on the way down. Across the gulf, on another spindle. Very far. Just once.” She looked at the suit he was sitting in, the humming keel. “I didn’t know what it was, then. I only knew I’d never seen anything like it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the suit too.</p>\n<p><em>There are only three suits.</em></p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything else.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She slept, in the end. She lay down on the planks with her head on her folded rope and her splinted wrist on her chest and was asleep in about a minute, the way riggers slept, the way soldiers slept, completely.</p>\n<p>He didn’t.</p>\n<p>He sat with his back against the strut, in the suit, with the helmet in his lap, and watched her. He watched the white streak in her hair, and the black stitches over her eye, and the slow rise and fall of her breathing in the almost-weightless calm. He watched the chalk, which had drifted over her and hung above her face in a small white cloud, glittering, turning. He watched the city far below them, still as a photograph — thirty-one thousand people caught in the middle of a single night while he sat and watched a woman sleep for hours.</p>\n<p>He thought about a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles. About a girl of seventeen lying on the floor of a gallery for months, letting the worst night in the world happen through her. About that girl getting up, one cycle, and deciding to learn their names.</p>\n<p>He thought about Teo’s voice. <em>Do it because you want to. Or because she wants you to. Anything but that.</em></p>\n<p>He found — examining himself, the way he’d examined himself for fear at the window in the Collar, patting his pockets — he found something he hadn’t expected. He didn’t have a word for it at first. It was small. It was very quiet. It was sitting somewhere under his breastbone, where the sketchbook was, under the neck-ring of the suit, against his heart.</p>\n<p>It was that he wanted to see what happened next.</p>\n<p>Not what happened in the Crown. Not whether the lock would turn or the Clause would work or the plate meant what he was beginning, in the back of his mind, very quietly, to be afraid it meant. Not that. Something smaller. He wanted to see her wake up. He wanted to see what she’d say when she did. He wanted to see the chalk fall, when they left the Lee, and her blow it off her palm, and the look on her face when she read it. He wanted the next round. And the one after that.</p>\n<p>He sat very still with it, the way you’d sit very still with a bird that had landed on your hand.</p>\n<p>It had been a long time. He’d forgotten what it felt like. He’d forgotten that it was possible to want, carefully, just to go on.</p>\n<p>Below them — so slowly that he had to watch for a long time to be sure of it — at the very centre of the city, at the foot of Spindle One, the first shutter of the morning began to rise.</p>"}, {"file": "14-lamp-weather.md", "label": "Chapter 13", "title": "Lamp-Weather", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5002, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Remember that the Lesion is not a place. It is a condition of the place around it. Near it, even the weather is a kind of arithmetic.</p><span class=\"attr\">Iris Hollowell, <em>On the Sealing of Debts</em> (withdrawn)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">Above the best Lee on Spindle Four there was a place the riggers called the Breach.</p>\n<p>Ione showed it to him from below, the morning they left the cache — if it was morning; the city under them had only just begun, very slowly, to open its eyes. She pointed up through the narrowing lattice. Two hundred metres above them, the spindle’s structure changed. The struts that had braced it all the way up, the great black beams that made the Lees, simply stopped. For a stretch of perhaps sixty metres, one whole side of the spindle was open — the lattice there had fallen or been stripped in the Founders’ time, or had never been finished — and there was only a single spine of keelstone running up through the gap like the backbone of a fish, with the rungs of an old ladder bolted to it, and on either side of it, nothing. Violet air. The Lace.</p>\n<p>“No Lees in the Breach,” said Ione. “Nothing to hold against the Lamp. You go up it in one go. Fast. You don’t stop. There’s a hut at the top, in the last big Lee, under the gallery. You don’t stop till the hut.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“Fast? A quarter of a shift. For me.” She looked at him. “For you, in that⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Longer.”</p>\n<p>“Longer.” She began to uncoil her rope. “And you don’t look at the Lace. It’s close up there. It’s very close. You look at the rungs.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He looked at the rungs.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. He looked at them as they came down past his visor one after the other, black iron, pitted, bolted to the black keelstone spine, and he looked at his gauntlets closing on them, and he looked at the rope going up from his harness to Ione, climbing above him with the coil on her shoulder, fast and neat and steady. He didn’t look out. He didn’t look down. He could feel the open air on both sides of him the way you can feel someone standing behind you in a dark room. He could feel the weight — three times what he’d weighed on the Tier Nine arterial, three and a half, the exo screaming in every joint, the keel’s note straining high in his spine.</p>\n<p>And he could feel the Lace.</p>\n<p>Not see it. He didn’t look. But he could feel it, off to the left, where the spindle was open: a kind of pressure on the side of his face. A prickle. The sense of something enormous and delicate hanging very near.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione above him, and he heard the soft puff of her blowing it, and a pause. “Crooked. Left. That’s fine. Keep coming.”</p>\n<p>He kept coming.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione, a while later. A pause. Longer. “<em>Very</em> crooked. Keep coming. Faster.”</p>\n<p>He went faster. His arms were two things he had borrowed from someone who hadn’t wanted them back. The rungs came down past his face.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>Silence.</p>\n<p>He looked up. He couldn’t help it. She was twenty metres above him, braced on the spine with her boots on a rung and her free hand held out flat in front of her, palm up. The puff of chalk had come off it. It hadn’t fallen. It hadn’t drifted sideways. It was going <em>up</em>. A little white cloud, rising slowly off her hand, straight up, toward the Lamp.</p>\n<p><em>When chalk falls up, pray.</em></p>\n<p>“Cas,” said Ione, very calmly. “Hold on.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It hit them like a wave hits a pier.</p>\n<p>First the weight went. All of it, all at once — the crushing three-and-a-half that had been on him for a thousand metres simply <em>vanished</em>, and he was hanging on the rungs weighing nothing, and his stomach came up into his throat and his body, braced against a weight that wasn’t there, threw itself away from the ladder. The exo jerked. His gauntlets clamped. He hung there, floating, attached to the spine by his fists.</p>\n<p>Then it came back. From the side.</p>\n<p>Down swung. Not gently, the way it had in the Works. It <em>whipped</em>. One moment there was no down, and the next down was off to his right, out through the open side of the spindle, into the violet air, and it was not three and a half, it was more — five, six, he didn’t know — and he was hanging off the side of the spine by his hands like a flag in a gale, his legs streaming out sideways over nothing, the whole weight of him and the suit trying to tear his arms out of their sockets and fling him out into the Lace.</p>\n<p>He heard the keel scream.</p>\n<p>And above him, he heard the rope go.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t loud. A sort of flat crack, like a whip, and a hiss. He looked up. Ione’s rope, where it ran from her harness down to his, had been lying across the edge of the keelstone spine — the raw broken edge where the lattice had fallen away. Keelstone didn’t break. But when it did, the edges were like glass. The rope had sawn itself across the edge in the swing, and parted, and the end of it came whipping down past his visor.</p>\n<p>And Ione came off the spine.</p>\n<p>She’d been braced with her boots on a rung and her free hand out for the chalk. When down swung, she’d grabbed. Her splinted hand had closed on the rung. And he watched it not hold.</p>\n<p>She came off. She went out, sideways, into the air on the open side of the spindle — the weight flinging her, the way it was trying to fling him — and she didn’t fall, she <em>flew</em>, out and down and past him, a small dark shape tumbling, her arms out, the white streak in her hair, her mouth open, and she was two metres from him and three and she was going out into the violet air toward the Lace⁠—</p>\n<p>He let go with one hand.</p>\n<p>He never remembered deciding. His right hand came off the rung and went out and the suit’s gauntlet closed on the loop of rope that still hung from her harness, a metre of frayed end, and the grip-assist clamped on it so hard that he felt the fibres crush. The weight of her came onto his arm like a steel bar. His left hand, alone on the rung, screamed. The exo screamed.</p>\n<p>He pulled.</p>\n<p>The keel’s bubble was a metre. That was all. A metre of stirred-flat spacetime round his body, where down was one direction instead of every direction at once. He hauled on the rope with an arm that had nothing left in it and dragged her in through the air toward him, and she came, and she hit him — hit the suit, chest to chest, her face against his visor, her arms coming round him — and was inside it.</p>\n<p>And the weather stopped.</p>\n<p>Not outside. Outside it went on — he could see it, over her shoulder: the violet air, the Lace, the open side of the spindle all tilting and swinging, the chalk-cloud Ione had blown whirling past in a spiral. But inside the bubble, inside the metre, down was simply down. The keel’s note was a scream in his spine but it held. It averaged it all out. Down was one way, and it was heavy, very heavy, but it was <em>one way</em>, and they were both in it, pressed together against the spine of Spindle Four, his left hand locked on a rung and his right arm round her and her arms round him, holding on.</p>\n<p>“<em>Don’t let go</em>,” she said against his visor. Her voice came through the helmet thin and close. “Don’t let go, don’t let go⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got you.”</p>\n<p>“⁠—<em>don’t let go</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got you, Ione. I’ve got you.”</p>\n<p>It went on for a long time. He never knew how long. It came in gusts. Down would swing round the outside of the bubble like a wind round a house, and he’d feel the keel strain to hold it, and the metre around them would flex and warp — the rungs in front of his face stretching, bending, as though seen through water — and hold, and settle. The weight surged and dropped. Once it dropped to nothing and he felt her start to float away from him, just slightly, and pulled her back hard, and she made a sound, and held on harder. Once it surged so hard that the spine of keelstone they were clinging to <em>groaned</em>, a vast deep sound through the metal, and a rung below them sheared off its bolts and went spinning out into the air and was gone.</p>\n<p>Her face was against his visor. He could see her eyes. They were very close and very wide, and for the first time since the top of the well there was nothing patient in them at all. They were frightened. They were a frightened seventeen-year-old’s eyes, in a thirty-year-old’s face, looking at him from three centimetres away through a sheet of glass.</p>\n<p>He held her. He didn’t have anything else to do.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>When it passed, it passed all at once. Down came back to down — straight down, the old crushing three-and-a-half, the Lamp pressing on them from above. The violet air outside stopped swinging. The chalk was gone.</p>\n<p>They hung there on the spine, the two of them, for a while longer. Neither of them moved.</p>\n<p>Then Ione said, into his visor, very quietly: “The hut’s forty metres up.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>“I can’t let go of you. If I let go of you I’m out of the bubble and I’ll weigh more than twice what I weigh at home and I’ve only got one hand.”</p>\n<p>“Then don’t let go of me.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll have to climb with me on you.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him through the glass for a moment longer. Then she shifted — got her legs round his waist, her good arm round his neck-ring, her splinted hand fisted in the webbing of his harness — and put her cheek against the side of his helmet, and held on.</p>\n<p>He climbed.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know how. He never knew, afterwards, how. Forty metres. A hundred and thirty-odd rungs, with the whole weight of both of them — less in the bubble; not less enough — on arms that had nothing in them, the exo whining in a high thin continuous note like a dentist’s drill, the keel shrieking in his spine. He climbed with her pressed against him, her breath hot on the side of his helmet, and he counted rungs because it was the only thing he knew how to do, and somewhere past a hundred he stopped being able to count, and just climbed.</p>\n<p>At the top of the Breach the lattice came back. Great black struts closing round them. Chalk falling crooked. A Lee — a deep one; the weight lifted off them like a coat taken off their shoulders — and in it, wedged into the angle of three massive beams, a hut.</p>\n<p>He got them onto its platform. He didn’t remember that either. He remembered lying on his back on old planks inside a low black room with Ione still wrapped round him, both of them shaking, and the keel’s note slowly coming down from its scream, and her face in the crook of his neck-ring, and her voice, muffled:</p>\n<p>“You let go of the rung.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“With one hand. In lamp-weather. In the Breach.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>A long pause.</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s ever done anything that stupid for me,” said Ione Tally. And then, after another pause, very quietly, into the neck-ring of his suit, she began to cry.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hut was keelstone. Riggers had built it — generations ago, in the Founders’ time or just after, when the riggers were still going up the spindles to tend the old lamp-lines — out of off-cut slabs of keelstone laid against the three great struts, so that the walls pulled with the struts and the Lee inside was deep and quiet and almost flat. There was a door, of iron plate. There was a window, a slit, looking out through the lattice at the Lace. There was a shelf with things on it that crumbled when you touched them. There was a water-catcher outside the door, and water in it.</p>\n<p>And it was calm. Down was down, and not very much of it — about what it had been in the city, Ione said. After the Breach it felt like floating.</p>\n<p>He took the suit off.</p>\n<p>He’d been in it since the foot of the spindle, days ago — four sleeps; five — and when it opened down the back and he stepped out of it in the grey liner he nearly fell, again, the way he had at every big Lee. His legs had forgotten how to carry him. Ione caught him. They sat down together on the planks, hard, and stayed there, backs against the black wall, not talking.</p>\n<p>The suit stood in the corner of the hut, the keel humming. It had dropped again. He could hear it. The storm had cost it.</p>\n<p>After a while he got up and went to it, and looked at the display on the inside of the helmet. The clocks said LOCAL 03:14 and OUTSIDE 11:62. There was no such time as 11:62. As he watched, OUTSIDE said 04:0? and then a string of symbols and then nothing. The radio icon was grey. The little green light on the recorder, in its chest compartment, was still lit.</p>\n<p>“It’s going,” said Ione, behind him.</p>\n<p>“The electronics. Yes. Ben said they would.”</p>\n<p>“Will it still⁠—” She stopped.</p>\n<p>“The keel’s mechanical. The exo’s got a manual fallback. The air’s mostly mechanical.” He put the helmet down. “It’ll still get us through the Lace.”</p>\n<p><em>Us</em>, he thought. He didn’t say anything else.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She washed his face. He didn’t ask her to. She got water from the catcher outside in a tin that didn’t crumble and a strip of cloth from her pack and knelt in front of him where he sat against the wall and washed his face, carefully, the way you’d clean a wound, and then his neck, where the neck-ring of the suit had rubbed it raw. Then she re-bound her own wrist, one-handed, with her teeth, and wouldn’t let him help.</p>\n<p>“Is it broken?”</p>\n<p>“It was broken before. It’s more broken now.” She tied it off. “It’ll do.”</p>\n<p>He watched her. The stitches over her eye. The white streak. The rope-scarred forearms. The splint.</p>\n<p>“Can I tell you something?” he said.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<p>“Nobody knows it,” he said. “Except a man in a cardigan who works for the secret police. And Teo, a bit. And the man in the cardigan only knows what I did, not⁠—” He stopped. “Not the rest.”</p>\n<p>She sat back on her heels. She didn’t speak. She didn’t nod. She just waited, the way she’d waited at the top of the well, the way she’d waited through a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles, as if there were nothing he could say that she hadn’t already seen, and she’d be interested to see it again.</p>\n<p>So he told her.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Margin Review. Year Seventy-Six. About Dr Adeyemi, sixty-eight, who walked everywhere very fast with his hands in his pockets, who used to put his palm flat on the walls of the Works and listen. About the nine months they’d spent on it, the two of them and a handful of analysts, going through seventy-five years of Rollover figures. About the night he’d first run the projections himself, alone, in the Review’s borrowed office on Tier Two, and watched the Margin line go down past one and keep going, and sat there until dawn. About the report. <em>A structure of obligation that cannot, even in principle, be discharged.</em> Adeyemi had written that. Cas had read it over his shoulder and thought it was the truest sentence he had ever seen.</p>\n<p>He told her about the meeting. The Ministry. A long table in a room with gold carpet. Very courteous people. Nobody raised their voice. They explained that the projections were, of course, only projections. That the Review had not taken into account the planned improvements to the Lattice. That publishing a figure like that — a figure like <em>negative margin</em> — in the current climate, with the export treaties being negotiated, would be irresponsible. Would cause panic. Would hurt people. Real people, now, today. That the future would find solutions; the future always had. That all that was needed was a revised conclusion. A small one. A sentence. <em>The Margin remains stable within projected parameters.</em></p>\n<p>“Adeyemi said no,” said Cas. “He stood up and said no, and walked out. They put him on leave. He died two years later. Heart, they said. He was seventy.” He looked at his hands. “They asked me.”</p>\n<p>“And you said yes.”</p>\n<p>“They didn’t make me. That’s the thing. That’s what nobody — they didn’t threaten me. They didn’t mention my family. They just asked. They put the revised report in front of me and a pen next to it and they asked, very politely, whether I’d be willing to sign. And I thought about it for — I don’t know. Ten seconds. And I thought: <em>it’ll probably be fine</em>. And I thought: <em>somebody else will fix it</em>. And I thought: <em>who am I to say</em>.” He was quiet. “And I noticed that it was a nice pen. That’s the thing I remember. I remember thinking what a nice pen it was. It wrote very smoothly.”</p>\n<p>He heard the sentence as he said it. <em>It’ll probably be fine.</em> He’d thought it again, not so long ago — at the top of a gallery stair in the Works, looking at a thread in a wall — and a boy had gone down the stair two at a time, grinning. He hadn’t noticed, then, that it was the same sentence. He noticed now.</p>\n<p>Ione said nothing.</p>\n<p>“They gave me the Standby. A flat by the Pit and a salary and a phone that never rang. And I sat in it for fifteen years.” He found that his voice had gone strange. “I didn’t do anything. I didn’t — I lost Sabine, because I couldn’t talk to her, because the only thing I could have talked about was the thing I couldn’t say. I saw Nell every other weekend and took her to the museum and didn’t say anything to her either. I got up every morning and did nothing and went to bed. The same day. Every day. Fifteen years. You said — you said about the Carousel. That it was only horrible for whoever was watching. I’ve been watching one day for fifteen years. The day with the pen in it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“When the Draw broke,” he said, “I sat on my bed and listened to the hammer, and I didn’t feel frightened. I felt⁠—” He made himself say it. “I felt relieved. I thought: <em>at last</em>. I thought: <em>now somebody will have to send me in</em>. And I thought I’d go in and it would — settle it. Somewhere in here. The Shear, or the Crown, or wherever. I thought I’d die in here and it would be⁠—” He stopped. “It seemed tidy.”</p>\n<p>He’d never said it out loud before. He’d never said it to himself.</p>\n<p>“I came in here to die,” said Casimir Hale. “That’s why. Teo asked me, at dinner, the night before, and I didn’t answer her. That’s why.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Ione was quiet for a long time.</p>\n<p>Outside the slit of window, the Lace hung in the violet air, very close, its threads moving slowly like weed in a current. The keel hummed in the corner. Far below, somewhere, the city was still in its night.</p>\n<p>“You think it’s a debt,” said Ione at last.</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“What you did. With the pen.” She was sitting with her back against the wall opposite him, her knees drawn up, her splinted wrist resting on them. “You think it’s a debt, and you’ve been paying it. For fifteen years. Every day. And you came in here to pay the rest of it off. All at once. With yourself.” She tilted her head. “Is that it?”</p>\n<p>“Something like that.”</p>\n<p>“And has it got any smaller?”</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. He shut it.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione. “It hasn’t. Has it. You’ve been paying it every day for fifteen years and it’s bigger than it was. Because that’s how your kind of debt works.” She said it without any cruelty at all, the way she’d explained the Lees. “You told us. In the Commons. You borrow, and you pay it back, and it’s always more. You pay the interest, and you pay the interest on the interest, and it grows, and it grows, and you can never, ever get to the bottom of it. That’s what you people <em>built</em>. You built a whole city on that. You built this whole place on it.” She moved her hand, taking in the hut, the spindle, the Lamp. “And then you went and built a man on it too.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“I watched a woman make the worst choice of her life,” said Ione, “a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. Ruth. At the board. <em>One-thirty-five.</em> She closes her eyes for half a second first. Every time. And every time I sat next to her and I thought, <em>don’t</em>. Every time. A hundred and seventy-five thousand times I thought <em>don’t</em>, and a hundred and seventy-five thousand times she did it anyway. Because it was already done. Because it was just light. Because she only ever got to do it once, and she’d done it, and there was nothing anyone could ever do about it, not her, not me, not anyone.” She looked at him steadily. “She never got to make another choice. Not in the gallery. The ghost never does. It just does the one thing, for ever.</p>\n<p>“But the real Ruth did. She went down the lift, and she made the next choice, and the one after. She got married. She had children. She wrote letters to a little girl for fifty years. She counted the Bells. She <em>built the Tollers</em>, Cas. My whole family. Everything I am. She made that out of the worst nine minutes of her life. She didn’t pay it off. You can’t pay it off. She just — went on. She made the next thing.”</p>\n<p>She leaned forward.</p>\n<p>“You’re not a ghost,” she said. “You’re not the light in the gallery. You signed it once. That’s all. Once. It’s true, and it’ll always be true, and there’s nothing you can do about it. But nobody makes you do it again every morning. That’s you. You do that. You’ve been standing in the gallery watching yourself sign it for fifteen years, thinking <em>don’t</em>. You can walk out. You can make the next thing.” She sat back. “That’s all. That’s all I know.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t speak. He sat against the black keelstone wall with his hands open on his knees and looked at her and his eyes stung and blurred and he let them.</p>\n<p>After a while she got up and came across the hut and sat down beside him, against the wall, close, her shoulder against his shoulder. She didn’t say anything else. She just sat there.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Later — much later — she said, “Nobody’s touched me in eleven years.”</p>\n<p>She said it to the window, not to him. Her shoulder was still against his.</p>\n<p>“Since I came down. The Unwatched. You don’t get touched. People won’t hand you things. They put them down and step back, so you have to pick them up. Gran touched me. And Pim, he doesn’t care, he’s eleven, he climbs on everyone. That’s all. Eleven years.” She was quiet. “In the Carousel, I used to lie next to Ruth. At the board. With my head on her shoulder. And she’d go right through me.”</p>\n<p>He turned his head and looked at her.</p>\n<p>She turned hers. They looked at each other, close, in the violet light from the slit of window.</p>\n<p>“You held on,” she said. “In the Breach. You had me by the rope and you pulled me in and you held on. And I could feel you. Through the suit. I could feel your arms.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know,” she said. “I know. You’re from Outside and I’m from here and we’re going up there, the two of us, to something that probably kills us. I know all of it. I’ve thought all of it. I’ve thought it since the top of the well.” She put her hand, her good hand, flat on his chest, on the grey liner, over his heart. “I’ve had eleven years of thinking. I’m tired of thinking.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand over hers.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was clumsy. That was what he remembered, afterwards, and was glad of: how clumsy it was.</p>\n<p>The liner was a one-piece with a seam down the back that had been designed to be opened by a machine, and neither of them could work out how to get it undone, and she laughed at him trying, and then he laughed, and then she was laughing so hard she had to put her forehead against his shoulder and stop. Her clothes were a rigger’s clothes, a tunic and a harness and a belt and trousers with keelstone-cloth knees, and every piece of them was knotted in some rigger’s way he’d never seen, and she wouldn’t show him, she made him work them out, one after another, and he did. Her wrist got in the way. His back, where the keel had sat for five sleeps, had a bruise across it the size of a dinner plate, and when she put her hand on it he hissed, and she took her hand away, and he put it back.</p>\n<p>The weight in the Lee was Holdfast weight. Heavy. Everything heavy — her body on his, his on hers, their hands, their breath — as if the world were pressing them together. She was strong. Much stronger than him, in that weight, and very sure, and then, suddenly, not sure at all, holding very still with her face against his neck and her breath going fast, and he held still too, and waited, and she said <em>it’s all right, it’s all right, I’m just⁠—</em> and didn’t finish, and didn’t need to.</p>\n<p>Afterwards they lay on the planks of the hut under his coat and hers, tangled, her head on his chest, and he could feel her heartbeat and his, in the heavy stillness, not quite together.</p>\n<p>“Your heart’s too fast,” she said drowsily.</p>\n<p>“It’s the weight.”</p>\n<p>“It’s not the weight.” She moved her head. “I’ve been listening to it since the top of the well. It’s always too fast. Outside hearts must all be too fast.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Mm.” She was quiet a while. Her fingers moved on his chest, very slightly, tracing something. He realised after a while that it was a knot. The riggers’ hitch. Over, under, round, through. “Cas.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“When it’s — whatever it is. Up there.” She stopped. “I don’t want to talk about it. I only want to say that I’m glad. That’s all. Whatever happens. I’m glad it was you that came up the well.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand on her hair. The white streak, under his fingers, was softer than the rest.</p>\n<p>“So am I,” he said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She slept. He didn’t, for a while.</p>\n<p>When he was sure she was asleep, he eased out from under her — very slowly, very carefully, putting his rolled-up coat under her head — and went across the hut to the suit in the corner, and opened the chest compartment, and took out the recorder. Its little green light was still on. He sat down on the planks with his back against the suit’s leg and held it.</p>\n<p>Then he pressed the button. The second channel.</p>\n<p>“Nell,” he said, very quietly, so as not to wake her.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what came next. He sat in the dim violet light and listened to Ione breathe and the keel hum, and let it come.</p>\n<p>“I’ve been thinking about what you said. About being scared. That it’s allowed, and you just have to do it anyway.” He paused. “I think I got something wrong. For a long time. I think I thought — I thought being brave meant not minding what happened to you. I thought if you didn’t mind, it couldn’t hurt. So I stopped minding. About anything. For years. I thought that was — I don’t know. Being grown up. Being sensible.” He stopped. “It isn’t. It’s just being scared all the time and calling it something else.”</p>\n<p>The keel hummed.</p>\n<p>“So I want to tell you something. And I want you to hear it from me, even if it’s a long time from now, even if it’s from a machine. Because I’ve never said it. Not to you, not to your mother, not to anyone. Not even to me.”</p>\n<p>He looked across the hut at Ione asleep under the coats, the white streak in her hair, her splinted hand curled under her chin.</p>\n<p>“I want to live, Nell,” said Casimir Hale. “I want to live. I don’t know if I’m going to. But I want to. I want to very much.”</p>\n<p>He held the button down a moment longer, letting it record the silence after, and the keel, and her breathing.</p>\n<p>Then he let go.</p>"}, {"file": "15-the-carousel.md", "label": "Chapter 14", "title": "The Carousel", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5965, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol, test designation Continuance Ninety-One.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ruth Tally, 01:58, the night of the Kindling (heard 175,000 times)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The spindles leaned together as they rose.</p>\n<p>He hadn’t seen it from the city — from the city they had looked like eight straight black pillars going up into the glare — but from the hut under the gallery he could see it plainly. They leaned in toward each other the way the poles of a tent lean in toward the ridge, so that at the Anvil they stood kilometres apart and up here, just below the Rim, they were close enough to be joined. And they were joined. Gantries ran between them: long, narrow, open walkways of steel lattice slung from spindle to spindle across the violet gulf, some of them intact, some of them sagging, some of them hanging in broken halves from one end like dead vines.</p>\n<p>The gantry from Four to One was intact. Ione had checked it on her scouting trip. Three hundred metres of steel walkway, a metre wide, with a handrail on each side, sagging slightly in the middle, running from a door in the lattice just above the hut straight across the gulf to Spindle One.</p>\n<p>And at its far end, wrapped round the outside of Spindle One like a ring on a finger, was the gallery.</p>\n<p>He could see it from the gantry’s start. A long, low, curving structure with a roof and windows, circling the spindle, its windows facing inward — toward the Lamp, toward the core. The windows were dark. And above it, not far above, the spindle ended in a shape he knew from the mural in the crisis room, from the old footage, from the textbooks: a lens of black keelstone perched on the tip of the pillar like a seed on a stalk. The Crown. And above the Crown, the Rim — the vast dark ring that ran round the Lamp, joining the tops of all eight spindles — and above the Rim, filling the whole sky, very close, so close he could see the finest of its threads moving, the Lace.</p>\n<p>He was in the suit again. The keel hummed. It had dropped again. He’d tried not to listen to how much.</p>\n<p>Ione stood beside him at the start of the gantry with her rope on her shoulder and her pack on her back, looking across at the gallery. She was very calm. She had been very calm all morning. She had eaten, and drunk, and re-bound her wrist, and checked his harness twice, and said almost nothing, and been very calm.</p>\n<p>Her hands were shaking.</p>\n<p>He saw it when she lifted one to point. A fine, fast tremor, like a leaf in a draught. She saw him see it, and looked at it herself, and put the hand down by her side, and closed it.</p>\n<p>“It’s the only way,” she said. “The Lace has closed the other gantries. The gallery’s the only way to the Rim, and the Rim’s the only way to the Crown.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I’m just telling you.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” he said. “Ione. I know.”</p>\n<p>She nodded. She didn’t look at him. She stepped out onto the gantry.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It swayed. Every step, it swayed — not much, a slow gentle swing under their feet like a rope bridge — and the handrails creaked, and the violet gulf opened beneath them on both sides, four kilometres of it, down to the Anvil and the tiny still city at its heart. He didn’t look down. He looked at Ione’s back. She walked quickly, lightly, one hand on the rail, not looking down either.</p>\n<p>Halfway across, she stopped.</p>\n<p>“This is where I found it,” she said.</p>\n<p>He looked down at the walkway. Steel lattice, a metre wide, pitted with rust. There was nothing there. It was just a place.</p>\n<p>“Right here.” She touched the key at her throat. “I’d walked across here four thousand times. I knew every bolt.” She pointed at one — a rusted bolt-head on the edge of the walkway, a little bigger than the others. “That one’s loose. It rattles if you step on the plate next to it.” She stepped on the plate next to it. It rattled. “Four thousand times. And one time I came across and there was a key lying here. Right here. Next to the loose bolt.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the place. It was just a place. A square of rusted steel lattice in the middle of a walkway in the middle of the air.</p>\n<p><em>Where it had never been, and then it was.</em></p>\n<p>“Come on,” said Ione. And went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The gallery’s door was open. It had always been open, she said. It had been open eleven years ago. It had been open for a hundred and sixty-eight years. It was a plain steel door in the gallery’s outer wall with a faded sign above it — CROWN SUPPORT RING — AIR PLANT — AUTHORISED ONLY — and beyond it, darkness.</p>\n<p>Ione stopped on the threshold.</p>\n<p>She stood there for a long time. He didn’t touch her. He stood behind her on the gantry and waited, and the gantry swayed, and the Lace hung overhead, and she stood in the doorway of the place where she had spent three years of her life, with her hands closed by her sides.</p>\n<p>Then she said, low, “It’ll come on when we’re in. Don’t be frightened of it. It can’t see you.”</p>\n<p>And went in.</p>\n<p>He followed.</p>\n<p>It was a corridor. Long and curving, curving away to the left and right, following the round of the spindle; he could see about thirty metres each way before it bent out of sight. The outer wall, behind him, was plain steel. The inner wall — the one facing the spindle, facing the core — was windows. A long unbroken band of thick glass panes, floor to ceiling, one after another round the whole curve. And along the foot of the windows ran a trough. A long steel tank, knee high, full of water, full of something green and thick and softly glowing. The air-garden. It smelled of the sea, of the algae halls in the Works, of greenbread. The water in it was still. The green in it was alive.</p>\n<p>The windows were dark. And then they weren’t.</p>\n<p>It came on the way a screen comes on. There was nothing, and then — all at once, all round the curve, in every pane — there was light. White-violet. Soft. Shifting. And in it, standing in the corridor, as solid as he was, as close as Ione⁠—</p>\n<p>People.</p>\n<p>A room. He was standing in a room. He could see the walls of the corridor through it, the steel, the trough, the windows — but over them, <em>in</em> them, lit from inside like figures in a stained-glass window, there was a room. A long lens-shaped room with black walls and a great curved window, and consoles, and chairs, and people at the consoles. A young man with a mug. A woman on a stool by a camera with a shaved head and a lanyard. A thin young man holding a tablet. A heavy man at a board with his sleeves rolled up. Through the great window of the room, hanging in the dark, a single point of white-violet light, hard and perfect as a nail.</p>\n<p>And in the middle of the room, combing his hair in the reflection of the window, a tall man with silver hair and a good dark jacket, with a key on a chain round his neck.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<p>The voice came from everywhere. From the windows, from the walls, from the air. It was thin and slightly hollow, like a voice in a recording, and perfectly clear.</p>\n<p>Cas stood in the corridor of the gallery with his hand on the wall and couldn’t move.</p>\n<p>“We’ll stop down,” said the woman with the camera. “You’ll look like a painting, sir.”</p>\n<p>And beside him, very softly, not looking at him, looking at the room — Ione said the next line. Her lips moved a half-second before the ghost’s did.</p>\n<p>“<em>Tally. How long until we’re ready?</em>”</p>\n<p>“Tally,” said Aldous Crane, without turning round. “How long until we’re ready?”</p>\n<p>And at the board — right there, three metres from Cas, standing with her hands on the edge of the console and her back to him — a woman in a white coat, thirty-eight years old, with dark hair pinned up and a mole on her jaw below her left ear, said:</p>\n<p>“We’re not.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He watched it all.</p>\n<p>He hadn’t meant to. He’d meant to keep moving — to follow Ione, who’d begun, slowly, to walk along the curve of the corridor, through the ghost-room, her hand trailing on the steel of the outer wall. But he couldn’t. He stood in the corridor with the light of the Crown moving over his visor and he watched it happen.</p>\n<p>Crane, with his good face, being patient. Ruth, at the board, being tired. <em>The protocol requires the interlocks off.</em> The aide with his tablet. <em>Director Obi has deferred to the Ministry.</em> He watched Crane say Maddy’s name. He watched Ruth’s face when he said it. He watched her put her thumb to her mouth and bite the side of it, without knowing she was doing it, while Crane talked about the Aurelian Compact and forty million homes.</p>\n<p>He watched her say <em>eleven minutes</em>.</p>\n<p>He watched them run the test. The call and response. <em>Rollover to manual. Interlocks off.</em> The green lights going amber on the board — he could read the board, he could read every figure on it, clear as print, lit up in ghost-light in the corridor wall. <em>One-oh-five. One-ten.</em> The coffee in Dieter’s mug shivering into rings. <em>Six is at plus nineteen.</em> The Lesion in the window, sharpening. Crane saying <em>five more</em> to the camera.</p>\n<p>Beside him, Ione said, very softly, “Now.”</p>\n<p>And Ruth closed her eyes. For about half a second.</p>\n<p>“One-thirty-five,” she said.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall began to run fast.</p>\n<p>He’d read it in pencil in a Log in the Tollers’ tower. He’d read it in the telemetry on a screen in a room with gold carpet. It wasn’t like reading it. The floor of the ghost-room dropped and everyone in it staggered, and the coffee mug left the desk and hung, and fell, slowly, slowly, its brown tongue of coffee curling out; and Fen at the tensioner board said <em>Six has unseated</em> a hair before his mouth moved; and in the great window of the ghost-room the Lesion <em>broke</em>.</p>\n<p>He watched it happen. The frost. The lace. The single hard point of light coming apart into edges on edges on edges, spreading outward, silently, in every direction. He’d seen the Lace for weeks now, from the city, from the spindle, filling the sky. He’d never seen it be born. It was the most terrible thing he had ever seen, and it was beautiful, and it made no sound.</p>\n<p>“Keystone,” said Ruth Tally, and ran.</p>\n<p>She ran right past him. Through him — he felt nothing; the light went over his visor and she was on the other side of him — and down the length of the ghost-room to its far end, where the corridor curved, and dropped to her knees and wrenched up a plate in the floor. He saw the plate. He saw the words on it, lit in ghost-light on the steel floor of the gallery, clear as a sign.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>He saw the keyhole under it: a narrow cross.</p>\n<p>And he saw — with a lurch that went through his whole body, that made him put out his hand to the wall — the mechanism under the plate. The housing. The black keelstone, the recessed ring round the keyhole. The fittings. He’d seen them before. He’d seen them in a schematic, in the back of a withdrawn paper, in a library at night twenty years ago. He knew what they were for. He knew what they would do, when the lock turned. He understood — standing in the gallery with the ghost-light running over him — exactly how the Clause had been built, and why, and what it would take to run it.</p>\n<p>Almost exactly.</p>\n<p><em>The sealing requires a witness.</em> He still didn’t know what that meant. But he knew, now, where the witness would have to stand.</p>\n<p>“Give me the key,” said Ruth.</p>\n<p>He watched Crane not give it to her. He watched Crane’s face do the thing it did — he’d read about it in her Log, <em>the moment he understood</em>, and now he saw it, the fear coming into that good face, the young frightened man coming out from behind the Deputy Minister. He watched Crane’s fingers close on the chain. Watched him begin, slowly, to lift it.</p>\n<p>He watched the gantry go.</p>\n<p>A single thread of the Lace — a hair of light — reached the frame of the ghost-Crown’s great window, and where it touched, the black keelstone went grey, and white, and sagged like wax. The walkway tore. Crane fell against the rail. The chain snapped.</p>\n<p>And the key fell.</p>\n<p>Not down. He’d wondered about that since the Log. He’d wondered about it since the mural. It left Crane’s chain and turned over once in the white-violet light and slid between the bars of the grating and fell <em>toward the window</em>. Toward the Lamp. Toward the light. Not down, the way things fell. Not up, the way the Lamp pushed. It fell <em>along</em> something — along a slope that wasn’t in space at all. And he understood, watching it, the way he’d understood the mechanism: near the Tear, the gradient of time was so steep that it was steeper than the gradient of anything else. Things near it didn’t fall down, or up. They fell <em>forward</em>. Into the future. Along the only slope that mattered. The key fell forward out of the Crown, through the glass as if the glass were a moment that had already passed, and into the Lace, and was gone.</p>\n<p>He knew where it had gone. He knew where it had come out: on a gantry, eleven years ago, at the feet of a girl of seventeen. He looked at the key round Ione’s neck. He didn’t know yet how it had got from one to the other.</p>\n<p>And at the far end of the ghost-room, on her knees by the Keystone, Ruth Tally looked up — at nothing. At an empty space in front of her, beside the open housing. She went completely still. Her face changed. Something in it opened. She looked at the empty space for about a second, as though someone were standing there, as though someone were looking back at her.</p>\n<p>“She always does that,” said Ione quietly, beside him. “Right there. At the Keystone, after the key goes. She looks at nothing like someone’s there.” She paused. “There’s never anyone there. I used to stand in the place she’s looking at. Hundreds of times. To see if it was me she saw.” She shook her head slightly. “It wasn’t.”</p>\n<p>The ghost-Ruth came back to herself. <em>Tally! Tally, the lift!</em> The Crown crew running. Ruth at the gate of the lift. The glass panel. The red lever.</p>\n<p>She pulled it.</p>\n<p>And the gallery windows went dark.</p>\n<p>For about three seconds, there was nothing. The corridor. The steel. The trough of green. The dark windows. Ione beside him, breathing, and the keel’s hum in his spine.</p>\n<p>Then, all round the curve, the light came on again.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They walked.</p>\n<p>The corridor curved round the spindle. The ghost-Crown went with them. It didn’t matter where you stood — the room was always there, in the windows, in the walls, laid over the gallery like a picture on glass. Nine minutes. <em>We’re not.</em> <em>Eleven minutes.</em> <em>One-thirty-five.</em> The key falling. Ruth looking at nothing. The lever. Dark. <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>Ione walked a little ahead of him, one hand trailing on the outer wall, and he saw that she was not looking at the ghosts. She was looking at the wall.</p>\n<p>The wall was covered in marks.</p>\n<p>He saw them after the second cycle: scratched into the steel, all along the outer wall of the corridor, at about the height of a woman’s shoulder. Fives. Strokes in fives, the fifth across the four, in rows. Row after row. They went along the wall as far as he could see in both directions, and when he looked up, they went up — above head height, up to the ceiling, where someone must have stood on the edge of the algae trough to reach. Thousands of them. Tens of thousands. Scratched with a nail, or a knife, or a rope-hook, into the steel. And every so often, among them, other marks. A word. A drawing. A face — a woman’s face, with her hair pinned up and a mole below her ear, drawn very carefully in scratched lines, over and over, twenty times, fifty.</p>\n<p>He stopped in front of one. It was very good. It had been done slowly, by someone with all the time in the world.</p>\n<p>“You counted,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I’m a Toller.” Ione didn’t stop walking. “I told you.” A pause. “There’s a place further round where I stopped. For the months on the floor. There’s a gap. You’ll see it.”</p>\n<p>He saw it. A stretch of wall about two metres long, where the fives simply stopped — and then, beyond it, started again, in a different hand. Stronger. Neater. As if someone had got up off the floor one day and decided to learn everybody’s name.</p>\n<p>There was a pallet of old rags on the floor under the window there, flattened, dusty. There was a tin cup by the algae trough. There was a length of rope, knotted into something complicated — a net, a sling — hanging from a pipe. There was a heap of little stones, polished smooth, that must have come up in someone’s pocket from the Anvil a long time ago, and been turned over and over in someone’s fingers until they shone.</p>\n<p>Ione walked past all of it without looking.</p>\n<p>The corridor curved. Ahead of them, round the bend, he could see a door — the gallery’s inner door, Ione had said, the one that led out onto the Rim. It was standing open. Beyond it, violet air, and the great dark curve of the Rim.</p>\n<p>They walked toward it. It came closer. They reached it. Ione stopped, just short of the threshold, and he stopped beside her.</p>\n<p>“Watch,” she said.</p>\n<p>She stepped through.</p>\n<p>And the door they had come in by — the outer door, the plain steel door with CROWN SUPPORT RING above it — was in front of them. They were standing just inside it, looking out at the gantry to Spindle Four. The gallery curved away on either side.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took him eleven cycles to believe it.</p>\n<p>He tried everything she’d tried. He knew she’d tried it all; he tried it anyway, because he had to, because he was a man who had to see a thing for himself before he could know it. He went through the inner door, and came in through the outer. He went through the outer door, onto the gantry, and walked twenty metres along it toward Spindle Four, and turned round, and came back — and the door he came back through was the inner one, the Rim door, and he was walking into the gallery from the other side. He went through a window. The panes were thick but one of them, down near the trough, was cracked, and he put his gauntlet through the crack and pulled out a piece and climbed through — and fell forward onto the floor of the gallery corridor, facing back the way he’d come, with the ghost-Crown lighting up around him. He tried the roof. There was a hatch. It opened onto the roof of the gallery, a curving strip of steel roof round the spindle’s side, and he climbed out onto it and walked along it and came down through the hatch he’d gone up.</p>\n<p>Every way out was a way in.</p>\n<p>“It’s a ring,” he said. He was standing in the middle of the corridor. His voice sounded strange. “It’s — the whole gallery’s been wrapped. Topologically. Every boundary’s been glued to another boundary. Space goes out one side and comes in the other.” He looked at the light in the windows. “There’s a filament. Through the gallery. Somewhere. The Tear’s run a thread through here and the space round it’s been — folded round it. Like a sleeve round an arm.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Ione. She was sitting on the edge of the algae trough, watching him. “I know.”</p>\n<p>“There’ll be a seam. There has to be. Where the edges meet. Where it’s been joined.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. There was one. There was one where the key came from. Where my — where the woman was standing.” She looked down at her hands. “It’s not there now. I’ve been looking for it for eleven cycles. I’ve been looking for anything. Anything different.” She was quiet. “There isn’t anything different. There’s never anything different. That’s the thing about it. That’s the whole thing.”</p>\n<p>The ghost-Crown moved round them. <em>Five more, Ruth.</em></p>\n<p>“The seam was only there,” said Ione, “because something came through it. The key came through it. The woman came through it. Something from outside came in, and where it came in, you could see the join. Like a thread pulled through cloth.” Her voice was very even. “Nothing’s come in. So there’s no join to see. There’s just the ring. And the light. And the nine minutes.”</p>\n<p>She put her hands on the edge of the trough, on either side of her, and held on to it.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to sit down now,” she said. “For a little while. I’m sorry.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d been afraid of it since the gantry. Since the doorway. Since he’d seen her hands shake.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the floor. Right there, by the trough, with her back against the green-lit steel and her knees up, in the place where the scratched fives stopped. She put her arms round her knees. She put her forehead down on her arms. And the ghost-Crown moved over her — Ruth running for the Keystone right through the place where she sat, the key falling through her, the light going over her like water over a stone — and she didn’t move.</p>\n<p>He knelt down in front of her. The suit made it hard. He did it anyway.</p>\n<p>“Ione.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“Ione. Look at me.”</p>\n<p>“I can hear you,” she said into her arms. Her voice was quite calm. “I’m not gone. I’m just — sitting. I’m going to sit for a bit. You can look for the seam. I’ll be here. I’ll just be here.”</p>\n<p><em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>He sat down beside her. He got the suit’s back against the trough and his shoulder against her shoulder, the way she’d sat beside him in the hut, and he sat there.</p>\n<p>The nine minutes went round. <em>We’re not.</em> He counted them. He couldn’t help it. Twelve. Fifteen. Twenty. He sat beside her and counted the cycles, and the light went over them, and she didn’t move except to breathe. Once, round about the twenty-fifth, he felt her shoulders start to shake, very slightly, against his arm, and he put his arm round her, the suit’s heavy arm, and she leaned into it and went still again.</p>\n<p>He talked to her.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what else to do. He talked about anything. He told her about Nell’s ceiling, the glow-in-the-dark stars, the constellations put up with a chart and a ruler. He told her their names. Betelgeuse, which was red. Rigel, which was blue. Sirius, the brightest, which the old sailors called the Dog Star. Deneb. Vega. Fomalhaut, which was lonely, which sat by itself in a part of the sky with nothing else bright near it. He told her about rain — what it was like, standing in it, on the Tier Nine arterial, the summer the Canopy failed over the northern tiers for a week, when he was a boy; how it came down so hard it bounced; how warm it was. He told her about the sea. About Teo in the Tessarine underpass with twenty-three people on a rope. About Ben, and MINISTER →, and I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS. About Adeyemi putting his palm flat on the walls of the Works and listening.</p>\n<p><em>One-thirty-five.</em></p>\n<p>“Keep talking,” she said, once, into her arms. That was all. So he kept talking.</p>\n<p>And while he talked, he thought.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t help that either. He thought about seams. About a thread of the Tear running through this gallery, and the space folded round it, and the place where the fold must be glued. He thought about how you’d find a seam in a thing you couldn’t see, if nothing came through it. He thought about frost on a window. About a hair of light crossing a storeroom and branching into itself, the same pattern at every scale. About a man in a hall of algae with a tape measure and eleven years, measuring a filament’s branches, month after month, finding the rule.</p>\n<p>He stopped talking.</p>\n<p>“Ione,” he said.</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>He took the sketchbook out of the suit. He had to open the neck-ring to get at it, and he did, and the warm algae-smelling air came in, and he pulled out the black cloth-bound book and opened it on his knee, in the ghost-light, and turned the pages. Thirty pages of Ben. Grease-pencil diagrams copied in his own pencil and Teo’s. The branching. The throats. <em>D = 2.73.</em> <em>IF THERE’S A SHEAR IT’LL HAVE SEAMS. GO THROUGH THE THROATS.</em></p>\n<p>“The fold follows the thread,” he said. He wasn’t sure if he was talking to her or to himself. “The space is folded round the filament. So the seam’s where the filament forks. It has to be. Ben showed it. Where it branches — the throat between the branches — that’s where the geometry’s weakest. That’s where it had to be glued.” He turned a page. “And the branching’s self-similar. Same ratio at every scale. So if I know where one fork is, I know where the next one has to be⁠—”</p>\n<p>He looked up. Round the gallery. At the ghost-light. At the key, falling through it, toward the window, toward the Lamp. At the thread of the Lace in the ghost-window, the first one, the one that touched the Crown’s frame.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the window,” he said. “The filament. The one that made this. It’s the first thread. The one that touched the Crown. It’s still here. It’s <em>in the light</em>.” He was on his knees now, the sketchbook in one hand. “Ione. Ione, you’ve watched that thread a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. You know it better than anyone alive. Where does it fork? The first fork. After it touches the frame. Where does it <em>branch</em>?”</p>\n<p>She lifted her head.</p>\n<p>Her face was wet. Her eyes were red. She looked at him for a moment as if she didn’t know who he was. Then she looked past him, at the ghost-window, where the Crown was just beginning its fourth minute. <em>Rollover to manual.</em></p>\n<p>“Wait,” she said. “It’s not — it doesn’t come for five minutes.”</p>\n<p>They waited. He knelt in front of her with the sketchbook open. She sat with her back against the trough, watching the window.</p>\n<p><em>One-thirty-five.</em></p>\n<p>The Lesion broke. The Lace was born. And a single thread of it — fine as a hair, bright as a bulb — came out of the frost and across the ghost-window and touched the frame.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Ione. She was on her knees too now. She was pointing. “<em>There.</em> It touches the frame there. And it goes along it, that way, for — about an arm. And then it splits. Two. There. And the left one goes up and the right one goes⁠—” she stopped “⁠—the right one goes toward the Keystone. Toward where Ruth is. It branches again just before it gets to her. I’ve watched it. I’ve watched it a thousand times, I used to lie on the floor and watch it — it branches just before it gets to her and one branch goes up and one goes⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Where does the one that goes up <em>go</em>?”</p>\n<p>She turned her head, following it. Up. Out of the ghost-window, across the ghost-ceiling, over their heads. Her eyes moved along something he couldn’t see.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said. She was looking at a place on the outer wall of the gallery, above the scratched fives, near the ceiling, on the curve of the corridor six metres away. “It goes there. It goes into the wall there. Every time.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the sketchbook. At the branching ratio. At the throats. Ben’s writing, in his own pencil. <em>THE SEAM IS ALWAYS BETWEEN TWO BRANCHES. NEVER ON ONE.</em></p>\n<p>“Then the seam’s between them,” he said. “Between the branch that goes to Ruth and the one that goes into the wall. In the throat.” He did it on the page, fast, the ratio, the angles. “It’ll be — there.” He pointed. A place in the middle of the corridor, a little way round the curve, at about head height, in empty air. “Somewhere there. Ione. You know what a seam looks like. You’ve seen one. Look.”</p>\n<p>She got up. She walked toward the place he’d pointed at. She walked slowly, the way she’d walked on the gantry, one hand out. The ghost-Crown moved round her. Ruth ran past her for the Keystone.</p>\n<p>She stopped. Her head tilted.</p>\n<p>“Oh,” she said.</p>\n<p>He came up beside her. He looked.</p>\n<p>It was almost nothing. A shimmer. The faintest ripple in the air, like the edge of heat over a road, like the Shear’s trembling curtain in the conduit drawn out thin as a blade. A vertical line of wrongness hanging in the middle of the corridor, a hand’s width wide, the height of a person, that you would never have seen if you hadn’t known exactly where to look. Through it, the corridor on the far side jumped very slightly, as if seen through a flaw in glass.</p>\n<p>“That’s it,” said Ione. Her voice was very small. “That’s what it looked like. When the woman was standing there. That’s exactly what it looked like.”</p>\n<p>“Ben,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“Ben found it.” He heard his own voice shaking. “Eleven years in a room with a tape measure. He found it.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked at him. Then at the shimmer. Then she held out her good hand, without looking, and he took it in his gauntlet, and they stepped through together.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They were on the Rim.</p>\n<p>It was a walkway. A vast one — ten metres wide, steel plate on keelstone, with a rail on the inner side — running round the top of the Vault in a great dark circle, joining the tops of all eight spindles, curving away on both sides until it met itself far across the sky. Behind them, the gallery; its inner door stood open, and through it he could see the corridor, and the windows, and the ghost-light moving in them. <em>We’re not.</em> Ahead of them, over the rail, the drop into the Vault — four kilometres of violet air — and across it, the Lamp.</p>\n<p>It was very close. He’d never been this close. It hung out there in the middle of the air, the single hard point, too bright to look at, and from it, in every direction, the Lace came out — the endless branching filigree, filling the whole sky above them — and its lowest threads hung down, just beyond the rail, fine and bright and moving slowly, close enough that he could see them branching into each other at scales so small his eyes couldn’t follow.</p>\n<p>Above and to the left, a little way along the Rim, Spindle One rose up past the rail and ended in the Crown.</p>\n<p>Ione let go of his hand.</p>\n<p>She walked to the rail. She put both her hands on it, the good one and the splinted one. She stood there looking out at the Lamp. And then her knees went, and she sat down on the steel plate of the Rim with her back against the rail and her hands over her face, and she cried.</p>\n<p>Not the way she’d cried in the hut. Not quiet. She cried the way a child cries — great gasping, heaving, ugly sobs that shook her whole body, that went on and on, that bent her over her knees. He’d never heard a sound like it. He sat down beside her on the steel plate and didn’t touch her, because he didn’t know if she’d want him to, and then touched her anyway, his gauntlet on her back, and she didn’t push it away.</p>\n<p>“I thought⁠—” she said. She couldn’t get it out. “I thought I’d never — I thought I’d never have to — see her again⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Every time. Every <em>time</em>. She closes her eyes. And I sit next to her. And I think <em>don’t</em>. And she⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“She was my <em>friend</em>,” said Ione Tally, into her hands. “She was my best friend and I had to watch her do it a hundred and seventy-five thousand times and she never once heard me say don’t.”</p>\n<p>He put his arms round her. She let him. She turned into the suit and put her face against the chestplate and held on to the harness and cried, and he held her, on the Rim, at the top of the world, with the Lamp hanging in the air in front of them and the Lace moving slowly overhead and the ghost-light still going round and round in the gallery behind them. <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>It went on for a long time.</p>\n<p>When it was over, she sat up. She wiped her face with her good hand. She looked at the gallery door, and the light moving in it. She looked at it for a while.</p>\n<p>Then she turned her back on it.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said, and her voice was hoarse, and steady. “That’s done.”</p>\n<p>She got up. She looked along the Rim toward Spindle One, where it rose past the rail and ended in the Crown. And beyond it — between them and the Crown, hanging down across the Rim in long bright curtains, filling the walkway from rail to wall, moving slowly — the lowest threads of the Lace.</p>\n<p>It was like a forest. A forest of frost. Of light. Thread on thread on thread, branching and branching, hanging down from the sky to the steel of the Rim and spreading across it in fine luminous roots, a hundred metres of it, between them and the Crown.</p>\n<p>“That’s the Lace,” said Ione. “That’s the bit that kills you.”</p>\n<p>He looked at it. He listened to the keel humming in his spine. It had dropped again. He knew exactly how much.</p>\n<p>“Hold on to me,” he said. “Whatever you see.”</p>"}, {"file": "16-the-lace.md", "label": "Chapter 15", "title": "The Lace", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 4840, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Zero isn’t nothing. Zero’s balanced.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ione Tally, on the Rim</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">She stood on his boots.</p>\n<p>That was how they did it, in the end. They tried it the way they’d done it in the Breach — her legs round his waist, her arms round his neck-ring — and found he couldn’t see past her to walk, and she couldn’t see behind him to guide, and the bubble, a metre across, barely held the two of them in that shape anyway. So she turned round, and stood with her back against his chestplate, and stepped up onto the toes of his armoured boots, and he put his arms round her, one across her chest and one across her waist, and held her there, pressed back against him, her head under his chin. The exo took the extra weight with a grunt.</p>\n<p>“My mother used to dance me like this,” she said. “At Kindling. When I was small. On her feet.”</p>\n<p>“I never did that with Nell.”</p>\n<p>“No?” She tipped her head back against his visor to look up at him. “You should.”</p>\n<p>The keel hummed. He could feel the bubble round them, now that he knew to feel for it — a sort of tightness, a stillness, a metre-wide egg of calm. With her inside it, pressed against him, it was full. There was nothing to spare. If she leaned out, even a little, even her hand, it would be outside.</p>\n<p>“Don’t lean,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Don’t put your hand out. Don’t touch anything. If you need to point, tell me.”</p>\n<p>“I know, Cas.” Her voice was very steady. She was looking ahead, along the Rim, at the forest of light hanging down across it. “Left foot.”</p>\n<p>He moved his left foot. She moved with it.</p>\n<p>They walked into the Lace.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The first thing was the light.</p>\n<p>Outside it — on the Rim by the gallery, on the spindle, in the city — the Lace had been a thing in the sky. Beautiful, terrible, far. In it, it was the air. The threads hung down all round them, fine and bright, some as thick as a wrist and some so fine they were only a glitter, branching and branching into themselves, reaching down from the sky to the steel of the Rim and spreading across it in fans and roots and deltas of white-violet light. They moved. Slowly, the way weed moves in a current. Close to, he could see them grow — see a branch put out a smaller branch, and that one a smaller, and that one a smaller, until the eye lost them and they became a kind of mist, a glow, a haze of edges on edges round every edge.</p>\n<p>And between them, on the Rim’s steel, there were arrows.</p>\n<p>White arrows. Chalk. Drawn quickly on the black steel plate, one every few metres, pointing ahead and a little left, between two great hanging threads.</p>\n<p>Ione went stiff against him.</p>\n<p>“Those are mine,” she said.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. They were rigger’s arrows — the same quick, slanting, three-stroke arrows she’d scratched on the struts of Spindle Four, the same hand. He’d have known them anywhere now.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been here,” he said. “Before. When you tried⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.” Her voice had gone very flat. “I never got this far. Not either time. The Lace was lower on the south side, I never got onto the Rim at all.” She was staring at the nearest arrow. “I’ve never been here. I’ve never drawn these.”</p>\n<p>“Then⁠—”</p>\n<p>“They’re fresh,” said Ione. “Look. The chalk’s still loose. You could blow it off.”</p>\n<p>They stood there on the Rim with the Lace moving round them.</p>\n<p>“Follow them,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I haven’t made them yet.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Cas. “Follow them anyway.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment. Then she said, “Left foot,” and they followed them.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He would never be able to tell anyone, afterwards, how far they went, or how long it took. The Rim was a hundred metres, perhaps, from the gallery door to the stair that went up to the Crown. It should have taken a few minutes. They walked it step by step, her feet on his boots, following the chalk arrows between the threads, and it went on, and on, and he lost the sense of it; time went strange, the way it had gone strange in the Shear, and he stopped trying to count.</p>\n<p>The threads were close. Some of them hung so close that they passed within an arm’s length of the bubble, and he could see — <em>not look</em>, he told himself, <em>don’t look</em> — he could see into them.</p>\n<p>He looked.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t help it. It was like the Lamp; it pulled the eye. A thread as thick as a finger hung just beside his visor, and he looked into it, and it wasn’t a thread. It was a tunnel. It opened, the way a pinhole opens when you put your eye to it, into a space — a vast space, bright and violet, and in it, very small, very far, a sphere. A dark sphere, with a point of light at its heart and eight black pillars rising from its floor, and a tiny tangled city at the pillars’ feet, and a ring round the top of it, and on the ring, a forest of light, and in the forest, two minute figures, pressed together, looking into a thread.</p>\n<p>He looked into the thread they were looking into.</p>\n<p>The Vault. The pillars. The city. The ring. The forest. Two figures. A thread.</p>\n<p>And into that one.</p>\n<p>It went down. It went down for ever. Every thread held the Vault, and every Vault held the ring and the forest and the two of them, and every one of those held a thread, and every thread — he was falling, he was falling down it, down the tunnel, the Vault and the Vault and the Vault opening and opening and opening in front of him, smaller and smaller, finer and finer, and none of them ever getting any smaller at all, every one exactly as large as the last, because size meant nothing here, because there was no bottom, because it was <em>edge all the way down</em>⁠—</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas.</em>”</p>\n<p>Her voice. Her head, knocking back hard against his visor.</p>\n<p>“Don’t look into them,” said Ione. “I said. Don’t look. Look at the arrows.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the arrows. His whole body was shaking inside the suit.</p>\n<p>“How did you know⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because I did it too,” she said. “Just now. Before you. I looked into one.” Her voice was very steady and very small. “Left foot.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They heard themselves before they spoke.</p>\n<p>It happened near the middle of the Rim, where the threads were thickest. They were standing still, between two great hanging roots of light, while Ione looked for the next arrow — and her voice said, clearly, close beside his ear, “<em>Right. Right, and then straight.</em>”</p>\n<p>She hadn’t spoken. Her mouth hadn’t moved. He was looking at the side of her face; he’d have seen.</p>\n<p>A second later, she said, “Right. Right, and then straight.” And stopped. And went rigid against him.</p>\n<p>“Did you hear⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>They stood there. The threads moved slowly round them.</p>\n<p>And then another voice — his voice, his own voice, tinny and close as if from inside his own helmet — said, “<em>Ione, I can’t see the</em>⁠—” and stopped, cut off. He hadn’t said it. He hadn’t said anything.</p>\n<p>“The sound’s coming loose,” he said. His own voice sounded strange to him, now, as if he couldn’t be sure which one was real. “Out of order. It’s the gradient, it’s so steep here that — that sound’s arriving before⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>It was the suit</em>,” said Ione’s voice.</p>\n<p>It came from nowhere. From the air, from the threads. Not her voice now, but her voice from somewhere else — cracked, hoarse, as if she’d been crying, as if she’d been shouting. Close and far at once.</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas, it was the suit, it was never — it was the suit —</em>”</p>\n<p>And gone.</p>\n<p>She’d gone white. He could see it in the side of her face, the blood gone out of her cheek, her lips pressed together.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t say that,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I haven’t said that. I don’t know what that means.” Her hand, her good hand, had come up and closed on his wrist where it crossed her chest. Very tight. “What suit? What does it — what does that <em>mean</em>, Cas?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know. I don’t know. Ione. We haven’t said it yet. We don’t know what it means because we haven’t said it <em>yet</em>.” He heard his own voice rising. He made it stop. “Look at the arrows. Please. Look at the arrows.”</p>\n<p>She looked at the arrows. After a long moment she said, “Straight,” and they went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw it about halfway.</p>\n<p>There was a place where the threads thinned — a gap, a clearing in the forest of light — and the Rim’s inner rail was right there beside them, and beyond the rail, the Vault. The drop. Four kilometres of violet air down to the tiny still city. And off to the left, rising up out of that gulf, the black lattice of Spindle Four — the spindle they had climbed — its upper structure hung with the lowest trailing threads of the Lace, so that from here it looked like a tree wrapped in cobwebs of light.</p>\n<p>And on it, a long way down, something was moving.</p>\n<p>He stopped. Ione stopped with him.</p>\n<p>It was a figure. Small, at this distance — half a kilometre down, perhaps more — climbing down the inside of the spindle’s lattice. Grey. Hulking. It went down a ladder, and along a walkway, and down again, slowly, carefully, the way you climb when you’re tired. And on its back, there was a light. A soft band of light round a rounded housing, the size of a keg.</p>\n<p>A keel.</p>\n<p>He knew it. He knew it before he could have known it. He’d lived beside it for a month and inside it for the best part of a week. He knew the shape of those shoulder plates, the gouge down the left side of the back where he’d hit a beam in a shaft in the Undercroft. He knew the housing on its back — the band of lights, the white number painted on it by a boy in a lab, <em>1</em>, Teo’s number, Teo’s keel. He knew the harness round the hardpoints, spliced in rigger’s strapping, the webbing that Ione had spent three nights making.</p>\n<p>His suit.</p>\n<p>His suit, coming down Spindle Four. Alone.</p>\n<p>Just one. He looked. He looked for a long time. There was nobody else on the spindle. Nobody with it. Nobody on the ladder above it or the walkway below. One grey figure in a keel suit, climbing down, alone, through the cobwebs of light, toward the city.</p>\n<p>As he watched, it stopped.</p>\n<p>It stood on a walkway, a small grey shape against the black lattice, and it turned. It tipped its head back — the visor, a dark curve, catching the Lamp’s light — and looked up. Up at the Rim. At the Lace. At them.</p>\n<p>And it raised its hand.</p>\n<p>It held it there. A small grey arm, raised, the palm out. For a long moment. Then it lowered it, and turned, and went on down.</p>\n<p>He stood with his arms round Ione on the Rim and didn’t move.</p>\n<p><em>It’s me</em>, he thought.</p>\n<p>It came into his head whole and finished, the way thoughts had come in the Shear. It’s me. That’s me, coming down. I come back. I get to the Crown and I do whatever it is and I come back down the spindle, alone, in my suit. And she⁠—</p>\n<p>He looked down at the top of her head, under his chin. At the white streak.</p>\n<p><em>There are only three suits.</em> One was at the bottom of the Anvil in a booth, in a chair, without its keel. One was in a hall in the Works, full of dust. And one was his. And nobody came down with it.</p>\n<p>And she⁠—</p>\n<p>“Did you see it?” said Ione.</p>\n<p>Her voice was very quiet. She was looking down at the spindle too. At the place where the figure had been. It was gone now, further down, behind the lattice.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>“That’s what I saw,” said Ione. “When I was seventeen. Coming down from the Rim. That’s what I saw, from the other spindle. That’s exactly what I saw.” She was quiet. “It raised its hand then too.”</p>\n<p>He said nothing.</p>\n<p>Neither did she. She stood against him, on his boots, with his arms round her, looking down at Spindle Four. He couldn’t see her face. He could feel her breathing — slow, even, very controlled — through the chestplate.</p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she knows what it means.</em></p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she’s known since she was seventeen. She’s known for eleven years. Someone comes down alone.</em></p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she’s been waiting at the top of the well for eleven years for the man who was going to come back without her.</em></p>\n<p>She said nothing. He said nothing. He tightened his arms round her, and felt her hand close on his wrist, and after a long time she said, “Left foot,” very quietly, and they went on.</p>\n<p>And somewhere under his breastbone, where the sketchbook was, a thing that had been small and quiet — the thing he’d found in the Lee watching her sleep, <em>I want to see what happens next</em> — turned over, and set its jaw, and became something else.</p>\n<p><em>No</em>, it said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It started with his hand.</p>\n<p>He’d been watching the arrows. Step by step, her feet on his boots, following the chalk. And he looked down at his right hand, where it lay across her waist, and it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>It was there. It was. He could feel it — her belt under his gauntlet, the warmth of her through the cloth. But when he looked at it, it was — thin. Faded. The grey plating of the gauntlet was grey, and then it was the colour of fog, and then he could see her belt through it, the buckle, the cloth, as if his hand were a picture of a hand painted on glass. And then it was solid again. And then it wasn’t.</p>\n<p>“Ione.”</p>\n<p>“I see it.” Her voice was tight. “Mine too.”</p>\n<p>He looked. Her hand on his wrist — her good hand, the scarred brown rigger’s hand — was going in and out. There, and fading, and there. Like a lamp flickering. Like a signal coming and going.</p>\n<p>“It’s the edge,” he said. “We’re near the edge. The Tear. It’s⁠—” He tried to think. He couldn’t think. “The boundary between being and not being is — the vacuum’s so stretched here that — things aren’t sure. Whether they⁠—”</p>\n<p>He looked up from his hand. And the Rim in front of them was flickering too. The steel plate. The rail. There, and fog, and there. The chalk arrows came and went. A whole section of the rail ahead of them simply wasn’t there — was a gap, a nothing, violet air — and then was there, solid, black, with rust on it. The threads of the Lace were the only things that didn’t flicker. They hung round them, bright and steady and moving slowly, as if they were the only things in the world that were sure.</p>\n<p>And then he looked down at the woman in his arms, and didn’t know who she was.</p>\n<p>It lasted a second. Less. He was holding someone. A short broad woman with dark hair and a white streak in it, standing on his boots, pressed back against his chest, her hand on his wrist. And he didn’t know her. He didn’t know her name. He didn’t know why he was holding her, or where he was, or why, and a terror went through him so absolute, so bottomless, that it was like falling into the thread again, falling down the tunnel of Vaults for ever. <em>Who is this. Who is this. Who am I holding.</em> He looked at the white streak. He looked at the scar on her forearm. He <em>knew</em> he knew them. He knew there was a word for them, a name, a whole life, and it wasn’t there. It was gone. It had flickered out, like the rail.</p>\n<p>Then it came back.</p>\n<p><em>Ione.</em></p>\n<p>It came back like the rail came back, all at once, solid and rusted and real. Ione. Ione Tally. The top of the well. <em>What time is it outside.</em> The loft. The hut. <em>I’m glad it was you.</em> It came back, all of it, and he was holding her so hard that she made a sound, and he couldn’t loosen his arms, he couldn’t make them loosen⁠—</p>\n<p>“Cas,” she said. “<em>Cas.</em> I’m here. I’m here.”</p>\n<p>“I didn’t know you.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“For a second. I didn’t — I didn’t know who you <em>were</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione. “I know. I didn’t know you either. Just now. I looked at your arm and I didn’t know whose it was.” Her voice was shaking. Her hand had moved from his wrist and was holding his gauntlet — holding it, pressing it against her waist, hard. “It came back. It came back. We’re here. Hold on. Hold on to me. Don’t let go of me, whatever you see. Don’t let go.”</p>\n<p>“I won’t.”</p>\n<p>“Whatever you see.”</p>\n<p>“I won’t,” he said. “I won’t.”</p>\n<p>They stood there, holding on to each other, on a Rim that came and went, in a forest of light that didn’t.</p>\n<p>Then she said, “Straight,” in a voice that was nearly steady, and they went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The worst of it was near the end.</p>\n<p>The stair to the Crown was in sight. Twenty metres ahead — a steel stair going up from the Rim, beside the black lattice of Spindle One’s top, to a hatch in the underside of the black lens of the Crown. The threads were thinner here. The arrows led straight to it.</p>\n<p>And the Lamp was very close.</p>\n<p>The Rim curved round it here, at its nearest. The inner rail was right beside them, and beyond it — twenty metres out, thirty, it was impossible to tell — hung the point. The Lesion. The Lamp. The hard white-violet nail driven through the world. He’d never been this close. Nobody had, since the Kindling. It was too bright to look at; he looked anyway; the visor darkened, darkened again, went almost black, and through it, the point burned.</p>\n<p>And he saw into it.</p>\n<p>He never had the words for it. Afterwards, when he tried — to himself, in the Crown, in the time that was left — the nearest he could come was that it was like looking into the thread, the tunnel of Vaults, only there was no Vault at the bottom. There was no bottom at all. There was the edge, and the edge, and the edge, going in, and in, and under the edge, at the very heart of it, where the vacuum had been stretched so far below nothing that it had split — he saw what nothing was.</p>\n<p>It was a ledger.</p>\n<p>That was the word that came to him. He didn’t choose it. It came up out of him from somewhere very deep, from fifteen years of figures on the backs of envelopes, from the Margin Review, from every sum he’d ever done. A ledger. Two columns. On one side, everything. Every star, every stone, every drop of water, every living thing, every city, every thought; all the positive, all the <em>something</em>, all of it, the whole of the universe that had ever been lit. And on the other side, exactly balancing it, the debt. The gravity. The stretched, straining, negative pull of everything on everything, the price of being. And at the bottom of the page, the sum.</p>\n<p>Zero.</p>\n<p>He’d read about it. As a student. An old idea, older than the Long Heat — a physicist had written a paper asking whether the universe itself might be nothing more than a fluctuation of the vacuum. A loan. Whether, if you added it all up, everything that existed and everything it owed, the total would come to exactly nothing. He’d thought it was a pretty idea. He’d thought it was a clever way to answer the question of where everything came from: <em>from nowhere, and it all cancels</em>.</p>\n<p>He wasn’t thinking it now. He was <em>seeing</em> it. He was standing on the Rim with his arms round a woman and looking into the torn place at the heart of the Draw, where the vacuum had been pulled so far below nothing that the bottom had come out of it, and he could see — not with his eyes; with something under his eyes — the whole of everything, balanced, on the edge of nothing. All of it borrowed. All of it owed. The stars on Nell’s ceiling. The city. The Commonweal and its six hundred million, and the Draw, and the Lamp, and Holdfast, and the forty-seven. Ben. Teo. Himself. Ione. All of it a loan against nothing, and all of it, in the end, summing to exactly zero.</p>\n<p><em>None of it’s real.</em></p>\n<p>It came into him the way the cold comes into you in winter water: not all at once, but everywhere.</p>\n<p><em>None of it’s real. None of it matters. It all cancels. Everything I’ve done and everything I haven’t. The pen. The truth in the Commons. The forty-seven. Ben’s eleven years. Ruth’s fifty. All of it. It all adds up to nothing. It always did. It always will. There’s nothing to pay because there was never anything to borrow. There’s nothing. There’s nothing. There’s only the sum, and the sum is⁠—</em></p>\n<p>He had stopped walking.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know when he’d stopped. He was standing on the Rim, still, with his arms round her, looking into the Lamp, and he could feel the weight of the zero on him like the weight of the whole Vault, like the weight at the top of the Breach, and his legs were going. His arms were going. He could feel the exo trying to hold him up and not knowing why, and he thought: <em>let it go. Let it go. What does it matter. It’s all zero. Let go⁠—</em></p>\n<p>“<em>Cas.</em>”</p>\n<p>Far away.</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas. Casimir. Look at me.</em>”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t. He was looking at the Lamp.</p>\n<p>Something hit his visor. Hard. The back of her head, slamming back against the glass. Once. Twice. The visor rang like a bell.</p>\n<p>“<em>Look at me.</em>”</p>\n<p>She’d turned round. On his boots, in his arms, inside the bubble, she’d turned round to face him — he felt her do it, felt her body twist against the chestplate — and now her face was in front of his visor, filling it, blotting out the Lamp. Her face, three centimetres away. Her eyes. Grey-green. Not patient. Wild.</p>\n<p>“I saw it too,” said Ione. “I saw it. Just now. I saw what you saw. I know. I know what it is.”</p>\n<p>“It’s zero,” he said. His voice came out of him from very far off. “It’s all zero, Ione. All of it. It cancels. None of it⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>I know.</em>”</p>\n<p>She had her hands on either side of his helmet. The good one and the splinted one. Holding his head. Making him look at her.</p>\n<p>“I watched the light in the gallery for three years,” she said. “It was nothing. It was <em>light</em>. It wasn’t anybody. It was nine minutes going round and round with nobody in it. It cancelled out, every time — Ruth said one-thirty-five and the key fell and she pulled the hammer and it went dark and started again and at the end of every cycle it added up to nothing. Nothing. Nothing had changed. Nothing ever would.” Her eyes were bright and wet and fierce. “And I loved them anyway. Do you hear me? I <em>loved them anyway</em>. It all cancelled and I loved them. That’s all. That’s all there is. Zero isn’t <em>nothing</em>, Cas.” She pressed her forehead against his visor. “Zero’s <em>balanced</em>.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“Count,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“You’re good at it. You count everything. You counted the hammer all night. You counted the rungs in the conduit. You counted Ben’s seconds.” She pulled back so that he could see her face. “Count me.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t understand.</p>\n<p>“<em>Count me</em>,” she said. “I’m one. I’m here. I’m one thing, and I’m here, and I’m not zero, I’m <em>one</em>. Count me.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her. At her face in front of his visor, filling it. Her eyes. The white streak. The stitches over her eye. The mole — he’d never noticed it — the tiny mole at the corner of her mouth. Her breath fogging the glass.</p>\n<p>“One,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Again.”</p>\n<p>“One.”</p>\n<p>“Again.”</p>\n<p>“One.” His voice was coming back. It was coming back from wherever it had gone, like the rail, like her name, solid and rusted and real. “One. One. You’re one. You’re here.”</p>\n<p>“I’m here,” said Ione. “And you’re one. And that’s two. And it doesn’t matter what the sum is. It doesn’t matter what it all comes to at the bottom of the page. We’re not the bottom of the page. We’re <em>here</em>.” She was crying. “Now turn round. Turn round, and look at the stair, and don’t look at that thing again, and walk. Please. Please, Cas. I can’t carry you. You have to walk.”</p>\n<p>He turned away from the Lamp.</p>\n<p>He looked at the stair. Twenty metres. Steel steps going up to a hatch in the black underside of the Crown. The chalk arrows leading straight to it, fresh and white on the flickering steel.</p>\n<p>“Left foot,” said Ione, and turned round again in his arms, and stood on his boots.</p>\n<p>He walked.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hatch was open. Of course it was open; the Crown crew had come down through it the night of the Kindling, running for the lift, and nobody had closed it after. It was a round steel hatch in the black keelstone underside of the Crown, at the top of the stair, and beyond it, darkness.</p>\n<p>They climbed the stair. On the last step, before the hatch, Ione stepped off his boots, out of his arms, onto the steel — and the bubble let her go, and she staggered, but the stair was in the lee of the Crown itself, the great keelstone lens overhead pulling against the Lamp, and the weight here was bearable. Just. She caught the rail. She stood there, breathing hard, holding on to it.</p>\n<p>He stood beside her. The keel hummed in his spine. It was low now. He could hear exactly how low. It had given them the Lace, and it had cost.</p>\n<p>Below them, down the stair, the Rim ran back through the forest of light toward the gallery, and the chalk arrows ran along it.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. Then he looked at Ione.</p>\n<p>She was looking at them too.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to have to draw those,” she said. Her voice was very quiet. “Aren’t I. On the way back.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Because I followed them. So I know the way. So I’ll draw them. So I can follow them.” She was quiet. “They were never anyone’s. They never came from anywhere. They’re just — true.”</p>\n<p>“Like the key,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Like the key.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t say anything else. She didn’t say <em>on the way back, alone</em>. She didn’t say <em>in your suit</em>. She didn’t look at the suit at all.</p>\n<p>Neither did he.</p>\n<p>He looked instead at the hatch, and the darkness in it, and he thought about a small grey figure on Spindle Four, climbing down alone, raising its hand. He thought: <em>that’s me. That’s me, coming back without her.</em> And the thing under his breastbone that had set its jaw said again, very quietly, very clearly: <em>No.</em></p>\n<p>He didn’t know yet what the Clause needed. He didn’t know what the plate meant, or what the witness was, or what he’d find under the floor. But he knew one thing, standing on the stair at the top of the world with the keel running down in his spine and the Lace moving slowly below him. He knew it the way he’d known the fractal number on the telemetry screen, the way he’d known it at the window in the Collar: <em>at last</em>.</p>\n<p>Whatever it asked for, he’d be the one who gave it. Whatever stayed up here, he’d be the one who stayed. And then the figure on Spindle Four would be wrong, and it would be her coming down, not him — her, alive — and he’d have beaten it. For once in his life he’d have looked at a projection and said <em>no</em> and made it false.</p>\n<p>It didn’t feel like dying. That was the strange thing. It didn’t feel like the window in the Collar at all. It felt like the opposite.</p>\n<p>“Come on,” he said.</p>\n<p>They went up into the Crown.</p>"}, {"file": "17-the-crown.md", "label": "Chapter 16", "title": "The Crown", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5313, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I am sorry. There was no other way to build it.</p><span class=\"attr\">Inscription, inner plate of the Keystone, signed I.H.</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The Crown was full of snow.</p>\n<p>That was what he thought, coming up through the hatch into the grey light: that it had snowed. That in the hundred and sixty-eight years since Ruth Tally and eleven others had run for the lift, snow had come into the highest room in the Draw and lain there, deep and soft and undisturbed, over everything.</p>\n<p>It was dust. It lay over the floor ankle-deep, and it lay over the consoles and the chairs and the desks in thick grey drifts, and it lay along the top of the great curved window in a soft grey ridge. When they walked through it, it rose round their boots in slow clouds and hung in the air and didn’t settle. The air was very still. It smelled of nothing. Of stone and age.</p>\n<p>He’d seen the Crown in the ghost-light. He’d seen it in the old footage, in the mural in the crisis room. Crisp. White coats. The needle swinging over. He’d seen it thirty minutes ago — a hundred and sixty-eight years ago — full of people, full of light.</p>\n<p>He stood in it now and didn’t recognise it.</p>\n<p>The consoles had furred. Their screens were grey and blind under the dust, their switches rusted into place, the plastic of their casings gone brittle and cracked and curled. Dieter’s mug was on the desk where he’d left it, and when Cas brushed the dust off it, it was not a mug. It was a shape of mug, in stone — the coffee in it gone to a brown crust, the ceramic crazed all over with fine cracks, like something dug out of a tomb. The chairs had collapsed on themselves. A clipboard hung on a hook by the door, and the paper on it had gone to a grey lace that fell apart when he breathed on it.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall had stopped. Its hands had fused to its face. They pointed at a time that meant nothing — twenty past something, or twenty to — and would never point at anything else.</p>\n<p>“It’s old,” said Ione. She was standing in the dust by the hatch with her arms wrapped round herself, looking round. “It’s so <em>old</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Time’s been going faster up here. Much faster.” He looked at the window. “And going round. This whole room’s been in the Fold since the Kindling. It’s had⁠—” he didn’t know “⁠—a very long time.”</p>\n<p>The window was the worst of it. The great curved sheet of armoured glass, forty metres long. Along its lower edge the walkway still hung where it had torn from its frame, sagging, twisted, half-fallen. The frame above it, where the first thread of the Lace had touched — the black keelstone that nothing could break — had gone white. A strip of it as wide as a hand, all the way along one section of the frame, gone white and soft and slumped like the wax on a candle. And through the glass, very close — so close that the Crown seemed to hang in it like a lantern hung in a tree — the Lace. Its threads came down past the window in bright curtains. One of them came <em>through</em>. He saw it and stopped. A single thread, fine as a hair, came in through the glass near the torn walkway as if the glass weren’t there, and crossed the room at waist height, and went down into the floor near the far end of the gallery — near the place where, in the ghost-light, Ruth had knelt.</p>\n<p>It moved, very slowly, the way the threads always moved. It didn’t flicker. It was the most real thing in the room.</p>\n<p>He understood the dust, then. A keelstone room, sealed on every side but a hatch, with a thread of the Tear running through it: the locker room in the Works, a thousand times over. For every hour the city below had lived since the Kindling, the Crown had been living at the speed of the Lace.</p>\n<p>“Don’t touch that,” he said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>And in the far end of the Crown, down by the thread, there was a faint light.</p>\n<p>He’d thought it was the Lace, at first. It wasn’t. It was the ghost-light — the same white-violet glow he’d seen going round in the gallery windows, but much fainter here, a thin shimmer in the dust-thick air, like the memory of a picture. The room’s own light, from that night, still just barely hanging in it. He could see, if he looked, the faint shapes of people. Moving. Running. Faded almost to nothing.</p>\n<p>At the far end of the room, by the Keystone, a woman in a white coat was on her knees in the dust, looking up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The plate was still open.</p>\n<p>It lay back on its hinge where Ruth had thrown it, a hundred and sixty-eight years ago, a square of brushed steel furred with dust. Cas knelt and wiped the dust off it with his gauntlet. The letters came up under his hand, engraved, clear.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>Under it, set into the floor, the housing. Black keelstone, smooth and round, the size of a dinner plate, with a recessed ring around its edge and, in the middle, a keyhole. A narrow cross.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt beside him. She knelt exactly where the ghost of Ruth was kneeling — he saw her do it, saw the faint white shape of the woman in the lab coat overlap with her, the two of them in the same place for a moment, one real and one light, both looking at the lock — and Ione didn’t flinch. She’d done it a hundred thousand times. She took the key off its cord.</p>\n<p>She held it for a moment.</p>\n<p>“Do you want to⁠—” she said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas. “It’s yours.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. Then she put the key into the lock.</p>\n<p>It went in. It went in the way a thing goes into the place it was made for: smoothly, completely, with a small soft sound like a breath let out. The cross-shaped bit sank into the cross-shaped hole and seated. She put her fingers on the bow.</p>\n<p>She turned it.</p>\n<p>It was stiff. It didn’t move, and then it did — a quarter turn, heavy, resisting, the way a lock resists that hasn’t been turned for a hundred and sixty-eight years or ever — and then there was a click. Deep. Not in the lock. In the floor. In the whole Crown. It went down through the keelstone into Spindle One like the first stroke of the hammer going down through Tier Nine, and he felt it in his knees.</p>\n<p>The housing opened.</p>\n<p>The recessed ring round its edge split into segments — six of them, black keelstone petals — and drew back, sliding under the floor, like the iris of an eye opening. And under it, where the housing had been, there was a hollow in the floor. And in the hollow, there was the machine.</p>\n<p>It was very simple. That was the first thing he saw. After a lifetime of the Draw, of its vast humming inhuman machinery, the rectifier halls and the tensioners the size of buildings, it was almost shocking how simple it was. A wheel: a small brass wheel set upright, about the size of a hand, finely toothed round its rim like the wheel of an old instrument, with a knurled grip. Beside it, a gauge: a round dial under glass, with a needle, and a single line drawn across its face from edge to edge, and a narrow band of colour on either side of the line. Beside that, a lever — red, with a black-and-yellow collar, exactly like the Dead Man’s Hammer by the lift. And round the whole of it, set into the rim of the hollow, a ring. A dial. Engraved with figures, very small, going round.</p>\n<p>And on the inside of the housing’s lid — on the back of one of the six petals, which had folded up and back and now stood upright at the edge of the hollow like a little headstone — there was a second plate.</p>\n<p>He read it. He read it on his knees in the dust with the thread of the Lace moving slowly a metre away and the ghost of Ruth Tally kneeling beside him looking up at nothing, and Ione beside her, and the keel humming low in his spine.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE — PROCEDURE<br><br>1. SET THE INTERVAL.<br>2. ARM. THE LATTICE WILL RELEASE ITS LAST HOLD. THE DEBT WILL BE CALLED.<br>IT WILL BE PAID AT THE END OF THE INTERVAL.<br>3. HOLD. AS THE PAYMENT GATHERS, ITS PHASE WILL WANDER.<br>KEEP THE NEEDLE ON THE LINE BY HAND. DO NOT LET IT CROSS.<br>4. AT THE END OF THE INTERVAL THE FOLD WILL CLOSE UPON THE PAYMENT<br>AND UPON ALL THAT IS WITHIN THE CROWN.<br>IT WILL CLOSE INTO A SINGLE INSTANT.<br>NOTHING WILL PASS OUT OF IT. NOTHING WILL PASS INTO IT. IT WILL NOT END.<br><br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS.<br>THE WITNESS DOES NOT RETURN.<br><br>I AM SORRY. THERE WAS NO OTHER WAY TO BUILD IT. — I.H.</div>\n<p>He read it twice.</p>\n<p>Then he sat back on his heels in the dust and didn’t move.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Tell me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She’d read it too. She was kneeling beside the hollow, looking at the plate, and her face was very calm, the way it had been calm all morning on Spindle Four, and her hands were in her lap, and they were perfectly steady. He noticed that. They weren’t shaking now.</p>\n<p>“Tell me what it does,” she said. “Plainly. I’ve watched it for three years and I never knew. Tell me.”</p>\n<p>So he told her.</p>\n<p>He did it plainly. The way he’d told her about the Lees, about the Shear, about the Bells. He’d spent his whole life learning to say complicated things so that they sounded simple, and then fifteen years saying simple things so that they sounded complicated, and he found now that the first skill had come back to him, all at once, like a language you’d spoken as a child.</p>\n<p>“The debt’s coming due anyway,” he said. “You know that. The Lamp’s going to pay it — all of it, all at once — when the Lace reaches the Rim. Thirty-odd rounds, down in the city. Less. Whenever. We don’t know exactly when. Nobody does.” He touched the ring round the hollow. The dial. “This lets you choose when. You set the time. You pull the lever — that’s <em>arming</em>. The Lattice lets go of the last thing it’s holding. The loan gets called. And at the end of the time you set, the payment comes. Right here. At the Lamp.”</p>\n<p>“And then everything ends.”</p>\n<p>“No. That’s the — that’s what she built. That’s the Clause.” He looked at the thread of the Lace crossing the room. “When the Lamp broke — the night of the Kindling — it didn’t just break. It broke <em>time</em>, up here. It made the Fold. The Lace, the Crown, the Carousel — you’ve seen it. Things going round. Keys falling forward. Arrows nobody drew. The Fold’s full of loops. And Hollowell knew it would be. She knew that if it ever broke badly enough to kill us, it would break badly enough to do <em>that</em>. And she built this to use it.”</p>\n<p>He took a breath.</p>\n<p>“At the moment the payment comes,” he said, “the Fold closes. Round the Lamp. Round the Crown. Round the payment. It closes up into a loop. A loop in time. A loop so short it’s — nothing. An instant. One instant, whose future is itself. The payment starts — the very first instant of it, the very first edge of the light — and then the loop closes, and that instant is joined to itself, end to beginning, and it’s sealed. It can’t go on to the next instant. There isn’t a next instant. The debt’s paid, and the payment never arrives. It’s always just about to. For ever.” He heard his voice go strange. “She built a sarcophagus. Out of a single moment.”</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“And the rest of it?” she said. “Holdfast? Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Outside the seal, everything goes back. The debt’s inside the instant, and the loan’s inside the instant, and they balance, and they’re sealed. Nothing’s pushing any more. The Lamp’s gone. Time down there comes back to — ordinary. The same as Outside. The Shear goes. The Drop’s a ladder.” He looked at her. “It works, Ione. I think it works. I think she was right.”</p>\n<p>“<em>The sealing requires a witness</em>,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Tell me that. Plainly.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the gauge. At the needle, resting now at one side of the dial, and the line across its face, and the narrow coloured bands on either side.</p>\n<p>“When you arm it,” he said, “the payment starts to gather. At the core. And as it gathers, it — wanders. Its phase. It won’t come in straight. It’ll come in the way the lamp-weather came in the Breach, in gusts, in swings. And the seal has to close on it exactly. Exactly in phase. Or the loop won’t close clean, and the payment gets out.” He touched the brass wheel. “So someone has to keep the needle on the line. By hand. Watching it. Turning the wheel, a little this way, a little that, as it wanders. All the way to the end. Right up to the instant.”</p>\n<p>“Why a person?” said Ione. “Why not — tie it? Lock it?”</p>\n<p>“Because it wanders. It won’t sit still. You’d need something that could see it and think.” He shook his head. “And nothing that thinks can live up here except a person. Not a machine. You’ve seen what the suit’s electronics are doing. Up here, a machine’s clocks come apart in a second. It has to be eyes. And a hand. And a head that can guess which way it’ll go next.” He looked at the plate. “That’s the witness. That’s all she meant. Somebody to <em>watch</em>.”</p>\n<p>“And the witness is inside the Crown,” said Ione. “When it closes.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“<em>All that is within the Crown.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Into a single instant.</em> <em>It will not end.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him.</p>\n<p>“And they’re in it,” she said. “The witness. For ever. In one instant. Living it.”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>She blinked.</p>\n<p>“Not living it,” he said. “Not the way you mean. Not over and over. You told me. On the spindle.” He found his voice was quite steady. “If a loop’s really closed, there’s nobody inside to be tired of it. It’s just once. It’s once, and it’s true, and it stays true. It’s only for ever for whoever’s outside.” He looked at the brass wheel. “It’s not a prison. It’s not the gallery. Whoever’s in it doesn’t go round. They’re just — there. In one instant. Once. Exactly as they are.” He paused. “It’s only for ever from out here.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer. She was looking at him with an expression he’d never seen on her face before and couldn’t read at all.</p>\n<p>He looked back at the plate.</p>\n<p><em>Default</em>, he thought. The word you couldn’t say in the Commonweal. The word Rask had never said, that the Ministry had never said in ninety-one years, that he’d never once said out loud in his life.</p>\n<p>“Default,” he said. Out loud, in the Crown.</p>\n<p>It sounded like a very small word.</p>\n<p>He found that he felt calm. Entirely calm. Calmer than he had felt at the window in the Collar, calmer than he’d felt in fifteen years. Calmer than he could remember feeling. He’d come in here for this. He hadn’t known what it was, and it was here, under a plate in the floor, and it was simple, and it was something he could do.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Take the key out,” he said. “We don’t need it any more. It’s done its⁠—”</p>\n<p>She was already reaching for it. It was still in the lock, in the middle of the hollow, standing up among the wheel and the gauge and the lever. She took hold of its bow and drew it out.</p>\n<p>The thread of the Lace moved.</p>\n<p>It had been lying across the room at waist height, fine as a hair, from the window to the floor. It had been lying there perfectly still except for its slow weed-in-a-current drift, a metre from the Keystone. And as Ione drew the key up out of the lock, the thread drifted — a few centimetres; a hand’s width — and one of its finest branches, a filament so fine it was almost only a glitter, swung across the hollow.</p>\n<p>It touched the key.</p>\n<p>It was nothing. A brush. Less than a second. There was a sound, very faint, very high, like a fingernail on glass. And Ione’s hand jerked, and the key came out of the lock, and she was holding it up in front of her, in the dust-thick air, staring at it.</p>\n<p>Along the shaft of the key, from the bow almost to the bit, there was a line.</p>\n<p>A fine bright line, scored into the black keelstone. Keelstone, that nothing could scratch. It shone faintly in the grey light, white-violet at first, then fading, as they watched, to a thin pale grey. A scratch. Straight, and very fine, and a little crooked at the end, where it ran off the edge of the shaft near the bit.</p>\n<p>Ione made a sound.</p>\n<p>She was staring at it. She turned the key over in her fingers — slowly, very slowly — and ran the ball of her thumb along the line. From the bow to the bit. And back. The way you’d trace a scar.</p>\n<p>“It’s always had this,” she said.</p>\n<p>He didn’t understand, for a moment.</p>\n<p>“This,” said Ione. Her voice was very thin. “This scratch. It’s always had it. Since the gantry. Since I picked it up. I’ve — every night, Cas. Every night for eleven years. I lie in the hammock and I hold it and I run my thumb along the scratch. Here. It’s how I go to sleep.” Her thumb moved along it. “It goes crooked at the end. Just here. I know it. I know exactly where it goes crooked. I’ve always wondered what did it. What could scratch keelstone.” She looked up at him. Her face was white. “<em>This</em> did it. Just now. It’s always had it because it’s just got it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the key in her hand. At the thin grey line along its shaft.</p>\n<p><em>The jinn must return exactly as it left.</em> The old paper. His professor, joking. <em>You’d have no way to know where it came from. Because it didn’t come from anywhere.</em></p>\n<p>“It has to go back,” he said.</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“The key,” he said. “It came from here. It came out of the Fold, onto the gantry, in the Carousel. For you to find. And now it’s — it’s done. It’s opened the lock. It’s got its scratch. It’s exactly as it was when you picked it up.” He made himself say it. “It has to go back. To the gantry. Eleven years ago. For you to find it.”</p>\n<p>“There’s no one else,” said Ione. Very quietly. “Is there. To take it.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Because I found it. So I have to leave it. So I can find it.” Her thumb was still on the scratch. “It never came from anywhere.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“It was never <em>made</em>. It never⁠—” She stopped. She closed her hand round the key. She held it against her chest, against her breastbone, the way she’d worn it on its cord for eleven years. “Cas. How? How do I⁠—”</p>\n<p>And then she stopped, and her head came up, and she turned — slowly — and looked down the length of the Crown, away from the Keystone, to the far end of the room. To the place where the floor stepped down, where a short steel stair went down into a lower space under the main deck.</p>\n<p>“The service deck,” she said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She’d seen it from below. That was what she told him, going down the stair. For three years, from the gantry — every time she crossed it, four thousand times — she’d seen the underside of the Crown above her, the black keelstone lens on the top of Spindle One, and in its underside, a row of small round windows. Portholes. The service deck, under the main floor, where the Crown’s machinery had been. And in one of the portholes, sometimes — not every cycle, but sometimes — there’d been a light.</p>\n<p>“Not the ghost-light,” she said. “Not the Crown’s light, going round. A different light. Steady. Grey. Like daylight through a door.” She was going down the stair ahead of him, one hand on the rail, the key clenched in the other. “I used to wonder what it was. I used to think it was someone up there. Watching.” She stopped at the bottom. “It was this.”</p>\n<p>The service deck was low and cramped and full of dead machinery — pumps, cable trunks, a bank of rusted cabinets — and dust, the same deep grey dust, and on the far side, in the curved outer wall, a row of small round portholes looking down.</p>\n<p>And in the middle of the deck, near the portholes, the air was wrong.</p>\n<p>He saw it at once. He’d have seen it anywhere now. A shimmer. A vertical line of wrongness hanging in the air a little above the floor, the height of a person, a hand’s width wide, like the seam in the Carousel — but brighter. Much brighter. It wasn’t a ripple. It was a <em>door</em>. A tall narrow slot of grey light standing in the middle of the dead deck, as if someone had cut the air with a knife and there was a grey afternoon on the other side of it. Round its edges, very faintly, the finest threads of the Lace came and went.</p>\n<p>And through it — when he stepped round to look through it — he saw the gantry.</p>\n<p>It was below. The slot of light looked <em>down</em>, as if it were a window in a floor; through it he could see the long steel walkway from Four to One, slung across the violet gulf, a little way below them. Pitted with rust. Sagging slightly in the middle. Exactly as it had been an hour ago, when they’d crossed it.</p>\n<p>Not exactly. He looked, and looked again. The rust was a little less. The handrail, which had been bent in one place near the middle, was straight.</p>\n<p>And at the near end of it — where it met the curved wall of the gallery, the ring round Spindle One — the gallery’s windows were lit. The ghost-light, going round.</p>\n<p>“That’s then,” said Ione. She was standing beside him, looking down through the slot. Her voice was very calm. “That’s the gantry. Then. Eleven years ago. When I was in there.” She looked at the gallery’s lit windows. “I’m in there. Right now. Then.”</p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything for a while.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know how long it stays open,” he said at last. “It might close. It might⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.” She looked down at the key in her hand. Then at him. “I’ll be quick.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know how it goes,” she said. “I’ve always known how it goes. I just never knew it was me.” She almost smiled. “I’ll put it on the gantry. By the loose bolt. And I’ll stand at the end, by the wall, where she saw me. Where I saw me. And she’ll come across, and find it, and look up. And I’ll turn away.” She put her hand flat on his chestplate. “And then I’ll come back. That’s all. That’s how it goes.”</p>\n<p>“How do you know you come back?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those grey-green eyes.</p>\n<p>“Because she found a seam,” said Ione, “where the woman had been standing. And went through it. And got out.” She took her hand off his chest. “I’ll leave it open for her. On my way back.”</p>\n<p>She stepped into the light.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He waited.</p>\n<p>It was seconds. He counted them — of course he counted them — standing in the dust of the service deck in the grey light from the slot, with the dead machinery all round him and the keel humming low in his spine. Eleven seconds. Twelve. He watched the slot. He couldn’t see her through it. He could see the gantry below, and the gulf, and the lit windows of the gallery, and no one.</p>\n<p>Twenty. Thirty.</p>\n<p>He thought about a girl of seventeen lying on the floor of the gallery below, letting the nine minutes go through her. He thought about her getting up, one cycle, and deciding to learn their names. He thought about her crossing the gantry four thousand times, knowing every bolt.</p>\n<p>Forty-one. Forty-two.</p>\n<p>She came out of the light.</p>\n<p>She came through it the way you’d come through a doorway in a high wind — stumbling, catching herself, her good hand out. And she was different. He saw it at once. It had been forty-two seconds and it had not been forty-two seconds for her; he could see it in her face. It was grey. Her eyes were red. Her lips were cracked, as though she hadn’t drunk for hours. There was rust on her knees, on her hands — the gantry’s rust — and dust in her hair, and she was shaking. Not a tremor. All of her.</p>\n<p>Her hands were empty.</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>She walked straight past him. She walked to the wall of the service deck, under the portholes, and put her hand on it, and stood there with her back to him, her head down, her other hand pressed against her mouth.</p>\n<p>“How long?” he said.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.” Her voice came out muffled. “Hours. Hours. I had to wait. I put it down — by the bolt — and I went to the end, by the wall, and I waited. And she didn’t come. And she didn’t come. And the light went round and round in the windows and I could hear it, <em>is it going to be too bright</em>, and she didn’t — and I thought, what if she doesn’t, what if I’ve got it wrong, what if it’s the wrong cycle, what if⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<p>“And then she came,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“She came out of the gallery door. Onto the gantry. And she was⁠—” Ione’s shoulders moved. “She was so <em>thin</em>, Cas. She was so thin. I didn’t — I don’t remember being that thin. Her arms were like sticks. Her hair was filthy, she’d been cutting it with a — with a bit of metal, you could see, it was all hacked. And she had the grey. Just starting. Just here.” She touched her own temple, the white streak, without turning round. “She was walking with her head down. Like she’d walked across there four thousand times. Because she had.”</p>\n<p>Her voice broke. She waited. She went on.</p>\n<p>“And she saw it. She stopped. She just — stopped. And stood there. She stood there for such a long time, looking at it. And then she crouched down and picked it up. And she looked at it in her hand. And she ran her thumb along it.” Ione’s own thumb moved, against the wall, along nothing. “Along the scratch. The first time. And then she looked up.”</p>\n<p>“And she saw you.”</p>\n<p>“And she saw me.” Ione turned round then. Her face was wet and grey and he had never seen anything so tired. “And she said <em>Mam?</em>”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“She said <em>Mam</em>,” said Ione Tally. “Like a question. Like a little girl. <em>Mam?</em> And I stood there at the end of the gantry, and I wanted⁠—” Her face twisted. “I wanted to say, <em>no, love, it’s not Mam, it’s you, it’s you, you get out, you get out of here and you go home and Gran’s waiting and you’ll be all right, you’ll be all right, you’ll be⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>She put both hands over her face.</p>\n<p>“But I remembered,” she said, through them. “I remembered she didn’t say anything. The woman. She didn’t say a word. She just turned away. I’ve remembered it for eleven years. So I knew. I knew I didn’t. And I stood there, and I tried to make my mouth — and I couldn’t. I don’t know if I couldn’t or if I just — didn’t. I don’t know. I don’t <em>know</em>. I stood there and I let her think I was Mam. I let her think Mam had come up to find her. I let her run along the gantry toward me shouting <em>Mam</em> and I turned my back on her and I walked away.”</p>\n<p>He crossed the deck. He put his arms round her. She let him. She put her face against the chestplate of the suit, and he held her, and she shook.</p>\n<p>“I left the seam open,” she said, into the suit, after a long time. “Where I’d been standing. I felt it close behind me when I came through, but not all at once. Slowly. Like a wound. I think she’ll — I know she’ll get through. I know she does. I’m here.” She laughed, a terrible small laugh. “I’m <em>here</em>. So she does.”</p>\n<p>“She does,” he said. “You did.”</p>\n<p>“I let her think I was her mother.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“She’s going to go down the spindle,” said Ione, “and look in a water-trough and see she’s twenty, and she’s going to spend eleven years thinking her mother came up to the top of the world to save her and then turned away. She’s going to wonder, every night, why she turned away.” Her voice was very quiet. “And it was me. It was always me.”</p>\n<p>He held her.</p>\n<p>Behind them, the slot of grey light hung in the dead air of the service deck. As they stood there, it narrowed. Slowly, like a door swinging shut. A hand’s width. A finger’s. A line. And then only a faint shimmer, and the threads of the Lace coming and going round the place where it had been, and then nothing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went back up the stair into the Crown.</p>\n<p>The hollow in the floor stood open. The machine in it: the small brass wheel, the gauge with its line, the red lever, the ring of the dial. The petal of keelstone standing upright at its edge with the plate on it. Beside it, the faint ghost of Ruth Tally, on her knees in the dust, looking up at nothing. At the place beside the Keystone, where someone would stand to reach the wheel.</p>\n<p>Ione went and stood by the hollow. She looked down at the plate. She read it again. He could see her lips move.</p>\n<p><em>The sealing requires a witness.</em></p>\n<p><em>The witness does not return.</em></p>\n<p>Then she looked up. Not at him.</p>\n<p>At the suit.</p>\n<p>He was standing in it, in the middle of the Crown, in the dust, with the keel humming low in his spine. And she looked at it — at the grey plates, the gouge down the left side, the housing on his back with its band of lights and its white painted <em>1</em>, the rigger’s harness she’d spent three nights splicing — the way you look at a thing you’ve seen before, a long time ago, from very far away.</p>\n<p>He looked at it too. He looked down at his own gauntlets. At the chestplate. At himself.</p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything at all.</p>"}, {"file": "18-the-witness.md", "label": "Chapter 17", "title": "The Witness", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5665, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine.</p><span class=\"attr\">Holdfast riggers’ wedding vow</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">“It’s me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it quite quietly, standing by the hollow in the dust, with her hands at her sides. As if she were telling him the time.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Listen to me.” She didn’t raise her voice. “I’ve thought about this. I’ve had eleven years to think about it, and I’ve had since the Rim, and I’ve had the — the hours. On the gantry. Waiting for her. Listen.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve already lived my forever.” She said it evenly. “Three years in that gallery. Three years of one night. I know what it is. I know what it’s like to be in one moment and not get out. I’m not frightened of it. I’m the only person in the world who isn’t. And you told me — you said it yourself, just now — it’s once. For whoever’s in it, it’s only once.” She looked at him. “I can do once.”</p>\n<p>“So can I.”</p>\n<p>“You’ve got a daughter.” Her voice caught on it, and went on. “You’ve got a little girl called Nell who collects stars, and a woman in a chair at the bottom of the Anvil who’s waiting to buy you a drink, and a whole city of — six hundred million people, you said, and a sky, and rain, and a sea. You’ve got an <em>ordinary</em> waiting for you. You haven’t lived it yet. You told me in the hut you’ve had fifteen years of the same day. You haven’t lived your ordinary.” She shook her head. “I’ve got Gran. And Gran’s eighty-three, and she’s dying, and she’s never once said so. That’s all I’ve got. That’s all I’ve had, since I came down. I’ve been a ghost in Holdfast for eleven years. I sat on the Tollers’ step and watched people step round me. I’ve already lived my forever, Cas. You haven’t lived your ordinary. It’s me.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her for a long time.</p>\n<p>“I saw it come down,” he said.</p>\n<p>She went still.</p>\n<p>“On the Rim. In the Lace. The suit.” He looked down at himself — at the grey plates, the gauntlets. “My suit. With your harness on it. Coming down Four. Alone. I saw it, and I thought: <em>that’s me</em>. That’s me coming back without her. I thought I was going to go home and you were going to die up here.” He heard his voice shake and didn’t stop it. “And I decided — right there, on the Rim, with you standing on my boots — that I wasn’t going to let it. I thought, <em>whatever it asks for, I’ll be the one who stays</em>. I thought if I stayed, the vision would be wrong. It’d be you coming down. Not me. I’d have <em>beaten</em> it. For once in my life I’d have looked at a projection and made it false.”</p>\n<p>Ione was staring at him.</p>\n<p>“I saw it too,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know. You told me. At seventeen⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No. I mean⁠—” She stopped. Her face had gone very strange. “I’ve been waiting eleven years at the top of the wells for a man in a suit to come up. And I always knew — I’ve always known, since I was seventeen — that he comes back down. Alone. I saw it. So I knew. From the first minute. From when I pulled you out.” She put her hand to her mouth. “I’ve known the whole time that you go home and I don’t. And on the Rim, when we saw it again, I thought — I decided — I’d make it wrong. I’d stay. So you’d go home to your girl. So it’d be — <em>not</em> you, coming down alone, leaving me. So it’d be you coming down alone because I <em>chose</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<p>They looked at each other across the hollow in the floor.</p>\n<p>“We both⁠—” he said.</p>\n<p>“We both saw you.” She was breathing very fast. “We both saw you come down, and we both decided to — we both⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped again.</p>\n<p>And then something happened to her face. He watched it happen. He watched it the way he’d watched Ben’s hand appear on the glass: all at once, whole, from nowhere. Her eyes went wide. Her hand came away from her mouth. She looked at him — at the suit — at the grey plates and the housing and the harness — and she took a step back from the hollow, and another, and her face crumpled.</p>\n<p>“Cas,” she said. Her voice cracked right down the middle. “Cas, it was the suit. It was never — it was the <em>suit</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>He heard it. He heard it twice — once now, in the Crown, from her mouth, and once on the Rim, in the Lace, from nowhere, an hour ago, a lifetime ago, <em>it was the suit</em>, in exactly that voice, exactly that crack in it — and the two of them rang together in his head like two strokes of the same bell, and he understood.</p>\n<p>They’d never seen <em>him</em>. Either of them. Not at seventeen, not on the Rim. They’d seen a suit. Grey plates and a humming keel and a harness spliced in rigger’s strapping, climbing down Spindle Four alone. Whoever was in it. They’d both looked at the suit and seen him inside it, because it was his, because who else would be in it — and it had never been a vision of who lived. It had only ever been a vision of who wore the suit.</p>\n<p>He looked down at himself.</p>\n<p>Then he looked at her. Short, broad, strong. Her rope on her shoulder. Her splinted wrist. The white streak.</p>\n<p>“It’s you,” he said. “Ione. It’s you coming down. It was always you.”</p>\n<p>“<em>No</em>.”</p>\n<p>“It was you in it. On the Rim, raising its hand. At seventeen. It was always⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.” She’d backed up against a console. Dust rose round her in a slow cloud. “No. No. Then it’s not — then it isn’t a <em>choice</em>. Then it was always going to be — I don’t get to — you don’t get to⁠—” She was shaking her head. “Then it’s just the key again. It’s just the arrows. It’s just something that’s true that nobody chose, and we’re only⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It’s already true,” said Cas. “And I’m still choosing it.”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He didn’t know he was going to say the next thing until he said it.</p>\n<p>“Ruth saw me.”</p>\n<p>Ione stared at him.</p>\n<p>“In the Log. Year Thirty-One. I read it in the Tower, the first night. I didn’t understand it. I didn’t — I read it twice and turned the page.” He could see it: the pencil, the short entry set apart with a line above and below. “She saw a man at the Keystone. At 02:09. After the key fell. Kneeling where she was kneeling. Dressed close, in grey, like a diver.” He looked down at his gauntlets. At the suit. Under it, he knew exactly what he was wearing: the grey liner. The one-piece. The thing that made all three of them look like depressed seals. “His hand on a wheel that wasn’t there. She said he looked at her like he knew her. She said he was very calm.” He looked up. “She said there was a cord knotted round his wrist.”</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“She’s been looking at me,” he said. “In the gallery. Every cycle. For three years, you watched her look up at the Keystone, at nothing, like someone was there. You stood in the place. You said it wasn’t you.” He swallowed. “It was me. She was looking at me. At — this. Now. Here.”</p>\n<p>Ione’s hand had gone to her pocket.</p>\n<p>He watched it. She didn’t seem to know she was doing it. Her good hand went into the pocket of her tunic and closed round something there, and came out, and opened, and lay in her palm. A length of thin cord. The cord from the wedding. The one she’d tied round his wrist on the rooftop in the Drop District with the drums going and the tower swaying, and taken off again with one tug. <em>Riggers’ hitch. Holds under load. Won’t slip. That’s the only one you’ll need.</em></p>\n<p>She looked at it in her hand.</p>\n<p>“It’s not on your wrist,” she said. Very quietly. “It’s not there yet.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“She saw it there. So it’s there. So someone ties it.” She looked up at him. “So <em>I</em> tie it. That’s the only way it gets there. I have to do it. I have to choose to do it. Like the arrows. Like the key.” Her voice broke again. “You’re asking me to <em>tie it on</em>.”</p>\n<p>“I’m not asking you anything,” said Cas. “It’s yours. It’s your rope.”</p>\n<p>She closed her hand round it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Why?” she said.</p>\n<p>It was a long time later. Or not long. They were sitting on the floor of the Crown, in the dust, by the hollow, side by side, their backs against the dead console. The keel hummed. The ghost of Ruth knelt by the Keystone, looking up. The thread of the Lace lay across the room, moving slowly. Outside the great window, the Lamp burned.</p>\n<p>“Tell me why,” said Ione. “Not the vision. Not the Log. Not what’s true. <em>Why</em>. You came in here to die. You told me. In the hut. You came in because it was tidy. Because you thought you owed it.” Her voice was quite steady now, very low. “If that’s why — if you’re doing this because you think you still owe it — then I’ll fight you. I’ll put you on the floor and take that suit off you myself and you can go home and I’ll stay, and it doesn’t matter what’s true. I will. I’ll make it false or I’ll die trying. I’m not letting you do it for that.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He thought about it the way she thought about questions — turning it over, looking at it from underneath — because she’d earned that, and because he needed to know.</p>\n<p>He thought about the window in the Collar. <em>At last. Almost a relief.</em> He thought about the Quiet, the dark, the small cold place with the simple shape: <em>everything I do adds to it</em>. He thought about the Rim, and the ledger, and the zero at the bottom of the page.</p>\n<p>He examined himself for it. He patted his pockets.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>“I’m not paying anything,” he said.</p>\n<p>She turned her head.</p>\n<p>“I came in to pay,” he said. “You’re right. I came in here to pay it off. All at once. With me. And you told me — in the hut — that you can’t. That it doesn’t get smaller. That it just grows.” He looked at the hollow. The brass wheel. “You were right. I’m not paying anything. There’s nothing to pay. There never was. You showed me the bottom of the page and it was zero, and you said zero’s balanced. It’s balanced. I don’t owe anything. I’m square.”</p>\n<p>“Then <em>why</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because I want to.”</p>\n<p>It was the truest thing he’d ever said. He heard it come out of him and knew it the way he’d known the fractal number on the telemetry screen. <em>I want to.</em></p>\n<p>“I want this,” he said. “Not the dying. It’s not dying. You told me. It’s once.” He looked at her. “Ione. For fifteen years I lived one day. The same day. The day with the pen. I stood in the gallery and watched myself sign it, over and over, thinking <em>don’t</em>, the way you watched Ruth. And I never once — I never had a moment I’d want. Not one. Not one moment in fifteen years that I’d have wanted to keep. And now I’ve got one.” He could hardly get it out. “I’ve got one. I want to be in it. I want to be in the moment where I know you’re safe. Where I know it worked. Where Nell’s going to see the stars and Teo’s going to get out of her chair and thirty-one thousand people are going to walk down a ladder. Where you’re going down Spindle Four in my suit, alive.” He shook his head. “That’s not paying. That’s the opposite of paying. It’s — it’s the only thing I’ve ever wanted that I’ve been sure of.”</p>\n<p>She was crying without any sound.</p>\n<p>“I want the moment,” said Casimir Hale. “I’m choosing it. Not because it’s true. Because I want it. It’s just — it’s true as well.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They chose the instant together.</p>\n<p>He showed her the dial. The ring round the rim of the hollow, engraved with Hollowell’s small precise figures. The interval. He worked it out with her, aloud, the way he’d worked out the curve in the Tollers’ tower, the way she’d worked out the Lees: how long for her to get back down the stair, and along the Rim through the Lace, and through the Carousel’s seam — Ben’s seam, still there, she said; she’d seen it on the way back from the gantry, still shimmering — and across the gantry, and down the spindle, and down through the Breach. How long before she was past the Breach, well below the Rim, in the Lees, out of the Fold. How long it would be before she was safe.</p>\n<p>“Three hours,” said Ione. “If I’m quick. If the weather’s kind.”</p>\n<p>“Three and a half,” he said. “In case it isn’t.”</p>\n<p>He set the ring. It turned under his fingers with a smooth, heavy click at each mark, like the dial of a safe.</p>\n<p>“From the Crown,” he said, “I’ll be able to see Four. Down there. Through the window. If I look.” He pointed through the great glass, down and to the left, past the glare of the Lamp, to where the black lattice of Spindle Four rose out of the violet gulf wrapped in its cobwebs of light. “I’ll have to watch the needle. But I’ll be able to see. And the keel’s got its lights. I’ll see you going down.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll see me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“All the way.”</p>\n<p>“And at the end⁠—”</p>\n<p>“At the end, you’ll be past the Breach. And I’ll look. And I’ll see your light.” He was quiet. “That’s the instant. That’s where I’ll be.”</p>\n<p>She was silent for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she said: “I’ll learn one for you.”</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“A star. When I get out. When I see them.” She looked at the window, at the Lamp, at the only star she’d ever had. “You said you never looked. You never had a favourite. You said Nell would have known in a second.” She wiped her face with the back of her good hand. “I’ll find one for you. I’ll ask Nell. I’ll go out under the — the sky, the real one, and I’ll pick one, and it’ll be yours. And I’ll look at it.”</p>\n<p>“Every night?”</p>\n<p>“They come every night?” she said. “You said they come every night.”</p>\n<p>“Every night.”</p>\n<p>“Then every night,” said Ione.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They didn’t talk about it after that. They talked about other things.</p>\n<p>He told her about rain again, because she asked. She asked what it tasted of. He said nothing, mostly, and sometimes dust, and in the Stacks, sometimes, of the metal of the gutters. She asked if you could drink it. He said yes. She said she’d drink it. She asked about the sea — whether it was loud — and he said yes, very, and that it never stopped, and she said that she’d lived with the Bell all her life so she’d be used to that. She asked what they ate, Outside. He told her. She was scandalised that nobody ate rat. He said she could start a fashion. She said she’d open a stall. <em>Holdfast’s Other Citizens, Roasted.</em> He laughed. It came out of him in the dust of the Crown, a real laugh, loud, and she looked at him with that expression he’d seen on the spindle — as if he’d surprised her — and then she laughed too, the rough short stiff laugh, and couldn’t stop, and put her forehead down on his shoulder.</p>\n<p>And then, at some point, neither of them was laughing. And she lifted her head. And he took off the helmet — he’d had it on since the Rim — and put it down in the dust. And she put her good hand on the side of his face, and looked at him, the way she’d looked at him at the top of the well, patiently, attentively, as if he were a view she’d seen many times and still liked.</p>\n<p>“I’m glad it was you,” she said. “That came up the well.”</p>\n<p>“I’m glad it was you that pulled me out.”</p>\n<p>She kissed him. It was very gentle. It went on for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Getting her into the suit was the hardest thing he had ever done.</p>\n<p>Not because of what it meant. Because of the straps. The suit was built for him, and she was a head shorter and twice as broad through the shoulders, and it opened down the back like a clam and she stepped into it and it closed round her — thunk, thunk, thunk — and it didn’t fit anywhere. The exo woke and took her weight and didn’t know what to do with her, and she stood in it swaying like a child in her father’s coat, her face tiny in the big curve of the visor, and said, <em>well, this is ridiculous</em>.</p>\n<p>So he fitted it to her. The way Ben had fitted it to him in the hangar, a hundred years ago. He knelt in the dust in the grey liner and went round the suit, strap by strap, buckle by buckle, taking in the harness at the shoulders and letting it out at the hips, shortening the legs of the exo by two notches, cinching the webbing she’d spliced for him so that it would hold her instead. His hands were shaking. He made them stop, and they shook anyway, and he went on.</p>\n<p>“Ione,” he said, at the back of the suit, feeling along the strapping under the keel housing, “I can’t see the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“⁠—buckle,” she said. “It’s lower. Left. There.”</p>\n<p>His hands stopped.</p>\n<p>He knelt there in the dust with his hands on the strap, and the words he’d said went through him, and the words he’d heard on the Rim went through him, and they rang together like the bell again — <em>Ione, I can’t see the —</em> in his own voice, out of the air, out of nowhere, in the Lace — and he found that he was laughing. Kneeling behind her in the dust, laughing, with his forehead against the keel housing.</p>\n<p>“What?” she said. “What is it?”</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” He found the buckle. It was lower. Left. There. He did it up. “Nothing. It’s just — that one was only a buckle.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t understand. Then she did. He heard her breath go in, and out, and then she was laughing too, in the suit, the sound thin and strange through the helmet speaker, and it went on until it wasn’t laughing any more, and they both stopped, and he finished the straps.</p>\n<p>When it was done she took three steps across the Crown in it, and turned, and took three back. She was steady. The exo had learned her. The keel hummed on her back. Her face in the visor was very small and very pale and very calm.</p>\n<p>“It’s heavy,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’ll get used to it.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got about three and a half hours.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll be fine.”</p>\n<p>“I know.” She looked at him through the visor. At him standing in the dust in the grey liner — the close grey one-piece, slippery, cool, like a diver’s. Like something worn under armour. She looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she opened the suit’s chest compartment and took out the cord.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She tied it the way she’d tied it on the rooftop. Quickly. Over, under, round, through. Her gauntlet had to come off for it — she worked the clasp with her splinted hand and pulled it off and let it drop in the dust — and with her bare fingers she tied the riggers’ hitch round his left wrist, above the cuff of the liner, flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped.</p>\n<p>Her fingers stopped when it was done. They stayed on the knot.</p>\n<p>“I’ll hold your rope,” said Ione Tally. Her voice was quite steady. “You hold mine.”</p>\n<p>He knew the rest. He’d heard it on a rooftop with the drums going and the whole tower stamping. He said it with her.</p>\n<p>“And if the one of us falls,” they said, “the other one hauls.”</p>\n<p>Nobody shouted <em>haul</em>. There was nobody to shout it. There was only the Crown, and the dust, and the ghost of Ruth Tally on her knees by the Keystone, looking up at the place where he was standing.</p>\n<p>Ione looked at the knot on his wrist. Then she looked at the ghost.</p>\n<p>“She’s looking at you,” she said. “Look. She’s looking right at you.”</p>\n<p>He looked. The faint white shape in the dust, the woman in the lab coat with her hair pinned up and the mole under her ear, kneeling by the open housing, her face turned up toward the place beside it. Toward him.</p>\n<p>“A hundred and seventy-five thousand times,” said Ione, “I watched her look at you. I stood where you’re standing. Hundreds of times. To see if it was me she saw.” Her voice broke, finally, completely. “It was you. It was always you. She was looking at you the whole time. She was looking at <em>you</em>.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They armed it together.</p>\n<p>He knelt by the hollow in the grey liner with the knot on his wrist. She knelt beside him in the suit. The red lever stood between them with its black-and-yellow collar, exactly like the one by the lift that Ruth had pulled. He put his hand on it. She put her bare hand over his.</p>\n<p>“So this is the start,” she said.</p>\n<p>“This is the start.”</p>\n<p>They pulled it.</p>\n<p>It moved easily. It moved like a thing that had been waiting to be moved. It came down through its collar and seated with a sound — not a click; a <em>toll</em>, deep, a single note going down through the keelstone into Spindle One and on down, and on, into the Vault, into the Anvil, into everything — and he felt it go through him, the way he’d felt the hammer go through Tier Nine. The Lattice letting go. The last hold released.</p>\n<p>Outside the great window, the Lamp changed.</p>\n<p>It didn’t grow. It didn’t dim. But its light — its hard white-violet — went, very faintly, warmer. A thread of gold in it. And round it, the Lace stirred. All of it, at once, the whole vast filigree across the sky, rippling, the way a curtain ripples when a door opens somewhere in the house. The thread lying across the Crown moved, and lifted, and drifted a little toward the window, as if something out there were drawing a breath.</p>\n<p>On the gauge, the needle trembled. And began to move.</p>\n<p>He put his hand on the brass wheel.</p>\n<p>It was warm. It turned under his fingers smooth and heavy and fine, a little click at each tooth, and the needle, which had begun to drift off the line toward the band of colour on its left, stopped, and hung, and came back. He held it there. He felt it wander, under his hand, in the wheel — a tug this way, a tug that way, like a fish on a line — and he held it on the line.</p>\n<p>“Go,” he said. He didn’t look at her. He couldn’t take his eyes off the needle. “Ione. Go.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t move for a moment.</p>\n<p>Then she leaned in — the suit’s helmet close to his bare face — and pressed her visor against his temple. Hard. Just for a second. He felt the cold of the glass. He heard her breathe.</p>\n<p>“Haul,” she said.</p>\n<p>Then she stood up, and turned, and he heard the suit’s boots go across the Crown — heavy, steady, booming on the keelstone floor, the keel’s hum going with them — and down the hatch. And the hum faded. And was gone.</p>\n<p>He held the needle on the line.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went down the stair onto the Rim, and the Lace was waiting for her.</p>\n<p>It was moving. It had been moving slowly all the time they’d been in it; now it moved like weed in a flood. The threads swayed and swung and reached, rippling with that long slow breath that had come into them when the lever went down. The light had a thread of gold in it. The air was full of a sound she had never heard in the Lace before — a high, faint, singing, like a finger round the rim of a glass.</p>\n<p>And there were no arrows.</p>\n<p>She stood at the foot of the stair and looked at the steel plate of the Rim running back through the forest of light toward the gallery. Bare. Black. No chalk. No white rigger’s arrows, three quick strokes, pointing the way between the threads.</p>\n<p>Of course there weren’t. She hadn’t drawn them yet.</p>\n<p>She had chalk. She always had chalk. She opened the pouch on the suit’s belt — he’d put it there, when he fitted the suit, without saying anything; she’d seen him do it — and took out a stick, and knelt on the steel at the foot of the stair, and drew an arrow. Three strokes. Pointing ahead, and a little left, between two great hanging roots of light.</p>\n<p>It looked exactly like the first one they’d seen.</p>\n<p>She stood up. She walked to where it pointed. She drew another. She knew where it went. She knew exactly where it went, because she’d followed it, an hour ago, standing on his boots with his arms round her. She’d followed it because it was there. It was there because she was drawing it. It had never been anyone’s. It had never come from anywhere. It was just true.</p>\n<p>She drew them all the way. Every one. Kneeling in the suit on the flickering steel of the Rim — it flickered; she didn’t look at it — with the threads swaying round her and the singing in the air and the keel humming round her in its metre of calm. She did not look into the threads. She did not look at the Lamp. She looked at the chalk in her bare fingers, and the steel, and the arrows.</p>\n<p>In the middle of the Rim, where the threads were thickest, she heard her own voice.</p>\n<p>“<em>Right. Right, and then straight.</em>”</p>\n<p>It came out of the air beside her. Her voice, from an hour ago. From before. She stopped with the chalk in her hand, kneeling, and she knew exactly where it was coming from. She knew that somewhere very close to her, in the same place and not in the same place, a man in a keel suit was standing with a woman on his boots, and the woman was looking for the next arrow, and the man had just heard her voice come out of nowhere and gone cold.</p>\n<p>She looked at the place where the next arrow had to go. Right. Right, and then straight.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Ione, to the air, to the man, to the girl on his boots. Her voice was shaking. “Right, and then straight. It’s all right. You’ll find them. They’re all here. I’ve drawn them for you.”</p>\n<p>She drew it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went through the Carousel without stopping.</p>\n<p>She came in by the Rim door, into the corridor, and the ghost-light came on round her — <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em> — and she walked through it. She didn’t look at the windows. She didn’t look at the scratched fives on the walls, or the pallet of rags, or the pile of smooth stones. She walked straight along the curve to the place in the middle of the corridor where Ben’s seam was, the hand’s-width shimmer between the two branches, and she stopped in front of it.</p>\n<p>And then she did stop. Just for a moment. She turned round.</p>\n<p>The ghost-room was round her. Ruth was at the board, with her back to her, her hair pinned up. It was the second minute. <em>The protocol requires the interlocks off.</em> Crane was talking. Ruth’s thumb had gone to her mouth.</p>\n<p>Ione stood in the corridor in the suit and looked at her.</p>\n<p>“I know who you’re looking at,” she said. “Later. At the Keystone. I know who it is now.” She swallowed. “He’s all right. He’s — he’s not frightened. You were right. He’s very calm.”</p>\n<p>Ruth bit the side of her thumb, and didn’t hear her, the way she’d never heard her.</p>\n<p>“Goodbye, Ruth,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She turned to face the outer wall — the gantry side, the way home — and stepped through the seam.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The gantry swayed under her. The keel hummed. The Lace, above and behind her, was singing louder now, and the light was more gold than violet, and the gulf beneath the gantry was full of a strange shifting glow, as if the whole Vault were lit from a new angle. She ran. She couldn’t run in the suit; she walked as fast as it would let her, three hundred metres of rusted steel lattice, past the loose bolt — she stepped on the plate beside it, and it rattled, and she nearly stopped, and didn’t — to the door in the lattice of Spindle Four.</p>\n<p>Down. The stairs. The ladders. The hut in the last Lee, its iron door standing open, the planks inside where they’d lain under his coat and hers. She didn’t stop.</p>\n<p>The Breach.</p>\n<p>It was worse than it had been. She’d known it would be. The payment was gathering at the core; the Lamp was pushing and swinging and pulling all at once; the lamp-weather in the Breach was a gale. She went down the single spine of keelstone with its ladder rungs, and down swung round her, and swung, and swung, and the keel’s bubble held. It held. She could feel it straining. She could feel it averaging the whole screaming world out over its metre of calm, and she understood now what he had felt on the way up with her wrapped round him, and she went down, rung after rung, not looking out, not looking down, the rungs coming up past her visor, black iron, pitted.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the Breach the lattice closed round her again. The great struts. A Lee. The weather dropped away.</p>\n<p>She stopped on the walkway, and put her bare hand on a strut, and stood there, breathing.</p>\n<p>She was past it. She was out of the Fold. She’d done it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know what time it was. The suit’s clocks had died on the Rim; she’d watched the numbers fall apart. She didn’t know how long she had, or whether she’d made it, or whether up there in the Crown the needle was still on the line and the wheel was still turning under his hand.</p>\n<p>She turned round. She tipped her head back. She looked up.</p>\n<p>The spindle rose above her, black lattice, narrowing, into the glare. The Breach. The hut. The gallery, a ring round the neighbouring pillar, its windows full of moving light. The gantry, a thread across the gulf. And above it all, the Rim, a dark circle round the Lamp, hung with the Lace. The Lace was singing. The Lamp was gold.</p>\n<p>And on the Rim, in the Lace — small, very far, at the edge of a clearing in the forest of light — there were two figures.</p>\n<p>She could see them. She could see them quite clearly. A grey figure in a keel suit, standing by the Rim’s inner rail. And another, smaller, standing in front of it, pressed back against it, the grey arms round her. Not moving. Two people, holding each other up, at the top of the world.</p>\n<p>Looking down at her.</p>\n<p>She stood on the walkway in the Lee with her bare hand on the strut and looked up at them for a long time. At herself, standing on his boots. At him, holding her. At the two of them, on the Rim, an hour ago, a lifetime ago, looking down at a small grey figure in a keel suit on Spindle Four, both of them sure it was him.</p>\n<p>She raised her hand.</p>\n<p>She held it there. The palm out. She didn’t know what else to do. She held it up, toward them, toward him, toward the girl on his boots who didn’t know yet, who would find out in the Crown with her voice cracking down the middle — <em>it was the suit</em> — and she held it there, and held it, until her arm shook.</p>\n<p>Then she lowered it.</p>\n<p>She turned, and went on down.</p>\n<p>She’d gone perhaps ten steps down the next flight of stairs when the light changed.</p>\n<p>It didn’t fade. It didn’t flicker. There was no sound. The Lamp — which had been gold, and singing, and very bright — was simply, between one step and the next, not there. And the Lace was not there. And the light was not there. And there was nothing above her and nothing round her and nothing below her but the dark.</p>\n<p>Ione Tally stood on the stair in the black, in his suit, with the keel humming round her in its little metre of calm, and the only light in the whole world was the soft band of it round the housing on her back, glowing faintly on the rungs.</p>\n<p>Above her, very high up, in the dark, something that had been about to happen was never going to happen.</p>\n<p>And somewhere in it, once, a man was looking down at her light.</p>"}, {"file": "19-the-bell.md", "label": "Chapter 18", "title": "The Bell", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": true, "words": 5144, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Then we are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Common Office, final response</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The first thing she noticed was that she was going to float away.</p>\n<p>She stood on the stair in the black with one hand on the rail and the keel humming round her, and her body told her — very clearly, very urgently, the way your body tells you that you’ve missed a step in the dark — that there was nothing holding her down. That she weighed nothing. That if she let go of the rail she’d drift up off the stair like a puff of chalk and be lost.</p>\n<p>She held on to the rail. She stood very still. She waited for it to pass.</p>\n<p>It didn’t pass. And after a while she understood that it wasn’t going to, because it wasn’t a gust. It wasn’t lamp-weather. It was just the weight. The Lamp had been pushing on her from above every minute of her life, every minute of every Holdfast life for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and it had stopped. The Anvil was still there, pulling. The world was still there, under the Anvil, pulling. But the push was gone. And what was left was⁠—</p>\n<p><em>Ordinary</em>, she thought. <em>This is what they weigh. Outside. This is ordinary.</em></p>\n<p>She let go of the rail. She didn’t float away. She stood on the stair and weighed what a woman weighs, which was less than she’d ever weighed in her life, and it was so strange that she laughed out loud in the suit — a short, cracked, frightened laugh that went out into the dark and didn’t come back.</p>\n<p>Because there was no echo. There was no sound at all.</p>\n<p>She hadn’t known there was a sound until it stopped. The Lamp had made one. The Lace had made one. It had been there all her life, under everything — under the Bell, under the voices in the lanes, under the wind in the ropes — so faint and so constant that nobody in Holdfast had ever heard it any more than they heard their own blood. A hum. A singing. It had been getting louder on the Rim, at the end, the singing in the gold light. And now it was gone, and the silence where it had been was so enormous that she could hear her own heart in the suit, and the keel’s hum, and nothing else in the world.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<p>Nothing. Black. Not dark — dark was the Quiet, dark was the tank with the lid shut. This was more than dark. It went up and up above her and didn’t stop, and there was nothing in it anywhere. No Lamp. No Lace. No Rim. No Crown. She tried to see where the Crown should be — up there, at the top of Spindle One, where he was — and there was nothing to see. Black on black. It could have been a hand’s width from her face. It could have been the edge of the world.</p>\n<p><em>Somewhere in it</em>, she thought. <em>Once.</em></p>\n<p>She stood on the stair for a long time, looking up at nothing, with the soft band of light round the keel on her back glowing on the rungs.</p>\n<p>Then she took out her chalk, and held her hand out flat, and blew.</p>\n<p>The chalk went off her palm in a little cloud, lit by the keel’s glow. And fell. Straight down. In a thin white curtain, straight as a plumb line, down past the stair and on into the dark.</p>\n<p>True chalk. On Spindle Four, three kilometres up. Where there had never, in a hundred and sixty-eight years, been anything but crooked.</p>\n<p>She watched it go until she couldn’t see it.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said to him, out loud, into the dark, in case he could hear. He couldn’t. She said it anyway. “There. It’s done. You did it.”</p>\n<p>Then she started to climb down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took her nine hours. She didn’t know it was nine hours; the suit’s clocks were dead, and she hadn’t brought anything else. She knew it afterwards, because the Tollers told her. At the time it was only stairs.</p>\n<p>Stairs in the dark. Ladders in the dark. The keel’s little glow on the rungs, a metre of soft light round her and black beyond it. She’d climbed Spindle Four a dozen times since she was nine, and she’d never climbed it like this — never with nothing pushing, never without the Lees. There were no Lees now. The struts were still there, the great black beams, and she went past them and felt them pull, faintly, the way the Anvil pulled; but there was no push for them to pull against, and so there was no calm, and no storm, and no difference between one place and the next. Only down. The same down everywhere. Straight chalk everywhere.</p>\n<p>It was easy. That was the horror of it, at first, and then the wonder. It was easy. Her legs were Holdfast legs, built for half as much again, and in the ordinary weight they felt like springs. She went down the ladders in the suit faster than she’d ever gone down them in her life. She stopped at the hut and didn’t go in. She stopped at the best Lee, the one with the four struts, where they’d slept and the chalk had hung in the air like a cloud — and blew chalk, and watched it fall straight, and stood there for a while, and went on.</p>\n<p>She thought about him. She thought about him the whole way down. She tried not to, and then she stopped trying.</p>\n<p>She thought about the needle. About his hand on the brass wheel, turning it, a little this way and a little that. About him looking down through the great window at a small light on Spindle Four. About what he’d seen, at the end. Her light, past the Breach. Her light, stopped on a walkway. Her arm raised.</p>\n<p><em>He saw me raise my hand.</em></p>\n<p>She had to stop on a ladder when she thought that, and hold on, and wait until she could see again.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know if he’d seen it. She didn’t know if his eyes had been on her or on the needle, whether he’d looked up in time. She’d never know. She understood, holding on to the ladder in the dark, that she was going to spend the rest of her life not knowing, and that she was going to have to decide what she believed, and that nobody would ever be able to tell her if she was right.</p>\n<p>She decided. Right there, on the ladder.</p>\n<p><em>He saw me.</em></p>\n<p>She went on down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At about two kilometres, she saw the city.</p>\n<p>She’d been looking down for it for hours. There’d been nothing — the dark below as black as the dark above, the keel’s glow on the rungs and nothing past it. And then she came round the corner of a flight of stairs onto a walkway on the spindle’s inner side, where you could see down through the open lattice to the Anvil, and there it was.</p>\n<p>It was lights.</p>\n<p>Not the Lamp’s light. Not violet, not white, not one great glare over everything. Little lights. Points. Tiny and warm and yellow, thousands and thousands of them, spread out across the black floor of the world. Scattered through the scaffold towers. Strung along the lanes. Clustered round the spindles’ feet. Some of them still, some of them moving — carried, she thought; lamps being carried through the lanes. Each one so small, alone, that it hardly lit anything. And all of them together making the shape of the city, laid out in the dark below her like a map drawn in fire.</p>\n<p>The lamps. The oil lamps. The ones they’d been making in their kitchens for eleven rounds, pressing algae, twisting wicks, while he was in the Quiet and the Stewardship was saying the Relief was a demon from the Drop. They’d lit them.</p>\n<p>She stood on the walkway and looked at it.</p>\n<p><em>Points of light</em>, he’d said. In the loft, lying on the floor, when she’d asked him what the stars were like. <em>Little points of light in the dark. Thousands of them. They don’t do anything. They’re just there.</em></p>\n<p>“Oh,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She stood there a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Pim was at the bottom.</p>\n<p>She heard him before she saw him. She was on the last ladder down into the wreck of the lift terminal at the foot of the spindle, and there was a lamp below, and voices, and then a high cracked voice shouted, “<em>There — there! There’s a light! On the — it’s the suit, it’s the suit, it’s the Relief, he’s —</em>”</p>\n<p>She came down off the ladder into the wreckage, into the lamp-light.</p>\n<p>Pim was there, and Osk, and Bett from the riggers’ tower, and a dozen others. Holding lamps. They’d been waiting at the foot of Spindle Four, in the dark, in the wreck. Pim was at the front. He had a clay lamp in each hand and a rat on each shoulder and his face was streaked black with soot and wet with crying, and he was running at her — at the suit — with his arms out and the lamps swinging and the rats clinging on⁠—</p>\n<p>And then he saw her face.</p>\n<p>He stopped. Just stopped, a metre away, his arms still out. The light from his lamps went up into the visor. She saw him see her. She saw him look at her face, small in the big helmet. And then look at the suit. And then look past her, at the ladder, up into the dark. And then back at her face.</p>\n<p>He didn’t say anything. He was eleven, and he’d been everywhere, and heard everything, and carried the Relief’s recorder out through a vent. He looked at her. And he put the lamps down on the ground, very carefully, one and then the other. And he came and put his arms round the suit’s leg and held on to it and pressed his face against the plating and didn’t say anything at all.</p>\n<p>Osk had taken off his hard hat.</p>\n<p>He stood there in the lamp-light at the foot of the spindle, the huge slow man, and held it against his chest the way he’d held it on the first day, at the top of the well, when he’d knelt. He didn’t kneel now. He looked at her. At the suit. And he bowed his head.</p>\n<p>Behind him, the others did the same.</p>\n<p>She opened the suit. She hadn’t known she was going to. She found the clasp at the neck and the seals and it opened down the back the way it had opened for Cas a hundred times, and she stepped out of it in her rigger’s clothes onto the rubble at the foot of Spindle Four, and nearly fell, because she was so light — and Osk caught her. The way he’d caught Cas, the first day. Under the arms. And held her up.</p>\n<p>“Stack your bones,” said Osk, gently.</p>\n<p>“I know how,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She stood. She looked at them. At the lamps. At the dark.</p>\n<p>“He’s up there,” she said. “He’s — it worked. It’s done. He did it. The Lamp’s closed.” Her voice was quite steady. “He’s not coming down. He’s the witness. He’s in it.”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything.</p>\n<p>“The Drop’s a road,” said Ione Tally. “He told you. When the Lamp goes out, the Drop’s a road. It’s out. The Drop’s a road.”</p>\n<p>Somewhere behind Osk, in the dark, somebody started to cry.</p>\n<p>And then — faintly, far off, from somewhere out in the city, carried on the still dark air — somebody else began to shout it. <em>The Drop’s a road.</em> And another voice took it up. And another. She heard it go out across Holdfast, voice by voice, lamp by lamp, the way the shutter-wave went out at dusk. <em>The Drop’s a road. The Drop’s a road. He said. When the Lamp goes out. He said.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went to the Tower first.</p>\n<p>She knew she should go to the Commons. She knew there’d be thousands there, and fear, and the Stewards, and Crane. She knew there were people in the lanes who hadn’t believed him — she could hear them, now and then, among the other voices: screaming, praying, somewhere a woman shrieking <em>demon, demon, he’s killed the Lamp</em> over and over. She knew all of it.</p>\n<p>She went to the Tower first.</p>\n<p>The square round it was full of lamps. The Tollers had put them out on every ledge and step and platform of the tower, all the way up round the column, so that the whole spiralling scaffold was outlined in little yellow flames, and the black keelstone pillar in the middle of it, with its hundred and sixty-eight years of chalk, gleamed in their light like wet stone. People were standing in the square, looking up at it. Just standing. Holding their own lamps.</p>\n<p>And the Bell was coming.</p>\n<p>She felt it before she heard it. It came up through the soles of her boots, through the keelstone of the square — not a swell. Not a tide. Not the long slow rising pressure she’d felt all her life, a little more often every year, until lately it had come every ten minutes. A <em>knock</em>. Short and sharp and deep, a single blow, like a fist on a door far below. And then silence. And then⁠—</p>\n<p>Another. And another.</p>\n<p>Once a second. Once a second. Once a second.</p>\n<p>She stood in the square with her hand on her chest and felt it, and the people standing round her with their lamps were feeling it too — she could see it in their faces, turned up toward the tower, toward the column, toward the Tollers on their platforms. Every face had the same look. A kind of fear. A kind of wonder. A kind of listening, the way you listen to a heartbeat when you’ve put your head on someone’s chest.</p>\n<p><em>Knock. Knock. Knock.</em></p>\n<p>She went into the Tower.</p>\n<p>The round room at the bottom was full of lamplight, and pendulums, and Tollers — every Toller there was, crammed in, standing round the walls, silent. Tobiah was at the column with a stick of chalk in his hand. He wasn’t writing. He was just standing there, with his hand raised and the chalk in it, crying behind his lens-spectacles, because there was no way to write fast enough. Every second, another. There was no way to keep up.</p>\n<p>And in her chair, with her hand flat on the stone, was Gran.</p>\n<p>She was so small. She’d always been small, but she was smaller now, sunk down into the chair, her felt slippers not reaching the floor. Her face was grey. Her breathing was short and quick and shallow, and there was a blanket over her knees, and Steward Harrow was asleep in her lap. Her eyes were closed.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt by the chair.</p>\n<p>The old woman’s eyes opened. Black and bright as a bird’s. They went over her, in about a second, the way they went over everything.</p>\n<p>“Hmph,” said Perpetua Tally. “You’re back.”</p>\n<p>“I’m back.”</p>\n<p>“On your own.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>Gran’s eyes held hers. They didn’t ask. They didn’t need to. They held her for a long moment, and something in the old face shifted, very slightly — not surprise; Gran was never surprised — and the knotted hand came off the column and found Ione’s hand and held it.</p>\n<p>“Did he?” said Gran.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She breathed. “Good. Good lad. Good.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came up through the column. <em>Knock.</em> And again. <em>Knock.</em> Gran’s head tipped a little, listening.</p>\n<p>“Listen to it,” she said. “Listen. One a second. Hear that? One a second.” Her mouth moved; it might have been a smile. “That’s how fast they live out there. That’s their <em>time</em>, Ione. That’s the Outside’s own time, coming up through my column. We’ve been counting their night for a hundred and sixty-eight years and now we’re in it. Now we’re <em>in</em> it.” She shut her eyes. “One a second. No wonder they never get anything done.”</p>\n<p>Ione laughed. She couldn’t help it. It came out of her as a sob.</p>\n<p>“Now,” said Gran. “The box.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah brought it. The dented steel box with the Works stencil. He put it on the old woman’s knees, on top of the blanket, beside the rat, and she laid both her hands on its lid.</p>\n<p>“Ruth’s Log,” she said. “And the letter. The last one. For the girl. For Maddy.” She pushed it, very slightly, toward Ione. “You take it. Out. You take it out to her. That’s the Tollers’ job. It’s always been the Tollers’ job. Somebody from this family carries that letter to that little girl. We promised Ruth.” Her breath rattled. “And you write the last page. In the Log. You write what happened. You write it properly. Every word.”</p>\n<p>“I will.”</p>\n<p>“Not now. Later. When you can.” She opened her eyes. “You’ll know when.”</p>\n<p>“Gran⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Hush.” The knotted fingers squeezed. “Hush now. I’m listening.”</p>\n<p>They listened. The Bell came up through the column. <em>Knock.</em> The pendulums on the platforms above them trembled on their wires. <em>Knock.</em> The lamps on the ledges bowed their flames. <em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” said Perpetua Tally, very quietly, with her eyes closed. “Off you go, when it’s time. Go and see if the Outside’s all it’s cracked up to be. Take them all with you.”</p>\n<p><em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>“Tell me how it ends,” she said.</p>\n<p><em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>And between that Bell and the next, with her granddaughter’s hand in hers and her other hand on the rat asleep in her lap, the Head Toller of Holdfast stopped breathing.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt by the chair. She didn’t move. The Bell came up through the floor, and the column, and the chair, and Gran’s still hand in hers, once a second, once a second, the way it would go on doing now for as long as anyone was there to feel it.</p>\n<p>After a while, Tobiah went to the column and made a single mark in chalk at the bottom of the last row. Not a stroke. A short line across, and a word beside it.</p>\n<p><em>PERPETUA.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Commons was full.</p>\n<p>Not with the sea of faces she’d seen from the back wall on the night the Relief came. With lamps. Thousands of them, held up, filling the great hall from wall to wall, so that the broken roof overhead was lit from below and the girders were gold and the strips of open sky between them were black — black, where the Lace had always been — and the Tollers’ banner still hung from the gantry, Seren’s line, white on black, lit by all those little flames.</p>\n<p>And they were quiet. That was what she noticed when she came in by the great doors with Osk beside her and Pim holding her hand. Twenty thousand people and more, and they were quiet. Some of them were crying. Some of them were holding each other. Here and there, someone was praying, very softly, the words of the Placard. But they were waiting. They’d been waiting, she understood, for hours. In the dark, with their lamps. The way they’d been told to.</p>\n<p><em>When the dark comes, don’t be afraid.</em></p>\n<p>The crowd saw her. She felt it go through them — a turning of heads, a stir — and they parted for her. Not stepping back from her, the way people had stepped back from the Unwatched for eleven years. Parting. Making a way. She walked down it toward the platform, and hands reached out as she went by and touched her arm, her shoulder, the rope on her shoulder, and she didn’t know what to do with it, so she let them.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane was on the platform.</p>\n<p>He was standing in front of the Placard. They’d brought it from the chapel — someone had carried it here, in its keelstone frame, and set it up on the platform on an easel, with a lamp before it. The yellowed laminate. The stick figure by its stick console. He was standing beside it in his black coat with the gold lamp on the breast, very straight, very old, holding a lamp of his own, and he watched her come down the aisle through the crowd, and when she reached the foot of the platform, by the brass rail of Muster Point B, he looked at her face.</p>\n<p>He didn’t ask either.</p>\n<p>He turned to the Placard. He lifted his lamp so that its light fell on the words. And in a clear, carrying voice — the voice that had said <em>let him finish</em> — he read it aloud.</p>\n<p>“Section Nine. In the event of containment loss. Remain at your station and await relief. Do not attempt to traverse the Lattice. Relief crews will be dispatched. You will not be forgotten.”</p>\n<p>He lowered the lamp.</p>\n<p>“Who keeps the Watch?” said Halloran Crane.</p>\n<p>The hall didn’t answer at once. Then, from somewhere at the back, raggedly — and then from everywhere, twenty thousand voices, quietly, uncertainly, the way you’d answer a question you’d answered all your life and suddenly didn’t know the meaning of:</p>\n<p>“<em>We keep the Watch.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Until?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Until Relief.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And when Relief comes?”</p>\n<p>The hall was silent.</p>\n<p>“Then,” said Halloran Crane, “we are relieved.”</p>\n<p>He set his lamp down on the platform in front of the Placard.</p>\n<p>“Relief has come,” he said. “It came up out of the Drop, as we were always told it would. It wasn’t what we were told. It was a man from the Outside, in a suit, who stood here and told us the truth, which we did not want. And then it went up the spindle with Ione Tally and closed the Lamp. The Lamp is out. The Watch is over.” He looked out at the lamps. “We are relieved. Every one of us. You are all relieved. You have kept your station for a hundred and sixty-eight years and you may leave it now.”</p>\n<p>The sound the hall made was not a cheer. It was not a groan. She’d never heard it before and she never heard it again. It was the sound, she thought afterwards, that a great many people make when they have been holding something up for a very long time, and are told they can put it down.</p>\n<p>Crane waited for it to pass.</p>\n<p>“There’s one more thing,” he said, “and then I’ll be done.”</p>\n<p>He looked down at her. At the foot of the platform, by the brass rail. He held her eyes.</p>\n<p>“Thirteen years ago,” said Halloran Crane, “Seren Tally painted the truth on the wall of this hall, and I gave her to the Drop for it. I had the order drawn up before she painted it. She was right, and I knew she was right, and I did it anyway, because I thought your calm was worth more than her life. I was wrong.” His voice didn’t shake at all. “I have nothing to offer her daughter. I’m not asking her for anything. I only wanted you all to hear me say it, once, in here, where she said it first.”</p>\n<p>The hall was very quiet.</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at him. At the old man on the platform, in the lamplight, beside the Placard.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this. For thirteen years. She’d thought about what she would say to him, if she ever had the chance, and it had been different every time — screams, curses, a knife, silence. She’d thought she would feel something enormous.</p>\n<p>She found she felt tired. Very tired. And the Bell was coming up through the floor once a second, and her Gran was dead in her chair in the Tower, and somewhere above her in the dark, once, a man was looking at her light.</p>\n<p>“You’ll walk out last,” she said.</p>\n<p>Crane looked at her.</p>\n<p>“When they go down,” said Ione. “All of them. You’ll stand at the top of the wells and see every one of them onto the ladders. Every single one. The children and the old ones and the Wardens and the ones who called my mother a heretic and the ones who spat at me in the lanes. Every one. And when they’ve all gone down, you’ll go down last.”</p>\n<p>It wasn’t forgiveness. She knew that. She didn’t know what it was. Something drier. Something that would do.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane bowed his head.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “I will.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went down the Drop.</p>\n<p>It took all night — or what would have been night; there were no nights now, and no days, only the dark and the lamps and the Bell. The riggers re-hung the ladders at every one of the fourteen wells, the ladders they’d cut free and hauled up on Kindling Round. They strung ropes down beside them, and lamp-lines, clay lamps hung on wire every ten metres all the way down the shafts, so that looking down a well was like looking down a throat lined with little fires. And Holdfast went down them.</p>\n<p>Family by family. Street by street. Wardens at the tops of the ladders, riggers on the ropes, Tollers with their ledgers counting names. Children on their parents’ backs. Old people lowered in rope slings, three riggers to each. Pim’s rats in a wire cage lashed to his shoulders, all of them, the whole Stewardship, squeaking. Bundles and bags and bedrolls. Lamps. Everyone had a lamp. The Tanks, the market, the Commons, the Crèche, all left behind, empty, lit, in the dark.</p>\n<p>People sang, going down. Not the Tending. She listened for it, and didn’t hear it once. They sang the riggers’ song, the hauling song, the one with no words but <em>haul</em> — a deep slow rhythm, one voice calling, all the voices answering, going down the lines of ladders into the dark. <em>Haul. Haul. Haul.</em> And under it, through the rungs, through the keelstone, once a second, the Bell.</p>\n<p>Ione went down Fourteen.</p>\n<p>She went first. Before anyone. Osk tried to stop her, and then didn’t. It was the well she’d pulled him out of. It was the ladder he’d come up. It was the well her mother had gone down, thirteen years ago, and not looked back. She went down it in her rigger’s clothes, with his suit strapped to a rope and lowered beside her — Osk had insisted; the Relief’s suit didn’t get left — and the dented steel box with Ruth’s Log in it lashed to her back, and her lamp hanging from her harness.</p>\n<p>Three hundred metres through the Anvil. Rung after rung, in the lamp-light. It was only a ladder. It was so easy in the ordinary weight that she kept thinking she’d missed something.</p>\n<p>Then the bottom of the Anvil. And below it, the place where the Shear had been.</p>\n<p>She stopped on the ladder, there, and hung on it, and looked.</p>\n<p>Nothing. A shaft. Round, black, ribbed with old pipe-brackets. A ladder going down it. The air was still. There was no curtain, no trembling, no wrongness. Nothing jumped or smeared. She could see straight down the shaft forty metres, fifty, sixty, to the bottom, where there was a glow — a light, a lamp, not hers — and a round window, lit.</p>\n<p>She went down through the place where the Shear had been, and felt nothing at all.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the conduit, at the foot of the ladder, there was a shape covered in foil.</p>\n<p>It lay crumpled against the wall. Someone had covered it — carefully, tenderly, from the head down, the silver foil tucked round it at the edges — with a blanket from a first-aid kit. The foil crackled a little in the air from the shaft. It shone in her lamp’s light.</p>\n<p>Ione stood at the foot of the ladder and looked at it.</p>\n<p>She knew. She’d known since the Tower, the first night, when he’d said <em>there was someone at the bottom of the conduit, in mended clothes, with a lamp carved from a hard hat</em>, and Gran had made that sound like a bird. She’d known. She knelt down beside it on the floor of the conduit and put her lamp down.</p>\n<p>She lifted the edge of the foil. Just a little. Just at the neck.</p>\n<p>There was a cord there. Twisted fibre, algae-thread, the kind Holdfast made. And on it, lying against the patched cloth, a little yellow lamp. Carved from the plastic of an old hard hat. Worn smooth and soft as soap. For a crib.</p>\n<p>She didn’t lift the foil any further. She didn’t need to.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Mam,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She put her hand flat on the foil, over where her mother’s heart would have been. She knelt there for a long time. The Bell came up through the floor, once a second.</p>\n<p>“I found the edge of the world,” she said. “It wasn’t what I thought. I went up to find you. You weren’t there. I was, though.” She was quiet. “You were right. About the line. About all of it. Everybody knows now. It’s on a banner in the Commons. Crane said it. In front of everyone.” She was quiet again. “Gran’s gone. Just now. She was listening to the Bell. It goes once a second now, Mam. Like a heart. You’d have liked that. You’d have wanted to count it.”</p>\n<p>She tucked the foil back round the little yellow lamp.</p>\n<p>“Somebody covered you up,” she said. “A lady. From Outside. She couldn’t stand you lying there. She didn’t know who you were. She did it anyway.”</p>\n<p>And behind her, from the lit window in the wall of the conduit, from the booth beyond it, there was a sound. A hatch opening. Boots on a ladder, coming down, slow and stiff, as if they’d been sitting for a long time.</p>\n<p>Ione stood up and turned round.</p>\n<p>A woman was coming down the hatch-ladder from the booth into the conduit. A broad, weathered woman in her fifties, in a heavy grey suit with its helmet off and nothing on its back where a keel should have been, with cropped iron-grey hair and a face like a well-used tool. She had a torch in one hand. She reached the bottom and turned and put the torch on Ione, and stood there looking at her. At her face. At the white streak in her hair. At the rope on her shoulder, and the box on her back, and the empty grey suit hanging on its rope from the ladder above her.</p>\n<p>Her face did something complicated.</p>\n<p>“You must be Ione,” said Teo Lisk. Her voice was hoarse. “He never shut up about you.”</p>"}, {"file": "20-stars.md", "label": "Chapter 19", "title": "Stars", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 5270, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>They’re always there. You just can’t see them.</p><span class=\"attr\">Nell Hale, aged ten, on the telephone</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The edge of the lee was half a metre from Teo Lisk’s boots when it stopped existing.</p>\n<p>She’d been watching it come for — she didn’t know. A long time. Two minutes, maybe, by the suit clock, which were the longest two minutes of her life, and she’d had some long ones. The shimmer on the floor, a hand’s height off the ground, like heat over a road. Crawling. The dust on the far side of it drifting, and the dust on her side lying still, and the line between them coming across the floor of Lattice Station 14-C toward the corner where she sat with her boots flat against the wall. A metre and a half. A metre. She’d stopped moving the chair, because there was nowhere left to move it. She’d folded her hands in her lap. She’d said <em>come on, Hale</em> a few more times, not out loud.</p>\n<p>Three-quarters of a metre. Half a metre.</p>\n<p>And then it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>It didn’t go away. It didn’t retreat. It was there, a shimmer, a line of wrongness crawling toward her boots — and then, between one breath and the next, there was nothing on the floor but floor. Dust, lying flat. All of it. On both sides.</p>\n<p>The room settled.</p>\n<p>She felt it. She couldn’t have said how. It was like the moment when a ship that’s been rolling in a swell comes into harbour and the deck goes still under you — not a movement, the end of a movement she hadn’t known was there. The air stopped being wrong. The floor stopped being a floor that might tilt. The whole room simply became a room, the way rooms were, and she was a woman sitting in a chair in the corner of it, and she weighed what she weighed.</p>\n<p>She sat there without moving.</p>\n<p>Then she got up, slowly, and went to the viewport.</p>\n<p>The Shear was gone. The trembling curtain across the conduit, forty metres up — gone. The ladder went up the shaft straight as a ruler, and she could see every rung, clean, not jumping, not smearing. It went up and up into the dark. And at the top — where there had always been, since she’d sat down in this chair, a disc of pale hard violet-white light, far up the shaft, too bright to look at for long⁠—</p>\n<p>Nothing. Dark.</p>\n<p>She stood with her hand flat on the glass. The glass with the names on it in black marker. MATTIAS. JOSS. BEN. SEREN (THE LADY). CAS.</p>\n<p>“Oh, Hale,” she said. “Oh, you did it. You bloody did it.”</p>\n<p>And then, because she was alone, and because nobody was ever going to know, she put her forehead against the glass and cried until she couldn’t stand up, and sat down on the floor under the viewport with her back against the wall, and cried some more.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hardline rang eleven minutes later.</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk.” Gaunt. But not Gaunt’s voice. Or Gaunt’s voice with something gone out of it — the softness, the patience, the lent-you-a-book. “Major. Are you — are you there?”</p>\n<p>“I’m here.”</p>\n<p>“Something’s happened.” A pause. She heard him breathe. “The shimmer over the Pit. It’s gone. It was there, and it’s — gone, just now, all at once, the birds went straight over it, a whole flock of them — and the clocks at the cordon have stopped. Not stopped running. Stopped running <em>fast</em>. They’re — they match. For the first time since two in the morning yesterday, they match.” Another breath. “And the output’s gone to zero. Not the fake zero. Real zero. The Draw’s — there’s nothing coming out of it. Nothing at all. It’s as if it isn’t there.” He stopped. “Major, what’s happened?”</p>\n<p>She looked at the dark disc at the top of the shaft.</p>\n<p>“He did it,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Did — reseated Six?”</p>\n<p>“No.” She found she didn’t care any more. She found she’d never cared. “No, Colonel. He never reseated anything. There was nothing to reseat. He went up to the Crown and he shut it. The Lamp. The Lesion. The whole thing. He found a woman with a key and they went up and shut it for good.” She wiped her face with the back of her hand. “It’s not coming back. There’s no more Draw. It’s done. The debt’s done. You’ll want to tell the Minister. You’ll want to tell her he defaulted.”</p>\n<p>The line was silent.</p>\n<p>“The grid,” said Gaunt at last. “Kovač was at half. Without anything from the Draw at all⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Then it’ll go dark.”</p>\n<p>“Six hundred million people.”</p>\n<p>“Then it’ll go dark, Colonel.” She leaned her head back against the wall. “It’ll go dark and they’ll be alive in it. That’s the trade. That’s the whole trade. Tell them to light a candle.”</p>\n<p>Another silence. And then, to her enormous surprise, a sound down the line that might have been a laugh. A small, broken, astonished sound.</p>\n<p>“Is Dr Hale⁠—” Gaunt began.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know,” said Teo. “I don’t know yet. I’m waiting.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Civil Defence teams came four hours later.</p>\n<p>She heard them long before she saw them: voices echoing down the galleries of the Lattice, boots on steel, the crackle of radios, somebody swearing at a door. They came in through the booth’s outer door with their lamps and their ropes and their stretchers — a sergeant she knew from the Kovač fire, Amara Kade, and eight others — and found her sitting in the chair by the viewport with her boots up on the dead monitors, eating the last of the ration paste out of the suit’s tube with her finger.</p>\n<p>“Major,” said Kade. She was staring. “Major, Christ. We’ve been — they said you’d — the Works is <em>walkable</em>, Major, there’s no weather, there’s nothing, it’s just a building now, we came straight in down Radial Nine like it was a Tuesday. We found the stairhead. And the — the algae hall. There’s writing on the glass⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“We’ve got a stretcher. We’ll have you out in two hours. There’s water, there’s⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m not going.”</p>\n<p>Kade stopped.</p>\n<p>“I’m waiting for someone,” said Teo. “Leave me some water. And a lamp. And a blanket; I gave mine away. And then go back up to the Collar and tell them to send everything they’ve got down here. Everything. Every team, every stretcher, every lamp, every blanket in Averna.”</p>\n<p>“Major, the Colonel said⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The Colonel,” said Teo Lisk, “can come down here and carry me out himself.” She finished the paste. “Go on. Go. And Kade — leave the hardline to me.”</p>\n<p>They went, in the end. Kade stayed, and two others, sitting on the floor of the booth by the door with their lamps, not asking. Teo sat in the chair.</p>\n<p>She went down through the hatch once, and sat beside the lady in the foil blanket for a while. Not talking. Just sitting. The Bell came up through the floor — the hammer, the old Dead Man’s Hammer, still going, once a second, the same as it had been going since two in the morning yesterday, the same as it had gone through the car and the canteen and the Collar. Only here, now, at the bottom of the conduit, it came up through the floor exactly as it did everywhere else. No longer stretched. No longer a groan. A knock. Once a second.</p>\n<p><em>Ordinary</em>, she thought.</p>\n<p>She dozed, in the chair, at some point. She dreamed of the flood in the Tessarine underpass. She dreamed of toast.</p>\n<p>She woke to singing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It came down the shaft.</p>\n<p>Faint, at first. So faint that she thought she was still dreaming. A deep, slow, rhythmic sound, far up the conduit, echoing down the round black walls. One voice, calling. Then many voices, answering. A word. The same word, over and over.</p>\n<p><em>Haul.</em></p>\n<p>She was at the viewport before she knew she’d moved. Kade and the others were on their feet behind her.</p>\n<p>Far up the shaft, in the dark — lights. Little lights. Yellow, warm, flickering. Not a torch. Flames. A line of them, coming down the shaft, one below another, strung down the side of the conduit like beads on a thread. Lamps. Somebody was hanging lamps down the shaft, one every few metres, all the way down. And beside the lamps, on the ladder⁠—</p>\n<p>Something grey.</p>\n<p>Her heart went. She saw it and her whole body went toward the glass, her hands flat on it. A grey shape, hulking, heavy, coming down the shaft. The suit. His suit. Grey plates, the housing on its back. It was coming down. It was coming down the shaft toward her and she was already moving to the hatch, she was already saying his name⁠—</p>\n<p>It turned on its rope.</p>\n<p>It was on a rope. It hung from a rope, and it was being lowered, slowly, beside the ladder, turning gently as it came, like a coat on a hanger. Its visor was dark. Its arms hung. There was nobody in it.</p>\n<p>She stopped with her hand on the hatch.</p>\n<p>Beside it, on the ladder, climbing down, was a woman.</p>\n<p>Teo watched her come. She watched her all the way down — through the place where the Shear had been, and past it, and on down, rung after rung, fast and neat and steady, a lamp swinging from her harness and a dented steel box on her back. Short. Broad. A rope on her shoulder. At the bottom she stepped off the ladder and stood for a moment in her lamp’s light, and then turned and knelt by the lady in the foil blanket.</p>\n<p>Teo couldn’t hear what she said. She saw her lift the edge of the foil, at the neck. She saw her put her hand flat on the blanket, over the heart. She saw her stay like that for a long time.</p>\n<p>She saw the white streak in her hair.</p>\n<p>She knew. She’d known since the first chirp. <em>There’s a woman.</em> After that, in nearly every one. A rigger. A key. A way of looking at you as if she’d read the end of the book. Teo had pictured her so often, sitting in this chair, that she almost didn’t need to look.</p>\n<p>She opened the hatch and went down the ladder.</p>\n<p>The woman stood up and turned round. Teo put the torch on her. She was younger than Teo had thought. Or older. It was hard to tell. She had a cut over one eye with black stitches in it, and a splinted wrist, and a face that had been crying and had stopped, and grey-green eyes that looked back at Teo — at her suit, at her face, at her hands — without any hurry at all. As if she’d seen her before. As if she’d been expecting her.</p>\n<p>“You must be Ione,” said Teo. “He never shut up about you.”</p>\n<p>The woman looked at her for a long moment.</p>\n<p>“And you’re Teo,” she said. Her accent was strange — flat, clipped. “You’re the one in the chair.”</p>\n<p>“I’m the one in the chair.”</p>\n<p>“You covered my mother.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked down at the foil blanket. At the little yellow lamp she’d tucked under it, all those hours ago, that had been a few minutes ago, that had been — she didn’t know. She looked back at the woman.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t know who she was,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione Tally. “You did it anyway.”</p>\n<p>They stood there, in the conduit, in the lamplight.</p>\n<p>“He’s not coming,” said Teo. It wasn’t a question.</p>\n<p>“No.” Ione didn’t look away. “He’s up there. In the Crown. He’s the witness. It needed someone to hold it until it closed, and whoever held it would be — in it. When it closed. For ever. Once.” She said it very plainly. “He’s in it. It’s a single instant. He’s in one instant, and it doesn’t end.”</p>\n<p>Teo stood without moving.</p>\n<p>She thought about the booth. About a man kneeling in front of her chair with his broken keel whining and a look on his face she’d seen on men on bridges. <em>It seemed tidy.</em></p>\n<p>“Did he want to?” she said.</p>\n<p>Ione looked at her. And something in her face changed — the patience went out of it, and something younger came in, and her eyes filled.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” she said. “He said — he said it was the only thing he’d ever wanted that he was sure of. He said he wasn’t paying anything. He said he wanted the moment.” She drew a breath. “He said you’d ask. He said, <em>Teo’ll ask if I wanted to</em>. He said to tell you yes. He said to tell you it wasn’t guilt.”</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk put her hand over her mouth.</p>\n<p>She stood there, in the conduit, with the lamps hanging down the shaft above them and the singing coming down, <em>haul, haul, haul</em>, and the Bell coming up through the floor, and she held her hand over her mouth for a long time. When she took it away, she was crying, and she didn’t care.</p>\n<p>“Then that’s all right,” she said. “Then that’s all right. The daft sod. Then it’s all right.”</p>\n<p>And the woman from the top of the world stepped forward and put her arms round her, suit and all, and held on. Like a rigger. Like somebody who hadn’t been touched for a long time and had recently remembered how.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo took the hardline off its hook with a hand that wasn’t quite steady.</p>\n<p>“Collar. This is Lisk. Fourteen-C. Get me Colonel Gaunt.” A pause. “Colonel. Listen to me very carefully, because I’m only going to say this once and then I’m going to be busy. I want every team you’ve got. I want every stretcher and every lamp and every length of rope in the Commonweal sent down Radial Nine. I want water. Tankers of it. I want food, anything, whatever you’ve got. I want doctors. And blankets. Bring blankets. Bring a <em>lot</em> of blankets.”</p>\n<p>A pause on the line.</p>\n<p>“For how many?” said Gaunt.</p>\n<p>Teo looked up the shaft. At the lamps, strung down it like beads on a thread. At the ladder. At the first of them coming down it now, behind Ione — a huge man in a long grey coat and a battered hard hat, carrying a little boy on his back; and behind him a woman with a baby in a sling; and behind her an old man in a rope harness, being lowered, three short broad figures guiding his ropes from above; and behind them, more, and more, faces in the lamplight, short and pale and broad and heavy-boned, all the way up the shaft, as far as she could see, singing.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand,” said Teo Lisk. “Give or take a rat.”</p>\n<p>The line was silent for a very long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They walked out through the Works in the dark.</p>\n<p>It took until dawn. Longer — it took all the next day, and into the night after, for the last of them — but the first of them walked out before dawn, and Ione walked with them.</p>\n<p>The Works was a building now. That was the thing that astonished the Civil Defence crews most, the ones who’d been waiting at the Collar for two days listening to the hammer: the Works was just a building. No weather. No gusts, no shears, no walls becoming floors. The filaments were gone from the halls. The corridors were long and dark and dripping and smelled of algae and ozone and old oil, and they went on for kilometres, and you walked down them, and that was all. Civil Defence had strung lamps along Radial Nine all the way from the Lattice to Gate Nine, and roped the stairs, and posted people at every turning, and Holdfast walked down it in a long, long line with its lamps and its bundles and its children and its rats, singing, and the hammer coming up through the floor once a second.</p>\n<p>Ione walked beside Teo.</p>\n<p>Teo told her things, as they walked. About the car and the Collar and the crisis room. About Ben, on the flatbed, hugging his cradle. About the canteen and the two-percent napkin. About the phone call to a gym on Tier Thirty. Ione listened. She didn’t say much. She looked at everything.</p>\n<p>At the stairhead where the four corridors met, the wall was hung with cloths: a Civil Defence jacket, a tarpaulin, high-vis vests, a dust sheet. People looked at them as they went by and didn’t ask. Teo didn’t explain.</p>\n<p>At the algae hall, Ione stopped.</p>\n<p>The gallery was on Radial Nine; the line of Holdfast walked right past its stair, and she went up it, and Teo went with her, and they stood at the long window looking down into B-7. It was a ruin. The tanks were dry and cracked and grey. The dust lay over everything like snow. On the catwalk, right against the glass, there was a heap of it, and a grey rag, and a scatter of small bones, and a stubby yellow grease pencil lying in the dust.</p>\n<p>And on the glass, faded, cracked, chalky, the writing.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<p>Ione read it twice.</p>\n<p>“That’s him,” she said. “Ben.”</p>\n<p>“That’s Ben.”</p>\n<p>“He found the seam,” said Ione. “In the Carousel. His map. Cas said. We’d have been in there for ever without him.” She put her hand flat on the glass, beside the words. On the other side of it, a centimetre away, in the dust, the grease pencil. “He got us out.”</p>\n<p>Teo couldn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“<em>It was long enough</em>,” said Ione. And stood there, with her hand on the glass, reading it again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Gate Nine stood open.</p>\n<p>The great hazard-striped door, five metres high, rolled all the way back. And beyond it, the Collar — the long evacuated corridors, every door open, every screen dark now, the MARGIN IRREGULARITY: REMAIN CALM gone with the power. Lamps along the floors. Civil Defence at every junction, with water and blankets and quiet voices, and doctors in masks at tables by the doors, and somewhere at the back, in charcoal overcoats, the Calm, standing very still and watching and not doing anything, because there was nothing, any more, that they could do.</p>\n<p>And beyond the Collar, the outer doors. And beyond those⁠—</p>\n<p>She walked out of the Draw at twenty minutes past four in the morning, two days after Continuance Day, into the Pit.</p>\n<p>Wind.</p>\n<p>It hit her in the face the moment she came through the doors. She stopped dead. It was cold — colder than anything had ever been in Holdfast — and it <em>moved</em>. It came at her from nowhere, from the dark, and pushed at her, and went round her, and tugged at her clothes and her hair and the rope on her shoulder, and went on, and came back. It smelled of — she didn’t know. Of nothing she had a word for. Of water, and stone, and distance.</p>\n<p>“That’s wind,” said Teo beside her.</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione. She didn’t know. He’d told her about it. It wasn’t like being told.</p>\n<p>Around her, Holdfast was coming out of the doors, and stopping, the way she’d stopped. Short broad people in patchwork clothes, holding their lamps, standing on the concrete apron in front of the Collar with their faces turned into the wind, their hair blowing, their mouths open. A child laughed — a high, startled, delighted laugh — and then another, and she looked, and they were jumping. The children. Jumping up and down on the concrete, because in the ordinary weight their Holdfast legs threw them into the air so high, higher than they’d ever jumped in their lives, and they were shrieking with it. Pim was jumping. Pim, with a cage of rats on his back, was leaping into the air like a flea and coming down and leaping again and screaming with laughter.</p>\n<p>And there were people on the rim.</p>\n<p>All round the Pit, on the rim road, high above them, she could see lights. Hundreds of lights. Torches, lanterns, the headlamps of vehicles. People. Standing along the edge of the crater in the dark, thousands of them, looking down. At the Draw. At the doors. At the long line of little flames coming out of it.</p>\n<p>Because the city behind them was dark.</p>\n<p>She looked past them. Past the rim, past the lights. At Averna. At the Outside. At the thing she’d been told about all her life and never once believed was there.</p>\n<p>It was a shape. A vast black shape against a slightly lesser black — the Stacks, rising in their terraced ranks, a hundred and forty levels, tier on tier on tier, going up and up and away into the distance on every side like mountains. And they were dark. Every one. Not a light in them. Here and there a tiny flicker — a candle in a window, a torch on a terrace, a fire — and otherwise, nothing. The great lanterns of the Stacks, that Cas had told her about, that had never gone out in ninety-one years, were out. The Canopy was out. The Draw was gone, and Averna was dark, the whole of it, all six hundred million, standing on their terraces and their balconies and their rooftops in the cold, in the wind, in the dark.</p>\n<p>Looking up.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She didn’t have words for it. She’d have them later — she’d find some, the way you find words for anything, eventually, by living with it — but she didn’t have them then, standing on the concrete apron of the Pit at twenty past four in the morning with the wind in her face.</p>\n<p>There were so many.</p>\n<p>That was the first thing. He’d told her. <em>Thousands of them.</em> She’d thought she understood. She’d thought: a thousand is the market on a busy round; a thousand is a few rows of the Tollers’ column. She hadn’t understood. The sky was full of them. The whole sky. It went up from the black edge of the Stacks on every side, black, blacker than the dark in the Vault after the Lamp went out, a black that went on and on and didn’t stop — and it was full of lights. Small ones. Tiny. Points. Some so bright they seemed to shiver. Some so faint she could only see them when she looked beside them instead of at them. White, mostly. Some blue. Some — yes — a little red, the way he’d said. Scattered across the whole of the black in no pattern she could see and every pattern she could imagine. Crowded, in places, so thick they ran together into a sort of mist, a pale river of light going right across the sky from one edge of the dark to the other.</p>\n<p>They didn’t move. They didn’t sing. They didn’t hum. They didn’t reach down toward her in long bright threads. They didn’t do anything. They were just there.</p>\n<p>Her knees went.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the concrete. She didn’t decide to. She just found she was sitting, with her lamp beside her and her hands flat on the cold ground and her head tipped back, looking up. And around her, she saw, Holdfast was doing the same. All along the apron, people were sitting down. Lying down. Lying on their backs on the concrete with their lamps beside them, looking up, the children who’d been jumping lying flat now with their arms spread out, silent. Osk was sitting with his hard hat in his lap and his mouth open. Pim had lain down on his back beside her with the cage of rats on his chest and was staring up with his eyes so wide they shone.</p>\n<p>Nobody spoke. Thirty-one thousand people came out of the Draw into the wind, one after another, all that night and all the next day, and every one of them, when they came out of the doors and looked up, stopped. And a lot of them sat down.</p>\n<p>And up on the rim — she saw, when she could look at anything but the sky — the people of Averna were doing it too. Standing along the edge of the Pit with their torches, their heads tipped back. Six hundred million of them, on their terraces and their balconies and their rooftops, in the dark, in the cold, looking up at a sky that no one in the city under sixty had ever seen. The same sky. At the same time. All of them together.</p>\n<p>She looked for one.</p>\n<p>She sat on the concrete and looked up at the thousands of them, and looked, and looked. A bright one. Not the brightest; there was one very bright one, low down, white and fierce and shivering, and she thought that must be the one he’d called the Dog Star, and it wasn’t his. She wanted one that was a little on its own. One that didn’t have anything else bright near it. She looked for a long time.</p>\n<p>There. Low in the south, over the black edge of the Stacks. A single star, quite bright, a little golden, sitting by itself in a dark patch of sky with nothing near it at all. Alone. Steady. Just there.</p>\n<p><em>Fomalhaut</em>, he’d said, in the gallery, while she sat against the algae trough with her head on her knees. <em>Which is lonely. Which sits by itself.</em></p>\n<p>She didn’t know if that was it. She’d ask Nell. She’d find out. It didn’t matter.</p>\n<p>“That one,” she said, out loud, to him, in case. “That one’s yours.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Calm came, eventually. Of course they did. They came across the apron in their charcoal overcoats with their tablets, which didn’t work, and their lists, which were paper now, and a tall silver-haired woman in a dark suit walking in front of them, very straight. And they began to talk about processing, and registration, and screening, and quarantine — which was fair, Teo said afterwards; these people had been sealed off from every germ in the world for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and a cold would kill half of them — and relocation for comfort.</p>\n<p>The silver-haired woman stopped in front of Ione, where she sat on the concrete.</p>\n<p>“I’m the Minister of Continuance,” she said. “Ondine Rask. I’m told you — came out first. That you were with Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at her from the ground. At her very straight back and her crease-less suit and her face, which was grey with exhaustion and entirely closed, like a door.</p>\n<p>She thought of a man with silver hair, fifteen storeys high on the side of a scaffold tower, holding up a key.</p>\n<p>“Your deputy,” she said, “ordered the test.”</p>\n<p>The Minister went quite still.</p>\n<p>“Aldous Crane,” said Ione. “He came up to the Crown with a camera crew and a key round his neck, and he told Ruth Tally to take the interlocks off, because you wanted a round number for Continuance Day. She said no. He said her daughter’s name. She said yes.” Her voice was very calm. “Then Six went, and she asked him for the key, and he wouldn’t give it to her. He held it. And it fell. And nineteen hundred people spent a hundred and sixty-eight years in there because of it.” She looked up at Rask with her grey-green eyes. “He’s been dead a hundred and thirty years. He wrote it down, before he died. I can show you. And Ruth Tally’s log. It’s in this box.”</p>\n<p>“How⁠—” said Rask. Her voice had gone thin. “How can you <em>possibly</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I watched him do it,” said Ione Tally. “A hundred and seventy-five thousand times.”</p>\n<p>Rask stared at her.</p>\n<p>“Who <em>are</em> you?” she said.</p>\n<p>Ione thought about it. She thought about it seriously, the way she thought about every question, turning it over. She looked at the dark city, and the thousands of lamps coming out of the doors behind her, and the sky.</p>\n<p>“I’m the night shift,” she said.</p>\n<p>Behind Rask, a little apart from the overcoats, a soft, older man in a brown cardigan buttoned wrong had stopped. He had a paper bag in his hand. He was looking at Ione, and at Teo, sitting beside her on the concrete, and at the dented steel box on the ground between them. His face in the lamplight was very tired, and very strange — as if something had broken in it, and something else, underneath, were trying to come out.</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk,” said Ansel Gaunt quietly. “Is Dr Hale⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He’s in the Crown,” said Teo. “He’s staying.”</p>\n<p>Gaunt looked at her. Then he looked at the box.</p>\n<p>“I think,” he said at last, slowly, like a man trying out words in a language he hasn’t spoken since childhood, “that I should like to hear about it. All of it. Properly.” He paused. “On the record.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The sky began to change a little after six.</p>\n<p>It went grey at the edge first, low in the east, behind the dark Stacks. Just a little. A paleness. And then more — a grey going to blue, going up the sky, pushing the black back. And the stars in that part of the sky began, one by one, to go out.</p>\n<p>Ione saw it happen. She was still sitting on the concrete, wrapped in a Civil Defence blanket, with Teo beside her in another and Pim asleep across both their laps with his rats. She’d been watching the lonely one in the south. And she looked up and saw the stars in the east going — fading, thinning, gone — and she sat up so fast that Pim woke with a yelp.</p>\n<p>“They’re going out,” she said.</p>\n<p>Her voice was high. She heard it and couldn’t stop it. The stars over the Stacks were going out, one after another, like the lamps in the lanes when the oil runs down, and the sky was going pale, and she was on her knees on the concrete with the blanket falling off her shoulders, and she couldn’t breathe.</p>\n<p>“They’re going out, Teo — they’re <em>going out</em> —”</p>\n<p>“Hey,” said Teo. “Hey. Hey. No. Look at me.”</p>\n<p>She looked at her.</p>\n<p>“It’s the sun,” said Teo Lisk. “It’s just the sun coming up. It’s morning. That’s all.” She put her hand on Ione’s arm. “They don’t go out. They’re still there. You just can’t see them in the day. They come back.”</p>\n<p>Ione stared at her.</p>\n<p>“They come back,” said Teo again. “Every night. When it gets dark. They come back.”</p>\n<p>“Every night?”</p>\n<p>“Every night.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked at the sky. At the grey turning to blue, and the stars fading out of it, and the lonely one, low in the south, still there, just barely, going faint, going. Every night. They’d go, and they’d come back. And go. And come back. For ever. The same, and never the same. The good kind.</p>\n<p>She sat back down on the concrete. She pulled the blanket back round her shoulders. She watched the lonely star go out, and the sky go blue, and the first light come up over the black shoulders of the Stacks and touch the rim of the Pit, and the faces of the people standing on it, and the long line of lamps still coming out of the doors of the Draw behind her.</p>\n<p>“Every night,” said Ione Tally, to him. “Did you hear that? They come back.”</p>"}, {"file": "21-epilogue-net-zero.md", "label": "Epilogue", "title": "Net Zero", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 4834, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Tell me how it ends.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally, Head Toller of Holdfast, last words</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The hammer stopped on the fourth day.</p>\n<p>Civil Defence found the flywheel on the third, in a vault in the Undercroft below the Lattice, still spinning — a disc of iron the size of a house, turning in the dark on its great bearing, driving its hammer down onto its keelstone anvil once a second the way Iris Hollowell had built it to, the way it had since 02:14 on the night of the Kindling. It took them a day to work out how to stop it. Nobody had ever designed a way. <em>When all else has failed</em>, Hollowell had written, <em>the station will say so</em>, and she had not imagined anyone wanting it to stop saying so.</p>\n<p>They stopped it at nine minutes past eleven on the fourth morning.</p>\n<p>In the Relief Camp on the Tallows flats, where thirty-one thousand people were sleeping in tents and prefabricated huts and the gutted shells of warehouses behind a fence of Civil Defence tape, it was the first silence in the history of Holdfast. People came out of the tents. They stood in the mud in the rain and looked at each other. Children cried. Old people sat down on the ground. For three nights afterwards, half the camp couldn’t sleep.</p>\n<p>The Tollers solved it. Tobiah, mostly. On the fifth night he sat down at a table in the Tollers’ hut with a wooden spoon and began, very quietly, to tap. Once a second. On the tabletop. And the Tollers in the hut, and then the people in the tents round it, lay in the dark and listened, and slept.</p>\n<p>They took it in turns after that. Every night, in the Relief Camp, somebody sat up and tapped. They were still doing it when Ione left. For all she knew, they were doing it still.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It rained.</p>\n<p>The Canopy was gone — its generators had died with the grid on the second day, and there was no Draw now to bring them back — and the autumn monsoon came in off the southern ocean for the first time in sixty years without anything to stop it, and it rained on Averna as it had not rained in living memory. It came down so hard it bounced. It ran off the black terraces of the Stacks in sheets and poured down the dead escalators and filled the Line tunnels and flooded the lower tiers, and Civil Defence went into the lower tiers with ropes and rubber boats, and Teo Lisk went with them, and so did Ione.</p>\n<p>She stood in it the first time for a whole afternoon.</p>\n<p>She stood in the mud outside the Tollers’ hut with her face turned up and her mouth open and let it come down on her. It was warm. It came down so hard it stung. She drank it. It tasted of nothing, mostly, and sometimes of dust, and sometimes, when it ran off the roof of the hut, of metal. Exactly the way he’d said.</p>\n<p>“You’ll catch your death,” said Teo, from the door.</p>\n<p>“I’ve never been wet,” said Ione. “Not like this. Not from above.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at her for a while. Then she came out and stood in it too.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There were colds.</p>\n<p>Nobody in Holdfast had had one for a hundred and sixty-eight years. Nobody had had anything that wasn’t already in the Vault when the Shear formed. The Commonweal’s doctors knew it would happen, and screened them and quarantined them and vaccinated them against everything they could think of, and it happened anyway. Coughs. Fevers. Things that Outside children shrugged off in a week went through the Relief Camp like fire through dry grass. Eleven people died of them in the first month. Most of them were old. Two of them were babies.</p>\n<p>One of the nurses who came to the camp in the second week was a slight, dark, quiet man of about thirty with very tired eyes, and on his third day Ione found him sitting on an upturned crate behind the medical tent in the rain, not doing anything, with a young dog asleep across his feet. A scruffy brown dog, all legs, with one ear up and one ear down.</p>\n<p>She knew who he was before she asked. Teo had told her. Teo had gone to his flat on Tier Twelve on the fifth day with a box of things from a locker in the Tallow Works lab, and come back without the box and with a very strange expression, and when Ione asked her about it, said only: <em>I bought him the bloody dog.</em></p>\n<p>“You’re Jonah,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the ground beside the crate, in the rain, and told him about the glass.</p>\n<p>She told him all of it. The way Teo had told it to her, walking out through the Works, and the way she’d seen it herself, standing at the long window of B-7 with her hand on the glass. The hall. The algae. The beard. The rat called Minister. MINISTER II. <em>I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS NOBODY CAME</em>, and Teo writing back <em>WE’RE HERE</em>. The map, and how it had got them out of the Carousel. <em>TELL JONAH. TELL HIM I SAID YES TO THE DOG.</em></p>\n<p>“He said,” said Ione, “<em>had a good life. Mostly algae.</em>”</p>\n<p>Jonah laughed.</p>\n<p>It came out of him suddenly, a real laugh, shocked, surprised, the way a laugh comes when you’re not expecting one, and he put his hand over his mouth, and laughed again — and then he wasn’t laughing. He sat on the crate in the rain with his hand over his mouth and his shoulders shaking, and the dog woke up and put its front paws on his knee and licked his wrist, and he couldn’t stop.</p>\n<p>Ione sat on the ground beside him and didn’t say anything. She’d learned that from Cas. From the hut. Sometimes you just sat there.</p>\n<p>“What’s the dog called?” she said, when he’d stopped.</p>\n<p>He wiped his face with the back of his hand. He looked down at it.</p>\n<p>“Minister,” said Jonah.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Halloran Crane walked out of Gate Nine last, at eleven minutes past ten on the second night after Continuance Day, eighteen hours after Ione had walked out ahead of him.</p>\n<p>He had stood at the top of Well One for nineteen hours. Every one of them. Every family, every child, every old man in a rope sling, every Warden and every rigger and every person who had ever called Seren Tally a heretic or stepped round her daughter in the lanes — he had seen them onto the ladder, one after another, and said their names, and when the last of them had gone down and the Commons behind him was empty and lit and silent, he had put out his lamp and gone down after them.</p>\n<p>Ondine Rask was waiting for him at the gate.</p>\n<p>Nobody knew what they said. They stood apart, on the concrete, in the rain, for about ten minutes — the Minister of Continuance in her dark suit and the First Steward of Holdfast in his black coat with its gold lamp — and nobody went near them. A Civil Defence sergeant called Kade said afterwards that she’d heard only one thing, as she passed with a stretcher, and that it had been the old man who said it, very gently.</p>\n<p><em>It’s over, Minister. We’re relieved.</em></p>\n<p>Rask resigned nine days later. The Directorate announced it as ill health. By then it didn’t matter what the Directorate announced.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It came out.</p>\n<p>It came out slowly, and then all at once, the way the Bells had come. Gaunt went first. The day after Gate Nine, Colonel Ansel Gaunt of the Office of Public Calm sat down in a room in the Collar in front of a Civil Defence recorder — not a Calm one — and began to talk, and didn’t stop for three days. He talked about the Margin Review. About Adeyemi. About a revised report and a nice pen. About a chip with a silver seal, and orders with <em>any means</em> underlined. About a Continuance Day address with footage from Year Eighty. He brought documents. He had kept copies of everything, it turned out, for thirty years, in a flat on Tier Six, in biscuit tins.</p>\n<p>“Things that are recorded,” he said, on the recording, “have a way of becoming history. I used to think that was the danger. I find I’ve changed my mind.”</p>\n<p>Then the Log. Ruth Tally’s Log, the whole of it, a hundred and sixty-eight years of it, the pencil getting smaller and smaller — the Tollers let the inquiry copy every page. And Aldous Crane’s private journal, which Halloran Crane handed over himself. <em>I did not give it to the Lamp. I held it.</em> And the recordings: a month of a man talking into a machine in a stone room, in a loft, on a spindle, about rats and weddings and funerals and a game called Drop, and near the end, flatly, plainly, about the Margin Review and what he had signed and why. He’d recorded it for Teo. Teo gave it to the inquiry. She didn’t ask anyone’s permission.</p>\n<p>It didn’t fix anything. Ione had thought, somehow, that it might. That the truth would come out and the Commonweal would look at it and something would change, the way the Lamp had gone out and the Drop had become a road. It didn’t happen like that. The Directorate was still the Directorate. The lights came back on in rotation, four hours a day per tier, from the old fusion plants at Kovač and Tessarine, and people queued for water and argued about whose fault it was. There were riots on Tier Four in the third week. There were strikes. There was an inquiry, and then another inquiry into the inquiry. The client states cut their trunk lines and sent angry envoys. The First Director gave an address about resilience and the spirit of the Tending.</p>\n<p>But things were <em>said</em>. That was the difference. In the lanes of the Stacks, on the terraces, in the queues for water, in the sports halls where people were still being relocated for comfort — people said things out loud. They said <em>default</em>. They said <em>Adeyemi</em>. They said <em>Aldous Crane held the key</em>. They said <em>thirty-one thousand people</em>. They said <em>the night shift</em>.</p>\n<p>On the big poster on the side of the Line station at the foot of the Tier Nine arterial — blue and gold, a child’s face turned up toward a golden flame, THE FUTURE WILL PAY in letters two metres high — somebody had sprayed, in rough white paint, underneath:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">NOT ANY MORE</div>\n<p>Nobody took it down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She took Ruth’s letter to Madeleine Tally on a Tuesday in the fourth week.</p>\n<p>Maddy lived with her grandmother on Tier Nine, in a flat that had been Ruth’s mother’s for thirty years, three streets from where Cas’s Standby flat had been. Ruth’s mother was sixty-seven. She had white hair and Ruth’s mole below her left ear, and when she opened the door and Ione told her who she was — who she <em>was</em>, the chain of it, Ruth and Hal and Hal’s daughter and her son and his, all the way down to a girl with a white streak in her hair standing on the landing with a steel box — she didn’t say anything at all. She just looked at Ione’s face. And then she stood back and let her in.</p>\n<p>Maddy was on the floor of the front room, doing a jigsaw.</p>\n<p>She was seven. She had dark hair in two plaits that someone had done badly, and her mother’s eyes, and she looked up from the jigsaw at the strange short broad woman in the patched clothes and said, “Are you from the Draw? Nana says the Draw’s where Mum is.”</p>\n<p>Ione knelt down on the carpet beside the jigsaw.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this for four weeks. She had thought about what she would say, and how, and she’d practised it, in the Tollers’ hut at night with Tobiah tapping. She had it all ready. She looked at the little girl and none of it was any use.</p>\n<p>“Your mum,” she said, “wrote you some letters.”</p>\n<p>She gave her the last one. Not the Log. Just the one page, the last page, the old woman’s shaking pencil, that Tobiah had copied out very carefully in large clear letters on a clean sheet so that a seven-year-old could read it, with the original folded inside it.</p>\n<p>Maddy read it. It took her a long time. She moved her lips. Her grandmother stood in the doorway with her hand over her mouth.</p>\n<p>“Eighty-eight,” said Maddy at last. She looked up. “Mum’s thirty-eight.”</p>\n<p>“She was thirty-eight,” said Ione, “when she went to work that night. And then she lived for a very long time. In a place where time went faster. She lived her whole life. She got to be eighty-eight.” She paused. “It was a good life. Mostly. She says so. You can read it. It’s all in there.”</p>\n<p>Maddy looked at the letter.</p>\n<p>“Is she coming home?” she said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione. “I’m sorry. She died a long time ago. A long time ago for her. For you, it was the night she went to work.” She didn’t know how to say it better. There wasn’t a better. “But she thought about you every day. Every single day. That’s true. I know it’s true. She wrote it down.”</p>\n<p>Maddy thought about that. She thought about it seriously, turning it over, the way Ione did, and Ione watched her do it and felt something go through her that she didn’t have a name for.</p>\n<p>“Was she happy?” said Maddy.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Ione. “She had a husband called Tomas who made her laugh. And three more children. You’ve got two brothers. One of them’s called Hal. And a sister. They’re all — they were all very old, a long time ago. And she had eleven grandchildren. And they had children. And I’m one of them.” She held out her hand, the one without the splint. “I’m your — I don’t know what I am. Tobiah worked it out. It’s a very long word. I’m your family.”</p>\n<p>Maddy looked at her hand. Then she took it, the way children take hands, without any fuss.</p>\n<p>“You’ve got a white bit in your hair,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“I went somewhere a long way up,” said Ione, “and I was there for a long time.”</p>\n<p>Maddy considered this. Then she turned back to the jigsaw. “Do you want to help? It’s a horse. I can’t find the sky bits.”</p>\n<p>Ione sat on the carpet with Ruth Tally’s daughter for the rest of the afternoon, and helped her find the sky.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Nell Hale lived on Tier Nine too, now. Sabine had moved them back there when the relocation ended, into Cas’s old Standby flat, because the Ministry had forgotten to reassign it and it was the only flat on the tier with a balcony that faced south. Nell had insisted. She’d said the stars were better that way.</p>\n<p>Teo had been three times. The first time, on the fifth day, she’d gone to the sports hall on Tier Thirty with the recordings in her pocket, as she’d promised, and sat with Nell on a gym mat in the corner while she listened, and Nell had listened to all of them — a month of them, rats and weddings and funerals and Osk and <em>I ate a rat, don’t tell your mother</em> — without stopping, for nine hours, with Teo beside her, and at the end of it she had not cried and she had not hit Teo. She had said, <em>can I keep them?</em> And Teo had said, <em>they’re yours</em>. And Nell had said, <em>he found one. He found a star. I said he might.</em></p>\n<p>The fourth time, Teo brought Ione.</p>\n<p>They went at night. It was one of the dark nights for Tier Nine — no power in the rotation — and the Stacks were black, and the sky over them was black, and in it, the stars. They came every night now. Averna had started, in the weeks since, to go out and look at them the way it used to go out and look at the Quota clocks. People sat on the terraces with blankets. Children lay on their backs on the arterial.</p>\n<p>Nell opened the door. She was ten. She had her mother’s grey eyes and a face that couldn’t leave a question alone, and she looked at Ione in the doorway — at her, all of her, the short broad shape and the patched clothes and the rope, which Ione still wore out of habit, over her shoulder — without blinking.</p>\n<p>“You’re Ione,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“He said I’d like you.” She said it as if reporting a fact. “On the tapes. He said, <em>I think if you ever meet her, you’ll like her. I don’t know why I think that.</em>”</p>\n<p>“He said that?”</p>\n<p>“He said it on the day with the rat.” Nell stood back from the door. “The stars are out. Do you want to see them? From the balcony? They’re better from the balcony.”</p>\n<p>They sat on the balcony, the three of them, and Sabine brought tea and then went inside and left them. The balcony faced south over the dark Stacks, and the sky over them was full, the pale river of light going across it, the bright white one low in the east that shivered.</p>\n<p>“That’s Sirius,” said Nell. “That’s the Dog Star. It’s the brightest. It’s not the closest, though. It just looks like it is.”</p>\n<p>“He told me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“He <em>told</em> you?”</p>\n<p>“On the Rim. In the gallery. He told me their names. I was⁠—” She stopped. “I was very frightened, and he told me their names. So I’d have something to think about.”</p>\n<p>Nell looked at her. Then she looked back up at the sky.</p>\n<p>“Is he dead?” she said.</p>\n<p>She said it very plainly. The way she’d said <em>is it broken</em> on the phone, Teo had told her. The way you’d ask the time.</p>\n<p>Ione had thought about this too. For four weeks. She’d thought about it harder than she’d thought about Maddy. She looked up at the sky, at the thousands of them, and said the only thing she had.</p>\n<p>“No,” she said. “He’s in one moment. It’s — it’s hard to explain. It’s only once. It’s once, and it doesn’t end. He’s not waiting, or stuck, or — it’s not like being in a room. It’s not like the Quiet. It’s just one moment, and he’s in it, and it’s always true.” She was quiet. “It’s only for ever for us. For him it’s once.”</p>\n<p>Nell thought about that. Ione watched her do it.</p>\n<p>“Is it a good moment?” said Nell.</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at the sky.</p>\n<p>“It’s the best one I know,” she said.</p>\n<p>Nell was quiet. Then she moved along the bench, not far, a little, so that her shoulder was against Ione’s arm. She just sat there.</p>\n<p>After a while, Ione pointed.</p>\n<p>“That one,” she said. “Low down. Over there. On its own. The one with nothing near it. What’s that one called?”</p>\n<p>Nell followed her finger. And sat up, delighted.</p>\n<p>“That’s <em>Fomalhaut</em>,” she said. “That’s in the Southern Fish. It’s the loneliest star. That’s what they call it. Because there’s nothing else bright near it, in all that bit of sky. It’s just by itself.”</p>\n<p>“He said,” said Ione. “In the gallery. He said, <em>Fomalhaut, which is lonely</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Did he have a favourite?” said Nell. “I asked him once. He said he didn’t know. He said he’d never looked.”</p>\n<p>“He hadn’t,” said Ione. “So I picked one for him.” She looked at the lonely star, low over the black Stacks, steady, golden, just there. “That one. I told him I’d pick one and look at it every night. That’s his.”</p>\n<p>Nell looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<p>“That’s a good one,” she said at last, seriously. “That’s the right one for him.”</p>\n<p>“I think so.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll look at it too,” said Nell Hale. “Every night. Then there’s two of us.”</p>\n<p>Teo, at the other end of the bench, had turned her face away and was looking very hard at the Stacks.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>In the sixth week, she went back into the Vault.</p>\n<p>The inquiry wanted a survey. Teo was leading it, because nobody else in the Commonweal would go in, and Teo asked her to come because she knew the spindles, and she said yes because she’d always been going to say yes.</p>\n<p>It was dark. That was the thing she hadn’t been ready for, coming up through the conduit into the Drop District with her lamp. Holdfast was dark. Every lamp in the lanes had burned out long ago. The shrines were dark. The scaffold towers stood up out of the black like the ribs of something dead, and the washing still hung on the lines between them, and the rat pens stood empty with their doors open, and in the Commons the Tollers’ banner still hung from the gantry in the dark, Seren’s line, white on black, and nobody to read it. It was so quiet. No Bell. No Lamp. No hum. The Anvil under her boots was cold.</p>\n<p>It was ordinary. That was the other thing. It weighed what everything weighed. The chalk fell straight.</p>\n<p>They went up Spindle One. There was no need for the Lees now, and no need for Four; Teo’s team had rigged ropes up the inside of the lattice, and it was only climbing, the whole four kilometres of it. It took them two days. They slept in a cache in the old best Lee, and the chalk didn’t hang in the air, and Ione lay awake on the planks for a long time.</p>\n<p>At the top, below the Rim, the gallery was dark. No ghost-light in the windows. Nothing going round. The Carousel was just a corridor — a curving steel corridor round the spindle, with an algae trough along its windows gone brown and dry, and the walls covered in scratched fives, and a pallet of rags, and a pile of smooth stones. Ione walked the whole way round it. It came back to where it started, because it was a ring, the ordinary way. She went out of the inner door onto the Rim and it was the Rim.</p>\n<p>And above the Rim, where the Crown had been, there was the thing.</p>\n<p>Teo’s instruments had been trying to see it for a week, from below, with lights and lasers and sounding gear. They’d got nothing. You shone a light up at it and the light went into it and didn’t come back. It was a sphere. They’d worked that much out, from the shape of the nothing: a sphere about four hundred metres across, hanging in the air at the top of the Vault where the Lamp had been, enclosing the top of Spindle One and the Crown and part of the Rim. It didn’t reflect. It didn’t glow. It didn’t pull or push; the chalk fell straight right up to its edge. The instruments said it had no mass. None. No energy. None. No temperature. They said, as far as they could say anything, that it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p><em>Net zero</em>, the inquiry’s physicists called it, in their report. The debt and the payment, sealed together, balancing. Summing to nothing.</p>\n<p>Ione stood on the Rim in the lamplight and looked at it.</p>\n<p>It was black. Not dark; <em>black</em>. A curve of perfect black rising out of the steel of the Rim in front of her, and going up, and curving over, and blotting out the whole top of the sky. The Rim ran into it and stopped. The stair to the Crown ran into it and stopped. Her lamp’s light went into it and stopped. It was like standing at the edge of the night.</p>\n<p>“Careful,” said Teo, behind her.</p>\n<p>“It’s all right,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She walked up to it. Along the Rim, past the place where the chalk arrows had been — they were gone; the Lace had taken them, or time had, or they’d been swept away by the thing that had happened here — to the foot of the stair that went up to where the hatch had been. The black began on the third step. She climbed the first two. She stood on the second step, a hand’s width from it.</p>\n<p>She took off her glove.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this too. She’d thought about it at night, lying in the Tollers’ hut, listening to Tobiah tap. She hadn’t known what it would be. Cold. Hot. Hard. Nothing. Whether her hand would go into it and not come back.</p>\n<p>She put her palm flat against it.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t cold. It wasn’t warm. It wasn’t hard, or soft. It wasn’t anything. Her hand stopped against it, flat, the way it would stop against glass — the way Ben’s had stopped, on the other side of the window in B-7 — and there was nothing there to feel. Not even the feeling of nothing. It was like the instant before you touch something. Like the last moment before a door opens. Like being just about to.</p>\n<p>She stood on the stair with her hand flat on the black and her forehead nearly touching it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know if he could hear. She was almost sure he couldn’t. <em>Nothing will pass into it.</em> She’d read the plate. But she’d thought about that too, at night, and decided — the way she’d decided on the ladder, coming down in the dark, <em>he saw me</em> — that it didn’t matter whether he could hear. She’d decided you said things anyway. The way Ruth had written her letters. The way the Tollers had counted a little girl’s night.</p>\n<p>“They come back,” she told him. “The stars. Every night. Teo said. I didn’t believe her, the first morning, I thought they’d gone out. But they come back. Every single night.” She was quiet. “I found you one. Fomalhaut. The lonely one. Nell says it’s right. She’s going to look at it too. So there’s two of us.”</p>\n<p>Her palm was flat on the black.</p>\n<p>“It rains,” she said. “It tastes of nothing. And metal, off the roofs. You were right. Jonah’s got the dog. He called it Minister. Teo bought it. Teo’s all right. She’s — she cried on Nell’s balcony and pretended she didn’t. Maddy’s seven. She’s doing a jigsaw of a horse. I helped her with the sky.”</p>\n<p>She stopped. She stood there for a while.</p>\n<p>“I wrote the last page,” she said. “In the Log. Gran said to. She said <em>tell me how it ends</em>. So I did. I wrote it all down. Everything. Properly. Every word.” Her voice was quite steady. “It ends with you.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet then. Behind her, on the Rim, Teo stood with her lamp, and didn’t come any closer, and didn’t say anything either.</p>\n<p>Then Ione Tally took her hand away from the dark, and put her glove back on, and came down the stair, and went home.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><em>From the Crown Log of the Hollowell Draw (the Tally Log), final page, in the hand of Ione Tally:</em></p>\n<blockquote class=\"doc\"><p>Year 169 of the Watch, the last. Bell 123,716, which was the last Bell counted in Holdfast by the old way. After it, they came one a second, and we couldn’t keep up, and then we didn’t need to.</p><p>The Lamp was closed from the Crown by Casimir Hale of Averna, Outside, with the key that was never made. He went up Spindle Four with me through the Lees and the Carousel and the Lace and he set the Clause and he kept the needle on the line by hand until the end, as the plate says. He is the witness. He did not return.</p><p>I want it written down here, because Gran said to write it properly, that he was not paying anything. He told me so. He said he wanted it. He said it was the only thing he had ever wanted that he was sure of. I believe him. I was there.</p><p>He is in one instant. It is the instant I was safe. He chose it.</p><p>It is only for ever for us. For him it is once.</p><p>Ruth — I don’t know if anyone will read this who knew you, except Maddy, and she was seven. But I want to say that the man you saw at the Keystone at 02:09 was real, and his name was Cas, and he was as calm as you said. You were right about him. You were right about everything.</p><p>Gran — this is how it ends.</p><p>We are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">I.T.</span></blockquote>"}, {"file": "22-coda-once.md", "label": "Coda", "title": "Once", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 948, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>It isn’t forever for the one inside. It’s once.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ione Tally</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">There is a moment.</p>\n<p>In it, a man is kneeling in the dust at the far end of the highest room in the world, with his hand on a small brass wheel.</p>\n<p>The wheel is warm. It has just turned, a single tooth, a little to the left, because the needle was drifting and he saw which way it would go next and got there first. Now the needle is on the line. Exactly on it. Not a hair either side. It is the best he has ever held it. It will never be held better.</p>\n<p>Through the great window, the Lamp has begun. At its heart, where the hard white point was, there is the first edge of something — a thread of gold, the very first thread, the first instant of a light that would have been bright enough to end the world. It is only just beginning. It is always only just beginning. The Lace hangs round it, singing, every branch of it lit gold at the tips, as if a frost had caught the morning.</p>\n<p>And down, and to the left, past the glare, on the black lattice of Spindle Four, below the Breach, there is a small soft light.</p>\n<p>Hers.</p>\n<p>It’s going down. Steadily. A flight of stairs, in the Lees, out of the Fold, the way it should. Safe.</p>\n<p>And just before this — it’s here too; everything is — the light stopped on a walkway, and a small grey arm came up. The palm out. Held there, toward the Rim, toward the Crown, until it shook. She was looking up at him. He saw it. Of everything, it’s the thing he sees most clearly, clearer than the needle, clearer than the gold.</p>\n<p>He saw.</p>\n<p>He isn’t counting. That’s the first thing he would notice, if there were a next thing to notice after the first. For the first time since 02:14 on the morning of Continuance Day, for the first time in fifteen years, perhaps in his life, there is nothing to count. Not the hammer. Not rungs. Not seconds. There is one of everything. There is one moment, and he is in it, and it is one.</p>\n<p><em>Count me</em>, she’d said. <em>I’m one.</em></p>\n<p>Everything is here.</p>\n<p>That’s the other thing. Nothing has to be remembered, because nothing is behind him; it is all simply here, in the moment, the way the whole Vault was in every thread of the Lace. Nell on the phone in a gym with a lizard in a box — <em>you don’t know that; there might be</em>. Teo’s tea, which was mostly a structural material. A boy on a flatbed hugging a cradle: <em>is there a form?</em> A palm on the other side of a pane of glass, and grease-pencil letters fading on it. Osk kneeling on the floor of a stone room: <em>you don’t push up; you stack your bones</em>. An old woman with eyes like a bird, correcting him to the minute. Soup. Rat. A rooftop swaying with stamping feet and a cord tied round his wrist and taken off again. A hut in a Lee and a heart going too fast. <em>I’m glad it was you.</em> The cord tied round his wrist again, and this time left there — he can feel it; flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped. <em>I’ll hold your rope.</em> A visor pressed hard against his temple for a second, cold. <em>Haul.</em></p>\n<p><em>So this is the start.</em></p>\n<p><em>This is the start.</em></p>\n<p>All of it. At once. Not going. Not coming. Here.</p>\n<p>And there is one more thing.</p>\n<p>At the Keystone, beside him, very close — so close that she is kneeling almost where he is kneeling — there is a woman in a white coat. Not light. Not a ghost made of nine minutes going round. A woman. Thirty-eight years old, with her hair pinned up and a mole below her left ear and the side of her thumb raw where she’s bitten it. She has just wrenched up a steel plate. She has just watched a key fall forward out of the world. It is 02:09 on the night of the Kindling, and she is looking up at the place beside the Keystone, at a man who isn’t there, and he is there, and she is looking at him.</p>\n<p>He turns his head and looks at her.</p>\n<p>He knows her. He has read her whole life in pencil. He has watched her close her eyes for half a second a hundred and seventy-five thousand times, through someone else’s eyes. He knows that she is about to pull a hammer and go down a lift and live for fifty years in a city of lamps and write letters to a little girl who will stay seven for all of them. He knows that she will marry a man who makes her laugh. He knows that she will think about this — about him, about this one look — every day for the rest of her life, and write it down once, in the thirty-first year, and never tell anyone.</p>\n<p>He’s sorry for her. He’s glad of her. Both at once. He’d tell her, if there were time.</p>\n<p>There isn’t time. There’s something better than time. There’s this.</p>\n<p>The needle is on the line. The gold is beginning. Her light is on the spindle, going down, and she raised her hand to him, and she is safe. He knows she is safe. He will always know it. That is the moment. That is the whole of it.</p>\n<p>It isn’t for ever. Not for him. It’s once.</p>\n<p>It is long enough.</p>"}]}], "defaultVersion": "v2", "chapters": [{"file": "00-prologue.md", "label": "Prologue", "title": "Nine Minutes", "part": "", "partStart": false, "words": 3972, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The vacuum will lend to anyone. It is the most patient creditor in the universe. It has never once asked to be repaid on time.</p><span class=\"attr\">Iris Hollowell, lecture notes, Year 3 of Continuance</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">At eight minutes to two in the morning on the eve of Continuance Day, Ruth Tally stood in the Crown of the Hollowell Draw and watched a man from the Ministry comb his hair in the reflection of the thing that powered the world.</p>\n<p>The Crown was the highest room in the station, a lens-shaped gallery of black keelstone perched at the top of Spindle One, four kilometres above the floor of the Vault. Its window was a single curved sheet of armoured glass forty metres long, and through the window, above and a little ahead, hung the Lesion. It did not look like much. People who came up for the first time always said that. They expected fire, or a whirlpool, or some dreadful eye. What they got was a point of white-violet light so small and hard it seemed less like an object than a flaw in the glass — a nail driven through the night. You could not look at it for long. You could not quite look away from it either. The engineers had a saying about that, which was that the Lesion looked back, and they said it as a joke, mostly.</p>\n<p>Deputy Minister Aldous Crane leaned close to his own reflection, which was superimposed on the Lesion’s, and pressed a strand of silver hair into place with two fingers.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” he asked the camera operator. “I don’t want to squint. The Minister squinted in the Year Eighty footage and she’s never forgiven the crew.”</p>\n<p>“We’ll stop down,” said the operator, a young woman with a shaved head and a Ministry lanyard. “You’ll look like a painting, sir.”</p>\n<p>“A painting.” Crane seemed to consider this and find it acceptable. On a chain around his neck, lying against his dark jacket like a pendant, was a key: a flat, heavy, blackish thing the length of a finger, cut from keelstone and stamped along its shaft. Ruth had seen it once before, in a photograph in the training manual. She had not expected to see it in her own control room, being worn as jewellery.</p>\n<p>“Tally,” said Crane, without turning round. “How long until we’re ready?”</p>\n<p>Ruth looked at the board. Her board: fourteen years she’d been in the Crown, six as shift engineer, and she could have read it with her eyes shut by the sound of the relays. The Lattice was at nominal. The Rollover was feeding at seventy-one percent, which was a number that would once have caused a scandal and now caused a memo. Tensioner Six was running a bearing temperature eleven degrees above the line, as it had been for three quarters, because its maintenance slot had been deferred three quarters running to keep output up for the export contracts.</p>\n<p>“We’re not,” she said.</p>\n<p>Now he turned.</p>\n<p>He had a good face for the Commonweal: open, confident, a little lined in the right places, the kind of face that could say <em>there is no cause for concern</em> and make you feel ashamed for having been concerned. He smiled at her with it.</p>\n<p>“Ruth. It is Ruth? Ruth, I understand. Believe me. I’ve read the protocol.”</p>\n<p>“Then you’ve read that the Deepening Test requires the Lattice interlocks to be taken offline for the duration.”</p>\n<p>“For eleven minutes.”</p>\n<p>“For eleven minutes during which, if anything happens to a tensioner, the Lattice can’t compensate on its own. It’s a test we run in the spring, with the full day crew, the Chief Engineer, and two spare tensioner rotors spun and waiting. Not at two in the morning with a night shift, the Chief on leave, and Six running hot.”</p>\n<p>“Six has been running hot since the summer,” said Crane. “I’ve seen the reports too. And Six is still here.”</p>\n<p>Behind her, at the tensioner desk, Fen Marsh made a small noise that could have been a cough. Ruth didn’t look round. Fen had two children and a mortgage on a Tier Twelve flat and had once, at a shift party, drunkenly explained to Ruth that he believed in the Continuance the way he believed in gravity: not with any enthusiasm, but because it was stupid not to.</p>\n<p>“Deputy Minister,” Ruth said, “respectfully. It’s not my call. It’s the Chief Engineer’s call, and he’s not here, so it goes to the Director of Operations, who is asleep in Tessarine and whom I tried to wake an hour ago.”</p>\n<p>“Director Obi has been informed,” said Crane’s aide, a narrow young man with a tablet, “and has deferred to the Ministry.”</p>\n<p>Ruth looked at him. He looked at the tablet.</p>\n<p>“At dawn,” Crane said, in the patient voice of a man explaining something to a child whom he likes, “the Minister of Continuance is going to stand in front of the Quota clock in Hollowell Square with a hundred thousand people and eleven heads of state watching and announce the Second Deepening. Thirty percent more output. Thirty percent, Ruth. Do you know what that means?”</p>\n<p>“It means thirty percent more debt.”</p>\n<p>A pause, very slight.</p>\n<p>“It means,” said Crane, “that the Aurelian Compact signs the trunk-line extension, which means we power forty million homes that are currently burning coal in the dark. It means the Canopy stays up through the storm season. It means your daughter — Maddy, isn’t it? — grows up in the safest, brightest city that has ever existed. And the Minister would like to show footage of the test, conducted tonight, live, successfully, from this room, by you, because a test is a promise and a promise is only worth something if you keep it.” He smiled again. “She asked for you, you know. By name. She said, ‘Tally’s the best we have.’ I’d hate to have to tell her she was wrong.”</p>\n<p>It was very gently done. That was the thing about the Commonweal: nobody ever raised their voice. Nobody ever said <em>or else</em>. They said your daughter’s name, and they said it kindly, and they mentioned that Maddy’s placement at the Upper Tier academy was, like all placements, subject to annual review by the Ministry, and they didn’t need to say anything else, and you heard it anyway.</p>\n<p>Ruth thought about Maddy, asleep in her nana’s flat on Tier Nine with her feet out of the covers and the glow-in-the-dark fish her father had stuck to the ceiling before he left. She thought about the eleven degrees on Six. She thought: <em>it will probably be fine</em>. That was the sentence. That was the whole machine. Ninety-one years of it had run on that sentence. It will probably be fine.</p>\n<p>“Eleven minutes,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Eleven minutes,” said Crane, and patted her shoulder, and went to stand where the light was best.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They ran it by the book because there was nothing else to run it by.</p>\n<p>“Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol, test designation Continuance Ninety-One.”</p>\n<p>“Lattice standing by.”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to manual.”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to manual, confirm.” Fen’s voice. “Feeding at seventy-one, holding.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks.” Ruth’s thumb hovered over the key switch. There was a moment when she could have not done it. She would remember that moment for the rest of her life, which would turn out to be a strange and very long life, in a place she could not at that moment have imagined; she would remember it the way you remember the last step before a stair you didn’t know was missing. “Interlocks off.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks off, confirm.”</p>\n<p>The Crown did not change. The window did not change. Down on the board, a row of green lights went amber, which meant only that the Lattice was now trusting its tensioners to hold themselves, the way a trapeze artist trusts her hands after the net’s been taken away.</p>\n<p>“Camera’s rolling,” said the operator softly.</p>\n<p>“Commence Deepening. Draw to one-oh-five.”</p>\n<p>“One-oh-five.”</p>\n<p>It came up through the floor first. It always did. There was a note the Lesion made when it was being worked — not a sound exactly, more a pressure in the long bones — and every engineer in the Draw knew it and none of them had a good word for it. Ruth’s grandmother, who had been Lattice crew in the first generation, used to call it <em>singing</em>. The Lesion was singing now, a long low swell that made the water in Dieter Hask’s cup shiver into rings.</p>\n<p>“One-ten.”</p>\n<p>“Output at sixty-six terawatts,” Dieter said from the output desk. “Sixty-six and a half.”</p>\n<p>“One-fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“Six is at plus fourteen,” said Fen. “Plus fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“Noted.”</p>\n<p>“One-twenty.”</p>\n<p>“Seventy-two terawatts.” Dieter’s voice had gone very flat. “That’s a record.”</p>\n<p>“Of course it is,” murmured Crane, for the camera.</p>\n<p>Out in the window, the Lesion had not grown, but it had sharpened; it had the look of something pulled taut. Ruth had seen it like this twice before, in spring tests, surrounded by the day crew with their coffee and their jokes and the Chief Engineer stalking up and down behind them humming. She had never seen it at night, with the Vault below them dark but for the work-lights on the spindles, eight black pillars rising out of the floor into the glare like the columns of a drowned temple.</p>\n<p>“One-twenty-five.”</p>\n<p>“Six is plus nineteen.”</p>\n<p>“One-thirty.”</p>\n<p>“Plus twenty-two. Ruth.”</p>\n<p>“I see it.”</p>\n<p>“Seventy-eight terawatts,” said Dieter, to nobody.</p>\n<p>“It’s over the line.”</p>\n<p>“I see it, Fen. Holding at one-thirty. Deputy Minister, we’re at the protocol maximum, you have your thirty percent, I’m bringing it down.”</p>\n<p>“Five more,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>She turned. He was not looking at her; he was looking at the camera, and his face was lit white-violet on one side, and he said it again, pleasantly, to the lens: “Five more, Ruth. Seventy-eight is a figure. Nobody remembers a figure. At dawn the Minister is going to stand in front of the Quota clock, and I would like the clock behind her to say <em>eighty</em>. Let’s give them a round number.”</p>\n<p>“Eighty is one-thirty-five. The protocol maximum is one-thirty.”</p>\n<p>“And the Minister has authorised one-thirty-five.” His aide held up the tablet as if it were a holy relic. “It’s in the order. Five more, and then you can bring it down, and we’ll all go home and watch ourselves on the news.”</p>\n<p>Afterwards — and there would be a great deal of afterwards — Ruth would try to decide what she had been thinking when she said <em>one-thirty-five</em>. She would write it down in a logbook, in pencil, in a hand that got smaller as the years went on and the paper ran out, and she would never get it right. The truest version she ever wrote was this: <em>I thought that I was tired. I thought that I had been told. I thought it would probably be fine.</em></p>\n<p>“One-thirty-five,” she said.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall of the Crown began to run fast.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was an ordinary clock, an analogue one with a sweep hand, put up in the Crown by some engineer decades ago as a joke about the station’s timekeeping. Ruth saw the sweep hand go round once in what felt like half a second. Then again. Then it was a blur.</p>\n<p>“Six,” said Fen. “Six is⁠—”</p>\n<p>The floor dropped. Not the floor: her weight. Everyone in the Crown staggered at once as though the room had fallen a metre and been caught. The camera operator went down on one knee. Dieter’s cup went off the desk and did not fall. Ruth saw it. It left the edge of the desk and hung, tilting, a little brown tongue of coffee reaching out of it in a slow curl, and it went down through the air as if through honey, and it took so long to fall that she had time to look away from it and look back.</p>\n<p>“Six has unseated,” Fen said, and his voice came to her strangely, the words arriving a hair before she saw his mouth make them. “Six is off its bearing. Six is off. Lattice is⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Rollover to full,” Ruth said. “Interlocks on. Interlocks <em>on</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Interlocks on.” The amber row went green, and went amber again, and went red.</p>\n<p>“Lattice not accepting.”</p>\n<p>“Draw to zero. Kill the Draw. <em>Kill it.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Draw at zero.” Dieter was hitting the panel with the flat of his hand. “Draw at zero, it’s — output’s still climbing, it’s not us, it’s not us drawing, it’s coming out on its own⁠—”</p>\n<p>And out in the window, the Lesion broke.</p>\n<p>She would try to describe it later, too, and fail. The nearest she came was this: it was like watching frost form on a pane of glass, if the frost were made of light, and if the pane were the world. The hard white point did not explode. It did not grow. Its <em>edge</em> — the edge that had always been a simple, perfect boundary — came apart into edges, and those edges into more edges, branching and branching into a filigree so fine and so endless that the eye slid off it and came back and slid off again. Lace. It spread outward in every direction at once, delicate, luminous, and it made no sound at all.</p>\n<p>Everyone in the Crown had stopped moving, except that they had not stopped; they were moving at different speeds. She saw Crane’s aide lift his tablet to his face in a single slow, graceful arc that took, perhaps, ten seconds. She saw the camera operator, on her knee by the window, flicker, and stand, and be on her knee again. She heard someone scream and then saw them open their mouth to do it.</p>\n<p><em>Time dilation</em>, said the calm part of Ruth’s mind, which was the part that had passed the exams. <em>Local gradients across the room. The boundary’s gone fractal. The core potential is spiking. We are in a region of⁠—</em></p>\n<p>“Keystone,” she said out loud, and ran.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Keystone was set into the floor at the far end of the Crown, under a hinged plate of brushed steel that nobody ever opened. The plate had words on it. Every engineer who had ever worked the Crown knew the words by heart, the way you know the words on a gravestone you walk past every day, without ever really reading them.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>Nobody knew what the last line meant. The consensus in the canteen was that Iris Hollowell had been a genius and a mystic and had lived on coffee and cigarettes and that geniuses were allowed to be cryptic on brass plates. The Clause itself was classified above Ruth’s level. What she knew was this: under the plate was a lock, and behind the lock was the only mechanism in the Draw that did not feed the Lesion but ended it, and the lock took one key, and the key was the Minister’s, and tonight it was hanging around the neck of a man in a good jacket who had wanted a round number.</p>\n<p>She dropped to her knees and wrenched the plate up. Beneath it, black keelstone, a smooth recessed housing, and in the middle of it a keyhole the shape of a narrow cross.</p>\n<p>“Crane.”</p>\n<p>He was standing where he had been standing, in the best light. The light was different now. It came in through the window in a shifting white-violet surf, and it lit him from every side, and his face in it looked very young and very frightened.</p>\n<p>“Give me the key.”</p>\n<p>“What will it do?”</p>\n<p>“It will close the Lesion.”</p>\n<p>“Close it.” He said it as if it were a word in a foreign language. “Close it how? For how long? We can’t — Ruth, the Minister⁠—”</p>\n<p>“There won’t be a Minister. There won’t be a Draw. Look out of the window, Aldous. There won’t be a <em>city</em>.”</p>\n<p>Something happened to his face then, and she thought later — much later, when she’d had more time than anyone should have to think about one face — that it was the moment he understood, and that what he understood was not that the world might end but that if he gave her the key, the thing he had been sent here to film would be the last day of the Hollowell Draw, and that it would be his name on it. She thought he was not a monster. She thought he was simply a man who had lived his whole life inside the Continuance and could not, in that second, imagine the outside of it. It is hard to give away the thing that holds up the sky.</p>\n<p>“There must be another way,” he said.</p>\n<p>“<em>Give me the key.</em>”</p>\n<p>He put his hand on it. He did. She saw his fingers close around the chain. She saw him start to lift it over his head.</p>\n<p>The gantry beneath the window buckled.</p>\n<p>A single thread of the Lace — as fine as a hair, as bright as a filament in a bulb — had reached the outer frame of the Crown. Where it touched, the keelstone did not break, because nothing broke keelstone. It <em>aged</em>. A strip of the frame as wide as her hand went grey, then white, then sagged like wax, and the grated walkway along the window, which was bolted to that frame, tore free along a third of its length and dropped half a metre with a shriek of metal.</p>\n<p>Crane was standing on the walkway.</p>\n<p>He fell against the rail. The chain around his neck caught on something — a bolt head, a twist of torn grating — and snapped. Ruth watched the key go. She watched it with the same terrible slowness as the coffee cup, and knew as she watched that it was not slow for the key; it was only slow for her. It turned over once in the white-violet light. It slid between the bars of the grating. And it fell — not down, toward the Vault floor, where things ought to fall — but <em>up and out</em>, toward the window, as if the Lesion’s light were the true ground and it was dropping home. The glass did not stop it. She never understood that part. It was on one side of the window, and then it was beyond it, a small black speck tumbling into the Lace, and then it was not anywhere.</p>\n<p>Crane made a sound she would hear for years.</p>\n<p>She was still on her knees by the Keystone. She looked down, stupidly, at the empty keyhole. Then she looked up.</p>\n<p>There was a man at the Keystone.</p>\n<p>He was kneeling where she was kneeling. That was the only way she could put it afterwards. He was kneeling <em>where she was</em>, in the same place, and she could see him perfectly, and he was not touching her. He was dressed in close grey, like a diver, like something worn under armour, and he had one hand on a small wheel she had never seen — a wheel that was not there, that the housing did not have — and around his wrist was a cord tied in a knot. His face was turned toward the window. It was an ordinary face, tired and lined and unshaven, and it was completely calm. It was the calmest face she had ever seen. He looked like a man who had been there for a very long time and could stay there for longer.</p>\n<p>Then he turned his head and looked at her.</p>\n<p>It lasted — she never knew. Less than a second. He looked at her as if he knew her. As if he were sorry for her, and glad of her, both at once.</p>\n<p>Then there was no one there.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Tally! Tally, the lift!”</p>\n<p>Fen had her by the arm. He had her by the arm, and then he had her by the arm again, a moment later, as if he’d had to do it twice. Everyone was moving now, in stutters, in slurs. The camera operator was dragging Crane back from the edge of the torn walkway; his face was grey and wet. Dieter had his board open and was yanking fuses out by hand as if that would do anything, as if anything would do anything.</p>\n<p>“We have to go down,” Fen said. “We have to go down <em>now</em>, the Lattice is — the whole — Ruth, if it touches the spindle⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” she said. “Yes. Evacuate the Crown.”</p>\n<p>They went. Eleven people and a camera. The lift was at the end of the gallery, a steel cage on a rack-and-pinion rail that ran four kilometres down the inside of Spindle One, and it held twelve, and they got in, and Ruth did not. She stood at the gate.</p>\n<p>“Ruth⁠—”</p>\n<p>“One second.”</p>\n<p>On the wall by the lift, behind a small glass panel, was a red lever with a black-and-yellow collar. Every room in the Draw that had controls also had one. The engineers called it the Dead Man’s Hammer. Pulling it did only one thing: it released a flywheel in the station’s deep structure that drove an iron hammer against a keelstone anvil, once per second, for as long as the flywheel turned — and the flywheel had been built to turn for days. It was the oldest alarm in the Draw, older than the electronics, older than the Ministry, a thing Iris Hollowell had insisted on over every objection. It meant one thing and one thing only, and every soul in the station would understand it, even if every screen and every speaker in the place was dead.</p>\n<p>It meant: <em>nobody is at the controls</em>.</p>\n<p>Ruth broke the glass with her elbow and pulled it.</p>\n<p>For a moment nothing happened. Then, deep below them, somewhere down in the black body of the station, something enormous let go.</p>\n<p>She stepped into the lift. Fen slammed the gate. The cage dropped.</p>\n<p>Through the lift’s mesh she saw the Crown go up and away from her — the torn walkway, the empty Keystone with its plate thrown back, the window full of spreading light — and then the black inner wall of the spindle, rushing. In her hand she was holding, she discovered, the station logbook, which she had no memory of picking up. She held it against her chest. Beside her, Aldous Crane was weeping silently with his eyes open. The camera operator had her arms around him. The young man with the tablet was staring at the tablet, which showed a clock, which was counting upwards faster than any clock should.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy</em>, thought Ruth. <em>Maddy, Maddy, Maddy.</em> She had the absurd conviction that if she said the name enough times, fast enough, she could make it reach her.</p>\n<p>Then, from far below, up through the rails and the cage and the soles of her boots and the bones of her legs, came the first stroke of the hammer.</p>\n<p>It was not a sound. It was too deep, and too long, and too slow, for a sound. It came up through the spindle like a tide coming in under a door. It lasted, and lasted, and lasted.</p>\n<p>Afterwards — for the rest of her life, and for the lives of her children, and their children, and theirs — they would call it the Bell.</p>\n<p>She looked at her watch. It said 02:14. It had taken nine minutes. It was the last time, for the rest of her life, that her watch and the world would agree.</p>"}, {"file": "01-the-standby.md", "label": "Chapter 1", "title": "The Standby", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": true, "words": 5493, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The Hollowell Draw is experiencing a minor margin irregularity. There is no danger to the public. Citizens are reminded that calm is care.</p><span class=\"attr\">Office of Public Calm, bulletin 1, 04:30, Continuance Day</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">By twenty past six Casimir Hale had counted fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty strokes of the hammer, and the phone had still not rung.</p>\n<p>He was sitting on the edge of his bed in the dark with his boots on. He had put the boots on at about a quarter past two, when the first stroke came up through the building like a truck driving over the roof of the world, and he had put on his trousers and his good shirt and his coat, and then he had sat down to wait, and he had been sitting ever since. At some point he had begun counting. He wasn’t sure why. It was the kind of thing a man did when he had spent fifteen years being paid to wait for a phone call and the call had finally come and it had not come by phone.</p>\n<p>The phone lay on the bedside table next to a glass of water he hadn’t drunk. It was a heavy black Ministry handset with no screen and a single button, and it had been issued to him on his first day as Standby with a small ceremony in an office on Tier Two, and in fifteen years it had never once made a sound. He dusted it on Sundays. He had once, drunk, tried to call his own number on it and found that it did not make outgoing calls. It was not a phone, really; it was a promise, or a threat, or a leash, and it had sat there by his bed for fifteen years like a small black animal that might one day wake up.</p>\n<p>Fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty-one.</p>\n<p>The hammer did not sound like a sound. It sounded the way it would feel to be very small and live inside a drum. Every second, precisely, the whole building shuddered — the window glass hummed, the water in the untouched glass flinched into a ring, the cutlery in the kitchen drawer gave a small metallic cough — and then there was a second of silence, and then it came again. The flat was on Tier Nine, a hundred metres above the ground and four kilometres from the rim of the Pit, and at this distance, through all that concrete and air and sleeping city, it should not have been possible to feel anything that happened inside the Draw at all.</p>\n<p>Cas knew what it was. He had known at 02:14, sitting up in bed with his heart going. He’d have known it in his sleep. There was a picture of that hammer in the second chapter of every textbook on vacuum engineering ever printed, and a caption underneath in Iris Hollowell’s own words: <em>When all else has failed, the station will say so.</em></p>\n<p>Fourteen thousand, seven hundred and sixty-two.</p>\n<p>At twenty-five past six there was a knock at the door.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There were two of them on the landing, a man and a woman in the charcoal overcoats of the Office of Public Calm, and behind them, a little apart, a third man who was older and softer and wore no overcoat at all, only a brown cardigan buttoned wrong.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” said the soft man. “I’m so sorry to wake you.”</p>\n<p>“You didn’t.”</p>\n<p>“No.” The man’s eyes went over him, the boots, the coat, the good shirt. Something that might have been approval, or pity, moved in his face. “No, I don’t suppose we did. Ansel Gaunt. We’ve never met, but I’ve read a great deal about you. Shall we?”</p>\n<p>The car was long and black and warm and smelled of ginger biscuits. There was a paper bag of them on the seat between them and Gaunt offered it to Cas and, when Cas shook his head, took one himself and ate it carefully over a handkerchief spread on his knee. The two in overcoats sat up front. The car pulled out into the lane and the hammer came up through its wheels and its seats and Gaunt’s biscuit, and Gaunt closed his eyes briefly each time it did, the way a man might wince at a toothache he had decided not to mention.</p>\n<p>“We didn’t phone,” said Gaunt, “because phones are recorded.”</p>\n<p>“By you.”</p>\n<p>“By us, yes. Which is precisely the problem. Things that are recorded have a way of becoming history. Things that aren’t recorded have a way of becoming⁠—” he searched for the word, dabbing a crumb from his lip “⁠—negotiable. The Minister would like this morning to remain negotiable for as long as possible.”</p>\n<p>“How long have you known?”</p>\n<p>“About the irregularity?”</p>\n<p>“About the Draw.”</p>\n<p>Gaunt looked at him mildly. “Since two-fourteen, Dr Hale. Like everyone else in the Pit district who was awake, or who was woken. It’s been rather hard to miss.” A pause. “If you mean when did we know that it was <em>serious</em> — I’d say we’re still finding that out. Which is why you’re in my car.”</p>\n<p>They came out of the lane onto the Tier Nine arterial and the city opened up around them.</p>\n<p>Averna at dawn on Continuance Day. Cas had lived in it all his life and it still, sometimes, stopped his breath. Ahead and above, the Stacks rose in their terraced ranks, tier on tier on tier, a hundred and forty levels of housing and gardens and plazas climbing into the grey, lit from within like vast lanterns, and above the highest of them the faint milky curve of the Canopy, the weather shield that had held back sixty years of storms. Down at street level the Continuance Day bunting was already up — blue and gold, THE FUTURE WILL PAY on every banner — and the public speakers on the lampposts were playing the Tending anthem on a soft loop, the children’s choir version, very sweet. There were people out, a lot of people for this hour. They were walking in the ordinary way, carrying the ordinary bags, and they were not talking, and every one of them, Cas saw, flinched very slightly once a second.</p>\n<p>“There’s been no announcement?”</p>\n<p>“There’s been a bulletin. Minor irregularity. No danger to the public. Calm is care.” Gaunt took another biscuit. “People are being very good. People are always very good at first.”</p>\n<p>The car turned south, toward the Pit. They passed a plaza with a Quota clock on a pillar in the middle — a big public display, the kind every district had, showing the Draw’s output in real time in tall gold numerals. Cas had grown up with those clocks. As a boy he’d stood in front of the one on Tier Four and watched the numbers climb on Deepening Day and cheered with everyone else. It said 00.00 TW. As they passed, a man in a Ministry jumpsuit on a ladder beside it reached up and switched it off, and it went dark, and a small crowd that had gathered to look at it stood looking at the dark instead.</p>\n<p>“The Minister felt,” said Gaunt, “that the number was unhelpful.”</p>\n<p>“Zero?”</p>\n<p>“The number before zero. For about forty minutes after two-fourteen the clocks showed an output of a hundred and nine terawatts. Then they started showing numbers that aren’t numbers. Then zero.” He folded his handkerchief. “I’m not a physicist, Dr Hale. But I can read a room, and I read a hundred and nine terawatts, and I read the faces of the engineers in the crisis room who read it, and I thought: I’ll go and fetch the man who wrote the memo.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“The Margin Review,” said Gaunt gently. “Year Seventy-Six. Section four, the projections. You were the junior author. I’ve read the original. Not the version that was published. The original, the one with your name on it and Dr Adeyemi’s. It had some very striking phrases.” He looked out of the window. “’A structure of obligation that cannot, even in principle, be discharged.’ I remember thinking that was a remarkable sentence to find in a technical report. It sounded like scripture.”</p>\n<p>“Adeyemi wrote that.”</p>\n<p>“Did he? He’s dead now, of course. Natural causes.” Gaunt said it without emphasis, the way you’d say a man was tall. “And you signed the revised version. The one that said the Margin was stable.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Nobody made you.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“No.” Gaunt seemed to think about that for a while. “That’s the thing I’ve never understood. Most of the people I talk to in my job, I can tell you exactly what made them. A child. A debt. A photograph. But you, Dr Hale — nobody lifted a finger. We simply asked, and you signed, and we gave you a flat and a phone and you sat by it for fifteen years like a good dog.” He turned from the window and met Cas’s eyes, and his own were very tired and very kind. “I’m not saying it to hurt you. I’m saying it because I want you to understand that I know exactly who you are. It will save us both time.”</p>\n<p>The hammer came up through the car.</p>\n<p>“How long,” said Cas, “until we get there?”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>You saw the Pit before you saw the Draw. You always did — it was fourteen kilometres across and three deep, a crater dug out of the old industrial belt in the Year Minus Six, and the Draw sat in the bottom of it like a vast black egg in a bowl. From the rim, on a normal morning, you saw the Collar first: a ring of converter halls and admin blocks and trunk-line heads, lit up and smoking, with the great superconducting lines marching away from it over the city on pylons like the legs of enormous insects. Behind the Collar, the dark bulk of the Works. Behind the Works, the dome.</p>\n<p>This morning, from the rim, you could hardly see it at all.</p>\n<p>The air over the Pit was moving. That was the only way to put it. It shimmered, the way air shimmers over a road on a hot day — except that it rose in a column, a clear, trembling pillar at least ten kilometres wide, going straight up into the grey dawn until it was lost against the Canopy. Through it the Draw looked wavering and uncertain, as if seen through water. The edges of things kept coming apart and knitting back.</p>\n<p>And there were birds. Hundreds of them — gulls, starlings, the fat grey Averna pigeons that lived on the Stacks — circling above the rim in a great slow wheel. None of them flew over the Pit. They came to its edge and turned away, came and turned away, around and around, like water circling a drain it would not go down.</p>\n<p>Their car stopped at a cordon of Civil Defence vehicles and Calm Office cars on the rim road. The hammer was much louder here: a vast, dull, full-body blow, once a second, that made the gravel on the road jump. A Civil Defence sergeant waved them through, and as they passed her Cas saw her glance at her wristwatch and then at a clock on the dashboard of her truck and frown.</p>\n<p>“The clocks near the rim run fast,” said Gaunt, who had seen him see it. “Twelve minutes fast, at the cordon, since two. They were eight minutes fast at five. It’s creeping out. Nobody seems able to tell me whether that’s important.”</p>\n<p>“It’s important.”</p>\n<p>“I thought it might be.” Gaunt took off his cardigan, folded it carefully, and laid it on the seat. Underneath he was wearing a very good grey shirt. “The Minister’s waiting.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The crisis room was in the Collar’s admin block, on the fourth floor, in a long room that had been built as a conference suite for foreign delegations. It still had the gold carpet and the portraits: Iris Hollowell, severe and hollow-cheeked, with a cigarette she’d been painted without; the first Minister of Continuance, cutting a ribbon; a sweeping mural of the Vault seen from the Crown, the eight spindles rising to the Lesion, the Lesion a gold star with painted rays. Someone had pushed the delegate tables together and covered them with screens and paper and cold coffee, and about twenty people were standing around them in the stunned, overlit way of people who had been awake all night doing something that wasn’t working.</p>\n<p>Cas knew about half of them. Engineers, mostly — Collar shift leads, a couple of the Works directors, a woman from the Lattice office he’d been at university with who looked at him across the room, went white, and looked away. People who had been told at some point, by someone, that the Standby was not somebody you talked to. He’d got used to that look. He’d seen it on the faces of old friends in the street.</p>\n<p>At the head of the table stood Ondine Rask.</p>\n<p>The Minister of Continuance was a tall, spare woman in her sixties with silver hair cut close to her skull and a dark suit without a crease in it. She had been Minister for nineteen years. She had presided over the First Deepening, and the export treaties, and the Canopy’s extension over the southern coast, and Cas had seen her face on every public screen in the city since he was a young man. It was said she slept four hours a night. It looked, this morning, as though she had slept none, and it looked as though she had decided that this was nobody’s business.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” she said. “Thank you for coming.”</p>\n<p>As though he’d had a choice. He nodded.</p>\n<p>“You’ll forgive me if I don’t waste your time. We have had a Continuance Event.” She said the phrase with no irony at all. “At approximately 02:05 this morning, during a scheduled test, Tensioner Six in the Lattice lost its seat. The interlocks failed to compensate. Since that time we have been unable to re-establish control of the Lesion, the Lattice, or the Rollover. We have no communication with the Crown or with any part of the station inside the Works. We evacuated the Collar at 03:10. At around the same time we lost the output. The Commonweal grid is running on reserve and on the old fusion plants at Tessarine and Kovač, which were not built to carry it, and which will begin to fail within eighteen hours. Those are the facts.” She put both hands flat on the table. “I have been assured by every engineer in this room that the situation can be recovered, and I have been assured by every engineer in this room that they do not know how. Colonel Gaunt tells me you might.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>She blinked.</p>\n<p>“Why does Colonel Gaunt tell you that,” said Cas. “I haven’t been inside the Draw in fifteen years. I haven’t been allowed to read a Lattice report since Year Seventy-Six. Everyone in this room knows the station better than I do now.”</p>\n<p>“Because,” said Gaunt, from the doorway, where he had settled himself on a gold chair with his hands folded, “everyone in this room has spent the night telling the Minister what they think she would like to hear, and I have some small professional experience in noticing when people are doing that. Whereas you, Dr Hale, have spent the last fifteen years with nothing whatsoever to lose.” He smiled at Rask. “It’s a rare quality, Minister. I’d use it while it lasts.”</p>\n<p>Rask looked at Gaunt for a long moment, then back at Cas.</p>\n<p>“What do you need?” she said.</p>\n<p>“Everything you’ve got. Starting with the last thing the Crown sent.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They gave him a chair and a screen and the Lattice woman — her name was Petra Imre, she’d sat behind him in second-year field theory and once lent him a pencil — brought him the telemetry without looking at him, and he went through it, and the room went on around him in a hush.</p>\n<p>It didn’t take long. That was the worst of it. Afterwards he’d think that if it had taken longer, if he’d had to hunt, he might have had time to get used to it on the way.</p>\n<p>The drone logs first. At 04:40 they had sent a survey drone into the Works through Gate Nine. It had flown for forty minutes by the Collar’s clock, and for nearly all of them its own clock had kept pace — running a little fast, a few minutes in the hour, the way the clocks at the cordon were running fast. Except for ninety seconds. Halfway through the flight the gravity had shoved it off its line and across a disused hall, and it had drifted within a few metres of something, and in those ninety seconds of the Collar’s time its clock had gained ten hours. The camera showed what it had passed. A thin line of white-violet light crossing the hall, fine as a hair on a lens. For ten hours of footage the frame held the hall and the line and nothing else, while a coil of cable on the floor beneath it went brown, and then orange, and then to powder. Then the drone drifted clear, and its clock slowed to a few minutes fast again, and it came home with eleven hours and six minutes on it and a battery that had lived every one of them and was dead.</p>\n<p>He knew why it had been ninety seconds and not forty minutes. It was the first thing they taught you about the Draw, in the first week of the first year, with a lamp and a sheet of card. Fast time spreads out from a thread the way light spreads from a lamp: in open air it thins with distance, and thins fast, so that a few metres off you’d hardly notice it. But it doesn’t like matter. Put something solid in its way and the solid takes the difference — all of it, fast on one face and slow on the other, the whole step squeezed into the thickness of the wall. Glass does it. Steel does it. Keelstone does it better than anything in the world, which was what the Lattice was <em>for</em>: a dam of keelstone round the core, holding the core’s time back the way a dam holds a lake, so that for ninety-one years the Crown’s clocks and the city’s had agreed to within a few seconds a day. There was a placard on every wall inside the Draw — he’d walked past hundreds of them, on placement — that said DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE. Most people who read it thought it was about the gravity.</p>\n<p>Then the Crown. The last telemetry burst before the link failed, 02:05:31. Most of it was screaming numbers — output, tensioner loads, core potential off every scale anyone had drawn. And one small, dry line at the bottom that the instruments computed automatically from the shape of the Lesion’s boundary, a figure that had read <em>2.000</em> — a perfect sphere, a clean, smooth edge — every second of every day for ninety-one years.</p>\n<p>It read <em>2.73</em>.</p>\n<p>He looked at it for a while. He was aware of the hammer. He was aware of his own hands on the table, which were very still.</p>\n<p>“What is it?” said Rask.</p>\n<p>“The boundary dimension.” He heard his voice from a small distance. “It’s a measure of the — it’s how rough the edge of the Lesion is. How much surface it has. A smooth sphere is two. A cloud is about two and a half. It’s gone to two point seven.”</p>\n<p>“And that means?”</p>\n<p>“It means the edge isn’t an edge any more. It’s fractal. It’s broken into⁠—” He moved his hand. He thought of frost on a pane. “Into edges, on edges, on edges. Infinitely. A boundary with no smooth part anywhere on it.”</p>\n<p>“Can it be repaired?”</p>\n<p>“It isn’t <em>damaged</em>, Minister. It’s⁠—” He stopped. He looked round the table. All those faces. They were looking at him the way people look at a doctor who has just come in from reading the scan.</p>\n<p>“I need to explain something,” he said, “and I need you to let me finish, and I need nobody in this room to call it an irregularity while I’m doing it.”</p>\n<p>Rask’s face didn’t move. “Go on.”</p>\n<p>“The Draw doesn’t make energy. Everyone in this room knows that. It borrows it. The Lattice holds a region of space below its ground state — below zero, below empty — and the difference is what we sell. The vacuum lends it to us, and the vacuum always gets it back. That’s not a theory, that’s not Hollowell, that’s field theory, it’s as old as the Long Heat. You can make a patch of negative energy. You can’t keep it. It has to be paid back by a pulse of positive energy, and the payment is always <em>more</em> than the loan. The longer you hold it, the bigger the payment. They called it quantum interest, before any of us were born.”</p>\n<p>“We are all aware of the principle of the Rollover, Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>“Then you’re aware that the Rollover isn’t a solution. It’s a refinancing.” He heard his voice sharpen and didn’t stop it. “Hollowell’s trick was to keep borrowing to pay the interest on what we’d already borrowed. Push the payment forward. Push it forward again. Ninety-one years. Every year a little more of the output goes back into the Lattice just to keep the loan from coming due, and every year the loan is bigger, and every year we deepened the draw to make up the output, which made the loan bigger. We were feeding it seventy percent. The Review projected it would pass a hundred percent inside twenty years — the Draw would be eating more than it made, just to stand still. We’d have had to feed it from the grid. For ever. That was always the shape of it. That was the <em>best case</em>.”</p>\n<p>“That report,” said Rask, very quietly, “was revised.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. By me.” He held her eyes. “It didn’t change the physics.”</p>\n<p>Nobody in the room was moving.</p>\n<p>“The Rollover’s gone,” he said. “Six unseated, the Lattice lost the geometry, and the boundary broke. There’s no mechanism holding the payment back any more. The debt’s coming due. Ninety-one years of it, with interest, and the interest compounds. It will be paid in one pulse, at the core, all at once.” He looked down at the screen, at the numbers, and did the arithmetic in his head because he had done it a hundred times, fifteen years ago, at night, on the backs of envelopes. “Order of ten to the twenty-six joules. The impact that killed the dinosaurs was about ten to the twenty-three and a half. So call it two or three hundred of those. At once. Here.”</p>\n<p>It was Petra Imre who spoke first. “When?”</p>\n<p>“The boundary’s still evolving. The clocks at the cordon are running faster; that tells me the core potential’s still off the scale and still shifting. When it starts to fall, the debt’s being called in. When it falls far enough⁠—” He made himself say it plainly. “Two days. Three at the outside. Maybe less.”</p>\n<p>Someone at the far end of the table sat down on the floor.</p>\n<p>“Evacuation,” said a Works director, a heavy man called Brask whom Cas vaguely remembered as a rugby player. “We evacuate the city⁠—”</p>\n<p>“To where? Six hundred million people, in two days. And it wouldn’t matter. It’s not a bomb. It’s the planet. The first second takes Averna. The shock and the heat do the rest. You could be on the far side of the world and it would find you in the first hour.”</p>\n<p>“Bury it,” said somebody else. “Collapse the Pit. Pour⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You can’t bury a debt.”</p>\n<p>“Then what,” said Rask, “do you propose?”</p>\n<p>And there it was. He’d known it was coming since the car. Maybe since 02:14. Maybe since Year Seventy-Six. He found that he was very calm.</p>\n<p>“There’s a mechanism in the Crown,” he said. “Hollowell designed it. It’s in the original schematics, and it’s in her last paper — the one that was withdrawn — and it’s called the Clause. It isn’t a repair. It doesn’t save the Draw. It ends it. The theory is that if the boundary ever failed badly enough — and it has — the same distortions that are going to kill us could be turned around and used to fold the core shut. Seal the whole thing. Debt and payment together. In a⁠—” he hesitated over her phrase “⁠—in a moment that doesn’t have a future. I don’t fully understand it. I don’t think anyone alive does. There’s a line on the Keystone plate that nobody’s ever explained. <em>The sealing requires a witness.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Hollowell’s final paper,” said Rask, “was withdrawn because it was unsound.”</p>\n<p>“It was withdrawn because it described how to turn the Draw off.”</p>\n<p>The silence went on for several strokes of the hammer.</p>\n<p>“The Clause is locked,” Cas said. “It takes the Minister’s key.”</p>\n<p>For the first time, something flickered in Ondine Rask’s face.</p>\n<p>“The key,” she said, “was in the custody of Deputy Minister Crane. Who is on site.”</p>\n<p>“On site.”</p>\n<p>“Deputy Minister Crane was attending the test. As the Ministry’s representative.” She said it perfectly evenly, and every engineer in the room looked at the table. “He is currently unaccounted for, along with the Crown crew and approximately nineteen hundred members of the night shift who were in the Lattice and the Vault at the time of the event.”</p>\n<p>Nineteen hundred.</p>\n<p>Cas had been trying not to do the next piece of arithmetic. He did it anyway. He found that he was doing it out loud, softly, the way he used to talk himself through proofs in the library at night.</p>\n<p>“The clocks at the cordon are twelve minutes fast after four hours,” he said. “That’s out here, where it’s had kilometres of open air to thin out in. The drone went within a few metres of a single thread and gained ten hours in ninety seconds.”</p>\n<p>He looked down at his hands.</p>\n<p>“And the Vault’s behind the dam,” he said. “There’s nowhere in there for it to thin out. The Vault’s sealed. If the core potential is as high as the Crown’s numbers say — and it’ll have been highest at first, right after the rupture, before the debt started to be called — then everywhere inside the Lattice will be running at the core’s speed, near enough. It isn’t hours. The ratio could be tens of thousands. Hundreds of thousands.” He looked up. “Minister, if anyone survived in there — if anyone was sheltered, if they had air and water — then in the four hours since two o’clock, they’ve lived⁠—”</p>\n<p>“That will do, Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>“⁠—years. Decades. By the time we get anyone in there, they could have been alive in there longer than the Commonweal has existed⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>That will not be repeated outside this room.</em>”</p>\n<p>It was the first time she’d raised her voice. It rang off the gold walls. In the doorway, Gaunt had closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>Rask straightened. She took her hands off the table.</p>\n<p>“We have,” she said, “three suits.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They were called keel suits. He had heard of them, the way you hear of the cathedrals in cities you’ll never visit: a Lattice maintenance programme from the Year Seventies, cancelled in the Eighties, three prototypes built in the Tallow Works labs and never used. Suits designed to let a human being work inside the Lattice’s gradients, where the pull of the tensioners and the push of the Lesion would crush a person like a paper cup. They were said to be almost unimaginably expensive. Each one had a sliver of keelstone at its heart, spun up like a gyroscope — keelstone, the hyperdense stuff the Lattice itself was made from, which cost more per gram than anything else on Earth because only the Draw could make it.</p>\n<p>“They’ll let a team get through the Works,” Rask said. “Possibly into the Lattice. Possibly further. Our engineers believe that from the Crown, with the proper sequence, Tensioner Six could be reseated manually. If Six is reseated, the Lattice can re-establish the geometry. If the geometry is re-established, the Rollover can resume. The Draw continues.” She said the last two words with a peculiar emphasis, as though laying them down on the table between them like a card. “Can Six be reseated from the Crown, Dr Hale?”</p>\n<p>He thought of the number at the bottom of the telemetry. 2.73. He thought of a tensioner rotor the size of a building sitting off its bearing in a Lattice that no longer had a shape to hold it. He thought of the Keystone.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>It was astonishingly easy. It always had been. That was the thing about lies in the Commonweal: there was never any resistance. You opened your mouth and they slid out like a coin into a slot.</p>\n<p>“Good.” Rask nodded, once. “Then you’ll lead the technical side. Civil Defence will lead the team. You’ll leave as soon as the suits are ready.” She turned her head. “Major Lisk.”</p>\n<p>He hadn’t noticed the woman by the window. She’d been standing in the corner behind the delegate screens with her arms folded and her back to the mural, watching, and she came forward now with the slightly rolling gait of someone who had spent a long time on boats or in boots. She was in her fifties, broad and weathered, with cropped iron-grey hair and a face like a well-used tool. The Civil Defence badge on her shoulder had been sewn back on by hand at least once. The ribbon bar under it went down two rows.</p>\n<p>“Teodora Lisk,” she said, and held out her hand.</p>\n<p>He shook it. It was a hard, dry hand with a missing fingertip.</p>\n<p>She didn’t let go at once. She held on a moment and looked at him — up, down, back up — with an expression he couldn’t read. It was the look, he thought afterwards, that a person gives a ladder before climbing it: not unfriendly; simply trying to work out how much weight it would take.</p>\n<p>“You’re the one who wrote the thing,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I’m the one who wrote the thing.”</p>\n<p>“Hm.” She let go. “Well. Congratulations on being right.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Later — an hour later, maybe two, after the plans and the arguments and a call to the Tallow Works that went on for forty minutes and ended with somebody in the lab crying — he found himself alone at the end of the corridor outside the crisis room, standing at a window.</p>\n<p>The window looked south over the Pit. From up here he could see the Draw properly: the Collar, the dark jumble of the Works, the dome rising out of the middle like the back of some enormous animal asleep in the crater. The shimmer rose over it, vast and clear and trembling. The birds wheeled at its edge. The hammer came up through the floor and through the glass under his fingers, once a second, once a second, and he found he had started counting again without meaning to. Twenty-one thousand and something.</p>\n<p>He thought about nineteen hundred people.</p>\n<p>He thought about a pen. He could remember it exactly: it had been a Ministry pen, blue, with the Continuance flame on the clip. He’d noticed at the time that it was a good pen, that it wrote smoothly. That was the thing he remembered most about the most important moment of his life — that he had noticed the pen was nice.</p>\n<p>He had never told anyone that. There’d never been anyone to tell.</p>\n<p>Down in the Pit something shifted in the shimmer, a great slow ripple running upward like a wave through a curtain. The birds broke their circle and scattered and came back.</p>\n<p>Two or three days. A pulse at the core. And three suits, and a lock that nobody had the key to, and a line on a brass plate that nobody understood.</p>\n<p>He should be afraid. He knew that. He examined himself for fear, the way you’d pat your pockets for keys, and found — not nothing. Something. But what he mostly found, when he looked, was a kind of settling. A quietness. The way a room goes quiet when the last guest leaves.</p>\n<p><em>At last</em>, some part of him said. He didn’t like the part of him that said it. He didn’t argue with it either.</p>\n<p>It was almost a relief.</p>"}, {"file": "02-keel.md", "label": "Chapter 2", "title": "Keel", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 5827, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>The keel is not a shield. It will not stop the water. It will keep you upright in it.</p><span class=\"attr\">MSE Type 4 Operator’s Handbook (draft, unpublished), Tallow Works Laboratory</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The suits arrived at the Collar at a quarter to eleven, on the back of a Civil Defence flatbed, lashed upright in their cradles under grey tarpaulins, so that as the truck came slowly down the ramp into the Gate Nine maintenance hangar they looked like three tall figures being brought to an execution with sacks over their heads.</p>\n<p>A young man was sitting on the flatbed with them, holding on to one of the cradles with both arms. When the truck stopped he didn’t let go. He stayed there, hugging the cradle, while the driver got out and the hangar crew came forward with the forklift, and he looked round the hangar with enormous eyes — at the Calm officers by the door, at the Civil Defence people, at Teo Lisk with her arms folded, at Cas — and said, to no one in particular, very fast:</p>\n<p>“Hi. Hello. I’m Ben. Benedek. Farr. I’m the — I built — well, I didn’t <em>build</em> them, a lot of people built them, but I built the keels, I mean I did the rotor bearings, and the housings, and the spin controllers, and most of the firmware, and I’m the only one left in the lab who still knows how they go together, because everyone else was reassigned when the programme got cut, and I wasn’t, because nobody remembered I was there, which was, honestly, the best four years of my life. And someone phoned the lab at seven o’clock this morning and said you need three people to go into the Draw, and I said who’s going to maintain the suits, and they said well, who <em>can</em>, and I said, well, me, I suppose, and they said right then.” He took a breath. “So I’m coming. I’m coming? I’m coming. Right. Good. Fine.” Another breath. “Is there a form?”</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk walked over to the flatbed, looked up at him, and held out a hand.</p>\n<p>“Come down, son,” she said. “There’s always a form.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He was twenty-six. He had a narrow, clever, anxious face, and hair that stood up at the back where he’d been running his hands through it, and he talked — Cas would learn over the next twenty hours — the way a pressure valve leaks: continuously, and more when the pressure went up. When he was working, though, he went quiet. When he stripped the tarpaulin off the first cradle and put his hand flat on the suit inside it, he stopped talking altogether, and stood there for a moment as though he were checking for a pulse.</p>\n<p>They were not beautiful. Cas had half expected them to be. They were squat and grey and massive, armoured in overlapping matte plates like the shell of some deep-sea crustacean, with thick ribbed joints at knee and hip and elbow and a helmet that was mostly visor, a wide dark curve of glass. On each back, where a diver would carry tanks, sat a rounded housing the size of a beer keg, sealed and bolted, with a band of small indicator lights around its waist. Someone had painted a number on each housing by hand in white paint: <em>1</em>, <em>2</em>, <em>3</em>. The paint on the <em>3</em> had run a little before it dried.</p>\n<p>“That’s the keel,” said Ben. He had his hand on the housing of number one. “Inside there’s a rotor. Keelstone. Eleven kilos of it, in a disc about so big.” He made a circle with his hands the size of a dinner plate. “Two millimetres thick. Doesn’t sound like much. It isn’t the weight, it’s what it does when you spin it. Keelstone’s — you know what keelstone is?”</p>\n<p>“Collapsed matter,” said Cas. “Pressure-stabilised by the Lattice fields when it’s made. About ten times denser than lead.”</p>\n<p>“Right, right, sorry, you’d know, you’re the — sorry.” Ben flushed. “So the rotor spins. Fast. Really, really fast. Two hundred thousand revs a minute at full, and the rim’s doing — well, you don’t want to know what the rim’s doing, the housing’s rated for it but only just. And when you spin something that dense that fast, it drags spacetime round with it. Frame-dragging. Tiny effect normally. With a keelstone rotor it’s not tiny. It gives you a sort of bubble, about a metre across, where the metric’s — smoothed. Stirred flat. Like putting a paddle in a river so the water round you goes one way instead of every way at once.” He looked at Teo, then at Cas, then back at the suit, and spoke to the suit. “In the Lattice, where the gradients are — you know. If you walk into a region where your feet weigh more than your head, or where your feet are living faster than your head, the bubble spreads it out. Averages it over the metre. So instead of being torn in half, you’re just — very uncomfortable.”</p>\n<p>“How uncomfortable?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Ben considered this seriously. “Have you ever been in a car crash?”</p>\n<p>“Three.”</p>\n<p>“Like the fourth one, but slower.”</p>\n<p>Teo grunted. It might have been a laugh.</p>\n<p>“The bubble’s only about a metre. Two people could share one if they were⁠—” Ben made a small squeezing gesture with his hands “⁠—closer than is professional. The suits are built for one. They’re heavy. They’ve got exoskeletons, the exo will carry most of it, but you’ll feel like you’re wearing a cathedral. Power cells for the life support and the exo and the electronics are good for about four days. The keel doesn’t need power once it’s spinning. The bearings are magnetic, there’s almost no friction — sat on the rig with nothing to push against, it’d spin for months. It’s the <em>work</em> that slows it. Every time the bubble has to hold something steep, it costs spin. Hold a lot, and it runs down fast. As it slows, the bubble shrinks. You’ll hear it. It hums. When the hum drops, that’s you running out of keel.”</p>\n<p>“And spinning it up,” said Cas, “takes⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Fourteen hours.” Ben winced. “Sorry. It’s a lot of mass. If you try to spin it faster you crack the housing, and then you’ve got eleven kilos of keelstone doing two hundred thousand revs on your back with nothing to hold it, and that’s⁠—” He stopped. “That’s a bad day. That’s a very, very bad day.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked up at the clock on the hangar wall. It said 10:58. His wristwatch said 10:51. The hangar was a kilometre inside the Collar; the time out here was already slipping.</p>\n<p>Fourteen hours. They’d go in before dawn. Hour twenty-five, or thereabouts. He tried to think of what the hour-twenty-five version of the Vault might be like, if the ratio was what he thought it was, and decided not to.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took Ben and the hangar crew until one o’clock to get the suits out of the cradles and onto the spin rigs — three low steel frames bolted to the hangar floor, humming with power cables — and when the rotors finally started to turn it was so gradual that at first there was no way to tell. Then, gradually, there was a sound. A long, low, rising tone, so deep you felt it in your back teeth before you heard it, the three suits singing in almost-unison under the hangar lights. The note crept upward very slowly. Ben said it would keep rising all night.</p>\n<p>Cas stood and listened to it. He would hear that note for the rest of his life.</p>\n<p>Across the hangar, by the big doors, Teo Lisk was talking to Ansel Gaunt.</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t hear them. The hum and the hammer between them drowned it out. Gaunt was doing the talking, softly, with his head bent and his hands folded in front of him like a priest, and Teo was listening with her arms crossed and her face shut. At one point Gaunt took something small out of his cardigan pocket and held it out to her, and after a moment she took it and put it in the breast pocket of her fatigues. Then Gaunt patted her arm, and went out, and Teo stood there alone in the doorway looking out at the dark mass of the Works for a while before she came back.</p>\n<p>When she passed Cas, she didn’t meet his eye.</p>\n<p>He filed it. He had spent fifteen years watching people not meet his eye; he’d got good at filing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The afternoon was drills.</p>\n<p>They put the suits on for the first time at three o’clock, with the keels barely turning, and Cas found out what Ben had meant by a cathedral. You didn’t so much put on a keel suit as get admitted into it. The liner came first — a close grey one-piece in some slippery, cool fabric, wired with sensors and a web of fine tubes for heat and water, that fitted like a diver’s wetsuit and made all three of them look, as Teo put it, like very depressed seals. Then the suit opened down the back like a clam, and you stepped in, and it closed around you with a series of heavy locking thunks, and for a moment you could not move at all, and then the exoskeleton woke up and took your weight and you stood up inside it and the world became a narrow bright picture framed in a dark curve of glass.</p>\n<p>It was slow. Everything was slow. The exo took a quarter-second to decide what your legs meant before it did it; walking felt like wading. On his back the keel’s hum came through the suit’s frame into his spine like a second, larger heartbeat. In his ears, the helmet’s speakers carried Teo swearing, steadily and inventively, as she tried to pick up a spanner in her gauntlet and sent it skittering across the floor for the ninth time.</p>\n<p>“Don’t grab,” said Ben’s voice. “It’s got a grip-assist. It’ll close harder than you mean it to. Just think about touching it.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>am</em> thinking about touching it.” Teo bent — the whole suit bent, with a long hydraulic sigh — and closed her gauntlet on the spanner for the tenth time, very slowly, like a woman lifting a sleeping cat.</p>\n<p>There was a short, flat crunch.</p>\n<p>She straightened up and held it out to them. The spanner had folded in the middle like a sandwich.</p>\n<p>“…Or that,” said Ben. “That’s the grip-assist. That’s — yes. It’s quite a lot of grip.”</p>\n<p>“Is this coming out of my pay?”</p>\n<p>“It’s the Ministry’s. I really don’t think they’re counting spanners today.” He was quiet for a second, and then, because he apparently couldn’t stop himself: “Everybody does one. I did a coffee mug. And a technician’s hand, a bit. He was fine. He was mostly fine.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at the folded spanner. Then she laid it down on the floor, very carefully, as if it might go off, and picked up the next one as though it were an egg. It stayed a spanner.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Ben, so relieved that Cas nearly smiled.</p>\n<p>They walked the length of the hangar and back. They climbed a ladder and came down it. They practised falling — the suits went over like felled trees and got up again with a hydraulic grunt — and practised hauling each other up, and practised the hand signals for when the radios failed, which Ben said they would, sooner or later.</p>\n<p>“The electronics hate gradients,” he said. “If a chip’s got one end living faster than the other end, its clocks go out of sync and it just — it gets confused. The keel helps, it smooths it over the bubble, but the bubble’s a metre and the Lattice is — well. The electronics will go first. The radios, the displays, the recorders. The life support’s mostly mechanical, the exo’s got a manual fallback, the keel’s purely mechanical once it’s spun. So you’ll keep breathing and walking. You just won’t be able to <em>talk</em> about it.”</p>\n<p>“Sounds like working for the Commonweal,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas found that he’d laughed. It came out of him in the suit, surprised, and he heard it come back to him over the radio, and he realised he couldn’t remember the last time he had laughed out loud at something another person had said. He couldn’t remember the last time there had been another person.</p>\n<p>Teo glanced across at him through two layers of visor. He couldn’t see her expression. But she didn’t say anything else for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At seven in the evening they ate.</p>\n<p>The Collar canteen had been evacuated at three in the morning in the middle of the night-shift meal, and nobody had been back. It was a long, low room with windows onto the Works, and the tables were still set: trays of food gone cold and stiff, cups with skins on them, a half-finished game of cards laid out on a table by the door. Somebody had been celebrating a birthday. There was a cake on the counter with most of its candles still in it and a paper crown lying beside it, and a card propped against the cake that said <em>HAPPY 40TH OSWIN — THE FUTURE WILL PAY (FOR THE CAKE)</em>.</p>\n<p>Nobody touched the cake.</p>\n<p>They found tins in the storeroom and heated them in the kitchen and ate at the table by the window, still in their liners, with the Works outside going on and on in the dark — cooling towers, gantries, the black cliff of a rectifier hall with red warning lights crawling across it — and the shimmer standing over all of it in the floodlights, and the hammer, and the hum of the keels from the hangar.</p>\n<p>Ben had a napkin. He’d been drawing on it since the soup.</p>\n<p>“Right,” he said. “Right. So I’ve been doing the numbers. On our survival odds.”</p>\n<p>“Oh, good,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“No, listen, it’s interesting. So. Okay. Let’s say the Works is about as bad as the drone said, which means the gravity’s doing weird things but the keels can handle it. Let’s say ninety percent for the Works. That’s generous but let’s say. Then the Lattice, we don’t know, but the keels were <em>designed</em> for the Lattice, so let’s say seventy. Then whatever’s past the Lattice⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The Shear,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up. “The what?”</p>\n<p>“If the core potential’s as high as I think, the Lattice is holding the whole difference between the core’s time and ours. Where the Lattice is solid, the difference sits inside the keelstone, and that’s fine; nobody’s walking through keelstone. But there are holes in it. Conduits, cooling trunks — the ways in. In a hole, the whole difference has to happen in the air. Over a few tens of metres, maybe. The time rate changing very fast over a very short distance.” He moved his hand, flat, across the table. “A shear layer. Like the boundary between two currents.”</p>\n<p>Ben thought about that. He wrote something on the napkin. “Okay. The Shear. Let’s say fifty. For the Shear. And then the Vault, and then the Crown, and then whatever you do in the Crown⁠—” He looked at Cas again, and something in his face was very young. “What <em>are</em> you going to do in the Crown, Dr Hale? Reseat Six? Can you actually⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Keep going with your sum, Ben.”</p>\n<p>Ben looked at him a moment longer. Then he looked down and kept going. “So — the Crown, let’s call it fifty, fifty for whatever. And then we have to get <em>back</em>, and that’s all of it again, in reverse, so you square it.” He did a fast calculation. “Which comes out at about — two percent. Each. Roughly. I mean, that’s very rough, the error bars are — well, the error bars are basically the whole napkin.”</p>\n<p>There was a short silence.</p>\n<p>“Two percent,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Roughly.”</p>\n<p>“Well.” She pushed her plate away and sat back and put her boots up on the chair beside her. “That’s better than Tessarine.”</p>\n<p>“What was Tessarine?” said Ben.</p>\n<p>So she told them. The Tessarine floods, Year Seventy-Nine, the spring the Canopy failed over the southern delta for eleven hours and the storm surge came up the river mouth and put six metres of water through the lower tiers of the city in forty minutes. She’d been a lieutenant. Her unit had gone into the Tier Two underpass with ropes and a rubber boat to get people out of a flooded Line station, and the water had kept rising while they were down there, and they’d had to swim out through the ticket hall in the pitch dark with the current pulling at them and twenty-three people on the ropes behind them, and they’d come out into the street and the street had been a river.</p>\n<p>“I did the numbers on that one too,” she said. “Afterwards. On the odds. It came out at about one in a hundred for the unit. We lost two.” She shrugged. “Numbers are for afterwards. You go in, you do the job, you do the numbers in the pub, and then you get very drunk.”</p>\n<p>“Did you get any of them out?” Ben asked. “The people?”</p>\n<p>“Twenty-one.” She looked at the window. “Two of them let go.”</p>\n<p>Ben folded his napkin in half, and in half again, and put it in the pocket of his liner.</p>\n<p>It was quiet for a while. The hammer came up through the table. The coffee in their cups shivered into rings, and settled, and shivered.</p>\n<p>“Why are you here, Hale?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>He looked at her. She was watching him over the rim of her cup, perfectly relaxed, and her eyes were not relaxed at all.</p>\n<p>“I’m the Standby.”</p>\n<p>“I know what you are. I’ve read your file. Gaunt gave me your file.” She didn’t look away. “I asked why.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He tried to think of an answer that was true and that he could say out loud in front of a twenty-six-year-old boy with a two-percent napkin in his pocket, and there wasn’t one.</p>\n<p>“Because it’s my job,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo held his eyes for a second longer. Then she snorted softly and looked back out of the window.</p>\n<p>“That’s what,” she said. “Not why. But all right.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Afterwards, there were the letters.</p>\n<p>Nobody said they were going to do it. It just happened, the way these things did. Ben went off to a corner of the hangar with his tablet propped against a toolbox and sat on the floor in front of it and talked to it for a long time, very quietly. Cas didn’t try to listen, but the hangar echoed, and every now and then a phrase came across to him between the hum and the hammer. <em>⁠— and the flat’s in both names so you won’t have to —</em> and <em>⁠— your mum’s recipe, I know I always said it was too salty —</em> and then, near the end, his voice breaking and going on anyway, <em>⁠— Jonah, I’m sorry, I’m sorry, I’m really sorry, I should’ve said yes to the dog.</em></p>\n<p>Teo wrote. She sat at a workbench with a Civil Defence notepad and a pencil and wrote two letters in a small square hand, and folded them, and wrote a name on each: <em>Mattias. Joss.</em> She put them in an envelope and wrote on the envelope, and put it in her breast pocket, next to the small thing Gaunt had given her.</p>\n<p>Cas sat at the other end of the bench with a sheet of paper in front of him for an hour and wrote nothing.</p>\n<p>He tried. He tried to write to Sabine and got as far as her name and couldn’t think what came after it that she didn’t already know. He tried to write to Nell and found that everything he wanted to say to her was either a lie or so large that it didn’t fit on paper. In the end he folded the blank sheet up and put it in his pocket, and took out instead the thing he’d brought from the flat without really thinking about it — a battered hardback sketchbook with a black cloth cover, a quarter full of old equations and older drawings — and turned it over in his hands.</p>\n<p>Then he went and found a phone.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Sabine answered on the eighth ring.</p>\n<p>“Cas.” A pause. Behind her, a lot of noise: voices, children, a loudspeaker saying something about blankets. “Where are you? I’ve been trying your — they said your number was⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m at work.”</p>\n<p>A silence. He could hear her understanding it.</p>\n<p>“At <em>work</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Where are you? What’s that noise?”</p>\n<p>“We’re in a school. A sports hall. Tier Thirty. They came round the whole of Nine at lunchtime and said we were being relocated for our comfort. For our <em>comfort</em>, Cas. They said it was a precaution. There’s four hundred people in here and one toilet.” Her voice dropped. “Everybody’s being very calm. It’s horrible. Nobody’s saying anything and everybody keeps flinching. Cas, what is that? That noise. That — thing. It’s been going since two in the morning. Is it the Draw? Is it⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Is Nell there?”</p>\n<p>“Cas⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Please, Sabine.”</p>\n<p>A breath. Then, away from the phone: “Nellie. Nell. It’s Dad.”</p>\n<p>A scuffle, a clatter, and then Nell’s voice, very close and very loud, the way she always talked on the phone, as if she thought she had to shout to make it reach.</p>\n<p>“Dad! Dad, we’re in a <em>gym</em>!”</p>\n<p>“I heard.”</p>\n<p>“There’s a girl here called Pip who’s got a lizard. A real one. It’s in a box. It’s called Dennis.” A breath. “Dad, are you at the Draw?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Is it broken?”</p>\n<p>He closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “It’s broken.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a second. She was ten. She had her mother’s grey eyes and his own inability to leave a question alone, and she collected stars. She had never seen one. No one her age in Averna had. The city’s light and the Canopy’s glow had wiped them from the sky before she was born, before he was born, and she had got interested in them anyway, the way some children get interested in dinosaurs — because they were enormous and far away and gone. She had glow-in-the-dark stars on her bedroom ceiling in their proper constellations, placed with a chart and a ruler, and she knew their names the way other children knew football teams. Betelgeuse. Deneb. Fomalhaut. She said them like spells.</p>\n<p>“Pip’s brother says,” she said carefully, “that if the Draw is broken, the lights will all go off. Like, <em>all</em> of them. Everywhere.”</p>\n<p>“They might.”</p>\n<p>“So we’d see them.”</p>\n<p>It took him a second. “The stars?”</p>\n<p>“If the lights all went off. If it was dark. We’d see them. Wouldn’t we? Properly?”</p>\n<p>“I suppose you would.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>knew</em> it.” She sounded so pleased. “I told Pip. He said they’d have gone. He said they probably weren’t even there any more. I said they’re <em>always</em> there, you just can’t see them.”</p>\n<p>“You’re right.”</p>\n<p>“I know I’m right.” A pause. Then, in a smaller voice: “Dad, are you going in there? Into the broken bit?”</p>\n<p>The hammer. The hum. Across the hangar, Ben still talking to his tablet with his forehead in his hand.</p>\n<p>He had been going to lie. He had the lie ready. <em>No, sweetheart, I’m just here to look at the numbers, I’ll be home soon.</em> It was sitting in his mouth, smooth and round, and it would have slid out of him like all the others.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said instead. “Tonight. Very early. With two other people.”</p>\n<p>“Is it dangerous?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Are you scared?”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “I’m very scared, Nell.”</p>\n<p>There was a long pause on the line. He listened to her breathe. He wondered what he’d just done — whether it was the worst thing he could have done to her or the only true thing he’d said to anyone in fifteen years, and whether those were different.</p>\n<p>“That’s allowed,” Nell said at last. She said it firmly, the way Sabine said things, the way she’d been told it herself, probably, in a sports hall, an hour ago. “Mum says that’s allowed. You’re allowed to be scared, you just have to do it anyway.”</p>\n<p>“Your mum’s right.”</p>\n<p>“Dad.” She’d lowered her voice to a whisper, as though telling him a secret. “When you’re in there. Will you look for stars?”</p>\n<p>“Nell, there aren’t any stars in there. It’s a power station. It’s all⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You don’t <em>know</em> that,” she said. “You haven’t been. You don’t know what’s in there. There might be.”</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. He shut it again.</p>\n<p>“All right,” he said. “I’ll look.”</p>\n<p>“Promise.”</p>\n<p>“I promise.”</p>\n<p>“Okay.” Satisfied. Then, abruptly, over her shoulder, “<em>Mum</em>, Dennis is out⁠—” and a clatter and a shriek and Sabine’s voice, distant, and then Sabine back on the line, breathless.</p>\n<p>“Cas? Sorry. A lizard. Cas, what did you tell her? She’s — she looks⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I told her the truth.”</p>\n<p>A long silence.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Sabine eventually. “That’ll be a first.” But her voice had gone strange, and when she spoke again it was very quiet. “Cas. Come back. Do you hear me? She needs you to come back.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” he said. And then, because it was the only true thing left, “I’m sorry, Bina.”</p>\n<p>He put the phone down before she could answer.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Continuance Day address went out at nine that night, fifteen hours late.</p>\n<p>They watched it on the canteen screen. The First Director of the Commonweal stood in Hollowell Square in front of the dark Quota clock with the city’s lights behind him and spoke for eleven minutes about sacrifice and endurance and the Tending, and about a minor technical irregularity at the Draw which was being handled with the utmost skill and would be resolved in good time, and about the future, which would pay. Then they played the footage. The Crown, the Lesion like a gold star through the window, engineers at their boards in crisp white coats, a triumphant needle swinging over. The numbers climbing. The crowd in the square cheering.</p>\n<p>“That’s from Year Eighty,” said Ben. “The First Deepening. My dad took me. I was fifteen, I thought it was the best day of my life. That’s — look, that’s the old console layout, they changed that in Eighty-Five.” He stared at the screen. “They’re showing old footage. They’re showing old footage and pretending it’s tonight.”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything. On the screen, the gold star shone steady and perfect. Outside the window, over the Works, the shimmer rose into the dark. Once a second, the whole canteen shook.</p>\n<p>Teo got up and turned the screen off.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At eleven Ben showed Cas the relay pucks.</p>\n<p>They were small, flat, hockey-puck-shaped things in orange casings, a box of forty, and Ben had been fiddling with them for hours at a workbench under a lamp while the keels sang behind him.</p>\n<p>“Radio repeaters,” he said. “We drop them as we go. They’ll bounce signal back through the Works to the Collar. And to each other. Only — here’s the thing, here’s the thing I realised this afternoon, it’s actually really interesting⁠—” and he was off, with his hands, the way he’d been off about the keels. “If there’s a time gradient between two radios, the signal gets stretched. Like, if you’re somewhere where time’s running slow and I’m somewhere where it’s running fast, then from where I am, your voice comes through slowed down, because it’s — you’re talking at slow-time speed, right? So from my side a one-second word from you takes, I don’t know, a minute to arrive. It gets redshifted, it gets smeared out. And the other way round, if I talk to you, I’m talking at fast-time speed, so I get squashed — it arrives all at once, a whole speech in a blip. So I’ve written some code that buffers everything and re-times it. It records the whole stretched-out signal, however long it takes to come in, and then when it’s finished it plays it back at the right speed. So we’ll still be able to understand each other. It just might take a while.”</p>\n<p>“How much of a while?”</p>\n<p>Ben shrugged. “Depends on the ratio. Ten to one, it’s fine, it’s like a bad phone line. A thousand to one, and your one-minute message takes⁠—” he did it in his head “⁠—seventeen hours to get to me. Doesn’t matter though. It’ll still get there.” He grinned tiredly. “If one of us ends up in fast time, the others’ll sound like whales. Big slow whale noises for hours. And then the suit goes <em>ding</em> and it’s you, talking normally, saying ‘Farr, you idiot, why did you bring the pucks in the wrong box.’”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the forty orange pucks. He thought about a ratio of tens of thousands. A one-minute message taking weeks to arrive. A voice spread so thin over time that it would come through as a single long unbroken note, like the hum of a keel.</p>\n<p>“That’s good work, Ben,” he said.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up at him, surprised, and then flushed right up to the roots of his standing-up hair.</p>\n<p>“Thanks,” he said. “I mean. Yeah. Thanks.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>A little after midnight Cas went and stood by the spin rigs.</p>\n<p>The note of the keels had climbed all night and was now high and pure and steady, a single sustained chord that filled the hangar the way light fills a room. The three suits stood in their frames in a row, grey and hulking, <em>1</em>, <em>2</em>, <em>3</em>, with their indicator bands glowing a steady green, and they looked, in the dim hangar with the Works dark beyond the doors, like three knights keeping a vigil in a chapel. He supposed that was what they were doing. He supposed that was what all three of them were doing.</p>\n<p>Teo came and stood beside him. She had a mug of something in each hand and she gave him one. It was tea, stewed black, with an unbelievable quantity of sugar in it.</p>\n<p>“Civil Defence tea,” she said. “It’s mostly a structural material.”</p>\n<p>They stood and drank it. The hammer went on.</p>\n<p>“I’ve got two boys,” Teo said, after a while, to the suits. “Mattias and Joss. Twenty-four and twenty-one. Mattias is an electrician in Kovač. Joss is at the university here, doing something with computers I don’t understand. Their father died eleven years ago. Heart.” She drank. “I used to tell them, when they were small and they were frightened of something — the dark, the dentist, whatever — I used to tell them, you do the thing, and you do it scared, and then it’s done, and the scared goes away and the done stays. That’s the deal. Scared goes, done stays.”</p>\n<p>“Nell’s mother tells her something like that.”</p>\n<p>“Smart woman.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.” He looked into the tea. “She is.”</p>\n<p>Teo was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“You told your girl the truth,” she said. “On the phone. I heard. Sorry. This place echoes like a church.”</p>\n<p>He had no answer to that.</p>\n<p>“Good,” said Teo. That was all. Then she knocked back the rest of her tea, and wiped her mouth with the back of her hand, and went off to try to sleep on a pile of tarpaulins in the corner.</p>\n<p>He stood there a while longer. The keels sang. Out in the dark, beyond the doors, the shimmer stood over the station like a ghost of a column, like a tower made of nothing.</p>\n<p><em>You don’t know what’s in there</em>, said Nell in his head. <em>There might be.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At three in the morning — hour twenty-five, by the count that had become the only count that mattered — Ben pronounced the keels spun.</p>\n<p>At half past three they suited up. The liner, cool and close; the clam of the suit closing around them, thunk, thunk, thunk; the exo taking their weight. The keels were at full now and the note came into Cas’s spine like a held breath. The world was a bright curve of visor. In his ears, the suits’ radios hissed and clicked, and then Ben said, “Comms check, one,” and Teo said, “One, loud and clear, unfortunately,” and Ben said, “Two,” and then, “Three?” and Cas said, “Three.”</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “Right. Let’s go and fix the fucking world.”</p>\n<p>They walked. Out of the hangar, down a service corridor, the three of them in a line, heavy and slow, their boots booming on the steel floor. The Collar was empty. Every door stood open. Every screen was on, showing the same message on a blue background — <em>MARGIN IRREGULARITY: REMAIN CALM</em> — and every light was on, and there was nobody, and their footsteps echoed down corridors that went on and on.</p>\n<p>At the end of the last corridor was Gate Nine: a great steel door five metres high, painted in hazard stripes, set in a black wall. A small group was waiting in front of it. Rask, very straight, in the same dark suit. Gaunt, in his cardigan, with his hands folded. Petra Imre from the Lattice office, who came forward and, without saying anything, put her hand flat on the chestplate of Cas’s suit for a second, and took it away, and stepped back with her eyes wet. A Civil Defence sergeant at the controls of the gate.</p>\n<p>Nobody made a speech. Cas was grateful for that. Rask looked at each of them in turn, and nodded once, and said, “The Commonweal is grateful,” and it was so obviously the wrong thing to say that it was almost moving.</p>\n<p>“Open it,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>The sergeant pulled a lever. Somewhere in the wall a motor began to grind.</p>\n<p>Gate Nine rolled back.</p>\n<p>Beyond it was the Works, dark and vast and humming, going on forever. Warm air came out of it, and a smell of ozone and hot metal and something green and rank like pondwater. The light inside was wrong — a sort of faint, shifting, pearly glow that seemed to come from nowhere in particular.</p>\n<p>And the hammer, which had been a sound for more than a day, was not a sound any more. In here it was simply the world. It came up through the floor and into the suits and through the suits into their bones, vast and slow and absolute, and all three of them stopped on the threshold as though they’d walked into a wall.</p>\n<p>Once a second. Once a second. Once a second.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Ben’s voice, small and brave in Cas’s ear. “That’s not ominous at all.”</p>\n<p>They went in.</p>"}, {"file": "03-the-works.md", "label": "Chapter 3", "title": "The Works", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 6458, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>SECTION 9 — IN THE EVENT OF CONTAINMENT LOSS: REMAIN AT YOUR STATION AND AWAIT RELIEF. DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE. RELIEF CREWS WILL BE DISPATCHED. YOU WILL NOT BE FORGOTTEN.</p><span class=\"attr\">Hollowell Draw emergency placard, standard issue, all levels</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The placard was the first thing they saw.</p>\n<p>It was screwed to the wall just inside Gate Nine, at head height, where every worker coming on shift for ninety-one years would have walked past it: a sheet of yellowed laminate in a steel frame, the words printed in the blocky black typeface the Ministry had used in the Year Twenties, with a little diagram of a stick figure standing beside a stick-figure console with its stick hands at its sides. The laminate had gone cloudy with age. Somebody, at some point, had drawn a moustache on the stick figure in marker pen, and somebody else had scrubbed most of it off.</p>\n<p>Teo stopped in front of it. Her helmet light moved slowly down the lines.</p>\n<p>“’You will not be forgotten,’” she read. “They’ll put that on my headstone.”</p>\n<p>“They’ll put it on everyone’s,” said Ben.</p>\n<p>The gate ground shut behind them. The sound of it closing was very final. Then there was only the hammer, and the three notes of the keels, and their breathing in the suits.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Cas had been inside the Draw many times, in another life. As a student on placement; as a junior on the Lattice survey team; as a member of the Margin Review, walking the Works with a clipboard behind Dr Adeyemi, who had been sixty-eight and walked everywhere very fast with his hands in his pockets, stopping every so often to lay his palm flat on a wall and listen. He had thought he remembered it. He had thought that fifteen years of not being allowed in had simply made it smaller in his mind, the way the house you grew up in shrinks when you go back.</p>\n<p>He had forgotten nothing. He had simply never understood it.</p>\n<p>The Works went on for four kilometres between the Collar and the Lattice, and in all that distance there was not one thing that had been built as part of a plan. Hollowell’s original station had been a sphere — the Lattice, the Vault, the Lesion — and a thin shell of machinery around it. Then the Commonweal had grown hungry, and every decade since, the Ministry had wanted more, and every decade the engineers had given it more the only way they could: by adding. Rectifier halls to convert the Draw’s raw output, and more rectifier halls when the first ones couldn’t keep up. Coolant galleries. Bioscrubber halls full of algae tanks to eat the carbon the crews breathed out, because it was cheaper to grow the air than to pump it four kilometres. Transformer vaults, cable tunnels, lift shafts, a spinal column of superconducting trunk lines as thick as a house. And all of it jammed in wherever it would go, on top of, around, and through everything that had been there before, until the whole thing had become less like a building than a reef — a vast, dim, dripping, humming structure that had grown by accretion for ninety-one years in the dark.</p>\n<p>Their route ran inward along what the maps called Radial Nine. The maps were optimistic. For the first hour it was a corridor, wide and high and lit by emergency strips at floor level, and they walked it in single file with Teo in front and Ben in the middle and Cas at the back, the keels humming, dropping an orange relay puck every two hundred metres. Then the corridor ended at a stair that went down into a transformer vault the size of a cathedral nave, full of grey steel cabinets in rows like pews, and they crossed it, and on the other side the corridor carried on at a different level, in a different typeface, painted a different colour. The signs changed every few hundred metres. RADIAL 9. R-9 (OLD). RADIAL NINE — WORKS SECTOR 4 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL. WORKS SECTOR 4 WAS HERE → with an arrow, in faded chalk, pointing at a wall.</p>\n<p>Once, early on, they passed the door of a bioscrubber hall: a great grey pressure bulkhead in a yellow hazard frame, with a narrow personnel lock beside it and a brass maker’s plate riveted to the steel. HAMMOND CONTAINMENT SYSTEMS. Ben patted it as he went by, the way you’d pat a dog you didn’t trust.</p>\n<p>“We had one of those on the keel test cell,” he said. “Worst door ever made. It only opens from the inside, so nobody out in the corridor can let the algae out by accident. And you have to bleed the hydraulics before it’ll crank, and it doesn’t tell you that, it just sits there looking at you. I lost a whole afternoon to one. I kicked it. It won.”</p>\n<p>“Did you get it open?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Eventually.” He sounded pleased with himself. “I know all its secrets now. We’re very close.”</p>\n<p>The smell was ozone and hot oil and algae and a faint sweetish undernote of rot, and the sound was the hammer, and under the hammer, everywhere, the Works itself: a vast layered murmur of machines that had not been told to stop. Pumps still pumping. Fans still turning. Somewhere far off, a relay clicking open and shut, open and shut, like a finger tapping on a desk. The rectifier halls were still live — there was power in them from somewhere, the Lesion’s leaking output finding its own way through the old conductors — and as they passed the doors they could hear the halls singing, a high, thin, electrical choir.</p>\n<p>And there was the light. The faint pearly glow Cas had seen from the gate was everywhere in here, in the corridors and halls and stairwells, sourceless and shifting. It came through gaps in walls and under doors. It lay on the floor in pale drifts like moonlight. When he held up his gauntlet in front of his visor, his hand cast no shadow.</p>\n<p>“Where’s that coming from?” said Ben.</p>\n<p>“The core,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Through four kilometres of — through all <em>this</em>?”</p>\n<p>“Not through it. Around it.” He didn’t really know how to explain it. He wasn’t sure he understood it himself. “The geometry’s bent. There are paths for light in here that shouldn’t exist. Some of it’s getting out along them.”</p>\n<p>“That’s⁠—” Ben started, and then, for once, didn’t finish.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The first gravity squall hit them in the second hour.</p>\n<p>They were in a long service gallery with pipes along both walls, walking on a grated catwalk above a drop into darkness, and Teo, in front, said, “Hold on⁠—” and then the floor tipped.</p>\n<p>It didn’t. The floor stayed exactly where it was. But <em>down</em> moved. Cas felt it go the way you’d feel a boat begin to roll: a slow, sickening swing, his weight sliding sideways inside the suit, the whole world leaning, and then the far wall of the gallery was below him and he was falling toward it. He grabbed. The suit’s gauntlet closed on the catwalk rail harder than he meant it to, crushing the steel, and held. His legs swung out. For a long moment he hung there, the three of them hanging there, dangling from a handrail over a wall that had become a floor, while the pipes around them groaned and a loose toolbox went tumbling past, end over end, and hit the far wall, and slid along it, and fell into the dark.</p>\n<p>Then down swung back. Slowly, like a pendulum. They dropped to their knees on the catwalk.</p>\n<p>“<em>Christ</em>,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Everybody okay?” Ben’s voice was high. “Everybody — is the keel — your keels okay? Check your — the indicator bands, are they⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Green,” said Cas. “Green. I’m fine.”</p>\n<p>“Green,” said Teo. “What the hell was that?”</p>\n<p>“Gravity weather.” Cas got up. His legs were shaking inside the exo; the exo held them. “The Lattice is still there, it’s still pulling, but it’s lost its shape. And the Lesion’s pushing. And every piece of keelstone in the Works is pulling in its own direction. They’re all out of balance. It’ll come in gusts.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Gusts.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And shears. And lees. Places where they cancel out and it’s calm.” He looked back along the gallery. “We should expect more.”</p>\n<p>They did get more. Over the next hours the Works taught them its weather the hard way. There were gusts like the first, where down swung sideways or backwards or, once, for three terrible seconds, straight up, so that they found themselves pressed against the ceiling of a stairwell looking down at the stairs. There were places where weight simply piled on — a stretch of corridor where each step was heavier than the last until the exo was groaning and Cas’s knees were on fire even inside it, and then, abruptly, it was gone. There was a stairwell where, for three steps in the middle, gravity doubled, and they learned to take those three steps at a run. There was a long, wide turbine hall where down was very slightly <em>forward</em>, so that crossing it they seemed to be walking downhill on a floor that was dead level, the three of them leaning backward against nothing like people walking into a gale.</p>\n<p>They learned to watch for it. Loose things were the tell. A cable swinging when there was no breeze. Dust drifting across a floor. Teo started carrying a handful of washers in her gauntlet and dropping one every few metres, watching where it fell.</p>\n<p>And their clocks began to drift.</p>\n<p>Each suit had a small display at the bottom of the visor with two times on it: OUTSIDE, fed by a signal from the Collar through the relay pucks, and LOCAL, the suit’s own clock. At the gate they’d kept step, second for second. An hour in, LOCAL had gained four minutes. Two hours in, nineteen. Ben started reading out the ratio every half hour like a man calling depths on a ship.</p>\n<p>“One point one.”</p>\n<p>“One point three.”</p>\n<p>“One point six. We’re living sixty percent faster than the Collar.”</p>\n<p>“Meaning?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“Meaning for every hour we spend in here, only about forty minutes go by out there.” Ben considered this. “I mean. Which is sort of good? We’ve got more time than we thought.”</p>\n<p>“It’s good for the city,” said Cas. “The debt’s on the Collar’s clock. Every hour we spend in here only costs them forty minutes of it.” He looked at the two numbers at the bottom of his visor. “It’s not good for us. The air in these suits is on our clock. So’s the power. So are the keels. So are we.”</p>\n<p>“So we’ve got more time,” said Ben, “and we use it up faster.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. And it gets stronger further in.” Cas looked along the corridor, inward, into the pearly dark. “By the Crown, an hour of the Collar’s could be days of ours.”</p>\n<p>Ben thought about that for a while.</p>\n<p>“That’s either really good,” he said, “or really bad.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Cas.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They found the first of the night shift in the fourth hour.</p>\n<p>They’d seen signs before that. Hard hats in corridors. A jacket hanging on a valve wheel. A lunch box, open, a sandwich still inside it, going green. An emergency locker torn open and emptied. Once, a set of muddy footprints in the dust of a floor that had been dry for years, going the other way, toward the gate, running. They were the prints of people who’d got out. The Collar had counted them: about four hundred had come out through the gates in the first hour after the rupture, from the outer Works, before the gradients got bad.</p>\n<p>The rest hadn’t.</p>\n<p>It was a stairhead, where a stair from the lower levels came up through the floor into a junction of four corridors. The night shift had come up that stair, running for the gate, maybe twenty of them. Cas never knew what had happened exactly. He guessed that it was a shear — a place where the gravity gradient had gone suddenly steep, a cliff of force, and had moved, perhaps only a few metres, perhaps only for a second — and that they had been coming up the stair into the junction at the moment it passed over them.</p>\n<p>They were on the wall.</p>\n<p>That was the only way he could ever put it, afterwards, to himself. They were on the far wall of the junction, the way paint is on a wall. Their shapes were still there. You could see where each of them had been — this one’s arm flung up, this one’s hard hat, the pale sole of a boot. You could see that one of them had been holding someone else’s hand. But they had been pressed into the wall, by a weight no body could bear, with such force and so suddenly that they had not so much been crushed as <em>printed</em>. The wall behind them was bowed inward. The blood was black and had run nowhere, because at the instant it happened there had been nowhere for it to run.</p>\n<p>Ben made a sound and turned away and went down on his knees in the middle of the junction and was sick in his helmet. The suit’s scrubbers caught it and whined. He stayed there, bent over, his gauntlets on the floor, breathing in harsh, wet gulps over the radio.</p>\n<p>Cas stood very still. He found that he was looking at the hand. The two hands. He could not stop looking at the two hands.</p>\n<p>Teo walked past them both.</p>\n<p>She walked right up to the wall. She stood in front of it for a moment. Then she took off her own jacket — she’d brought a Civil Defence field jacket, strapped to the thigh of her suit, for when they reached somewhere she could take the suit off — and unfolded it and reached up and laid it over the part of the wall where the faces had been. It didn’t cover much. She looked around, and found a tarpaulin in a maintenance alcove, and a couple of high-vis vests hanging on a hook, and a dust sheet, and she hung them all. She did it methodically and without hurry, while Ben knelt on the floor being sick and Cas stood staring, and when she’d finished there was a patchwork of cloth on the wall and you could no longer see anything under it but shapes.</p>\n<p>Then she came back and put her gauntlet on Ben’s shoulder.</p>\n<p>“Up you get, son.”</p>\n<p>“I can’t⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You can. You’ll do it scared. Up.”</p>\n<p>He got up.</p>\n<p>Teo looked at Cas through two visors. He couldn’t see her face. He could hear her breathing, over the radio, harsh and slow and very controlled.</p>\n<p>“How many more of those,” she said, “are we going to find?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“Right.” She turned toward the corridor that ran inward. “Then let’s not stand about.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw the filament first.</p>\n<p>It was in a hall they had to cross in the fifth hour — a long, high, gloomy space that had been a coolant-pump hall once, before the pumps were moved, and was now a storeroom, full of pallets and crates and racks of spares going back to the ceiling in the pearly dark. Their route crossed it diagonally. They were halfway across when Cas saw something at the far end and stopped, and said, “Wait,” and the others stopped.</p>\n<p>It was a thread of light.</p>\n<p>It came out of the far wall of the hall about ten metres up and crossed the space in front of them and went into the floor. It was as fine as a hair and as bright as the filament in a bulb — the same white-violet as the Lesion, the colour that wasn’t quite a colour — and it wasn’t straight. It branched. It went a little way and split in two, and each of those split, and each of those, so that what came out of the wall as a single line went into the floor as a fine, spreading fan of threads, a luminous root system, a river delta drawn in light. And where it branched, the branches branched, smaller and smaller, finer and finer, until the eye lost them and they became a sort of glow, a faint shimmering mist of edges around every edge. When he tried to look at any part of it closely, his eye slid off. When he looked away, he could still see it on the inside of his eyelids.</p>\n<p>It made no sound.</p>\n<p>“What,” said Ben, “is that.”</p>\n<p>“Part of the Lesion,” said Cas. He was surprised how steady his voice was. “Part of the boundary. It’s — when it broke, it didn’t break into pieces. It broke into more edge. The edge is growing outward. That’s one of its threads.”</p>\n<p>“It came through four kilometres of⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It doesn’t come <em>through</em> things. It’s not in the Works the way we’re in the Works. It’s where the Works has been — bent.” He didn’t have a better word for it. “Don’t go near it.”</p>\n<p>They didn’t need telling. But they couldn’t help looking. And what they saw, the closer they looked, was worse than the light.</p>\n<p>Around the filament, for a metre or two on every side, the hall was different. The racks it passed near weren’t grey steel any more; they were brown and orange and flaking, rusted through into lace, sagging on themselves, and in one place a whole rack had simply crumbled and lay on the floor in a heap of rust-red powder with the plastic crates it had held collapsed into brittle shards on top. A section of cable tray that ran close to one branch was grey-white and furred. The paint on the wall beside the place where the thread came out had peeled and curled and fallen and lay in drifts. And on the floor, close to the fan of threads, there was a potted plant.</p>\n<p>Someone must have brought it in. A night-shift worker, making the storeroom a bit nicer. A spider plant, the kind that grew in every office in Averna, in a green plastic pot. Except that it had grown. Its pale leaves had gone up in a fountain higher than a man’s head and spilled out across the floor in long runners, and the runners had put down roots in the dust of the floor and grown new plants, and those had grown, and all of it — the whole great sprawling green-white tangle, three metres across — was dead, and dry, and grey, and dusty, as though it had grown for years and years and then withered and then stood for years more after that. In a hall where, by the Collar’s clock, only twenty-nine hours had passed.</p>\n<p>“It’s fast,” said Cas. “Near the thread. Time’s fast.”</p>\n<p>Ben already had his instrument out — a small sensor on a pole that measured the local clock rate — and was holding it out as far toward the threads as he dared, his arm at full stretch. The number on its display leapt as he moved it. Two. Nine. Forty. He took one step closer and it read four hundred.</p>\n<p>“Back up,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>He backed up. The number fell. He lowered the pole very slowly and stood looking at the dead plant.</p>\n<p>“It’s only near it,” said Teo. “A couple of metres. The rest of the hall’s fine.”</p>\n<p>“The hall’s open.” Cas nodded back at the doors they’d come in by. Both of them were standing wide, wedged with fire-stops, the way the night shift must have left them. So was the pair at the far end. “It’s got somewhere to thin out into. The whole Works. It’s like a heater in a room with the windows open — hot near the bars, nothing across the room.” He looked at the thread. “Shut those doors and it’d be a different story.”</p>\n<p>“That thing,” said Ben quietly, “is going to be everywhere, isn’t it. Further in.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>They went round the far side of the hall, as far from the filament as the racks allowed. Nobody talked. When they reached the far door Cas looked back once. From here the thread was just a pale line crossing the gloom, a scratch on the air. It could have been a trick of the light. It could have been nothing. Except that he could see, very faintly, in the glow around its finest branches, that it was moving. Growing. Very slowly branching into itself.</p>\n<p>And as he watched, it jumped. A new fork ran out from one of its branches all at once, fast as frost racing across a pane — a hand’s length in a blink — and then it was back to its slow crawl, as if nothing had happened. He’d seen it do that once before, while they were crossing. Half an hour of crawling. Then a jump.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They found out what Cas had meant about shut doors twenty minutes later.</p>\n<p>It was a locker room — a small one, off a side corridor, the kind a pump crew would change in at the start of a shift. Its door was shut. It was a steel door with a small square window of wired glass at head height, and the window was wrong.</p>\n<p>Ben saw it first. “Why’s it doing that?”</p>\n<p>The glass was moving. Not the pane; the light in it. It rippled, like the air over a road in summer, like the shimmer over the Pit — a slow trembling that never stopped and never went anywhere, held inside a pane of glass the size of a book. All round the door, in a band a finger wide, the paint on the frame had crazed into a fine web of cracks. Nowhere else.</p>\n<p>Teo shone her light through the window.</p>\n<p>The room beyond was a ruin. Everything in it was grey. The lockers had rusted to brown lace and slumped against each other; a bench had folded in half; a jacket on a hook had gone to threads and the threads had gone to powder; and the floor was deep in a soft grey dust that lay over everything like snow. And through the middle of the room, from the ceiling to the floor, ran a single thread of white-violet light, fine as a hair.</p>\n<p>There was a clock on the wall above the lockers. Its hands weren’t moving. They were a faint grey disc.</p>\n<p>Ben had his pole out. He held it up to the door, not touching. “One point seven,” he said. He brought it closer to the window, very carefully, until the sensor was a finger’s width from the pane. “One point seven. One point⁠—” He let the tip touch the glass, just barely, and the display went mad: a string of digits climbing too fast to read, and then a row of dashes. He snatched it back.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the glass,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“<em>What</em> is?”</p>\n<p>“All of it. The whole difference.” He could see it now that he knew what he was looking at: the ripple, the crazed paint, a line drawn round the room in the only place the difference could live. “The thread’s in a shut room. There’s nowhere for the fast time to thin out, so the whole room’s come up to the thread’s speed. And the walls and the door and that window are holding the drop between it and us. All of it. Out here it’s one point seven. A centimetre away it’s whatever the thread is. Hundreds. Thousands.”</p>\n<p>“It’s a voltage divider,” said Ben slowly. He was staring at the window. “Sorry — it’s a circuit thing. Resistors in a line, the biggest drop’s across the biggest resistor. The air’s the wire. The wall’s the resistor. So all of it sits across the wall.” He looked at Cas. “Is that right? Is that stupid?”</p>\n<p>“It’s not stupid. It’s right.”</p>\n<p>“So the Lattice⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Is the biggest resistor there is.” Cas looked along the corridor, inward. “And if there’s a Shear, that’s why.”</p>\n<p>Teo was looking at the door handle.</p>\n<p>“And if somebody opened that,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Then the drop’s got nowhere to be but the doorway. All of it, in the gap. Through whoever’s holding the handle.” He didn’t say what that would do to the hand on the handle. He didn’t have to. “And then out down the corridor, a front of it, thinning as it spreads. You wouldn’t want to be standing where we’re standing.”</p>\n<p>Nobody touched the door.</p>\n<p>“A blister,” said Ben. “It’s a blister. There’s a skin, and all this — stuff — inside, and you mustn’t pop it.” He swallowed. “Sorry. I name things when I’m frightened.”</p>\n<p>“You name things all the time,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“I’m frightened all the time.”</p>\n<p>“Right, then.” Teo straightened. She said it the way she’d have briefed a crew on a flood barrier. “Rules. One: we don’t open a shut room with a thread in it. Ever. Two: we don’t shut ourselves in a room with one.” She looked at each of them in turn. “Anybody not got that?”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything.</p>\n<p>They went on. Cas looked back once at the little square window, trembling in its door, and thought about the placard inside Gate Nine. <em>Do not attempt to traverse the Lattice.</em> People had walked past that sign for ninety-one years thinking it was a rule about machinery. It was a rule about this. There was no way round a drop; you could only spread it out, and nobody had ever built a road long enough to spread out the kind of difference he thought was waiting at the core. Nobody had needed to. The station had been built to run on ordinary time, with the Lattice holding back a few seconds a day.</p>\n<p><em>You will not be forgotten</em>, he thought, and didn’t like how it sounded.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At the end of the fifth hour they found a lee and stopped to rest.</p>\n<p>It was a small control room for a bank of pumps that had long since been removed, a box of a room with a desk and two chairs and a window onto nothing, and when they came into it the gravity just — went soft. After hours of gusts and shears and weights coming and going it was so calm, so ordinary, that all three of them stood in the doorway for a moment, swaying, like sailors come ashore. Teo dropped a washer. It fell straight down and bounced.</p>\n<p>“Oh, thank God,” said Ben, and sat down on the floor.</p>\n<p>They didn’t take the suits off. They couldn’t, here. But they sat, and drank from the suits’ water lines, and ate the paste that came out of the suits’ ration tubes, which tasted, Ben said, like somebody had described chicken to a machine. Their clocks said LOCAL 05:02 — five hours since the gate — and OUTSIDE 29:07, twenty-nine hours since the hammer began. The ratio in here was one point nine.</p>\n<p>Teo sat on the desk. For a while she was silent. Then she reached into the chest pocket of her liner, under the suit’s neck-ring, with two fingers, working carefully in the gauntlet, and brought out something small. She held it up.</p>\n<p>It was a data chip. A little black wafer the size of a fingernail, with the Office of Public Calm’s seal on it in silver.</p>\n<p>“Gaunt gave me this,” she said.</p>\n<p>Ben looked up.</p>\n<p>“Orders,” said Teo. “Sealed. For my eyes. I read them last night.” She turned the chip over in her fingers. “They say that the mission objective is the restoration of Continuance. They say that the technical lead, Dr Casimir Hale, has a documented history of unreliability on the question of Continuance. They say that in the event that Dr Hale attempts any action which would result in — let me get it right⁠—” she half closed her eyes “⁠—‘the permanent default of the Hollowell Draw’, I’m authorised to prevent that action by any means.” She looked at Cas. “Any means. They underlined it.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“You knew,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“I saw Gaunt give you something.”</p>\n<p>“And you didn’t say anything.”</p>\n<p>“I thought you’d tell me. Or you wouldn’t.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him for a while. Then she looked at the chip.</p>\n<p>On the floor of the little control room, along one wall, ran a coolant channel — an open steel gutter about a hand’s width across, with a steady, fast stream of cloudy water racing along it and vanishing through a grating into the dark under the floor. Teo leaned down and held the chip over it.</p>\n<p>“Tell me I’m not going to regret this,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’re probably going to regret this.”</p>\n<p>“Tell me the truth, then.” Her voice was flat. “All of it. Right now. Or I’ll keep it.”</p>\n<p>So he did.</p>\n<p>He told her Six couldn’t be reseated. That it had never been possible — that a Lattice with a fractal core had no geometry left to seat anything in, and every engineer in the crisis room had known it and had said <em>yes, Minister</em> anyway, and so had he. He told her about the Clause, and the Keystone, and that the only thing in the Draw that could stop what was coming would also end the Draw for ever, which was why nobody in the Ministry would say its name. He told her the key had been around the neck of Deputy Minister Aldous Crane when the Lesion broke, and that he didn’t know where Crane was, or whether he was alive, or whether the key still existed. He told her that he didn’t know what the words on the plate meant. He told her that he didn’t know, even if they reached the Crown, even if they found the key, even if they got the Keystone open, whether the thing would work, or whether anyone could survive working it, or whether Hollowell had been a genius or a madwoman or both.</p>\n<p>“So,” said Teo, when he’d finished. “Let me get this right. We’re walking into hell on a maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She dropped the chip into the coolant channel. It hit the water and was gone, whisked away down the gutter and through the grating into the dark, and she didn’t even watch it go. “I hate certainties. They always want something from you.”</p>\n<p>Ben was staring at her.</p>\n<p>“Major,” he said. “Major, that’s — I mean, isn’t that⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Treason? Probably.” Teo stretched, as far as the suit let her, and her joints cracked over the radio. “I don’t give a damn about the Draw, Farr. Never have. I care about the six hundred million people up there who are about to be very dead, and I care about my boys, and I care about getting a drink afterwards with a man who owes me one.” She tipped her visor at Cas. “That’s you, by the way. You owe me one. For the chip.”</p>\n<p>“I owe you one,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Good. I’ll have a large one.”</p>\n<p>Ben looked from one of them to the other. Then he laughed — a thin, shocked, helpless little laugh, like air going out of a tyre — and put his helmet in his gauntlets and kept laughing, and after a while it wasn’t clear that it was laughing any more, and Teo reached over and put her hand on his back and left it there until it stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They moved on at the start of the sixth hour.</p>\n<p>The map said that Radial Nine ran on for another kilometre and a half to the Lattice, but the map also said it went straight through a bioscrubber hall — B-7, one of the big algae halls from the Year Fifties — and when they got to the hall’s outer door the light coming around its edges was brighter than anywhere they had been.</p>\n<p>Ben went ahead, up a short stair to an observation gallery that ran along one side of the hall, and they heard him stop.</p>\n<p>“Dr Hale,” he said. “You should see this.”</p>\n<p>It was a long gallery with a wall of thick glass, looking down into the hall. The hall was enormous — three hundred metres long, forty wide — and filled with algae tanks: great clear columns of water, rank after rank of them, each as tall as a house and full of drifting green, lit from within by their own grow-lamps, so that the whole hall glowed a deep, underwater emerald. Pipes ran between them. Walkways ran between the pipes. It was beautiful in a way nothing else in the Works had been. It looked like the inside of a lung. It looked like the bottom of the sea.</p>\n<p>And along the far wall of it, from floor to ceiling, ran a filament.</p>\n<p>This one was bigger. Not a hair; a vein. And it wasn’t in the hall. It was in the wall — in the thickness of it, under its skin, the way a vein runs under the skin of a wrist: a great branching tree of light glowing up through the grey concrete from inside, brightest where the panels had cracked. Its finest twigs reached almost to the wall’s face and stopped there, pressed up against the inside of it like fingers against a window. In front of it, the first few ranks of tanks had gone strange colours: black, brown, a thick, clotted, vivid green; one had simply cracked and drained and stood empty with a scum of dried grey slime up its sides. Past those, the tanks looked the way tanks should. In the nearest ones, below the gallery, the bubbles rose at the lazy speed that bubbles rise at.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the wall,” said Ben. “It’s not in the room. That’s — that’s good? That’s good, isn’t it. The wall’s holding it.”</p>\n<p>“The wall’s holding most of it.” Cas watched the ruined tanks at the far end. “Some of it leaks through. That end’s fast. This end isn’t.”</p>\n<p>“And if it comes out of the wall?”</p>\n<p>Nobody answered him. Nobody needed to. They’d stood in front of the locker room door half an hour ago.</p>\n<p>Teo was reading the map on her visor. “The route goes through the hall. In by the big door down there, down the south side, out at the far end. Three hundred metres.” She pointed. “The other way round’s the long way. Three hours. Back past the transformer vaults and round — through that weather.”</p>\n<p>Nobody wanted the weather. They’d skirted the edge of it on the way in, a stretch where the floor had gone sideways three times in a hundred metres, and Cas had listened to all three keels labour through every gust. Every hour of that would cost the rotors spin. Spin they were going to need at the Shear.</p>\n<p>“And the hall’s shut,” said Teo. “It’s a bioscrubber. It’s sealed by design.” She looked at Cas. “Rule two.”</p>\n<p>“Rule two,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>They’d be ten minutes crossing it. Ten minutes inside a sealed room with a thread in its wall — exactly the thing they’d said out loud, half an hour ago, that they would never do.</p>\n<p>“Then we open it first,” said Ben.</p>\n<p>They looked at him.</p>\n<p>“Vent it. Before we go in.” He was leaning on the glass, looking down at the hall’s main door at the foot of the gallery stair: a grey pressure bulkhead three metres high, in a yellow hazard frame, with a personnel lock beside it. “That’s a Hammond. That’s the one I kicked. If we crack the big door and leave it open, the hall’s not shut any more, it’s just part of the Works. Then if the thread does come out of the wall, it’s the storeroom. Fast at that end, fine at this end. And we walk down the south side and out, and it doesn’t matter what the thread does.”</p>\n<p>“And to crack the big door,” said Cas, “somebody has to be inside.”</p>\n<p>Ben’s face did something complicated. “Yeah,” he said. “That’s the — yeah. It only opens from inside. That’s the whole point of it. You go in through the personnel lock, and the lock won’t open its inside door till its outside door’s shut, so the hall stays sealed while you’re doing it. Then you walk ten metres to the control pillar, and bleed the hydraulics, and crank. Four minutes. Five. Then it’s open, and you two come through.”</p>\n<p>“Five minutes shut in a room with a thread in it,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“In its <em>wall</em>. It’s in the wall. It’s been in the wall the whole time we’ve been standing here.”</p>\n<p>“Then I’ll do it.”</p>\n<p>“You don’t know the sequence. There’s a thing you have to do with the bleed valve first or it just sits there, I told you, I lost a whole afternoon⁠—” He turned to Cas. “It’s faster if it’s me. It’s a lot faster. And faster’s the point, isn’t it? Faster’s the whole point.”</p>\n<p>They both looked at Cas.</p>\n<p>He was the technical lead. Rask had said so, in a room with gold carpet, and he’d said yes, and this was what it meant. He looked at the tree of light in the wall. He did the arithmetic, because he always did the arithmetic. The thread in the storeroom had crawled for half an hour between its jumps, and most of its jumps had gone nowhere much. This one had sat under its skin of concrete, not moving, for as long as they’d been watching. The chance that it would break out into the air in the next five minutes, rather than the next hour, or the next day, or never — he couldn’t know it. He could guess. He guessed it at one in twenty. Less. Against three hours of weather and a keel’s worth of spin.</p>\n<p><em>It’ll probably be fine</em>, he thought.</p>\n<p>The sentence sat in his head, smooth and round and reasonable. It did not occur to him that anyone had ever thought it before.</p>\n<p>“Five minutes,” he said. “In and out. If anything changes — anything — you come straight back through the lock.”</p>\n<p>“Five minutes,” said Ben, and grinned at them suddenly, hugely, like a boy let out of school. “Right. Right, you absolute antique. Let’s have you.”</p>\n<p>He went down the stair two at a time, his keel humming, his boots ringing on the steel treads, and the outer door of the personnel lock swung open for him, and he went into it, and it swung shut.</p>"}, {"file": "04-blister.md", "label": "Chapter 4", "title": "Blister", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 4704, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>If one of us ends up in fast time, the others’ll sound like whales.</p><span class=\"attr\">Benedek Farr</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">From the gallery they watched him come out of the lock into the hall.</p>\n<p>He went quickly, the way he did everything, the keel’s hum rising and falling in their radios as he moved, his helmet light bobbing between the great glowing columns of the tanks. The control box for the bulkhead’s manual cycle was on a pillar ten metres inside the hall. Ben reached it. They saw him open its cover. They heard him say, “Bleed valve first, you horrible thing,” and heard the squeal of a valve wheel, and then a long, thin hiss.</p>\n<p>“Ninety seconds for the pressure to come off,” he said. “Then crank. Told you. I know all its secrets.”</p>\n<p>Teo let out her breath. Cas watched the wall.</p>\n<p>He was watching it when the filament moved.</p>\n<p>Far off at the other end of the hall, the great tree of light under the wall’s skin didn’t move so much as <em>grow</em>, all at once — a flicker of new branches running out from its tips like frost racing across a pond, the jump he’d watched in the storeroom, only bigger. And one of them didn’t stop at the wall’s face. A single fine thread came out through the concrete as if the concrete were smoke, and ran across the ceiling of the hall faster than he could follow, fine as spider silk, and went down into the floor about forty metres from where Ben was standing.</p>\n<p>Nothing slammed. Nothing needed to. Every door in the hall was already shut. That had been the point of the lock.</p>\n<p>The glass in front of Teo’s face rippled.</p>\n<p>It was the locker-room window, made huge. The whole long pane of the gallery, from end to end, took on that slow, trembling shimmer, like heat over a road, and held it. Along the window frame the paint crazed in a band a finger wide, with a tiny, glassy crackle like ice forming on a pond.</p>\n<p>And Ben⁠—</p>\n<p>Ben flickered.</p>\n<p>That was what it looked like. One moment he was standing at the pillar with his hand on the valve wheel, and the next he was standing somewhere else — a step to the left, turned round — and the next he was at the lock, and the next he was back at the pillar, and none of it was motion. It was like watching a film with most of the frames cut out. He jumped from place to place in stutters. His helmet light flared and moved and flared and moved.</p>\n<p>In their ears, the radio made a noise.</p>\n<p>It was a squeal. A high, thin, rising chirp, like a bird, like a bat, like a whistle blown in a dream, so brief it was almost not there, and then another, and another.</p>\n<p>“Ben!” Teo was at the glass, her gauntlets flat on it. “Ben! <em>Farr!</em>”</p>\n<p>The suit’s radio chimed. A little amber light came on at the bottom of Cas’s visor: RECEIVING — BUFFERING — RETIMING. Ben’s code. It had caught the chirps and was stretching them back out.</p>\n<p>And then, suddenly, Ben’s voice, perfectly clear, perfectly normal, in their ears:</p>\n<p>“⁠—what happened, what happened, the glass — Cas? Teo? The glass is all — oh no. Oh no, no, no, no. I can see you, I can see you up there, you’re not moving, why aren’t you — oh no. Oh, no. Okay. Okay. Okay.”</p>\n<p>It stopped. The light went off.</p>\n<p>Down on the floor of the hall, Ben was at the inner door of the lock, both gauntlets flat against it. Not opening it. Just leaning there. Then he was gone. Then he was at the pillar. Then he was standing still — for a whole second, perfectly still — in the middle of the hall, looking up at them.</p>\n<p>The radio chirped.</p>\n<p>RECEIVING — BUFFERING — RETIMING.</p>\n<p>“Okay.” His voice again. Different. Steadier, as though he had had time to steady it, as though he had sat down somewhere and breathed for a while. “Okay. I’ve got the pole out. I’m reading forty-eight thousand. Forty-eight thousand to one. It came out of the wall forty metres from me, and the whole hall’s come up to its speed. It’s a blister. It’s the locker room. The drop’s all in the walls and the doors — it’s in your glass, I can see it from here, the window’s gone all wavy.” A breath. “Don’t open the door. Listen to me. Don’t open the door. You know why. It all goes through the doorway, and then down the corridor and up the stair and through you. The keels won’t hold forty-eight thousand. Nothing will. And I can’t open it from in here, either, not the lock, not the big one — it’d go through me on the way out.” A pause. “The lock’s right there. I’m looking at it. It’s about ten metres away. That’s a stupid thing to be able to see.” Another pause. “I’m okay. I’m okay. I’m just going to think.”</p>\n<p>The light went off.</p>\n<p>The radio never spoke again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Forty-eight thousand,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>She said it very quietly. She was still at the glass.</p>\n<p>“A minute for us,” said Cas, “is a month for him.”</p>\n<p>He heard himself say it. He heard how calm it sounded.</p>\n<p>“Then we open the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He said no.”</p>\n<p>“I heard what he said. We’ve got keels. We’ve got⁠—” She turned on him. “What do you mean, <em>no</em>? That’s a <em>boy</em>, down there⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It’s in the glass. Two to forty-eight thousand, across a centimetre. If we open that door, it’s across the doorway instead, and then it comes down the corridor like a wave, and whoever it passes through has one half of their body living twenty-four thousand times faster than the other half. The keel averages over a metre. It isn’t enough. Nothing’s enough.” He made himself go on. “He knows the keels better than anyone alive. He said no.”</p>\n<p>Teo stared at him. Then she looked back down through the glass. Below them, in the emerald light, Ben was moving in stutters from tank to tank.</p>\n<p>“There’s got to be a way,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Maybe. If there is, he’ll find it before we do. He’s got more time.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t say <em>one in twenty</em>. He would say it later, to himself, for a long time.</p>\n<p>It was Ben who worked out how to talk.</p>\n<p>They didn’t see him do it. They saw him vanish from the floor, and then — perhaps a minute later — he was there on the other side of the glass, suddenly, right in front of them. The algae tanks stood in rows below the gallery, and along their tops ran a maintenance catwalk that came right up against the gallery window, and he was standing on it, a foot away from them through the glass. He had taken his helmet off.</p>\n<p>His face was thinner. That was the first thing. It had been perhaps a minute, for them — and in that minute he had found a razor from somewhere and shaved, and cut his own hair, badly, very short, and gone dark under the eyes and hollow in the cheeks. He looked at them. His eyes moved from Teo to Cas and back again. Then he held up a hand, and in it was a stubby yellow grease pencil, the kind engineers used to mark pipes.</p>\n<p>He wrote on the glass. He wrote fast; they couldn’t see his hand move. The letters simply appeared, backwards to him, forwards to them.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOW LONG</div>\n<p>Cas looked round. There was a supervisor’s desk in the gallery, and in its drawer, a marker. He took his right gauntlet off — the gallery was calm, a lee of sorts; the suit let him — and pulled the cap off the marker with his teeth and wrote, carefully, in capitals, backwards so Ben would read them the right way round:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">3 MIN</div>\n<p>It took him perhaps fifteen seconds.</p>\n<p>When he stepped back, Ben had written underneath:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">3 MONTHS</div>\n<p>And then, a flicker, and more:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">YOUR LETTERS TAKE A WEEK<br>WROTE HOW LONG. WAITED 8 DAYS TO SEE 3 MIN<br>KEEP IT SHORT</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There was a moment — Cas would think about it a great deal, afterwards — when they could have gone.</p>\n<p>The route through the hall was closed. But the long way round was still there. Three hours, the map said. Three hours to go round B-7 and rejoin Radial Nine on the far side, and then another kilometre and a half to the Lattice, and whatever was past the Lattice, and whatever was past that. Every minute they stood on this gallery was a minute of the city’s, and the city didn’t have many. The debt was coming due. Somewhere out there a pulse was gathering itself at the core, and there was a lock in the Crown and perhaps a key and certainly six hundred million people, and none of it was going to wait.</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him. He looked at her.</p>\n<p>Neither of them said it. Teo took off her own right gauntlet, and sat down on the floor of the gallery with her back against the desk, and after a moment Cas sat down beside her. The glass was in front of them. On the other side of it, in the green light, a man they had known for a day began to live the rest of his life.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They didn’t see most of it. They saw it the way you’d see a life through a keyhole — in flashes, in fragments, the long stretches between cut out. When Ben stood still for a while, they saw him. When he moved, he was a flicker, a smear, a sense of someone having just been there. He’d be at the glass, and then the catwalk would be empty, and then — a minute later, a month later — he’d be back.</p>\n<p>He came back with a beard.</p>\n<p>It happened in no time at all. One flicker, he was clean-shaven and hollow-eyed. The next, there was a dark scruff along his jaw. The next, it was a beard: short, rough, reddish-brown, which neither of them had known his hair would grow. He’d taken the suit off entirely. He was wearing the grey liner and, over it, a pair of stained orange overalls from some locker, with the sleeves rolled up. He looked at them through the glass — they could see him breathing — and then wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I’M OK<br>AIR GOOD (ALGAE)<br>WATER GOOD (PIPES)<br>FOOD: ALGAE<br>:(</div>\n<p>Teo laughed. It came out of her like a sob.</p>\n<p>Ben saw it. Or saw something — saw her face change, very slowly, over days, perhaps. He grinned. It was so quick they barely saw it, but it was there, a flash of teeth in the beard, and then he was gone again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<div class=\"sign\">TELL JONAH</div>\n<p>Four minutes later, it was still there on the glass. Underneath it, in smaller letters:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">TELL HIM I SAID YES TO THE DOG</div>\n<p>Cas didn’t know what it meant. Teo did. She’d been across the hangar the night before, and she’d heard. She turned her face away, into the shoulder of her suit, and stayed that way for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Twenty minutes in — nearly two years, for Ben — the catwalk against the glass had become a place.</p>\n<p>He’d dragged things up there. A pallet, to sleep on, with a foam mat from a first-aid station and an emergency blanket, silver on one side. A crate for a table. A row of algae-paste tins, some full, some empty and scrubbed and used for things — a pencil pot, a cup, a tiny pot with a green shoot in it. He’d rigged a grow-lamp on a cable to hang over it all. He’d rigged a sort of curtain, from a tarpaulin, that he could draw across the glass. He didn’t draw it often.</p>\n<p>And there was a rat.</p>\n<p>They noticed it one flicker when Ben was sitting cross-legged on the pallet, eating something out of a tin with his fingers. It was sitting on his knee. A big, sleek, brown-grey Works rat, with a long pink tail curled round it and its paws folded, looking at them through the glass with its black-bead eyes as though it too were waiting to see what happened. Ben pointed at it and wrote an arrow on the glass, and a word:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MINISTER →</div>\n<p>Teo laughed again. This time it was nearly a real laugh.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>And then, for a while, there was nothing good.</p>\n<p>They’d known it would come. You couldn’t be alone in a room for that long and not have it come. Cas had lived alone for fifteen years and he knew. But he had had a city outside his window, and a phone, even a phone that never rang, and the possibility — even if he never took it — of walking down a street and buying a newspaper and speaking to a stranger. Ben had a rat, and algae, and two statues behind a pane of glass who took a week to write four letters.</p>\n<p>He came to the glass one flicker with his hair grown out wild, and his beard wild, and his eyes red, and wrote in huge letters, slanting, tearing the grease pencil:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I CAN’T<br>I CAN’T DO THIS<br>I CAN’T DO THIS I CAN’T I CAN’T</div>\n<p>And then he hit the glass. They didn’t see his fist move. They just saw the glass shake, very slightly, and a starburst of cracks appear in the outer pane, in front of Teo’s face, and Ben’s hand on the glass on the other side with blood smeared from the knuckles, and his forehead pressed against it, and his shoulders shaking.</p>\n<p>For one long second Cas thought of the locker-room door, and the doorway, and what a hole in that glass would mean for all three of them. The cracks stayed where they were. They were in the outer skin. The inner pane held.</p>\n<p>Teo put her bare hand flat on the glass over his.</p>\n<p>She held it there. She held it there for a long time — two minutes, three — and on the other side of the glass, Ben stood with his forehead against it, perfectly still. And they understood, both of them at the same moment, what he was seeing: a hand, very slowly, over days and days, rising to the glass and coming to rest there. A hand that stayed. That didn’t go away. That was still there when he woke up, and still there when he went to sleep, for as long as he needed it to be there.</p>\n<p>Then he was gone. The glass was empty, except for the smears, and the cracks, and the huge slanting letters.</p>\n<p>He didn’t come back for a long time. Seven minutes. Eight months.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>When he came back he was different.</p>\n<p>He was thinner — much thinner — and his beard had been cut short with something, scissors or a knife, and his hair was tied back with a piece of wire. He was clean. He’d found clothes: a Works jumpsuit, too big, belted with cable. He moved — when he moved slowly enough for them to see — carefully, deliberately, like someone who has made a decision about how to live and is living it. He looked at them through the glass for a long moment. Then he wiped the old words off with his sleeve, and wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SORRY</div>\n<p>And under it, after a pause:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I’M STUDYING IT</div>\n<p>And an arrow, pointing down the length of the hall, at the far wall, where the great tree of light was still spreading slowly into the tanks.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>That was when it started to fill the glass.</p>\n<p>For the next hour — the next five years — Ben Farr studied the thing that was killing him, and wrote down what he learned where they could see it.</p>\n<p>He wrote fast, and they saw it arrive in bursts. A diagram would appear: the filament’s tree, sketched in grease pencil in quick sure lines, branches labelled. A column of numbers. An arrow. A question mark that was later crossed out, and replaced with an answer. The glass became a page, and then a notebook, and then a blackboard in a lecture hall. He ran out of room and wiped whole sections off and started again, and Cas — sitting cross-legged on the gallery floor with his black sketchbook open on his knee and a pencil in his bare hand — copied everything, frantically, as fast as it came, because he knew that anything he didn’t copy in the minute it was there might be wiped away the next.</p>\n<p>It was very good work.</p>\n<p>Ben had measured the filament. With the pole, with a tape, with a stopwatch and his own eyes, month after month, year after year, standing as close as he dared. He’d watched how it grew — how every branch split into branches with the same pattern, the same angles, at every scale. He’d counted. He’d worked out the branching ratio. He’d worked out that the pattern was self-similar, and computed, from his own measurements, the number that described how rough the thing was — how much edge it had.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">D = 2.73</div>\n<p>Cas stopped drawing. He sat for a moment, looking at the number. The same number that had been at the bottom of the last telemetry burst from the Crown. Ben had found it with a tape measure. He’d found it alone, in a room, with a rat, from first principles.</p>\n<p>“What?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“He’s right,” said Cas. “That’s all. He’s right.”</p>\n<p>There was more. Ben had noticed that the filament didn’t grow at random; it grew along lines. He’d mapped where the hall’s structure ran — the pillars, the beams, the old coolant lines that had once connected this hall to the Lattice — and found that the filament’s branches followed them, followed the lines where the Lattice had once pulled hardest on the station’s frame. Stress lines. The Tear was growing outward along the ghosts of the forces that had held it in. And where two branches forked, there was always, between them, a narrow gap — a throat — where the gradient was gentlest. A seam.</p>\n<p>He drew the Lattice. From memory, from the hall’s own schematics which he’d found in a cabinet, from reasoning. He drew the inner face of it, where Cas had said there would be a shear. He drew where the stress lines would run through it. He drew where the throats would be.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IF THERE’S A SHEAR<br>IT’LL HAVE SEAMS<br>HERE HERE HERE<br>GO THROUGH THE THROATS</div>\n<p>And arrows. And numbers. And a map.</p>\n<p>Cas copied it until his hand cramped. Teo held the sketchbook steady on his knee when his hand shook. At one point, without either of them saying anything, she took the pencil from him and did a whole section herself, the list of numbers, in her small square hand, while he flexed his fingers.</p>\n<p>On the other side of the glass Ben was thirty-two, and then thirty-three, and then thirty-four. His hair had started to go grey at the temples. He had lines around his eyes from squinting at the light. Minister had died — they saw the day he came to the glass with the rat lying in his cupped hands, and wrote MINISTER. OLD AGE. 3. GOOD INNINGS — and a year later there was another rat, smaller and darker, and he wrote MINISTER II, and pointed at it, and grinned in the beard.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS<br>NOBODY CAME</div>\n<p>Teo wrote back:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">WE’RE HERE</div>\n<p>It took her fifteen seconds to write it. It took him, on the other side, more than a week to watch it arrive. When it was finished, he stood looking at it. Then he wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">I KNOW</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Near the end — though they didn’t know it was near the end — he came to the glass and sat on the pallet with his arms around his knees and looked at them, not writing anything.</p>\n<p>He was thirty-seven. He was greying at the temples and in the beard, and there were deep lines at the corners of his mouth, and he had the look of a man who has been very unhappy and then, over a long time, has found his way to something that was not happiness but was near it, and could live there. He had been alone in that hall for eleven years. He was older now than Cas had been when he signed the revised Margin Review.</p>\n<p>He wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IS IT STILL TODAY FOR YOU</div>\n<p>Cas wrote back:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SAME DAY</div>\n<p>Ben read it — slowly, over days — and they saw him nod. Then he wrote, carefully, smaller than before, so they had to lean close:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">STRANGE<br>YOU’VE BEEN SITTING THERE MY WHOLE LIFE<br>FEELS LIKE YOU’RE THE ONES WHO ARE STUCK</div>\n<p>Cas looked at those words. He found that his eyes were stinging. He found that he could not remember when that had started.</p>\n<p>And then Ben wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">GO</div>\n<p>Teo made a small sound.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">YOU HAVE TO GO<br>I’M FINE<br>HAD A GOOD LIFE<br>MOSTLY ALGAE</div>\n<p>“No,” said Teo, to the glass.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THE WORLD’S ENDING OUT THERE<br>GO AND STOP IT<br>YOU’VE GOT THE MAP</div>\n<p>Cas looked down at the sketchbook in his lap. Thirty pages of it, dense with grease-pencil diagrams in his own pencil and Teo’s. Eleven years of a man’s life.</p>\n<p>He looked at Teo. She was crying, openly, silently, her face wet in the visor-light, her jaw set.</p>\n<p>“Two more minutes,” she said. “Two more minutes and then we go. I’m not leaving him the minute he tells us to. I’m not having him think⁠—” She didn’t finish.</p>\n<p>“Two more minutes,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>He picked up the marker. He wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">2 MORE MIN</div>\n<p>And then, after a moment, because it seemed important and because there was no one else to say it:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU BEN</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He never got to read the second line.</p>\n<p>They saw it happen at the far end of the hall first. The tree of light on the far wall flared — the whole of it, every branch, at once, bright enough that the gallery windows went white and Cas’s visor darkened automatically — and then it grew. Not slowly now. Not with the patient frost-crawl they’d been watching for two hours. It <em>surged</em>. New branches ran out of it in every direction, racing across the ceiling and down between the tanks, fork after fork after fork, and the fine thread that had come down in the middle of the hall two hours ago thickened as they watched, from a hair to a finger to a rope of light as thick as an arm.</p>\n<p>On the other side of the glass, Ben stood up.</p>\n<p>He was already writing. The letters came so fast now that they seemed to appear all at once — whole lines at a time — and Cas understood, with a lurch that went all the way down into his stomach, what that meant.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">IT’S SPEEDING UP</div>\n<p>And, under it, instantly:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">RATIO CLIMBING<br>100K<br>400K<br>3M<br>POLE CAN’T READ IT<br>IT’S OK</div>\n<p>And under that, all at once, the whole bottom of the glass:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<p>He put the pencil down.</p>\n<p>He put his hand flat on the glass. His palm, his long engineer’s fingers, dirty at the knuckles, with the white scar across two of them where he’d hit the glass all those years ago.</p>\n<p>Cas put his own bare hand flat against it on the other side.</p>\n<p>For a moment — less than a second — they were like that. Two palms, a centimetre of glass between them.</p>\n<p>Then Ben aged.</p>\n<p>He was thirty-seven, and then there was grey all through his beard, and his face had fallen in around the eyes, and he was fifty. He was sixty. The hand on the glass thinned under Cas’s palm, the knuckles swelling, the skin spotting and loosening over the bones. His back curved. His hair went white and thinned and was gone. He was an old man — a very old man, eighty, ninety — standing with his hand on the glass and his eyes, still his eyes, looking out through a face that Cas didn’t know at all. He was smiling. Cas was almost sure he was smiling.</p>\n<p>Then he wasn’t standing. He was sitting, on the pallet. He was lying on it. He was still.</p>\n<p>And then he was not a man. The overalls sagged. The shape under the silver blanket collapsed, and yellowed, and was bones, and the bones went grey and dry and cracked, and the blanket went brittle and split. The pallet rotted to splinters under it. The tins rusted to brown lace. The little pot with the green shoot in it burst with a sudden crown of leaves that browned and fell. Out in the hall the tanks bloomed — all of them, at once, a fantastic eruption of green, thick and rank and clotting against the glass — and then darkened, and went black, and drained, and cracked, and stood empty, with the grey slime dried to crusts up their sides. Dust fell from the ceiling in a slow endless snow. The grow-lamps flickered and died one by one. The paint peeled off the pillars and lay on the floor in drifts, and the drifts went to dust, and the dust settled over everything, deep and grey, like a snowfall on a ruin a thousand years old.</p>\n<p>It took about thirty seconds.</p>\n<p>Then the light at the far end of the hall dimmed back to its ordinary glow. The rope of light in the middle of the hall thinned, and thinned, and was a hair again, and drifted a little on the air, and was gone, into the floor, somewhere else.</p>\n<p>Cas was still standing with his hand on the glass.</p>\n<p>On the other side of it, very close, there was a heap of grey dust under a brittle grey rag, and a scatter of small bones that had been a rat, and, lying in the dust where it had been put down, a stubby yellow grease pencil.</p>\n<p>Above it, on the glass, the writing was still there. The grease had outlasted him. It was faded now, cracked and chalky, but you could still read it.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo was sick in her helmet vent.</p>\n<p>She did it without any fuss, turning her head, and the suit’s scrubbers whined, and she knelt there afterwards on the gallery floor with her head down. Then she got up.</p>\n<p>Cas hadn’t moved. He was looking at the pencil.</p>\n<p>“Hale.” Her voice was hoarse.</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“<em>Hale.</em>”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“We have to go now,” said Teo. “We said two minutes. That was two minutes.”</p>\n<p>He looked back at the glass. At IT WAS LONG ENOUGH. At the dust. At a centimetre of glass in front of his bare palm, and a smear on its other side where, a minute ago and sixty years ago, another palm had been.</p>\n<p>He took his hand away.</p>\n<p>He put his gauntlet back on. He closed the sketchbook — thirty pages, dense with numbers and diagrams, a map of a thing no one had ever seen, drawn by a man who had spent his whole life looking at it — and put it inside the chest of his suit, next to his heart, under the neck-ring, where it would be safe.</p>\n<p>They took the long way round. Three hours. For the first hour of it, neither of them said anything at all. They walked through the Works in the pearly light with the hammer coming up through the floor, once a second, once a second, and every so often Cas found that his lips were moving, and that he was counting.</p>\n<p>He wasn’t counting the hammer. He was counting how many seconds it had been since he’d put his hand on the glass.</p>\n<p>He knew what he would do if he ever reached a number that was long enough. He just didn’t know what the number was.</p>"}, {"file": "05-the-shear.md", "label": "Chapter 5", "title": "The Shear", "part": "Part One · The Draw", "partStart": false, "words": 6708, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Guilt’s a lousy reason, Hale. Die for something useful or don’t bother.</p><span class=\"attr\">Major Teodora Lisk, Civil Defence</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">It was Teo who talked first, in the end. They were going down a long ramp between two walls of cable trunking, with the hammer coming up through the soles of their boots, and she said, out of nowhere, “He was a good kid.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“I only knew him a day.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“No. I knew him eleven years.” She was quiet for a while. “Does that count? It feels like it counts.”</p>\n<p>Cas thought about it. He thought about a man with a beard and a rat on his knee, standing at a pane of glass for more than a week to watch two words arrive, and writing <em>I KNOW</em>.</p>\n<p>“It counts,” he said.</p>\n<p>They walked. The ramp went down and turned and went down.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell Jonah,” said Teo. “If I get back. About the dog.”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know what it means.”</p>\n<p>“It means they’d been arguing about getting a dog. He wanted one and Ben kept saying they didn’t have room.” She laughed, a short, raw sound. “Eleven years in a room full of algae and that’s the thing he wanted us to get right.”</p>\n<p>Cas didn’t answer. He found that he couldn’t.</p>\n<p>He found that grief, for him, had a peculiar shape. It came as arithmetic. He kept doing sums. A minute for them, a month for Ben. The hundred and twenty minutes they’d sat on the gallery floor. Eleven years, four months, give or take. The thirty seconds at the end, and how many years in them — sixty? A hundred? Ben’s pole hadn’t been able to read it. He did the sums over and over, the way you run your tongue over a broken tooth, and every time they came out the same, and every time it was unbearable, and he did them again.</p>\n<p>And under all of them, one more. The one he did most. <em>One in twenty. Less.</em></p>\n<p>“I said yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo didn’t stop walking. “You did the sum,” she said. “I watched you do it. You had the face.” A few more steps. “It was a good sum. I’d have done the same one. It came out wrong.” She was quiet for a while. “That’s what odds are, Hale. If they never came out wrong, they’d be called something else.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer. She didn’t make him.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Lattice lay below and inward, and to reach it they had to go down.</p>\n<p>This was the part of the plan Cas had drawn on the back of a Ministry briefing sheet in the crisis room, a hundred years ago, yesterday: the Vault was a sphere, its floor was the Anvil, and the Anvil was pierced from below by fourteen great conduits — cooling and power trunks that ran down through the keelstone plate into the Works beneath. The Crown was at the top of the Vault, four kilometres up Spindle One. There was no way into the Vault from the side; the Lattice was solid keelstone all around. The only way in was up through the floor.</p>\n<p>So they went down, into the Works under the Vault, through a region the old engineers had called the Undercroft, where everything was older and heavier and the Lattice’s pull came from above as well as from the side and the gravity weather was worst of all.</p>\n<p>It was in a shaft that the Undercroft nearly killed him.</p>\n<p>It was an old lift shaft, the cage long gone, with a steel ladder running down one wall and a gridwork of beams crossing it every ten metres. They were climbing down it, Teo first, Cas above, the keels humming loud in the narrow space, and Teo was calling out the washers — dropping one every few rungs and watching it fall straight down the middle of the shaft, glinting in her helmet light, until it vanished — and then she dropped one and it went sideways.</p>\n<p>“<em>Squall⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>It hit. Down went sideways and then <em>up</em> and then sideways again, a great slow roll like a ship broaching, and Cas’s hands were on the ladder and then they were not. He didn’t let go. The ladder moved. Or he moved; there wasn’t a way to tell. One moment he was clinging to steel rungs with the shaft below him, and the next the shaft was beside him and he was falling across it, and a beam came at him out of the dark, and he hit it back-first with his whole weight and the weight of the suit.</p>\n<p>He heard the keel. Over everything — over Teo shouting his name, over the scream of the beam as the suit’s plating ground along it, over his own breath — he heard the keel’s note change. It went from a clean high hum to something rough, something with a rattle in it, like a coin in a spinning drum.</p>\n<p>Then down swung back, and he was lying across the beam with his legs hanging over the shaft, and the note in his spine was wrong.</p>\n<p>“Hale! Cas! Talk to me⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m here.” He didn’t move. He was afraid to. “I’m here. I’m all right.”</p>\n<p>“Your keel⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>Teo was beside him on the beam — she’d climbed across, somehow, fast, in the suit — and had her light on his back. He heard her breath go in and not come out.</p>\n<p>“The housing’s cracked,” she said. “There’s a — there’s a split down the side, about the length of my hand. There’s light coming out of it. It’s — is it supposed to do that? Hale, is it supposed to⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>He reached back with his gauntlet and touched it. Through the gauntlet, through the plating, he could feel the rattle — a tiny eccentric wobble in something that had been perfectly balanced. The bearing. The crack had let the housing flex, and the rotor had kissed its bearing, just once, just slightly, and now it was running out of true.</p>\n<p><em>A bad day</em>, Ben had said. <em>A very, very bad day.</em></p>\n<p>He looked at the band of indicator lights around the housing’s waist, reflected in Teo’s visor. Green. Green. One amber.</p>\n<p>“How long?” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“It’ll run down faster. The bubble’ll shrink as it slows. Hours, instead of days. Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“And if it goes?”</p>\n<p>“If the bearing goes⁠—” He didn’t finish. “It won’t go. It’ll just run down.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at him through two visors.</p>\n<p>“You’re lying,” she said.</p>\n<p>“A bit.”</p>\n<p>“Right.” She got her shoulder under his arm. “Up.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He walked. He kept walking. For the first hour after the shaft he could hear the rattle all the time, faint and patient in the keel’s note, like a clock ticking in another room; after that he stopped hearing it, the way you stop hearing a clock, and only noticed it when the corridor went quiet and it was suddenly there, ticking, ticking. Teo heard it too. He could tell because she stopped talking whenever it was loud. She walked a little closer to him than she had before.</p>\n<p>They came to the Lattice at the end of the twelfth hour.</p>\n<p>He had seen it before, from inside, as a student — but only in pieces, through inspection ports, in schematics. He had never seen it like this. They came out of a tunnel onto a long gallery, a sort of balcony running along the outside of the Lattice’s outermost ring, and the Lattice was simply there, filling the world. A wall. A black wall so high that its top was lost above them in the pearly dark and so long that it curved away on both sides into nothing, smooth and seamless and utterly lightless, as if someone had cut a slice out of the universe. It hummed. You didn’t hear it; you felt it, in your chest, a vast deep tone below the hammer. It was keelstone, all of it — every cubic metre of that wall weighed as much as a locomotive — and they could feel it pulling at them, a steady sideways tug toward it, as if the wall were very gently trying to draw them in.</p>\n<p>“God,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing. He had found an inspection port — a round window of thick crystal set into the gallery’s floor, looking down and in through a gap between two of the outer rings. He knelt by it and looked.</p>\n<p>It went down a long way. Ring behind ring behind ring, black on black, each one a curved cliff, the gaps between them lit by that sourceless pearly light. And far down, far in, he could see one of the great tensioner rotors — the enormous spinning keelstone drums set into the rings that held the Lattice’s shape. It was turning slowly. It was perfectly true.</p>\n<p>And beyond it, deeper, where there should have been another⁠—</p>\n<p>“That’s Six,” he said.</p>\n<p>Teo knelt beside him.</p>\n<p>It was hard to see at first. Tensioner Six was the size of a building: a drum of black keelstone two hundred metres across, set on a magnetic bearing in a cradle in the Lattice. Only it wasn’t in its cradle. It was lying tilted in the gap between two rings, like a coin that has fallen on its edge and leaned against a wall, one rim jammed against the ring above and the other sunk in the cradle below, and it was not turning. Around it, the rings themselves were wrong. They weren’t curved properly. They sagged toward Six and bulged away from it, as though the whole Lattice around it had been squeezed in a fist and then let go. And through the gaps around it, very faintly, threads of that white-violet light came branching outward from somewhere deeper in.</p>\n<p>“Can it be⁠—” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>He said it without thinking. He’d known it since the crisis room, since the number at the bottom of the screen. He’d known it since the car. But he hadn’t <em>seen</em> it, and now he had, and he found that seeing it made it into a thing that could be said out loud without any effort at all.</p>\n<p>“It can’t be reseated. Not from the Crown, not from anywhere. There’s nothing to seat it in. The Lattice has lost its shape. Even if you stood it back up it would just fall over again.” He sat back on his heels. “Every engineer in that room knew it. Rask probably knows it. They all said it anyway.”</p>\n<p>“You said it.”</p>\n<p>“I said it.”</p>\n<p>Teo got up. She stood looking out at the black wall, her arms folded over the chest of her suit.</p>\n<p>“It’s funny,” she said. “I’ve been in Civil Defence twenty-nine years. Floods, fires, the Kovač stack. Every time, the Ministry says it’s under control and we go in and it isn’t. Every time. And you know what? Every time, I believed them a bit. Right up until I saw it. Every time.” She shook her head. “I used to think I was an idiot for that. I’ve decided it’s worse than that. I’ve decided it’s the whole bloody country.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Lattice Station 14-C was a control booth for Conduit 14, and they found it an hour later by following Ben’s map.</p>\n<p>That was the thing. Cas had not been sure, until they found it, whether the map was real — whether a man in a room, however clever, however many years he’d had, could have reasoned his way from the branching of a filament to the shape of a thing he’d never seen. But Ben had drawn the Lattice’s stress lines, and where they ran, and where they crossed the conduits, and on page twenty-six of the sketchbook, in Cas’s pencil copying Ben’s grease-pencil, there was a circle around one conduit and three words: <em>14 — THROAT HERE</em>. And when they came round the curve of the Lattice’s inner gallery, there was the door. LATTICE STATION 14-C. CONDUIT 14 ACCESS. AUTHORISED PERSONNEL ONLY.</p>\n<p>And there was a lee. They felt it as they came through the door: the pull of the black wall let go of them, and the gravity weather, which had been gusting all through the Undercroft, simply stopped. The booth was a small, bare room with a bank of dead monitors along one wall and a big thick viewport in the other, and a hatch in the floor by the viewport, and a chair, and a water cooler, and a first-aid cabinet, and it was as calm and still and ordinary as an office on a Sunday afternoon.</p>\n<p>Teo dropped a washer. It fell straight down and bounced.</p>\n<p>“Station air,” said Cas, checking the panel by the door. “Pressure’s good. You could take the helmet off in here.”</p>\n<p>“Could I,” said Teo. She had gone to the viewport. “Hale. Come and look at this.”</p>\n<p>He went.</p>\n<p>The viewport looked into the bottom of Conduit 14. It was a vertical shaft, round, maybe six metres across, lined with black keelstone and ribbed with the brackets of old cooling pipes that had been stripped out long ago. A steel ladder went up one side of it. The shaft went up and up, lit by the pearly glow, and at the top of it, very far up, there was a disc of light that was brighter than anything they’d seen, a pale, hard, violet-white.</p>\n<p>And about forty metres up the shaft, something was happening to the air.</p>\n<p>It trembled. That was the only word. It was like the shimmer over the Pit, the column of moving air they’d seen from the rim; but here it was close, and dense, and you could see it for what it was. A layer. A horizontal curtain across the shaft, a few metres thick, where the air was — <em>wrong</em>. Where things seen through it jumped and smeared and doubled. Where the ladder, going up through it, seemed to bend and break and join again, as though you were looking at it through moving water, or through the heat of a fire, or through tears.</p>\n<p>“The Shear,” said Teo.</p>\n<p>“A hole in the dam,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>He could see it now, the whole shape of it, the way he’d seen it in the locker-room glass. The Lattice was holding the entire difference between the core and the Works, sitting in its keelstone the way the drop had sat in that little square window. And here, where the conduit went through, the difference had nowhere to be but in the air.</p>\n<p><em>The whole Vault’s a blister</em>, he thought. <em>And we’re standing at the door.</em></p>\n<p>Cas looked at his visor display. He’d dropped a relay puck at the booth door; the suit was comparing its own clock with the puck’s, with the one before, with the Collar’s. LOCAL RATIO: 1.9.</p>\n<p>He looked up at the trembling curtain. If Ben’s map was right — if everything he’d guessed in the crisis room was right — then on the far side of it the ratio wasn’t two. It was hundreds. It might be thousands.</p>\n<p>“Forty metres,” he said. “From two to — I don’t know. Seven hundred? Across forty metres.”</p>\n<p>“And the keel spreads it over one.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And yours is broken.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” said Teo. “Look down.”</p>\n<p>He looked down.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the shaft, on the floor of the conduit just beyond the viewport, lying in a heap against the foot of the ladder, was a body.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They opened the floor hatch and went down to it, and Teo knelt beside it with her light, and for a long time neither of them said anything at all.</p>\n<p>It had fallen. That much was clear. It lay crumpled at the foot of the ladder, the way a body lies when it’s come down a long way. It was very small — shorter than Teo — and very broad; even in death, even ruined, you could see the width of the shoulders, the thickness of the bones.</p>\n<p>And it was ruined in a way that made no sense.</p>\n<p>The legs were bone. Clean bone, grey and dry and old, the clothes over them rotted to threads. The pelvis was bone. But from the waist up, the body was <em>leather</em> — dark brown, mummified, shrunken tight on the frame, like the bog bodies Cas had seen as a boy in the Museum of the Long Heat — and the face was worse, because the face was not quite either. The left side of it was dry skin over a skull. The right side, from the cheekbone to the chin, was — Cas made himself look — was still almost a face. Wasted, sunken, grey. But skin. Hair. An eyelid.</p>\n<p>“It went through,” Cas said. His own voice sounded strange to him. “Through the Shear. Coming down. Different parts of it spent different amounts of time in there. It was on the ladder, it — no.” He stopped. He made himself think. He looked at the legs, and then at the face. “It fell. It came off the ladder and turned over, and it came through head first. The head came <em>out</em> first. Into the slow side, down here. The legs were still up in the fast side. For a second, maybe. Maybe less. The legs lived — years? Longer? — while the head lived a second.” He heard himself. “It was dead before it got here. It was dead before it finished falling.”</p>\n<p>Teo said nothing.</p>\n<p>“But that’s not⁠—” He stopped again.</p>\n<p>“That’s not what’s wrong,” said Teo. “Is it.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>She reached out with her gauntlet and, very gently, touched the clothes.</p>\n<p>They weren’t station clothes. That was what was wrong. They weren’t a jumpsuit or overalls or a lab coat or anything issued by the Ministry to anyone who had ever worked in the Draw. They were a sort of loose tunic, belted, and trousers, and they had been made. By hand. You could see the stitching — big, careful, uneven stitches, the kind you’d make with a needle you’d filed yourself, and the thread was something coarse, twisted from fibres. And the cloth itself was a patchwork. Squares and strips of a dozen different fabrics: orange high-vis, grey jumpsuit cotton, a white that might once have been a lab coat, a blue that might once have been a curtain, all sewn together, and patched, and re-patched, and patched again over the patches, as if the garment had been worn and mended for years. For decades. By more than one person.</p>\n<p>Around the neck, on a cord of the same twisted fibre, hung a little ornament.</p>\n<p>Teo lifted it with her gauntlet. It was yellow plastic — the thick, glossy plastic of an old-style hard hat — carved into a shape: a tiny, flat, stylised flame, or a lamp, with rays around it. Somebody had carved it very carefully with a knife, and then it had been handled — held, rubbed, worried between finger and thumb — for so long that all the edges were worn smooth and round and the yellow had gone pale and soft as old soap.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” said Teo, very quietly. “Who is this?”</p>\n<p>He had been doing arithmetic since they came through the hatch. He’d been doing it without meaning to, the way he always did, the way he’d done it in the crisis room with Rask telling him to stop. The body had fallen down the conduit. So it had come from above. From inside. From the Vault. It had come through the Shear. It wore clothes that had been sewn by hand, from station fabric, and worn and mended for longer than the station had been broken. It wore a lamp carved from a hard hat. Its bones were thick. Its bones were very, very thick, and it was very small and very broad, the way a person might grow up — the way a <em>child</em> might grow up — somewhere the gravity was high.</p>\n<p><em>If anyone survived in there</em>, he had said to Rask, <em>they could have been alive in there longer than the Commonweal has existed.</em></p>\n<p>“They lived,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Who did?”</p>\n<p>“The night shift. Some of them. In the Vault. They lived. They had air — the Vault’s got air, it’s a sealed volume, it’s got scrubbers. Water. Light. They lived.” He looked at the patched sleeve, the fine careful stitches of someone who had had all the time in the world to learn to sew. “And they had children. And the children had children. And somebody — decades on, for them — got dressed in the clothes their grandmother mended and hung this round their neck and came down a ladder to find out what was at the bottom.”</p>\n<p>Teo was very still.</p>\n<p>“Hale,” she said. “What’s in there?”</p>\n<p>He looked up the shaft, at the trembling curtain of the Shear, and above it, the far bright disc of light.</p>\n<p>“People,” he said. “Or there were.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took her about ten minutes to decide. It took him most of those ten minutes to realise she’d decided.</p>\n<p>He was checking his keel. He’d found a way to see into the crack in the housing with his helmet light turned down, and through it he could see the rim of the rotor going round, a black blur edged with a thin bright line where it was catching the light, and every time it came round there was the tiniest flicker — a wobble, a stutter, the bright line jumping. He counted the stutters. He timed them against the keel’s note. The note had dropped since the shaft. He could hear it had dropped.</p>\n<p>He did the arithmetic. He didn’t like it.</p>\n<p>“How long,” said Teo behind him.</p>\n<p>“Four hours. Five. Before the bubble’s too small to matter.”</p>\n<p>“And in the Shear?”</p>\n<p>He didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“In the Shear, Hale. Forty metres of it. Through the throat, on the map, where it’s gentlest. With a keel with a crack down the side and a wobble in the bearing. Going up.”</p>\n<p>“The throat’s gentler. Ben said⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Ben said <em>gentler</em>. Ben didn’t say gentle.” Her voice was very calm. “If that bearing goes in there, what happens?”</p>\n<p>He closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>“The rotor comes loose,” he said. “It’s eleven kilos of keelstone at two hundred thousand revs. It goes through the housing and the suit and me and the conduit wall and probably the Lattice. And the bubble goes with it. So the Shear gets what’s left.” He opened his eyes. “Either way, I’d be dead before I noticed.”</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “So you’re taking mine.”</p>\n<p>He turned round.</p>\n<p>She was standing by the viewport with her helmet off. He hadn’t heard her take it off. Her face was grey with tiredness and streaked where she’d been crying at the glass, and her cropped hair was flattened with sweat, and she looked older than she had at the gate and very much more certain.</p>\n<p>“No,” he said.</p>\n<p>“The housings come off. Farr showed me on the rig. They’re built to hot-swap, that’s what the spin rigs were for, you spin them on the rig and clip them on. Four clamps and a coupling. We take yours off and put mine on. Five minutes.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ve got a suit with no keel, in the Lattice, four kilometres from the gate.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got a suit with a broken keel, in a lee, with station air and a water cooler and a first-aid kit and a chair.” She jerked her head at the room. “I’ll stay here. It’s calm here. I’ll sit in the chair. I’ll be the most comfortable person in the whole bloody Draw.”</p>\n<p>“And when the lee moves?”</p>\n<p>“Then I’ll move with it.” She shrugged. “I’m good at moving. I’ve been doing it all day.”</p>\n<p>“Teo⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Listen to me.” She came across the room. She had to look up at him. “Listen. You’re the one who knows what’s up there. You’re the one who’s read the paper. You’re the one who knows what the Clause is and what the plate means⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I <em>don’t</em> know what the plate⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You know more than me. Which is everything. I don’t know how to turn a key in a Keystone. I don’t know what a Keystone <em>is</em>. I don’t know how to talk to people who wear their grandmother’s curtains. I can carry you. That’s what I’m for. I carry people out of things. And I can’t carry you through that.” She pointed at the viewport. At the trembling air. “So I’m giving you my keel instead. And you’re going to take it. Because if you don’t, you’re going to try to go through on yours, and you’ll die in there, and I’ll have to sit in this bloody chair and watch it, like I watched that boy. And I’m not doing that twice in one day, Hale. I’m not.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>He wanted to say: <em>it should be me who stays</em>. He wanted to say: <em>I’m the one who signed it. I’m the one who owes</em>. He had come in here — he knew it, he had known it at the window in the Collar, <em>at last</em>, <em>almost a relief</em> — he had come in here half hoping that something would happen in the dark that would settle it. He had been thinking, since the shaft, since the rattle in the keel, that perhaps this was how it would be settled. Quietly. In the Shear. Tidily. And he’d been thinking, underneath that, very quietly, that it would be fair.</p>\n<p>She saw it. He watched her see it. Something changed in her face, and she stepped in closer, and her voice dropped.</p>\n<p>“Oh, no you don’t,” she said. “No. I read your file. I sat next to you all day. I know that look. I’ve seen it on men on bridges.” She put her bare hand flat on the chestplate of his suit, where Petra Imre had put hers at the gate. “Guilt’s a lousy reason, Hale. It’s the worst reason there is. It makes you stupid and it makes you selfish and it makes you think dying is a thing you <em>do</em>, instead of a thing that happens. Die for something useful or don’t bother. You hear me? If you’re going to die in there, you do it because it saves somebody. Not because it makes you feel square. Nobody’s keeping score. Nobody ever was.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t say anything. He couldn’t.</p>\n<p>“Now turn round,” said Teo, “and let me get that bloody thing off your back.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took eleven minutes, not five.</p>\n<p>They did it in the middle of the booth, in the lee, with Cas on his knees and Teo behind him. She had to take her own suit’s back plate off first to get at the clamps — she did it by feel, swearing, with her helmet under her arm — and then his. The keels had to come off at full spin; there was no way to stop them. When the first clamp let go on his housing the whole thing <em>lurched</em> in her hands, gyroscopic, fighting her, and for a second it swung sideways and he felt the bubble around him go lopsided and the room went strange — the far wall stretching, the floor tilting, the light doing something he didn’t have words for — and Teo said <em>hold still hold still hold still</em>, and got the second clamp, and the third, and then it was off, and his back was cold and light and the world was just a room.</p>\n<p>She lowered it to the floor. It sat there, humming its wrong, rattling note. Through the crack in its side a thin bright line of light went round and round.</p>\n<p>Then she took off her own.</p>\n<p>She did it the same way, slower, more carefully, and when it was off she was standing in the middle of the booth with no keel at all, and for the first time Cas saw what that meant. The lee was calm — but not perfectly calm. Nothing in here was. Without the bubble around her, he could see her sway, very slightly, as if standing on the deck of a ship at anchor. He saw her put a hand out to the back of the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right,” she said. “Right. Hold still.”</p>\n<p>She clipped it onto him. Four clamps. The coupling. The suit took it with a thunk, and the note came into his spine — clean, high, true, without a rattle, the note from the hangar — and the room went steady around him like a held breath.</p>\n<p>He stood up. Teo stepped back.</p>\n<p>“Good,” she said. “That’s good. How’s that feel?”</p>\n<p>“Like yours.”</p>\n<p>She laughed. She put her back plate back on. She put her helmet on the chair, and sat down in it, heavily, and looked at him.</p>\n<p>“You’ll drop pucks,” she said. “One at the bottom, one at the top.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ll talk to me.”</p>\n<p>“If the radios⁠—”</p>\n<p>“You’ll <em>talk</em> to me, Hale. You’ll send me news. I’ll be sitting here in this chair with nothing to do, I’ll be bored out of my skull.” She tipped her head back and looked at the ceiling. “Farr said the — the ratio thing. If you’re up there in the fast bit and I’m down here in the slow, what does that do?”</p>\n<p>“Anything I send you arrives squashed. Ten minutes of me in a couple of seconds. You’ll hear it as a chirp. The suit’ll stretch it back out.”</p>\n<p>“Birds,” said Teo. “Farr said whales. So I get birds.”</p>\n<p>“And anything you send me arrives stretched. A minute of you would take — hours. Six, maybe. More.”</p>\n<p>“So you’ll be up there for weeks, and I’ll be down here for an hour or two.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment.</p>\n<p>“That’s all right,” she said. “I’ve waited longer than that for a relief crew.”</p>\n<p>He knelt down beside the chair so he could see her face. He didn’t know what to say. He had not had a friend in fifteen years, and he’d known her for a day and a half, and she had just given him the only thing she had that could keep her alive, and he did not know what you said to that.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Don’t,” she said. “I’ll cry, and then I’ll have to kill you.” She put her hand on the side of his helmet and held it there. “Go on, then. Go and find your people in their curtains. And when you’ve found them, you tell them there’s a woman down here in a chair who wants to hear all about it.”</p>\n<p>He put his arms around her.</p>\n<p>It was absurd. It was two people in armoured suits, one of them kneeling, trying to hold each other through a centimetre of plating and a helmet ring, clumsy as two statues embracing. She put her arms around him and patted his back plate twice, hard, the way you’d pat a horse. Then she pushed him away.</p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” said Teo Lisk.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He went down through the hatch and dropped a puck at the foot of the ladder, beside the body in its patchwork clothes. He looked at the little yellow lamp for a moment. Then he started to climb.</p>\n<p>It was a ladder. That was all it was, for the first thirty metres. Rungs in his gauntlets, the keel humming clean and high in his spine, the black curved wall of the conduit sliding past, the hammer coming up through the rails. He climbed on the back side of the ladder, pressed close to the wall, because Ben’s map said that was where the throat was — a narrow column of gentler gradient running up the conduit’s rear face where two stress lines forked apart. He could see the trembling curtain above him, coming closer. Through it the ladder seemed to kink sideways and back. Above it, very far up, the pale bright disc.</p>\n<p>“Talking to you,” said Teo’s voice in his ear. “Just so you know. I’ll keep talking till you’re through. Tell me when you’re there.”</p>\n<p>“Almost.”</p>\n<p>“How’s the view?”</p>\n<p>“Ladder.”</p>\n<p>“Very good. Very descriptive. They’ll put you on the radio.”</p>\n<p>He reached the curtain.</p>\n<p>He’d known it would be bad. He had done the arithmetic. He had not understood — because arithmetic can’t make you understand it — what it would be like to have a body in two places in time.</p>\n<p>It started with his hands. He reached up for the next rung and his right hand was <em>there</em> before he’d reached — he saw it, in his helmet light, already closed on the rung, already gripping, while he could still feel the motion of reaching in his shoulder. He stared at it. It was his hand. It was ahead of him. It was living a little faster than the rest of him and it had got to the future first. And then his arm caught up, and his hand was his again, and the next rung was above him and his hand was already on it.</p>\n<p>He climbed. There was nothing else to do. He pressed himself against the wall where Ben had said and climbed, and the keel’s note went higher and higher in his spine as it fought, and the world came apart.</p>\n<p>Sound went first. He heard his boot hit the rung below him, and then he felt his foot move to put it there. He heard his own breath in the helmet and then felt his chest rise. He heard Teo’s voice in his ear saying <em>⁠— still with me? —</em> and it was wrong, it was <em>slow</em>, it was sliding down like a record played at the wrong speed, <em>⁠— stiiill wiiith meee —</em>, and then he heard his own voice answering <em>⁠— yes —</em> and didn’t remember saying it, and then felt his mouth make the word.</p>\n<p>His heart. He could hear his heart. He could hear it the way you’d hear footsteps in the room above: first the beat, then, a moment later, the feeling of it in his chest. An echo, but the wrong way round. His blood was arriving at his head before it left his heart. Something in him understood that this could not go on for very long, that there was a limit to how much of this a body could be asked to do, and that he was coming up to it.</p>\n<p>The ladder went up through the curtain. The curtain was forty metres thick. He was halfway.</p>\n<p>His thoughts came finished. That was the strangest part. He’d be climbing, and a thought would be there in his head, whole and complete — <em>the rung is loose</em> — and only afterwards would he feel himself think it, feel the words assemble, feel himself notice the rung. It was as though some part of him up ahead in time were leaving notes for the rest of him to find.</p>\n<p>He put his hand on the loose rung. He’d known it was loose. He moved his hand to the next one.</p>\n<p>Teo’s voice was a groan now. It came in his ear like the lowing of some enormous animal a long way off, a vast, slow, falling note with no words in it, and he knew it was her, still talking, still saying <em>still with me</em> and <em>how’s the view</em> and <em>nearly there</em>, and he could not understand any of it, and he held on to the sound of it anyway, the way you’d hold on to a rope in the dark. It went down and down. It went below anything he could hear. It became a pressure in his ears. Then it was gone.</p>\n<p>And then — like coming up out of deep water — he was through.</p>\n<p>It was over as suddenly as it had begun. His hand closed on a rung and he felt it close at the same time. His breath went in and his chest went up together. His heart beat, and he heard it beat, at once, one thing. He hung on the ladder, his helmet pressed against the black wall, shaking so hard the exo was fighting to hold him, and breathed, and breathed, and breathed.</p>\n<p>He looked down. Below him, the Shear. Through it, the ladder going down, jumping and smearing. And far down at the bottom of the conduit, very small, the round lit window of Lattice Station 14-C, and in it, a figure in a chair.</p>\n<p>She wasn’t moving. She was sitting perfectly still, like a photograph. Like a statue. He watched her for a long time and she did not move at all.</p>\n<p>He took an orange puck from his belt and pressed it to the wall. It stuck.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said. “I’m through.”</p>\n<p>There was no answer. He hadn’t expected one. She’d hear him almost at once; to her, his whole climb would be a blur and a squeak. But even if she answered the moment she heard, her few words would have to crawl up to him — he did the arithmetic — over the better part of an hour.</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The conduit went on. Above the Shear it went up through the Anvil itself, through a plate of keelstone three hundred metres thick, and the climbing was different. There was no weather. There was only weight. It came on gradually and then it didn’t stop — a steady, crushing, <em>reasonable</em> weight, as if the whole world were pressing on him from above, patient and enormous. The exo groaned. His arms shook. Every rung was a decision.</p>\n<p>He climbed. He counted rungs, because counting was what he did. They were thirty centimetres apart; three hundred metres was a thousand of them. At a hundred, he stopped and rested with his arms looped through the ladder and the keel singing in his spine and his heart going like a fist on a door. At two hundred. At three hundred. At six hundred his arms were not his arms any more, they were two things he had borrowed, and he was using them anyway.</p>\n<p>The light above got brighter. It was not the pearly glow of the Works. It was something harder, whiter, bluer — a light like a welding arc, like the Lesion in the Crown’s window in the old footage, like the nail of light Iris Hollowell had driven through the world. It came down the shaft and lit the rungs white. He could not look straight up into it.</p>\n<p>And there was sound. He noticed it gradually, the way you notice rain. Coming down the shaft, faint, under the hammer — which was not a hammer any more, he realised, not in here; it had stopped being a once-a-second blow somewhere back in the Shear and become something else, a long, slow tremor that came up through the rails every so often and lasted and lasted — under that, from above, there was a sound he knew and could not place. A murmur. A noise with a texture to it, rising and falling. Voices? No. Too many for voices. Water? No.</p>\n<p>He climbed. Eight hundred. Nine hundred. The disc of light was very close now; he could see the rim of the conduit against it, a black circle, and something moving across it, some shadow, some shape.</p>\n<p>At nine hundred and sixty rungs his right hand missed.</p>\n<p>It just — didn’t close. He reached, and the arm that he’d borrowed didn’t have anything left in it, and his fingers brushed the rung and slid off, and for a moment he hung by his left hand alone with the weight of the whole world pulling on him and three hundred metres of keelstone and forty metres of Shear and two kilometres of Undercroft below him, and he thought, very clearly, the way thoughts had come in the Shear, whole and finished: <em>oh. Here, then.</em></p>\n<p>And a hand came down out of the light and took hold of his wrist.</p>\n<p>It was a bare hand. Small, broad, very strong, the knuckles scarred. It closed on his wrist above the gauntlet with a grip like a vice. And a voice above him said, perfectly calmly, in an accent he’d never heard, all the vowels flattened and the consonants clipped:</p>\n<p>“Don’t let go. I’ve got you. Pim — get his other arm.”</p>"}, {"file": "06-what-time-is-it-outside.md", "label": "Chapter 6", "title": "What Time Is It Outside", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": true, "words": 4223, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Remain at your station. Await relief. You will not be forgotten.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Placard, Section 9 (the Watch’s first scripture)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They got him over the lip of the well on his belly, like a seal hauled onto a rock, and then he was lying on his back on something hard and hot, and the light was so bright that his visor went black to protect him and he was blind.</p>\n<p>He lay there. He had no choice about it. The weight was on him like a second body lying on top of his own — heavier, much heavier, than it had been in the conduit — and every breath was a push against it. The exo was whining. His arms were two dead things. The keel sang in his spine, high and clean, and under it, coming up through whatever he was lying on, was the long slow swell of the hammer.</p>\n<p>Someone was touching his suit.</p>\n<p>Small, strong, quick hands. They went round the neck-ring of his helmet, pressing, feeling. They found the seals at his shoulders, and the joints at his elbows, and the coupling of the keel. They were checking him. They were checking him the way he’d have checked a suit himself: methodically, in order, the places where air could get out.</p>\n<p>“Don’t take the helmet off yet,” said the voice. The same voice from the well. “Breathe slow. The air’s thick here. You’ll want to open it slowly when you open it. Let your ears get used to it.”</p>\n<p>His visor was clearing. It was adjusting to the glare, dimming it by stages, and a shape came out of the white. A face. A woman’s face, bent over him, very close.</p>\n<p>“Can you hear me?”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. His voice came out of the suit’s speaker cracked and strange.</p>\n<p>“Good.” She sat back on her heels. “What time is it outside?”</p>\n<p>For a moment he didn’t understand the question. It was so simple. It was the question you’d ask a stranger at a bus stop. He looked at the display at the bottom of his visor, where the two clocks were — OUTSIDE, fed by the last good signal from the pucks; LOCAL, his own — and read it off without thinking.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours,” he said. “And a bit.”</p>\n<p>She closed her eyes.</p>\n<p>She was quite still for perhaps five seconds. He could see her lips moving very slightly, the way his did when he was doing sums. Then she opened her eyes again, and there was something in them he couldn’t read — not surprise, not fear. Something more like the look of a person who has been carrying a heavy thing for a very long time and has just been told how much further they have to carry it.</p>\n<p>“Then it’s sooner than they think,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Is he the Relief?”</p>\n<p>That was another voice, higher, breathless. A boy. Cas turned his head inside the helmet and saw him — a thin, pale, very dirty boy of about eleven, crouched on the other side of him with his hands on Cas’s arm and his eyes as big as coins. He had a rat on his shoulder. It was a fat, sleek, brown-and-white rat with a red cord tied round its neck like a collar, and it was washing its face.</p>\n<p>“Is he? Ione. Is he? Is he the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He’s a man in a suit,” said the woman. “Get his legs.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They couldn’t lift him. Nobody could have lifted him. But there was a cart — a flat wooden thing on four small iron wheels, with a long handle, the kind of cart you’d use to haul rope — and between them, with a lot of heaving and a lot of the boy swearing in an accent so thick Cas caught one word in four, they rolled him onto it. He lay on it on his back. The weight pressed him down into the planks.</p>\n<p>Then the woman stood up, and took the cart’s handle, and turned it so that his head was at the high end, and for the first time he could see.</p>\n<p>He was lying at the top of a well, on a kind of stone lip, a round platform of black keelstone with a waist-high wall around it made of scrap: sheet metal, bits of pipe, a stripped-out door, all bolted and wired and welded together. Beside him, in the middle of the platform, was the mouth of the conduit he’d come up — a black round hole six metres across with the top of the ladder hooked over its edge. Around the hole, someone had painted on the black stone, in white, in big careful letters that went all the way round like writing on a coin: WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT · WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT.</p>\n<p>And beyond the wall was Holdfast.</p>\n<p>He did not know it was called that yet. He didn’t know anything. He lay on the rope-cart with the weight on his chest and the sweat running into his eyes inside the helmet and he looked, and for a long time — he never knew how long — he forgot to breathe.</p>\n<p>It was a city.</p>\n<p>It went away from him across a plain of black stone as flat as a table, and it was built of everything. Of scaffolding: great towers of scaffolding pipe, bolted and lashed and welded, ten, twenty storeys high, with floors laid across them made of steel plate and planking and doors. Of containers: the big steel cargo boxes the Draw used to move parts, stacked in terraces, painted every colour, with windows cut in their sides and shutters hung over the windows. Of keelstone: slabs of it, black and shining, off-cuts and rejects from the Lattice’s manufacture, laid as walls and floors and roofs, so that parts of the city looked as if they’d been carved from a single piece of night. It was stacked and crammed and piled together, lane on lane, tower on tower, bridges and walkways and ladders running between everything, lines of washing strung across every gap. There were gardens. Green things grew everywhere, in tanks and pots and troughs and on the roofs, climbing up the scaffolding in thick ropes of vine. There was smoke — thin grey threads of it rising straight up from a hundred chimneys. There was noise: the murmur he’d heard coming down the shaft, and it was voices after all. Thousands of voices. A city’s worth of people talking, calling, laughing, hammering, a baby crying somewhere, somebody singing.</p>\n<p>And above it, rising out of it, the spindles.</p>\n<p>There were eight. He’d seen them in the old footage, in the mural in the crisis room, in schematics: the great maintenance pylons that ran from the floor of the Vault to the Rim four kilometres up. He had never understood, from a picture, what four kilometres meant. They went up out of the city like trees out of undergrowth — black latticework towers, each one a hundred metres across at the base, the city piled up around their feet like drifts of leaves — and they went up, and up, and up, narrowing, until they were lost in the glare.</p>\n<p>And the glare⁠—</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<p>The Lesion hung at the top of the sky.</p>\n<p>It was a point. A single point of white-violet light, too hard and too bright to look at, like looking at the sun through a pinhole. The eight spindles rose toward it on every side and ended short of it in a thin dark ring, far up, that must be the Rim. Beyond the Rim the Vault curved up and away on all sides — black, a vast black dome, a sky made of the inside of a stone — and it should have been dark, and it wasn’t. Because spreading out from the point of light, across the whole top of the sky, was the Lace.</p>\n<p>He had seen its threads in the Works. A hair of light crossing a storeroom. A root system spreading down a wall. This was the thing they had grown from. It spread out from the Lesion in every direction at once, an unending, branching, glowing filigree, finer than frost, larger than weather, filling the upper sky from horizon to horizon like the veins in a leaf held up to the sun. It moved. Very slowly, almost too slowly to see, it was moving — branching, reaching, growing — and in places its longest threads came down out of the sky in fine bright streamers toward the tops of the spindles, like the tendrils of some enormous luminous jellyfish trailing in the current. It was the most beautiful thing he had ever seen. It was the most frightening. He couldn’t decide which; his mind kept sliding between the two and couldn’t find anywhere to stand.</p>\n<p>The light it gave was violet and white and very even, like the light of an overcast noon, and there were no shadows anywhere, and it was warm. It was as warm as a summer afternoon in the southern tiers. Sweat was running down his back inside the liner.</p>\n<p>The hammer came.</p>\n<p>It came up through the stone under him, through the cart, through the planks, into his spine. But it was not the hammer. Not the blow, once a second, that he had listened to in his flat and in the Collar and in the Works for thirty hours. It was a swell. It began so gently he hardly noticed it — a faint rising pressure in the floor, like the first lift of a wave under a boat — and it rose, and rose, and went on rising, and at its height the whole city seemed to hum with it, every loose shutter faintly rattling, every scaffold pole faintly singing, the water in a trough nearby shivering into rings; and then, just as slowly, it fell away. It lasted — he counted without meaning to — about a minute and a half.</p>\n<p>He knew what it was. He had known it the instant it started. It was one strike of Ruth Tally’s hammer, arriving in here stretched.</p>\n<p>He looked at his visor. LOCAL RATIO: 703.</p>\n<p>Out there the hammer fell once a second. In here, one of those seconds would last seven hundred and three — nearly twelve minutes — and the blow itself, the instant of iron meeting keelstone, was the minute and a half he had just felt go through him.</p>\n<p>“There,” said the boy, softly, beside him. He had his hand on the wall of the platform, flat, feeling it. “That’s the Bell. Did you feel it? That’s the Bell.”</p>\n<p>“I felt it.”</p>\n<p>“There’s never been this many,” the boy said. “Not ever. Gran Perpetua says⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Pim,” said the woman.</p>\n<p>The boy shut his mouth.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She was standing by the cart’s handle with one hand on it, looking down at him, and now that his visor had cleared and his eyes had adjusted, he could see her properly.</p>\n<p>She was short. Everyone here would be short — he’d see that soon enough — but she was short even so, and broad and dense through the shoulders and hips in a way that made him think of the body in the conduit, of the thick heavy bones. She wore the same kind of clothes: a patched tunic belted at the waist, a rigger’s harness over it, loops of rope on one shoulder, heavy trousers with the knees reinforced in keelstone-grey cloth. Her forearms were bare and brown and roped with muscle, and they were scarred — pale lines across them, old burns, the marks of a lifetime of hauling rope. Her hands were the hands that had caught him.</p>\n<p>Her hair was dark and cut short, roughly, as if with a knife. On the left side, above the temple, there was a streak of white in it. Not grey. White. A single clean stripe of it running back from her forehead, as though someone had drawn a line there in chalk.</p>\n<p>And her face⁠—</p>\n<p>He could never, afterwards, describe her face well. He tried. He’d try for a long time. It was an ordinary face, broad-boned, weathered by the heat, and young, he thought — thirty, perhaps? — though the white streak made it hard to tell. It was not her face exactly. It was the way she looked at things. She looked at him on the cart the way you’d look at a view you had seen many times and still liked: patiently, attentively, without hurry, as though there were nothing he could possibly do or say that she hadn’t already seen, and she’d be interested to see it again. Her eyes were grey-green and very steady. They didn’t hurry. He had never seen eyes that hurried less.</p>\n<p>Around her neck, on a cord, hung something black.</p>\n<p>It was a key. A flat, dark, heavy-looking thing, the length of a finger, with a blunt cross-shaped bit. It lay against her tunic just below her collarbone. It didn’t shine. It seemed, in the shadowless light, to drink the light in. He looked at it and something moved at the very back of his mind — some shape he half recognised, some picture from a manual — and then his tired brain let it go.</p>\n<p>“What’s your name?” he said.</p>\n<p>She considered the question. “Do you need it?”</p>\n<p>“I’d like it.”</p>\n<p>“Ione,” said the boy, before she could answer. “She’s Ione. Ione Tally. I’m Pim. This is Steward Harrow.” He pointed at the rat. “He’s named after a Steward. They all are. Mine are.”</p>\n<p>“Pim.”</p>\n<p>“Well, he asked.”</p>\n<p>The woman — Ione — looked at the boy. Then back at Cas. Something almost like a smile moved at the corner of her mouth, and was gone.</p>\n<p>“You’ll be all right on the cart,” she said. “The weight will be bad for a few rounds. Don’t try to stand. Drink when they give you water. It’s clean.” She looked away, across the city, at something he couldn’t see. Her head was up. She was listening.</p>\n<p>He heard it too, then. A whistle. High and shrill, somewhere off among the scaffold towers. Another answered it. Then a third, closer.</p>\n<p>“Wardens,” said Pim.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” Ione let go of the cart handle. “They watch the wells. Someone will have seen us from the towers.” She looked down at Cas again, and there was something quick and serious in her face now. “Listen to me. There isn’t time for most of it, so listen. They’re going to call you the Relief. All of them. They’ll kneel down. They’ll cry. They’ll take you to the First Steward and he’ll call you the Relief too. Don’t let them tell you what that means.”</p>\n<p>“What does it mean?”</p>\n<p>“It means whatever they need it to mean.” The whistles were closer. “When they’re done with you — and they won’t be done with you for a while — find the Tollers. Ask anyone for Toller’s Tower. Ask for Perpetua.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those unhurried eyes. And he had the strangest feeling — a feeling he’d come to know well, over the weeks that followed, and never get used to — that she already knew exactly what he was going to say next, and was simply waiting, politely, for him to say it.</p>\n<p>“Because,” she said, “you came up out of the floor in a suit I’ve been waiting eleven years to see. And because you’re going to want to know how long you’ve got.”</p>\n<p>Then she turned, and put her hand on the boy’s shoulder, and said something to him too fast and too thick for Cas to follow. Pim nodded hard. And Ione Tally stepped to the wall of the platform, and put one hand on the top of it, and vaulted over it — lightly, easily, in that crushing weight, as though it were nothing — and dropped out of sight.</p>\n<p>“She’s Unwatched,” Pim told him, in a whisper, as if it were an explanation. “She can’t be here when they come. She’d get the Quiet.” He patted Cas’s gauntlet with a grimy hand. “Don’t worry. I’ll stay. I’m not anything. Nobody Quiets me. Steward Harrow, get <em>down</em> from there.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Wardens came up the ramp to the well-platform at a run — six of them, then ten — and stopped dead.</p>\n<p>They were big, for Holdfast. That was the first thing he learned about Wardens, and the thing he’d learn about all of them: they were the big ones, the men and women who’d grown a head taller than everyone else and twice as wide, and they wore it like a uniform. They had actual uniforms too: long coats of heavy grey cloth with a yellow lamp stitched on the breast, and on their heads — he stared — on their heads, hard hats. Old Works hard hats, cracked and mended and painted grey and polished to a shine, with the yellow lamp painted on the front. They carried staves: long poles of scaffold pipe with the ends wrapped in cord.</p>\n<p>They stopped at the top of the ramp in a ragged line, breathing hard, and looked at the man in the grey suit lying on the rope-cart beside the Drop well, and nobody said anything at all.</p>\n<p>Then one of them — the biggest, a slab of a man with a slow, square, heavy face and a grey beard cut close — took off his hard hat. He held it against his chest. And very slowly, as though his knees didn’t quite want to, he knelt down on the black stone.</p>\n<p>One by one, the others did the same.</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said the big man. His voice was deep and unsteady. “Lamp keep us. It’s the Relief.”</p>\n<p>“It’s the Relief,” said the others, raggedly. One of them, a young woman at the end of the line, had begun to cry.</p>\n<p>“I <em>said</em>,” said Pim, to no one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They carried him through the city.</p>\n<p>Not on the rope-cart — the big Warden, whose name was Osk, sent for something better, and it came: a litter, a real one, a sort of stretcher-chair made of scaffold pipe and canvas, with poles for eight bearers. They lifted him onto it, suit and all, grunting, and it took all eight of them, big as they were, and they set off with him through the streets of Holdfast at a slow, heavy, lurching walk.</p>\n<p>He saw it from there, the whole way, lying on his back with the Lace above him and the city going past on either side. And the city saw him.</p>\n<p>Word had got there ahead of them. He never knew how; Pim, probably, who had vanished down the ramp the moment the litter came and was nowhere to be seen. But by the time the Wardens had carried him fifty metres from the well, there were people in the lanes, and by the time they’d gone a hundred there were crowds. They came out of doorways and down ladders and up from under walkways. They leaned out of container windows and hung off scaffold towers and stood on roofs. They were short — all of them, every one, men and women both, broad and stocky and heavy-boned — and they were pale, a strange sallow pallor under the violet light, and their clothes were the same patchwork of reused fabric as the dead body in the conduit, a thousand different mendings, every colour. They were silent at first. They stood and stared at the litter going by. And then someone — an old woman in a doorway — put her hand to her mouth and said, “<em>Relief</em>,” in a cracked voice, and it went through the crowd like wind through grass.</p>\n<p><em>Relief. Relief. It’s the Relief.</em></p>\n<p>They began to kneel. Not all of them. But many. They knelt in the lanes as he went by, and put their hands on their chests, or touched their foreheads to the ground, or held up children to look at him. They wept. Some of them sang: a thin, wavering, old-fashioned melody that he felt he almost knew. Some of them reached out, as the litter passed, just to touch the side of it, or the edge of his suit. The Wardens let them.</p>\n<p>He had never been so frightened in his life. Not in the Shear. Not on the gallery floor.</p>\n<p>He tried to look at things. It was the only defence he had. He looked at the lanes: narrow, twisting, paved in black keelstone polished smooth by a century of feet. He looked at the signs. There were signs everywhere, and he knew them — he had walked past them as a young man, on placement and on survey. STAIRWELL C. MUSTER POINT B. NO NAKED FLAMES. KEEP THIS DOOR CLOSED. They were station signs, the old enamelled plates from the Vault’s walls and walkways, and they had been taken down and put up again as street names: a lane of containers with a sign bolted over its entrance that read CORRIDOR 9 — AUTHORISED PERSONNEL ONLY, and a crossroads marked with a big yellow EMERGENCY ASSEMBLY AREA plate that had been lovingly repainted so many times the letters stood up from it in ridges. He looked at the shrines: little niches in walls, with a lamp in them — an actual lamp, a clay dish of oil with a wick — and a picture behind it, painted, of a point of light with rays. He looked at a rat pen: a big wire cage built into the angle of two walls, full of fat, sleek, contented rats, dozens of them, with a sign over it that said, in careful hand-painting, HOLDFAST’S OTHER CITIZENS — PLEASE DO NOT FEED.</p>\n<p>He looked at the mural.</p>\n<p>It was painted across the whole side of a scaffold tower, fifteen storeys high, and the litter went right past its foot so that he saw it from below, rising over him. It showed a man. A tall man in a dark jacket, painted enormous, standing on a high place with his arm raised toward a white star in a violet sky. The man’s face was noble and grieving, with silver hair. And in his raised hand he was holding up a key. A small black key, the length of a finger, on a broken chain. Beneath the man, in a crowd, painted small, were people in white coats and orange overalls, looking up at him and weeping. Across the bottom of the mural, in letters a metre high, it said:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THE GIVING<br>FOUNDER CRANE GAVE THE KEY TO THE LAMP<br>THAT IT MIGHT NEVER BE PUT OUT</div>\n<p>Cas stared at it until the litter carried him past.</p>\n<p>The key was black. It was the length of a finger. It had a blunt, cross-shaped bit.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Steward’s House stood at the foot of Spindle One.</p>\n<p>He knew it was Spindle One because it was the biggest; because the others were further off and it was right there, filling half the sky, a black lattice tower a hundred and fifty metres across rising straight up from the heart of the city into the light; because at its foot, where in the old schematics there had been a lift terminal — the great steel shed where the Crown’s lift came down — there was now a building. It was the only building he’d seen in Holdfast that looked as if it had been built all at once, on purpose. It was made of keelstone slabs laid with great care, black and shining, with a wide stair up to a doorway and tall narrow windows with coloured glass in them — bottle glass, he’d find out later, and lamp lenses, and the glass from old instrument dials, melted and set in lead. Over the door, carved into the stone, was the lamp with its rays.</p>\n<p>They carried him up the stair and through the door into a long, high, cool hall, shadowed, lit by oil lamps, and set the litter down. The Wardens stepped back. The door closed behind them, and the noise of the crowd outside, which had been rising all the way here like a tide, dropped to a murmur.</p>\n<p>A man was waiting for him.</p>\n<p>He was old. Sixty, perhaps, or more — it was hard to tell with Holdfast faces. He was taller than most of them, lean where the others were broad, and he wore a long coat of plain dark cloth, very well mended, and no hard hat. His hair was silver, and there was a lot of it, swept back from a high forehead. He had a long, grave, intelligent face. It was, Cas realised with a small cold shock, a face he had seen before. He’d seen it a quarter of an hour ago, fifteen storeys high, holding up a key.</p>\n<p>The old man came and stood beside the litter. He looked down at Cas without speaking. At the suit, the keel, the visor. At the Ministry crest, stencilled on the shoulder plate, which Cas had never noticed until he saw the man’s eyes go to it and stop there and go very still.</p>\n<p>Then the man smiled. It was a tired, careful, courteous smile, and there was something underneath it that Cas couldn’t read at all.</p>\n<p>“Welcome to Holdfast,” said First Steward Halloran Crane. “Relief. You’re a hundred and sixty-eight years late.”</p>"}, {"file": "07-remain-at-your-station.md", "label": "Chapter 7", "title": "Remain at Your Station", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4844, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Who keeps the Watch? — We keep the Watch. — Until? — Until Relief. — And when Relief comes? — Then we are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Common Office, as said in the Commons every seventh round</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They got him out of the suit in a small stone room at the back of the Steward’s House, and it nearly killed him.</p>\n<p>In the suit, with the exo taking most of it, the weight had been a crushing thing but a bearable one. Out of it — in the grey liner, standing on his own legs on the black stone floor — it was something else. It was like being filled with sand. His arms hung off his shoulders like buckets. His knees shook. His heart, which had been going hard since the ladder, went harder, labouring to push blood that weighed half as much again as it should up into a head that felt as though it were being slowly pressed into his neck. He took two steps and his vision went grey at the edges and the big Warden, Osk, caught him under the arms and lowered him into a chair like a man putting down a sack of cement.</p>\n<p>“Slow,” said Osk. “Slow. Everyone’s slow the first rounds. Even babies.”</p>\n<p>“Babies?”</p>\n<p>“From the Crèche.” Osk had a voice like gravel being poured. “Born heavy, we are, but you still have to learn to stand in it. Takes a year.” He considered Cas. “Take you longer, maybe.”</p>\n<p>They brought him water in a tin cup. It was warm and tasted of iron and something green, and it was the best thing he had ever drunk. They brought him food: a flat, dense, dark-green cake, like a biscuit or a very heavy bread, with a smell of pond and salt. He looked at it.</p>\n<p>“Greenbread,” said Osk. “Algae. Cricket flour. Salt. Eat it slow.”</p>\n<p>He ate it slow. It tasted of the sea, if the sea had been baked. He ate all of it.</p>\n<p>The suit stood in the corner, empty and hulking, the keel humming quietly to itself on its back. Osk kept looking at it. Everyone who came into the room — and a lot of people came into the room, Wardens and Stewards in grey and black coats, on one pretext or another — looked at it, and then at him, and then looked away quickly, as though they had seen something they weren’t meant to.</p>\n<p>Then Halloran Crane came in, and everyone else went out, except Osk, who stood by the door with his hard hat under his arm and his eyes on the floor.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Crane sat down opposite him. He had brought a lamp — a real lamp, a clay dish of oil with a floating wick — and he set it on the table between them, though the room was lit perfectly well by the violet glow through the window. He folded his hands. They were long, fine, old hands, with an ink stain on the middle finger of the right.</p>\n<p>“You’ll forgive me,” he said, “if I’m not quite sure how to begin. There are protocols for this. There is, in fact, an entire liturgy. I’ve read it many times. I’ve never expected to use it.” He smiled slightly. “My grandfather used to say that it had been written by people who never really believed it would be needed, for people who never really believed it would come. I find myself unable to recall a single word of it.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing. He didn’t know what to say.</p>\n<p>“So instead,” said Crane, “I’ll ask you something simple. What is the Outside?”</p>\n<p>“The Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Where you came from. Below the Drop. Beyond it.” Crane’s voice was quite steady. “Our histories say that when the Lamp was kindled, the world beyond the Watch was burned. That the Founders were the last of it. That we keep the Watch for a world that is gone, until Relief comes out of the dark. I’ve always found that a very beautiful idea. I’ve never been quite sure I believed it.” He looked at the suit in the corner, at the stencilled crest on its shoulder. “So. What is it?”</p>\n<p>“It’s a city,” said Cas. “It’s called Averna. It’s big. Six hundred million people. It’s where the Draw — where this place — sends its power. It’s⁠—” He stopped. He thought of the Tier Nine arterial at dawn, the bunting, the anthem. “It’s fine. It’s still there. Nothing’s burned.”</p>\n<p>“And how long,” said Crane, “since the Kindling? Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours.”</p>\n<p>Crane didn’t move.</p>\n<p>He was so still that for a moment Cas wondered if he’d heard. He sat with his long hands folded on the table and his eyes on the little flame of the lamp, and nothing in his face changed, nothing at all, and the stillness went on and on until it became a thing in the room, a thing you could feel pressing on you like the weight.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours,” Crane said at last.</p>\n<p>“And a bit.”</p>\n<p>“A day. A day and a half.” Crane closed his eyes. “My grandfather’s grandfather’s grandfather was born in this city. And lived here, and died here, and was given to the Tanks. So was his son. So was his. And outside⁠—” He opened his eyes. “Outside, it’s still the same night.”</p>\n<p>“Nearly.”</p>\n<p>“Nearly.” He breathed out. It was almost a laugh. “The Tollers will be very pleased with themselves. They’ve always said so. In a way that one could, if one was careful, pretend not to hear.” He looked at Cas. “Is there anyone else? Coming after you?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Is there relief?”</p>\n<p>Cas thought of Rask. The crisis room. Three suits. Ben in the hall of algae. Teo in a chair.</p>\n<p>“There’s me,” he said.</p>\n<p>Crane nodded slowly, as if that were the answer he’d expected. Then he stood up.</p>\n<p>“Come,” he said. “I’d like to show you something. Osk, help the Relief. Slowly.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The chapel was at the heart of the Steward’s House, a small square room of black keelstone with no windows, lit by a single lamp that burned in a niche at the far end. There was nothing else in it. No benches, no altar, no pictures. Only the lamp, and behind the lamp, set into the wall in a frame of polished keelstone as though it were the most precious object in the world, a sheet of yellowed laminate.</p>\n<p>Cas knew it before he could read it. He’d walked past it at Gate Nine a day and a half ago. He’d walked past a hundred of its twins, years ago, when he was still allowed in.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SECTION 9 — IN THE EVENT OF CONTAINMENT LOSS:<br>REMAIN AT YOUR STATION AND AWAIT RELIEF.<br>DO NOT ATTEMPT TO TRAVERSE THE LATTICE.<br>RELIEF CREWS WILL BE DISPATCHED.<br>YOU WILL NOT BE FORGOTTEN.</div>\n<p>The stick figure beside its stick console. The Ministry typeface from the Year Twenties. Someone had once drawn a moustache on the stick figure at Gate Nine; nobody had ever drawn anything on this one. It had been cleaned — lovingly, with soft cloths, for generations — until the laminate had gone as clear and pale as horn.</p>\n<p>“The Placard,” said Crane quietly. “Our first scripture. It was on the wall of the assembly bay the night of the Kindling. The Founders gathered under it. Nineteen hundred of them. The Lamp had just been lit — the sky had just filled with light — the Drop had just taken the first of them who tried to go down. And Founder Crane stood under this and read it aloud to them. <em>Remain at your station and await relief.</em> And they did.” He looked at it. “They’ve been doing it ever since.”</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“You’re thinking it’s an evacuation notice,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>“It <em>is</em> an evacuation notice.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.” Crane didn’t seem offended. If anything, he seemed faintly amused, in a tired way. “My ancestor was a practical man. He needed something to hold nineteen hundred terrified people together in the dark, and he needed it within a week. This was on the wall. It had the great advantage of being true.” He turned his head. “Look at the floor.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked. Running along the base of the chapel wall and out through the door, painted onto the black stone in gold leaf, worn almost away by feet in places and repainted over the wear so many times that it stood up from the floor like a ridge of metal, was a line of arrows. He knew them too. They were the arrows from the Vault’s walkways, the little painted chevrons that showed you the way to your muster point in an emergency.</p>\n<p>“They lead to Muster Point B,” said Crane. “In the Commons. It’s the holiest place in Holdfast. Pilgrims come to it on their knees on the seventh round. It is the place, you see, where they were told to wait.” He paused. “My ancestor had a sense of humour. I think it was the only thing that kept him alive.”</p>\n<p>Cas made a sound. He didn’t know whether it was a laugh or something else. Crane looked at him with that tired, courteous half-smile.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Crane. “I’ve often felt that way myself.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Back in the small stone room, with the lamp on the table and a fresh cup of iron-tasting water, Crane told him the history.</p>\n<p>He told it well. He’d told it, Cas guessed, many times, to many children, in the Commons on high days. The Kindling: the night the Lamp was lit, the Founders at their posts, the great light filling the sky. Founder Crane in the Crown, who saw that the Lamp must never go out, and gave the key into it so that it never could. The Descent: nineteen hundred souls coming down the spindles into the Vault as the world beyond burned. The Drop, which took all those who tried to go down after it. The Sealing — the dark days when they understood they could not leave. The Watch: the Founders’ vow to keep their station. The Crèche, the first children born under the Lamp. The Tanks, where the dead went to feed the living. The Tallies, the Bell, the rounds and the shifts. A hundred and sixty-eight years. Seven generations. Thirty-one thousand souls.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand, four hundred and six, at the last count. Four hundred and seven, now.” Crane inclined his head at him. “The Founders were fruitful. They had to be. There was so little else to do.” He folded his hands. “And now I’m going to ask you something, and I’d be grateful if you’d answer it as honestly as you answered the last.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>“The Bells are coming faster,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>He said it softly. By the door, Osk shifted his feet.</p>\n<p>“In the Founders’ time,” said Crane, “the Bell came once every three rounds. Once every nine shifts. You could go days without feeling it. In my grandmother’s time, once every two. In my own childhood, once a round. When I became Steward, three. Seven, eight years ago. Now there are more than a hundred. It comes while you’re eating. It comes while you’re sleeping. The Stewardship teaches that this is the Quickening — that the Lamp’s heart beats faster as Relief draws near.” He looked up at Cas. “And now Relief is here. So. The Quickening. What is it?”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at him for a long moment.</p>\n<p>“The Bell’s not the Lamp’s heart,” he said. “It’s an alarm. A hammer, down in the station, outside the — below the Drop. It strikes once a second. Outside seconds. My seconds. It’s been going since the Kindling. You’re hearing each stroke stretched out, because time in here runs faster than out there. That’s why a day and a half outside is a hundred and sixty-eight years in here.”</p>\n<p>“And it’s quickening because⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because time in here is slowing down. Coming back toward ours. The thing that makes time run fast in here is the Lamp. The Lamp is⁠—” He looked for words. There were no Holdfast words for it. He used his own. “The Lamp is a debt. Everything the station ever gave the city outside, it borrowed. From the — from the ground of things. The emptiness. And the emptiness always takes it back. Plus interest. For ninety years the station kept putting off paying it. The night of the Kindling, it stopped being able to. And now the debt’s being called in. Slowly at first. Then faster. The Bells are the sound of it coming due.”</p>\n<p>“And when it comes due?”</p>\n<p>“All at once,” said Cas. “At the Lamp. It’ll be — everything. This city, and the station, and mine, and the world.”</p>\n<p>“When?”</p>\n<p>“The Bells will tell you better than I can. My guess⁠—” He made himself say it. “Weeks. Your weeks. Maybe less. And the Lace⁠—” he nodded at the window, where the violet glow came in “⁠—the Lace is part of it. When it reaches the Rim, it’s over.”</p>\n<p>Crane was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” he said at last. “I thought it was something like that. I have thought it for some years.”</p>\n<p>“You knew?”</p>\n<p>“I have the Tollers’ numbers. The true ones. They come to me every seventh round, as they’ve come to every First Steward since the Founders.” He moved his hand slightly on the table. “I can read a curve, Relief. I’m not a physicist. But I can read a curve.”</p>\n<p>“And you haven’t told them.”</p>\n<p>“No.” Crane looked at him steadily. “I haven’t told them.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“I want to ask you for something,” said Crane.</p>\n<p>The lamp flickered between them. Outside the window, the long swell of the Bell began, rising up through the floor.</p>\n<p>“In an hour,” said Crane, “I’m going to have to take you to the Commons. There is no way to avoid it. Half the city saw you carried here. The other half has heard. They’ll come whether I call them or not, and it will be better if I call them. There are twenty thousand people who will stand in the Commons tonight and look at you.” He waited while the Bell reached its height and the shutters on the window hummed faintly and fell still. “They’ve been waiting for you for a hundred and sixty-eight years. Every one of them was told as a child that one day you’d come. And now you have, and in a few weeks, by your own account, they’re all going to die.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.” Crane accepted the correction gravely. “Maybe. So I’d like to ask you — when you stand up in front of them tonight — to tell them it will be all right.”</p>\n<p>Cas said nothing.</p>\n<p>“I’m not asking you to lie,” said Crane. “I’m asking you to be kind. They’re going to look at you and they’re going to want to know one thing, and it isn’t the physics. It’s whether they’re alone. Tell them they’re not. Tell them the Outside remembers them. Tell them you’ve come to help. Tell them to keep the Watch.” His voice was very gentle. “Let them be calm. For the time they have left. Let them spend it with their children instead of screaming in the lanes. Let them die, if they’re going to die, with the Lamp over them and the Relief among them and the Watch kept, instead of — the other way. I’ve read the old books. I know what the other way looks like.” He paused. “It’s the only mercy I have to give them. I’m asking you to help me give it.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the lamp. At the little flame, steady in its dish of oil.</p>\n<p>“Can it be stopped?” said Crane.</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>The old man’s eyes moved very slightly.</p>\n<p>“There’s a mechanism,” said Cas. “In the Crown. At the top of Spindle One. The woman who built this place designed it for exactly this. It won’t save the station, but it might — it might close the Lamp. Seal it. Before the debt comes due.” He hesitated. “It’s locked. It needs a key.”</p>\n<p>Crane had gone quite white.</p>\n<p>“A key,” he said.</p>\n<p>“A black key. About so long.” Cas held up a finger. “Keelstone. With a⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Our Founder gave the key to the Lamp.”</p>\n<p>“He dropped it.”</p>\n<p>The words were out before he could stop them. They sat on the table between them next to the lamp. Crane looked at them.</p>\n<p>“In Holdfast,” he said finally, very softly, “we say <em>gave</em>.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Commons had been Assembly Bay One. The sign was still over the great doors — ASSEMBLY BAY 1 — CAPACITY 2,500 — and someone had painted over the capacity with a lamp. It was a vast hangar of a place, the biggest single space in the Vault’s floor, where in the station’s working days the Lattice crews had mustered by the hundred at the start of every shift to be briefed and sent up the spindles. Its roof had been partly stripped for materials generations ago, so that it was open to the sky in long strips between the girders, and through the gaps the violet light came down, and the Lace.</p>\n<p>There were twenty thousand people in it. There were more outside, packed into the lanes around it, up on the roofs, hanging off the scaffolds. He could hear them before he saw them — a sound like the sea, like surf on a beach — and when the Wardens carried him in through a side door onto a raised platform at the front of the hall, the sound stopped, all at once, as though a door had shut on it.</p>\n<p>He was standing. He had insisted. Osk was at one elbow and another Warden at the other, and he was in the grey liner with a Holdfast coat over it that someone had found for him, and he was standing, and it was taking everything he had.</p>\n<p>The faces went back and back. A sea of them. Pale, broad, heavy-boned faces under the violet light, upturned, silent, every one of them looking at him. Men and women and children. Old people held up by younger ones. Babies. He saw a woman in the front row with a baby asleep against her shoulder and a little girl of five or six holding her hand, standing on tiptoe, straining to see. He saw an old man weeping without any sound. He saw Wardens along the walls with their staves and their hard hats. He saw, on the wall behind him, a big board — a sort of scoreboard, black, with white numerals painted on hanging plates — that said BELL 119,472 and under it INTERVAL: 12 and a word he couldn’t read.</p>\n<p>Muster Point B was a yellow-painted square on the floor at the foot of the platform. It was surrounded by a low rail of polished brass. Inside it, on the black stone, the paint was worn down to bare stone in the shape of knees.</p>\n<p>Crane stood beside him. He raised his hands. And the whole hall — twenty thousand voices — began to sing.</p>\n<p>Cas knew the tune.</p>\n<p>It took him a few bars. It was slow, much slower than he knew it, and the words were different — worn smooth, the way words get worn when they’re passed through a hundred mouths, then a thousand, then a hundred thousand; the rhymes had slipped and the lines had changed and whole verses had grown out of nothing. But under it, unmistakable, was the Tending. The anthem. The children’s choir version he’d heard on the Tier Nine lampposts at dawn yesterday, thirty-three hours ago, a hundred and sixty-eight years ago. <em>Tend the flame, tend the flame, for the ones who come after.</em> Here, it had become: <em>Tend the Lamp, tend the Lamp, till the Relief comes after.</em> Twenty thousand people, singing it very slowly under the Lace, in a hall where the paint on the floor had been worn through by knees.</p>\n<p>He had to hold on to Osk’s arm.</p>\n<p>When they’d finished, Crane spoke. It was a call-and-response, and the whole hall knew it.</p>\n<p>“Who keeps the Watch?”</p>\n<p>“<em>We keep the Watch.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Until?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Until Relief.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And when Relief comes?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Then we are relieved.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said Halloran Crane, into the silence, “has come.”</p>\n<p>The sound the hall made was not a cheer. It was something older than cheering. It went up into the girders and the gaps in the roof and out into the lanes, where the people outside took it up, until the whole city seemed to be making it. Somewhere at the back someone was screaming. People were kneeling, falling to their knees in waves, clinging to each other. The woman in the front row with the baby had her face in her free hand. The little girl was jumping up and down.</p>\n<p>Crane turned to him. He said nothing. He just looked at him, courteously, and stepped back.</p>\n<p>Cas faced the hall.</p>\n<p>He had meant to be vague. He’d lain on the bed in the small stone room while they waited, and planned it: a few careful sentences that wouldn’t be lies and wouldn’t be the truth. <em>I’ve come from the Outside. The Outside is still there. I’ve come to try to help.</em> All true. All nothing. He’d been good at sentences like that for fifteen years. He had a whole professional life of them.</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. And he saw the little girl in the front row. She’d stopped jumping. She was standing very still, holding her mother’s hand, looking up at him with an expression of such total, perfect, open trust that it went into him like a blade.</p>\n<p>“I’ve come from Outside,” he said.</p>\n<p>His voice was thin. It didn’t carry. But the hall was so silent that it did.</p>\n<p>“The Outside’s still there. It hasn’t forgotten you.” He swallowed. “I was sent to help you. And I — we’re working on it. There are people working on it. Keep the Watch. Keep it a little longer. It’s going to be⁠—”</p>\n<p>He stopped.</p>\n<p>The little girl was looking at him.</p>\n<p>“It’s going to be all right,” said Casimir Hale.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d been loved before. He supposed he had. Sabine had loved him, once, for a while, before the Review and the Standby and the silence. His mother had loved him. Nell loved him, in the fierce unforgiving way of ten-year-olds.</p>\n<p>He had never been loved like this.</p>\n<p>It came up at him out of that hall like heat out of a furnace. Twenty thousand people, weeping, singing, reaching toward him, calling out to him. A man at the front threw something up onto the platform — a little carved lamp on a cord, yellow plastic, like the one on the body in the conduit — and then someone else threw another, and another, until the platform was scattered with them. Mothers held up their babies. An old woman at the brass rail of Muster Point B was kissing the floor. Every face that he looked at looked back at him with a joy that was so complete, so helpless, that it didn’t seem possible it could fit inside a person.</p>\n<p>It felt wonderful.</p>\n<p>That was the thing he’d remember afterwards, lying awake. Not the guilt — that came later, and it came hard. But in the moment, standing there with Osk holding his arm and the whole of Holdfast pouring its love up at him like light: it felt <em>wonderful</em>. It felt like the warmest, easiest, most natural thing in the world. He had told them a lie and they loved him for it, and every nerve in his body wanted to stay there, in that, forever, and tell them another.</p>\n<p>That was the horror of it. That was the whole machine. He’d thought he understood the Commonweal. He’d been inside it all his life. He had never once, until that moment, understood why it worked.</p>\n<p>Then he saw her.</p>\n<p>She was at the very back of the hall, by the great doors, standing in a gap in the crowd with her back against the wall and her arms folded. Everyone around her was singing. She wasn’t. Everyone around her was weeping or kneeling or reaching up toward the platform. She was standing perfectly still, looking at him over twenty thousand heads, with the white streak in her hair and the black key at her throat.</p>\n<p>There was nothing in her face. No anger. No contempt. No disappointment, even — he looked for it, and it wasn’t there. There was only patience. That same unhurried patience he’d seen at the top of the well, the look of someone watching a scene they’d seen before, many times, and would wait through to the end.</p>\n<p>It was worse than anything else could have been.</p>\n<p>She met his eyes. And across the whole length of the Commons, over the singing, very clearly, she moved her lips. One word.</p>\n<p><em>Tollers.</em></p>\n<p>Then the crowd shifted, and she was gone.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They gave him a room in the Steward’s House. It was small and square, with a bed that was a wooden frame strung with rope and a mattress stuffed with something that crackled, and a window with a shutter, and a basin, and the suit standing in the corner humming to itself. Osk sat outside the door on a stool. Cas heard him settle there, and sigh, and heard the stool creak under his weight.</p>\n<p>He lay on the bed. The weight lay on him. He could feel his heart labouring. He could feel every one of his ribs.</p>\n<p>After a while he got up, which took a long time, and went to the suit, and took the little recorder out of its chest compartment. It was a slim grey Ministry thing, standard issue, with a crystal store that would outlast the suit. It had a channel that fed the suit’s radio, through the orange puck at the top of the well, down through the Shear, to another puck, and another, and a chair in Lattice Station 14-C.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor with his back against the bed, and pressed the button.</p>\n<p>“Teo,” he said.</p>\n<p>And then he couldn’t think what came after. So he just talked.</p>\n<p>“I’m through. I’m through, I’m alive, the keel held. Your keel. It’s — there’s a city in here, Teo. Up the conduit, on the Anvil. A city. Thirty-one thousand people. It’s a hundred and sixty-eight years old. They’ve got — they’ve got rats, they keep them, they name them after their officials. They’ve got a hymn that’s the Tending. They’ve got the Section 9 placard in a chapel with a candle in front of it. They thought I was a — they called me the Relief. They carried me through the streets. They⁠—” He stopped. “They asked me to tell them it would be all right. So I did. Within an hour. I stood up in front of twenty thousand people and I lied to them and it was the best I’ve felt in fifteen years.” He put his head back against the bed frame. “I don’t know what I’m doing, Teo. I thought I would. I don’t. I’ll send more. Are you all right? You won’t hear this for — I don’t know. Don’t answer. Yes, answer. I’ll wait.”</p>\n<p>He pressed the button again. He sat there.</p>\n<p>Then, after a while, he changed the channel. There was a second channel on the recorder, one that didn’t go anywhere. It just stored.</p>\n<p>“Nell,” he said.</p>\n<p>He sat there on the floor of a stone room in a city that shouldn’t exist, under a sky full of light.</p>\n<p>“You were right,” he said. “There’s one. There’s a star in here. It’s — it’s not a very nice one. It’s too bright to look at. They call it the Lamp. There’s a sort of lace round it, all across the sky, like frost, and it moves. Everything here’s lit by it. There’s no night. They close their shutters when they want to sleep.” He paused. “You’d have liked it. I think. You’d have been frightened of it, and then you’d have wanted to know what it was made of. That’s what I did.”</p>\n<p>He sat there a while longer, holding the recorder.</p>\n<p>“I’m still scared,” he told her. “You said that’s allowed.”</p>\n<p>He pressed the button, and put the recorder down on the floor beside him, and closed his eyes.</p>\n<p>He was nearly asleep when something small and hard hit the shutter.</p>\n<p>He opened his eyes. It came again: <em>tock</em>. A pebble. And a third time. He got up — slowly, slowly — and crossed the room, and eased the shutter open a crack.</p>\n<p>Below the window, in the narrow lane that ran along the back of the Steward’s House, there was a small, thin, very dirty boy with a rat on his shoulder, grinning up at him.</p>\n<p>“She says,” whispered Pim, “the Tollers want you. She says come now, while the Wardens are drunk. She says bring your notebook.”</p>"}, {"file": "08-the-tollers.md", "label": "Chapter 8", "title": "The Tollers", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 6793, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Let them count the Bells. Nobody ever died of counting. It’s drawing the line that does it.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally, Head Toller, in conversation</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">Holdfast at night was not dark. It couldn’t be. The Lamp never set, and the Lace never dimmed, and the violet light lay over the city at every hour exactly as it had lain at noon. But the city made its own night. At the end of second shift a horn blew from the top of the Steward’s House, a long low note, and all across Holdfast the shutters came down — clattering, banging, sliding, squealing on their runners, thousands of them, a sound like a whole city clearing its throat — and when it was over, the lanes were in shadow. Not darkness. A deep, violet, shuttered dusk, lit here and there by the little clay oil lamps in the shrine-niches, where the flames stood up very straight in the still, warm air.</p>\n<p>The Wardens outside the Steward’s House were not, in fact, drunk. They were asleep. Osk was asleep on his stool outside Cas’s door with his hard hat in his lap and his chin on his chest, snoring like distant machinery, and when Cas eased the door open and edged past him in the dark, moving so slowly that each step took a breath, one of Osk’s eyes opened.</p>\n<p>They looked at each other.</p>\n<p>Osk’s eye closed again. The snoring resumed.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Pim had brought the rope-cart. Cas lay in it, under a tarpaulin, and the boy hauled it through the lanes by its long handle, grunting, with Steward Harrow riding on his shoulder, and the cart’s iron wheels made a small patient rattle on the keelstone that echoed off the shuttered walls.</p>\n<p>“It’s not far,” Pim whispered. “It’s right by the Bell Column. Everyone knows where it is. Nobody goes, except Tollers.” He hauled. “And me. And Ione. I bring them rats.”</p>\n<p>“To eat?”</p>\n<p>“<em>No.</em>” He sounded scandalised. “To <em>keep</em>. Gran Perpetua likes the brown ones.” A pause. “Well. Sometimes to eat.”</p>\n<p>The lanes twisted and climbed and dropped. Once they went under a walkway where someone was sitting in the shadows, smoking something that smelled of burnt grass, and the someone said, “Pim,” and Pim said, “Brother Coll,” and that was all. Once a Warden went by at the end of a lane with a lamp on a pole, and Pim stopped dead and held his breath and the cart was still until the light had gone. Once, through a gap between two containers, Cas saw the Commons — its broken roof, the girders black against the Lace — and heard, very faintly, singing still going on inside it. Some of them hadn’t gone home. Some of them were still there, singing the Tending, in the middle of the night, for him.</p>\n<p>He shut his eyes under the tarpaulin.</p>\n<p>The Bell came as they arrived. He felt it start in the cart’s wheels, rising, and it rose and rose and went on rising, stronger than he’d felt it anywhere in the city, until the tarpaulin over him was trembling and the cart’s handle was humming in Pim’s hands. It didn’t fade after ninety seconds. It went on — lingering, ringing, a long reverberation that seemed to come up out of the ground and hang in the air around them like the note after a struck bell.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Pim, pleased, as though he’d arranged it. “That’s why it’s here. It comes up here first. You can feel it best here. Gran says it’s the only place in Holdfast where the Bell’s <em>honest</em>.”</p>\n<p>Cas pushed the tarpaulin back.</p>\n<p>Toller’s Tower stood at the edge of a small square, and it was the strangest building he’d seen yet. At its heart was a pillar — a single massive column of black keelstone, perhaps three metres across, rising out of the Anvil and up, bare and smooth, thirty metres or more into the air, where it ended in nothing. Some structural member of the old Vault, some support for a gantry long since stripped. Around it, clinging to it, the Tollers had built their tower: a scaffold frame that went up the column in a spiral, storey after storey, each floor a platform of plank and plate lashed to the pipe, each one hung with things. As Cas looked up at it in the violet dusk he saw pendulums — dozens of pendulums, hanging from the platforms on long wires and rods and chains, some of them swinging, most of them still. He saw great glass carboys of water on shelves, with pipes and taps and marks on their sides. He saw a huge wooden wheel, like a mill-wheel, turning very slowly. He saw, at the very top, a platform with a sort of long tube mounted on a swivel, pointing up at the sky.</p>\n<p>And he saw that the whole column — the whole three-metre, thirty-metre pillar of black keelstone, as far up as he could see — was covered in white marks.</p>\n<p>“Come on,” said Pim, hauling at the cart. “She’s waiting. She hates waiting. She says she’s done enough of it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The door at the bottom of the tower was open, and inside was a single round room built around the base of the column. It was lit by a dozen lamps. It was cluttered beyond belief — shelves and shelves of ledgers and papers and jars and instruments, a workbench covered in springs and gears, a pot of something steaming on a little stove — and it smelled of oil and paper and algae soup. There were people in it. Four or five, in the long grey smocks he’d come to recognise as the Tollers’, all of them turning to look at the door as it opened.</p>\n<p>And in a chair beside the column, with one hand resting flat on the black stone, sat a very small, very old woman.</p>\n<p>She was tiny. Even for Holdfast, even hunched in a chair, she was tiny — a little bent bundle of grey smock and white hair, her feet in felt slippers not reaching the floor. Her face was so wrinkled it looked like crumpled paper, and her hands were knotted like roots. But her eyes, when they came up to Cas’s, were black and sharp and bright as a bird’s, and they went over him from head to foot in about a second.</p>\n<p>“You’re the Relief?” said Perpetua Tally.</p>\n<p>“So I’m told.”</p>\n<p>“You look like you need a sit-down.” She jerked her chin at the Tollers. “Tobiah. Chair. Before he falls on my instruments.”</p>\n<p>A young man with round spectacles made of instrument lenses brought a chair. Cas sat in it. It was the most grateful he’d ever been for a chair in his life. Pim, behind him, had pulled the cart inside and was busy letting Steward Harrow down onto the floor, where the rat ran immediately to the old woman’s slippers and sat up on them.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Harrow,” said Perpetua, without looking down. “You’re getting fat.” Her eyes stayed on Cas. “So. Thirty-three hours.”</p>\n<p>“She told you.”</p>\n<p>“Ione told me what you said. At the well.” Perpetua’s hand moved very slightly on the column, the way you might stroke an animal. “Thirty-three hours and a bit. That was what you said? <em>And a bit.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Hmph.” She turned her head. “Tobiah. Last Bell.”</p>\n<p>The young man with the lens-spectacles went to a ledger on a stand by the column — a vast, thick, leather-bound thing, its pages dense with columns of figures — and ran his finger down the latest page.</p>\n<p>“Bell one hundred and nineteen thousand, four hundred and eighty-nine,” he read. “Interval eleven minutes fifty. Our reckoning, Outside⁠—” he did something quick with a slide-rule “⁠—thirty-three hours, eleven minutes, and twenty-nine seconds.”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-three hours, eleven minutes, twenty-nine seconds,” said Perpetua Tally to the Relief. “<em>And a bit.</em>” She sniffed. “They send us a man from Outside after a hundred and sixty-eight years, and he can’t tell the time.”</p>\n<p>Cas stared at her.</p>\n<p>“You knew,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Of course we knew.” She lifted her hand from the column and waved it, a small impatient gesture, at the walls, the ledgers, the pendulums, the whole cluttered tower. “What did you think we were doing in here? Knitting?”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They had always known.</p>\n<p>That was the thing she told him, over the next hour, in the round room at the foot of the tower, with the lamps burning and the soup going round in tin cups and the Tollers perched on stools and shelves listening. They had always known. Ruth Tally had known — Ruth, who’d been a shift engineer, who had pulled the hammer herself, who’d understood exactly what it was and what it meant. <em>One Bell, one second</em>, Ruth had told her children. <em>We are living faster than the world. The Outside is not burned. It is only slow.</em> And her children had told theirs. And for seven generations the Tallys and those who married into them and those they took on as apprentices had counted every Bell, and marked it, and measured it, and kept the count in secret, and known.</p>\n<p>“Look at it,” said Perpetua, and pointed up at the column. “Go on. Look properly.”</p>\n<p>He looked.</p>\n<p>The white marks on the black stone were tallies. Strokes, in chalk and white paint and something scratched, in groups — he stood up, painfully, with Tobiah’s arm under his, and went close — in groups of five, the fifth stroke across the four. Rows of them. Twelve groups to a row: sixty strokes. And every sixty rows, a long ruled line right round the column, and a number painted beside it.</p>\n<p><em>1.</em></p>\n<p><em>2.</em></p>\n<p><em>3.</em></p>\n<p>He tilted his head back and looked up the column. Up and up, spiralling round it, rows and rows and rows of tiny white strokes, and every so often a ruled line, and a number. He could see them going up into the shadows of the upper floors. <em>15. 16.</em> Up past the pendulums. <em>22. 23.</em></p>\n<p>“Outside Hours,” said Perpetua. “Three thousand six hundred Bells. One hour, Outside. The first Hour took twenty-seven years. The second took twenty-three. The one we’re in now will take about a month. Ruth marked the first one herself, with a nail, in the stone. Tobiah — show him.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah held up a lamp to a place near the bottom of the column, just above head height, where the stone was dark and smooth and polished by hands. There, among the chalk strokes, was a line that hadn’t been chalked. It had been scratched. Deep, wavering, careful, a line gouged into keelstone — which nothing could scratch, Cas thought, and then realised that it had taken her years, years of going back to it and going over it and going over it with a nail, until it was deep enough to last.</p>\n<p>Beside it, scratched the same way, a single word.</p>\n<p><em>MADDY</em></p>\n<p>“Her daughter,” said Perpetua. “Outside. She was seven.”</p>\n<p>Cas couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“We’ve been counting her night,” said Perpetua Tally. “All of us. For a hundred and sixty-eight years. That’s what the Tower’s for. Not the Bells. The Bells are just how you count it. We’ve been counting the night of a little girl called Maddy Tally who went to sleep in her gran’s flat on the Outside and hasn’t woken up yet.” She put her hand back on the column. “Thirty-three hours. She’ll be awake by now. She’ll be wondering where her mam is.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came.</p>\n<p>It rose up through the floor, up through the column under the old woman’s hand, and the whole tower hummed with it — the pendulums trembling on their wires, the water in the carboys shivering into rings, the lamps’ flames bowing — and it hung there, ringing, for longer than Cas could hold his breath. Every Toller in the room had gone still. They were counting. He could see it in their faces. Silently, all of them, the way people pray.</p>\n<p>When it had faded, Tobiah went to the column with a stub of chalk and made a stroke.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“The Stewardship knows,” said Perpetua. “Obviously. We send the true count to the First Steward every seventh round, and have done since Founder Crane. He knows the Outside’s alive. They’ve all known. They keep it quiet because the Founder’s Word says the Outside burned, and if the Outside didn’t burn then what are we doing here, and why haven’t we gone, and who told us to stay?” She sipped her soup. “Holdfast isn’t built on the Watch, Relief. It’s built on not asking that question.”</p>\n<p>“They let people count the Bells,” said Cas slowly.</p>\n<p>“Oh, they <em>let</em> people count the Bells. There’s the Public Tally in the Commons, big as a house, you’ll have seen it. Bell number, interval. Everyone knows the Bells are quickening. You can’t hide it. You feel it in your teeth every twelve minutes.” She put down her cup. “Let them count the Bells. Nobody ever died of counting. It’s drawing the line that does it.”</p>\n<p>“The line.”</p>\n<p>“Tobiah.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah brought a book. A big, flat, cloth-bound ledger, very old, and he opened it on the workbench under a lamp and turned it round so Cas could see. It was a graph. Hand-drawn, in ink, on paper ruled in faint squares, and it went on across the page and over onto the next and the next — a hundred and sixty-eight years of it, a column of intervals down the side and a row of Bell numbers along the bottom — and it was a curve.</p>\n<p>He knew it the instant he saw it. It was the curve he’d drawn himself on the backs of envelopes, fifteen years ago, at night, in his flat. It fell away from the top of the page steeply at first, and then less steeply, and then less, a long smooth swoop sliding down toward the bottom of the page — Bell intervals of three days, then a day, then hours, then minutes — never quite reaching the bottom, always getting closer.</p>\n<p>He took the sketchbook out of his coat. His hands were shaking a little. He found a clean page, and a pencil, and did the fit — three points, then five, then ten, off the Tollers’ ledger — and worked the numbers, and there it was. An exponential. A decay, as clean as a textbook, with a time constant of — he converted it — five and a half, six hours. Outside hours.</p>\n<p>“That’s the debt,” he said. “That’s it coming due. That’s the hill coming down.”</p>\n<p>“We don’t know what it is,” said Perpetua. “We know what it <em>does</em>.”</p>\n<p>“And where it goes?” He looked up. “Have you drawn it forward?”</p>\n<p>There was a small silence in the tower.</p>\n<p>“Seren drew it forward,” said Perpetua.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She told it quickly. She didn’t dress it. He thought afterwards that she had probably told it that way, quickly and plainly, many times, to herself.</p>\n<p>Seren Tally had been Perpetua’s daughter. A Toller, like her mother, but a better one: a genius with numbers, with a mind that could hold a curve and see where it was going. Seren had drawn the line forward. Seren had worked out that the Bells would come faster and faster, and at the rate they were going, sooner or later — in her daughter’s lifetime — would come so fast they’d run together into a single note. She’d measured the Lace with the sighting tube at the top of the tower, round after round for ten years, and found it was coming down: not steadily, but faster every year. She’d worked out that it would reach the Rim — the ring round the Lamp at the spindles’ tops — before the Bells ran together. And she’d guessed, without knowing why, that when it reached the Rim, that would be the end of everything.</p>\n<p>“She took it to the First Steward,” said Perpetua. “Halloran. He’d not been Steward long. He thanked her very kindly and told her it must not be spoken. She told him that people had a right to know how long they had. He told her that people had a right to be calm.” She turned her cup in her knotted hands. “She painted it on the wall of the Commons. The line, and the date. Big. In the night, with Ione holding the ladder. Ione was fifteen.”</p>\n<p>“And they⁠—”</p>\n<p>“They gave her to the Drop.”</p>\n<p>Nobody in the room moved.</p>\n<p>“It’s what they do,” said Perpetua, “for the ones who won’t keep the Watch. It’s very old. Founder Crane did it. It’s not an execution. It’s a <em>release</em>. You’re free to go. Down the well. Nobody’s ever stopped at the bottom.” She said it flatly. “Halloran signed it. I suppose he had to. He came to see me, after. He sat in that chair you’re in and he cried.” She looked at Cas with her black bird’s eyes. “I let him. I thought he’d earned it. It’s the only thing I’ve ever given him.”</p>\n<p>Cas thought of the body at the foot of the conduit. The patched, mended clothes. The little yellow lamp worn smooth by fingers.</p>\n<p>“When?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Thirteen years ago.”</p>\n<p>He did the arithmetic. He couldn’t stop himself. Thirteen years ago the Bells had come once every five or six hours; the ratio had been twenty thousand, not seven hundred. He ran it back along the Tollers’ curve. Nineteen hours, outside. Nineteen hours before he’d climbed the ladder in 14-C. She had gone down the Drop at about the time he’d been in a hangar in the Collar, learning to walk in a keel suit.</p>\n<p>“I think I saw her,” he said.</p>\n<p>Perpetua looked at him.</p>\n<p>“At the bottom of the conduit,” he said. “There was — someone. In mended clothes. With a lamp. Carved from a hard hat.”</p>\n<p>“Yellow,” said Perpetua. Her voice had changed.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>The old woman sat without moving. Then she took her hand off the column and put both her hands in her lap, and looked down at them, and her face did something he wasn’t ready for. It crumpled — the whole of it, the paper-crumple of it, all at once — and her mouth opened, and she made a small, dry, high sound, like a bird. Tobiah was beside her at once, kneeling by her chair. She didn’t look at him. She sat there with her hands in her lap and made that small sound, over and over, and the tears ran down the creases of her face and dripped off her chin onto the grey smock.</p>\n<p>“I gave her that,” said Perpetua Tally. “I carved it. When she was a baby. For her crib.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was Tobiah who brought out the Log, in the end. Perpetua had gone quiet, sitting with her eyes closed and her hand back on the column, and nobody had spoken, and then Tobiah had got up and gone to a shelf and taken down a box — a heavy steel box with a Works stencil on its side, very old, very dented — and put it on the workbench and opened it.</p>\n<p>Inside, wrapped in layer on layer of cloth, was a book.</p>\n<p>It was a Ministry logbook. He knew the type: A4, hard-backed, blue cloth, with the Continuance flame stamped on the cover and HOLLOWELL DRAW — CROWN — SHIFT LOG in faded gold. The station had issued them by the thousand. This one was swollen to twice its thickness, crammed with extra pages sewn in, bristling with slips of paper, its spine cracked and mended with thread, its corners worn to round. It looked like it had been opened ten thousand times.</p>\n<p>“Ruth’s,” said Tobiah quietly. “She had it in her hand in the lift. She didn’t know she’d picked it up.”</p>\n<p>Cas opened it.</p>\n<p>The first pages were the ordinary Crown log for the night of the test, in a neat engineer’s pencil. <em>01:50 Dep. Min. Crane + crew arr. for Deepening footage. 01:52 Advised Dep. Min. of T6 bearing temp (+11). Advised protocol requires Chief Eng. Advised interlocks.</em> He could see her in the pencil: the neatness, the care, the way she wrote <em>advised</em>. <em>01:58 Dir. Obi has deferred to Ministry. Proceeding under protest. Note: under protest.</em></p>\n<p>Then a gap. A blank line.</p>\n<p>Then, in the same pencil but a different hand — a hand that shook — <em>02:05 T6 unseated at Draw 135. Boundary failure. Interlocks failed. Draw would not zero. Time grad. across Crown, severe. Attempted Hollowell Clause. Dep. Min. Crane had the key and would not give it. At 02:09 approx gantry failed, key lost, fell out through window glass into the Lesion as if it were called. Clause not possible. 02:14 abandoned Crown. Pulled the Hammer.</em></p>\n<p>And under that, very small:</p>\n<p><em>I did it. I said 135. It was me.</em></p>\n<p>He turned the page.</p>\n<p>After that, it was a different book. The log format was gone. The pencil went on, but it was a diary now, a history, a record. The first days in the Vault. The Drop — the first men who’d tried the conduits, and what had come back up. Aldous Crane under the Placard. The Sealing. Names, lists of names, the living and the dead. Rations. Water. The algae. The first birth. Page after page, and the handwriting getting smaller and smaller as the paper ran out, until it was so small he had to hold the book up to the lamp to read it, and in places the pages were sewn in from other things: the backs of forms, the insides of packets, a sheet of pale thin stuff that looked like it had been made from algae pulp. The years went by in the margins. <em>Year 3. Year 7. Year 20, Bell 2,560.</em> He turned pages and turned pages. It was Ruth Tally’s whole life. It was all there.</p>\n<p>And there were letters.</p>\n<p>They came every few pages, all through the book, set off from the rest with a line drawn across the page. Every one of them began the same way.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy —</em></p>\n<p>He read the first one. It was dated <em>Day 4</em>.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy — I don’t know if you’ll ever read this. I’m going to write it anyway because otherwise I’ll go mad. Mum is in a place a very long way away and I can’t come home yet. It isn’t because I don’t want to. There’s a big light here and it’s got stuck and I have to help. I’m sorry I didn’t say goodbye properly. I had to go to work early. You were asleep with your feet out of the covers. I tucked them in. Did you notice? Probably not. Be good for Nana. I love you more than the whole Draw. — Mum.</em></p>\n<p>He turned pages. <em>Maddy — today we planted the first beans.</em> <em>Maddy — the Bell only comes every three days now, and I’ve started to feel like it’s your breathing.</em> <em>Maddy — I got married today. His name is Tomas and he works the Anvil pumps and he makes me laugh. I know that’s strange. I hope you don’t mind. You’d like him.</em> <em>Maddy — you have a brother. I’ve called him Hal.</em> <em>Maddy — twenty years. For you it’s about forty minutes. I’ve done the sum a thousand times. You’re still asleep. I lie awake and think about you asleep, and it’s the only thing that helps.</em></p>\n<p>One entry, near the middle of the book — <em>Year 31</em> — wasn’t a letter. It was set apart from the rest by a line above and below, and it was short, and he read it twice without understanding it.</p>\n<p><em>I have never told anyone this. At 02:09, after the key fell, I saw a man at the Keystone. Kneeling where I knelt. Dressed close, in grey, like a diver. A cord knotted round his wrist. His hand on a wheel that isn’t there. He looked at me as if he knew me. He was so calm. Then there was no one. I have thought about him every day for thirty-one years. I think he was the witness. I think, whoever he is, he is still there.</em></p>\n<p>He turned the page. There were more letters.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t read any more. He sat with the book open on his knees and the lamp burning and his eyes blurred.</p>\n<p>Then he turned to the last page.</p>\n<p>The pencil was almost too faint to read. The hand was an old woman’s hand, shaking badly, the letters large and wandering because the eyes that made them couldn’t see small any more.</p>\n<p><em>Maddy — I am eighty-eight. Year 50. Bell 7,100. For you it is about two hours, so it’s still the middle of the night, and you’ve probably turned over and pushed the covers off again.</em></p>\n<p><em>I’ve had a whole life here, baby. It was a good one, mostly. I had your brothers and your sister and I had Tomas for forty years and I have eleven grandchildren and they count the Bells for me now because my eyes are gone. I made a lot of mistakes. The biggest one was the first one. I’m sorry for it every day.</em></p>\n<p><em>I want you to know I thought about you every single day. Every one. Nineteen thousand days, give or take. I never once let myself forget you were there, asleep, in the same night. It was the thing that held me up.</em></p>\n<p><em>If someone ever brings you this — and they will; I’ve told them to — then you’ll be seven, and I’ll have been gone a long time, and that’s all right. Don’t be sad. I had my life. You go and have yours. Be brave. Be kind. Tell the truth, even when it’s hard. Especially then.</em></p>\n<p><em>Love you more than the whole Draw.</em></p>\n<p><em>Mum.</em></p>\n<p>He closed the book.</p>\n<p>“She’s seven,” he said. He didn’t mean to say it aloud. He heard it come out of him. “She’s still seven. Right now. It’s the day after Continuance Day. It’s — she’ll have had her breakfast. She’s seven.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Perpetua Tally. She had opened her eyes. She was looking at him steadily across the lamplit room with her wet, bright, black eyes. “We know, Relief. We’ve always known. What we didn’t know — what we never had — was anyone who’d <em>seen</em> it.”</p>\n<p>She reached out, with a knotted old hand, and touched his knee.</p>\n<p>“Thank you,” she said. “You’ve no idea. Thank you.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was then that Ione came down from the roof.</p>\n<p>He heard her before he saw her — a light, quick tread on the spiral stair that ran up round the column, coming down fast from the upper floors — and then she swung round the last turn and dropped the last few steps and was in the room. She had a rope over her shoulder and a notebook in her hand and chalk on her fingers, and she stopped at the foot of the stair and looked round at all of them: at Perpetua with her wet face and Tobiah kneeling, and the Tollers on their stools, and Pim with his rat, and Cas with the Log on his knees.</p>\n<p>“You’ve read it,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She came across the room and crouched in front of his chair so that her face was level with his, the way you’d crouch to talk to a child, or to someone sick. She looked at him for a moment with that unhurried, attentive gaze. Then she said: “The Lamp can be closed. There’s a lock in the Crown. I have its key.”</p>\n<p>And she lifted the cord from round her neck and held out her hand.</p>\n<p>The key lay on her palm.</p>\n<p>It was black. It was the length of a finger. It had a flat bow and a straight shaft and a blunt, cross-shaped bit, and it was dense — he could see it was dense, it sat in her hand heavier than a thing that size had any right to — and it didn’t reflect the lamplight. It took the light in and gave nothing back. Along the shaft, stamped into it in small square letters:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL · KEYSTONE · 0001</div>\n<p>He’d seen it in a mural, fifteen storeys high. He’d seen it in a pencil log, in the shaking hand of a woman who’d watched it fall. He’d seen it, he thought, somewhere else too — a picture in the training manual, a photograph in the back of Hollowell’s withdrawn paper: <em>the Minister’s key</em>. There had only ever been one.</p>\n<p>“That’s not possible,” he said.</p>\n<p>“No,” agreed Ione.</p>\n<p>“It fell. Into the Lesion. Ruth <em>saw</em> it.” He heard his voice rising. “Your Founder — it’s on a <em>wall</em>, it’s on a wall fifteen storeys high⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Then where did you <em>get</em> it?”</p>\n<p>“I found it,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it simply. Then she sat back on her heels and looked at the key in her hand, and a little of the unhurried patience went out of her face, and something else came into it. Something older. More careful.</p>\n<p>“When I was seventeen,” she said, “I went up. Past where anyone goes. And I got lost, up there, in a place called the Carousel. I’ll tell you about it one day. It’s not a story for tonight.” She turned the key over. “There’s a gantry in the Carousel. A walkway, across a gap. I walked across it — I don’t know. Four thousand times? More? I knew every bolt in it. I knew every mark on it. And one time I walked across it and there was a key lying on it. Right in the middle. Where it had never been, not one time in four thousand. And then it was.”</p>\n<p>Cas stared at her.</p>\n<p>“I picked it up,” said Ione. “I’ve had it ever since.”</p>\n<p>The floor of the round room seemed to tilt under his chair. It wasn’t the weight. It was something in his head. He was thinking of a paper he’d read as a student, an old, strange, half-joking paper from before the Long Heat, that his professor had assigned as a curiosity: two physicists asking what would happen if a time machine were real, and whether an object could go round a loop in time and come back to where it started. They’d called it a jinn. An object with no maker. An object that existed because it existed. That was never made, and never destroyed, and simply went round.</p>\n<p><em>You could find one lying on a walkway</em>, his professor had joked, <em>where it had never been. And you’d have no way to know where it came from. Because it didn’t come from anywhere.</em></p>\n<p>“A jinn,” he said.</p>\n<p>“A what?”</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” He didn’t take his eyes off the key. “Nothing. A word. Go on.”</p>\n<p>“There isn’t any more,” said Ione. “Except that I knew what it was, because I’d been watching the Crown. In the Carousel, you can see the Crown. The Crown on the night of the Kindling. For three years, I watched it.” She looked up at him. “I watched Ruth try to open the lock under the floor. I watched Founder Crane refuse to give her the key. I watched the walkway go. I saw the key on his chain. I know the lock. I know the plate over it. I could draw you the lock with my eyes shut. And I came down, two days later, with this in my hand, and I told them all: <em>the Lamp can be closed</em>. There’s a lock in the Crown and this is the key and if we get it up there we can put the Lamp out.”</p>\n<p>“And they called you mad,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“They called me Unwatched.” She shrugged very slightly. “It means the same.”</p>\n<p>“She’s not mad,” said Perpetua, from her chair. Her voice was hoarse but perfectly steady. “She’s the only one of us who’s ever <em>been</em> anywhere.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the key. Then at Ione’s face. Then at the key.</p>\n<p>“She isn’t mad,” he said.</p>\n<p>Every head in the room turned.</p>\n<p>“I’ve read the paper that lock was built from,” he said. “The woman who built this place — Hollowell — she wrote it. The last thing she wrote before she died. The Ministry buried it. It’s called the Clause. It’s a way to seal the core — the Lamp — if it ever broke the way it’s broken. Fold it shut. For ever. It’s in the Crown, under a plate in the floor.” He looked at Ione. “There are words on the plate.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Hollowell Clause</em>,” said Ione at once, softly, as if reciting. “<em>For use in catastrophic boundary failure only. The sealing requires a witness.</em>”</p>\n<p>“You’ve read it.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve read it over Ruth’s shoulder a hundred and seventy-five thousand times.” She held his eyes. “Do you know what it means? The last part?”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas. “Nobody does.”</p>\n<p>She nodded slowly, as though that too were something she had half expected.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Why haven’t you gone?” he said, later.</p>\n<p>It was very late. The Tollers had drifted back to their instruments, or to sleep on pallets in the upper floors. Perpetua was dozing in her chair with her hand on the column and Steward Harrow asleep in her lap. Pim was asleep on the floor under the workbench, curled round nothing. The lamps had burned low. Ione sat cross-legged on the floor with her back against a shelf of ledgers, turning the key over and over in her fingers, and Cas sat in his chair with the Log on his knees because he could not bring himself to put it down.</p>\n<p>“I’ve tried,” said Ione. “Twice. After I came back. I went up again at nineteen and again at twenty-three. By the south spindles, both times. Not by the Carousel. I wasn’t⁠—” A pause, very slight. “I wasn’t going back through the Carousel.” She turned the key. “There’s a place, above the Lees, before the Rim, where the Lace comes down. It’s lower every year. You have to go through it to get to the top of Spindle One. And the Lace kills you.”</p>\n<p>“How?”</p>\n<p>“The way it killed my mother, in the Drop.” She said it without any particular weight. “Everything goes different speeds. You come apart. I watched a rat go into it once, on a rope. It went in a rat and came out⁠—” she made a small gesture “⁠—dust, and a bit of fur, and something still moving. I turned back both times. You can’t get through the Lace without something to hold you together.” She looked at the grey suit standing in the corner where Pim had helped him prop it, the keel humming quietly on its back. “I’ve been watching the wells for eleven years.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“Because once, up there, when I was seventeen, I saw someone coming down.” She looked at him, and her gaze was very direct. “Far off. In a suit like that. With a thing on its back, humming. Coming down alone.” She tipped her head at the corner. “That suit. Or one like it. So I knew one would come. Sooner or later. Up out of the floor.” She almost smiled. “You took your time.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what to say to that. He found that he was thinking — in the part of his mind that never stopped doing sums — <em>someone in a keel suit coming down alone. From the Crown. In the future, seen from the past.</em> And under that, a cold slow thought he didn’t want to look at: <em>there are only three suits</em>.</p>\n<p>“You’re the first thing to come up through the floor in my lifetime,” said Ione, “that could get through the Lace.”</p>\n<p>“And you’ve got the key.”</p>\n<p>“And I’ve got the key.”</p>\n<p>“And you know the way.”</p>\n<p>“I know the way,” said Ione, “as far as the Lace. And I know the Crown. Every inch of it. I know which panel sticks and which lamp was flickering and where Ruth put her coffee cup down. And I know the lock.” She held up the key between two fingers. “You know what the lock does. You know the paper. You know what it <em>means</em>. I’ve watched it for three years and I don’t know what it means.”</p>\n<p>“Neither do I. Not all of it.”</p>\n<p>“More than me.”</p>\n<p>“More than you.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. He looked at her. Neither of them said anything.</p>\n<p>“Spindle Four,” she said at last. “It’s dead. Nobody uses it, nobody watches it. The Stewards think it’s cursed because the lift fell in the Founders’ time. It goes all the way up. I’ll need to go up it first, on my own, and look at the Lees. They move. They’ve moved since I was up there. Everything’s moved. And you⁠—” She looked at him critically. “You need to be able to stand up.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“For the Lees? Ten rounds. Twenty. For you to stand properly⁠—” She considered. “More.”</p>\n<p>“We don’t have more.”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione Tally. “We don’t.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She took him up to the top of the tower before dawn.</p>\n<p>It took a long time. He had to stop on every landing. She waited on every landing, not helping unless he asked, which he didn’t, and then did, and she put her shoulder under his arm without comment and took most of his weight up the last three flights as though he were a bag of rope.</p>\n<p>At the top was the platform with the long tube on its swivel. A sighting tube, she said. Seren had built it. It was pointed up at the sky at a steep angle, sighted on the top of Spindle Two, and there were marks scratched on the brass ring at its base — dozens of them, hundreds, each with a small number beside it.</p>\n<p>“Look,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He bent to the eyepiece. Through the tube he saw the top of Spindle Two, very far off, a dark lattice against the glare, and the Rim beyond it, a dark line. And coming down out of the sky above it, the long bright trailing threads of the Lace, like the tendrils of some vast drifting creature. There were crosshairs in the tube — two fine wires. The lowest tendril hung just below the crossing.</p>\n<p>“Every round, at dawn, I sight the lowest thread,” said Ione, “and mark it.”</p>\n<p>She showed him yesterday’s mark on the brass ring, and the day before’s, and the one before. He could see the pattern without her explaining. The marks crept down the ring. Close together, years ago. Further apart. Now each one was a clear finger’s width below the last.</p>\n<p>He put his eye back to the tube and looked at the gap between the lowest thread and the dark line of the Rim. He looked at the marks. He did the sum, and then he did it again with the marks spreading the way they were spreading. Sixty rounds. A few more, perhaps. Perhaps less.</p>\n<p>Then, because he couldn’t not, he turned it into his own time. A month of Holdfast to every hour outside, Perpetua had said, and the months getting shorter. Sixty rounds in here was about two and a half hours out there. Hour thirty-five. Thirty-six. He had stood in a room with gold carpet and told the Minister of Continuance <em>two days, three at the outside</em>, and the Outside’s clock had been sitting at hour thirty-three when he climbed out of the well. The debt wasn’t coming at the end of the week. It was coming this afternoon.</p>\n<p>And in here, it was two months away.</p>\n<p>He stood with his eye to the brass and understood, properly, for the first time, the one thing in this place that was on his side. Out there, on the Collar’s clock, he’d have had a couple of hours to climb four kilometres and open a lock that had been shut for a hundred and sixty-eight years. In here, every round he spent — learning to stand, learning the Lees, learning anything — cost the world outside about two minutes.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t much time. It was more than anyone else was ever going to get.</p>\n<p>Behind them, down in the city, the horn blew from the Steward’s House for first shift. And all across Holdfast, in a great clattering rolling wave that started at the centre and spread outward to the edges, the shutters came up, and the violet light poured into ten thousand windows, and the city woke.</p>\n<p>Ione took a nail from her pocket and scratched a new mark on the brass ring. A finger’s width below yesterday’s.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said. “Now you know how long.”</p>"}, {"file": "09-greenbread.md", "label": "Chapter 9", "title": "Greenbread", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4829, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine. And if the one of us falls, the other one hauls.</p><span class=\"attr\">Holdfast riggers’ wedding vow</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">He learned to stand the way the babies did: badly, and in public, and with a great many people watching.</p>\n<p>Osk taught him. It was not, Cas gathered, a thing Osk had been told to do. It was simply a thing Osk had decided needed doing, on the first morning, when he opened the door of the little stone room and found the Relief of Holdfast on his hands and knees on the floor, trying to get up, and failing, and trying again.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Osk. “Not like that. That’s how you break a knee.”</p>\n<p>He came in and knelt down beside him. He was so big that kneeling, he was nearly Cas’s height standing.</p>\n<p>“Babies,” he said, “in the Crèche. First thing we teach them. You don’t push up. The weight wins if you push. You <em>roll</em>.” He demonstrated, a huge slow shifting of hips and shoulders. “Get your bones under you. Stack them. Like scaffold. Then the weight goes down through the bones into the floor and the floor holds it, not you. You’re not holding it. You’re just <em>stacked</em>.” He looked at Cas. “Try.”</p>\n<p>Cas tried. It took him four attempts. On the fifth, something shifted in his hips and he found his feet under him and came up — not pushing, somehow, just unfolding upward like a ladder being raised — and he was standing, and it didn’t hurt, or not as much, and he was so surprised that he laughed.</p>\n<p>Osk nodded, grave as a judge.</p>\n<p>“Good,” he said. “Now do it a hundred times.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the second round, a little after the start of second shift, his suit began to groan.</p>\n<p>He was in his room in the Steward’s House, lying on the rope bed. The suit stood in the corner where it always stood, humming its keel-note to itself, and without any warning it made a sound. A low sound. A long, slow, deep sound like a foghorn heard across miles of water, or like a whale — <em>Farr said whales</em>, Teo had said, <em>so I get birds</em> — it came out of the suit’s helmet speaker and filled the little room, vast and slow and falling, and it went on, and on, and didn’t stop.</p>\n<p>He sat up. He knew what it was. He’d known what it was the instant it started.</p>\n<p>It went on for the rest of that round and into the next.</p>\n<p>He didn’t have to listen. The suit was catching every second of it, and at the end it would give the whole thing back to him at the proper speed. He listened anyway. He sat on the floor of the stone room with his back against the bed and his knees up and stayed with it, and slept for some of it, there on the floor, and woke to it still going, and he didn’t go to the Tollers that night, and when Osk opened the door once in the middle of it to see what in the name of the Lamp the noise was, Cas just held up a hand, and Osk looked at the suit, and at him, and went out again and shut the door without a sound.</p>\n<p>It was a voice. He could hear, if he listened, that it was a voice — the rise and fall of it, the rhythm of speech slowed so far down that it was just a shape — and he sat there and let it pour over him, a single human sentence stretched over most of an hour, and tried to guess the words, and couldn’t.</p>\n<p>At the end of it the suit went <em>ding</em>.</p>\n<p>And then, suddenly, perfectly clearly, as though she were in the room:</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk. “Right. Your first chirp’s come through. You want to know what it sounds like this end? It sounds like a budgie fell into my radio. About three minutes after you went up that ladder. Then the suit sat there chewing on it for a minute and a half and went <em>ding</em>, and then it was you. Talking about rats.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand over his face.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been gone five minutes,” said Teo, “and apparently you’ve found a city, and they’ve made you a prophet, and they’ve got the Section 9 placard in a church. I leave you alone for <em>five minutes</em>, Hale.” A pause. He heard her breathe. “Thirty-one thousand. Christ. Thirty-one thousand people. Up that ladder. I keep looking at the ladder. I can see it from the chair, through the window. It just looks like a ladder.”</p>\n<p>A longer pause.</p>\n<p>“You lied to them. Okay. You told me that like it’s the end of the world. It isn’t. You’re not the first man who ever stood up in front of a crowd and told them it’d be all right because it was easier than breaking their hearts. I’ve done it. I’ve done it on the top of a flood barrier with the water coming up, and I’d do it again, and so would you.” Another pause. “But you’ll tell them. Not today. Not till you’ve got something to give them instead. But you’ll tell them the truth before you go up that spindle, Hale, or I’ll come up that ladder in this chair and tell them myself.”</p>\n<p>He laughed. He couldn’t help it. It came out wet.</p>\n<p>“Also,” said Teo, “I’m fine. You’ll want to know that. The chair’s fine. The lee’s fine. It’s not moving. I found a biscuit in the first-aid kit. It’s a bit old. Nobody’s called. The Collar’ll be wondering where we are. Let them wonder.” A small snort. “Prophet inside an hour. That’s a Civil Defence record, that.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“I put the blanket from the first-aid kit over her,” said Teo, more quietly. “The lady at the bottom of the ladder. With the little lamp. I couldn’t stand her lying there. I went down through the hatch and put it over her. I hope that’s all right. Whoever she was.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t breathe for a moment.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo. “That’s three minutes of me you’ve had. That’ll have taken you — I don’t know. Most of a day? Don’t answer that, I can’t work it out, Farr was the one for sums.” And her voice caught, just slightly, on the name, and went on. “Send me more. Send me all of it. I’ve got nothing to do in this chair. Tell your congregation there’s a woman down here says hello.” A breath. “And look after yourself, you daft sod.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He sat on the floor for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then he got up — rolled, stacked his bones, stood, the way Osk had taught him — and took out the recorder, and pressed the button, and told Teo about Osk, and learning to stand like a baby, and a tower full of pendulums, and an old woman who’d corrected him to the minute, and a Log full of letters to a little girl called Maddy who was seven. And he told her that the lady at the bottom of the ladder was called Seren, and that she’d been the cleverest Toller in two hundred years, and that she’d painted the truth on the wall of the Commons, and that her mother had carved the little lamp, and that it had been for her crib. And he told her <em>thank you for the blanket</em>, and found that he couldn’t say anything else for a while, and just sat there with the button pressed, not saying anything, letting it record the silence.</p>\n<p>After that he sent her something every round. Whatever the round had held, into the recorder, last thing at night: a round’s worth of Holdfast at a time. He didn’t wait for answers. He’d done the sum. To her, down there in her chair, they’d be arriving about every four minutes, like a tide coming in.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He stood up a hundred times a round, for sixteen rounds, the way Osk had told him to, and in between he was the Relief.</p>\n<p>That was the other thing he learned. How to be it.</p>\n<p>It was a job. Crane made it clear, gently, from the first morning, that it was a job and that it had duties, and that the duties were public. The Relief was carried through the lanes on the litter, on high rounds, so that people could see him. The Relief sat in a chair on the steps of the Steward’s House for an hour each first shift, and people came up the steps one by one and knelt and touched his hand, or his coat, or just looked at him, and went away weeping. The Relief blessed things. Nobody had told him he was going to bless things. A woman came up the steps with a new baby in a sling and held it out to him and said <em>bless her, Relief</em>, and he looked at Crane standing beside the chair and Crane looked back with no expression at all, and so he put his hand on the baby’s head — so small, so warm, so heavy for its size — and said <em>bless you</em>, and the woman burst into tears, and after that there was no stopping it. He blessed babies. He blessed a new rope for a rigger crew. He blessed the Tanks, standing on a gallery over the great algae vats where the dead of Holdfast went to feed the living, looking down at the slow green water, saying <em>bless</em>, <em>bless</em>, and thinking of Ben.</p>\n<p>He did not say it would be all right again. He never said it again. But he didn’t have to. He had said it once, and they had heard it, and it was in every face that looked up at him from the foot of the Steward’s steps.</p>\n<p>Crane watched him do all of it with that tired, courteous half-smile, and at the end of each round thanked him, and never once asked him how he felt.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At night he went to the Tollers.</p>\n<p>Osk slept outside his door every night on the stool, and every night, when Cas went past him in the shuttered dusk, Osk slept a little more deeply, and never once woke. On the fourth night, Cas found that someone had oiled the hinge of his door so that it didn’t squeak.</p>\n<p>Pim brought the cart the first few nights. After that, Cas walked. It took him most of a shift to get across the city the first time on his own feet, stopping every twenty steps with his hand on a wall. By the tenth night it took him an hour. By the fourteenth, he could do it without stopping, if he didn’t mind that his heart was going like a hammer when he got there, and his knees shook, and he had to sit on the step of the tower for a while before he could climb it.</p>\n<p>They didn’t only work at the tower. More often, as the rounds went on, he found himself in Ione’s loft.</p>\n<p>She lived at the top of a scaffold tower in the Drop District, not far from the well he’d come up — a rickety, swaying, rope-lashed stack of platforms twenty storeys high, where the riggers lived who worked the spindles, and who were, Pim told him with great satisfaction, the roughest people in Holdfast. Her loft was the very top platform, walled with canvas and scrap and open on one side to the sky, so that from the hammock that hung across it you could lie and look straight up past the black mass of Spindle Four into the Lamp. It was full of rope. Rope hung from every beam in coils, in hanks, spliced and whipped and knotted, a hundred kinds. There were harnesses on hooks, and chalk in jars, and plumb bobs of every size hanging on strings, and a workbench, and a little stove, and a shelf of books — station manuals, mostly, their covers long gone, and a few Holdfast things: hand-copied, hand-bound, the pages made of pale algae-paper.</p>\n<p>They worked there. That was what they told themselves they were doing, anyway. There was a great deal to do. The suit’s harness had to be re-rigged for climbing; its hardpoints weren’t built for rope, and she spent three nights splicing a web of rigger’s strapping round it that would let her belay it — him — from above. There were supplies to make: greenbread baked hard as brick, water in skins of rendered algae-leather, cakes of cricket fat. There were the Lees.</p>\n<p>The Lees were the heart of it. She explained them to him on the fifth night, lying on her back on the loft floor with a plumb bob hanging on a string from her fingers, watching it.</p>\n<p>“Look,” she said. “Down here, the Lamp pushes down and the Anvil pulls down and it all goes the same way. Straight. The bob hangs straight.” It did. “But go up a spindle. The further up you go, the harder the Lamp pushes. You weigh more and more. By two kilometres up you weigh half as much again as you do here. By three, twice. By the Rim⁠—” she shrugged “⁠—nobody knows. Nobody’s been to the Rim and come back except me, and I wasn’t counting.”</p>\n<p>“So how did you get there?”</p>\n<p>“The Lees.” She moved the bob. “The spindles are full of keelstone. Struts, braces, cross-members, great thick beams of it. And keelstone pulls, you know that. Like the Anvil pulls. So near a big strut, the strut’s pull goes against the Lamp’s push. They cancel. Not all the way. But some. So there are places up there — pockets — where you weigh less. Sometimes much less. You can rest in them. You can climb from one to the next.” She let the bob swing. “That’s a Lee. The riggers have always known them. The lower ones. I know the high ones.”</p>\n<p>“And they move.”</p>\n<p>“The Lamp’s getting weaker. You know that. The Lightening.” She held the string still. “So the balance moves. Every year, the Lees move up. Or sideways. Or close up and open somewhere else. I haven’t been past the lower ones in five years.” She looked at him. “I’ll have to go and look.”</p>\n<p>She went on the eighth round.</p>\n<p>She went in the night, alone, with two coils of rope and a bag of chalk and a skin of water, and he stood at the foot of Spindle Four in the shuttered dusk and watched her climb until she was a speck and then nothing. She’d said two rounds. She was gone three. He spent those three rounds being the Relief, blessing babies on the steps of the Steward’s House and not hearing a word anyone said to him, and at night lying on the rope bed in the stone room staring at the ceiling, until on the third night Pim’s pebble hit his shutter and he went down to the lane and found her sitting on the Tollers’ step with her hands bandaged in strips of cloth and a rope burn across one cheek, eating greenbread.</p>\n<p>“There’s a way,” she said. “It’s not a good way. But there’s a way.”</p>\n<p>He sat down beside her on the step. His knees shook. He didn’t know if it was the climb down the lane.</p>\n<p>“You said two rounds,” he said.</p>\n<p>She looked at him sideways. And for once there was something in her face that wasn’t patient at all. It was surprised.</p>\n<p>“You were worried,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She went on looking at him for a while. Then she looked away, down the lane, and ate her greenbread.</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s been worried about me,” she said, “in about eleven years.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He ate rat on the eleventh round.</p>\n<p>It was at the market in the Commons Lanes, where the stalls were built into the shells of old cargo containers with their doors swung open, and everything that Holdfast grew or made or salvaged was sold for tokens stamped out of old sheet metal. Ione had taken him there — in the middle of a round, openly, with a hood up over his face because by now there was not a soul in Holdfast who wouldn’t have known him. Greenbread, stacked in dark bricks. Cricket flour in sacks, and the crickets themselves in wire boxes, chirping. Mushrooms of a dozen pale kinds, grown on bales of old insulation in the dark under the Anvil’s edge. Algae in every form: cakes, noodles, a thick green paste in jars, a fermented green wine that Ione said would take the skin off your throat. Salt. Oil. Thread twisted from algae fibre. Needles filed from wire.</p>\n<p>And at one stall, turning on a spit over a little charcoal fire, rats.</p>\n<p>“Go on,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been eating it for ten rounds,” she said. “It’s in the soup. The Tollers’ soup. You had two bowls.”</p>\n<p>“I⁠—” He stopped. “Did I?”</p>\n<p>“Two bowls. You said it was very good.” Her mouth twitched. “Go on.”</p>\n<p>It was very good. It tasted, as everyone who has ever eaten rat has said, like chicken — like very rich, slightly sweet, dark-meat chicken, with a smoky crackle to the skin — and he ate a whole one standing in the lane with the juice running down his wrist, and Ione watched him do it with an expression he’d never seen on her face before: a kind of wicked delight, quickly hidden.</p>\n<p>Pim, who had appeared out of nowhere as he always did, was scandalised.</p>\n<p>“Not <em>mine</em>,” he said. “Not Steward Harrow. Not Steward Crane. Not <em>any</em> of the Stewards. Only the <em>market</em> rats.”</p>\n<p>“Pim names his after the Stewardship,” Ione explained gravely. “All of them. He has a First Steward. He has a Warden-Captain. He has three Toller-Seconds.”</p>\n<p>“I have a Relief,” said Pim. “Now.” He produced it from inside his shirt: a small, fierce, grey rat with a torn ear, which bit him. “He’s new. He’s not very nice yet.”</p>\n<p>“Relief,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“He came up out of a drain,” said Pim, “so.”</p>\n<p>Ione laughed. It was the first time Cas had heard her laugh — really laugh, out loud — and it surprised him so much that he just stood there in the lane with the rat bones in his hand and listened to it. It was a short, low, rough laugh, as if it didn’t get used much and had got stiff, and it stopped almost as soon as it started, and she looked away quickly, as though she’d been caught at something.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw a wedding on the thirteenth round.</p>\n<p>Riggers. Two of them, a man and a woman, both short and broad and scarred about the forearms like Ione, married on the rooftop of the riggers’ tower in the Drop District under the open sky. The whole tower came up — forty or fifty people, crammed onto the platforms and the ladders and the ropes — and a Steward in a black coat stood between the two of them and said words, and then the riggers said their own. They tied their wrists together with a length of rope, the woman’s left to the man’s right, in a knot Cas didn’t know, and they said it together:</p>\n<p>“I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine. And if the one of us falls, the other one hauls.”</p>\n<p>And everyone on the platforms and the ladders shouted <em>haul!</em> and stamped until the whole tower swayed.</p>\n<p>Afterwards there was green wine, and it did take the skin off his throat, and there was music: a drum made of an old coolant drum, and a sort of fiddle strung with wire, and someone playing a pipe cut from conduit. And they danced — the Holdfast dance, which was slow and heavy and stamping, all bent knees and planted feet, a dance made for people who weighed half as much again as they should, and which shook the tower to its lashings. Cas sat on a coil of rope with his back against a beam and watched it. Ione sat beside him.</p>\n<p>“You don’t dance?” he said.</p>\n<p>“I dance.” She drank. “Not at weddings. It makes people nervous. The Unwatched at your wedding. It’s like a crow at a christening.”</p>\n<p>“What’s a christening?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. “It’s in one of the old books. I don’t know. I assumed you’d know.”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know either.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him a moment longer, and then something in her face gave, and she laughed again — shorter this time, into her cup.</p>\n<p>“The knot,” he said. “On their wrists. What is it?”</p>\n<p>She put her cup down. She took his left hand — without asking, the way she’d checked his seals at the well, practically — and took a length of thin cord from her pocket and, very quickly, tied it round his wrist. Her fingers moved too fast for him to follow. Over, under, round, through. When she took her hands away there was a knot on his wrist: flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped.</p>\n<p>“Riggers’ hitch,” she said. “Holds under load. Won’t slip. Won’t jam. Comes off in a second if you know where to pull and never comes off if you don’t.”</p>\n<p>He looked at it.</p>\n<p>“That’s the only one you’ll need,” said Ione, and took it off again with one tug, and put the cord back in her pocket.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw a funeral on the fourteenth. An old man, a Toller, one of Perpetua’s cousins. They carried him through the lanes on a board, wrapped in a white sheet with the lamp painted on it, and the Tollers walked behind, and at every Bell the procession stopped and everyone stood still and silent until it passed. At the Tanks they laid him on a sort of stone slope running down into the green water, and a Steward said: “He’s clocked off. His shift is done. We thank him for his watch.” And they tipped the board, and he slid down into the vat, and the green closed over him, and everyone said <em>clocked off, clocked off</em>, softly, like a breath going out.</p>\n<p>“Clocked off,” Cas said afterwards to Ione, walking back. “That’s — the station used to say that. When you went off shift.”</p>\n<p>“We know,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He saw children playing a game in a lane. They stood in a line with their backs to a wall, and one of them stood in front of them, facing them, and shut her eyes and fell straight backwards, stiff as a plank. The others caught her. Every time. They shrieked with laughter. Then the next one.</p>\n<p>“What’s it called?” he asked Pim.</p>\n<p>“Drop,” said Pim, as if it were obvious. “It’s called Drop.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the fifteenth round, when he’d sent Teo the round’s news, he switched to the second channel and said:</p>\n<p>“Nell. I’m going to tell you about a day. Just an ordinary day. Nobody’s ever — I don’t think I’ve ever told anyone about an ordinary day. I don’t think I had any, for a long time. I think they were all the same day, and I didn’t want to tell anyone about it.</p>\n<p>“This morning I stood up by myself. There’s a man here called Osk who’s teaching me. He’s the biggest man I’ve ever seen and he talks like gravel. He says I’m nearly as good as a baby now.</p>\n<p>“Then I sat on some steps and people came and touched my hand. They think I’m — it doesn’t matter what they think I am. I’m not it. I let them think it. I’m not proud of that.</p>\n<p>“Then I ate a rat. It was delicious. Don’t tell your mother.</p>\n<p>“Then I went to a place where a very old lady counts time. She’s funny, Nell. She’d make you laugh. She says rude things about everyone and then gives them soup. She had a daughter who was very brave. She lost her.</p>\n<p>“And then I went to see my friend. She lives at the top of a tower full of rope, where you can lie in a hammock and look straight up at the Lamp — the star, the one I told you about. She’s never seen a real one. A real star. I tried to tell her what they’re like. I told her about the ones on your ceiling. Betelgeuse. Deneb. I told her they’re far away and they don’t move and there are thousands of them. She asked me what colour they are. I said white, mostly. Some are blue. Some are a bit red. She asked me which one was my favourite and I didn’t know. I’ve never looked. I’ve never once looked, Nell. You’d have known in a second.”</p>\n<p>He was quiet for a while.</p>\n<p>“I think,” he said, “that if you ever meet her, you’ll like her. I don’t know why I think that. You’ll never meet her. But I think you would.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the sixteenth round, the Lace touched Spindle Six.</p>\n<p>He didn’t see it happen. He was on the steps of the Steward’s House, blessing a rope. But he heard it — everyone in Holdfast heard it — a sound from the sky like a great sheet of glass being drawn across stone, high and thin and long, and then a flash, very brief, very bright, from the top of Spindle Six on the far side of the city, and then silence. People stopped in the lanes. Everyone looked up. On the steps, the rigger who’d brought the rope to be blessed dropped it.</p>\n<p>Six was the spindle nearest the Lace’s lowest tendril. There had been a rigger crew working it that round — they went up every round, to tend the old lamp-lines and the water-catchers the riggers had strung on the spindle’s upper lattice, a kilometre and a half up. Six of them. Ione knew them all. So did the rigger on the steps.</p>\n<p>At the end of the round the Stewardship put up a notice in the Commons, beneath the Public Tally, written in beautiful careful letters on a board:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MARROW TELL AND HIS CREW<br>HAVE BEEN TAKEN INTO THE LAMP’S CROWN<br>A BLESSING ON THE WATCH<br>THE QUICKENING IS GLORY</div>\n<p>Ione went up after them the next round, with two others from the riggers’ tower. She came back at the end of it and said nothing, and went up to her loft, and when Cas climbed the twenty storeys that night — it took him an hour; he did it — she was sitting in her hammock with her knees up, looking at the Lamp.</p>\n<p>“What did you find?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Rope,” she said. “Six harnesses. Their tools. The water-catchers they were tending, all rusted through to lace. Marrow’s knife. I gave that to his daughter.” She didn’t look at him. “And bones. The kind of bones you’d find in a Founder’s grave, if the Founders had graves. Old and white and dry. And a bit of Tamsin’s hair, still in her clip. She’d have been thirty next round.” She was quiet. “The Lamp’s crown. A blessing on the Watch.”</p>\n<p>He sat down on the loft floor.</p>\n<p>“Crane wants another address,” he said. “On Kindling Round. Three rounds from now. The whole city in the Commons. He says they need to see the Relief.”</p>\n<p>“Of course he does.”</p>\n<p>“He wants me to tell them the Quickening’s glory. That Marrow Tell’s crew are in the Lamp’s crown.” He looked at his hands. “He hasn’t said it like that. But that’s what he wants.”</p>\n<p>Ione said nothing. The hammock creaked, very gently, as she moved.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to tell them,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>She turned her head then and looked at him.</p>\n<p>“All of it,” he said. “The Outside. The test. Aldous Crane. The debt. How long. The lock and the key and you. All of it. In the Commons. On the Bell.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those unhurried grey-green eyes. And for the first time since the top of the well he couldn’t tell — he really couldn’t — whether she had known he was going to say it.</p>\n<p>“They’ll put you in the Quiet,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Probably.”</p>\n<p>“Do you know what the Quiet is?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“It’s a coolant tank. Under the Steward’s House. They seal the lid. No light. No sound but the Bell.” She said it very evenly. “My mother was in it for eleven rounds before they gave her to the Drop.”</p>\n<p>“I’m still going to tell them.”</p>\n<p>Ione Tally looked at him a moment longer. Then she lay back in her hammock and looked up at the Lamp, and something happened at the corner of her mouth — so small he almost didn’t see it.</p>\n<p>“Good,” she said. “I know how to get people out of the Quiet.”</p>"}, {"file": "10-the-second-address.md", "label": "Chapter 10", "title": "The Second Address", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4727, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Tell them the Drop isn’t open. Say it twice. They’ll only hear it once.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">They spent the three rounds before the Bell getting it right.</p>\n<p>Not the speech. He didn’t write a speech. He tried, the first night, in the loft, and Ione watched him cross out three pages and then took the pencil out of his hand and said, “Don’t. You’ll make it pretty. Say it the way you said it to me.” So he didn’t write it. But they planned everything around it.</p>\n<p>The Tollers would be on the roof of the Commons. The Commons roof was open in long strips between the girders, and there was a ridge of old gantry running along the top of it that the riggers used for stringing lamp-lines on high days; Tobiah and three others would be up there with a banner. It was already painted. Perpetua had had it painted on the first night, on a sheet of canvas thirty metres long, and it lay folded in the upper floor of Toller’s Tower like a sail: Seren’s line. The Tollers’ curve, drawn huge, in white on black, with a hundred and sixty-eight years of Bells falling away along it and the line carried forward past the end of the count to the place where it met the bottom of the canvas. And beside that place, in letters two metres high, the number Seren had worked out thirteen years ago and the Tollers had been refining every round since.</p>\n<p>“When they see it,” said Perpetua, “they’ll know it’s true. Nobody in Holdfast would believe a word from the Outside. They’ll believe a Tally. They’ve been counting their whole lives.”</p>\n<p>The riggers would be at the doors. Ione had asked them. Not all of them — the riggers were as split as anyone, and some of them went to the Commons on the seventh round and knelt at Muster Point B — but a few dozen, the ones who’d known Marrow Tell and his crew, the ones who’d climbed Six the round after and seen the bones. They would be at the doors and on the ladders, with rope. Nobody had said what the rope was for.</p>\n<p>And Perpetua told him, on the last night, sitting in her chair with her hand on the column:</p>\n<p>“Tell them the Drop isn’t open.”</p>\n<p>“I will.”</p>\n<p>“Say it twice. They’ll only hear it once.” She looked at him with her black bird’s eyes. “You’re going to tell twenty thousand people that the Outside’s alive and that a man came up out of the floor from it. Some of them are going to hear that and nothing else. You understand? Nothing else. You could talk for a shift. They’ll hear <em>the Outside is alive, and he came up the Drop</em>.” She took her hand off the column and laid it on his knee, the way she had the first night. “Say it twice, Relief. Say it three times. It won’t be enough. But say it.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Kindling Round fell three rounds later — the first round of the year One Hundred and Sixty-Nine, a hundred and sixty-eight years to the round since the Lamp was lit — and the whole of Holdfast had been getting ready for it for a week.</p>\n<p>There had never been a Kindling Round like it. That was what everyone said. Every year of the Watch, on this round, Holdfast remembered the night the Founders stood under the Placard and counted the first Bell. And this year — in a year when the Bells came more than a hundred a round, when you could feel them while you ate and while you slept — <em>the Relief had come</em>. It was a sign. It was the greatest festival in the history of the Watch. The lanes had been hung with lamp-strings for three rounds: little clay lamps strung on wire from wall to wall, hundreds of them, so that even in the day the lanes glittered. Every shrine had been repainted. Children had been making paper lamps. The Public Tally board in the Commons had been hung with a garland of green algae-cloth and the numbers repainted in gold.</p>\n<p>The Commons was fuller than it had been the night he arrived. He hadn’t thought that was possible. They packed the floor to the walls. They filled the girders — people had climbed up into the roof trusses and sat along them in rows, their legs dangling, like birds on a wire. They stood outside in the lanes so thick that a Warden trying to get through had to be passed over their heads. The noise was the sea. When the Wardens carried Cas in on the litter — he’d asked to walk; Crane had said, kindly, <em>no</em> — the sea rose up into something like a storm, and it was a long time before it went down.</p>\n<p>He stood on the platform. Osk was at his elbow. Crane beside him, in his black coat, with a new lamp embroidered on its breast in gold thread for the occasion. The Public Tally board behind them: BELL 121,944.</p>\n<p>He looked for Ione. He couldn’t see her.</p>\n<p>He looked up. Along the ridge of the gantry, high up in the open roof, in the violet glare of the Lace, there were four small figures in grey smocks, sitting in a row with something long and folded across their knees.</p>\n<p>Crane led the singing. The Tending, slow and worn. Twenty thousand voices, and more outside, the whole city singing, under the Lace. <em>Tend the Lamp, tend the Lamp, till the Relief comes after.</em> Cas listened to every word of it. He made himself. He stood there on the platform and let it wash over him and thought about a children’s choir on the Tier Nine lampposts at dawn, and about what those children had been singing about, and what these people were.</p>\n<p>Then the call and response. <em>Who keeps the Watch?</em> The Bell came during it — 121,945 — rising up through the floor, and the whole Commons fell still and silent and counted it, and then went on. <em>Until Relief.</em></p>\n<p>Then Crane turned to him, and smiled, and stepped back.</p>\n<p>BELL 121,946.</p>\n<p>He had about ten minutes before the next one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“I lied to you,” said Casimir Hale.</p>\n<p>He didn’t raise his voice. He’d learned, the first night, that he didn’t have to. The Commons was so silent when he spoke that the smallest sound carried to the back.</p>\n<p>“The first night I came. I stood here and I told you it was going to be all right. I don’t know that. I didn’t know it then. I said it because I looked at your faces and I didn’t want to be the one who took it away.” He could see the front row. There was a little girl there — not the same one, a different one. He looked at her. He made himself go on looking at her. “That’s what people do where I come from. They say what’s easy. They’ve been doing it for ninety years. It’s why you’re here.”</p>\n<p>A murmur. Very faint. Like wind starting.</p>\n<p>“So I’m going to tell you the truth now. All of it. It’s going to be hard to hear, and I’m sorry. And some of it you’re not going to believe, and that’s all right. But I need you to listen to all of it. Not just the start. All of it. Please.”</p>\n<p>He took a breath.</p>\n<p>“The Outside is alive.”</p>\n<p>The murmur became a sound.</p>\n<p>“It isn’t burned. It never was. It’s a city. A big one, bigger than you can imagine. It’s called Averna. It has six hundred million people in it and all of them are alive right now, today, this minute. And it’s the same night there. It’s still the same night that the Lamp was kindled.” He waited for the sound to go down. It didn’t, quite. “You know that. Some of you know that. The Tollers know it. They’ve always known. Every Bell you’ve ever counted is one second of the Outside’s time. A hundred and twenty-two thousand Bells is thirty-four hours, near enough. That’s all it’s been, out there. A day and a half. The Outside isn’t gone. It’s slow.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Lies!</em>” Somebody, at the back. A man’s voice, high and cracked. Then another voice, shouting him down. Then others. The sound rose.</p>\n<p>“There’s a little girl in the Outside,” said Cas, into it, “called Maddy Tally. She’s seven. She went to sleep on the night of the Kindling in her grandmother’s flat, and her mother went to work at the station and didn’t come home. Her mother was Ruth Tally. Your Founder. The one who counted the first Bell.” The sound faltered. “Ruth wrote her letters for fifty years. She never stopped. She died in here, an old woman, with eleven grandchildren. And Maddy’s still seven. Right now, while I’m talking. She’ll be having her lunch.”</p>\n<p>It went quiet then. Really quiet. Not the silence of listening; something else. A silence like the moment after a glass is dropped, before it hits the floor.</p>\n<p>“The Kindling,” said Cas, “was an accident.”</p>\n<p>He felt Crane move beside him. He didn’t look round.</p>\n<p>“The station — this place, the Draw — makes power. It sends it Outside. The night of the Kindling, there was a test. The people who ran the Outside wanted more power, and they wanted to film the station making it, for a festival. The engineers said it wasn’t safe. The man in charge made them do it anyway. His name was Aldous Crane.”</p>\n<p>The sound the Commons made then was something he’d never heard a crowd make. A kind of groan. A kind of gasp. Twenty thousand people drawing in breath together.</p>\n<p>“He was your Founder. He was a — he wasn’t a bad man. He was a frightened one. Ruth Tally tried to shut the Lamp off, that night, in the Crown. There was a lock, and there was a key, and he had the key round his neck, and she asked him for it, and he wouldn’t give it to her. And then there was an accident, and the key fell, and it was lost.” He made himself say it. “He didn’t give the key to the Lamp. He dropped it.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Heresy!</em>” A woman’s voice this time, very near the front, shrill with horror. “Heresy, it’s — <em>Steward!</em> Steward, stop him⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Let him finish.”</p>\n<p>It was Crane.</p>\n<p>Cas turned his head. The old man was standing quite still beside him, very straight in his black coat with its new gold lamp, and his face was the colour of the paper in the Log, and his long hands were clasped very tightly in front of him. He wasn’t looking at Cas. He was looking out at the Commons.</p>\n<p>“Let him finish,” said Halloran Crane again, and his voice carried to the back of the hall.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He told them the rest.</p>\n<p>He told them what the Lamp was. He did it the way he’d done it for Crane — a debt, a loan from the emptiness, and the emptiness always wants it back — and he watched their faces, and he saw some of them understand, and some of them not, and some of them refuse to. He told them what the Bells meant. That the Quickening was the debt coming due. That it was going to come faster and faster. That the Lace was part of it, and it was coming down, and that when it reached the Rim at the top of the spindles, it would all be over. Not just Holdfast. Everything.</p>\n<p>“Marrow Tell and his crew,” he said, “weren’t taken into the Lamp’s crown. They weren’t blessed. They died. The Lace touched the top of Six and they grew old and died in the time it takes to draw a breath. The riggers who went up after them found their bones.” He looked out at the hall. “The Quickening isn’t glory. It’s a clock. And it’s nearly run down.”</p>\n<p>He heard someone crying. He heard someone praying, in a low fast mutter, the words of the Placard. <em>Remain at your station. Await relief.</em></p>\n<p>BELL 121,947 came while he was talking. He stopped, and waited, and everyone waited, and it rose and hung and fell, and when it had gone he said:</p>\n<p>“There’s one chance.”</p>\n<p>He looked for her again. And this time he found her. She was at the back, where she’d been the first night, by the great doors, in the gap in the crowd, with her arms folded and her back against the wall. The riggers were around her. She was looking at him. She wasn’t patient now. She was very still, and her eyes were very bright.</p>\n<p>“There’s a way to close the Lamp,” he said. “The woman who built this station put it there. It’s in the Crown, at the top of Spindle One. It’s a lock. It’s been there all along. It needs a key.” He lifted his hand and pointed, over twenty thousand heads, at the back of the hall. “And the key is here. Ione Tally has it.”</p>\n<p>Every head in the Commons turned.</p>\n<p>“She found it, eleven years ago, up there. The key your Founder dropped. She came down with it and told you the Lamp could be closed. And you called her mad. And her mother, Seren Tally, drew the line of the Bells and told you how long you had left, and you gave her to the Drop.” He heard his voice shaking and let it. “They were right. Both of them. They were right all along.”</p>\n<p>And up on the roof, on the gantry, in the glare of the Lace, the Tollers stood up and let the banner fall.</p>\n<p>It came down out of the open roof between the girders like a great black sail unfurling, thirty metres of it, and snapped taut, and hung there over the whole of the Commons. The curve. White on black. A hundred and sixty-eight years of Bells, falling away in a long smooth swoop, and the line going on past the end of the count, down, down, to the bottom edge of the canvas. And beside it, in white letters two metres tall, a single number, and a single word.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">ROUND 48<br>THE LACE MEETS THE RIM</div>\n<p>It was the first round of the year.</p>\n<p>He heard the Commons read it. He heard twenty thousand people do the sum.</p>\n<p>He’d done his own, at the top of the Tollers’ tower. Forty-seven rounds. Out there, it was about two hours. Hour thirty-six, on a clock he’d once thought had days left on it. In here, it was all the time there was.</p>\n<p>“We’re going to go up,” he said, very loudly now, over the sound that was starting. “Ione Tally and me. To the Crown. To try. And if it works⁠—” he had to shout “⁠—<em>listen to me</em>. If it works, the Lamp will go out.”</p>\n<p>The sound stopped, a little. Enough.</p>\n<p>“The Lamp will go out, and it will be dark. Really dark. Darker than you’ve ever seen. And you mustn’t be afraid of it.” He was looking at the little girl in the front row. She was holding her mother’s skirt in both fists. “When the Lamp goes out, it means we did it. It means the debt’s closed. It means the Shear’s gone and time in here is the same as time out there. And it means the Drop will be a road. You’ll be able to walk down it. All of you. Out. Into the Outside. Into the same night.”</p>\n<p>He held on to Osk’s arm. He was very tired suddenly.</p>\n<p>“But not before,” he said. “Listen. <em>Not before</em>. The Drop is not open now. Nobody goes near the wells. Nobody. Not until the Lamp goes out. If you go down now, the Shear will kill you, the way it killed everyone who ever tried. The way it killed Seren. I came up through it in a suit made for it and it nearly killed me. The Drop is not open. Do you hear me? The Drop is not open.”</p>\n<p><em>Say it twice.</em></p>\n<p>“The Drop is <em>not open</em>.”</p>\n<p>And then, into the silence, because it was the only thing left and because it was the truest thing he knew:</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s coming. There’s no Relief. I’m not it. There never was one. I’m just what came.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>For a moment, nothing happened.</p>\n<p>Then the Commons came apart.</p>\n<p>He’d never know, afterwards, how it started. He’d go over it again and again, lying in the dark, and never be able to find the first moment. It was as though the hall had been holding a single breath for a hundred and sixty-eight years and had just let it out. Everyone was shouting. Everyone was moving. People were on their feet, on their knees, clinging to each other, pushing, surging toward the platform, surging away from it. Someone was screaming <em>heresy, heresy</em> over and over in a voice like a torn sail. Someone else had climbed up onto the brass rail of Muster Point B and was reading the Placard aloud at the top of his lungs. A knot of men at the side of the hall were fighting — fists, and then staves, the Wardens going in — and a woman near the front had her arms round a stranger and was sobbing into his coat, and the little girl in the front row was crying with her face in her mother’s skirt.</p>\n<p>And there was the sound he’d been afraid of.</p>\n<p>He heard it start at the back, near the doors. Near Ione. A man’s voice, high and wild, carrying over everything the way a single voice sometimes can:</p>\n<p>“<em>The Outside’s alive! He came up the Drop! He came up it — it’s open — we can go down, we can go down⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>“<em>No!</em>”</p>\n<p>That was him. He was shouting. He was at the front of the platform, shouting over twenty thousand people, <em>no, no, it isn’t open, it isn’t⁠—</em>, and nobody could hear him. He could see the words not reaching. He could see it happen at the back of the hall: the knot of people round that one voice, turning, pushing toward the great doors. And the doors opening, and the lanes outside, and the people in the lanes turning to see what was happening, and hearing it — <em>it’s open, he came up it, we can go down</em> — and turning to run.</p>\n<p>He saw Ione. He saw her step out into the doorway in front of them with her arms out, shouting, and the riggers beside her. He saw the crowd hit them like a wave hitting a wall. He saw the riggers go down under it, and Ione go down, and the wave go over them and out into the lanes.</p>\n<p>“<em>Osk.</em>”</p>\n<p>The big Warden was already moving.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Afterwards, what he remembered was pieces.</p>\n<p>The cart. Pim, white-faced, dragging it through a side door, and Osk lifting him into it bodily, and then Osk himself between the shafts, hauling it at a run through the glittering lamp-strung lanes with the cart bouncing and banging and Cas clinging to its sides. People running beside them, past them, all going the same way. Toward the Drop District. Toward the wells.</p>\n<p>The well. The same well, the one he’d come up. The WATCH · REMAIN · AWAIT circle painted round its mouth. There were people all round it. A crowd, pressing up the ramp to the platform, pressing against the scrap-metal wall. And on the platform, round the rim of the conduit, a line of them, going over. Going down the ladder. One after another, their hands on the rungs, their faces set and wild and joyful, climbing down into the dark toward the Outside.</p>\n<p>He was out of the cart. He didn’t remember getting out. He was on the platform — Osk had cleared a way, Osk was a wall — and he was at the lip of the well, grabbing at people. Grabbing at coats, at arms. <em>No. No, it isn’t open, please, it isn’t⁠—</em> A man shook him off without even looking at him. A woman slapped his hand away. <em>He came up it</em>, someone said, <em>he came up it, he’s lying, he wants to keep it for himself —</em></p>\n<p>There was a father with a child.</p>\n<p>A young man, stocky and wide like all of them, with a little boy of four or five on his back, the boy’s arms round his neck. He was at the ladder. He had one foot on the first rung.</p>\n<p>Cas got the boy.</p>\n<p>He didn’t decide to. He simply had him. He had the child under the arms and was pulling, and the little boy was screaming, and the father was screaming, twisting round on the ladder, reaching back, <em>give him to me, give him, he’s mine —</em> and Cas held on, and fell backwards onto the platform with the child on top of him, and the father hung there on the ladder with his face working, looking at his son, looking down into the dark.</p>\n<p>“Come back up,” said Cas. He was crying. “Please. Please. Come back up. It isn’t open. I swear to you. I swear it.”</p>\n<p>The father looked at him for a moment with an expression he would never forget. Then he looked at his son. Then he looked down again, into the well, toward the Outside, and his face changed. It filled with something. Hope. Joy. The same joy Cas had seen in the Commons the first night, turned up at him like light.</p>\n<p>“I’ll come back for him,” the father said. “I’ll come back up for him. When I’ve seen it.”</p>\n<p>And he went down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The screaming started about a quarter of an hour later.</p>\n<p>It came up the well. Faint, at first, and very far, from three hundred metres down, where the conduit came out of the bottom of the Anvil into the Shear. It came up the shaft the way the Bell did — stretched, somehow. Slowed. Strange. It didn’t sound like screaming. It sounded like the lowing of cattle. Like the long slow groan of Teo’s voice through the suit. It went on and on, and more voices joined it, one by one, as more of them reached the bottom. It went on for a long time. Nobody who was on that platform ever forgot it.</p>\n<p>The people still on the ladder stopped. They hung there, on the rungs, listening. Then they began to come back up.</p>\n<p>The riggers came. Ione came — with blood on her face from a cut over her eye and her left arm held against her body — and a dozen riggers behind her with rope and knives. They went to every well in the District. There were fourteen. At every one they cut the tops of the ladders free and hauled them up so that nobody else could go down. It took most of a shift. At some of the wells they had to fight to do it.</p>\n<p>The Tollers counted. That was what Tollers did. They went from well to well with their ledgers and asked the people on the platforms who had gone down, and wrote down the names.</p>\n<p>Forty-seven.</p>\n<p>He was sitting on the platform of the well with his back against the scrap-metal wall and the little boy asleep in his lap when Osk came to arrest him.</p>\n<p>He had been sitting there for hours. The boy had cried himself to sleep. Nobody had come for him. Nobody had known who he was. Cas had sat with him, and held him, and listened to the sounds coming up the well until they stopped, and then listened to the silence, and then to the Bell.</p>\n<p>Osk stood over him without saying anything. His hard hat was gone. There was a long scrape down one side of his face.</p>\n<p>“The First Steward’s asked for you,” he said at last.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I’m to bring you.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>Osk looked down at the boy.</p>\n<p>“I’ll take him,” said a voice, and it was Pim — Pim with his face streaked black and grey, Pim who had been everywhere, who had carried water and run messages and dragged the cart — and he knelt down and held out his arms. “I know his gran. On Pump Lane. I’ll take him to her.”</p>\n<p>Cas gave him the boy. The child didn’t wake. Pim staggered a little under the weight and settled him, and looked at Cas, and didn’t say anything, and went.</p>\n<p>Osk put out a hand. Cas took it. The big man pulled him up — gently, the way he’d taught him, <em>stack your bones</em> — and stood holding his arm.</p>\n<p>“I told them,” said Cas. “I told them three times.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Osk. “I heard.”</p>\n<p>Across the platform, at the top of the ramp, there was a disturbance. Wardens — six of them, eight — with staves. And in the middle of them, struggling, Ione. She’d seen Osk take his arm. She was fighting to get to him. She got one arm free and a Warden caught it and she twisted, and the one holding her bad arm wrenched it — not hard, but it was the bad arm — and she cried out, and dropped, and rolled, and was up, and was over the scrap-metal wall of the platform in one long movement, the way she’d gone over it the first day, and gone.</p>\n<p>The Wardens shouted and ran to the wall and looked over it and didn’t follow.</p>\n<p>“Good,” said Osk very softly, to no one.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Quiet was a coolant tank.</p>\n<p>It was underneath the Steward’s House, down a long iron stair into the roots of the building, where the old lift terminal had had its machinery. A steel drum, three metres across, three metres deep, set into the keelstone floor, with a round lid on a hinge like the lid of a well. There was a ladder down into it. When Osk took him down the stair and opened the lid, the smell that came up was old water and old rust and something else, something human, very faint, that had soaked into the metal over a hundred and sixty-eight years.</p>\n<p>“Down,” said Osk.</p>\n<p>He went down. It wasn’t far. At the bottom his feet touched a steel floor, damp. He looked up. Osk’s face in the round of the lid, with the lamp-light behind it. For a moment Osk looked as though he were going to say something.</p>\n<p>Then the lid came down.</p>\n<p>It was the darkest dark he had ever been in. Not the dark of a room at night, where there’s always some light, somewhere, a line under a door, a glow from a window. The Lamp had never set on Holdfast; he hadn’t seen real dark for nearly twenty rounds. This was total. He held his hand in front of his face and saw nothing. He shut his eyes and opened them and it made no difference at all.</p>\n<p>And it was silent. The Steward’s House was above him, and the city above that, and the city was — he knew it was — in chaos, in grief, in the streets, the whole of Holdfast awake and shouting. He couldn’t hear any of it. The steel walls and the keelstone and the stone of the House lay over him like a hand over a mouth, and there was nothing, nothing, only the sound of his own breath and his own heart.</p>\n<p>He sat down on the damp steel floor with his back against the curved wall.</p>\n<p>He sat there.</p>\n<p>Some time later — he had no idea how long — it came up through the floor. Rising. Humming in the steel. Filling the tank like water filling a glass, until the whole drum was ringing faintly with it, a single deep note in the dark. It hung. It lasted. It went away.</p>\n<p>The Bell. The hundred and twenty-one thousand, nine hundred and forty-eighth, or the forty-ninth. He didn’t know. He’d lost count, for the first time since his flat on Tier Nine. The first time since 02:14.</p>\n<p>He found that he was counting again. Not the Bell.</p>\n<p>Forty-seven.</p>"}, {"file": "11-the-quiet.md", "label": "Chapter 11", "title": "The Quiet", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4974, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Keep them calm. It is the only mercy I have left to give, and God knows I owe them one.</p><span class=\"attr\">Private journal of Aldous Crane, Year 2 of the Watch (never published)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">In the Quiet, there was nothing to do but count and think, and he was very good at both.</p>\n<p>He counted the Bells. He’d lost the number the first time, in the dark, when the lid came down; so he started again from one. One. Two. He counted his heartbeats between them, to know the interval. About eight hundred. Ten minutes, near enough, if his heart was going the way he thought it was. He counted the rivets he could reach on the curved steel wall of the tank by feel, running his fingers along the seams, and found there were two hundred and eight, and counted them again, and found two hundred and eight again, and felt a small, pathetic, animal comfort in that. He counted the drips from somewhere overhead that fell, every so often, into a puddle in the middle of the floor he couldn’t see: one every forty breaths, give or take.</p>\n<p>And he thought.</p>\n<p>He thought about forty-seven.</p>\n<p>He tried not to. He’d been trying not to since the well, and he was no better at not thinking about things than he’d ever been. He thought about the father on the ladder. <em>I’ll come back for him. When I’ve seen it.</em> He thought about the sound that had come up the conduit, that long, slow, stretched lowing, and what it had actually been — what forty metres of Shear did to a body going down it without a keel — and he knew exactly what it had been, because he’d seen the body at the bottom of Conduit 14, and he’d done the arithmetic, and he could not stop doing it now. He thought about Seren, who’d been the cleverest Toller in two hundred years, and he thought about the Tollers’ ledgers, the names in careful ink. He thought about Ione going down under the crowd at the doors.</p>\n<p>He’d said it three times. <em>The Drop is not open.</em> Three times.</p>\n<p>And they had heard <em>the Outside is alive, and he came up it</em>, and nothing else. Just as Perpetua had said. He’d known they would. He’d known it the way you know a sum. And he had said it anyway, because it was true, and because he had decided he was going to be a man who said true things, and forty-seven people had heard the true thing he said and climbed down a ladder toward it and been torn to pieces in the dark.</p>\n<p>That was what truth did. That was what it had done, the first time he’d tried it in fifteen years.</p>\n<p>He sat with his back against the steel and his knees up and the Bell coming up through the floor, and he found himself in a place he knew very well. He had lived in it for fifteen years. It was quite a small place, and quite a cold one, and it had a very simple shape. It went like this: <em>Everything I do adds to it.</em> The lie added to it. The truth added to it. Silence added to it; he’d tried silence, for fifteen years, and every day of silence had been another day of the Draw eating the future, another day of the Margin falling, another day of interest on interest on interest. There was no move that didn’t make it bigger. There was no way to pay it down. There was only the debt, growing, and him inside it, and the only question that had ever mattered was how and when he could stop.</p>\n<p>He’d come into the Draw thinking the Shear might settle it. Or the Crown. Or whatever was in the Crown. <em>At last</em>, he’d thought, at the window in the Collar. <em>Almost a relief.</em></p>\n<p>He thought it now, in the dark. He didn’t argue with it.</p>\n<p>Then he thought about Teo, in a chair, saying <em>oh no you don’t</em>, and he put his forehead down on his knees and stayed like that for a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d counted nine hundred and forty Bells — no. He’d lost it again. He’d counted something. It had been a long time. His mouth was dry. There was water in the puddle and he’d drunk from it, on his knees, lapping at it with his hand like a dog, and it had tasted of rust.</p>\n<p>The lid opened.</p>\n<p>Light came down. Not much — a lamp, a single flame — but after the dark it was like a knife in his eyes, and he threw his arm over his face. When he lowered it, there was a figure coming down the ladder. Slowly, carefully, an old man’s descent, one rung at a time, with a lamp in one hand and something under his other arm.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane reached the bottom. He stood for a moment, getting his breath. Then he set the lamp down on the steel floor between them, and sat down beside it, cross-legged, with a small grunt, his long black coat pooling round him. He put the thing from under his arm in his lap. It was a book.</p>\n<p>For a while he was silent. He looked at the lamp. Cas looked at him.</p>\n<p>“I used to come down here as a boy,” said Crane at last. “My father was Steward. He used to bring me. He’d lower me in, and shut the lid, and leave me for an hour. Sometimes two. He said a Steward had to know what the Quiet was, if he was going to put people in it.” He looked round at the curved steel walls in the lamplight, the rust, the rivets. “He was right, I suppose. I’ve never put anyone in here without remembering it.”</p>\n<p>“Seren?”</p>\n<p>Crane didn’t flinch. He’d been expecting it.</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds,” he said. “I came down every round. The way I’ve come down now. She wouldn’t speak to me. On the eleventh round she said one thing. She said, <em>You know I’m right.</em> And I said yes.” He looked at the lamp. “And then I signed the order, and the next round they took her up to Well Fourteen, and she went down it. She didn’t look back. I was there. I made myself be there.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came. They both sat through it without moving.</p>\n<p>“How many?” said Cas when it had gone. “Out there. Since.”</p>\n<p>“In the city? Nobody else has died.” Crane’s voice was very level. “There’s been fighting. There was fighting in the Commons and there’s been fighting in the lanes since. The Wardens have been — busy. There are people who want your head. There are more people who want mine. The riggers have taken the Drop District and won’t let a Warden in it. The Tollers’ banner is still hanging in the Commons. I haven’t had it taken down.” He paused. “Half the city is saying the Relief is a demon sent up from the Drop to lure the Watch to its death. And the other half is making lamps.”</p>\n<p>“Lamps?”</p>\n<p>“Lamps. Oil lamps. Wicks, dishes. Every oil press in Holdfast has been running for two rounds. People are pressing algae in their kitchens.” Crane looked at him. “They’re getting ready for the dark, Relief. You told them the Lamp would go out. Some of them believed you.”</p>\n<p>Cas found that he couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t come down here to tell you that,” said Crane. “I came down to ask you why.”</p>\n<p>“Why what?”</p>\n<p>“Why you did it.” The old man’s face in the lamplight was very tired, and very intelligent, and entirely serious. “I asked you to be kind. You were kind, the first time. You gave them nineteen rounds of peace. Nineteen rounds of believing that someone had come for them. And then you stood up in the Commons and you took it away. And forty-seven of them are dead in the Drop. And I would very much like to understand what you thought you were doing.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It went on for a long time.</p>\n<p>He’d think about it afterwards — how long, how quiet, the two of them in the bottom of a steel tank with a single flame between them and the Bell coming and going through the floor. He’d think that he had never in his life had a conversation like it. He’d think that it was the kind of conversation he should have had fifteen years ago, with someone, anyone, and never had.</p>\n<p>“Truth,” said Halloran Crane, “is a luxury.”</p>\n<p>He said it without contempt. The way you’d say that a coat was warm.</p>\n<p>“It’s a thing for people with futures. You can afford to tell people the truth when they have time to do something with it. When they can change their minds, and their lives, and fix what’s broken. My ancestor stood under the Placard on the night of the Kindling in front of nineteen hundred people who had just watched their world end. Their families were gone. Their homes were gone. The sky was on fire. The ones who tried to leave had come back up the wells in — in pieces. What was the truth going to do for them? <em>We did this. It was my fault. We will all die here, and nobody is coming, and there is nothing beyond the Drop but a world that has forgotten us.</em> That was the truth, as far as he knew it. Do you know what nineteen hundred people do with that truth, in the dark, in the first week?”</p>\n<p>“Tell me.”</p>\n<p>“They kill each other,” said Crane. “It’s in the Log. Ruth Tally’s own Log. The Tollers think I haven’t read it. I’ve read it ten times. There were riots in the first week. There was a man who took an axe to the scrubbers because he wanted it all to be over. Eleven people died. And my ancestor stood up under that sign and read it aloud and told them it was a promise. <em>You will not be forgotten.</em> And they stopped.” He opened his hands. “His lie kept nineteen hundred terrified people alive for a hundred and sixty-eight years. It gave us children, and the Tanks, and the Crèche, and the Commons, and every rope and wall and lamp in this city. It gave us thirty-one thousand people. That is what a lie can do.” He looked at Cas. “And your truth, Relief, in a single round? Forty-seven.”</p>\n<p>Cas didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“It killed Seren,” he said at last.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“And my — the people I work for, outside. They’ve been telling the same kind of lie for ninety years. <em>It’s stable. It’s safe. The future will pay.</em> I signed one of them. I signed my name under it. And that lie didn’t keep anyone alive, it just — it just kept everything going. It kept the Draw going. It kept them feeding it. Right up until it broke and took nineteen hundred people with it and put them in here. That’s what your ancestor was. He was one of us. He wasn’t lying to keep your founders alive. He was lying because it’s what we <em>do</em>. It’s the only thing we know how to do.”</p>\n<p>Crane was quiet.</p>\n<p>“Your lie kept them alive,” said Cas. “Fine. It kept them alive so they could die calm. That’s all it was ever going to do. It kept them in here. It kept anyone from ever trying — ever <em>once</em> trying — to go up and see if the thing could be closed. You had the Tollers’ numbers. You had Seren’s line. You had a girl who came down from the top of the world with the key in her hand. And you kept them calm. You gave them lamps to pray to and a board that counted the Bells and a notice that said the Quickening was glory. And in forty-seven rounds the Lace was going to touch the Rim and every one of them was going to die in the middle of a hymn.” He heard his voice shaking. “I’d rather they died knowing. I’d rather they had forty-seven rounds of knowing, and could do something with it, even if all they do is make lamps. I’d rather <em>I</em> had. I’d rather I’d known, fifteen years ago, that it was all right to say it. That it wasn’t going to kill anyone to say it.”</p>\n<p>“It did kill people.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Forty-seven.”</p>\n<p>“I <em>know</em>.” It came out of him too loudly and rang off the steel walls, and he put his hands over his face. “I know. I know. I know.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came. They waited for it.</p>\n<p>When it had gone, Crane spoke again, lower.</p>\n<p>“I signed her order,” he said. “Seren’s. You know that. What you don’t know is that I read her numbers, and I checked them myself, and I knew she was right, and I had the order drawn up before she ever painted the wall. I had it drawn up the round she came to me. Just in case.” He looked at the lamp. “Just in case she was the kind of person who’d paint it on the wall. And she was. And I was the kind of person who’d already drawn up the order. That’s what I’ve had to live with. Not that I did it. That I’d got it ready.”</p>\n<p>Cas lowered his hands.</p>\n<p>The old man opened the book in his lap. It was small, bound in cracked black leather, its pages yellow and brittle. He turned them with great care until he found the one he wanted, and then held it out.</p>\n<p>“Aldous Crane’s journal,” he said. “Private. It’s passed from Steward to Steward. Nobody else has ever read it. This is from the second year.”</p>\n<p>Cas took it. He held it close to the lamp. The hand was a fine, sloping, educated hand, a Ministry hand, that had begun beautifully and gone to pieces.</p>\n<p><em>I did not give it to the Lamp. I will write that here where nobody will see it, once, so that it has been said. I did not give it. I held it. She asked me for it and I held it, because I was afraid of what they would say, and then the walkway went and it was gone. That is the truth. It is the only truth I have.</em></p>\n<p><em>They sing about me now. They have painted me on a wall. Every time I walk past it I think I will go mad.</em></p>\n<p><em>But they are calm. The riots have stopped. The children are being born. And so I will keep it, this lie, I will keep it until I die, and I will make my son keep it after me, because it is the only thing standing between them and the dark. Keep them calm. It is the only mercy I have left to give, and God knows I owe them one.</em></p>\n<p>Cas read it twice. Then he closed the book and held it in his lap and didn’t give it back.</p>\n<p>“He knew,” he said.</p>\n<p>“He knew.” Crane took the book from him gently. “Every Steward has known. We’ve known it for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and every one of us has kept it, because he asked us to, and because it seemed like mercy.” He stood up, slowly, his knees cracking. “I’ve been thinking, since the Commons, that perhaps it wasn’t mercy he owed them. That perhaps mercy was just the thing he could afford.”</p>\n<p>He picked up the lamp. Then, after a moment, he put it down again on the floor.</p>\n<p>“Can you close it?” he said. “Really? You and the Tally girl?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“You don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“I know nobody’s ever tried,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>Crane stood looking down at him in the flickering light.</p>\n<p>“I let you finish,” he said at last. “In the Commons. Do you know why?”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Because my ancestor didn’t let Ruth Tally finish.” He turned toward the ladder. “I’ve wondered my whole life what she’d have said.”</p>\n<p>He climbed. It took him a long time. At the top he paused, and looked down, and for a moment Cas thought he was going to say something else. But he only looked. Then the lid came down.</p>\n<p>He’d left the lamp.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It burned for a long time. Cas sat and watched it. It was the only light he’d ever been glad of in Holdfast — a little, yellow, living flame, not the Lamp’s violet glare, just a wick in a dish of algae oil — and he watched it burn down, and made himself not count.</p>\n<p>When it had nearly gone out, the lid opened again.</p>\n<p>This time it opened fast. It banged back on its hinge and a big shape filled the round of light at the top and a voice like gravel said, “<em>Now</em>, Relief. Up. Quick as you can,” and a rope came slithering down into the tank.</p>\n<p>He looked up. Osk.</p>\n<p>“Stack your bones,” said Osk. “Come on.”</p>\n<p>He got up. He stacked his bones. He took the rope, and Osk hauled, and he came up out of the Quiet the way he’d come up out of the Drop — dragged over the lip like a fish — and lay on the stone floor at the top gasping, and there was a lamp, and faces over him.</p>\n<p>Ione. Her left wrist was bound in a splint of wood and cord, and there was a line of black stitches over her right eye where the cut had been, and she looked at him lying on the floor with her unhurried grey-green eyes and said, “You’re thinner.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve been in a tank.”</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds. You’d be thinner.” She held out her good hand. “Up.”</p>\n<p>Pim was there too, very dirty, with no rat, for once, on his shoulder. He was carrying the recorder in both hands like a precious thing. “I got it out of your room,” he whispered. “Through the vent. There’s a vent. It’s very small. I got stuck. And the suit’s in the lane, in the cart, under a tarp, it took four of us, and Brother Coll, and one of the riggers is sitting on it.”</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds?” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Eleven,” said Ione. “The same as my mother.” Her face didn’t change. “We couldn’t get to you till Osk had the night watch on the Quiet. Tonight he has it.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at Osk.</p>\n<p>The big man was coiling the rope. He didn’t look up.</p>\n<p>“My brother,” said Osk. “Tam. He went over. At Nine. The round you spoke.” He finished the coil. “He heard you say the Outside was alive. He was always like that, Tam. Heard the first thing and ran. All his life.” He hung the coil on his shoulder. “He didn’t go because you told him the truth. He went because he’d been told a lie so long he couldn’t wait another round to see if it was true.” He looked at Cas then, finally. “I know which one killed him.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went through the shuttered city in the violet dusk with the suit on the cart under a tarpaulin, Osk between the shafts and Pim and two riggers pushing, and Ione walking beside Cas with her good hand under his elbow.</p>\n<p>It was different. He could feel it before he could see it. The lanes were as dim and still as they’d been every night — but there were lights in them now. Not the shrine lamps. Other lights. In windows, behind shutters that had been left open a crack. In doorways. On the steps of houses. Little clay lamps, and tin lamps, and lamps made of old instrument housings with a wick stuck in the top, burning, one or two in every lane. People had put them out. He didn’t understand why at first. Then he did.</p>\n<p><em>They’re getting ready for the dark.</em></p>\n<p>On the side of a scaffold tower near the Commons, someone had painted over part of the mural. Not the whole thing. Only the words. THE GIVING was still there, and the tall grieving man holding up his key. But under it, where it had said FOUNDER CRANE GAVE THE KEY TO THE LAMP, someone had painted, in rough white letters, crossing it out:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HE DROPPED IT</div>\n<p>And under that, in a different hand, in yellow:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SEREN WAS RIGHT</div>\n<p>They came to Toller’s Tower by the back lanes. Perpetua was waiting in the doorway. She was standing — he’d never seen her stand — leaning on Tobiah’s arm, very small and very bent, with a lamp in her other hand. She looked at Cas as he came up the step. She looked him up and down in about a second, as she had the first night.</p>\n<p>“Eleven rounds,” she said. “Hmph. You look worse than I do.”</p>\n<p>“I feel worse than you do.”</p>\n<p>“Nobody feels worse than I do. I’m a hundred and eight.” She wasn’t. She was eighty-three; Ione had told him. She jerked her head at the room behind her. “In. Quick. The Wardens’ll be at the Quiet in half a shift.”</p>\n<p>Inside, at the foot of the column, with the lamps burning and the pendulums swinging, she lowered herself back into her chair with a long sigh and put her hand on the stone.</p>\n<p>“Forty-seven,” she said. “We wrote them all down. Every name. We’ll read them out at the Tanks, when it’s time. All forty-seven.” She looked at him. “And then we’ll read out the other number.”</p>\n<p>“What other number?”</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand, three hundred and sixty,” said Perpetua Tally. “The ones who didn’t go. The ones who heard you say <em>not before</em>. The ones who went home and made lamps.” Her black eyes were steady on his. “You didn’t do the forty-seven, Relief. A hundred and sixty-eight years did the forty-seven. You did the other number.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t answer. She didn’t seem to expect him to.</p>\n<p>“Now,” she said. “You’ll be wanting your suit.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They got him into it in the round room at the bottom of the tower. It took four of them. The liner, which hung off him now; he’d lost weight in the Quiet, and before. The clam of the suit closing — thunk, thunk, thunk — and the exo waking, and taking his weight, and for the first time in eleven rounds, for the first time in Holdfast, his body didn’t hurt. The keel came into his spine. Teo’s keel, clean and high and true. It had dropped. He could hear it had dropped, since the Shear — a semitone, perhaps; less. It was still singing.</p>\n<p>And there was a light on in the corner of his visor. Amber. A small steady glow.</p>\n<p>MESSAGE — BUFFERED — READY.</p>\n<p>“It groaned,” said Pim. “In your room. For ages. In the middle of the round when they took you. The Wardens were scared of it. They said it was the Drop talking. Nobody would go in.” He looked anxious. “Then it went <em>ding</em>.”</p>\n<p>Cas looked at the light for a moment. Then he played it.</p>\n<p>“Hale.” Teo’s voice, in his ear, very close. He closed his eyes. “Got all of them. The Tollers and the rats and the wedding and the — and her. Seren. Thank you for telling me her name.” A pause. “And the last one. You’re going to tell them. All of it. On the — what did you call it — Kindling Round.” Another pause. He could hear her breathing. “Christ, Hale. Good. Good. I’m proud of you. You won’t want to hear that, so I’m saying it into a machine that’s going to take a day to get to you, and you can’t stop me.”</p>\n<p>He heard her shift in the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right. Outside news, since you’ll want it. The Collar’s been on the hardline. Gaunt. I told him you were reseating Six. I’ve never lied to the Calm in my life before today and I’ll tell you what, it’s easy. It’s the easiest thing I’ve ever done. That frightens me more than the Lattice does.” A breath. “Averna’s browning out. They’re saying on the line that the fusion plants at Kovač are down to half, and the Canopy’s flickering, and they’ve started rolling blackouts on the upper tiers. There’s no announcement. Just the lights going off one tier at a time. People are very calm, Gaunt says. He sounds like he’s going to cry.</p>\n<p>“And I checked on your girl.”</p>\n<p>He opened his eyes.</p>\n<p>“Civil Defence has the relocation lists. I’ve still got a login, from in here, on the hardline — don’t ask. She’s in the school on Tier Thirty with her mother, like you said. She’s fine. There’s a note on the list next to her name from the welfare officer. It says <em>asking everyone about stars</em>.” A pause, and something that might have been a laugh. “So she’s fine.</p>\n<p>“I’m fine. The lee’s fine. It moved a bit, about twenty-five minutes ago — the edge came across the floor, maybe a hand’s width. I moved the chair. It’s fine. I’m bored out of my skull. I’ve read the fire regulations twice.” She was quiet for a moment. “Go and do it, Cas. Whatever it is. And then bring them out. All of them. Bring them out and I’ll be here.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He stood in the suit in the middle of the Tollers’ round room, with the lamps burning and the pendulums swinging and Perpetua watching him from her chair, and he didn’t say anything at all.</p>\n<p>It was the first time she’d used his name.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Perpetua said goodbye to him first. She did it the way she did everything: quickly, and without dressing it.</p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” she said. “Off you go. Up the cursed spindle with my granddaughter. Close the Lamp.” She sniffed. “Try not to fall off.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll try.”</p>\n<p>“I’m keeping the Log.” She put her knotted hand on the steel box on the workbench beside her. “Somebody should be here, with it, when you come back with the end of it. Somebody should write the last page.” She looked at him with her bright black eyes. “You’ll tell me how it ends. Won’t you.”</p>\n<p>“If I can.”</p>\n<p>“Hmph.” She took her hand off the box. “That’ll do.”</p>\n<p>Then she looked at Ione.</p>\n<p>Ione was standing by the door with her rope over her shoulder and her wrist in its splint and the key at her throat. She’d been waiting. She didn’t come forward. And Perpetua didn’t say anything to her. She only looked. It went on and on — the old woman in the chair and the young one by the door, the lamps between them, the pendulums swinging, the Bell coming up through the column and fading — and Cas understood that he was watching eleven years go by. Eleven years of a girl who came down from the top of the world with grey in her hair and a key in her hand, and a grandmother who’d lost her daughter to the Drop and then watched the granddaughter go up after something nobody else believed was there. Eleven years of that girl being called mad in the lanes. Eleven years of her sitting on this step in the dusk, eating greenbread.</p>\n<p>“Go on,” said Perpetua Tally, very softly. “Go and finish it, Ione.”</p>\n<p>Ione nodded once. She turned and went out.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Spindle Four stood at the far edge of the city, in the Drop District, alone.</p>\n<p>The riggers had let them through. They were on every corner, in every lane of the District — the riggers’ tower had become a fortress, lamps on every platform, ropes across every approach — and they stood aside for the cart and watched it go by in silence. Some of them touched the tarpaulin as it passed. One of them, a woman with a scarred face, said, “Haul, Ione,” under her breath, and Ione said, “Haul, Bett.”</p>\n<p>The spindle’s foot was a ruin. The lift terminal at its base had collapsed in the Founders’ time, when the lift fell — the whole cage and a kilometre of rack-rail coming down together, Pim said, with a sound they’d heard in the Commons — and nobody had ever cleared it. The wreckage lay in a great twisted heap of rusted girders and snapped rail and fallen keelstone, overgrown with pale vine. And behind it, rising out of it, the spindle went up.</p>\n<p>He’d seen it every day from the loft. He’d seen all of them every day. He’d never stood at the foot of one before.</p>\n<p>It was a hundred metres across at its base. It went straight up out of the wreckage into the light, a vast black lattice of struts and braces and cross-members, open, skeletal, the violet glare of the Lamp coming through it in a million bright diamonds, and it went up. And up. And up. It didn’t narrow, at first; then, very gradually, it did. It went up until the struts blurred into a texture and the texture into a line and the line into nothing, into the glare, into the fine bright falling tendrils of the Lace, four kilometres over his head.</p>\n<p>Osk and Pim and the riggers got him out of the cart. He stood in the suit at the foot of the wreck, looking up. Ione stood beside him, with her head tipped back.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Pim, in a very small voice. “It’s big.”</p>\n<p>Ione took her rope off her shoulder and began to uncoil it, favouring the splinted wrist, and started tying it into the harness they’d spliced for the suit, her fingers moving fast.</p>\n<p>“Don’t look up,” she said, without looking at him. “It only gets taller.”</p>"}, {"file": "12-interlude-two-hours-in-a-chair.md", "label": "Interlude", "title": "Two Hours in a Chair", "part": "Part Two · Holdfast", "partStart": false, "words": 4190, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Nobody’s keeping score. Nobody ever was.</p><span class=\"attr\">Teo Lisk, to a man in a keel suit, Lattice Station 14-C</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\"><strong>Minute 0.</strong></p>\n<p>She watched him go up the ladder, and then she watched him stop being a person.</p>\n<p>It was the strangest thing she had ever seen, and she had seen a stack fire in Kovač turn a forty-storey housing tower into a candle. He climbed. Ordinary. Grey suit, humming keel, her keel, gauntlets on the rungs, one after the other, climbing on the back side of the ladder where the boy’s map said to climb. Thirty metres. Thirty-five. And then he reached the part of the shaft where the air was wrong, the trembling curtain, and he went into it — and he <em>stuttered</em>. That was the only word. He was on one rung and then three rungs up and then two rungs up again and then five. Bits of him were ahead of other bits. For a second she saw his arm reaching up out of the top of the curtain while his boots were still under it and the middle of him was a smear, a wet grey streak on the air, like a thumbprint on a lens.</p>\n<p>Then he was above it. And he was moving so fast that she couldn’t follow him. A blur, going up the shaft like something fired. Then a dot. Then nothing.</p>\n<p>“Talking to you,” she said into her radio. “Still here. Keep going. Nearly there.”</p>\n<p>Her radio made a noise like a bird.</p>\n<p>She sat back in the chair.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk to the empty room. “Right, then.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 1.</strong></p>\n<p>She took stock, because that was what you did.</p>\n<p>Station air: good. Pressure: good. There was a panel by the door that told her so, and she believed it, because her ears weren’t popping and she wasn’t dizzy. Water: a cooler, two-thirds full, with a stack of paper cups. Food: the suit’s ration paste, which was vile, and a first-aid cabinet, which on inspection contained bandages, burn gel, a foil blanket, a bottle of painkillers with most of the painkillers gone, and a single packet of digestive biscuits, one-third eaten, with a sell-by date nine months ago.</p>\n<p>She ate one. It was soft. She ate another.</p>\n<p>The suit without its keel was just a suit. Heavy. The exo worked. She could walk about. Every so often the room seemed to sway, very slightly, like the deck of a ship, and she’d put her hand out to the back of the chair and it would pass. The lee, the calm patch, the pocket of ordinary. It filled the booth. Just about. She’d dropped washers at the edges: by the door, by the far wall, in the corners. Straight down, every one. Good.</p>\n<p>She looked out of the viewport at the ladder going up into the trembling air, and at the bottom of the ladder, the shape lying crumpled at its foot.</p>\n<p>“Farr,” she said. “You’d have had a theory about all this.”</p>\n<p>The room didn’t answer. She hadn’t really expected it to.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 2.</strong></p>\n<p>She went down through the hatch with the foil blanket.</p>\n<p>It was stupid. It served no purpose. She knew that. She knelt beside the body at the bottom of the conduit — the little broad body in its patchwork clothes, its legs white bone and its face half a face — and unfolded the blanket, and laid it over, from the head down, and tucked it round. The foil crackled. It shone in the pearly light. The little yellow lamp on its cord was still showing at the neck; she tucked that under too.</p>\n<p>“There you go, love,” she said. “Whoever you are.”</p>\n<p>She went back up. She shut the hatch.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 3.</strong></p>\n<p>The radio chirped.</p>\n<p>Not like before. Not the bird-squeaks she’d been getting since he went through, the little ragged scraps of him arriving squashed. This was a single long <em>tweeee</em>, rising, about the length of a breath, and then a click.</p>\n<p>And the little amber light came on at the bottom of her visor. RECEIVING. BUFFERING. RETIMING.</p>\n<p>She sat forward in the chair.</p>\n<p>It took the suit a minute and a half to chew it out. She watched the light the whole time. Then it went <em>ding</em>, and there he was, Casimir Hale, his dry, tired, clever voice in her ear — and it was <em>long</em>. It went on and on. She sat in the chair and listened to him talk, and her mouth fell open, and after a while she realised it had been open for some time, and shut it.</p>\n<p><em>There’s a city in here, Teo.</em></p>\n<p>Thirty-one thousand. A hundred and sixty-eight years. Rats with the names of officials. The Tending, sung wrong. The Section 9 placard in a church.</p>\n<p><em>They called me the Relief.</em></p>\n<p>She laughed out loud. She couldn’t help it. She sat alone in a booth at the bottom of the world and laughed until she had to wipe her eyes with the back of her gauntlet, and her visor got in the way, so she took the helmet off and laughed some more.</p>\n<p>And then he said the other thing. <em>I lied to them. Within an hour.</em> And he said it the way you’d tell a doctor where it hurt.</p>\n<p>She stopped laughing.</p>\n<p>It had been three minutes. Three minutes, on her suit clock, since he’d climbed up that ladder. He’d got to the top, and been pulled out by a stranger, and carried through a city, and made a saint, and stood up in front of twenty thousand people, and gone to bed, and sat on the floor and talked to her about it — and all of that was <em>a day</em>, for him. Most of a day. While she’d eaten two biscuits and covered a dead woman with a blanket.</p>\n<p>“Bloody hell, Hale,” she said.</p>\n<p>Then she thought about it for a moment, and put the helmet back on, and pressed the button on her own recorder, and said: <em>Right. Your first chirp’s come through.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 11.</strong></p>\n<p>The hardline rang.</p>\n<p>She hadn’t known it was there until it rang. It was a telephone — an old one, a handset on a hook on the wall by the dead monitors, grey plastic, a curly cord. It rang with a bell. An actual bell. She stared at it for three rings and then got up and picked it up.</p>\n<p>“Lattice Station Fourteen-C.”</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk.” Gaunt. Of course it was. That soft voice, as if he were ringing to ask whether she’d enjoyed a book he’d lent her. “What a relief. We weren’t sure the line would still be live. We lost your pucks about twenty minutes ago; this is the old copper, it runs all the way round the Lattice. I’ve been ringing every station on it.” A pause. “Is Dr Hale with you?”</p>\n<p>She looked out of the viewport at the ladder.</p>\n<p>“No,” she said.</p>\n<p>“No?”</p>\n<p>“He’s gone up. Through the Shear. With my keel. His was damaged.” She kept her voice very flat. “Farr’s dead.”</p>\n<p>A silence on the line.</p>\n<p>“I’m very sorry,” said Gaunt, and to her considerable surprise, it sounded as though he were. “He was — very young.” Another pause. “And Dr Hale is — proceeding?”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Proceeding to the Crown.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“To reseat Six.”</p>\n<p>The chip. Silver seal. <em>Any means.</em> Underlined. She’d dropped it in a coolant channel and watched it go.</p>\n<p>“He’s reseating Six,” said Teo Lisk. “As we speak.”</p>\n<p>She’d never lied to the Calm in her life. Not once. Not in twenty-nine years. You didn’t. It wasn’t that anyone had ever told her not to; it was simply that you didn’t, the way you didn’t put your hand in a fire. And it came out of her as smooth and easy as water out of a tap, and Gaunt said, “Oh, that’s good. That’s very good. The Minister will be so relieved,” and she stood there holding the grey plastic handset and felt it go all the way through her, cold, like swallowing ice.</p>\n<p><em>Easy</em>. It was the easiest thing she’d ever done.</p>\n<p>“Major,” said Gaunt, “I won’t keep you. I only wanted you to know — the grid’s failing. Kovač is at half. We’ve started the rolling cuts on the upper tiers. We’re telling people it’s maintenance.” His voice changed, very slightly. “People are being very calm. I’ve never seen anything like it. They sit in the dark in their flats and wait for the lights to come back. Nobody’s asking. Nobody’s asking anything.” He stopped. “I find I’d almost rather they did.”</p>\n<p>She let the silence sit.</p>\n<p>“Well,” said Gaunt. “Do give Dr Hale my regards, if you speak to him.”</p>\n<p>He hung up.</p>\n<p>She put the handset back on its hook. She sat back down in the chair. After a while she noticed that her hands were shaking, and she put them flat on her knees and held them there until they stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minutes 12 to 60.</strong></p>\n<p>The chirps kept coming. About every four minutes. A long rising <em>tweeee</em>, a click, a minute and a half of amber light, a <em>ding</em>. And then Cas, talking.</p>\n<p>She got used to the rhythm of it. She’d sit in the chair with her boots up on the dead monitor desk and wait for the next one, the way you wait for the next episode of something. Four minutes. Four. Five. A round of his, she worked out, for every four minutes of hers: he was sending her a day at a time. She’d hear the chirp and her whole body would go still.</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer them. She started to, twice, and stopped. Anything she said would land on him most of a day later, in the middle of whatever he was doing, and what he was doing — she could hear it — was better than anything she had to tell him from a chair. She’d answer when there was something worth a day of his time.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Tollers. About a tower full of pendulums, and an old woman with eyes like a bird who’d made him soup, and a column of black stone with every Bell of a hundred and sixty-eight years chalked on it in fives. He told her about a Log. About letters to a little girl called Maddy who was seven. That one she had to stop and play again, and then she sat with it for a while, with the helmet off, looking at the ladder, before she could listen to the next.</p>\n<p>He told her about the market. About rat. About a boy called Pim who named his rats after officials.</p>\n<p>He told her about Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t say much, at first. <em>There’s a woman.</em> She’d pulled him out of the well. She had a white streak in her hair. She was a rigger. She had a key. She’d been somewhere, up near the top, and come back different, and nobody believed her. That was all, the first time. But then the next chirp he mentioned her again. And the next. <em>Ione says. Ione showed me. I was up in Ione’s loft.</em> She was in every one. By the fifth or sixth, Teo realised she could picture her: a short, broad, scarred woman with a rope on her shoulder and a way of looking at you that made you feel she’d read the end of the book.</p>\n<p>And his voice was changing.</p>\n<p>She noticed that too, around minute forty. She played an early one again to be sure, and then a later one, one after the other. The early ones were him: dry, careful, flat, a man reading out a list. The later ones weren’t. They were — she didn’t have the word. They had <em>weather</em> in them. He laughed, sometimes. He stopped in the middle of sentences because he’d seen something out of a window and wanted to tell her what it was. He talked about a wedding on a rooftop and she could hear, under the words, that he had been happy at it. For an hour, on a rooftop, with a cup of something that took the skin off his throat, he’d been happy.</p>\n<p>“Well,” Teo said to nobody. “Look at you.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 34.</strong></p>\n<p>She found the login by accident.</p>\n<p>She’d been poking at the dead monitors out of boredom, and one of them wasn’t dead — a little grey terminal under the desk, on the same old copper as the hardline, humming to itself. It had a Civil Defence emblem on its login screen. Some maintenance link. She typed in her own service number and password, not expecting anything, and it let her in.</p>\n<p>She sat and looked at it for a moment. Then she pulled up the relocation lists.</p>\n<p>It took her four minutes to find Nell Hale. Tier Thirty, Hollowmere Academy sports hall, with mother Sabine Hale-Okoro. Status: well. And a note in the welfare column in the slightly harried shorthand of a volunteer who’d been awake for thirty hours: <em>Child v. calm. Asking everyone re: stars (?). Has lizard (not hers).</em></p>\n<p>Teo laughed out loud in the empty room.</p>\n<p>Then she typed in her own sons’ names.</p>\n<p>Joss first. University accommodation, relocated to a transit hall on Tier Forty. Status: well. No note.</p>\n<p>Mattias. Kovač. Kovač wasn’t on the relocation lists; Kovač had its own. She dug. It took her ten minutes. She found him in the end on a Kovač municipal list, a crew list, not a relocation list: <em>M. Lisk, electrician, Kovač Fusion Station B, emergency staff, on shift</em>. On shift. At the plant that was holding up the whole grid at half power.</p>\n<p>Of course he was.</p>\n<p>She sat looking at his name on the screen for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she took the marker out of the pocket of her liner — the black marker from the supervisor’s desk in B-7, the one Hale had written backwards with on their side of the glass; she’d picked it up off the gallery floor on the way out, without telling him, because she hadn’t been able to leave it — and she got up and went to the viewport, and in the bottom corner, small, where she’d see it from the chair, she wrote:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">MATTIAS<br>JOSS</div>\n<p>She looked at them. Then, underneath, she added:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">BEN</div>\n<p>And then, after a moment:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">SEREN (THE LADY)</div>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 52.</strong></p>\n<p>The lee moved.</p>\n<p>She was halfway through a message — the one about the wedding, the riggers tying their wrists together — when she felt the chair tip. Very slightly. A lean to the left, like a car taking a bend. She sat up. The room swayed. On the floor by the far wall, where she’d dropped a washer when she first came in, the washer slid.</p>\n<p>It slid about a hand’s width across the floor, toward the corner, and stopped.</p>\n<p>She watched the corner. She watched it until her eyes ached. Nothing else happened. The room steadied.</p>\n<p>She got up and dragged the chair a metre to the right, into the middle of the room, and sat back down in it, and pressed play again. When she recorded her next message, she mentioned it. <em>The edge came across the floor, maybe a hand’s width. I moved the chair.</em> Casual. As if she were telling him the weather.</p>\n<p>It was the weather. That was the thing. It was only the weather.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 64.</strong></p>\n<p><em>I’m going to tell them.</em></p>\n<p>She listened to that one sitting very still. He said it quietly, at the end of a long message about a rigger crew that had been caught by the Lace and turned to bones. Kindling Round. Three rounds away, for him. Everything. The Outside, the test, the Deputy Minister, the debt, the lock, the key, Ione. In front of the whole city.</p>\n<p>She knew what it would cost him. She’d sat next to him at the canteen window in the Collar and watched him not answer <em>why</em>. She’d seen his face at the booth when he thought the Shear might settle things for him. She knew.</p>\n<p>She played it again. She played it a good many times, over the next quarter of an hour, with the helmet off and her boots up and her eyes on the ladder.</p>\n<p>Then, at minute seventy-seven, she pressed record and said, <em>Christ, Hale. Good.</em> And a lot of other things, most of which she’d forgotten as soon as she’d said them, except that she’d told him she was proud of him, and she’d meant it, and she’d been glad he couldn’t hear it for a day.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minutes 78 to 127.</strong></p>\n<p>He didn’t send anything else for a long time.</p>\n<p>She understood, doing the sum on the back of the fire regulations with a ballpoint she found in the desk, that for him it would be — what? Rounds and rounds. Days. Weeks. She didn’t know what had happened. She sat in the chair and waited.</p>\n<p>She read the fire regulations again. She did the sum again. She ate the last digestive. She went down the hatch and sat for a while with the lady in the blanket, just sitting, not saying anything, because it seemed wrong to leave her alone. She came back up. She dropped washers. She moved the chair once more, at minute ninety-seven, when the washer by the door slid, and again at minute a hundred and eighteen, when the one in the far corner did. The room was smaller now. Not much. You wouldn’t notice, unless you were dropping washers. The calm part of it was a circle in the middle of the floor, about three metres across, and the chair was in it, and she was in the chair.</p>\n<p>At minute a hundred and ten, the hardline rang. She let it ring. It rang twenty times and stopped.</p>\n<p>She talked to Ben. Out loud. She told him about Mattias on shift at Kovač, and about Joss, who’d wanted to be a pilot when he was five and a poet at fifteen and was now doing something with computers she couldn’t pronounce. She told him about her husband, Anders, who’d dropped dead in the kitchen eleven years ago in the middle of making toast, and how she’d come home from a shift and found the toast still in the toaster, and how for a year afterwards she couldn’t be in the room when anybody made toast. She told him Jonah would get the dog. She’d see to it. She’d buy the bloody dog herself.</p>\n<p>At minute a hundred and twenty-three she realised she’d been crying for some time, and stopped, and blew her nose on a bandage from the first-aid kit.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 128.</strong></p>\n<p><em>Tweeeeeeeeeeee.</em></p>\n<p>It was the longest chirp yet. It went on for three breaths. Then a click, and another <em>tweeee</em>, shorter, and a third, and the amber light came on and stayed on for nearly four minutes while the suit chewed, and she sat forward in the chair with both hands gripping its arms.</p>\n<p><em>Ding.</em></p>\n<p>He’d been in a tank. Eleven rounds, in the dark.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Commons. He told it plainly, flatly, the old way, the way he’d talked at the gate — a man reading a list — and she could hear what it was costing him to do it that way, because it was the only way he could do it at all. The truth, and the banner, and a man shouting <em>it’s open</em>. Forty-seven. A little boy he’d pulled off a ladder. A father who’d said <em>I’ll come back for him</em>. The sound that came up the well.</p>\n<p>She sat with her fist pressed against her mouth.</p>\n<p>Then the Quiet. Then an old man with a lamp. Then a big Warden with a rope. Then the Tollers’ tower again, and the old woman, and the number she’d given him — <em>thirty-one thousand, three hundred and sixty</em> — and then, in a different voice, a lighter voice, almost wondering: <em>Teo, they’re making lamps. The whole city. They’re putting them out in the lanes. They’re getting ready for the dark.</em></p>\n<p>And then:</p>\n<p><em>We’re going up. Tonight. Spindle Four. Ione and me. The electronics will go, up there, Ben said they would, so this might be the last you get. If it works, the Lamp goes out. The Shear goes. Time in here will be the same as time out there. They’ll be able to walk down. Thirty-one thousand of them. All at once, down fourteen wells, into the Works. Come and get them, Teo. Bring everyone. Bring blankets. Bring⁠—</em></p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p><em>There’s another file. It’s for Nell. It’s everything I’ve recorded for her, since I got here. It’s about a month. I don’t know if I’ll — if you get out, and I don’t. Would you give it to her? Please. And tell her⁠—</em></p>\n<p>Another pause, longer.</p>\n<p><em>No. It’s in the file. It’s all in the file.</em></p>\n<p>And then, very quietly:</p>\n<p><em>Thank you for the keel.</em></p>\n<p><em>Ding.</em></p>\n<p>A second file arrived behind it. The suit showed it on her visor: a little icon, a size, a name. NELL. A month. A month of a man talking to his daughter, squashed into a chirp three breaths long.</p>\n<p>She sat in the chair and looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 130.</strong></p>\n<p>She recorded her answer.</p>\n<p>She did it with the helmet off, holding the recorder close to her mouth, sitting forward in the chair with her elbows on her knees. She didn’t plan it. She knew it would be the last one — knew it the way you know, sometimes, at the top of a flood barrier with the water coming up, that this is the last thing you’ll get to say to somebody for a while, maybe ever. So she said it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know, afterwards, all of what she said. Some of it she remembered. That she’d give Nell the file herself, by hand, and sit with her while she listened. That she’d tell Jonah about the dog. That he was to look after the rigger with the white streak in her hair, because it sounded as though nobody had for a while. That he was to come back and buy her the large one he owed her. That her sons were called Mattias and Joss and she’d written their names on the window where she could see them, and she’d written Ben’s under, and Seren’s, and if he didn’t mind she’d write his, too, now.</p>\n<p>That guilt was a lousy reason. That she’d said it before and she’d say it again because he was the kind of thick-headed bastard who needed to hear things twice.</p>\n<p>That she was fine.</p>\n<p>That she’d be here.</p>\n<p><em>Bring them out, Cas.</em></p>\n<p>She pressed send. The suit chirped, a tiny sound, a squeak, two whole minutes of her gone up the ladder in a blink. She sat back.</p>\n<p>She took the marker and went to the window and wrote, under SEREN (THE LADY):</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">CAS</div>\n<p>She stood looking at it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><strong>Minute 132.</strong></p>\n<p>The washer by the door slid.</p>\n<p>Not a hand’s width. It slid a metre, rattling across the floor, and hit the leg of the desk, and spun, and lay still. And the one by the far wall slid, toward the corner. And the room <em>leaned</em>.</p>\n<p>She put her hand out to the chair.</p>\n<p>The edge of the lee was coming across the floor. She could see it now. She hadn’t been able to see it before, but she could now: a line on the floor, a sort of shimmer, a ripple in the air a hand’s height off the ground, like heat over a road. On one side of it, the dust lay flat. On the other side it was moving, drifting, crawling very slowly toward the corners. The line was about a metre from the door. As she watched, it moved closer.</p>\n<p>She did the thing you did. She took stock. Station air: good. Pressure: good. Suit: no keel. Exit: through the door, into a Lattice full of weather, four kilometres from the gate, or through the hatch, into the conduit, beside the lady in the blanket, where the Shear was forty metres up and the weather was whatever the weather was down there.</p>\n<p>The line moved closer.</p>\n<p>She took the chair and dragged it to the far corner of the room, the corner by the viewport, as far from the door as she could get, and set it down, and sat in it, and put her boots up flat against the wall.</p>\n<p>The line was two metres away now. Then a metre and a half. The room swayed, and swayed back. On the other side of the viewport, in the conduit, the Shear trembled on the air as it always had, the ladder going up through it, jumping and smearing. Above it, far up, the pale bright disc of light.</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk sat in her chair in the corner with her boots against the wall and her hands folded in her lap, and watched it come.</p>\n<p>“Come on, Hale,” she said.</p>"}, {"file": "13-the-lees.md", "label": "Chapter 12", "title": "The Lees", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": true, "words": 5794, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Chalk falls true in a Lee. Chalk falls crooked out of one. When chalk falls up, pray.</p><span class=\"attr\">Riggers’ saying, Holdfast</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">For the first kilometre, it was only climbing.</p>\n<p>That was how Ione put it, at the bottom, tying him in. <em>The first kilometre’s only climbing. After that it’s the Lees.</em> And he’d nodded as if he understood, the way he’d nodded at so much in Holdfast, and found out what it meant by doing it.</p>\n<p>Spindle Four was hollow. From the city it looked like a solid black tower, but from inside it was a cage — a vast open lattice of keelstone struts, a hundred metres across at the base, rising around them in a great latticed tube, with the violet light of the Lamp pouring in through every gap in a million bright slanting bars. Up the inside of the lattice, clinging to it, ran the old maintenance ways: iron ladders and iron stairs and narrow steel walkways, bolted to the struts, zigzagging up and up the inside of the tube in flights and landings and switchbacks until they vanished into the glare. The lift’s rack-rail had run up the centre once. Its stump still stood there, a twisted black mast rising out of the wreckage at the bottom and ending, ragged, a few hundred metres up, where it had snapped.</p>\n<p>They climbed the stairs. Where there were no stairs, they climbed the ladders. Where the ladders had rusted through or fallen away — and there were many places where they had — Ione went up the bare lattice itself, finding holds in the struts with her hands and her boots, her splinted wrist held a little stiffly, the rope paying out behind her from the coil on her shoulder. When she reached the next walkway she’d tie off and call down, and he’d come up after her on the rope. The suit’s exo did most of it. The keel hummed. The harness she’d spliced round his hardpoints held.</p>\n<p>It was only climbing. It was the hardest thing he’d ever done.</p>\n<p>Because he got heavier.</p>\n<p>He felt it begin at about three hundred metres: not a sudden thing, not a gust or a shear like the Works, but a slow, patient, steady increase, as though someone were adding sand to his pockets a grain at a time. At five hundred, the exo’s whine had changed pitch. At seven hundred, he noticed his breathing — not from effort, but from weight, the sheer work of lifting his own ribs. The suit’s display, when he looked, said LOCAL G: 1.8. At the bottom of the spindle, in Holdfast, it had said 1.4.</p>\n<p>“You’ll weigh half as much again as you do in the city by two kilometres,” Ione said, when he told her. She was sitting on a walkway above him, eating greenbread, her legs dangling over the drop. “Twice by three. By the Rim — I don’t know. I wasn’t counting.”</p>\n<p>“How do <em>you</em> do it?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with mild surprise. “I was born to it,” she said. “We all were. Holdfast’s heavy. I’ve been climbing spindles since I was nine.” She considered. “And I’m small. The small ones do better. Less to lift.”</p>\n<p>She held her hand out flat, palm up, over the edge of the walkway. There was a little heap of white chalk in it. She blew on it, gently, and the chalk went off her palm in a puff, and drifted, and fell. He watched it. It fell straight down, in a thin white curtain, all the way into the bars of violet light below until he lost it.</p>\n<p>“That’s the last true chalk for a while,” she said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At a kilometre, the Lees began.</p>\n<p>He understood what she’d meant, then, about the chalk. The Lamp’s push, which had been growing steadily as they climbed, began to <em>vary</em>. You’d climb a flight of stairs, heavier and heavier, and come out onto a landing near one of the great keelstone struts — the huge black beams that braced the lattice, some of them five or ten metres thick — and suddenly the weight would ease. Not all of it. Some. As though a hand pressing on your shoulders had lifted a little. And then you’d step away from the strut and it would come back.</p>\n<p>“That’s the strut pulling,” said Ione. “Against the Lamp. It’s like the Anvil. It’s keelstone, it pulls. Near it, the pull and the push fight. You weigh less.” She blew chalk. It went off her palm and drifted — not down, but down and <em>sideways</em>, toward the strut, in a long slanting curve. “See? It’s pulling the chalk, too. Crooked chalk. That’s a Lee. A little one.”</p>\n<p>The Lees were how you climbed a spindle. He learned them over the next three rounds — their rounds, sleep to sleep. Down in the city, the rounds had already begun to slip behind them. There were little ones by every strut, where you could stand and breathe. There were big ones — rare, precious — where two or three struts came together, where the pulls added up and cancelled the Lamp’s push almost completely, where the chalk fell so crooked that it hung in the air, turning slowly, and you could lie down on the walkway and feel as light as you’d felt in the city. Those were where you rested. Those were where you slept. The riggers had built little caches in the big ones, generations ago: a platform of planks, a water-catcher of canvas, a box of stores bolted to the strut. Most of them had rotted away. Ione knew the ones that hadn’t.</p>\n<p>And the Lees moved. That was the thing that had sent her up alone on the eighth round, while he sat on the steps of the Steward’s House blessing babies and not hearing a word anyone said to him. The Lamp was getting weaker — the Lightening, the debt coming due — and as it weakened, the balance between its push and the struts’ pull shifted. Lees she’d climbed through at seventeen had closed up. New ones had opened. Some had drifted a few metres up a strut. Some had split in two. She’d found them all, on that trip, by chalk and plumb bob and feel, and marked them. As they climbed he saw her marks: little white arrows scratched into the black keelstone with a nail, and numbers, and here and there a word. <em>GOOD. SMALL. BAD — GO RIGHT. SLEEP.</em></p>\n<p>“You did all this in three rounds?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Three down there. More like five up here.” She didn’t look round. “The higher you go, the more of it you get. And I was a lot faster without you.”</p>\n<p>He laughed. It came out of him before he could stop it, a real laugh, surprised, loud in the suit, and he heard it echo round the inside of the spindle, and she glanced down at him from the ladder above with an expression he couldn’t read.</p>\n<p>“What?” he said.</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” She went on climbing. “You don’t do that much.”</p>\n<p>“Laugh?”</p>\n<p>“Mm.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He thought about the hangar, and a spanner folded in half, and Teo saying <em>Sounds like working for the Commonweal</em>. He thought about the canteen and the tea that was mostly a structural material. He thought about the gallery floor and MINISTER →.</p>\n<p>“I’m getting better,” he said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the second round of the climb — the second time they slept — he looked down.</p>\n<p>They were at two kilometres. They’d stopped in a big Lee, one of the rigger caches, a platform of old planks lashed to the junction of three struts, with a canvas water-catcher and a steel box of rotted stores and a view straight down through the open lattice of the spindle to the city. He was lying on his back on the platform, in the suit, feeling almost light for the first time in a round, and he rolled over onto his front and looked down through the gap between the planks.</p>\n<p>Holdfast was very small.</p>\n<p>It lay at the bottom of the world, a tiny intricate patch of scaffold and container and keelstone spread across the black disc of the Anvil, the other seven spindles rising out of it like the pillars of a temple seen from its roof. The Commons was a speck. The Drop District was a smudge at the edge. Smoke went up from it in threads so fine he could hardly see them.</p>\n<p>And as he watched, it began to go dark.</p>\n<p>It started at the centre — at the Steward’s House, at the foot of Spindle One — and went outward. A wave. A sort of dimming, a darkening, spreading out across the city from the middle to the edges. The shutters, he realised. The shutters coming down at the end of second shift. The night horn — he couldn’t hear it; he was too high, too far — and ten thousand shutters closing.</p>\n<p>But it was slow. He’d stood in the lanes and heard that wave go past, the clatter and bang of it; it took a minute, perhaps, to cross the whole city. From here it crept. It spread across Holdfast the way ink spreads into wet paper, lane by lane, so slowly that he could watch a single street go dark, house by house, and count his breaths between the houses. He lay there on the planks and watched it for what felt like three or four minutes before it reached the edges.</p>\n<p>“What⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Watch the smoke,” said Ione. She’d rolled over beside him on the planks and was looking down too, her chin on her folded arms.</p>\n<p>He watched the smoke. The thin grey threads going up from the city’s chimneys. They were drifting, the way they always drifted, the Lamp’s heat stirring them — but slowly. Far too slowly. They leaned and curled like threads of ink in syrup, as though the air down there had thickened.</p>\n<p>“We’re faster than them,” said Ione. “Up here. The higher you go, the faster you live. Down there, that’s a minute. Up here, it’s three, near enough. And it gets longer. By the top of the Lees you’ll look down and they’ll be a picture. Nothing moving at all. We’ll sleep a whole sleep and wake up and they’ll still be in the same night.” She blew a little puff of chalk out over the edge and watched it drift away sideways, turning. “We’ll be at the top before they’ve had two rounds down there. Maybe less.”</p>\n<p>“It’s holding its breath,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” She sounded pleased that he’d seen it. “That’s what I thought. When I was seventeen. I lay here — right here, this cache — and looked down at it and thought, <em>the city’s holding its breath</em>. And I thought: if I stayed up here, if I stayed long enough, high enough, the whole of Holdfast would stand still for me. Nobody would grow up. Nobody else would go down a well. I could keep them all exactly as they were, as long as I liked.” She was quiet. “I didn’t, though. I went on up.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her lying beside him, her chin on her arms, looking down at the still city. At the white streak in her hair, bright in the Lamp’s light. At the stitches over her eye.</p>\n<p>“Why?” he said. “At seventeen. Why did you go up?”</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer for a while. Down below, the city went dark.</p>\n<p>“I’ll tell you,” she said. “Later. When we’re higher. It’s easier to tell higher up.” She rolled onto her back and put her arm over her eyes. “Sleep. We’ve got a long way.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo’s last message finished arriving on the third round.</p>\n<p>It had begun on the first, a few hours above the wreck at the spindle’s foot. The suit had started to groan in the middle of a climb — a long, low, falling note from the helmet speaker, so deep it was almost below hearing, so slow it didn’t seem to move at all — and he’d stopped on the ladder and hung there, listening, until Ione called down <em>what is it</em> and he said <em>Teo</em>.</p>\n<p>It went on for two rounds. It was much longer than any of the others: she’d talked for a couple of minutes, and they were climbing all the while into faster time, so that her minutes had further and further to stretch to reach them. It groaned under their climbing and through their sleeps, a vast slow wordless sound filling the lattice around them like the voice of the spindle itself. On the third round, halfway between two kilometres and three, he felt it begin to fall away, and he stopped in the next Lee and sat on the planks with his back against a strut and his knees up and listened to the last of it, and Ione sat beside him and didn’t say anything.</p>\n<p>Then <em>ding</em>.</p>\n<p>He switched it to the suit’s outer speaker so that Ione could hear.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Teo Lisk. “Last one. I know it’s the last one. You said the electronics would go and I believe you, you’re the sort of pessimist who’s usually right.” A breath. “Got your — got all of it. The Commons. The tank. Your old man with the lamp. The lamps in the lanes.” Her voice caught and went on. “The forty-seven. I’m sorry, Cas. I’m so sorry. I know what you’re doing with that, right now, wherever you are. I can hear you doing it from here. Stop it.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“I’ve got Nell’s file. A month of you. I’m going to give it to her myself. By hand. I’ll go to Tier Thirty and I’ll find her in her gym with her lizard and I’ll sit with her while she listens, and if she wants to cry I’ll let her, and if she wants to hit me I’ll let her do that too. You’ve got my word. And I’ll tell Jonah about the dog. I’ll get him the bloody dog myself.</p>\n<p>“And you look after that rigger. The one with the white streak. You never said much about her, but you didn’t have to, you said her name forty times in a month. It sounds like nobody’s looked after her in a long while. Somebody should.”</p>\n<p>Beside him, Ione went very still.</p>\n<p>“You owe me a drink,” said Teo. “A large one. Don’t forget. I’ve written my boys’ names on the window, here, where I can see them from the chair. Mattias and Joss. Mattias is on shift at Kovač, holding up the grid. Of course he is. Joss is fine. I’ve written Ben’s under. And the lady’s — Seren’s. And I’ve written yours. I hope you don’t mind. It seemed right.”</p>\n<p>A long pause. He could hear her breathe.</p>\n<p>“Guilt’s a lousy reason, Cas. I said it at the booth and I’m saying it again because you’re the kind of thick-headed bastard who needs to hear things twice. Don’t you dare go up there and do something noble because you think you owe it. Do it because it’s useful. Or because you want to. Or because <em>she</em> wants you to. Anything. Anything but that.”</p>\n<p>A breath.</p>\n<p>“I’m fine. The lee’s fine. I’ll be here.” A pause. “Bring them out, Cas.”</p>\n<p><em>Ding</em>.</p>\n<p>The suit hissed. The display at the bottom of his visor flickered, and showed a line of text — SIGNAL LOST — and flickered again, and showed nothing. The little icon for the radio went grey.</p>\n<p>He sat on the planks with his back against the strut. Ione sat beside him. Neither of them spoke. Far down through the lattice, the city lay in its night, the smoke standing up from it like wire.</p>\n<p>“She sounds,” said Ione eventually, “like somebody I’d like.”</p>\n<p>“You would.”</p>\n<p>“<em>The rigger with the white streak.</em>” She said it slowly, as if trying the words for size. Then she looked at him sideways. “Forty times?”</p>\n<p>He felt his face go hot inside the helmet.</p>\n<p>“She was exaggerating,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Mm,” said Ione, and looked away, down at the sleeping city. But he saw the corner of her mouth, and it had moved.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>On the fourth round they felt the last Bell.</p>\n<p>They were at nearly three kilometres. He weighed — the display said, before the display began to lie to him — two point nine times what he’d weighed on the Tier Nine arterial, a thousand years ago, a day and a half ago. The exo was screaming. They moved from Lee to Lee now in short, brutal dashes, never more than a few dozen metres between them, Ione going first and then hauling him, both of them resting in every crooked-chalk pocket they could find, sometimes for an hour, before the next. Below them the city had stopped. It lay in its shuttered dusk like a painting of itself, the same night it had been in when they’d looked down from two kilometres, the smoke from its chimneys standing up in fine grey threads that didn’t move at all.</p>\n<p>The Bell came up the spindle.</p>\n<p>It came so slowly that at first he didn’t know it for what it was. A pressure. A faint rising hum in the strut under his hand, very deep, like a tide coming in under a pier. It rose. And rose. It took so long to rise that he had time to notice it, and wonder, and understand, and turn to look at Ione. She had her palm flat against the strut, and her eyes closed.</p>\n<p>It went on rising. Minutes. The whole spindle was humming with it, every strut, the walkways vibrating faintly under them, a vast slow note going through the lattice like a wind through a forest. It reached its height. It held there. It began, so slowly he couldn’t be sure of it for a long time, to fall.</p>\n<p>It lasted, he guessed, the better part of an hour.</p>\n<p>When it had finally gone, Ione took her hand off the strut and opened her eyes.</p>\n<p>“One hundred and twenty-three thousand, seven hundred and fourteen,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been counting?”</p>\n<p>“I’m a Toller.” She said it as though it were obvious. “I’ve always counted. Even when they threw me out.” She looked down at the city far below. “That’s the last one we’ll hear.”</p>\n<p>“The last⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Up here, they get too slow. The next one’s hours off, and by the time it comes we’ll be higher, and up there they take a round to rise and a round to fall. You won’t be able to tell it from the spindle breathing.” She looked at him. “That was the last Bell. For us. Unless we come down.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p><em>Unless we come down.</em></p>\n<p>Neither of them pursued it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>That night — that sleep; there were no nights, up here — they stopped in the best Lee on the spindle.</p>\n<p>It was at the junction of four great struts, three kilometres up, where the lattice narrowed and the black beams came together from four directions in a vast knot of keelstone, and between them there was a pocket of calm so deep that Ione’s chalk, when she blew it, didn’t fall at all. It hung in front of her face in a small white cloud, turning, glittering in the violet light, like dust in a sunbeam. There was a cache: a platform of planks, a water-catcher with water in it, a box. And it was so light — so wonderfully, dizzyingly light — that when he stepped onto the platform he nearly fell, overbalancing, his body braced for a weight that wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>He took the helmet off. He hadn’t taken it off for four rounds. The air was thin and warm and smelled of hot stone and chalk. He sat on the planks, in the suit, with the helmet in his lap, and put his head back against the strut, and breathed.</p>\n<p>Ione sat opposite him with her back against another strut. She’d taken off her harness and her boots. She’d unwrapped the splint on her wrist and was flexing it, slowly, wincing. Above them, through the narrowing lattice, the Lace hung in the sky — very close now, much closer than from the city, its fine bright threads hanging down in long trailing streamers toward the spindle-tops, moving very slowly, like weed in a current. Below them the city lay frozen in the middle of its night.</p>\n<p>“I was seventeen,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t move. He didn’t want to startle it.</p>\n<p>“My mother had gone down the Drop two years before. I’d held the ladder for her, when she painted the wall. They didn’t give me to the Drop. They said I was a child.” She flexed her wrist. “I think they thought I’d be quieter, without her. I was. For two years I was very quiet. I counted Bells. I worked the ropes. I didn’t talk to anyone except Gran.”</p>\n<p>A pause.</p>\n<p>“And then one round I went up. I didn’t tell anyone. I didn’t decide to, really. I just went. I told myself I was going to find the edge of the world. Where the Lamp stopped and something else started. I thought if I went high enough I’d see over it. Over the Rim. Into whatever was on the other side.” She almost smiled. “I was seventeen. And I think⁠—” She stopped. “I think I thought I might find her. Up there. I knew she’d gone <em>down</em>. I watched her go down. But I thought — if everything goes round, up there, the way people said — if the Lamp was a place where things went round⁠—”</p>\n<p>She didn’t finish.</p>\n<p>“I climbed this spindle,” she said. “I didn’t know the Lees then, not the high ones. I found them. I nearly died four times. I got to the top of the Lees — about half a kilometre above us — and there’s a gallery there. Round the outside of Spindle One, near the top. A walkway, a ring, going right round the spindle, with a roof over it and windows. The old crews used to use it. It had an air-garden in it — a long tank of algae all the way round, to keep the air fresh for the Crown. And a gantry, across from Four to One. I went across the gantry. I went into the gallery.” She looked at the chalk hanging in the air in front of her. “And the light came on.”</p>\n<p>“The light.”</p>\n<p>“The Crown’s light. The night of the Kindling. It’s — it’s still there. In that gallery. You’ll see. It goes round and round. The light from those nine minutes. It’s trapped. It goes round the spindle in the gallery windows and never gets out. And when you’re in it, you’re⁠—” she searched “⁠—you’re <em>in the Crown</em>. On the night. You can see it all. The people. The Crown crew, in their white coats, at their boards. The Lamp in the window, before it broke. Founder Crane with his key on its chain and his hair all combed. Ruth.” She said the name gently. “Ruth at the board. I didn’t know who any of them were. Not then. I thought they were ghosts. They were all lit up, sort of, from inside. You could see through them. You could walk through them.”</p>\n<p>“And then?”</p>\n<p>“And then it all happens. Six goes. The Lamp breaks. The walkway goes. The key falls. Ruth pulls the hammer. They all run for the lift. Nine minutes.” She paused. “And then it starts again.”</p>\n<p>The chalk turned slowly in the air between them.</p>\n<p>“I tried to leave. Of course I did. I walked out of the gallery the way I’d come in. And I walked straight back into the gallery, at the other end, where I’d started. As if the gallery was a ring and I’d just gone round it. Every door. Every window. The gantry back to Four — I walked across it and I was coming back across it the other way, into the gallery. Everything led back in.” She said it very plainly. “And the light started again. Ruth at the board. <em>Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol.</em> Nine minutes. And again. And again.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“Three years.”</p>\n<p>He’d known. She’d told him, in the Tower, that first night. It was different, hearing her say it here, at the top of the world, with the chalk hanging in the air.</p>\n<p>“Three years,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I counted.” She almost smiled again. “Of course I counted. A hundred and seventy-five thousand, two hundred and — some. Nine minutes each. I lost count a few times. There were a few months when I didn’t count anything.” She looked at her wrist, flexing it. “There were a few months when I didn’t do anything at all but lie on the floor of the gallery and let them happen through me. Ruth walked through me. Crane walked through me. The key fell through me. Again and again and again and I lay there and let it.”</p>\n<p>“How did you — food? Water? For three years?”</p>\n<p>“The air-garden.” She glanced at him. “Algae. And the gallery had a condensate trap for the Crown’s water. I lived on algae and condensation for three years.” A pause. “You’d have liked that. Your friend. The one in the hall.”</p>\n<p>“Ben.”</p>\n<p>“Ben,” she agreed. “He’d have understood.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t answer.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“It wasn’t a time loop,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it later, after a long silence, when the chalk had drifted a little way off and hung near the strut.</p>\n<p>“Not for me. I thought it was, for years. I thought I was going round and round in time. But I wasn’t. I got older. My hair⁠—” she touched the white streak “⁠—that happened in there. My body went on. I remembered everything. Every cycle, I remembered every cycle before it. I was living forward. The whole time. Just — in a place that went round.” She looked at him. “It was <em>them</em> that went round. The ghosts. The light. They were the loop. Not me.”</p>\n<p>“The light’s on closed paths,” said Cas slowly. “Round the spindle. Trapped. And the gallery⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The gallery joins up with itself. You walk out and you walk in.” She nodded. “Yes. That’s it. That’s what I worked out, eventually. I was the only thing in there that was going <em>forward</em>. Everything else was going round.”</p>\n<p>“That’s worse,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.” She didn’t look away. “That’s what I learned. That’s the thing I learned in there, in three years, that nobody in Holdfast would ever believe.” She held his eyes. “Ruth lived those nine minutes once. Just once. In the Crown, on the night of the Kindling. It was the worst nine minutes of her life and she lived them one time and then she went down the lift and had a whole life after, and Tomas and her children and eleven grandchildren, and died an old woman. The light in the gallery isn’t <em>her</em>. It isn’t reliving it. It isn’t tired of it. It doesn’t know. It’s just the same nine minutes, being true, over and over.” She looked at the chalk. “I’m the one who lived them a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. I’m the only one who ever did. The horror of it was never in the loop. It was in whoever was watching.”</p>\n<p>He sat without moving.</p>\n<p>“If a loop’s really closed,” said Ione, “if nothing in it goes forward, if nothing in it can remember — then there’s nobody inside it to be tired of it. It’s just once. It’s once, and it’s true, and it stays true. It’s only forever for whoever’s outside, looking in.” She shrugged a little, as if embarrassed by how much she’d said. “That’s all. That’s all I know. I worked it out on the floor of a gallery when I was nineteen. In there, nothing happened once. Out here, everything does.”</p>\n<p>He thought about fifteen years. He thought about a flat on Tier Nine and a phone that never rang and a man who got up every day and did the same nothing and went to bed. The same day, over and over, for fifteen years. And a pen, a nice blue Ministry pen, that he’d picked up once, one time, and signed with.</p>\n<p>He had lived that one moment — the pen — five and a half thousand times. Every morning. He was the only one who had.</p>\n<p>“How did you stay sane?” he said. “In there. Three years.”</p>\n<p>Ione thought about it seriously. She always thought about his questions seriously; he’d noticed that, in the loft, in the Tower. As if every question deserved to be turned over and looked at from underneath before you answered.</p>\n<p>“I decided to love them,” she said.</p>\n<p>“The ghosts?”</p>\n<p>“The people.” She looked up at the Lace. “After the months on the floor. I got up one cycle and I thought: I can hate this, or I can learn it. And I started watching. Properly. Every one of them. I learned all their names — they say each other’s names, you pick it up. Fen at the tensioner board. Dieter at output, who drinks his coffee too hot and burns his tongue every single time. Oksana with the camera, who’s frightened of heights and never looks out of the window. The boy with the tablet, who’s in love with Oksana.” A small smile. “And Ruth. I learned Ruth best. I sat beside her at the board. For three years. I know her better than I know anyone living. She has a mole, here⁠—” she touched her own jaw below the ear “⁠—and she bites her thumb when Crane talks. Just the side of it. She doesn’t know she does it. And when she says <em>one-thirty-five</em>, she closes her eyes first, for about half a second. She doesn’t know she does that either.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment.</p>\n<p>“She was my best friend,” said Ione, “for three years. And she never heard a single word I said to her.”</p>\n<p>The chalk hung in the air. Far below, the city didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“And the key?” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“One cycle,” said Ione, “it was there. On the gantry. After a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles where it wasn’t. It was the only different thing that had ever happened.” She touched it where it hung at her throat. “And I looked up from it. And far off — at the far end of the gantry, where it meets the wall of Spindle One, a long way off, further than it should have looked — there was someone standing. A woman. Not lit up. Not a ghost. Real. Standing where no ghost ever stood. With grey in her hair.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t breathe.</p>\n<p>“I thought it was my mother,” said Ione simply. “I shouted. <em>Mam.</em> I ran along the gantry toward her. And she turned away. And where she’d been standing, when I got close, the air was — wrong. Shimmering. A seam. I went into it and I was out. On the Rim. Out of the gallery. Out of the loop.” She let go of the key. “I never saw her again. I came down the spindle in two rounds. Gran met me at the bottom. She said I’d been gone for two days. I looked in a water-trough when I got home and I was twenty, and I had this.” She touched the white streak.</p>\n<p>“And the figure in the suit?” he said. “Coming down?”</p>\n<p>“That was on the way down. Across the gulf, on another spindle. Very far. Just once.” She looked at the suit he was sitting in, the humming keel. “I didn’t know what it was, then. I only knew I’d never seen anything like it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the suit too.</p>\n<p><em>There are only three suits.</em></p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything else.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She slept, in the end. She lay down on the planks with her head on her folded rope and her splinted wrist on her chest and was asleep in about a minute, the way riggers slept, the way soldiers slept, completely.</p>\n<p>He didn’t.</p>\n<p>He sat with his back against the strut, in the suit, with the helmet in his lap, and watched her. He watched the white streak in her hair, and the black stitches over her eye, and the slow rise and fall of her breathing in the almost-weightless calm. He watched the chalk, which had drifted over her and hung above her face in a small white cloud, glittering, turning. He watched the city far below them, still as a photograph — thirty-one thousand people caught in the middle of a single night while he sat and watched a woman sleep for hours.</p>\n<p>He thought about a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles. About a girl of seventeen lying on the floor of a gallery for months, letting the worst night in the world happen through her. About that girl getting up, one cycle, and deciding to learn their names.</p>\n<p>He thought about Teo’s voice. <em>Do it because you want to. Or because she wants you to. Anything but that.</em></p>\n<p>He found — examining himself, the way he’d examined himself for fear at the window in the Collar, patting his pockets — he found something he hadn’t expected. He didn’t have a word for it at first. It was small. It was very quiet. It was sitting somewhere under his breastbone, where the sketchbook was, under the neck-ring of the suit, against his heart.</p>\n<p>It was that he wanted to see what happened next.</p>\n<p>Not what happened in the Crown. Not whether the lock would turn or the Clause would work or the plate meant what he was beginning, in the back of his mind, very quietly, to be afraid it meant. Not that. Something smaller. He wanted to see her wake up. He wanted to see what she’d say when she did. He wanted to see the chalk fall, when they left the Lee, and her blow it off her palm, and the look on her face when she read it. He wanted the next round. And the one after that.</p>\n<p>He sat very still with it, the way you’d sit very still with a bird that had landed on your hand.</p>\n<p>It had been a long time. He’d forgotten what it felt like. He’d forgotten that it was possible to want, carefully, just to go on.</p>\n<p>Below them — so slowly that he had to watch for a long time to be sure of it — at the very centre of the city, at the foot of Spindle One, the first shutter of the morning began to rise.</p>"}, {"file": "14-lamp-weather.md", "label": "Chapter 13", "title": "Lamp-Weather", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5002, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Remember that the Lesion is not a place. It is a condition of the place around it. Near it, even the weather is a kind of arithmetic.</p><span class=\"attr\">Iris Hollowell, <em>On the Sealing of Debts</em> (withdrawn)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">Above the best Lee on Spindle Four there was a place the riggers called the Breach.</p>\n<p>Ione showed it to him from below, the morning they left the cache — if it was morning; the city under them had only just begun, very slowly, to open its eyes. She pointed up through the narrowing lattice. Two hundred metres above them, the spindle’s structure changed. The struts that had braced it all the way up, the great black beams that made the Lees, simply stopped. For a stretch of perhaps sixty metres, one whole side of the spindle was open — the lattice there had fallen or been stripped in the Founders’ time, or had never been finished — and there was only a single spine of keelstone running up through the gap like the backbone of a fish, with the rungs of an old ladder bolted to it, and on either side of it, nothing. Violet air. The Lace.</p>\n<p>“No Lees in the Breach,” said Ione. “Nothing to hold against the Lamp. You go up it in one go. Fast. You don’t stop. There’s a hut at the top, in the last big Lee, under the gallery. You don’t stop till the hut.”</p>\n<p>“How long?”</p>\n<p>“Fast? A quarter of a shift. For me.” She looked at him. “For you, in that⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Longer.”</p>\n<p>“Longer.” She began to uncoil her rope. “And you don’t look at the Lace. It’s close up there. It’s very close. You look at the rungs.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He looked at the rungs.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. He looked at them as they came down past his visor one after the other, black iron, pitted, bolted to the black keelstone spine, and he looked at his gauntlets closing on them, and he looked at the rope going up from his harness to Ione, climbing above him with the coil on her shoulder, fast and neat and steady. He didn’t look out. He didn’t look down. He could feel the open air on both sides of him the way you can feel someone standing behind you in a dark room. He could feel the weight — three times what he’d weighed on the Tier Nine arterial, three and a half, the exo screaming in every joint, the keel’s note straining high in his spine.</p>\n<p>And he could feel the Lace.</p>\n<p>Not see it. He didn’t look. But he could feel it, off to the left, where the spindle was open: a kind of pressure on the side of his face. A prickle. The sense of something enormous and delicate hanging very near.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione above him, and he heard the soft puff of her blowing it, and a pause. “Crooked. Left. That’s fine. Keep coming.”</p>\n<p>He kept coming.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione, a while later. A pause. Longer. “<em>Very</em> crooked. Keep coming. Faster.”</p>\n<p>He went faster. His arms were two things he had borrowed from someone who hadn’t wanted them back. The rungs came down past his face.</p>\n<p>“Chalk,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>Silence.</p>\n<p>He looked up. He couldn’t help it. She was twenty metres above him, braced on the spine with her boots on a rung and her free hand held out flat in front of her, palm up. The puff of chalk had come off it. It hadn’t fallen. It hadn’t drifted sideways. It was going <em>up</em>. A little white cloud, rising slowly off her hand, straight up, toward the Lamp.</p>\n<p><em>When chalk falls up, pray.</em></p>\n<p>“Cas,” said Ione, very calmly. “Hold on.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It hit them like a wave hits a pier.</p>\n<p>First the weight went. All of it, all at once — the crushing three-and-a-half that had been on him for a thousand metres simply <em>vanished</em>, and he was hanging on the rungs weighing nothing, and his stomach came up into his throat and his body, braced against a weight that wasn’t there, threw itself away from the ladder. The exo jerked. His gauntlets clamped. He hung there, floating, attached to the spine by his fists.</p>\n<p>Then it came back. From the side.</p>\n<p>Down swung. Not gently, the way it had in the Works. It <em>whipped</em>. One moment there was no down, and the next down was off to his right, out through the open side of the spindle, into the violet air, and it was not three and a half, it was more — five, six, he didn’t know — and he was hanging off the side of the spine by his hands like a flag in a gale, his legs streaming out sideways over nothing, the whole weight of him and the suit trying to tear his arms out of their sockets and fling him out into the Lace.</p>\n<p>He heard the keel scream.</p>\n<p>And above him, he heard the rope go.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t loud. A sort of flat crack, like a whip, and a hiss. He looked up. Ione’s rope, where it ran from her harness down to his, had been lying across the edge of the keelstone spine — the raw broken edge where the lattice had fallen away. Keelstone didn’t break. But when it did, the edges were like glass. The rope had sawn itself across the edge in the swing, and parted, and the end of it came whipping down past his visor.</p>\n<p>And Ione came off the spine.</p>\n<p>She’d been braced with her boots on a rung and her free hand out for the chalk. When down swung, she’d grabbed. Her splinted hand had closed on the rung. And he watched it not hold.</p>\n<p>She came off. She went out, sideways, into the air on the open side of the spindle — the weight flinging her, the way it was trying to fling him — and she didn’t fall, she <em>flew</em>, out and down and past him, a small dark shape tumbling, her arms out, the white streak in her hair, her mouth open, and she was two metres from him and three and she was going out into the violet air toward the Lace⁠—</p>\n<p>He let go with one hand.</p>\n<p>He never remembered deciding. His right hand came off the rung and went out and the suit’s gauntlet closed on the loop of rope that still hung from her harness, a metre of frayed end, and the grip-assist clamped on it so hard that he felt the fibres crush. The weight of her came onto his arm like a steel bar. His left hand, alone on the rung, screamed. The exo screamed.</p>\n<p>He pulled.</p>\n<p>The keel’s bubble was a metre. That was all. A metre of stirred-flat spacetime round his body, where down was one direction instead of every direction at once. He hauled on the rope with an arm that had nothing left in it and dragged her in through the air toward him, and she came, and she hit him — hit the suit, chest to chest, her face against his visor, her arms coming round him — and was inside it.</p>\n<p>And the weather stopped.</p>\n<p>Not outside. Outside it went on — he could see it, over her shoulder: the violet air, the Lace, the open side of the spindle all tilting and swinging, the chalk-cloud Ione had blown whirling past in a spiral. But inside the bubble, inside the metre, down was simply down. The keel’s note was a scream in his spine but it held. It averaged it all out. Down was one way, and it was heavy, very heavy, but it was <em>one way</em>, and they were both in it, pressed together against the spine of Spindle Four, his left hand locked on a rung and his right arm round her and her arms round him, holding on.</p>\n<p>“<em>Don’t let go</em>,” she said against his visor. Her voice came through the helmet thin and close. “Don’t let go, don’t let go⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got you.”</p>\n<p>“⁠—<em>don’t let go</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got you, Ione. I’ve got you.”</p>\n<p>It went on for a long time. He never knew how long. It came in gusts. Down would swing round the outside of the bubble like a wind round a house, and he’d feel the keel strain to hold it, and the metre around them would flex and warp — the rungs in front of his face stretching, bending, as though seen through water — and hold, and settle. The weight surged and dropped. Once it dropped to nothing and he felt her start to float away from him, just slightly, and pulled her back hard, and she made a sound, and held on harder. Once it surged so hard that the spine of keelstone they were clinging to <em>groaned</em>, a vast deep sound through the metal, and a rung below them sheared off its bolts and went spinning out into the air and was gone.</p>\n<p>Her face was against his visor. He could see her eyes. They were very close and very wide, and for the first time since the top of the well there was nothing patient in them at all. They were frightened. They were a frightened seventeen-year-old’s eyes, in a thirty-year-old’s face, looking at him from three centimetres away through a sheet of glass.</p>\n<p>He held her. He didn’t have anything else to do.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>When it passed, it passed all at once. Down came back to down — straight down, the old crushing three-and-a-half, the Lamp pressing on them from above. The violet air outside stopped swinging. The chalk was gone.</p>\n<p>They hung there on the spine, the two of them, for a while longer. Neither of them moved.</p>\n<p>Then Ione said, into his visor, very quietly: “The hut’s forty metres up.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>“I can’t let go of you. If I let go of you I’m out of the bubble and I’ll weigh more than twice what I weigh at home and I’ve only got one hand.”</p>\n<p>“Then don’t let go of me.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll have to climb with me on you.”</p>\n<p>“All right.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him through the glass for a moment longer. Then she shifted — got her legs round his waist, her good arm round his neck-ring, her splinted hand fisted in the webbing of his harness — and put her cheek against the side of his helmet, and held on.</p>\n<p>He climbed.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know how. He never knew, afterwards, how. Forty metres. A hundred and thirty-odd rungs, with the whole weight of both of them — less in the bubble; not less enough — on arms that had nothing in them, the exo whining in a high thin continuous note like a dentist’s drill, the keel shrieking in his spine. He climbed with her pressed against him, her breath hot on the side of his helmet, and he counted rungs because it was the only thing he knew how to do, and somewhere past a hundred he stopped being able to count, and just climbed.</p>\n<p>At the top of the Breach the lattice came back. Great black struts closing round them. Chalk falling crooked. A Lee — a deep one; the weight lifted off them like a coat taken off their shoulders — and in it, wedged into the angle of three massive beams, a hut.</p>\n<p>He got them onto its platform. He didn’t remember that either. He remembered lying on his back on old planks inside a low black room with Ione still wrapped round him, both of them shaking, and the keel’s note slowly coming down from its scream, and her face in the crook of his neck-ring, and her voice, muffled:</p>\n<p>“You let go of the rung.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“With one hand. In lamp-weather. In the Breach.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>A long pause.</p>\n<p>“Nobody’s ever done anything that stupid for me,” said Ione Tally. And then, after another pause, very quietly, into the neck-ring of his suit, she began to cry.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hut was keelstone. Riggers had built it — generations ago, in the Founders’ time or just after, when the riggers were still going up the spindles to tend the old lamp-lines — out of off-cut slabs of keelstone laid against the three great struts, so that the walls pulled with the struts and the Lee inside was deep and quiet and almost flat. There was a door, of iron plate. There was a window, a slit, looking out through the lattice at the Lace. There was a shelf with things on it that crumbled when you touched them. There was a water-catcher outside the door, and water in it.</p>\n<p>And it was calm. Down was down, and not very much of it — about what it had been in the city, Ione said. After the Breach it felt like floating.</p>\n<p>He took the suit off.</p>\n<p>He’d been in it since the foot of the spindle, days ago — four sleeps; five — and when it opened down the back and he stepped out of it in the grey liner he nearly fell, again, the way he had at every big Lee. His legs had forgotten how to carry him. Ione caught him. They sat down together on the planks, hard, and stayed there, backs against the black wall, not talking.</p>\n<p>The suit stood in the corner of the hut, the keel humming. It had dropped again. He could hear it. The storm had cost it.</p>\n<p>After a while he got up and went to it, and looked at the display on the inside of the helmet. The clocks said LOCAL 03:14 and OUTSIDE 11:62. There was no such time as 11:62. As he watched, OUTSIDE said 04:0? and then a string of symbols and then nothing. The radio icon was grey. The little green light on the recorder, in its chest compartment, was still lit.</p>\n<p>“It’s going,” said Ione, behind him.</p>\n<p>“The electronics. Yes. Ben said they would.”</p>\n<p>“Will it still⁠—” She stopped.</p>\n<p>“The keel’s mechanical. The exo’s got a manual fallback. The air’s mostly mechanical.” He put the helmet down. “It’ll still get us through the Lace.”</p>\n<p><em>Us</em>, he thought. He didn’t say anything else.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She washed his face. He didn’t ask her to. She got water from the catcher outside in a tin that didn’t crumble and a strip of cloth from her pack and knelt in front of him where he sat against the wall and washed his face, carefully, the way you’d clean a wound, and then his neck, where the neck-ring of the suit had rubbed it raw. Then she re-bound her own wrist, one-handed, with her teeth, and wouldn’t let him help.</p>\n<p>“Is it broken?”</p>\n<p>“It was broken before. It’s more broken now.” She tied it off. “It’ll do.”</p>\n<p>He watched her. The stitches over her eye. The white streak. The rope-scarred forearms. The splint.</p>\n<p>“Can I tell you something?” he said.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<p>“Nobody knows it,” he said. “Except a man in a cardigan who works for the secret police. And Teo, a bit. And the man in the cardigan only knows what I did, not⁠—” He stopped. “Not the rest.”</p>\n<p>She sat back on her heels. She didn’t speak. She didn’t nod. She just waited, the way she’d waited at the top of the well, the way she’d waited through a hundred and seventy-five thousand cycles, as if there were nothing he could say that she hadn’t already seen, and she’d be interested to see it again.</p>\n<p>So he told her.</p>\n<p>He told her about the Margin Review. Year Seventy-Six. About Dr Adeyemi, sixty-eight, who walked everywhere very fast with his hands in his pockets, who used to put his palm flat on the walls of the Works and listen. About the nine months they’d spent on it, the two of them and a handful of analysts, going through seventy-five years of Rollover figures. About the night he’d first run the projections himself, alone, in the Review’s borrowed office on Tier Two, and watched the Margin line go down past one and keep going, and sat there until dawn. About the report. <em>A structure of obligation that cannot, even in principle, be discharged.</em> Adeyemi had written that. Cas had read it over his shoulder and thought it was the truest sentence he had ever seen.</p>\n<p>He told her about the meeting. The Ministry. A long table in a room with gold carpet. Very courteous people. Nobody raised their voice. They explained that the projections were, of course, only projections. That the Review had not taken into account the planned improvements to the Lattice. That publishing a figure like that — a figure like <em>negative margin</em> — in the current climate, with the export treaties being negotiated, would be irresponsible. Would cause panic. Would hurt people. Real people, now, today. That the future would find solutions; the future always had. That all that was needed was a revised conclusion. A small one. A sentence. <em>The Margin remains stable within projected parameters.</em></p>\n<p>“Adeyemi said no,” said Cas. “He stood up and said no, and walked out. They put him on leave. He died two years later. Heart, they said. He was seventy.” He looked at his hands. “They asked me.”</p>\n<p>“And you said yes.”</p>\n<p>“They didn’t make me. That’s the thing. That’s what nobody — they didn’t threaten me. They didn’t mention my family. They just asked. They put the revised report in front of me and a pen next to it and they asked, very politely, whether I’d be willing to sign. And I thought about it for — I don’t know. Ten seconds. And I thought: <em>it’ll probably be fine</em>. And I thought: <em>somebody else will fix it</em>. And I thought: <em>who am I to say</em>.” He was quiet. “And I noticed that it was a nice pen. That’s the thing I remember. I remember thinking what a nice pen it was. It wrote very smoothly.”</p>\n<p>He heard the sentence as he said it. <em>It’ll probably be fine.</em> He’d thought it again, not so long ago — at the top of a gallery stair in the Works, looking at a thread in a wall — and a boy had gone down the stair two at a time, grinning. He hadn’t noticed, then, that it was the same sentence. He noticed now.</p>\n<p>Ione said nothing.</p>\n<p>“They gave me the Standby. A flat by the Pit and a salary and a phone that never rang. And I sat in it for fifteen years.” He found that his voice had gone strange. “I didn’t do anything. I didn’t — I lost Sabine, because I couldn’t talk to her, because the only thing I could have talked about was the thing I couldn’t say. I saw Nell every other weekend and took her to the museum and didn’t say anything to her either. I got up every morning and did nothing and went to bed. The same day. Every day. Fifteen years. You said — you said about the Carousel. That it was only horrible for whoever was watching. I’ve been watching one day for fifteen years. The day with the pen in it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“When the Draw broke,” he said, “I sat on my bed and listened to the hammer, and I didn’t feel frightened. I felt⁠—” He made himself say it. “I felt relieved. I thought: <em>at last</em>. I thought: <em>now somebody will have to send me in</em>. And I thought I’d go in and it would — settle it. Somewhere in here. The Shear, or the Crown, or wherever. I thought I’d die in here and it would be⁠—” He stopped. “It seemed tidy.”</p>\n<p>He’d never said it out loud before. He’d never said it to himself.</p>\n<p>“I came in here to die,” said Casimir Hale. “That’s why. Teo asked me, at dinner, the night before, and I didn’t answer her. That’s why.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Ione was quiet for a long time.</p>\n<p>Outside the slit of window, the Lace hung in the violet air, very close, its threads moving slowly like weed in a current. The keel hummed in the corner. Far below, somewhere, the city was still in its night.</p>\n<p>“You think it’s a debt,” said Ione at last.</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“What you did. With the pen.” She was sitting with her back against the wall opposite him, her knees drawn up, her splinted wrist resting on them. “You think it’s a debt, and you’ve been paying it. For fifteen years. Every day. And you came in here to pay the rest of it off. All at once. With yourself.” She tilted her head. “Is that it?”</p>\n<p>“Something like that.”</p>\n<p>“And has it got any smaller?”</p>\n<p>He opened his mouth. He shut it.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione. “It hasn’t. Has it. You’ve been paying it every day for fifteen years and it’s bigger than it was. Because that’s how your kind of debt works.” She said it without any cruelty at all, the way she’d explained the Lees. “You told us. In the Commons. You borrow, and you pay it back, and it’s always more. You pay the interest, and you pay the interest on the interest, and it grows, and it grows, and you can never, ever get to the bottom of it. That’s what you people <em>built</em>. You built a whole city on that. You built this whole place on it.” She moved her hand, taking in the hut, the spindle, the Lamp. “And then you went and built a man on it too.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“I watched a woman make the worst choice of her life,” said Ione, “a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. Ruth. At the board. <em>One-thirty-five.</em> She closes her eyes for half a second first. Every time. And every time I sat next to her and I thought, <em>don’t</em>. Every time. A hundred and seventy-five thousand times I thought <em>don’t</em>, and a hundred and seventy-five thousand times she did it anyway. Because it was already done. Because it was just light. Because she only ever got to do it once, and she’d done it, and there was nothing anyone could ever do about it, not her, not me, not anyone.” She looked at him steadily. “She never got to make another choice. Not in the gallery. The ghost never does. It just does the one thing, for ever.</p>\n<p>“But the real Ruth did. She went down the lift, and she made the next choice, and the one after. She got married. She had children. She wrote letters to a little girl for fifty years. She counted the Bells. She <em>built the Tollers</em>, Cas. My whole family. Everything I am. She made that out of the worst nine minutes of her life. She didn’t pay it off. You can’t pay it off. She just — went on. She made the next thing.”</p>\n<p>She leaned forward.</p>\n<p>“You’re not a ghost,” she said. “You’re not the light in the gallery. You signed it once. That’s all. Once. It’s true, and it’ll always be true, and there’s nothing you can do about it. But nobody makes you do it again every morning. That’s you. You do that. You’ve been standing in the gallery watching yourself sign it for fifteen years, thinking <em>don’t</em>. You can walk out. You can make the next thing.” She sat back. “That’s all. That’s all I know.”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t speak. He sat against the black keelstone wall with his hands open on his knees and looked at her and his eyes stung and blurred and he let them.</p>\n<p>After a while she got up and came across the hut and sat down beside him, against the wall, close, her shoulder against his shoulder. She didn’t say anything else. She just sat there.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Later — much later — she said, “Nobody’s touched me in eleven years.”</p>\n<p>She said it to the window, not to him. Her shoulder was still against his.</p>\n<p>“Since I came down. The Unwatched. You don’t get touched. People won’t hand you things. They put them down and step back, so you have to pick them up. Gran touched me. And Pim, he doesn’t care, he’s eleven, he climbs on everyone. That’s all. Eleven years.” She was quiet. “In the Carousel, I used to lie next to Ruth. At the board. With my head on her shoulder. And she’d go right through me.”</p>\n<p>He turned his head and looked at her.</p>\n<p>She turned hers. They looked at each other, close, in the violet light from the slit of window.</p>\n<p>“You held on,” she said. “In the Breach. You had me by the rope and you pulled me in and you held on. And I could feel you. Through the suit. I could feel your arms.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know,” she said. “I know. You’re from Outside and I’m from here and we’re going up there, the two of us, to something that probably kills us. I know all of it. I’ve thought all of it. I’ve thought it since the top of the well.” She put her hand, her good hand, flat on his chest, on the grey liner, over his heart. “I’ve had eleven years of thinking. I’m tired of thinking.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand over hers.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It was clumsy. That was what he remembered, afterwards, and was glad of: how clumsy it was.</p>\n<p>The liner was a one-piece with a seam down the back that had been designed to be opened by a machine, and neither of them could work out how to get it undone, and she laughed at him trying, and then he laughed, and then she was laughing so hard she had to put her forehead against his shoulder and stop. Her clothes were a rigger’s clothes, a tunic and a harness and a belt and trousers with keelstone-cloth knees, and every piece of them was knotted in some rigger’s way he’d never seen, and she wouldn’t show him, she made him work them out, one after another, and he did. Her wrist got in the way. His back, where the keel had sat for five sleeps, had a bruise across it the size of a dinner plate, and when she put her hand on it he hissed, and she took her hand away, and he put it back.</p>\n<p>The weight in the Lee was Holdfast weight. Heavy. Everything heavy — her body on his, his on hers, their hands, their breath — as if the world were pressing them together. She was strong. Much stronger than him, in that weight, and very sure, and then, suddenly, not sure at all, holding very still with her face against his neck and her breath going fast, and he held still too, and waited, and she said <em>it’s all right, it’s all right, I’m just⁠—</em> and didn’t finish, and didn’t need to.</p>\n<p>Afterwards they lay on the planks of the hut under his coat and hers, tangled, her head on his chest, and he could feel her heartbeat and his, in the heavy stillness, not quite together.</p>\n<p>“Your heart’s too fast,” she said drowsily.</p>\n<p>“It’s the weight.”</p>\n<p>“It’s not the weight.” She moved her head. “I’ve been listening to it since the top of the well. It’s always too fast. Outside hearts must all be too fast.”</p>\n<p>“Maybe.”</p>\n<p>“Mm.” She was quiet a while. Her fingers moved on his chest, very slightly, tracing something. He realised after a while that it was a knot. The riggers’ hitch. Over, under, round, through. “Cas.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“When it’s — whatever it is. Up there.” She stopped. “I don’t want to talk about it. I only want to say that I’m glad. That’s all. Whatever happens. I’m glad it was you that came up the well.”</p>\n<p>He put his hand on her hair. The white streak, under his fingers, was softer than the rest.</p>\n<p>“So am I,” he said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She slept. He didn’t, for a while.</p>\n<p>When he was sure she was asleep, he eased out from under her — very slowly, very carefully, putting his rolled-up coat under her head — and went across the hut to the suit in the corner, and opened the chest compartment, and took out the recorder. Its little green light was still on. He sat down on the planks with his back against the suit’s leg and held it.</p>\n<p>Then he pressed the button. The second channel.</p>\n<p>“Nell,” he said, very quietly, so as not to wake her.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what came next. He sat in the dim violet light and listened to Ione breathe and the keel hum, and let it come.</p>\n<p>“I’ve been thinking about what you said. About being scared. That it’s allowed, and you just have to do it anyway.” He paused. “I think I got something wrong. For a long time. I think I thought — I thought being brave meant not minding what happened to you. I thought if you didn’t mind, it couldn’t hurt. So I stopped minding. About anything. For years. I thought that was — I don’t know. Being grown up. Being sensible.” He stopped. “It isn’t. It’s just being scared all the time and calling it something else.”</p>\n<p>The keel hummed.</p>\n<p>“So I want to tell you something. And I want you to hear it from me, even if it’s a long time from now, even if it’s from a machine. Because I’ve never said it. Not to you, not to your mother, not to anyone. Not even to me.”</p>\n<p>He looked across the hut at Ione asleep under the coats, the white streak in her hair, her splinted hand curled under her chin.</p>\n<p>“I want to live, Nell,” said Casimir Hale. “I want to live. I don’t know if I’m going to. But I want to. I want to very much.”</p>\n<p>He held the button down a moment longer, letting it record the silence after, and the keel, and her breathing.</p>\n<p>Then he let go.</p>"}, {"file": "15-the-carousel.md", "label": "Chapter 14", "title": "The Carousel", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5965, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Crown to Lattice, stand by for Deepening protocol, test designation Continuance Ninety-One.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ruth Tally, 01:58, the night of the Kindling (heard 175,000 times)</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The spindles leaned together as they rose.</p>\n<p>He hadn’t seen it from the city — from the city they had looked like eight straight black pillars going up into the glare — but from the hut under the gallery he could see it plainly. They leaned in toward each other the way the poles of a tent lean in toward the ridge, so that at the Anvil they stood kilometres apart and up here, just below the Rim, they were close enough to be joined. And they were joined. Gantries ran between them: long, narrow, open walkways of steel lattice slung from spindle to spindle across the violet gulf, some of them intact, some of them sagging, some of them hanging in broken halves from one end like dead vines.</p>\n<p>The gantry from Four to One was intact. Ione had checked it on her scouting trip. Three hundred metres of steel walkway, a metre wide, with a handrail on each side, sagging slightly in the middle, running from a door in the lattice just above the hut straight across the gulf to Spindle One.</p>\n<p>And at its far end, wrapped round the outside of Spindle One like a ring on a finger, was the gallery.</p>\n<p>He could see it from the gantry’s start. A long, low, curving structure with a roof and windows, circling the spindle, its windows facing inward — toward the Lamp, toward the core. The windows were dark. And above it, not far above, the spindle ended in a shape he knew from the mural in the crisis room, from the old footage, from the textbooks: a lens of black keelstone perched on the tip of the pillar like a seed on a stalk. The Crown. And above the Crown, the Rim — the vast dark ring that ran round the Lamp, joining the tops of all eight spindles — and above the Rim, filling the whole sky, very close, so close he could see the finest of its threads moving, the Lace.</p>\n<p>He was in the suit again. The keel hummed. It had dropped again. He’d tried not to listen to how much.</p>\n<p>Ione stood beside him at the start of the gantry with her rope on her shoulder and her pack on her back, looking across at the gallery. She was very calm. She had been very calm all morning. She had eaten, and drunk, and re-bound her wrist, and checked his harness twice, and said almost nothing, and been very calm.</p>\n<p>Her hands were shaking.</p>\n<p>He saw it when she lifted one to point. A fine, fast tremor, like a leaf in a draught. She saw him see it, and looked at it herself, and put the hand down by her side, and closed it.</p>\n<p>“It’s the only way,” she said. “The Lace has closed the other gantries. The gallery’s the only way to the Rim, and the Rim’s the only way to the Crown.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I’m just telling you.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” he said. “Ione. I know.”</p>\n<p>She nodded. She didn’t look at him. She stepped out onto the gantry.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It swayed. Every step, it swayed — not much, a slow gentle swing under their feet like a rope bridge — and the handrails creaked, and the violet gulf opened beneath them on both sides, four kilometres of it, down to the Anvil and the tiny still city at its heart. He didn’t look down. He looked at Ione’s back. She walked quickly, lightly, one hand on the rail, not looking down either.</p>\n<p>Halfway across, she stopped.</p>\n<p>“This is where I found it,” she said.</p>\n<p>He looked down at the walkway. Steel lattice, a metre wide, pitted with rust. There was nothing there. It was just a place.</p>\n<p>“Right here.” She touched the key at her throat. “I’d walked across here four thousand times. I knew every bolt.” She pointed at one — a rusted bolt-head on the edge of the walkway, a little bigger than the others. “That one’s loose. It rattles if you step on the plate next to it.” She stepped on the plate next to it. It rattled. “Four thousand times. And one time I came across and there was a key lying here. Right here. Next to the loose bolt.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the place. It was just a place. A square of rusted steel lattice in the middle of a walkway in the middle of the air.</p>\n<p><em>Where it had never been, and then it was.</em></p>\n<p>“Come on,” said Ione. And went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The gallery’s door was open. It had always been open, she said. It had been open eleven years ago. It had been open for a hundred and sixty-eight years. It was a plain steel door in the gallery’s outer wall with a faded sign above it — CROWN SUPPORT RING — AIR PLANT — AUTHORISED ONLY — and beyond it, darkness.</p>\n<p>Ione stopped on the threshold.</p>\n<p>She stood there for a long time. He didn’t touch her. He stood behind her on the gantry and waited, and the gantry swayed, and the Lace hung overhead, and she stood in the doorway of the place where she had spent three years of her life, with her hands closed by her sides.</p>\n<p>Then she said, low, “It’ll come on when we’re in. Don’t be frightened of it. It can’t see you.”</p>\n<p>And went in.</p>\n<p>He followed.</p>\n<p>It was a corridor. Long and curving, curving away to the left and right, following the round of the spindle; he could see about thirty metres each way before it bent out of sight. The outer wall, behind him, was plain steel. The inner wall — the one facing the spindle, facing the core — was windows. A long unbroken band of thick glass panes, floor to ceiling, one after another round the whole curve. And along the foot of the windows ran a trough. A long steel tank, knee high, full of water, full of something green and thick and softly glowing. The air-garden. It smelled of the sea, of the algae halls in the Works, of greenbread. The water in it was still. The green in it was alive.</p>\n<p>The windows were dark. And then they weren’t.</p>\n<p>It came on the way a screen comes on. There was nothing, and then — all at once, all round the curve, in every pane — there was light. White-violet. Soft. Shifting. And in it, standing in the corridor, as solid as he was, as close as Ione⁠—</p>\n<p>People.</p>\n<p>A room. He was standing in a room. He could see the walls of the corridor through it, the steel, the trough, the windows — but over them, <em>in</em> them, lit from inside like figures in a stained-glass window, there was a room. A long lens-shaped room with black walls and a great curved window, and consoles, and chairs, and people at the consoles. A young man with a mug. A woman on a stool by a camera with a shaved head and a lanyard. A thin young man holding a tablet. A heavy man at a board with his sleeves rolled up. Through the great window of the room, hanging in the dark, a single point of white-violet light, hard and perfect as a nail.</p>\n<p>And in the middle of the room, combing his hair in the reflection of the window, a tall man with silver hair and a good dark jacket, with a key on a chain round his neck.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<p>The voice came from everywhere. From the windows, from the walls, from the air. It was thin and slightly hollow, like a voice in a recording, and perfectly clear.</p>\n<p>Cas stood in the corridor of the gallery with his hand on the wall and couldn’t move.</p>\n<p>“We’ll stop down,” said the woman with the camera. “You’ll look like a painting, sir.”</p>\n<p>And beside him, very softly, not looking at him, looking at the room — Ione said the next line. Her lips moved a half-second before the ghost’s did.</p>\n<p>“<em>Tally. How long until we’re ready?</em>”</p>\n<p>“Tally,” said Aldous Crane, without turning round. “How long until we’re ready?”</p>\n<p>And at the board — right there, three metres from Cas, standing with her hands on the edge of the console and her back to him — a woman in a white coat, thirty-eight years old, with dark hair pinned up and a mole on her jaw below her left ear, said:</p>\n<p>“We’re not.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He watched it all.</p>\n<p>He hadn’t meant to. He’d meant to keep moving — to follow Ione, who’d begun, slowly, to walk along the curve of the corridor, through the ghost-room, her hand trailing on the steel of the outer wall. But he couldn’t. He stood in the corridor with the light of the Crown moving over his visor and he watched it happen.</p>\n<p>Crane, with his good face, being patient. Ruth, at the board, being tired. <em>The protocol requires the interlocks off.</em> The aide with his tablet. <em>Director Obi has deferred to the Ministry.</em> He watched Crane say Maddy’s name. He watched Ruth’s face when he said it. He watched her put her thumb to her mouth and bite the side of it, without knowing she was doing it, while Crane talked about the Aurelian Compact and forty million homes.</p>\n<p>He watched her say <em>eleven minutes</em>.</p>\n<p>He watched them run the test. The call and response. <em>Rollover to manual. Interlocks off.</em> The green lights going amber on the board — he could read the board, he could read every figure on it, clear as print, lit up in ghost-light in the corridor wall. <em>One-oh-five. One-ten.</em> The coffee in Dieter’s mug shivering into rings. <em>Six is at plus nineteen.</em> The Lesion in the window, sharpening. Crane saying <em>five more</em> to the camera.</p>\n<p>Beside him, Ione said, very softly, “Now.”</p>\n<p>And Ruth closed her eyes. For about half a second.</p>\n<p>“One-thirty-five,” she said.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall began to run fast.</p>\n<p>He’d read it in pencil in a Log in the Tollers’ tower. He’d read it in the telemetry on a screen in a room with gold carpet. It wasn’t like reading it. The floor of the ghost-room dropped and everyone in it staggered, and the coffee mug left the desk and hung, and fell, slowly, slowly, its brown tongue of coffee curling out; and Fen at the tensioner board said <em>Six has unseated</em> a hair before his mouth moved; and in the great window of the ghost-room the Lesion <em>broke</em>.</p>\n<p>He watched it happen. The frost. The lace. The single hard point of light coming apart into edges on edges on edges, spreading outward, silently, in every direction. He’d seen the Lace for weeks now, from the city, from the spindle, filling the sky. He’d never seen it be born. It was the most terrible thing he had ever seen, and it was beautiful, and it made no sound.</p>\n<p>“Keystone,” said Ruth Tally, and ran.</p>\n<p>She ran right past him. Through him — he felt nothing; the light went over his visor and she was on the other side of him — and down the length of the ghost-room to its far end, where the corridor curved, and dropped to her knees and wrenched up a plate in the floor. He saw the plate. He saw the words on it, lit in ghost-light on the steel floor of the gallery, clear as a sign.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>He saw the keyhole under it: a narrow cross.</p>\n<p>And he saw — with a lurch that went through his whole body, that made him put out his hand to the wall — the mechanism under the plate. The housing. The black keelstone, the recessed ring round the keyhole. The fittings. He’d seen them before. He’d seen them in a schematic, in the back of a withdrawn paper, in a library at night twenty years ago. He knew what they were for. He knew what they would do, when the lock turned. He understood — standing in the gallery with the ghost-light running over him — exactly how the Clause had been built, and why, and what it would take to run it.</p>\n<p>Almost exactly.</p>\n<p><em>The sealing requires a witness.</em> He still didn’t know what that meant. But he knew, now, where the witness would have to stand.</p>\n<p>“Give me the key,” said Ruth.</p>\n<p>He watched Crane not give it to her. He watched Crane’s face do the thing it did — he’d read about it in her Log, <em>the moment he understood</em>, and now he saw it, the fear coming into that good face, the young frightened man coming out from behind the Deputy Minister. He watched Crane’s fingers close on the chain. Watched him begin, slowly, to lift it.</p>\n<p>He watched the gantry go.</p>\n<p>A single thread of the Lace — a hair of light — reached the frame of the ghost-Crown’s great window, and where it touched, the black keelstone went grey, and white, and sagged like wax. The walkway tore. Crane fell against the rail. The chain snapped.</p>\n<p>And the key fell.</p>\n<p>Not down. He’d wondered about that since the Log. He’d wondered about it since the mural. It left Crane’s chain and turned over once in the white-violet light and slid between the bars of the grating and fell <em>toward the window</em>. Toward the Lamp. Toward the light. Not down, the way things fell. Not up, the way the Lamp pushed. It fell <em>along</em> something — along a slope that wasn’t in space at all. And he understood, watching it, the way he’d understood the mechanism: near the Tear, the gradient of time was so steep that it was steeper than the gradient of anything else. Things near it didn’t fall down, or up. They fell <em>forward</em>. Into the future. Along the only slope that mattered. The key fell forward out of the Crown, through the glass as if the glass were a moment that had already passed, and into the Lace, and was gone.</p>\n<p>He knew where it had gone. He knew where it had come out: on a gantry, eleven years ago, at the feet of a girl of seventeen. He looked at the key round Ione’s neck. He didn’t know yet how it had got from one to the other.</p>\n<p>And at the far end of the ghost-room, on her knees by the Keystone, Ruth Tally looked up — at nothing. At an empty space in front of her, beside the open housing. She went completely still. Her face changed. Something in it opened. She looked at the empty space for about a second, as though someone were standing there, as though someone were looking back at her.</p>\n<p>“She always does that,” said Ione quietly, beside him. “Right there. At the Keystone, after the key goes. She looks at nothing like someone’s there.” She paused. “There’s never anyone there. I used to stand in the place she’s looking at. Hundreds of times. To see if it was me she saw.” She shook her head slightly. “It wasn’t.”</p>\n<p>The ghost-Ruth came back to herself. <em>Tally! Tally, the lift!</em> The Crown crew running. Ruth at the gate of the lift. The glass panel. The red lever.</p>\n<p>She pulled it.</p>\n<p>And the gallery windows went dark.</p>\n<p>For about three seconds, there was nothing. The corridor. The steel. The trough of green. The dark windows. Ione beside him, breathing, and the keel’s hum in his spine.</p>\n<p>Then, all round the curve, the light came on again.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They walked.</p>\n<p>The corridor curved round the spindle. The ghost-Crown went with them. It didn’t matter where you stood — the room was always there, in the windows, in the walls, laid over the gallery like a picture on glass. Nine minutes. <em>We’re not.</em> <em>Eleven minutes.</em> <em>One-thirty-five.</em> The key falling. Ruth looking at nothing. The lever. Dark. <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>Ione walked a little ahead of him, one hand trailing on the outer wall, and he saw that she was not looking at the ghosts. She was looking at the wall.</p>\n<p>The wall was covered in marks.</p>\n<p>He saw them after the second cycle: scratched into the steel, all along the outer wall of the corridor, at about the height of a woman’s shoulder. Fives. Strokes in fives, the fifth across the four, in rows. Row after row. They went along the wall as far as he could see in both directions, and when he looked up, they went up — above head height, up to the ceiling, where someone must have stood on the edge of the algae trough to reach. Thousands of them. Tens of thousands. Scratched with a nail, or a knife, or a rope-hook, into the steel. And every so often, among them, other marks. A word. A drawing. A face — a woman’s face, with her hair pinned up and a mole below her ear, drawn very carefully in scratched lines, over and over, twenty times, fifty.</p>\n<p>He stopped in front of one. It was very good. It had been done slowly, by someone with all the time in the world.</p>\n<p>“You counted,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I’m a Toller.” Ione didn’t stop walking. “I told you.” A pause. “There’s a place further round where I stopped. For the months on the floor. There’s a gap. You’ll see it.”</p>\n<p>He saw it. A stretch of wall about two metres long, where the fives simply stopped — and then, beyond it, started again, in a different hand. Stronger. Neater. As if someone had got up off the floor one day and decided to learn everybody’s name.</p>\n<p>There was a pallet of old rags on the floor under the window there, flattened, dusty. There was a tin cup by the algae trough. There was a length of rope, knotted into something complicated — a net, a sling — hanging from a pipe. There was a heap of little stones, polished smooth, that must have come up in someone’s pocket from the Anvil a long time ago, and been turned over and over in someone’s fingers until they shone.</p>\n<p>Ione walked past all of it without looking.</p>\n<p>The corridor curved. Ahead of them, round the bend, he could see a door — the gallery’s inner door, Ione had said, the one that led out onto the Rim. It was standing open. Beyond it, violet air, and the great dark curve of the Rim.</p>\n<p>They walked toward it. It came closer. They reached it. Ione stopped, just short of the threshold, and he stopped beside her.</p>\n<p>“Watch,” she said.</p>\n<p>She stepped through.</p>\n<p>And the door they had come in by — the outer door, the plain steel door with CROWN SUPPORT RING above it — was in front of them. They were standing just inside it, looking out at the gantry to Spindle Four. The gallery curved away on either side.</p>\n<p>“Is it going to be too bright?” said Aldous Crane. “I don’t want to squint.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took him eleven cycles to believe it.</p>\n<p>He tried everything she’d tried. He knew she’d tried it all; he tried it anyway, because he had to, because he was a man who had to see a thing for himself before he could know it. He went through the inner door, and came in through the outer. He went through the outer door, onto the gantry, and walked twenty metres along it toward Spindle Four, and turned round, and came back — and the door he came back through was the inner one, the Rim door, and he was walking into the gallery from the other side. He went through a window. The panes were thick but one of them, down near the trough, was cracked, and he put his gauntlet through the crack and pulled out a piece and climbed through — and fell forward onto the floor of the gallery corridor, facing back the way he’d come, with the ghost-Crown lighting up around him. He tried the roof. There was a hatch. It opened onto the roof of the gallery, a curving strip of steel roof round the spindle’s side, and he climbed out onto it and walked along it and came down through the hatch he’d gone up.</p>\n<p>Every way out was a way in.</p>\n<p>“It’s a ring,” he said. He was standing in the middle of the corridor. His voice sounded strange. “It’s — the whole gallery’s been wrapped. Topologically. Every boundary’s been glued to another boundary. Space goes out one side and comes in the other.” He looked at the light in the windows. “There’s a filament. Through the gallery. Somewhere. The Tear’s run a thread through here and the space round it’s been — folded round it. Like a sleeve round an arm.”</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Ione. She was sitting on the edge of the algae trough, watching him. “I know.”</p>\n<p>“There’ll be a seam. There has to be. Where the edges meet. Where it’s been joined.”</p>\n<p>“Yes. There was one. There was one where the key came from. Where my — where the woman was standing.” She looked down at her hands. “It’s not there now. I’ve been looking for it for eleven cycles. I’ve been looking for anything. Anything different.” She was quiet. “There isn’t anything different. There’s never anything different. That’s the thing about it. That’s the whole thing.”</p>\n<p>The ghost-Crown moved round them. <em>Five more, Ruth.</em></p>\n<p>“The seam was only there,” said Ione, “because something came through it. The key came through it. The woman came through it. Something from outside came in, and where it came in, you could see the join. Like a thread pulled through cloth.” Her voice was very even. “Nothing’s come in. So there’s no join to see. There’s just the ring. And the light. And the nine minutes.”</p>\n<p>She put her hands on the edge of the trough, on either side of her, and held on to it.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to sit down now,” she said. “For a little while. I’m sorry.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He’d been afraid of it since the gantry. Since the doorway. Since he’d seen her hands shake.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the floor. Right there, by the trough, with her back against the green-lit steel and her knees up, in the place where the scratched fives stopped. She put her arms round her knees. She put her forehead down on her arms. And the ghost-Crown moved over her — Ruth running for the Keystone right through the place where she sat, the key falling through her, the light going over her like water over a stone — and she didn’t move.</p>\n<p>He knelt down in front of her. The suit made it hard. He did it anyway.</p>\n<p>“Ione.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“Ione. Look at me.”</p>\n<p>“I can hear you,” she said into her arms. Her voice was quite calm. “I’m not gone. I’m just — sitting. I’m going to sit for a bit. You can look for the seam. I’ll be here. I’ll just be here.”</p>\n<p><em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>He sat down beside her. He got the suit’s back against the trough and his shoulder against her shoulder, the way she’d sat beside him in the hut, and he sat there.</p>\n<p>The nine minutes went round. <em>We’re not.</em> He counted them. He couldn’t help it. Twelve. Fifteen. Twenty. He sat beside her and counted the cycles, and the light went over them, and she didn’t move except to breathe. Once, round about the twenty-fifth, he felt her shoulders start to shake, very slightly, against his arm, and he put his arm round her, the suit’s heavy arm, and she leaned into it and went still again.</p>\n<p>He talked to her.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know what else to do. He talked about anything. He told her about Nell’s ceiling, the glow-in-the-dark stars, the constellations put up with a chart and a ruler. He told her their names. Betelgeuse, which was red. Rigel, which was blue. Sirius, the brightest, which the old sailors called the Dog Star. Deneb. Vega. Fomalhaut, which was lonely, which sat by itself in a part of the sky with nothing else bright near it. He told her about rain — what it was like, standing in it, on the Tier Nine arterial, the summer the Canopy failed over the northern tiers for a week, when he was a boy; how it came down so hard it bounced; how warm it was. He told her about the sea. About Teo in the Tessarine underpass with twenty-three people on a rope. About Ben, and MINISTER →, and I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS. About Adeyemi putting his palm flat on the walls of the Works and listening.</p>\n<p><em>One-thirty-five.</em></p>\n<p>“Keep talking,” she said, once, into her arms. That was all. So he kept talking.</p>\n<p>And while he talked, he thought.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t help that either. He thought about seams. About a thread of the Tear running through this gallery, and the space folded round it, and the place where the fold must be glued. He thought about how you’d find a seam in a thing you couldn’t see, if nothing came through it. He thought about frost on a window. About a hair of light crossing a storeroom and branching into itself, the same pattern at every scale. About a man in a hall of algae with a tape measure and eleven years, measuring a filament’s branches, month after month, finding the rule.</p>\n<p>He stopped talking.</p>\n<p>“Ione,” he said.</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer.</p>\n<p>He took the sketchbook out of the suit. He had to open the neck-ring to get at it, and he did, and the warm algae-smelling air came in, and he pulled out the black cloth-bound book and opened it on his knee, in the ghost-light, and turned the pages. Thirty pages of Ben. Grease-pencil diagrams copied in his own pencil and Teo’s. The branching. The throats. <em>D = 2.73.</em> <em>IF THERE’S A SHEAR IT’LL HAVE SEAMS. GO THROUGH THE THROATS.</em></p>\n<p>“The fold follows the thread,” he said. He wasn’t sure if he was talking to her or to himself. “The space is folded round the filament. So the seam’s where the filament forks. It has to be. Ben showed it. Where it branches — the throat between the branches — that’s where the geometry’s weakest. That’s where it had to be glued.” He turned a page. “And the branching’s self-similar. Same ratio at every scale. So if I know where one fork is, I know where the next one has to be⁠—”</p>\n<p>He looked up. Round the gallery. At the ghost-light. At the key, falling through it, toward the window, toward the Lamp. At the thread of the Lace in the ghost-window, the first one, the one that touched the Crown’s frame.</p>\n<p>“It’s in the window,” he said. “The filament. The one that made this. It’s the first thread. The one that touched the Crown. It’s still here. It’s <em>in the light</em>.” He was on his knees now, the sketchbook in one hand. “Ione. Ione, you’ve watched that thread a hundred and seventy-five thousand times. You know it better than anyone alive. Where does it fork? The first fork. After it touches the frame. Where does it <em>branch</em>?”</p>\n<p>She lifted her head.</p>\n<p>Her face was wet. Her eyes were red. She looked at him for a moment as if she didn’t know who he was. Then she looked past him, at the ghost-window, where the Crown was just beginning its fourth minute. <em>Rollover to manual.</em></p>\n<p>“Wait,” she said. “It’s not — it doesn’t come for five minutes.”</p>\n<p>They waited. He knelt in front of her with the sketchbook open. She sat with her back against the trough, watching the window.</p>\n<p><em>One-thirty-five.</em></p>\n<p>The Lesion broke. The Lace was born. And a single thread of it — fine as a hair, bright as a bulb — came out of the frost and across the ghost-window and touched the frame.</p>\n<p>“There,” said Ione. She was on her knees too now. She was pointing. “<em>There.</em> It touches the frame there. And it goes along it, that way, for — about an arm. And then it splits. Two. There. And the left one goes up and the right one goes⁠—” she stopped “⁠—the right one goes toward the Keystone. Toward where Ruth is. It branches again just before it gets to her. I’ve watched it. I’ve watched it a thousand times, I used to lie on the floor and watch it — it branches just before it gets to her and one branch goes up and one goes⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Where does the one that goes up <em>go</em>?”</p>\n<p>She turned her head, following it. Up. Out of the ghost-window, across the ghost-ceiling, over their heads. Her eyes moved along something he couldn’t see.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said. She was looking at a place on the outer wall of the gallery, above the scratched fives, near the ceiling, on the curve of the corridor six metres away. “It goes there. It goes into the wall there. Every time.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the sketchbook. At the branching ratio. At the throats. Ben’s writing, in his own pencil. <em>THE SEAM IS ALWAYS BETWEEN TWO BRANCHES. NEVER ON ONE.</em></p>\n<p>“Then the seam’s between them,” he said. “Between the branch that goes to Ruth and the one that goes into the wall. In the throat.” He did it on the page, fast, the ratio, the angles. “It’ll be — there.” He pointed. A place in the middle of the corridor, a little way round the curve, at about head height, in empty air. “Somewhere there. Ione. You know what a seam looks like. You’ve seen one. Look.”</p>\n<p>She got up. She walked toward the place he’d pointed at. She walked slowly, the way she’d walked on the gantry, one hand out. The ghost-Crown moved round her. Ruth ran past her for the Keystone.</p>\n<p>She stopped. Her head tilted.</p>\n<p>“Oh,” she said.</p>\n<p>He came up beside her. He looked.</p>\n<p>It was almost nothing. A shimmer. The faintest ripple in the air, like the edge of heat over a road, like the Shear’s trembling curtain in the conduit drawn out thin as a blade. A vertical line of wrongness hanging in the middle of the corridor, a hand’s width wide, the height of a person, that you would never have seen if you hadn’t known exactly where to look. Through it, the corridor on the far side jumped very slightly, as if seen through a flaw in glass.</p>\n<p>“That’s it,” said Ione. Her voice was very small. “That’s what it looked like. When the woman was standing there. That’s exactly what it looked like.”</p>\n<p>“Ben,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“Ben found it.” He heard his own voice shaking. “Eleven years in a room with a tape measure. He found it.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked at him. Then at the shimmer. Then she held out her good hand, without looking, and he took it in his gauntlet, and they stepped through together.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They were on the Rim.</p>\n<p>It was a walkway. A vast one — ten metres wide, steel plate on keelstone, with a rail on the inner side — running round the top of the Vault in a great dark circle, joining the tops of all eight spindles, curving away on both sides until it met itself far across the sky. Behind them, the gallery; its inner door stood open, and through it he could see the corridor, and the windows, and the ghost-light moving in them. <em>We’re not.</em> Ahead of them, over the rail, the drop into the Vault — four kilometres of violet air — and across it, the Lamp.</p>\n<p>It was very close. He’d never been this close. It hung out there in the middle of the air, the single hard point, too bright to look at, and from it, in every direction, the Lace came out — the endless branching filigree, filling the whole sky above them — and its lowest threads hung down, just beyond the rail, fine and bright and moving slowly, close enough that he could see them branching into each other at scales so small his eyes couldn’t follow.</p>\n<p>Above and to the left, a little way along the Rim, Spindle One rose up past the rail and ended in the Crown.</p>\n<p>Ione let go of his hand.</p>\n<p>She walked to the rail. She put both her hands on it, the good one and the splinted one. She stood there looking out at the Lamp. And then her knees went, and she sat down on the steel plate of the Rim with her back against the rail and her hands over her face, and she cried.</p>\n<p>Not the way she’d cried in the hut. Not quiet. She cried the way a child cries — great gasping, heaving, ugly sobs that shook her whole body, that went on and on, that bent her over her knees. He’d never heard a sound like it. He sat down beside her on the steel plate and didn’t touch her, because he didn’t know if she’d want him to, and then touched her anyway, his gauntlet on her back, and she didn’t push it away.</p>\n<p>“I thought⁠—” she said. She couldn’t get it out. “I thought I’d never — I thought I’d never have to — see her again⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Every time. Every <em>time</em>. She closes her eyes. And I sit next to her. And I think <em>don’t</em>. And she⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“She was my <em>friend</em>,” said Ione Tally, into her hands. “She was my best friend and I had to watch her do it a hundred and seventy-five thousand times and she never once heard me say don’t.”</p>\n<p>He put his arms round her. She let him. She turned into the suit and put her face against the chestplate and held on to the harness and cried, and he held her, on the Rim, at the top of the world, with the Lamp hanging in the air in front of them and the Lace moving slowly overhead and the ghost-light still going round and round in the gallery behind them. <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em></p>\n<p>It went on for a long time.</p>\n<p>When it was over, she sat up. She wiped her face with her good hand. She looked at the gallery door, and the light moving in it. She looked at it for a while.</p>\n<p>Then she turned her back on it.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said, and her voice was hoarse, and steady. “That’s done.”</p>\n<p>She got up. She looked along the Rim toward Spindle One, where it rose past the rail and ended in the Crown. And beyond it — between them and the Crown, hanging down across the Rim in long bright curtains, filling the walkway from rail to wall, moving slowly — the lowest threads of the Lace.</p>\n<p>It was like a forest. A forest of frost. Of light. Thread on thread on thread, branching and branching, hanging down from the sky to the steel of the Rim and spreading across it in fine luminous roots, a hundred metres of it, between them and the Crown.</p>\n<p>“That’s the Lace,” said Ione. “That’s the bit that kills you.”</p>\n<p>He looked at it. He listened to the keel humming in his spine. It had dropped again. He knew exactly how much.</p>\n<p>“Hold on to me,” he said. “Whatever you see.”</p>"}, {"file": "16-the-lace.md", "label": "Chapter 15", "title": "The Lace", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 4840, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Zero isn’t nothing. Zero’s balanced.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ione Tally, on the Rim</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">She stood on his boots.</p>\n<p>That was how they did it, in the end. They tried it the way they’d done it in the Breach — her legs round his waist, her arms round his neck-ring — and found he couldn’t see past her to walk, and she couldn’t see behind him to guide, and the bubble, a metre across, barely held the two of them in that shape anyway. So she turned round, and stood with her back against his chestplate, and stepped up onto the toes of his armoured boots, and he put his arms round her, one across her chest and one across her waist, and held her there, pressed back against him, her head under his chin. The exo took the extra weight with a grunt.</p>\n<p>“My mother used to dance me like this,” she said. “At Kindling. When I was small. On her feet.”</p>\n<p>“I never did that with Nell.”</p>\n<p>“No?” She tipped her head back against his visor to look up at him. “You should.”</p>\n<p>The keel hummed. He could feel the bubble round them, now that he knew to feel for it — a sort of tightness, a stillness, a metre-wide egg of calm. With her inside it, pressed against him, it was full. There was nothing to spare. If she leaned out, even a little, even her hand, it would be outside.</p>\n<p>“Don’t lean,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“Don’t put your hand out. Don’t touch anything. If you need to point, tell me.”</p>\n<p>“I know, Cas.” Her voice was very steady. She was looking ahead, along the Rim, at the forest of light hanging down across it. “Left foot.”</p>\n<p>He moved his left foot. She moved with it.</p>\n<p>They walked into the Lace.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The first thing was the light.</p>\n<p>Outside it — on the Rim by the gallery, on the spindle, in the city — the Lace had been a thing in the sky. Beautiful, terrible, far. In it, it was the air. The threads hung down all round them, fine and bright, some as thick as a wrist and some so fine they were only a glitter, branching and branching into themselves, reaching down from the sky to the steel of the Rim and spreading across it in fans and roots and deltas of white-violet light. They moved. Slowly, the way weed moves in a current. Close to, he could see them grow — see a branch put out a smaller branch, and that one a smaller, and that one a smaller, until the eye lost them and they became a kind of mist, a glow, a haze of edges on edges round every edge.</p>\n<p>And between them, on the Rim’s steel, there were arrows.</p>\n<p>White arrows. Chalk. Drawn quickly on the black steel plate, one every few metres, pointing ahead and a little left, between two great hanging threads.</p>\n<p>Ione went stiff against him.</p>\n<p>“Those are mine,” she said.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. They were rigger’s arrows — the same quick, slanting, three-stroke arrows she’d scratched on the struts of Spindle Four, the same hand. He’d have known them anywhere now.</p>\n<p>“You’ve been here,” he said. “Before. When you tried⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.” Her voice had gone very flat. “I never got this far. Not either time. The Lace was lower on the south side, I never got onto the Rim at all.” She was staring at the nearest arrow. “I’ve never been here. I’ve never drawn these.”</p>\n<p>“Then⁠—”</p>\n<p>“They’re fresh,” said Ione. “Look. The chalk’s still loose. You could blow it off.”</p>\n<p>They stood there on the Rim with the Lace moving round them.</p>\n<p>“Follow them,” he said.</p>\n<p>“I haven’t made them yet.”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Cas. “Follow them anyway.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet for a moment. Then she said, “Left foot,” and they followed them.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He would never be able to tell anyone, afterwards, how far they went, or how long it took. The Rim was a hundred metres, perhaps, from the gallery door to the stair that went up to the Crown. It should have taken a few minutes. They walked it step by step, her feet on his boots, following the chalk arrows between the threads, and it went on, and on, and he lost the sense of it; time went strange, the way it had gone strange in the Shear, and he stopped trying to count.</p>\n<p>The threads were close. Some of them hung so close that they passed within an arm’s length of the bubble, and he could see — <em>not look</em>, he told himself, <em>don’t look</em> — he could see into them.</p>\n<p>He looked.</p>\n<p>He couldn’t help it. It was like the Lamp; it pulled the eye. A thread as thick as a finger hung just beside his visor, and he looked into it, and it wasn’t a thread. It was a tunnel. It opened, the way a pinhole opens when you put your eye to it, into a space — a vast space, bright and violet, and in it, very small, very far, a sphere. A dark sphere, with a point of light at its heart and eight black pillars rising from its floor, and a tiny tangled city at the pillars’ feet, and a ring round the top of it, and on the ring, a forest of light, and in the forest, two minute figures, pressed together, looking into a thread.</p>\n<p>He looked into the thread they were looking into.</p>\n<p>The Vault. The pillars. The city. The ring. The forest. Two figures. A thread.</p>\n<p>And into that one.</p>\n<p>It went down. It went down for ever. Every thread held the Vault, and every Vault held the ring and the forest and the two of them, and every one of those held a thread, and every thread — he was falling, he was falling down it, down the tunnel, the Vault and the Vault and the Vault opening and opening and opening in front of him, smaller and smaller, finer and finer, and none of them ever getting any smaller at all, every one exactly as large as the last, because size meant nothing here, because there was no bottom, because it was <em>edge all the way down</em>⁠—</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas.</em>”</p>\n<p>Her voice. Her head, knocking back hard against his visor.</p>\n<p>“Don’t look into them,” said Ione. “I said. Don’t look. Look at the arrows.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the arrows. His whole body was shaking inside the suit.</p>\n<p>“How did you know⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because I did it too,” she said. “Just now. Before you. I looked into one.” Her voice was very steady and very small. “Left foot.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They heard themselves before they spoke.</p>\n<p>It happened near the middle of the Rim, where the threads were thickest. They were standing still, between two great hanging roots of light, while Ione looked for the next arrow — and her voice said, clearly, close beside his ear, “<em>Right. Right, and then straight.</em>”</p>\n<p>She hadn’t spoken. Her mouth hadn’t moved. He was looking at the side of her face; he’d have seen.</p>\n<p>A second later, she said, “Right. Right, and then straight.” And stopped. And went rigid against him.</p>\n<p>“Did you hear⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>They stood there. The threads moved slowly round them.</p>\n<p>And then another voice — his voice, his own voice, tinny and close as if from inside his own helmet — said, “<em>Ione, I can’t see the</em>⁠—” and stopped, cut off. He hadn’t said it. He hadn’t said anything.</p>\n<p>“The sound’s coming loose,” he said. His own voice sounded strange to him, now, as if he couldn’t be sure which one was real. “Out of order. It’s the gradient, it’s so steep here that — that sound’s arriving before⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>It was the suit</em>,” said Ione’s voice.</p>\n<p>It came from nowhere. From the air, from the threads. Not her voice now, but her voice from somewhere else — cracked, hoarse, as if she’d been crying, as if she’d been shouting. Close and far at once.</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas, it was the suit, it was never — it was the suit —</em>”</p>\n<p>And gone.</p>\n<p>She’d gone white. He could see it in the side of her face, the blood gone out of her cheek, her lips pressed together.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t say that,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“I haven’t said that. I don’t know what that means.” Her hand, her good hand, had come up and closed on his wrist where it crossed her chest. Very tight. “What suit? What does it — what does that <em>mean</em>, Cas?”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I don’t know. I don’t know. Ione. We haven’t said it yet. We don’t know what it means because we haven’t said it <em>yet</em>.” He heard his own voice rising. He made it stop. “Look at the arrows. Please. Look at the arrows.”</p>\n<p>She looked at the arrows. After a long moment she said, “Straight,” and they went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He saw it about halfway.</p>\n<p>There was a place where the threads thinned — a gap, a clearing in the forest of light — and the Rim’s inner rail was right there beside them, and beyond the rail, the Vault. The drop. Four kilometres of violet air down to the tiny still city. And off to the left, rising up out of that gulf, the black lattice of Spindle Four — the spindle they had climbed — its upper structure hung with the lowest trailing threads of the Lace, so that from here it looked like a tree wrapped in cobwebs of light.</p>\n<p>And on it, a long way down, something was moving.</p>\n<p>He stopped. Ione stopped with him.</p>\n<p>It was a figure. Small, at this distance — half a kilometre down, perhaps more — climbing down the inside of the spindle’s lattice. Grey. Hulking. It went down a ladder, and along a walkway, and down again, slowly, carefully, the way you climb when you’re tired. And on its back, there was a light. A soft band of light round a rounded housing, the size of a keg.</p>\n<p>A keel.</p>\n<p>He knew it. He knew it before he could have known it. He’d lived beside it for a month and inside it for the best part of a week. He knew the shape of those shoulder plates, the gouge down the left side of the back where he’d hit a beam in a shaft in the Undercroft. He knew the housing on its back — the band of lights, the white number painted on it by a boy in a lab, <em>1</em>, Teo’s number, Teo’s keel. He knew the harness round the hardpoints, spliced in rigger’s strapping, the webbing that Ione had spent three nights making.</p>\n<p>His suit.</p>\n<p>His suit, coming down Spindle Four. Alone.</p>\n<p>Just one. He looked. He looked for a long time. There was nobody else on the spindle. Nobody with it. Nobody on the ladder above it or the walkway below. One grey figure in a keel suit, climbing down, alone, through the cobwebs of light, toward the city.</p>\n<p>As he watched, it stopped.</p>\n<p>It stood on a walkway, a small grey shape against the black lattice, and it turned. It tipped its head back — the visor, a dark curve, catching the Lamp’s light — and looked up. Up at the Rim. At the Lace. At them.</p>\n<p>And it raised its hand.</p>\n<p>It held it there. A small grey arm, raised, the palm out. For a long moment. Then it lowered it, and turned, and went on down.</p>\n<p>He stood with his arms round Ione on the Rim and didn’t move.</p>\n<p><em>It’s me</em>, he thought.</p>\n<p>It came into his head whole and finished, the way thoughts had come in the Shear. It’s me. That’s me, coming down. I come back. I get to the Crown and I do whatever it is and I come back down the spindle, alone, in my suit. And she⁠—</p>\n<p>He looked down at the top of her head, under his chin. At the white streak.</p>\n<p><em>There are only three suits.</em> One was at the bottom of the Anvil in a booth, in a chair, without its keel. One was in a hall in the Works, full of dust. And one was his. And nobody came down with it.</p>\n<p>And she⁠—</p>\n<p>“Did you see it?” said Ione.</p>\n<p>Her voice was very quiet. She was looking down at the spindle too. At the place where the figure had been. It was gone now, further down, behind the lattice.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said.</p>\n<p>“That’s what I saw,” said Ione. “When I was seventeen. Coming down from the Rim. That’s what I saw, from the other spindle. That’s exactly what I saw.” She was quiet. “It raised its hand then too.”</p>\n<p>He said nothing.</p>\n<p>Neither did she. She stood against him, on his boots, with his arms round her, looking down at Spindle Four. He couldn’t see her face. He could feel her breathing — slow, even, very controlled — through the chestplate.</p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she knows what it means.</em></p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she’s known since she was seventeen. She’s known for eleven years. Someone comes down alone.</em></p>\n<p>He thought: <em>she’s been waiting at the top of the well for eleven years for the man who was going to come back without her.</em></p>\n<p>She said nothing. He said nothing. He tightened his arms round her, and felt her hand close on his wrist, and after a long time she said, “Left foot,” very quietly, and they went on.</p>\n<p>And somewhere under his breastbone, where the sketchbook was, a thing that had been small and quiet — the thing he’d found in the Lee watching her sleep, <em>I want to see what happens next</em> — turned over, and set its jaw, and became something else.</p>\n<p><em>No</em>, it said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It started with his hand.</p>\n<p>He’d been watching the arrows. Step by step, her feet on his boots, following the chalk. And he looked down at his right hand, where it lay across her waist, and it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>It was there. It was. He could feel it — her belt under his gauntlet, the warmth of her through the cloth. But when he looked at it, it was — thin. Faded. The grey plating of the gauntlet was grey, and then it was the colour of fog, and then he could see her belt through it, the buckle, the cloth, as if his hand were a picture of a hand painted on glass. And then it was solid again. And then it wasn’t.</p>\n<p>“Ione.”</p>\n<p>“I see it.” Her voice was tight. “Mine too.”</p>\n<p>He looked. Her hand on his wrist — her good hand, the scarred brown rigger’s hand — was going in and out. There, and fading, and there. Like a lamp flickering. Like a signal coming and going.</p>\n<p>“It’s the edge,” he said. “We’re near the edge. The Tear. It’s⁠—” He tried to think. He couldn’t think. “The boundary between being and not being is — the vacuum’s so stretched here that — things aren’t sure. Whether they⁠—”</p>\n<p>He looked up from his hand. And the Rim in front of them was flickering too. The steel plate. The rail. There, and fog, and there. The chalk arrows came and went. A whole section of the rail ahead of them simply wasn’t there — was a gap, a nothing, violet air — and then was there, solid, black, with rust on it. The threads of the Lace were the only things that didn’t flicker. They hung round them, bright and steady and moving slowly, as if they were the only things in the world that were sure.</p>\n<p>And then he looked down at the woman in his arms, and didn’t know who she was.</p>\n<p>It lasted a second. Less. He was holding someone. A short broad woman with dark hair and a white streak in it, standing on his boots, pressed back against his chest, her hand on his wrist. And he didn’t know her. He didn’t know her name. He didn’t know why he was holding her, or where he was, or why, and a terror went through him so absolute, so bottomless, that it was like falling into the thread again, falling down the tunnel of Vaults for ever. <em>Who is this. Who is this. Who am I holding.</em> He looked at the white streak. He looked at the scar on her forearm. He <em>knew</em> he knew them. He knew there was a word for them, a name, a whole life, and it wasn’t there. It was gone. It had flickered out, like the rail.</p>\n<p>Then it came back.</p>\n<p><em>Ione.</em></p>\n<p>It came back like the rail came back, all at once, solid and rusted and real. Ione. Ione Tally. The top of the well. <em>What time is it outside.</em> The loft. The hut. <em>I’m glad it was you.</em> It came back, all of it, and he was holding her so hard that she made a sound, and he couldn’t loosen his arms, he couldn’t make them loosen⁠—</p>\n<p>“Cas,” she said. “<em>Cas.</em> I’m here. I’m here.”</p>\n<p>“I didn’t know you.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“For a second. I didn’t — I didn’t know who you <em>were</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione. “I know. I didn’t know you either. Just now. I looked at your arm and I didn’t know whose it was.” Her voice was shaking. Her hand had moved from his wrist and was holding his gauntlet — holding it, pressing it against her waist, hard. “It came back. It came back. We’re here. Hold on. Hold on to me. Don’t let go of me, whatever you see. Don’t let go.”</p>\n<p>“I won’t.”</p>\n<p>“Whatever you see.”</p>\n<p>“I won’t,” he said. “I won’t.”</p>\n<p>They stood there, holding on to each other, on a Rim that came and went, in a forest of light that didn’t.</p>\n<p>Then she said, “Straight,” in a voice that was nearly steady, and they went on.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The worst of it was near the end.</p>\n<p>The stair to the Crown was in sight. Twenty metres ahead — a steel stair going up from the Rim, beside the black lattice of Spindle One’s top, to a hatch in the underside of the black lens of the Crown. The threads were thinner here. The arrows led straight to it.</p>\n<p>And the Lamp was very close.</p>\n<p>The Rim curved round it here, at its nearest. The inner rail was right beside them, and beyond it — twenty metres out, thirty, it was impossible to tell — hung the point. The Lesion. The Lamp. The hard white-violet nail driven through the world. He’d never been this close. Nobody had, since the Kindling. It was too bright to look at; he looked anyway; the visor darkened, darkened again, went almost black, and through it, the point burned.</p>\n<p>And he saw into it.</p>\n<p>He never had the words for it. Afterwards, when he tried — to himself, in the Crown, in the time that was left — the nearest he could come was that it was like looking into the thread, the tunnel of Vaults, only there was no Vault at the bottom. There was no bottom at all. There was the edge, and the edge, and the edge, going in, and in, and under the edge, at the very heart of it, where the vacuum had been stretched so far below nothing that it had split — he saw what nothing was.</p>\n<p>It was a ledger.</p>\n<p>That was the word that came to him. He didn’t choose it. It came up out of him from somewhere very deep, from fifteen years of figures on the backs of envelopes, from the Margin Review, from every sum he’d ever done. A ledger. Two columns. On one side, everything. Every star, every stone, every drop of water, every living thing, every city, every thought; all the positive, all the <em>something</em>, all of it, the whole of the universe that had ever been lit. And on the other side, exactly balancing it, the debt. The gravity. The stretched, straining, negative pull of everything on everything, the price of being. And at the bottom of the page, the sum.</p>\n<p>Zero.</p>\n<p>He’d read about it. As a student. An old idea, older than the Long Heat — a physicist had written a paper asking whether the universe itself might be nothing more than a fluctuation of the vacuum. A loan. Whether, if you added it all up, everything that existed and everything it owed, the total would come to exactly nothing. He’d thought it was a pretty idea. He’d thought it was a clever way to answer the question of where everything came from: <em>from nowhere, and it all cancels</em>.</p>\n<p>He wasn’t thinking it now. He was <em>seeing</em> it. He was standing on the Rim with his arms round a woman and looking into the torn place at the heart of the Draw, where the vacuum had been pulled so far below nothing that the bottom had come out of it, and he could see — not with his eyes; with something under his eyes — the whole of everything, balanced, on the edge of nothing. All of it borrowed. All of it owed. The stars on Nell’s ceiling. The city. The Commonweal and its six hundred million, and the Draw, and the Lamp, and Holdfast, and the forty-seven. Ben. Teo. Himself. Ione. All of it a loan against nothing, and all of it, in the end, summing to exactly zero.</p>\n<p><em>None of it’s real.</em></p>\n<p>It came into him the way the cold comes into you in winter water: not all at once, but everywhere.</p>\n<p><em>None of it’s real. None of it matters. It all cancels. Everything I’ve done and everything I haven’t. The pen. The truth in the Commons. The forty-seven. Ben’s eleven years. Ruth’s fifty. All of it. It all adds up to nothing. It always did. It always will. There’s nothing to pay because there was never anything to borrow. There’s nothing. There’s nothing. There’s only the sum, and the sum is⁠—</em></p>\n<p>He had stopped walking.</p>\n<p>He didn’t know when he’d stopped. He was standing on the Rim, still, with his arms round her, looking into the Lamp, and he could feel the weight of the zero on him like the weight of the whole Vault, like the weight at the top of the Breach, and his legs were going. His arms were going. He could feel the exo trying to hold him up and not knowing why, and he thought: <em>let it go. Let it go. What does it matter. It’s all zero. Let go⁠—</em></p>\n<p>“<em>Cas.</em>”</p>\n<p>Far away.</p>\n<p>“<em>Cas. Casimir. Look at me.</em>”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t. He was looking at the Lamp.</p>\n<p>Something hit his visor. Hard. The back of her head, slamming back against the glass. Once. Twice. The visor rang like a bell.</p>\n<p>“<em>Look at me.</em>”</p>\n<p>She’d turned round. On his boots, in his arms, inside the bubble, she’d turned round to face him — he felt her do it, felt her body twist against the chestplate — and now her face was in front of his visor, filling it, blotting out the Lamp. Her face, three centimetres away. Her eyes. Grey-green. Not patient. Wild.</p>\n<p>“I saw it too,” said Ione. “I saw it. Just now. I saw what you saw. I know. I know what it is.”</p>\n<p>“It’s zero,” he said. His voice came out of him from very far off. “It’s all zero, Ione. All of it. It cancels. None of it⁠—”</p>\n<p>“<em>I know.</em>”</p>\n<p>She had her hands on either side of his helmet. The good one and the splinted one. Holding his head. Making him look at her.</p>\n<p>“I watched the light in the gallery for three years,” she said. “It was nothing. It was <em>light</em>. It wasn’t anybody. It was nine minutes going round and round with nobody in it. It cancelled out, every time — Ruth said one-thirty-five and the key fell and she pulled the hammer and it went dark and started again and at the end of every cycle it added up to nothing. Nothing. Nothing had changed. Nothing ever would.” Her eyes were bright and wet and fierce. “And I loved them anyway. Do you hear me? I <em>loved them anyway</em>. It all cancelled and I loved them. That’s all. That’s all there is. Zero isn’t <em>nothing</em>, Cas.” She pressed her forehead against his visor. “Zero’s <em>balanced</em>.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her.</p>\n<p>“Count,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“You’re good at it. You count everything. You counted the hammer all night. You counted the rungs in the conduit. You counted Ben’s seconds.” She pulled back so that he could see her face. “Count me.”</p>\n<p>He didn’t understand.</p>\n<p>“<em>Count me</em>,” she said. “I’m one. I’m here. I’m one thing, and I’m here, and I’m not zero, I’m <em>one</em>. Count me.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her. At her face in front of his visor, filling it. Her eyes. The white streak. The stitches over her eye. The mole — he’d never noticed it — the tiny mole at the corner of her mouth. Her breath fogging the glass.</p>\n<p>“One,” he said.</p>\n<p>“Again.”</p>\n<p>“One.”</p>\n<p>“Again.”</p>\n<p>“One.” His voice was coming back. It was coming back from wherever it had gone, like the rail, like her name, solid and rusted and real. “One. One. You’re one. You’re here.”</p>\n<p>“I’m here,” said Ione. “And you’re one. And that’s two. And it doesn’t matter what the sum is. It doesn’t matter what it all comes to at the bottom of the page. We’re not the bottom of the page. We’re <em>here</em>.” She was crying. “Now turn round. Turn round, and look at the stair, and don’t look at that thing again, and walk. Please. Please, Cas. I can’t carry you. You have to walk.”</p>\n<p>He turned away from the Lamp.</p>\n<p>He looked at the stair. Twenty metres. Steel steps going up to a hatch in the black underside of the Crown. The chalk arrows leading straight to it, fresh and white on the flickering steel.</p>\n<p>“Left foot,” said Ione, and turned round again in his arms, and stood on his boots.</p>\n<p>He walked.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hatch was open. Of course it was open; the Crown crew had come down through it the night of the Kindling, running for the lift, and nobody had closed it after. It was a round steel hatch in the black keelstone underside of the Crown, at the top of the stair, and beyond it, darkness.</p>\n<p>They climbed the stair. On the last step, before the hatch, Ione stepped off his boots, out of his arms, onto the steel — and the bubble let her go, and she staggered, but the stair was in the lee of the Crown itself, the great keelstone lens overhead pulling against the Lamp, and the weight here was bearable. Just. She caught the rail. She stood there, breathing hard, holding on to it.</p>\n<p>He stood beside her. The keel hummed in his spine. It was low now. He could hear exactly how low. It had given them the Lace, and it had cost.</p>\n<p>Below them, down the stair, the Rim ran back through the forest of light toward the gallery, and the chalk arrows ran along it.</p>\n<p>He looked at them. Then he looked at Ione.</p>\n<p>She was looking at them too.</p>\n<p>“I’m going to have to draw those,” she said. Her voice was very quiet. “Aren’t I. On the way back.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Because I followed them. So I know the way. So I’ll draw them. So I can follow them.” She was quiet. “They were never anyone’s. They never came from anywhere. They’re just — true.”</p>\n<p>“Like the key,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Like the key.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t say anything else. She didn’t say <em>on the way back, alone</em>. She didn’t say <em>in your suit</em>. She didn’t look at the suit at all.</p>\n<p>Neither did he.</p>\n<p>He looked instead at the hatch, and the darkness in it, and he thought about a small grey figure on Spindle Four, climbing down alone, raising its hand. He thought: <em>that’s me. That’s me, coming back without her.</em> And the thing under his breastbone that had set its jaw said again, very quietly, very clearly: <em>No.</em></p>\n<p>He didn’t know yet what the Clause needed. He didn’t know what the plate meant, or what the witness was, or what he’d find under the floor. But he knew one thing, standing on the stair at the top of the world with the keel running down in his spine and the Lace moving slowly below him. He knew it the way he’d known the fractal number on the telemetry screen, the way he’d known it at the window in the Collar: <em>at last</em>.</p>\n<p>Whatever it asked for, he’d be the one who gave it. Whatever stayed up here, he’d be the one who stayed. And then the figure on Spindle Four would be wrong, and it would be her coming down, not him — her, alive — and he’d have beaten it. For once in his life he’d have looked at a projection and said <em>no</em> and made it false.</p>\n<p>It didn’t feel like dying. That was the strange thing. It didn’t feel like the window in the Collar at all. It felt like the opposite.</p>\n<p>“Come on,” he said.</p>\n<p>They went up into the Crown.</p>"}, {"file": "17-the-crown.md", "label": "Chapter 16", "title": "The Crown", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5313, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I am sorry. There was no other way to build it.</p><span class=\"attr\">Inscription, inner plate of the Keystone, signed I.H.</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The Crown was full of snow.</p>\n<p>That was what he thought, coming up through the hatch into the grey light: that it had snowed. That in the hundred and sixty-eight years since Ruth Tally and eleven others had run for the lift, snow had come into the highest room in the Draw and lain there, deep and soft and undisturbed, over everything.</p>\n<p>It was dust. It lay over the floor ankle-deep, and it lay over the consoles and the chairs and the desks in thick grey drifts, and it lay along the top of the great curved window in a soft grey ridge. When they walked through it, it rose round their boots in slow clouds and hung in the air and didn’t settle. The air was very still. It smelled of nothing. Of stone and age.</p>\n<p>He’d seen the Crown in the ghost-light. He’d seen it in the old footage, in the mural in the crisis room. Crisp. White coats. The needle swinging over. He’d seen it thirty minutes ago — a hundred and sixty-eight years ago — full of people, full of light.</p>\n<p>He stood in it now and didn’t recognise it.</p>\n<p>The consoles had furred. Their screens were grey and blind under the dust, their switches rusted into place, the plastic of their casings gone brittle and cracked and curled. Dieter’s mug was on the desk where he’d left it, and when Cas brushed the dust off it, it was not a mug. It was a shape of mug, in stone — the coffee in it gone to a brown crust, the ceramic crazed all over with fine cracks, like something dug out of a tomb. The chairs had collapsed on themselves. A clipboard hung on a hook by the door, and the paper on it had gone to a grey lace that fell apart when he breathed on it.</p>\n<p>The clock on the wall had stopped. Its hands had fused to its face. They pointed at a time that meant nothing — twenty past something, or twenty to — and would never point at anything else.</p>\n<p>“It’s old,” said Ione. She was standing in the dust by the hatch with her arms wrapped round herself, looking round. “It’s so <em>old</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Time’s been going faster up here. Much faster.” He looked at the window. “And going round. This whole room’s been in the Fold since the Kindling. It’s had⁠—” he didn’t know “⁠—a very long time.”</p>\n<p>The window was the worst of it. The great curved sheet of armoured glass, forty metres long. Along its lower edge the walkway still hung where it had torn from its frame, sagging, twisted, half-fallen. The frame above it, where the first thread of the Lace had touched — the black keelstone that nothing could break — had gone white. A strip of it as wide as a hand, all the way along one section of the frame, gone white and soft and slumped like the wax on a candle. And through the glass, very close — so close that the Crown seemed to hang in it like a lantern hung in a tree — the Lace. Its threads came down past the window in bright curtains. One of them came <em>through</em>. He saw it and stopped. A single thread, fine as a hair, came in through the glass near the torn walkway as if the glass weren’t there, and crossed the room at waist height, and went down into the floor near the far end of the gallery — near the place where, in the ghost-light, Ruth had knelt.</p>\n<p>It moved, very slowly, the way the threads always moved. It didn’t flicker. It was the most real thing in the room.</p>\n<p>He understood the dust, then. A keelstone room, sealed on every side but a hatch, with a thread of the Tear running through it: the locker room in the Works, a thousand times over. For every hour the city below had lived since the Kindling, the Crown had been living at the speed of the Lace.</p>\n<p>“Don’t touch that,” he said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>And in the far end of the Crown, down by the thread, there was a faint light.</p>\n<p>He’d thought it was the Lace, at first. It wasn’t. It was the ghost-light — the same white-violet glow he’d seen going round in the gallery windows, but much fainter here, a thin shimmer in the dust-thick air, like the memory of a picture. The room’s own light, from that night, still just barely hanging in it. He could see, if he looked, the faint shapes of people. Moving. Running. Faded almost to nothing.</p>\n<p>At the far end of the room, by the Keystone, a woman in a white coat was on her knees in the dust, looking up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The plate was still open.</p>\n<p>It lay back on its hinge where Ruth had thrown it, a hundred and sixty-eight years ago, a square of brushed steel furred with dust. Cas knelt and wiped the dust off it with his gauntlet. The letters came up under his hand, engraved, clear.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE<br>FOR USE IN CATASTROPHIC BOUNDARY FAILURE ONLY<br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS</div>\n<p>Under it, set into the floor, the housing. Black keelstone, smooth and round, the size of a dinner plate, with a recessed ring around its edge and, in the middle, a keyhole. A narrow cross.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt beside him. She knelt exactly where the ghost of Ruth was kneeling — he saw her do it, saw the faint white shape of the woman in the lab coat overlap with her, the two of them in the same place for a moment, one real and one light, both looking at the lock — and Ione didn’t flinch. She’d done it a hundred thousand times. She took the key off its cord.</p>\n<p>She held it for a moment.</p>\n<p>“Do you want to⁠—” she said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas. “It’s yours.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him. Then she put the key into the lock.</p>\n<p>It went in. It went in the way a thing goes into the place it was made for: smoothly, completely, with a small soft sound like a breath let out. The cross-shaped bit sank into the cross-shaped hole and seated. She put her fingers on the bow.</p>\n<p>She turned it.</p>\n<p>It was stiff. It didn’t move, and then it did — a quarter turn, heavy, resisting, the way a lock resists that hasn’t been turned for a hundred and sixty-eight years or ever — and then there was a click. Deep. Not in the lock. In the floor. In the whole Crown. It went down through the keelstone into Spindle One like the first stroke of the hammer going down through Tier Nine, and he felt it in his knees.</p>\n<p>The housing opened.</p>\n<p>The recessed ring round its edge split into segments — six of them, black keelstone petals — and drew back, sliding under the floor, like the iris of an eye opening. And under it, where the housing had been, there was a hollow in the floor. And in the hollow, there was the machine.</p>\n<p>It was very simple. That was the first thing he saw. After a lifetime of the Draw, of its vast humming inhuman machinery, the rectifier halls and the tensioners the size of buildings, it was almost shocking how simple it was. A wheel: a small brass wheel set upright, about the size of a hand, finely toothed round its rim like the wheel of an old instrument, with a knurled grip. Beside it, a gauge: a round dial under glass, with a needle, and a single line drawn across its face from edge to edge, and a narrow band of colour on either side of the line. Beside that, a lever — red, with a black-and-yellow collar, exactly like the Dead Man’s Hammer by the lift. And round the whole of it, set into the rim of the hollow, a ring. A dial. Engraved with figures, very small, going round.</p>\n<p>And on the inside of the housing’s lid — on the back of one of the six petals, which had folded up and back and now stood upright at the edge of the hollow like a little headstone — there was a second plate.</p>\n<p>He read it. He read it on his knees in the dust with the thread of the Lace moving slowly a metre away and the ghost of Ruth Tally kneeling beside him looking up at nothing, and Ione beside her, and the keel humming low in his spine.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">HOLLOWELL CLAUSE — PROCEDURE<br><br>1. SET THE INTERVAL.<br>2. ARM. THE LATTICE WILL RELEASE ITS LAST HOLD. THE DEBT WILL BE CALLED.<br>IT WILL BE PAID AT THE END OF THE INTERVAL.<br>3. HOLD. AS THE PAYMENT GATHERS, ITS PHASE WILL WANDER.<br>KEEP THE NEEDLE ON THE LINE BY HAND. DO NOT LET IT CROSS.<br>4. AT THE END OF THE INTERVAL THE FOLD WILL CLOSE UPON THE PAYMENT<br>AND UPON ALL THAT IS WITHIN THE CROWN.<br>IT WILL CLOSE INTO A SINGLE INSTANT.<br>NOTHING WILL PASS OUT OF IT. NOTHING WILL PASS INTO IT. IT WILL NOT END.<br><br>THE SEALING REQUIRES A WITNESS.<br>THE WITNESS DOES NOT RETURN.<br><br>I AM SORRY. THERE WAS NO OTHER WAY TO BUILD IT. — I.H.</div>\n<p>He read it twice.</p>\n<p>Then he sat back on his heels in the dust and didn’t move.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Tell me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She’d read it too. She was kneeling beside the hollow, looking at the plate, and her face was very calm, the way it had been calm all morning on Spindle Four, and her hands were in her lap, and they were perfectly steady. He noticed that. They weren’t shaking now.</p>\n<p>“Tell me what it does,” she said. “Plainly. I’ve watched it for three years and I never knew. Tell me.”</p>\n<p>So he told her.</p>\n<p>He did it plainly. The way he’d told her about the Lees, about the Shear, about the Bells. He’d spent his whole life learning to say complicated things so that they sounded simple, and then fifteen years saying simple things so that they sounded complicated, and he found now that the first skill had come back to him, all at once, like a language you’d spoken as a child.</p>\n<p>“The debt’s coming due anyway,” he said. “You know that. The Lamp’s going to pay it — all of it, all at once — when the Lace reaches the Rim. Thirty-odd rounds, down in the city. Less. Whenever. We don’t know exactly when. Nobody does.” He touched the ring round the hollow. The dial. “This lets you choose when. You set the time. You pull the lever — that’s <em>arming</em>. The Lattice lets go of the last thing it’s holding. The loan gets called. And at the end of the time you set, the payment comes. Right here. At the Lamp.”</p>\n<p>“And then everything ends.”</p>\n<p>“No. That’s the — that’s what she built. That’s the Clause.” He looked at the thread of the Lace crossing the room. “When the Lamp broke — the night of the Kindling — it didn’t just break. It broke <em>time</em>, up here. It made the Fold. The Lace, the Crown, the Carousel — you’ve seen it. Things going round. Keys falling forward. Arrows nobody drew. The Fold’s full of loops. And Hollowell knew it would be. She knew that if it ever broke badly enough to kill us, it would break badly enough to do <em>that</em>. And she built this to use it.”</p>\n<p>He took a breath.</p>\n<p>“At the moment the payment comes,” he said, “the Fold closes. Round the Lamp. Round the Crown. Round the payment. It closes up into a loop. A loop in time. A loop so short it’s — nothing. An instant. One instant, whose future is itself. The payment starts — the very first instant of it, the very first edge of the light — and then the loop closes, and that instant is joined to itself, end to beginning, and it’s sealed. It can’t go on to the next instant. There isn’t a next instant. The debt’s paid, and the payment never arrives. It’s always just about to. For ever.” He heard his voice go strange. “She built a sarcophagus. Out of a single moment.”</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“And the rest of it?” she said. “Holdfast? Outside?”</p>\n<p>“Outside the seal, everything goes back. The debt’s inside the instant, and the loan’s inside the instant, and they balance, and they’re sealed. Nothing’s pushing any more. The Lamp’s gone. Time down there comes back to — ordinary. The same as Outside. The Shear goes. The Drop’s a ladder.” He looked at her. “It works, Ione. I think it works. I think she was right.”</p>\n<p>“<em>The sealing requires a witness</em>,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Tell me that. Plainly.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the gauge. At the needle, resting now at one side of the dial, and the line across its face, and the narrow coloured bands on either side.</p>\n<p>“When you arm it,” he said, “the payment starts to gather. At the core. And as it gathers, it — wanders. Its phase. It won’t come in straight. It’ll come in the way the lamp-weather came in the Breach, in gusts, in swings. And the seal has to close on it exactly. Exactly in phase. Or the loop won’t close clean, and the payment gets out.” He touched the brass wheel. “So someone has to keep the needle on the line. By hand. Watching it. Turning the wheel, a little this way, a little that, as it wanders. All the way to the end. Right up to the instant.”</p>\n<p>“Why a person?” said Ione. “Why not — tie it? Lock it?”</p>\n<p>“Because it wanders. It won’t sit still. You’d need something that could see it and think.” He shook his head. “And nothing that thinks can live up here except a person. Not a machine. You’ve seen what the suit’s electronics are doing. Up here, a machine’s clocks come apart in a second. It has to be eyes. And a hand. And a head that can guess which way it’ll go next.” He looked at the plate. “That’s the witness. That’s all she meant. Somebody to <em>watch</em>.”</p>\n<p>“And the witness is inside the Crown,” said Ione. “When it closes.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“<em>All that is within the Crown.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“<em>Into a single instant.</em> <em>It will not end.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>She looked at him.</p>\n<p>“And they’re in it,” she said. “The witness. For ever. In one instant. Living it.”</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>She blinked.</p>\n<p>“Not living it,” he said. “Not the way you mean. Not over and over. You told me. On the spindle.” He found his voice was quite steady. “If a loop’s really closed, there’s nobody inside to be tired of it. It’s just once. It’s once, and it’s true, and it stays true. It’s only for ever for whoever’s outside.” He looked at the brass wheel. “It’s not a prison. It’s not the gallery. Whoever’s in it doesn’t go round. They’re just — there. In one instant. Once. Exactly as they are.” He paused. “It’s only for ever from out here.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t answer. She was looking at him with an expression he’d never seen on her face before and couldn’t read at all.</p>\n<p>He looked back at the plate.</p>\n<p><em>Default</em>, he thought. The word you couldn’t say in the Commonweal. The word Rask had never said, that the Ministry had never said in ninety-one years, that he’d never once said out loud in his life.</p>\n<p>“Default,” he said. Out loud, in the Crown.</p>\n<p>It sounded like a very small word.</p>\n<p>He found that he felt calm. Entirely calm. Calmer than he had felt at the window in the Collar, calmer than he’d felt in fifteen years. Calmer than he could remember feeling. He’d come in here for this. He hadn’t known what it was, and it was here, under a plate in the floor, and it was simple, and it was something he could do.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Take the key out,” he said. “We don’t need it any more. It’s done its⁠—”</p>\n<p>She was already reaching for it. It was still in the lock, in the middle of the hollow, standing up among the wheel and the gauge and the lever. She took hold of its bow and drew it out.</p>\n<p>The thread of the Lace moved.</p>\n<p>It had been lying across the room at waist height, fine as a hair, from the window to the floor. It had been lying there perfectly still except for its slow weed-in-a-current drift, a metre from the Keystone. And as Ione drew the key up out of the lock, the thread drifted — a few centimetres; a hand’s width — and one of its finest branches, a filament so fine it was almost only a glitter, swung across the hollow.</p>\n<p>It touched the key.</p>\n<p>It was nothing. A brush. Less than a second. There was a sound, very faint, very high, like a fingernail on glass. And Ione’s hand jerked, and the key came out of the lock, and she was holding it up in front of her, in the dust-thick air, staring at it.</p>\n<p>Along the shaft of the key, from the bow almost to the bit, there was a line.</p>\n<p>A fine bright line, scored into the black keelstone. Keelstone, that nothing could scratch. It shone faintly in the grey light, white-violet at first, then fading, as they watched, to a thin pale grey. A scratch. Straight, and very fine, and a little crooked at the end, where it ran off the edge of the shaft near the bit.</p>\n<p>Ione made a sound.</p>\n<p>She was staring at it. She turned the key over in her fingers — slowly, very slowly — and ran the ball of her thumb along the line. From the bow to the bit. And back. The way you’d trace a scar.</p>\n<p>“It’s always had this,” she said.</p>\n<p>He didn’t understand, for a moment.</p>\n<p>“This,” said Ione. Her voice was very thin. “This scratch. It’s always had it. Since the gantry. Since I picked it up. I’ve — every night, Cas. Every night for eleven years. I lie in the hammock and I hold it and I run my thumb along the scratch. Here. It’s how I go to sleep.” Her thumb moved along it. “It goes crooked at the end. Just here. I know it. I know exactly where it goes crooked. I’ve always wondered what did it. What could scratch keelstone.” She looked up at him. Her face was white. “<em>This</em> did it. Just now. It’s always had it because it’s just got it.”</p>\n<p>He looked at the key in her hand. At the thin grey line along its shaft.</p>\n<p><em>The jinn must return exactly as it left.</em> The old paper. His professor, joking. <em>You’d have no way to know where it came from. Because it didn’t come from anywhere.</em></p>\n<p>“It has to go back,” he said.</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“The key,” he said. “It came from here. It came out of the Fold, onto the gantry, in the Carousel. For you to find. And now it’s — it’s done. It’s opened the lock. It’s got its scratch. It’s exactly as it was when you picked it up.” He made himself say it. “It has to go back. To the gantry. Eleven years ago. For you to find it.”</p>\n<p>“There’s no one else,” said Ione. Very quietly. “Is there. To take it.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“Because I found it. So I have to leave it. So I can find it.” Her thumb was still on the scratch. “It never came from anywhere.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“It was never <em>made</em>. It never⁠—” She stopped. She closed her hand round the key. She held it against her chest, against her breastbone, the way she’d worn it on its cord for eleven years. “Cas. How? How do I⁠—”</p>\n<p>And then she stopped, and her head came up, and she turned — slowly — and looked down the length of the Crown, away from the Keystone, to the far end of the room. To the place where the floor stepped down, where a short steel stair went down into a lower space under the main deck.</p>\n<p>“The service deck,” she said.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She’d seen it from below. That was what she told him, going down the stair. For three years, from the gantry — every time she crossed it, four thousand times — she’d seen the underside of the Crown above her, the black keelstone lens on the top of Spindle One, and in its underside, a row of small round windows. Portholes. The service deck, under the main floor, where the Crown’s machinery had been. And in one of the portholes, sometimes — not every cycle, but sometimes — there’d been a light.</p>\n<p>“Not the ghost-light,” she said. “Not the Crown’s light, going round. A different light. Steady. Grey. Like daylight through a door.” She was going down the stair ahead of him, one hand on the rail, the key clenched in the other. “I used to wonder what it was. I used to think it was someone up there. Watching.” She stopped at the bottom. “It was this.”</p>\n<p>The service deck was low and cramped and full of dead machinery — pumps, cable trunks, a bank of rusted cabinets — and dust, the same deep grey dust, and on the far side, in the curved outer wall, a row of small round portholes looking down.</p>\n<p>And in the middle of the deck, near the portholes, the air was wrong.</p>\n<p>He saw it at once. He’d have seen it anywhere now. A shimmer. A vertical line of wrongness hanging in the air a little above the floor, the height of a person, a hand’s width wide, like the seam in the Carousel — but brighter. Much brighter. It wasn’t a ripple. It was a <em>door</em>. A tall narrow slot of grey light standing in the middle of the dead deck, as if someone had cut the air with a knife and there was a grey afternoon on the other side of it. Round its edges, very faintly, the finest threads of the Lace came and went.</p>\n<p>And through it — when he stepped round to look through it — he saw the gantry.</p>\n<p>It was below. The slot of light looked <em>down</em>, as if it were a window in a floor; through it he could see the long steel walkway from Four to One, slung across the violet gulf, a little way below them. Pitted with rust. Sagging slightly in the middle. Exactly as it had been an hour ago, when they’d crossed it.</p>\n<p>Not exactly. He looked, and looked again. The rust was a little less. The handrail, which had been bent in one place near the middle, was straight.</p>\n<p>And at the near end of it — where it met the curved wall of the gallery, the ring round Spindle One — the gallery’s windows were lit. The ghost-light, going round.</p>\n<p>“That’s then,” said Ione. She was standing beside him, looking down through the slot. Her voice was very calm. “That’s the gantry. Then. Eleven years ago. When I was in there.” She looked at the gallery’s lit windows. “I’m in there. Right now. Then.”</p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything for a while.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know how long it stays open,” he said at last. “It might close. It might⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.” She looked down at the key in her hand. Then at him. “I’ll be quick.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know how it goes,” she said. “I’ve always known how it goes. I just never knew it was me.” She almost smiled. “I’ll put it on the gantry. By the loose bolt. And I’ll stand at the end, by the wall, where she saw me. Where I saw me. And she’ll come across, and find it, and look up. And I’ll turn away.” She put her hand flat on his chestplate. “And then I’ll come back. That’s all. That’s how it goes.”</p>\n<p>“How do you know you come back?”</p>\n<p>She looked at him with those grey-green eyes.</p>\n<p>“Because she found a seam,” said Ione, “where the woman had been standing. And went through it. And got out.” She took her hand off his chest. “I’ll leave it open for her. On my way back.”</p>\n<p>She stepped into the light.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He waited.</p>\n<p>It was seconds. He counted them — of course he counted them — standing in the dust of the service deck in the grey light from the slot, with the dead machinery all round him and the keel humming low in his spine. Eleven seconds. Twelve. He watched the slot. He couldn’t see her through it. He could see the gantry below, and the gulf, and the lit windows of the gallery, and no one.</p>\n<p>Twenty. Thirty.</p>\n<p>He thought about a girl of seventeen lying on the floor of the gallery below, letting the nine minutes go through her. He thought about her getting up, one cycle, and deciding to learn their names. He thought about her crossing the gantry four thousand times, knowing every bolt.</p>\n<p>Forty-one. Forty-two.</p>\n<p>She came out of the light.</p>\n<p>She came through it the way you’d come through a doorway in a high wind — stumbling, catching herself, her good hand out. And she was different. He saw it at once. It had been forty-two seconds and it had not been forty-two seconds for her; he could see it in her face. It was grey. Her eyes were red. Her lips were cracked, as though she hadn’t drunk for hours. There was rust on her knees, on her hands — the gantry’s rust — and dust in her hair, and she was shaking. Not a tremor. All of her.</p>\n<p>Her hands were empty.</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>She walked straight past him. She walked to the wall of the service deck, under the portholes, and put her hand on it, and stood there with her back to him, her head down, her other hand pressed against her mouth.</p>\n<p>“How long?” he said.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know.” Her voice came out muffled. “Hours. Hours. I had to wait. I put it down — by the bolt — and I went to the end, by the wall, and I waited. And she didn’t come. And she didn’t come. And the light went round and round in the windows and I could hear it, <em>is it going to be too bright</em>, and she didn’t — and I thought, what if she doesn’t, what if I’ve got it wrong, what if it’s the wrong cycle, what if⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<p>“And then she came,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“She came out of the gallery door. Onto the gantry. And she was⁠—” Ione’s shoulders moved. “She was so <em>thin</em>, Cas. She was so thin. I didn’t — I don’t remember being that thin. Her arms were like sticks. Her hair was filthy, she’d been cutting it with a — with a bit of metal, you could see, it was all hacked. And she had the grey. Just starting. Just here.” She touched her own temple, the white streak, without turning round. “She was walking with her head down. Like she’d walked across there four thousand times. Because she had.”</p>\n<p>Her voice broke. She waited. She went on.</p>\n<p>“And she saw it. She stopped. She just — stopped. And stood there. She stood there for such a long time, looking at it. And then she crouched down and picked it up. And she looked at it in her hand. And she ran her thumb along it.” Ione’s own thumb moved, against the wall, along nothing. “Along the scratch. The first time. And then she looked up.”</p>\n<p>“And she saw you.”</p>\n<p>“And she saw me.” Ione turned round then. Her face was wet and grey and he had never seen anything so tired. “And she said <em>Mam?</em>”</p>\n<p>He couldn’t speak.</p>\n<p>“She said <em>Mam</em>,” said Ione Tally. “Like a question. Like a little girl. <em>Mam?</em> And I stood there at the end of the gantry, and I wanted⁠—” Her face twisted. “I wanted to say, <em>no, love, it’s not Mam, it’s you, it’s you, you get out, you get out of here and you go home and Gran’s waiting and you’ll be all right, you’ll be all right, you’ll be⁠—</em>”</p>\n<p>She put both hands over her face.</p>\n<p>“But I remembered,” she said, through them. “I remembered she didn’t say anything. The woman. She didn’t say a word. She just turned away. I’ve remembered it for eleven years. So I knew. I knew I didn’t. And I stood there, and I tried to make my mouth — and I couldn’t. I don’t know if I couldn’t or if I just — didn’t. I don’t know. I don’t <em>know</em>. I stood there and I let her think I was Mam. I let her think Mam had come up to find her. I let her run along the gantry toward me shouting <em>Mam</em> and I turned my back on her and I walked away.”</p>\n<p>He crossed the deck. He put his arms round her. She let him. She put her face against the chestplate of the suit, and he held her, and she shook.</p>\n<p>“I left the seam open,” she said, into the suit, after a long time. “Where I’d been standing. I felt it close behind me when I came through, but not all at once. Slowly. Like a wound. I think she’ll — I know she’ll get through. I know she does. I’m here.” She laughed, a terrible small laugh. “I’m <em>here</em>. So she does.”</p>\n<p>“She does,” he said. “You did.”</p>\n<p>“I let her think I was her mother.”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“She’s going to go down the spindle,” said Ione, “and look in a water-trough and see she’s twenty, and she’s going to spend eleven years thinking her mother came up to the top of the world to save her and then turned away. She’s going to wonder, every night, why she turned away.” Her voice was very quiet. “And it was me. It was always me.”</p>\n<p>He held her.</p>\n<p>Behind them, the slot of grey light hung in the dead air of the service deck. As they stood there, it narrowed. Slowly, like a door swinging shut. A hand’s width. A finger’s. A line. And then only a faint shimmer, and the threads of the Lace coming and going round the place where it had been, and then nothing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went back up the stair into the Crown.</p>\n<p>The hollow in the floor stood open. The machine in it: the small brass wheel, the gauge with its line, the red lever, the ring of the dial. The petal of keelstone standing upright at its edge with the plate on it. Beside it, the faint ghost of Ruth Tally, on her knees in the dust, looking up at nothing. At the place beside the Keystone, where someone would stand to reach the wheel.</p>\n<p>Ione went and stood by the hollow. She looked down at the plate. She read it again. He could see her lips move.</p>\n<p><em>The sealing requires a witness.</em></p>\n<p><em>The witness does not return.</em></p>\n<p>Then she looked up. Not at him.</p>\n<p>At the suit.</p>\n<p>He was standing in it, in the middle of the Crown, in the dust, with the keel humming low in his spine. And she looked at it — at the grey plates, the gouge down the left side, the housing on his back with its band of lights and its white painted <em>1</em>, the rigger’s harness she’d spent three nights splicing — the way you look at a thing you’ve seen before, a long time ago, from very far away.</p>\n<p>He looked at it too. He looked down at his own gauntlets. At the chestplate. At himself.</p>\n<p>Neither of them said anything at all.</p>"}, {"file": "18-the-witness.md", "label": "Chapter 17", "title": "The Witness", "part": "Part Three · The Ascent", "partStart": false, "words": 5665, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>I’ll hold your rope. You hold mine.</p><span class=\"attr\">Holdfast riggers’ wedding vow</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">“It’s me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She said it quite quietly, standing by the hollow in the dust, with her hands at her sides. As if she were telling him the time.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Cas.</p>\n<p>“Listen to me.” She didn’t raise her voice. “I’ve thought about this. I’ve had eleven years to think about it, and I’ve had since the Rim, and I’ve had the — the hours. On the gantry. Waiting for her. Listen.”</p>\n<p>“Ione⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’ve already lived my forever.” She said it evenly. “Three years in that gallery. Three years of one night. I know what it is. I know what it’s like to be in one moment and not get out. I’m not frightened of it. I’m the only person in the world who isn’t. And you told me — you said it yourself, just now — it’s once. For whoever’s in it, it’s only once.” She looked at him. “I can do once.”</p>\n<p>“So can I.”</p>\n<p>“You’ve got a daughter.” Her voice caught on it, and went on. “You’ve got a little girl called Nell who collects stars, and a woman in a chair at the bottom of the Anvil who’s waiting to buy you a drink, and a whole city of — six hundred million people, you said, and a sky, and rain, and a sea. You’ve got an <em>ordinary</em> waiting for you. You haven’t lived it yet. You told me in the hut you’ve had fifteen years of the same day. You haven’t lived your ordinary.” She shook her head. “I’ve got Gran. And Gran’s eighty-three, and she’s dying, and she’s never once said so. That’s all I’ve got. That’s all I’ve had, since I came down. I’ve been a ghost in Holdfast for eleven years. I sat on the Tollers’ step and watched people step round me. I’ve already lived my forever, Cas. You haven’t lived your ordinary. It’s me.”</p>\n<p>He looked at her for a long time.</p>\n<p>“I saw it come down,” he said.</p>\n<p>She went still.</p>\n<p>“On the Rim. In the Lace. The suit.” He looked down at himself — at the grey plates, the gauntlets. “My suit. With your harness on it. Coming down Four. Alone. I saw it, and I thought: <em>that’s me</em>. That’s me coming back without her. I thought I was going to go home and you were going to die up here.” He heard his voice shake and didn’t stop it. “And I decided — right there, on the Rim, with you standing on my boots — that I wasn’t going to let it. I thought, <em>whatever it asks for, I’ll be the one who stays</em>. I thought if I stayed, the vision would be wrong. It’d be you coming down. Not me. I’d have <em>beaten</em> it. For once in my life I’d have looked at a projection and made it false.”</p>\n<p>Ione was staring at him.</p>\n<p>“I saw it too,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know. You told me. At seventeen⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No. I mean⁠—” She stopped. Her face had gone very strange. “I’ve been waiting eleven years at the top of the wells for a man in a suit to come up. And I always knew — I’ve always known, since I was seventeen — that he comes back down. Alone. I saw it. So I knew. From the first minute. From when I pulled you out.” She put her hand to her mouth. “I’ve known the whole time that you go home and I don’t. And on the Rim, when we saw it again, I thought — I decided — I’d make it wrong. I’d stay. So you’d go home to your girl. So it’d be — <em>not</em> you, coming down alone, leaving me. So it’d be you coming down alone because I <em>chose</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<p>They looked at each other across the hollow in the floor.</p>\n<p>“We both⁠—” he said.</p>\n<p>“We both saw you.” She was breathing very fast. “We both saw you come down, and we both decided to — we both⁠—”</p>\n<p>She stopped again.</p>\n<p>And then something happened to her face. He watched it happen. He watched it the way he’d watched Ben’s hand appear on the glass: all at once, whole, from nowhere. Her eyes went wide. Her hand came away from her mouth. She looked at him — at the suit — at the grey plates and the housing and the harness — and she took a step back from the hollow, and another, and her face crumpled.</p>\n<p>“Cas,” she said. Her voice cracked right down the middle. “Cas, it was the suit. It was never — it was the <em>suit</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>He heard it. He heard it twice — once now, in the Crown, from her mouth, and once on the Rim, in the Lace, from nowhere, an hour ago, a lifetime ago, <em>it was the suit</em>, in exactly that voice, exactly that crack in it — and the two of them rang together in his head like two strokes of the same bell, and he understood.</p>\n<p>They’d never seen <em>him</em>. Either of them. Not at seventeen, not on the Rim. They’d seen a suit. Grey plates and a humming keel and a harness spliced in rigger’s strapping, climbing down Spindle Four alone. Whoever was in it. They’d both looked at the suit and seen him inside it, because it was his, because who else would be in it — and it had never been a vision of who lived. It had only ever been a vision of who wore the suit.</p>\n<p>He looked down at himself.</p>\n<p>Then he looked at her. Short, broad, strong. Her rope on her shoulder. Her splinted wrist. The white streak.</p>\n<p>“It’s you,” he said. “Ione. It’s you coming down. It was always you.”</p>\n<p>“<em>No</em>.”</p>\n<p>“It was you in it. On the Rim, raising its hand. At seventeen. It was always⁠—”</p>\n<p>“No.” She’d backed up against a console. Dust rose round her in a slow cloud. “No. No. Then it’s not — then it isn’t a <em>choice</em>. Then it was always going to be — I don’t get to — you don’t get to⁠—” She was shaking her head. “Then it’s just the key again. It’s just the arrows. It’s just something that’s true that nobody chose, and we’re only⁠—”</p>\n<p>“It’s already true,” said Cas. “And I’m still choosing it.”</p>\n<p>She stopped.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>He didn’t know he was going to say the next thing until he said it.</p>\n<p>“Ruth saw me.”</p>\n<p>Ione stared at him.</p>\n<p>“In the Log. Year Thirty-One. I read it in the Tower, the first night. I didn’t understand it. I didn’t — I read it twice and turned the page.” He could see it: the pencil, the short entry set apart with a line above and below. “She saw a man at the Keystone. At 02:09. After the key fell. Kneeling where she was kneeling. Dressed close, in grey, like a diver.” He looked down at his gauntlets. At the suit. Under it, he knew exactly what he was wearing: the grey liner. The one-piece. The thing that made all three of them look like depressed seals. “His hand on a wheel that wasn’t there. She said he looked at her like he knew her. She said he was very calm.” He looked up. “She said there was a cord knotted round his wrist.”</p>\n<p>Ione didn’t move.</p>\n<p>“She’s been looking at me,” he said. “In the gallery. Every cycle. For three years, you watched her look up at the Keystone, at nothing, like someone was there. You stood in the place. You said it wasn’t you.” He swallowed. “It was me. She was looking at me. At — this. Now. Here.”</p>\n<p>Ione’s hand had gone to her pocket.</p>\n<p>He watched it. She didn’t seem to know she was doing it. Her good hand went into the pocket of her tunic and closed round something there, and came out, and opened, and lay in her palm. A length of thin cord. The cord from the wedding. The one she’d tied round his wrist on the rooftop in the Drop District with the drums going and the tower swaying, and taken off again with one tug. <em>Riggers’ hitch. Holds under load. Won’t slip. That’s the only one you’ll need.</em></p>\n<p>She looked at it in her hand.</p>\n<p>“It’s not on your wrist,” she said. Very quietly. “It’s not there yet.”</p>\n<p>“No.”</p>\n<p>“She saw it there. So it’s there. So someone ties it.” She looked up at him. “So <em>I</em> tie it. That’s the only way it gets there. I have to do it. I have to choose to do it. Like the arrows. Like the key.” Her voice broke again. “You’re asking me to <em>tie it on</em>.”</p>\n<p>“I’m not asking you anything,” said Cas. “It’s yours. It’s your rope.”</p>\n<p>She closed her hand round it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>“Why?” she said.</p>\n<p>It was a long time later. Or not long. They were sitting on the floor of the Crown, in the dust, by the hollow, side by side, their backs against the dead console. The keel hummed. The ghost of Ruth knelt by the Keystone, looking up. The thread of the Lace lay across the room, moving slowly. Outside the great window, the Lamp burned.</p>\n<p>“Tell me why,” said Ione. “Not the vision. Not the Log. Not what’s true. <em>Why</em>. You came in here to die. You told me. In the hut. You came in because it was tidy. Because you thought you owed it.” Her voice was quite steady now, very low. “If that’s why — if you’re doing this because you think you still owe it — then I’ll fight you. I’ll put you on the floor and take that suit off you myself and you can go home and I’ll stay, and it doesn’t matter what’s true. I will. I’ll make it false or I’ll die trying. I’m not letting you do it for that.”</p>\n<p>He thought about it. He thought about it the way she thought about questions — turning it over, looking at it from underneath — because she’d earned that, and because he needed to know.</p>\n<p>He thought about the window in the Collar. <em>At last. Almost a relief.</em> He thought about the Quiet, the dark, the small cold place with the simple shape: <em>everything I do adds to it</em>. He thought about the Rim, and the ledger, and the zero at the bottom of the page.</p>\n<p>He examined himself for it. He patted his pockets.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>“I’m not paying anything,” he said.</p>\n<p>She turned her head.</p>\n<p>“I came in to pay,” he said. “You’re right. I came in here to pay it off. All at once. With me. And you told me — in the hut — that you can’t. That it doesn’t get smaller. That it just grows.” He looked at the hollow. The brass wheel. “You were right. I’m not paying anything. There’s nothing to pay. There never was. You showed me the bottom of the page and it was zero, and you said zero’s balanced. It’s balanced. I don’t owe anything. I’m square.”</p>\n<p>“Then <em>why</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Because I want to.”</p>\n<p>It was the truest thing he’d ever said. He heard it come out of him and knew it the way he’d known the fractal number on the telemetry screen. <em>I want to.</em></p>\n<p>“I want this,” he said. “Not the dying. It’s not dying. You told me. It’s once.” He looked at her. “Ione. For fifteen years I lived one day. The same day. The day with the pen. I stood in the gallery and watched myself sign it, over and over, thinking <em>don’t</em>, the way you watched Ruth. And I never once — I never had a moment I’d want. Not one. Not one moment in fifteen years that I’d have wanted to keep. And now I’ve got one.” He could hardly get it out. “I’ve got one. I want to be in it. I want to be in the moment where I know you’re safe. Where I know it worked. Where Nell’s going to see the stars and Teo’s going to get out of her chair and thirty-one thousand people are going to walk down a ladder. Where you’re going down Spindle Four in my suit, alive.” He shook his head. “That’s not paying. That’s the opposite of paying. It’s — it’s the only thing I’ve ever wanted that I’ve been sure of.”</p>\n<p>She was crying without any sound.</p>\n<p>“I want the moment,” said Casimir Hale. “I’m choosing it. Not because it’s true. Because I want it. It’s just — it’s true as well.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They chose the instant together.</p>\n<p>He showed her the dial. The ring round the rim of the hollow, engraved with Hollowell’s small precise figures. The interval. He worked it out with her, aloud, the way he’d worked out the curve in the Tollers’ tower, the way she’d worked out the Lees: how long for her to get back down the stair, and along the Rim through the Lace, and through the Carousel’s seam — Ben’s seam, still there, she said; she’d seen it on the way back from the gantry, still shimmering — and across the gantry, and down the spindle, and down through the Breach. How long before she was past the Breach, well below the Rim, in the Lees, out of the Fold. How long it would be before she was safe.</p>\n<p>“Three hours,” said Ione. “If I’m quick. If the weather’s kind.”</p>\n<p>“Three and a half,” he said. “In case it isn’t.”</p>\n<p>He set the ring. It turned under his fingers with a smooth, heavy click at each mark, like the dial of a safe.</p>\n<p>“From the Crown,” he said, “I’ll be able to see Four. Down there. Through the window. If I look.” He pointed through the great glass, down and to the left, past the glare of the Lamp, to where the black lattice of Spindle Four rose out of the violet gulf wrapped in its cobwebs of light. “I’ll have to watch the needle. But I’ll be able to see. And the keel’s got its lights. I’ll see you going down.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll see me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“All the way.”</p>\n<p>“And at the end⁠—”</p>\n<p>“At the end, you’ll be past the Breach. And I’ll look. And I’ll see your light.” He was quiet. “That’s the instant. That’s where I’ll be.”</p>\n<p>She was silent for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she said: “I’ll learn one for you.”</p>\n<p>“What?”</p>\n<p>“A star. When I get out. When I see them.” She looked at the window, at the Lamp, at the only star she’d ever had. “You said you never looked. You never had a favourite. You said Nell would have known in a second.” She wiped her face with the back of her good hand. “I’ll find one for you. I’ll ask Nell. I’ll go out under the — the sky, the real one, and I’ll pick one, and it’ll be yours. And I’ll look at it.”</p>\n<p>“Every night?”</p>\n<p>“They come every night?” she said. “You said they come every night.”</p>\n<p>“Every night.”</p>\n<p>“Then every night,” said Ione.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They didn’t talk about it after that. They talked about other things.</p>\n<p>He told her about rain again, because she asked. She asked what it tasted of. He said nothing, mostly, and sometimes dust, and in the Stacks, sometimes, of the metal of the gutters. She asked if you could drink it. He said yes. She said she’d drink it. She asked about the sea — whether it was loud — and he said yes, very, and that it never stopped, and she said that she’d lived with the Bell all her life so she’d be used to that. She asked what they ate, Outside. He told her. She was scandalised that nobody ate rat. He said she could start a fashion. She said she’d open a stall. <em>Holdfast’s Other Citizens, Roasted.</em> He laughed. It came out of him in the dust of the Crown, a real laugh, loud, and she looked at him with that expression he’d seen on the spindle — as if he’d surprised her — and then she laughed too, the rough short stiff laugh, and couldn’t stop, and put her forehead down on his shoulder.</p>\n<p>And then, at some point, neither of them was laughing. And she lifted her head. And he took off the helmet — he’d had it on since the Rim — and put it down in the dust. And she put her good hand on the side of his face, and looked at him, the way she’d looked at him at the top of the well, patiently, attentively, as if he were a view she’d seen many times and still liked.</p>\n<p>“I’m glad it was you,” she said. “That came up the well.”</p>\n<p>“I’m glad it was you that pulled me out.”</p>\n<p>She kissed him. It was very gentle. It went on for a while.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Getting her into the suit was the hardest thing he had ever done.</p>\n<p>Not because of what it meant. Because of the straps. The suit was built for him, and she was a head shorter and twice as broad through the shoulders, and it opened down the back like a clam and she stepped into it and it closed round her — thunk, thunk, thunk — and it didn’t fit anywhere. The exo woke and took her weight and didn’t know what to do with her, and she stood in it swaying like a child in her father’s coat, her face tiny in the big curve of the visor, and said, <em>well, this is ridiculous</em>.</p>\n<p>So he fitted it to her. The way Ben had fitted it to him in the hangar, a hundred years ago. He knelt in the dust in the grey liner and went round the suit, strap by strap, buckle by buckle, taking in the harness at the shoulders and letting it out at the hips, shortening the legs of the exo by two notches, cinching the webbing she’d spliced for him so that it would hold her instead. His hands were shaking. He made them stop, and they shook anyway, and he went on.</p>\n<p>“Ione,” he said, at the back of the suit, feeling along the strapping under the keel housing, “I can’t see the⁠—”</p>\n<p>“⁠—buckle,” she said. “It’s lower. Left. There.”</p>\n<p>His hands stopped.</p>\n<p>He knelt there in the dust with his hands on the strap, and the words he’d said went through him, and the words he’d heard on the Rim went through him, and they rang together like the bell again — <em>Ione, I can’t see the —</em> in his own voice, out of the air, out of nowhere, in the Lace — and he found that he was laughing. Kneeling behind her in the dust, laughing, with his forehead against the keel housing.</p>\n<p>“What?” she said. “What is it?”</p>\n<p>“Nothing.” He found the buckle. It was lower. Left. There. He did it up. “Nothing. It’s just — that one was only a buckle.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t understand. Then she did. He heard her breath go in, and out, and then she was laughing too, in the suit, the sound thin and strange through the helmet speaker, and it went on until it wasn’t laughing any more, and they both stopped, and he finished the straps.</p>\n<p>When it was done she took three steps across the Crown in it, and turned, and took three back. She was steady. The exo had learned her. The keel hummed on her back. Her face in the visor was very small and very pale and very calm.</p>\n<p>“It’s heavy,” she said.</p>\n<p>“You’ll get used to it.”</p>\n<p>“I’ve got about three and a half hours.”</p>\n<p>“You’ll be fine.”</p>\n<p>“I know.” She looked at him through the visor. At him standing in the dust in the grey liner — the close grey one-piece, slippery, cool, like a diver’s. Like something worn under armour. She looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<p>Then she opened the suit’s chest compartment and took out the cord.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She tied it the way she’d tied it on the rooftop. Quickly. Over, under, round, through. Her gauntlet had to come off for it — she worked the clasp with her splinted hand and pulled it off and let it drop in the dust — and with her bare fingers she tied the riggers’ hitch round his left wrist, above the cuff of the liner, flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped.</p>\n<p>Her fingers stopped when it was done. They stayed on the knot.</p>\n<p>“I’ll hold your rope,” said Ione Tally. Her voice was quite steady. “You hold mine.”</p>\n<p>He knew the rest. He’d heard it on a rooftop with the drums going and the whole tower stamping. He said it with her.</p>\n<p>“And if the one of us falls,” they said, “the other one hauls.”</p>\n<p>Nobody shouted <em>haul</em>. There was nobody to shout it. There was only the Crown, and the dust, and the ghost of Ruth Tally on her knees by the Keystone, looking up at the place where he was standing.</p>\n<p>Ione looked at the knot on his wrist. Then she looked at the ghost.</p>\n<p>“She’s looking at you,” she said. “Look. She’s looking right at you.”</p>\n<p>He looked. The faint white shape in the dust, the woman in the lab coat with her hair pinned up and the mole under her ear, kneeling by the open housing, her face turned up toward the place beside it. Toward him.</p>\n<p>“A hundred and seventy-five thousand times,” said Ione, “I watched her look at you. I stood where you’re standing. Hundreds of times. To see if it was me she saw.” Her voice broke, finally, completely. “It was you. It was always you. She was looking at you the whole time. She was looking at <em>you</em>.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They armed it together.</p>\n<p>He knelt by the hollow in the grey liner with the knot on his wrist. She knelt beside him in the suit. The red lever stood between them with its black-and-yellow collar, exactly like the one by the lift that Ruth had pulled. He put his hand on it. She put her bare hand over his.</p>\n<p>“So this is the start,” she said.</p>\n<p>“This is the start.”</p>\n<p>They pulled it.</p>\n<p>It moved easily. It moved like a thing that had been waiting to be moved. It came down through its collar and seated with a sound — not a click; a <em>toll</em>, deep, a single note going down through the keelstone into Spindle One and on down, and on, into the Vault, into the Anvil, into everything — and he felt it go through him, the way he’d felt the hammer go through Tier Nine. The Lattice letting go. The last hold released.</p>\n<p>Outside the great window, the Lamp changed.</p>\n<p>It didn’t grow. It didn’t dim. But its light — its hard white-violet — went, very faintly, warmer. A thread of gold in it. And round it, the Lace stirred. All of it, at once, the whole vast filigree across the sky, rippling, the way a curtain ripples when a door opens somewhere in the house. The thread lying across the Crown moved, and lifted, and drifted a little toward the window, as if something out there were drawing a breath.</p>\n<p>On the gauge, the needle trembled. And began to move.</p>\n<p>He put his hand on the brass wheel.</p>\n<p>It was warm. It turned under his fingers smooth and heavy and fine, a little click at each tooth, and the needle, which had begun to drift off the line toward the band of colour on its left, stopped, and hung, and came back. He held it there. He felt it wander, under his hand, in the wheel — a tug this way, a tug that way, like a fish on a line — and he held it on the line.</p>\n<p>“Go,” he said. He didn’t look at her. He couldn’t take his eyes off the needle. “Ione. Go.”</p>\n<p>She didn’t move for a moment.</p>\n<p>Then she leaned in — the suit’s helmet close to his bare face — and pressed her visor against his temple. Hard. Just for a second. He felt the cold of the glass. He heard her breathe.</p>\n<p>“Haul,” she said.</p>\n<p>Then she stood up, and turned, and he heard the suit’s boots go across the Crown — heavy, steady, booming on the keelstone floor, the keel’s hum going with them — and down the hatch. And the hum faded. And was gone.</p>\n<p>He held the needle on the line.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went down the stair onto the Rim, and the Lace was waiting for her.</p>\n<p>It was moving. It had been moving slowly all the time they’d been in it; now it moved like weed in a flood. The threads swayed and swung and reached, rippling with that long slow breath that had come into them when the lever went down. The light had a thread of gold in it. The air was full of a sound she had never heard in the Lace before — a high, faint, singing, like a finger round the rim of a glass.</p>\n<p>And there were no arrows.</p>\n<p>She stood at the foot of the stair and looked at the steel plate of the Rim running back through the forest of light toward the gallery. Bare. Black. No chalk. No white rigger’s arrows, three quick strokes, pointing the way between the threads.</p>\n<p>Of course there weren’t. She hadn’t drawn them yet.</p>\n<p>She had chalk. She always had chalk. She opened the pouch on the suit’s belt — he’d put it there, when he fitted the suit, without saying anything; she’d seen him do it — and took out a stick, and knelt on the steel at the foot of the stair, and drew an arrow. Three strokes. Pointing ahead, and a little left, between two great hanging roots of light.</p>\n<p>It looked exactly like the first one they’d seen.</p>\n<p>She stood up. She walked to where it pointed. She drew another. She knew where it went. She knew exactly where it went, because she’d followed it, an hour ago, standing on his boots with his arms round her. She’d followed it because it was there. It was there because she was drawing it. It had never been anyone’s. It had never come from anywhere. It was just true.</p>\n<p>She drew them all the way. Every one. Kneeling in the suit on the flickering steel of the Rim — it flickered; she didn’t look at it — with the threads swaying round her and the singing in the air and the keel humming round her in its metre of calm. She did not look into the threads. She did not look at the Lamp. She looked at the chalk in her bare fingers, and the steel, and the arrows.</p>\n<p>In the middle of the Rim, where the threads were thickest, she heard her own voice.</p>\n<p>“<em>Right. Right, and then straight.</em>”</p>\n<p>It came out of the air beside her. Her voice, from an hour ago. From before. She stopped with the chalk in her hand, kneeling, and she knew exactly where it was coming from. She knew that somewhere very close to her, in the same place and not in the same place, a man in a keel suit was standing with a woman on his boots, and the woman was looking for the next arrow, and the man had just heard her voice come out of nowhere and gone cold.</p>\n<p>She looked at the place where the next arrow had to go. Right. Right, and then straight.</p>\n<p>“Right,” said Ione, to the air, to the man, to the girl on his boots. Her voice was shaking. “Right, and then straight. It’s all right. You’ll find them. They’re all here. I’ve drawn them for you.”</p>\n<p>She drew it.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went through the Carousel without stopping.</p>\n<p>She came in by the Rim door, into the corridor, and the ghost-light came on round her — <em>Is it going to be too bright?</em> — and she walked through it. She didn’t look at the windows. She didn’t look at the scratched fives on the walls, or the pallet of rags, or the pile of smooth stones. She walked straight along the curve to the place in the middle of the corridor where Ben’s seam was, the hand’s-width shimmer between the two branches, and she stopped in front of it.</p>\n<p>And then she did stop. Just for a moment. She turned round.</p>\n<p>The ghost-room was round her. Ruth was at the board, with her back to her, her hair pinned up. It was the second minute. <em>The protocol requires the interlocks off.</em> Crane was talking. Ruth’s thumb had gone to her mouth.</p>\n<p>Ione stood in the corridor in the suit and looked at her.</p>\n<p>“I know who you’re looking at,” she said. “Later. At the Keystone. I know who it is now.” She swallowed. “He’s all right. He’s — he’s not frightened. You were right. He’s very calm.”</p>\n<p>Ruth bit the side of her thumb, and didn’t hear her, the way she’d never heard her.</p>\n<p>“Goodbye, Ruth,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She turned to face the outer wall — the gantry side, the way home — and stepped through the seam.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The gantry swayed under her. The keel hummed. The Lace, above and behind her, was singing louder now, and the light was more gold than violet, and the gulf beneath the gantry was full of a strange shifting glow, as if the whole Vault were lit from a new angle. She ran. She couldn’t run in the suit; she walked as fast as it would let her, three hundred metres of rusted steel lattice, past the loose bolt — she stepped on the plate beside it, and it rattled, and she nearly stopped, and didn’t — to the door in the lattice of Spindle Four.</p>\n<p>Down. The stairs. The ladders. The hut in the last Lee, its iron door standing open, the planks inside where they’d lain under his coat and hers. She didn’t stop.</p>\n<p>The Breach.</p>\n<p>It was worse than it had been. She’d known it would be. The payment was gathering at the core; the Lamp was pushing and swinging and pulling all at once; the lamp-weather in the Breach was a gale. She went down the single spine of keelstone with its ladder rungs, and down swung round her, and swung, and swung, and the keel’s bubble held. It held. She could feel it straining. She could feel it averaging the whole screaming world out over its metre of calm, and she understood now what he had felt on the way up with her wrapped round him, and she went down, rung after rung, not looking out, not looking down, the rungs coming up past her visor, black iron, pitted.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the Breach the lattice closed round her again. The great struts. A Lee. The weather dropped away.</p>\n<p>She stopped on the walkway, and put her bare hand on a strut, and stood there, breathing.</p>\n<p>She was past it. She was out of the Fold. She’d done it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know what time it was. The suit’s clocks had died on the Rim; she’d watched the numbers fall apart. She didn’t know how long she had, or whether she’d made it, or whether up there in the Crown the needle was still on the line and the wheel was still turning under his hand.</p>\n<p>She turned round. She tipped her head back. She looked up.</p>\n<p>The spindle rose above her, black lattice, narrowing, into the glare. The Breach. The hut. The gallery, a ring round the neighbouring pillar, its windows full of moving light. The gantry, a thread across the gulf. And above it all, the Rim, a dark circle round the Lamp, hung with the Lace. The Lace was singing. The Lamp was gold.</p>\n<p>And on the Rim, in the Lace — small, very far, at the edge of a clearing in the forest of light — there were two figures.</p>\n<p>She could see them. She could see them quite clearly. A grey figure in a keel suit, standing by the Rim’s inner rail. And another, smaller, standing in front of it, pressed back against it, the grey arms round her. Not moving. Two people, holding each other up, at the top of the world.</p>\n<p>Looking down at her.</p>\n<p>She stood on the walkway in the Lee with her bare hand on the strut and looked up at them for a long time. At herself, standing on his boots. At him, holding her. At the two of them, on the Rim, an hour ago, a lifetime ago, looking down at a small grey figure in a keel suit on Spindle Four, both of them sure it was him.</p>\n<p>She raised her hand.</p>\n<p>She held it there. The palm out. She didn’t know what else to do. She held it up, toward them, toward him, toward the girl on his boots who didn’t know yet, who would find out in the Crown with her voice cracking down the middle — <em>it was the suit</em> — and she held it there, and held it, until her arm shook.</p>\n<p>Then she lowered it.</p>\n<p>She turned, and went on down.</p>\n<p>She’d gone perhaps ten steps down the next flight of stairs when the light changed.</p>\n<p>It didn’t fade. It didn’t flicker. There was no sound. The Lamp — which had been gold, and singing, and very bright — was simply, between one step and the next, not there. And the Lace was not there. And the light was not there. And there was nothing above her and nothing round her and nothing below her but the dark.</p>\n<p>Ione Tally stood on the stair in the black, in his suit, with the keel humming round her in its little metre of calm, and the only light in the whole world was the soft band of it round the housing on her back, glowing faintly on the rungs.</p>\n<p>Above her, very high up, in the dark, something that had been about to happen was never going to happen.</p>\n<p>And somewhere in it, once, a man was looking down at her light.</p>"}, {"file": "19-the-bell.md", "label": "Chapter 18", "title": "The Bell", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": true, "words": 5144, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Then we are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">The Common Office, final response</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The first thing she noticed was that she was going to float away.</p>\n<p>She stood on the stair in the black with one hand on the rail and the keel humming round her, and her body told her — very clearly, very urgently, the way your body tells you that you’ve missed a step in the dark — that there was nothing holding her down. That she weighed nothing. That if she let go of the rail she’d drift up off the stair like a puff of chalk and be lost.</p>\n<p>She held on to the rail. She stood very still. She waited for it to pass.</p>\n<p>It didn’t pass. And after a while she understood that it wasn’t going to, because it wasn’t a gust. It wasn’t lamp-weather. It was just the weight. The Lamp had been pushing on her from above every minute of her life, every minute of every Holdfast life for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and it had stopped. The Anvil was still there, pulling. The world was still there, under the Anvil, pulling. But the push was gone. And what was left was⁠—</p>\n<p><em>Ordinary</em>, she thought. <em>This is what they weigh. Outside. This is ordinary.</em></p>\n<p>She let go of the rail. She didn’t float away. She stood on the stair and weighed what a woman weighs, which was less than she’d ever weighed in her life, and it was so strange that she laughed out loud in the suit — a short, cracked, frightened laugh that went out into the dark and didn’t come back.</p>\n<p>Because there was no echo. There was no sound at all.</p>\n<p>She hadn’t known there was a sound until it stopped. The Lamp had made one. The Lace had made one. It had been there all her life, under everything — under the Bell, under the voices in the lanes, under the wind in the ropes — so faint and so constant that nobody in Holdfast had ever heard it any more than they heard their own blood. A hum. A singing. It had been getting louder on the Rim, at the end, the singing in the gold light. And now it was gone, and the silence where it had been was so enormous that she could hear her own heart in the suit, and the keel’s hum, and nothing else in the world.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<p>Nothing. Black. Not dark — dark was the Quiet, dark was the tank with the lid shut. This was more than dark. It went up and up above her and didn’t stop, and there was nothing in it anywhere. No Lamp. No Lace. No Rim. No Crown. She tried to see where the Crown should be — up there, at the top of Spindle One, where he was — and there was nothing to see. Black on black. It could have been a hand’s width from her face. It could have been the edge of the world.</p>\n<p><em>Somewhere in it</em>, she thought. <em>Once.</em></p>\n<p>She stood on the stair for a long time, looking up at nothing, with the soft band of light round the keel on her back glowing on the rungs.</p>\n<p>Then she took out her chalk, and held her hand out flat, and blew.</p>\n<p>The chalk went off her palm in a little cloud, lit by the keel’s glow. And fell. Straight down. In a thin white curtain, straight as a plumb line, down past the stair and on into the dark.</p>\n<p>True chalk. On Spindle Four, three kilometres up. Where there had never, in a hundred and sixty-eight years, been anything but crooked.</p>\n<p>She watched it go until she couldn’t see it.</p>\n<p>“There,” she said to him, out loud, into the dark, in case he could hear. He couldn’t. She said it anyway. “There. It’s done. You did it.”</p>\n<p>Then she started to climb down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It took her nine hours. She didn’t know it was nine hours; the suit’s clocks were dead, and she hadn’t brought anything else. She knew it afterwards, because the Tollers told her. At the time it was only stairs.</p>\n<p>Stairs in the dark. Ladders in the dark. The keel’s little glow on the rungs, a metre of soft light round her and black beyond it. She’d climbed Spindle Four a dozen times since she was nine, and she’d never climbed it like this — never with nothing pushing, never without the Lees. There were no Lees now. The struts were still there, the great black beams, and she went past them and felt them pull, faintly, the way the Anvil pulled; but there was no push for them to pull against, and so there was no calm, and no storm, and no difference between one place and the next. Only down. The same down everywhere. Straight chalk everywhere.</p>\n<p>It was easy. That was the horror of it, at first, and then the wonder. It was easy. Her legs were Holdfast legs, built for half as much again, and in the ordinary weight they felt like springs. She went down the ladders in the suit faster than she’d ever gone down them in her life. She stopped at the hut and didn’t go in. She stopped at the best Lee, the one with the four struts, where they’d slept and the chalk had hung in the air like a cloud — and blew chalk, and watched it fall straight, and stood there for a while, and went on.</p>\n<p>She thought about him. She thought about him the whole way down. She tried not to, and then she stopped trying.</p>\n<p>She thought about the needle. About his hand on the brass wheel, turning it, a little this way and a little that. About him looking down through the great window at a small light on Spindle Four. About what he’d seen, at the end. Her light, past the Breach. Her light, stopped on a walkway. Her arm raised.</p>\n<p><em>He saw me raise my hand.</em></p>\n<p>She had to stop on a ladder when she thought that, and hold on, and wait until she could see again.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know if he’d seen it. She didn’t know if his eyes had been on her or on the needle, whether he’d looked up in time. She’d never know. She understood, holding on to the ladder in the dark, that she was going to spend the rest of her life not knowing, and that she was going to have to decide what she believed, and that nobody would ever be able to tell her if she was right.</p>\n<p>She decided. Right there, on the ladder.</p>\n<p><em>He saw me.</em></p>\n<p>She went on down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>At about two kilometres, she saw the city.</p>\n<p>She’d been looking down for it for hours. There’d been nothing — the dark below as black as the dark above, the keel’s glow on the rungs and nothing past it. And then she came round the corner of a flight of stairs onto a walkway on the spindle’s inner side, where you could see down through the open lattice to the Anvil, and there it was.</p>\n<p>It was lights.</p>\n<p>Not the Lamp’s light. Not violet, not white, not one great glare over everything. Little lights. Points. Tiny and warm and yellow, thousands and thousands of them, spread out across the black floor of the world. Scattered through the scaffold towers. Strung along the lanes. Clustered round the spindles’ feet. Some of them still, some of them moving — carried, she thought; lamps being carried through the lanes. Each one so small, alone, that it hardly lit anything. And all of them together making the shape of the city, laid out in the dark below her like a map drawn in fire.</p>\n<p>The lamps. The oil lamps. The ones they’d been making in their kitchens for eleven rounds, pressing algae, twisting wicks, while he was in the Quiet and the Stewardship was saying the Relief was a demon from the Drop. They’d lit them.</p>\n<p>She stood on the walkway and looked at it.</p>\n<p><em>Points of light</em>, he’d said. In the loft, lying on the floor, when she’d asked him what the stars were like. <em>Little points of light in the dark. Thousands of them. They don’t do anything. They’re just there.</em></p>\n<p>“Oh,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She stood there a long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Pim was at the bottom.</p>\n<p>She heard him before she saw him. She was on the last ladder down into the wreck of the lift terminal at the foot of the spindle, and there was a lamp below, and voices, and then a high cracked voice shouted, “<em>There — there! There’s a light! On the — it’s the suit, it’s the suit, it’s the Relief, he’s —</em>”</p>\n<p>She came down off the ladder into the wreckage, into the lamp-light.</p>\n<p>Pim was there, and Osk, and Bett from the riggers’ tower, and a dozen others. Holding lamps. They’d been waiting at the foot of Spindle Four, in the dark, in the wreck. Pim was at the front. He had a clay lamp in each hand and a rat on each shoulder and his face was streaked black with soot and wet with crying, and he was running at her — at the suit — with his arms out and the lamps swinging and the rats clinging on⁠—</p>\n<p>And then he saw her face.</p>\n<p>He stopped. Just stopped, a metre away, his arms still out. The light from his lamps went up into the visor. She saw him see her. She saw him look at her face, small in the big helmet. And then look at the suit. And then look past her, at the ladder, up into the dark. And then back at her face.</p>\n<p>He didn’t say anything. He was eleven, and he’d been everywhere, and heard everything, and carried the Relief’s recorder out through a vent. He looked at her. And he put the lamps down on the ground, very carefully, one and then the other. And he came and put his arms round the suit’s leg and held on to it and pressed his face against the plating and didn’t say anything at all.</p>\n<p>Osk had taken off his hard hat.</p>\n<p>He stood there in the lamp-light at the foot of the spindle, the huge slow man, and held it against his chest the way he’d held it on the first day, at the top of the well, when he’d knelt. He didn’t kneel now. He looked at her. At the suit. And he bowed his head.</p>\n<p>Behind him, the others did the same.</p>\n<p>She opened the suit. She hadn’t known she was going to. She found the clasp at the neck and the seals and it opened down the back the way it had opened for Cas a hundred times, and she stepped out of it in her rigger’s clothes onto the rubble at the foot of Spindle Four, and nearly fell, because she was so light — and Osk caught her. The way he’d caught Cas, the first day. Under the arms. And held her up.</p>\n<p>“Stack your bones,” said Osk, gently.</p>\n<p>“I know how,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She stood. She looked at them. At the lamps. At the dark.</p>\n<p>“He’s up there,” she said. “He’s — it worked. It’s done. He did it. The Lamp’s closed.” Her voice was quite steady. “He’s not coming down. He’s the witness. He’s in it.”</p>\n<p>Nobody said anything.</p>\n<p>“The Drop’s a road,” said Ione Tally. “He told you. When the Lamp goes out, the Drop’s a road. It’s out. The Drop’s a road.”</p>\n<p>Somewhere behind Osk, in the dark, somebody started to cry.</p>\n<p>And then — faintly, far off, from somewhere out in the city, carried on the still dark air — somebody else began to shout it. <em>The Drop’s a road.</em> And another voice took it up. And another. She heard it go out across Holdfast, voice by voice, lamp by lamp, the way the shutter-wave went out at dusk. <em>The Drop’s a road. The Drop’s a road. He said. When the Lamp goes out. He said.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She went to the Tower first.</p>\n<p>She knew she should go to the Commons. She knew there’d be thousands there, and fear, and the Stewards, and Crane. She knew there were people in the lanes who hadn’t believed him — she could hear them, now and then, among the other voices: screaming, praying, somewhere a woman shrieking <em>demon, demon, he’s killed the Lamp</em> over and over. She knew all of it.</p>\n<p>She went to the Tower first.</p>\n<p>The square round it was full of lamps. The Tollers had put them out on every ledge and step and platform of the tower, all the way up round the column, so that the whole spiralling scaffold was outlined in little yellow flames, and the black keelstone pillar in the middle of it, with its hundred and sixty-eight years of chalk, gleamed in their light like wet stone. People were standing in the square, looking up at it. Just standing. Holding their own lamps.</p>\n<p>And the Bell was coming.</p>\n<p>She felt it before she heard it. It came up through the soles of her boots, through the keelstone of the square — not a swell. Not a tide. Not the long slow rising pressure she’d felt all her life, a little more often every year, until lately it had come every ten minutes. A <em>knock</em>. Short and sharp and deep, a single blow, like a fist on a door far below. And then silence. And then⁠—</p>\n<p>Another. And another.</p>\n<p>Once a second. Once a second. Once a second.</p>\n<p>She stood in the square with her hand on her chest and felt it, and the people standing round her with their lamps were feeling it too — she could see it in their faces, turned up toward the tower, toward the column, toward the Tollers on their platforms. Every face had the same look. A kind of fear. A kind of wonder. A kind of listening, the way you listen to a heartbeat when you’ve put your head on someone’s chest.</p>\n<p><em>Knock. Knock. Knock.</em></p>\n<p>She went into the Tower.</p>\n<p>The round room at the bottom was full of lamplight, and pendulums, and Tollers — every Toller there was, crammed in, standing round the walls, silent. Tobiah was at the column with a stick of chalk in his hand. He wasn’t writing. He was just standing there, with his hand raised and the chalk in it, crying behind his lens-spectacles, because there was no way to write fast enough. Every second, another. There was no way to keep up.</p>\n<p>And in her chair, with her hand flat on the stone, was Gran.</p>\n<p>She was so small. She’d always been small, but she was smaller now, sunk down into the chair, her felt slippers not reaching the floor. Her face was grey. Her breathing was short and quick and shallow, and there was a blanket over her knees, and Steward Harrow was asleep in her lap. Her eyes were closed.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt by the chair.</p>\n<p>The old woman’s eyes opened. Black and bright as a bird’s. They went over her, in about a second, the way they went over everything.</p>\n<p>“Hmph,” said Perpetua Tally. “You’re back.”</p>\n<p>“I’m back.”</p>\n<p>“On your own.”</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>Gran’s eyes held hers. They didn’t ask. They didn’t need to. They held her for a long moment, and something in the old face shifted, very slightly — not surprise; Gran was never surprised — and the knotted hand came off the column and found Ione’s hand and held it.</p>\n<p>“Did he?” said Gran.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Good.” She breathed. “Good. Good lad. Good.”</p>\n<p>The Bell came up through the column. <em>Knock.</em> And again. <em>Knock.</em> Gran’s head tipped a little, listening.</p>\n<p>“Listen to it,” she said. “Listen. One a second. Hear that? One a second.” Her mouth moved; it might have been a smile. “That’s how fast they live out there. That’s their <em>time</em>, Ione. That’s the Outside’s own time, coming up through my column. We’ve been counting their night for a hundred and sixty-eight years and now we’re in it. Now we’re <em>in</em> it.” She shut her eyes. “One a second. No wonder they never get anything done.”</p>\n<p>Ione laughed. She couldn’t help it. It came out of her as a sob.</p>\n<p>“Now,” said Gran. “The box.”</p>\n<p>Tobiah brought it. The dented steel box with the Works stencil. He put it on the old woman’s knees, on top of the blanket, beside the rat, and she laid both her hands on its lid.</p>\n<p>“Ruth’s Log,” she said. “And the letter. The last one. For the girl. For Maddy.” She pushed it, very slightly, toward Ione. “You take it. Out. You take it out to her. That’s the Tollers’ job. It’s always been the Tollers’ job. Somebody from this family carries that letter to that little girl. We promised Ruth.” Her breath rattled. “And you write the last page. In the Log. You write what happened. You write it properly. Every word.”</p>\n<p>“I will.”</p>\n<p>“Not now. Later. When you can.” She opened her eyes. “You’ll know when.”</p>\n<p>“Gran⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Hush.” The knotted fingers squeezed. “Hush now. I’m listening.”</p>\n<p>They listened. The Bell came up through the column. <em>Knock.</em> The pendulums on the platforms above them trembled on their wires. <em>Knock.</em> The lamps on the ledges bowed their flames. <em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>“Go on, then,” said Perpetua Tally, very quietly, with her eyes closed. “Off you go, when it’s time. Go and see if the Outside’s all it’s cracked up to be. Take them all with you.”</p>\n<p><em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>“Tell me how it ends,” she said.</p>\n<p><em>Knock.</em></p>\n<p>And between that Bell and the next, with her granddaughter’s hand in hers and her other hand on the rat asleep in her lap, the Head Toller of Holdfast stopped breathing.</p>\n<p>Ione knelt by the chair. She didn’t move. The Bell came up through the floor, and the column, and the chair, and Gran’s still hand in hers, once a second, once a second, the way it would go on doing now for as long as anyone was there to feel it.</p>\n<p>After a while, Tobiah went to the column and made a single mark in chalk at the bottom of the last row. Not a stroke. A short line across, and a word beside it.</p>\n<p><em>PERPETUA.</em></p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Commons was full.</p>\n<p>Not with the sea of faces she’d seen from the back wall on the night the Relief came. With lamps. Thousands of them, held up, filling the great hall from wall to wall, so that the broken roof overhead was lit from below and the girders were gold and the strips of open sky between them were black — black, where the Lace had always been — and the Tollers’ banner still hung from the gantry, Seren’s line, white on black, lit by all those little flames.</p>\n<p>And they were quiet. That was what she noticed when she came in by the great doors with Osk beside her and Pim holding her hand. Twenty thousand people and more, and they were quiet. Some of them were crying. Some of them were holding each other. Here and there, someone was praying, very softly, the words of the Placard. But they were waiting. They’d been waiting, she understood, for hours. In the dark, with their lamps. The way they’d been told to.</p>\n<p><em>When the dark comes, don’t be afraid.</em></p>\n<p>The crowd saw her. She felt it go through them — a turning of heads, a stir — and they parted for her. Not stepping back from her, the way people had stepped back from the Unwatched for eleven years. Parting. Making a way. She walked down it toward the platform, and hands reached out as she went by and touched her arm, her shoulder, the rope on her shoulder, and she didn’t know what to do with it, so she let them.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane was on the platform.</p>\n<p>He was standing in front of the Placard. They’d brought it from the chapel — someone had carried it here, in its keelstone frame, and set it up on the platform on an easel, with a lamp before it. The yellowed laminate. The stick figure by its stick console. He was standing beside it in his black coat with the gold lamp on the breast, very straight, very old, holding a lamp of his own, and he watched her come down the aisle through the crowd, and when she reached the foot of the platform, by the brass rail of Muster Point B, he looked at her face.</p>\n<p>He didn’t ask either.</p>\n<p>He turned to the Placard. He lifted his lamp so that its light fell on the words. And in a clear, carrying voice — the voice that had said <em>let him finish</em> — he read it aloud.</p>\n<p>“Section Nine. In the event of containment loss. Remain at your station and await relief. Do not attempt to traverse the Lattice. Relief crews will be dispatched. You will not be forgotten.”</p>\n<p>He lowered the lamp.</p>\n<p>“Who keeps the Watch?” said Halloran Crane.</p>\n<p>The hall didn’t answer at once. Then, from somewhere at the back, raggedly — and then from everywhere, twenty thousand voices, quietly, uncertainly, the way you’d answer a question you’d answered all your life and suddenly didn’t know the meaning of:</p>\n<p>“<em>We keep the Watch.</em>”</p>\n<p>“Until?”</p>\n<p>“<em>Until Relief.</em>”</p>\n<p>“And when Relief comes?”</p>\n<p>The hall was silent.</p>\n<p>“Then,” said Halloran Crane, “we are relieved.”</p>\n<p>He set his lamp down on the platform in front of the Placard.</p>\n<p>“Relief has come,” he said. “It came up out of the Drop, as we were always told it would. It wasn’t what we were told. It was a man from the Outside, in a suit, who stood here and told us the truth, which we did not want. And then it went up the spindle with Ione Tally and closed the Lamp. The Lamp is out. The Watch is over.” He looked out at the lamps. “We are relieved. Every one of us. You are all relieved. You have kept your station for a hundred and sixty-eight years and you may leave it now.”</p>\n<p>The sound the hall made was not a cheer. It was not a groan. She’d never heard it before and she never heard it again. It was the sound, she thought afterwards, that a great many people make when they have been holding something up for a very long time, and are told they can put it down.</p>\n<p>Crane waited for it to pass.</p>\n<p>“There’s one more thing,” he said, “and then I’ll be done.”</p>\n<p>He looked down at her. At the foot of the platform, by the brass rail. He held her eyes.</p>\n<p>“Thirteen years ago,” said Halloran Crane, “Seren Tally painted the truth on the wall of this hall, and I gave her to the Drop for it. I had the order drawn up before she painted it. She was right, and I knew she was right, and I did it anyway, because I thought your calm was worth more than her life. I was wrong.” His voice didn’t shake at all. “I have nothing to offer her daughter. I’m not asking her for anything. I only wanted you all to hear me say it, once, in here, where she said it first.”</p>\n<p>The hall was very quiet.</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at him. At the old man on the platform, in the lamplight, beside the Placard.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this. For thirteen years. She’d thought about what she would say to him, if she ever had the chance, and it had been different every time — screams, curses, a knife, silence. She’d thought she would feel something enormous.</p>\n<p>She found she felt tired. Very tired. And the Bell was coming up through the floor once a second, and her Gran was dead in her chair in the Tower, and somewhere above her in the dark, once, a man was looking at her light.</p>\n<p>“You’ll walk out last,” she said.</p>\n<p>Crane looked at her.</p>\n<p>“When they go down,” said Ione. “All of them. You’ll stand at the top of the wells and see every one of them onto the ladders. Every single one. The children and the old ones and the Wardens and the ones who called my mother a heretic and the ones who spat at me in the lanes. Every one. And when they’ve all gone down, you’ll go down last.”</p>\n<p>It wasn’t forgiveness. She knew that. She didn’t know what it was. Something drier. Something that would do.</p>\n<p>Halloran Crane bowed his head.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” he said. “I will.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They went down the Drop.</p>\n<p>It took all night — or what would have been night; there were no nights now, and no days, only the dark and the lamps and the Bell. The riggers re-hung the ladders at every one of the fourteen wells, the ladders they’d cut free and hauled up on Kindling Round. They strung ropes down beside them, and lamp-lines, clay lamps hung on wire every ten metres all the way down the shafts, so that looking down a well was like looking down a throat lined with little fires. And Holdfast went down them.</p>\n<p>Family by family. Street by street. Wardens at the tops of the ladders, riggers on the ropes, Tollers with their ledgers counting names. Children on their parents’ backs. Old people lowered in rope slings, three riggers to each. Pim’s rats in a wire cage lashed to his shoulders, all of them, the whole Stewardship, squeaking. Bundles and bags and bedrolls. Lamps. Everyone had a lamp. The Tanks, the market, the Commons, the Crèche, all left behind, empty, lit, in the dark.</p>\n<p>People sang, going down. Not the Tending. She listened for it, and didn’t hear it once. They sang the riggers’ song, the hauling song, the one with no words but <em>haul</em> — a deep slow rhythm, one voice calling, all the voices answering, going down the lines of ladders into the dark. <em>Haul. Haul. Haul.</em> And under it, through the rungs, through the keelstone, once a second, the Bell.</p>\n<p>Ione went down Fourteen.</p>\n<p>She went first. Before anyone. Osk tried to stop her, and then didn’t. It was the well she’d pulled him out of. It was the ladder he’d come up. It was the well her mother had gone down, thirteen years ago, and not looked back. She went down it in her rigger’s clothes, with his suit strapped to a rope and lowered beside her — Osk had insisted; the Relief’s suit didn’t get left — and the dented steel box with Ruth’s Log in it lashed to her back, and her lamp hanging from her harness.</p>\n<p>Three hundred metres through the Anvil. Rung after rung, in the lamp-light. It was only a ladder. It was so easy in the ordinary weight that she kept thinking she’d missed something.</p>\n<p>Then the bottom of the Anvil. And below it, the place where the Shear had been.</p>\n<p>She stopped on the ladder, there, and hung on it, and looked.</p>\n<p>Nothing. A shaft. Round, black, ribbed with old pipe-brackets. A ladder going down it. The air was still. There was no curtain, no trembling, no wrongness. Nothing jumped or smeared. She could see straight down the shaft forty metres, fifty, sixty, to the bottom, where there was a glow — a light, a lamp, not hers — and a round window, lit.</p>\n<p>She went down through the place where the Shear had been, and felt nothing at all.</p>\n<p>At the bottom of the conduit, at the foot of the ladder, there was a shape covered in foil.</p>\n<p>It lay crumpled against the wall. Someone had covered it — carefully, tenderly, from the head down, the silver foil tucked round it at the edges — with a blanket from a first-aid kit. The foil crackled a little in the air from the shaft. It shone in her lamp’s light.</p>\n<p>Ione stood at the foot of the ladder and looked at it.</p>\n<p>She knew. She’d known since the Tower, the first night, when he’d said <em>there was someone at the bottom of the conduit, in mended clothes, with a lamp carved from a hard hat</em>, and Gran had made that sound like a bird. She’d known. She knelt down beside it on the floor of the conduit and put her lamp down.</p>\n<p>She lifted the edge of the foil. Just a little. Just at the neck.</p>\n<p>There was a cord there. Twisted fibre, algae-thread, the kind Holdfast made. And on it, lying against the patched cloth, a little yellow lamp. Carved from the plastic of an old hard hat. Worn smooth and soft as soap. For a crib.</p>\n<p>She didn’t lift the foil any further. She didn’t need to.</p>\n<p>“Hello, Mam,” said Ione Tally.</p>\n<p>She put her hand flat on the foil, over where her mother’s heart would have been. She knelt there for a long time. The Bell came up through the floor, once a second.</p>\n<p>“I found the edge of the world,” she said. “It wasn’t what I thought. I went up to find you. You weren’t there. I was, though.” She was quiet. “You were right. About the line. About all of it. Everybody knows now. It’s on a banner in the Commons. Crane said it. In front of everyone.” She was quiet again. “Gran’s gone. Just now. She was listening to the Bell. It goes once a second now, Mam. Like a heart. You’d have liked that. You’d have wanted to count it.”</p>\n<p>She tucked the foil back round the little yellow lamp.</p>\n<p>“Somebody covered you up,” she said. “A lady. From Outside. She couldn’t stand you lying there. She didn’t know who you were. She did it anyway.”</p>\n<p>And behind her, from the lit window in the wall of the conduit, from the booth beyond it, there was a sound. A hatch opening. Boots on a ladder, coming down, slow and stiff, as if they’d been sitting for a long time.</p>\n<p>Ione stood up and turned round.</p>\n<p>A woman was coming down the hatch-ladder from the booth into the conduit. A broad, weathered woman in her fifties, in a heavy grey suit with its helmet off and nothing on its back where a keel should have been, with cropped iron-grey hair and a face like a well-used tool. She had a torch in one hand. She reached the bottom and turned and put the torch on Ione, and stood there looking at her. At her face. At the white streak in her hair. At the rope on her shoulder, and the box on her back, and the empty grey suit hanging on its rope from the ladder above her.</p>\n<p>Her face did something complicated.</p>\n<p>“You must be Ione,” said Teo Lisk. Her voice was hoarse. “He never shut up about you.”</p>"}, {"file": "20-stars.md", "label": "Chapter 19", "title": "Stars", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 5270, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>They’re always there. You just can’t see them.</p><span class=\"attr\">Nell Hale, aged ten, on the telephone</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The edge of the lee was half a metre from Teo Lisk’s boots when it stopped existing.</p>\n<p>She’d been watching it come for — she didn’t know. A long time. Two minutes, maybe, by the suit clock, which were the longest two minutes of her life, and she’d had some long ones. The shimmer on the floor, a hand’s height off the ground, like heat over a road. Crawling. The dust on the far side of it drifting, and the dust on her side lying still, and the line between them coming across the floor of Lattice Station 14-C toward the corner where she sat with her boots flat against the wall. A metre and a half. A metre. She’d stopped moving the chair, because there was nowhere left to move it. She’d folded her hands in her lap. She’d said <em>come on, Hale</em> a few more times, not out loud.</p>\n<p>Three-quarters of a metre. Half a metre.</p>\n<p>And then it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p>It didn’t go away. It didn’t retreat. It was there, a shimmer, a line of wrongness crawling toward her boots — and then, between one breath and the next, there was nothing on the floor but floor. Dust, lying flat. All of it. On both sides.</p>\n<p>The room settled.</p>\n<p>She felt it. She couldn’t have said how. It was like the moment when a ship that’s been rolling in a swell comes into harbour and the deck goes still under you — not a movement, the end of a movement she hadn’t known was there. The air stopped being wrong. The floor stopped being a floor that might tilt. The whole room simply became a room, the way rooms were, and she was a woman sitting in a chair in the corner of it, and she weighed what she weighed.</p>\n<p>She sat there without moving.</p>\n<p>Then she got up, slowly, and went to the viewport.</p>\n<p>The Shear was gone. The trembling curtain across the conduit, forty metres up — gone. The ladder went up the shaft straight as a ruler, and she could see every rung, clean, not jumping, not smearing. It went up and up into the dark. And at the top — where there had always been, since she’d sat down in this chair, a disc of pale hard violet-white light, far up the shaft, too bright to look at for long⁠—</p>\n<p>Nothing. Dark.</p>\n<p>She stood with her hand flat on the glass. The glass with the names on it in black marker. MATTIAS. JOSS. BEN. SEREN (THE LADY). CAS.</p>\n<p>“Oh, Hale,” she said. “Oh, you did it. You bloody did it.”</p>\n<p>And then, because she was alone, and because nobody was ever going to know, she put her forehead against the glass and cried until she couldn’t stand up, and sat down on the floor under the viewport with her back against the wall, and cried some more.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The hardline rang eleven minutes later.</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk.” Gaunt. But not Gaunt’s voice. Or Gaunt’s voice with something gone out of it — the softness, the patience, the lent-you-a-book. “Major. Are you — are you there?”</p>\n<p>“I’m here.”</p>\n<p>“Something’s happened.” A pause. She heard him breathe. “The shimmer over the Pit. It’s gone. It was there, and it’s — gone, just now, all at once, the birds went straight over it, a whole flock of them — and the clocks at the cordon have stopped. Not stopped running. Stopped running <em>fast</em>. They’re — they match. For the first time since two in the morning yesterday, they match.” Another breath. “And the output’s gone to zero. Not the fake zero. Real zero. The Draw’s — there’s nothing coming out of it. Nothing at all. It’s as if it isn’t there.” He stopped. “Major, what’s happened?”</p>\n<p>She looked at the dark disc at the top of the shaft.</p>\n<p>“He did it,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Did — reseated Six?”</p>\n<p>“No.” She found she didn’t care any more. She found she’d never cared. “No, Colonel. He never reseated anything. There was nothing to reseat. He went up to the Crown and he shut it. The Lamp. The Lesion. The whole thing. He found a woman with a key and they went up and shut it for good.” She wiped her face with the back of her hand. “It’s not coming back. There’s no more Draw. It’s done. The debt’s done. You’ll want to tell the Minister. You’ll want to tell her he defaulted.”</p>\n<p>The line was silent.</p>\n<p>“The grid,” said Gaunt at last. “Kovač was at half. Without anything from the Draw at all⁠—”</p>\n<p>“Then it’ll go dark.”</p>\n<p>“Six hundred million people.”</p>\n<p>“Then it’ll go dark, Colonel.” She leaned her head back against the wall. “It’ll go dark and they’ll be alive in it. That’s the trade. That’s the whole trade. Tell them to light a candle.”</p>\n<p>Another silence. And then, to her enormous surprise, a sound down the line that might have been a laugh. A small, broken, astonished sound.</p>\n<p>“Is Dr Hale⁠—” Gaunt began.</p>\n<p>“I don’t know,” said Teo. “I don’t know yet. I’m waiting.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Civil Defence teams came four hours later.</p>\n<p>She heard them long before she saw them: voices echoing down the galleries of the Lattice, boots on steel, the crackle of radios, somebody swearing at a door. They came in through the booth’s outer door with their lamps and their ropes and their stretchers — a sergeant she knew from the Kovač fire, Amara Kade, and eight others — and found her sitting in the chair by the viewport with her boots up on the dead monitors, eating the last of the ration paste out of the suit’s tube with her finger.</p>\n<p>“Major,” said Kade. She was staring. “Major, Christ. We’ve been — they said you’d — the Works is <em>walkable</em>, Major, there’s no weather, there’s nothing, it’s just a building now, we came straight in down Radial Nine like it was a Tuesday. We found the stairhead. And the — the algae hall. There’s writing on the glass⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I know.”</p>\n<p>“We’ve got a stretcher. We’ll have you out in two hours. There’s water, there’s⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I’m not going.”</p>\n<p>Kade stopped.</p>\n<p>“I’m waiting for someone,” said Teo. “Leave me some water. And a lamp. And a blanket; I gave mine away. And then go back up to the Collar and tell them to send everything they’ve got down here. Everything. Every team, every stretcher, every lamp, every blanket in Averna.”</p>\n<p>“Major, the Colonel said⁠—”</p>\n<p>“The Colonel,” said Teo Lisk, “can come down here and carry me out himself.” She finished the paste. “Go on. Go. And Kade — leave the hardline to me.”</p>\n<p>They went, in the end. Kade stayed, and two others, sitting on the floor of the booth by the door with their lamps, not asking. Teo sat in the chair.</p>\n<p>She went down through the hatch once, and sat beside the lady in the foil blanket for a while. Not talking. Just sitting. The Bell came up through the floor — the hammer, the old Dead Man’s Hammer, still going, once a second, the same as it had been going since two in the morning yesterday, the same as it had gone through the car and the canteen and the Collar. Only here, now, at the bottom of the conduit, it came up through the floor exactly as it did everywhere else. No longer stretched. No longer a groan. A knock. Once a second.</p>\n<p><em>Ordinary</em>, she thought.</p>\n<p>She dozed, in the chair, at some point. She dreamed of the flood in the Tessarine underpass. She dreamed of toast.</p>\n<p>She woke to singing.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It came down the shaft.</p>\n<p>Faint, at first. So faint that she thought she was still dreaming. A deep, slow, rhythmic sound, far up the conduit, echoing down the round black walls. One voice, calling. Then many voices, answering. A word. The same word, over and over.</p>\n<p><em>Haul.</em></p>\n<p>She was at the viewport before she knew she’d moved. Kade and the others were on their feet behind her.</p>\n<p>Far up the shaft, in the dark — lights. Little lights. Yellow, warm, flickering. Not a torch. Flames. A line of them, coming down the shaft, one below another, strung down the side of the conduit like beads on a thread. Lamps. Somebody was hanging lamps down the shaft, one every few metres, all the way down. And beside the lamps, on the ladder⁠—</p>\n<p>Something grey.</p>\n<p>Her heart went. She saw it and her whole body went toward the glass, her hands flat on it. A grey shape, hulking, heavy, coming down the shaft. The suit. His suit. Grey plates, the housing on its back. It was coming down. It was coming down the shaft toward her and she was already moving to the hatch, she was already saying his name⁠—</p>\n<p>It turned on its rope.</p>\n<p>It was on a rope. It hung from a rope, and it was being lowered, slowly, beside the ladder, turning gently as it came, like a coat on a hanger. Its visor was dark. Its arms hung. There was nobody in it.</p>\n<p>She stopped with her hand on the hatch.</p>\n<p>Beside it, on the ladder, climbing down, was a woman.</p>\n<p>Teo watched her come. She watched her all the way down — through the place where the Shear had been, and past it, and on down, rung after rung, fast and neat and steady, a lamp swinging from her harness and a dented steel box on her back. Short. Broad. A rope on her shoulder. At the bottom she stepped off the ladder and stood for a moment in her lamp’s light, and then turned and knelt by the lady in the foil blanket.</p>\n<p>Teo couldn’t hear what she said. She saw her lift the edge of the foil, at the neck. She saw her put her hand flat on the blanket, over the heart. She saw her stay like that for a long time.</p>\n<p>She saw the white streak in her hair.</p>\n<p>She knew. She’d known since the first chirp. <em>There’s a woman.</em> After that, in nearly every one. A rigger. A key. A way of looking at you as if she’d read the end of the book. Teo had pictured her so often, sitting in this chair, that she almost didn’t need to look.</p>\n<p>She opened the hatch and went down the ladder.</p>\n<p>The woman stood up and turned round. Teo put the torch on her. She was younger than Teo had thought. Or older. It was hard to tell. She had a cut over one eye with black stitches in it, and a splinted wrist, and a face that had been crying and had stopped, and grey-green eyes that looked back at Teo — at her suit, at her face, at her hands — without any hurry at all. As if she’d seen her before. As if she’d been expecting her.</p>\n<p>“You must be Ione,” said Teo. “He never shut up about you.”</p>\n<p>The woman looked at her for a long moment.</p>\n<p>“And you’re Teo,” she said. Her accent was strange — flat, clipped. “You’re the one in the chair.”</p>\n<p>“I’m the one in the chair.”</p>\n<p>“You covered my mother.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked down at the foil blanket. At the little yellow lamp she’d tucked under it, all those hours ago, that had been a few minutes ago, that had been — she didn’t know. She looked back at the woman.</p>\n<p>“I didn’t know who she was,” she said.</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione Tally. “You did it anyway.”</p>\n<p>They stood there, in the conduit, in the lamplight.</p>\n<p>“He’s not coming,” said Teo. It wasn’t a question.</p>\n<p>“No.” Ione didn’t look away. “He’s up there. In the Crown. He’s the witness. It needed someone to hold it until it closed, and whoever held it would be — in it. When it closed. For ever. Once.” She said it very plainly. “He’s in it. It’s a single instant. He’s in one instant, and it doesn’t end.”</p>\n<p>Teo stood without moving.</p>\n<p>She thought about the booth. About a man kneeling in front of her chair with his broken keel whining and a look on his face she’d seen on men on bridges. <em>It seemed tidy.</em></p>\n<p>“Did he want to?” she said.</p>\n<p>Ione looked at her. And something in her face changed — the patience went out of it, and something younger came in, and her eyes filled.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” she said. “He said — he said it was the only thing he’d ever wanted that he was sure of. He said he wasn’t paying anything. He said he wanted the moment.” She drew a breath. “He said you’d ask. He said, <em>Teo’ll ask if I wanted to</em>. He said to tell you yes. He said to tell you it wasn’t guilt.”</p>\n<p>Teo Lisk put her hand over her mouth.</p>\n<p>She stood there, in the conduit, with the lamps hanging down the shaft above them and the singing coming down, <em>haul, haul, haul</em>, and the Bell coming up through the floor, and she held her hand over her mouth for a long time. When she took it away, she was crying, and she didn’t care.</p>\n<p>“Then that’s all right,” she said. “Then that’s all right. The daft sod. Then it’s all right.”</p>\n<p>And the woman from the top of the world stepped forward and put her arms round her, suit and all, and held on. Like a rigger. Like somebody who hadn’t been touched for a long time and had recently remembered how.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Teo took the hardline off its hook with a hand that wasn’t quite steady.</p>\n<p>“Collar. This is Lisk. Fourteen-C. Get me Colonel Gaunt.” A pause. “Colonel. Listen to me very carefully, because I’m only going to say this once and then I’m going to be busy. I want every team you’ve got. I want every stretcher and every lamp and every length of rope in the Commonweal sent down Radial Nine. I want water. Tankers of it. I want food, anything, whatever you’ve got. I want doctors. And blankets. Bring blankets. Bring a <em>lot</em> of blankets.”</p>\n<p>A pause on the line.</p>\n<p>“For how many?” said Gaunt.</p>\n<p>Teo looked up the shaft. At the lamps, strung down it like beads on a thread. At the ladder. At the first of them coming down it now, behind Ione — a huge man in a long grey coat and a battered hard hat, carrying a little boy on his back; and behind him a woman with a baby in a sling; and behind her an old man in a rope harness, being lowered, three short broad figures guiding his ropes from above; and behind them, more, and more, faces in the lamplight, short and pale and broad and heavy-boned, all the way up the shaft, as far as she could see, singing.</p>\n<p>“Thirty-one thousand,” said Teo Lisk. “Give or take a rat.”</p>\n<p>The line was silent for a very long time.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>They walked out through the Works in the dark.</p>\n<p>It took until dawn. Longer — it took all the next day, and into the night after, for the last of them — but the first of them walked out before dawn, and Ione walked with them.</p>\n<p>The Works was a building now. That was the thing that astonished the Civil Defence crews most, the ones who’d been waiting at the Collar for two days listening to the hammer: the Works was just a building. No weather. No gusts, no shears, no walls becoming floors. The filaments were gone from the halls. The corridors were long and dark and dripping and smelled of algae and ozone and old oil, and they went on for kilometres, and you walked down them, and that was all. Civil Defence had strung lamps along Radial Nine all the way from the Lattice to Gate Nine, and roped the stairs, and posted people at every turning, and Holdfast walked down it in a long, long line with its lamps and its bundles and its children and its rats, singing, and the hammer coming up through the floor once a second.</p>\n<p>Ione walked beside Teo.</p>\n<p>Teo told her things, as they walked. About the car and the Collar and the crisis room. About Ben, on the flatbed, hugging his cradle. About the canteen and the two-percent napkin. About the phone call to a gym on Tier Thirty. Ione listened. She didn’t say much. She looked at everything.</p>\n<p>At the stairhead where the four corridors met, the wall was hung with cloths: a Civil Defence jacket, a tarpaulin, high-vis vests, a dust sheet. People looked at them as they went by and didn’t ask. Teo didn’t explain.</p>\n<p>At the algae hall, Ione stopped.</p>\n<p>The gallery was on Radial Nine; the line of Holdfast walked right past its stair, and she went up it, and Teo went with her, and they stood at the long window looking down into B-7. It was a ruin. The tanks were dry and cracked and grey. The dust lay over everything like snow. On the catwalk, right against the glass, there was a heap of it, and a grey rag, and a scatter of small bones, and a stubby yellow grease pencil lying in the dust.</p>\n<p>And on the glass, faded, cracked, chalky, the writing.</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">THANK YOU FOR STAYING<br>IT WAS LONG ENOUGH</div>\n<p>Ione read it twice.</p>\n<p>“That’s him,” she said. “Ben.”</p>\n<p>“That’s Ben.”</p>\n<p>“He found the seam,” said Ione. “In the Carousel. His map. Cas said. We’d have been in there for ever without him.” She put her hand flat on the glass, beside the words. On the other side of it, a centimetre away, in the dust, the grease pencil. “He got us out.”</p>\n<p>Teo couldn’t answer.</p>\n<p>“<em>It was long enough</em>,” said Ione. And stood there, with her hand on the glass, reading it again.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Gate Nine stood open.</p>\n<p>The great hazard-striped door, five metres high, rolled all the way back. And beyond it, the Collar — the long evacuated corridors, every door open, every screen dark now, the MARGIN IRREGULARITY: REMAIN CALM gone with the power. Lamps along the floors. Civil Defence at every junction, with water and blankets and quiet voices, and doctors in masks at tables by the doors, and somewhere at the back, in charcoal overcoats, the Calm, standing very still and watching and not doing anything, because there was nothing, any more, that they could do.</p>\n<p>And beyond the Collar, the outer doors. And beyond those⁠—</p>\n<p>She walked out of the Draw at twenty minutes past four in the morning, two days after Continuance Day, into the Pit.</p>\n<p>Wind.</p>\n<p>It hit her in the face the moment she came through the doors. She stopped dead. It was cold — colder than anything had ever been in Holdfast — and it <em>moved</em>. It came at her from nowhere, from the dark, and pushed at her, and went round her, and tugged at her clothes and her hair and the rope on her shoulder, and went on, and came back. It smelled of — she didn’t know. Of nothing she had a word for. Of water, and stone, and distance.</p>\n<p>“That’s wind,” said Teo beside her.</p>\n<p>“I know,” said Ione. She didn’t know. He’d told her about it. It wasn’t like being told.</p>\n<p>Around her, Holdfast was coming out of the doors, and stopping, the way she’d stopped. Short broad people in patchwork clothes, holding their lamps, standing on the concrete apron in front of the Collar with their faces turned into the wind, their hair blowing, their mouths open. A child laughed — a high, startled, delighted laugh — and then another, and she looked, and they were jumping. The children. Jumping up and down on the concrete, because in the ordinary weight their Holdfast legs threw them into the air so high, higher than they’d ever jumped in their lives, and they were shrieking with it. Pim was jumping. Pim, with a cage of rats on his back, was leaping into the air like a flea and coming down and leaping again and screaming with laughter.</p>\n<p>And there were people on the rim.</p>\n<p>All round the Pit, on the rim road, high above them, she could see lights. Hundreds of lights. Torches, lanterns, the headlamps of vehicles. People. Standing along the edge of the crater in the dark, thousands of them, looking down. At the Draw. At the doors. At the long line of little flames coming out of it.</p>\n<p>Because the city behind them was dark.</p>\n<p>She looked past them. Past the rim, past the lights. At Averna. At the Outside. At the thing she’d been told about all her life and never once believed was there.</p>\n<p>It was a shape. A vast black shape against a slightly lesser black — the Stacks, rising in their terraced ranks, a hundred and forty levels, tier on tier on tier, going up and up and away into the distance on every side like mountains. And they were dark. Every one. Not a light in them. Here and there a tiny flicker — a candle in a window, a torch on a terrace, a fire — and otherwise, nothing. The great lanterns of the Stacks, that Cas had told her about, that had never gone out in ninety-one years, were out. The Canopy was out. The Draw was gone, and Averna was dark, the whole of it, all six hundred million, standing on their terraces and their balconies and their rooftops in the cold, in the wind, in the dark.</p>\n<p>Looking up.</p>\n<p>She looked up.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She didn’t have words for it. She’d have them later — she’d find some, the way you find words for anything, eventually, by living with it — but she didn’t have them then, standing on the concrete apron of the Pit at twenty past four in the morning with the wind in her face.</p>\n<p>There were so many.</p>\n<p>That was the first thing. He’d told her. <em>Thousands of them.</em> She’d thought she understood. She’d thought: a thousand is the market on a busy round; a thousand is a few rows of the Tollers’ column. She hadn’t understood. The sky was full of them. The whole sky. It went up from the black edge of the Stacks on every side, black, blacker than the dark in the Vault after the Lamp went out, a black that went on and on and didn’t stop — and it was full of lights. Small ones. Tiny. Points. Some so bright they seemed to shiver. Some so faint she could only see them when she looked beside them instead of at them. White, mostly. Some blue. Some — yes — a little red, the way he’d said. Scattered across the whole of the black in no pattern she could see and every pattern she could imagine. Crowded, in places, so thick they ran together into a sort of mist, a pale river of light going right across the sky from one edge of the dark to the other.</p>\n<p>They didn’t move. They didn’t sing. They didn’t hum. They didn’t reach down toward her in long bright threads. They didn’t do anything. They were just there.</p>\n<p>Her knees went.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the concrete. She didn’t decide to. She just found she was sitting, with her lamp beside her and her hands flat on the cold ground and her head tipped back, looking up. And around her, she saw, Holdfast was doing the same. All along the apron, people were sitting down. Lying down. Lying on their backs on the concrete with their lamps beside them, looking up, the children who’d been jumping lying flat now with their arms spread out, silent. Osk was sitting with his hard hat in his lap and his mouth open. Pim had lain down on his back beside her with the cage of rats on his chest and was staring up with his eyes so wide they shone.</p>\n<p>Nobody spoke. Thirty-one thousand people came out of the Draw into the wind, one after another, all that night and all the next day, and every one of them, when they came out of the doors and looked up, stopped. And a lot of them sat down.</p>\n<p>And up on the rim — she saw, when she could look at anything but the sky — the people of Averna were doing it too. Standing along the edge of the Pit with their torches, their heads tipped back. Six hundred million of them, on their terraces and their balconies and their rooftops, in the dark, in the cold, looking up at a sky that no one in the city under sixty had ever seen. The same sky. At the same time. All of them together.</p>\n<p>She looked for one.</p>\n<p>She sat on the concrete and looked up at the thousands of them, and looked, and looked. A bright one. Not the brightest; there was one very bright one, low down, white and fierce and shivering, and she thought that must be the one he’d called the Dog Star, and it wasn’t his. She wanted one that was a little on its own. One that didn’t have anything else bright near it. She looked for a long time.</p>\n<p>There. Low in the south, over the black edge of the Stacks. A single star, quite bright, a little golden, sitting by itself in a dark patch of sky with nothing near it at all. Alone. Steady. Just there.</p>\n<p><em>Fomalhaut</em>, he’d said, in the gallery, while she sat against the algae trough with her head on her knees. <em>Which is lonely. Which sits by itself.</em></p>\n<p>She didn’t know if that was it. She’d ask Nell. She’d find out. It didn’t matter.</p>\n<p>“That one,” she said, out loud, to him, in case. “That one’s yours.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The Calm came, eventually. Of course they did. They came across the apron in their charcoal overcoats with their tablets, which didn’t work, and their lists, which were paper now, and a tall silver-haired woman in a dark suit walking in front of them, very straight. And they began to talk about processing, and registration, and screening, and quarantine — which was fair, Teo said afterwards; these people had been sealed off from every germ in the world for a hundred and sixty-eight years, and a cold would kill half of them — and relocation for comfort.</p>\n<p>The silver-haired woman stopped in front of Ione, where she sat on the concrete.</p>\n<p>“I’m the Minister of Continuance,” she said. “Ondine Rask. I’m told you — came out first. That you were with Dr Hale.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at her from the ground. At her very straight back and her crease-less suit and her face, which was grey with exhaustion and entirely closed, like a door.</p>\n<p>She thought of a man with silver hair, fifteen storeys high on the side of a scaffold tower, holding up a key.</p>\n<p>“Your deputy,” she said, “ordered the test.”</p>\n<p>The Minister went quite still.</p>\n<p>“Aldous Crane,” said Ione. “He came up to the Crown with a camera crew and a key round his neck, and he told Ruth Tally to take the interlocks off, because you wanted a round number for Continuance Day. She said no. He said her daughter’s name. She said yes.” Her voice was very calm. “Then Six went, and she asked him for the key, and he wouldn’t give it to her. He held it. And it fell. And nineteen hundred people spent a hundred and sixty-eight years in there because of it.” She looked up at Rask with her grey-green eyes. “He’s been dead a hundred and thirty years. He wrote it down, before he died. I can show you. And Ruth Tally’s log. It’s in this box.”</p>\n<p>“How⁠—” said Rask. Her voice had gone thin. “How can you <em>possibly</em>⁠—”</p>\n<p>“I watched him do it,” said Ione Tally. “A hundred and seventy-five thousand times.”</p>\n<p>Rask stared at her.</p>\n<p>“Who <em>are</em> you?” she said.</p>\n<p>Ione thought about it. She thought about it seriously, the way she thought about every question, turning it over. She looked at the dark city, and the thousands of lamps coming out of the doors behind her, and the sky.</p>\n<p>“I’m the night shift,” she said.</p>\n<p>Behind Rask, a little apart from the overcoats, a soft, older man in a brown cardigan buttoned wrong had stopped. He had a paper bag in his hand. He was looking at Ione, and at Teo, sitting beside her on the concrete, and at the dented steel box on the ground between them. His face in the lamplight was very tired, and very strange — as if something had broken in it, and something else, underneath, were trying to come out.</p>\n<p>“Major Lisk,” said Ansel Gaunt quietly. “Is Dr Hale⁠—”</p>\n<p>“He’s in the Crown,” said Teo. “He’s staying.”</p>\n<p>Gaunt looked at her. Then he looked at the box.</p>\n<p>“I think,” he said at last, slowly, like a man trying out words in a language he hasn’t spoken since childhood, “that I should like to hear about it. All of it. Properly.” He paused. “On the record.”</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>The sky began to change a little after six.</p>\n<p>It went grey at the edge first, low in the east, behind the dark Stacks. Just a little. A paleness. And then more — a grey going to blue, going up the sky, pushing the black back. And the stars in that part of the sky began, one by one, to go out.</p>\n<p>Ione saw it happen. She was still sitting on the concrete, wrapped in a Civil Defence blanket, with Teo beside her in another and Pim asleep across both their laps with his rats. She’d been watching the lonely one in the south. And she looked up and saw the stars in the east going — fading, thinning, gone — and she sat up so fast that Pim woke with a yelp.</p>\n<p>“They’re going out,” she said.</p>\n<p>Her voice was high. She heard it and couldn’t stop it. The stars over the Stacks were going out, one after another, like the lamps in the lanes when the oil runs down, and the sky was going pale, and she was on her knees on the concrete with the blanket falling off her shoulders, and she couldn’t breathe.</p>\n<p>“They’re going out, Teo — they’re <em>going out</em> —”</p>\n<p>“Hey,” said Teo. “Hey. Hey. No. Look at me.”</p>\n<p>She looked at her.</p>\n<p>“It’s the sun,” said Teo Lisk. “It’s just the sun coming up. It’s morning. That’s all.” She put her hand on Ione’s arm. “They don’t go out. They’re still there. You just can’t see them in the day. They come back.”</p>\n<p>Ione stared at her.</p>\n<p>“They come back,” said Teo again. “Every night. When it gets dark. They come back.”</p>\n<p>“Every night?”</p>\n<p>“Every night.”</p>\n<p>Ione looked at the sky. At the grey turning to blue, and the stars fading out of it, and the lonely one, low in the south, still there, just barely, going faint, going. Every night. They’d go, and they’d come back. And go. And come back. For ever. The same, and never the same. The good kind.</p>\n<p>She sat back down on the concrete. She pulled the blanket back round her shoulders. She watched the lonely star go out, and the sky go blue, and the first light come up over the black shoulders of the Stacks and touch the rim of the Pit, and the faces of the people standing on it, and the long line of lamps still coming out of the doors of the Draw behind her.</p>\n<p>“Every night,” said Ione Tally, to him. “Did you hear that? They come back.”</p>"}, {"file": "21-epilogue-net-zero.md", "label": "Epilogue", "title": "Net Zero", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 4834, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>Tell me how it ends.</p><span class=\"attr\">Perpetua Tally, Head Toller of Holdfast, last words</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">The hammer stopped on the fourth day.</p>\n<p>Civil Defence found the flywheel on the third, in a vault in the Undercroft below the Lattice, still spinning — a disc of iron the size of a house, turning in the dark on its great bearing, driving its hammer down onto its keelstone anvil once a second the way Iris Hollowell had built it to, the way it had since 02:14 on the night of the Kindling. It took them a day to work out how to stop it. Nobody had ever designed a way. <em>When all else has failed</em>, Hollowell had written, <em>the station will say so</em>, and she had not imagined anyone wanting it to stop saying so.</p>\n<p>They stopped it at nine minutes past eleven on the fourth morning.</p>\n<p>In the Relief Camp on the Tallows flats, where thirty-one thousand people were sleeping in tents and prefabricated huts and the gutted shells of warehouses behind a fence of Civil Defence tape, it was the first silence in the history of Holdfast. People came out of the tents. They stood in the mud in the rain and looked at each other. Children cried. Old people sat down on the ground. For three nights afterwards, half the camp couldn’t sleep.</p>\n<p>The Tollers solved it. Tobiah, mostly. On the fifth night he sat down at a table in the Tollers’ hut with a wooden spoon and began, very quietly, to tap. Once a second. On the tabletop. And the Tollers in the hut, and then the people in the tents round it, lay in the dark and listened, and slept.</p>\n<p>They took it in turns after that. Every night, in the Relief Camp, somebody sat up and tapped. They were still doing it when Ione left. For all she knew, they were doing it still.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It rained.</p>\n<p>The Canopy was gone — its generators had died with the grid on the second day, and there was no Draw now to bring them back — and the autumn monsoon came in off the southern ocean for the first time in sixty years without anything to stop it, and it rained on Averna as it had not rained in living memory. It came down so hard it bounced. It ran off the black terraces of the Stacks in sheets and poured down the dead escalators and filled the Line tunnels and flooded the lower tiers, and Civil Defence went into the lower tiers with ropes and rubber boats, and Teo Lisk went with them, and so did Ione.</p>\n<p>She stood in it the first time for a whole afternoon.</p>\n<p>She stood in the mud outside the Tollers’ hut with her face turned up and her mouth open and let it come down on her. It was warm. It came down so hard it stung. She drank it. It tasted of nothing, mostly, and sometimes of dust, and sometimes, when it ran off the roof of the hut, of metal. Exactly the way he’d said.</p>\n<p>“You’ll catch your death,” said Teo, from the door.</p>\n<p>“I’ve never been wet,” said Ione. “Not like this. Not from above.”</p>\n<p>Teo looked at her for a while. Then she came out and stood in it too.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>There were colds.</p>\n<p>Nobody in Holdfast had had one for a hundred and sixty-eight years. Nobody had had anything that wasn’t already in the Vault when the Shear formed. The Commonweal’s doctors knew it would happen, and screened them and quarantined them and vaccinated them against everything they could think of, and it happened anyway. Coughs. Fevers. Things that Outside children shrugged off in a week went through the Relief Camp like fire through dry grass. Eleven people died of them in the first month. Most of them were old. Two of them were babies.</p>\n<p>One of the nurses who came to the camp in the second week was a slight, dark, quiet man of about thirty with very tired eyes, and on his third day Ione found him sitting on an upturned crate behind the medical tent in the rain, not doing anything, with a young dog asleep across his feet. A scruffy brown dog, all legs, with one ear up and one ear down.</p>\n<p>She knew who he was before she asked. Teo had told her. Teo had gone to his flat on Tier Twelve on the fifth day with a box of things from a locker in the Tallow Works lab, and come back without the box and with a very strange expression, and when Ione asked her about it, said only: <em>I bought him the bloody dog.</em></p>\n<p>“You’re Jonah,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>He looked up.</p>\n<p>She sat down on the ground beside the crate, in the rain, and told him about the glass.</p>\n<p>She told him all of it. The way Teo had told it to her, walking out through the Works, and the way she’d seen it herself, standing at the long window of B-7 with her hand on the glass. The hall. The algae. The beard. The rat called Minister. MINISTER II. <em>I HAVE HAD 8 BIRTHDAYS NOBODY CAME</em>, and Teo writing back <em>WE’RE HERE</em>. The map, and how it had got them out of the Carousel. <em>TELL JONAH. TELL HIM I SAID YES TO THE DOG.</em></p>\n<p>“He said,” said Ione, “<em>had a good life. Mostly algae.</em>”</p>\n<p>Jonah laughed.</p>\n<p>It came out of him suddenly, a real laugh, shocked, surprised, the way a laugh comes when you’re not expecting one, and he put his hand over his mouth, and laughed again — and then he wasn’t laughing. He sat on the crate in the rain with his hand over his mouth and his shoulders shaking, and the dog woke up and put its front paws on his knee and licked his wrist, and he couldn’t stop.</p>\n<p>Ione sat on the ground beside him and didn’t say anything. She’d learned that from Cas. From the hut. Sometimes you just sat there.</p>\n<p>“What’s the dog called?” she said, when he’d stopped.</p>\n<p>He wiped his face with the back of his hand. He looked down at it.</p>\n<p>“Minister,” said Jonah.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Halloran Crane walked out of Gate Nine last, at eleven minutes past ten on the second night after Continuance Day, eighteen hours after Ione had walked out ahead of him.</p>\n<p>He had stood at the top of Well One for nineteen hours. Every one of them. Every family, every child, every old man in a rope sling, every Warden and every rigger and every person who had ever called Seren Tally a heretic or stepped round her daughter in the lanes — he had seen them onto the ladder, one after another, and said their names, and when the last of them had gone down and the Commons behind him was empty and lit and silent, he had put out his lamp and gone down after them.</p>\n<p>Ondine Rask was waiting for him at the gate.</p>\n<p>Nobody knew what they said. They stood apart, on the concrete, in the rain, for about ten minutes — the Minister of Continuance in her dark suit and the First Steward of Holdfast in his black coat with its gold lamp — and nobody went near them. A Civil Defence sergeant called Kade said afterwards that she’d heard only one thing, as she passed with a stretcher, and that it had been the old man who said it, very gently.</p>\n<p><em>It’s over, Minister. We’re relieved.</em></p>\n<p>Rask resigned nine days later. The Directorate announced it as ill health. By then it didn’t matter what the Directorate announced.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>It came out.</p>\n<p>It came out slowly, and then all at once, the way the Bells had come. Gaunt went first. The day after Gate Nine, Colonel Ansel Gaunt of the Office of Public Calm sat down in a room in the Collar in front of a Civil Defence recorder — not a Calm one — and began to talk, and didn’t stop for three days. He talked about the Margin Review. About Adeyemi. About a revised report and a nice pen. About a chip with a silver seal, and orders with <em>any means</em> underlined. About a Continuance Day address with footage from Year Eighty. He brought documents. He had kept copies of everything, it turned out, for thirty years, in a flat on Tier Six, in biscuit tins.</p>\n<p>“Things that are recorded,” he said, on the recording, “have a way of becoming history. I used to think that was the danger. I find I’ve changed my mind.”</p>\n<p>Then the Log. Ruth Tally’s Log, the whole of it, a hundred and sixty-eight years of it, the pencil getting smaller and smaller — the Tollers let the inquiry copy every page. And Aldous Crane’s private journal, which Halloran Crane handed over himself. <em>I did not give it to the Lamp. I held it.</em> And the recordings: a month of a man talking into a machine in a stone room, in a loft, on a spindle, about rats and weddings and funerals and a game called Drop, and near the end, flatly, plainly, about the Margin Review and what he had signed and why. He’d recorded it for Teo. Teo gave it to the inquiry. She didn’t ask anyone’s permission.</p>\n<p>It didn’t fix anything. Ione had thought, somehow, that it might. That the truth would come out and the Commonweal would look at it and something would change, the way the Lamp had gone out and the Drop had become a road. It didn’t happen like that. The Directorate was still the Directorate. The lights came back on in rotation, four hours a day per tier, from the old fusion plants at Kovač and Tessarine, and people queued for water and argued about whose fault it was. There were riots on Tier Four in the third week. There were strikes. There was an inquiry, and then another inquiry into the inquiry. The client states cut their trunk lines and sent angry envoys. The First Director gave an address about resilience and the spirit of the Tending.</p>\n<p>But things were <em>said</em>. That was the difference. In the lanes of the Stacks, on the terraces, in the queues for water, in the sports halls where people were still being relocated for comfort — people said things out loud. They said <em>default</em>. They said <em>Adeyemi</em>. They said <em>Aldous Crane held the key</em>. They said <em>thirty-one thousand people</em>. They said <em>the night shift</em>.</p>\n<p>On the big poster on the side of the Line station at the foot of the Tier Nine arterial — blue and gold, a child’s face turned up toward a golden flame, THE FUTURE WILL PAY in letters two metres high — somebody had sprayed, in rough white paint, underneath:</p>\n<div class=\"sign\">NOT ANY MORE</div>\n<p>Nobody took it down.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>She took Ruth’s letter to Madeleine Tally on a Tuesday in the fourth week.</p>\n<p>Maddy lived with her grandmother on Tier Nine, in a flat that had been Ruth’s mother’s for thirty years, three streets from where Cas’s Standby flat had been. Ruth’s mother was sixty-seven. She had white hair and Ruth’s mole below her left ear, and when she opened the door and Ione told her who she was — who she <em>was</em>, the chain of it, Ruth and Hal and Hal’s daughter and her son and his, all the way down to a girl with a white streak in her hair standing on the landing with a steel box — she didn’t say anything at all. She just looked at Ione’s face. And then she stood back and let her in.</p>\n<p>Maddy was on the floor of the front room, doing a jigsaw.</p>\n<p>She was seven. She had dark hair in two plaits that someone had done badly, and her mother’s eyes, and she looked up from the jigsaw at the strange short broad woman in the patched clothes and said, “Are you from the Draw? Nana says the Draw’s where Mum is.”</p>\n<p>Ione knelt down on the carpet beside the jigsaw.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this for four weeks. She had thought about what she would say, and how, and she’d practised it, in the Tollers’ hut at night with Tobiah tapping. She had it all ready. She looked at the little girl and none of it was any use.</p>\n<p>“Your mum,” she said, “wrote you some letters.”</p>\n<p>She gave her the last one. Not the Log. Just the one page, the last page, the old woman’s shaking pencil, that Tobiah had copied out very carefully in large clear letters on a clean sheet so that a seven-year-old could read it, with the original folded inside it.</p>\n<p>Maddy read it. It took her a long time. She moved her lips. Her grandmother stood in the doorway with her hand over her mouth.</p>\n<p>“Eighty-eight,” said Maddy at last. She looked up. “Mum’s thirty-eight.”</p>\n<p>“She was thirty-eight,” said Ione, “when she went to work that night. And then she lived for a very long time. In a place where time went faster. She lived her whole life. She got to be eighty-eight.” She paused. “It was a good life. Mostly. She says so. You can read it. It’s all in there.”</p>\n<p>Maddy looked at the letter.</p>\n<p>“Is she coming home?” she said.</p>\n<p>“No,” said Ione. “I’m sorry. She died a long time ago. A long time ago for her. For you, it was the night she went to work.” She didn’t know how to say it better. There wasn’t a better. “But she thought about you every day. Every single day. That’s true. I know it’s true. She wrote it down.”</p>\n<p>Maddy thought about that. She thought about it seriously, turning it over, the way Ione did, and Ione watched her do it and felt something go through her that she didn’t have a name for.</p>\n<p>“Was she happy?” said Maddy.</p>\n<p>“Yes,” said Ione. “She had a husband called Tomas who made her laugh. And three more children. You’ve got two brothers. One of them’s called Hal. And a sister. They’re all — they were all very old, a long time ago. And she had eleven grandchildren. And they had children. And I’m one of them.” She held out her hand, the one without the splint. “I’m your — I don’t know what I am. Tobiah worked it out. It’s a very long word. I’m your family.”</p>\n<p>Maddy looked at her hand. Then she took it, the way children take hands, without any fuss.</p>\n<p>“You’ve got a white bit in your hair,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“Why?”</p>\n<p>“I went somewhere a long way up,” said Ione, “and I was there for a long time.”</p>\n<p>Maddy considered this. Then she turned back to the jigsaw. “Do you want to help? It’s a horse. I can’t find the sky bits.”</p>\n<p>Ione sat on the carpet with Ruth Tally’s daughter for the rest of the afternoon, and helped her find the sky.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>Nell Hale lived on Tier Nine too, now. Sabine had moved them back there when the relocation ended, into Cas’s old Standby flat, because the Ministry had forgotten to reassign it and it was the only flat on the tier with a balcony that faced south. Nell had insisted. She’d said the stars were better that way.</p>\n<p>Teo had been three times. The first time, on the fifth day, she’d gone to the sports hall on Tier Thirty with the recordings in her pocket, as she’d promised, and sat with Nell on a gym mat in the corner while she listened, and Nell had listened to all of them — a month of them, rats and weddings and funerals and Osk and <em>I ate a rat, don’t tell your mother</em> — without stopping, for nine hours, with Teo beside her, and at the end of it she had not cried and she had not hit Teo. She had said, <em>can I keep them?</em> And Teo had said, <em>they’re yours</em>. And Nell had said, <em>he found one. He found a star. I said he might.</em></p>\n<p>The fourth time, Teo brought Ione.</p>\n<p>They went at night. It was one of the dark nights for Tier Nine — no power in the rotation — and the Stacks were black, and the sky over them was black, and in it, the stars. They came every night now. Averna had started, in the weeks since, to go out and look at them the way it used to go out and look at the Quota clocks. People sat on the terraces with blankets. Children lay on their backs on the arterial.</p>\n<p>Nell opened the door. She was ten. She had her mother’s grey eyes and a face that couldn’t leave a question alone, and she looked at Ione in the doorway — at her, all of her, the short broad shape and the patched clothes and the rope, which Ione still wore out of habit, over her shoulder — without blinking.</p>\n<p>“You’re Ione,” she said.</p>\n<p>“Yes.”</p>\n<p>“He said I’d like you.” She said it as if reporting a fact. “On the tapes. He said, <em>I think if you ever meet her, you’ll like her. I don’t know why I think that.</em>”</p>\n<p>“He said that?”</p>\n<p>“He said it on the day with the rat.” Nell stood back from the door. “The stars are out. Do you want to see them? From the balcony? They’re better from the balcony.”</p>\n<p>They sat on the balcony, the three of them, and Sabine brought tea and then went inside and left them. The balcony faced south over the dark Stacks, and the sky over them was full, the pale river of light going across it, the bright white one low in the east that shivered.</p>\n<p>“That’s Sirius,” said Nell. “That’s the Dog Star. It’s the brightest. It’s not the closest, though. It just looks like it is.”</p>\n<p>“He told me,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>“He <em>told</em> you?”</p>\n<p>“On the Rim. In the gallery. He told me their names. I was⁠—” She stopped. “I was very frightened, and he told me their names. So I’d have something to think about.”</p>\n<p>Nell looked at her. Then she looked back up at the sky.</p>\n<p>“Is he dead?” she said.</p>\n<p>She said it very plainly. The way she’d said <em>is it broken</em> on the phone, Teo had told her. The way you’d ask the time.</p>\n<p>Ione had thought about this too. For four weeks. She’d thought about it harder than she’d thought about Maddy. She looked up at the sky, at the thousands of them, and said the only thing she had.</p>\n<p>“No,” she said. “He’s in one moment. It’s — it’s hard to explain. It’s only once. It’s once, and it doesn’t end. He’s not waiting, or stuck, or — it’s not like being in a room. It’s not like the Quiet. It’s just one moment, and he’s in it, and it’s always true.” She was quiet. “It’s only for ever for us. For him it’s once.”</p>\n<p>Nell thought about that. Ione watched her do it.</p>\n<p>“Is it a good moment?” said Nell.</p>\n<p>Ione looked up at the sky.</p>\n<p>“It’s the best one I know,” she said.</p>\n<p>Nell was quiet. Then she moved along the bench, not far, a little, so that her shoulder was against Ione’s arm. She just sat there.</p>\n<p>After a while, Ione pointed.</p>\n<p>“That one,” she said. “Low down. Over there. On its own. The one with nothing near it. What’s that one called?”</p>\n<p>Nell followed her finger. And sat up, delighted.</p>\n<p>“That’s <em>Fomalhaut</em>,” she said. “That’s in the Southern Fish. It’s the loneliest star. That’s what they call it. Because there’s nothing else bright near it, in all that bit of sky. It’s just by itself.”</p>\n<p>“He said,” said Ione. “In the gallery. He said, <em>Fomalhaut, which is lonely</em>.”</p>\n<p>“Did he have a favourite?” said Nell. “I asked him once. He said he didn’t know. He said he’d never looked.”</p>\n<p>“He hadn’t,” said Ione. “So I picked one for him.” She looked at the lonely star, low over the black Stacks, steady, golden, just there. “That one. I told him I’d pick one and look at it every night. That’s his.”</p>\n<p>Nell looked at it for a long time.</p>\n<p>“That’s a good one,” she said at last, seriously. “That’s the right one for him.”</p>\n<p>“I think so.”</p>\n<p>“I’ll look at it too,” said Nell Hale. “Every night. Then there’s two of us.”</p>\n<p>Teo, at the other end of the bench, had turned her face away and was looking very hard at the Stacks.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p>In the sixth week, she went back into the Vault.</p>\n<p>The inquiry wanted a survey. Teo was leading it, because nobody else in the Commonweal would go in, and Teo asked her to come because she knew the spindles, and she said yes because she’d always been going to say yes.</p>\n<p>It was dark. That was the thing she hadn’t been ready for, coming up through the conduit into the Drop District with her lamp. Holdfast was dark. Every lamp in the lanes had burned out long ago. The shrines were dark. The scaffold towers stood up out of the black like the ribs of something dead, and the washing still hung on the lines between them, and the rat pens stood empty with their doors open, and in the Commons the Tollers’ banner still hung from the gantry in the dark, Seren’s line, white on black, and nobody to read it. It was so quiet. No Bell. No Lamp. No hum. The Anvil under her boots was cold.</p>\n<p>It was ordinary. That was the other thing. It weighed what everything weighed. The chalk fell straight.</p>\n<p>They went up Spindle One. There was no need for the Lees now, and no need for Four; Teo’s team had rigged ropes up the inside of the lattice, and it was only climbing, the whole four kilometres of it. It took them two days. They slept in a cache in the old best Lee, and the chalk didn’t hang in the air, and Ione lay awake on the planks for a long time.</p>\n<p>At the top, below the Rim, the gallery was dark. No ghost-light in the windows. Nothing going round. The Carousel was just a corridor — a curving steel corridor round the spindle, with an algae trough along its windows gone brown and dry, and the walls covered in scratched fives, and a pallet of rags, and a pile of smooth stones. Ione walked the whole way round it. It came back to where it started, because it was a ring, the ordinary way. She went out of the inner door onto the Rim and it was the Rim.</p>\n<p>And above the Rim, where the Crown had been, there was the thing.</p>\n<p>Teo’s instruments had been trying to see it for a week, from below, with lights and lasers and sounding gear. They’d got nothing. You shone a light up at it and the light went into it and didn’t come back. It was a sphere. They’d worked that much out, from the shape of the nothing: a sphere about four hundred metres across, hanging in the air at the top of the Vault where the Lamp had been, enclosing the top of Spindle One and the Crown and part of the Rim. It didn’t reflect. It didn’t glow. It didn’t pull or push; the chalk fell straight right up to its edge. The instruments said it had no mass. None. No energy. None. No temperature. They said, as far as they could say anything, that it wasn’t there.</p>\n<p><em>Net zero</em>, the inquiry’s physicists called it, in their report. The debt and the payment, sealed together, balancing. Summing to nothing.</p>\n<p>Ione stood on the Rim in the lamplight and looked at it.</p>\n<p>It was black. Not dark; <em>black</em>. A curve of perfect black rising out of the steel of the Rim in front of her, and going up, and curving over, and blotting out the whole top of the sky. The Rim ran into it and stopped. The stair to the Crown ran into it and stopped. Her lamp’s light went into it and stopped. It was like standing at the edge of the night.</p>\n<p>“Careful,” said Teo, behind her.</p>\n<p>“It’s all right,” said Ione.</p>\n<p>She walked up to it. Along the Rim, past the place where the chalk arrows had been — they were gone; the Lace had taken them, or time had, or they’d been swept away by the thing that had happened here — to the foot of the stair that went up to where the hatch had been. The black began on the third step. She climbed the first two. She stood on the second step, a hand’s width from it.</p>\n<p>She took off her glove.</p>\n<p>She’d thought about this too. She’d thought about it at night, lying in the Tollers’ hut, listening to Tobiah tap. She hadn’t known what it would be. Cold. Hot. Hard. Nothing. Whether her hand would go into it and not come back.</p>\n<p>She put her palm flat against it.</p>\n<p>It wasn’t cold. It wasn’t warm. It wasn’t hard, or soft. It wasn’t anything. Her hand stopped against it, flat, the way it would stop against glass — the way Ben’s had stopped, on the other side of the window in B-7 — and there was nothing there to feel. Not even the feeling of nothing. It was like the instant before you touch something. Like the last moment before a door opens. Like being just about to.</p>\n<p>She stood on the stair with her hand flat on the black and her forehead nearly touching it.</p>\n<p>She didn’t know if he could hear. She was almost sure he couldn’t. <em>Nothing will pass into it.</em> She’d read the plate. But she’d thought about that too, at night, and decided — the way she’d decided on the ladder, coming down in the dark, <em>he saw me</em> — that it didn’t matter whether he could hear. She’d decided you said things anyway. The way Ruth had written her letters. The way the Tollers had counted a little girl’s night.</p>\n<p>“They come back,” she told him. “The stars. Every night. Teo said. I didn’t believe her, the first morning, I thought they’d gone out. But they come back. Every single night.” She was quiet. “I found you one. Fomalhaut. The lonely one. Nell says it’s right. She’s going to look at it too. So there’s two of us.”</p>\n<p>Her palm was flat on the black.</p>\n<p>“It rains,” she said. “It tastes of nothing. And metal, off the roofs. You were right. Jonah’s got the dog. He called it Minister. Teo bought it. Teo’s all right. She’s — she cried on Nell’s balcony and pretended she didn’t. Maddy’s seven. She’s doing a jigsaw of a horse. I helped her with the sky.”</p>\n<p>She stopped. She stood there for a while.</p>\n<p>“I wrote the last page,” she said. “In the Log. Gran said to. She said <em>tell me how it ends</em>. So I did. I wrote it all down. Everything. Properly. Every word.” Her voice was quite steady. “It ends with you.”</p>\n<p>She was quiet then. Behind her, on the Rim, Teo stood with her lamp, and didn’t come any closer, and didn’t say anything either.</p>\n<p>Then Ione Tally took her hand away from the dark, and put her glove back on, and came down the stair, and went home.</p>\n<div class=\"brk\">⁂</div>\n<p><em>From the Crown Log of the Hollowell Draw (the Tally Log), final page, in the hand of Ione Tally:</em></p>\n<blockquote class=\"doc\"><p>Year 169 of the Watch, the last. Bell 123,716, which was the last Bell counted in Holdfast by the old way. After it, they came one a second, and we couldn’t keep up, and then we didn’t need to.</p><p>The Lamp was closed from the Crown by Casimir Hale of Averna, Outside, with the key that was never made. He went up Spindle Four with me through the Lees and the Carousel and the Lace and he set the Clause and he kept the needle on the line by hand until the end, as the plate says. He is the witness. He did not return.</p><p>I want it written down here, because Gran said to write it properly, that he was not paying anything. He told me so. He said he wanted it. He said it was the only thing he had ever wanted that he was sure of. I believe him. I was there.</p><p>He is in one instant. It is the instant I was safe. He chose it.</p><p>It is only for ever for us. For him it is once.</p><p>Ruth — I don’t know if anyone will read this who knew you, except Maddy, and she was seven. But I want to say that the man you saw at the Keystone at 02:09 was real, and his name was Cas, and he was as calm as you said. You were right about him. You were right about everything.</p><p>Gran — this is how it ends.</p><p>We are relieved.</p><span class=\"attr\">I.T.</span></blockquote>"}, {"file": "22-coda-once.md", "label": "Coda", "title": "Once", "part": "Part Four · Relief", "partStart": false, "words": 948, "html": "<blockquote class=\"epi\"><p>It isn’t forever for the one inside. It’s once.</p><span class=\"attr\">Ione Tally</span></blockquote>\n<p class=\"first\">There is a moment.</p>\n<p>In it, a man is kneeling in the dust at the far end of the highest room in the world, with his hand on a small brass wheel.</p>\n<p>The wheel is warm. It has just turned, a single tooth, a little to the left, because the needle was drifting and he saw which way it would go next and got there first. Now the needle is on the line. Exactly on it. Not a hair either side. It is the best he has ever held it. It will never be held better.</p>\n<p>Through the great window, the Lamp has begun. At its heart, where the hard white point was, there is the first edge of something — a thread of gold, the very first thread, the first instant of a light that would have been bright enough to end the world. It is only just beginning. It is always only just beginning. The Lace hangs round it, singing, every branch of it lit gold at the tips, as if a frost had caught the morning.</p>\n<p>And down, and to the left, past the glare, on the black lattice of Spindle Four, below the Breach, there is a small soft light.</p>\n<p>Hers.</p>\n<p>It’s going down. Steadily. A flight of stairs, in the Lees, out of the Fold, the way it should. Safe.</p>\n<p>And just before this — it’s here too; everything is — the light stopped on a walkway, and a small grey arm came up. The palm out. Held there, toward the Rim, toward the Crown, until it shook. She was looking up at him. He saw it. Of everything, it’s the thing he sees most clearly, clearer than the needle, clearer than the gold.</p>\n<p>He saw.</p>\n<p>He isn’t counting. That’s the first thing he would notice, if there were a next thing to notice after the first. For the first time since 02:14 on the morning of Continuance Day, for the first time in fifteen years, perhaps in his life, there is nothing to count. Not the hammer. Not rungs. Not seconds. There is one of everything. There is one moment, and he is in it, and it is one.</p>\n<p><em>Count me</em>, she’d said. <em>I’m one.</em></p>\n<p>Everything is here.</p>\n<p>That’s the other thing. Nothing has to be remembered, because nothing is behind him; it is all simply here, in the moment, the way the whole Vault was in every thread of the Lace. Nell on the phone in a gym with a lizard in a box — <em>you don’t know that; there might be</em>. Teo’s tea, which was mostly a structural material. A boy on a flatbed hugging a cradle: <em>is there a form?</em> A palm on the other side of a pane of glass, and grease-pencil letters fading on it. Osk kneeling on the floor of a stone room: <em>you don’t push up; you stack your bones</em>. An old woman with eyes like a bird, correcting him to the minute. Soup. Rat. A rooftop swaying with stamping feet and a cord tied round his wrist and taken off again. A hut in a Lee and a heart going too fast. <em>I’m glad it was you.</em> The cord tied round his wrist again, and this time left there — he can feel it; flat and neat and interlocking, like two hands clasped. <em>I’ll hold your rope.</em> A visor pressed hard against his temple for a second, cold. <em>Haul.</em></p>\n<p><em>So this is the start.</em></p>\n<p><em>This is the start.</em></p>\n<p>All of it. At once. Not going. Not coming. Here.</p>\n<p>And there is one more thing.</p>\n<p>At the Keystone, beside him, very close — so close that she is kneeling almost where he is kneeling — there is a woman in a white coat. Not light. Not a ghost made of nine minutes going round. A woman. Thirty-eight years old, with her hair pinned up and a mole below her left ear and the side of her thumb raw where she’s bitten it. She has just wrenched up a steel plate. She has just watched a key fall forward out of the world. It is 02:09 on the night of the Kindling, and she is looking up at the place beside the Keystone, at a man who isn’t there, and he is there, and she is looking at him.</p>\n<p>He turns his head and looks at her.</p>\n<p>He knows her. He has read her whole life in pencil. He has watched her close her eyes for half a second a hundred and seventy-five thousand times, through someone else’s eyes. He knows that she is about to pull a hammer and go down a lift and live for fifty years in a city of lamps and write letters to a little girl who will stay seven for all of them. He knows that she will marry a man who makes her laugh. He knows that she will think about this — about him, about this one look — every day for the rest of her life, and write it down once, in the thirty-first year, and never tell anyone.</p>\n<p>He’s sorry for her. He’s glad of her. Both at once. He’d tell her, if there were time.</p>\n<p>There isn’t time. There’s something better than time. There’s this.</p>\n<p>The needle is on the line. The gold is beginning. Her light is on the spindle, going down, and she raised her hand to him, and she is safe. He knows she is safe. He will always know it. That is the moment. That is the whole of it.</p>\n<p>It isn’t for ever. Not for him. It’s once.</p>\n<p>It is long enough.</p>"}]};
